window.ATLAS_DATA = {"meta":{"title":"A Categorical Atlas of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning: Cones, Orbits, and Gauges","built":"2026-10-06","counts":{"methods":382,"papers":138,"hf_peft":82,"classified":382,"propositions":41}},"methods":[{"id":"residual-adapter","name":"Residual Adapters","full_name":"Residual adapter modules for multi-domain ResNets","paper_title":"Learning multiple visual domains with residual adapters","authors":"Rebuffi et al.","year":2017,"venue":"NeurIPS 2017","arxiv":"1705.08045","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08045","code_url":"https://github.com/srebuffi/residual_adapters","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"g(x;\\alpha) = x + \\alpha * x,\\quad \\alpha\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times C\\times 1\\times 1}\\ (\\text{bank of }1{\\times}1\\text{ filters, wrapped with domain-specific BN});\\quad \\text{frozen: domain-agnostic }3{\\times}3\\text{ filters } w_1,w_2;\\ \\text{trained per domain: } \\alpha,\\ \\text{BN scale/shift}","trainable_params":"2(C^2+5C)\\ \\text{per residual block (vs. } 2(h^2C^2+hC)\\text{ shared, } h=3)","locus":"inside every ResNet residual block, in series after each 3x3 convolution (with batch-norm)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Serial insertion of a linear near-identity endomorphism \\(I+\\alpha\\) of the \\(C\\)-channel feature space (a per-pixel \\(C\\times C\\) matrix, i.e. 1x1 conv) composed with domain-specific BN affine maps; full-rank, no bottleneck, no nonlinearity. The CVPR 2018 follow-up (Rebuffi et al., arXiv:1803.10082) moves the 1x1 adapter in parallel to the 3x3 convolution.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"At inference every piece is linear or affine: BN, the 1x1 adapter \\(I+\\alpha\\) and the BN that follows sit between a frozen 3x3 convolution and the ReLU (Fig. 2). They therefore fold exactly into a domain-specific 3x3 filter bank plus bias (per tap \\(W' = D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)W\\)). The paper keeps them unmerged so that one backbone is shared across domains.","identity_at_init":"Exactly the identity when \\(\\alpha=0\\) (residual form); in the paper adapters are first co-trained with the backbone on ImageNet, then only domain-specific parameters (with strong weight decay on \\(\\alpha\\)) are trained per domain.","key_idea":"Split a multi-domain ResNet into frozen domain-agnostic filters plus tiny domain-specific residual 1x1 adapters that collapse to the identity at zero, so one network can be steered across very different visual domains; this is the origin of the term 'adapter' later imported to NLP by Houlsby et al.","results_note":"On the Visual Decathlon (10 image domains) residual adapters adding ~11% parameters per domain are close to or better than full per-domain fine-tuning while avoiding forgetting.","relations":[],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08045","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1705.08045","https://github.com/srebuffi/residual_adapters"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Sylvestre-Alvise Rebuffi","Hakan Bilen","Andrea Vedaldi"],"bibkey":"rebuffi2017learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{rebuffi2017learning,\n  title = {{Learning multiple visual domains with residual adapters}},\n  author = {Sylvestre-Alvise Rebuffi and Hakan Bilen and Andrea Vedaldi},\n  year = {2017},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1705.08045},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08045}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Inserted as a linear residual box \\(I+\\alpha\\) with domain-specific BN (a pointed extension, neutral at \\(\\alpha=0\\)). Every piece is affine and sits between a frozen \\(3\\times3\\) conv and the ReLU, so it descends along the merge \\(\\mu\\) to a weight-space object, as SSF and (IA)\\(^3\\) do in VIII.B.","shape":"Merged, per tap: \\(W'=D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)W_0=G\\,W_0\\), with \\(G\\) ranging over all of \\(M_C(\\mathbb R)\\). The image is the linear left ideal \\(\\{GW_0\\}\\) (the closure of the \\(GL_C\\) orbit), of dimension \\(C^2\\) when the unfolding \\(W_0\\in\\mathbb R^{C\\times9C}\\) has full row rank.","base_point":"\\(G\\mapsto GW_0\\) is linear and injective, so every point, including \\(\\alpha=0\\), is regular.","gauge":"The two BN scales \\(D_1,D_2\\) are redundant with \\(\\alpha\\): \\((\\alpha,D_1,D_2)\\mapsto D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)\\) has a \\(2C\\)-dimensional generic fibre swept by a diagonal torus acting affinely on \\(\\alpha\\). The two BN shifts collapse to one bias.","covariance":"\\(M_C\\,gW_0h^{-1}=g\\,(M_C W_0)\\,h^{-1}\\) for all \\(g,h\\): the left ideal is basis-free.","merge":"Folds into the neighbouring frozen \\(3\\times3\\) conv by R1 (\\(W'=D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)W\\)), with the BN shift in the conv bias. The paper keeps it unmerged so that one backbone is shared across domains.","rebasing":"\\(G_2(G_1W_0)=(G_2G_1)W_0\\): the shape is the full monoid \\(M_C\\), independent of the base, so cycles gain nothing."},"categorical_reading":"A linear pointed extension that descends along the merge to the R-action object \\(W_0\\mapsto G\\,W_0\\), the orbit closure of \\(GL_C\\) acting on output channels. It is LoRA-FA whose frozen down-projection is \\(W_0\\) itself, and the full-matrix ancestor of the diagonal methods (IA)\\(^3\\) and SSF.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,D_1,D_2)=D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)-I\\ \\ \\text{as the trainable }B,\\ \\text{with }A_0=W_0,\\ r=C","note":"\\(GW_0=W_0+(G-I)W_0\\), so it is LoRA-FA with frozen \\(A_0=W_0\\) (unfolded, \\(C\\times9C\\)), pointed at \\(B=0\\). Here \\(W_0\\) is the base conv with the base BN affine maps folded in (so \\(D_1^0=D_2^0=I\\) and \\(h(q_0)=0\\)); the merged BN shift is a bias change, so the exact target is LoRA-FA\\((W_0)\\oplus\\) BitFit (A5). Composing with LoRA-FA\\((A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA (A2) gives residual adapter \\(\\to\\) LoRA\\(_C\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\((G-I)W_0\\) has rank up to \\(C\\), so no LoRA\\(_r\\) with \\(r<C\\) simulates it."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","test":"O2","reason":"Houlsby's correction \\(U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\) lies in the \\(r\\)-dimensional \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,U\\); the residual adapter's full-rank linear correction \\(\\alpha x\\) does not, for \\(r<C\\)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"houlsby-adapter","name":"Adapter (Houlsby)","full_name":"Bottleneck adapter modules (serial, two per layer)","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning for NLP","authors":"Houlsby et al.","year":2019,"venue":"ICML 2019","arxiv":"1902.00751","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00751","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/adapter-bert","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = F(x)\\quad \\text{(frozen sublayer, up to its output projection)};\\quad y \\leftarrow y + W_{up}\\,\\sigma(W_{down}\\,y + b_{down}) + b_{up};\\quad \\text{then } \\mathrm{LN}(x+y);\\quad W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ \\text{trained};\\quad \\text{LayerNorm and head also trained};\\quad \\text{all pretrained weights frozen}","trainable_params":"2\\,(2rd + r + d)\\ \\text{per layer (two adapters)} + \\text{LayerNorm parameters}","locus":"two serial adapters per Transformer layer: after the attention output projection and after the FFN, each before the residual add and LayerNorm","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Bottleneck MLP inserted serially with an internal residual connection: each frozen sublayer \\(F\\) is post-composed with the near-identity endomorphism \\(\\mathrm{id}+W_{up}\\sigma W_{down}\\) of \\(\\mathbb{R}^d\\) (rank-\\(r\\) bottleneck, nonlinear).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The nonlinearity inside the bottleneck prevents folding the adapter into the frozen linear weights, so inference keeps the extra sequential modules and latency.","identity_at_init":"Near-identity, not exact. Projection weights are drawn from a zero-mean Gaussian with std \\(10^{-2}\\) truncated at 2 std, and biases are zero, so the adapter output is close to 0 and the internal skip makes the module approximately the identity. The paper's init-scale sweep finds performance robust for std \\(\\le 10^{-2}\\) and degrading for larger scales.","key_idea":"Insert small bottleneck modules with a skip connection into every Transformer layer and train only them (plus LayerNorms), so one frozen BERT serves many tasks with a few percent new parameters per task.","results_note":"On GLUE with BERT-Large, adapters reach a mean score of 80.0 vs 80.4 for full fine-tuning while adding 3.6% parameters per task, and remain competitive across 26 text-classification tasks.","relations":[{"target":"residual-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Transfers Rebuffi et al.'s residual adapter idea to Transformers, adding a bottleneck and a nonlinearity."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"dual-to","note":"Serial (post-composition) vs parallel (sum with the sublayer) insertion of the same bottleneck module, as analysed by He et al. (2022)."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main alternative PEFT family; LoRA is a linear, parallel, mergeable update."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00751","https://arxiv.org/html/1902.00751","https://github.com/google-research/adapter-bert","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Neil Houlsby","Andrei Giurgiu","Stanislaw Jastrzebski","Bruna Morrone","Quentin de Laroussilhe","Andrea Gesmundo","Mona Attariyan","Sylvain Gelly"],"bibkey":"houlsby2019parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{houlsby2019parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning for NLP}},\n  author = {Neil Houlsby and Andrei Giurgiu and Stanislaw Jastrzebski and Bruna Morrone and Quentin de Laroussilhe and Andrea Gesmundo and Mona Attariyan and Sylvain Gelly},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1902.00751},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.00751}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"New serial boxes \\(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\) after the attention and FFN projections; \\(U=0\\) makes them the identity.","shape":"No weight-space image: the boxes are non-linear and sit on an extension, compared through \\(\\Phi_{\\tilde f}\\).","base_point":"Neutral at \\(U=0\\), \\(b=0\\); only \\(U\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4, function-level). The paper's init (std \\(10^{-2}\\), truncated) is near-neutral: the \\(\\approx\\) convention, classifying the exactly pointed variant as VIII.B does.","gauge":"The reference implementation (google-research/adapter-bert, feedforward_adapter) uses GELU, and AdapterHub's Houlsby default is swish, so by Lemma VI.2(c) only the discrete \\(S_r\\) on hidden units survives: no continuous gauge. VIII.B's 'perm \\(\\ltimes\\mathbb R_{>0}^r\\)' is the ReLU case (the positive per-unit slide R3 through \\((D,b_{down},U)\\)), which applies to Pfeiffer-type ReLU bottlenecks, not to Houlsby as published.","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire. VIII.B leaves this cell blank.","merge":"Thm VI.3(c): for generic \\(U,D\\) the modified box function is non-affine in the box input, so no box-local merge exists.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained adapter into an enlarged base, insert a fresh one) the stages compose, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ F\\) with \\(S=\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\): the action-type closure (generated monoid)."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed extension that post-composes each frozen sublayer with the near-identity endomorphism \\(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\), so it is of action type on the extension, and other methods meet it only through the realisation. The non-affine \\(\\sigma\\) is exactly the M∞ obstruction of Thm VI.3(c); with \\(\\sigma=\\mathrm{id}\\) it would be the serial linear adapter of Prop VI.4(b), isomorphic to LoRA when \\(W_0\\) is invertible.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"For generic \\(U,D\\) the edit \\(y\\mapsto y+U\\sigma(Dy)\\) is non-affine, so the realised sublayer \\((\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot))\\circ F\\) is not box-locally mergeable (Thm VI.3(c)), while LoRA's realisations keep every linear box linear."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"The serial correction \\(U\\sigma(D\\,F(x))\\) reads the sublayer output, a parallel correction \\(U'\\sigma(D'x)\\) reads its input. For a non-linear FFN \\(F\\) the two families are generically disjoint off \\(U=0\\) (the serial/parallel split of He et al.)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"pfeiffer-adapter","name":"Pfeiffer Adapter","full_name":"Pfeiffer (single sequential bottleneck) adapter, SeqBn","paper_title":"AdapterFusion: Non-Destructive Task Composition for Transfer Learning","authors":"Pfeiffer et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EACL 2021","arxiv":"2005.00247","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247","code_url":"https://github.com/adapter-hub/adapters","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"a = \\mathrm{LN}\\big(x + F(x)\\big)\\ (F = \\text{frozen FFN sublayer, LN its frozen post-LayerNorm});\\quad y = W_{up}\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(W_{down}\\,a + b_{down}) + b_{up} + F(x);\\quad h = \\mathrm{LN}(y + x);\\quad W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ \\text{trained; backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"2rd + r + d\\ \\text{per layer (one adapter; default reduction factor } d/r = 16)","locus":"one adapter per Transformer layer, only after the feed-forward block","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Same algebraic shape as the Houlsby adapter (serial residual bottleneck MLP) but inserted once per layer, after the FFN, with the residual taken from the FFN sublayer; found by an architecture search over positions, residuals, LayerNorms, reduction factors and nonlinearities.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear bottleneck in series with the FFN cannot be absorbed into frozen weights.","identity_at_init":"Approximate only. The paper adds 'randomly initialized' adapters, and the AdapterHub default (init_weights='bert') is \\(N(0, 0.02^2)\\) weights with zero biases. The bottleneck output is small but not zero, and there is no exact zero-init guarantee.","key_idea":"A single adapter after the FFN performs on par with Houlsby's two adapters per layer while halving the inserted modules and compute; it became the default sequential adapter (SeqBnConfig) of AdapterHub and the building block of AdapterFusion and MAD-X.","results_note":"In the paper's architecture search on SST-2, CommonsenseQA and argument mining, the single post-FFN adapter matched the Houlsby configuration with half as many adapters.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps only the FFN-side adapter of the Houlsby design."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247","https://arxiv.org/html/2005.00247","https://arxiv.org/html/2005.00052","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.06820"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Jonas Pfeiffer","Aishwarya Kamath","Andreas Rücklé","Kyunghyun Cho","Iryna Gurevych"],"bibkey":"pfeiffer2020adapterfusion","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pfeiffer2020adapterfusion,\n  title = {{AdapterFusion: Non-Destructive Task Composition for Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Jonas Pfeiffer and Aishwarya Kamath and Andreas Rücklé and Kyunghyun Cho and Iryna Gurevych},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL)},\n  eprint = {2005.00247},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"One serial ReLU bottleneck per layer after the FFN; \\(U=0\\) is the identity.","shape":"Function-level only (non-linear inserted box).","base_point":"Neutral at \\(U=0\\); AdapterHub's default \\(\\mathcal N(0,0.02^2)\\) init is near-neutral (\\(\\approx\\) convention).","gauge":"ReLU: the positive per-unit slide \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}^r\\) (R3) on \\((D,b_{down},U)\\), times \\(S_r\\).","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"Thm VI.3(c): for generic \\(U,D\\) the modified box function is non-affine in the box input, so no box-local merge exists.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained adapter into an enlarged base, insert a fresh one) the stages compose, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ F\\) with \\(S=\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\): the action-type closure (generated monoid)."},"categorical_reading":"The same serial bottleneck extension as Houlsby, at one site per layer, but it reads the post-LN block output \\(\\mathrm{LN}(x+F(x))\\) and writes into \\(F(x)\\). That is a different wire, so it is Houlsby's sibling in VIII.B rather than a sub-object: no freezing of Houlsby's coordinates produces it.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"Pfeiffer's correction depends on \\(x\\) through \\(\\mathrm{LN}(x+F(x))\\), Houlsby's FFN correction only through \\(F(x)\\). A width-\\(r\\) bottleneck of \\(F(x)\\) cannot recover the residual \\(x\\), so Houlsby generically does not reproduce Pfeiffer's functions."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Non-affine inserted box (Thm VI.3(c)); no weight of the original architecture realises it."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"mad-x","name":"MAD-X","full_name":"Multiple ADapters for Cross-lingual transfer (language, task and invertible adapters)","paper_title":"MAD-X: An Adapter-Based Framework for Multi-Task Cross-Lingual Transfer","authors":"Pfeiffer et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EMNLP 2020","arxiv":"2005.00052","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00052","code_url":"https://github.com/adapter-hub/adapters","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{LA}_l(h_l,\\rho_l) = U_l\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(D_l h_l) + \\rho_l,\\quad \\mathrm{TA}_l(h_l,\\rho_l) = U'_l\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(D'_l\\,\\mathrm{LA}_l(h_l,\\rho_l)\\big) + \\rho_l;\\quad A_{inv}:\\ o_1 = F(e_2)+e_1,\\ o_2 = G(o_1)+e_2,\\ \\ A_{inv}^{-1}:\\ e_2 = o_2 - G(o_1),\\ e_1 = o_1 - F(e_2);\\ \\ F,G\\ \\text{bottleneck MLPs};\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"\\underbrace{2r_Ld}_{\\text{language}} + \\underbrace{2r_Td}_{\\text{task}}\\ \\text{per layer} + \\underbrace{\\tfrac{d^2}{2}}_{\\text{invertible (once)}}\\ (\\text{+ biases; paper: } r_L = d/2,\\ r_T = d/16)","locus":"language and task adapters stacked after the FFN in every layer; invertible adapter on the input embeddings with its inverse before the tied output embeddings","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Serial composition (stacking) \\(\\mathrm{TA}\\circ\\mathrm{LA}\\) of two bottleneck adapters that share the FFN residual, plus an invertible NICE additive-coupling bijection \\(A_{inv}\\) on token embeddings whose exact inverse is applied before the tied output embedding; language modules are swappable at inference (zero-shot transfer by replacing \\(\\mathrm{LA}^{src}\\) with \\(\\mathrm{LA}^{tgt}\\)).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear bottlenecks and coupling layers must be executed at inference; swapping modules is the point of the design.","identity_at_init":"Not explicitly enforced: adapters use the standard small random init of Pfeiffer adapters; the coupling \\(A_{inv}\\) equals the identity only when \\(F=G=0\\).","key_idea":"Factor cross-lingual transfer into separately trained, swappable language adapters (MLM on unlabeled text), task adapters stacked on top, and invertible embedding adapters, so a task adapter trained in one language transfers zero-shot to another by swapping the language adapter.","results_note":"With XLM-R Base, full MAD-X improves over XLM-R by more than 5 F1 on average for cross-lingual NER (largest gains on low-resource and unseen languages) and is competitive on XQuAD.","relations":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Language and task adapters use the Pfeiffer architecture."},{"target":"adapterfusion","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative way to compose adapters: stacking/swapping instead of attention-based fusion."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00052","https://arxiv.org/html/2005.00052","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/adapter_composition.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Jonas Pfeiffer","Ivan Vulić","Iryna Gurevych","Sebastian Ruder"],"bibkey":"pfeiffer2020mad","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pfeiffer2020mad,\n  title = {{MAD-X: An Adapter-Based Framework for Multi-Task Cross-Lingual Transfer}},\n  author = {Jonas Pfeiffer and Ivan Vulić and Iryna Gurevych and Sebastian Ruder},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2005.00052},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00052}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Stacked serial ReLU bottlenecks \\(\\mathrm{TA}\\circ\\mathrm{LA}\\) plus an invertible NICE coupling on the embeddings, with its inverse before the tied output embedding; all are the identity at \\(U=0\\), \\(F=G=0\\).","shape":"Function-level (non-linear adapters and coupling layers).","base_point":"Neutral at \\(U_{LA}=U_{TA}=0\\), \\(F=G=0\\); the paper uses the small random Pfeiffer init (\\(\\approx\\) convention).","gauge":"ReLU positive per-unit slides in both bottlenecks (R3).","covariance":"The additive coupling \\(o_1=F(e_2)+e_1\\), \\(o_2=G(o_1)+e_2\\) needs a fixed split of the embedding coordinates into halves. This block partition is not in the network and breaks neuron permutations across halves: \\(\\Pi\\).","merge":"Thm VI.3(c): for generic \\(U,D\\) the modified box function is non-affine in the box input, so no box-local merge exists. The coupling must also be executed.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level MAD-X already is two stages of serial composition (LA then TA), and NICE couplings compose as a group of bijections."},"categorical_reading":"A word in the composition monoid of serial extensions: \\(\\mathrm{TA}\\circ\\mathrm{LA}\\) is two composition stages, and swapping \\(\\mathrm{LA}^{src}\\) for \\(\\mathrm{LA}^{tgt}\\) re-bases the task adapter on a different language stage. The invertible adapter conjugates the whole network by a NICE coupling, \\(E^\\top A_{inv}^{-1}\\circ\\mathrm{body}\\circ A_{inv}E\\), an honest group element whose coordinate split is imposed structure.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"adapterfusion","test":"O1","reason":"MAD-X composes adapters (input-independent stacking); AdapterFusion's output is, per token, a softmax-convex combination of \\(V\\)-projected adapter outputs. A composite \\(\\mathrm{TA}(\\mathrm{LA}(h))\\) is generically not such a combination."},{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"The invertible adapter edits the tied input and output embeddings, which a per-layer Pfeiffer adapter never touches. So MAD-X's realisations leave Pfeiffer's image, even with LA neutral."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adapterfusion","name":"AdapterFusion","full_name":"AdapterFusion: attention-based composition of task adapters","paper_title":"AdapterFusion: Non-Destructive Task Composition for Transfer Learning","authors":"Pfeiffer et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EACL 2021","arxiv":"2005.00247","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247","code_url":"https://github.com/adapter-hub/adapters","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"s_{l,t} = \\mathrm{softmax}_{n}\\big(h_{l,t}^{\\top}Q_l \\cdot z_{l,t,n}^{\\top}K_l\\big),\\quad z'_{l,t,n} = z_{l,t,n}^{\\top}V_l,\\quad o_{l,t} = \\sum_{n=1}^{N} s_{l,t,n}\\,z'_{l,t,n};\\quad \\text{frozen: backbone and } N \\text{ trained task adapters } \\Phi_n;\\ \\text{trained: } Q_l,K_l,V_l\\ (\\text{and head})","trainable_params":"3d^2 + 3d\\ \\text{per layer}\\ (Q_l,K_l,V_l\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d})","locus":"a fusion layer after the \\(N\\) parallel task adapters at the FFN position of every layer","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Input-dependent convex combination: a dot-product attention whose query is the FFN output and whose keys/values are the outputs of \\(N\\) frozen adapters, i.e. a context-dependent point of the simplex \\(\\Delta^{N-1}\\) mixing \\(V\\)-projected adapter outputs (two-stage: knowledge extraction, then composition).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"All \\(N\\) adapters and the attention mixer run at inference; the mixing weights depend on the input.","identity_at_init":"No: \\(Q\\) and \\(K\\) are random; \\(V\\) is initialized to an identity diagonal plus 1e-6 noise (and L2-regularized toward it), so at init the layer outputs an attention-weighted average of adapter outputs, not the base model.","key_idea":"Separate knowledge extraction (train one adapter per task, non-destructively) from knowledge composition (learn a small attention layer that mixes the frozen adapters per token for a target task), avoiding catastrophic forgetting and task interference of multi-task fine-tuning.","results_note":"Across 16 NLU tasks with BERT-base, AdapterFusion improves over full fine-tuning and over single-task and multi-task adapters on the target task.","relations":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Fuses single-task Pfeiffer adapters."},{"target":"adamix","type":"competes-with","note":"Both mix several adapters per layer; AdaMix routes stochastically and averages weights instead of attending over outputs."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"composes bottleneck adapters"},{"target":"mole","type":"competes-with","note":"learned gating over outputs of frozen trained modules"},{"target":"attempt","type":"competes-with","note":"the prompt-tuning analogue: attention over frozen source prompts"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247","https://arxiv.org/html/2005.00247","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/adapter_composition.html","https://aclanthology.org/2021.eacl-main.39/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","composition"],"authors_full":["Jonas Pfeiffer","Aishwarya Kamath","Andreas Rücklé","Kyunghyun Cho","Iryna Gurevych"],"bibkey":"pfeiffer2020adapterfusion","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pfeiffer2020adapterfusion,\n  title = {{AdapterFusion: Non-Destructive Task Composition for Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Jonas Pfeiffer and Aishwarya Kamath and Andreas Rücklé and Kyunghyun Cho and Iryna Gurevych},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL)},\n  eprint = {2005.00247},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.00247}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(T=X\\) (\\(F_X\\) in VIII.B): per token, a softmax point of the simplex \\(\\Delta^{N-1}\\) mixes the \\(V\\)-projected outputs of \\(N\\) frozen adapters, so the effective adapter depends on the input.","shape":"Function-level: an input-dependent mixture of non-linear adapters on an extension.","base_point":"A neutral point exists (\\(V=0\\) kills the fusion output), but the init is \\(V\\approx I\\), random \\(Q,K\\). At init the layer outputs an attention average of the frozen adapters' outputs, not the base model. VIII.B leaves this blank.","gauge":"\\(Q,K\\) enter only through the bilinear form \\(h^\\top QK^\\top z\\), so \\(GL_d\\) acts by \\((Q,K)\\mapsto(Qg,Kg^{-\\top})\\): the query–key gauge on an internal wire.","covariance":"\\(Q,K,V\\) are dense \\(d\\times d\\) maps, so the mixer is basis-free on the hidden wire.","merge":"Input-dependent softmax mixing; even a constant gate would leave a mixture of non-linear adapters, so it is M∞ in all cases (VIII.B, axes.json).","rebasing":"n/a: a family over inputs with nothing merged into the base."},"categorical_reading":"A family over inputs: \\(x\\mapsto s(x)\\in\\Delta^{N-1}\\) picks, per token, a point of the simplex over \\(N\\) frozen adapters, a \\(T\\)-point of the \\(N\\)-fold adapter extension with \\(T=X\\), not an object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\). It separates knowledge extraction (the frozen adapters) from composition (the \\(T\\)-point), and its only continuous gauge is the query–key slide.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"adamix","test":"O1","reason":"AdaMix deploys one adapter with input-independent weights; it cannot realise AdapterFusion's input-dependent softmax mixtures of \\(N\\) adapters."},{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"LoraHub's composition weights are constants chosen post hoc (and act on weight-space LoRAs); AdapterFusion's are a function of the token, so its realisations lie outside any constant combination."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adapterdrop","name":"AdapterDrop","full_name":"AdapterDrop: dropping adapters from lower Transformer layers","paper_title":"AdapterDrop: On the Efficiency of Adapters in Transformers","authors":"Rücklé et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EMNLP 2021","arxiv":"2010.11918","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11918","code_url":"https://github.com/adapter-hub/adapters","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h^{(l)} = \\begin{cases} F_l\\big(h^{(l-1)}\\big) & l \\le n \\\\ A_l\\big(F_l(h^{(l-1)})\\big) & l > n \\end{cases},\\qquad n \\text{ fixed (specialized) or } n\\sim\\mathcal{U}\\{0,\\dots,11\\}\\ \\text{per batch (robust)};\\ \\text{backbone frozen, adapters } A_l \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"(L-n)\\,(2rd + r + d)\\ \\text{for specialized AdapterDrop (adapters only in the top } L-n \\text{ layers)};\\ L\\,(2rd+r+d)\\ \\text{for robust AdapterDrop (all adapters trained, } n \\text{ resampled per batch)}","locus":"adapters kept only in the upper \\(L-n\\) layers; the first \\(n\\) layers are the unmodified backbone","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Restricts the set of insertion sites to a final segment of the layer chain, so the composite factors as (adapted top) \\(\\circ\\) (shared frozen bottom); the shared prefix can be computed once for many tasks. The robust variant samples \\(n\\) per batch (stochastic depth over adapters). Also prunes AdapterFusion to its most-attended adapters.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Remaining adapters are ordinary nonlinear bottleneck adapters.","identity_at_init":"Inherited from the underlying adapter initialization (approximate identity); the first \\(n\\) layers are exactly the backbone.","key_idea":"Lower layers matter little for adapters, so removing their adapters (during training and/or inference) speeds up training and multi-task inference with small accuracy loss; one robustly trained model can be run with any number of dropped layers.","results_note":"With RoBERTa-base on GLUE, dropping adapters from the first five layers keeps 97.1% (specialized) / 95.4% (robust) of performance and makes simultaneous inference on 8 tasks 39% faster; pruning AdapterFusion from 8 to 2 adapters keeps performance.","relations":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Applied to Pfeiffer and Houlsby adapters."},{"target":"adapterfusion","type":"builds-on","note":"AdapterFusion pruning removes the least-activated adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11918","https://arxiv.org/html/2010.11918"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Andreas Rücklé","Gregor Geigle","Max Glockner","Tilman Beck","Jonas Pfeiffer","Nils Reimers","Iryna Gurevych"],"bibkey":"ruckle2020adapterdrop","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ruckle2020adapterdrop,\n  title = {{AdapterDrop: On the Efficiency of Adapters in Transformers}},\n  author = {Andreas Rücklé and Gregor Geigle and Max Glockner and Tilman Beck and Jonas Pfeiffer and Nils Reimers and Iryna Gurevych},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2010.11918},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11918}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A placement and dropping recipe on Pfeiffer/Houlsby adapters; the coordinates are those of the underlying adapter.","shape":"Function-level, as the underlying adapter (non-linear inserted boxes).","base_point":"Inherited from the adapter; the dropped bottom layers are exactly the backbone.","gauge":"Inherited: the ReLU per-unit slide for Pfeiffer adapters (the coordinates recorded here); with Houlsby's GELU adapters only discrete \\(S_r\\) survives.","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"The remaining adapters are ordinary non-linear bottlenecks (Thm VI.3(c)).","rebasing":"n/a (M∞), as for the underlying adapter."},"categorical_reading":"Specialised AdapterDrop is the sub-object of the adapter extension obtained by freezing the bottom-\\(n\\) adapters at their neutral point (A2). That also truncates the backprop cone (Obs IV.4) and makes the frozen bottom segment shared across tasks. Robust AdapterDrop is a family over the drop depth \\(n\\in\\{0,\\dots,11\\}\\), trained jointly.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_{>n})=\\big((U_l{=}0,\\,D_l{=}D_l^0)_{l\\le n},\\ q_{>n}\\big)","note":"Specialised AdapterDrop is the Pfeiffer adapter with the bottom-\\(n\\) adapters frozen at the neutral point; pointed, since all-neutral maps to all-neutral."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_{>n})=\\big((U_l{=}0,\\,D_l{=}D_l^0)_{l\\le n},\\ q_{>n}\\big)","note":"The same freezing when AdapterDrop is applied to Houlsby adapters (two per layer)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"k-adapter","name":"K-Adapter","full_name":"K-Adapter: knowledge-specific side adapters","paper_title":"K-Adapter: Infusing Knowledge into Pre-Trained Models with Adapters","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2020,"venue":"Findings of ACL-IJCNLP 2021","arxiv":"2002.01808","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01808","code_url":"https://github.com/microsoft/k-adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"a_k = \\mathcal{A}_k\\big([\\,h_{l_k}\\,;\\,a_{k-1}\\,]\\big),\\ \\ \\mathcal{A}_k = \\text{down-proj} \\to N\\ \\text{Transformer layers} \\to \\text{up-proj (skip across the projections)},\\ \\ l_k\\in\\{0,11,23\\};\\quad \\text{output } [\\,h_L\\,;\\,a_K\\,];\\quad \\text{knowledge pre-training: backbone frozen, } \\mathcal{A}\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"\\approx 42\\text{M per knowledge adapter during knowledge pre-training (RoBERTa-Large frozen; } K=3 \\text{ adapter layers at backbone layers } \\{0,11,23\\},\\ N=2,\\ H_A=768,\\ H_d=768,\\ H_u=1024);\\ \\text{downstream fine-tuning updates RoBERTa with the adapters frozen}","locus":"outside plug-in (side network) reading hidden states of backbone layers \\(\\{0, 11, 23\\}\\); its output is concatenated with the backbone's final hidden state","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Side network: a separate small Transformer stack that reads (by concatenation) intermediate backbone states and never writes back into the backbone; the final representation is the concatenation (a product) of backbone and side-network features, and multiple knowledge adapters are combined by further concatenation.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"A separate network with its own Transformer layers runs alongside the backbone.","identity_at_init":"No: adapters are randomly initialized; the backbone representation is left untouched (outputs are concatenated, not added), so original features stay available but the head sees a new concatenated feature.","key_idea":"Inject different kinds of knowledge (factual relations from Wikidata, dependency syntax) into independent side adapters trained with the backbone frozen, enabling continual, non-interfering knowledge infusion. Note: for downstream tasks the paper keeps the adapters fixed and fine-tunes RoBERTa, so it is a modular knowledge-infusion method rather than a downstream PEFT method.","results_note":"K-Adapter (factual + linguistic) improves RoBERTa-Large on entity typing (OpenEntity +1.38 F1), relation classification and question answering.","relations":[{"target":"side-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent side-network design; K-Adapter reads intermediate layers and fuses by concatenation."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Plugs adapters outside the backbone instead of inside each layer."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01808","https://arxiv.org/html/2002.01808","https://github.com/microsoft/k-adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Ruize Wang","Duyu Tang","Nan Duan","Zhongyu Wei","Xuanjing Huang","Jianshu ji","Guihong Cao","Daxin Jiang","Ming Zhou"],"bibkey":"wang2020k","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2020k,\n  title = {{K-Adapter: Infusing Knowledge into Pre-Trained Models with Adapters}},\n  author = {Ruize Wang and Duyu Tang and Nan Duan and Zhongyu Wei and Xuanjing Huang and Jianshu ji and Guihong Cao and Daxin Jiang and Ming Zhou},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2021},\n  eprint = {2002.01808},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01808}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A side network of Transformer layers reads backbone layers \\(\\{0,11,23\\}\\) and never writes back; the output is the product \\([h_L;a_K]\\).","shape":"Function-level: a separate network whose features are concatenated.","base_point":"Neutral up to the projection \\(\\pi_1\\): \\(h_L\\) is kept as a product factor, so any head that ignores \\(a_K\\) (a zero head block) recovers \\(f\\). The adapters themselves start random. Strictly the codomain changes (\\([h_L;a_K]\\) instead of \\(h_L\\)), so this is neutrality in the product sense, \\(\\pi_1\\circ\\tilde f=f\\) at every parameter value, not a neutral point of Def VI.1; a randomly initialised head on \\(a_K\\) is not neutral at the start.","gauge":"The side Transformer's internal gauges: per-head \\(GL\\) on the query–key and value–output wires.","covariance":"Dense down- and up-projections read the backbone states, so the family is basis-free on the hidden wire.","merge":"A separate network runs at inference.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). Further knowledge adapters are added by further products \\([h_L;a_K;a'_K]\\), which a linear head reads additively."},"categorical_reading":"A side extension that never writes back: the representation is the product \\(h_L\\times a_K\\), so the base function survives as the projection \\(\\pi_1\\), and knowledge sources are added by further products. Downstream the paper fine-tunes the backbone with the adapters frozen, so only the knowledge-infusion stage is a PEFT method in this framework.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","test":"O2","reason":"K-Adapter's features contain the full \\(d\\)-dimensional \\(h_L\\), whose values span \\(\\mathbb R^d\\) over inputs; LST's pre-head output \\(\\mathrm{Up}(h^g_N)\\) always lies in \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,\\mathrm{Up}\\), of dimension \\(\\le d/r<d\\) (a cut of rank \\(d/r\\), Thm II.10). A cut-rank argument across different codomains, so heuristic as an arrow test."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"compacter","name":"Compacter","full_name":"Compacter (Compact Adapter with low-rank PHM layers)","paper_title":"Compacter: Efficient Low-Rank Hypercomplex Adapter Layers","authors":"Karimi Mahabadi et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NeurIPS 2021","arxiv":"2106.04647","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647","code_url":"https://github.com/rabeehk/compacter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h \\leftarrow h + U\\,\\mathrm{GeLU}(Dh),\\quad D=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i\\otimes(s_i^{D}t_i^{D\\top}),\\ U=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i\\otimes(s_i^{U}t_i^{U\\top}),\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}\\ \\text{shared by all adapters and layers},\\ s_i^{D}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{r}{n}\\times\\rho},\\ t_i^{D}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d}{n}\\times\\rho},\\ s_i^{U}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d}{n}\\times\\rho},\\ t_i^{U}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{r}{n}\\times\\rho}\\ (\\rho=1\\ \\text{default});\\ d=\\text{hidden size},\\ r=\\text{bottleneck};\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"2\\rho(d+r)\\ \\text{per adapter} + n^{3}\\ \\text{shared};\\ \\text{model total } 4L(d+r)+n^{3}\\ (\\rho=1)","locus":"bottleneck adapters after attention and FFN sublayers (Compacter++: after the FFN only)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Adapter projections are sums of \\(n\\) Kronecker products of shared 'slow' \\(n \\times n\\) matrices \\(A_i\\) with layer-specific rank-\\(\\rho\\) 'fast' matrices \\(s_i t_i^{\\top}\\) (low-rank PHM, LPHM).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Serial bottleneck adapter with a nonlinearity; adds inference latency and cannot be folded.","identity_at_init":"Not specified as an exact zero-init; relies on the adapter residual connection as in Houlsby adapters.","key_idea":"Combine hypercomplex (Kronecker-sum) layers, cross-layer sharing of the slow factors, and rank-one fast factors to bring adapter cost from \\(O(kd)\\) to \\(O(k+d)\\).","results_note":"On GLUE with T5-base, Compacter (0.073%) and Compacter++ (0.047% of parameters) perform on par with full fine-tuning and outperform it on SuperGLUE and in low-resource settings.","relations":[{"target":"phm-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"adds cross-layer sharing of A_i and low-rank B_i"},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"same insertion points and bottleneck design"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both reach ~0.05-0.1% trainable parameters"},{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Compacter++ uses the single post-FFN placement."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.04647","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2106.04647","https://arxiv.org/html/2106.04647","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","shape"],"authors_full":["Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi","James Henderson","Sebastian Ruder"],"bibkey":"mahabadi2021compacter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mahabadi2021compacter,\n  title = {{Compacter: Efficient Low-Rank Hypercomplex Adapter Layers}},\n  author = {Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi and James Henderson and Sebastian Ruder},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2106.04647},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Inserts new boxes \\(h\\mapsto h+U\\,\\mathrm{GeLU}(Dh)\\) after the sublayers; \\(U=0\\) is a neutral point.","shape":"Function-level: a non-linear bottleneck adapter whose projections are low-rank PHM sums \\(\\sum_iA_i\\otimes(s_it_i^\\top)\\).","base_point":"The extension is pointed at \\(U=0\\) (table: neutral). The paper only states a near-identity init through the residual connection, as for Houlsby.","gauge":"Disagrees with the table's 'GL on PHM index'. A \\(GL_n\\) mixing of the index sends the rank-\\(\\rho\\) fast weights \\(s_it_i^\\top\\) to sums of rank greater than \\(\\rho\\), leaving the parameter space, so only monomial mixing survives. The continuous gauge is the torus of per-term slides \\((s_i,t_i)\\mapsto(cs_i,t_i/c)\\), together with \\(A_i\\mapsto a_iA_i\\) compensated in every layer (times \\(GL_\\rho\\) per term if \\(\\rho>1\\)). GeLU adds only permutations (Lemma VI.2).","covariance":"Kronecker (PHM) reshape of the bottleneck and hidden dimensions: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\) (table).","merge":"The serial box is non-affine (GeLU): M∞.","rebasing":"n/a: an extension is not merged into \\(\\theta\\), so merge-and-restart is undefined (each cycle would add new boxes)."},"categorical_reading":"Compacter is a method on the Houlsby extension \\(\\Theta\\times\\Theta'\\), obtained from the PHM adapter by tying the slow weights \\(A_i\\) across all boxes and restricting the fast weights to rank \\(\\rho\\). It maps by A3 into the PHM adapter and into the dense Houlsby adapter, but no weight-space method simulates it, because its box function is non-affine.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"phm-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big(\\{A_i\\},\\{s_i^{\\bullet,\\ell},t_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}\\}\\big)=\\big(A_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}=A_i,\\;B_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}=s_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}t_i^{\\bullet,\\ell\\top}\\big)_{\\ell,\\ \\bullet\\in\\{D,U\\}}","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared slow weights and the rank-\\(\\rho\\) product; neutral point maps to neutral point (Houlsby placement; Compacter++ maps to the Pfeiffer placement instead)."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(W_{down}=\\textstyle\\sum_iA_i\\otimes s_i^Dt_i^{D\\top},\\;W_{up}=\\sum_iA_i\\otimes s_i^Ut_i^{U\\top}\\big)","note":"Same extension and placement, with a GeLU bottleneck and matching bias convention; Houlsby's dense projections contain Compacter's PHM ones."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"kadaptation","test":"O1","reason":"For generic parameters Compacter's box function \\(h+U\\,\\mathrm{GeLU}(Dh)\\) is non-affine in \\(h\\), so its functional image is not contained in that of the weight-space Kronecker-sum update KAdaptation (M1), even though both use the same LPHM parametrisation."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Same reason: only with \\(\\sigma=\\mathrm{id}\\) would the adapter be a linear insertion comparable to LoRA (Prop VI.4)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"hyperformer","name":"HyperFormer","full_name":"HyperFormer / HyperFormer++: shared hypernetworks for task-conditioned adapters","paper_title":"Parameter-efficient Multi-task Fine-tuning for Transformers via Shared Hypernetworks","authors":"Karimi Mahabadi et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2106.04489","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.47/","code_url":"https://github.com/rabeehk/hyperformer","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"(U_{\\tau}^{l},D_{\\tau}^{l})=\\big(W^{U^l},W^{D^l}\\big)I_{\\tau},\\ \\ I_{\\tau}=h_I(z_{\\tau})\\ \\ (\\text{HyperFormer++: }I_{\\tau}=h'_I(z_{\\tau},l_i,p_j)\\text{ with one hypernetwork shared by all layers});\\quad A_{\\tau}^{l}(x)=LN_{\\tau}^{l}\\big(U_{\\tau}^{l}\\operatorname{GeLU}(D_{\\tau}^{l}x)\\big)+x;\\ \\text{trained: hypernetworks, task/layer/position embeddings, layer norms; T5 frozen}","trainable_params":"\\text{HyperFormer++ (whole model, }T\\text{ tasks): }t(T+4+2L)+8te+2t(2rd+2d)\\ \\ (t=64,\\ e=128;\\ \\text{task/layer/position embeddings, two task projectors, adapter and LayerNorm hypernetworks for encoder and decoder}),\\ \\text{a new task adds }t;\\ \\text{HyperFormer: one hypernetwork per layer with }t(2rd+2d)\\ \\text{weights; T5 LayerNorms also trained; 0.29\\% added parameters per task reported}","locus":"adapters after the attention and feed-forward blocks of every T5 layer, plus conditional layer norms","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Linear hypernetwork mapping a (task, layer, position) embedding to the down/up projections of a residual bottleneck adapter and to layer-norm parameters; knowledge shared through the hypernetwork, specialization through task embeddings.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The generated modules are non-linear bottleneck adapters inserted into the computation graph.","identity_at_init":"No explicit guarantee; generated adapters sit in a residual branch \\(A(x) = \\mathrm{LN}(\\dots) + x\\).","key_idea":"Instead of separate adapters per task, generate all adapters from shared hypernetworks conditioned on task and position embeddings, sharing information across tasks while keeping task-specific modules.","results_note":"Improves multi-task GLUE performance with only 0.29% added parameters per task and gives substantial gains in few-shot domain generalization.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"generates bottleneck adapters"},{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both condition a hypernetwork on task representations; T2L generates LoRAs from text"},{"target":"polytropon","type":"competes-with","note":"routing over modules vs generating modules"},{"target":"compacter","type":"competes-with","note":"Same authors; parameter sharing via hypernetwork vs via shared Kronecker factors."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04489","https://arxiv.org/html/2106.04489","https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.47/","https://github.com/rabeehk/hyperformer"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","composition"],"authors_full":["Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi","Sebastian Ruder","Mostafa Dehghani","James Henderson"],"bibkey":"mahabadi2021parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mahabadi2021parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-efficient Multi-task Fine-tuning for Transformers via Shared Hypernetworks}},\n  author = {Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi and Sebastian Ruder and Mostafa Dehghani and James Henderson},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2106.04489},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.47/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A \\(T\\)-point of the bottleneck-adapter extension, \\(T\\) = (task, layer, position) embeddings: \\(a(z)=(W^Uh_I(z),W^Dh_I(z))\\) (VIII.B row 'Text-to-LoRA / HyperFormer').","shape":"Per task the generated module is a non-linear adapter on an extension (function-level).","base_point":"No zero init: random hypernetworks generate \\(U_\\tau\\ne0\\), so each task starts with a defect. A neutral point exists (\\(U_\\tau=0\\) and a zero output-LN shift). VIII.B leaves this blank.","gauge":"\\(GL_t\\) on the task-embedding wire, \\((W^Ug^{-1},W^Dg^{-1},g\\circ h_I)\\), and another \\(GL\\) on the \\(z\\)-wire before \\(h_I\\)'s first layer. The generated adapter adds only \\(S_r\\) (GeLU).","covariance":"Generated \\(U_\\tau,D_\\tau\\) are dense: basis-free on the hidden wire.","merge":"VIII.B writes 'M1 per task' for the shared Text-to-LoRA/HyperFormer row. That holds for Text-to-LoRA, but HyperFormer's generated module \\(x\\mapsto x+\\mathrm{LN}_\\tau(U_\\tau\\mathrm{GeLU}(D_\\tau x))\\) is non-affine (Thm VI.3(c)), so it is M∞ even for a fixed task.","rebasing":"n/a: a family over tasks, nothing merged."},"categorical_reading":"HyperFormer is a \\(T\\)-point \\(a:T\\to Q_{\\rm adapter}\\) of the adapter extension, given by a linear hypernetwork whose \\(GL_t\\) slide on the task-embedding wire is its gauge. Tasks share \\(a\\) and specialise through \\(z_\\tau\\). Unlike Text-to-LoRA, each fibre \\(a(z_\\tau)\\) is a non-linear adapter with an output LayerNorm, so no task's model lifts to \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"text-to-lora","test":"O1","reason":"For each task Text-to-LoRA's realisation is a weight-space LoRA, which keeps every linear box linear; HyperFormer's per-task box functions are non-affine, so they lie outside T2L's image."},{"target":"compacter","test":"O2","reason":"Compacter's \\(U,D\\) are sums of \\(n\\) Kronecker products (\\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\)); HyperFormer's generated \\(U_\\tau,D_\\tau\\) are dense, with Kronecker rank up to \\(\\min(m_1n_1,m_2n_2)>n\\) (Thm II.11)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"parallel-adapter","name":"Parallel Adapter","full_name":"(Scaled) Parallel Adapter","paper_title":"Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning","authors":"He et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2110.04366","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","code_url":"https://github.com/jxhe/unify-parameter-efficient-tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h \\leftarrow F(x) + s\\cdot W_{up}\\,\\sigma(W_{down}\\,x),\\quad x = \\text{input of the frozen sublayer } F\\ (\\text{FFN or attention});\\ \\ s=1\\ (\\text{PA}),\\ s>1\\ \\text{fixed (scaled PA, e.g. } s=4);\\ \\ W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2rd\\ (+\\,r + d \\text{ biases})\\ \\text{per adapted sublayer}","locus":"in parallel to the FFN (preferred) or attention sublayer, reading the sublayer input","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Additive parallel composition: the frozen sublayer map \\(F\\) is replaced by \\(F + s\\,g\\) with \\(g = W_{up}\\sigma W_{down}\\) a bottleneck MLP of the same input (a sum of two morphisms with common domain), versus the serial post-composition \\((\\mathrm{id}+g)\\circ F\\) of sequential adapters.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The bottleneck is nonlinear and \\(F\\) (an FFN) is nonlinear, so \\(F+sg\\) cannot be rewritten as the original architecture; with \\(\\sigma = \\mathrm{id}\\) around a single linear map it collapses to LoRA, which is mergeable.","identity_at_init":"Exact for the scaled PA as implemented: LoRA-style init (\\(W_{down}\\) Kaiming-uniform, \\(W_{up} = 0\\)), so the parallel branch outputs 0 at init.","key_idea":"Viewing prefix tuning as a gated parallel adapter on attention outputs suggests moving adapters from serial to parallel insertion, placing them at the FFN, and borrowing LoRA's scaling; parallel insertion beats serial at equal budget (a similar parallel adapter was proposed concurrently by Zhu et al., 2021 for MT).","results_note":"On XSum summarization and WMT16 en-ro translation, the FFN parallel adapter outperforms the FFN sequential adapter by 1.7 ROUGE-2 and 0.8 BLEU at the same parameter budget.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA is a scaled parallel adapter around a single linear map with \\sigma = identity."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"He et al. show prefix tuning equals a softmax-gated parallel adapter on head outputs; PA transfers this insertion form."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"dual-to","note":"Parallel vs serial insertion of the same bottleneck."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.04366","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Junxian He","Chunting Zhou","Xuezhe Ma","Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick","Graham Neubig"],"bibkey":"he2021unified","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2021unified,\n  title = {{Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Junxian He and Chunting Zhou and Xuezhe Ma and Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick and Graham Neubig},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2110.04366},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The sublayer \\(F\\) is replaced by \\(F+s\\,U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\), a sum of two morphisms with a common domain; \\(U=0\\) is neutral.","shape":"Function-level for non-linear \\(\\sigma\\); with \\(\\sigma=\\mathrm{id}\\) it is LoRA, with image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) (Prop VI.4(a)).","base_point":"Exactly neutral as implemented (LoRA-style init, \\(W_{up}=0\\)).","gauge":"He et al.'s implementation (petl_factory Adapter_Layer) uses ReLU, so VIII.B's 'same' as Houlsby reads here as the ReLU per-unit slide (R3) \\(\\times S_r\\); the scale \\(s\\) is a fixed constant.","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"VIII.B: M∞ (M1 if linear). \\(\\sigma\\) is non-affine (Thm VI.3(c)), and \\(F\\) is a non-linear FFN.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze and re-insert) parallel bottlenecks add, \\(F+\\sum_kU_k\\sigma(D_k\\,\\cdot)\\) = one bottleneck of width \\(Kr\\): the additive closure (span of the bottleneck cone), as for LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"The additive dual of the serial adapter: \\(F+s\\,U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\) is a Minkowski sum on an extension, so at function level its stages add like LoRA's. Around a single linear map, \\(\\sigma=\\mathrm{id}\\) gives LoRA (Prop VI.4(a)), and with ReLU \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\to\\mathrm{PA}_{2r}\\) via \\(h(B,A)=([B,-B],[A;-A])\\), since \\(\\mathrm{ReLU}(t)-\\mathrm{ReLU}(-t)=t\\). A GELU parallel bottleneck would embed by freezing (A2) in AIM's joint MLP adapter and in Res-Tuning's FFN Res-Adapter, but He et al.'s PA is ReLU, so those are not arrows out of this object.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"adaptformer","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_{down},b_{down},W_{up},b_{up})=\\big(W_{down},b_{down},\\tfrac{s}{0.1}W_{up},\\tfrac{s}{0.1}b_{up}\\big)","note":"An isomorphism: the same ReLU bottleneck on the FFN input, differing only in the fixed scale (\\(s\\) against AdaptFormer's \\(0.1\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"For non-affine \\(\\sigma\\) the realised box \\(F+sU\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\) is outside \\(\\Phi_f(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA})\\) (Thm VI.3(c)); only \\(\\sigma=\\mathrm{id}\\) gives LoRA."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"A parallel correction \\(U\\sigma(Dx)\\) of the sublayer input is generically not a serial correction \\(U'\\sigma(D'F(x))\\) of its output."},{"target":"mam-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"MAM contains a parallel adapter, yet PA is not a sub-object of it: \\(f\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{PA}\\) at \\(U=0\\), but no MAM parameter realises \\(f\\), because the prefix summand is unpointed (Thm VI.5(f))."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"mam-adapter","name":"MAM Adapter","full_name":"Mix-And-Match Adapter","paper_title":"Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning","authors":"He et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2110.04366","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","code_url":"https://github.com/jxhe/unify-parameter-efficient-tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{attention: } \\mathrm{head} = \\mathrm{Attn}\\big(xW_q,\\ [P_k; CW_k],\\ [P_v; CW_v]\\big),\\ P_k,P_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d/n_h}\\ (\\ell=30);\\quad \\text{FFN: } h \\leftarrow \\mathrm{FFN}(x) + s\\cdot W_{up}\\,\\sigma(W_{down}\\,x),\\ r=512;\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"2\\ell d + 2rd\\ \\text{per layer at inference }(\\ell = 30,\\ r = 512;\\ \\text{prefix trained through an MLP reparameterization})","locus":"prefix vectors at every attention layer (small budget) plus a scaled parallel adapter at every FFN (large budget)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Sum of two of the unified-view instances in each layer: a softmax-gated parallel modification of attention heads (prefix tuning) and a scaled parallel bottleneck on the FFN, with the parameter budget deliberately allocated mostly to the FFN.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Prefixes add attention slots and the FFN adapter is nonlinear.","identity_at_init":"Partial: the scaled parallel adapter starts at 0 (\\(W_{up} = 0\\)), but prefixes change attention from the first step (prefix tuning has no exact identity init).","key_idea":"Transfer the favourable design choices found by the unified analysis: parallel insertion, FFN modification with large capacity, scaling composition, and multi-head attention modification with a tiny prefix.","results_note":"With 6.7% tuned parameters, MAM matches full fine-tuning on XSum and en-ro and is the best of the compared PEFT methods; with RoBERTa-base and 0.5% parameters it reaches 87.4 MNLI / 94.2 SST-2 vs 87.6 / 94.6 for full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"combines","note":"Scaled parallel adapter at the FFN."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"combines","note":"Prefix tuning (l=30) at attention."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.04366","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/method_combinations.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Junxian He","Chunting Zhou","Xuezhe Ma","Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick","Graham Neubig"],"bibkey":"he2021unified","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2021unified,\n  title = {{Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Junxian He and Chunting Zhou and Xuezhe Ma and Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick and Graham Neubig},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2110.04366},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"VIII.B lists 'E with input-dependent gates'. MAM has no gates; it is prefix tuning (\\(\\ell=30\\)) on attention plus a scaled parallel adapter on the FFN. The E/E° test of axes.json, solving \\(\\tilde f(\\theta_0,p,-)=f(\\theta_0,-)\\), fails because on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) no prefix leaves the output unchanged (Thm VI.5(f)). So it is E°.","shape":"Function-level (prefix and non-linear adapter).","base_point":"VIII.B: 'mixed'. The adapter part is neutral at \\(W_{up}=0\\), but the prefix has no neutral value, so the sum has no point realising \\(f\\): unpointed.","gauge":"The prefix is trained through Li-Liang's MLP reparametrisation (an embedding \\(E\\in\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}\\) fed to Linear(\\(d\\to800\\))-Tanh-Linear), whose first internal wire carries the slide \\((E,W_1)\\mapsto(Eg^\\top,W_1g^{-1})\\), \\(g\\in GL_d\\). The parallel adapter adds its ReLU per-unit torus (R3). Only the materialised inference parametrisation (free prefix vectors, discrete \\(S_\\ell\\)) would leave the torus alone.","covariance":"The prefix keys and values are free vectors in each head's key and value space, and transform with the query–key gauge; the adapter is dense. Basis-free.","merge":"The prefix is a softmax-gated parallel branch (generically non-affine, Thm VI.5(f)) and the adapter is non-linear.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level both summands are additive: prefixes concatenate in the \\(\\uplus\\)-monoid of key–value multisets (Thm VI.5(a)) and bottlenecks add."},"categorical_reading":"A sum, on an extension, of a prefix on attention and a scaled parallel adapter on the FFN. Because the prefix summand is unpointed, the sum is an unpointed extension, and not a \\(\\oplus\\)-word in \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\) (Exposé I). Prefix tuning embeds in it by freezing the adapter at \\(U=0\\) (A2), but the parallel adapter does not embed, since no prefix value is neutral.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"For \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generic prefix's input-dependent gate \\(\\lambda_q\\) makes the head non-affine (Thm VI.5(f)), so no weight-space realisation exists."},{"target":"unipelt","test":"O1","reason":"MAM's FFN correction is parallel, \\(U\\sigma(Dx)\\) of the FFN input; UniPELT's only bottleneck is serial after the FFN, and its LoRA acts on \\(W_q,W_v\\), not on the FFN. A parallel FFN correction is generically not reproduced."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"unipelt","name":"UniPELT","full_name":"UniPELT: unified, gated parameter-efficient language model tuning","paper_title":"UniPELT: A Unified Framework for Parameter-Efficient Language Model Tuning","authors":"Mao et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2110.07577","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07577","code_url":"https://github.com/morningmoni/UniPELT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h'_A = \\mathcal{G}_A\\,W_{up}\\,\\phi(W_{down}\\,h_{FN}) + h_F;\\quad K' = [\\mathcal{G}_P P_K;\\,K],\\ V' = [\\mathcal{G}_P P_V;\\,V];\\quad W = W_0 + \\mathcal{G}_L\\,\\Delta W_{\\text{LoRA}};\\quad \\mathcal{G}_m = \\mathrm{sigmoid}\\big(\\mathrm{FFN}_m(\\cdot)\\big)\\in(0,1),\\ m\\in\\{A,P,L\\};\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"2rd\\ (\\text{adapter}) + 2\\ell d\\ (\\text{prefix}) + 2\\,r'(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ (\\text{LoRA on } W_q,W_v) + O(d)\\ \\text{gates, per layer}","locus":"sequential adapter after the FFN, prefixes at attention keys/values, LoRA on \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\), each with its own gate in every layer","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Input-dependent sigmoid-gated sum of three heterogeneous submodules inside each layer (serial adapter, prefix, LoRA): a learned, per-example mixture over PEFT 'experts', with each gate able to switch its submodule off.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Adapter and prefix branches are not mergeable and the LoRA gate depends on the input.","identity_at_init":"Not exact: inherits submodule initializations (LoRA \\(B = 0\\) and near-zero adapters, but prefixes perturb attention from the start).","key_idea":"Rather than choosing one PEFT method per task, include adapter, prefix tuning and LoRA as submodules and learn gates that activate whichever combination suits the data and task.","results_note":"On GLUE, UniPELT gains 1-4 points over the best individual submodule in low-resource settings, often exceeds the upper bound of its submodules, and matches or beats fine-tuning with more data.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"combines","note":"Gated sequential bottleneck adapter submodule. The paper cites Houlsby et al. but places one adapter after the FFN (Pfeiffer-style placement, also AdapterHub UniPELTConfig = SeqBn reduction 16)."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"combines","note":"Gated prefix submodule."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Gate replaces LoRA's constant scaling."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07577","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.07577","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/method_combinations.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Yuning Mao","Lambert Mathias","Rui Hou","Amjad Almahairi","Hao Ma","Jiawei Han","Wen-tau Yih","Madian Khabsa"],"bibkey":"mao2021unipelt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mao2021unipelt,\n  title = {{UniPELT: A Unified Framework for Parameter-Efficient Language Model Tuning}},\n  author = {Yuning Mao and Lambert Mathias and Rui Hou and Amjad Almahairi and Hao Ma and Jiawei Han and Wen-tau Yih and Madian Khabsa},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2110.07577},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07577}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"VIII.B lists 'E with input-dependent gates (\\(F_X\\))'. The gates \\(\\mathcal G_m=\\mathrm{sigmoid}(\\mathrm{FFN}_m(\\cdot))\\) do make it a family over \\(X\\). But the prefix gate scales \\(P_K,P_V\\), not the prefix's softmax mass: as \\(\\mathcal G_P\\to0\\) the prefix keys go to \\(0\\), and the prefix still takes weight \\(\\ell/(\\ell+Z_q(C))\\) of the attention. So no parameter value reproduces \\(f\\), and by axes.json's E/E° test it is E°.","shape":"Function-level; not a \\(\\oplus\\)-word (Exposé I).","base_point":"VIII.B: 'mixed'. LoRA (\\(B=0\\)) and the adapter (\\(U=0\\)) are neutral, but the gated prefix has no neutral value (and \\(\\mathcal G_P\\in(0,1)\\) never vanishes): unpointed.","gauge":"The LoRA summand keeps \\(GL_r\\), \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\), since its gate multiplies \\(BA\\) as a whole. The adapter adds only discrete or per-unit symmetries.","covariance":"All parts are dense (LoRA \\(GL\\times GL\\), dense bottleneck, free prefix vectors, gate FFNs): basis-free.","merge":"VIII.B says 'M∞ (LoRA part M1)'. Here even the LoRA part is M∞: \\(W_0x+\\mathcal G_L(x)BAx\\) is non-affine in \\(x\\) because the gate depends on the input (Thm VI.3(c)).","rebasing":"n/a: nothing merges. At function level the parts close differently (serial adapter by composition, prefix in the \\(\\uplus\\)-monoid, LoRA additively), so there is no single closure."},"categorical_reading":"An input-gated combination of three summands (serial adapter, gated prefix, LoRA): a family over inputs \\(X\\) of points of \\(\\mathrm{Ad}\\oplus\\mathrm{Prefix}\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}\\) on an extension. Gating the prefix keys and values does not gate its softmax mass, so no gate value is neutral and the object is unpointed. Input-dependence also costs LoRA its mergeability.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Even the LoRA summand realises the non-affine \\(W_0x+\\mathcal G_L(x)BAx\\); with the prefix and adapter, UniPELT's realisations lie outside \\(\\Phi_f(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA})\\)."},{"target":"mam-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"UniPELT's serial post-FFN correction \\(U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\) of the FFN output is generically not MAM's parallel correction of the FFN input, and MAM has no LoRA on \\(W_q,W_v\\)."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adamix","name":"AdaMix","full_name":"Mixture-of-Adaptations","paper_title":"AdaMix: Mixture-of-Adaptations for Parameter-efficient Model Tuning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EMNLP 2022","arxiv":"2205.12410","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12410","code_url":"https://github.com/microsoft/AdaMix","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{train: } x \\leftarrow x + f\\big(x\\,\\mathcal{W}^{down}_{i,k}\\big)\\,\\mathcal{W}^{up}_{i,j},\\ \\ (j,k)\\ \\text{sampled uniformly per step};\\ \\ \\mathcal{L} = \\mathrm{CE} + \\tfrac12\\big(\\mathrm{KL}(z^{\\mathcal{A}}\\Vert z^{\\mathcal{B}}) + \\mathrm{KL}(z^{\\mathcal{B}}\\Vert z^{\\mathcal{A}})\\big);\\quad \\text{inference: } \\mathcal{W}'^{up}_{i} = \\tfrac1M\\sum_{j}\\mathcal{W}^{up}_{ij},\\ \\mathcal{W}'^{down}_{i} = \\tfrac1M\\sum_{j}\\mathcal{W}^{down}_{ij}","trainable_params":"(M+1)\\,rd\\ (+\\text{biases})\\ \\text{per adapter during training in the default setting (} M \\text{ down-projections, one shared up-projection, Sec. 3.4)},\\ 2Mrd \\text{ without sharing};\\ 2rd\\ \\text{per adapter after merging (default } M=4)","locus":"Houlsby adapter positions (or LoRA matrices when the underlying method is LoRA)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"A sparse mixture of \\(M\\) adapter 'experts' per layer with random (parameter-free) routing during training, consistency-regularized between two stochastic routes, and collapsed by weight averaging into a single adapter of the original size for inference (a model-soup within one layer).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The averaged adapter is still a nonlinear bottleneck (mergeable into \\(W\\) only when the underlying method is LoRA).","identity_at_init":"Inherits the underlying PEFT initialization (approximate identity for adapters; exact for LoRA with \\(B = 0\\)).","key_idea":"Train several adaptation modules per layer with stochastic routing (same FLOPs as one), then average them into one module, gaining ensemble-like benefits at the cost and size of a single adapter.","results_note":"Tuning 0.1-0.2% of parameters, AdaMix outperforms full fine-tuning on all GLUE tasks with RoBERTa-large (0.23%) and beats LoRA and adapters on E2E NLG with GPT-2.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Mixture of Houlsby adapters."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Also instantiated as a mixture of LoRA modules."},{"target":"adapterfusion","type":"competes-with","note":"Mixes adapters without input-dependent routing at inference."},{"target":"model-soups","type":"builds-on","note":"inference-time weight averaging of experts"},{"target":"smear","type":"competes-with","note":"SMEAR compares to AdaMix as a non-adaptive routing baseline"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12410","https://arxiv.org/html/2205.12410","https://github.com/microsoft/AdaMix"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","composition"],"authors_full":["Yaqing Wang","Sahaj Agarwal","Subhabrata Mukherjee","Xiaodong Liu","Jing Gao","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Jianfeng Gao"],"bibkey":"wang2022adamix","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2022adamix,\n  title = {{AdaMix: Mixture-of-Adaptations for Parameter-efficient Model Tuning}},\n  author = {Yaqing Wang and Sahaj Agarwal and Subhabrata Mukherjee and Xiaodong Liu and Jing Gao and Ahmed Hassan Awadallah and Jianfeng Gao},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2205.12410},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12410}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A training recipe whose deployed object is one Houlsby adapter, the weight average of \\(M\\) experts; the coordinates are the adapter's. Instantiated on LoRA, it would carry LoRA's coordinates.","shape":"Function-level, as the deployed Houlsby adapter (non-linear inserted box).","base_point":"Inherited (near-neutral Houlsby init; exactly neutral when built on zero-init LoRA).","gauge":"Inherited from the Houlsby adapter: the paper leaves \\(f\\) unspecified and the Houlsby reference adapter is GELU, so only the discrete \\(S_r\\) survives (a ReLU instantiation would give the positive torus). The factor-wise average is defined only up to that \\(S_r\\), and the shared up-projection is a joint gauge fixing (cf. Prop V.4(b)).","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"The averaged adapter is still a non-linear bottleneck (M1 only for the LoRA instantiation).","rebasing":"n/a (M∞)."},"categorical_reading":"At training time AdaMix is a family over random routes \\((j,k)\\in[M]^2\\) of adapters; for inference it is collapsed by the factor-wise averaging map, an A3 arrow into a single adapter. Like LoraHub's factor-wise rule, the average depends on the gauge. Sharing one up-projection pins the hidden-unit permutations of all experts to a common gauge, which is why weight averaging is meaningful here.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((W^{down}_k)_{k\\le M},\\,W^{up}\\big)=\\big(\\tfrac1M\\textstyle\\sum_kW^{down}_k,\\ W^{up}\\big)","note":"The inference collapse is a linear pointed map into one Houlsby adapter; experts at the neutral point (\\(W^{up}=0\\)) go to the neutral point. Average the \\(W^{up}_j\\) too when they are not shared."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adapterfusion","test":"O1","reason":"AdapterFusion trains only \\(Q,K,V\\) over \\(N\\) frozen adapters, so its realisations are softmax mixtures of fixed adapter outputs. The new averaged adapter that AdaMix trains is generically not among them; conversely, AdaMix does not route at inference."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"llama-adapter","name":"LLaMA-Adapter","full_name":"LLaMA-Adapter (adaption prompts with zero-initialized gated attention)","paper_title":"LLaMA-Adapter: Efficient Fine-tuning of Language Models with Zero-init Attention","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2303.16199","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16199","code_url":"https://github.com/OpenGVLab/LLaMA-Adapter","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"AdaptionPromptConfig(adapter_len, adapter_layers)","formula_latex":"S_l = \\tfrac{Q_lK_l^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d}},\\ \\ K_l = [P_l;T_l;t_l]W_k,\\ V_l = [P_l;T_l;t_l]W_v;\\quad S^{g}_l = \\big[\\mathrm{softmax}(S^{K}_l)\\cdot\\tanh(g_l);\\ \\mathrm{softmax}(S^{M+1}_l)\\big];\\quad t^{o}_l = W_o\\,S^{g}_l V_l;\\ \\ P_l\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d}\\ \\text{and per-head gates } g_l\\ \\text{trained (top } L' \\text{ layers)};\\ W_{q,k,v,o}\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"L'\\ell d + L' n_h\\ \\ (\\ell = 10,\\ L' = 30 \\Rightarrow \\approx 1.2\\text{M for LLaMA-7B})","locus":"self-attention of the topmost \\(L'\\) layers: learnable adaption prompts used as extra keys/values (through the frozen \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\)) with a gated, separately normalized softmax","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Learned prompt tokens prepended at each upper layer and attended through their own softmax whose output is multiplied by a zero-initialized tanh gate; because the word-token softmax is kept separate, the layer equals the original attention plus an additive gated prefix branch. In He et al.'s unified view this is a zero-gated parallel attention branch, whose softmax bottleneck has width \\(\\ell\\) and whose projections are tied to the frozen \\(W_{q,k,v,o}\\).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The adaption prompts add attention slots in the top layers at inference.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(g_l = 0\\) gives \\(\\tanh(g_l) = 0\\), so the prompt branch contributes nothing and the separately normalized word-token attention is unchanged.","key_idea":"Prefix-like prompts in the upper layers combined with a zero-initialized gate inject instruction-following ability progressively without disturbing the pretrained model at the start of training; the same zero-init attention also injects image tokens for multimodal reasoning.","results_note":"Fine-tunes frozen LLaMA-7B on 52K self-instruct demonstrations with 1.2M parameters in under one hour on 8 A100s, giving instruction-following comparable to fully fine-tuned Alpaca-7B; also extends to ScienceQA and other multimodal benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Prompts in upper layers, routed through frozen K/V projections and zero-gated."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both learn soft tokens; LLaMA-Adapter injects them inside attention with a zero gate."},{"target":"vpt","type":"competes-with","note":"Both route prompts through the frozen key/value projections; LLaMA-Adapter adds a separate softmax and zero gate."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"dual-to","note":"With the separate softmax the prompt term is an ungated-content, zero-gated parallel attention branch, cf. He et al. (2022)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16199","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.16199","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/adaption_prompt","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/llama_adapter","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/llama_adapter","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/adaption_prompt/layer.py","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.16199","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/llama_adapter.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Renrui Zhang","Jiaming Han","Chris Liu","Peng Gao","Aojun Zhou","Xiangfei Hu","Shilin Yan","Pan Lu","Hongsheng Li","Yu Qiao"],"bibkey":"zhang2023llama","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023llama,\n  title = {{LLaMA-Adapter: Efficient Fine-tuning of Language Models with Zero-init Attention}},\n  author = {Renrui Zhang and Jiaming Han and Chris Liu and Peng Gao and Aojun Zhou and Xiangfei Hu and Shilin Yan and Pan Lu and Hongsheng Li and Yu Qiao},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2303.16199},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16199}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Adds a separately normalised prompt branch \\(\\tanh(g)\\,\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)\\) in the top \\(L'\\) layers; \\(g=0\\) makes it the identity (Thm VI.5(d)).","shape":"VIII.B: Fun (a separately normalised prefix).","base_point":"Neutral at \\(g=0\\) (VIII.B); only the gate moves to first order (Lemma II.4, function-level).","gauge":"No continuous gauge: the prompts pass through the frozen \\(W_k,W_v\\), and a gate per head is injective near generic points. Only the discrete \\(S_\\ell\\) permuting prompt tokens remains. VIII.B leaves this blank.","covariance":"The prompts are free residual-stream vectors fed straight into the frozen \\(W_k,W_v\\) (no norm), so a change of basis is absorbed by \\(P\\mapsto Pg^\\top\\): basis-free. VIII.B leaves this blank.","merge":"Extra attention slots with their own softmax: non-affine in the input once the prompt has two or more tokens (generic prompts, Thm VI.5(f)); with one prompt token the head stays affine.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level separately normalised branches add: \\(\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\sum_k\\tanh(g_k)\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P_k)\\)."},"categorical_reading":"A separately normalised prefix, \\(\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\tanh(g)\\,\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)\\), uses two perspective maps (Thm VI.5(d)): content attention is untouched, and \\(g=0\\) is a neutral point. So its categorical contribution over prefix tuning is pointedness. At \\(g=0\\) only the gate moves to first order.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"llama-adapter-v2","rule":"A2","h":"h(P,g)=\\big(P,\\,g,\\ s=\\mathbf 1,\\ b=0,\\ \\gamma=\\gamma_0,\\ \\text{visual branch at its zero gate}\\big)","note":"V1 is V2 with the bias/scale, norm and early visual-fusion parameters frozen at their neutral base values. This assumes V2 keeps V1's zero-gated adaption prompts in the last \\(L'\\) layers."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"\\(f\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\) (at \\(g=0\\)), but prefix tuning is unpointed (Thm VI.5(f)). Moreover LLaMA-Adapter never rescales content attention, while a prefix multiplies it by \\(1-\\lambda_q\\) (Thm VI.5(b),(d))."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Gated extra softmax slots are non-affine in the input once the prompt has two or more tokens (generic prompts, Thm VI.5(f); one prompt token stays affine); no weight-space realisation exists."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"llama-adapter-v2","name":"LLaMA-Adapter V2","full_name":"LLaMA-Adapter V2: parameter-efficient visual instruction model","paper_title":"LLaMA-Adapter V2: Parameter-Efficient Visual Instruction Model","authors":"Gao et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2304.15010","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.15010","code_url":"https://github.com/OpenGVLab/LLaMA-Adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = W_0x \\;\\to\\; y = s\\cdot(W_0x + b),\\ \\ b\\ \\text{init } 0,\\ s\\ \\text{init } 1\\ \\text{(every linear layer; released code applies } s \\text{ to the input: } y = W_0(s\\odot x) + b);\\ \\ +\\ \\text{zero-gated adaption prompts in the last } L' \\text{ layers and visual tokens with zero-init attention in early layers; norms unfrozen; } W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per linear layer (scale + bias)} + \\text{norm gains} + L'\\ell d + \\text{gates} + \\text{early visual prompts/projection}\\ \\ (\\text{paper: bias/scale/norm part} \\approx 5\\text{M} = 0.04\\%;\\ \\approx 14\\text{M new parameters over LLaMA in total})","locus":"every linear layer (bias + scale), all normalization layers, adaption prompts in the last layers, visual prompts in early layers","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Composite of (i) affine reparameterization of every linear map by a diagonal scale and bias (a BitFit/SSF-like action, mergeable), (ii) LLaMA-Adapter zero-gated prefix attention in the upper layers, and (iii) early fusion of visual tokens, with disjoint parameter groups trained on image-text vs instruction data.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The scale/bias part can be folded into \\(W_0\\), but the gated adaption prompts and visual-token attention cannot.","identity_at_init":"Exact: bias 0, scale 1 and zero-initialized attention gates leave the model unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Unlock more capacity cheaply (bias + scale on all linear layers, unfrozen norms), inject visual tokens early and instruction prompts late, and train the two parameter groups on disjoint data to avoid interference between image-text alignment and instruction following; expert captioning/OCR models can be plugged in at inference.","results_note":"Adds about 0.04% (~5M) new parameters to LLaMA for language instruction tuning and obtains open-ended multimodal instruction following trained only on image-text pairs plus language instruction data.","relations":[{"target":"llama-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Adds bias/scale tuning, early visual fusion and disjoint-parameter joint training."},{"target":"bitfit","type":"combines","note":"Bias tuning of linear layers (plus scale)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.15010","https://arxiv.org/html/2304.15010","https://github.com/OpenGVLab/LLaMA-Adapter/blob/main/llama_adapter_v2_chat65b/llama/model.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Peng Gao","Jiaming Han","Renrui Zhang","Ziyi Lin","Shijie Geng","Aojun Zhou","Wei Zhang","Pan Lu","Conghui He","Xiangyu Yue","Hongsheng Li","Yu Qiao"],"bibkey":"gao2023llama","bibtex":"@article{gao2023llama,\n  title = {{LLaMA-Adapter V2: Parameter-Efficient Visual Instruction Model}},\n  author = {Peng Gao and Jiaming Han and Renrui Zhang and Ziyi Lin and Shijie Geng and Aojun Zhou and Wei Zhang and Pan Lu and Conghui He and Xiangyu Yue and Hongsheng Li and Yu Qiao},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2304.15010},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.15010},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.15010}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A hybrid: a weight-space part (per-layer scale and bias, unfrozen norms) plus LLaMA-Adapter's zero-gated prompts and early visual tokens. The extension part decides the kind.","shape":"Function-level, because of the gated prompt branches; the scale/bias part alone would be Lin.","base_point":"Exactly neutral at init: \\(b=0\\), \\(s=\\mathbf 1\\), zero attention gates.","gauge":"Norm gains and the consumers' input scales are related by a diagonal slide (R4/R6): \\((\\gamma,s_q,s_k,s_v)\\mapsto(\\gamma t,s_q/t,s_k/t,s_v/t)\\), a \\(d\\)-dimensional torus. Prompts add only \\(S_\\ell\\).","covariance":"The released code scales each linear layer's input (\\(y=W_0(s\\odot x)+b\\)): a one-sided torus, \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) per box. The paper's \\(s\\odot(W_0x+b)\\) is the mirror \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times GL\\).","merge":"The scale/bias part merges (M1→, as (IA)\\(^3\\)/BitFit), but the gated prompt and visual branches do not.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞ overall). The scale/bias part alone is idempotent, and the prompt branches add at function level."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed extension that sums an R-part (a per-layer torus-and-translation action of (IA)\\(^3\\)/BitFit type, which merges) with LLaMA-Adapter's zero-gated prefix (which does not). The norm gains and the consumers' input scales are related by a diagonal slide, which is its torus gauge. The disjoint parameter groups trained on different data are a training recipe, not a change of object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"llama-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"V1 provably leaves content attention and all lower-layer computation unchanged (Thm VI.5(d)), an absolute invariant; V2's bias and scale on every linear layer break it."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"side-tuning","name":"Side-Tuning","full_name":"Side-Tuning: additive side networks","paper_title":"Side-Tuning: A Baseline for Network Adaptation via Additive Side Networks","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2019,"venue":"ECCV 2020","arxiv":"1912.13503","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.13503","code_url":"https://github.com/jozhang97/side-tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"R(x) = \\alpha\\,B(x) + (1-\\alpha)\\,S(x),\\quad B\\ \\text{frozen base},\\ S\\ \\text{trainable side network},\\ \\alpha\\ \\text{learned or scheduled (e.g. } \\alpha(N) = \\tfrac{k}{k+N})","trainable_params":"|\\theta_S| + 1\\ \\ (\\text{side network of any size: a copy of } B, \\text{ a distilled small net, or a tiny MLP})","locus":"representation level: late fusion of base and side features before the task decoder","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Late-fusion side network: an \\(\\alpha\\)-blend (convex combination) of frozen base features and side-network features computed from the same input, with no access to intermediate backbone activations; \\(\\alpha = 1\\) is feature extraction, \\(\\alpha = 0\\) with \\(S\\) a copy of \\(B\\) is fine-tuning.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The side network is a separate forward computation that must run at inference.","identity_at_init":"Exact when \\(S\\) is initialized as a copy of \\(B\\) (the paper's default when architectures match): \\(R(x) = B(x)\\) for any \\(\\alpha\\); otherwise \\(S\\) is initialized by distillation from \\(B\\) (approximate).","key_idea":"Adapt a frozen network by learning a residual side network whose output is blended with the base representation; it never degrades the base task and treats the base as a black box (it can even be a non-network).","results_note":"Competitive with fine-tuning on transfer and incremental-learning benchmarks (iCIFAR, iTaskonomy, Habitat RL, SQuAD v2 with BERT) without forgetting, often with side networks much smaller than the base.","relations":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"LST extends side networks with ladder connections to intermediate activations for memory efficiency."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.13503","https://arxiv.org/html/1912.13503","http://sidetuning.berkeley.edu"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Jeffrey O Zhang","Alexander Sax","Amir Zamir","Leonidas Guibas","Jitendra Malik"],"bibkey":"zhang2019side","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2019side,\n  title = {{Side-Tuning: A Baseline for Network Adaptation via Additive Side Networks}},\n  author = {Jeffrey O Zhang and Alexander Sax and Amir Zamir and Leonidas Guibas and Jitendra Malik},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {1912.13503},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.13503}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A late-fusion side network: \\(R=\\alpha B+(1-\\alpha)S\\), with \\(S\\) trainable.","shape":"Function-level: a separate network blended with the frozen one.","base_point":"Neutral at \\(\\alpha=1\\) (the schedule \\(\\alpha(N)=k/(k+N)\\) starts there), or for any \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(S\\) is a copy of \\(B\\). By the zero-gate lemma, \\(S\\) is dead to first order at \\(\\alpha=1\\).","gauge":"The side network's own symmetries: positive per-unit (ReLU) or per-channel (BN) rescalings. \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(S\\) have no exact joint slide, because a sum of two networks is not one network of the same class.","covariance":"The blend is basis-free on the representation wire whenever \\(S\\) ends in a dense layer.","merge":"The side network is a separate forward computation.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level side networks add: \\(\\alpha'(\\alpha B+(1-\\alpha)S)+(1-\\alpha')S'\\)."},"categorical_reading":"A late-fusion side extension \\(R=\\alpha B+(1-\\alpha)S\\), pointed at \\(\\alpha=1\\). With \\(S\\) a copy of \\(B\\) and \\(\\alpha=0\\) it contains full fine-tuning at the function level: Full FT \\(\\to\\) side-tuning pointed at \\((0,B)\\), \\(h(\\theta)=(0,\\theta)\\). So it sits above the terminal object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\) and is not an object of it.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","test":"O2","reason":"Side-tuning's output contains the full-width \\(B(x)\\); LST's prediction sees the backbone only through ladders into a side state of width \\(d/r\\), a cut of rank \\(d/r<d\\) (Thm II.10)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"ladder-side-tuning","name":"LST","full_name":"Ladder Side-Tuning","paper_title":"LST: Ladder Side-Tuning for Parameter and Memory Efficient Transfer Learning","authors":"Sung et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2206.06522","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.06522","code_url":"https://github.com/ylsung/Ladder-Side-Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h^{g}_{i} = \\mathrm{Layer}^{g}_{i}\\big(\\mu_i\\,\\mathrm{Down}_i(h^{f}_{i}) + (1-\\mu_i)\\,h^{g}_{i-1}\\big),\\ \\ \\mu_i = \\mathrm{sigmoid}(\\alpha_i/T),\\ \\alpha_i\\ \\text{init } 0,\\ T=0.1;\\quad \\hat y = \\mathrm{LMHead}\\big(\\mathrm{Up}(h^{g}_{N})\\big);\\ \\ \\text{backbone } f \\text{ frozen and run forward-only; side network } g,\\ \\mathrm{Down}_i,\\ \\mathrm{Up}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\approx |\\theta_f|/r^{2} + O(L\\,d^{2}/r)\\ \\ (\\text{side Transformer with all widths divided by the reduction factor } r,\\ \\text{plus ladder projections})","locus":"a separate thin side network fed by shortcut ('ladder') connections from every backbone layer (and the embeddings); predictions come from the side network through the frozen LM head","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Side network = \\(1/r\\)-width pruned copy of the backbone receiving, at each depth, a gated convex combination of a linearly down-projected backbone activation and its own previous state; information flows only backbone -> side, so no gradient enters the backbone.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The side network is extra computation at inference (it produces the prediction).","identity_at_init":"No: the output is produced by the side network, so the model does not equal the backbone at init; instead the side network is initialized by structural pruning of backbone weights (Fisher-information row importance) and gates start at \\(\\mu = 0.5\\).","key_idea":"Parameter efficiency is not memory efficiency: by never backpropagating through the backbone (gradients only flow through a small ladder-connected side network), training memory drops far below adapters or LoRA.","results_note":"On GLUE with T5-base, LST saves 69% of full fine-tuning memory (2.7x more savings than Adapter/LoRA at similar parameter usage) and beats Adapter and LoRA in the low-memory regime; it also scales to T5-3B and CLIP-T5 vision-language tasks.","relations":[{"target":"side-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Adds ladder connections to intermediate activations and pruning-based init."},{"target":"k-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Both read intermediate backbone states into a side network."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Compared in the memory-accuracy trade-off."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.06522","https://arxiv.org/html/2206.06522","https://github.com/ylsung/Ladder-Side-Tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Yi-Lin Sung","Jaemin Cho","Mohit Bansal"],"bibkey":"sung2022lst","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sung2022lst,\n  title = {{LST: Ladder Side-Tuning for Parameter and Memory Efficient Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Yi-Lin Sung and Jaemin Cho and Mohit Bansal},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2206.06522},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.06522}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"VIII.B: E with base point '\\(\\approx\\)'. The prediction is \\(\\mathrm{LMHead}(\\mathrm{Up}(h^g_N))\\), and \\(h^g_N\\) has width \\(d/r\\). Solving \\(\\tilde f(\\theta_0,p,-)=f(\\theta_0,-)\\) would need \\(\\mathrm{Up}(h^g_N(x))=h^f_N(x)\\) for all \\(x\\), impossible because \\(\\mathrm{Up}\\) has rank \\(\\le d/r<d\\) while the backbone's final states span \\(\\mathbb R^d\\) (cut-rank, Thm II.10). So no neutral point exists, even approximately for generic inputs: E°.","shape":"VIII.B: Fun (a side network via trainable down-projection ladders).","base_point":"Unpointed by the cut-rank argument; the pruning-based init only approximates \\(f\\) and cannot reach it.","gauge":"The side Transformer's internal gauges (per-head query–key and value–output \\(GL\\)).","covariance":"The ladders \\(\\mathrm{Down}_i\\) and \\(\\mathrm{Up}\\) are dense linear maps: basis-free on the backbone's hidden wire. VIII.B leaves this blank.","merge":"The side network produces the prediction at inference.","rebasing":"n/a: no merge, and a second side network would replace the first one's prediction rather than compose with it."},"categorical_reading":"An extension whose prediction factors through a side network of width \\(d/r\\). By the cut-rank bound, \\(\\mathrm{LMHead}\\circ\\mathrm{Up}\\) has rank \\(\\le d/r\\), so no parameter value reproduces \\(f\\): LST is unpointed, not merely near-pointed. Its contribution is on the cotangent side: the ladders cut the backprop cone, so the backbone's backward pass never runs (Obs IV.4).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","test":"O3","reason":"A pointed arrow would send LST's base point to PA's neutral point, so both would realise \\(f\\); LST's base point cannot (cut-rank \\(d/r\\)). The same holds for every neutral-pointed extension."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"res-tuning","name":"Res-Tuning","full_name":"Res-Tuning / Res-Tuning-Bypass: tuners unbound from the backbone","paper_title":"Res-Tuning: A Flexible and Efficient Tuning Paradigm via Unbinding Tuner from Backbone","authors":"Jiang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2310.19859","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19859","code_url":"https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"x' = \\mathrm{OP}(x) + \\text{Res-Tuner}(x),\\ \\ \\mathrm{OP}\\in\\{\\mathrm{MHA},\\mathrm{FFN},\\text{block}\\}\\ \\text{frozen};\\quad \\text{Bypass: } x^{b}_{0} = x_0,\\ \\ x^{b}_{l} = \\lambda\\,\\text{Res-Tuner}_{h}(x_l) + (1-\\lambda)\\,\\text{Res-Tuner}_{v}(x^{b}_{l-1}),\\ \\lambda = \\mathrm{sigmoid}(\\cdot)\\ \\text{init } 0.5","trainable_params":"\\sum_{\\text{tuned ops}} |\\theta_{\\text{tuner}}|\\ \\ (\\text{tuner-dependent, e.g. } 2rd \\text{ per bottleneck tuner})","locus":"tuners in parallel to MHA, FFN and whole blocks (Res-Tuning), or a detached bypass network fed by every block output (Res-Tuning-Bypass)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"'Unbinding': prefix, prompt and adapter tuning are rewritten (with empirical equivalence) as frozen operation plus a parallel residual tuner, so tuners become free-standing modules added to backbone operations; the Bypass variant assembles horizontal (backbone \\(\\to\\) bypass) and vertical (bypass \\(\\to\\) bypass) tuners into a ladder-like side network.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Tuners are generally nonlinear (attention or MLP) modules, and the Bypass variant is a separate network.","identity_at_init":"Depends on the tuner instantiation (zero-initialized output projections give the identity in the attached form); the Bypass variant does not reproduce the backbone at init.","key_idea":"Decoupling tuner design from the backbone structure lets any tuner be attached anywhere and combined freely, and detaching the tuners into a bypass avoids backpropagating through the backbone and allows one backbone pass for many tasks.","results_note":"State-of-the-art VTAB-1K accuracy among PETL methods at similar parameter counts; Res-Tuning-Bypass cuts training memory by 49.7% and multi-task inference time by 80.9% on discriminative tasks, and by 70.7% / 83.6% for Stable Diffusion generation.","relations":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"generalizes","note":"Any tuner placed in parallel to any frozen operation."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"Prefix tuning unbound as a separate attention over prefixes added to MHA."},{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Bypass is a ladder-style side network for memory efficiency."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19859","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.19859","https://res-tuning.github.io/","https://github.com/modelscope/ms-swift/blob/main/swift/tuners/restuning.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Zeyinzi Jiang","Chaojie Mao","Ziyuan Huang","Ao Ma","Yiliang Lv","Yujun Shen","Deli Zhao","Jingren Zhou"],"bibkey":"jiang2023res","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jiang2023res,\n  title = {{Res-Tuning: A Flexible and Efficient Tuning Paradigm via Unbinding Tuner from Backbone}},\n  author = {Zeyinzi Jiang and Chaojie Mao and Ziyuan Huang and Ao Ma and Yiliang Lv and Yujun Shen and Deli Zhao and Jingren Zhou},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2310.19859},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19859}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Tuners are attached in parallel to frozen operations, \\(x'=\\mathrm{OP}(x)+\\text{Res-Tuner}(x)\\), with zero-initialised output projections. The Bypass variant is a ladder side network (unpointed, like LST).","shape":"Function-level (attention or MLP tuners).","base_point":"Neutral in the attached form (zero output projection); the Bypass variant does not reproduce the backbone at init.","gauge":"Tuner-dependent; recorded for the default bottleneck Res-Adapter, which is GELU (ModelScope SWIFT), so only discrete \\(S_r\\). Its optional channel-wise output scaling adds a torus (as Adapter+), and the separately normalised Res-Prefix tuner with free keys has a continuous key-translation gauge \\(K_P\\mapsto K_P+\\mathbf 1c^\\top\\) (a softmax over the prefix tokens alone is shift-invariant; Jacobian deficiency \\(d_k\\) per head), absent from joint-softmax prefix tuning.","covariance":"Dense tuner projections: basis-free.","merge":"Non-linear tuners, and the Bypass variant is a separate network.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level parallel tuners add."},"categorical_reading":"'Unbinding' rewrites every tuner as a parallel summand \\(\\mathrm{OP}+\\mathrm{Tuner}\\), moving it into the additive, parallel-adapter form. For prefix and prompt tuning this replaces the single softmax by a separately normalised branch, which is a different object (Thm VI.5(b) against (d)), not an equivalent one. The Bypass variant is an LST-type ladder.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"The unbound prefix tuner is a separately normalised branch: its family contains \\(f\\) and never rescales content attention. Prefix tuning is unpointed and multiplies content weights by \\(1-\\lambda_q\\). The 'equivalence' is empirical, not an isomorphism."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"controlnet","name":"ControlNet","full_name":"ControlNet: trainable-copy side network with zero convolutions","paper_title":"Adding Conditional Control to Text-to-Image Diffusion Models","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICCV 2023","arxiv":"2302.05543","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05543","code_url":"https://github.com/lllyasviel/ControlNet","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y_c = \\mathcal{F}(x;\\Theta) + \\mathcal{Z}\\big(\\mathcal{F}(x + \\mathcal{Z}(c;\\Theta_{z1});\\Theta_c);\\Theta_{z2}\\big),\\quad \\Theta\\ \\text{frozen},\\ \\Theta_c \\leftarrow \\Theta\\ \\text{(trainable copy)},\\ \\mathcal{Z} = 1{\\times}1\\ \\text{conv with zero weights and bias}","trainable_params":"|\\Theta_c| + |\\Theta_{z}| + |\\mathcal{E}|\\ \\ (\\text{trainable copy of the 12 encoder blocks and the middle block of the SD U-Net, the zero convolutions, and a tiny 4-layer conv encoder } \\mathcal{E} \\text{ mapping the } 512^2 \\text{ condition to } 64^2)","locus":"trainable copy of the U-Net encoder and middle block; its outputs are added through zero convolutions to the skip connections and middle block of the frozen U-Net","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Side network = trainable copy of the encoder that additionally receives the condition \\(c\\), connected to the frozen network at both ends through zero-initialized 1x1 convolutions; it extends the input object from \\(x\\) to \\((x, c)\\) and adds residual corrections to the frozen decoder's skip features.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The copy processes the extra condition input and must run at inference.","identity_at_init":"Exact: both zero convolutions output 0 at the first step, so \\(y_c = F(x; \\Theta)\\) and the trainable copy starts as the pretrained encoder.","key_idea":"Reuse the deep pretrained encoder as a backbone for learning a new conditioning signal by training a copy of it and connecting it with zero convolutions, which protects the frozen model from harmful gradients early in training.","results_note":"Adds spatial controls (edges, depth, segmentation, human pose, etc.) to Stable Diffusion, with training robust on both small (<50k) and large (>1M) datasets.","relations":[{"target":"side-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Copy-initialized side network added to a frozen model."},{"target":"t2i-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Lighter-weight alternative for spatial conditioning."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05543","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.05543","https://github.com/lllyasviel/ControlNet"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Lvmin Zhang","Anyi Rao","Maneesh Agrawala"],"bibkey":"zhang2023adding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023adding,\n  title = {{Adding Conditional Control to Text-to-Image Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Lvmin Zhang and Anyi Rao and Maneesh Agrawala},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2302.05543},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05543}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A trainable copy of the encoder, connected by zero \\(1\\times1\\) convolutions, that also extends the input object \\(X\\to X\\times C\\) (the condition \\(c\\)). It is pointed at the zero convolutions.","shape":"Function-level: a side copy adds residuals to skip and middle features.","base_point":"Exactly neutral: both zero convolutions output \\(0\\). At step one only the outer zero conv \\(\\mathcal Z_2\\) has non-zero gradient; the copy and \\(\\mathcal Z_1\\) are dead to first order (zero-gate lemma).","gauge":"Internal gauges of the copy (per-head \\(GL\\) in its attention blocks); the zero convolutions are dense channel maps.","covariance":"\\(1\\times1\\) zero convolutions are dense on channels at both ends: basis-free on the U-Net feature wires.","merge":"The copy processes the extra input \\(c\\) and must run at inference.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level ControlNets add (multi-ControlNet sums residuals)."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed side extension over the enlarged input \\(X\\times C\\): a copy of the encoder joined at both ends by zero convolutions. At initialisation the copy is dead to first order, and only the outer zero convolution moves. Its corrections are additive residuals on skip features, so several ControlNets compose by \\(\\oplus\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"t2i-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"T2I-Adapter's correction \\(F_c=\\mathcal F_{AD}(C)\\) is independent of the latent and the timestep, an invariant of its image. ControlNet's residuals depend on \\(x\\) through the copy, so they lie outside T2I's image."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"t2i-adapter","name":"T2I-Adapter","full_name":"T2I-Adapter: lightweight condition adapters for text-to-image diffusion","paper_title":"T2I-Adapter: Learning Adapters to Dig out More Controllable Ability for Text-to-Image Diffusion Models","authors":"Mou et al.","year":2023,"venue":"AAAI 2024","arxiv":"2302.08453","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08453","code_url":"https://github.com/TencentARC/T2I-Adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"F_c = \\mathcal{F}_{AD}(C),\\quad \\hat F^{\\,i}_{enc} = F^{\\,i}_{enc} + F^{\\,i}_{c},\\ i\\in\\{1,2,3,4\\};\\quad \\text{multi-condition: } F_c = \\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\omega_k\\,\\mathcal{F}^{k}_{AD}(C_k);\\ \\ \\text{SD frozen, } \\mathcal{F}_{AD}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\approx 77\\text{M per adapter (18M small, 5M tiny variants)}","locus":"external condition encoder whose four multi-scale features are added to the frozen U-Net encoder features","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Additive feature injection from a small external network (pixel-unshuffle, convolutions and residual blocks): each condition defines a translation of the frozen encoder's intermediate states at four scales, and several adapters compose by weighted sum without retraining.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The adapter processes the extra condition input at inference.","identity_at_init":"Not guaranteed by construction (no zero-init is described); the original model is recovered exactly when no adapter is attached or its weight \\(\\omega_k = 0\\).","key_idea":"Align the internal knowledge of a frozen text-to-image model with external control signals using small, composable condition encoders that only need one pass per generation.","results_note":"Supports sketch, depth, segmentation, keypose and color-palette control of Stable Diffusion with ~77M parameters, and multi-condition control by composing adapters without extra training.","relations":[{"target":"controlnet","type":"competes-with","note":"Smaller external encoder instead of a trainable copy of the U-Net encoder."},{"target":"ip-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Image-prompt vs structural-condition adapters for frozen diffusion models."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08453","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.08453","https://github.com/TencentARC/T2I-Adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Chong Mou","Xintao Wang","Liangbin Xie","Yanze Wu","Jian Zhang","Zhongang Qi","Ying Shan","Xiaohu Qie"],"bibkey":"mou2023t2i","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mou2023t2i,\n  title = {{T2I-Adapter: Learning Adapters to Dig out More Controllable Ability for Text-to-Image Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Chong Mou and Xintao Wang and Liangbin Xie and Yanze Wu and Jian Zhang and Zhongang Qi and Ying Shan and Xiaohu Qie},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2302.08453},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08453}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An external condition encoder whose multi-scale features are added to the frozen encoder states; the extension also enlarges the input to \\(X\\times C\\).","shape":"Function-level, over the condition object.","base_point":"No zero init, so at the start \\(F_c\\ne0\\): a pointing defect. A neutral point exists (\\(\\omega=0\\), or adapter output \\(0\\)).","gauge":"Positive per-unit slides through the ReLU residual blocks of the condition encoder.","covariance":"The output convolutions are dense on channels: basis-free on the U-Net feature wire. Pixel-unshuffle structures only the condition input.","merge":"For fixed \\(C\\) the edit is a constant translation of four encoder feature maps, but it varies over space. A bias slot is position-uniform, so nothing absorbs it (Thm VI.3(a)). It is computed once per generation.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). Multi-condition use is literally additive: \\(F_c=\\sum_k\\omega_k\\mathcal F^k_{AD}(C_k)\\)."},"categorical_reading":"An additive feature-injection extension over the condition object \\(C\\). The correction \\(F_c\\) is independent of the latent and the timestep, so for fixed \\(C\\) it is a constant, spatially varying translation of four encoder states, and multi-condition use is the \\(\\oplus\\) of adapters weighted by \\(\\omega_k\\). It is not zero-initialised, so it starts with a pointing defect.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"controlnet","test":"O3","reason":"Base-point mismatch: T2I's initial point does not realise the frozen model (no zero init), while ControlNet's does (zero convolutions). So no pointed arrow T2I \\(\\to\\) ControlNet exists, and their injection sites differ (encoder states against skip and middle outputs)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"ip-adapter","name":"IP-Adapter","full_name":"IP-Adapter: image prompt adapter with decoupled cross-attention","paper_title":"IP-Adapter: Text Compatible Image Prompt Adapter for Text-to-Image Diffusion Models","authors":"Ye et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2308.06721","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06721","code_url":"https://github.com/tencent-ailab/IP-Adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"Z^{new} = \\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V) + \\lambda\\cdot\\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K',V'),\\quad Q = ZW_q,\\ K = c_tW_k,\\ V = c_tW_v\\ \\text{(frozen)},\\ \\ K' = c_iW'_k,\\ V' = c_iW'_v\\ \\text{(trained, init from } W_k,W_v)","trainable_params":"2\\,d_{c}\\,d\\ \\text{per cross-attention layer } (W'_k,W'_v) + \\text{image-embedding projection}\\ \\ (\\approx 22\\text{M for SD 1.5, 16 layers})","locus":"a parallel image cross-attention branch added to every cross-attention layer of the U-Net, sharing the frozen query","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Decoupled cross-attention: a second attention over new image-prompt tokens (with its own key/value projections and its own softmax) is added to the frozen text cross-attention output; \\(\\lambda\\) scales the branch. Structurally this is the same decoupled-softmax parallel attention branch as LLaMA-Adapter's gated prompts, but its keys/values come from an input (the image embedding) through new trainable projections.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The image branch attends over extra tokens derived from the image prompt at inference.","identity_at_init":"Not at init (\\(W'_k, W'_v\\) are copied from \\(W_k, W_v\\) so the branch is active from the start); the original model is recovered exactly with \\(\\lambda = 0\\).","key_idea":"Instead of concatenating image and text features into the frozen cross-attention, add a separate cross-attention for image features so a small adapter gives strong image-prompt control while remaining compatible with text prompts and structural controls.","results_note":"A ~22M-parameter IP-Adapter is comparable to or better than fully fine-tuned image-prompt models and remains compatible with text prompts and ControlNet-style controls.","relations":[{"target":"t2i-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Another lightweight conditioning adapter for frozen diffusion models."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06721","https://arxiv.org/html/2308.06721","https://github.com/tencent-ailab/IP-Adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Hu Ye","Jun Zhang","Sibo Liu","Xiao Han","Wei Yang"],"bibkey":"ye2023ip","bibtex":"@article{ye2023ip,\n  title = {{IP-Adapter: Text Compatible Image Prompt Adapter for Text-to-Image Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Hu Ye and Jun Zhang and Sibo Liu and Xiao Han and Wei Yang},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2308.06721},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.06721},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.06721}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A decoupled cross-attention branch \\(\\lambda\\,\\mathrm{Attn}(Q,c_iW'_k,c_iW'_v)\\) over a new input object (image-prompt tokens).","shape":"Function-level: an extra softmax branch.","base_point":"\\(W'_v=0\\) (or \\(\\lambda=0\\)) is neutral, because the branch has its own softmax; but \\(W'_{k,v}\\) are initialised as copies of \\(W_{k,v}\\), so the branch is active from the start: a defect.","gauge":"The trained image projection (Linear + LayerNorm) feeds only the trainable \\(W'_k,W'_v\\), so the LN slide \\((\\gamma,\\beta,W'_k,W'_v)\\mapsto(t\\gamma,t\\beta,W'_k\\mathrm{diag}(t)^{-1},W'_v\\mathrm{diag}(t)^{-1})\\) is an exact gauge (R6). LN's mean-zero output adds a key-translation gauge through \\(W'_k\\) alone, \\(W'_k\\mapsto W'_k+c\\,a^\\top/(\\beta\\cdot a)\\) with \\(a=\\mathbf 1\\oslash\\gamma\\), plus LN's input scale and centring invariances on the projection. [Num: both slides exact to \\(10^{-14}\\); Jacobian deficiency \\(44\\) of \\(168\\), and \\(4=d_k\\) of \\(48\\) with the projection frozen.]","covariance":"\\(W'_k,W'_v\\) are dense and transform with the frozen head's query–key gauge: basis-free.","merge":"Extra attention over input-dependent tokens: non-affine (Thm VI.5).","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). Several decoupled branches add at function level."},"categorical_reading":"Structurally LLaMA-Adapter's separately normalised branch, \\(\\mathrm{Attn}(Q,K,V)+\\lambda\\,\\mathrm{Attn}(Q,K',V')\\), but with keys and values computed from an auxiliary input: an extension over \\(X\\times C_{\\rm img}\\). \\(V'=0\\) is neutral, but the copy initialisation starts it with a defect.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"llama-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"IP-Adapter's branch keys and values are functions of the image input \\(c_i\\); LLaMA-Adapter's prompt keys and values are constants, so it cannot realise image-dependent branches."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adaptformer","name":"AdaptFormer","full_name":"AdaptFormer (AdaptMLP parallel bottleneck for ViTs)","paper_title":"AdaptFormer: Adapting Vision Transformers for Scalable Visual Recognition","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2205.13535","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13535","code_url":"https://github.com/ShoufaChen/AdaptFormer","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\tilde x_\\ell = W_{up}\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(W_{down}\\,\\mathrm{LN}(x'_\\ell)\\big),\\quad x_\\ell = \\mathrm{MLP}\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(x'_\\ell)\\big) + s\\cdot\\tilde x_\\ell + x'_\\ell,\\ \\ s = 0.1;\\ \\ \\text{ViT frozen, } W_{down},W_{up}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2rd + r + d\\ \\text{per block}","locus":"in parallel to the MLP (FFN) block of every ViT layer","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Scaled parallel bottleneck adapter on the FFN (AdaptMLP), i.e. the vision instance of the scaled parallel adapter with \\(s < 1\\).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear bottleneck in parallel with a nonlinear MLP.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W_{up}\\) and all adapter biases are zero-initialized (\\(W_{down}\\) Kaiming-normal), so the branch outputs 0 at init.","key_idea":"Adapt ViTs through a lightweight parallel branch next to the MLP rather than through token-space prompts, which scales to the many tokens of video.","results_note":"With ViT-B, ~1.5% extra parameters give about 10% and 19% relative improvement over full fine-tuning on Something-Something v2 and HMDB51, and it beats VPT on image benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"equivalent-to","note":"Same functional form (scaled parallel adapter at the FFN), concurrently proposed for vision."},{"target":"vpt","type":"competes-with","note":"Main vision PEFT baseline."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13535","https://arxiv.org/html/2205.13535","https://github.com/ShoufaChen/AdaptFormer"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Shoufa Chen","Chongjian Ge","Zhan Tong","Jiangliu Wang","Yibing Song","Jue Wang","Ping Luo"],"bibkey":"chen2022adaptformer","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2022adaptformer,\n  title = {{AdaptFormer: Adapting Vision Transformers for Scalable Visual Recognition}},\n  author = {Shoufa Chen and Chongjian Ge and Zhan Tong and Jiangliu Wang and Yibing Song and Jue Wang and Ping Luo},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2205.13535},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13535}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A scaled parallel ReLU bottleneck next to the ViT MLP; \\(W_{up}=0\\) is neutral.","shape":"Function-level, as the parallel adapter.","base_point":"Exactly neutral: \\(W_{up}\\) and the biases are zero-initialised.","gauge":"ReLU positive per-unit slide (R3) \\(\\times S_r\\).","covariance":"Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"Thm VI.3(c): for generic \\(U,D\\) the modified box function is non-affine in the box input, so no box-local merge exists.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze and re-insert) parallel bottlenecks add, \\(F+\\sum_kU_k\\sigma(D_k\\,\\cdot)\\) = one bottleneck of width \\(Kr\\): the additive closure (span of the bottleneck cone), as for LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"The scaled parallel adapter at the FFN with \\(s=0.1\\), isomorphic to He et al.'s parallel adapter by rescaling \\(W_{up}\\). It is the vision instance of the additive extension.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_{down},b_{down},W_{up},b_{up})=\\big(W_{down},b_{down},\\tfrac{0.1}{s}W_{up},\\tfrac{0.1}{s}b_{up}\\big)","note":"An isomorphism: the same ReLU bottleneck reading the MLP input \\(\\mathrm{LN}(x')\\); only the fixed scale differs."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vpt","test":"O1","reason":"\\(f\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{AdaptFormer}\\) (at \\(W_{up}=0\\)), but VPT's prompt tokens always take attention mass, so VPT is unpointed and cannot simulate it."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Non-affine parallel branch (Thm VI.3(c))."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"convpass","name":"Convpass","full_name":"Convolutional Bypasses for ViT","paper_title":"Convolutional Bypasses Are Better Vision Transformer Adapters","authors":"Jie and Deng","year":2022,"venue":"ECAI 2024","arxiv":"2207.07039","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07039","code_url":"https://github.com/JieShibo/PETL-ViT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"X' \\leftarrow X + \\mathrm{MHSA/MLP}\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(X)\\big) + s\\cdot\\mathrm{Convpass}\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(X)\\big),\\quad \\mathrm{Convpass} = \\mathrm{Conv}^{\\uparrow}_{1\\times1}\\circ\\mathrm{GELU}\\circ\\mathrm{Conv}_{3\\times3}\\circ\\mathrm{GELU}\\circ\\mathrm{Conv}^{\\downarrow}_{1\\times1}\\ \\text{on tokens reshaped to the patch grid ([cls] as a } 1{\\times}1 \\text{ image)}","trainable_params":"2L\\big((2r+1)d + 9r^2 + 2r\\big)\\ \\text{in total (two Convpass per layer; paper } r = 8)","locus":"in parallel to both MHSA and MLP in every ViT block","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Parallel convolutional bottleneck (ResNet-style 1x1-3x3-1x1 block on the reshaped token grid) that injects spatial-locality inductive bias; unrolled, each layer becomes an ensemble of transformer, CNN and hybrid paths.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The 3x3 convolution mixes neighbouring tokens and the block is nonlinear, so it cannot be absorbed into per-token frozen weights.","identity_at_init":"Exact in the official code: up-projection weights and biases are zero-initialized (down-projection Xavier; 3x3 conv initialized to identity at its centre tap).","key_idea":"Language-oriented adapters lack visual inductive bias; a convolutional bypass restores 2D locality during fine-tuning and works especially well in the low-data regime.","results_note":"With under 0.5% extra parameters, Convpass outperforms language-oriented PETL modules (Adapter, LoRA, VPT, NOAH) on VTAB-1K and few-shot fine-grained classification.","relations":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Parallel insertion with a scaling factor, with a convolutional bottleneck."},{"target":"adaptformer","type":"competes-with","note":"Vision adapter baseline."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07039","https://arxiv.org/html/2207.07039","https://github.com/JieShibo/PETL-ViT","https://ebooks.iospress.nl/doi/10.3233/FAIA240489"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Shibo Jie","Zhi-Hong Deng"],"bibkey":"jie2022convolutional","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jie2022convolutional,\n  title = {{Convolutional Bypasses Are Better Vision Transformer Adapters}},\n  author = {Shibo Jie and Zhi-Hong Deng},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {ECAI},\n  eprint = {2207.07039},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07039}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Parallel convolutional bottlenecks (\\(1\\times1\\)–\\(3\\times3\\)–\\(1\\times1\\) on the patch grid) next to both MHSA and MLP; a zero up-projection is neutral.","shape":"Function-level: non-linear and token-mixing.","base_point":"Exactly neutral in the official code (zero up-projection weights and biases).","gauge":"Both activations are GELU and the \\(3\\times3\\) conv is channel-dense, so only discrete \\(S_r\\) survives (Lemma VI.2(c)).","covariance":"Channel-dense projections and convolution: basis-free on the channel wire. The \\(2\\)D grid is structure imposed on token positions, which Def II.14's channel groups do not see; it is what blocks merging.","merge":"The \\(3\\times3\\) convolution mixes neighbouring tokens, so the edit is not position-uniform and no per-token linear box can absorb it (Thm VI.3(a)); it is also non-affine.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze and re-insert) parallel bottlenecks add, \\(F+\\sum_kU_k\\sigma(D_k\\,\\cdot)\\) = one bottleneck of width \\(Kr\\): the additive closure (span of the bottleneck cone), as for LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"A parallel extension whose bottleneck contains a \\(3\\times3\\) convolution over the patch grid, so its correction at a token depends on the token's neighbours: a token-mixing edit outside every per-token method. With the convolution frozen at its centre-tap identity init it becomes a per-token parallel adapter with activation \\(\\mathrm{GELU}\\circ\\mathrm{GELU}\\) (an A2 sub-object).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"PA's correction at token \\(i\\) depends only on \\(x_i\\), an invariant of its image (token locality); Convpass's depends on the \\(3\\times3\\) grid neighbourhood."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Token mixing and non-affinity put its realisations outside \\(\\Phi_f(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA})\\)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"st-adapter","name":"ST-Adapter","full_name":"Spatio-Temporal Adapter","paper_title":"ST-Adapter: Parameter-Efficient Image-to-Video Transfer Learning","authors":"Pan et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2206.13559","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13559","code_url":"https://github.com/linziyi96/st-adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{ST\\text{-}Adapter}(X) = X + f\\big(\\mathrm{DWConv3D}(XW_{down})\\big)\\,W_{up},\\quad X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{T\\times N\\times d}\\ \\text{reshaped to } T\\times h\\times w\\times r \\text{ before the depth-wise } 3{\\times}1{\\times}1 \\text{ convolution};\\ \\text{image backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"2rd + k_Tk_Hk_W\\,r + O(d + r)\\ \\text{per adapter } (k = 3{\\times}1{\\times}1)","locus":"one adapter before the MHSA of every ViT block (operating on the \\(T\\) frames' patch tokens)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Serial residual bottleneck with a depth-wise spatio-temporal convolution in the compressed space, i.e. a token-mixing linear operator across neighbouring frames inserted between down- and up-projection; it gives an image model temporal reasoning. (The official code has no activation between the depth-wise convolution and \\(W_{up}\\), and adapts only the patch tokens, leaving [CLS] unchanged.)","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The 3D convolution mixes tokens across frames and positions, so it cannot be folded into per-token frozen weights.","identity_at_init":"Exact in the official code: depth-wise 3D-conv weights and all biases are zero-initialized, so the adapter outputs \\(X\\) at init.","key_idea":"Turn a frozen image transformer into a video model by inserting cheap adapters whose bottleneck contains a depth-wise 3D convolution for temporal modelling.","results_note":"With CLIP ViT-B/16, ST-Adapter matches full fine-tuning on Kinetics-400 (82.0 vs 81.7) and SSv2 (66.3 vs 66.1) while updating 7.2M vs 121.6M parameters.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Adds a spatio-temporal operator inside the bottleneck."},{"target":"aim","type":"competes-with","note":"Image-to-video adaptation by adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13559","https://arxiv.org/html/2206.13559","https://github.com/linziyi96/st-adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Junting Pan","Ziyi Lin","Xiatian Zhu","Jing Shao","Hongsheng Li"],"bibkey":"pan2022st","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pan2022st,\n  title = {{ST-Adapter: Parameter-Efficient Image-to-Video Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Junting Pan and Ziyi Lin and Xiatian Zhu and Jing Shao and Hongsheng Li},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2206.13559},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.13559}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A serial residual bottleneck before each MHSA, with a depthwise \\(3\\times1\\times1\\) spatio-temporal convolution in the compressed space.","shape":"Function-level. The official code has no activation after the convolution, which makes the adapter an affine but token-mixing operator \\(X\\mapsto X+\\mathrm{DWConv3D}(XW_{down})W_{up}\\); still no weight-space image.","base_point":"Exactly neutral in the official code (depthwise kernels and all biases zero).","gauge":"Per-channel slide between \\(W_{down}\\) and the depthwise convolution, both linear: a torus on bottleneck channels, times \\(S_r\\). In the official activation-free code a second channel torus sits between the depthwise convolution and \\(W_{up}\\), and the conv bias trades against \\(b_{up}\\) [Num: Jacobian deficiency \\(9=2r+r\\) at \\(r=3\\)].","covariance":"\\(W_{down},W_{up}\\) are dense, so the outer wire is basis-free; the depthwise convolution privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge).","merge":"The edit mixes tokens across frames and positions, so it is not position-uniform, and no per-token box absorbs it (Thm VI.3(a)), even in the activation-free version.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained adapter, insert a fresh one) the stages compose, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ F\\) with \\(S=\\{\\mathrm{id}+W_{up}\\,\\mathrm{DWConv}(W_{down}\\,\\cdot)\\}\\): the action-type closure."},"categorical_reading":"A serial bottleneck extension whose bottleneck contains a depthwise temporal convolution: a linear, token-mixing operator in the compressed space. It is M∞ because it is not a per-token edit, not because of an activation. The per-channel convolution privileges only the bottleneck basis (a torus gauge), not the residual wire.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"Houlsby's correction is per-token; ST-Adapter's mixes neighbouring frames, so it lies outside Houlsby's image."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"aim","name":"AIM","full_name":"Adapting Image Models (spatial, temporal and joint adaptation)","paper_title":"AIM: Adapting Image Models for Efficient Video Action Recognition","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2302.03024","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03024","code_url":"https://github.com/taoyang1122/adapt-image-models","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"z^{T}_{l} = z_{l-1} + \\mathrm{Ad}_T\\big(\\text{T-MSA}(\\mathrm{LN}(z_{l-1}))\\big),\\ \\ z^{S}_{l} = z^{T}_{l} + \\mathrm{Ad}_S\\big(\\text{S-MSA}(\\mathrm{LN}(z^{T}_{l}))\\big),\\ \\ z_l = z^{S}_{l} + \\mathrm{MLP}\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(z^{S}_{l})\\big) + s\\cdot\\mathrm{Ad}_J\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(z^{S}_{l})\\big);\\ \\ \\text{T-MSA} = \\text{S-MSA frozen, applied along time};\\ s = 0.5","trainable_params":"3\\,(2rd + r + d)\\ \\text{per block } (r = d/4)","locus":"three adapters per ViT block: after the reused temporal attention (no skip), after spatial attention (with skip), and parallel to the MLP (no skip, scaled by \\(s\\))","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Reuses the frozen spatial self-attention as a temporal attention by re-indexing tokens (the same frozen operator acting along a different axis), and adds serial adapters after both attentions plus a scaled parallel adapter at the MLP.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear adapters and an additional (reused) attention pass at inference.","identity_at_init":"Exact. The second FC layer of every adapter is zero-initialized, and the temporal and joint (MLP-parallel) adapters have no internal skip connection, so both branches output 0 while the spatial adapter (with skip) is the identity. The model equals the image model at init.","key_idea":"Equip a frozen image transformer with spatio-temporal reasoning without new attention weights: reuse its attention over time and train only lightweight adapters.","results_note":"Achieves comparable or better accuracy than fully fine-tuned video models with far fewer tunable parameters on four action-recognition benchmarks (Kinetics-400/700, Something-Something v2, Diving-48).","relations":[{"target":"st-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Image-to-video adaptation with adapters."},{"target":"adaptformer","type":"builds-on","note":"Joint adaptation is a scaled parallel adapter at the MLP."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03024","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.03024","https://github.com/taoyang1122/adapt-image-models"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Taojiannan Yang","Yi Zhu","Yusheng Xie","Aston Zhang","Chen Chen","Mu Li"],"bibkey":"yang2023aim","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2023aim,\n  title = {{AIM: Adapting Image Models for Efficient Video Action Recognition}},\n  author = {Taojiannan Yang and Yi Zhu and Yusheng Xie and Aston Zhang and Chen Chen and Mu Li},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2302.03024},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.03024}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Inserts a new box, a temporal attention pass whose weights are tied to the frozen spatial attention, neutralised by a zero-initialised adapter without skip. It adds a serial spatial adapter and a scaled parallel MLP adapter.","shape":"Function-level (non-linear adapters and an extra attention pass).","base_point":"Exactly neutral: every adapter's second FC is zero, so the temporal and joint branches output \\(0\\) and the spatial adapter (with skip) is the identity.","gauge":"GELU adapters: only the discrete \\(S_r\\) per adapter.","covariance":"Dense adapters, and the reused attention keeps the base geometry: basis-free.","merge":"An extra attention pass along time plus non-linear adapters.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). At function level the parts close differently (serial \\(\\mathrm{Ad}_S\\) by composition, residual \\(\\mathrm{Ad}_T\\) and parallel \\(\\mathrm{Ad}_J\\) additively)."},"categorical_reading":"An extension that inserts a new box, temporal attention, with weights tied to the frozen spatial attention and neutralised by a zero-initialised adapter, together with a serial spatial adapter and a scaled parallel MLP adapter. A GELU parallel adapter embeds in AIM by freezing the temporal and spatial adapters at zero (A2); He et al.'s ReLU PA does not, since AIM's adapters are GELU (official vit_clip.py).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"adaptformer","test":"O1","reason":"AIM's temporal pass re-applies the frozen attention across frames, so its realisations mix tokens over time. AdaptFormer is per-token on a frame-wise ViT and cannot reproduce them."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"repadapter","name":"RepAdapter","full_name":"Re-parameterizable visual adapter","paper_title":"Towards Efficient Visual Adaption via Structural Re-parameterization","authors":"Luo et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2302.08106","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08106","code_url":"https://github.com/luogen1996/RepAdapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{Ad}(x) = x + s\\big(W_u(W_dx + b_d) + b_u\\big),\\ \\ W_d\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}\\ \\text{dense},\\ W_u\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ \\text{group-wise (block-diagonal, } k \\text{ groups)};\\quad \\text{placed before a frozen projection } W_0:\\ W_0' = W_0\\,(I + sW_uW_d),\\ \\ b_0' = b_0 + sW_0(W_ub_d + b_u)","trainable_params":"rd + \\tfrac{rd}{k} + r + d\\ \\text{per adapter (two adapters per block)}","locus":"sequentially before MHA (i.e. feeding \\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\)) and before the FFN (feeding \\(W_1\\)) in every ViT block, after LayerNorm","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Activation-free serial adapter \\(I + sW_uW_d\\) (identity plus a rank-\\(\\le r\\), group-structured matrix) placed immediately before a frozen linear map, so it is absorbed by right multiplication \\(W_0 \\mapsto W_0(I + sW_uW_d)\\); equivalently an additive update \\(\\Delta W = sW_0W_uW_d\\). Structurally it is a low-rank, token-space instance of Rebuffi et al.'s linear residual adapter \\(I+\\alpha\\), although RepAdapter does not cite that work. One adapter feeds \\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\) and \\(W_v\\) together, so their merged updates share the same factor.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Without nonlinearity the adapter is an affine map preceding a frozen linear projection, so it folds exactly into that projection's weight and bias, giving zero extra inference cost.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W_u\\) and both biases are zero-initialized (\\(W_d\\) Xavier), so the adapter is the identity at init.","key_idea":"Removing the nonlinearity of visual adapters costs no accuracy, and a linear adapter placed before a projection can be re-parameterized into it, giving adapter-style training with LoRA-like zero inference overhead.","results_note":"Outperforms prior PETL methods on VTAB-1k on average with zero added inference latency, and transfers to ConvNeXt, Swin, CLIP, segmentation and video tasks.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both have zero inference overhead after merging, and RepAdapter reports +1.6% average VTAB-1k over LoRA. RepAdapter's merged update Delta W = sW_0W_uW_d (plus a bias shift) is rank <= r, but B = sW_0W_u is tied to W_0, W_u is group-wise, and A = W_d is shared by all projections fed by the same adapter. It is a constrained LoRA-type update, not an equivalent one."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Serial bottleneck without the nonlinearity."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08106","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.08106","https://github.com/luogen1996/RepAdapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Gen Luo","Minglang Huang","Yiyi Zhou","Xiaoshuai Sun","Guannan Jiang","Zhiyu Wang","Rongrong Ji"],"bibkey":"luo2023efficient","bibtex":"@article{luo2023efficient,\n  title = {{Towards Efficient Visual Adaption via Structural Re-parameterization}},\n  author = {Gen Luo and Minglang Huang and Yiyi Zhou and Xiaoshuai Sun and Guannan Jiang and Zhiyu Wang and Rongrong Ji},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2302.08106},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.08106},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08106}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An activation-free serial adapter before frozen projections. It descends along the merge to the weight-space object \\(W_0\\mapsto W_0(I+sW_uW_d)\\), with \\(b_0'=b_0+sW_0(W_ub_d+b_u)\\): kind R, as for SSF in VIII.B.","shape":"\\(\\rho-\\theta_0=sW_0W_uW_d\\) is bilinear: a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), the transport by \\(W_0\\cdot\\) of \\(\\{UD:U\\ \\text{group-wise}\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"\\(W_u=0\\), \\(W_d\\) Xavier: only \\(W_u\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4). \\(\\dim S_1=rd/k\\) against \\(d=rd/k+rd-r^2/k\\), a positive deficit.","gauge":"\\((W_ug^{-1},gW_d)\\) with \\(g\\in\\prod_{j=1}^kGL_{r/k}\\) block-diagonal, so that \\(W_u\\) stays group-wise (as GraLoRA's \\(\\prod GL_{r/k}\\)).","covariance":"\\(\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(gW_0h^{-1})=g\\,\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(W_0)\\,h^{-1}\\) requires \\(h\\,\\mathrm{BlkDiag}=\\mathrm{BlkDiag}\\) up to right gauge, i.e. \\(h\\) in the block normaliser. So \\(GL\\) on the output side and \\(N_{\\rm blk}\\) on the input side: imposed groups, \\(\\Pi\\). With \\(k=1\\) it would be \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Fuses into the consumer projections \\(W_q,W_k,W_v\\) (or \\(W_1\\)) by Thm VI.3(a). Side conditions: all consumers unshared, and a bias slot for the shift. One adapter feeds \\(q,k,v\\) together.","rebasing":"An action: \\(W_0(I+sU_1D_1)(I+sU_2D_2)\\cdots\\). The shape contains \\(I+tE_{ab}\\) for every \\(a,b\\) and every real \\(t\\) (a rank-one matrix inside one row block), so already one factor can have \\(\\det<0\\) (\\(t=-2\\)) or be singular (\\(t=-1\\)). The generated Lie algebra is \\(\\mathfrak{gl}(d)\\) (Thm V.3(b)) and the generated monoid is all of \\(M_d(\\mathbb R)\\): \\(R_\\infty=W_0\\cdot M_d(\\mathbb R)\\) (the closure of \\(W_0\\cdot GL(d)\\); \\(=\\Theta\\) for invertible \\(W_0\\)), with relative update rank \\(\\le Kr\\) after \\(K\\) cycles. Because one adapter feeds \\(q,k,v\\), the reach is the tied set \\(\\{(W_qM,W_kM,W_vM)\\}\\), not \\(\\Theta^3\\)."},"categorical_reading":"A linear serial adapter that descends along the merge to the R-action object \\(W_0\\mapsto W_0(I+sW_uW_d)\\). That object is a constrained LoRA with \\(B=sW_0W_u\\) tied to \\(W_0\\), group-wise \\(W_u\\), and one \\(A=W_d\\) shared by every projection the adapter feeds. Rebasing composes right multiplications and walks the generated monoid, reaching \\(W_0\\cdot M_d(\\mathbb R)\\), tied across the projections one adapter feeds.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_u,W_d)=\\big((sW_0^{(x)}W_u,\\ W_d)\\big)_{x\\in\\{q,k,v\\}}","note":"The merged update \\(\\Delta W^{(x)}=sW_0^{(x)}W_uW_d\\) is a point of LoRA\\(_r^{\\times3}\\) pointed at \\((0,W_d^0)\\) (\\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum); the bias shift needs \\(\\oplus\\) BitFit (A5). With a shared \\(A\\) and tied \\(B\\) it is a constrained LoRA, not an equivalent one."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mona","name":"Mona","full_name":"Multi-cognitive Visual Adapter","paper_title":"5%>100%: Breaking Performance Shackles of Full Fine-Tuning on Visual Recognition Tasks","authors":"Yin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"CVPR 2025","arxiv":"2408.08345","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.08345","code_url":"https://github.com/Leiyi-Hu/mona","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"x_{norm} = s_1\\cdot\\mathrm{LN}(x_0) + s_2\\cdot x_0;\\ \\ f_{dw}(z) = z + \\tfrac13\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\omega^{i}_{dw}\\,\\hat\\otimes\\,z\\ (3{\\times}3,5{\\times}5,7{\\times}7);\\ \\ f_{pw}(z) = z + \\omega_{pw}\\,\\bar\\otimes\\,z;\\ \\ x = x_0 + U^{l}\\,\\sigma\\big(f_{pw}(f_{dw}(D^{l}x_{norm}))\\big)","trainable_params":"(2r+3)d + r^2 + 84r + 2\\ \\text{per Mona module; two per block } (r = 64)","locus":"two serial Mona modules per Swin block, after the MSA and after the MLP","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Serial residual bottleneck whose input is a learned blend of normalized and raw features and whose compressed space is processed by averaged multi-scale depth-wise convolutions and a 1x1 convolution, each with its own skip connection.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear, token-mixing convolutional bottleneck.","identity_at_init":"No. The paper states no identity-at-init guarantee, and the official code leaves the up-projection \\(U^l\\) at PyTorch's default (Kaiming-uniform) init, so the adapter output is not zero at init. In code \\(s_1\\) and \\(s_2\\) are per-channel vectors initialized to 1e-6 and 1.","key_idea":"Make visual adapters 'see' like vision models (multi-scale depth-wise filters) and let them re-normalize the frozen features, so adapter tuning can surpass full fine-tuning on dense prediction tasks. First released in Nov 2023 as arXiv:2311.15010 ('Adapter is All You Need for Tuning Visual Tasks').","results_note":"With Swin backbones, Mona surpasses full fine-tuning on COCO instance segmentation (about +1%), ADE20K, Pascal VOC, DOTA/STAR oriented detection and image classification.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Serial bottleneck with visual filters and scaled LayerNorm."},{"target":"convpass","type":"competes-with","note":"Convolutional visual adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.08345","https://arxiv.org/html/2408.08345","https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.15010","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.15010","https://github.com/Leiyi-Hu/mona"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Dongshuo Yin","Leiyi Hu","Bin Li","Youqun Zhang","Xue Yang"],"bibkey":"yin20245","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yin20245,\n  title = {{5\\%>100\\%: Breaking Performance Shackles of Full Fine-Tuning on Visual Recognition Tasks}},\n  author = {Dongshuo Yin and Leiyi Hu and Bin Li and Youqun Zhang and Xue Yang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2408.08345},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.08345}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A serial residual bottleneck with a learned input blend and multi-scale depthwise convolutions; \\(U=0\\) would be neutral.","shape":"Function-level (non-linear and token-mixing).","base_point":"The official code leaves \\(U^l\\) at the default Kaiming-uniform init, so the adapter output is non-zero at the start: a defect, although a neutral point (\\(U=0\\)) exists.","gauge":"The per-channel \\(s_1,s_2\\) and the columns of the dense \\(D\\) are related by a diagonal slide \\((s_1,s_2,D)\\mapsto(s_1t,s_2t,D\\,\\mathrm{diag}(t)^{-1})\\): a \\(d\\)-dimensional torus.","covariance":"The per-channel blend \\(s_1\\odot\\mathrm{LN}(x)+s_2\\odot x\\) and a coordinate LayerNorm fix the standard basis of the wire; only neuron permutations survive. \\(U\\) is dense, but the wire is a single endomorphism: Mon on both sides.","merge":"Token-mixing convolutions and a non-linear bottleneck (Thm VI.3(a),(c)).","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained module, insert a fresh one) the stages compose, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ F\\) with \\(S\\) the family of Mona modules: the action-type closure."},"categorical_reading":"A serial bottleneck extension whose input is a learned blend \\(s_1\\odot\\mathrm{LN}(x)+s_2\\odot x\\) and whose bottleneck runs multi-scale depthwise convolutions. Token mixing makes it M∞, and the per-channel blend fixes the standard basis of the wire (coordinate geometry). The default up-projection is not zero, so it starts with a pointing defect.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"Mona's depthwise \\(3/5/7\\) convolutions mix neighbouring tokens; Houlsby's correction is per-token, an invariant of its image."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"clip-adapter","name":"CLIP-Adapter","full_name":"CLIP-Adapter: residual feature adapters for CLIP","paper_title":"CLIP-Adapter: Better Vision-Language Models with Feature Adapters","authors":"Gao et al.","year":2021,"venue":"IJCV 2024","arxiv":"2110.04544","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04544","code_url":"https://github.com/gaopengcuhk/CLIP-Adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"A_v(f) = \\mathrm{ReLU}(f^{\\top}W^{v}_{1})W^{v}_{2},\\quad f^{\\star} = \\alpha\\,A_v(f)^{\\top} + (1-\\alpha)\\,f;\\ \\ (\\text{optionally } W^{\\star} = \\beta A_t(W)^{\\top} + (1-\\beta)W);\\quad \\text{logits} \\propto W^{\\star}f^{\\star};\\ \\text{CLIP encoders frozen}","trainable_params":"2dr\\ \\text{per adapted branch } (r = d/4;\\ \\text{default: visual branch only})","locus":"on the final CLIP image embedding (and optionally the text-generated classifier weights), outside the encoders","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Residual-blended bottleneck MLP on the output embedding: a convex combination with residual ratio \\(\\alpha\\) of the frozen feature and its adapted image, i.e. post-composition of the encoder with an endomorphism of the embedding space.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"A nonlinear MLP after the encoder (negligible cost, but not foldable).","identity_at_init":"No exact identity: the bottleneck is randomly initialized; closeness to zero-shot CLIP is controlled by the residual ratio \\(\\alpha\\) (small for generic datasets, larger for fine-grained or shifted domains).","key_idea":"For few-shot CLIP transfer, a tiny residual feature adapter at the end of the encoder, blended with the original feature, beats learning prompts (CoOp) while avoiding overfitting.","results_note":"Outperforms zero-shot CLIP, linear-probe CLIP and CoOp on 11 few-shot classification datasets across shot settings, with large gains on fine-grained datasets.","relations":[{"target":"coop","type":"competes-with","note":"Feature adapter vs learned text prompts for few-shot CLIP."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Bottleneck adapter applied once to the output feature with a residual ratio."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04544","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.04544","https://github.com/gaopengcuhk/CLIP-Adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Peng Gao","Shijie Geng","Renrui Zhang","Teli Ma","Rongyao Fang","Yongfeng Zhang","Hongsheng Li","Yu Qiao"],"bibkey":"gao2021clip","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gao2021clip,\n  title = {{CLIP-Adapter: Better Vision-Language Models with Feature Adapters}},\n  author = {Peng Gao and Shijie Geng and Renrui Zhang and Teli Ma and Rongyao Fang and Yongfeng Zhang and Hongsheng Li and Yu Qiao},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {IJCV},\n  eprint = {2110.04544},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04544}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A residual-blended bottleneck MLP post-composed with the frozen encoder on the output embedding.","shape":"Function-level (a non-linear endomorphism of the embedding space).","base_point":"After CLIP's feature normalisation, \\(W_2=0\\) gives \\((1-\\alpha)f/\\lVert(1-\\alpha)f\\rVert=f/\\lVert f\\rVert\\), a neutral point. The bottleneck is randomly initialised, so it starts with a defect controlled by \\(\\alpha\\).","gauge":"The ReLU positive per-unit slide between \\(W_1\\) and \\(W_2\\).","covariance":"\\(W_1,W_2\\) are dense and the blend \\(\\alpha A+(1-\\alpha)\\mathrm{id}\\) is basis-free on the embedding wire. This is the paper's form; the official code adds a second ReLU after \\(W_2\\), which privileges the embedding basis and would cut the covariance to positive monomials (Mon\\(\\times\\)Mon).","merge":"A non-linear MLP after the encoder (negligible cost, but not foldable).","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained adapter, insert a fresh one) the stages compose on the embedding, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ\\mathrm{enc}\\) with \\(S=\\{\\mathrm{id}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}A_v\\}\\) (after normalisation): the action-type closure."},"categorical_reading":"A serial residual extension on the output embedding: \\(f^\\star=\\alpha A_v(f)+(1-\\alpha)f\\), which after CLIP's normalisation is \\((\\mathrm{id}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}A_v)\\circ\\mathrm{enc}\\). That is a single Houlsby-type block at the end of the encoder. \\(W_2=0\\) is neutral, but the random initialisation is not.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"coop","test":"O1","reason":"CoOp moves only the class weight vectors, so its logits stay linear functionals of the frozen normalised image embedding. CLIP-Adapter's logits are non-linear in that embedding."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"tip-adapter","name":"Tip-Adapter","full_name":"Training-free CLIP-Adapter (cache model); Tip-Adapter-F","paper_title":"Tip-Adapter: Training-free Adaption of CLIP for Few-shot Classification","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ECCV 2022","arxiv":"2207.09519","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.09519","code_url":"https://github.com/gaopengcuhk/Tip-Adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{logits} = \\alpha\\,\\varphi\\big(f_{test}F_{train}^{\\top}\\big)L_{train} + f_{test}W_c^{\\top},\\quad \\varphi(x) = \\exp\\big(-\\beta(1-x)\\big);\\quad F_{train}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{NK\\times d}\\ \\text{(cached CLIP features)},\\ L_{train}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{NK\\times N};\\ \\text{Tip-Adapter-F trains } F_{train}\\ \\text{only}","trainable_params":"0\\ (\\text{Tip-Adapter});\\ \\ NK\\,d\\ (\\text{Tip-Adapter-F: cache keys})","locus":"on the final CLIP image embedding: a key-value cache built from the \\(K\\)-shot \\(N\\)-class training set, blended with CLIP's zero-shot logits","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Non-parametric key-value cache acting as a two-layer adapter whose first layer is the matrix of cached features (with exponential activation) and whose second layer is the one-hot label matrix, added residually to the zero-shot classifier; Tip-Adapter-F unfreezes the keys as an initialization.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The cache (\\(NK\\) keys) must be stored and queried at inference.","identity_at_init":"With \\(\\alpha = 0\\) the model is zero-shot CLIP exactly; the cache is constructed (not randomly initialized) from few-shot features, so no training is needed.","key_idea":"Set the adapter's weights directly from the few-shot data (keys = image features, values = labels), making CLIP adaptation training-free; optionally fine-tune the keys for a few epochs.","results_note":"Training-free Tip-Adapter is comparable to CoOp and CLIP-Adapter, and Tip-Adapter-F sets the state of the art on 16-shot ImageNet with only 20 epochs (vs 200 for CoOp/CLIP-Adapter), across 11 datasets. First released Nov 2021 as arXiv:2111.03930.","relations":[{"target":"clip-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"A CLIP-Adapter whose bottleneck weights are set from the cache (features and one-hot labels) with an exponential activation."},{"target":"coop","type":"competes-with","note":"Few-shot CLIP adaptation baseline."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.09519","https://arxiv.org/html/2207.09519","https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03930","https://github.com/gaopengcuhk/Tip-Adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Renrui Zhang","Zhang Wei","Rongyao Fang","Peng Gao","Kunchang Li","Jifeng Dai","Yu Qiao","Hongsheng Li"],"bibkey":"zhang2022tip","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2022tip,\n  title = {{Tip-Adapter: Training-free Adaption of CLIP for Few-shot Classification}},\n  author = {Renrui Zhang and Zhang Wei and Rongyao Fang and Peng Gao and Kunchang Li and Jifeng Dai and Yu Qiao and Hongsheng Li},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2207.09519},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.09519}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A key–value cache module added in logit space, \\(\\alpha\\,\\varphi(fF^\\top)L+fW_c^\\top\\); \\(\\alpha=0\\) (or an empty cache) recovers zero-shot CLIP.","shape":"Function-level: a kernel expansion over cached features.","base_point":"The base point \\(q_0=F_{train}\\) (cached features) does not realise zero-shot CLIP: a defect by design, since the training-free cache already is the adaptation (compare LoRA-SB). Tip-Adapter-F trains the keys from there. With \\(\\alpha\\) fixed (a hyperparameter), no key value makes the cache term vanish (\\(\\varphi>0\\)); equal keys make it class-uniform, so the softmax output equals zero-shot CLIP while the logits differ by a shift. Exact pointedness needs \\(\\alpha=0\\).","gauge":"No continuous gauge: \\(\\varphi=\\exp(-\\beta(1-\\cdot))\\) is non-homogeneous and the keys are free rows. Only the discrete permutations of cache entries, together with the label rows, remain.","covariance":"A cosine-similarity kernel on L2-normalised features, with keys built from data: covariant (with the data transformed) under \\(CO(d)\\), not \\(GL\\).","merge":"The cache is stored and queried at inference; \\(\\exp\\) of inner products is non-affine.","rebasing":"n/a (M∞). Appending cache entries adds kernel terms in logit space."},"categorical_reading":"A cache extension in logit space whose keys are set from data: its base point is a pointing defect by design, and Tip-Adapter-F descends from that point rather than from \\(f\\). The exponential cosine kernel on normalised features makes its geometry orthogonal (\\(CO\\times CO\\)), not \\(GL\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"clip-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"CLIP-Adapter moves only the normalised image feature, so its logit vector always lies on \\(\\tau\\,W(S^{d-1})\\), the image of the unit sphere under the fixed class matrix. Tip's additive kernel term leaves that surface."},{"target":"coop","test":"O1","reason":"CoOp's logits stay linear in the frozen normalised image embedding; Tip adds a non-linear kernel term in that embedding."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adapter-plus","name":"Adapter+","full_name":"Adapter+ (post-adapter with channel-wise scaling)","paper_title":"Adapters Strike Back","authors":"Steitz and Roth","year":2024,"venue":"CVPR 2024","arxiv":"2406.06820","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06820","code_url":"https://github.com/visinf/adapter_plus","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = \\mathrm{FFN}(x) + x;\\quad y \\leftarrow y + s\\odot\\big(W_{up}\\,\\mathrm{GELU}(W_{down}\\,y + b_{down}) + b_{up}\\big),\\quad s\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ \\text{learned channel-wise scaling};\\ \\ \\text{ViT frozen}","trainable_params":"2rd + r + 2d\\ \\text{per ViT block}","locus":"Post-Adapter position: once per ViT block, after the FFN including its residual add","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Serial residual bottleneck at the end of each block whose correction is post-composed with a learned diagonal map \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\) (channel-wise scaling); systematic study of positions (pre, post, parallel, intermediate), initializations and normalization.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Nonlinear serial bottleneck.","identity_at_init":"Near-identity: Houlsby initialization (weights from a zero-mean Gaussian with std 0.01 truncated at 2 std, zero biases), which beat BERT and LoRA-style inits in the paper's ablation.","key_idea":"Plain adapters were under-reported for ViTs; placed after the block (Post-Adapter), Houlsby-initialized, without LayerNorm and with a learned channel-wise scale, they give the best parameter-accuracy trade-off.","results_note":"Reaches 77.6% average accuracy on VTAB (ViT-B/16) without per-task hyperparameter optimization and 90.7% on FGVC, the best parameter-accuracy trade-off among the compared methods.","relations":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses the post-FFN position found by Pfeiffer et al."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"Houlsby initialization."},{"target":"vpt","type":"competes-with","note":"Re-evaluated against VPT, SSF, LoRA, FacT, SPT."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06820","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.06820","https://github.com/visinf/adapter_plus","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"authors_full":["Jan-Martin O. Steitz","Stefan Roth"],"bibkey":"steitz2024adapters","bibtex":"@inproceedings{steitz2024adapters,\n  title = {{Adapters Strike Back}},\n  author = {Jan-Martin O. Steitz and Stefan Roth},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2406.06820},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06820}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A serial post-block bottleneck (GELU) with a channel-wise scale on its output; \\(W_{up}=0\\) is neutral.","shape":"Function-level (non-linear inserted box).","base_point":"Houlsby init (std \\(0.01\\), truncated) is near-neutral: the \\(\\approx\\) convention, classifying the exactly pointed variant.","gauge":"\\(s\\) is pure gauge on the function family: \\((s,W_{up},b_{up})\\mapsto(st,\\mathrm{diag}(t)^{-1}W_{up},\\mathrm{diag}(t)^{-1}b_{up})\\), a \\(d\\)-dimensional torus. GELU allows no hidden-unit slide.","covariance":"\\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)W_{up}\\) ranges over the same dense matrices as \\(W_{up}\\), so the family, and hence its covariance, is that of a plain dense bottleneck. Function-level analogue of Def II.14: \\(U,D\\) are dense, so the inserted family \\(\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\) is invariant under conjugation by any \\(g\\in GL_d\\) on the wire, \\(g(\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(Dg^{-1}\\cdot))=\\mathrm{id}+(gU)\\sigma((Dg^{-1})\\cdot)\\). \\(\\sigma\\) privileges only the internal bottleneck basis (gauge), not the wire.","merge":"Thm VI.3(c): for generic \\(U,D\\) the modified box function is non-affine in the box input, so no box-local merge exists.","rebasing":"n/a: M∞, so merge-and-restart into \\(\\Theta\\) is undefined. At function level (freeze the trained adapter into an enlarged base, insert a fresh one) the stages compose, \\(R_K=S^K\\circ F\\) with \\(S=\\{\\mathrm{id}+U\\sigma(D\\,\\cdot)\\}\\): the action-type closure (generated monoid)."},"categorical_reading":"A serial post-block bottleneck whose channel-wise scale \\(s\\) is pure gauge on the function family, since \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)W_{up}\\) ranges over the same matrices as \\(W_{up}\\). Like LoRA+'s ratio, \\(s\\) can only change the metric (training dynamics), not what is expressible.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Non-affine serial box (Thm VI.3(c)); no weight-space realisation."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"shadow-peft","name":"ShadowPEFT","full_name":"ShadowPEFT: shadow network for parameter-efficient fine-tuning","paper_title":"ShadowPEFT: Shadow Network for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Li et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2604.19254","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19254","code_url":"https://github.com/ShadowLLM/shadow-peft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"ShadowConfig(r, shadow_alpha, shadow_model)","formula_latex":"s^{(0)} = f_{\\text{shadow}}(x);\\ \\ \\delta^{(\\ell)} = h^{(\\ell-1)}_{out} - s^{(\\ell-1)},\\ \\ h^{(\\ell)} = h^{(\\ell-1)}_{out} + \\alpha\\,W^{(\\ell)}_{up}W^{(\\ell)}_{down}\\,\\delta^{(\\ell)},\\ \\ h^{(\\ell)}_{out} = f^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{base}}(h^{(\\ell)});\\ \\ s^{(\\ell)} = (1-g^{(\\ell)})\\odot s^{(\\ell-1)} + g^{(\\ell)}\\odot T^{(\\ell)}(h^{(\\ell)}_{out}),\\ g^{(\\ell)} = \\sigma\\big(G^{(\\ell)}(h^{(\\ell)}_{out})\\big);\\ \\ f_{\\text{base}}\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"|\\theta_{\\text{shadow}}| + \\sum_{\\ell}\\big(2rd + 4d\\,h_g\\big) + |\\text{shadow head}|\\ \\ (W_{down},W_{up}\\ \\text{bias-free};\\ T^{(\\ell)}, G^{(\\ell)}\\ \\text{are bias-free two-layer MLPs of width } h_g;\\ \\text{HF default } h_g = r = 8,\\ \\text{one-layer mirror shadow backbone})","locus":"input of every targeted decoder block (shadow injection) plus a small parallel shadow backbone whose state is carried through depth","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Two-way coupled side network: a persistent shadow state evolves through depth by GRU-like gated convex updates from the backbone outputs, and injects a low-rank (bottleneck) correction of the discrepancy \\(h - s\\) into each frozen block's input; adaptation is an input-dependent trajectory rather than a static weight delta.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The correction depends on an evolving, input-dependent shadow state; HF PEFT raises an error on merge for this reason.","identity_at_init":"Exact for the base pathway: \\(W_{up}\\) is zero-initialized, so the injected correction is 0 and the frozen model's function is unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Concentrate all trainable adaptation in a compact shadow model coupled layer-wise to the frozen LLM; because the shadow is trained as a full predictor it can be detached for shadow-only (e.g. edge) inference and initialized from a smaller pretrained model.","results_note":"On Qwen backbones at three scales, ShadowPEFT is competitive with or better than LoRA and DoRA at closely matched trainable-parameter budgets on generation and understanding benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Side network fed by backbone states, but with shadow-to-base injection (two-way coupling) and no aim of avoiding backbone backprop."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main baseline; LoRA/DoRA at matched budgets."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Inserted modules, but in a parallel stateful network rather than per-layer bottlenecks."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19254","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.19254","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/shadow","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/shadow","https://github.com/ShadowLLM/shadow-peft","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/shadow","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/shadow/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.21.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Xianming Li","Zongxi Li","Tsz-fung Andrew Lee","Jing Li","Haoran Xie","Qing Li"],"bibkey":"li2026shadowpeft","bibtex":"@article{li2026shadowpeft,\n  title = {{ShadowPEFT: Shadow Network for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Xianming Li and Zongxi Li and Tsz-fung Andrew Lee and Jing Li and Haoran Xie and Qing Li},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2604.19254},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.19254},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19254}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A shadow state is carried through depth and injects \\(\\alpha W_{up}W_{down}(h-s)\\) into each block input; \\(W_{up}=0\\) leaves the base pathway unchanged.","shape":"Function-level: an input-dependent, stateful side network.","base_point":"Exactly neutral for the base pathway (\\(W_{up}=0\\)); only \\(W_{up}\\) moves to first order (zero-gate lemma).","gauge":"The bias-free bottleneck \\(W_{up}W_{down}\\) carries LoRA's \\(GL_r\\), \\((W_{up}g^{-1},gW_{down})\\).","covariance":"The GRU-like update \\((1-g)\\odot s+g\\odot T(h)\\) is Hadamard on the residual wire, and \\(s\\) must live in the residual space to form \\(h-s\\). That fixes the standard basis; diagonals and permutations commute with \\(\\odot\\): Mon on both sides.","merge":"The correction depends on an evolving input-dependent state and edits the residual stream (Thm VI.3(a) excludes residual-stream edits). HF PEFT raises an error on merge.","rebasing":"n/a: a stateful coupled network with no merge."},"categorical_reading":"A two-way coupled side extension: a shadow state evolves through depth by Hadamard-gated convex updates and feeds a low-rank correction of \\(h-s\\) into each frozen block. \\(W_{up}=0\\) is neutral, the bias-free bottleneck carries LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge, and the input-dependent state makes it M∞.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"\\(f\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{ShadowPEFT}\\) (at \\(W_{up}=0\\)), but LST's prediction passes through a width-\\(d/r\\) side state and can never realise \\(f\\) (cut-rank)."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Input-dependent stateful corrections on the residual stream lie outside \\(\\Phi_f(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA})\\)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"loft-orthogonal","name":"LOFT","full_name":"Low-Rank Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Task-Aware Support Selection","paper_title":"LOFT: Low-Rank Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Task-Aware Support Selection","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2605.11872","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11872","code_url":"https://github.com/Kris-camp/loft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0\\,S(P_r,T_r),\\quad S(P_r,T_r)=I_{d_{in}}+P_r^{\\top}(T_r-I_r)P_r,\\quad P_r\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ P_rP_r^{\\top}=I_r\\ \\text{(support, frozen)},\\quad T_r=Q(E)\\in SO(r),\\ E\\in\\mathrm{Skew}(r)\\ \\text{(trained, }E_0=0;\\ \\text{paper: Cayley; released code: 5-term Cayley--Neumann)};\\quad \\text{supports: } P_r=V_r^{\\top}\\ \\text{(principal)},\\ \\text{top right singular subspace of } G=\\nabla_W L(W_0)\\ \\text{(GradSVD)},\\ \\text{top invariant subspace of } \\operatorname{skew}(W_0^{\\top}G)\\ \\text{(SkewGrad)}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{r(r-1)}{2}","locus":"any linear layer; acts on the input side (right multiplication of \\(W_0\\))","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"Right action of \\(SO(V)\\), the rotations of the chosen \\(r\\)-dimensional input subspace \\(V=\\operatorname{row}P_r\\) that fix \\(V^{\\perp}\\) pointwise (exactly orthogonal only with an exact \\(Q\\); the code's Cayley–Neumann default is approximate). The induced additive update \\(\\Delta W=W_0P_r^{\\top}(T_r-I_r)P_r\\) has rank at most \\(r\\). The paper's multi-factor primitive \\(W_0\\prod_{\\ell}S(P^{(\\ell)}_{r_\\ell},T^{(\\ell)}_{r_\\ell})\\), with general transforms and, for HRA, learnable width-one supports, recovers full and block OFT, GOFT, BOFT and HRA, and PSOFT up to its two scaling vectors.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0S(P_r,T_r)\\) is again a weight matrix, equal to \\(W_0+W_0P_r^{\\top}(T_r-I_r)P_r\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(E=0\\) gives \\(T_r=I_r\\) and \\(S=I\\), so the model is unchanged. The support \\(P_r\\) is computed once before training: from the SVD of \\(W_0\\) (principal), or from the gradient \\(G\\) of the downstream loss at \\(W_0\\) estimated on a small calibration set (GradSVD, SkewGrad), then frozen.","key_idea":"Separate where an orthogonal update acts (the support \\(P_r\\)) from how it acts (the in-subspace transform \\(T_r\\)), and choose the support from the task: the first-order training signal of a right-subspace rotation is \\(P_r\\operatorname{skew}(W_0^{\\top}G)P_r^{\\top}\\), whose norm is largest when \\(P_r\\) spans the top invariant subspace of \\(\\operatorname{skew}(W_0^{\\top}G)\\).","results_note":"With SkewGrad support at \\(r=46\\), the GLUE average on DeBERTaV3-base is 88.73 against 88.04 for PSOFT, and the VTAB-1K average on ViT-B/16 is 73.8 against 73.4, with 0.07M trainable parameters; principal-support LOFT matches PSOFT (88.06).","relations":[{"target":"psoft","type":"special-case-of","note":"Principal-support LOFT is PSOFT with both scaling vectors frozen at 1, when both use the same exactly orthogonal transform."},{"target":"hra","type":"competes-with","note":"HRA is an instance of the paper's multi-factor primitive with learnable width-one supports, not of fixed-support LOFT; LOFT's image is one leaf of HRA's image."},{"target":"oft","type":"competes-with","note":"Block OFT is an instance of the paper's multi-factor primitive with coordinate-block supports; single-factor LOFT maps into full-block exact-Cayley OFT."},{"target":"goft","type":"competes-with","note":"GOFT is a depth-L composition of width-2 coordinate-pair supports in the paper's multi-factor primitive."},{"target":"boft","type":"competes-with","note":"BOFT is a multi-stage composition of butterfly-block supports in the paper's multi-factor primitive."},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"competes-with","note":"Both choose a starting frame from the gradient at W_0: LoRA-GA for an additive low-rank update, LOFT (GradSVD, SkewGrad) for an orthogonal support."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11872","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2605.11872","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.11872v1 (pages 1-11)","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum"],"authors_full":["Lanxin Zhao","Bamdev Mishra","Pratik Jawanpuria","Lequan Lin","Dai Shi","Junbin Gao","Andi Han"],"bibkey":"zhao2026loft","bibtex":"@article{zhao2026loft,\n  title = {{LOFT: Low-Rank Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Task-Aware Support Selection}},\n  author = {Lanxin Zhao and Bamdev Mishra and Pratik Jawanpuria and Lequan Lin and Dai Shi and Junbin Gao and Andi Han},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2605.11872},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.11872},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11872}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Read with an exactly orthogonal \\(Q\\) and a frozen support: \\(\\rho(E)=W_0S(P_r,Q(E))\\) lies in the leaf \\(W_0\\cdot SO(V)\\). If \\(\\operatorname{rank}W_0P_r^{\\top}=r\\), \\(W_0\\cdot O(V)=(W_0+W_0P_r^{\\top}\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}P_r)\\cap W_0\\cdot O(n)\\). The released Cayley–Neumann default is not exactly orthogonal and leaves the leaf.","base_point":"\\(S_1=W_0P_r^{\\top}\\mathfrak{so}(r)P_r\\), of dimension \\(r(r-1)/2=d\\) when \\(\\operatorname{rank}W_0P_r^{\\top}=r\\): regular, unlike HRA's paired initialisation (Thm II.7(b)).","gauge":"\\(E\\mapsto W_0P_r^{\\top}Q(E)P_r\\) is injective near \\(0\\) when \\(W_0P_r^{\\top}\\) has full column rank.","covariance":"OxO for the principal and GradSVD supports, whose frames come from SVDs of \\(W_0\\) or \\(G\\). The SkewGrad support is covariant under the larger group \\(GL(m)\\times CO(n)\\), since \\(W_0^{\\top}G\\) is unchanged by an output change of basis. In this catalogue block OFT is GLxCO and GOFT is \\(\\Pi\\).","rebasing":"With the support frozen across cycles the image is a subgroup orbit and merge-and-restart gains nothing. With finitely many supports revisited, the reach is the orbit of the connected group whose Lie algebra the \\(\\mathfrak{so}(V_k)\\) generate (Thm V.3(b)); numerically SO(n) for generic supports, r >= 2, after ceil(n/r) of them. The principal support does not move when re-selected."},"categorical_reading":"LOFT fixes one leaf of the HRA meet: for injective \\(W_0\\), \\((W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap W_0\\cdot SO(n)\\) is the union over \\(V\\in\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)\\) of the leaves \\(W_0\\cdot SO(V)\\). HRA moves its leaf while it trains; LOFT freezes it and chooses it, from the principal singular subspace (PSOFT without its tori; for injective \\(W_0\\) and a Cayley map, LoRA-XS meet exact full-block Cayley OFT) or from the gradient at \\(\\theta_0\\). Read with an exactly orthogonal transform, it is an orbit method with a regular base point and no gauge.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto Q=-P_r^{\\top}EP_r\\in\\mathfrak{so}(n)","note":"Into exact full-block Cayley OFT (block size n), with the same Cayley map on both sides. With the catalogue's convention W = W_0 C(Q)^T, h(E) = -P_r^T E P_r; with HF's exact code R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, W = W_0 R^T, it is h(E) = +P_r^T E P_r. Uses C(P_r^T E P_r) = I + P_r^T (C(E) - I) P_r."},{"target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto R=\\Sigma_r\\big(C(E)-I\\big)\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"Principal support, needs sigma_r(W_0) > 0; LoRA-XS pointed at R = 0. Holds for any transform Q, exact or not."},{"target":"psoft","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto\\big(R=C(E)^{\\top},\\ \\alpha=\\beta=\\mathbf 1\\big)","note":"Principal support, same exactly orthogonal transform on both sides; freeze PSOFT's two scaling vectors at 1 (A2 up to E -> -E). With alpha = beta = 1, PSOFT's W_res + B R^T A equals W_0 (I + V_r (R^T - I) V_r^T)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto\\big(B=W_0P_r^{\\top}(C(E)-I_r),\\ A=P_r\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_r pointed at (B, A) = (0, P_r), a T_A-type pointing with a fixed row-orthonormal A_0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hra","test":"O3","reason":"For r >= 4 there is no smooth pointed arrow LOFT_r -> HRA_r at HF's paired initialisation, although Im LOFT(P_r) lies in Im HRA_r (r even): S_1(LOFT) = W_0 so(V) is not contained in S_1(HRA) = W_0 {Omega : P_{U^perp} Omega P_{U^perp} = 0} (Thm II.7(b)), because dim(V cap U^perp) >= r/2 >= 2."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"preft","name":"PreFT","full_name":"Prefill-only Finetuning (prefill-only LoRA, \\( \\text{LoRA}_P \\), and prefill-only DiReFT, \\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\))","paper_title":"PreFT: Prefill-only finetuning for efficient inference","authors":"Lanpouthakoun et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2605.14217","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14217","code_url":"https://github.com/stanfordnlp/preft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_i=W_0x_i+m_i\\,\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BAx_i\\ \\ (\\text{LoRA}_P),\\qquad h^{(l)}_i\\leftarrow h^{(l)}_i+m_i\\,s_r\\,W_2^{\\top}\\big(W_1h^{(l)}_i+b\\big)\\ \\ (\\text{DiReFT}_P),\\qquad m_i=\\mathbf{1}[\\,i\\le\\ell\\,];\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ W_1,W_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r},\\ s_r=1/\\sqrt{r},\\ \\alpha=32;\\ \\ \\text{decode positions } i>\\ell \\text{ run the frozen model and see the adapter only through the KV cache of positions } \\le\\ell;\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ (A,B)\\ \\text{or}\\ (W_1,W_2,b)\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix for LoRA}_P\\ (\\text{same as LoRA});\\ \\ L\\,(2rd+r)\\ \\text{per model for DiReFT}_P\\ (\\text{one residual-stream intervention per layer})","locus":"Token-time locus: only the prompt positions \\( i\\le\\ell \\) processed at prefill, never the generated tokens. \\( \\text{LoRA}_P \\) targets \\( W_q,W_k,W_v,W_o,W_{gate},W_{up},W_{down} \\) at every layer; \\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\) edits the residual stream after every block (post-MLP). Generated tokens reach the adapter only through the keys and values cached at prompt positions.","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"A position mask placed on an existing adapter. Writing an adapter as \\( \\langle\\Phi_\\phi,\\mathcal F,\\mathcal P\\rangle \\) (intervention, target modules, target positions), PreFT keeps \\( \\Phi_\\phi \\) and \\( \\mathcal F \\) and shrinks \\( \\mathcal P=\\{1,\\dots,\\ell+T\\} \\) to \\( \\mathcal P=\\{1,\\dots,\\ell\\} \\). For \\( \\text{LoRA}_P \\) the per-token update is the rank-\\( r \\) matrix \\( m_i\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\), so the effective weight depends on the token: \\( W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\) on prompt tokens and \\( W_0 \\) on generated tokens. For \\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\) the per-token update is a rank-\\( r \\) affine edit \\( s_rW_2^{\\top}(W_1h+b) \\) of the residual stream at prompt positions. Attention is causal, so the whole effect on token \\( i>\\ell \\) is carried by the adapted \\( (K,V) \\) entries of positions \\( \\le\\ell \\) at every layer. No KV entries are added, unlike prefix or KV-cache tuning.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The update is gated by position, so no single matrix \\( W' \\) reproduces it: prompt tokens need \\( W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\) and generated tokens need \\( W_0 \\). A single-adapter deployment could prefill with a merged copy and decode with \\( W_0 \\), but that doubles weight memory and defeats multi-adapter batching. The design goal is that decode runs the bare base model, so no adapter weights are touched at decode.","identity_at_init":"Yes. \\( \\text{LoRA}_P \\) keeps LoRA's init (Kaiming-uniform \\( A \\), \\( B=0 \\), scaling \\( \\alpha/r \\)). \\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\) uses the paper's zero-delta init \\( W_1=0 \\), \\( b=0 \\), \\( W_2 \\) with orthonormal rows (App. C.1 and the released preft/adapters/direft.py), so \\( \\Phi(h)=h \\) at step 0. App. A.2 states the roles the other way round (orthonormal down-projection, zero up-projection and bias); either assignment gives a zero delta at init.","key_idea":"Multi-adapter serving is throughput-bound at decode, where each request's adapter matmul is memory-bound, so PreFT applies the adapter only to the \\( \\ell \\) prompt tokens processed at prefill and runs every decode step on the frozen base model. The adapter steers generation only through the KV cache it shaped during prefill, and the method optimises accuracy per unit of serving throughput rather than per parameter.","results_note":"Serving 512 adapters on Llama 3.1 70B (4 H100s, Punica workload, vLLM fork), prefill-only DiReFT reaches 1.9x and prefill-only LoRA 1.87x the throughput of all-position multi-LoRA, while on RLVR (GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval) prefill-only adapters trail all-position ones by a mean of about 2 points.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"\\( \\text{LoRA}_P \\) is LoRA with its parametrisation and init unchanged, restricted to prompt positions"},{"target":"loreft","type":"builds-on","note":"\\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\) is ReFT's DiReFT variant applied to all prompt positions (ReFT uses a few prefix/suffix positions), with a new zero-delta init and a \\( 1/\\sqrt r \\) prefactor for learning-rate transfer across ranks"},{"target":"alora","type":"dual-to","note":"mirror-image position masks on the same LoRA update: aLoRA is off for the prompt and on from the invocation sequence onward (to reuse the base model's prompt KV cache), PreFT is on for the prompt and off for generation (to drop adapter cost at decode); PreFT cites aLoRA as prior position-masked LoRA"},{"target":"s-lora","type":"combines","note":"S-LoRA amortises per-request adapter cost with batched gather kernels and CPU-GPU paging; PreFT removes the adapter cost at decode instead, reuses S-LoRA-style paging (as implemented in vLLM) for multi-ReFT serving, and states that it composes with such paging and kernels at prefill"},{"target":"punica","type":"competes-with","note":"throughput is benchmarked on the Punica workload against vLLM's Punica-style multi-LoRA kernels"},{"target":"cartridges","type":"competes-with","note":"both adapt a model through the KV cache seen at decode; Cartridges adds trained KV entries (extra attention work at every decode step), PreFT adds none and only reshapes the prompt's own cache; the paper calls KV-cache methods complementary and does not benchmark them"},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"soft prompts also act at decode only through extra KV entries; the paper expects their throughput to fall between PEFTs and PreFTs but does not benchmark them"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14217","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.14217","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/151570","https://github.com/stanfordnlp/preft","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stanfordnlp/preft/main/preft/adapters/lora.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Andrew Lanpouthakoun","Aryaman Arora","Zhengxuan Wu","Dhruv Pai","Ben Keigwin","Dan Jurafsky","Christopher Potts"],"bibkey":"lanpouthakoun2026preft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lanpouthakoun2026preft,\n  title = {{PreFT: Prefill-only finetuning for efficient inference}},\n  author = {Andrew Lanpouthakoun and Aryaman Arora and Zhengxuan Wu and Dhruv Pai and Ben Keigwin and Dan Jurafsky and Christopher Potts},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2605.14217},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14217}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A \\(T\\)-point over token positions: \\(a(i)=q\\) on prompt positions \\(i\\le\\ell\\) and \\(a(i)=q_0\\) on generated positions, so \\(\\rho\\circ a\\) takes only the values \\(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\) and \\(\\theta_0\\). It is aLoRA's position gate mirrored: on for the prompt, off for decoding.","shape":"No single weight-space image: prompt and decode positions see different weights, and generated tokens feel the adapter only through the cached \\((K,V)\\) of positions \\(\\le\\ell\\). Compared through the realisation \\(\\Phi\\).","base_point":"\\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_P\\) keeps \\(B_0=0\\): the frozen function at every position, with first-order image \\(\\{XA_0\\}\\) on prompt positions (\\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex, as aLoRA). \\(\\mathrm{DiReFT}_P\\) at its released init (\\(W_1=0\\), \\(b=0\\), orthonormal \\(W_2\\)) is a neutral residual-stream box, also first-order deficient (only \\(W_1,b\\) move).","gauge":"\\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_P\\): \\(GL_r\\) on the single adapter. \\(\\mathrm{DiReFT}_P\\): \\((W_2,W_1,b)\\mapsto(gW_2,\\,g^{-\\top}W_1,\\,g^{-\\top}b)\\), again \\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"On each prompt position the update is LoRA's basis-free cone (\\(\\mathrm{DiReFT}_P\\): a dense rank-\\(r\\) affine edit of the residual wire): function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Thm VI.3(a): a position-gated edit has no position-independent weight. Prefilling with a merged copy and decoding with \\(W_0\\) uses two weights; it is not a merge.","rebasing":"Undefined: nothing merges into the base (M\\(\\infty\\))."},"categorical_reading":"PreFT is LoRA (or DiReFT) pulled back along the prompt-position gate, the mirror image of aLoRA's gate: a family \\(T\\to Q\\) that sits at \\(q\\) on positions \\(\\le\\ell\\) and at \\(q_0\\) afterwards. Under causal attention the generated tokens see the adapter only through the prompt's \\((K,V)\\) cache, so the realised map is not \\(\\Phi_f(\\theta)\\) for any single \\(\\theta\\). Being pointed after \\(\\ell\\) is what lets decoding run on the frozen weights, and also what blocks merging.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"loreft","rule":"A3","h":"(W_1,W_2,b)\\mapsto\\big(R=(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{-1/2}W_2,\\ W=R+s_r(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{1/2}W_1,\\ b'=s_r(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{1/2}b\\big)","note":"DiReFT_P instance, into LoReFT intervening at every prompt position (prefix length >= ell) after every block: s_r W_2^T(W_1 h + b) = R^T((W - R)h + b'). Smooth where W_2 has full row rank. Pointed: the released init (W_1 = 0, b = 0, W_2 with orthonormal rows) goes to LoReFT's neutral point W = R, b = 0. Checked numerically (error 2e-16). The target is LoReFT pointed at its neutral point (W = R, b = 0), not at pyreft's default init, which is a defect."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"At function level the realised map (adapted prompt KV cache, frozen decode weights) is not Phi_f of any single weight for generic prompts, so no weight-space LoRA lifts it (M-infinity). On each prompt position the update is still a LoRA update."},{"target":"alora","test":"O1","reason":"Complementary gates on the same LoRA update. For a fixed invocation position aLoRA leaves the prompt before t_inv at W_0 and adapts later tokens; PreFT adapts the prompt and leaves every generated token at W_0. For generic prompts neither family of realised maps contains the other."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"orthomerge","name":"OrthoMerge","full_name":"Orthogonal Model Merging","paper_title":"Orthogonal Model Merging","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2602.05943","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05943","code_url":"https://github.com/Sphere-AI-Lab/OrthoMerge","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{OFT experts: } W_i=W_0R_i^{\\top},\\ R_i=(I+Q_i)(I-Q_i)^{-1},\\ Q_i^{\\top}=-Q_i;\\ \\ \\text{other experts: } R_i^{\\top}=U_iV_i^{\\top},\\ U_i\\Sigma_iV_i^{\\top}=\\mathrm{SVD}\\big(W_0^{\\top}W_i^{\\mathrm{tgt}}\\big)\\ \\big(\\text{Procrustes } \\min_{R^{\\top}R=I}\\|W_i^{\\mathrm{tgt}}-W_0R^{\\top}\\|_F\\big),\\ Q_i=(R_i-I)(R_i+I)^{-1};\\ \\ Q_m=\\frac{\\sum_i\\|Q_i\\|_F}{\\|\\sum_iQ_i\\|_F}\\cdot\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}Q_i,\\ \\ R_m=(I+Q_m)(I-Q_m)^{-1};\\ \\ W_m=W_0R_m^{\\top}+\\mathrm{EuclidMerge}\\big(\\{\\rho_i\\}\\big),\\ \\rho_i=W_i-W_0R_i^{\\top}\\ (\\rho_i=0\\ \\text{for OFT experts});\\ \\ W_i^{\\mathrm{tgt}}\\in\\{W_i,\\ W_0+\\tau_i^{\\mathrm{conf}}\\};\\ \\text{training-free, all weights frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ (\\text{training-free});\\ \\text{merge state per matrix: } N \\text{ generators } Q_i\\in\\mathfrak{so}(d_{in})\\ (\\text{block-diagonal for OFT experts}) \\text{ and, for non-OFT experts, residuals } \\rho_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}","locus":"Weights of every linear layer of the merged experts: the input-side rotation of OFT-adapted layers, or, through orthogonal-residual decoupling, every layer of LoRA or fully fine-tuned experts (Llama-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-3B, Llama-3.2-3B MergeBench experts, Qwen2.5-VL-7B). Post hoc, no training.","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Merging in the Cayley chart of the orthogonal group. Each expert contributes a rotation \\( R_i\\in O(d_{in}) \\), read off an OFT checkpoint or obtained by orthogonal Procrustes from \\( W_0 \\) to a target weight. The inverse Cayley map sends it to a skew-symmetric generator \\( Q_i\\in\\mathfrak{so}(d_{in}) \\); the generators are averaged linearly and rescaled by \\( c=\\sum_i\\|Q_i\\|_F/\\|\\sum_iQ_i\\|_F\\ge1 \\) to undo norm loss from cancellation, then mapped back by the Cayley map. The merged multiplicative factor is exactly orthogonal, so \\( W_0R_m^{\\top} \\) keeps the singular values of \\( W_0 \\) (hence its spectral norm and hyperspherical energy) for any number of experts. For non-OFT experts each weight is split as \\( W_i=W_0R_i^{\\top}+\\rho_i \\) (orthogonal plus residual), and the residuals are merged by a Euclidean rule (TA, TIES or TSV-M). The Procrustes target is \\( W_i \\) (OrthoMerge-G) or \\( W_0+\\tau_i^{\\mathrm{conf}} \\), where \\( \\tau_i^{\\mathrm{conf}} \\) keeps only the output-neuron rows of \\( \\tau_i=W_i-W_0 \\) whose cosine with the mean task vector is negative (OrthoMerge-C). The Cayley chart covers only orthogonal matrices without eigenvalue \\( -1 \\), so a reflection returned by Procrustes would fall outside it; the paper does not discuss this case.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is an ordinary weight matrix \\( W_m=W_0R_m^{\\top}+\\rho_m \\) in the base architecture (the merged OFT rotation can be multiplied into \\( W_0 \\)), so the merged model has no inference overhead.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free merge). Trivially consistent: if every expert equals \\( W_0 \\) then \\( Q_i=0 \\), \\( R_m=I \\), \\( \\rho_i=0 \\) and \\( W_m=W_0 \\) (taking \\( c=1 \\) when \\( \\sum_iQ_i=0 \\)).","key_idea":"Average OFT rotations in their Lie algebra rather than in weight space: map each \\( R_i \\) to \\( Q_i\\in\\mathfrak{so}(d_{in}) \\) with the inverse Cayley transform, average with a norm-restoring factor, and map back, so the merge stays on \\( O(d_{in}) \\) and preserves the spectrum of \\( W_0 \\). An orthogonal-plus-residual split by Procrustes extends the rule to LoRA and fully fine-tuned experts.","results_note":"Merging five OFT-finetuned Llama-3.1-8B experts (math, code, ScienceQA, CommonsenseQA, Social-IQA), OrthoMerge reaches 46.25% average task accuracy, above the experts' own 44.71% and every Euclidean baseline (TA, TIES, DARE, TSV-M), with the best out-of-domain average (41.80% on M-ARC and AGIEval).","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"merges OFT's Cayley-parameterised rotations through their skew-symmetric generators; the merged factor is again an OFT-type rotation of \\( W_0 \\)"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"combines","note":"task arithmetic is the default Euclidean rule for the residuals \\( \\rho_i \\) after orthogonal-residual decoupling"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"TIES can be used as the residual-merging rule; TIES+OrthoMerge-C is best on the VLM merge"},{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"combines","note":"TSV-M can be used as the residual-merging rule; TSV-M+OrthoMerge-C gives the best task average (42.07%) on the Llama-3.2-3B MergeBench merge and TSV-M+OrthoMerge-G the best IFEval retention among TSV variants on the VLM merge; also a Euclidean baseline"},{"target":"dare","type":"competes-with","note":"Euclidean merging baseline applied in weight space to both LoRA and OFT experts"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05943","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.05943","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/61531","https://spherelab.ai/OrthoMerge/","https://github.com/Sphere-AI-Lab/OrthoMerge"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Sihan Yang","Kexuan Shi","Weiyang Liu"],"bibkey":"yang2026orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2026orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Model Merging}},\n  author = {Sihan Yang and Kexuan Shi and Weiyang Liu},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.05943},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05943}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and training-free: OFT rotations \\(R_i\\) (or Procrustes rotations of other experts) go to \\(Q_i\\in\\mathfrak{so}(d_{in})\\) by the inverse Cayley map, are averaged with the norm-restoring factor \\(c=\\sum_i\\|Q_i\\|_F/\\|\\sum_iQ_i\\|_F\\), and come back by the Cayley map.","shape":"No tunable coefficient in the OFT-expert rule (uniform \\(1/N\\), \\(c\\) fixed by norms), so the output is a single point (post-hoc convention). It is the point \\(Q_m\\) of the orbit chart \\(Q\\mapsto W_0C(Q)^{\\top}\\). For non-OFT experts the residual rule (TA's \\(\\lambda\\)) adds a coefficient line.","base_point":"A single point that does not arise from a coefficient value at \\(\\theta_0\\); generically \\(\\neq\\theta_0\\), as for soups and RegMean.","gauge":"No trained coefficients. Read as the family \\(\\lambda\\mapsto W_0C(\\sum_i\\lambda_iQ_i)^{\\top}\\), it would be injective near 0 for independent \\(Q_i\\).","covariance":"OFT experts: \\(W_0\\mapsto gW_0h^{-1}\\) conjugates every \\(R_i\\) by \\(h\\in CO(d_{in})\\) (block normaliser for block OFT), and the Cayley map, the average and the Frobenius norms commute with that conjugation: \\(GL\\times CO\\), as OFT. The Procrustes instance needs \\(g\\) orthogonal too (\\(CO\\times CO\\)), and a TIES residual rule cuts it to monomials.","merge":"\\(W_0R_m^{\\top}+\\rho_m\\) is an ordinary weight.","rebasing":"A one-shot post-hoc operation."},"categorical_reading":"OrthoMerge is task arithmetic in the Cayley chart of the orbit \\(W_0\\cdot SO(d_{in})\\) rather than in \\(T_{\\theta_0}\\Theta\\). The merged point \\(W_0C(Q_m)^{\\top}\\) stays in the \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) level set that every OFT expert preserves, which no linear merge of OFT experts does. For non-OFT experts it first splits each weight into an orbit point and a residual, merges the two parts by the orbit rule and by a Euclidean rule, and adds them.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"\\ast\\mapsto Q_m=\\tfrac{c}{N}\\textstyle\\sum_iQ_i","note":"OFT experts sharing one block partition, exact Cayley on both sides: the merged point is a point of OFT's image (unpointed slice, as soups into task arithmetic). With OFT written as W_0 C(Q)^T, C(Q) = (I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1} (OrthoMerge's own convention), h(*) = Q_m; under HF's R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, h(*) = -Q_m. Experts trained with HF's default Cayley-Neumann map are read through exact Cayley only approximately."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"For N >= 2 OFT experts with generic generators, W_0(C(Q_m)^T - I) is not in span{W_0(C(Q_i)^T - I)}, because the Cayley map is not linear. Conversely task arithmetic's family leaves the K_out level set that OrthoMerge's output keeps."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"prism-dp-lora","name":"PRISM","full_name":"Projected Riemannian Invariant Subspace Mechanism (gauge-invariant tangent-space DP-LoRA)","paper_title":"PRISM: Gauge-Invariant Tangent-Space Differentially Private LoRA","authors":"Wang and Zhang","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight, with oral presentation)","arxiv":"2606.00944","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.00944","code_url":"https://github.com/osu-srml/PRISM-DP-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+BA,\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\mathcal{P}_{B,A}(G)=G-(I-\\Pi_B)\\,G\\,(I-\\Pi_A),\\ \\ \\Pi_B=B(B^{\\top}B)^{\\dagger}B^{\\top},\\ \\Pi_A=A^{\\top}(AA^{\\top})^{\\dagger}A;\\ \\ \\widehat{\\Delta Z}=\\frac{1}{b}\\sum_{i=1}^{b}\\min\\!\\Big(1,\\frac{C}{s_i}\\Big)\\mathcal{P}_{B,A}(G_i)+\\frac{\\sigma C}{b}\\,\\mathcal{P}_{B,A}(\\Xi),\\ \\ G_i=\\nabla_{\\Delta W}\\ell_i,\\ s_i=\\Big(\\sum_{\\text{modules}}\\|\\mathcal{P}_{B,A}(G_i)\\|_F^2\\Big)^{1/2},\\ \\Xi\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d_{out}\\times d_{in}});\\ \\ BA\\leftarrow\\mathrm{Retr}_r\\big(BA-\\eta\\,\\mathcal{A}(\\widehat{\\Delta Z})\\big),\\ \\mathrm{Retr}_r=\\text{truncated rank-}r\\text{ SVD},\\ \\mathcal{A}=\\text{Adam-style first moments of the noisy factor lifts, preconditioned by }(V+\\lambda I_r)^{-1/2}\\text{ from }r\\times r\\text{ second moments, with DP-aware floors }\\lambda;\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ (A,B)\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (as LoRA)};\\ \\text{optimizer state: first moments of size } r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ and two } r\\times r \\text{ second-moment matrices per module}","locus":"Any LoRA-adapted linear layer (experiments: \\( W_q,W_k,W_v,W_{up},W_{down} \\) of Gemma-3-4B-pt, Gemma-2-9B, Gemma-3-12B-pt). The method changes how per-example gradients are clipped and noised and how the factors are updated, not the adapter shape.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Plain LoRA on the fixed-rank manifold \\( \\mathcal M_r \\), with DP-SGD moved from the factors to the base of the gauge bundle \\( (B,A)\\mapsto BA \\), whose fibres are the orbits \\( (B,A)\\sim(BR,R^{-1}A) \\), \\( R\\in GL(r) \\). Per-example gradients are projected onto the tangent space \\( T_{BA}\\mathcal M_r=\\{\\delta B\\,A+B\\,\\delta A\\} \\) (dimension \\( r(d_{in}+d_{out}-r) \\)) by \\( \\mathcal P_{B,A} \\), which depends only on \\( \\mathrm{col}(B) \\) and \\( \\mathrm{row}(A) \\) and is therefore gauge invariant. The projection is lifted to the factors canonically as \\( \\Delta B=g_B(AA^{\\top})^{\\dagger}-\\tfrac12\\Pi_Bg_B(AA^{\\top})^{\\dagger} \\), \\( \\Delta A=(B^{\\top}B)^{\\dagger}g_A-\\tfrac12(B^{\\top}B)^{\\dagger}g_A\\Pi_A \\) with \\( g_B=GA^{\\top} \\), \\( g_A=B^{\\top}G \\), so that \\( \\Delta B\\,A+B\\,\\Delta A=\\mathcal P_{B,A}(G) \\). Clipping uses the intrinsic Frobenius norm summed over all modules. The noise is the tangent projection of an isotropic Gaussian, sampled through factor lifts \\( \\Xi_B=(I-\\Pi_B)\\Omega_B(AA^{\\top})^{-1/2} \\), \\( \\Xi_A=(B^{\\top}B)^{-1/2}\\Omega_A^{\\top} \\); it is isotropic on \\( T_{BA}\\mathcal M_r \\) with \\( \\mathbb E\\|\\mathcal P(\\Xi)\\|_F^2=r(d_{in}+d_{out}-r) \\), independent of \\( \\|A\\|_F,\\|B\\|_F \\). The noisy step is post-processed by right/left rank-space preconditioners \\( (V+\\lambda I_r)^{-1/2} \\) built from \\( r\\times r \\) second moments, with floors \\( \\lambda\\asymp(\\sigma C/b)^2\\,\\mathrm{tr}(\\cdot^{-1})/r \\), and then retracted to rank \\( r \\) by truncated SVD. Because the step is additive in the tangent space, the bilinear noise term \\( \\eta^2\\xi_B\\xi_A \\) of factor-space DP never appears, and the retraction error is \\( O(\\eta^2) \\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The released adapter is an ordinary low-rank product and merges like LoRA; privacy follows from post-processing. Because the official code trains on a spectrally re-based base \\( W_{res}=W_0-U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^{\\top} \\), it saves the adapter re-expressed against the original \\( W_0 \\) as the rank-\\( 2r \\) pair \\( [B,\\,B_0]\\,[A;\\,-A_0] \\) (PiSSA-style conversion in spectral_rebase_adapter_inplace), which merges into \\( W_0 \\) the same way.","identity_at_init":"The paper does not state an init. The tangent projectors and noise sampler need full-column-rank \\( B \\) and full-row-rank \\( A \\), which rules out LoRA's \\( B=0 \\). The official code uses a PiSSA-style spectral residual init, \\( W_0\\leftarrow W_0-U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^{\\top} \\), \\( B=U_r(\\Sigma_r/s)^{1/2} \\), \\( A=(\\Sigma_r/s)^{1/2}V_r^{\\top} \\) with LoRA scale \\( s \\), so \\( W_{res}+sBA \\) equals the original \\( W_0 \\) at step 0 (function-preserving with non-degenerate factors).","key_idea":"LoRA factors are defined only up to \\( (B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A) \\), so DP-SGD on the factors clips and noises a gauge-dependent quantity and injects a bilinear \\( \\eta^2\\xi_B\\xi_A \\) term whose energy can be made arbitrarily large without changing \\( BA \\). PRISM clips and noises the projection of per-example gradients onto the tangent space of the rank-\\( r \\) manifold at \\( BA \\), which gives gauge-invariant updates with closed-form intrinsic noise \\( \\tfrac{\\sigma C}{b}\\sqrt{r(d_{in}+d_{out}-r)} \\).","results_note":"On GLUE8 and Math-10K with Gemma-3-4B-pt, PRISM has the best average utility under DP (0.690 at epsilon=6 and 0.680 at epsilon=3, delta=1e-5) against FFA-LoRA, LoRA-RITE, AdamW, LoRA+ and LAMB, while LoRA-RITE is best without DP.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same adapter; only the private optimisation of the factors changes"},{"target":"ffa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"FFA-LoRA freezes \\( A \\) so DP noise enters as \\( \\xi_BA_0 \\) with no bilinear term, but its size still depends on the frozen factor's norm; PRISM removes that dependence; DP baseline"},{"target":"lora-rite","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-RITE makes a deterministic optimizer transformation-invariant; PRISM argues this does not make the randomized clip-and-noise step invariant and extends invariance to it, while also using \\( r\\times r \\) rank-space second moments; baseline (best without DP)"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"structural kinship, not cited: PRISM's canonical lift starts from the scaled gradients \\( g_B(AA^{\\top})^{\\dagger} \\) and \\( (B^{\\top}B)^{\\dagger}g_A \\) of Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA and subtracts half the overlap term; both rest on the quotient geometry of fixed-rank matrices (PRISM cites Mishra et al. 2014, not Zhang and Pilanci)"},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"optimizer baseline under DP"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.00944","https://arxiv.org/html/2606.00944","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/oral/71157","https://github.com/osu-srml/PRISM-DP-LoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/osu-srml/PRISM-DP-LoRA/main/src/prism_cli/optim/prism.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/osu-srml/PRISM-DP-LoRA/main/src/prism_cli/trainers.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Shihao Wang","Xueru Zhang"],"bibkey":"wang2026prism","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2026prism,\n  title = {{PRISM: Gauge-Invariant Tangent-Space Differentially Private LoRA}},\n  author = {Shihao Wang and Xueru Zhang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2606.00944},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.00944}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An optimizer-only change on a LoRA-type object. Per-example gradients are projected onto \\(T_{BA}\\mathcal M_r\\), clipped and noised there, lifted canonically to the factors and retracted. The parametrisation is the released spectral-residual one.","shape":"Released init (spectral_init_peft_model): \\(W_{\\rm res}=W_0-[W_0]_r\\) with \\(sB_0A_0=[W_0]_r\\), so the image is PiSSA's translated cone \\(W_{\\rm res}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\). The tangent projectors need full-rank factors, which rules out LoRA's \\(B=0\\) apex: a Type II start is forced.","base_point":"S stratum: \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point, \\(\\dim S_1=r(m+n-r)\\).","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). Clip, noise and step depend only on \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\), \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\) and \\(\\mathcal P_{B,A}(G)\\), so the randomised update, noise included, descends to the quotient \\(\\mathcal M_r=Q^{\\rm full}/GL_r\\). LoRA-RITE's descent covers only the deterministic step.","covariance":"The init frame is a truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\) (\\(CO\\times CO\\), Prop II.16). The Frobenius tangent projection and the isotropic Gaussian noise are \\(O\\times O\\)-equivariant (in law).","merge":"The code saves the adapter on the original \\(W_0\\) as the rank-\\(2r\\) pair \\([B,B_0][A;-A_0]\\), an ordinary LoRA that fuses.","rebasing":"As PiSSA: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"PRISM keeps PiSSA's object and moves DP-SGD from \\(Q\\) to the base \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) of the gauge bundle \\((B,A)\\mapsto BA\\). Clipping and noise act on the tangent projection \\(\\mathcal P_{B,A}(G)\\), so the whole randomised step, noise included, is a function of \\(BA\\), and the bilinear \\(\\eta^2\\xi_B\\xi_A\\) term of factor-space DP disappears. It is the stochastic counterpart of the descended cometrics of Exposé III (LoRA-Pro's projected gradient, Riemannian preconditioning).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\sqrt s\\,B,\\ \\sqrt s\\,A)","note":"Isomorphism onto PiSSA_r (scale 1) on the same residual W_0 - [W_0]_r. It sends PRISM's q_0 = (U_r(Sigma_r/s)^{1/2}, (Sigma_r/s)^{1/2}V_r^T) to PiSSA's (U_r Sigma_r^{1/2}, Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^T). Only the optimiser differs. Checked numerically."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ B_0],\\ [A;\\ -A_0]\\big)","note":"The released spectral_rebase_adapter_inplace: PRISM_r into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0 (same scale s), pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,B_0],[A_0;-A_0]) with product 0 (Thm II.1, as for PiSSA). Checked numerically.","target_instance":"LoRA_{2r} at the degenerate pointing"}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"At equal rank, a Type II smooth point against LoRA's Type I apex: W_0 - [W_0]_r + M_{<=r} and W_0 + M_{<=r} are incomparable (Thm II.2(d)). So there is no rank-r arrow to plain LoRA or to LoRA-RITE's object.","target_instance":"LoRA_r at the T_A apex"}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"keeplora","name":"KeepLoRA","full_name":"KeepLoRA: residual-subspace gradient adaptation for continual learning","paper_title":"KeepLoRA: Continual Learning with Residual Gradient Adaptation","authors":"Luo et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2601.19659","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19659","code_url":"https://github.com/MaolinLuo/KeepLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=U_0\\Sigma_0V_0^{\\top},\\ P_W=V_{0,[:,1:p]}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times p}\\ \\big(\\text{smallest }p\\text{ with }\\|\\Sigma_{0,[1:p]}\\|_F^2\\ge\\epsilon_w\\|W_0\\|_F^2\\big);\\ \\ \\hat G_t=G_t\\big(I-P_WP_W^{\\top}-M_{t-1}M_{t-1}^{\\top}\\big),\\ G_t=\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W;\\mathcal{D}^t)\\ \\text{at the first step};\\ \\ \\hat G_t=USV^{\\top}:\\ A_t=V_{[:,1:r]}^{\\top}\\ (\\text{frozen}),\\ B_t^{(0)}=U_{[:,1:r]}S_{[1:r]};\\ \\ W=W'+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_tA_t,\\ W'=W-\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_t^{(0)}A_t\\ \\text{frozen},\\ B_t\\ \\text{trained}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta W=-\\tfrac{\\eta\\alpha^2}{r^2}\\,G\\,A_t^{\\top}A_t;\\ \\ \\text{after task }t:\\ W\\leftarrow W'+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_tA_t,\\ M_t=[M_{t-1},\\ \\text{top-}m\\ \\text{directions in }\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}\\text{ of }(I-P_WP_W^{\\top}-M_{t-1}M_{t-1}^{\\top})X_t]\\ (m\\text{ set by energy threshold }\\epsilon_f)","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per task (only }B_t\\text{; }A_t\\text{ frozen)}","locus":"Linear (attention) weight matrices of CLIP ViT-B/16 image and text encoders and of LLaVA's language model; the constraint acts on the input side \\( \\mathbb R^{d_{in}} \\) of each adapted matrix.","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Per task, a LoRA with frozen down-projection \\( A_t \\) whose rows span a subspace of the residual space \\( \\{P_W,M_{t-1}\\}^{\\perp}\\subset\\mathbb R^{d_{in}} \\). \\( P_W \\) holds the principal input-side singular vectors of the pre-trained weight (general knowledge) and \\( M_{t-1} \\) the dominant input-feature directions of past tasks. \\( A_t \\) is the top-\\( r \\) right singular subspace of the first-step gradient after projecting out \\( [P_W,M_{t-1}] \\), and \\( B_t \\) starts at \\( U_{[:,1:r]}S_{[1:r]} \\) with \\( W'=W-\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_t^{(0)}A_t \\) absorbing the offset. Training only \\( B_t \\) makes every step a projected gradient step \\( \\Delta W\\propto-GA_t^{\\top}A_t \\) (Prop. 3.1), so \\( \\Delta W\\,[P_W,M_{t-1}]=0 \\): outputs on old-task feature directions and on the principal input subspace of the pre-trained weight are untouched. After each task the adapter is merged and \\( M \\) grows, with \\( p+k\\le d_{in} \\) bounding the stored memory. KeepLoRA+ adds trainable class prototypes for CLIP classification.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each task's \\( \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_tA_t \\) is merged into \\( W \\) after training (Algorithm 1), so inference uses a single dense matrix with no task identity and no extra modules (KeepLoRA+ keeps its class prototypes).","identity_at_init":"Yes, by re-basing: \\( B_t^{(0)}A_t\\ne0 \\), but the frozen weight is replaced by \\( W'=W-\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_t^{(0)}A_t \\), so \\( W'+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_t^{(0)}A_t=W \\) at the start of every task.","key_idea":"General knowledge sits in the principal singular subspace of the pre-trained weights and task knowledge in the residual subspace, so KeepLoRA initialises each task's LoRA from the SVD of the first-step gradient projected away from both that principal subspace and past-task feature directions, then freezes \\( A_t \\). Training only \\( B_t \\) turns every update into gradient descent confined to the residual subspace, which balances retention of pre-trained knowledge, retention of past tasks and plasticity.","results_note":"On the MTIL benchmark with CLIP ViT-B/16 (order I, no extra architecture), KeepLoRA reaches Transfer/Average/Last of 69.0/77.5/86.1 versus 67.4/76.3/85.2 for InfLoRA and 67.1/72.5/79.2 for SD-LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"inflora","type":"builds-on","note":"same frozen-A / train-B design with A drawn from a subspace orthogonal to past-task features (DualGPM-style); KeepLoRA adds the pre-trained principal subspace \\( P_W \\) to the protected set and takes A from the projected gradient rather than from projected features"},{"target":"o-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"continual-LoRA baseline that encourages orthogonality between task subspaces through a penalty rather than a hard projection"},{"target":"sd-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"continual-LoRA baseline (decoupled magnitude and direction); KeepLoRA is reported better on MTIL Transfer/Average/Last"},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue, not cited: both initialise LoRA from the SVD of the first-step gradient; KeepLoRA first projects the gradient onto the residual subspace and freezes A"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue, not cited: training only B with A frozen is LoRA-FA's update rule; KeepLoRA chooses the frozen A by projected gradient SVD instead of at random"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19659","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.19659","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10009355","https://github.com/MaolinLuo/KeepLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Mao-Lin Luo","Zi-Hao Zhou","Yi-Lin Zhang","Yuanyu Wan","Tong Wei","Min-Ling Zhang"],"bibkey":"luo2026keeplora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{luo2026keeplora,\n  title = {{KeepLoRA: Continual Learning with Residual Gradient Adaptation}},\n  author = {Mao-Lin Luo and Zi-Hao Zhou and Yi-Lin Zhang and Yuanyu Wan and Tong Wei and Min-Ling Zhang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2601.19659},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19659}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per task, LoRA with a designed frozen \\(A_t\\) and a trained \\(B_t\\), re-based so that the start reproduces \\(W_{t-1}\\); the branch is merged after each task.","shape":"\\(W'+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA_t=W_{t-1}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}(B-B_t^{(0)})A_t\\): the linear space \\(W_{t-1}+\\mathbb R^{d_{out}}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\), \\(d=rd_{out}\\), with \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\perp[P_W,M_{t-1}]\\).","base_point":"Linear and injective, hence regular. The offset \\(B_t^{(0)}=U_rS_r\\) is absorbed by \\(W'\\) and changes neither the image nor \\(S_1\\).","gauge":"None: \\(A_t\\) has orthonormal rows.","covariance":"\\(P_W\\) is a truncated-SVD frame of \\(W_0\\), and \\(A_t\\) is the top right singular subspace of a projected gradient (LoRA-GA's frame type): \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\), scale-invariant. The past-feature basis \\(M\\) is a data frame on the input side.","merge":"Each task's \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_tA_t\\) is folded into \\(W\\).","rebasing":"One stage per task with a moving designed frame (InfLoRA's scheme). Each stage alone is idempotent; after \\(T\\) tasks the reach is \\(W_0+\\{X:\\mathrm{row}\\,X\\subseteq\\sum_t\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\}\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KeepLoRA is LoRA-FA re-based once per task. Its frozen frame comes from the SVD of the first-step gradient after the principal input subspace of \\(W_0\\) and past-task features have been projected out. By the degree-one collapse each stage is full fine-tuning projected onto \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\), so \\(\\Delta W\\,[P_W,M_{t-1}]=0\\) by construction. The nonzero \\(B_t^{(0)}\\) is a split pointing of a linear method, so it changes only the parametrisation.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto B-B_t^{(0)}","note":"Per task: LoRA-FA(A_t) over the base W_{t-1} (same scale), pointed at 0. Checked numerically."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto\\big(B-B_t^{(0)},\\ A_t\\big)","note":"Per task, into LoRA pointed at (0, A_t), a T_A point with orthonormal A_t (composite with FA(A_t) -> LoRA)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"inflora","test":"O1","reason":"Per task both are FA-type linear spaces, but KeepLoRA's row A_t comes from a projected-gradient SVD and InfLoRA's from projected activations. For generic data the row spaces differ, so the stage images are not nested."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-pre","name":"LoRA-Pre","full_name":"LoRA-Pre: low-rank online-linear-regression compression of optimizer momenta","paper_title":"Taming Momentum: Rethinking Optimizer States Through Low-Rank Approximation","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (Oral)","arxiv":"2602.24283","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.24283","code_url":"https://github.com/mrflogs/LoRA-Pre","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"G_t=\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_{t-1})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ m\\approx m_Bm_A,\\ m_B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ m_A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}:\\ \\ m_B\\leftarrow(1-\\gamma_1)m_B+\\gamma_1G_tm_A^{\\top}(m_Am_A^{\\top})^{-1},\\ \\ m_A\\leftarrow(1-\\gamma_1)m_A+\\gamma_1(m_B^{\\top}m_B)^{-1}m_B^{\\top}G_t,\\ \\ 1-\\gamma_1=\\sqrt{\\beta_1}\\ (\\text{both factors updated simultaneously from their previous values; App. C damps the inverses as }(m_Am_A^{\\top}+\\lambda_{d}I)^{-1},\\ (m_B^{\\top}m_B+\\lambda_{d}I)^{-1},\\ \\lambda_{d}>0);\\ \\ v\\approx(v_Bv_A)^{\\circ2}\\ \\text{updated the same way with }|G_t|\\ \\text{in place of }G_t,\\ 1-\\gamma_2=\\beta_2^{1/4};\\ \\ M_t=\\beta_1m_Bm_A+(1-\\beta_1)G_t,\\ V_t=\\beta_2(v_Bv_A)^{\\circ2}+(1-\\beta_2)G_t^{\\circ2},\\ \\ W_t=W_{t-1}-\\gamma\\Big(\\frac{\\hat M_t}{\\sqrt{\\hat V_t+\\epsilon}}+\\lambda W_{t-1}\\Big);\\ \\ \\text{Muon variant: } O_t=\\mathrm{NS}_5\\big(\\mu\\,m_Bm_A+G_t\\big)\\ (\\text{factor steps use }\\tfrac{\\gamma_1}{1-\\mu}G_t\\text{ in place of }\\gamma_1G_t,\\ 1-\\gamma_1=\\sqrt{\\mu}),\\ W_t=W_{t-1}-\\gamma(O_t+\\lambda W_{t-1});\\ \\ \\text{all of }W\\text{ trained, only the momenta are factored}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{per matrix (full-parameter training)};\\ \\text{optimizer state } 2r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(Adam) or } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(Muon) instead of } 2d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{or } d_{out}d_{in}","locus":"Optimizer states of all attention and MLP weight matrices (other parameters use standard Adam): Llama pre-training from 60M to 1B parameters on C4, and full fine-tuning of Llama-2-7B and Llama-3.1-8B on MetaMath100k.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"No adapter: the weight step is the usual full-rank Adam (or Muon) step, but the EMA momenta are stored as rank-\\( r \\) factors. The EMA update \\( m\\leftarrow\\beta m+(1-\\beta)g \\) is one gradient step with learning rate \\( 1-\\beta \\) on \\( \\tfrac12\\|m-g\\|_F^2 \\), so momentum is the weight of an online linear regressor. LoRA-Pre replaces \\( m \\) by \\( m_Bm_A \\) and takes one exact Newton step per factor on \\( \\tfrac12\\|m_Bm_A-g\\|_F^2 \\) (Hessians \\( m_Am_A^{\\top}\\otimes I \\) and \\( I\\otimes m_B^{\\top}m_B \\)). The result is a simultaneous (Jacobi-style) EMA of each factor toward the least-squares fit of the new gradient given the other factor's previous value, with \\( 1-\\gamma_1=\\sqrt{\\beta_1} \\) so that the product decays at Adam's rate \\( \\beta_1 \\). The second moment stays positive through the Hadamard-square parametrisation \\( v=(v_Bv_A)^{\\circ2} \\), with \\( v_Bv_A \\) regressed onto \\( |g| \\). For Muon the regression target is \\( \\tfrac12\\|m-g\\|_F^2-\\tfrac{\\mu}{1-\\mu}\\langle m,g\\rangle \\). Since the Adam ratio \\( \\hat M_t/\\sqrt{\\hat V_t} \\) is elementwise, the weight update is full-rank even though the state is rank-\\( r \\). LoRA's update is rank-\\( r \\), and GaLore's step lies in a rank-\\( r \\) projected subspace. Adafactor's rank-1 factored second moment is an earlier instance of factoring optimizer state.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"There is no auxiliary module: updates are written straight into W, so the result is an ordinary dense checkpoint with the original architecture and unchanged inference cost.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparametrisation, training starts at \\( W_0 \\) (random init for pre-training). The factored states start at \\( m_B=v_B=0 \\), \\( m_A,v_A\\sim\\mathcal N(0,0.02) \\), so the reconstructed momenta start at 0 as in Adam. Theorem 3.1 assumes full-rank factors and Algorithm 1 writes plain inverses, which are singular at the first step because \\( m_{B,0}=0 \\). Appendix C.1 defines the update with Tikhonov-damped pseudo-inverses \\( (m_B^{\\top}m_B+\\lambda_d I)^{-1}m_B^{\\top} \\) and \\( m_A^{\\top}(m_Am_A^{\\top}+\\lambda_d I)^{-1} \\), \\( \\lambda_d>0 \\), which are well defined at \\( m_B=0 \\); the value of \\( \\lambda_d \\) used in experiments is not reported and the code is not released.","key_idea":"An EMA momentum update is one step of online gradient descent on \\( \\tfrac12\\|m-g\\|_F^2 \\), so momentum is an online linear regressor and can be compressed like any model. LoRA-Pre factors \\( m \\) (and \\( v=(v_Bv_A)^{\\circ2} \\)) into rank-\\( r \\) matrices updated by closed-form Newton steps, which cuts optimizer memory for Adam and Muon in both pre-training and fine-tuning.","results_note":"Pre-training Llama-1B on C4 at rank 512, LoRA-Pre Adam reaches 13.53 validation perplexity versus 15.64 for GaLore and 15.56 for full-state Adam, and fine-tuning on MetaMath100k at rank 8 it beats LoRA by 3.14 points on Llama-3.1-8B and 6.17 points on Llama-2-7B (GSM8K/MATH-500 average).","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"both cut optimizer memory for full-parameter training with rank-r state; GaLore projects gradients into an SVD subspace (rank-r steps), LoRA-Pre keeps full-rank steps and factors the momenta themselves; main baseline"},{"target":"fira","type":"competes-with","note":"GaLore successor with norm-based scaling of the complementary gradient; pre-training baseline"},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"competes-with","note":"Flora compresses momentum with resampled random projections; LoRA-Pre learns the compression factors by online least squares"},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"low-rank pre-training baseline (periodic LoRA merges with optimizer resets)"},{"target":"sltrain","type":"competes-with","note":"sparse-plus-low-rank pre-training baseline"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"fine-tuning baseline at equal rank (+3.14 on Llama-3.1-8B, +6.17 on Llama-2-7B); LoRA restricts the update to rank r, LoRA-Pre restricts only the optimizer state"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.24283","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.24283","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/oral/10011116","https://openreview.net/forum?id=9Q0dNBYeEY","https://github.com/mrflogs/LoRA-Pre"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Zhengbo Wang","Jian Liang","Ran He","Zilei Wang","Tieniu Tan"],"bibkey":"wang2026taming","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2026taming,\n  title = {{Taming Momentum: Rethinking Optimizer States Through Low-Rank Approximation}},\n  author = {Zhengbo Wang and Jian Liang and Ran He and Zilei Wang and Tieniu Tan},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2602.24283},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.24283}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":null,"rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). The weight step is Adam's (or Muon's) full step; only the EMA moments are stored as rank-\\(r\\) factors, updated by closed-form Newton steps. In the cotangent table it shares Flora's row (compress only the optimiser state), with learned instead of random factors.","shape":"\\(M_t=\\beta_1m_Bm_A+(1-\\beta_1)G_t\\) contains the raw gradient and the Adam ratio is elementwise (Muon: \\(\\mathrm{NS}_5\\) of a full-rank matrix), so one step can move in any direction: the per-step image is \\(\\Theta\\) (Lin, \\(d=\\dim\\Theta\\), as Fira and APOLLO).","base_point":"No reparametrisation: training starts at \\(\\theta_0\\).","gauge":"\\(\\rho=\\mathrm{id}\\). The factored state has its own \\(GL_r\\) redundancy \\((m_Bg^{-1},gm_A)\\), and the Newton factor updates respect it; that is a symmetry of the optimiser state, not of \\(\\rho\\).","covariance":"The image is \\(\\Theta\\), so \\(GL\\times GL\\). The update rule itself is only Mon\\(\\times\\)Mon (elementwise Adam ratio).","merge":"Writes \\(W\\) directly; nothing to merge.","rebasing":"Null: LoRA-Pre takes full-space steps and compresses only its moments, so it has no per-period shape to merge and restart. This is Flora's row in Exposé VIII.B (rebasing '–') and conventions deviation (3) of theory/axes.json, which gives exactly this reason for nulling Flora's rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Pre is Full FT with a compressed optimiser state. Its object is the terminal \\((\\Theta,\\theta_0,\\mathrm{id})\\); the low rank lives only in the momenta, which are themselves trained as an online low-rank regressor of the gradient stream. Unlike GaLore, whose step is confined to a moving rank-\\(r\\) period, nothing confines its update, and every difference from Adam lies in the induced cometric.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"adam-mini","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"Both are the terminal object with forward map id_Theta; they differ only in optimiser data (Def III.1)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"A LoRA-Pre step is full rank (the raw gradient sits inside the Adam ratio); a GaLore period has rank <= r."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"LoRA_r's updates have rank <= r; LoRA-Pre's full-rank steps reach rank min(m,n)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"structlora","name":"StructLoRA","full_name":"StructLoRA: information-bottleneck direction filtering with graph-based layer coordination","paper_title":"Not All Directions Matter: Towards Structured and Task-Aware Low-Rank Model Adaptation","authors":"Xiao et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ACL 2026 (long)","arxiv":"2603.14228","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14228","code_url":"https://github.com/xixiaouab/StructLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta\\tilde W_l=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_l\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_l)\\,A_l,\\ \\ m_l\\in[0,1]^{r}\\ (\\text{IB gate: variational-IB KL penalty; paper: Gumbel-Softmax relaxation with a Gaussian or Bernoulli/Beta choice; code: Bernoulli logits, Gumbel-sigmoid relaxation, KL to a Bernoulli prior});\\ \\ u_l^{(0)}=\\mathrm{vec}(\\Delta\\tilde W_l),\\ \\ u_l^{(t+1)}=u_l^{(t)}+\\sigma\\Big(\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{N}(l)\\cup\\{l\\}}\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{d_ld_j}}\\,u_j^{(t)}\\Theta^{(t)}\\Big),\\ \\ \\Delta\\tilde W_l^{\\mathrm{final}}=\\mathrm{reshape}\\big(W_ou_l^{(T)}\\big);\\ \\ \\min\\ \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{task}}\\big(f(X;W_0+\\Delta\\tilde W^{\\mathrm{final}})\\big)+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{IB}}\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{IB}}(m);\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ A_l,B_l,m_l,\\Theta,W_o\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\text{gate and GNN discarded after the final update is merged}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+r\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, plus a shared training-only coordinator } (\\Theta^{(t)},W_o)\\ \\text{(stated as}<0.05\\%\\ \\text{of model parameters)}","locus":"\\( W_q \\) and \\( W_v \\) of every Transformer layer (LLaMA-7B/13B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, ViT-B/16, LLaVA-1.5-7B; RoBERTa-base on GLUE). The coordinator acts across depth on the stack of per-layer updates.","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA with a learned diagonal gate between the factors, \\( B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m)\\,A \\): a sparsifying selector over the \\( r \\) rank-one directions, like SoRA's gate but trained with a variational-IB KL to a Bernoulli prior. A training-time map across layers is added on top: the vectorised filtered updates \\( U=[u_1;\\dots;u_L] \\) pass through a shallow residual GCN on a depth graph (adjacent layers plus semantic edges between layers with highly aligned updates or gradients). To first order this is a Laplacian smoothing step \\( U\\leftarrow U-\\eta(\\mathbf L\\otimes I)U \\), which lowers the drift energy \\( \\sum_l\\|u_{l+1}-u_l\\|^2 \\). Section 3.3 and the released code merge the coordinated \\( \\Delta\\tilde W_l^{\\mathrm{final}} \\), which in general no longer has rank \\( \\le r \\) because the GCN mixes and nonlinearly maps updates from several layers. Appendix E.7 says instead that the gated \\( \\Delta\\tilde W \\) is merged. The released code's coordinator is a dense linear encoder on \\( \\mathrm{vec}(\\Delta\\tilde W_l) \\) of width \\( d_{out}d_{in} \\), which does not match the stated \\( <0.05\\% \\) overhead at LLaMA scale.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Whichever update is deployed, the gated \\( \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m)\\,A \\) (Appendix E.7) or the coordinator output \\( \\Delta\\tilde W^{\\mathrm{final}} \\) (Sec. 3.3 and the code's merge_structlora), it is a fixed matrix added to \\( W_0 \\), so inference cost equals the base model's.","identity_at_init":"The gated LoRA path is the identity at init (\\( B=0 \\), Kaiming-uniform \\( A \\), gate logits 2.0 in the code). The coordinated update is not guaranteed to be: in the released code the coordinator returns \\( u+\\mathrm{dec}(\\mathrm{enc}(u)) \\) with default nn.Linear biases, so \\( \\Delta\\tilde W^{\\mathrm{final}}\\ne0 \\) at step 0 whenever it is active. The paper does not discuss this.","key_idea":"Not all \\( r \\) LoRA directions carry task signal, and layers adapted independently drift apart. StructLoRA gates the rank-one directions with an information-bottleneck filter and, during training only, smooths the per-layer updates across depth with a shallow GCN (a Laplacian smoother), then merges the result so inference costs the same as LoRA.","results_note":"On GLUE with RoBERTa-base, StructLoRA reports an 86.5 average, 0.5 points above Sensitivity-LoRA, the strongest dynamic-rank baseline.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"keeps LoRA's interface, rank and scaling; adds training-only gating and coordination"},{"target":"sora","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue, not cited: SoRA also gates rank-one directions with a vector between B and A (proximal L1); StructLoRA uses a variational-IB/Bernoulli gate"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"dynamic rank-allocation baseline (masks singular values of P Lambda Q)"},{"target":"dylora","type":"competes-with","note":"dynamic-rank baseline"},{"target":"lora-dropout","type":"competes-with","note":"untargeted stochastic sparsity baseline; StructLoRA's gate is task-aware"},{"target":"loraprune","type":"competes-with","note":"structured-pruning baseline"},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-variant baseline"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14228","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.14228","https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.97/","https://aclanthology.org/events/acl-2026/","https://xixiaouab.github.io/StructLoRA/","https://github.com/xixiaouab/StructLoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xixiaouab/StructLoRA/main/src/structlora/coordinator.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/xixiaouab/StructLoRA/main/src/structlora/layers.py"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"authors_full":["Xi Xiao","Chenrui Ma","Yunbei Zhang","Chen Liu","Zhuxuanzi Wang","Yanshu Li","Lin Zhao","Guosheng Hu","Tianyang Wang","Hao Xu"],"bibkey":"xiao2026not","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xiao2026not,\n  title = {{Not All Directions Matter: Towards Structured and Task-Aware Low-Rank Model Adaptation}},\n  author = {Xi Xiao and Chenrui Ma and Yunbei Zhang and Chen Liu and Zhuxuanzi Wang and Yanshu Li and Lin Zhao and Guosheng Hu and Tianyang Wang and Hao Xu},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2603.14228},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14228}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Read with the released transform: merge_structlora merges the coordinator output \\(u_l+\\mathrm{Dec}(H_l)\\), with \\(u_l=\\mathrm{vec}(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_l\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_l)A_l)\\) and \\(H\\) a one-layer residual GCN over the encoded \\(u_l\\). Sec. 3.3 agrees; App. E.7 says instead that the gated update is merged.","shape":"One GraphCoordinator serves all adapted matrices, and its decoder \\(\\mathrm{Dec}(z)=Dz+c\\) is a trainable dense map into \\(\\mathbb R^{\\max d_{out}d_{in}}\\) (checked in the released coordinator.py). The bias \\(c\\) translates every box freely, and \\(D\\) moves the boxes independently when the \\(H_l\\) are linearly independent (at most 128 boxes). So the reachable set is all of the adapted boxes, a flat full-dimensional germ, and the rank bound \\(r\\) no longer constrains the deployed update.","base_point":"At \\(B=0\\) the coordinated delta is \\(\\mathrm{Dec}(H(0))\\neq0\\) (default nn.Linear biases), so \\(\\rho(q_0)\\neq\\theta_0\\): a defect, with entries of order \\(1/\\sqrt{128}\\). The paper's bias-free formula would start at \\(\\theta_0\\). Under the paper's bias-free Sec. 3.3 formula (\\(\\sigma(0)=0\\), no decoder bias) the start would be pointed but first-order deficient (only \\(B\\) moves at \\(B=0\\)): an apex. All other coordinates are the same for the paper's formula and the code.","gauge":"The decoder alone has \\(129\\max d_{out}d_{in}\\) entries, far more than the adapted boxes, and the GELU message layer leaves no linear group acting on the whole fibre: non-linear. The gated part alone has SoRA's fibre \\(r^2+r\\).","covariance":"The image is the whole adapted box, so \\(GL\\times GL\\). Flatten-and-pad aligns coordinates across boxes of different shapes; that matters for the dynamics, not for the image.","merge":"The coordinated delta is one static matrix per box.","rebasing":"The image is already the whole box, so cycles gain nothing."},"categorical_reading":"As released, StructLoRA is not a low-rank method. The training-only coordinator's decoder is a dense trainable map back into weight space, and its output is what gets merged, so the deployed update ranges over every adapted box and starts at a defect. Only with the coordinator off (App. E.7, or merge_structlora called without a coordinator) is the object SoRA's gated cone \\(W_0+\\{B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m)A\\}\\) pointed at the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. In that reading the GCN smoothing is a training device that \\(\\rho\\) does not see.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"sora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,B,m)\\mapsto\\big(B,\\ \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}m,\\ A\\big)","note":"Gated instance only (coordinator off): W_0 + (alpha/r) B diag(m) A is SoRA's map with gate g = (alpha/r) m, SoRA re-pointed at (0, (alpha/r) m_0, A_0) with m_0 = sigmoid(2) from the code's gate logits; same T_A stratum. The released coordinated instance has no such arrow (see the obstruction).","source_instance":"gated (coordinator off, App. E.7)"},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,B,m)\\mapsto\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m),\\ A\\big)","note":"Gated instance only, pointed at (0, A_0).","source_instance":"gated (coordinator off, App. E.7)"}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Released coordinated instance: the deployed update reaches rank min(m,n) through the decoder's dense output, against r for LoRA_r.","source_instance":"released coordinated update"}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"fedrot-lora","name":"FedRot-LoRA","full_name":"Federated LoRA with rotational alignment of client factors","paper_title":"FedRot-LoRA: Mitigating Rotational Misalignment in Federated LoRA","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2602.23638","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23638","code_url":"https://github.com/haoran-zh/FedRot-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=(W_0+\\bar B^{t}\\bar A^{t})x,\\ \\ \\bar B^{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\bar A^{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\quad \\text{client } i \\text{ trains } (B_i^t,A_i^t) \\text{ starting from } (\\bar B^{t-1},\\bar A^{t-1});\\quad R_i^{t,*}=\\arg\\min_{R^\\top R=I_r,\\ \\det R>0}\\begin{cases}\\|R^\\top A_i^t-\\bar A^{t-1}\\|_F^2, & t\\ \\text{odd}\\\\ \\|B_i^tR-\\bar B^{t-1}\\|_F^2, & t\\ \\text{even}\\end{cases}\\ \\ (\\text{orthogonal Procrustes, closed form via the SVD of an } r\\times r \\text{ correlation matrix});\\quad R_{i,\\mathrm{soft}}^t=\\Pi_{SO(r)}\\big((1-\\lambda)I_r+\\lambda R_i^{t,*}\\big);\\quad \\tilde A_i^t=R_{i,\\mathrm{soft}}^{t\\top}A_i^t,\\ \\ \\tilde B_i^t=B_i^tR_{i,\\mathrm{soft}}^t\\ \\ (\\tilde B_i^t\\tilde A_i^t=B_i^tA_i^t);\\quad \\bar A^t=\\tfrac1N\\textstyle\\sum_i\\tilde A_i^t,\\ \\ \\bar B^t=\\tfrac1N\\sum_i\\tilde B_i^t;\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained on clients},\\ R\\ \\text{computed in closed form}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (per client; same communication as FedIT); } R_i^t\\in SO(r)\\ \\text{is computed, not trained}","locus":"LoRA-adapted linear layers in federated fine-tuning (RoBERTa-Large on GLUE; Llama 3-8B on GSM8K and HumanEval), implemented in FederatedScope-LLM","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Each client trains a rank-\\(r\\) LoRA \\(\\Delta W=BA\\). The contribution is the aggregation map: before uploading, each client picks a representative of its factor pair's orbit under the \\(SO(r)\\) subgroup of the \\(GL(r)\\) gauge action \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A)\\). The representative is chosen by orthogonal Procrustes against last round's global factors, aligning \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) in alternate rounds. The server then averages factors, so the global adapter stays rank \\(r\\) and \\(\\bar B\\bar A\\) approximates \\(\\tfrac1N\\sum_iB_iA_i\\) up to the aggregation error \\(E^t=\\bar B\\bar A-\\tfrac1N\\sum_iB_iA_i\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The global adapter \\(\\bar B^t\\bar A^t\\) is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) LoRA product and folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W_0=\\bar B^0\\bar A^0=0\\) (Algorithm 1, line 2; the scalar example in Fig. 2 starts from \\(\\bar B^0=0\\)), so training starts at \\(W_0\\). Each alignment preserves every client's update exactly, since \\(\\tilde B_i\\tilde A_i=B_iRR^\\top A_i=B_iA_i\\).","key_idea":"Because \\((BR)(R^\\top A)=BA\\) for every rotation \\(R\\), clients can reach equivalent but rotated factors, and averaging them gives a \\(\\bar B\\bar A\\) far from \\(\\tfrac1N\\sum_iB_iA_i\\). Fixing this gauge with a closed-form Procrustes rotation toward last round's global factors (softened by \\(\\lambda\\); \\(\\lambda=0\\) is plain FedIT averaging) shrinks the aggregation-error term in the nonconvex convergence bound. It costs \\(O(dr^2+r^3)\\) per client and adds no communication.","results_note":"On GLUE with RoBERTa-Large (\\(r=4\\), Dirichlet \\(h=0.5\\), \\(N=50\\) clients; Table 1), FedRot-LoRA averages 87.34% accuracy versus 82.44% for RoLoRA and 76.80% for FedIT.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Plain LoRA factors on each client; only the aggregation changes."},{"target":"fedit","type":"generalizes","note":"With rotation strength \\(\\lambda=0\\), FedRot-LoRA reduces to FedIT's naive factor-wise averaging (Section 3, after Eq. 6)."},{"target":"ffa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"FFA-LoRA makes aggregation exact by freezing \\(A\\) (losing expressivity). FedRot keeps both factors trainable and instead reduces the error by aligning the rotation gauge. FFA-LoRA is a baseline in Table 1."},{"target":"flora-federated","type":"competes-with","note":"FLoRA stacks client factors for exact aggregation at rank \\(Nr\\) and higher communication. FedRot keeps rank \\(r\\) and communication unchanged (cited in related work; not a Table 1 baseline)."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"Structural analogue, not a baseline: KnOTS aligns several task LoRAs in a shared SVD basis before merging, FedRot aligns each client's factors to the previous global adapter by a Procrustes rotation before averaging. FedRot does not cite KnOTS."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23638","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.23638","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/66566","https://openreview.net/forum?id=2X8Qi3VdjA","https://github.com/haoran-zh/FedRot-LoRA","https://github.com/haoran-zh/FedRot-LoRA/blob/main/federatedscope/rotation_alignment_tools.py","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2602.23638"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Haoran Zhang","Dongjun Kim","Seohyeon Cha","Haris Vikalo"],"bibkey":"zhang2026fedrot","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026fedrot,\n  title = {{FedRot-LoRA: Mitigating Rotational Misalignment in Federated LoRA}},\n  author = {Haoran Zhang and Dongjun Kim and Seohyeon Cha and Haris Vikalo},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.23638},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.23638}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The global object is one LoRA\\(_r\\) pair kept across rounds. Clients train from the previous global factors, and the contribution is the aggregation map, a training procedure on this object (as HetLoRA).","shape":"LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\); the averaged pair stays rank \\(r\\).","base_point":"\\(\\bar B^0\\bar A^0=0\\) (Algorithm 1), with LoRA's \\(B=0\\): the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex.","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) has \\(GL_r\\) fibres. Each client fixes the \\(SO(r)\\) part by Procrustes against last round's global factors (softened by \\(\\lambda\\)). The \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_r\\) part stays free, so factor averaging is still bilinear and not a function of the client adapters \\(B_iA_i\\) (Prop V.4(a,b)). \\(\\lambda=0\\) is FedIT.","covariance":"The image is LoRA's cone, \\(GL\\times GL\\). The aggregation is less covariant: the Procrustes rotation minimises Frobenius distances between factors, \\(\\|R^\\top A_i-\\bar A\\|_F\\) and \\(\\|B_iR-\\bar B\\|_F\\), which are invariant under \\(A\\mapsto Ah^{-1}\\), \\(B\\mapsto gB\\) only for \\(h\\in CO(n)\\), \\(g\\in CO(m)\\). The aggregation rule is therefore \\(CO\\times CO\\)-equivariant, which does not affect the image.","merge":"\\(\\bar B\\bar A\\) is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) LoRA.","rebasing":"As LoRA; the global adapter is not merged between rounds."},"categorical_reading":"FedRot-LoRA is FedIT with a partial gauge fixing: before uploading, each client replaces \\((B_i,A_i)\\) by the representative of its \\(SO(r)\\)-orbit closest to the previous global pair. This removes the rotational part of the aggregation error \\(\\bar B\\bar A-\\tfrac1N\\sum_iB_iA_i\\), but not the part coming from the non-compact half of \\(GL_r\\). That is why Prop V.4 needs a shared factor (FFA-LoRA), stacking (FLoRA) or a gauge-invariant statistic (FLoRG) for exactness.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The global pair is a LoRA_r point, pointed at (0, A_0); only the aggregation differs."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ffa-lora","test":"O2","reason":"d(LoRA_r) = r(m+n-r) > rm = d(FFA-LoRA): both factors train, so the row space moves."},{"target":"florg","test":"O1","reason":"FedRot's image W_0 + M_{<=r} contains -Delta W whenever it contains Delta W; FLoRG's W_0 + L PSD_{<=r} R never contains -Delta W for Delta W != 0."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"rlpo-rlmo","name":"LoRA-RLPO / LoRA-RLMO","full_name":"Geometry-preserving orthonormal LoRA initialization for RLVR (principal / minor variants)","paper_title":"Geometry-Preserving Orthonormal Initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation in RLVR","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2606.31813","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31813","code_url":"https://github.com/Richard-ZZZ/geometry-preserving-orthonormal-init-rlvr","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=U\\Sigma V^\\top;\\quad h=\\big(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\big)x,\\quad B_0=0_{d_{out}\\times r},\\quad A_0=V_{[:,1:r]}^\\top\\ (\\text{LoRA-RLPO, top-}r)\\ \\ \\text{or}\\ \\ A_0=V_{[:,-r:]}^\\top\\ (\\text{LoRA-RLMO, bottom-}r),\\ \\ A_0A_0^\\top=I_r;\\quad \\max_{A,B}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{y\\sim\\pi_\\theta}[R(x,y)]-\\beta\\,\\mathrm{KL}(\\pi_\\theta\\|\\pi_{ref})\\ \\ (\\text{general RLVR objective, Eq. 9; the main DAPO runs drop the explicit KL term; GRPO for Qwen2.5-7B});\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen and not modified (no residual)},\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained};\\quad \\tfrac1T\\|W_T^{LoRA}-W_T^{full}\\|_F\\le M\\eta\\,\\Gamma_T(\\eta)\\,\\|I_{d_{in}}-A_0^\\top A_0\\|_2+O(\\eta^2),\\ \\ \\|I_{d_{in}}-A_0^\\top A_0\\|_2=\\max\\big(1,\\max_i|1-s_i(A_0)^2|\\big)\\ge1\\ \\text{for } r<d_{in},\\ \\text{with equality iff every singular value } s_i(A_0)\\le\\sqrt2\\ (\\text{in particular if } A_0A_0^\\top=I_r)","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (}r=16\\text{ in the main experiments)}","locus":"all linear layers (attention and MLP projections). Main runs: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B with DAPO on DAPO-Math-17k (\\(r=16\\), \\(\\alpha=32\\)). Appendix: Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with GRPO (\\(r=32\\)), Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct on MBPP-style code generation, and an SFT check on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (GLUE CoLA and MRPC, GSM8K)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Plain rank-\\(r\\) LoRA \\(\\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\); only the starting point changes. \\(A_0\\) is the transpose of \\(r\\) right singular vectors of \\(W_0\\) (top \\(r\\) for RLPO, bottom \\(r\\) for RLMO), so it has orthonormal rows and no singular-value scaling, and \\(B_0=0\\). PiSSA and MiLoRA use \\(B_0=U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\), \\(A_0=\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\) and a residual base \\(W_0-B_0A_0\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training, \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\) is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) matrix added to the untouched \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(B_0=0\\) gives \\(\\Delta W=0\\), and \\(W_0\\) is left untouched, unlike PiSSA, MiLoRA and OLoRA, which subtract \\(B_0A_0\\) from \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"In RLVR with \\(B_0=0\\), Theorem 5.2 bounds the gap between LoRA and full fine-tuning after \\(T\\) steps by a term proportional to \\(\\|I-A_0^\\top A_0\\|_2\\ge1\\). A row-orthonormal \\(A_0\\) attains this minimum and also caps the first update at full fine-tuning's (\\(\\|\\Delta W_1\\|_F\\le\\eta\\|G_0\\|_F\\), Proposition 5.3). The minimum is not unique: any \\(A_0\\) whose singular values are at most \\(\\sqrt2\\) attains it, including, with high probability, the \\(\\mathcal N(0,1/n)\\) LoRA baseline in Table 1, so the bound alone does not separate orthonormal from Gaussian \\(A_0\\). Taking \\(A_0\\) from \\(W_0\\)'s principal or minor right singular vectors keeps PiSSA's and MiLoRA's subspaces but drops their singular-value scaling, which inflates the first update by at least \\(\\sigma_r\\) (Theorem 4.1), and their nonzero \\(B_0\\). The authors argue this scaling explains the KL blow-up and reward collapse of PiSSA and MiLoRA in RL.","results_note":"With DAPO on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B (\\(r=16\\), cosine LR, Table 1), LoRA-RLPO averages 65.03% over GSM8K, MATH500 and AIME22-24, versus 63.76% for LoRA-RLMO, 62.40% for LoRA, 51.71% for MiLoRA and 4.57% for PiSSA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps LoRA's \\(B_0=0\\) and replaces the Gaussian \\(A_0\\) with an orthonormal, SVD-derived one."},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"RLPO uses the same top-\\(r\\) singular subspace without \\(\\Sigma^{1/2}\\) scaling, without a nonzero \\(B_0\\) and without a residual base; PiSSA collapses under RLVR in the paper's runs (4.57% average in Table 1)."},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"RLMO is the minor-subspace counterpart with orthonormal \\(A_0\\) and \\(B_0=0\\); the paper attributes MiLoRA's RLVR instability to singular-value scaling, not to near-zero tail initialization (Appendix D.1)."},{"target":"olora","type":"competes-with","note":"The paper uses an 'OLoRA' control with \\(B_0=U_r\\), \\(A_0=V_r^\\top\\) and a residual base (Eq. 7), and proves PiSSA's first update is at least \\(\\sigma_r\\) times that control's (Theorem 4.1). Published OLoRA (and HF PEFT init_lora_weights='olora') instead uses the QR factors \\(B_0=Q_{[:,1:r]}\\), \\(A_0=R_{[1:r,:]}\\), so the control is an SVD-orthonormal variant, not OLoRA as published. RLPO goes further and sets \\(B_0=0\\)."},{"target":"asylora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cites the asymmetry result that a fixed random orthonormal \\(A\\) works well, and gives an RLVR-specific reason to prefer an orthonormal \\(A_0\\)."},{"target":"mica","type":"competes-with","note":"Both target the minor singular subspace of \\(W_0\\). MiCA freezes \\(B=U_{[:,-r:]}\\) and zero-initializes \\(A\\); RLMO sets \\(A_0=V_{[:,-r:]}^\\top\\), \\(B_0=0\\) and trains both factors. MiCA is not cited."},{"target":"evaluating-peft-rlvr","type":"builds-on","note":"Gives a theoretical account of that benchmark's finding that PiSSA and MiLoRA underperform LoRA and become unstable under RLVR, and disputes its near-zero-initialization explanation for MiLoRA (Appendix D.1)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31813","https://arxiv.org/html/2606.31813","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63355","https://openreview.net/forum?id=Xo95FS2GTK","https://github.com/Richard-ZZZ/geometry-preserving-orthonormal-init-rlvr","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2606.31813"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Ruijia Zhang","Jiacheng Zhu","Hanqing Zhu","Laixi Shi"],"bibkey":"zhang2026geometry","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026geometry,\n  title = {{Geometry-Preserving Orthonormal Initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation in RLVR}},\n  author = {Ruijia Zhang and Jiacheng Zhu and Hanqing Zhu and Laixi Shi},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2606.31813},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.31813}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Plain LoRA on an untouched \\(W_0\\). Only the pointing \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) changes: the top or bottom right singular subspace of \\(W_0\\), with orthonormal rows and no \\(\\Sigma\\) scaling.","shape":"\\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), LoRA's cone (no residual subtraction).","base_point":"\\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum at the point \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,V_{[:,1:r]}^\\top\\) (RLPO) or \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,V_{[:,-r:]}^\\top\\) (RLMO) of \\(\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)\\): an apex with deficit \\(r(n-r)\\) and \\(S_1=\\{XV_r^\\top\\}\\). Same stratum as EVA, at a different point.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"The image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant; only the pointing frame is spectral (\\(CO(m)\\times CO(n)\\)), as for EVA.","merge":"\\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\) fuses into the untouched \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-RLPO/RLMO is LoRA pointed in the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum at the top or bottom right singular subspace of \\(W_0\\): PiSSA's and MiLoRA's subspaces with their Type II split removed. That puts the start back at the apex and keeps \\(\\|I-A_0^\\top A_0\\|_2=1\\), the quantity in the paper's bound on the LoRA-to-full-FT gap. Every \\(A_0\\) with singular values \\(\\le\\sqrt2\\) attains that bound, so the orthonormal frame is one point of a large set of equally good pointings.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,A)\\mapsto(B,A)","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_r pointed at (0, V_{[:,1:r]}^T) (resp. (0, V_{[:,-r:]}^T)). Into LoRA with a random A_0 there is only a weak morphism (a different point of Gr(r,n))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O3","reason":"Same top-r subspace, but RLPO is a Type I apex (B_0 = 0, W_0 unsplit) and PiSSA a Type II smooth point: different strata, incomparable images at equal rank (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"milora","test":"O3","reason":"RLMO against MiLoRA: the same bottom-r subspace, Type I apex against Type II split."},{"target":"eva","test":"O3","reason":"Both are T_A points, but RLPO's row A_0 is a weight frame and EVA's an activation frame. For generic data S_1 = {X V_r^T} and {X V_act^T} are not nested, so there is no pointed arrow either way."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"nara","name":"NaRA","full_name":"Noise-aware Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"NaRA: Noise-Aware LoRA for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Diffusion LLMs","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2605.29716","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29716","code_url":"https://github.com/generaldi/NaRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+B\\,C(\\lambda)\\,A\\,x,\\quad C(\\lambda)=I_r+\\eta\\,\\mathcal{F}_\\phi(e_\\lambda),\\quad e_\\lambda=\\cos(2\\pi k\\lambda)\\oplus\\sin(2\\pi k\\lambda),\\ k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32},\\ k\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma^2I)\\ \\text{fixed};\\quad \\lambda=m/L_s\\in[0,1]\\ (\\text{fraction of masked response tokens});\\quad \\Delta W_{l,m}(\\lambda)=B_{l,m}\\,C(\\lambda)\\,A_{l,m},\\ \\ B_{l,m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_{l,m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ A,B\\ (\\text{per module})\\ \\text{and one globally shared MLP}\\ \\mathcal{F}_\\phi:\\mathbb{R}^{64}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\eta=0.1","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix} + |\\phi|\\ \\text{once per model (shared MLP } 64\\to256\\to512\\to r^2;\\ \\approx6.7\\times10^{5}\\ \\text{for } r=32)","locus":"q, k, v, o attention projections of masked-diffusion LLMs (LLaDA-8B-Base and LLaDA-8B-Instruct); one image-diffusion experiment (SDXL, DreamBooth) in Section 6.6","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W(\\lambda)=BC(\\lambda)A\\). Static per-module factors \\(B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\) and \\(A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\) sandwich a dynamic \\(r\\times r\\) core that is a smooth function of the scalar noise level \\(\\lambda\\). One Fourier-feature MLP, shared by every adapted module, produces the core once per denoising step. The update therefore moves continuously along the denoising trajectory inside fixed rank-\\(r\\) column and row spaces.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For a fixed \\(\\lambda\\), \\(W_0+BC(\\lambda)A\\) is a dense matrix. But \\(\\lambda\\) changes at every denoising step (and can differ across sequences in a batch), so no single merged weight exists. The paper lists non-mergeability as a limitation (Appendix, Table 10); re-merging at every step is possible in principle (catalogue's inference, not the paper's).","identity_at_init":"\\(B=0\\) gives \\(\\Delta W(\\lambda)=0\\) for every \\(\\lambda\\). The hypernetwork's last layer is zero-initialized, so \\(C(\\lambda)=I_r\\) and the initial gradients equal LoRA's.","key_idea":"Masked-diffusion LLMs see very different inputs at different noise levels, so a static \\(\\Delta W\\) helps mostly at mid noise. NaRA sandwiches a noise-conditioned core \\(C(\\lambda)=I_r+\\eta\\mathcal F_\\phi(e_\\lambda)\\) between static LoRA factors, giving a continuous family \\(\\Delta W(\\lambda)\\). Theorem 4.1 shows this family can represent any finite set of updates whose combined column and row spaces have dimension \\(\\le r\\); the extra parameters are under 0.01% of the model. Timestep-conditioned LoRAs already existed for image diffusion (TC-LoRA, TimeStep Master); NaRA is the first noise-aware PEFT for diffusion LLMs.","results_note":"On LLaDA-8B-Instruct commonsense reasoning (\\(r=32\\), Table 1), NaRA averages 84.09% versus 80.18% for LoRA and 82.73% for HiRA, with 0.425% versus 0.417% trainable parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"\\(\\eta=0\\) (so \\(C=I_r\\)) recovers LoRA exactly."},{"target":"moslora","type":"competes-with","note":"Structural comparison (MoSLoRA is not cited). A \\(\\lambda\\)-independent hypernetwork output gives a static \\(r\\times r\\) core between \\(B\\) and \\(A\\), but NaRA's core is shared by every adapted module, whereas MoSLoRA learns one mixer per module, so neither method contains the other. Only the module-specific hypernetwork ablation (Section 6.3) would contain MoSLoRA."},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"Strongest baseline on commonsense reasoning (82.73% vs NaRA's 84.09%, Table 1). On mathematical reasoning (Table 2) plain LoRA and P-Tuning beat HiRA."},{"target":"dora","type":"combines","note":"Appendix M applies the same noise-conditioned core inside DoRA."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29716","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.29716","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/61250","https://openreview.net/forum?id=s8UGIRF38a","https://github.com/generaldi/NaRA","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09561","https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/8716aa6a02bcc3c8e69a3a42be192236-Abstract-Conference.html","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2605.29716"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Shuaidi Wang","Zhan Zhuang","Ruping Huang","Yu Zhang"],"bibkey":"wang2026nara","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2026nara,\n  title = {{NaRA: Noise-Aware LoRA for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Diffusion LLMs}},\n  author = {Shuaidi Wang and Zhan Zhuang and Ruping Huang and Yu Zhang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2605.29716},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.29716}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A \\(T\\)-point over the noise level \\(T=[0,1]\\): \\(\\lambda\\mapsto(B,\\,I_r+\\eta\\mathcal F_\\phi(e_\\lambda),\\,A)\\), with one hypernetwork shared by all modules.","shape":"Each fibre \\(W_0+BC(\\lambda)A\\) is a point of LoRA's cone, and for fixed \\((B,A)\\) all fibres share \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\) and \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) and a zero-initialised last layer (\\(C=I_r\\)): every fibre is the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex with \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\), and the hypernetwork gets no first-order gradient (zero-gate lemma).","gauge":"\\((B_j,A_j)\\mapsto(B_jG^{-1},HA_j)\\) for all modules at once, with \\(C(\\lambda)\\mapsto GC(\\lambda)H^{-1}\\) absorbed by the shared hypernetwork's linear last layer (weights and bias), plus one relative scalar per extra module: \\(GL_r\\times GL_r\\times(\\mathbb R^\\times)^{M-1}\\). Generic fibre \\(2r^2+1\\) for two modules (checked).","covariance":"Fibrewise LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"For fixed \\(\\lambda\\) the fibre fuses. \\(\\lambda\\) changes every denoising step, so the merge is per fibre (as Text-to-LoRA per task).","rebasing":"Undefined for a family whose fibre changes with \\(\\lambda\\)."},"categorical_reading":"NaRA is a section of LoRA over the noise-level line: a smooth map \\(\\lambda\\mapsto W_0+BC(\\lambda)A\\) whose fibres move inside one fixed pair of rank-\\(r\\) column and row spaces, with an \\(r\\times r\\) core produced by one hypernetwork for all modules. Static LoRA is the constant section (\\(\\eta=0\\)). Fibrewise it is MoSLoRA with the mixer generated, and tied across modules.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\lambda(B,A,\\phi)=\\big(B\\,C_\\phi(\\lambda),\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over noise levels, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0); pointed because B starts at 0."},{"target":"moslora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\lambda(B,A,\\phi)=\\big(B,\\ C_\\phi(\\lambda)/s,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise, into MoSLoRA re-pointed at (0, I_r/s, A_0), with s MoSLoRA's scale. Its default random-mixer pointing differs, but S_1 = {XA_0} on both sides."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hira","test":"O1","reason":"A rank-one fibre update uv^T lies in HiRA_r's image only if (uv^T) / W_0 (entrywise) = D_u (1/W_0) D_v has rank <= r, which fails for generic W_0 when r < min(m,n)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lime","name":"LiME","full_name":"Lightweight Mixture of Experts","paper_title":"LiME: Lightweight Mixture of Experts for Efficient Multimodal Multi-task Learning","authors":"Kowsher et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight)","arxiv":"2604.02338","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02338","code_url":"https://github.com/Kowsher/LiME","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\hat z\\odot\\mathcal{P}(x)+\\gamma\\,(\\hat z\\odot p_s),\\quad \\hat z=\\delta_\\phi(x)\\ (\\text{e.g. } \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BAx,\\ r=2,\\ \\alpha=4),\\quad \\mathcal{P}(x)=\\sum_{i\\in S_\\theta(x)}\\tilde w_i(x)\\,p_i,\\ \\ p_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}};\\quad w(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{(1-\\gamma_r)\\,z_{1:E}/\\|z_{1:E}\\|_\\infty+\\gamma_r\\,\\hat z_{1:E}/\\|\\hat z_{1:E}\\|_\\infty}{\\tau}\\Big),\\ \\ z=W_0x;\\quad S_\\theta(x)=\\{i:w_i(x)\\ge\\theta\\max_jw_j(x)\\},\\ \\ \\tilde w_i=w_i\\big/\\textstyle\\sum_{j\\in S_\\theta}w_j;\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\phi,\\{p_i\\},p_s,\\gamma\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\text{router has no parameters};\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\alpha\\mathcal{L}_{imp}+\\beta\\,\\mathrm{KL}(\\bar p\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal{U});\\ \\ \\text{defaults: } E=4,\\ \\gamma_r=0.7,\\ \\tau=0.5,\\ \\theta=0.7,\\ n\\text{-gram window }3","trainable_params":"|\\phi|+E\\,d_{out}+d_{out}+1\\ \\text{per adapted layer (LoRA: } |\\phi|=r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{), versus } E|\\phi|\\ \\text{plus a } d\\times E \\text{ router for MoE-LoRA}","locus":"q, k, v, o attention projections in both the SigLIP vision encoder and the Qwen2 LLM of LLaVA-OneVision-Qwen2-7B (216 target layers; 0.5B model for ablations; also Molmo2-8B); wraps LoRA, DoRA, LoRA-FA, SliceFine (\\(r=2\\), \\(\\alpha=4\\)) or prompt tuning (10 virtual tokens); \\(E=4\\) experts, n-gram routing window 3","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"The output \\(\\hat z\\) of one shared PEFT module is rescaled element-wise by a convex combination of \\(E\\) expert vectors. A parameter-free softmax router chooses the combination from normalized slices of \\(W_0x\\) and \\(\\hat z\\), with confidence-adaptive top-\\(k\\) and one decision per n-gram window. For LoRA this is \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathcal P(x)+\\gamma p_s)\\,BA\\): a token-dependent diagonal rescaling of the rows of one rank-\\(r\\) update.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\mathcal P(x)\\) depends on each token (or n-gram window) through the router, so \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathcal P(x)+\\gamma p_s)BA\\) varies per token. Only with \\(E=1\\) does it collapse to a static, mergeable \\(\\mathrm{diag}(p_1+\\gamma p_s)BA\\).","identity_at_init":"With LoRA, the released code zero-initializes \\(B\\), so \\(\\hat z=0\\) and \\(h=W_0x\\) exactly. Initialization is \\(p_i\\sim\\mathcal U(0.9,1.1)\\), \\(p_s\\sim\\mathcal N(0,0.1^2)\\), \\(\\gamma=0\\), so LiME also starts close to the plain shared PEFT module.","key_idea":"MoE-PEFT methods normally keep \\(E\\) adapter copies plus a learned router. LiME keeps one PEFT module and lets \\(E\\) vectors \\(p_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\) rescale its output, so expert cost becomes \\(E\\,d_{out}\\) and works with any PEFT method. Routing logits come for free from the first \\(E\\) coordinates of the frozen output and the PEFT output. Theorem 2 states that the optimal-risk gap to expert-specific PEFT is \\(O(\\bar\\varepsilon)\\) if the modulation approximates it within \\(\\bar\\varepsilon\\); the approximation error itself is assumed, not bounded.","results_note":"On MMT-47 with LLaVA-OneVision-Qwen2-7B (Table 2), LiME-LoRA has the best commonsense-reasoning average (84.98%), with 3.49M trainable parameters versus 10.79M for MoELoRA. In the GLUE efficiency study (one epoch on one H100, about 894M total parameters), LiME-LoRA trains 0.52M parameters versus 1.97M for MoELoRA and runs 23% faster.","relations":[{"target":"moelora","type":"competes-with","note":"Structural competitor: MOELoRA (2310.18339) replicates LoRA experts under a task-id gate. LiME does not cite it; LiME's 'MoELoRA' baseline is a different paper, Luo et al. 2024 (arXiv 2402.12851), which the catalogue does not list. Against that baseline LiME uses 3-4 times fewer trainable parameters."},{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"HydraLoRA shares \\(A\\) and routes among several \\(B\\) heads. LiME shares the whole adapter and routes among diagonal output scalings. HydraLoRA is a Table 2 baseline."},{"target":"mixlora","type":"competes-with","note":"MoE-PEFT baseline with replicated LoRA experts and a learned router."},{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited as an adapter-replicating MoE-PEFT design."},{"target":"mov","type":"competes-with","note":"MoV already mixes routed (IA)\\(^3\\) scaling vectors with a learned router. LiME applies routed scaling vectors to a shared PEFT output and removes the router. LiME does not cite MoV."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"LiME-LoRA."},{"target":"dora","type":"combines","note":"LiME-DoRA."},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"combines","note":"LiME-LoRA-FA."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"combines","note":"LiME-PromptTuning shows the wrapper also works for non-adapter PEFT."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02338","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.02338","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/64728","https://openreview.net/forum?id=KRSZj8z5Lr","https://github.com/Kowsher/LiME","https://github.com/Kowsher/LiME/blob/main/LiMELoRA.py","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2604.02338"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Md Kowsher","Haris Mansoor","Nusrat Jahan Prottasha","Ozlem Garibay","Victor Zhu","Zhengping Ji","Chen Chen"],"bibkey":"kowsher2026lime","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kowsher2026lime,\n  title = {{LiME: Lightweight Mixture of Experts for Efficient Multimodal Multi-task Learning}},\n  author = {Md Kowsher and Haris Mansoor and Nusrat Jahan Prottasha and Ozlem Garibay and Victor Zhu and Zhengping Ji and Chen Chen},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2604.02338},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.02338}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GLxTorus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens (n-gram windows): \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathcal P(x)+\\gamma p_s)\\,BA\\) for LiME-LoRA, with a parameter-free router that reads the first \\(E\\) coordinates of \\(W_0x\\) and of \\(\\hat z\\).","shape":"Token-dependent output scalings of one shared adapter: no single weight-space image.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) gives \\(\\hat z=0\\) and the frozen function at every token (neutral). The router's normalisation \\(\\hat z_{1:E}/\\|\\hat z_{1:E}\\|_\\infty\\) is \\(0/0\\) there, so routing is undefined at the base point itself.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the rank wire; the torus \\((B,p_i,p_s)\\mapsto(DB,D^{-1}p_i,D^{-1}p_s)\\) with \\(D_{1:E}=cI\\), \\(c>0\\) (the router sees \\(\\hat z_{1:E}\\) only up to positive scale); \\((\\gamma,p_s)\\mapsto(c\\gamma,p_s/c)\\); and the translation \\((p_i,p_s)\\mapsto(p_i+v,\\,p_s-v/\\gamma)\\), since the routing weights sum to 1. Fibre \\(r^2+(d_{out}-E+1)+1+d_{out}\\) (checked with a dense router). Nearest key GL × torus.","covariance":"The Hadamard output scalings allow at most Mon on the output side, and the router reads the fixed first \\(E\\) output coordinates of \\(W_0x\\) and \\(\\hat z\\), so only permutations fixing \\(\\{1,\\dots,E\\}\\) survive: imposed structure \\(\\Pi\\) (Lemma VI.2 test). GL on the input side.","merge":"Thm VI.3 for routed mixtures: the scaling changes per token. M1 only for \\(E=1\\).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"LiME is an \\(X\\)-point of row scalings of one shared LoRA update: MoV's routed scaling vectors, moved from \\(W_0\\) to the adapter's output. Per token its weight \\(W_0+\\mathrm{diag}(v(x))BA\\) lies in LoRA\\(_r\\) with a fixed row space and a token-dependent column space \\(\\mathrm{diag}(v(x))\\,\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\). The router borrows coordinates of \\(W_0x\\) and \\(\\hat z\\) as logits. That is what makes it parameter-free, and also what fixes a privileged output basis.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,(p_i)_i,p_s,\\gamma)=\\big(\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathcal P(x)+\\gamma p_s)\\,B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0). Piecewise smooth only: h_x is smooth where the selected set S_theta(x) is locally constant and z-hat_{1:E}(x) != 0. At q_0 (B = 0) the router is 0/0; h_x is continuous there with h_x(q_0) = (0, A_0), because P(x) is a convex combination and hence bounded, but it is not differentiable (the infinity-norm normalisation flips with the sign of B). At q_0 the arrow certifies the inclusion of each realised fibre in Im LoRA_r rather than a smooth pointed morphism."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hydralora","test":"O1","reason":"Per token both have the form (mixture of heads) times a shared A, but LiME's confidence-adaptive top-k (theta = 0.7) and n-gram windows switch experts discontinuously, while HydraLoRA's dense softmax gates are continuous in the token. So for generic trained parameters LiME's routed functions are not HydraLoRA's (the MixLoRA argument)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"agzo","name":"AGZO","full_name":"Activation-Guided Zeroth-Order optimization","paper_title":"AGZO: Activation-Guided Zeroth-Order Optimization for LLM Fine-Tuning","authors":"Lin et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2601.17261","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17261","code_url":"https://github.com/LouisLINWEI/AGZO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{for each linear layer } W_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}} \\text{ with batch inputs } H_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times m}:\\ \\ \\nabla_{W_\\ell}f=Q_\\ell H_\\ell^\\top\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\mathrm{row}(\\nabla_{W_\\ell}f)\\subseteq\\mathrm{col}(H_\\ell);\\quad U_\\ell=\\mathrm{orth}\\big((H_\\ell H_\\ell^\\top)^{K}H_\\ell\\Omega\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r}\\ \\ (\\text{power iteration inside the } f_0=f(W;\\mathcal{B}) \\text{ forward pass; called } A_\\ell \\text{ in the paper});\\quad \\Delta_\\ell=R_\\ell U_\\ell^\\top,\\ R_\\ell\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d_{out}\\times r})\\ \\ (\\text{dense Gaussian } u_\\ell \\text{ for non-linear-layer parameters});\\quad \\widehat\\nabla_{W_\\ell}=\\frac{f(W+\\mu\\Delta;\\mathcal{B})-f_0}{\\mu}\\,\\Delta_\\ell,\\qquad W_\\ell\\leftarrow W_\\ell-\\eta\\,\\widehat\\nabla_{W_\\ell};\\ \\ \\text{all weights trained in place, no backpropagation};\\ r=1,\\ K=3","trainable_params":"\\text{all } d_{out}d_{in} \\text{ entries of each linear layer (full-parameter ZO); each step's update has rank} \\le r\\ (r=1);\\ \\text{extra state } d_{in}r \\text{ per layer for } U_\\ell","locus":"all parameters (full-parameter zeroth-order fine-tuning); activation-guided perturbations on linear layers (attention and FFN projections), dense Gaussian perturbations elsewhere; Qwen3-0.6B/4B and openPangu-Embedded-1B, plus Qwen3-8B in the appendix","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A randomized finite-difference (SPSA) gradient estimate with rank-\\(r\\) perturbations \\(R_\\ell U_\\ell^\\top\\). Their row space is the top-\\(r\\) left singular subspace of the layer's current input activations. In expectation the estimate is \\(\\nabla_{W_\\ell}F_{\\mu,U}(W)\\,U_\\ell U_\\ell^\\top\\): the gradient of a subspace-smoothed objective, projected onto the activation subspace.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"AGZO edits the model's own weights in place; there is no adapter to merge.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: there is no reparameterization, and training starts at \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"A linear layer's gradient \\(Q_\\ell H_\\ell^\\top\\) has its row space inside the span of the layer's input activations, and that span is spectrally concentrated. AGZO therefore perturbs only along \\(R_\\ell U_\\ell^\\top\\), with \\(U_\\ell\\) the top activation directions found by power iteration in the same forward pass. The expected cosine with the true gradient is \\(\\beta_{d_{out}r}\\|GU\\|_F/\\|G\\|_F\\), versus \\(\\beta_{d_{out}d_{in}}\\) for MeZO (Theorem 5.4, Corollary 5.5). Theorem 5.6 makes the gain strict when the activations are rank-deficient and the average upstream-gradient energy along the top-\\(r\\) activation directions is at least the average over all of them.","results_note":"On Qwen3-0.6B, AGZO beats MeZO and LOZO on most SuperGLUE-style tasks, e.g. CB 0.892 versus 0.803 (MeZO) and 0.760 (LOZO), against 0.946 for first-order fine-tuning, with almost the same peak memory as MeZO.","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"generalizes","note":"With \\(U_\\ell=I\\) (full activation space) the estimator has MeZO's dense Gaussian form (Corollary 5.5), up to AGZO's one-sided versus MeZO's two-sided finite difference."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Both confine updates to a data-derived low-rank subspace. GaLore uses the gradient's top singular vectors (first-order); AGZO uses the activations', which contain the gradient's row space (zeroth-order). AGZO does not cite GaLore."},{"target":"zo-muon","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use low-rank ZO perturbations. AGZO's subspace is activation-informed on the input side; ZO-Muon's is random on the output side and adds Muon-style orthogonalization. Neither paper cites the other."},{"target":"zo-bench","type":"builds-on","note":"The released code is built on the ZO-LLM benchmark codebase."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17261","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.17261","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/60499","https://openreview.net/forum?id=zfVxpXEZti","https://github.com/LouisLINWEI/AGZO","https://github.com/Yining-Jiang/ActivationGuidedZO-NPU","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2601.17261"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Wei Lin","Yining Jiang","Qingyu Song","Qiao Xiang","Hong Xu"],"bibkey":"lin2026agzo","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lin2026agzo,\n  title = {{AGZO: Activation-Guided Zeroth-Order Optimization for LLM Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Wei Lin and Yining Jiang and Qingyu Song and Qiao Xiang and Hong Xu},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2601.17261},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17261}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":null,"rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). The gradient is replaced by a one-sided finite difference along \\(\\Delta_\\ell=R_\\ell U_\\ell^\\top\\), where \\(U_\\ell\\) is the top activation subspace found by power iteration in the same forward pass: compression \\(g\\mapsto\\langle\\Delta,g\\rangle\\), anchor \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda\\Delta\\).","shape":"Each step is the line \\(\\theta_t+\\mathbb R\\Delta_t\\) (\\(d=1\\)), with \\(\\Delta_t\\) of rank \\(\\le r\\) per matrix (\\(r=1\\)) and its row space in the current activation subspace.","base_point":"No reparametrisation; each step is a linear method at its own base.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"\\(R_\\ell\\) is Gaussian (output side, \\(O(m)\\) in law) and \\(U_\\ell\\) an activation frame (\\(CO(n)\\), data): covariance holds only in law.","merge":"Writes \\(W\\) directly; nothing to merge.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme of lines resampled every step. The reach is \\(\\Theta\\): \\(R_\\ell\\) is resampled and the activation frames move with the data."},"categorical_reading":"AGZO is MeZO with its random line restricted to the slice \\(\\{XU_\\ell^\\top\\}\\), which contains the row space of every layer gradient \\(Q_\\ell H_\\ell^\\top\\). The compression is still one directional derivative, but along a rank-\\(r\\) direction whose input side is a data frame. Per step it is LoRA-FA\\((U_\\ell^\\top)\\) along a single random line, re-based every step.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda R_t","note":"Per step at base theta_t: the line theta_t + lambda R_t U_t^T is a line of LoRA-FA(A_0 := U_t^T), pointed at 0. Linear layers only: AGZO's dense Gaussian perturbation of the other parameters (norms, biases, embeddings) lies outside LoRA-FA's boxes."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mezo","test":"O1","reason":"Per step, AGZO's direction R U^T has rank <= r on every linear layer, while MeZO's Gaussian direction has full rank almost surely. So the step lines differ, and AGZO's line is not in MeZO's. Read in law: the rank-<=r directions form a measure-zero set that MeZO's Gaussian direction hits with probability 0."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"zo-muon","name":"ZO-Muon","full_name":"Zeroth-order Muon via subspace gradient orthogonalization","paper_title":"Powering Up Zeroth-Order Training via Subspace Gradient Orthogonalization","authors":"Lang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2602.17155","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17155","code_url":"https://github.com/OPTML-Group/ZO-Muon","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{for each weight } W_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}:\\ P_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ P_\\ell^\\top P_\\ell=I_r\\ (\\text{QR of a Gaussian, resampled every } v=100 \\text{ steps});\\quad \\widehat G_{Z,\\ell}=\\frac{1}{N_q}\\sum_{i=1}^{N_q}\\frac{f(W+\\mu P\\Psi_i)-f(W)}{\\mu}\\,\\Psi_{i,\\ell},\\ \\ \\Psi_{i,\\ell}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I_{r\\times d_{in}});\\quad O_\\ell=P_\\ell\\,\\mathrm{msign}(\\widehat G_{Z,\\ell}),\\ \\ \\mathrm{msign}(G)=(GG^\\top)^{-1/2}G=U_kV_k^\\top\\ \\text{for } G\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ (\\text{the paper's } G(G^\\top G)^{-1/2} \\text{ needs a pseudo-inverse here; computed by Newton--Schulz});\\quad W_\\ell\\leftarrow W_\\ell-\\eta\\,O_\\ell;\\ \\ \\text{first-order analogue } O=P\\,\\mathrm{msign}(P^\\top\\nabla_Wf),\\ \\text{equal to } \\mathrm{msign}(\\nabla_Wf) \\text{ when } P \\text{ spans the gradient's column space};\\ \\text{all weights trained, no backpropagation; 1-D parameters (biases, norms) use dense Gaussian RGE};\\ N_q=4,\\ r\\in\\{64,128\\}\\ \\text{for LLMs};\\ N_q=16,\\ r=64\\ \\text{for ViTs}","trainable_params":"\\text{all } d_{out}d_{in} \\text{ entries of each weight matrix (full-parameter ZO); each step's update has rank} \\le r;\\ \\text{state: } P_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}","locus":"all weight matrices (full-parameter zeroth-order fine-tuning) of OPT-1.3B/13B, LLaMA3-8B and Gemma2-2B (SuperGLUE, SQuAD) and ViT-B/16, ViT-L/16 (CIFAR-10/100)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A multi-query forward-difference gradient estimate (RGE) is taken in a random rank-\\(r\\) left subspace \\(\\mathrm{col}(P)\\), which is lazily resampled. The \\(r\\times d_{in}\\) estimate is orthogonalized by the matrix sign function and lifted back by \\(P\\). With an exact matrix sign each update \\(P\\,\\mathrm{msign}(\\widehat G_Z)\\) is a rank-\\(\\le r\\) partial isometry whose nonzero singular values equal 1; the Newton–Schulz iteration used in practice makes them only approximately 1.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"ZO-Muon edits the model's own weights in place; there is no adapter to merge.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: there is no reparameterization, and training starts at \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"Estimate the ZO gradient of the projected variable \\(Z\\) (\\(W\\approx PZ\\), with \\(P\\) random and column-orthonormal) by multi-query RGE. This cuts the dimension behind the estimator's variance from \\(d_{out}d_{in}\\) to \\(rd_{in}\\). Then orthogonalize the estimate Muon-style and lift it back with \\(P\\). Orthogonalizing full-space MeZO estimates (ZO-Muon-V0) does not help, but inside the subspace the matrix sign acts as a spectral noise filter, making ZO-Muon a zeroth-order approximation of low-rank Muon.","results_note":"Versus MeZO, ZO-Muon needs 24.7% of the queries to reach 0.90 on SST-2 (OPT-1.3B, Fig. 2a) and reaches 72.4% versus 47.3% on CIFAR-100 with ViT-L (Table 1). The abstract attributes this +25.1-point gain to ViT-B, but Table 1 shows it is ViT-L.","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"builds-on","note":"Same seed-regenerated, forward-only RGE. ZO-Muon adds a random orthonormal subspace, multiple queries and Muon-style orthogonalization; applying \\(\\mathrm{msign}\\) to MeZO directly (ZO-Muon-V0) fails."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Zeroth-order counterpart of projected low-rank updates with periodic subspace refresh (GaLore is cited). GaLore uses gradient SVD; ZO-Muon uses a random \\(P\\), since informed sketches gave no gain (Table A3)."},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"competes-with","note":"Both rely on a random projection that is resampled periodically, rather than a learned subspace, to keep low-rank optimizer state cheap. Flora is cited."},{"target":"agzo","type":"competes-with","note":"Low-rank ZO with a random output-side subspace plus orthogonalization, versus AGZO's activation-informed input-side subspace. Neither paper cites the other."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17155","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.17155","https://github.com/OPTML-Group/ZO-Muon","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2602.17155"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-1"],"authors_full":["Yicheng Lang","Changsheng Wang","Yihua Zhang","Mingyi Hong","Zheng Zhang","Wotao Yin","Sijia Liu"],"bibkey":"lang2026powering","bibtex":"@article{lang2026powering,\n  title = {{Powering Up Zeroth-Order Training via Subspace Gradient Orthogonalization}},\n  author = {Yicheng Lang and Changsheng Wang and Yihua Zhang and Mingyi Hong and Zheng Zhang and Wotao Yin and Sijia Liu},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2602.17155},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.17155},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17155}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":null,"rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\): multi-query forward differences in a random column-orthonormal left frame \\(P_\\ell\\) (resampled every 100 steps), a matrix sign on the \\(r\\times d_{in}\\) estimate, and the anchor \\(Z\\mapsto P_\\ell Z\\).","shape":"Per period the linear space \\(\\{W_k+P_kX\\}\\), \\(d=rd_{in}\\), as GaLore with a random projector (left side). The matrix sign changes the step within the period, not the period's image.","base_point":"Each period is a linear method at its own base.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"Haar \\(P_\\ell\\) on the output side and Gaussian queries on the input side: covariance in law.","merge":"Writes \\(W\\) directly; nothing to merge.","rebasing":"Moving random left frames: a rebasing scheme reaching \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ZO-Muon is the zeroth-order version of a one-sided GaLore period with a random frame: per period it is FB\\((P_k)\\), the frozen-\\(B\\) sub-object of LoRA, trained by forward differences and orthogonalised steps. The subspace is what makes the matrix sign useful, since it then acts on an \\(r\\times d_{in}\\) estimate whose variance scales with \\(rd_{in}\\) rather than \\(d_{out}d_{in}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"X\\mapsto(P_k,\\ X)","note":"Per period at base W_k: FB(P_k) is LoRA with B frozen at P_k, pointed at (P_k, 0) (stratum T_B), as for GaLore's left-projection case and Online Subspace Descent. Weight matrices only; 1-D parameters (biases, norms) get dense Gaussian RGE and lie outside the frozen-B slice."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O1","reason":"Per period, a Haar-random frame P_k against GaLore's gradient-SVD frame: the period images are generically not nested."},{"target":"mezo","test":"O2","reason":"A ZO-Muon period has d = r d_in > 1, the dimension of a MeZO step line."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"flexlora-entropy","name":"FlexLoRA","full_name":"FlexLoRA: Entropy-Guided Flexible Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"FlexLoRA: Entropy-Guided Flexible Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2601.22905","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22905","code_url":"https://github.com/Chongjie-Si/Subspace-Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+s\\,P_\\ell\\Lambda_\\ell Q_\\ell\\,x,\\ \\ s=\\alpha/r_{init}\\ \\text{(released code)},\\quad P_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r_\\ell},\\ \\Lambda_\\ell=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_{r_\\ell}),\\ Q_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_\\ell\\times d_{in}};\\quad \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\gamma\\textstyle\\sum_\\ell\\big(\\|P_\\ell^{\\top}P_\\ell-I\\|_F^2+\\|Q_\\ell Q_\\ell^{\\top}-I\\|_F^2\\big);\\quad I(\\Lambda_\\ell)=-\\frac{1}{\\log r_\\ell}\\sum_{i=1}^{r_\\ell}s_i\\log(s_i+\\epsilon),\\ \\ s_i=\\frac{\\lambda_i^2}{\\sum_j\\lambda_j^2};\\quad \\text{every }\\Delta_T\\text{ steps: the }b(t)\\text{ lowest-}I\\text{ matrices with }r_\\ell>1\\text{ drop their smallest-}\\lambda\\text{ triplet; the }b(t)\\text{ highest-}I\\text{ matrices append }(p,\\lambda{=}0,q),\\ p,q\\sim\\mathcal{N};\\ \\ b(t)=\\mathrm{round}\\big(b_0(1-\\tfrac{t-t_{warmup}}{T-t_{final}})^3\\big);\\quad W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ P_\\ell,\\Lambda_\\ell,Q_\\ell\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, with } r_\\ell \\text{ changing during training. Each reallocation removes one triplet from each of } b(t) \\text{ matrices and adds one to each of } b(t) \\text{ others, so } \\textstyle\\sum_\\ell r_\\ell \\text{ stays at the initial budget; the parameter count itself can drift when the adapted matrices differ in } d_{in}+d_{out}","locus":"DeBERTaV3-base for GLUE (the paper's rank-allocation figure shows query, key, value, attention-output, intermediate and output dense matrices), the Q, K, V, Up and Down projections of LLaMA3-8B for commonsense reasoning, and ViT-B/16 (ImageNet-22K) for VTAB-1K, whose target modules the paper does not list","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"An SVD-form additive update \\(\\Delta W_\\ell=P_\\ell\\Lambda_\\ell Q_\\ell\\) of rank \\(r_\\ell\\). It uses the AdaLoRA parameterization, with a soft orthogonality penalty on \\(P_\\ell\\) and \\(Q_\\ell\\), and it moves rank between matrices during training. Allocation uses one score per matrix, the Shannon entropy of the normalized spectral energies \\(s_i=\\lambda_i^2/\\sum_j\\lambda_j^2\\) divided by \\(\\log r_\\ell\\), which lies in \\([0,1]\\). Low entropy means the energy sits in a few directions, so the matrix can give up rank. High entropy means the energy is spread out, so the matrix should gain rank. Pruning removes the triplet with the smallest \\(\\lambda\\) from each of the \\(b(t)\\) lowest-entropy matrices. Expansion appends a Gaussian pair \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\lambda=0\\) to each of the \\(b(t)\\) highest-entropy matrices, so insertion leaves the function unchanged. \\(b(t)\\) follows a cubic decay schedule between a warm-up and a final freeze phase. The released code differs from the paper in three details: its schedule is \\(b_0(1-p^3)\\) rather than \\(b_0(1-p)^3\\), with \\(p\\) the elapsed fraction of the allocation phase; pruning removes the triplet with the smallest signed \\(\\lambda\\) rather than the smallest magnitude; and the orthogonality penalty uses the unsquared Frobenius norm, averaged over factors.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(P_\\ell\\Lambda_\\ell Q_\\ell\\) is a dense \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\). Ranks change only during training.","identity_at_init":"Exact. \\(\\Lambda=0\\) at initialization: in the released flexlora.py the singular-value vector is zero-initialized and \\(P,Q\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,0.02^2)\\), so \\(\\Delta W=0\\). Every expanded direction also starts at \\(\\lambda=0\\) (the paper's zero-impact initialization), so growing a rank does not change the current output.","key_idea":"FlexLoRA replaces per-direction sensitivity scores with a matrix-level score, the normalized entropy of each matrix's learned singular values \\(\\Lambda_\\ell\\). Under a fixed global budget it moves rank from low-entropy to high-entropy matrices, and new directions enter with \\(\\lambda=0\\) so that expansion is function-preserving.","results_note":"With DeBERTaV3-base FlexLoRA averages 89.1 on GLUE against 88.1 for AdaLoRA at the same 1.9M parameters; with LLaMA3-8B on eight commonsense tasks it scores 85.2 at rank 8 (AdaLoRA 85.1) and 85.5 at rank 32.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"Same \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) parameterization, orthogonality penalty and \\(\\Lambda=0\\) initialization. FlexLoRA replaces AdaLoRA's per-triplet sensitivity scores with a matrix-level spectral-entropy score and adds rank expansion. AdaLoRA is its main baseline."},{"target":"elalora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not compared in the paper. Both prune and expand ranks during training. ElaLoRA scores individual directions by gradient-based importance, while FlexLoRA scores whole matrices by spectral entropy."},{"target":"increlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not compared in the paper. IncreLoRA only grows rank. FlexLoRA moves rank in both directions and keeps the total fixed."},{"target":"dylora","type":"competes-with","note":"DyLoRA (\\(r=8\\), 0.9M parameters) is a dynamic-rank baseline in FlexLoRA's GLUE table."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Fixed-rank baseline at the same parameter budget."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22905","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.22905","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10006948","https://openreview.net/forum?id=tqnkbdYWWm","https://github.com/Chongjie-Si/Subspace-Tuning/blob/main/loralib/loralib/flexlora.py","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Muqing Liu","Chongjie Si","Yuheng Jia"],"bibkey":"liu2026flexlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2026flexlora,\n  title = {{FlexLoRA: Entropy-Guided Flexible Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Muqing Liu and Chongjie Si and Yuheng Jia},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2601.22905},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22905}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"AdaLoRA's \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) parametrisation with a soft orthogonality penalty. Ranks move between matrices by a spectral-entropy score: pruned triplets are dropped and new ones appended at \\(\\lambda=0\\).","shape":"Per matrix \\(W_0+\\{P\\Lambda Q\\}=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_\\ell}\\). With moving \\(r_\\ell\\) the trained object is a union, over allocations with \\(\\sum_\\ell r_\\ell\\) fixed, of products of cones.","base_point":"\\(\\Lambda_0=0\\) with \\(P,Q\\sim\\mathcal N(0,0.02^2)\\): \\(S_1=\\mathrm{span}\\{p_iq_i^\\top\\}\\), \\(\\dim S_1=r_\\ell\\), as AdaLoRA. Expansion at \\(\\lambda=0\\) re-enters this apex for the new direction, so growth is function-preserving.","gauge":"AdaLoRA's fibre \\(r(r+1)\\): non-linear (two torus edges plus absorption of \\(\\Lambda\\)).","covariance":"The score is a function of \\(\\Lambda\\) only, and the image is LoRA's: basis-free.","merge":"\\(P_\\ell\\Lambda_\\ell Q_\\ell\\) fuses.","rebasing":"As AdaLoRA: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"FlexLoRA is AdaLoRA's object with a different budget dynamic. Rank leaves matrices whose learned spectrum \\(\\Lambda_\\ell\\) is concentrated and enters matrices whose spectrum is spread, moving between nested sub-cones (A2 truncations) and their \\(\\lambda=0\\) extensions (A5 stages). Because the score reads \\(\\Lambda\\) alone, it inherits the gauge dependence of \\(P\\Lambda Q\\): \\(\\Lambda\\) equals the singular values of \\(\\Delta W\\) only when the orthogonality penalty is exactly satisfied.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same P Lambda Q object pointed at Lambda = 0 with the same frames, at any fixed allocation; only the rank schedule differs."},{"target":"elalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object; both prune and expand inside the rank filtration, with different scores."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at (0, Q_0)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"dylora","test":"O3","reason":"dim S_1(FlexLoRA) = r (Lambda_0 = 0), while DyLoRA's S_1 = {XA_0} is m r-dimensional and does not contain span{p_i q_i^T} for generic frames: no pointed arrow at the default pointings."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"fouriermoe","name":"FourierMoE","full_name":"Fourier Mixture-of-Experts Adaptation","paper_title":"FourierMoE: Fourier Mixture-of-Experts Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Jiang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026","arxiv":"2604.01762","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01762","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\eta\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}(x)}g_i(x)\\,\\Delta W_i\\,x,\\quad g(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\big(\\mathcal{G}_\\Phi(x)\\big),\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}(x)=\\mathrm{TopK}_k\\,g(x);\\quad \\Delta W_i=\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(E_i)=\\frac{1}{d_{out}d_{in}}\\sum_{u,v}E_i(u,v)\\,f_ug_v^{\\top},\\ \\ (f_u)_q=e^{j2\\pi uq/d_{out}},\\ (g_v)_y=e^{j2\\pi vy/d_{in}};\\quad E_i(u,v)=c_{i,uv}\\in\\mathbb{C}\\ \\text{for }(u,v)\\in\\Omega_i,\\ \\ E_i(\\langle-u\\rangle,\\langle-v\\rangle)=\\overline{c_{i,uv}},\\ \\ 0\\ \\text{elsewhere};\\quad \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\lambda\\,Z\\sum_{i=1}^{Z}f_iP_i;\\quad W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\{c_{i,uv}\\}\\ \\text{and router }\\Phi\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\Omega_i\\ (|\\Omega_i|=n)\\ \\text{fixed, sampled from a Gaussian band-pass around centre frequency }\\mathcal{D}_{c,i}","trainable_params":"\\mathcal{O}(Zn+Z\\,d_{in})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (App. G, Eq. 15). Read literally (}n\\text{ complex coefficients, i.e. }2n\\text{ reals, per expert, plus one }Z\\times d_{in}\\text{ router per adapted matrix), this is }Z(2n+d_{in})\\text{. For the LLaMA-2 7B setting (}Z=8,\\ n=8192\\text{, six projections, 32 layers) that is about 33M parameters (0.49\\%), far above the reported 0.06\\%, so the paper's actual accounting is unresolved}","locus":"The Q, K, V, Up, Down and Gate projections (plus O for math) of LLaMA-2 7B, Gemma 7B, LLaMA-3 8B and Qwen2.5-14B; Q and V only for RoBERTa-large (GLUE) and CLIP ViT-B/32 (image classification). \\(Z=8\\) experts throughout, with top-4 routing for commonsense reasoning and top-2 elsewhere","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A token-routed mixture of \\(Z\\) dense spectral experts. Each expert update \\(\\Delta W_i\\) is the inverse 2-D DFT of a sparse, Hermitian-symmetric spectrum supported on a fixed set \\(\\Omega_i\\) of \\(n\\) frequencies. \\(\\Omega_i\\) is drawn from a Gaussian band-pass around a per-expert centre frequency, so each expert is band-limited. \\(\\Delta W_i\\) is therefore a real sum of 2-D sinusoids \\(2|c|\\cos(2\\pi(uq/d_{out}+vy/d_{in})+\\angle c)\\), and each conjugate pair contributes rank at most 2. The router keeps the top \\(k\\) experts per token and mixes their spatial updates linearly, so the applied update \\(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}(x)}g_i(x)\\Delta W_i\\) depends on the input, with rank at most the number of non-zero spectral entries of the selected experts (App. H), which with conjugate partners counted is at most \\(2kn\\). Unlike FourierFT, the coefficients are complex, so the experts learn phase as well as amplitude. Catalogue observation, not from the paper: \\(\\mathrm{Re}\\,\\mathcal{F}^{-1}(F)=\\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\big(\\tfrac12(F+\\bar F^{\\sharp})\\big)\\) with \\(\\bar F^{\\sharp}(u,v)=\\overline{F(\\langle-u\\rangle,\\langle-v\\rangle)}\\) for every \\(F\\). Taking the real part of an unsymmetrized complex spectrum therefore reaches the same set of updates, and the substantive change from FourierFT is the learned phase rather than the symmetry constraint.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The gate values \\(g_i(x)\\) and the active set \\(\\mathcal{S}(x)\\) change from token to token. Each \\(\\Delta W_i\\) can be computed once per forward pass and cached, but no single static matrix reproduces the layer unless the routing is frozen.","identity_at_init":"Not specified. The paper's algorithm only says that the complex coefficients are initialized and that the router starts random. \\(\\Delta W=0\\) at the start would require zero coefficients (the FourierFT paper draws its coefficients from \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\)), and no code is public.","key_idea":"FourierMoE moves MoE-LoRA into the frequency domain. Each expert owns a band of 2-D Fourier frequencies with learnable complex coefficients (amplitude and phase), and a token-level top-\\(k\\) router decides which bands, and therefore how much rank, each token's weight update receives.","results_note":"In the multi-task commonsense setting on LLaMA-2 7B FourierMoE averages 83.95 against 82.73 for GOAT, the strongest mixture-of-PEFT baseline, using 0.06% trainable parameters; on seven GLUE tasks with RoBERTa-large it averages 91.40 against 86.73 for FourierFT.","relations":[{"target":"fourierft","type":"builds-on","note":"Each expert is a FourierFT-style IDFT of a sparse spectrum. FourierMoE adds complex coefficients with conjugate symmetry, band-limited per-expert supports and token routing; FourierFT is a baseline."},{"target":"mov","type":"combines","note":"Uses MoLoRA-style token-level top-\\(k\\) routing with a load-balancing loss, with spectral experts in place of LoRA experts. MoLoRA is a baseline."},{"target":"goat","type":"competes-with","note":"GOAT is the strongest mixture-of-PEFT baseline. FourierMoE reports +1.22 points on LLaMA-2 7B commonsense with about 16x fewer trainable parameters (0.06% vs 0.96%)."},{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"Mixture-of-PEFT baseline in the multi-task commonsense and math experiments."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01762","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.01762","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/151989","https://openreview.net/forum?id=WJwzyoFMtf","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Juyong Jiang","Fan Wang","Hong Qi","Sunghun Kim","Jing Tang"],"bibkey":"jiang2026fouriermoe","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jiang2026fouriermoe,\n  title = {{FourierMoE: Fourier Mixture-of-Experts Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Juyong Jiang and Fan Wang and Hong Qi and Sunghun Kim and Jing Tang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2604.01762},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01762}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: top-\\(k\\) routing over \\(Z\\) spectral experts, \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\eta\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S(x)}g_i(x)\\,\\mathcal F^{-1}(E_i)\\).","shape":"A token-dependent mixture: no single weight-space image. Each expert is a fixed-support linear map of its complex coefficients (rank \\(\\le2\\) per conjugate pair).","base_point":"Not specified, and no code. Read at the exactly pointed variant (zero coefficients, the convention used for FourierFT): every expert is \\(0\\), the frozen function at every token (neutral), and the router gets no first-order gradient. A FourierFT-style \\(\\mathcal N(0,1)\\) start would be a defect.","gauge":"Per expert none, \\(+1\\) per self-conjugate frequency (dead imaginary part) and \\(+2\\) per conjugate pair sampled twice (complex coefficients \\(c_1,c_2\\) at \\((u,v)\\) and \\((-u,-v)\\) enter only through \\(c_1+\\bar c_2\\); FourierFT's real coefficients give \\(+1\\)). The router's softmax translation is a kernel-type redundancy (as for MoV).","covariance":"The Fourier frame imposes a cyclic order on both sides (\\(\\Pi_{\\rm cyc}\\), as FourierFT); the router is dense on the input.","merge":"Routing changes per token; M1 only with frozen routing.","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"FourierMoE is an \\(X\\)-point of a complex-coefficient FourierFT: the router chooses, per token, which frequency bands the update uses. Learning the phase as well as the amplitude takes each expert out of FourierFT's point-symmetric subspace \\(\\Delta W_{j,l}=\\Delta W_{-j,-l}\\) (the sine patterns are point-antisymmetric), so the experts share FourierFT's frame but are not FourierFT experts. Fibrewise the method still factors through LoRA-FA with a frozen trigonometric input frame.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((c_i)_i,\\Phi\\big)=\\eta\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S_\\Phi(x)}g_{\\Phi,i}(x)\\,B(c_i),\\ \\ B(c)=\\tfrac{2}{d_{out}d_{in}}\\big[\\,a_k\\cos\\theta_k-b_k\\sin\\theta_k,\\ -(a_k\\sin\\theta_k+b_k\\cos\\theta_k)\\,\\big]_k","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA-FA(A_F) with the frozen input frame A_F = [cos phi_k; sin phi_k] over the union of the experts' supports. Here c_k = a_k + i b_k, and theta_k, phi_k are the output and input phase vectors of frequency (u_k, v_k); the factor is 1 instead of 2 for self-conjugate frequencies. Pointed at 0 for the zero-coefficient pointing. Checked numerically (2e-17). As for routed mixtures in general, the top-k selection makes h_x only piecewise smooth in the router parameters; it is smooth in the coefficients c_i, and h_x(q_0) = 0 for every router value."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"A complex coefficient c = a + ib contributes -2b sin(theta + phi), a point-antisymmetric pattern. FourierFT's real spectrum keeps every update in the point-symmetric subspace Delta W_{j,l} = Delta W_{-j,-l}, so no expert with b != 0, and no routed mixture of such experts, lies in FourierFT's image. Checked numerically."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"smoa","name":"SMoA","full_name":"Spectrum Modulation Adapter","paper_title":"SMoA: Spectrum Modulation Adapter for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026","arxiv":"2605.21147","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21147","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\widetilde W_0=P_{out}W_0P_{in}^{\\top},\\quad M_k=\\widetilde W_0[\\mathcal{O}_k,\\mathcal{I}_k]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{K}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{K}};\\quad \\Delta W=P_{out}^{\\top}\\,\\mathrm{blkdiag}\\big((B_1A_1)\\odot M_1,\\dots,(B_KA_K)\\odot M_K\\big)\\,P_{in},\\quad B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{K}\\times\\rho},\\ A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\rho\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{K}},\\ \\rho=\\tfrac{r}{K};\\quad h=(W_0+\\Delta W)x;\\quad W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\text{permutations }P_{out},P_{in}\\ \\text{(fixed once from the SVD of }W_0\\text{) and anchors }M_k\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\{A_k,B_k\\}_{k=1}^{K}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{r}{K}(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, i.e. }1/K\\text{ of rank-}r\\text{ LoRA}","locus":"Query, key, value and projection layers (the paper's wording, App. D) of Llama-2-7B and Llama-3-8B; \\(K=2\\) by default, with reference ranks \\(r\\in\\{16,32\\}\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"A block-diagonal HiRA in permuted coordinates. Write \\(\\tilde B=\\mathrm{blkdiag}(B_1,\\dots,B_K)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\) and \\(\\tilde A=\\mathrm{blkdiag}(A_1,\\dots,A_K)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\). Because \\(\\mathrm{blkdiag}(B_kA_k)\\odot\\widetilde W_0=\\mathrm{blkdiag}((B_kA_k)\\odot M_k)\\), the update equals \\(\\Delta W=(P_{out}^{\\top}\\tilde B\\tilde AP_{in})\\odot W_0\\) (catalogue rewriting). This is a Hadamard modulation of the frozen weight by a rank-\\(r\\) matrix whose factors are block-sparse in a coordinate order fixed once from the SVD of \\(W_0\\). The paper does not spell out the rule beyond grouping coordinates with similar spectral roles. Off-diagonal blocks of \\(\\widetilde W_0\\) are never modulated. Proposition 1 bounds the rank by \\(\\sum_k\\min(s_k,\\rho\\,\\mathrm{rank}\\,M_k)\\) with \\(s_k=\\min(d_{out},d_{in})/K\\), against \\(r\\) for LoRA. With \\(K=1\\) the permutations are absorbed into the factors and SMoA is exactly HiRA. Without the Hadamard factor it becomes block-diagonal mini-LoRAs (MELoRA's shape) in permuted coordinates. A second catalogue observation follows. \\(\\tilde B\\tilde A\\) has rank at most \\(r\\), so at budget \\(r\\) SMoA's update set lies inside rank-\\(r\\) HiRA's set \\(\\{W_0\\odot(BA)\\}\\), reached with \\(1/K\\) of HiRA's parameters. The rank-ceiling gain of Proposition 1 is over LoRA, not over HiRA.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is a dense matrix once it is scattered back with \\(P_{out}^{\\top}\\) and \\(P_{in}\\). It is added to \\(W_0\\) after training, so inference has no extra branch.","identity_at_init":"Not stated. The paper follows HiRA's training setup; if one factor of each block is zero-initialized as in HiRA, \\(\\Delta W=0\\) at the start. The SVD-based reordering does not change the layer.","key_idea":"Pretrained weights carry information in their long spectral tails, so one global low-rank branch under-covers them. SMoA reorders \\(W_0\\) by its spectral structure, cuts it into \\(K\\) aligned diagonal blocks, and attaches a rank-\\(r/K\\) HiRA-style (Hadamard-modulated) branch to each block. This raises the rank ceiling while using \\(1/K\\) of LoRA's parameters.","results_note":"With \\(r=32\\) and \\(K=2\\) (half of rank-32 LoRA's parameters) SMoA averages 81.85% on eight commonsense tasks with Llama-2-7B (HiRA at \\(r=32\\): 81.42%) and 87.39% with Llama-3-8B (HiRA: 86.73%), and reaches 72.33% on GSM8K with Llama-3-8B against 65.89% for LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"hira","type":"generalizes","note":"With \\(K=1\\) the permutations are absorbed into the factors and the update is exactly HiRA's \\(W_0\\odot(BA)\\). For \\(K>1\\) at the same \\(r\\), SMoA's update set is a subset of HiRA's. HiRA is the strongest baseline."},{"target":"melora","type":"combines","note":"Takes MELoRA's block-diagonal direct sum of small branches, in spectrum-permuted coordinates, and modulates each block entrywise by the matching diagonal block of \\(W_0\\). MELoRA is a baseline."},{"target":"mora","type":"competes-with","note":"Another high-rank-at-low-budget design (square matrix with fixed compression); a baseline."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"The rank ceiling exceeds \\(r\\) at \\(r(d_{in}+d_{out})/K\\) parameters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21147","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.21147","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/153896","https://openreview.net/forum?id=HtLt3XukFH","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Yongkang Liu","Xing Li","Mengjie Zhao","Shanru Zhang","Zijing Wang","Qian Li","Shi Feng","Feiliang Ren","Daling Wang","Hinrich Schütze"],"bibkey":"liu2026smoa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2026smoa,\n  title = {{SMoA: Spectrum Modulation Adapter for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Yongkang Liu and Xing Li and Mengjie Zhao and Shanru Zhang and Zijing Wang and Qian Li and Shi Feng and Feiliang Ren and Daling Wang and Hinrich Schütze},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2605.21147},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.21147}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An additive update on \\(W_0\\), Hadamard-modulated by \\(W_0\\) itself.","shape":"\\(\\Delta W=(P_{out}^\\top\\tilde B\\tilde AP_{in})\\odot W_0\\) (checked): \\((D_{W_0})_*\\) of a block-diagonal LoRA (MELoRA's shape) in coordinates permuted once from the SVD of \\(W_0\\). A transported product of \\(K\\) block cones of rank \\(r/K\\), inside HiRA\\(_r\\)'s image.","base_point":"Not stated. With HiRA's zero factor (the setup the paper follows) every block is in the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum: an apex, with MELoRA's deficit \\(\\sum_k\\tfrac rK(\\tfrac{d_{in}}K-\\tfrac rK)\\) carried by the transport.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_{k=1}^KGL_{r/K}\\); the transport preserves it.","covariance":"The Hadamard factor gives HiRA's Mon×Mon, and the SVD-derived ordering is covariant under signed permutations and homotheties but not under non-uniform diagonal scalings: nearest key MonxMon. If the grouping rule were not permutation-equivariant (the paper does not state it), the block partition would be an imposed structure (\\(\\Pi\\)).","merge":"The scattered-back \\(\\Delta W\\) is one dense matrix.","rebasing":"With the permutations frozen, \\((1+X)\\odot Y\\) stays block-diagonal, so \\(R_\\infty=W_0+W_0\\odot\\{\\text{block-diagonal in permuted coordinates}\\}\\): off-diagonal blocks and the zeros of \\(W_0\\) never move. The span of the shape, not \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"SMoA is MELoRA transported by HiRA's \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent Hadamard frame \\(D_{W_0}\\), after a spectral reordering of rows and columns; equivalently, HiRA\\(_r\\) with block-sparse factors. Its rank ceiling exceeds LoRA's, but at budget \\(r\\) its update set is a subset of HiRA\\(_r\\)'s, reached with \\(1/K\\) of the parameters. So its gain over HiRA is in parameter count, not expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"(B_k,A_k)_k\\mapsto\\big(P_{out}^\\top\\,\\mathrm{blkdiag}(B_1,\\dots,B_K),\\ \\mathrm{blkdiag}(A_1,\\dots,A_K)\\,P_{in}\\big)","note":"Into HiRA_r pointed at (0, blkdiag(A_{k,0}) P_in), a T_A point (zero-factor init assumed). Checked numerically (exact)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"max rank sum_k min(s_k, (r/K) rank M_k) > r (Prop. 1)."},{"target":"melora","test":"O1","reason":"MELoRA's block-diagonal updates are additive in the standard coordinates; SMoA's are W_0-modulated blocks in permuted coordinates, which keep the zeros of W_0 and use a different partition. Neither image contains the other. At equal budget the rank argument also applies: an SMoA block (B_k A_k) . M_k has rank up to min(s_k, rho rank M_k) = s_k for generic W_0 (numerically 6 against r = 4 at 8 x 6, K = 2), while every MELoRA block has rank <= r/K."}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"bayesian-lora-sgp","name":"Bayesian-LoRA","full_name":"Bayesian-LoRA (sparse-GP inducing-variable posterior over both LoRA factors)","paper_title":"Bayesian-LoRA: Probabilistic Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Lin et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2601.21003","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21003","code_url":"https://github.com/moulelin/Bayesian-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=\\big(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\big)x,\\quad A\\mid U^A\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\big(T_r^AU^AT_c^A,\\ \\lambda^2\\Sigma_A\\big),\\ \\ B\\mid U^B\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\big(T_r^BU^BT_c^B,\\ \\lambda^2\\Sigma_B\\big);\\quad T_r=Z_r^{\\top}K_r^{-1},\\ T_c=K_c^{-1}Z_c,\\ K_r=Z_rZ_r^{\\top}+D_r^2,\\ K_c=Z_cZ_c^{\\top}+D_c^2;\\quad U=T_\\phi(U_0),\\ U_0\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mathbf{m},\\mathrm{diag}\\,\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}^2)\\ \\text{(masked autoregressive flow)},\\ \\ p(U)=\\mathcal{N}(\\mathbf{0},K_c\\otimes K_r);\\quad \\max\\ \\mathbb{E}_{q}\\big[\\log p(\\mathcal{D}\\mid W)\\big]-\\mathrm{KL}\\big(q_\\phi(U)\\,\\|\\,p(U)\\big)-\\tfrac{D}{2}\\big(\\lambda^2-1-2\\log\\lambda\\big);\\quad W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ Z_r,Z_c,D_r,D_c,\\mathbf{m},\\boldsymbol{\\sigma},\\phi,\\lambda\\ \\text{trained (per factor and layer)}","trainable_params":"\\approx c\\,d_{in}+r\\,d_{out}+r_L(r+c)+O(rc)\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, with an } r\\times c \\text{ inducing matrix per factor and LoRA rank } r_L\\text{. The inducing locations } Z_c^A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{c\\times d_{in}} \\text{ and } Z_r^B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{out}} \\text{ dominate; the variational means and standard deviations, diagonal noise, flow and } \\lambda \\text{ add } O(rc) \\text{ (derived from Eqs. 6, 13 and 14 and the released code). Paper total: 4.9M, against 4.48M for LoRA on Q, K and the LM head of Llama-2-7B with } r=c=9\\text{; 4.48M is LoRA at rank 8, the rank used in the released configs}","locus":"Q, K and LM-head weights of Llama-2-7B for commonsense reasoning (inducing size \\(r=c=9\\)); also Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct and Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 for MATH (App. Table 22)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A stochastic rank-\\(r\\) additive update. Both LoRA factors are random matrices generated from small \\(r\\times c\\) inducing matrices through a Kronecker-structured sparse-GP conditional: a bilinear projection \\(T_rUT_c\\) plus Gaussian noise of scale \\(\\lambda\\). The posterior over each inducing matrix is a diagonal Gaussian pushed through a masked autoregressive flow. The ELBO's KL over \\(U\\) is estimated from base samples (Prop. 4.1), and its conditional-KL term is in closed form. Every sample has rank at most \\(r\\), and the posterior mean \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\bar B\\bar A\\) has exactly LoRA's shape. A point-mass \\(q(U)\\) with \\(\\lambda\\to0\\) recovers a deterministic LoRA update in the reparameterization \\(A=T_r^AU^AT_c^A\\), \\(B=T_r^BU^BT_c^B\\) (Corollary 4.2).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"In deterministic mode the posterior mean \\(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\bar B\\bar A\\) is merged with no added latency. Calibrated predictions need \\(N\\) Monte Carlo samples of \\((A,B)\\) at test time. Latency is \\(1.52\\times\\) LoRA's with \\(N=2\\) and \\(2.79\\times\\) with \\(N=4\\), the setting used in the accuracy and calibration tables.","identity_at_init":"Not exact, and the paper does not state it. The released training script first applies PEFT LoRA, then replaces both LoRA factor layers on each key layer with InducingLinear modules. Their inducing means are drawn from \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) and their inducing locations are random, so both sampled factors are non-zero at step 0 and \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\neq0\\). The update is small because the prior scale is divided by the square root of the fan-in. The repository also has an InducingAdapter wrapper whose learnable output scale starts at 0, but the training script never uses it.","key_idea":"The Kronecker-factored sparse-GP conditional mean \\(T_rUT_c\\) has the same bilinear shape as LoRA's \\(BA\\). Bayesian-LoRA puts a flow-enriched variational posterior on small inducing matrices that generate both LoRA factors, giving end-to-end calibrated Bayesian LoRA with plain LoRA as the zero-uncertainty limit.","results_note":"On six commonsense benchmarks with Llama-2-7B Bayesian-LoRA has the best accuracy on five and cuts ECE by 84% relative to MAP LoRA on WinoGrande-S, at 1.23x LoRA's training time and 0.42M extra parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA is the point-mass, \\(\\lambda\\to0\\) limit (Corollary 4.2), up to the bilinear reparameterization of the factors through inducing variables."},{"target":"laplace-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Laplace-LoRA fits a posterior after training, while Bayesian-LoRA learns a variational posterior end to end. The Laplace variants (LA, LLLA) are baselines."},{"target":"blob","type":"competes-with","note":"BLoB puts a mean-field Gaussian on \\(A\\) only. Bayesian-LoRA makes both factors stochastic through sparse-GP inducing variables with a flow posterior. BLoB is a baseline."},{"target":"monteclora","type":"competes-with","note":"Also learns a distribution over LoRA parameters during training (a Gaussian mixture on \\(A\\)); not compared in the paper."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21003","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.21003","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/60552","https://openreview.net/forum?id=z3rQmLk4Io","https://github.com/moulelin/Bayesian-LoRA","https://github.com/moulelin/Bayesian-LoRA/blob/HEAD/bnn/bayesianize.py","https://github.com/moulelin/Bayesian-LoRA/blob/HEAD/bnn/nn/mixins/variational/inducing_simple3.py","https://github.com/moulelin/Bayesian-LoRA/blob/HEAD/configs/inducing/inducing8.yaml","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Moule Lin","Shuhao Guan","Andrea Patane","David Gregg","Goetz Botterweck"],"bibkey":"lin2026bayesian","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lin2026bayesian,\n  title = {{Bayesian-LoRA: Probabilistic Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Moule Lin and Shuhao Guan and Andrea Patane and David Gregg and Goetz Botterweck},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2601.21003},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21003}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over a noise base, trained jointly by an ELBO (as BLoB). Both factors are sampled from Kronecker sparse-GP conditionals \\(T_rUT_c\\) plus noise of scale \\(\\lambda\\), with a flow posterior on the inducing matrices.","shape":"Every sample, and the posterior mean, lies in LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_L}\\).","base_point":"Released code: inducing means \\(\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)\\) and random locations make both sampled factors nonzero at step 0, so \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\neq0\\): a small defect. The unused InducingAdapter wrapper with zero output scale would have been neutral.","gauge":"The factors are rational in \\((Z,D,U)\\) through \\(K^{-1}\\). The rank-wire \\(GL_{r_L}\\) lifts to these coordinates only in its orthogonal part, and the generic fibre is not a linear-group orbit: non-linear.","covariance":"The image is LoRA's (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The diagonal noise and diagonal \\(D\\) make the distribution itself only Mon-covariant.","merge":"The posterior mean fuses (deterministic mode). The calibrated predictive averages \\(N=4\\) sampled adapters at test time, so it is M\\(\\infty\\) (as BLoB).","rebasing":"Undefined: the calibrated object does not merge."},"categorical_reading":"Bayesian-LoRA is a family over a noise space whose samples are LoRA points with both factors random, generated from small inducing matrices through a bilinear GP conditional \\(T_rUT_c\\) that has LoRA's own shape. Its mean projects to a LoRA point (Corollary 4.2). Its distribution, however, lives on \\(Q\\) through a parametrisation that is not gauge-invariant, so the calibrated uncertainty depends on the factor representative, as for Laplace-LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\bar B,\\ \\bar A\\big),\\ \\ \\bar B=T_r^B\\,\\mathbb E_q[U^B]\\,T_c^B,\\ \\ \\bar A=T_r^A\\,\\mathbb E_q[U^A]\\,T_c^A","note":"Projection to the posterior mean (B and A are independent, so E[BA] = B-bar A-bar), scales absorbed. Pointed at h(q_0), which is not LoRA's B = 0 pointing because the released init is a defect, so the arrow lives in the unpointed slice."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"blob","test":"O1","reason":"BLoB's samples keep the trained col B, since its B is deterministic. Bayesian-LoRA samples B as well, so its posterior support leaves every fixed FB(B) slice."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"florg","name":"FLoRG","full_name":"Federated Low-rank Gram-matrix aggregation","paper_title":"FLoRG: Federated Fine-tuning with Low-rank Gram Matrices and Procrustes Alignment","authors":"Meng et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2602.17095","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17095","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{t}=W_0+L\\,(A^{t})^{\\top}A^{t}R,\\quad L\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times k},\\ R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{fixed semi-orthogonal},\\ A^{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times k},\\ k=\\min(d_{in},d_{out});\\quad \\text{client } n:\\ A_n^{t+\\frac12}=A^{t}-\\eta\\,A^{t}\\big(H_n^{t}+H_n^{t\\top}\\big),\\ \\ H_n^{t}=L^{\\top}\\nabla_WF_n(W^{t})R^{\\top};\\quad \\text{server: } Q^{t+1}=\\tfrac1N\\sum_n(A_n^{t+\\frac12})^{\\top}A_n^{t+\\frac12}=(P^{t+1})^{\\top}\\Lambda^{t+1}P^{t+1},\\ \\ \\tilde A^{t+1}=(\\Lambda^{t+1})^{1/2}P^{t+1},\\ \\ A^{t+1}=S^{\\star}\\tilde A^{t+1},\\ \\ S^{\\star}=UV^{\\top}\\ \\text{where } A^{t}(\\tilde A^{t+1})^{\\top}=U\\Sigma V^{\\top};\\quad W_0,L,R\\ \\text{frozen};\\ A\\ \\text{trained};\\quad \\text{experiments use a LoRA scaling factor of 16 and AdamW (App. A.6)}","trainable_params":"r\\,k=r\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (a single factor); per round each client uploads } A_n \\text{ and downloads } A^{t}\\text{, } rk \\text{ numbers each way}","locus":"LoRA-adapted weight matrices of OPT-125M, RoBERTa-large and Llama-3.2-3B (the paper does not list the modules); \\(r=4\\), \\(N=20\\) clients","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A rank-\\(\\le r\\) additive update confined to the cone \\(\\{LQR:\\ Q\\succeq0,\\ \\mathrm{rank}\\,Q\\le r\\}\\): a PSD Gram core \\(Q=A^{\\top}A\\) between two fixed semi-orthogonal frames (the paper says only that they are initialized with orthogonal columns). The core is invariant under the gauge \\(A\\mapsto OA\\), \\(O\\in O(r)\\), and is linear to average, so the server aggregates \\(Q\\) exactly (no \\(B_iA_j\\) cross terms) from one factor per client. Re-factoring the aggregate is ambiguous up to that gauge, and \\(\\mathrm{rank}\\,Q^{t+1}\\le\\min(k,Nr)\\) can exceed \\(r\\). Orthogonal Procrustes picks the rank-\\(r\\) factor closest to the previous round's \\(A^{t}\\), which removes the drift term from the convergence bound. Two catalogue observations, not from the paper: because \\(Q\\succeq0\\), \\(-\\Delta W\\) is never reachable for \\(\\Delta W\\neq0\\); and when \\(\\mathrm{rank}\\,Q^{t+1}>r\\), the \\(r\\times r'\\) map \\(S^{\\star}\\) projects the aggregate, so the broadcast update is no longer exactly the client average. The paper's constraint \\((S^{t})^{\\top}S^{t}=I_{r'}\\) cannot hold when \\(r'>r\\); in that case the solution \\(S^{\\star}=UV^{\\top}\\) has orthonormal rows.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(L(A^{t})^{\\top}A^{t}R\\) is a dense \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) matrix that is added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not exact. \\(W^{1}=W_0+L(A^{1})^{\\top}A^{1}R\\), and \\(A^{1}=0\\) cannot be used because \\(\\nabla_AF=A(H+H^{\\top})\\) vanishes there. The paper does not specify how \\(A^{1}\\) is initialized; \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) get semi-orthogonal initialization, which beats Kaiming and SVD initialization in its ablation.","key_idea":"FLoRG trains a single factor \\(A\\) of a symmetric update \\(LA^{\\top}AR\\) and aggregates its Gram matrix \\(A^{\\top}A\\), which is gauge-invariant. Federated averaging becomes exact and uplink traffic halves. The server then re-factors the average and fixes the \\(O(r)\\) gauge by Procrustes alignment to the previous round.","results_note":"With 20 clients, Dirichlet non-IID GLUE splits and rank 4 FLoRG beats FedIT, FeDeRA, FFA-LoRA, FedSA-LoRA and FedEx-LoRA in most cases, and it transmits up to 2041x fewer parameters than FedEx-LoRA to reach a target QNLI accuracy.","relations":[{"target":"ffa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both make aggregation exact with one trained factor. FFA-LoRA freezes \\(A\\) and averages \\(B\\), which is linear. FLoRG averages the Gram matrix of a single trained factor, which is quadratic but gauge-invariant. FFA-LoRA is a baseline."},{"target":"flora-federated","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited in FLoRG's related work (stacking-based aggregation) but not a baseline. FLoRA makes aggregation exact by stacking client factors, so its rank grows with the number of clients. FLoRG keeps rank \\(r\\) by re-factoring."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Replaces the two LoRA factors \\(BA\\) with the tied symmetric form \\(LA^{\\top}AR\\)."},{"target":"singlora","type":"competes-with","note":"FLoRG cites SingLoRA as reformulating LoRA with a single matrix. SingLoRA's centralized update \\(AA^{\\top}\\) has the same single-factor Gram form. FLoRG places it between fixed frames \\(L,R\\) to handle rectangular weights and exploits its linearity for exact federated averaging. On a square weight with \\(L=R^{\\top}=I\\), FLoRG's update is SingLoRA's without the ramp \\(u(t)\\)."},{"target":"fedit","type":"competes-with","note":"FedIT, which averages \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) separately, is the first of FLoRG's five baselines."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17095","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.17095","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10007750","https://openreview.net/forum?id=kntrZOm2AQ","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Chuiyang Meng","Ming Tang","Vincent W. S. Wong"],"bibkey":"meng2026florg","bibtex":"@inproceedings{meng2026florg,\n  title = {{FLoRG: Federated Fine-tuning with Low-rank Gram Matrices and Procrustes Alignment}},\n  author = {Chuiyang Meng and Ming Tang and Vincent W. S. Wong},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2602.17095},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.17095}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A single trained factor in \\(W_0+LA^\\top AR\\) with frozen semi-orthogonal frames. The federation averages the Gram core \\(A^\\top A\\) and re-factors it every round.","shape":"\\(W_0+L\\,\\mathrm{PSD}_{\\le r}(k)\\,R\\): a PSD determinantal cone between two frames, \\(d=rk-r(r-1)/2\\) (checked), closed under positive scalings only, so \\(-\\Delta W\\) is never reachable.","base_point":"\\(A=0\\) is the vertex, where \\(\\nabla_AF=A(H+H^\\top)\\) vanishes, so the method must start at some \\(A^1\\neq0\\): a pointing defect \\(LA^{1\\top}A^1R\\) (the initialisation is unspecified).","gauge":"\\(O(r)\\), \\(A\\mapsto OA\\), fibre \\(r(r-1)/2\\) (checked): the compact form of \\(GL_r\\), coded GL as for SingLoRA and PoLAR. The server fixes it by Procrustes against the previous round.","covariance":"The frames are frozen with orthogonal columns (random in the reading of the paper's ablation): covariance in law, \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\).","merge":"\\(LA^\\top AR\\) is one dense matrix.","rebasing":"\\(R_K=W_0+L\\,\\mathrm{PSD}_{\\le Kr}R\\), converging to the convex cone \\(W_0+L\\,\\mathrm{PSD}\\,R\\) rather than to a span; 'span' is the nearest value (as SingLoRA)."},"categorical_reading":"FLoRG is SingLoRA's Gram form placed between two frozen frames. Its trained quantity is the \\(O(r)\\)-invariant core \\(Q=A^\\top A\\), which is linear to average, so federated aggregation acts on a gauge-invariant statistic and is exact before re-factoring. The price is the image: a PSD cone, so \\(L^\\top\\Delta WR^\\top\\) can only move in positive semidefinite directions.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"A\\mapsto\\big(s\\,LA^\\top,\\ AR\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_r, pointed at h(A^1) (unpointed slice: both factors nonzero)."},{"target":"singlora","rule":"A3","h":"A\\mapsto\\sqrt{sr/\\alpha}\\,A^\\top","note":"Square weights with L = R^T = I only; otherwise FLoRG is a frame transport of SingLoRA, an automorphism rather than an arrow. Into SingLoRA after its ramp (u = 1), in the unpointed slice."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ffa-lora","test":"O1","reason":"FFA-LoRA's updates keep the fixed row space row A_0; FLoRG's row space row(AR) moves with A, so its image is not inside FFA-LoRA's."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loda-cl","name":"LoDA","full_name":"Low-rank Decomposition and Adaptation","paper_title":"Task-Driven Subspace Decomposition for Knowledge Sharing and Isolation in LoRA-based Continual Learning","authors":"He et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2603.00191","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00191","code_url":"https://github.com/HHHLF/LoDA_ICML2026","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=\\big(W^{t-1}+w_GB_GA_G+B_IA_I\\big)x;\\quad S^{i}=X^{i\\top}X^{i},\\ S^{1:t-1}=\\textstyle\\sum_{i<t}S^{i};\\quad A_G=U_G^{\\top},\\ U_G=\\text{top-}r\\ \\text{eigenvectors of } S^{1:t-1}+S^{t};\\quad A_I=\\mathrm{QR}(U_I^{\\top}),\\ U_I=\\text{top-}r\\ \\text{generalized eigenvectors of } S^{t}u=\\mu\\,S^{1:t-1}u\\ \\ (\\text{via } S^{1:t-1}=LL^{\\top},\\ U_I=L^{-\\top}\\,\\mathrm{top}_r\\,\\mathrm{eig}(L^{-1}S^{t}L^{-\\top}));\\quad A_G,A_I\\ \\text{frozen};\\ B_G,B_I\\ \\text{trained from }0\\text{ with GAO};\\quad \\text{after task } t:\\ W^{t}=W^{t-1}+w_GB_G\\Lambda_GA_G+B_IA_I,\\ \\ \\Lambda_G=\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma_j),\\ \\ \\gamma_j=\\frac{\\lambda\\,a_jS^{t}a_j^{\\top}}{a_j(\\lambda S^{t}+S^{1:t-1})a_j^{\\top}}\\in[0,1],\\ a_j=A_G^{(j)}","trainable_params":"2r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per task (}B_G,B_I\\text{), or } r\\,d_{out} \\text{ on task 1, where the isolated branch is disabled. } A_G,A_I \\text{ are designed, not trained, and both branches are merged after each task, so inference adds no parameters}","locus":"All attention blocks of an ImageNet-21K ViT-B/16 at rank 32; also CLIP and self-supervised ViT backbones","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Each task adds two rank-\\(r\\) additive branches whose down-projections are frozen and designed from second moments of the layer inputs. The shared branch spans the top-\\(r\\) eigenspace of the accumulated old-plus-new statistics, for knowledge sharing. The isolated branch spans the top-\\(r\\) generalized eigenvectors of the pencil \\((S^{t},S^{1:t-1})\\), the directions with the largest ratio of new-task to old-task projection energy, instead of an approximate null space of old tasks. Training only \\(B\\) is gradient descent restricted to \\(\\mathrm{row}(A)\\), with output change scaled by the projection energy \\(\\|Ax\\|^2\\) (Thm 3.1). The up-projections are trained with GAO, a two-subset look-ahead update that favors gradients agreeing across label-disjoint halves of a batch. Before merging, each rank-1 unit of the shared branch is shrunk by a closed-form ridge-type factor \\(\\gamma_j\\in[0,1]\\) that trades new-task fit against drift on old-task features. Both branches are then folded into the backbone.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After each task \\(w_GB_G\\Lambda_GA_G+B_IA_I\\) is added to the backbone and the LoRA matrices are discarded, so inference uses a single weight matrix and needs no task identity.","identity_at_init":"Exact for each task. \\(B_G=B_I=0\\) when a task starts, and on task 1 the isolated branch is disabled, so the layer equals \\(W^{t-1}\\). \\(A_G\\) and \\(A_I\\) are set beforehand from cached layer inputs.","key_idea":"With a frozen down-projection, LoRA learns only along directions the inputs excite (projection energy \\(\\|Ax\\|^2\\)). LoDA therefore builds one subspace that old and new tasks both excite, for transfer, and one that the new task excites far more than old tasks, for isolation. Null-space designs instead use old-task null spaces, which the new task may barely use.","results_note":"Without feature replay LoDA beats the strongest baseline, CoSO, by 0.80-1.70 points in final accuracy on its class-incremental benchmarks with a ViT-B/16, and the classifier-aligned LoDA+CA beats the replay-based MACIL by 0.15-5.11 points.","relations":[{"target":"inflora","type":"competes-with","note":"InfLoRA's frozen down-projection spans new-task directions inside the approximate null space of old tasks. LoDA replaces it with a generalized-eigenvector (energy-ratio) subspace, adds a shared branch, and beats InfLoRA in the paper's subspace-isolation comparison (Table 4)."},{"target":"o-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited in related work but not a baseline (O-LoRA is a language-model method). O-LoRA uses soft orthogonality between task subspaces; LoDA uses hard subspaces designed from data."},{"target":"sd-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-based class-incremental baseline."},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited by LoDA; the link is structural. On each task the two branches together form one LoRA-FA-style update \\([w_GB_G,\\ B_I][A_G;A_I]\\), whose frozen rank-\\(2r\\) down-projection is designed from data rather than drawn at random."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00191","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.00191","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63718","https://openreview.net/forum?id=UJJfkcJ7K9","https://github.com/HHHLF/LoDA_ICML2026","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-2"],"authors_full":["Lingfeng He","De Cheng","Huaijie Wang","Xi Yang","Nannan Wang","Xinbo Gao"],"bibkey":"he2026task","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2026task,\n  title = {{Task-Driven Subspace Decomposition for Knowledge Sharing and Isolation in LoRA-based Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Lingfeng He and De Cheng and Huaijie Wang and Xi Yang and Nannan Wang and Xinbo Gao},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2603.00191},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00191}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per task, two LoRA branches with frozen data-designed down-projections and trained up-projections (GAO look-ahead updates), shrunk and merged after the task.","shape":"\\(W^{t-1}+X_GA_G+X_IA_I=W^{t-1}+\\mathbb R^{d_{out}}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}[A_G;A_I]\\): linear, of dimension \\(2rd_{out}\\) (\\(rd_{out}\\) on task 1).","base_point":"\\(B_G=B_I=0\\) on a linear method: regular.","gauge":"None for independent rows of \\([A_G;A_I]\\).","covariance":"\\(A_G\\) is a top eigenspace of an input second moment (\\(CO(n)\\)). The generalised eigenspace of the pencil \\((S^t,S^{1:t-1})\\) is even \\(GL(n)\\)-covariant, and the QR step keeps its row space. The output side is free: GL×CO, as InfLoRA.","merge":"\\(w_GB_G\\Lambda_GA_G+B_IA_I\\) is folded into the backbone after each task.","rebasing":"One stage per task with designed frames: InfLoRA's rebasing scheme."},"categorical_reading":"LoDA is InfLoRA's scheme with a rank-\\(2r\\) frozen frame per task, split into a shared part (top eigenspace of the old-plus-new statistics) and an isolated part (top generalised eigenvectors of new against old statistics). Each stage is LoRA-FA\\(([A_G;A_I])\\). The ridge-type shrink \\(\\Lambda_G\\) and the GAO updates choose a point of that linear stage and leave its image unchanged.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"(B_G,B_I)\\mapsto[\\,w_GB_G,\\ B_I\\,]","note":"Per task, into LoRA-FA([A_G; A_I]) over W^{t-1}, pointed at 0. The pre-merge shrink replaces w_G B_G by w_G B_G Lambda_G, another point of the same stage."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B_G,B_I)\\mapsto\\big([\\,w_GB_G,\\ B_I\\,],\\ [A_G;A_I]\\big)","note":"Per task, into LoRA_{2r} pointed at (0, [A_G; A_I])."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"inflora","test":"O1","reason":"InfLoRA's frame lies in an approximate null space of old-task inputs; LoDA's isolated frame maximises a new-to-old energy ratio and its shared frame is excited by old tasks. For generic data the stage images are not nested."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lragent","name":"LRAgent","full_name":"LRAgent: KV cache sharing for multi-LoRA agents (BaseShared / BaseLRShared with Flash-LoRA-Attention)","paper_title":"LRAgent: Efficient KV Cache Sharing for Multi-LoRA LLM Agents","authors":"Jeon et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2602.01053","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01053","code_url":"https://github.com/hjeon2k/LRAgent","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_v^{(i)}=W_0+B_iA_i,\\ \\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ (i=1,\\dots,N),\\ \\ A_i=A\\ \\text{for all }i\\ \\text{(shared-}A\\text{; used in the main experiments and required by BaseLRShared)};\\ \\ \\text{value cache for context token }t:\\ v^{base}_t=W_0x_t\\ \\text{(computed once by the agent that first processes }t\\text{ and reused by every agent; the key cache is shared the same way)};\\ \\ \\text{LR cache: }c_t=Ax_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r}\\ \\text{(one shared cache, BaseLRShared) or }c^{(i)}_t=A_ix^{(i)}_t\\ \\text{(per agent, recomputed from agent }i\\text{'s own hidden states over context it has not yet processed, BaseShared)};\\ \\ o^{(i)}=\\sum_t p_t\\big(v^{base}_t+B_ic_t\\big)=\\sum_t p_t\\,v^{base}_t+B_i\\Big(\\sum_t p_t\\,c_t\\Big)\\ \\text{(Flash-LoRA-Attention evaluates the right-hand form)};\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ A,\\{B_i\\}\\ \\text{trained as LoRA on }W_q,W_v\\ (r=8)","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{in}+N\\,r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix for }N\\text{ agents with shared }A\\ \\ (N\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ for independent multi-LoRA})","locus":"LoRA (\\(r=8\\)) on the query and value projections of every attention layer, one up-projection per agent role (plan, action, reflect) on a shared down-projection; cache sharing acts on the value cache, and the key cache is shared outright","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Serving-time decomposition of each agent's value cache into a shared full-width part \\(W_0x_t\\) and an \\(r\\)-dimensional part \\(Ax_t\\) that is expanded by the agent's own \\(B_i\\). With a shared \\(A\\) the agents differ only in the output map \\(B_i\\) (HydraLoRA's asymmetric layout with a fixed one-hot assignment of heads to agent roles), so the \\(r\\)-dimensional cache is common to all agents as well. Associativity \\(P(V_{base}+V_{lr}B_i^{\\top})=PV_{base}+(PV_{lr})B_i^{\\top}\\) moves the expansion after the attention weighting, so the expansion cost per decoding step is \\(\\Theta(Lr+r\\,d_{out})\\) instead of \\(\\Theta(L\\,r\\,d_{out})\\) for context length \\(L\\). In BaseShared each agent still rebuilds its own LR cache over context it has not processed (the paper's 'LR prefill'), so compute stays \\(O(NL^2d_{model})\\) and only memory falls to about \\(1/N\\); BaseLRShared also skips that prefill. Reusing a base cache that another agent computed is an approximation, because \\(x_t\\) depends on that agent's adapters.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For one agent alone, \\(W_0+B_iA\\) folds into a plain matrix, but the cache-sharing scheme needs the unmerged form: it stores \\(W_0x_t\\) and \\(Ax_t\\) separately so that every agent can reuse them.","identity_at_init":"The paper does not state the LoRA initialization (training follows AutoAct). With the usual \\(B_i=0\\), every agent starts at \\(W_0\\); all agents then coincide, so the shared caches are exact at that point. Once the \\(B_i\\) differ, cache sharing is approximate: agents reuse base caches (and, in BaseLRShared, LR caches) built from other agents' hidden states.","key_idea":"On a shared context, agents' base value caches \\(W_0x_t\\) are nearly identical (average cosine similarity 0.95 to 0.97 across agent pairs) while their adapter outputs are nearly uncorrelated (0.02 to 0.05). LRAgent therefore shares the base cache, stores the adapter part in its native rank-\\(r\\) form \\(Ax_t\\), shares that too once all agents use one \\(A\\), and applies \\(B_i\\) only after attention has summed over tokens.","results_note":"On multi-hop HotpotQA and ScienceQA agents built on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct and Ministral-8B-Instruct, BaseLRShared with Flash-LoRA-Attention reaches up to 2.46x the throughput and up to 4.44x lower time-to-first-token than non-shared caching (measured on an emulated trace of 2k to 66k tokens), with an average accuracy drop of at most 1.5 points (at most 0.7 points for BaseShared).","relations":[{"target":"hydralora","type":"builds-on","note":"Adopts HydraLoRA's shared-\\(A\\), per-head-\\(B\\) layout (cited as Tian et al. 2024, with MTL-LoRA), replacing the token-wise router by a fixed agent-to-head assignment. Sharing \\(A\\) is what lets the low-rank cache \\(Ax\\) be shared across agents (BaseLRShared)."},{"target":"s-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both cut the cost of serving many LoRAs on one backbone by keeping the shared base term \\(W_0x\\) separate from the per-adapter low-rank terms: S-LoRA for batching and adapter memory, LRAgent for the KV cache of agents that share a context. The paper does not cite S-LoRA (its multi-LoRA serving reference is Xia et al. 2024, LoRA-Inlaid), so no lineage is claimed."},{"target":"punica","type":"competes-with","note":"Both cut the cost of running many LoRAs on one backbone: Punica batches the low-rank matmuls across adapters, while LRAgent removes redundant prefill and KV memory across adapters that share a context. The paper does not cite Punica."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01053","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.01053 (v2, Sections 2-4, Appendix C)","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json (event 61572, Poster, Accept (regular))","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/61572","https://openreview.net/forum?id=ooRukY3zjQ","https://github.com/hjeon2k/LRAgent","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["Hyesung Jeon","Hyeongju Ha","Jae-Joon Kim"],"bibkey":"jeon2026lragent","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jeon2026lragent,\n  title = {{LRAgent: Efficient KV Cache Sharing for Multi-LoRA LLM Agents}},\n  author = {Hyesung Jeon and Hyeongju Ha and Jae-Joon Kim},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.01053},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.01053}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over agent roles, \\(i\\mapsto(B_i,A)\\), with a shared down-projection (HydraLoRA's layout with a fixed one-hot head assignment), served with shared base and low-rank value caches.","shape":"Each agent's fibre \\(W_0+B_iA\\) is a point of LoRA\\(_r\\); for fixed \\(A\\) all fibres lie in the one-sided slice \\(\\{YA\\}\\).","base_point":"The initialisation is not stated (AutoAct setup). With \\(B_i=0\\) every agent sits at the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex and the shared caches are exact.","gauge":"One \\(GL_r\\) on the shared rank wire, \\((A,B_i)\\mapsto(gA,B_ig^{-1})\\) for all agents at once. The low-rank cache \\(Ax_t\\) transforms by \\(g\\) and \\(B_i\\), applied after attention, by \\(g^{-1}\\), so the serving scheme is gauge-safe.","covariance":"Each fibre is basis-free.","merge":"Each fibre fuses, but the cache scheme needs \\(W_0x_t\\) and \\(Ax_t\\) separately: unmerged by design, as S-LoRA.","rebasing":"Undefined for a serving family."},"categorical_reading":"LRAgent is a tenant-type family whose tenants are agent roles sharing one context. Because the fibres share \\(A\\), associativity \\(P(V_{\\rm base}+V_{\\rm lr}B_i^\\top)=PV_{\\rm base}+(PV_{\\rm lr})B_i^\\top\\) lets one rank-\\(r\\) cache serve every agent. Reusing caches computed from another agent's hidden states is an approximation that leaves the family: once the \\(B_i\\) differ, the served function is no longer \\(\\Phi_f\\) of any single fibre.","entry_type":"software","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_i(A,B_1,\\dots,B_N)=(B_i,\\ A)","note":"Fibrewise over agents, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0)."},{"target":"s-lora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,(B_i)_i)\\mapsto\\big((B_i,A)\\big)_i","note":"Into S-LoRA's family over tenants with tied A_u = A. Exact only without cross-agent cache reuse, which changes the realised function."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hydralora","test":"O1","reason":"The same context token is served with a different head for each agent role, while HydraLoRA's softmax router is a function of the token alone and never exactly one-hot: tenant indexing is not token routing."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"geora","name":"GeoRA","full_name":"Geometry-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation for RLVR","paper_title":"GeoRA: Geometry-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation for RLVR","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ACL 2026","arxiv":"2601.09361","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09361","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_{res}\\,x+s\\,BA\\,x,\\ \\ s=\\alpha/r;\\ \\ W_{Geo}=W_0\\odot(M_{Spec}\\lor M_{Euc}),\\ \\ (M_{Euc})_{ij}=\\mathbb{I}\\big(|(W_0)_{ij}|\\le\\tau_\\rho(|W_0|)\\big),\\ \\ (M_{Spec})_{ij}=\\mathbb{I}\\big(|(\\hat W_r)_{ij}|\\le\\tau_\\rho(|\\hat W_r|)\\big),\\ \\hat W_r=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0),\\ \\tau_\\rho=\\rho\\text{-quantile};\\ \\ W_{Geo}=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ B_0=U_{:,1:r}\\Sigma_{1:r,1:r}^{1/2},\\ A_0=\\Sigma_{1:r,1:r}^{1/2}V_{:,1:r}^{\\top};\\ \\ W_{res}=W_0-s\\,B_0A_0\\ \\text{(frozen)};\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{trained from }(B_0,A_0)\\ \\text{with an RLVR objective (GRPO)}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all linear layers ('all-linear'), \\(r=16\\), \\(\\alpha=32\\), \\(\\rho=0.2\\) in the experiments","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"PiSSA's residual-anchored rank-\\(r\\) reparametrization (base point moved to a frozen \\(W_{res}\\), \\(\\Delta W\\) factors through \\(r\\) dimensions), with the starting factors taken from the top-\\(r\\) SVD of a sparse masked copy \\(W_{Geo}\\) of \\(W_0\\) instead of \\(W_0\\) itself. The mask keeps the entries that are small either in \\(W_0\\) or in its rank-\\(r\\) principal reconstruction \\(\\hat W_r\\). With \\(\\rho=0.2\\) each mask keeps 20% of entries and they overlap on 4.55% (Qwen3-8B), so \\(W_{Geo}\\) keeps about 35% of the entries. The construction is data-free: no RL gradient or update enters the initialization.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_{res}+s\\,BA\\) is a plain matrix. As a delta on the original weights the update is \\(s(BA-B_0A_0)\\), as for PiSSA.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W_{res}x+s\\,B_0A_0x=W_0x\\) by construction of \\(W_{res}\\).","key_idea":"RLVR updates avoid the principal and large-magnitude weights. GeoRA builds a data-free proxy for that region by masking \\(W_0\\) to its low-magnitude entries, in weight space and in the rank-\\(r\\) principal reconstruction, and starts a dense rank-\\(r\\) adapter on the top-\\(r\\) SVD of the masked matrix, with the remainder frozen as an anchor. RLVR then trains in this region at the hardware cost of dense LoRA, not sparse fine-tuning.","results_note":"With GRPO on DeepMath-103K and \\(r=16\\), Qwen3-8B-Base trained with GeoRA scores 23.75 on AIME24, against 19.58 for LoRA, 22.50 for PiSSA, 20.42 for MiLoRA and 23.33 for full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"builds-on","note":"Same frozen-residual parametrization \\(W=W_{res}+sBA\\) with an SVD start; the SVD is of the masked \\(W_{Geo}\\), not of \\(W_0\\). Under RLVR at an aggressive learning rate (\\(5\\times10^{-5}\\), Qwen3-4B), PiSSA's reward collapses late in training while GeoRA's does not."},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"MiLoRA starts on \\(W_0\\)'s minor singular directions. GeoRA takes the top directions of a low-magnitude masked copy of \\(W_0\\), and its ablation finds tail-\\(r\\) initialization worst."},{"target":"lift","type":"dual-to","note":"LIFT tunes the largest-magnitude entries of the rank-\\(r\\) truncation \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0)\\) ('principal weights'). GeoRA's spectral mask keeps the smallest \\(\\rho\\)-fraction of the same truncation, unions it with \\(W_0\\)'s smallest entries, and compresses the result into a dense rank-\\(r\\) adapter. GeoRA cites LIFT."},{"target":"path-not-taken-rlvr","type":"builds-on","note":"Motivated by the finding that RLVR updates stay off the principal directions, in low-magnitude, low-curvature regions; GeoRA's masks are a weight-only proxy for that region."},{"target":"rl-finetunes-small-subnetworks","type":"builds-on","note":"RL fine-tuning touches a small subnetwork. GeoRA replaces sparse fine-tuning of such a subnetwork, which modern hardware runs poorly, with a dense low-rank adapter."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09361","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.09361 (v4, Sections 3-5, Appendices A-D)","https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1110/","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2601.09361?fields=title,year,venue,citationCount,externalIds","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["Jiaying Zhang","Lei Shi","Jiguo Li","Jun Xu","Jiuchong Gao","Jinghua Hao","Renqing He"],"bibkey":"zhang2026geora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026geora,\n  title = {{GeoRA: Geometry-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation for RLVR}},\n  author = {Jiaying Zhang and Lei Shi and Jiguo Li and Jun Xu and Jiuchong Gao and Jinghua Hao and Renqing He},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2601.09361},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09361}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"PiSSA's residual-anchored LoRA, with the starting factors taken from the top-\\(r\\) SVD of a magnitude-masked copy \\(W_{\\rm Geo}=W_0\\odot(M_{\\rm Spec}\\lor M_{\\rm Euc})\\).","shape":"\\((W_0-sB_0A_0)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) with \\(sB_0A_0=s[W_{\\rm Geo}]_r\\): the cone translated to a vertex other than PiSSA's.","base_point":"S stratum (\\(B_0A_0\\) of rank \\(r\\)): \\(\\theta_0\\) is smooth, \\(\\dim S_1=r(m+n-r)\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"Entrywise magnitude quantiles of \\(W_0\\) and of \\([W_0]_r\\) commute with signed permutations and homotheties, but not with non-uniform diagonal scalings or rotations. Nearest key MonxMon (as for TIES's \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_N\\)).","merge":"\\(W_{\\rm res}+sBA\\) is a plain matrix.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as PiSSA."},"categorical_reading":"GeoRA is another point of the Type II stratum: \\(P=s[W_{\\rm Geo}]_r\\), the principal part of the low-magnitude region of \\(W_0\\), chosen without data as a proxy for where RLVR updates go. The masks fix a coordinate basis, so unlike PiSSA's its pointing is not rotation-natural. As with every split, its updates measured from \\(\\theta_0\\) reach rank \\(2r\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ -B_0],\\ [A;\\ A_0]\\big)","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0 (same scale), pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) with product 0, as for PiSSA.","target_instance":"LoRA_{2r} at the degenerate pointing"}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Different points of the S stratum (masked-SVD P against weight-SVD P) give translated cones with different vertices, hence incomparable images (Thm II.2(a),(d))."},{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"At equal rank, a Type II smooth point against LoRA's Type I apex.","target_instance":"LoRA_r at the T_A apex"}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"matryoshka-lora","name":"MatryoshkaLoRA","full_name":"MatryoshkaLoRA (nested-rank LoRA trained through a fixed diagonal rank weighting)","paper_title":"MatryoshkaLoRA: Learning Accurate Hierarchical Low-Rank Representations for LLM Fine-Tuning","authors":"Modoranu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2605.07850","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07850","code_url":"https://github.com/IST-DASLab/MatryoshkaLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{train: }h=\\big(W_0+B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\,A\\big)x,\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times R},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{R\\times d_{in}},\\ \\ p=\\sum_{r\\in S}s_r\\,\\mathbf{1}_{[1,r]}\\ (\\mathbf{1}_{[1,r]}\\in\\{0,1\\}^R\\text{ has ones in its first }r\\text{ entries}),\\ \\text{i.e. }p_j=\\sum_{r\\in S,\\ r\\ge j}s_r,\\ \\ \\text{so }B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\,A=\\sum_{r\\in S}s_r\\,B_{:,1:r}A_{1:r,:};\\ \\ S=\\{1,2,4,\\dots,R\\},\\ s_r\\in\\{1,\\,1/r,\\,1/\\sqrt{r}\\};\\ \\ \\text{deploy at any }k\\in S:\\ h=\\big(W_0+s_k\\,B_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:}\\big)x;\\ \\ W_0,\\ p\\ \\text{frozen (one }p\\text{ for all layers)};\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained}\\ (A\\ \\text{Kaiming},\\ B=0)","trainable_params":"R(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same placement as LoRA; the released run scripts wrap all seven linear projections of every Llama block (query, key, value, output, gate, up and down) of Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct (\\(R=32\\)) and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (\\(R=256\\))","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A rank-\\(R\\) LoRA with a frozen, non-increasing diagonal \\(\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\) inserted between the factors, so the training update is the sum of all nested prefix adapters \\(\\sum_{r\\in S}s_rB_{:,1:r}A_{1:r,:}\\). The diagonal multiplies the gradient of the \\(j\\)-th column of \\(B\\) and row of \\(A\\) by \\(p_j\\), so leading rank-1 components get the most signal. The train-time adapter, weighted by \\(p\\), is never deployed: inference drops \\(p\\) and uses a single prefix \\(s_kB_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:}\\). The paper (Appendix B) justifies this as a first-order surrogate of the multi-rank objective that DyLoRA samples one rank at a time: for convex weights \\(\\lambda_r\\), \\(\\sum_r\\lambda_r\\ell(W_0+\\Delta_r)=\\ell\\big(W_0+\\sum_r\\lambda_r\\Delta_r\\big)+O\\big(\\sum_r\\lambda_r\\|\\Delta_r\\|_F^2\\big)\\) with \\(\\Delta_r=s_rB_{:,1:r}A_{1:r,:}\\), which gives \\(p=\\sum_r\\lambda_rs_r\\mathbf{1}_{[1,r]}\\). The main-text \\(p\\) takes every \\(\\lambda_r=1\\), which is \\(|S|\\) times the uniform-weight surrogate.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After choosing the deployment rank \\(k\\), \\(s_kB_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:}\\) is a plain rank-\\(k\\) matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Yes: the released code initializes \\(B=0\\) (with \\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform), so \\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\,A=0\\) and every prefix adapter is zero.","key_idea":"Train every nested rank at once in a single forward pass: a fixed diagonal \\(p_j=\\sum_{r\\in S,\\,r\\ge j}s_r\\) between \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) turns the sum of all prefix adapters into one rank-\\(R\\) product. Every prefix then receives gradient at every step, unlike DyLoRA, whose sampled rank \\(b\\) gives components beyond \\(b\\) no gradient at that step. LoRA is \\(p=s_R\\mathbf{1}\\), and DyLoRA is a per-step random truncation \\(p=s_b\\mathbf{1}_{[1,b]}\\).","results_note":"Fine-tuning Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct on GSM8K (8-shot) with maximum rank \\(R=32\\), MatryoshkaLoRA reaches an area under the rank-accuracy curve (AURAC) of 38.4%, against 34.5% for LoRA and 34.9% for DyLoRA, and its sub-rank \\(r=1\\) (35.8%) already beats every sub-rank of either baseline. The pre-trained model scores 34.7%, so LoRA and DyLoRA barely move it in this setting.","relations":[{"target":"dylora","type":"generalizes","note":"In the paper's framework DyLoRA is the per-step random choice \\(p=s_b\\mathbf{1}_{[1,b]}\\). MatryoshkaLoRA replaces the sampled rank with the deterministic \\(p=\\sum_r s_r\\mathbf{1}_{[1,r]}\\), a first-order surrogate of the same multi-rank objective, so every prefix gets gradient at every step."},{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"\\(p=s_R\\mathbf{1}_R\\) recovers standard rank-\\(R\\) LoRA."},{"target":"sora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same \\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g)\\,A\\) shape. SoRA learns a sparse gate \\(g\\) to prune ranks; MatryoshkaLoRA freezes a non-increasing \\(g=p\\) to order the rank-1 components so that every prefix is usable. The paper does not cite SoRA."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07850","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.07850 (v1, Sections 2-3, Appendices A-B)","https://github.com/IST-DASLab/MatryoshkaLoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IST-DASLab/MatryoshkaLoRA/main/adapters/matryoshka.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IST-DASLab/MatryoshkaLoRA/main/adapters/lora.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IST-DASLab/MatryoshkaLoRA/main/scripts/bash_run.sh","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["Ionut-Vlad Modoranu","Mher Safaryan","Dan Alistarh"],"bibkey":"modoranu2026matryoshkalora","bibtex":"@article{modoranu2026matryoshkalora,\n  title = {{MatryoshkaLoRA: Learning Accurate Hierarchical Low-Rank Representations for LLM Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Ionut-Vlad Modoranu and Mher Safaryan and Dan Alistarh},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2605.07850},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.07850},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07850}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Train with a frozen non-increasing \\(\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\) between the factors; deploy a prefix \\(s_kB_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:}\\), \\(k\\in S=\\{1,2,4,\\dots,R\\}\\).","shape":"A filtration of cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le k}\\), \\(k\\in S\\), with the training map \\(W_0+B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(p)A\\) on the top one.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) (released code): the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex at every level.","gauge":"Only \\(g\\) that preserve every deployed prefix and commute with \\(\\mathrm{diag}(p)\\) survive: block-diagonal over the dyadic blocks \\((r_{i-1},r_i]\\) of \\(S\\), on which \\(p\\) is constant. So \\(\\prod_iGL_{r_i-r_{i-1}}\\) (\\(1+1+4\\) for \\(R=4\\), checked): between DyLoRA's torus and LoRA's \\(GL_R\\), coded GL.","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cones.","merge":"The deployed prefix is a plain rank-\\(k\\) matrix.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\) at the deployed rank."},"categorical_reading":"MatryoshkaLoRA trains DyLoRA's filtration in one pass. The frozen gate \\(p=\\sum_{r\\in S}s_r\\mathbf 1_{[1,r]}\\) makes the training update the sum of all nested prefix adapters, a first-order surrogate of DyLoRA's sampled-rank objective. Coarsening DyLoRA's truncation levels to the dyadic set \\(S\\) enlarges the training gauge from a torus to a product of \\(GL\\)'s on the dyadic blocks.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"sora","rule":"A2","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(B,\\ p,\\ A)","note":"Freezing SoRA's gate at p (and dropping its l1 penalty), SoRA re-pointed at (0, p, A_0): the training-time map."},{"target":"dylora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Instance s_r = c/r only (DyLoRA's alpha = c): the same underlying LoRA_R pointed at (0, A_0), with each deployed prefix s_kB_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:} equal to DyLoRA's level k. For the released s_r = 1/sqrt(r) (and for s_r = 1) no single h exists: matching level k needs B_{:,j} -> (k s_k/alpha) B_{:,j} for every j <= k, and k s_k is not constant over S (1, 1.414, 2 for S = {1,2,4}), so the comparison holds only level by level, through the LoRA_k arrow. The objectives differ in any case: a deterministic p against a sampled b."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big(s_kB_{:,1:k},\\ A_{1:k,:}\\big)","note":"Deployed at rank k, into LoRA_k pointed at (0, A_{0,1:k})."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loco-orthogonal","name":"LoCO","full_name":"Low-rank Compositional Orthogonal Fine-tuning","paper_title":"LoCO: Low-rank Compositional Rotation Fine-tuning","authors":"Nguyen et al.","year":2026,"venue":"IJCAI 2026","arxiv":"2605.15916","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15916","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0\\tilde R\\,x,\\ \\ \\tilde R=I_{d_{in}}+2\\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\\big(I_{2r}-Y_i^{\\top}X_i\\big)^{-1}Y_i^{\\top},\\ \\ X_i=[U_i\\mid -V_i],\\ Y_i=[V_i\\mid U_i],\\ U_i,V_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r};\\ \\ \\text{each summand is }R_i-I\\text{ for the Cayley rotation }R_i=(I-\\mathcal{A}_i)^{-1}(I+\\mathcal{A}_i),\\ \\mathcal{A}_i=U_iV_i^{\\top}-V_iU_i^{\\top}\\in\\mathfrak{so}(d_{in})\\ \\text{(Woodbury form)},\\ \\text{and }\\tilde R\\text{ is the first-order (parallel) approximation of }R_1R_2\\cdots R_n;\\ \\ \\text{inference temperature }t:\\ \\mathcal{A}_i\\mapsto t\\,\\mathcal{A}_i\\ (t=1\\text{ in training});\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\{U_i,V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2\\,n\\,r\\,d_{in}","locus":"input side of the adapted linear layers: all linear modules of DeBERTa-V3-base (GLUE), \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) of LLaMA-2-7B (math), the FLUX.1 diffusion transformer (OminiControl setup) and ViT-B/16 (VTAB-1k)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"Right action on \\(W_0\\) of rotations generated by low-rank skew-symmetric matrices. One component is a Cayley rotation that maps the learned subspace \\(\\mathrm{span}(U_i,V_i)\\) (dimension at most \\(2r\\)) into itself and fixes its orthogonal complement pointwise, computed through a \\(2r\\times 2r\\) inverse in \\(O(d_{in}r^2+r^3)\\). Several components are combined by summing their deviations from \\(I\\) rather than multiplying them. Equivalently, \\(\\tilde R\\) is the Cayley formula for \\(\\sum_i\\mathcal{A}_i\\) with the off-diagonal blocks \\(Y_i^{\\top}X_j\\) (\\(i\\ne j\\)) of the \\(2nr\\times 2nr\\) core dropped. \\(\\tilde R\\) is exactly orthogonal only for \\(n=1\\); in general \\(\\|I-\\tilde R^{\\top}\\tilde R\\|_F\\le n(n-1)\\gamma^2\\) when \\(\\|R_i-I\\|_F\\le\\gamma\\). The induced additive update \\(W_0(\\tilde R-I)\\) has rank at most \\(2nr\\). The LLaMA-2-7B math result uses \\(n=1\\), so it exercises the exact Cayley rotation, not the parallel approximation.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"For a fixed temperature, \\(W_0\\tilde R\\) is a dense \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) matrix and replaces \\(W_0\\) at no inference cost.","identity_at_init":"Approximate. The paper only says parameters start near zero, so \\(\\tilde R=I+O(\\|U_i\\|\\|V_i\\|)\\); \\(\\tilde R=I\\) holds exactly if, for example, every \\(V_i=0\\). At inference \\(t=0\\) recovers \\(W_0\\) exactly.","key_idea":"The tangent space \\(\\mathfrak{so}(d)\\) of the rotation group admits a low-rank parametrization \\(UV^{\\top}-VU^{\\top}\\). Woodbury then makes its Cayley rotation cost \\(O(dr^2)\\) instead of \\(O(d^3)\\), and a first-order sum of several such rotations gives a richer, nearly orthogonal transform that can be computed in parallel. Scaling the generator by a temperature \\(t\\) at test time interpolates the adaptation strength.","results_note":"Fine-tuning \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) of LLaMA-2-7B on MetaMathQA-40K with 0.12% trainable parameters and a single component (\\(n=1\\), \\(r=16\\)), LoCO reaches 50.19 on GSM8K and 8.40 on MATH, against 49.35 and 8.39 for OFT, 50.04 and 7.45 for HRA (Table 2; the paper's text quotes 49.65 for HRA) and 45.58 and 6.85 for BOFT.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"Same Cayley-parametrized right multiplication \\(W_0R\\). The skew generator is low-rank (\\(UV^{\\top}-VU^{\\top}\\)) rather than block-diagonal, and its inverse is computed through Woodbury."},{"target":"hra","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are input-side orthogonal transforms equal to \\(I\\) plus a low-rank term (rank at most \\(r\\) for HRA, at most \\(2nr\\) for LoCO). HRA composes \\(r\\) reflections sequentially; LoCO sums \\(n\\) Cayley rotations in parallel."},{"target":"boft","type":"competes-with","note":"Both compose several orthogonal factors into a dense rotation: BOFT multiplies butterfly factors exactly and in sequence, while LoCO sums low-rank Cayley factors to first order."},{"target":"goft","type":"competes-with","note":"A LoCO component with \\(r=1\\), \\(U=a\\,e_i\\) and \\(V=e_j\\) is the Givens rotation by angle \\(2\\arctan a\\) in plane \\((i,j)\\). LoCO learns the planes and combines them additively rather than as an exact product."},{"target":"loft-orthogonal","type":"competes-with","note":"LOFT fixes an \\(r\\)-dimensional input support and trains \\(T\\in SO(r)\\) on it. A single LoCO component instead rotates a learned subspace \\(\\mathrm{span}(U,V)\\) of dimension at most \\(2r\\)."},{"target":"oftv2","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are input-centric and never form the \\(d\\times d\\) rotation. OFTv2 keeps a block-diagonal generator with a Neumann-series inverse; LoCO uses a low-rank generator with an exact Woodbury inverse."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15916","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.15916 (v1, Section 3, Section 4.1.2, Appendix A.2)","https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2026/","https://2026.ijcai.org/accepted-papers/","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["An Nguyen","Jaesik Choi","Anh Tong"],"bibkey":"nguyen2026loco","bibtex":"@inproceedings{nguyen2026loco,\n  title = {{LoCO: Low-rank Compositional Rotation Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {An Nguyen and Jaesik Choi and Anh Tong},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI)},\n  eprint = {2605.15916},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.15916}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Meet","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Right multiplication \\(W_0\\tilde R\\). Read with one component (\\(n=1\\)), the instance behind the LLaMA results, where \\(\\tilde R=C(UV^\\top-VU^\\top)\\) is an exact Cayley rotation computed by Woodbury.","shape":"\\(\\mathrm{Im}=W_0\\cdot\\{R\\in SO(d_{in}):\\operatorname{rank}(R-I)\\le2r,\\ -1\\notin\\mathrm{spec}\\,R\\}\\), for injective \\(W_0\\) an open dense part of the meet \\((W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le2r})\\cap W_0SO(d_{in})\\). Its dimension is \\(2rd_{in}-2r^2-r\\) (checked), that of HRA\\(_{2r}\\)'s meet. For \\(n>1\\) the parallel sum is only approximately orthogonal (\\(\\|I-\\tilde R^\\top\\tilde R\\|_F\\le n(n-1)\\gamma^2\\)) and leaves the meet.","base_point":"The paper starts near zero. At the exactly pointed \\(V=0\\), \\(S_1=W_0\\{U\\delta V^\\top-\\delta VU^\\top\\}\\) has dimension \\(rd_{in}-r(r+1)/2<d\\) (checked): an apex, the analogue of HRA's paired initialisation (\\(S_1=0\\) at \\(U=V=0\\)).","gauge":"\\(UV^\\top-VU^\\top=ZJZ^\\top\\) with \\(Z=[U\\ V]\\), so the generic fibre is the linear action \\(Z\\mapsto ZM\\), \\(M\\in Sp(2r,\\mathbb R)\\), of dimension \\(r(2r+1)\\) (checked). Nearest key GL (a linear group on the \\(2r\\)-dimensional internal wire); HRA's fibre is non-linear.","covariance":"A right orthogonal action built with the Euclidean form of the input side: \\(GL\\times CO(d_{in})\\), as HRA.","merge":"For a fixed temperature, \\(W_0\\tilde R\\) is one dense matrix.","rebasing":"The image contains \\(W_0\\cdot SO(V)\\) near \\(e\\) for every \\(2r\\)-plane \\(V\\), and these generate \\(SO(d_{in})\\). So re-merging reaches \\(W_0SO(d_{in})\\), in \\(\\lceil d_{in}/2r\\rceil\\) cycles (Thm V.3(b), as HRA)."},"categorical_reading":"One LoCO component is the Cayley chart of HRA's meet: rotations of a learned \\(2r\\)-plane, \\((W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le2r})\\cap W_0\\cdot SO(d_{in})\\) minus the eigenvalue \\(-1\\) locus, with the same image germ as HRA\\(_{2r}\\). HRA parametrises the meet by reflections, with a non-linear fibre. LoCO's low-rank skew generator \\(ZJZ^\\top\\) instead has the linear symplectic gauge \\(Sp(2r)\\). Its multi-component sum and test-time temperature leave the orbit; they are approximations.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V)\\mapsto Q=-(UV^\\top-VU^\\top)","note":"n = 1, into exact full-block Cayley OFT (block size d_in), with OFT written as W_0 C(Q)^T and C(Q) = (I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1}, so that C(Q)^T = C(-Q). Under HF's exact R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, h = +(UV^T - VU^T). Pointed: V = 0 goes to Q = 0. Checked numerically."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V)\\mapsto\\big(2W_0X(I_{2r}-Y^\\top X)^{-1},\\ Y^\\top\\big),\\ \\ X=[U\\,|\\,-V],\\ Y=[V\\,|\\,U]","note":"Woodbury form of W_0(C(A) - I): into LoRA_{2r} (LoRA_{2nr} for n components, by concatenation). Pointed at h(U_0, 0) = (2W_0[U_0, 0], [0; U_0^T]), whose product is 0 with both factors nonzero, outside the three strata (as HRA's arrow). Checked numerically."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oftv2","test":"O1","reason":"LoCO (n = 1) is exactly orthogonal and keeps K_out; OFTv2's Cayley-Neumann images leave the K_out level set, so near theta_0 the two images meet only at theta_0."},{"target":"loft-orthogonal","test":"O1","reason":"LOFT fixes its support V, so its image is the leaf W_0 . SO(V); LoCO rotates learned 2r-planes, which leave any fixed V with dim V < d_in."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Meet"},{"id":"compress-then-merge","name":"Compress-then-Merge (CtM)","full_name":"Compress-then-Merge: merging several LoRAs into one low-rank LoRA inside learned shared subspaces","paper_title":"Compress then Merge: From Multiple LoRAs into One Low-Rank Adapter","authors":"He et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2606.03723","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03723","code_url":"https://github.com/ZhengbaoHe/compress-then-merge","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W^{(t)}=B^{(t)}A^{(t)}\\ (t=1,\\dots,T);\\ \\ \\Delta W^{(t)}_{tgt}=\\lambda^{(t)}\\frac{\\Delta W^{(t)}}{\\|\\Delta W^{(t)}\\|_F},\\ \\ \\lambda^{(t)}=\\beta\\|\\Delta W^{(t)}\\|_F+(1-\\beta)\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{s=1}^{T}\\|\\Delta W^{(s)}\\|_F;\\ \\ (U,V)=\\arg\\min_{U^{\\top}U=V^{\\top}V=I_r,\\ \\{C^{(t)}\\}}\\sum_{t}\\big\\|\\Delta W^{(t)}_{tgt}-UC^{(t)}V^{\\top}\\big\\|_F^2\\ \\ \\text{(Tucker-2 by HOSVD+HOOI, solved in Core Space: }U=U_BU_{core},\\ V=V_AV_{core});\\ \\ O^{(t)}=U^{\\top}\\Delta W^{(t)}V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r},\\ O_{merged}=\\mathcal{M}\\big(\\{O^{(t)}\\}\\big),\\ \\ \\Delta W_{LoRA}=U\\,O_{merged}\\,V^{\\top},\\ U\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r};\\ \\ \\text{nothing is trained and no data enters the merge (}\\beta\\text{ and the rule's hyperparameters are chosen on a validation holdout)}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(training-free); the output is one rank-}r\\text{ LoRA, }r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ per adapted matrix (e.g. }B=U\\,O_{merged},\\ A=V^{\\top})","locus":"layer-wise, every LoRA-adapted matrix (\\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\), \\(W_o\\) in the experiments: eight rank-16 CLIP ViT-B/32 LoRAs and six LLaMA3-8B NLI LoRAs, the public KnOTS checkpoints)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Merging inside a shared two-sided rank-\\(r\\) subspace: all tasks are represented in the same frame \\(U\\,\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}V^{\\top}\\), chosen by a Tucker-2 decomposition of the norm-rebalanced task tensor with an identity task-mode factor. The base rule \\(\\mathcal{M}\\) (TIES, DARE-TIES, Iso-C, ...) acts on the \\(r\\times r\\) coordinates, so \\(\\mathrm{rank}(\\Delta W_{LoRA})\\le r\\) by construction. For a linear rule \\(\\mathcal{M}=\\sum_tc_t(\\cdot)\\), the result is \\(UU^{\\top}\\big(\\sum_tc_t\\Delta W^{(t)}\\big)VV^{\\top}\\), a two-sided orthogonal projection of the task-arithmetic sum onto subspaces fitted to the whole collection. Merge-then-compress instead takes the top-\\(r\\) singular subspace of the merged sum, which large-norm adapters dominate. Core Space's lossless bases \\(U_B, V_A\\) of the concatenated factors reduce the subspace search to \\((Tr_{in})\\times(Tr_{in})\\) matrices without changing the optimum.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is a single rank-\\(r\\) update \\(U\\,O_{merged}V^{\\top}\\) that can stay a LoRA or fold into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable: CtM is a post-hoc, data-free merge of already trained LoRAs and has no training phase.","key_idea":"Impose the rank-\\(r\\) bottleneck before merging, not after. CtM learns shared left and right \\(r\\)-dimensional subspaces from the (norm-rebalanced) LoRAs, projects each LoRA to \\(r\\times r\\) coordinates, and merges there. The result is a rank-\\(r\\) LoRA by construction, and the rank budget is not spent mostly on large-norm adapters or intruder directions, as truncating the merged sum would.","results_note":"Merging eight rank-16 CLIP ViT-B/32 LoRAs into one LoRA, CtM with DARE-TIES reaches 70.68 average normalized accuracy, against 65.91 for the best merge-then-compress baseline (DARE-TIES in Core Space) and 66.34 for the best low-rank-aware baseline (Iso-CTS); CtM with Iso-C (66.27) does not beat Iso-CTS. On six LLaMA3-8B NLI LoRAs, CtM with DARE-TIES reaches 95.63.","relations":[{"target":"core-space-merging","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses Core Space's lossless projection onto the bases of the concatenated factors to solve its Tucker problem in a \\((Tr_{in})\\times(Tr_{in})\\) space. Unlike Core Space, CtM's projection is lossy by design (rank \\(r\\)), so no post-hoc truncation is needed."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"KnOTS aligns LoRAs in a lossless joint-SVD basis, so a rank-\\(r\\) output still needs truncated SVD; CtM's shared basis has rank \\(r\\) from the start."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"TIES is one of the base rules \\(\\mathcal{M}\\) applied to the \\(r\\times r\\) coordinates; CtM with TIES or DARE-TIES gives the best single-LoRA results."},{"target":"dare","type":"combines","note":"DARE-TIES applied in CtM's \\(r\\times r\\) coordinate space is the best configuration on both CLIP ViT-B/32 (70.68) and LLaMA3-8B NLI (95.63)."},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"combines","note":"Task Arithmetic is one of the extra base rules in Appendix D.4. With \\(\\mathcal{M}\\) a weighted sum, CtM returns \\(UU^{\\top}\\big(\\sum_tc_t\\Delta W^{(t)}\\big)VV^{\\top}\\), the task-arithmetic merge projected onto the learned shared subspaces."},{"target":"lora-lego","type":"competes-with","note":"Another data-free method that outputs a single rank-\\(r\\) LoRA (by clustering rank-1 units); it is a baseline that CtM outperforms (62.36 on CLIP ViT-B/32)."},{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"combines","note":"TSV is one of the extra base rules in Appendix D.4. Inside CtM's \\(r\\times r\\) coordinates its per-task budget shrinks to about \\(r/T\\) directions (two for \\(r=16\\), \\(T=8\\)), and CtM gives almost no gain over merge-then-compress for TSV."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03723","https://arxiv.org/html/2606.03723 (v1, Sections 3-5, Appendices A, B, D.4)","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json (event 61546, Poster, Accept (regular))","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/61546","https://openreview.net/forum?id=p32nWlgwYC","https://github.com/ZhengbaoHe/compress-then-merge","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["Zhengbao He","Ruiqi Ding","Zhehao Huang","Ruikai Yang","Tao Li","Xiaolin Huang"],"bibkey":"he2026compress","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2026compress,\n  title = {{Compress then Merge: From Multiple LoRAs into One Low-Rank Adapter}},\n  author = {Zhengbao He and Ruiqi Ding and Zhehao Huang and Ruikai Yang and Tao Li and Xiaolin Huang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2606.03723},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03723}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and data-free: a Tucker-2 fit of the norm-rebalanced task tensor gives shared orthonormal frames \\(U,V\\) of rank \\(r\\). Each LoRA is projected to \\(O^{(t)}=U^\\top\\Delta W^{(t)}V\\), and a base rule merges the \\(r\\times r\\) cores.","shape":"Read on the inner rule's coefficients (as KnOTS and Core Space). With task arithmetic inside, \\(\\lambda\\mapsto W_0+U(\\sum_t\\lambda_tO^{(t)})V^\\top=W_0+UU^\\top(\\sum_t\\lambda_t\\Delta W^{(t)})VV^\\top\\): a linear method inside \\(W_0+U\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}V^\\top\\).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"Injective for linearly independent projected cores.","covariance":"HOSVD/HOOI frames and Frobenius norm rebalancing are \\(CO\\times CO\\)-equivariant. A coordinatewise rule (TIES, DARE) in core coordinates keeps only signed permutations of the core frames.","merge":"The output is one rank-\\(r\\) LoRA, which fuses.","rebasing":"Idempotent with the merged update frozen."},"categorical_reading":"CtM compresses on both sides before merging: every task goes through the same rank-\\(r\\) frame pair \\((U,V)\\) fitted to the whole collection, so the output is a LoRA\\(_r\\) point by construction. With a linear rule it is task arithmetic followed by the orthogonal projection \\(X\\mapsto UU^\\top XVV^\\top\\), which is lossy where Core Space's projection is lossless. Its content is the choice of a frame that large-norm adapters cannot monopolise.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"\\lambda\\mapsto\\big(U\\textstyle\\sum_t\\lambda_tO^{(t)},\\ V^\\top\\big)","note":"Task arithmetic inside: into LoRA_r pointed at (0, V^T), a T_A point with orthonormal rows. Checked numerically."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"UU^T(sum_t lambda_t Delta W^(t))VV^T generically leaves span{Delta W^(t)}, because the projection is lossy once T r > r. So CtM is not a reweighted task arithmetic."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"srr","name":"SRR (Structured Residual Reconstruction)","full_name":"Structured Residual Reconstruction (Preserve-Then-Quantize)","paper_title":"Preserve-Then-Quantize: Balancing Rank Budgets for Quantization Error Reconstruction in LLMs","authors":"Cho et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2602.02001","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02001","code_url":"https://github.com/cyoonjun/srr","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0\\approx\\hat W=Q+B^{(1)}A^{(1)}+B^{(2)}A^{(2)};\\ \\ B^{(1)}A^{(1)}=\\mathrm{SVD}_{k^\\star}\\big(W_0S^{\\top}\\big)S^{-\\top},\\ \\ Q=\\mathcal{Q}\\big(W_0-B^{(1)}A^{(1)}\\big),\\ \\ E=W_0-B^{(1)}A^{(1)}-Q,\\ \\ B^{(2)}A^{(2)}=\\mathrm{SVD}_{r-k^\\star}\\big(ES^{\\top}\\big)S^{-\\top};\\ \\ k^\\star=\\arg\\min_{0\\le k\\le r}\\rho_k\\big(W_0S^{\\top}\\big)\\,\\rho_{r-k}\\big(\\tilde ES^{\\top}\\big),\\ \\ \\rho_p(M)=1-\\frac{\\sum_{j\\le p}\\sigma_j(M)^2}{\\|M\\|_F^2},\\ \\tilde E_{ij}\\sim\\mathcal{U}[-1,1];\\ \\ S\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{activation-aware scaling (e.g. QERA-exact)};\\ \\ \\text{QPEFT: }h=\\big(Q+B^{(1)}A^{(1)}+B^{(2)}A^{(2)}\\big)x,\\ Q\\ \\text{frozen},\\ B^{(1)},A^{(1)},B^{(2)},A^{(2)}\\ \\text{trained, with the gradients of }(B^{(1)},A^{(1)})\\ \\text{scaled by }\\gamma\\in(0,1)\\ (\\gamma=0.1)","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\ (k^\\star\\text{ preserved plus }r-k^\\star\\text{ reconstruction ranks})","locus":"every quantized linear layer; QPEFT runs on RoBERTa-base (GLUE, MXINT 4/3/2-bit with ranks 8/8/64) and on LLaMA-2-7B and LLaMA-3.1-8B (SlimPajama with \\(r=8\\), GSM8K with \\(r=64\\)); \\(\\gamma=0.1\\) by default","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A low-rank-plus-quantized split of \\(W_0\\) in which a rank budget \\(r\\) is divided, per matrix, between preserving the top-\\(k^\\star\\) singular subspace of the activation-scaled weight in high precision and reconstructing the scaled quantization error of the remainder with the other \\(r-k^\\star\\) ranks. \\(k=0\\) is ordinary quantization-error reconstruction (QERA, LQER, ZeroQuant-V2); \\(k=r\\) preserves structure only, as in LQ-LoRA and SVDQuant. \\(k^\\star\\) minimizes a closed-form proxy of the scaled reconstruction error, built from the spectrum of \\(W_0S^{\\top}\\) and a single uniform-noise probe. For QPEFT the two low-rank blocks become one rank-\\(r\\) adapter on a frozen \\(Q\\), and the high-energy preserved block is damped by gradient scaling.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"As with LoftQ and QLoRA, \\(Q+BA\\) is a plain matrix only in high precision. Keeping the model in the low-bit format requires either leaving the adapter unmerged or re-quantizing \\(Q+BA\\).","identity_at_init":"Approximate: \\(Q+B^{(1)}A^{(1)}+B^{(2)}A^{(2)}\\approx W_0\\), with scaled error \\(\\|(W_0-\\hat W)S^{\\top}\\|_F\\) left by the rank-\\(r\\) budget. The QPEFT start is \\(\\hat W\\), not the quantized \\(Q\\) alone.","key_idea":"Quantizing the dominant directions of an anisotropic weight creates large, high-rank error that a rank-\\(r\\) correction cannot undo. SRR therefore keeps the leading \\(k^\\star\\) scaled singular directions out of the quantizer and spends only the remaining \\(r-k^\\star\\) ranks on error reconstruction. \\(k^\\star\\) is chosen per matrix from \\(\\rho_k(W_0S^{\\top})\\rho_{r-k}(\\tilde ES^{\\top})\\), and the resulting \\(Q+BA\\) is a QPEFT starting point whose preserved block trains with damped gradients.","results_note":"In 2-bit MXINT QPEFT of RoBERTa-base on GLUE, SRR raises the average score by 5.9 points over QERA and by more than 10 points over LoftQ and LQ-LoRA (gains of 1.5 and 4.7 points over QERA at 4 and 3 bits).","relations":[{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"Both start QPEFT from \\(Q+BA\\) close to \\(W_0\\). One LoftQ iteration is SRR's \\(k=0\\) end with \\(S=I\\) (quantize, then fit the residual); further LoftQ iterations alternate quantizing \\(W_0-BA\\) and refitting \\(BA\\), with no explicit rank split. SRR splits the rank once per matrix and beats five-iteration LoftQ by more than 10 GLUE points at 2 bits."},{"target":"lq-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"LQ-LoRA's low-rank-plus-quantized decomposition is the paper's preservation-only end (\\(k=r\\)), iterated. SRR picks \\(k\\) per matrix and adds an error-reconstruction block; the comparison uses the same QERA-exact scaling for both."},{"target":"pissa","type":"generalizes","note":"With \\(S=I\\), \\(k^\\star=r\\) and no gradient damping, SRR's QPEFT start is QPiSSA: the principal rank-\\(r\\) part forms the adapter and the residual is quantized."},{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"Same QPEFT setting: a frozen low-bit backbone plus a trainable low-rank adapter. SRR changes how the adapter is initialized and how its update is scaled."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02001","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.02001 (v2, Sections 2-5, Appendix A.3)","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json (event 65260, Poster, Accept (regular))","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/65260","https://openreview.net/forum?id=FHJdLypBf3","https://ai-isl.github.io/srr","https://github.com/cyoonjun/srr","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-3"],"authors_full":["Yoonjun Cho","Dongjae Jeon","Soeun Kim","Moongyu Jeon","Albert No"],"bibkey":"cho2026preserve","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cho2026preserve,\n  title = {{Preserve-Then-Quantize: Balancing Rank Budgets for Quantization Error Reconstruction in LLMs}},\n  author = {Yoonjun Cho and Dongjae Jeon and Soeun Kim and Moongyu Jeon and Albert No},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.02001},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02001}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"QPEFT on a frozen quantised \\(Q\\) with one rank-\\(r\\) adapter \\([B^{(1)}\\ B^{(2)}][A^{(1)};A^{(2)}]\\). Its initialisation splits the rank budget per matrix between preserved scaled singular directions (\\(k^\\star\\)) and reconstruction of the scaled quantisation error (\\(r-k^\\star\\)).","shape":"\\(Q+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) with \\(Q=\\mathcal Q(W_0-B^{(1)}A^{(1)})\\) and a rank-\\(r\\) start: the cone translated to a quantised apex, with \\(\\theta_0\\) near a smooth point (as LoftQ and LQ-LoRA).","base_point":"Pointed up to the attained scaled residual \\(\\|(W_0-\\hat W)S^\\top\\|_F\\): a small defect (an approximate section, Prop V.2(c)).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the merged adapter. The gradient damping \\(\\gamma\\) of the preserved block is a cometric choice that respects only \\(GL_{k^\\star}\\times GL_{r-k^\\star}\\).","covariance":"The MXINT grid lives in the standard basis and the activation scaling \\(S\\) is a data frame: Mon×Mon (grid), as LoftQ.","merge":"An exact merge of the high-precision adapter leaves the low-bit type.","rebasing":"As LoftQ: \\(\\to\\Theta\\) in exact arithmetic, with a requantisation at each merge."},"categorical_reading":"SRR interpolates between the two orders of Prop V.2(b): \\(k^\\star=r\\) is split-then-quantise (LQ-LoRA, QPiSSA) and \\(k^\\star=0\\) is quantise-then-fit (one LoftQ step), with \\(k^\\star\\) chosen per matrix from a closed-form proxy. The object is always a translated cone over a quantised apex with an approximate section. What moves with \\(k^\\star\\) is the apex \\(Q\\) and how much defect is left.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ -B_0],\\ [A;\\ A_0]\\big)","note":"In the slice at the attained start theta_0' = Q + B_0A_0: SRR_r into LoRA_{2r} over theta_0', at a degenerate pointing (as LoftQ)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"A Type II smooth start over Q against QLoRA's apex at kappa theta_0, with different apexes as well (as LQ-LoRA)."},{"target":"loftq","test":"O1","reason":"For k* > 0 the apex Q = q(W_0 - B^(1)A^(1)) differs from LoftQ's Q_T by a generically full-rank quantisation error, so the translated cones are not nested. At k* = 0 with S = I, SRR's start is one-iteration LoftQ."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"prompt2effect","name":"Prompt2Effect","full_name":"Prompt2Effect: weight-driven hypernetwork for SVD-canonicalized LoRA prediction","paper_title":"Prompt2Effect: Training-Free Image-to-Video Model Specialization via LoRA Generation","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ECCV 2026","arxiv":"2606.13971","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13971","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"X_W^{l}=\\big[\\,W_0^{l}P_B^{l}\\,;\\,(W_0^{l})^{\\top}P_A^{l}\\,\\big]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(d_{out}+d_{in})\\times d_h},\\ \\ Q_W^{l}=\\mathrm{CrossAttn}(Q_{\\text{learned}},X_W^{l},X_W^{l})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d_h};\\ \\ X_{\\mathcal{E}}=H_{\\mathcal{E}}\\big(\\{Q_W^{l}+e_l+e_M^{l}\\}_{l=1}^{L},\\,E_{\\text{text}}(c_{\\text{eff}})\\big);\\ \\ X_{\\mathcal{D}}^{l}=\\mathrm{CrossAttn}(X_W^{l},X_{\\mathcal{E}}^{l},X_{\\mathcal{E}}^{l});\\ \\ \\hat B^{l}=\\mathrm{Head}_B\\big(X_{\\mathcal{D}}^{l}[:d_{out}]\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\ \\hat A^{l}=\\mathrm{Head}_A\\big(X_{\\mathcal{D}}^{l}[d_{out}:]\\big)^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ h=(W_0^{l}+\\hat B^{l}\\hat A^{l})\\,x;\\ \\ \\text{targets: }\\Delta W_{\\star}^{l}=U^{l}S^{l}(V^{l})^{\\top}\\Rightarrow B^{\\star,l}=U^{l}(S^{l})^{1/2},\\ A^{\\star,l}=(S^{l})^{1/2}(V^{l})^{\\top};\\ \\ \\min_{\\phi}\\ \\frac{1}{2L}\\sum_{l=1}^{L}\\Big(\\frac{\\|\\hat A^{l}-A^{\\star,l}\\|_F^2}{\\|A^{\\star,l}\\|_F^2+\\epsilon}+\\frac{\\|\\hat B^{l}-B^{\\star,l}\\|_F^2}{\\|B^{\\star,l}\\|_F^2+\\epsilon}\\Big);\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0,\\ E_{\\text{text}};\\ \\text{trained once: hypernetwork } \\phi=(H_{\\mathcal{E}},H_{\\mathcal{D}},P_A,P_B,Q_{\\text{learned}},e);\\ \\text{nothing trained per effect}","trainable_params":"|\\phi|\\approx 1.3\\text{B (one hypernetwork, trained once by NMSE regression on the SVD-canonicalised factors of 70 per-effect LoRAs for the 11B proprietary model, }\\approx12\\%\\text{ of its size; 49 rank-32 community LoRAs for Wan2.1)};\\ \\text{per new effect: } 0\\ \\text{trained, generated adapter } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix } (r=128;\\ r=32\\ \\text{on Wan2.1})","locus":"LoRA target layers (a selected set of \\(L\\) linear layers) of a DiT image-to-video diffusion model: Snap's proprietary 11B I2V model, with the findings reproduced on Wan2.1-I2V-14B-480P","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Amortised LoRA generation: one 1.3B Transformer hypernetwork maps \\((c_{\\text{eff}},\\{W_0^{l}\\}_{l=1}^{L})\\) to all layer-wise rank-\\(r\\) factor pairs \\((\\hat B^{l},\\hat A^{l})\\) in a single forward pass. The frozen base matrix is tokenised CUR-style into its \\(d_{out}\\) rows and \\(d_{in}\\) columns at full rank (truncating \\(W_0\\) to its top singular directions hurt in ablation). These tokens are compressed by learned-query cross-attention to \\(n=256\\) latents and mixed with the prompt by cross-attention. They are then decompressed by cross-attention that uses the original row and column tokens as queries, so the rows of \\(\\hat B^{l}\\) and the columns of \\(\\hat A^{l}\\) are indexed by the same coordinates as \\(W_0^{l}\\). The regression targets are SVD-canonicalised: the gauge orbit \\(\\{(BR,R^{-1}A):R\\in GL(r)\\}\\) of each trained LoRA is replaced by its balanced representative \\((US^{1/2},S^{1/2}V^{\\top})\\), which is unique up to joint sign flips of paired singular vectors (and rotations inside repeated singular values). The deployed object is an ordinary additive low-rank delta \\(\\Delta W^{l}=\\hat B^{l}\\hat A^{l}\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each generated update \\(\\Delta W^{l}=\\hat B^{l}\\hat A^{l}\\) is a plain low-rank additive delta that can be folded into \\(W_0^{l}\\) for a given effect. Generated deltas also blend linearly in weight space: the paper composes two effects as \\(\\Delta W=0.5\\,\\Delta W_A+0.5\\,\\Delta W_B\\). The hypernetwork is needed only to produce new effects.","identity_at_init":"Not identity by design, since a non-zero \\(\\Delta W^{l}\\) is the output. Dropping the generated LoRA recovers the frozen base. When the prediction is used to initialise LoRA fine-tuning, training starts from \\(B_0=\\hat B,\\ A_0=\\hat A\\) instead of \\(B_0=0\\).","key_idea":"A LoRA delta is defined relative to \\(W_0\\), so the generator is conditioned on the full-rank base weights (tokenised by rows and columns) as well as the effect prompt. The factorisation gauge \\((B,A)\\sim(BR,R^{-1}A)\\) is removed by regressing SVD-canonical factors \\((US^{1/2},S^{1/2}V^{\\top})\\). Together these two choices make one-pass LoRA synthesis stable at video-DiT scale, where a HyperDreamBooth-style generator trains unstably and scores zero on the VLM judge.","results_note":"On Snap's 11B image-to-video model, the zero-shot predicted LoRAs score 56.93 overall on the VLM judge for in-distribution effects, against 57.57 for per-effect trained LoRAs and 42.57 for the base model, while replacing about 56 GPU-hours of training with 3.3 s of hypernetwork inference. Used as an initialisation, 100 fine-tuning steps reach an overall VLM score of 56.33 on out-of-distribution effects, versus 56.70 for a 1,000-step LoRA trained from scratch.","relations":[{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited prompt-to-LoRA hypernetwork. Prompt2Effect follows the same recipe of regressing a library of trained LoRAs from a text prompt with learned layer and module-type embeddings, and adds conditioning on the base weights and SVD-canonicalised targets."},{"target":"hyperdreambooth","type":"competes-with","note":"A HyperDreamBooth-style hypernetwork is the main learned baseline. Scaled to video LoRAs it trains unstably and gives near-zero VLM scores."},{"target":"drag-and-drop-llms","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited prompt-to-weights generator for LLMs. Prompt2Effect targets video DiTs and adds base-weight conditioning."},{"target":"doc-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same amortised LoRA-generation line (not cited). D2L conditions on the frozen model's activations over a document and trains by context distillation. Prompt2Effect conditions on the frozen weights and an effect prompt and trains by regression on a LoRA library."},{"target":"learning-on-loras-gl-equivariance","type":"competes-with","note":"Two treatments of the same \\(GL(r)\\) symmetry of LoRA factors (not cited). Prompt2Effect picks a canonical SVD representative of each orbit; Learning on LoRAs builds GL-equivariant networks that act on whole orbits."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13971","https://arxiv.org/html/2606.13971","https://eccv.ecva.net/virtual/2026/poster/3453","https://xiaomeng-yang.github.io/Prompt2Effect/","https://github.com/LukeLIN-web/Prompt2Effect","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-4"],"authors_full":["Xiaomeng Yang","Yanyu Li","Gordon Guocheng Qian","Ivan Skorokhodov","Viacheslav Ivanov","Avalon Vinella","Xuan Zhang","Yanzhi Wang","Sergey Tulyakov","Anil Kag"],"bibkey":"yang2026prompt2effect","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2026prompt2effect,\n  title = {{Prompt2Effect: Training-Free Image-to-Video Model Specialization via LoRA Generation}},\n  author = {Xiaomeng Yang and Yanyu Li and Gordon Guocheng Qian and Ivan Skorokhodov and Viacheslav Ivanov and Avalon Vinella and Xuan Zhang and Yanzhi Wang and Sergey Tulyakov and Anil Kag},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2606.13971},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13971}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over effect prompts, generated from \\((c_{\\rm eff},\\{W_0^l\\})\\) by one hypernetwork in one pass; nothing is trained per effect.","shape":"Each effect's fibre \\(W_0+\\hat B\\hat A\\) is a point of LoRA's cone (rank 128, or 32 on Wan2.1).","base_point":"The generated update is nonzero by design and is used directly or as the start of LoRA fine-tuning (\\(B_0=\\hat B\\), \\(A_0=\\hat A\\)): a pointing defect, as HyperDreamBooth.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the generated rank wire. The NMSE targets are the balanced SVD representatives \\((US^{1/2},S^{1/2}V^\\top)\\), which fix the gauge up to joint sign flips (and rotations inside repeated singular values). So the regression is a gauge-fixed objective, not a gauge-invariant one.","covariance":"Image level: each fibre lies in LoRA's cone and the dense heads can reach any factor pair, so the fibrewise image is basis-free, \\(GL\\times GL\\) (as Text-to-LoRA). The generator itself is not \\(GL\\)-equivariant: it reads \\(W_0\\) through fixed learned projections \\(P_A,P_B\\); at most it is equivariant under permutations of the row and column tokens, and only if no positional encoding is attached to them (the paper does not say).","merge":"M1 per effect; generated deltas also blend linearly.","rebasing":"Undefined for a zero-shot family."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt2Effect is Text-to-LoRA conditioned on the base point as well as the prompt. The hypernetwork reads \\(W_0\\) by rows and columns and writes \\(\\hat B\\) and \\(\\hat A\\) in the same coordinates, and it removes LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge by regressing onto the canonical SVD section of the factor bundle. The instability the paper reports for HyperDreamBooth-style generation is what a gauge-sensitive factor target predicts.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\big(\\hat B_\\phi(c,W_0),\\ \\hat A_\\phi(c,W_0)\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over effect prompts, into LoRA_r pointed at h_c(q_0) (unpointed slice: the family is a defect by design)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hyperdreambooth","test":"O1","reason":"HyperDreamBooth's fibres lie in W_0 + P_out M_{<=r} P_in with frozen random frames, so their row spaces stay in row P_in. Prompt2Effect generates dense factors whose row spaces range over all of R^{d_in}."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"giva","name":"GiVA","full_name":"Gradient-Informed Bases for Vector-Based Adaptation","paper_title":"GiVA: Gradient-Informed Bases for Vector-Based Adaptation","authors":"Gangwar et al.","year":2026,"venue":"AISTATS 2026","arxiv":"2604.21901","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21901","code_url":"https://github.com/neerajgangwar/giva","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\Gamma B\\,\\Lambda A\\,x,\\quad \\Gamma=\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma),\\ \\gamma\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}},\\ \\ \\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda),\\ \\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r};\\ \\ G=\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0)\\ \\text{(full fine-tuning gradient on one batch)},\\ G\\approx U\\Sigma V^{\\top}\\ \\text{(low-rank SVD)};\\ \\ A=V_r^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{with } B^{\\top}B=I_r\\ \\ (\\text{TopSV-B: } U_{[:,1:r]},\\ \\text{SecSV-B: } U_{[:,r+1:2r]},\\ \\text{Rand-B: QR of a Gaussian});\\ \\ \\gamma_0=0,\\ \\lambda_0=\\mathbf{1};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0,\\ A,\\ B\\ \\text{(per layer)};\\ \\text{trained: } \\gamma,\\ \\lambda","trainable_params":"d_{out}+r\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (plus frozen per-layer bases, } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{, stored once per task)}","locus":"any linear layer; paper: all attention and MLP projections of RoBERTa-base/large (GLUE); query, key, value, up and down projections (plus gate for Phi-3) of Qwen2-0.5B, Phi-3-3.8B and OLMo-2-7B (commonsense reasoning), OLMo-2-7B (MetaMathQA, Code-Feedback) and all attention and MLP projections of Mistral-7B (Alpaca instruction tuning); DINOv2-ViT-B/14 and CLIP-ViT-L/14 (image classification)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"This is VeRA-type vector adaptation \\(\\Delta W=\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\): two trainable diagonals around a frozen rank-\\(r\\) pair. Unlike VeRA, the frozen pair is per-layer and task-informed rather than shared and random. \\(A\\) spans the top-\\(r\\) right singular subspace of the one-batch full fine-tuning gradient \\(G\\), and \\(B\\) is any matrix with orthonormal columns. Theorem 1 shows this choice minimises \\(\\|GA^{\\top}B^{\\top}BA-G\\|_F\\): it makes \\(GA^{\\top}B^{\\top}BA=GV_rV_r^{\\top}\\) the best rank-\\(r\\) approximation of \\(G\\) (Eckart-Young). The objective comes from a first-order expansion at \\(\\Gamma_0=0,\\Lambda_0=I\\) that writes \\(\\nabla_\\Gamma\\mathcal{L}\\) as the full matrix \\(GA^{\\top}B^{\\top}\\). For the diagonal \\(\\Gamma\\) actually trained, only the diagonal of that matrix is used, so the matching is exact only for this relaxation. After training, the row space of \\(\\Delta W\\) lies in the fixed subspace \\(\\mathrm{span}(V_r)\\) and its column space in \\(\\Gamma\\,\\mathrm{span}(B)\\). The trainable freedom is an \\(r\\)-vector rescaling of the frozen directions plus a \\(d_{out}\\)-vector rescaling of output coordinates.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training, \\(\\Delta W=\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\) is a fixed matrix of rank at most \\(r\\) that folds into \\(W_0\\), as with VeRA.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(\\gamma_0=0\\) gives \\(\\Delta W_0=0\\). Because \\(\\Gamma_0=0\\), the gradient with respect to \\(\\lambda\\) is zero at the first step, so only \\(\\gamma\\) moves initially.","key_idea":"Vector-based adapters such as VeRA need very high rank (e.g. \\(r=1024\\)) because their frozen bases are random and carry no task information. GiVA sets the bases to the dominant right singular vectors of the first-step full fine-tuning gradient, with \\(B\\) orthonormal. The same \\(d_{out}+r\\) trainable scalars then suffice at one-eighth of VeRA's rank or less (from \\(8\\times\\) lower on MetaMathQA and image classification to \\(128\\times\\) lower on GLUE with RoBERTa-base), which brings training time close to LoRA's.","results_note":"On GLUE with RoBERTa-base, GiVA at \\(r=8\\) (0.08M trainable parameters, Rand-B) averages 83.8, versus 83.6 for VeRA at \\(r=1024\\) (0.16M) and 83.3 for LoRA at \\(r=4\\) (0.66M); parameter counts exclude the classification heads. Fine-tuning OLMo-2-7B on MetaMathQA takes 2h57m, versus 2h54m for LoRA and 3h26m for VeRA.","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"builds-on","note":"Same \\(\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\) parameterisation and scaling-vector initialisation. The frozen shared random bases are replaced by per-layer gradient-SVD bases."},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"builds-on","note":"Borrows the first-step full-gradient SVD initialisation. LoRA-GA then trains \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), whereas GiVA freezes them and trains only the two scaling vectors. The SecSV-B variant mirrors LoRA-GA's use of singular vectors \\(r+1\\) to \\(2r\\)."},{"target":"lora-one","type":"builds-on","note":"The TopSV-B variant uses the top left singular vectors of the gradient, as LoRA-One does for LoRA."},{"target":"lora-sb","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited. Also freezes gradient-initialised low-rank bases but trains a tiny \\(r\\times r\\) core (\\(r^2\\) entries); GiVA trains \\(d_{out}+r\\) diagonal entries instead."},{"target":"randlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Compared vector-based baseline with frozen random bases and full-rank updates."},{"target":"pvera","type":"competes-with","note":"Another VeRA derivative (not cited). It keeps random bases and makes the bottleneck stochastic instead of task-informed."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21901","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.21901","https://virtual.aistats.org/virtual/2026/poster/14009","https://neerajgangwar.com/publications/","https://github.com/neerajgangwar/giva","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-4"],"authors_full":["Neeraj Gangwar","Rishabh Deshmukh","Michael Shavlovsky","Hancao Li","Vivek Mittal","Lexing Ying","Nickvash Kani"],"bibkey":"gangwar2026giva","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gangwar2026giva,\n  title = {{GiVA: Gradient-Informed Bases for Vector-Based Adaptation}},\n  author = {Neeraj Gangwar and Rishabh Deshmukh and Michael Shavlovsky and Hancao Li and Vivek Mittal and Lexing Ying and Nickvash Kani},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS)},\n  eprint = {2604.21901},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21901}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"VeRA's \\(\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\) with per-layer frozen bases from the one-batch gradient SVD (\\(A=V_r^\\top\\); \\(B\\) orthonormal: TopSV, SecSV or random).","shape":"The toric cone \\(\\{\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\}\\), \\(d=d_{out}+r-1\\) (checked), rank \\(\\le r\\), with its row space fixed at \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,V_r^\\top\\).","base_point":"\\(\\gamma_0=0\\), \\(\\lambda_0=\\mathbf 1\\): only \\(\\gamma\\) moves to first order (\\(\\dim S_1=d_{out}\\), checked), an apex as for VeRA.","gauge":"\\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\): \\((\\gamma,\\lambda)\\mapsto(c\\gamma,\\lambda/c)\\).","covariance":"Under \\(W_0\\mapsto gW_0h^{-1}\\) the gradient goes to \\(g^{-\\top}Gh^{\\top}\\), so the default frames (\\(A=V_r^\\top\\); \\(B\\) from \\(U\\), SecSV-B in the released code, or TopSV-B) transport for \\(g,h\\) in \\(CO\\), and \\(\\Gamma\\) cuts the output side to monomials: the exact group is \\(\\mathbb R^\\times B_m\\times CO(d_{in})\\) (bare singular vectors, so scale-invariant as for LoRA-GA). Nearest key OxO, the gradient-spectral-frame key of LoRA-GA, GaLore and KeepLoRA; the output cut by \\(\\Gamma\\) plays the role of VeRA's Mon factor. Rand-B makes the output frame random, covariant only in law (VeRA's 'frame').","merge":"\\(\\Gamma B\\Lambda A\\) fuses.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\) LoRA-FA\\((V_r^\\top)\\) when \\(B\\) has no zero entries (Thm V.3, Cor 3, as VeRA)."},"categorical_reading":"GiVA is VeRA with its frozen random pair replaced by a task-informed one. It has the same toric cone of dimension \\(d_{out}+r-1\\), now inside LoRA-FA\\((V_r^\\top)\\) with the gradient's dominant right singular frame, which makes the one-sided first-order step \\(GA^\\top A\\) the best rank-\\(r\\) approximation of \\(G\\). The gain over VeRA is in the frame, not the shape: the fixed row space is chosen rather than drawn.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\Gamma B\\Lambda","note":"Into LoRA-FA(V_r^T), pointed at 0 (gamma_0 = 0), as VeRA -> LoRA-FA(A)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\lambda)\\mapsto(\\Gamma B\\Lambda,\\ V_r^\\top)","note":"Composite with FA -> LoRA, pointed at (0, V_r^T)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"Both fix their row space, GiVA at row V_r^T (a gradient frame) and VeRA at row A (a random frame). For generic data the two row spaces differ."},{"target":"lora-sb","test":"O1","reason":"LoRA-SB's updates B R A keep col B fixed; GiVA's column space Gamma col B moves with gamma."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"activation-prompt","name":"AP (Activation Prompt)","full_name":"Activation Prompt: universal additive perturbation of one intermediate activation map","paper_title":"Visual prompting reimagined: The power of the Activation Prompts","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"AISTATS 2026","arxiv":"2604.06440","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06440","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"z^{(k)}=f_{\\theta^{(k)}}\\big(z^{(k-1)}\\big),\\ z^{(0)}=x;\\quad z^{(l)}\\ \\leftarrow\\ z^{(l)}+\\delta^{(l)}\\ \\text{at a single chosen layer } l,\\ \\ \\delta^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D'\\times P'}\\ \\ (\\text{channels}\\times\\text{spatial positions for CNNs};\\ d\\times\\text{tokens for ViTs});\\quad \\min_{\\delta^{(l)},\\,W_{head}}\\ \\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}|}\\sum_{(x,y)\\in\\mathcal{D}}\\ell\\big(f_{\\theta}(x,\\delta^{(l)});y\\big);\\ \\ l=0\\ \\text{recovers additive input-level VP};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: backbone } \\theta;\\ \\text{trained: } \\delta^{(l)}\\ \\text{and the linear head}","trainable_params":"D'P'=|z^{(l)}|\\ \\text{(one activation-sized tensor, e.g. } d\\times\\text{tokens at a ViT block input)}+|W_{head}|;\\ \\text{Table 2 totals, which also count the trained head: } 0.12\\text{M (ResNet-101)},\\ 0.16\\text{M (ViT-L/16)}\\ \\text{(the paper does not state the exact prompt shape; a full } 197\\times1024\\ \\text{ViT-L/16 token map would be}\\approx0.20\\text{M)}","locus":"a single intermediate activation map: by default the input of the third-to-last residual block of ResNet-101 (CNNs prefer deep layers) and the input of the third Transformer block of ViT-L/16 (ViTs prefer shallow layers); also tested on CLIP and Swin","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"An input-independent additive offset \\(z^{(l)}\\mapsto z^{(l)}+\\delta^{(l)}\\) on the full activation tensor of one layer. Each channel and each position gets its own value, so this is a constant bias map rather than a per-channel bias. Input-level additive VP is the case \\(l=0\\). Proposition 1 (Appendix C.2) matches AP with shift-only normalisation tuning at the same layer under unit effective scale: for CNNs, if \\(\\gamma_d/\\sigma_d=1\\) and \\(\\delta^{(l)}\\) is the same \\(\\delta_*\\) at every position, AP equals BatchNorm tuning with \\(\\beta_d=w_d^{(l)}\\delta_*+\\mu_d\\); for ViTs, if \\(\\gamma_d/\\sigma_{b,p}=1\\) and the LayerNorm mean \\(\\mu_{b,p}=\\mu_p\\) is the same for every sample, AP equals LayerNorm tuning with \\(\\beta_d=\\delta^{(l)}_{p,d}+\\mu_p\\), i.e. \\(\\delta^{(l)}=\\beta-\\mu\\). AP is therefore a position-resolved relative of a single-layer normalisation shift. Unlike VPT, it adds no tokens and touches one layer. Backpropagation only has to reach layer \\(l\\), so a deep \\(l\\) in a CNN cuts training memory and time.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\delta^{(l)}\\) is a fixed tensor added to the activations, so inference costs one addition and no extra matmuls; the paper claims no additional inference overhead but does not discuss folding. Folding into existing parameters is possible only for a position-uniform \\(\\delta^{(l)}\\) at a point where the next operations are affine (for example the output bias of the preceding block's last linear layer in a ViT residual stream). A position-specific \\(\\delta^{(l)}\\) needs a per-position bias, which standard layers lack, so it is stored as a constant bias map.","identity_at_init":"Exact when \\(\\delta^{(l)}=0\\), because the frozen network is then unchanged. The paper does not state how \\(\\delta^{(l)}\\) is initialised. The linear head is trained as in linear probing.","key_idea":"Moving the universal additive prompt from pixels to one intermediate activation turns visual prompting into a single-layer activation shift that is provably related to normalisation tuning. The best layer depends on the architecture (deep for CNNs, shallow for ViTs), which the authors tie to where global features live. Theorem 1, for a two-layer ViT on a four-pattern data model, gives zero generalisation error with \\(N=\\Theta(P)\\) samples when AP is at the first layer and with \\(N=\\Theta(P^{2}\\log P)\\) when it is at the second, where \\(P\\) is the number of tokens; these are sufficient sample sizes, which the paper reads as shallow AP needing fewer samples.","results_note":"Across 19 datasets from FGVC, VTAB and five other benchmarks, AP beats input-level VP on every dataset for both ResNet-101 and ViT-L/16, with average gains of about 4% and 1.5%. On ResNet-101 it also nearly halves training memory relative to VP (6.3 GB vs 12.2 GB) and cuts time per epoch from 72 s to 41 s. On VTAB-1k few-shot it beats VP by 4.3 points (ResNet-101) and about 1 point (ViT-L/16).","relations":[{"target":"visual-prompting","type":"generalizes","note":"Additive input-level VP is AP at layer \\(l=0\\) (the padding-frame VP prompt is a restriction of the full-image \\(\\delta^{(0)}\\)); the paper uses AP to explain why VP lags."},{"target":"ln-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Norm-Tune (Basu et al. 2023, which trains all normalisation affine parameters, the same mechanism as LN-tuning) is a main baseline. Proposition 1 links single-layer AP to unit-scale, shift-only normalisation tuning at that layer under extra conditions, but AP does not contain multi-layer \\(\\gamma,\\beta\\) tuning, so it does not generalize LN-tuning."},{"target":"vpt","type":"competes-with","note":"VPT prepends tokens at the input or at every layer; AP adds a bias map at one chosen layer. On ViT-L/16 (Table 3) AP beats VPT-deep on the VTAB and 'Others' averages (90.25 vs 89.24, 91.09 vs 90.14) but trails it on FGVC (85.30 vs 86.34), with fewer parameters (0.16M vs 0.25M), less memory and higher throughput."},{"target":"red","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Both edit activations without touching weights. RED applies a per-channel scale and bias, shared across positions, at every FFN output; AP is bias-only but position-specific and confined to one layer."},{"target":"lofit","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. LoFiT adds learned offset vectors to selected attention-head outputs; AP adds an offset to an entire activation map of one layer."},{"target":"ssf","type":"competes-with","note":"SSF's scale-and-shift feature modulation in every layer is a compared PEFT baseline (Table A8); SSF is better on FGVC and slightly worse on VTAB."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06440","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.06440","https://virtual.aistats.org/virtual/2026/poster/13832","https://openreview.net/forum?id=7onqhBs7NV","https://openreview.net/forum?id=0b328CMwn1","https://web.archive.org/web/20240228131526/https://openreview.net/forum?id=0b328CMwn1","https://openreview.net/forum?id=3gwNb8qZDr","https://research.ibm.com/publications/visual-prompting-reimagined-the-power-of-activation-prompts"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-4"],"authors_full":["Yihua Zhang","Hongkang Li","Yuguang Yao","Aochuan Chen","Shuai Zhang","Pin-Yu Chen","Meng Wang","Sijia Liu"],"bibkey":"zhang2023visual","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023visual,\n  title = {{Visual prompting reimagined: The power of the Activation Prompts}},\n  author = {Yihua Zhang and Hongkang Li and Yuguang Yao and Aochuan Chen and Shuai Zhang and Pin-Yu Chen and Meng Wang and Sijia Liu},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS)},\n  eprint = {2604.06440},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06440}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A translation box \\(z\\mapsto z+\\delta^{(l)}\\) on one intermediate activation wire, neutral at \\(\\delta=0\\): a pointed extension, visual prompting moved from the pixel wire to layer \\(l\\).","shape":"Affine in \\(\\delta\\); every channel and position has its own value, \\(d=D'P'\\).","base_point":"The initialisation is not stated. The exactly pointed variant \\(\\delta=0\\) is neutral and, being linear, has full first-order reach.","gauge":"Injective; a kernel of the next linear map would add translations, as for VP after a patch embedding.","covariance":"The prompt is a full vector at every position of the wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of that wire. Positions are the network's own tokens or pixels, not an imposed frame: function-level GL×GL. VP's border frame is what makes VP \\(\\Pi\\).","merge":"Position-dependent values have no per-position slot after layer 0 (Thm VI.3(a)). A position-uniform \\(\\delta\\) would fuse into a preceding bias (M1→), and at \\(l=0\\) on a ViT it slides onto the positional embeddings.","rebasing":"Undefined for an M\\(\\infty\\) method; translations would compose additively."},"categorical_reading":"Activation prompting inserts a single translation box on the activation wire at layer \\(l\\), neutral at zero. Input-level additive VP is the case \\(l=0\\), and a position-uniform \\(\\delta\\) is a bias edit, which is why Proposition 1 can match it with shift-only normalisation tuning at that layer. Its position resolution is what keeps it from being a weight-space method; its depth changes only how far the backward pass must reach.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"bitfit","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta=\\mathbf 1\\otimes v\\ \\mapsto\\ \\Delta b_{\\rm down}^{(l-1)}=v","note":"Position-uniform instance only (the hypothesis of Prop. 1), on a ViT whose residual stream at layer l is last written by the biased MLP output projection of block l-1: the shift is that bias edit, BitFit pointed at 0. The released position-specific delta has no such arrow (M-infinity)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vpt","test":"O1","reason":"f(theta_0) is AP's value at delta = 0, while no VPT parameter reproduces f (extra tokens always draw attention mass), so Im AP is not inside Im VPT."},{"target":"ln-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"LayerNorm shifts are shared across positions; a position-specific delta at one layer is not a (gamma, beta) edit. Prop. 1's matching needs a position-uniform delta, unit effective scale and batch-invariant statistics."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"stable-lora","name":"Stable-LoRA","full_name":"Stable-LoRA: early-step weight shrinkage of the LoRA down-projection","paper_title":"Stable-LoRA: Stabilizing Feature Learning of Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2603.05204","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05204","code_url":"https://github.com/Yize-Wu/Stable-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+s\\,BAx,\\quad B_0=0,\\ \\ A_0\\ \\text{entries sampled from } [-1/d_{in},\\,1/d_{in}];\\ \\ \\text{while not stable: if } \\|A_t\\|_F/d_{in}>\\|B_t\\|_F/d_{out}\\ \\text{then } A_t\\leftarrow(1-\\lambda)A_t\\ \\text{else set stable};\\ \\ A_{t+1}=A_t-\\eta g_A^t-\\eta wA_t,\\ \\ B_{t+1}=B_t-\\eta g_B^t-\\eta wB_t\\ \\ (g=\\text{AdamW-processed gradients},\\ w=\\text{weight decay});\\ \\ \\lambda\\in\\{5\\times10^{-4},10^{-3},2\\times10^{-3},5\\times10^{-3}\\}\\ \\text{(searched)};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{trained: } A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(identical to LoRA; the shrinkage is in place, so no extra memory)}","locus":"any LoRA-adapted linear layer; paper default q_proj and v_proj (\\(r=8\\)) of Qwen2-0.5B/1.5B and LLaMA-3.2-1B/3B, with ablations on qkvo and all seven projections and a Llama-3.1-8B math run (Appendix D.2)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"The parameterisation is plain LoRA, \\(\\Delta W=sBA\\); only the optimisation trajectory changes. During the first few steps, an in-place multiplicative decay \\(A\\leftarrow(1-\\lambda)A\\), independent of the learning rate, is applied to the down-projection only, before the AdamW update. It stops once the balance condition \\(\\|A\\|_F/d_{in}\\le\\|B\\|_F/d_{out}\\) holds, after which training is ordinary AdamW (Algorithm 1 uses one flag; the released optimizer keeps a separate stop flag per adapted matrix). Because only \\(A\\) is shrunk, each shrink step rescales the current update \\(\\Delta W\\) by \\((1-\\lambda)\\). This differs from a gauge move \\((A,B)\\mapsto(cA,B/c)\\), which would leave \\(\\Delta W\\) unchanged. After \\(N\\) shrink steps the initialisation enters \\(A_N\\) only through \\((1-\\lambda)^{N}A_0\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained adapter is a standard \\(sBA\\) and merges like LoRA; the procedure changes only how \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are reached.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(B_0=0\\) gives \\(\\Delta W_0=0\\). Shrinking \\(A\\) while \\(B=0\\) leaves the function unchanged; later shrink steps scale \\(\\Delta W\\) by \\((1-\\lambda)\\).","key_idea":"The paper writes \\(v=\\Theta(n^{\\gamma[v]})\\) for scaling in the width \\(n\\) and assumes Adam-normalised gradients with \\(\\gamma[g_A^tZ]=1\\). Under these conditions, LoRA is self-stabilised, meaning feature updates \\(\\Delta Y_t=\\Theta(1)\\) for all \\(t\\), when \\(\\gamma[s]+2\\gamma[\\eta]+1=0\\), \\(\\gamma[A_0Z]\\le\\gamma[\\eta]+1\\) and \\(\\gamma[B_0]\\le\\gamma[\\eta]\\) (Theorem 3.1). The usual non-zero \\(A_0\\) violates the \\(A_0\\) condition at practical learning rates, and the violation persists through training. Stable-LoRA therefore keeps \\(A_0\\)'s early benefit (escaping the saddle at zero) but decays it geometrically until the balance condition holds.","results_note":"On Qwen2 (0.5B, 1.5B) and LLaMA-3.2 (1B, 3B) multiple-choice QA, Stable-LoRA has the best or tied-best accuracy on every dataset and model against AdamW-LoRA, LoRA+, Riemannian preconditioning and LoRA-RITE, with gains of up to 4 points over AdamW (average gains 0.5 to 2.1 points), at 0.6% extra training time and no extra memory. It also beats AdamW-LoRA on MetaMathQA-to-GSM8K chain-of-thought training (LLaMA-3.2-1B/3B and Llama-3.1-8B).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Same parameterisation; only the training procedure changes."},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA+ restores width-stable feature learning with \\(\\eta_B>\\eta_A\\) (baseline run with \\(\\eta_B=4\\eta_A\\)). Stable-LoRA keeps one learning rate and decays \\(A\\) early."},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative route to stable LoRA feature learning (\\(r\\times r\\) preconditioners), used as a baseline. It costs +8.3% training time versus +0.6% for Stable-LoRA in their timing (Table 5)."},{"target":"lora-rite","type":"competes-with","note":"Transformation-invariant LoRA optimiser, used as a baseline. It costs +46% training time versus +0.6% for Stable-LoRA (Table 5)."},{"target":"hayou-lora-init-dynamics","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps the initialisation \\(B_0=0,\\ A_0\\neq0\\) that Hayou et al. favour. Stable-LoRA shows that this non-zero \\(A_0\\) breaks width-stability unless the learning rate is large enough, and removes its long-term effect by shrinkage."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05204","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.05204","https://openreview.net/forum?id=xSa19DAieH","https://web.archive.org/web/20260419154753/https://openreview.net/forum?id=xSa19DAieH","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10006643","https://github.com/Yize-Wu/Stable-LoRA","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-4"],"authors_full":["Yize Wu","Ke Gao","Ling Li","Yanjun Wu"],"bibkey":"wu2025stable","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2025stable,\n  title = {{Stable-LoRA: Stabilizing Feature Learning of Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Yize Wu and Ke Gao and Ling Li and Yanjun Wu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2603.05204},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05204}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The same \\(\\rho\\) as LoRA. In the first steps an in-place decay \\(A\\leftarrow(1-\\lambda)A\\) precedes each AdamW step until \\(\\|A\\|_F/d_{in}\\le\\|B\\|_F/d_{out}\\).","shape":"LoRA's cone.","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\), \\(A_0\\sim\\mathcal U[-1/d_{in},1/d_{in}]\\): the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. Shrinking \\(A\\) while \\(B=0\\) keeps \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\), so the pointing stays at the same point of the stratum.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\). The shrink is not a gauge move: it scales \\(\\Delta W\\) by \\((1-\\lambda)\\), a decay on one factor (a non-gradient update). The stop test uses Frobenius norms, so the procedure is equivariant only under \\(O(r)\\).","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"A standard \\(sBA\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"Stable-LoRA is LoRA with a transient change of trained-method data: an early decay of \\(A\\), independent of the learning rate, that removes the long-term imprint of a nonzero \\(A_0\\) on width scaling, after which training is plain AdamW. As an object it is LoRA. Like LoRA+ (rates) and LoRA-RITE (preconditioner), all of its content is in the trajectory.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule differs (the shrink is not a gauge move)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O2","reason":"d(LoRA_r) = r(m+n-r) > mr = d(LoRA-FA): shrinking A early does not freeze it."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mdpd","name":"MDPD","full_name":"Masked Dual Path Distillation with fading side networks","paper_title":"Memory-Efficient Transfer Learning with Fading Side Networks via Masked Dual Path Distillation","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"CVPR 2026","arxiv":"2604.09088","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09088","code_url":"https://github.com/Zhang-VKk/MDPD","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"b^{1:L}=\\Phi_{1:L}\\big(x;\\theta_0,\\{\\gamma_l,\\beta_l\\}_{l=1}^{L}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times D_B},\\ \\ s^{l}=\\Theta_l\\big(s^{l-1},\\mathrm{fuse}(b^{l},b^{L})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times D_S},\\ D_S<D_B;\\ \\ Y^{B}=W_B\\,\\mathrm{GAP}(b^{L}),\\ Y^{S}=W_S\\,\\mathrm{GAP}(s^{L});\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}(Y^{S},y)+\\lambda_{log}\\sum_i\\big(Y^{S}_i-\\phi(Y^{B})_i\\big)^2+\\lambda_{sha}\\sum_{l\\in\\text{shallow}}\\big\\|b^{l}-s^{l}M_{down}M_{up}\\big\\|_F^2+\\lambda_{deep}\\sum_{l\\in\\text{deep}}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}m_i\\big\\|b^{l}_i-\\mathcal{G}(\\bar s^{l})_i\\big\\|^2,\\ \\ \\bar s^{l}_i=\\begin{cases}f_{mask}, & m_i=1\\ (\\text{mask ratio }\\lambda)\\\\ s^{l}_i, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: backbone weights } \\theta_0;\\ \\text{trained: per-block LayerNorm } (\\gamma_l,\\beta_l),\\ W_B,\\ \\text{side network } \\Theta,\\ W_S,\\ \\text{bottlenecks } M_{down},M_{up},\\ \\mathcal{G},\\ f_{mask};\\ \\ \\text{inference: } \\hat y=W_B\\,\\mathrm{GAP}\\big(\\Phi_{1:L}(x;\\theta_0,\\{\\gamma_l,\\beta_l\\})\\big)\\ \\text{(side network discarded)}","trainable_params":"\\text{training: } |\\Theta|+\\sum_l|\\phi_l|\\ (\\approx(1+D_S+D_B)d_b+D_B\\ \\text{each})+|\\mathcal{G}|+|f_{mask}|+|W_S|+|W_B|+2D_B\\ \\text{per backbone LayerNorm};\\ \\ \\text{kept at inference: } 2D_B\\ \\text{per LayerNorm}+|W_B|","locus":"training: a thin side network (widths divided by a reduction factor, initialised from pretrained weights) fed by the backbone's per-layer features, plus distillation bottlenecks and a generator; deployed: the LayerNorm affine parameters of every backbone block and a new task head (ViT-B on VTAB-1K, T5-base/large on GLUE, VSE-infinity, CLIP4Clip, CLIP-ViL and MDETR for vision-language tasks)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"A training-only ladder side network serves as scaffolding and is discarded at inference. Two distillation paths couple it to the frozen backbone. In the feature path (backbone to side), each side layer is regressed onto the backbone's features: directly through a bottleneck \\(\\phi(s)=sM_{down}M_{up}\\) in shallow layers, and in deep layers by regenerating the teacher features from masked student tokens with a small convolutional generator \\(\\mathcal{G}\\) (two \\(3\\times3\\) convolutions and a ReLU). In the logit path (side to backbone), the task-trained side head teaches a new backbone head \\(W_B\\). The paper states that the only trainable state left in the backbone is the per-block LayerNorm \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) plus \\(W_B\\), so the deployed model is a normalisation-tuned backbone plus head; the paper does not say what is tuned in its CNN backbones (ResNeXt-101 in VSE\\(\\infty\\), ResNet-101 in MDETR), which have no LayerNorm, and T5's LayerNorm has no shift. The side network acts as an auxiliary teacher whose task knowledge is distilled into that selective subset.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The deployed model changes only normalisation affine parameters and adds a head, with no extra modules or tokens: the new \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) replace the pretrained values in place, and where a LayerNorm feeds only linear maps they could also be folded into those maps. The side network, the bottlenecks \\(\\phi\\) and the generator \\(\\mathcal{G}\\) are discarded.","identity_at_init":"Backbone features are unchanged at initialisation: \\((\\gamma_l,\\beta_l)\\) start at their pretrained values, and the side network does not feed back into the backbone. The task heads \\(W_B,W_S\\) are new, so the output layer is not identity-initialised.","key_idea":"Memory-efficient side-tuning keeps its side network at inference and pays for it in latency. MDPD instead uses the side network as a training-time teacher, mutually distilled with the frozen backbone. Its task knowledge ends up in the backbone's LayerNorm \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) and head, so it can be dropped at deployment.","results_note":"On vision-language tasks MDPD improves accuracy over UniPT and SHERL at almost the same training memory and cuts SHERL's inference time by at least 25.2% (the abstract's figure; the contribution list says 22.5%). On VTAB-1K with ViT-B its mean of 78.3 is the best among the compared PETL and METL methods (HST 78.1, SHERL 77.9), at 2.1 GB training memory.","relations":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses an LST-style side network with ladder inputs from the backbone layers and LST's T5 layer-dropping, and is compared against LST. Unlike LST, the side network is dropped at inference."},{"target":"ln-tuning","type":"combines","note":"Not cited (MDPD cites Kim et al. 2021 for normalisation tuning). The deployed model is a LayerNorm-tuned backbone plus head; MDPD learns those LayerNorm parameters through distillation from a side network instead of the task loss alone."},{"target":"res-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Res-Tuning and its memory-efficient Bypass variant are both compared baselines on VTAB-1K (means 76.3 and 72.3)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09088","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.09088","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2026/html/Zhang_Memory-Efficient_Transfer_Learning_with_Fading_Side_Networks_via_Masked_Dual_CVPR_2026_paper.html","https://github.com/Zhang-VKk/MDPD","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-4"],"authors_full":["Yutong Zhang","Jiaxin Chen","Honglin Chen","Kaiqi Zheng","Shengcai Liao","Hanwen Zhong","Weixin Li","Yunhong Wang"],"bibkey":"zhang2026memory","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026memory,\n  title = {{Memory-Efficient Transfer Learning with Fading Side Networks via Masked Dual Path Distillation}},\n  author = {Yutong Zhang and Jiaxin Chen and Honglin Chen and Kaiqi Zheng and Shengcai Liao and Hanwen Zhong and Weixin Li and Yunhong Wang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2604.09088},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.09088}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The deployed object: the LayerNorm affine parameters of every backbone block plus a new head. The ladder side network, bottlenecks and generator are training-only teachers and are discarded.","shape":"A coordinate subspace (norm parameters and head), as LN-tuning.","base_point":"\\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) start at their pretrained values, so the backbone is unchanged at initialisation. The head is new and not identity-initialised, as in every probing setup.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"As LN-tuning: the gains are diagonal (privileged basis), an input-side scaling of the consuming boxes once folded.","merge":"Replaces the norm parameters in place; folding into the next linear box needs a pre-norm block, as for LN-tuning.","rebasing":"A fixed coordinate block: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"MDPD is LN-tuning reached through a different training signal. A side network (LST-type, an unpointed extension) is trained on the task and mutually distilled with the frozen backbone, and only the backbone's norm parameters and a head survive. The side network lives entirely on the cotangent side: it shapes the gradient that the selective block receives, which the deployed object cannot see.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"ln-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same deployed object (LayerNorm (gamma, beta) of every block, with the same new task head on both sides); only the training signal differs."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"The frozen backbone function (pretrained norm parameters, with the same head on both sides) is in Im MDPD, while LST is unpointed: its side network's up-projection has rank <= d/r, so no LST parameter reproduces it. So Im MDPD is not inside Im LST. LST also keeps its side network at inference."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"riemannion","name":"Riemannion","full_name":"Riemannion (RiemannLoRA): Muon on the fixed-rank manifold, with a locally optimal Riemannian-gradient initialisation (LOI)","paper_title":"LoRA meets Riemannion: Muon Optimizer for Parametrization-independent Low-Rank Adapters","authors":"Bogachev et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2507.12142","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12142","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"X:=\\Delta W\\in\\mathcal{M}_r=\\{X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}:\\operatorname{rank}X=r\\},\\ X=UGV^{\\top}\\ (U^{\\top}U=V^{\\top}V=I_r);\\ \\ P_X(Z)=UU^{\\top}Z+(I-UU^{\\top})ZVV^{\\top};\\ \\ G_t=P_{X_t}\\big(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W'+X_t)\\big)\\ \\text{(one backward pass of a rank-}2r\\text{ LoRA: }\\ \\partial_{Z_1,Z_2}\\mathcal{L}(W'+Z_1V^{\\top}+UZ_2^{\\top})\\text{)};\\ \\ M_t=\\beta\\,P_{X_t}(M_{t-1})+G_t;\\ \\ \\widetilde M_t=P_{X_t}\\big(\\mathrm{Ortho}_{2r}(M_t)\\big),\\ \\mathrm{Ortho}_{2r}(M)=U_MV_M^{\\top}\\ \\text{(singular values of the rank-}\\le 2r\\ M\\text{ set to 1)};\\ \\ X_{t+1}=\\mathrm{SVD}_r\\big(X_t-\\eta\\,\\widetilde M_t\\big);\\ \\ \\text{LOI: } X_0\\in\\arg\\max_{X\\in\\mathcal{M}_r}\\|P_X\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0)\\|_F^2,\\ \\text{used: } X_0=\\alpha\\,U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^{\\top}\\ (\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0)=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}),\\ W'=W_0-X_0;\\ \\ h=(W'+X_t)\\,x;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0\\ (\\text{shifted to } W');\\ \\text{trained: the point } X\\in\\mathcal{M}_r\\text{, stored as thin factors}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{stored per adapted matrix (intrinsic dimension } r(d_{in}+d_{out}-r)\\text{ of }\\mathcal{M}_r\\text{)};\\ \\text{heavy-ball momentum kept as a rank-}\\le 2r\\text{ tangent vector, } 2r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"LoRA target matrices: attention and MLP projections of Llama-3-8B (commonsense reasoning); q, k, v and output projections of every attention layer of Stable Diffusion 2 (subject-driven generation)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"The function class is LoRA's (an additive rank-\\(r\\) update \\(\\Delta W=X\\)), but the optimisation variable is the point \\(X\\) on the fixed-rank manifold \\(\\mathcal{M}_r\\), which is the quotient \\((\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}_*\\times\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r}_*)/GL(r)\\) of full-rank factor pairs under \\((B,A)\\sim(BS,S^{-1}A)\\); every step is therefore independent of the factorisation gauge. The Riemannian gradient \\(P_X(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L})\\) has rank \\(\\le 2r\\) and is obtained without forming \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}\\), by differentiating \\(\\mathcal{L}(W'+Z_1V^{\\top}+UZ_2^{\\top})\\) at \\(Z_1=0\\) (a rank-\\(2r\\) LoRA forward and backward pass). The Muon step is the linear-minimisation-oracle direction over the spectral-norm ball restricted to the tangent space, approximated by \\(P_X(\\mathrm{Ortho}_{2r}(M_t))\\); the singular values of this direction stayed in \\((0.9,1.1)\\) in their runs. Momentum is moved by projection transport and the retraction is a thin truncated SVD (Algorithm 4 also applies a decoupled weight decay \\(\\gamma\\) inside the retraction step), so a step costs \\(O((d_{in}+d_{out})r^2+r^3)\\) plus one backward pass, as for factor-wise Muon. LOI chooses the starting point whose tangent space captures most of the full gradient. Theorem 5.1 parametrises all maximisers by \\(S\\in GL_{2r}\\) acting on the top-\\(2r\\) gradient singular pairs (assuming \\(\\sigma_{2r}\\neq\\sigma_{2r+1}\\)); the experiments use \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha I_r,I_r)\\), i.e. \\(X_0=\\alpha U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^{\\top}\\), computed by a randomised SVD with \\(2(q+1)\\) extra backward passes (about 0.25% of fine-tuning time). The paper writes the orthogonalisation as \\(\\mathrm{Ortho}_r\\) although it sets the first \\(2r\\) singular values to 1; the formula here calls it \\(\\mathrm{Ortho}_{2r}\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained update is a rank-\\(r\\) matrix in thin factored form, so \\(W=W'+X_T\\) (with \\(W'=W_0-X_0\\) under LOI) folds into one dense weight exactly as LoRA does.","identity_at_init":"Under LOI the frozen weight is shifted, \\(W'=W_0-X_0\\), so \\(W'+X_0=W_0\\) and the model is unchanged at step 0. \\(\\alpha\\) is kept small, which is safe because no Gram matrix is inverted. A variant with LoRA-style zero initialisation is also reported.","key_idea":"Optimise the LoRA update as a point on the fixed-rank manifold \\(\\mathcal{M}_r\\) rather than as two factors, so the step cannot depend on the \\(GL(r)\\) gauge \\((B,A)\\sim(BS,S^{-1}A)\\), and take Muon's orthogonalised (spectral-norm steepest-descent) step inside the tangent space. A matching initialisation picks the rank-\\(r\\) point whose tangent space is best aligned with the full fine-tuning gradient.","results_note":"On eight commonsense-reasoning tasks with Llama-3-8B at rank 16, Riemannion averages 88.1% versus 87.1% for AdamW-trained LoRA, 86.8% for Riemannian-preconditioned AdamW, 86.6% for DoRA, 84.5% for LoRA-RITE and 83.0% for factor-wise Muon.","relations":[{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main geometry-aware baseline (RPrecAdamW, 86.8 vs 88.1). Zhang and Pilanci precondition each factor with the other factor's \\(r\\times r\\) Gram inverse inside an Adam-style update, which keeps a dependence on the parametrisation; Riemannion works on the fixed-rank manifold itself and inverts no Gram matrix."},{"target":"lora-rite","type":"competes-with","note":"Both aim at transformation-invariant LoRA updates. LoRA-RITE equilibrates an adaptive update in factor space; Riemannion gets invariance by construction on the manifold. In the paper's Llama-3-8B table LoRA-RITE scores 84.5 vs 88.1."},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"generalizes","note":"LOI maximises tangent-space alignment with the full gradient, and the paper states the link to LoRA-GA. Substituting a block-anti-diagonal \\(S\\) into Theorem 5.1 gives \\(U_{r,2r}\\Sigma_{r,2r}S_{21}S_{12}^{-1}\\Sigma_{1,r}V_{1,r}^{\\top}\\), which is LoRA-GA's product initialisation (proportional to \\(U_{r+1:2r}V_{1:r}^{\\top}\\)) when \\(S_{21}S_{12}^{-1}\\propto(\\Sigma_{r,2r}\\Sigma_{1,r})^{-1}\\); the paper itself uses the mirrored member \\(\\alpha U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^{\\top}\\). Both shift \\(W_0\\) by the initial update."},{"target":"fixed-rank-riemannian-quotient","type":"builds-on","note":"The geometry of rank-\\(r\\) matrices, here as the \\(GL(r)\\) quotient of factor pairs. Riemannion does not cite this paper; it runs Muon with the embedded-manifold version of the same geometry (tangent projection, truncated-SVD retraction, projection transport; Vandereycken 2013, Absil and Oseledets 2015)."},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline: 86.6 vs 88.1 average on Llama-3-8B commonsense reasoning at rank 16."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps LoRA's additive rank-\\(r\\) update and forward pass; only the optimiser, the representation of the iterate and the initialisation change."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12142","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.12142","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10009014","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2507.12142","https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12142v1","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/optimizers"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Vladimir Bogachev","Vladimir Aletov","Alexander Molozhavenko","Denis Bobkov","Vera Soboleva","Aibek Alanov","Maxim Rakhuba"],"bibkey":"bogachev2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bogachev2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA meets Riemannion: Muon Optimizer for Parametrization-independent Low-Rank Adapters}},\n  author = {Vladimir Bogachev and Vladimir Aletov and Alexander Molozhavenko and Denis Bobkov and Vera Soboleva and Aibek Alanov and Maxim Rakhuba},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2507.12142},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12142}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The trained model is \\(f(W'+X)\\) with the original architecture: LoRA's additive rank-\\(r\\) update, but the iterate is the point \\(X\\in\\mathcal M_r\\) itself and the frozen weight is shifted to \\(W'=W_0-X_0\\) (LOI).","shape":"\\((W_0-X_0)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): LoRA's cone translated to the vertex \\(W_0-X_0\\), with \\(X_0=\\alpha U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^\\top\\) of rank \\(r\\), so \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point (stratum S, as LoRA-GA). The paper's zero-init variant is LoRA's apex cone instead.","base_point":"\\(S_1=T_{X_0}\\mathcal M_r\\), of dimension \\(r(m+n-r)=d\\): regular (Thm II.2(b), stratum S), and \\(W'+X_0=W_0\\) exactly.","gauge":"The trained variable is the point \\(X\\in\\mathcal M_r=(\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}_*\\times\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}_*)/GL_r\\), so \\(\\rho\\) is an embedding. The thin-factor storage carries a \\(GL_r\\) fibre that no step sees (tangent projection, Muon direction and truncated-SVD retraction are computed on the quotient): the gauge is quotiented out, not merely respected (with the factors read as \\(Q\\) it would be \\(GL_r\\), all of it respected, as undamped scaled GD).","covariance":"LOI uses bare singular frames of \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L(W_0)\\) without singular values: scale-invariant, \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\) as for LoRA-GA, coded OxO. The Muon polar factor is \\(O\\times O\\)-equivariant as well; the zero-init variant would be GLxGL.","merge":"\\(W'+X_T\\) is one dense matrix stored as thin factors, as for LoRA.","rebasing":"A fixed-rank shape closed under scaling: \\(\\to\\Theta\\) after \\(\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil\\) merges, as LoRA (Thm V.3(a))."},"categorical_reading":"Riemannion changes the object and not only the metric: its parameter space is the fixed-rank manifold \\(\\mathcal M_r\\), the quotient of LoRA's factor space by \\(GL_r\\), so \\(\\rho\\) is an embedding and the gauge question disappears instead of being answered by a gauge-respecting cometric (scaled GD, Thm III.12(c)). LOI is a Type II pointing: the rank-\\(r\\) start \\(\\alpha U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^\\top\\) in the gradient's top-\\(2r\\) frame with a compensated residual, the mirrored member of the family that contains LoRA-GA's start.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"X\\mapsto\\big([\\,X(B_0^{+}X)^{+},\\ -B_0\\,],\\ [\\,B_0^{+}X;\\ A_0\\,]\\big),\\qquad X_0=B_0A_0","note":"LOI instance. Thm II.1 conversion: Riemannion_r into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0, at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) (product 0, as for LoRA-GA and PiSSA). The first block is a smooth local section of the quotient map near X_0 (rank r), with h(X_0) = ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]); checked numerically (error 2e-15). With the stored thin factors (B,A) it reads h(B,A) = ([B,-B_0],[A;A_0])."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-ga","test":"O1","reason":"Different points of stratum S: LOI's vertex W_0 - alpha U_{1:r}V_{r+1:2r}^T against LoRA-GA's W_0 - P_GA with P_GA proportional to U_{r+1:2r}V_{1:r}^T (the paper's own Thm 5.1 contains both; it uses the mirrored member). Translated cones with distinct vertices are not nested (Thm II.2(a),(d))."},{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"LOI instance: a smooth Type II start against LoRA's T_A apex at equal rank, so the images are incomparable (Thm II.2(d)). The paper's zero-init variant is LoRA itself (h = id)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"poet-x","name":"POET-X","full_name":"POET-X: scalable reparameterized training by orthogonal equivalence transformation (memory-efficient POET)","paper_title":"POET-X: Memory-efficient LLM Training by Scaling Orthogonal Transformation","authors":"Qiu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight, oral presentation)","arxiv":"2603.05500","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05500","code_url":"https://github.com/Sphere-AI-Lab/poet","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_{RP}=R\\,W_0\\,P,\\ \\ W_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}\\ (m=d_{in},\\ n=d_{out};\\ \\text{fixed random initialisation, e.g. normalised Gaussian}),\\ \\ R\\in O(m),\\ P\\in O(n);\\ \\ y=W_{RP}^{\\top}x=P^{\\top}W_0^{\\top}R^{\\top}x\\ \\ (\\text{so in the }h=Wx\\text{ convention }W=P^{\\top}W_0^{\\top}R^{\\top}:\\ R\\text{ acts on the input side, }P\\text{ on the output side});\\ \\ \\text{round }k:\\ R_k=\\Psi_k^{\\top}\\operatorname{Diag}\\big(\\tilde G_k^{1},\\dots,\\tilde G_k^{\\lceil m/b\\rceil}\\big)\\Psi_k,\\ P_k=\\Phi_k^{\\top}\\operatorname{Diag}\\big(\\tilde H_k^{1},\\dots,\\tilde H_k^{\\lceil n/b\\rceil}\\big)\\Phi_k\\ \\ (\\Psi_k,\\Phi_k\\text{ random permutations});\\ \\ \\tilde G=(I+Q)(I+Q+Q^{2}+Q^{3})=I+2Q+2Q^{2}+2Q^{3}+Q^{4}\\approx(I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1},\\ Q=-Q^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times b}\\ \\text{(Cayley--Neumann, }k=3\\text{; only the strict upper triangle of }Q\\text{ stored)};\\ \\ y=P_k^{\\top}\\big(W_{k-1}^{\\top}(R_k^{\\top}x)\\big)\\ \\text{(input-centric: three matrix--vector products)};\\ \\ \\text{every 400 steps (`POET reset gap'): }W_k=R_kW_{k-1}P_k,\\ Q\\leftarrow0,\\ \\text{new permutations};\\ \\ \\text{frozen within a round: }W_{k-1};\\ \\text{trained: the skew blocks of }R_k,P_k\\ (\\text{embeddings, LM head and norms are trained directly})","trainable_params":"\\big(\\lceil d_{in}/b\\rceil+\\lceil d_{out}/b\\rceil\\big)\\tfrac{b(b-1)}{2}=\\tfrac{(b-1)(d_{in}+d_{out})}{2}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per round when }b\\mid d_{in},d_{out};\\ \\text{Llama-3B }(d=2560,\\ d_{ff}=7168,\\ 32\\text{ layers},\\ \\textstyle\\sum(d_{in}+d_{out})=1{,}589{,}248):\\ 127.5\\times1{,}589{,}248=202.6\\text{M }(b=256)\\text{ or }255.5\\times1{,}589{,}248=406.1\\text{M }(b=512)\\text{ POET parameters};\\ \\text{adding the }164.0\\text{M embedding, LM-head and norm parameters reproduces the reported }366.64\\text{M and }570.06\\text{M of }2764.47\\text{M};\\ \\text{the merged }W\\text{ is a full matrix}","locus":"Linear layers of Llama-architecture LLMs trained from scratch on C4: Llama-3B for the perplexity tables (60B tokens; 10B for POET-XQ), Llama-8B for the wall-clock curves, and 8B and 13B in the memory and throughput benchmarks. \\(W_0\\) is a random initialisation, not a pretrained weight. The original POET was also used to fine-tune BART-large (its App. L).","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"Two-sided orthogonal equivalence: in the paper's convention \\(W_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{out}}\\) and \\(y=(RW_0P)^{\\top}x\\), so \\(R\\in O(d_{in})\\) acts on the input side and \\(P\\in O(d_{out})\\) on the output side, and the weight moves on the orbit \\(\\{RW_0P\\}\\) of the \\(O(d_{in})\\times O(d_{out})\\) action, so with exact orthogonality its singular values equal those of \\(W_0\\) throughout training (the Cayley--Neumann truncation makes this approximate). OFT is the one-sided case \\(P=I\\). Large orthogonal matrices are reached by stochastic primitive optimisation: each round trains only permutation-conjugated block-diagonal factors \\(\\Psi^{\\top}\\operatorname{Diag}(\\tilde G^{j})\\Psi\\) with \\(b\\times b\\) blocks, merges them into the weight after a fixed number of steps (merge-then-reinitialise; 400 steps in the reported runs) and redraws the permutations, so the accumulated transform \\((R_K\\cdots R_1)W_0(P_1\\cdots P_K)\\) can range over the full groups. POET-X keeps this algorithm and changes how it is computed: an input-centric order \\(P^{\\top}(W^{\\top}(R^{\\top}x))\\), so no transformed weight is stored for backpropagation; batched per-block products instead of materialised block-diagonal matrices; permutations as index maps, with two of the four folded into the frozen weight once per round; skew blocks stored as \\(b(b-1)/2\\) parameters; fused Triton kernels for the Cayley--Neumann forward and backward; and a recomputation variant (POET-X\\(_{\\text{mem}}\\)) that also allows low-bit frozen weights (POET-XQ).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(R_kW_{k-1}P_k\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape. Merging into the weight happens periodically by design and at the end of training, so inference uses one ordinary matrix with no overhead.","identity_at_init":"\\(Q=0\\) gives \\(\\tilde G=I\\), hence \\(R_k=P_k=I\\) and \\(W=W_{k-1}\\) at the start of every round (and \\(W=W_0\\) at the start of training).","key_idea":"Learn each weight as an orthogonal equivalence transform \\(W=RW_0P\\) of a fixed matrix, which pins its spectrum, and make this trainable at LLM scale by optimising only permuted block-diagonal orthogonal factors that are periodically merged. POET-X's contribution is the implementation: an input-centric, kernel-fused computation that brings POET's memory down to LoRA-like levels and its speed close to AdamW.","results_note":"Pretraining Llama-3B on 60B C4 tokens, POET-X with \\(b=512\\) (570M trainable parameters) reaches validation perplexity 12.05 versus 12.69 for AdamW and 11.45 for Muon, using 68.5 GB of GPU memory versus 81.0 GB and 70.9 GB.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"generalizes","note":"OFT's \\(W=RW_0\\) is the one-sided case \\(P=I\\) on a pretrained \\(W_0\\). POET acts from both sides on a random \\(W_0\\) and merges periodically; both preserve the singular values of \\(W_0\\) (OFT preserves hyperspherical energy as well)."},{"target":"boft","type":"builds-on","note":"BOFT builds \\(R\\) from butterfly products of permuted block-diagonal orthogonal factors. POET-X uses one permuted block-diagonal factor per side per round and gets coverage from redrawn permutations across merge rounds. The POET-X paper cites BOFT only as related sparse-training work."},{"target":"oftv2","type":"builds-on","note":"Same group. POET-X carries OFTv2's input-centric (matrix-free) computation into the two-sided case, where the gradient for the second orthogonal factor otherwise needs the full weight. The Cayley--Neumann parameterisation comes from the original POET (arXiv 2506.08001) and is shared with OFTv2."},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both pretrain with a parameter-efficient update that is merged into the weight and re-initialised periodically. ReLoRA's update is additive and low-rank; POET-X's is multiplicative, orthogonal and spectrum-preserving."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Memory-efficient pretraining baseline: 14.88 vs 12.05 validation perplexity on Llama-3B (60B tokens)."},{"target":"apollo","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline: 12.97 vs 12.05 on Llama-3B; in the 8-bit setting Q-APOLLO reaches 20.49 vs 14.78 for POET-XQ."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05500","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.05500","https://spherelab.ai/poetx/","https://github.com/Sphere-AI-Lab/poet","https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08001","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.08001","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/oral/71112","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2603.05500","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Zeju Qiu","Lixin Liu","Adrian Weller","Han Shi","Weiyang Liu"],"bibkey":"qiu2026poet","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qiu2026poet,\n  title = {{POET-X: Memory-efficient LLM Training by Scaling Orthogonal Transformation}},\n  author = {Zeju Qiu and Lixin Liu and Adrian Weller and Han Shi and Weiyang Liu},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2603.05500},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05500}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A merge-and-restart scheme of two-sided action methods: each round trains \\(W\\mapsto P_k^\\top W_{k-1}R_k^\\top\\) (convention \\(y=Wx\\): \\(R_k\\in O(d_{in})\\) on the input side, \\(P_k\\in O(d_{out})\\) on the output side), merges every 400 steps, resets \\(Q=0\\) and redraws the permutations. \\(\\theta_0\\) is a random initialisation (pretraining), not a pretrained weight.","shape":"Per round the orbit of \\(W_{k-1}\\) under permutation-conjugated block-diagonal factors on both sides. With exact Cayley it lies in \\(O(m)W_{k-1}O(n)\\) (spectrum fixed); the released Cayley–Neumann factor \\((I+Q)(I+Q+Q^2+Q^3)=C(Q)(I-Q^4)\\) is only quasi-orthogonal, \\(R^\\top R=(I-Q^4)^2\\) (both checked to \\(10^{-16}\\)), coded Orb as OFTv2.","base_point":"At \\(Q=0\\), \\(\\dim S_1=\\tfrac{b-1}2(d_{in}+d_{out})\\), the number of skew parameters, for generic \\(W\\) (checked: 30 of 30 at \\(m=8,n=12,b=4\\)): regular, every round starting at \\(W_{k-1}\\).","gauge":"None: the per-round orbit map has full rank at \\(Q=0\\) and at generic \\(Q\\) (checked: 30 of 30 at \\(d_{out}=8,d_{in}=12,b=4\\)), and the Cayley–Neumann map is a local diffeomorphism at \\(0\\). A skew pair fixing \\(W\\) would need \\(\\mathrm{range}\\,\\Omega_{in}\\subseteq\\ker W\\); for unblocked factors with \\(d_{in}\\neq d_{out}\\) this leaves an \\(\\mathfrak{so}(|d_{in}-d_{out}|)\\) stabiliser, but a generic kernel meets no \\(b\\)-coordinate block when \\(b\\le\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\) (true for \\(b=256,512\\)), so the block-diagonal round has trivial gauge.","covariance":"A fixed draw imposes a block partition on both sides (\\(\\Pi\\)), but the uniformly redrawn permutations make the law invariant under signed permutations and homotheties on each side: \\(\\mathbb R^\\times B_{d_{out}}\\times\\mathbb R^\\times B_{d_{in}}\\) in law, nearest key MonxMon (as LoRA-SB and DARE). The exact-Cayley accumulated orbit is \\(CO\\times CO\\).","merge":"\\(P_k^\\top W_{k-1}R_k^\\top\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape; merging is part of the schedule.","rebasing":"Itself a rebasing scheme. Exact Cayley with redrawn permutations connects the block hypergraphs, so the reach is \\(SO(d_{out})\\,W_0\\,SO(d_{in})\\) (Thm V.3(b),(c)), spectrum fixed. With the released Cayley–Neumann factors and \\(\\lVert Q\\rVert_{\\rm op}\\le2^{1/4}\\) every factor is a contraction, so repeated merges shrink every singular value monotonically (Thm V.3, Cor 4)."},"categorical_reading":"POET-X is the multiplicative twin of ReLoRA, run on both sides of the weight: a rebasing scheme whose per-round shape is a two-sided block-orthogonal orbit, with redrawn random permutations doing the work that butterfly factors do in BOFT, connecting the block hypergraph across rounds (Thm V.3(c)). Its spectrum-preservation property holds for exactly orthogonal factors only: with the released Cayley–Neumann map each merge is a contraction in the operating regime, so over many merges the spectrum drifts downward rather than staying fixed.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"oftv2","test":"O2","reason":"At equal block size b a POET-X round has d = (b-1)(d_in+d_out)/2 skew parameters on both sides, against OFTv2's d_in(b-1)/2 on the input side. OFTv2 maps into a round (freeze P at I, A2), not conversely."},{"target":"relora","test":"O2","reason":"Per stage, P^T W R^T - W has rank up to min(m,n) for generic block-orthogonal factors, while a ReLoRA stage update has rank at most r."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"lora-mixer","name":"LoRA-Mixer","full_name":"LoRA-Mixer: mixture of LoRA experts routed into the attention/SSM projections, with a Routing Specialization Balance Loss (RSL)","paper_title":"LoRA-Mixer: Coordinate Modular LoRA Experts Through Serial Attention Routing","authors":"Li et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2507.00029","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00029","code_url":"https://github.com/hustcselwb/LoRA-Mixer","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\sum_{e\\in\\operatorname{TopK}(p(x))}p_e(x)\\,B_eA_ex,\\ \\ p(x)=\\operatorname{softmax}\\big(G(x)\\big)\\in\\Delta^{E-1},\\ B_e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\ (\\text{soft fusion over all } E\\text{ experts while training the router; sparse top-}K\\text{ at inference});\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}_{total}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\alpha\\,\\mathcal{L}_{RSL}+\\beta\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\|\\theta_i-\\theta_i^{(0)}\\|^{2},\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}_{RSL}=\\alpha\\sum_{i}\\bar p_i\\bar f_i-\\lambda\\,\\mathbb{E}_x\\big[\\mathcal{H}(p(x))\\big];\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0\\text{ (and public or pre-trained experts in the plug-and-play regime); trained: the router } G\\text{ (and optionally the experts)}","trainable_params":"E\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{experts}+\\text{a router per adapted projection (about }0.04\\%\\text{ of LLaMA3-8B in total)};\\ 3.88\\%\\text{ of LLaMA3-8B with six trained experts; router only when frozen public LoRAs are composed}","locus":"Linear projections that feed and read out the token mixer: the q/k/v/o attention projections of LLaMA3-8B and Mistral-7B, and the projection layers of the Falcon-Mamba-7B mixer (App. A.11 describes the in-, out-, x- and dt-projections without saying exactly which are adapted). Plug-and-play routing over public LoRAHub LoRAs is shown on Flan-T5, and the LoRA-LEGO comparison uses LLaMA2-7B. The abstract contrasts this with methods that 'primarily' target FFN blocks.","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Token-level mixture of \\(E\\) low-rank deltas on a frozen projection, \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_ep_e(x)B_eA_e\\), placed on the attention (or SSM) input and output projections, so the routed update passes through the token mixer in series instead of acting as a parallel branch or an FFN switch. Experts come from three sources: public LoRAs (e.g. LoRAHub modules) kept frozen, independently trained LoRAs, or experts trained jointly with deterministic hard routing by domain label. The router is then trained on a small mixed set (about 2k examples) with soft fusion; inference uses sparse top-\\(K\\) fusion, and accuracy rises from \\(K=1\\) to \\(K=3\\) and then falls in their ablation. RSL adds an entropy term to the Switch-style load-balancing loss. As printed, \\(\\mathcal{L}_{RSL}\\) subtracts \\(\\lambda\\mathbb{E}[\\mathcal{H}(p)]\\), which rewards high routing entropy and matches the paper's strong-convexity argument, while other passages describe the term as lowering entropy to sharpen routing. A quadratic anchor keeps selected experts near their first-stage weights. The router architecture is not specified beyond its size. The paper writes each expert as \\(\\Delta W^{(e)}=A^{(e)}B^{(e)}\\) with \\(A^{(e)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\); formula_latex uses \\(B_eA_e\\) with \\(B_e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\), the same product with the letters swapped.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The routing weights \\(p(x)\\) change with every token, so \\(\\sum_ep_e(x)B_eA_e\\) has no single static form to fold into \\(W_0\\); only a fixed, input-independent expert choice could be merged.","identity_at_init":"Not identity when composing trained LoRAs: the frozen experts act from the first step and only the router is learned. The paper does not state the initialisation of experts trained from scratch, and the official repository contains only a README.","key_idea":"Put a routed mixture of LoRA experts into the projections that feed and read out attention or the SSM, so expert outputs pass through the token mixer, and train the router with a load-balance-plus-entropy loss that needs little data. Off-the-shelf LoRAs can then be composed plug-and-play, on Transformers and Mamba alike.","results_note":"On LLaMA3-8B with six experts (3.88% trainable parameters, 48% of MixLoRA's 8.08%), LoRA-Mixer beats MixLoRA on all seven tasks of the main table, for example 65.53 vs 64.44 on GSM8K and 57.32 vs 55.49 on HumanEval.","relations":[{"target":"mixlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main LoRA-MoE baseline: top-k LoRA experts inside the FFN. LoRA-Mixer routes on attention/SSM projections with about half the trainable parameters."},{"target":"mole","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline. MoLE learns gating over frozen trained LoRA layers; the paper describes it as close in inference cost but coarser-grained and weaker."},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"Gradient-free few-shot composition of LoRAHub modules (baseline). LoRA-Mixer reuses the same kind of public modules but learns token-level routing."},{"target":"lora-lego","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline on CoLA, SST-2, MRPC and RTE with LLaMA2-7B; LoRA-Mixer is better on three of the four."},{"target":"phatgoose","type":"competes-with","note":"Post-hoc routing over independently trained adapters; compared in and out of distribution (QQP, RTE, MRPC)."},{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. X-LoRA also learns token-level mixing over frozen trained LoRAs, but scales every adapter of a layer from the hidden state instead of top-K routing on the mixer projections."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00029","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.00029","https://github.com/hustcselwb/LoRA-Mixer","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10010501","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2507.00029","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Wenbing Li","Zikai Song","Hang Zhou","Yunyao Zhang","Junqing Yu","Wei Yang"],"bibkey":"li2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-Mixer: Coordinate Modular LoRA Experts Through Serial Attention Routing}},\n  author = {Wenbing Li and Zikai Song and Hang Zhou and Yunyao Zhang and Junqing Yu and Wei Yang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2507.00029},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00029}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_{e\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(p(x))}p_e(x)B_eA_e\\) on the q/k/v/o (or SSM) projections, with a softmax router; experts are frozen public LoRAs (plug-and-play) or trained.","shape":"Token-dependent mixture: no single weight-space image, and top-\\(K\\) makes the realised functions discontinuous at routing ties.","base_point":"The experts are trained or public LoRAs acting from the first routing step, and the softmax weights never vanish together, so no router value gives the frozen function: unpointed (as MoLE). The init of experts trained from scratch is not stated (the repository holds only a README).","gauge":"With trained experts (main table) \\(GL_r\\) on each expert's rank wire, since the scalar gate slides through \\(B_eA_e\\); router-only training leaves only the softmax translation, a kernel-type redundancy.","covariance":"Dense LoRA experts and a dense router transform together: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\) (routed-mixture convention).","merge":"Thm VI.3: token-dependent gates; M1 only for a frozen expert choice.","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Mixer is an \\(X\\)-point of a top-\\(K\\) LoRA mixture placed on the projections that feed and read out the token mixer, so the routed update passes through attention or the SSM scan in series. Fibrewise it is a rank-\\(Er\\) LoRA whose blocks are weighted by the routing scores; what is new is the locus and the balance-plus-entropy loss, not the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_e,A_e)_e,G\\big)=\\big(\\,[\\,\\mathbb 1_{e\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(x)}\\,p_e(x)\\,B_e\\,]_{e=1}^{E},\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_E]\\,\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens (per adapted projection), into LoRA_{Er} pointed at h(q_0) (unpointed slice, as PHATGOOSE -> LoRA); smooth away from top-K ties. A renormalisation over the selected set, if used, is absorbed into p_e."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mixlora","test":"O1","reason":"MixLoRA routes experts only on the FFN and puts static, input-independent LoRAs on q, k, v, o. LoRA-Mixer's token-dependent updates on the attention projections are therefore generically not MixLoRA realisations (and LoRA-Mixer leaves the FFN frozen)."},{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"Token-dependent gates give functions that generically no single weight realises (M-infinity), so they are not in LoraHub's static weight-space image (as for MoLE and X-LoRA)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"in-place-ttt","name":"In-Place TTT","full_name":"In-Place Test-Time Training: the MLP down-projection as chunk-wise fast weights with an LM-aligned target","paper_title":"In-Place Test-Time Training","authors":"Feng et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (oral)","arxiv":"2604.06169","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06169","code_url":"https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/In-Place-TTT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"Z=\\phi(HW_{gate}^{\\top})\\odot(HW_{up}^{\\top})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d_{ff}},\\ \\text{split into chunks } Z_{[i]}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{C\\times d_{ff}};\\ \\ O_{[i]}=Z_{[i]}\\big(W_{down}^{(i)}\\big)^{\\top};\\ \\ \\hat V=\\operatorname{Conv1D}(X_0)\\,W_{target}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d};\\ \\ W_{down}^{(i+1)}=W_{down}^{(i)}+\\eta\\,\\hat V_{[i]}^{\\top}Z_{[i]}\\ \\ \\big(\\text{one gradient step on } -\\langle Z_{[i]}W^{\\top},\\hat V_{[i]}\\rangle_F\\big);\\ \\ W_{down}^{(0)}=W_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d_{ff}}\\ \\text{(pretrained), reset at document boundaries};\\ \\ \\text{equivalently } W_{down}^{(i)}=W_0+\\eta\\textstyle\\sum_{j<i}\\hat V_{[j]}^{\\top}Z_{[j]}\\ \\text{(a prefix sum, so chunks run in parallel)};\\ \\ \\text{slow weights (all, plus new } W_{target}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times d}\\text{ and a depthwise kernel-5 Conv1D) trained by continual LM training; fast weights updated in closed form at inference}","trainable_params":"\\text{outer loop: the whole model (continual training, AdamW, lr } 5\\times10^{-6}\\text{) plus } d^{2}+5d\\text{ new parameters per TTT layer } (W_{target},\\ \\text{depthwise Conv1D});\\ \\text{inner loop: a fast-weight state } d\\times d_{ff}\\text{ per TTT layer, changed by rank-}\\le C\\text{ increments, with no gradients stored}","locus":"Down-projection \\(W_{down}\\) of the gated MLP in a subset of layers (every sixth layer, \\([0,6,\\dots,36]\\), in the released long-context Qwen3 config): Qwen3-4B-Base, LLaMA-3.1-8B and Qwen3-14B-Base as a drop-in, and 0.5B to 4B models trained from scratch.","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"At inference the only parameters that change are the MLP down-projections of the TTT layers. Each change is the closed-form gradient step of a linear (negative inner-product) loss, so the delta added after chunk \\(i\\) is the rank-\\(\\le C\\) outer-product sum \\(\\eta\\hat V_{[i]}^{\\top}Z_{[i]}\\) between the MLP's intermediate activations and an LM-aligned target. The target mixes nearby future-token embeddings through a short convolution and a learned \\(d\\times d\\) map, so the fast weight stores associations from keys to the tokens that follow them; an induction-head argument shows that the next-token target raises the correct logit while a reconstruction target does not. Apply-then-update keeps the scheme causal: chunk \\(i\\) is processed with \\(W_0+\\eta\\sum_{j<i}\\Delta_j\\), so the convolution may look ahead inside a chunk. The released code uses a centred depthwise kernel of size 5 confined to each chunk and, by default, the normalised MLP input rather than the token embedding as the target source. Large chunks give parallelism (\\(C=512\\) to \\(1024\\) is best in the ablation, 4096 in the released config, whose inner rate is \\(\\eta=3\\)), and inference clips each \\(\\|\\Delta_i\\|_F\\) to \\(\\tau\\) (\\(\\tau=10^{-5}\\) for all reported Qwen3-4B evaluations). The slow weights, including the new target modules, are learned by ordinary continual pretraining through the unrolled updates.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The fast-weight delta \\(\\eta\\sum_{j<i}\\hat V_{[j]}^{\\top}Z_{[j]}\\) depends on the current context and is reset at every document boundary, so there is nothing static to fold; the small target modules stay as extra parameters at inference.","identity_at_init":"The Conv1D kernel is zero-initialised (and \\(W_{target}\\) is a small random diagonal), so \\(\\hat V=0\\) and every fast-weight delta vanishes when continual training starts: the warm-started model computes exactly the pretrained function. At test time each document starts from \\(W_{down}^{(0)}=W_0\\).","key_idea":"Turn an existing MLP's down-projection into test-time-training fast weights instead of adding a new recurrent layer, update it once per large chunk with a closed-form step, and point the update at next-token information rather than a reconstruction of the current token. Test-time weight adaptation then becomes a drop-in upgrade of a pretrained LLM that is compatible with context parallelism.","results_note":"After identical continual training of Qwen3-4B-Base, In-Place TTT raises RULER accuracy at 64k, 128k and 256k from 74.3, 74.8 and 41.7 to 78.7, 77.0 and 43.9, with near-equal scores at 4k to 32k.","relations":[{"target":"seal","type":"competes-with","note":"Both let an LLM change its own weights from new information. SEAL writes self-edits and applies LoRA SFT steps inside an RL outer loop; In-Place TTT applies a closed-form update to MLP down-projections inside the forward pass, chunk by chunk, with no backpropagation at inference."},{"target":"sparse-memory-finetuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Selective-locus counterpart. Sparse Memory Finetuning updates only the memory-layer slots that a batch activates and keeps the change; In-Place TTT updates a fixed locus (\\(W_{down}\\) of chosen layers) transiently per context and then resets it."},{"target":"doc-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both internalise a context into weights. Doc-to-LoRA generates a persistent LoRA from a document with a hypernetwork; In-Place TTT accumulates an additive update to \\(W_{down}\\) online, of rank at most \\(C\\) per chunk, over contexts up to 128k tokens and beyond."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06169","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.06169","https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/In-Place-TTT","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ByteDance-Seed/In-Place-TTT/HEAD/hf_models/hf_qwen3/modeling_qwen3.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ByteDance-Seed/In-Place-TTT/HEAD/configs/pretrain/qwen3_longct.yaml","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/oral/10008405","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2604.06169","https://iclr.cc/Conferences/2026/Dates","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Guhao Feng","Shengjie Luo","Kai Hua","Ge Zhang","Di He","Wenhao Huang","Tianle Cai"],"bibkey":"feng2025place","bibtex":"@inproceedings{feng2025place,\n  title = {{In-Place Test-Time Training}},\n  author = {Guhao Feng and Shengjie Luo and Kai Hua and Ge Zhang and Di He and Wenhao Huang and Tianle Cai},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2604.06169},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06169}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over (document, chunk) positions: chunk \\(i\\) runs with \\(W_{down}^{(i)}=W_0+\\eta\\sum_{j<i}\\hat V_{[j]}^\\top Z_{[j]}\\), written in closed form from the context by the slow modules \\(W_{target}\\) and a depthwise Conv1D. The outer loop also continually trains every backbone weight, which lies outside the slice; the coordinates describe the inference-time object over the warm-started base.","shape":"Different chunks of one document see different weights, so there is no single weight-space image (compared through \\(\\Phi\\)); each fibre is a rank-\\(\\le(i-1)C\\) additive update of \\(W_{down}\\).","base_point":"The Conv1D kernel is zero-initialised, so \\(\\hat V=0\\) and every fast-weight delta vanishes: the frozen function at every position (neutral). There \\(W_{target}\\) is a zero-gated factor, so only the kernel moves to first order (Lemma II.4).","gauge":"\\((k_c,\\ \\text{row }c\\text{ of }W_{target})\\mapsto(\\lambda_ck_c,\\ \\lambda_c^{-1}\\cdot)\\) slides through the depthwise convolution: a \\(d\\)-dimensional torus (\\(\\eta\\) is a fixed hyperparameter).","covariance":"The update is a gradient step \\(\\eta\\hat V^\\top Z\\) whose input side is the box's own activations, which transform as a covector, so it is covariant on the input side only under \\(CO(d_{ff})\\); the output side is absorbed by the dense trainable \\(W_{target}\\) (\\(GL(d)\\)). GLxCO.","merge":"A position-gated edit (Thm VI.3(a)): no position-independent weight, and it is reset at every document boundary.","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"In-Place TTT is a context-indexed section of 'fine-tune \\(W_{down}\\) only': a causal fast-weight memory written by one closed-form gradient step per chunk, i.e. a chunked linear-attention branch living inside an existing MLP box. Per (document, chunk) fibre it is a LoRA on \\(W_{down}\\) whose \\(A\\) factor is the activations of the earlier chunks and whose \\(B\\) factor is the generated targets, pointed at \\(B=0\\) by the zero convolution.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{c,i}(W_{target},k)=\\big(\\eta\\,[\\hat V_{[1]}^\\top\\ \\cdots\\ \\hat V_{[i-1]}^\\top],\\ [Z_{[1]};\\dots;Z_{[i-1]}]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over (document c, chunk index i), into LoRA_{(i-1)C} on W_down (scale 1), pointed at (0,[Z_{[1]};...;Z_{[i-1]}]) because the zero kernel gives V-hat = 0: a T_A-type point whose A_0 is the context's own activations. Uses sum_j V_j^T Z_j = [V_1^T ... V_{i-1}^T][Z_1;...;Z_{i-1}] (checked, 2e-15). Backbone fixed (inference); for long contexts the rank is capped at min(d, d_ff)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"doc-to-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Doc-to-LoRA generates one static update per document, the same at every position; In-Place TTT's W_down changes from chunk to chunk inside a document, so for generic contexts its position-dependent realised map is not that of any per-document weight."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"fzoo","name":"FZOO","full_name":"FZOO: Fast Zeroth-Order Optimizer (batched one-sided Rademacher estimates with a loss-std-normalised step)","paper_title":"FZOO: Fast Zeroth-Order Optimizer for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models towards Adam-Scale Speed","authors":"Dang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2506.09034","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09034","code_url":"https://github.com/DKmiyan/FZOO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"l_0=\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_t;\\mathcal{B}_t),\\ \\ l_i=\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_t+\\epsilon u_i;\\mathcal{B}_t),\\ u_i\\overset{iid}{\\sim}\\operatorname{Unif}\\{\\pm1\\}^{D}\\ (i=1,\\dots,N);\\ \\ g_t=\\frac{1}{\\epsilon N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}(l_i-l_0)\\,u_i;\\ \\ \\sigma_t^{2}=\\frac{1}{N-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\Big(l_i-\\frac1N\\sum_{j}l_j\\Big)^{2};\\ \\ \\theta_{t+1}=\\theta_t-\\eta_t\\,\\frac{g_t}{\\sigma_t};\\ \\ \\mathbb{E}\\sigma_t^{2}=\\epsilon^{2}\\|\\nabla\\mathcal{L}\\|^{2}+O(\\epsilon^{3}),\\ \\mathbb{E}\\|g_t\\|^{2}=\\tfrac{N+D-1}{N}\\|\\nabla\\mathcal{L}\\|^{2}+O(\\epsilon)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ g_t/\\sigma_t\\approx c\\,g_t/\\|g_t\\|;\\ \\ N+1\\text{ forward passes per step, no backpropagation; } \\theta\\text{ = all } D\\text{ weights or a PEFT subset}","trainable_params":"\\text{all } D\\text{ parameters (full fine-tuning) or a PEFT subset (prefix-tuning in the paper); inference-level memory, as for MeZO}","locus":"All parameters (full-parameter) or the parameters of a PEFT module (prefix-tuning) of RoBERTa-large, the OPT family (125M to 66B; the abstract says 350M to 66B, and OPT-125M is used for the F1 and speed experiments), Phi-2 and Llama-3 (8B)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A zeroth-order gradient estimate built from \\(N\\) one-sided finite differences that share one unperturbed loss; to first order it is the rank-\\(N\\) random projection \\(\\frac1N\\sum_iu_iu_i^{\\top}\\nabla\\mathcal{L}\\). The step is divided by the empirical standard deviation of the \\(N\\) perturbed losses, which estimates \\(\\epsilon\\|\\nabla\\mathcal{L}\\|\\), so the update is a zeroth-order normalised-SGD step: large in flat regions, small in steep ones. Rademacher signs turn the perturbed matrix products into additions and subtractions, so the \\(N\\) perturbed forward passes are fused into one batched kernel (\\(1.92\\times\\) faster on OPT-125M with \\(N=8\\)). The FZOO-R variant reuses half of the previous batch's losses when estimating \\(\\sigma_t\\).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"FZOO only changes how updates are computed. In full-parameter mode it edits the model's own weights in place, so there is nothing to merge; in FZOO (prefix) the trained parameters are a prefix, which stays in the input and cannot be folded into the weights.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: there is no reparameterisation and training starts from \\(\\theta_0\\).","key_idea":"Make MeZO-style fine-tuning converge in far fewer forward passes by averaging several one-sided Rademacher probes per step and dividing the step by the spread of their losses, which turns the update into a normalised-SGD step at inference-level memory.","results_note":"Across RoBERTa-large, OPT (350M to 66B), Phi-2 and Llama-3 on 11 tasks, the abstract reports that FZOO beats MeZO by +3% in accuracy with 3x fewer forward passes, and by +5.6% with 18x fewer forward passes on RoBERTa-large (the unit is not stated; in Table 1, \\(k=16\\), the averages are 71.4 vs 67.4).","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"builds-on","note":"Direct successor and main baseline. FZOO replaces MeZO's single two-sided Gaussian SPSA probe and fixed step with \\(N\\) one-sided Rademacher probes and a step normalised by the loss standard deviation."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"combines","note":"FZOO (prefix) runs the zeroth-order update on prefix parameters only, for further memory savings."},{"target":"agzo","type":"competes-with","note":"Later MeZO successor in the catalogue; not compared in the FZOO paper."},{"target":"zo-muon","type":"competes-with","note":"Later zeroth-order optimiser (February 2026); not compared in the FZOO paper."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09034","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.09034","https://github.com/DKmiyan/FZOO","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10009867","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2506.09034","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Sizhe Dang","Yangyang Guo","Yanjun Zhao","Haishan Ye","Xiaodong Zheng","Guang Dai","Ivor Tsang"],"bibkey":"dang2025fzoo","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dang2025fzoo,\n  title = {{FZOO: Fast Zeroth-Order Optimizer for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models towards Adam-Scale Speed}},\n  author = {Sizhe Dang and Yangyang Guo and Yanjun Zhao and Haishan Ye and Xiaodong Zheng and Guang Dai and Ivor Tsang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2506.09034},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09034}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":null,"rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). The gradient is replaced by \\(N\\) one-sided Rademacher finite differences sharing one unperturbed loss: compression \\(g\\mapsto(\\langle u_i,g\\rangle)_{i\\le N}\\), anchor \\(c\\mapsto\\tfrac1N\\sum_ic_iu_i\\), and the step is divided by the loss spread \\(\\sigma_t\\approx\\epsilon\\lVert\\nabla\\mathcal L\\rVert\\) (zeroth-order normalised SGD).","shape":"Each step lies in \\(\\theta_t+\\mathrm{span}\\{u_1,\\dots,u_N\\}\\) (\\(d=N\\), resampled every step); MeZO is the line case.","base_point":"No reparametrisation; each step is a linear method at its own base.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"Rademacher probes are invariant in law under signed permutations and homotheties, not under rotations or non-uniform scalings: \\(\\mathbb R^\\times B\\) on each side in law, nearest key MonxMon. MeZO's Gaussian probes give \\(O(\\Theta)\\) in law ('frame').","merge":"Writes \\(\\theta\\) directly; nothing to merge. The FZOO-prefix variant trains a prefix, which is M\\(\\infty\\).","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme of \\(N\\)-planes resampled every step; the reach is \\(\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 6)."},"categorical_reading":"FZOO is MeZO with an \\(N\\)-dimensional random slice per step instead of a line, and a step length set by the observed loss spread. The change of probe law from Gaussian to Rademacher shows up in the classification: it trades MeZO's rotation covariance in law for signed-permutation covariance, the price of turning the perturbed matrix products into additions.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"mezo","test":"O2","reason":"Per step FZOO's slice span{u_1,...,u_N} has d = N > 1 = d(MeZO step line). For N = 1 the step line is a Rademacher line, which a Gaussian line meets only at theta_t almost surely (O1)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"ld-mole","name":"LD-MoLE","full_name":"LD-MoLE: Learnable Dynamic routing for Mixture of LoRA Experts (Sparsegen router with a predicted, token-wise sparsity factor)","paper_title":"LD-MoLE: Learnable Dynamic Routing for Mixture of LoRA Experts","authors":"Zhuang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2509.25684","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25684","code_url":"https://github.com/eshentw/LD-MoLE","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_t=W_0x_t+\\frac{\\alpha}{r}\\sum_{i=1}^{E}p_{t,i}\\,B_iA_ix_t\\ \\ (\\alpha/r=16/8=2\\text{ in the released code; the paper's Eq. 7 has no scale}),\\ \\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ u_t=W_{gate}x_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{E};\\ \\ \\lambda_t=f(x_t)<1\\ \\text{(an MLP shared by all modules with the same } d_{in}\\text{; in the code } f(x)=\\mathrm{LogSigmoid}(\\mathrm{MLP}(x))+1-\\epsilon,\\ \\epsilon=10^{-2})\\text{;}\\ \\ p_t=\\arg\\min_{p\\in\\Delta^{E-1}}\\|p-u_t\\|_2^{2}-\\lambda_t\\|p\\|_2^{2}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ p_{t,i}=\\Big[\\frac{u_{t,i}-\\tau_t}{1-\\lambda_t}\\Big]_+,\\ \\tau_t=\\frac{U_k-1+\\lambda_t}{k},\\ k=\\max\\{k:1-\\lambda_t+k\\,u_{(k)}>U_k\\},\\ U_k=\\textstyle\\sum_{j\\le k}u_{(j)};\\ \\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{LM}+\\alpha_{lb}\\,E\\sum_i\\mathcal{F}_i\\mathcal{P}_i+\\beta\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(\\lambda_{lower}(k^{\\star})-\\lambda_t\\big),\\ \\lambda_{lower}(k)=1-\\big(U_k-k\\,u_{(k+1)}\\big);\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{trained: the experts, } W_{gate}\\text{ and } f","trainable_params":"E\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+E\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix } (E=8,\\ r=8)+\\text{one shared }\\lambda\\text{-MLP per distinct } d_{in}\\ (64\\,d_{in}+129\\text{ in the released code});\\ 2.33\\%\\text{ to }4.23\\%\\text{ of the base model in the paper}","locus":"Every linear projection (q, k, v, o, gate, up, down) of Qwen3-1.7B, Llama-3.2-3B and an 8B Llama-3 model","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A mixture of \\(E\\) LoRA deltas per projection with token-wise sparse weights, \\(\\Delta W(x_t)=\\frac{\\alpha}{r}\\sum_ip_{t,i}B_iA_i\\). TopK is replaced by Sparsegen: the Euclidean projection of the gate logits onto the simplex with a quadratic bonus \\(\\lambda\\|p\\|^2\\), equivalently sparsemax applied to \\(u/(1-\\lambda)\\). It is piecewise linear, differentiable almost everywhere, and always keeps at least one expert, unlike ReLU routing. A small MLP predicts \\(\\lambda_t\\) from the token, so the number of active experts varies by token and by layer. Because \\(\\lambda\\) fixes the support analytically, a target count \\(k\\) becomes the closed-form \\(\\lambda\\)-interval of Proposition 2, enforced by a hinge on \\(\\lambda\\) (the main results use \\(\\beta=0\\)). In the released code \\(f\\) is a two-layer MLP (hidden width 64, output bias initialised to \\(-2.5\\)) ending in a LogSigmoid, to which \\(1-\\epsilon\\) with \\(\\epsilon=10^{-2}\\) is added, so \\(\\lambda_t\\in(-\\infty,0.99)\\), and the denominator \\(1-\\lambda_t\\) is clamped at \\(\\epsilon\\); the gate has no bias; all experts are evaluated densely and then weighted; and every \\(B_i\\) starts at zero. The paper writes each expert as \\(\\Delta W_i=A_iB_i\\) with \\(A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\); formula_latex uses \\(B_iA_i\\) with the letters swapped.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"\\(p_t\\) changes from token to token, so the mixture cannot be folded into a static \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not discussed in the paper. The released code zero-initialises every \\(B_i\\), so \\(\\Delta W(x)=0\\) and the model starts at the frozen base.","key_idea":"Replace TopK routing in mixture-of-LoRA fine-tuning with a closed-form, differentiable Sparsegen projection whose sparsity \\(\\lambda\\) is predicted per token by a shared MLP, so the number of experts per token and per layer is learned rather than fixed. The closed form also yields an analytic \\(\\lambda\\)-interval for exactly \\(k\\) active experts, used as a sparsity loss.","results_note":"On Llama-3.2-3B, LD-MoLE averages 82.05 over nine benchmarks versus 81.42 for ReLU-routed ReMoLE and 78.72 for the best TopK MoLA configuration, with eight rank-8 experts on every projection (3.28% trainable parameters).","relations":[{"target":"mole","type":"builds-on","note":"LD-MoLE cites MoLE (Wu et al.) as the origin of the mixture-of-LoRA-experts framework it works in."},{"target":"mixlora","type":"competes-with","note":"TopK LoRA experts with a load-balancing loss; LD-MoLE removes the fixed k (cited, not compared directly)."},{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited mixture-of-LoRA variant; not compared."},{"target":"lora-mixer","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent ICLR 2026 mixture-of-LoRA router, not compared. LoRA-Mixer keeps a fixed top-K on attention/SSM projections and shapes routing with an entropy-augmented balance loss; LD-MoLE learns a per-token, per-layer expert count on all projections."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25684","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.25684","https://github.com/eshentw/LD-MoLE","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eshentw/LD-MoLE/HEAD/peft/utils/sparsegen.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eshentw/LD-MoLE/HEAD/peft/tuners/lora.py","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10011554","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2509.25684","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"authors_full":["Yuan Zhuang","Yi Shen","Yuexin Bian","Qing Su","Shihao Ji","Yuanyuan Shi","Fei Miao"],"bibkey":"zhuang2025ld","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhuang2025ld,\n  title = {{LD-MoLE: Learnable Dynamic Routing for Mixture of LoRA Experts}},\n  author = {Yuan Zhuang and Yi Shen and Yuexin Bian and Qing Su and Shihao Ji and Yuanyuan Shi and Fei Miao},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2509.25684},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25684}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: \\(\\Delta W(x_t)=\\tfrac\\alpha r\\sum_ip_{t,i}B_iA_i\\) on every projection, with Sparsegen routing \\(p_t=\\arg\\min_{p\\in\\Delta^{E-1}}\\lVert p-u_t\\rVert^2-\\lambda_t\\lVert p\\rVert^2\\) and a per-token \\(\\lambda_t\\) from a shared MLP.","shape":"Token-dependent mixture: no single weight-space image. Sparsegen is piecewise linear and continuous, so unlike top-\\(K\\) the realised functions have no jumps, while weights still vanish exactly on open sets of tokens.","base_point":"Every \\(B_i\\) is zero-initialised in the released code: the frozen function at every token (neutral); gate and \\(\\lambda\\)-MLP get no first-order gradient (zero gate).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on each expert's rank wire (the routing weight slides through \\(B_iA_i\\)); the simplex projection is invariant under \\(u\\mapsto u+c\\mathbf 1\\), so the bias-free gate also has the kernel-type shift \\(W_{gate}\\mapsto W_{gate}+\\mathbf 1v^\\top\\).","covariance":"Dense LoRA experts and a dense gate: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Token-dependent \\(p_t\\) (Thm VI.3).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"LD-MoLE is a LoRA mixture whose router is a Euclidean projection onto the simplex with a learned quadratic bonus, so the number of active experts is a per-token output and the routing is continuous. Fibrewise it is LoRA\\(_{Er}\\) with token-dependent block weights, pointed at \\(B=0\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_i,A_i)_i,W_{gate},f\\big)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{rs}\\,[\\,p_{x,1}B_1\\ \\cdots\\ p_{x,E}B_E\\,],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_E]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_{Er} with scale s, pointed at (0,[A_1^0;...;A_E^0]) since every B_i starts at 0 (as HydraLoRA -> LoRA). h_x is continuous and piecewise smooth (Sparsegen's support changes across hyperplanes)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loramoe","test":"O1","reason":"LoRAMoE's softmax weights are strictly positive and its experts sit on the FFN only. LD-MoLE's Sparsegen weights vanish exactly on open sets of tokens and its experts also edit the attention projections; for generic experts (linearly independent B_iA_ix) equal realised functions would force equal weights, so its sparse routings are not LoRAMoE realisations."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"ar1-zo","name":"AR1-ZO","full_name":"AR1-ZO: Alternating Rank-1 Zeroth-Order optimisation of LoRA with topology-aware scaling","paper_title":"AR1-ZO: Topology-Aware Rank-1 Zeroth-Order Queries for High-Rank LoRA Fine-Tuning","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026","arxiv":"2605.19767","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19767","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\tfrac{\\gamma}{r}BAx=W_0x+\\alpha\\sum_{k=1}^{r}b_k a_k^{\\top}x,\\ \\ \\gamma=\\alpha r,\\ \\ B=[b_1,\\dots,b_r]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A=[a_1,\\dots,a_r]^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{step } t:\\ k(t)=1+(t\\bmod r)\\ (\\text{same atom index in every adapted matrix}),\\ \\ z_t=(z_b,z_a)\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I_{d_{out}+d_{in}}),\\ \\ \\ell_{\\pm}=\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_t\\pm\\mu P_tz_t),\\ \\ \\hat g_t=\\frac{\\ell_+-\\ell_-}{2\\mu}\\,z_t=(\\hat g_{b,t},\\hat g_{a,t}),\\ \\ b_{k(t)}\\leftarrow b_{k(t)}-\\eta_t\\hat g_{b,t},\\ \\ a_{k(t)}\\leftarrow a_{k(t)}-\\eta_t\\hat g_{a,t};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0 \\text{ and every atom } j\\neq k(t) \\text{ (shared by both endpoints)};\\ \\text{trained: all } r \\text{ atoms, one per step, from two forward passes and no backpropagation}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{stored per adapted matrix } (r=64 \\text{ in the main runs});\\ \\text{only } d_{in}+d_{out} \\text{ coordinates are perturbed and updated per step}","locus":"LoRA-adapted linear layers of OPT-2.7B/13B and Qwen3-1.7B/32B. The mechanism diagnostics use the 28 v_proj matrices of Qwen3-1.7B; the target modules of the main runs are not listed.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"The stored object is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) LoRA delta \\(\\Delta W=\\alpha\\sum_k b_ka_k^{\\top}\\). AR1-ZO changes only how a two-point zeroth-order oracle measures it. Each query perturbs one matched rank-1 atom \\((b_k,a_k)\\), a block of dimension \\(q=d_{out}+d_{in}\\). Lemma 4.1 shows that, for nonzero factors, this is the smallest complete LoRA-native block: one-sided blocks miss part of the first-order variation \\(\\delta b_k a_k^{\\top}+b_k\\delta a_k^{\\top}\\). The estimator's dimension term is therefore \\(q\\), not \\(rq\\). The second ingredient is scale. Under the canonical coefficient \\(\\gamma/r=\\alpha/r\\), the active finite-difference signal is \\(\\Theta(1/r)\\). Against a rank-independent evaluation-noise floor its SNR is \\(\\Theta(1/r^2)\\), and \\(\\mathbb{E}\\cos(\\hat\\nabla_k\\mathcal{L},\\nabla_k\\mathcal{L})\\to0\\) once \\(r\\gg r_c=\\Theta(\\alpha\\mu\\|g\\|/\\sigma_\\xi)\\). The paper calls this directional collapse. Setting \\(\\gamma=\\alpha r\\) gives every queried atom the rank-free coefficient \\(\\alpha\\). In forward-map terms, the method is LoRA with scale \\(\\alpha\\) (instead of \\(\\alpha/r\\) or \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt r\\)) trained by cyclic block-coordinate ZO over rank-1 atoms. Rank then appears only as coverage: over a cycle the stationarity measure is \\(\\tfrac1r\\sum_k\\|\\nabla_k\\mathcal{L}\\|^2\\). The convergence bound (Theorem 4.4(ii)) separately assumes a stable represented-adapter scale, which the paper uses to rule out output inflation. This matters because under \\(\\gamma=\\alpha r\\) the joint output scale \\(\\alpha\\sum_kb_ka_k^{\\top}\\) is no longer normalised by \\(r\\) (this last point is the entry's reading, not the paper's wording). A rank-\\(m\\) block query uses \\(\\gamma=\\alpha r/m\\) and has dimension \\(mq\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained adapter is a plain additive delta \\(\\Delta W=\\alpha BA\\) and folds into \\(W_0\\) exactly like LoRA. The atom schedule and \\(\\gamma=\\alpha r\\) affect training only.","identity_at_init":"Yes. \\(B\\) is initialised at zero and \\(A\\) at random (Appendix B.1), so \\(\\Delta W=\\alpha\\sum_k b_ka_k^{\\top}=0\\) at step 0.","key_idea":"In zeroth-order LoRA, rank should set storage and coverage, not the dimension of each query or the signal it measures. AR1-ZO perturbs one complete rank-1 atom \\((b_k,a_k)\\) per two-forward-pass query and gives it the coefficient \\(\\gamma/r=\\alpha\\) instead of \\(\\alpha/r\\). This removes the \\(1/r^2\\) decay of the finite-difference SNR that makes naive atomwise ZO collapse at high rank.","results_note":"With \\(r=64\\) and \\(\\alpha=16\\) on OPT-2.7B (Table 1), AR1-ZO beats MeZO-LoRA on all six tasks, e.g. SQuAD F1 77.8 vs 64.6 and DROP 27.8 vs 23.9. The same atom schedule with canonical \\(\\alpha/r\\) scaling (ZO-Alt-naive) collapses to 19.2 and 6.3.","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses the same two-point SPSA/RGE oracle on paired mini-batches, restricted to one LoRA atom per query."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps the rank-\\(r\\) LoRA parameterisation and the \\(B=0\\) init. Only the coefficient (\\(\\gamma=\\alpha r\\)) and the per-step update set change."},{"target":"rslora","type":"competes-with","note":"Competing rank-scaling rule. rsLoRA uses \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\) for joint first-order updates; AR1-ZO gives each queried atom the coefficient \\(\\alpha\\). In the paper's SQuAD ablation (Fig. 7), both \\(\\alpha/r\\) and \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\) stagnate under atomwise ZO (F1 about 0.17, against 0.770 for \\(\\gamma=\\alpha r\\))."},{"target":"agzo","type":"competes-with","note":"Another way to cut ZO perturbation dimension. AGZO perturbs in an activation-derived rank-\\(r\\) subspace of the dense weight; AR1-ZO uses the native LoRA atoms, with no auxiliary basis and no activation hooks. AGZO is cited in AR1-ZO's related work but is not a baseline."},{"target":"zo-muon","type":"competes-with","note":"Another 2026 low-dimensional ZO fine-tuning scheme, using a random rank-\\(r\\) subspace plus orthogonalisation. AR1-ZO instead exposes the stored LoRA atoms one at a time. AR1-ZO does not cite ZO-Muon, so this is a structural comparison, not a reported baseline."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19767","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.19767","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=7eOsaBX3qO","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2605.19767"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Ziye Chen","Hongbin Lin","Chenyu Zhang","Xiangda Yan","Yongjie Yang","Yao Shu"],"bibkey":"chen2026ar1","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2026ar1,\n  title = {{AR1-ZO: Topology-Aware Rank-1 Zeroth-Order Queries for High-Rank LoRA Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Ziye Chen and Hongbin Lin and Chenyu Zhang and Xiangda Yan and Yongjie Yang and Yao Shu},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2605.19767},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19767}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA's \\(\\rho=W_0+\\alpha\\sum_kb_ka_k^\\top\\) (coefficient \\(\\gamma/r=\\alpha\\) instead of \\(\\alpha/r\\)), trained by cyclic block-coordinate two-point zeroth-order queries on one rank-1 atom \\((b_k,a_k)\\) at a time; oracle and schedule are trained-method data.","shape":"LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\) with random \\(A_0\\) (App. B.1): the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\(\\rho\\). The atomwise oracle respects only the atom torus \\((b_k,a_k)\\mapsto(cb_k,a_k/c)\\) with the cyclic order fixed, and its Gaussian probes are not torus-invariant either.","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone (the Gaussian probes in \\(b,a\\) are \\(O\\)-invariant in law).","merge":"A plain \\(\\alpha BA\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"AR1-ZO is LoRA rescaled by \\(r\\) and trained one rank-1 atom at a time from two forward passes; as an object it is LoRA, by an isomorphism of the rsLoRA kind. Its content is in the trained-method data: each query has dimension \\(d_{in}+d_{out}\\) and the coefficient \\(\\alpha\\) keeps the finite-difference signal independent of the rank, so rank becomes coverage rather than query dimension.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{s}B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Isomorphism onto LoRA_r with scale s (as rsLoRA = LoRA), pointed: B_0 = 0 maps to 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O2","reason":"Over a cycle every atom moves in both factors, so d(AR1-ZO) = r(m+n-r) > mr = d(LoRA-FA)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"pico","name":"Pico","full_name":"Pico: Pre-merge interference calibration in output-space","paper_title":"Crowded in B-Space: Calibrating Shared Directions for LoRA Merging","authors":"Tang and Yang","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026","arxiv":"2604.16826","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.16826","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{per layer and target module, with LoRAs } \\Delta W_t=B_tA_t\\ (t=1,\\dots,T;\\ \\text{scale absorbed into } B_t):\\ \\ B_{\\text{all}}=[B_1,\\dots,B_T]=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ \\ s_j=\\frac{\\sigma_j^2}{\\sum_k\\sigma_k^2},\\ \\ \\alpha_j=\\frac{1}{1+(T-1)s_j},\\ \\ S=I_{d_{out}}+U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha-\\mathbf{1})\\,U^{\\top},\\ \\ \\tilde B_t=SB_t;\\ \\ \\Delta W_{\\text{calib}}=\\mathcal{M}\\big(\\tilde B_1A_1,\\dots,\\tilde B_TA_T\\big)\\ \\ (\\mathcal{M}\\in\\{\\text{TA},\\text{TIES},\\text{TSV-M},\\dots\\}),\\ \\ \\Delta W_{\\text{Pico}}=\\gamma\\,\\Delta W_{\\text{calib}},\\ \\ \\gamma=\\frac{\\tfrac1T\\sum_t\\|B_tA_t\\|_F}{\\|\\Delta W_{\\text{calib}}\\|_F};\\ \\ W=W_0+\\Delta W_{\\text{Pico}};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0 \\text{ and all } A_t,B_t;\\ \\text{nothing is trained and no data is used}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(data-free post-hoc merge); per adapted matrix: one SVD of the } d_{out}\\times Tr \\text{ stack plus } T \\text{ left multiplications}","locus":"Every LoRA-adapted matrix (q_proj and v_proj in the experiments) of Llama-3.1-8B, and of Qwen3-4B-Base in an appendix. Four domain LoRAs (math, code, finance, medical) of rank 8 to 64.","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Pico left-multiplies each output factor, before merging, by one layer-wise operator \\(S=U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)\\,U^{\\top}+(I-UU^{\\top})\\). \\(S\\) is symmetric positive definite: its eigenvalues are \\(\\alpha_j\\in[1/T,1]\\) on \\(\\mathrm{col}(B_{\\text{all}})\\) and 1 on the orthogonal complement. It therefore shrinks output directions that carry a large share of the stacked \\(B\\) energy. The strongest shrinkage, \\(\\alpha_j\\to1/T\\) as \\(s_j\\to1\\), undoes the \\(T\\)-fold counting that a summed (Task Arithmetic) merge gives a direction used equally by all \\(T\\) tasks. A single scalar \\(\\gamma\\) then restores the mean source Frobenius norm. \\(A_t\\) is untouched; in the ablation, calibrating \\(A\\) or the full \\(BA\\) instead lowers accuracy. Two consequences are derived here. (i) Task Arithmetic is linear, so \\(\\mathcal{M}(SB_tA_t)=S\\,\\mathcal{M}(B_tA_t)\\) and Pico-TA equals the post-merge output-space reshaping \\(\\gamma S\\Delta W_{\\text{TA}}\\); applying \\(S\\) before merging matters only for non-linear mergers such as TIES and TSV-M. (ii) \\(s_j\\) is a direction's share of the stacked spectrum, only a proxy for cross-task sharing, so a direction dominated by one task's energy is shrunk too. \\(U\\) is the left singular basis of the concatenated \\(B\\)'s. It is the same output basis Core Space builds (\\(U_B\\)). KnOTS instead takes the SVD of the stacked products \\([B_1A_1,\\dots,B_TA_T]\\). Its left singular vectors span the same column space only when every \\(A_t\\) has full row rank, and they form a different basis.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is one additive delta per layer, \\(\\Delta W_{\\text{Pico}}=\\gamma\\,\\mathcal{M}(\\{SB_tA_t\\})\\), of rank at most \\(Tr\\) for TA. It folds into \\(W_0\\) like any merged LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Nothing is trained. With a single adapter (\\(T=1\\)), every \\(\\alpha_j=1\\), so \\(S=I\\) and the calibration step is the identity. Under Task Arithmetic, \\(\\gamma\\) then cancels any merge weight and \\(\\Delta W_{\\text{Pico}}=B_1A_1\\) exactly; under TIES, the merger's own trimming still changes the single adapter.","key_idea":"Across independently trained LoRAs, the output factors \\(B_t\\) reuse a few shared directions: pairwise overlap \\(O_B\\gg O_A\\), and the effective rank of \\(B\\) is about 3 at LoRA rank 64. A linear merge counts these directions \\(T\\) times. Pico shrinks them before merging with the spectral weights \\(\\alpha_j=1/(1+(T-1)s_j)\\) and then restores the update norm.","results_note":"On Llama-3.1-8B with four rank-16 domain LoRAs, Pico raises the eight-benchmark average from 0.4093 to 0.4430 under Task Arithmetic, from 0.3859 to 0.4328 under TIES and from 0.3473 to 0.4305 under TSV-M. Pico+TA (0.4430) also beats a jointly trained multi-task LoRA (0.3688).","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"combines","note":"Plug-in calibration before TA (+3.4 points). For TA it equals left-multiplying the merged delta by \\(S\\) and rescaling."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"Calibrated factors are then merged by TIES (+4.7 points average)."},{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"combines","note":"Calibrated factors are then merged by TSV-M (+8.3 points, the largest gain)."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use a shared output basis from an SVD, but of different stacks. KnOTS factorises the stacked updates \\(B_tA_t\\) and merges the task-specific right factors; Pico factorises the stacked \\(B_t\\) and rescales the shared output directions by their energy share. KnOTS is a Table 1 baseline that Pico beats under all three mergers (overall 0.4088, 0.4207 and 0.3523, against Pico's 0.4430, 0.4328 and 0.4305)."},{"target":"core-space-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Uses the same left singular basis of the concatenated \\(B_t\\) (Core Space's \\(U_B\\)), but calibrates in it instead of projecting to a small core. Core Space is a baseline that Pico beats in all three merger blocks of Table 1."},{"target":"dare","type":"competes-with","note":"Sparsify-and-rescale interference reduction; a baseline in every merger block of Table 1."},{"target":"lori","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRI reduces merge interference at training time, with frozen random (near-orthogonal) \\(A_t\\) and sparse \\(B_t\\). Pico is post-hoc and data-free on ordinary LoRAs. Pico does not cite LoRI, so this is a structural comparison."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.16826","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.16826","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=PtEkosDXWk","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2604.16826"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Yixuan Tang","Yi Yang"],"bibkey":"tang2026crowded","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tang2026crowded,\n  title = {{Crowded in B-Space: Calibrating Shared Directions for LoRA Merging}},\n  author = {Yixuan Tang and Yi Yang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2604.16826},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.16826}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and data-free: every output factor is left-multiplied by \\(S=I+U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha-\\mathbf 1)U^\\top\\) (\\(U\\) from the SVD of \\([B_1,\\dots,B_T]\\), \\(\\alpha_j=1/(1+(T-1)s_j)\\)); a base rule \\(\\mathcal M\\) merges the \\(SB_tA_t\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) restores the mean source norm.","shape":"No tunable coefficient (\\(\\alpha\\), \\(S\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) are closed-form, and \\(\\gamma\\) cancels any overall merge weight): a single point (post-hoc convention).","base_point":"A single point, generically \\(\\neq\\theta_0\\); for \\(T=1\\) under task arithmetic it returns the adapter \\(B_1A_1\\) itself.","gauge":"No coefficient family. Note: \\(S\\) depends on \\(\\sum_tB_tB_t^\\top\\), not on the products, so Pico is invariant under per-adapter \\(O(r)\\) gauges but not under \\(GL_r\\) (checked: output change \\(5\\times10^{-15}\\) under an orthogonal gauge, 0.37 under a generic one): a factor-level rule (Prop V.4(a)).","covariance":"\\(S\\) is built from an SVD of the stacked \\(B_t\\) and transports under \\(g\\in CO(d_{out})\\) (\\(S\\mapsto gSg^{-1}\\)); \\(A_t\\) is untouched, but the norm-restoring \\(\\gamma\\) is a ratio of Frobenius norms, which is invariant only under \\(CO\\) on each side. With task arithmetic inside the group is exactly \\(CO(d_{out})\\times CO(d_{in})\\): OxO (checked: \\(2\\times10^{-15}\\) under \\(CO\\times CO\\), \\(4\\times10^{-2}\\) under \\(CO\\times GL\\), \\(0.35\\) under a generic output \\(GL\\)). TIES or TSV-M inside cut it further.","merge":"One additive delta per layer, of rank \\(\\le Tr\\) under task arithmetic.","rebasing":"A one-shot post-hoc operation."},"categorical_reading":"Pico is a spectral reweighting of the shared output space before merging: under task arithmetic it is the post-merge map \\(\\Delta W\\mapsto\\gamma S\\Delta W\\), so its effect on nonlinear mergers comes only from applying \\(S\\) first. Because \\(S\\) is read off the \\(B\\) factors rather than the adapters, it depends on how each LoRA's scale is split between \\(B\\) and \\(A\\), which is what the design rule 'combine on products, or after a joint gauge fixing' (Prop V.4) warns about.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=\\big([\\,\\gamma SB_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\gamma SB_T\\,],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_T]\\big)","note":"Pico-TA instance: the merged point is a point of LoRA_{Tr}'s image (unpointed slice, LoRA pointed at h(*)), as model soups into task arithmetic."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"For T >= 2 and generic adapters, gamma S sum_t B_tA_t leaves span{B_tA_t}: S mixes the shared output directions, so Pico-TA is not a reweighted task arithmetic."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"frameft","name":"FrameFT","full_name":"FrameFT: fine-tuning with sparse coefficients in tight fusion frames","paper_title":"Fine-Tuning of Transformer models with Frames","authors":"Adepu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2608.26430","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26430","code_url":"https://github.com/vsingh-group/FrameFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\Delta W,\\ \\ \\Delta W=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{d_{out}d_{in}}}\\,P_{out}\\,C\\,P_{in}^{\\top}=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{d_{out}d_{in}}}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}P_{out,i}\\,C_i\\,P_{in,i}^{\\top};\\ \\ P_{out}=[P_{out,1},\\dots,P_{out,k}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times k\\rho_{out}},\\ P_{in}=[P_{in,1},\\dots,P_{in,k}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times k\\rho_{in}}\\ \\ \\text{(Parseval tight fusion frames from Spectral Tetris, } \\textstyle\\sum_iP_{\\cdot,i}P_{\\cdot,i}^{\\top}=I\\text{)};\\ \\ C=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(C_1,\\dots,C_k),\\ C_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\rho_{out}\\times\\rho_{in}},\\ \\ \\mathrm{supp}(C)=\\Omega,\\ |\\Omega|=n_c \\text{ random and shared by all layers};\\ \\ \\text{(paper form; the released code sets the block size of } C \\text{ independently of } \\rho\\text{: } 4\\times4 \\text{ for Llama-2-7B, } 40\\times40 \\text{ for Llama-2-13B, one full block for Gemma-2-2B, 128 blocks for Gemma-2-9B and Llama-3.1-8B)};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0,\\ P_{out},\\ P_{in},\\ \\Omega;\\ \\text{trained: the } n_c \\text{ entries } C_{\\Omega} \\text{ of each adapted matrix}","trainable_params":"n_c\\ \\text{per adapted matrix } (n_c=1000 \\text{ for RoBERTa, } 5000 \\text{ for the 2B--13B LLMs, i.e. } 320\\text{K in total on Llama-2-7B } W_q,W_v);\\ \\text{frames and support are regenerated from } (d,\\rho) \\text{ and a seed, so neither is stored}","locus":"\\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) in every Transformer block (RoBERTa-base/large, Llama-2-7B/13B, Gemma-2-2B/9B, Llama-3.1-8B, ViT-B/L)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A fixed two-sided frame expansion with a sparse block-diagonal core. \\(\\Delta W=\\sum_iP_{out,i}C_iP_{in,i}^{\\top}\\) couples the \\(i\\)-th output subspace only with the \\(i\\)-th input subspace, and only \\(n_c\\) core entries are free. Rank is not capped by a small \\(r\\): \\(\\mathrm{rank}\\,\\Delta W\\le\\mathrm{rank}\\,C\\le n_c\\). The general definition allows redundant frames (\\(k\\rho>d\\)), but every reported experiment uses \\(k\\rho=d\\) with Parseval normalisation. The main GLUE, instruction-tuning and ViT runs use \\(\\rho=2\\), and the Appendix D ablation varies \\(\\rho\\) from 2 to 256, still with \\(k\\rho=d\\). Then \\(\\sum_iP_iP_i^{\\top}=I\\) with ranks summing to \\(d\\) forces the subspaces to be mutually orthogonal, so \\(P_{out}\\) and \\(P_{in}\\) are square orthogonal matrices and the frame is not redundant. Running the released construct_real_tff confirms this: the matrix is orthogonal to \\(10^{-5}\\) to \\(10^{-4}\\) (float32) for \\(d=768\\) to \\(4096\\), and its non-zero fraction is about \\(2/\\rho\\) (99.6 to 99.9% at \\(\\rho=2\\), about 50% at \\(\\rho=4\\), about 25% at \\(\\rho=8\\)). At \\(\\rho=2\\) the Spectral Tetris unit-norm tight frame in \\(\\mathbb{C}^1\\) is all ones, so the modulated frame is the realified \\((d/2)\\)-point DFT basis: each 2-D subspace is the realification of one conjugated DFT row. In this regime FrameFT is FourierFT-like: sparse coefficients in a fixed DFT-type basis on both sides. The block-sparse frames that the paper credits for throughput appear only at \\(\\rho\\ge4\\). The released code also decouples the coefficient blocks from \\(\\rho\\). Llama-2-7B uses \\(4\\times4\\) blocks, Llama-2-13B \\(40\\times40\\), Gemma-2-2B one full block (no block-diagonal constraint, so a random sparse support over the whole two-sided realified-DFT basis), and Gemma-2-9B and Llama-3.1-8B 128 blocks. Four of the twelve released GLUE scripts use \\(\\rho=4\\) (tff_l 4), although the paper states \\(\\rho=2\\) for GLUE. Lemma 3.1: if \\(f(W)\\) is \\(L\\)-smooth, the reparameterised loss \\(f(C)\\) is \\(LB_{out}B_{in}\\)-smooth, unlike LoRA's factored \\((A,B)\\) landscape. Gradient descent with \\(\\eta\\le1/\\tilde L\\) therefore gets the standard \\(O(1/T)\\) stationarity rate (Theorem 3.2).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is a dense additive matrix computed from the frames and the sparse core. The released tuner's merge() adds it to \\(W_0\\), so inference cost is unchanged.","identity_at_init":"Depends on the configuration. The released instruction-tuning scripts set init_std 0, so \\(C=0\\) and \\(\\Delta W=0\\) at step 0. The GLUE scripts do not pass init_std, so the default 1.0 (GLUE/args.py and the FrameConfig default) applies: the \\(n_c\\) coefficients start as \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) samples and \\(\\Delta W\\neq0\\) at initialisation, as in FourierFT. The tuner's reset_frame_parameters, which would zero the coefficients, is defined but never called. The paper's Algorithm 1 only says 'Init'.","key_idea":"Replace LoRA's learned factor subspaces with fixed, algorithmically generated Parseval fusion frames, shared by all layers of the same width, and learn only a sparse block-diagonal coefficient matrix between them. Storage drops to \\(n_c\\) scalars per matrix, and the coefficient-space loss inherits the smoothness of the weight-space loss.","results_note":"Instruction-tuned on Alpaca with 320K trainable parameters, Llama-2-7B averages 63.62 on eight LM-eval-harness tasks, against 63.18 for LoRA (16.7M) and 63.22 for FourierFT (320K) (Table 3). LoRA stays 0.2 to 0.7 points ahead on Llama-2-13B, Gemma-2-9B and Llama-3.1-8B. On GLUE, FrameFT averages 86.1 (RoBERTa-base, 24K parameters) and 88.7 (RoBERTa-large, 48K) (Table 1).","relations":[{"target":"fourierft","type":"competes-with","note":"FourierFT is a baseline in Tables 1 to 3. Same recipe: a few trainable coefficients in a fixed basis shared across layers. In every reported setting (\\(k\\rho=d\\), mostly \\(\\rho=2\\)), FrameFT's basis is itself a realified DFT, so the main difference is block-diagonal rather than scattered 2D-frequency support (derived here; the paper does not present FrameFT as building on FourierFT)."},{"target":"waveft","type":"competes-with","note":"Sparse coefficients under a fixed wavelet synthesis instead of fusion frames. FrameFT does not cite WaveFT, so this is a structural comparison."},{"target":"svft","type":"competes-with","note":"Also a sparse core between fixed bases, but SVFT's bases are the singular vectors of \\(W_0\\), stored per layer. FrameFT's bases depend only on \\((d,\\rho)\\). SVFT is a baseline in Tables 1 and 3."},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"Core between frozen SVD bases of \\(W_0\\). FrameFT replaces the weight-derived bases with data-independent frames. LoRA-XS is a RoBERTa-large GLUE baseline (Table 1), where the two tie on average (88.7)."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"FrameFT matches or beats LoRA on GLUE (12 to 17 times fewer trainable parameters) and on Alpaca for Llama-2-7B and Gemma-2-2B. On Llama-2-13B, Gemma-2-9B and Llama-3.1-8B, LoRA is 0.2 to 0.7 points ahead with about 40 to 65 times more parameters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26430","https://arxiv.org/html/2608.26430","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=KCfbSVIheg","https://github.com/vsingh-group/FrameFT","https://github.com/vsingh-group/FrameFT/blob/main/peft/src/peft/tuners/frame/layer.py","https://github.com/vsingh-group/FrameFT/blob/main/InstructionTuning/launch_scripts/Llama-2-7b-frameft.sh","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2608.26430"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Harshavardhan Adepu","Li Zhang","Sanjiv Kumar","Vikas Singh"],"bibkey":"adepu2026fine","bibtex":"@inproceedings{adepu2026fine,\n  title = {{Fine-Tuning of Transformer models with Frames}},\n  author = {Harshavardhan Adepu and Li Zhang and Sanjiv Kumar and Vikas Singh},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2608.26430},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26430}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Additive \\(W_0+c\\,P_{out}CP_{in}^\\top\\) (\\(c=\\alpha/\\sqrt{d_{out}d_{in}}\\)) with fixed Parseval fusion frames, a block-diagonal core \\(C\\) and a random support \\(\\Omega\\) shared by all layers; only \\(C_\\Omega\\) trains.","shape":"Linear in \\(C_\\Omega\\), \\(d=n_c\\). Every reported run has \\(k\\rho=d\\), so the frames are square orthogonal matrices (at \\(\\rho=2\\) the realified DFT basis) and \\(\\Delta W\\) is a coordinate mask in that two-sided frame.","base_point":"With init_std 0 (the instruction-tuning scripts) \\(C=0\\): regular. The GLUE scripts keep the default \\(\\mathcal N(0,1)\\) coefficients, a pointing defect as for HF's FourierFT default.","gauge":"None: orthogonal frames make \\(C\\mapsto P_{out}CP_{in}^\\top\\) injective.","covariance":"The frames depend only on \\((d,\\rho)\\) and impose a DFT-type cyclic order on both sides, and the block structure a partition: \\(\\Pi\\), as FourierFT.","merge":"One dense additive matrix.","rebasing":"Idempotent with frames and support frozen (Thm V.3, Cor 2)."},"categorical_reading":"FrameFT is a SHiRA-type coordinate mask transported by a fixed two-sided frame \\(X\\mapsto P_{out}XP_{in}^\\top\\), which in all reported settings is a realified DFT on both sides; the transport is \\(\\theta_0\\)-independent, unlike SVFT's. It is a FourierFT relative that couples each output frequency block only with the matching input block, through a dense \\(\\rho\\times\\rho\\) core.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(C_\\Omega)=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{d_{out}d_{in}}}\\,P_{out}\\,C_{:,J},\\qquad A_0=(P_{in})_{:,J}^\\top","note":"J = columns of C met by Omega; into LoRA-FA_{|J|} with the frozen frame rows A_0 (LoRA-FA scale 1; otherwise divide h by it), pointed at 0 (as SVFT -> LoRA-FA). Checked numerically (6e-17)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"FourierFT's image is spanned by a few real 2-D cosine patterns cos(2 pi (j x/d_out + l y/d_in)) at sampled frequencies (rank 2 each, point-symmetric); FrameFT's atoms are rank-one products of realified (d/2)-point DFT vectors on each side, block-diagonal in frequency. A rank-one atom would need both (j,l) and (j,-l) sampled at matching frequencies, so for generic supports Im FrameFT is not contained in Im FourierFT."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The maximum rank reaches n_c (block-diagonal core), far above r at matched budgets."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"prefixmemory-tuning","name":"PrefixMemory-Tuning","full_name":"PrefixMemory-Tuning (PMT; titled Prefix-Tuning+ in arXiv v1 and v2)","paper_title":"PrefixMemory-Tuning: Modernizing Prefix-Tuning by Decoupling the Prefix from Attention","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2506.13674","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13674","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{for each layer } l \\text{ and attention head } h:\\ \\ o_i^{\\top}=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\le i}\\mathrm{sim}(q_i,k_j)\\,v_j^{\\top}}{\\sum_{j\\le i}\\mathrm{sim}(q_i,k_j)}+\\phi(q_i)^{\\top}M,\\ \\ q_i=W_Qx_i,\\ k_j=W_Kx_j,\\ v_j=W_Vx_j,\\ \\ \\mathrm{sim}(q,k)=\\exp\\!\\big(q^{\\top}k/\\sqrt{d_K}\\big),\\ \\ \\phi\\in\\{\\mathrm{elu},\\mathrm{gelu}\\}\\ \\text{elementwise } (d_\\phi=d_K),\\ \\ M\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_\\phi\\times d_V}\\ (\\text{per head; the paper's single-head notation writes } M\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_\\phi\\times d});\\ \\ \\text{derived from prefix-tuning with prefix } s_1,\\dots,s_\\ell \\text{ by (i) taking the prefix out of the softmax with a fixed weight } \\lambda,\\ \\text{(ii) linearising } \\mathrm{sim}(\\cdot,\\cdot)\\approx\\phi(\\cdot)^{\\top}\\phi(\\cdot),\\ \\text{(iii) replacing } \\textstyle\\sum_{j\\le\\ell}\\phi(W_Ks_j)(W_Vs_j)^{\\top} \\text{ by a free } M \\text{, dropping the normaliser and absorbing } \\lambda;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: all base weights};\\ \\text{trained: } \\{M_{l,h}\\}","trainable_params":"L\\cdot H\\cdot d_\\phi d_V\\ \\text{per model } (=L\\,d\\,d_K \\text{ for multi-head attention with elementwise } \\phi):\\ 16.91\\text{M } (0.25\\%) \\text{ on LLaMA2-7B-Chat, } 9.51\\text{M } (0.31\\%) \\text{ on Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct};\\ \\text{both reported counts exceed } LHd_Kd_V \\text{ by exactly } L\\cdot d\\text{, consistent with one extra } d\\text{-vector per layer that the text does not describe (it equals } Hd_\\phi\\text{, the size of one Eq.-(6) normaliser } N\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_\\phi} \\text{ per head; suggestive, not confirmed)}","locus":"Every attention head of every layer: a query-conditioned branch added to each head's output before \\(W_O\\) (LLaMA2-7B-Chat, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-Math-7B)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"An additive, query-conditioned bias outside the softmax: \\(o_i\\mapsto o_i+M^{\\top}\\phi(q_i)\\). Unlike a prefix, it is not renormalised against the input's attention mass. Prefix-tuning gives \\(o^{pt}_i=(1-\\alpha_i)o_i+\\sum_{j\\le\\ell}\\alpha_{ij}W_Vs_j\\), where \\(\\alpha_i\\) depends on the input and prefix lengths; this is the paper's tradeoff argument. In PMT the input path keeps weight 1 and the memory path is independent of length. A structural reading, derived here: through the frozen output projection, each layer gains the parallel branch \\(\\sum_hW_O^{(h)}M_h^{\\top}\\phi(W_Q^{(h)}x_i)\\). That is a parallel adapter whose down-projection is the frozen query projection \\(W_Q\\) (width \\(d_K\\) per head and \\(Hd_K=d\\) in total for both LLaMA2-7B and Qwen2.5-3B, so it is not a bottleneck), whose nonlinearity is elu or gelu, and whose trained map is block-diagonal (one \\(M_h^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_V\\times d_K}\\) per head), read out through the frozen \\(W_O\\). It is the linear-kernel counterpart of He et al.'s view of prefix-tuning as a gated parallel adapter. Memory capacity scales with \\(d_\\phi\\times d_V\\) rather than with prefix length. Remark 1 notes that a trainable \\(\\phi_W(x)=\\mathrm{ReLU}(Wx+b)\\) would turn the branch into a one-hidden-layer MLP; Table 2 reports such an 'MLP Kernel' variant, better than elu on some tasks and worse on others. The paper does not say whether \\(q_i\\) is taken before or after RoPE; after RoPE the branch would depend on position.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\phi(q_i)^{\\top}M\\) is a non-linear (elu/gelu) function of the query, so the branch cannot be folded into \\(W_Q,W_K,W_V\\) or \\(W_O\\) and stays at inference. It costs about one extra \\(d_K\\times d_V\\) product per head per token and does not grow the KV cache.","identity_at_init":"Not stated, and the linked code repository is empty. With \\(M=0\\) the branch vanishes and \\(o^{PMT}_i=o_i\\) exactly. Prefix-tuning has no such parameter value: any prefix with \\(\\ell\\ge1\\) takes softmax mass \\(\\alpha_i>0\\) from the input.","key_idea":"Prefix-tuning underperforms on deep LLMs because the prefix shares each head's softmax normaliser with the input, so prefix influence and input specificity trade off with sequence length. PMT moves the prefix out of the softmax, linearises it with a kernel feature map \\(\\phi\\), and replaces the prefix key-value sum with a free memory matrix \\(M\\), giving \\(o_i=\\mathrm{Attn}_i+\\phi(q_i)^{\\top}M\\).","results_note":"With one training example per class, PMT reaches 71.2% on BigBench with LLaMA2-7B-Chat and 76.6% with Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, above LoRA (\\(r=64\\)), prefix-tuning and full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Derived from the prefix-tuning attention output by decoupling the softmax, kernel-linearising, and freeing the prefix key-value sum. With the normaliser removed, prefix-tuning is not an exact member of the PMT family, so PMT relaxes it rather than strictly generalising it."},{"target":"prefix-tuning-capabilities-limitations","type":"builds-on","note":"Starts from Petrov et al.'s decomposition \\(o^{pt}_i=(1-\\alpha_i)o_i+\\sum_j\\alpha_{ij}W_Vs_j\\). Argues that their explanation (prefixes cannot change the attention pattern) holds for shallow transformers but not for deep LLMs."},{"target":"llama-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"LLaMA-Adapter already gives adaption prompts their own softmax, separate from the token softmax, with a zero-init gate; this is PMT's step (i). PMT further linearises the prompt term and replaces it with a free matrix. The paper does not cite LLaMA-Adapter."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"special-case-of","note":"Derived here: through \\(W_O\\), the PMT branch is a parallel adapter with a frozen down-projection \\(W_Q^{(h)}\\) per head (total width \\(d\\), so not a bottleneck), an elementwise \\(\\phi\\), and a trained block-diagonal map of per-head \\(M_h^{\\top}\\) read out through the frozen \\(W_O\\)."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"In Table 1, PMT beats LoRA \\(r=64\\) (33.55M parameters on LLaMA2-7B-Chat, about twice PMT's 16.91M) on all three few-shot datasets for both models, and Table 7 shows it ahead of LoRA \\(r=8\\) to \\(32\\). It also beats a LoRA baseline in CFT math (Table 3) and in AlpacaEval 2 win-rate deltas (Table 4); the LoRA rank used in those two tables is not stated."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13674","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.13674","https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13674v1","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=LvUMpZE44r","https://github.com/haonan3/PrefixMemory-Tuning","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2506.13674"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Haonan Wang","Brian Chen","Siquan Li","Xinhe Liang","Hwee Kuan Lee","Kenji Kawaguchi","Tianyang Hu"],"bibkey":"wang2025prefixmemory","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2025prefixmemory,\n  title = {{PrefixMemory-Tuning: Modernizing Prefix-Tuning by Decoupling the Prefix from Attention}},\n  author = {Haonan Wang and Brian Chen and Siquan Li and Xinhe Liang and Hwee Kuan Lee and Kenji Kawaguchi and Tianyang Hu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2506.13674},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.13674}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A pointed extension: each head gains the query-conditioned branch \\(\\phi(q_i)^\\top M_h\\), added to its output before \\(W_O\\) and outside the softmax; the base is frozen and \\(\\{M_{l,h}\\}\\) trains.","shape":"Function-level: through \\(W_O\\) the branch is \\(\\sum_hW_O^{(h)}M_h^\\top\\phi(W_Q^{(h)}x)\\), a non-linear branch parallel to the attention sublayer.","base_point":"\\(M=0\\) removes the branch exactly (neutral). No init is stated and the repository is empty, so this reads the natural zero start; prefix tuning has no such point.","gauge":"None: \\(M_h\\) sits between the frozen elementwise \\(\\phi\\circ W_Q^{(h)}\\) and the frozen, generically injective \\(W_O^{(h)}\\), so \\(M\\mapsto\\) branch is injective.","covariance":"A change of basis of the residual wire is carried by the frozen \\(W_Q,W_O\\): function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\), as prefix tuning.","merge":"\\(\\phi\\) (elu or gelu) is non-affine in the input (Thm VI.3(c)); the branch stays at inference.","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"PMT turns prefix tuning's renormalised prompt contribution into an ungated kernel-feature memory: by Thm VI.5(b) a prefix rescales content attention by \\(1-\\lambda_q\\), while PMT keeps the content path at weight 1 and adds \\(M^\\top\\phi(q)\\). That buys a neutral point (as LLaMA-Adapter's gate does) and makes the method a parallel branch around attention whose down-projection is the frozen \\(W_Q\\), whose activation is \\(\\phi\\) (elu or gelu) and whose up-projection is \\(W_O\\) times a trained block-diagonal map. Because \\(\\phi\\) is not ReLU, this is not the catalogued (ReLU) parallel adapter, so no arrow into it is recorded.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"f lies in Im PMT (at M = 0) but not in Im prefix tuning, which is unpointed (Thm VI.5(f)); moreover a prefix multiplies content attention by 1 - lambda_q, which PMT never does."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"For generic M the branch is non-affine in the input (elu or gelu), while every LoRA setting keeps the attention projections linear, so no weight-space LoRA realises it (Thm VI.3(c))."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"The catalogued parallel adapter is He et al.'s ReLU bottleneck, whose branch U ReLU(D x) is piecewise linear in its input. PMT's branch sum_h W_O^(h) M_h^T phi(W_Q^(h) x) with phi = elu or gelu is real-analytic and non-affine on open sets of (normalised) inputs, so for generic M it equals no finite ReLU branch: Im PMT is not inside Im PA. PMT factors only through a phi-activated parallel branch with a frozen down-projection W_Q, which is not a catalogued object (the parallel-adapter entry excludes GELU-bottleneck arrows for the same reason)."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"fourtune","name":"FourTune","full_name":"FourTune: fully 4-bit (W4A4G4) LoRA post-training with a frozen low-rank numerical stabilizer","paper_title":"FourTune: Towards Fully 4-Bit Efficient Post-Training for Diffusion Models","authors":"Xue et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2607.05711","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05711","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0\\approx R+L_{\\text{stab}},\\ \\ L_{\\text{stab}}=L_1L_2\\ \\text{(low-rank outlier branch from the SVDQuant decomposition, kept in high precision)},\\ \\ R \\text{ quantised offline to NVFP4 with } 16\\times16 \\text{ block scales};\\ \\ h=\\mathrm{GEMM}_{4\\text{bit}}\\big(Q(R),Q(x)\\big)+L_{\\text{stab}}\\,x+BA\\,x;\\ \\ \\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x}=\\mathrm{GEMM}_{4\\text{bit}}\\big(Q(R)^{\\top},Q(g)\\big)+L_{\\text{stab}}^{\\top}g+(BA)^{\\top}g,\\ \\ g=\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial h}\\ \\text{(4-bit gradients through the backbone; block scales let } Q(R)^{\\top} \\text{ be used without requantising)};\\ \\ \\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial B}=g\\,(Ax)^{\\top},\\ \\ \\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial A}=B^{\\top}g\\,x^{\\top};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } R,\\ L_{\\text{stab}};\\ \\text{trained: } B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix } (r=64 \\text{ in the experiments});\\ \\text{also frozen: the high-precision rank-}k \\text{ stabilizer, } k(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ entries (} k \\text{ not stated), and the 4-bit residual}","locus":"Linear layers (GEMMs) of the diffusion backbone: the FLUX.1-dev (12B) and Qwen-Image (20B) DiTs, plus SDXL in Appendix B. The MLP's FC1 GEMM is fused with the FC2 quantisation kernel.","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A QLoRA-type composite with three parallel branches on each linear layer. The first is a frozen 4-bit residual GEMM (W4A4). The second is a frozen high-precision low-rank stabilizer \\(L_{\\text{stab}}\\) that absorbs the quantisation-sensitive outlier directions of \\(W_0\\); this is SVDQuant's decomposition, reused here during training. The third is a trainable rank-\\(r\\) LoRA \\(BA\\). The trainable update is the ordinary additive \\(BA\\); what changes is the arithmetic. QLoRA dequantises and computes in W4A16G16, while FourTune runs the forward and input-gradient GEMMs in 4-bit (W4A4G4). Its \\(16\\times16\\) block-wise scales let the transposed weight be used in the backward pass without requantisation. The LoRA down-projection, the stabilizer and activation quantisation share one fused kernel, and the 4-bit GEMM is fused with the up-projections. The block granularity is \\(16\\times16\\) instead of the usual \\(1\\times16\\) groups (Table 5: LPIPS 0.227 vs 0.203 for reconstruction). An INT4 variant on an RTX 4090 is reported in Appendix B. Without the stabilizer, naive W4A4G4 training shows gradient explosion in the distillation runs (Fig. 8). The stabilizer and the LoRA have the same algebraic form (high-precision low-rank maps of \\(x\\)), so the trained LoRA can be concatenated into the stabilizer branch: \\([L_1\\ B][L_2;A]\\).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) can be concatenated into the high-precision low-rank branch, \\(L_{\\text{stab}}+BA=[L_1\\ B]\\,[L_2;A]\\) (rank \\(k+r\\)), which keeps the trained function exactly in a W4A4 inference engine. Folding it into the 4-bit residual \\(R\\) requires requantisation. Folding it into a BF16 \\(W_0\\) gives \\(W_0+BA\\), which differs from the training-time function by the quantisation error of \\(R\\).","identity_at_init":"Not stated. Under standard LoRA initialisation (\\(BA=0\\)) the model starts at the quantised base \\(Q(R)Q(x)+L_{\\text{stab}}x\\approx W_0x\\), not exactly at \\(W_0x\\).","key_idea":"FourTune aims to make PEFT faster than BF16 LoRA, not only smaller, by running the whole frozen backbone in native 4-bit for weights, activations and gradients. A frozen high-precision low-rank stabilizer \\(L_{\\text{stab}}\\) carries the outlier directions. \\(16\\times16\\) block scales and fused LoRA and MLP kernels keep the 4-bit backward pass efficient.","results_note":"In end-to-end FLUX.1-dev customization training, FourTune uses 14.51 GB against 29.35 GB for BF16 LoRA. A step takes 0.86 s on an RTX 5090, 2.27x faster than BF16 LoRA and 2.79x faster than NF4 QLoRA, at matched generation quality.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"Same design of a frozen 4-bit base plus LoRA. QLoRA dequantises for compute (W4A16G16); FourTune computes in W4A4G4."},{"target":"qerl","type":"competes-with","note":"Also NVFP4 base plus LoRA (QeRL targets RL for LLMs). QeRL dequantises weights for compute; FourTune quantises activations and gradients as well."},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"Both handle the quantised-base mismatch with a high-precision low-rank component. LoftQ puts it into the trainable LoRA initialisation; FourTune keeps it as a separate frozen stabilizer."},{"target":"sparselora","type":"competes-with","note":"Training-time acceleration baseline for LoRA (contextual channel sparsity). It underperforms FourTune on \\(\\pi\\)-Flow distillation, even at 50% sparsity."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"The trainable branch is a standard rank-64 LoRA."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05711","https://arxiv.org/html/2607.05711","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=RpwnrBkht2","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2607.05711"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Bowen Xue","Zihan Min","Xingyang Li","Zhekai Zhang","Haocheng Xi","Lvmin Zhang","Maneesh Agrawala","Jun-Yan Zhu","Song Han","Yujun Lin","Muyang Li"],"bibkey":"xue2026fourtune","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xue2026fourtune,\n  title = {{FourTune: Towards Fully 4-Bit Efficient Post-Training for Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Bowen Xue and Zihan Min and Xingyang Li and Zhekai Zhang and Haocheng Xi and Lvmin Zhang and Maneesh Agrawala and Jun-Yan Zhu and Song Han and Yujun Lin and Muyang Li},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2607.05711},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05711}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"QLoRA-type: a frozen NVFP4 residual \\(Q(R)\\), a frozen high-precision rank-\\(k\\) stabiliser \\(L_{stab}=L_1L_2\\) (SVDQuant) and a trainable LoRA \\(BA\\), computed in W4A4G4. The 4-bit activation quantiser changes the network itself (\\(f\\mapsto\\tilde f\\)), so the slice is over the quantised architecture.","shape":"\\(Q(R)+L_{stab}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): LoRA's cone at the quantised base \\(\\kappa'\\theta_0=Q(R)+L_{stab}\\) (Prop V.2(a)).","base_point":"Defect \\(e=Q(R)+L_{stab}-W_0\\), the quantisation error of the SVDQuant residual, on top of the activation quantisation; a zero-factor LoRA start is assumed (not stated).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"The NVFP4 grid with \\(16\\times16\\) block scales lives in the standard basis: Mon×Mon (grid), as QLoRA.","merge":"Folding \\(BA\\) into \\(Q(R)\\) leaves the 4-bit type (or requantises lossily); concatenation into the high-precision branch, \\([L_1\\ B][L_2;A]\\), is exact but raises its rank from \\(k\\) to \\(k+r\\). Coded Mq.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\) in exact arithmetic, as QLoRA."},"categorical_reading":"FourTune is QLoRA over a different base change: the quantiser is SVDQuant's split into a high-precision low-rank part plus a 4-bit residual, kept frozen during training, and both passes run in 4-bit arithmetic. As an object it is LoRA at the translate \\(Q(R)+L_{stab}\\). The stabiliser and the adapter have the same algebraic form, which is why the trained LoRA can be absorbed exactly into the low-rank branch, but not into the 4-bit weight.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O1","reason":"Different quantisers give different translates of the same cone: Q(R) + L_stab and NF4(W_0) differ by a generically full-rank matrix, and c + M_{<=r} lies in c' + M_{<=r} only if c = c' (proof of Thm II.2(a)). The activation quantisers differ as well (A4 against A16)."},{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"rho(q_0) = Q(R) + L_stab != theta_0, and theta_0 is not in the image unless rank e <= r (Prop V.2(a)): no pointed arrow."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"gradfix","name":"GradFix","full_name":"GradFix: gradient-sign masking for task-vector transport across pre-trained models","paper_title":"Gradient-Sign Masking for Task Vector Transport Across Pre-Trained Models","authors":"Rinaldi et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2510.09658","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09658","code_url":"https://github.com/fillo-rinaldi/GradFix","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\tau_A=\\theta_A^{ft}-\\theta_A\\ \\ (\\text{per matrix } \\Delta W_A=W_A^{ft}-W_A);\\ \\ \\hat s_i=\\mathrm{sign}\\Big(-\\sum_{(x_n,y_n)\\in\\mathcal{D}_s}\\mathrm{sign}\\big(\\nabla_\\theta\\ell(f_{\\theta_B}(x_n),y_n)\\big)_i\\Big)\\ \\ (\\text{majority vote over } N=|\\mathcal{D}_s| \\text{ labelled target examples});\\ \\ m_i=\\mathbb{1}\\{\\mathrm{sign}(\\tau_{A,i})=\\hat s_i\\},\\ \\ \\theta_B^{\\text{trans}}=\\theta_B+\\alpha\\,(m\\odot\\tau_A);\\ \\ \\text{full-data form: } g=\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_B),\\ \\ g^{\\top}\\big(\\alpha\\,m\\odot\\tau_A\\big)=-\\alpha\\textstyle\\sum_im_i|g_i||\\tau_{A,i}|\\le0;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } \\theta_A,\\ \\theta_A^{ft},\\ \\theta_B;\\ \\text{nothing is trained; } \\alpha \\text{ is chosen on a validation set}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(training-free); each transport builds one binary mask } m\\in\\{0,1\\}^{|\\theta|} \\text{ from } N \\text{ per-sample target gradients, plus the scalar } \\alpha","locus":"All parameters covered by the source task vector: the CLIP ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 image encoders (text encoder frozen), and T5-base for language tasks (T5 v1.1 as source, FLAN-T5 as target).","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A coordinatewise Hadamard filter on a task vector, in parameter coordinates. The transported delta keeps \\(\\tau_A\\) on the sign-agreement set \\(\\{i:\\mathrm{sign}\\,\\tau_{A,i}=\\mathrm{sign}(-g_i)\\}\\) unchanged and zeros it elsewhere. This makes it a base-change map: a displacement measured at base point \\(\\theta_A\\) is projected onto the target's local descent orthant at \\(\\theta_B\\), with no permutation alignment and no parameter updates. The first-order descent property \\(g^{\\top}\\delta\\le0\\) holds by construction for the full-data gradient, so the update lowers the loss for sufficiently small \\(\\alpha\\). With few samples the paper's lemma is per coordinate: under an independence assumption and \\(p_i>1/2\\), \\(\\Pr[\\hat s_i=\\mathrm{sign}(-g_i)]\\ge1-e^{-2N(p_i-1/2)^2}\\) (Hoeffding). A guarantee for the whole vector would need a union bound that the paper does not state. The filter acts on coordinates, not on functions, so it depends on the basis. One consequence, derived here: for a factored adapter, \\(m\\odot(BA)\\) is in general full-rank, so a LoRA task vector would be transported as a masked dense matrix, not as low-rank factors. The paper's experiments use full fine-tuning task vectors. For multi-task merging, merge-then-mask with a consensus target-sign mask works best; for several sources fine-tuned on one task, mask-then-merge works best. Magnitude-weighted masks underperform, which the paper reads as directions transferring across pre-trainings while magnitudes do not.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The result is a single additive weight delta \\(\\alpha\\,m\\odot\\tau_A\\) on \\(\\theta_B\\). It composes with Task Arithmetic and TIES (mask-then-merge or merge-then-mask) and adds no inference overhead.","identity_at_init":"Nothing is trained. \\(\\alpha=0\\), or an empty mask, returns the target base \\(\\theta_B\\) exactly.","key_idea":"A task vector trained on an old base \\(\\theta_A\\) transfers to a new release \\(\\theta_B\\) once its coordinates whose sign disagrees with the target's anti-gradient are removed. A few target gradients, combined by a majority vote of signs and with no update steps, rebase \\(\\tau_A\\) onto \\(\\theta_B\\) with a first-order descent guarantee \\(g^{\\top}\\delta\\le0\\).","results_note":"With one labelled example per class, transporting a DataComp-XL CLIP ViT-B/16 EuroSAT task vector to a LAION-2B ViT-B/16 gives 61.94%. Naive task-vector addition gives 49.58%, zero-shot 49.41%, and few-shot fine-tuning on the same samples 56.61%.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"Extends task-vector addition to a different base point. Naive \\(\\theta_B+\\tau_A\\) stays near zero-shot when the bases differ."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"Used with TA and TIES in multi-task merging (merge-then-mask) and multi-source merging (mask-then-merge). TIES elects signs by task-vector mass; GradFix elects them by target anti-gradient votes."},{"target":"trans-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both move fine-tuning knowledge to a new base model. Trans-LoRA re-trains the adapter by distillation on synthetic data; GradFix is training-free masking from a few labelled samples. GradFix does not cite Trans-LoRA, so this is a structural comparison."},{"target":"lora-x","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are training-free cross-model transfer. LoRA-X projects onto the target's SVD subspaces; GradFix applies a coordinatewise gradient-sign mask. GradFix does not cite LoRA-X, so this is a structural comparison."},{"target":"git-re-basin","type":"competes-with","note":"The paper frames transport against model rebasin (Ainsworth et al., cited in Sec. 3). Rebasin aligns two pre-trained models by a weight permutation; GradFix transfers a task vector between bases without any permutation, by sign masking. The paper's permutation-based baseline TransFusion is not catalogued."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09658","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09658","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://openreview.net/forum?id=CQnpxZNMvT","https://github.com/fillo-rinaldi/GradFix","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2510.09658"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-1"],"authors_full":["Filippo Rinaldi","Aniello Panariello","Giacomo Salici","Fengyuan Liu","Marco Ciccone","Angelo Porrello","Simone Calderara"],"bibkey":"rinaldi2025gradient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{rinaldi2025gradient,\n  title = {{Gradient-Sign Masking for Task Vector Transport Across Pre-Trained Models}},\n  author = {Filippo Rinaldi and Aniello Panariello and Giacomo Salici and Fengyuan Liu and Marco Ciccone and Angelo Porrello and Simone Calderara},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2510.09658},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09658}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and training-free: a task vector \\(\\tau_A\\) computed at base \\(\\theta_A\\) is masked by sign agreement with the target's anti-gradient, \\(m_i=\\mathbb 1\\{\\mathrm{sign}\\,\\tau_{A,i}=\\hat s_i\\}\\) (majority vote over a few labelled samples), and added to a new base \\(\\theta_B\\) with a validation-tuned \\(\\alpha\\).","shape":"The coefficient line \\(\\theta_B+\\alpha(m\\odot\\tau_A)\\), \\(d=1\\).","base_point":"\\(\\alpha=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_B\\); the slice is the one at the new base.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"The mask compares the signs of a vector and a covector: under \\(\\theta\\mapsto D\\theta\\) with \\(D\\) invertible diagonal, \\(\\tau\\mapsto D\\tau\\) and \\(g\\mapsto D^{-1}g\\), so every sign agreement survives; permutations too, rotations not. Exactly \\(\\mathrm{Mon}_N\\) (magnitude-free, larger than TIES's \\(\\mathbb R^\\times B_N\\)): MonxMon.","merge":"One additive delta on \\(\\theta_B\\).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the mask frozen; recomputing the mask at the merged point is a new transport."},"categorical_reading":"GradFix is a base-change map for task vectors: a displacement measured at \\(\\theta_A\\) is projected, coordinate by coordinate, onto the descent orthant \\(\\{\\delta:\\delta_ig_i\\le0\\}\\) of the loss at \\(\\theta_B\\), which is exactly its first-order guarantee. Being coordinatewise it is monomial-natural, not rotation-natural, and it would turn a LoRA task vector into a masked dense matrix.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\alpha\\,(m\\odot\\tau_A)","note":"Per transport, over the base theta_B: the line lies in FISH with mask M = supp(m), pointed at 0 (as Sparse Memory Finetuning -> FISH)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"When the mask keeps part but not all of the support of tau_A, m (.) tau_A is not a multiple of tau_A, so the line differs from task arithmetic's theta_B + lambda tau_A (which stays near zero-shot across bases, as the paper reports)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"coa-lora","name":"CoA-LoRA","full_name":"CoA-LoRA: Configuration-Aware LoRA (a hypernetwork maps each layer's quantisation configuration to an \\(r\\times r\\) adjustment of that layer's LoRA)","paper_title":"On-the-Fly Adaptation to Quantization: Configuration-Aware LoRA for Efficient Fine-Tuning of Quantized LLMs","authors":"Ye et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2509.25214","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25214","code_url":"https://github.com/rG223/CoA-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=\\big(\\widetilde W^{(i)}_{\\mathbf C}+L_{1,i}\\,(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i))\\,L_{2,i}\\big)x,\\quad U_\\theta(Q_i)=\\gamma\\cdot\\mathrm{reshape}_{r\\times r}\\Big(\\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta\\big(\\mathrm{LN}[\\,e(b_{0,i}),e(b_{1,i}),e(b_{2,i}),e(B_{0,i}),e(B_{1,i}),e(\\text{module}_i),e(\\text{block}_i)\\,]\\big)\\Big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r};\\ \\ \\mathbf C=\\{c_i\\}_{i=1}^{N},\\ c_i=[b_{0,i},b_{1,i},b_{2,i},B_{0,i},B_{1,i}]\\ (\\text{NormalFloat bit-widths and block sizes of layer } i);\\ \\ W_0^{(i)}-\\widetilde W^{(i)}_{\\mathbf C}\\approx U\\Sigma V^{\\top}\\Rightarrow L_{1,i}=U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ L_{2,i}=\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\min_\\theta\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\mathbf C\\in\\mathcal C}\\,\\mathcal L\\big(\\{\\widetilde W^{(i)}_{\\mathbf C}+L_{1,i}(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i))L_{2,i}\\}_{i=1}^{N};\\mathcal D\\big),\\ \\ \\mathcal C\\ \\text{refined by Pareto/EHVI search};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: quantised base } \\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C};\\ \\text{trained: mapper } \\theta\\ (\\text{embeddings, LayerNorm, 2-layer MLP, scalar }\\gamma);\\ \\text{the released code also trains one LoRA pair } (L_1,L_2)\\ \\text{shared by all configurations}","trainable_params":"|\\theta|=129\\,r^{2}+O(10^{4})\\ \\text{per model, shared by all } N \\text{ adapted matrices and all configurations }(2{,}121{,}265\\text{ to }2{,}121{,}281 \\text{ at } r=128,\\ 0.03\\text{--}0.5\\%\\ \\text{of the base, Table A.1};\\ \\approx 0.54\\text{M at the main-experiment rank } r=64);\\ \\text{plus } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix for the LoRA factors (SVD-initialised; frozen in the paper's Eq. (3), trained once and shared in the released code)}","locus":"Every LoRA-targeted linear layer of a NormalFloat-quantised model, each with its own per-layer configuration: query, key, value, output.dense and intermediate.dense of RoBERTa-Large; q/k/v/o/gate/up/down projections of Qwen2.5-1.5B, Qwen2.5-3B and LLaMA-2-7B","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"A family of additive rank-\\(r\\) updates indexed by the discrete space of per-layer NormalFloat configurations \\(\\mathbf C=\\{c_i\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\), with \\(c_i\\in\\{2,3,4,8\\}^2\\times\\{\\text{bf16},\\text{fp16},\\text{fp32}\\}\\times\\{16,32,64\\}\\times\\{16,64,256\\}\\) (432 choices per layer, \\(432^{N}\\) per model). One small hypernetwork \\(\\theta\\) maps a layer's configuration embedding, plus its module-type and block-index embeddings, to an \\(r\\times r\\) core \\(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i)\\) placed between the LoRA factors: \\(\\Delta W_i(\\mathbf C)=L_{1,i}(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i))L_{2,i}\\). The core acts only on the rank space. When it is invertible, the update keeps column space \\(\\mathrm{col}(L_{1,i})\\) and row space \\(\\mathrm{row}(L_{2,i})\\), so the configuration moves the update only inside a fixed \\(r\\times r\\) coordinate window, as in LoRA-XS. In the paper's Eq. (2) the factors \\(L^{(\\mathbf C)}_{1,i},L^{(\\mathbf C)}_{2,i}\\) are the rank-\\(r\\) SVD of the quantisation residual \\(W_0-\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\) for that configuration, so a new configuration also needs \\(W_0\\) for the residual SVD. In the released GLUE code (run_glue.py, lorasync mode), one LoRA pair initialised from a single configuration's residual SVD is trained jointly with \\(\\theta\\) and loaded into every configuration's model, so in practice one adapter is shared and only the core varies. The training set \\(\\mathcal C\\) is itself optimised. It starts from 50 configurations of 2.25 to 7.25 average bits chosen by an integer program, then alternates training \\(\\theta\\) with a search step: a Gaussian-process surrogate of accuracy, an expected-hypervolume-improvement acquisition maximised by finite-difference coordinate moves, and Pareto filtering inside 40 bit-width segments.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For a fixed configuration \\(\\mathbf C\\), the generated update \\(L_{1,i}(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i))L_{2,i}\\) is a static rank-\\(r\\) matrix, computed with one mapper forward pass per layer and kept beside the quantised weights. Folding it into \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\) needs dequantisation, and requantising would change the configuration, as with QLoRA. The mapper \\(\\theta\\) has to be kept to serve other configurations.","identity_at_init":"Not identity relative to the quantised base. In the code, the last MLP layer starts with \\(\\mathcal N(0,(10^{-3})^2)\\) weights and zero bias, so \\(I_r+U_\\theta\\approx I_r\\). The LoRA factors start as the rank-\\(r\\) SVD of \\(W_0-\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\), so the initial model \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}+L_1L_2\\) is the best rank-\\(r\\) correction of the quantisation error. It approximates \\(W_0\\), not \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\).","key_idea":"The quantisation configuration becomes a conditioning input. A hypernetwork of about 2M parameters turns each layer's bit-width and block-size choice into an \\(r\\times r\\) adjustment \\(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i)\\) of that layer's LoRA, so one training run serves any per-layer configuration instead of one QLoRA or LQ-LoRA run per configuration. The configurations used for training are chosen by a Pareto search over accuracy and average bit-width.","results_note":"On four GLUE tasks with RoBERTa-Large, one training run of about an hour covers 50 configurations from 2.5 to 6.25 bits, with accuracy comparable to or better than QLoRA and LQ-LoRA, which need 20-40 minutes of fine-tuning per configuration.","relations":[{"target":"lq-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited and used as the strongest baseline. The base LoRA factors come from the low-rank-plus-quantised (LPQ) decomposition of LQ-LoRA, and the code reuses its NF configuration space and integer-program bit allocation. LQ-LoRA needs one fine-tuning run per configuration; CoA-LoRA amortises over configurations."},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Per-configuration baseline. A dedicated QLoRA adapter is fine-tuned for each bit-width, so cost grows linearly with the number of configurations."},{"target":"asymmetry-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (with Flora) for the claim that most of the adaptation signal sits in one factor, which motivates adjusting only through the \\(r\\times r\\) core. The paper places the signal in \\(L_{2,i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times n}\\), its input-side factor, whereas Zhu et al. find the output-side factor matters more; because the core sits between the factors, \\((I+U)L_2\\) and \\(L_1(I+U)\\) give the same update and the placement is side-neutral."},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Same shape: an \\(r\\times r\\) core between SVD-derived factors. LoRA-XS trains one core per task from the SVD of \\(W_0\\); CoA-LoRA generates the core \\(I+U_\\theta\\) per quantisation configuration, on factors from the SVD of the quantisation residual."},{"target":"hyperformer","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Both generate adapter parameters from layer and condition embeddings with a shared hypernetwork. HyperFormer conditions on task and layer and generates adapter weights; CoA-LoRA conditions on quantisation configuration, module type and block index and generates only an \\(r\\times r\\) LoRA core."},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited with the asymmetry paper (Hao et al., 2024) as evidence that adaptation concentrates in one LoRA factor, the stated motivation for generating only an \\(r\\times r\\) adjustment per layer."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25214","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.25214","https://github.com/rG223/CoA-LoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rG223/CoA-LoRA/HEAD/models/lora_utils.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rG223/CoA-LoRA/HEAD/run_glue.py","https://openreview.net/forum?id=9OUg0nJE72","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10011118"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Rongguang Ye","Ming Tang","Edith C. H. Ngai"],"bibkey":"ye2025fly","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ye2025fly,\n  title = {{On-the-Fly Adaptation to Quantization: Configuration-Aware LoRA for Efficient Fine-Tuning of Quantized LLMs}},\n  author = {Rongguang Ye and Ming Tang and Edith C. H. Ngai},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2509.25214},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.25214}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over per-layer quantisation configurations \\(\\mathbf C\\) (\\(432^N\\) of them): \\(\\Delta W_i(\\mathbf C)=L_{1,i}(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_i))L_{2,i}\\) on \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\), with the \\(r\\times r\\) core generated by one hypernetwork from configuration, module-type and block embeddings. Read in the released instance, where one LoRA pair is trained jointly with \\(\\theta\\) and shared by all configurations.","shape":"Each fibre lies in \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) and starts at a rank-\\(r\\) point: LoRA's cone translated to a quantised apex (Cone*, as LQ-LoRA). With the paper's frozen factors (Eq. 3) a fibre is only the \\(r^2\\)-dimensional core window \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}+L_1\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}L_2\\) (Lin).","base_point":"\\(I_r+U_\\theta\\approx I_r\\) at init and \\(L_1L_2=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0-\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C})\\): pointed up to the attained residual (an approximate section, Prop V.2(c)). In the code the shared pair comes from one configuration's residual, so the other configurations start with a larger defect.","gauge":"\\((L_1,L_2)\\mapsto(L_1G^{-1},GL_2)\\) with the core conjugated, \\(C\\mapsto GCG^{-1}\\) (which keeps the identity term), absorbed by the mapper's linear last layer; plus the slide of \\(\\gamma\\) against that layer. Coded GL.","covariance":"NormalFloat grids and block sizes live in the standard basis: Mon×Mon (grid), as QLoRA and LQ-LoRA.","merge":"For a fixed configuration the update is a static rank-\\(r\\) matrix, but folding it into \\(\\widetilde W_{\\mathbf C}\\) needs dequantisation (requantising changes the configuration), and the mapper must stay to serve the others.","rebasing":"Undefined for a family whose fibre changes with the configuration."},"categorical_reading":"CoA-LoRA is a section of LQ-LoRA over the space of per-layer quantisation configurations: one hypernetwork turns each configuration into an \\(r\\times r\\) move inside a fixed pair of column and row spaces, as NaRA does over noise levels. Every fibre lives in a different translate of LoRA's cone, one per quantised base, and is pointed only up to that configuration's attained residual.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lq-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{\\mathbf C}(\\theta,L_1,L_2)=\\big(L_1\\,(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_{\\mathbf C})),\\ L_2\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over configurations, per layer, into LQ-LoRA over the quantised base W~_C, pointed at h_C(q_0) (approximately (L_1, L_2), since the mapper's last layer starts at N(0,1e-6)). This is LQ-LoRA's own start when W~_C and the factors come from the same low-rank-plus-quantised split at that configuration."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"A rank-r start that compensates the quantisation error (a smooth point over W~_C) against QLoRA's apex at kappa theta_0, with different apexes as well (as LQ-LoRA)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"low-rank-ternary-adaptation","name":"Low-Rank Ternary Adaptation","full_name":"Ternary multiplicative adaptation: a Kronecker-factorised ternary keep/zero/flip mask applied elementwise to ternary weights","paper_title":"Low-Rank Ternary Adaptation for Fine-Tuning Transformers","authors":"Manolache et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ECCV 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2608.24469","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24469","code_url":"https://github.com/alexmanoo/ternary_adaptation","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\,W_{\\text{tern}},\\ W_{\\text{tern}}\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ h=\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\big(W_{\\text{tern}}\\odot\\Delta_{\\text{tern}}\\big)x,\\quad \\Delta_{\\text{tern}}=A\\otimes B,\\ \\ A=\\mathrm{Tern}(\\bar A)\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^{p_1\\times q_1},\\ B=\\mathrm{Tern}(\\bar B)\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^{p_2\\times q_2},\\ \\ p_1p_2=d_{out},\\ q_1q_2=d_{in};\\ \\ \\mathrm{Tern}:\\ \\text{entrywise threshold set from the mean absolute value of each factor, gradients passed to the real proxies } \\bar A,\\bar B \\text{ by a straight-through estimator};\\ \\ W_{\\text{merged}}=W_{\\text{tern}}\\odot(A\\otimes B)\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_{\\text{tern}}\\ \\text{and its scales } s;\\ \\text{trained: } \\bar A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p_1\\times q_1},\\ \\bar B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p_2\\times q_2}","trainable_params":"p_1q_1+p_2q_2\\ \\text{per adapted matrix}\\ (p_1p_2=d_{out},\\ q_1q_2=d_{in});\\ 2d\\ \\text{for a square } d\\times d \\text{ layer only when } d \\text{ is a perfect square } (p_i=q_i=\\sqrt d);\\ \\text{Llama-3.2-1B's } 2048\\times 2048 \\text{ layers use } (32\\times32,\\ 64\\times64)=5120=2.5d,\\ 512\\text{-wide k/v use } 2560,\\ \\text{MLP layers } 10240;\\ \\approx 7.4\\times10^{5}\\ \\text{in total} \\approx 0.06\\%\\ \\text{of Llama-3.2-1B (Table 5)}","locus":"All self-attention and feed-forward linear layers of ternary Transformers, output head excluded: SpinQuant-ternarised Llama-3.2-1B/3B, the pre-trained ternary BitNet b1.58 2B4T and Falcon-E-1B/3B-Instruct, and the ternary ViT-B/16 encoder of TernaryCLIP","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"A Hadamard (entrywise) action of the multiplicative monoid \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\) on ternary weights. Each mask entry keeps (\\(+1\\)), zeroes (\\(0\\)) or flips (\\(-1\\)) the corresponding weight. \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\) is closed under multiplication and \\(\\odot\\) commutes with the row scaling \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\), so adapted and merged weights stay ternary with unchanged scales. The mask is constrained to a Kronecker product \\(A\\otimes B\\) of two small ternary factors, so \\(\\mathrm{rank}(A\\otimes B)=\\mathrm{rank}(A)\\,\\mathrm{rank}(B)\\) is bounded by \\(\\min(p_1,q_1)\\min(p_2,q_2)\\) and reaches \\(\\min(d_{out},d_{in})\\) for the balanced factor shapes of Table 5, with only \\(p_1q_1+p_2q_2\\) proxies. Despite the title, the update is neither low-rank nor additive. Written additively it is \\(\\Delta W=\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\,\\big(W_{\\text{tern}}\\odot(A\\otimes B-\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^{\\top})\\big)\\). Because \\(0\\cdot\\Delta_{ij}=0\\), zero weights can never be reactivated (zero-locking). The weight-transition analysis confirms that no weight leaves 0 and that gains come from sign changes of nonzero weights. The 'balanced' and 'normalized' initialisations draw signed factors and pre-compensate the base, \\(W_{\\text{tern}}\\leftarrow(\\mathrm{sign}\\bar A\\otimes\\mathrm{sign}\\bar B)\\odot W_{\\text{tern}}\\). A \\(\\pm1\\) mask is an involution under \\(\\odot\\), so the step-0 forward weights equal the pretrained ones.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The merged weight \\(W_{\\text{tern}}\\odot(A\\otimes B)\\) lies again in \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}\\) with the original scales. It replaces the base layer with no dequantisation, requantisation, extra parameters or latency, and the proxies \\(\\bar A,\\bar B\\) are discarded.","identity_at_init":"Yes, by construction. All-ones factors give \\(A\\otimes B=\\mathbf 1\\mathbf 1^{\\top}\\) and \\(W'_{\\text{tern}}=W_{\\text{tern}}\\). The balanced and normalized schemes used in the main experiments have signed factors but pre-multiply the base by \\(\\mathrm{sign}\\bar A\\otimes\\mathrm{sign}\\bar B\\), so the step-0 forward weights are again the pretrained ternary weights.","key_idea":"Additive LoRA updates leave the ternary domain, so the update is made multiplicative: a ternary keep/zero/flip mask applied entrywise to ternary weights. The mask is parameterised as a Kronecker product \\(A\\otimes B\\) of two small ternary matrices, trained through real-valued proxies with a straight-through estimator. The fine-tuned model merges into a strictly ternary model with no dequantisation.","results_note":"On SpinQuant-ternarised Llama-3.2-3B fine-tuned on Alpaca, the merged ternary model raises the 9-task zero-shot average from 31.9 to 38.3 and cuts WikiText-2 perplexity from 45.6 to 22.3, ahead of QLoRA merged and requantised to ternary (37.5 and 22.9).","relations":[{"target":"lokr","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited Kronecker-factorised PEFT. LoKr adds \\(A\\otimes B\\) to the weight; here the Kronecker product is ternary and acts by Hadamard multiplication on ternary weights."},{"target":"krona","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited Kronecker adapter, the same factorisation idea for high rank from few parameters, used additively in floating point."},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main PEFT baseline on the same ternary backbone. QLoRA's floating-point adapter must be merged and requantised to stay at 1.58 bits, which costs accuracy."},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited. QA-LoRA merges without dequantisation by folding the low-rank update into group-wise zero points, but the merged weights cannot stay in \\(\\{-1,0,1\\}\\)."},{"target":"piggyback","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Piggyback also thresholds real proxies with a straight-through estimator and multiplies the resulting unstructured \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) mask into frozen weights. Ternary adaptation adds sign flips and constrains the mask to a Kronecker product."},{"target":"kadaptation","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (with LoKr and KronA) as a Kronecker-product adapter that preserves rank with few parameters; KAdaptation adds a floating-point Kronecker update to ViT weights, whereas here the Kronecker factors are ternary and multiply the weights entrywise."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24469","https://arxiv.org/html/2608.24469","https://github.com/alexmanoo/ternary_adaptation","https://eccv.ecva.net/virtual/2026/poster/5924"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Alexandru-Dragos Manolache","Yunqiang Li","Jan van Gemert"],"bibkey":"manolache2026low","bibtex":"@inproceedings{manolache2026low,\n  title = {{Low-Rank Ternary Adaptation for Fine-Tuning Transformers}},\n  author = {Alexandru-Dragos Manolache and Yunqiang Li and Jan van Gemert},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2608.24469},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24469}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Action type on a ternary checkpoint: \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\big(W_{tern}\\odot(A\\otimes B)\\big)\\), with ternary Kronecker factors obtained by thresholding real proxies (straight-through estimator).","shape":"The orbit of \\(\\theta_0\\) under the Hadamard monoid of ternary Kronecker masks \\(\\{A\\otimes B\\}\\subset\\{-1,0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}\\): a finite set (\\(d=0\\)), as Piggyback's Boolean orbit. The STE surrogate treats it as \\(W\\odot(\\bar A\\otimes\\bar B)\\), a Hadamard-transported rank-one Kronecker cone; that relaxation is training data, not \\(\\rho\\).","base_point":"All-ones factors (or the sign-compensated balanced init) give \\(\\theta_0\\) exactly; with \\(d=0\\), \\(\\dim S_1=0=d\\): regular trivially (Piggyback). (In the STE relaxation the all-ones point is also regular: \\(\\dim S_1=p_1q_1+p_2q_2-1\\).)","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) depends on the proxies only through \\(\\mathrm{Tern}(\\bar A),\\mathrm{Tern}(\\bar B)\\), whose thresholds are mean-relative: the generic fibre is an open code cell of full dimension \\(p_1q_1+p_2q_2\\). Its only exhibited linear symmetries are the positive scalings of each factor (Tern is scale-invariant) and the sign slide \\((A,B)\\mapsto(-A,-B)\\); per-entry positive scalings, which give Piggyback's torus, change the cell (checked: 99.95% of random ones), so the fibre is not a torus orbit: non-linear, as L4Q's code cells.","covariance":"Hadamard structure (Mon×Mon) composed with an imposed Kronecker reshape, which breaks neuron permutations: \\(\\Pi\\), as LoKr and KronA.","merge":"\\(W_{tern}\\odot(A\\otimes B)\\) is again ternary with unchanged scales: the exact merge stays in the quantised type (the opposite of Mq).","rebasing":"\\((A\\otimes B)\\odot(C\\otimes D)=(A\\odot C)\\otimes(B\\odot D)\\) (checked), so ternary Kronecker masks form a submonoid: idempotent. Zeros can never be reactivated."},"categorical_reading":"Ternary adaptation replaces the additive update, which leaves the ternary grid, by a monoid action that preserves it. The merge is type-preserving by construction, so the quantised-type obstruction of Prop V.2(d) disappears, and so does any gain from merge-and-restart, since the masks form a submonoid. The Kronecker constraint is what makes a sign-flip mask cheap, and it is also an imposed structure.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"piggyback","test":"O1","reason":"Piggyback's masks are {0,1}-valued, so its image keeps every sign of W_0; a ternary mask flips the signs of nonzero weights (about 26% of weights flip in the paper's transition analysis)."},{"target":"lokr","test":"O1","reason":"The ternary update diag(s)(W_tern (.) (A (x) B - 11^T)) carries the generic zero/sign pattern of W_tern, so its Van Loan-Pitsianis rearrangement has rank > 1 (checked: 6 for the same split, 4 for another), while LoKr's update C (x) BA is a single Kronecker term (rearrangement rank 1). Hence Im ternary is not inside Im LoKr. (Conversely LoKr can create entries where W_0 is zero, which the zero-locking ternary action cannot.)"}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"adarank","name":"AdaRank","full_name":"AdaRank: Adaptive Rank Pruning (test-time learned binary masks on the singular components of task vectors)","paper_title":"AdaRank: Adaptive Rank Pruning for Enhanced Model Merging","authors":"Lee et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2503.22178","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.22178","code_url":"https://github.com/david3684/AdaRank","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\tau_i^{l}=W_i^{l}-W_0^{l}=U_i^{l}\\Sigma_i^{l}V_i^{l\\top}\\ (i=1,\\dots,T);\\quad W^{l}(B)=W_0^{l}+\\lambda^{l}\\sum_{i=1}^{T}U_i^{l}\\,\\mathrm{diag}(B_i^{l})\\,\\Sigma_i^{l}\\,V_i^{l\\top},\\ \\ B_i^{l}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\min(d_{out},d_{in})};\\ \\ B_{i,r}^{l}=\\mathbf 1\\{\\sigma(\\tilde b_{i,r}^{l}/T_{\\text{temp}})\\ge 0.5\\}\\ \\ (\\text{straight-through estimator},\\ T_{\\text{temp}}=10);\\ \\ \\min_{\\tilde b,\\lambda}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal D_i^{\\text{test}}}H\\big(f(W(B),x)\\big)\\ \\ (\\text{output entropy on unlabeled test inputs});\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0 \\text{ and all } (U_i,\\Sigma_i,V_i);\\ \\text{optimised at test time: mask logits } \\tilde b \\text{ and layer coefficients } \\lambda^{l}","trainable_params":"T\\cdot\\min(d_{out},d_{in})\\ \\text{mask logits per weight matrix (one per singular component per task), plus one } \\lambda^{l}\\ \\text{per layer; all discarded after merging}","locus":"Every weight matrix of the task vectors of models fine-tuned from one backbone: CLIP ViT-B/32 and ViT-L/14 image encoders (8, 14 and 20 tasks), and RoBERTa and GPT-2 (7 NLP tasks)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Merging in the SVD basis of each task matrix with a learned 0/1 selection of singular triplets: \\(\\Delta W^{l}=\\lambda^{l}\\sum_{i}\\sum_{r}B^{l}_{i,r}\\sigma^{l}_{i,r}u^{l}_{i,r}v^{l\\top}_{i,r}\\). Each layer's mask set \\(\\{0,1\\}^{T\\times\\min(d_{out},d_{in})}\\) contains top-\\(k\\) truncation (\\(B_{i,r}=\\mathbf 1[r\\le k]\\), as in SVD merging and TSV-M) and full-rank Task Arithmetic (\\(B=\\mathbf 1\\)) as special points. The kept rank and the kept indices can vary by task and layer, and need not be the top ones. AdaRank plugs into a base merger. With Task Arithmetic \\(\\tau_i=W_i-W_0\\); with CART \\(\\tau_i=W_i-\\bar W\\) around the average weight; with TSV-M the concatenated \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) are whitened before masking; Iso-CTS is a fourth base merger in the experiments. Masks start at the base method's best setting and are optimised AdaMerging-style, by straight-through estimation of an entropy objective on unlabeled test batches. The motivation is that adding a top singular component of one task often raises the total multi-task loss.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After test-time adaptation the masks are frozen and the output is one static weight set of the backbone's size. No masks, routers or per-task parameters are kept at inference, unlike router-based merging.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable as an adapter. The masks start by reproducing the base merger: \\(B=\\mathbf 1\\) with \\(\\lambda=0.3\\) is Task Arithmetic, the top 16\\% of components for CART, and top-\\(k\\) for TSV-M. Setting all \\(B=0\\) recovers \\(W_0\\) (or \\(\\bar W\\) for CART).","key_idea":"When merging, the top-\\(k\\) singular components of a task vector are not always the right ones to keep: large components of one task can raise the other tasks' losses, and the useful rank varies by task and layer. AdaRank instead learns a binary keep/prune mask on every singular component by test-time entropy minimisation, replacing the fixed top-\\(k\\) rule of SVD-based merging.","results_note":"Added to Task Arithmetic on 8-, 14- and 20-task vision merging, AdaRank raises average accuracy by 18.6% (ViT-B/32) and 11.1% (ViT-L/14), outperforming all static merging baselines.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"generalizes","note":"With all masks equal to 1, AdaRank's merge is layer-wise Task Arithmetic; it is also the default base merger."},{"target":"adamerging","type":"builds-on","note":"Adopts AdaMerging's test-time entropy minimisation and learns its layer coefficients jointly with the masks; AdaRank beats AdaMerging, which tunes only the coefficients."},{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"builds-on","note":"Plugs into TSV-M: its fixed top-\\(k\\) truncation of whitened task singular vectors is one mask, and AdaRank replaces it with a learned one."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Element-wise trimming and sign election, as opposed to spectral pruning; a static baseline."},{"target":"dare","type":"competes-with","note":"Random element-wise drop-and-rescale of task vectors; cited as an element-wise alternative to spectral pruning."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.22178","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.22178","https://github.com/david3684/AdaRank","https://openreview.net/forum?id=fTygcJVOni","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10008234"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Chanhyuk Lee","Jiho Choi","Chanryeol Lee","Donggyun Kim","Seunghoon Hong"],"bibkey":"lee2025adarank","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lee2025adarank,\n  title = {{AdaRank: Adaptive Rank Pruning for Enhanced Model Merging}},\n  author = {Chanhyuk Lee and Jiho Choi and Chanryeol Lee and Donggyun Kim and Seunghoon Hong},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2503.22178},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.22178}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc, at test time: \\(W^l(B)=W_0^l+\\lambda^l\\sum_iU_i^l\\,\\mathrm{diag}(B_i^l)\\,\\Sigma_i^lV_i^{l\\top}\\) with binary keep/prune masks on every singular triplet and one coefficient per layer, fitted by entropy minimisation on unlabeled test inputs (straight-through estimator).","shape":"For each mask a layer-wise line; the coefficient family is the finite union of these lines through \\(\\theta_0\\), a cone (a union of linear spaces, as Diff pruning).","base_point":"Optimisation starts at the base merger (for TA: \\(B=\\mathbf 1\\), \\(\\lambda=0.3\\)), not at \\(\\theta_0\\): a defect, as AdaMerging. \\(B=0\\) or \\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"The mask logits enter only through \\(B_{i,r}=\\mathbb 1[\\tilde b_{i,r}\\ge0]\\) (\\(\\sigma(\\tilde b/T)\\ge0.5\\)), so the generic fibre is an open orthant cell, exactly an orbit of the positive torus \\(\\tilde b\\mapsto c\\odot\\tilde b\\) (fibre dimension \\(T\\min(d_{out},d_{in})\\) per matrix), as for Piggyback and Masking; for a fixed mask \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda D(B)\\) is injective when \\(D(B)\\neq0\\).","covariance":"Per-task singular frames are \\(CO\\times CO\\)-covariant and masking whole triplets commutes with them (TA base): OxO, as KnOTS and TSV-M.","merge":"One static weight set after adaptation.","rebasing":"With frames frozen the shape is closed under scaling, so \\(R_\\infty=W_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{\\sigma_{i,k}u_{i,k}v_{i,k}^\\top\\}\\) per layer (Thm V.3(a))."},"categorical_reading":"AdaRank enlarges task arithmetic's coefficient line to a union of lines indexed by subsets of singular triplets: top-\\(k\\) truncation (SVD merging, TSV-M) and full task arithmetic are two of its points, and test-time entropy picks another. It is a spectral-coordinate analogue of DARE and TIES, with a learned mask instead of a random or magnitude-based one.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"adamerging","test":"O1","reason":"For a mask that keeps part of a task's spectrum, sum_i U_i diag(B_i) Sigma_i V_i^T leaves span{tau_i^l}, while AdaMerging's family stays in that span (B = 1 gives its layer-constant points)."},{"target":"ties-merging","test":"O1","reason":"TIES trims and elects signs coordinatewise in weight coordinates; AdaRank keeps or prunes whole singular triplets. Their merged vectors differ generically."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"slao","name":"SLAO (Merge before Forget)","full_name":"SLAO: Single LoRA continual learning with Orthogonal initialization via continual merging","paper_title":"Merge before Forget: A Single LoRA Continual Learning via Continual Merging","authors":"Qiao et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2512.23017","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23017","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=\\big(W_0+B^{t}_{\\text{merge}}A^{t}_{\\text{merge}}\\big)x,\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{task }1:\\ B^{1}_{\\text{merge}}=B_{\\text{ft},1},\\ A^{1}_{\\text{merge}}=A_{\\text{ft},1}\\ (\\text{standard LoRA});\\ \\ \\text{task } i\\ge 2:\\ Q_iR_i=\\mathrm{QR}(A_{\\text{ft},i-1}^{\\top}),\\ Q_i\\leftarrow Q_i\\,\\mathrm{sign}(\\mathrm{diag}R_i),\\ \\ A^{(0)}_{\\text{ft},i}=Q_i^{\\top}\\ (A^{(0)}A^{(0)\\top}=I_r),\\ \\ B^{(0)}_{\\text{ft},i}=B_{\\text{ft},i-1};\\ \\ (B_{\\text{ft},i},A_{\\text{ft},i})=\\text{fine-tune on }\\mathcal D_i;\\ \\ A^{i}_{\\text{merge}}=A_{\\text{ft},i},\\ \\ B^{i}_{\\text{merge}}=B^{i-1}_{\\text{merge}}+\\lambda(i)\\big(B_{\\text{ft},i}-B^{i-1}_{\\text{merge}}\\big),\\ \\ \\lambda(i)=1/\\sqrt{i};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{trained per task: one LoRA pair } (B_{\\text{ft},i},A_{\\text{ft},i})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, independent of the number of tasks } T\\ (\\text{storage: the merged LoRA plus the current fine-tuned LoRA, } 2r(d_{in}+d_{out}))","locus":"LoRA on the query and value projections of every attention layer (Appendix B), fixed rank 8: Llama-2-7B/13B-chat, Llama-3.2-3B, Qwen2.5-3B/7B; a rank ablation over 4, 8 and 16 on Llama-2-13B-chat finds 8 best on the standard CL benchmark and 4 best on the large-number-of-tasks benchmark","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A constant-memory recursion on one LoRA pair over a task stream, in three steps. (1) \\(A\\) is rebased: \\(A^{(0)}_{\\text{ft},i}=Q_i^{\\top}=R_i^{-\\top}A_{\\text{ft},i-1}\\) with \\(\\mathrm{diag}R_i>0\\). This moves the previous task's \\(A\\) inside its own row space to the representative with orthonormal rows. (2) \\(B\\) is warm-started from the previous fine-tuned \\(B\\) without the compensating factor \\(R_i^{\\top}\\). The starting product \\(B_{\\text{ft},i-1}R_i^{-\\top}A_{\\text{ft},i-1}\\) therefore differs from the previous adapter unless \\(R_i=I_r\\). (3) After training, \\(A\\) is overwritten (\\(A^{i}_{\\text{merge}}=A_{\\text{ft},i}\\)) and only \\(B\\) is averaged, with weight \\(1/\\sqrt i\\) taken from the OPCM continual-merging schedule. The deployed update is \\(B^{t}_{\\text{merge}}A_{\\text{ft},t}=\\sum_{i}c^{(t)}_{i}B_{\\text{ft},i}A_{\\text{ft},t}\\) with \\(c^{(t)}_{i}=\\lambda(i)\\prod_{j=i+1}^{t}(1-\\lambda(j))\\), so every past \\(B\\) is paired with the latest \\(A\\). This is only approximately coherent: each \\(A^{(0)}_{\\text{ft},i}\\) spans the row space of \\(A_{\\text{ft},i-1}\\), and training moves it by the small drift bounded below. The paper's Theorem 1, a Flora-style small-step analysis with \\(A^{(0)}A^{(0)\\top}=I_r\\), writes \\(A^s=A^{(0)}+\\eta A^{(0)}f_A(s)\\) with \\(\\|f_A(s)\\|_2\\le\\eta L^2/(1-\\eta^2L^2)\\), so \\(A\\) drifts by at most \\(\\eta^2L^2/(1-\\eta^2L^2)\\), i.e. \\(O(\\eta^2L^2)\\), while \\(\\Delta B_i\\approx-\\eta\\big(\\sum_s\\nabla_W\\mathcal L_i^{s}\\big)A^{(0)\\top}_i\\) accumulates each task's gradient in the current \\(A\\) basis. An NTK-regime forgetting/intransigence bound in \\(\\|B_tA_t-B_iA_i\\|_F\\) motivates keeping \\(\\|A_t-A_i\\|_F\\) small and merging \\(B\\) rather than \\(A\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After any task, the deployed object is one LoRA \\(B^{t}_{\\text{merge}}A^{t}_{\\text{merge}}\\) that can be folded into \\(W_0\\), or kept as a side adapter while the stream continues. There are no per-task modules and no router.","identity_at_init":"Only for the first task (standard \\(B=0\\), Gaussian \\(A\\)). Later tasks start training from \\(B_{\\text{ft},i-1}R_i^{-\\top}A_{\\text{ft},i-1}\\ne 0\\), and the merged LoRA is never zero after task 1.","key_idea":"Continual learning becomes sequential merging inside one LoRA. Each new task starts from an orthonormal-row rebasing of the previous \\(A\\) together with the previous \\(B\\). After training, \\(A\\) is replaced and \\(B\\) is averaged with weight \\(1/\\sqrt i\\), exploiting the asymmetry that \\(B\\) carries task-specific, near-orthogonal updates. Memory stays at one LoRA however many tasks arrive.","results_note":"On the standard CL, large-number-of-tasks and SuperNI benchmarks with Llama-2-7B-chat, the paper reports that SLAO outperforms all data-free baselines, including O-LoRA, InfLoRA, CorDA, MagMax, KnOTS, LoRA-LEGO and OPCM; its own discussion notes that MagMax is comparable on the standard CL benchmark. SAPT-LoRA, which replays generated pseudo-samples, is not data-free and is excluded from that claim.","relations":[{"target":"o-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline. O-LoRA keeps and freezes every past LoRA and constrains new ones to be orthogonal during training, so memory grows linearly in \\(T\\). SLAO keeps one LoRA and imposes orthogonality only through the initialisation of \\(A\\)."},{"target":"inflora","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline. InfLoRA stores all previous LoRAs and gradient subspaces and places each new \\(A\\) in an interference-free subspace. SLAO takes the new \\(A\\) from the QR basis of the previous \\(A\\)."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-merging baseline, applied sequentially. It treats \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) symmetrically and has no time-aware scaling."},{"target":"lora-lego","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-merging baseline (rank-wise clustering), applied sequentially; it weights tasks equally."},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"builds-on","note":"Theorem 1 extends Hao et al.'s Flora dynamics (\\(A\\) stays near its initialisation while \\(B\\) accumulates gradients projected onto \\(A\\)) to an orthonormal \\(A^{(0)}\\) and a nonzero warm-started \\(B^{(0)}\\)."},{"target":"asymmetry-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (Zhu et al., 2024b) for the asymmetric roles of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), which motivate merging only \\(B\\) and replacing \\(A\\)."},{"target":"corda","type":"competes-with","note":"Knowledge-preserving decomposition baseline, which degrades on SuperNI."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23017","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.23017","https://openreview.net/forum?id=i1Rj7yU6eF","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10008003"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Fuli Qiao","Mehrdad Mahdavi"],"bibkey":"qiao2025merge","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qiao2025merge,\n  title = {{Merge before Forget: A Single LoRA Continual Learning via Continual Merging}},\n  author = {Fuli Qiao and Mehrdad Mahdavi},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2512.23017},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23017}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"One LoRA pair over a task stream: before task \\(i\\ge2\\), \\(A\\) is replaced by \\(Q_i^\\top=R_i^{-\\top}A_{ft,i-1}\\) (orthonormal rows) and \\(B\\) by \\(B_{ft,i-1}\\); after training, \\(A\\) is overwritten and only \\(B\\) is averaged with weight \\(1/\\sqrt i\\). \\(W_0\\) is never merged into.","shape":"Every stage and every deployed model lies in LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"Task 1 is the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. Each later stage starts at \\(W_0+B_{ft,i-1}R_i^{-\\top}A_{ft,i-1}\\), which differs by design from the deployed model \\(W_0+B^{i-1}_{merge}A_{ft,i-1}\\) (no compensating \\(R_i^\\top\\), and the raw rather than the merged \\(B\\)): a pointing defect.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\(\\rho\\). The QR step fixes the gauge of \\(A\\) on the Stiefel slice without the compensating move on \\(B\\), and the \\(B\\)-only average pairs past \\(B\\)'s with the latest \\(A\\): a factor-level rule, exact only to the extent that all \\(A\\)'s share one row space (Prop V.4(b)).","covariance":"The image is LoRA's basis-free cone; the QR normalisation is only \\(O(d_{in})\\)-equivariant.","merge":"The deployed \\(B^t_{merge}A^t_{merge}\\) is one LoRA and fuses.","rebasing":"Not a rebasing scheme: the base \\(W_0\\) is never re-pointed and every stage has the same shape, so the stream's own reach stays \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) for every length (unlike O-LoRA's \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le Tr}\\)). The axis value is that of the shape: LoRA's cone, closed under scaling, so merge-and-restart gives \\(\\to\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3(a))."},"categorical_reading":"SLAO is continual merging carried out inside one LoRA: the stream is a schedule of re-pointings within a fixed cone, each a non-gauge move (QR rebasing of \\(A\\), warm start of \\(B\\)) followed by a factor-level average of \\(B\\). It works to the extent that every \\(A\\) descends from one row space, which is the shared-factor condition under which factor-wise merging is exact (Prop V.4(b)). Memory stays constant because the rank never grows.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"First stage: a pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_r at (0, A_0). Later stages start at W_0 + B_{ft,i-1}Q_i^T and map only by a weak morphism (LoRA re-pointed at that start)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"inflora","test":"O2","reason":"Each SLAO stage trains both factors, d = r(m+n-r), while an InfLoRA stage is LoRA-FA(A_t) with d = rm."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"rain-merging","name":"RAIN-Merging","full_name":"RAIN-Merging: Reasoning-Aware Instruction-attention guided Null-space projection Merging","paper_title":"RAIN-Merging: A Gradient-Free Method to Enhance Instruction Following in Large Reasoning Models with Preserved Thinking Format","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (oral)","arxiv":"2602.22538","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22538","code_url":"https://github.com/K1nght/RAIN-Merging","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta_I^{k}=W_I^{k}-W_B^{k}\\ (\\text{instruction-model task vector of module } k\\in\\{Q,K,V,O,\\text{FFN}\\}\\text{ in every layer});\\ \\ H_k=[h^{k}_t]_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\text{think}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times|\\Omega|}\\ (\\text{inputs of module } k \\text{ in the reasoning model at the thinking-marker positions, 150-sample reasoning calibration set});\\ \\ \\Delta_I^{\\perp,k}=\\Delta_I^{k}\\big(I-H_k(H_k^{\\top}H_k)^{+}H_k^{\\top}\\big)\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta_I^{\\perp,k}h^{k}_t=0\\ \\ \\forall t\\in\\Omega_{\\text{think}};\\ \\ g^{\\tilde k}=\\mathbb{E}_x\\big[a^{\\tilde k}-\\rho\\,u^{\\tilde k}\\big],\\ \\tilde H^{\\tilde k}=1+\\mathbb{E}_x[u^{\\tilde k}],\\ \\alpha^{\\tilde k}_\\star=\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}\\big(g^{\\tilde k}/\\tilde H^{\\tilde k}\\big)\\ \\ (a,u:\\ \\text{mean attention from instruction-constrained / unconstrained response tokens to instruction tokens, 365 IFEval-style samples});\\ \\ \\theta_\\star=\\theta_R+\\lambda\\bigoplus_{k}\\alpha^{k}_\\star\\,\\Delta_I^{\\perp,k};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } \\theta_R,\\theta_I,\\theta_B;\\ \\text{nothing is gradient-trained}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{gradient-trained; computed in closed form: one coefficient } \\alpha^{\\tilde k} \\text{ per attention head (FFN modules use the layer mean) and one global } \\lambda\\ (\\text{grid search over } [0,1.5])","locus":"Q, K, V, O and FFN weight matrices of an R1-style reasoning model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B/7B/14B/32B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B. The instruction task vector \\(\\theta_I-\\theta_B\\) comes from Qwen2.5-1.5B/7B/14B/32B-Instruct or Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and their bases. For 1.5B and 7B the reasoning model was distilled from Qwen2.5-Math, so it does not share the instruction model's base.","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A two-stage, data-dependent but gradient-free transformation of a full task vector before it is added to a different model, the reasoning model \\(\\theta_R\\), rather than to the base \\(\\theta_B\\) it was computed from (for the 1.5B and 7B pairs \\(\\theta_R\\) is not even fine-tuned from \\(\\theta_B\\): it descends from Qwen2.5-Math). Stage 1 right-multiplies each module's task matrix by the orthogonal projector onto the complement of the span of that module's inputs at the thinking special tokens. This is the matrix form of the paper's vectorised Eq. (4), since \\(\\Phi=H^{\\top}\\otimes I\\). The merged module then reproduces the reasoning model's outputs exactly on those inputs. Proposition 1 bounds the logit change at the marker tokens by \\(C_1\\|\\Phi\\,\\mathrm{vec}(\\Delta)\\|_2+C_2\\|\\Delta\\|_2^2\\), so after projection only the second-order term remains; the paper states the resulting KL bound as \\(O(\\|\\Delta\\|_2^2)\\), although its own inequalities give \\(O(\\|\\Delta\\|_2^4)\\). Algorithm 1 uses the ridge version \\(I-\\Phi^{\\top}(\\Phi\\Phi^{\\top}+I)^{-1}\\Phi\\), which in matrix form is \\(\\Delta\\mapsto\\Delta(I+HH^{\\top})^{-1}\\). The released code (nullspace_merge_qkvo_ffn.py) uses a ridge of \\(10^{-4}\\) solved by conjugate gradient, on positions within 5 tokens after <think> and before </think>. It also relaxes the constraint set: Q and K are constrained through linearised attention logits between each marker and its neighbours, and V, O and the three FFN matrices through projections of the output change onto two random unit readout directions per position. The full \\(\\Delta h=0\\) is not imposed. Stage 2 builds a diagonal quadratic model of an attention proxy (alignment minus \\(\\rho=10\\) times leakage), with coefficients \\(g\\) and \\(\\tilde H\\) measured at \\(\\alpha=\\mathbf 1\\). After dropping the \\(\\alpha_{(0)}\\) offset, its maximiser is \\(\\mathrm{clip}_{[0,1]}(g/\\tilde H)\\), not the Newton step \\(\\mathbf 1+g/\\tilde H\\). This gives per-head scalings. The result is a block-wise rescaling of a projected task vector: the same algebraic class as task arithmetic with per-module coefficients.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is one static weight set \\(\\theta_\\star\\) with the reasoning model's architecture. Projectors and calibration statistics are used only offline.","identity_at_init":"Not an adapter. With \\(\\lambda=0\\) (or all \\(\\alpha=0\\)) the result is the reasoning model \\(\\theta_R\\). Stage 2 expands around \\(\\alpha=\\mathbf 1\\), the plain projected merge.","key_idea":"The goal is to add instruction following to a reasoning model by task arithmetic without breaking its <think>...</think> format. The instruction task vector is first projected onto the null space of the reasoning model's module inputs at the thinking-marker tokens, so those positions compute exactly as before. It is then rescaled per attention head by a closed-form score that rewards attention from instruction-constrained output tokens to the instruction span and penalises leakage.","results_note":"Merging Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct into DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, RAIN-Merging outperforms all merging baselines (Task Arithmetic, SLERP, Karcher, TIES, DARE, ACM, LEWIS, AIM) and SFT on the same calibration data on instruction-following, mathematical-reasoning and general-capability benchmarks, while trailing the instruction model slightly on instruction following.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"Starts from the instruction-model task vector and adds it with per-module coefficients; plain Task Arithmetic is a baseline that hurts reasoning."},{"target":"gradient-projection-memory","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited in the related-work appendix (Appendix D) as a null-space precedent from continual learning; the null-space step in Sec. 3 itself cites Adam-NSCL (Wang et al., 2021). GPM projects gradients off the span of stored input features; RAIN applies the same right-projector to a task vector, using features at the thinking tokens."},{"target":"lora-null","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited in the related-work appendix (Appendix D). LoRA-Null puts adapters in the null space of pre-trained activations to preserve knowledge; RAIN projects a full task vector off the activations that carry the thinking format."},{"target":"tangent-task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited to read the near-orthogonality of the reasoning and instruction task vectors as weak coupling (weight disentanglement), which makes lightweight merging plausible."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Data-free baseline (and the base for the DARE/ACM/LEWIS/AIM variants); it prunes from parameter statistics and ignores the output-format mismatch."},{"target":"dare","type":"competes-with","note":"Data-free baseline (DARE-TIES)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22538","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.22538","https://github.com/K1nght/RAIN-Merging","https://openreview.net/forum?id=PO2iULmu5e","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/oral/10009682"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Zhehao Huang","Yuhang Liu","Baijiong Lin","Yixin Lou","Zhengbao He","Hanling Tian","Tao Li","Xiaolin Huang"],"bibkey":"huang2025rain","bibtex":"@inproceedings{huang2025rain,\n  title = {{RAIN-Merging: A Gradient-Free Method to Enhance Instruction Following in Large Reasoning Models with Preserved Thinking Format}},\n  author = {Zhehao Huang and Yuhang Liu and Baijiong Lin and Yixin Lou and Zhengbao He and Hanling Tian and Tao Li and Xiaolin Huang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2602.22538},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.22538}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and gradient-free: the instruction task vector \\(\\Delta_I=\\theta_I-\\theta_B\\) is right-projected off the span of each module's inputs at the thinking markers, rescaled per head in closed form, and added to a different model \\(\\theta_R\\) with a grid-searched \\(\\lambda\\).","shape":"The coefficient line \\(\\theta_R+\\lambda\\bigoplus_k\\alpha^k_\\star\\Delta_I^{\\perp,k}\\) (\\(\\alpha_\\star\\) closed-form, \\(\\lambda\\) tuned): \\(d=1\\).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_R\\); for 1.5B and 7B, \\(\\theta_R\\) is not even fine-tuned from \\(\\theta_B\\), so the task vector is transported across bases.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"The orthogonal (or ridge) projector \\(I-H(H^\\top H)^+H^\\top\\) acts on the input side and is covariant under \\(CO(d_{in})\\) (\\(H\\mapsto hH\\)); left multiplication leaves the output side free; Stage 2's per-head coefficients follow the network's own head partition. GLxCO.","merge":"One static weight set.","rebasing":"Idempotent with the projectors frozen."},"categorical_reading":"RAIN-Merging is task arithmetic across bases with a null-space constraint: Stage 1 makes the added vector vanish on the thinking-marker activations, so the merged modules compute exactly as \\(\\theta_R\\) there, and Stage 2 is a closed-form per-head reweighting. The family is still a line through \\(\\theta_R\\) along one projected, block-rescaled vector, so the content is in the choice of vector, not in the family.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"The projected, per-head rescaled vector generically leaves span{Delta_I}: Delta_I(I - P_H) is a multiple of Delta_I only if Delta_I already annihilates the marker inputs."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"smartfed-more","name":"SmartFed (MoRE + EEQA)","full_name":"SmartFed: Mixture of Rank-Wise Experts (MoRE) with Elastic Expert Quota Allocation (EEQA) for reusing existing LoRAs in federated fine-tuning","paper_title":"Elastic Mixture of Rank-Wise Experts for Knowledge Reuse in Federated Fine-Tuning","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight)","arxiv":"2512.00902","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.00902","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\{(B_n,A_n)\\}_{n=1}^{N}\\ \\text{retrieved pre-trained LoRAs (frozen)},\\ \\ E_m(x)=b_m a_m^{\\top}x,\\ \\ b_m=(B_n)_{:,i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}},\\ a_m^{\\top}=(A_n)_{i,:}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times d_{in}},\\ \\ m=1,\\dots,M,\\ M=\\sum_n r_n;\\ \\ g=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_{\\text{router}}x),\\ W_{\\text{router}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\tilde g=g\\odot\\mathbf m,\\ \\mathbf m=\\mathrm{TopK}_{q_j}(g)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{M};\\ \\ h=W_0x+\\sum_{m=1}^{M}\\tilde g_m\\,b_m a_m^{\\top}x;\\ \\ s_{E_m}=1-\\cos(x,E_m(x)),\\ S^{\\text{Sum}}_j=\\sum_m\\tilde g_m s_{E_m},\\ \\alpha_j=\\mathrm{softmax}_j(S^{\\text{Sum}}_j),\\ q_j=\\mathrm{EEQA}\\big(\\alpha;\\ \\textstyle\\sum_j q_j=KJ,\\ q_j\\le M\\big);\\ \\ \\text{frozen: } W_0 \\text{ and every LoRA expert};\\ \\text{trained (federated, FedAvg): the routers } W_{\\text{router}}","trainable_params":"M\\,d_{in}=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_n r_n\\big)d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (router only)};\\ 64\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{in the paper's setting (two rank-32 LoRAs on } W_q,W_v)","locus":"Query and value projections of every attention layer, where the reused LoRAs sit, in LLaMA2-7B/13B and Qwen2-7B; one router per adapted matrix","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A library of frozen LoRAs is split into a pool of rank-one experts \\(\\{b_ma_m^{\\top}\\}_{m=1}^{M}\\) per matrix and recombined per token by a softmax router with top-\\(q_j\\) sparsification: \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_m\\tilde g_m(x)\\,b_ma_m^{\\top}=\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\tilde g(x))\\,\\widehat A\\), with \\(\\widehat B=[B_1|\\cdots|B_N]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times M}\\) and \\(\\widehat A=[A_1;\\cdots;A_N]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M\\times d_{in}}\\). This is an input-dependent diagonal core in the concatenated LoRA basis. It avoids the cross terms \\(B_1A_2,\\ B_2A_1\\) that factor averaging creates (the paper's Eq. 5), and it is finer than MoLE's one gate per LoRA. The kept gates are not renormalised after top-\\(K\\) masking. EEQA redistributes a fixed total budget \\(KJ\\) of active experts over the \\(J\\) adapted matrices, in proportion to softmax-normalised importance (gate-weighted cosine deviation between a matrix's input and its experts' outputs), capped at \\(M\\) per matrix and topped up greedily. Only router weights and importance scores travel between devices and server.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The gates \\(\\tilde g(x)\\) depend on each token through a softmax and a top-\\(K\\) mask, so \\(\\Delta W(x)\\) changes per token and cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\). The full expert pool must be kept. Fixing the gates to constants \\(c\\) would give a static merge \\(\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,\\widehat A\\), but that is not what is trained.","identity_at_init":"No. The experts are pre-trained nonzero LoRAs, so at initialisation the model already applies a gated mixture of their rank-one components; it is not the bare base model.","key_idea":"Instead of training LoRA from scratch on each device, SmartFed reuses existing task LoRAs. Each LoRA is split into its rank-one components, and a federated-trained router sparsely mixes them per token. A budget allocator gives more active experts to the matrices whose experts change representations most.","results_note":"Across three skill-composition tasks on LLaMA2-7B/13B and Qwen2-7B, SmartFed reports up to 10.21% higher average performance, 3.95x faster convergence, 31.47x lower communication cost and 3.61x lower energy use than existing federated and LoRA-reuse methods.","relations":[{"target":"mole","type":"builds-on","note":"MoLE gates whole LoRA outputs. MoRE moves the gate down to rank-one components and adds top-\\(K\\) sparsity, which also bounds latency as more LoRAs are retrieved. MoLE is also a federated-adapted baseline."},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline. LoraHub merges retrieved LoRAs into one static composite; SmartFed routes per token instead."},{"target":"lora-flow","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline: dynamic layer-wise fusion weights over whole LoRAs."},{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited as the inspiration for non-uniform, importance-driven budget allocation across matrices (EEQA); AdaLoRA is also adapted to the federated setting as a knowledge-free baseline."},{"target":"lora-lego","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. LoRA-LEGO also splits LoRAs into rank-wise units, but clusters them into one static merged LoRA rather than routing them per token."},{"target":"pem-arithmetic","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline ('Linear Arithmetic', Zhang et al., 2023a). Weighted averaging of the LoRA factors produces the cross terms \\(\\lambda_1\\lambda_2B_1A_2\\) and \\(\\lambda_1\\lambda_2B_2A_1\\) of the paper's Eq. (5), which MoRE avoids by routing rank-one components."},{"target":"fedit","type":"competes-with","note":"Knowledge-free baseline: FedIT trains a LoRA from scratch with FedAvg on each task, whereas SmartFed trains only the router over retrieved LoRAs."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.00902","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.00902","https://openreview.net/forum?id=HdsEUNo4C4","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/65026"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-2"],"authors_full":["Yebo Wu","Jingguang Li","Zhijiang Guo","Li Li"],"bibkey":"wu2025elastic","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2025elastic,\n  title = {{Elastic Mixture of Rank-Wise Experts for Knowledge Reuse in Federated Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Yebo Wu and Jingguang Li and Zhijiang Guo and Li Li},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2512.00902},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.00902}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: frozen retrieved LoRAs are split into rank-one experts \\(b_ma_m^\\top\\) and recombined per token, \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\tilde g(x))\\,\\widehat A\\), with a softmax router, top-\\(q_j\\) masking and a budget allocator (EEQA); only the routers train (federated).","shape":"A token-dependent diagonal core in the concatenated LoRA basis: no single weight-space image, and top-\\(K\\) masking makes it discontinuous at ties.","base_point":"The experts are nonzero pre-trained LoRAs and no router value switches them all off (softmax; top-\\(K\\) keeps \\(K\\ge1\\)): unpointed.","gauge":"Router only: the softmax translation \\(W_{router}\\mapsto W_{router}+\\mathbf 1v^\\top\\) is a kernel-type redundancy. The rank-one split depends on each library LoRA's factorisation, so MoRE is invariant under per-LoRA monomial changes \\(\\mathrm{Mon}_r\\) but not under its \\(GL_r\\) gauge.","covariance":"Experts and router transform together: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\) (routed-mixture convention).","merge":"Token-dependent gates (Thm VI.3); constant gates would give the static \\(\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,\\widehat A\\).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"MoRE is MoLE moved down from whole LoRAs to their rank-one atoms: per token it is a point of LoRA\\(_M\\) with frozen concatenated factors and a routed diagonal core, which avoids the cross terms of factor averaging. Splitting a LoRA into atoms is not a function of its product \\(BA\\), so the expert pool, and hence the method, depends on the representative in which each library LoRA was stored.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(W_{router})=\\big(\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\tilde g_x(W_{router})),\\ \\widehat A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_M (M = sum_n r_n) with A frozen at A-hat, pointed at h(q_0) (unpointed slice); smooth away from top-K ties."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mole","test":"O1","reason":"MoLE gives every rank-one atom of a library LoRA the same weight G_n(x); MoRE weights the atoms of one LoRA independently and sparsely. For generic libraries (linearly independent atoms b_m a_m^T x) its realisations are not MoLE's."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"rotmole","name":"RotMoLE","full_name":"RotMoLE: Rotatable Mixture of Low-rank Experts (MoE-LoRA with a rotation gate)","paper_title":"RotMoLE: Enhancing Mixture of Low-Rank Experts through Rotational Gating Mechanism","authors":"Sun et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2605.25565","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25565","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\sum_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(g(x))}g_i(x)\\,B_i\\,R_i(x)\\,A_i\\,x,\\quad g(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_gx),\\ \\ \\theta(x)=2\\pi\\,\\sigma(W_\\theta x)-\\pi\\in(-\\pi,\\pi)^{n};\\ \\ r=2:\\ R_i=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta_i&-\\sin\\theta_i\\\\ \\sin\\theta_i&\\cos\\theta_i\\end{pmatrix};\\ \\ r>2:\\ e_{1,i}=\\frac{A_ix}{\\|A_ix\\|},\\ e_{2,i}=\\frac{q_i-(q_i^{\\top}e_{1,i})e_{1,i}}{\\|q_i-(q_i^{\\top}e_{1,i})e_{1,i}\\|},\\ R_i=[e_{1,i}\\ e_{2,i}]\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta_i&-\\sin\\theta_i\\\\ \\sin\\theta_i&\\cos\\theta_i\\end{pmatrix}[e_{1,i}\\ e_{2,i}]^{\\top},\\ \\text{so } R_iA_ix=\\cos\\theta_i\\,A_ix+\\sin\\theta_i\\,\\|A_ix\\|\\,e_{2,i};\\ \\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ W_g,W_\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times d_{in}},\\ q_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r};\\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{trained: } \\{A_i,B_i,q_i\\}_{i=1}^{n},\\ W_g,\\ W_\\theta","trainable_params":"n\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+2n\\,d_{in}+n\\,r\\ \\text{per adapted matrix } (n \\text{ experts, scaling gate } W_g,\\ \\text{rotation gate } W_\\theta,\\ \\text{centres } q_i;\\ \\text{the } nr \\text{ term is absent when } r=2);\\ \\text{overhead over a plain MoE-LoRA: } n\\,d_{in}\\,(+\\,n\\,r)","locus":"The query, key, value and output projections of only the first quarter of the layers (layers 0-6 of the 28-layer Llama-3.2-3B, 0-8 of the 36-layer Qwen-2.5-3B, 0-9 of the 40-layer GLM-4-9B); experiments use \\(r\\in\\{2,3,4,6\\}\\), \\(n\\in\\{2,3,4,5\\}\\) experts and top-\\(k\\) with \\(k\\in\\{2,3\\}\\), trained with SGD (learning rate \\(3\\times10^{-4}\\), linear decay)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Per-token mixture of \\(n\\) LoRA experts in which the router returns, for each selected expert, a scale \\(g_i(x)\\) and a rotation \\(R_i(x)\\in SO(r)\\) that acts in that expert's rank space, between \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\). For \\(r>2\\) the rotation is a single planar (Givens-type) rotation by \\(\\theta_i(x)\\) in \\(\\mathrm{span}(A_ix,q_i)\\). It turns the code \\(A_ix\\) towards or away from a learned per-expert centre \\(q_i\\) and preserves \\(\\|A_ix\\|\\). The printed \\(R_i=[e_1\\,e_2]\\,\\mathrm{Rot}(\\theta_i)\\,[e_1\\,e_2]^{\\top}\\) is a rank-2 matrix. Because \\(A_ix\\) lies in that plane, it acts on \\(A_ix\\) exactly like the full rotation \\(I_r+(\\cos\\theta_i-1)(e_1e_1^{\\top}+e_2e_2^{\\top})+\\sin\\theta_i(e_2e_1^{\\top}-e_1e_2^{\\top})\\). Gauge reading (derived here): a constant \\(R\\) placed between \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) would be absorbed by the LoRA gauge, \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,A)\\), and add nothing. RotMoLE's \\(R_i\\) depends on \\(x\\), both through \\(\\theta_i(x)\\) and through the plane, which contains \\(A_ix\\). The expert map \\(x\\mapsto B_iR_i(x)A_ix\\) is therefore a nonlinear, norm-preserving reshaping of each expert's code, and it enlarges the function class. The construction uses the Euclidean norm and Gram-Schmidt in rank space. Each expert is therefore invariant under \\((B_i,A_i,q_i)\\mapsto(cB_iO,\\,c^{-1}O^{\\top}A_i,\\,O^{\\top}q_i)\\) for \\(O\\in O(r)\\) and \\(c>0\\), with \\(R_i\\mapsto O^{\\top}R_iO\\) (the unit vector \\(e_1\\) does not see the scale \\(c\\)), but not under a general \\(G\\in GL(r)\\): the residual gauge group of each expert shrinks from \\(GL(r)\\) to the similarity group \\(\\mathbb{R}_{>0}\\times O(r)\\). For \\(r=2\\) the fixed-basis rotation commutes with \\(\\mathbb{R}_{>0}\\times SO(2)\\); a reflection additionally needs \\(W_\\theta\\mapsto-W_\\theta\\). Top-\\(k\\) selection uses only \\(g\\). The paper does not treat the degenerate case \\(q_i\\parallel A_ix\\).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The scales \\(g_i(x)\\), the angles \\(\\theta_i(x)\\) and the plane \\(\\mathrm{span}(A_ix,q_i)\\) all depend on the token. \\(\\sum_i g_iB_iR_i(x)A_i\\) is therefore not a fixed matrix and cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Yes. \\(B_i=0\\) (the LoRA convention, stated in Appendix B) gives \\(\\Delta W x=0\\). \\(W_\\theta=0\\) gives \\(\\theta_i=0\\), so \\(R_iA_ix=A_ix\\) and the model starts as a conventional MoE-LoRA. The centres \\(q_i\\) are Kaiming-uniform initialised.","key_idea":"A scalar gate can only rescale an expert's output along a fixed direction. LoRA experts have an \\(r\\)-dimensional bottleneck, so an input-dependent rotation of the code \\(A_ix\\) costs only \\(n\\,d_{in}+n\\,r\\) extra parameters. Each selected expert can then also be turned, which lets a few shared experts serve several tasks differently.","results_note":"On a jointly trained CSQA/OBQA/SIQA mixture with Qwen-2.5-3B (\\(r=6\\), \\(n=4\\), \\(k=2\\)), RotMoLE averages 45.82% accuracy, against 41.42 for AdaMoLE, 35.66 for MoLA and 34.57 for MoCLE; AdaMoLE is better on CSQA (49.39 vs 43.00). Against the plain MoE-LoRA baseline on the QA mixture, RotMoLE has the higher average in all 8 configurations and the higher accuracy on every task in 7 of them (the exception is OBQA with Llama-3.2-3B, \\(r=3\\), \\(n=4\\), \\(k=2\\)). On the SST-2/MRPC/QNLI mixture the gains are small (75.87 to 76.03 and 73.19 to 73.42 average).","relations":[{"target":"loramoe","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (Dou et al., 2024) as a canonical MoE-LoRA, \\(h=W_0x+\\sum_i g_iB_iA_ix\\) with a scalar softmax gate. RotMoLE adds a rotation gate inside each expert's rank space."},{"target":"mixlora","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (Li et al., 2024) as the top-\\(k\\) MoE-LoRA form. RotMoLE keeps top-\\(k\\) selection on the scalar gate values and adds per-expert rotations."},{"target":"moslora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both put an \\(r\\times r\\) map between \\(B\\) and \\(A\\). MoSLoRA's mixer is input-independent, so the LoRA gauge absorbs it and LoRA's function class is unchanged. RotMoLE's rotation is token-dependent and does change it. MoSLoRA is not cited."},{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"Another way to diversify LoRA experts under a router: HydraLoRA shares \\(A\\) and routes over several \\(B\\) heads, while RotMoLE keeps full experts and rotates their codes. HydraLoRA is not cited."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25565","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.25565","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/148910","https://openreview.net/forum?id=uGtHLqoW8K"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":["Mengyang Sun","Maochuan Dou","Tao Feng","Dan Zhang","Yihao Wang","Junpeng Liu","Yifan Zhu","Jie Tang"],"bibkey":"sun2026rotmole","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sun2026rotmole,\n  title = {{RotMoLE: Enhancing Mixture of Low-Rank Experts through Rotational Gating Mechanism}},\n  author = {Mengyang Sun and Maochuan Dou and Tao Feng and Dan Zhang and Yihao Wang and Junpeng Liu and Yifan Zhu and Jie Tang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2605.25565},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.25565}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(g(x))}g_i(x)\\,B_iR_i(x)A_i\\), with a scalar gate and an input-dependent rotation \\(R_i(x)\\in SO(r)\\) of each expert's code.","shape":"Token-dependent; for \\(r>2\\), \\(R_i(x)A_ix=\\cos\\theta_i\\,A_ix+\\sin\\theta_i\\,\\lVert A_ix\\rVert\\,e_{2,i}\\) is a norm-preserving non-linear reshaping of the code, so even a single expert map leaves the linear class.","base_point":"\\(B_i=0\\) and \\(W_\\theta=0\\): every expert is zero and \\(R_i=I\\), the frozen function at every token (neutral); training starts as a plain top-\\(k\\) MoE-LoRA.","gauge":"Per expert \\((B_i,A_i,q_i)\\mapsto(cB_iO,\\ c^{-1}O^\\top A_i,\\ O^\\top q_i)\\) with \\(c>0\\), \\(O\\in O(r)\\): the similarity group \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}\\times O(r)\\), not \\(GL_r\\) (Gram–Schmidt and the norm use the rank-space metric). Nearest key GL (convention \\(O(r)\\to GL\\)).","covariance":"Experts, scaling gate and rotation gate read the input densely: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Scales, angles and rotation planes change per token (Thm VI.3).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"RotMoLE gives each routed LoRA expert an input-dependent rotation inside its rank space. A constant rotation there would be absorbed by LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge and add nothing; a token-dependent one cannot be absorbed, and because it uses the Euclidean structure of the code space it cuts the residual gauge down to the similarity group.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_i,A_i,q_i)_i,W_g,W_\\theta\\big)=\\big(\\,[\\,\\mathbb 1_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(x)}\\,g_i(x)\\,B_iR_i(x)\\,]_{i=1}^{n},\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_n]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_{nr} pointed at (0,[A_1^0;...;A_n^0]), since every B_i starts at 0. Smooth away from routing ties and from A_ix = 0 (r > 2)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mixlora","test":"O1","reason":"For r > 2 and W_theta != 0 an expert output contains sin(theta_i(x)) ||A_ix|| e_{2,i}(x), which is not differentiable on {A_ix = 0}; MixLoRA's realisations are analytic on each routing cell (softmax gate times linear experts), so generic RotMoLE functions are not MixLoRA's. MixLoRA's routed experts also sit on the FFN, RotMoLE's on q, k, v, o."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"scalora","name":"ScaLoRA","full_name":"ScaLoRA: Optimally Scaled Low-Rank Adaptation (with the intermittent variant ScaLoRA-I)","paper_title":"ScaLoRA: Optimally Scaled Low-Rank Adaptation for Efficient High-Rank Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2510.23818","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23818","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_t=W_0^{(t)}+B_tA_t,\\ B_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ (\\text{paper: } \\mathbf A=B,\\ \\mathbf B^{\\top}=A);\\ \\text{every step (ScaLoRA) or every } I \\text{ steps (ScaLoRA-I)}:\\ \\tilde B_t=B_t\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha_t),\\ \\tilde A_t=\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta_t)\\,A_t,\\ \\ W_0^{(t)}\\leftarrow W_0^{(t)}+B_tA_t-\\tilde B_t\\tilde A_t\\ (\\text{merge and freeze; } W_t \\text{ unchanged}),\\ \\ (B_{t+1},A_{t+1})=\\mathrm{AdamW}(\\tilde B_t,\\tilde A_t)\\ \\text{with moments carried over as } m(\\nabla_{\\tilde B})=m(\\nabla_B)\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta_t),\\ v(\\nabla_{\\tilde B})=v(\\nabla_B)\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta_t)^{2},\\ m(\\nabla_{\\tilde A})=\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha_t)m(\\nabla_A),\\ v(\\nabla_{\\tilde A})=\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha_t)^{2}v(\\nabla_A);\\ \\ (\\alpha_t,\\beta_t)=\\arg\\min_{\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r}}\\Big\\|\\tfrac{1}{L}G_t-\\eta\\,B_t\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)^{2}B_t^{\\top}G_t-\\eta\\,G_tA_t^{\\top}\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta)^{2}A_t\\Big\\|_F^{2},\\ G_t=\\nabla\\ell(W_t);\\ \\text{if } \\big[(S_{out}^{\\top}S_{out})\\odot(S_{in}^{\\top}S_{in})\\big]v=\\lambda,\\ S_{out}=[B_t,\\ G_tA_t^{\\top}],\\ S_{in}=[G_t^{\\top}B_t,\\ A_t^{\\top}],\\ \\lambda=\\big[\\|(B_t^{\\top}G_t)_{i,:}\\|^{2};\\ \\|(A_tG_t^{\\top})_{i,:}\\|^{2}\\big]_{i=1}^{r},\\ \\text{has a solution } v\\ge0:\\ [\\alpha_t;\\beta_t]=\\pm(L\\eta)^{-1/2}v^{\\circ1/2},\\ \\text{else the closed-form scalar scaling } (\\alpha_t\\mathbf 1,\\beta_t\\mathbf 1);\\ \\text{frozen between re-splits: } W_0^{(t)};\\ \\text{trained: } B,A;\\ L \\text{ (Lipschitz constant) is a tuned hyperparameter}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, as in LoRA, with AdamW moments for the factors only. The base matrix is rewritten at every re-split, so the deliverable is the dense merged } W_T \\text{, not a detachable adapter}","locus":"LoRA on the query, key and value projections, output.dense and intermediate.dense of DeBERTaV3-base (GLUE, \\(r=4\\), scaling 8, AdaLoRA protocol); on the q, k, v, up and down projections of LLaMA2-7B and LLaMA3-8B (commonsense reasoning, \\(r=8\\), scaling 16, setup of Lion et al. 2025) and of Gemma-3-12B-pt (MetaMathQA, \\(r=8\\)). ScaLoRA-I uses \\(I=10\\) in the commonsense runs","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Rebasing inside a single LoRA run. Before each step the current product is re-split as \\(W_t=\\tilde W_0^{(t)}+\\tilde B_t\\tilde A_t\\) with column- and row-scaled factors. The difference \\(B_t\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbf 1-\\alpha_t\\circ\\beta_t)\\,A_t\\) is merged into the frozen weight. The summed increments therefore no longer telescope to \\(B_TA_T\\), and \\(W_T-W_0\\) can reach high rank: average rank 54 at \\(r=4\\) on RTE, and 1,347 to 3,703 singular values above 0.005 at \\(r=8\\) on LLaMA2-7B across the eight commonsense tasks (ScaLoRA-I falls to 20 on ARC-e and ARC-c). Gauge reading (derived here): where \\(\\alpha\\circ\\beta>0\\), write \\(\\alpha=c\\circ s\\) and \\(\\beta=c^{-1}\\circ s\\) elementwise. The move is then a diagonal (maximal-torus) gauge transformation \\((B,A)\\mapsto(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c),\\mathrm{diag}(c)^{-1}A)\\), which leaves \\(W\\) fixed but changes the next step because LoRA's gradient descent is not gauge-invariant. It is followed by a per-direction rescaling \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s^{\\circ2})\\) of the adapter, whose complement is merged. Only diagonal scalings are used, because they are the transformations under which Adam's moment estimates transform in closed form (Lemmas 3.3 and 3.6); a full-matrix or row-wise transform is intractable. The unrestricted optimum of the same surrogate (Theorem 3.2) takes \\(\\tilde B\\) and \\(\\tilde A^{\\top}\\) from \\(2r\\) top singular vectors of \\(G_t\\), split into two sets of \\(r\\), each up to an \\(O(r)\\) factor. LoRA-GA's initialisation is the \\(t=0\\), identity-rotation case. In the paper's printed Theorem 3.7, the second block matrix lists \\(\\mathbf B_t\\) before \\(\\mathbf A_t^{\\top}\\nabla\\ell\\) and needs a transpose. The pairing given in formula_latex, \\(S_{in}=[G^{\\top}B,\\ A^{\\top}]\\), is the one consistent with the stated \\(\\lambda\\) and objective (re-derived here). The paper's 'fixed subspace' remark is the telescoping identity \\(\\sum_t\\Delta W_t=B_TA_T\\), i.e. a rank-\\(r\\) bound on vanilla LoRA's cumulative update.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapter is merged into the base at every re-split, and the final model is the dense \\(W_T\\). Because \\(W_0^{(t)}\\) itself changes, \\(W_T-W_0\\) is high-rank and cannot be shipped as a rank-\\(r\\) adapter. The paper states this as a limitation: ScaLoRA stores the entire merged matrix.","identity_at_init":"Yes. Standard LoRA initialisation has one zero factor; the paper puts the Gaussian on the \\(d_{out}\\times r\\) factor and zeros on the \\(d_{in}\\) side. This gives \\(W_0^{(0)}+B_0A_0=W_0\\), and every later re-split preserves \\(W_t\\) exactly.","key_idea":"Vanilla LoRA's increments telescope, \\(\\sum_t\\Delta W_t=B_TA_T\\), so the cumulative update never exceeds rank \\(r\\). ScaLoRA merges and re-factors at every step and rescales Adam's moments to match, so it accumulates a high-rank update with no restarts. The column scaling of the current factors is chosen in closed form to minimise a smoothness upper bound on the next loss, which is the same as best matching the full fine-tuning step \\(-G_t/L\\).","results_note":"With \\(r=8\\), ScaLoRA-I averages 74.75 on eight commonsense tasks with LLaMA2-7B and ScaLoRA averages 77.85 with LLaMA3-8B. Both are above LoRA (73.63/76.83), ReLoRA, LoRA-GA, MoRA and HiRA, and above rank-32 LoRA (74.52/77.54). On GLUE with DeBERTaV3-base (\\(r=4\\)) ScaLoRA averages 88.98 against 88.13 for LoRA. On LLaMA3-8B it needs about 0.01 GB more memory than LoRA (MoRA 1.08 GB, HiRA 7.83 GB), but ScaLoRA, like MoRA and HiRA, takes 50% or more extra fine-tuning time; ScaLoRA-I is close to LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline. ReLoRA also merges low-rank increments into the base, but only rarely, with re-initialised factors, reset optimizer state and LR re-warm-up. ScaLoRA re-splits at every step, keeps scaled factors and transforms their moments."},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"generalizes","note":"The paper states that LoRA-GA's gradient-SVD initialisation is the \\(t=0\\), identity-rotation case (\\(P_0=Q_0=I_r\\)) of Theorem 3.2's optimal-adapter condition. LoRA-GA is also a commonsense baseline."},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"High-rank baseline. The paper reports that HiRA's Hadamard update has high Euclidean rank but intrinsic rank \\(r\\), and needs \\(O(d_{out}d_{in})\\) extra memory, against ScaLoRA's \\(O((d_{out}+d_{in}+r)r)\\)."},{"target":"mora","type":"competes-with","note":"High-rank baseline (square-matrix compress/decompress); 1.08 GB of extra memory over LoRA on LLaMA3-8B."},{"target":"cola","type":"competes-with","note":"Another rebasing scheme: tune a LoRA, merge it, start a new one and reset optimizer state. ScaLoRA avoids such restarts. COLA is not cited."},{"target":"delta-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Also feeds low-rank increments into the base weight without restarts, but by adding the product's per-step change rather than by an optimal re-split. Delta-LoRA is not cited."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23818","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.23818","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63892"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":["Yilang Zhang","Xiaodong Yang","Yiwei Cai","Georgios B. Giannakis"],"bibkey":"zhang2025scalora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025scalora,\n  title = {{ScaLoRA: Optimally Scaled Low-Rank Adaptation for Efficient High-Rank Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Yilang Zhang and Xiaodong Yang and Yiwei Cai and Georgios B. Giannakis},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2510.23818},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23818}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A rebasing scheme inside one run: every step (ScaLoRA) or every \\(I\\) steps (ScaLoRA-I) the product is re-split \\(W_t=\\widetilde W_0^{(t)}+\\widetilde B_t\\widetilde A_t\\) with \\(\\widetilde B=B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)\\), \\(\\widetilde A=\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta)A\\); the complement \\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbf 1-\\alpha\\circ\\beta)A\\) is merged into the frozen weight and Adam's moments are rescaled in closed form.","shape":"Each stage is LoRA's cone translated to \\(\\widetilde W_0^{(t)}\\) and started at the rank-\\(r\\) point \\(\\widetilde B_t\\widetilde A_t\\): a translated cone (Cone*), unlike ReLoRA's restarts at the apex.","base_point":"The re-split is function-preserving and compensated, so every stage starts at a smooth point (stratum S) wherever \\(\\alpha\\circ\\beta\\) has no zero entry; stage 0 is LoRA's zero-factor apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) per stage. Where \\(\\alpha\\circ\\beta>0\\), write \\((\\alpha,\\beta)=(c\\circ s,\\ c^{-1}\\circ s)\\): a torus gauge move \\(c\\) (no effect on \\(W\\), but on the next Adam step) followed by a per-direction rescaling \\(s^{\\circ2}\\) whose complement is merged.","covariance":"The reach is that of a LoRA rebasing scheme, basis-free; the choice of \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\) uses Frobenius norms (\\(O\\times O\\)) and the moment transport is equivariant only under the torus.","merge":"Merged at every re-split; the deliverable is the dense \\(W_T\\).","rebasing":"Itself a rebasing scheme with reach \\(\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 1); the paper measures cumulative ranks of 54 at \\(r=4\\) and over a thousand at \\(r=8\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ScaLoRA is ReLoRA without restarts: every step is a merge-and-restart, but the restart lands at a rescaled copy of the current factors, a split smooth point, instead of the apex, and the optimizer state is transported instead of reset. Its one degree of freedom per re-split is a diagonal rescaling, the largest family under which Adam's moments transform in closed form, chosen to best match a full fine-tuning step.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\,B,\\ -\\widetilde B_t\\,],\\ [\\,A;\\ \\widetilde A_t\\,]\\big)","note":"Per stage: the stage over W~_0^{(t)} = W_t - B~_tA~_t factors through LoRA_{2r} on the pre-split weight W_t (Thm II.1 conversion), at the degenerate pointing ([B~_t,-B~_t],[A~_t;A~_t])."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"relora","test":"O3","reason":"Per stage ScaLoRA restarts at a smooth split point and ReLoRA at the apex (B = 0): different strata, so the stage images are incomparable at equal rank (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"delta-lora","test":"O2","reason":"After K re-splits ScaLoRA's cumulative update can reach rank min((K+1)r, m, n) (measured: 54 at r = 4), while Delta-LoRA's total update has rank at most 2r."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"lora-s","name":"LoRA-S (AdamS, LRACS)","full_name":"LoRA-S: Sylvester-equation horizontal-lift scheme for LoRA optimizers (Adam-Sylvester and LRACS)","paper_title":"LoRA-S: An Efficient Low Rank Adaptation scheme via Sylvester equation","authors":"Zheng et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=Guo2XGgxZA","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+BA,\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}_*^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}_*^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ (\\text{paper: } M=B,\\ N=A^{\\top},\\ X=MN^{\\top});\\ G_t=\\nabla_W\\mathcal L;\\ \\text{matrix-space direction } \\dot X_t=-\\eta\\,\\tilde X_t:\\ \\text{AdamS: } m_t=\\beta_1m_{t-1}+(1-\\beta_1)G_t,\\ v_t=\\beta_2v_{t-1}+(1-\\beta_2)G_t^{\\odot2},\\ \\tilde X_t=m_t/\\sqrt{v_t};\\ \\text{LRACS: } \\tilde X_t=\\mathrm{Diag}(m_q^{t})^{-1/2}\\,G_t\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(m_s^{t})^{-1/2}\\ (\\text{row and column statistics, as in RACS});\\ \\text{Sylvester: } (B^{\\top}B)(AA^{\\top})K_t+K_t(B^{\\top}B)(AA^{\\top})=B^{\\top}\\dot X_tA^{\\top};\\ \\text{lift: } \\dot B=(\\dot X_tA^{\\top}-BAA^{\\top}K_t)(AA^{\\top})^{-1},\\ \\ \\dot A=(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}(B^{\\top}\\dot X_t-K_tB^{\\top}BA);\\ \\ (B,A)\\leftarrow(B+\\dot B,\\ A+\\dot A);\\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{trained: } B,A\\ (\\text{no weight decay})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (plain LoRA factors). Optimizer state: } O(d_{out}d_{in}) \\text{ matrix-space Adam moments for AdamS (the poster mentions a low-memory variant) and } O(d_{out}+d_{in}+1) \\text{ for LRACS}","locus":"LoRA-adapted weights of transformer-based models. The poster reports Mix-of-Show image generation with LoRA ranks \\(r=4,8,16\\) and GPT-2 medium on E2E NLG (\\(r=4\\)); the poster does not name the base diffusion model.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Optimizer for plain LoRA factors, built on the quotient \\((\\mathbb R_*^{d_{out}\\times r}\\times\\mathbb R_*^{r\\times d_{in}})/GL(r)\\cong\\mathcal M_r\\). The base optimizer (Adam or RACS) preconditions the full-matrix gradient in matrix space. An \\(r\\times r\\) Sylvester equation and a horizontal lift then map the resulting direction \\(\\dot X\\) to factor updates. Derived here from the poster's formulas: \\(\\dot BA+B\\dot A=P_B\\dot X+\\dot XP_A-P_B\\dot XP_A\\), with \\(P_B=B(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}B^{\\top}\\) and \\(P_A=A^{\\top}(AA^{\\top})^{-1}A\\). So the first-order change of \\(W\\) is the orthogonal projection of \\(\\dot X\\) onto the tangent space \\(T_{BA}\\mathcal M_r\\). The Sylvester matrix \\(K\\) is exactly what removes the double-counted term \\(P_B\\dot XP_A\\), which the \\(K=0\\) lift contains; that lift is scaled GD, i.e. Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA. Setting \\(K=\\epsilon I\\) instead turns the \\(K\\) terms into \\(-\\epsilon B\\) and \\(-\\epsilon A\\), i.e. decoupled weight decay on both factors. This is the paper's reading of weight decay as a crude approximation of \\(K\\), and the reason it removes the decay hyperparameter. The update is equivariant under \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A)\\), with \\(K\\mapsto R^{\\top}KR^{-\\top}\\), so the induced \\(W\\) update depends only on \\(BA\\). This is the poster's Theorem 1: transformation invariance plus the paper's 'efficient feature learning' condition. Each step costs \\(\\Omega(d_{out}d_{in}r)+O(r^{3}+(d_{out}+d_{in})r^{2})\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the optimizer changes. The trained adapter is a standard \\(BA\\) that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not specified in the material available. The lift needs \\((AA^{\\top})^{-1}\\) and \\((B^{\\top}B)^{-1}\\), i.e. full-rank factors in \\(\\mathbb R_*\\), so LoRA's usual \\(B=0\\) start lies outside its domain. The paper presumably uses a nonzero or damped start, which the poster does not show.","key_idea":"Precondition in matrix space, then lift. Any matrix-space optimizer becomes a gauge-invariant LoRA optimizer once its direction is lifted to the factors through the quotient geometry, with an \\(r\\times r\\) Sylvester solve fixing the correction term. The \\(\\epsilon I\\) weight-decay term of factor optimizers is reinterpreted as a crude approximation of that Sylvester matrix and dropped.","results_note":"According to the official ICLR poster, with rank-4 LoRA on GPT-2 medium (E2E NLG) LRACS reaches BLEU 70.4, against 69.3 for LoRA-RITE and 68.6 for AdamW. On Mix-of-Show image customisation, AdamS at rank 16 reaches CLIP 32.64 / FID 46.32, against 31.90 / 55.68 for LoRA-RITE.","relations":[{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"generalizes","note":"With \\(K=0\\) and \\(\\dot X=-\\eta G\\), the lift is exactly the scaled-GD step \\(\\dot B=-\\eta GA^{\\top}(AA^{\\top})^{-1}\\), \\(\\dot A=-\\eta(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}B^{\\top}G\\) of Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA, which appears as the 'Scaled GD' baseline on the poster (ScaledAdamW is cited as Zhang and Pilanci, 2024). LoRA-S adds the Sylvester correction and allows arbitrary matrix-space preconditioners (derived here)."},{"target":"lora-rite","type":"competes-with","note":"Main transformation-invariant baseline on the poster. LoRA-RITE preconditions in orthonormal factor bases; LoRA-S preconditions in matrix space and then lifts."},{"target":"lora-pro","type":"competes-with","note":"Citation not checked (full paper unavailable). LoRA-Pro's equivalent gradient is also the tangent-space projection \\(P_BG+GP_A-P_BGP_A\\), and it fixes its free \\(r\\times r\\) matrix with a Sylvester equation. LoRA-S applies the same projection to a preconditioned matrix-space direction (derived here)."},{"target":"fixed-rank-riemannian-quotient","type":"builds-on","note":"Conceptual relation: the abstract's quotient manifold \\(\\mathbb R_*^{m\\times r}\\times\\mathbb R_*^{n\\times r}/\\sim\\) and its horizontal lifts are the fixed-rank quotient geometry developed there, which LoRA-S instantiates for LoRA. Whether the paper cites it could not be checked because the paper PDF was unavailable."}],"sources":["https://openreview.net/forum?id=Guo2XGgxZA","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10010449","https://iclr.cc/media/PosterPDFs/ICLR%202026/10010449.png","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://mlanthology.org/iclr/2026/zheng2026iclr-loras/","https://github.com/CleverWing/lora_s"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"zheng2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zheng2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-S: An Efficient Low Rank Adaptation scheme via Sylvester equation}},\n  author = {Zheng and others},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=Guo2XGgxZA}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA's \\(\\rho=W_0+BA\\). The optimizer preconditions the full gradient in matrix space (AdamS, LRACS), projects the direction onto \\(T_{BA}\\mathcal M_r\\) through an \\(r\\times r\\) Sylvester solve and lifts it horizontally to the factors.","shape":"LoRA's cone.","base_point":"Read at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. The lift needs \\((B^\\top B)^{-1}\\), so it is undefined at \\(B=0\\) (as undamped scaled GD); the start actually used is not shown in the available material (poster only).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\), all of it respected: the step is equivariant under \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A)\\) with \\(K\\mapsto R^\\top KR^{-\\top}\\), and the optimizer state lives in matrix space (the poster's Theorem 1; class (c) of Thm III.12).","covariance":"The image is LoRA's basis-free cone; the matrix-space Adam preconditioner is coordinatewise, so the trajectory is only monomially covariant.","merge":"A standard \\(BA\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-S is LoRA with a descending optimizer: any matrix-space preconditioner, followed by the orthogonal projection onto the fixed-rank tangent space and a horizontal lift. A zero Sylvester matrix gives scaled GD (Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA) and \\(K=\\epsilon I\\) gives decoupled weight decay on both factors; as an object it is LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule (trained-method data) differs, as for LoRA-Pro and Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"diablo","name":"DiaBlo","full_name":"DiaBlo: fine-tuning only the diagonal blocks of weight matrices","paper_title":"DiaBlo: Diagonal Blocks Are Sufficient For Finetuning","authors":"Gurses et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2506.03230","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03230","code_url":"https://github.com/ziyangjoy/DiaBlo","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=(W_0+D)\\,x,\\quad D=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(D_1,\\dots,D_N)=\\bigoplus_{i=1}^{N}D_i,\\ \\ D_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{N}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{N}};\\ \\ (Dx)_i=D_ix_i\\ (\\text{one batched matmul over the } N \\text{ input and output slices});\\ \\ \\nabla_{D_i}\\mathcal L=\\big(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L\\big)_{ii};\\ \\ D=0 \\text{ at init};\\ \\text{frozen: } W_0 \\text{ (in particular every off-diagonal block)};\\ \\text{trained: } D_1,\\dots,D_N","trainable_params":"\\frac{d_{in}d_{out}}{N}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (typically } N\\in\\{32,64,128\\}\\text{; } N=64 \\text{ is about 1.04\\% of Llama2-7B with } q,k,v,\\mathrm{up},\\mathrm{down}\\text{ adapted)}","locus":"Linear projections of the LLM: q, k, v, up and down (FP16 commonsense and MetaMathQA runs), or q, k, v, o, gate, up and down (BF16 commonsense, code, safety, MT-Bench and, according to Appendix A.4.5, the quantized runs), on Llama2-7B/13B, Llama3-8B, LLaMA-13B, Mistral-7B and Llama-3.2-3B (block-count ablation); all linear layers except embeddings and the LM head for T5-base (GLUE). Table 4's 70M trainable parameters for Llama2-7B at \\(N=64\\) match q, k, v, up and down rather than all seven projections, so the paper is internally inconsistent about the quantized runs' modules","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"A fixed block-diagonal sparsity pattern on \\(\\Delta W\\): \\(N\\) independent dense blocks on the diagonal. Block \\(i\\) maps the \\(i\\)-th slice of \\(d_{in}/N\\) input coordinates to the \\(i\\)-th slice of \\(d_{out}/N\\) output coordinates, so \\(\\mathrm{rank}(\\Delta W)\\) can reach \\(\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\) with \\(d_{in}d_{out}/N\\) parameters. The update set is a linear subspace, not a product manifold. There is therefore no \\(GL(r)\\) gauge, the loss restricted to it is the original loss (convex in \\(D\\) whenever it is convex in \\(W\\)), and Adam on \\(D\\) is Adam on the masked entries of \\(W\\). With decoupled weight decay (the quantized runs use decay 1.0) AdamW shrinks \\(D\\), i.e. pulls \\(W_{ii}\\) towards \\(W_{0,ii}\\) rather than towards 0. The block structure is tied to the coordinate basis of the hidden units: it is not invariant to permuting or rotating them. Relative to neighbouring shapes, factoring each block at rank \\(r/N\\) gives MELoRA, a full \\(N\\times N\\) grid of low-rank blocks gives GraLoRA, and choosing the blocks by gradient magnitude gives SMT. Linear least squares (Theorem 1): if the inputs \\(X\\) are generic of rank \\(r\\) and \\(N\\le d_{in}/r\\), every DiaBlo minimiser is a full fine-tuning minimiser. This uses \\(d_{in}d_{out}/N\\ge d_{out}r\\) parameters, against LoRA's \\((d_{in}+d_{out})r\\). Nonlinear case (Theorem 2): if \\(X\\) and \\(g_Y\\) are generic of ranks \\(r_1\\) and \\(r_2\\) and \\(N\\ge\\lceil r_1N/d_{in}\\rceil\\lceil r_2N/d_{out}\\rceil\\), DiaBlo's stationary points are stationary for full fine-tuning. Sizes that \\(N\\) does not divide use blocks of \\(\\lceil d/N\\rceil\\) with zero padding.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(D\\) is a \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) block-diagonal matrix added to \\(W_0\\). It can be folded in, or stored compactly as an \\(N\\times\\frac{d_{out}}{N}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{N}\\) tensor. On a quantized base (the GPTQ-DiaBlo and MagR-DiaBlo runs), merging needs re-quantization.","identity_at_init":"Yes. \\(D=0\\), and since there is no product the gradient \\(\\nabla_{D_i}\\mathcal L=(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L)_{ii}\\) is nonzero from the first step. There is no LoRA-style zero-factor stall and no special initialisation.","key_idea":"Update a structured subset of the weights directly instead of a low-rank product. Training only the diagonal blocks keeps LoRA-like memory and speed, since the forward pass is one batched matmul. It removes the bilinear factorisation's sensitivity to initialisation and optimizer. Under low-rank activations and gradients it provably reaches full fine-tuning minimisers in the linear case, and full fine-tuning stationary points in general.","results_note":"On commonsense reasoning DiaBlo reaches an 87.3 average with Llama3-8B (\\(N=64\\)), against 85.2 for DoRA \\(r=32\\) and 80.8 for LoRA \\(r=32\\). The paper lists this run as 1.04% of parameters, but q, k, v, up and down blocks at \\(N=64\\) come to about 0.89% of Llama3-8B by our count. On MetaMathQA with Llama2-7B (\\(N=32\\), 2.09%) it averages 43.4 on GSM8K/MATH, against 43.2 for full fine-tuning. Training speed matches LoRA \\(r=32\\): 170 minutes per epoch on Commonsense-170k, against 480 for DoRA.","relations":[{"target":"melora","type":"generalizes","note":"MELoRA factors each diagonal block as \\(B_iA_i\\) of rank \\(r/N\\), while DiaBlo trains the same blocks at full block rank. For equal \\(N\\), MELoRA's update set is a subset of DiaBlo's. MELoRA is not cited."},{"target":"smt","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited baseline. SMT selects the sub-matrices with the largest warm-up gradients; DiaBlo fixes the pattern to the diagonal blocks. DiaBlo beats SMT on commonsense reasoning with fewer parameters (83.5 at 0.52% vs 81.8 at 0.84% on Llama2-7B)."},{"target":"gralora","type":"competes-with","note":"GraLoRA tiles the full \\(N\\times N\\) block grid with low-rank adapters; DiaBlo keeps only the diagonal tiles but makes them dense. GraLoRA is not cited."},{"target":"miss","type":"competes-with","note":"Another structured, non-product shape: MiSS replicates one small trainable shard across the matrix (rank at most \\(r\\)), while DiaBlo gives each diagonal block its own dense parameters. MiSS is not cited."},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Strongest LoRA-family baseline on commonsense reasoning. DiaBlo exceeds it on Llama2-7B, Llama3-8B and LLaMA-13B."},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"Quantized-base baseline. DiaBlo fine-tunes GPTQ/MagR-quantized Llama2 models with no joint quantization and initialisation procedure, and MagR-DiaBlo matches or exceeds LoftQ and ApiQ at 4 and 2 bits."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03230","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.03230","https://github.com/ziyangjoy/DiaBlo","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":["Selcuk Gurses","Aozhong Zhang","Yanxia Deng","Xun Dong","Xin Li","Naigang Wang","Penghang Yin","Zi Yang"],"bibkey":"gurses2025diablo","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gurses2025diablo,\n  title = {{DiaBlo: Diagonal Blocks Are Sufficient For Finetuning}},\n  author = {Selcuk Gurses and Aozhong Zhang and Yanxia Deng and Xun Dong and Xin Li and Naigang Wang and Penghang Yin and Zi Yang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2506.03230},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03230}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Additive \\(W_0+\\mathrm{blkdiag}(D_1,\\dots,D_N)\\), the dense diagonal blocks trained directly.","shape":"A fixed coordinate subspace (the diagonal blocks), \\(d=d_{in}d_{out}/N\\), maximum rank \\(\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\).","base_point":"\\(D=0\\) and no product: \\(S_1\\) is the whole subspace (regular), and \\(\\nabla_{D_i}\\mathcal L=(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L)_{ii}\\) from the first step.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"The block partition is imposed: a permutation that mixes blocks moves the image off the diagonal blocks (Lemma VI.2 test), so \\(\\Pi\\), as SMT, MoRA and GraLoRA.","merge":"One block-diagonal matrix (requantised on the GPTQ and MagR bases).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the blocks fixed."},"categorical_reading":"DiaBlo is a fixed-mask selective method whose mask is block-diagonal: the linear shape between MELoRA (each block factored at rank \\(r/N\\)) and full fine-tuning. Linearity is the point: it has no gauge and no apex, so the zero-factor stall and initialisation sensitivity of bilinear adapters do not arise, at the price of an imposed coordinate partition.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id},\\qquad M=\\mathrm{supp}\\,\\mathrm{blkdiag}(\\mathbf 1_{d_{out}/N\\times d_{in}/N},\\dots)","note":"FISH taken with the fixed block-diagonal mask instead of its Fisher-selected one, pointed at 0."},{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"D\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_{\\rm blk}D\\big)","note":"The block support is one fixed coordinate set, a stratum of Diff pruning's learned-support cone (within the L0 budget when k >= d_in d_out / N), as SMT -> Diff pruning."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The maximum rank min(d_in, d_out) exceeds r."},{"target":"melora","test":"O2","reason":"At equal N, d(DiaBlo) = d_in d_out / N exceeds MELoRA's sum over blocks of (r/N)(d_in/N + d_out/N - r/N); MELoRA maps into DiaBlo (factor each block), not conversely."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"qzo","name":"QZO","full_name":"QZO: Quantized Zeroth-order Optimization (Q-SPSA with directional-derivative clipping)","paper_title":"Fine-tuning Quantized Neural Networks with Zeroth-order Optimization","authors":"Shang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2505.13430","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.13430","code_url":"https://github.com/maifoundations/QZO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat W=\\Delta\\odot\\overline W,\\ \\ \\overline W\\ \\text{frozen low-bit codes (GPTQ int4 with group size } g=128\\text{, or AQLM 2-bit codebooks)},\\ \\ \\Delta=S\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{1\\times g},\\ S\\in\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge0}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}/g}\\ (\\text{channel-wise } S\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}} \\text{ for AQLM});\\ \\ d=\\frac{\\mathcal L((\\Delta+\\epsilon z)\\odot\\overline W;\\mathcal B)-\\mathcal L((\\Delta-\\epsilon z)\\odot\\overline W;\\mathcal B)}{2\\epsilon},\\ z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I)\\ \\text{on the scale entries, regenerated from a seed};\\ \\ d'=\\mathrm{clip}(d,-C,C);\\ \\ S\\leftarrow\\max(S-\\eta\\,d'z,\\ 0);\\ \\text{frozen: the integer codes};\\ \\text{trained: the quantization scales (in the AQLM runs, non-quantized parameters are also updated by plain SPSA)};\\ \\text{no backward pass and no optimizer state}","trainable_params":"\\frac{d_{out}d_{in}}{g}\\ \\text{scales per quantized matrix } (g=128;\\ 5.03\\times10^{7}\\ \\text{for OPT-6.7B, about } 0.76\\%);\\ d_{out}\\ \\text{for channel-wise AQLM scales};\\ \\text{optimizer memory } O(1) \\text{ (a seed and a scalar)}","locus":"Quantization scales of all quantized linear layers: GPTQ 4-bit (group size 128) OPT-6.7B, Llama-2-7B and Llama-3.1-8B; AQLM 2-bit (one 16-bit codebook) Llama-2-13B, with channel-wise scales plus the non-quantized parts trained by plain SPSA; the NF4-quantized (bitsandbytes) DiT of Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"PEQA-type parametrisation trained with MeZO-style SPSA. The parametrisation is a group-wise multiplicative rescaling of frozen integer codes, \\(\\hat W=(S\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{1\\times g})\\odot\\overline W\\); per channel it is the left diagonal action \\(\\hat W=\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\,\\overline W\\). The gradient estimate is a rank-one random projection \\(zz^{\\top}\\nabla_S\\mathcal L\\) of the scale gradient, computed from two forward passes of the quantized model. Weights are never de-quantized or re-quantized, and activations are not stored. Directional-derivative clipping caps the scalar \\(d\\) at \\(C\\) (100 in the paper), and the projection \\(\\max(\\cdot,0)\\) keeps the scales non-negative. Every code stays fixed, so the reachable set is \\(\\{(S\\otimes\\mathbf 1)\\odot\\overline W\\}\\), of dimension \\(d_{out}d_{in}/g\\). The paper's Theorem 1 states that the clipped estimate is unbiased. Its proof (Appendix A) passes from the clipped to the unclipped sum with only the remark that the two agree as \\(\\epsilon\\to0\\), which ignores that the clipping event depends on \\(z\\). In general a clipped SPSA estimate is biased. In the idealised case of a fixed batch, \\(\\epsilon\\to0\\) and Gaussian \\(z\\), \\(\\mathbb E[\\mathrm{clip}(d)\\,z]=c\\,\\nabla_S\\mathcal L\\) with \\(c\\in(0,1]\\), a shrinkage along the true gradient (derived here). The second-moment bound \\(\\mathbb E\\|d'z\\|^{2}\\le\\mathbb E\\|dz\\|^{2}\\) holds unconditionally. The paper's per-coordinate variance inequality is derived through Theorem 1 and is therefore not established in general.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained parameters are the quantization scales themselves. The result is an ordinary GPTQ or AQLM model with the same codes and inference kernels.","identity_at_init":"No adapter is added. Training starts from the post-training-quantized model, whose GPTQ/AQLM scales approximate but do not equal \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"Zeroth-order training removes gradients and optimizer states, and quantization shrinks the weights. The two meet in the continuous quantization scales, which SPSA can perturb and update while the discrete codes stay fixed. Clipping the directional derivative keeps the noisy estimate stable.","results_note":"On 4-bit GPTQ Llama-2-7B, QZO needs 5.0 GB, against 14.8 GB for 16-bit MeZO and 26.0 GB for 16-bit SGD fine-tuning, and beats MeZO on four of five tasks (for example SST-2 90.0 vs 83.5). On OPT-6.7B it trails MeZO on most tasks.","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses MeZO's seed-regenerated SPSA and ZO-SGD, applied to the quantization scales of a 4- or 2-bit model instead of 16-bit weights. MeZO is the main baseline."},{"target":"peqa","type":"combines","note":"Trains the same object as PEQA, group-wise quantization scales over frozen integer codes, but without backpropagation, by combining it with MeZO-style estimation. PEQA is not cited."},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Appendix comparison on OPT-6.7B: first-order QLoRA (5.6 GB) is more accurate than QZO (4.8 GB). A two-phase QZO+QLoRA variant (zeroth-order LoRA training, then QZO on the remaining scales) closes part of the gap at 4.9 GB."},{"target":"agzo","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are zeroth-order fine-tuning methods; AGZO is not cited. AGZO reduces estimator variance by perturbing 16-bit weights only along activation subspaces; QZO perturbs quantization scales and clips the directional derivative."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.13430","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.13430","https://github.com/maifoundations/QZO","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":["Sifeng Shang","Jiayi Zhou","Chenyu Lin","Minxian Li","Kaiyang Zhou"],"bibkey":"shang2025fine","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shang2025fine,\n  title = {{Fine-tuning Quantized Neural Networks with Zeroth-order Optimization}},\n  author = {Sifeng Shang and Jiayi Zhou and Chenyu Lin and Minxian Li and Kaiyang Zhou},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2505.13430},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.13430}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Action type on a quantised model: \\(\\hat W=(S\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{1\\times g})\\odot\\overline W\\), group-wise scales over frozen integer codes (PEQA-type), trained by seeded two-point SPSA with directional-derivative clipping and a nonnegativity projection.","shape":"Linear in the scales, \\(d=d_{out}d_{in}/g\\); the projection keeps \\(S\\ge0\\), an orthant whose interior contains the start.","base_point":"Starts at the post-training-quantised model, not at \\(\\theta_0\\): a defect, as PEQA.","gauge":"None while every group has a nonzero code.","covariance":"Groups of \\(g=128\\) consecutive input columns impose a partition: \\(\\Pi\\), as EfficientQAT's group-wise scales; the channel-wise AQLM instance is Mon×Mon (PEQA).","merge":"The trained parameters are the quantised model's own scales: same codes, same kernels, no type change.","rebasing":"Scales compose multiplicatively over frozen codes, a submonoid: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"QZO is PEQA's object, with EfficientQAT's group-wise scales, trained by MeZO's estimator: the continuous part of a quantised model is exactly the part a zeroth-order method can perturb without leaving the grid. The clipping belongs to the trained-method data; it biases the estimate towards a shrinkage along the gradient rather than leaving it unbiased.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"efficientqat","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ S\\mapsto S","note":"The same group-wise scale family over frozen integer codes (EfficientQAT's E2E-QP stage), valid over common codes, zero-points and group size; both are pointed at their own quantised start."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"peqa","test":"O2","reason":"Group-wise scales have d = d_out d_in / g > d_out, so the GPTQ instance does not factor through per-channel PEQA (as EfficientQAT)."},{"target":"qlora","test":"O1","reason":"QZO's start GPTQ(W_0) (on-grid codes with fitted scales) differs from QLoRA's vertex NF4(W_0) by a generically full-rank matrix, so it is not in NF4(W_0) + M_{<=r}: Im QZO is not inside Im QLoRA (as EfficientQAT -/-> QLoRA)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"perk","name":"PERK","full_name":"PERK: Parameter Efficient Reasoning over Knowledge (meta-learned test-time LoRA memory)","paper_title":"PERK: Long-Context Reasoning as Parameter-Efficient Test-Time Learning","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2507.06415","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06415","code_url":"https://github.com/eric11eca/perk","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\phi^{(0)}=\\theta_{\\mathrm{adapter}}=\\{(B_l,A_l)\\},\\ \\ \\phi^{(n+1)}=\\mathrm{AdamW}_{\\alpha_{l,n}}\\big(\\phi^{(n)},\\ \\nabla_{\\phi}\\mathcal L^{\\psi}_{\\mathrm{NLL}}(\\phi^{(n)};\\mathcal K)\\big),\\ n=0,\\dots,N-1\\ (N=4,\\ \\text{differentiable AdamW}),\\ \\ \\mathcal L^{\\psi}_{\\mathrm{NLL}}(\\phi;\\mathcal K)=-\\sum_{c\\in\\mathrm{chunks}(\\mathcal K)}\\sum_{t}w_\\psi(c)_t\\,\\log p_{W_0,\\phi}(c_t\\mid c_{<t})\\ (\\approx256\\text{-token chunks processed as one batch; } w_\\psi \\text{ a two-layer MLP token weighter}),\\ \\ \\text{answer } q \\text{ with } h=(W_0+B^{(N)}A^{(N)})x\\ \\text{and no context in the prompt};\\ \\ \\min_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{adapter}},\\{\\alpha_{l,n}\\},\\psi}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{(\\mathcal K,q,y)}\\,\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{reason}}\\big(W_0,\\phi^{(N)}(\\theta_{\\mathrm{adapter}},\\mathcal K);q,y\\big),\\ \\ \\nabla_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{adapter}}}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{reason}}\\approx\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{reason}}}{\\partial\\phi^{(N)}}\\prod_{n=N-T}^{N-1}J^{(n)},\\ J^{(n)}=\\frac{\\partial\\phi^{(n+1)}}{\\partial\\phi^{(n)}}\\ (=I-\\alpha H^{(n)}\\text{ for plain GD}),\\ \\ T\\in\\{1,2\\}\\ (\\text{truncated unrolling});\\ \\text{frozen: } W_0;\\ \\text{meta-trained: the LoRA initialisation, per-layer-per-step inner learning rates } \\alpha_{l,n},\\ \\text{token weighter } \\psi;\\ \\text{test-time trained: the LoRA, once per context}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix with } r=256\\ (\\text{rsLoRA}),\\ \\text{on all linear modules of every layer for models up to 1B and of the top 4 layers for 7B/8B models};\\ \\text{plus } L\\times N \\text{ meta-learned inner learning rates and a two-layer MLP token weighter. The same adapter is re-trained for every new context}","locus":"All linear modules (attention and MLP): every layer of GPT-2, Qwen-2.5-0.5B and LLaMA-3.2-1B; only the top 4 layers of Qwen-2.5-7B and LLaMA-3.1-8B","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Bi-level, MAML-style meta-learning over a LoRA. The inner loop is test-time training of \\(\\Delta W=BA\\) by \\(N=4\\) differentiable AdamW steps on the token-weighted NLL of the context. The context is chunked into a parallel batch, which decouples its length from the native window; chunk order is kept only through 'Document i:' tags in the text. The outer loop optimises the adapter's initial state \\((B_0,A_0)\\), the inner learning rates and the token-weighting network, so that the post-adaptation model answers questions without seeing the context. Meta-gradients are truncated to the last \\(T\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) inner steps, so meta-training on 8K-token contexts fits on one GPU. The LoRA serves as a fast-weight memory: the context is written into parameters by gradient descent, not by a hypernetwork forward pass. Gauge remark (derived here): the inner loop is plain adaptive gradient descent on the factors, which is not invariant under \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A)\\). The outer loop therefore selects a particular representative of the initial adapter's orbit, and its conditioning decides what four inner steps can write. The learned object is that representative, not just the product \\(B_0A_0\\).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"After adaptation \\(B^{(N)}A^{(N)}\\) is an ordinary low-rank delta and could be folded into \\(W_0\\). It is specific to one context, however, and is regenerated by test-time training for each new one, so it is kept as a swappable module.","identity_at_init":"Not identity by design. Inference starts from the meta-learned adapter \\(\\theta_{\\mathrm{adapter}}\\), which is generally nonzero, so the model differs from the base even before test-time training. The base weights stay frozen, so dropping the adapter recovers the base model. The LoRA initialisation at the start of meta-training is not stated beyond 'default' PEFT settings.","key_idea":"Treat a LoRA as a fast-weight memory written by a few gradient steps on the context at test time. Meta-learn the adapter's starting point, step sizes and token weights, MAML-style with truncated unrolling to fit 8K-token contexts, so that the written adapter supports reasoning over long inputs rather than in-context retrieval.","results_note":"Trained on 8K-token contexts, PERK beats standard long-context fine-tuning (FT-ICR) on HotpotQA/TriviaQA by about 20 absolute points at the 0.5B scale and 15 at 7B. On BabiLong, PERK (Qwen-2.5-7B) leads FT-ICR by 23 points on average when extrapolating to 32K tokens.","relations":[{"target":"doc-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same goal of internalising a context into a LoRA. Doc-to-LoRA amortises the write into one hypernetwork forward pass trained by context distillation; PERK writes it with meta-learned gradient steps at test time. D2L appeared later and is not cited."},{"target":"shine","type":"competes-with","note":"SHINE is a single-pass in-context hypernetwork from context to LoRA; PERK is the gradient-based, test-time-training counterpart. SHINE is not cited."},{"target":"generative-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Also maps a context to a parameter delta, but with a forward-pass generator instead of inner-loop optimisation. Not cited."},{"target":"cartridges","type":"competes-with","note":"Cartridges compresses a corpus offline into a trained KV cache by self-study distillation, once per corpus. PERK trains a LoRA per context in four meta-learned steps. Not cited."},{"target":"rslora","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses rank-stabilised scaling for its rank-256 adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06415","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.06415","https://perk-long-context.web.app","https://github.com/eric11eca/perk","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-3"],"authors_full":["Zeming Chen","Angelika Romanou","Gail Weiss","Antoine Bosselut"],"bibkey":"chen2025perk","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2025perk,\n  title = {{PERK: Long-Context Reasoning as Parameter-Efficient Test-Time Learning}},\n  author = {Zeming Chen and Angelika Romanou and Gail Weiss and Antoine Bosselut},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2507.06415},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06415}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over contexts: \\(c\\mapsto\\phi^{(N)}(\\theta_{adapter},\\{\\alpha_{l,n}\\},\\psi;c)\\), \\(N=4\\) differentiable AdamW steps on a token-weighted NLL of the context, from a meta-learned LoRA start with meta-learned inner rates and token weighter.","shape":"Each fibre is a point of LoRA\\(_r\\)'s cone (rank 256, rsLoRA scale).","base_point":"The meta-learned start \\(\\theta_{adapter}\\) is generically nonzero, so the model differs from the base before test-time training: a defect.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the rank wire. The inner AdamW is not \\(GL_r\\)-invariant (Thm III.12), so the outer loop selects a representative of the start's orbit; the learned object is that representative, not only \\(B_0A_0\\).","covariance":"Fibrewise LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Each context's \\(B^{(N)}A^{(N)}\\) fuses; it is regenerated per context (merge per fibre, as Text-to-LoRA per task).","rebasing":"Undefined for a family whose fibre changes with the context."},"categorical_reading":"PERK is a gradient-based amortiser: where Doc-to-LoRA or SHINE map a context to a LoRA by one hypernetwork pass, PERK maps it by four unrolled optimizer steps from a learned start. Because Adam sees only a finite shadow of the gauge, the meta-learned quantity includes a choice of factorisation, which is part of what the four steps can write.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\theta_{adapter},\\alpha,\\psi)=\\phi^{(N)}(\\theta_{adapter},\\alpha,\\psi;c)","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, into LoRA_r (rsLoRA scale absorbed) pointed at h_c(q_0) (defect): the unrolled inner loop is an explicit map, smooth for Adam with eps > 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"doc-to-lora","test":"O1","reason":"PERK writes rank-256 updates into every linear module (the top 4 layers at 7B/8B), Doc-to-LoRA only into the MLP down-projections; a PERK context with a nonzero attention update is outside Doc-to-LoRA's image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"vapt","name":"VAPT","full_name":"Visual Adaptive Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Revisit Visual Prompt Tuning: The Expressiveness of Prompt Experts","authors":"Le et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2501.18936","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18936","code_url":"https://github.com/Minhchuyentoancbn/VAPT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\tilde X^{(l)}=[x^{(l)}_1,\\dots,x^{(l)}_N]^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N\\times d}\\ \\mapsto\\ X^{(l)}_{\\text{img}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H\\times W\\times d};\\ \\ X^{(l)}_{\\text{conv}}=F^{(l)}*X^{(l)}_{\\text{img}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H'\\times W'\\times d}\\ \\ (F^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K\\times K}\\text{ shared by all }d\\text{ channels});\\ \\ G^{(l)}_j=\\sum_{k=1}^{H'W'}\\alpha^{(l)}_{j,k}\\,x^{\\text{conv},(l)}_k;\\ \\ p^{(l)}_j(\\tilde X^{(l)})=W^{(2)}\\sigma\\big(W^{(1)}G^{(l)}_j\\big),\\ j=1,\\dots,\\ell;\\ \\ \\tilde h_{m}=\\mathrm{Attn}\\Big(\\begin{bmatrix}\\tilde X^{(l)}\\\\P^{(l)}(\\tilde X^{(l)})\\end{bmatrix}W^{Q}_m,\\begin{bmatrix}\\tilde X^{(l)}\\\\P^{(l)}(\\tilde X^{(l)})\\end{bmatrix}W^{K}_m,\\begin{bmatrix}\\tilde X^{(l)}\\\\P^{(l)}(\\tilde X^{(l)})\\end{bmatrix}W^{V}_m\\Big)\\ \\text{(prompt-position outputs discarded)};\\ \\ \\text{frozen: all ViT weights};\\ \\text{trained: }\\alpha^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times H'W'},\\ F^{(l)},\\ \\text{a per-block LayerNorm},\\ W^{(1)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},W^{(2)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ (\\text{one feature projector shared by all blocks}),\\ \\text{head}","trainable_params":"L\\,\\ell\\,H'W'+L\\,K^{2}+2rd\\ \\text{per model (token-wise projectors, channel-shared kernels, shared feature projector; paper Eq. (15)), plus per-block LayerNorm and the head; e.g. }0.36\\%\\text{ of ViT-B/16 on average over the 24 FGVC/VTAB tasks (VPT-Deep: }L\\ell d\\text{). Released code: block 1 keeps }\\ell d\\text{ static VPT prompts and each token-wise projector also has an }\\ell\\text{-vector bias}","locus":"input token sequence of every ViT block (VPT-Deep insertion point), with each block's prompts generated from that block's own patch tokens; paper: ViT-B/16 (supervised ImageNet-21K, MAE, MoCo v3), ViT-L/H, SETR-PUP on ADE20K, the CLIP ViT-B/32 image encoder","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Input-conditioned deep prompts. At block \\(l\\) the \\(N\\) patch tokens are laid out as an \\(H\\times W\\times d\\) map, convolved with one \\(K\\times K\\) kernel \\(F^{(l)}\\) reused across all \\(d\\) channels, and pooled by \\(\\ell\\) learned token-wise weightings \\(\\alpha^{(l)}_{j,\\cdot}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H'W'}\\). Each pooled vector \\(G^{(l)}_j\\) passes through a bottleneck MLP \\(g(u)=W^{(2)}\\sigma(W^{(1)}u)\\) with \\(r\\ll d\\) that is shared by all blocks. Because convolution and pooling are linear, \\(G^{(l)}_j=W_jX\\) for some \\(W_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times Nd}\\), so each prompt expert is \\(f_{N+j}(X)=W_m^{V\\top}W^{(2)}\\sigma(W^{(1)}_jX)\\) with \\(W^{(1)}_j=W^{(1)}W_j\\). It is therefore a one-hidden-layer function of the whole sequence, where VPT's expert \\(W_m^{V\\top}p_j\\) is a constant. The prompts are re-generated at every block from \\(\\tilde X^{(l)}\\), not from the first-layer input or a [CLS] query, and their attention outputs are dropped as in VPT-Deep. In the released code the first block keeps ordinary static VPT prompts (VPT_DEPTH=1). The token-wise projector also carries a bias and is zero-initialised, and the shared projector is residual, \\(p=G+W^{(2)}\\sigma(W^{(1)}G)\\), a detail not in the paper's Eq. (12).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prompts are extra tokens that depend on the current input \\(\\tilde X^{(l)}\\). They lengthen the attention sequence to \\(N+\\ell\\) and cannot be folded into \\(W^{Q},W^{K},W^{V}\\). Inference keeps the generator, at most about 0.6% more FLOPs than VPT (0.08-0.59% on the five FGVC datasets, Appendix E.7).","identity_at_init":"No. In the released code the token-wise projectors (weight and bias) are zero-initialised and the shared projector is bias-free and residual, so the generated prompts start as zero vectors. A zero prompt token is not inert: the pre-norm ViT block maps it through LayerNorm to the LayerNorm bias, and the key and value projections (with their biases) then give it a non-zero key and value, so it still takes softmax mass from the real tokens. The first block also uses randomly initialised static prompts. The paper describes no zero-output mechanism.","key_idea":"In the attention-as-mixture-of-experts reading, VPT adds prompt experts \\(W_m^{V\\top}p_j\\) that are constant in the input, while the pretrained experts \\(W_m^{V\\top}x_j\\) are linear in it. VAPT makes each prompt a cheap nonlinear function of the current block's tokens (channel-shared convolution, then token pooling, then a shared bottleneck MLP). This uses fewer parameters than VPT because \\(H'W'<d\\), and least-squares prompt estimation reaches the optimal \\(\\mathcal{O}_P([\\log n/n]^{1/2})\\) rate for prompts fitted by a single atom (\\([\\log n/n]^{1/4}\\) when several fitted atoms crowd one true prompt).","results_note":"On ViT-B/16 pretrained on ImageNet-21K, VAPT reaches 72.91% mean VTAB-1K and 89.58% FGVC accuracy, 7.34 and 1.04 points above full fine-tuning, while tuning 0.36% of backbone parameters.","relations":[{"target":"vpt","type":"builds-on","note":"Same VPT-Deep insertion point and frozen backbone. The per-block static prompt matrix \\(P^{(l)}\\) is replaced by prompts generated from that block's own tokens, using fewer parameters (\\(L\\ell H'W'\\) versus \\(L\\ell d\\)). The bias-free generator in the paper cannot output a general constant prompt, so this is a successor rather than a strict generalisation."},{"target":"le-2025-revisiting-prefix","type":"builds-on","note":"Same group and the same attention/prompt-to-MoE correspondence. The Voronoi-loss least-squares analysis of prompt estimation is reused, here for input-dependent prompt experts."},{"target":"prefix-tuning-capabilities-limitations","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited as motivation (Petrov et al.): static prompts can only add a bias to an attention block's output. VAPT's input-dependent prompt experts are meant to escape that limitation."},{"target":"idpg","type":"competes-with","note":"Instance-dependent prompt generation for NLP (not cited). IDPG generates prompts once from the input sentence. VAPT regenerates them at every block from that block's tokens, with a convolution-plus-pooling generator."},{"target":"late-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"LPT generates one instance-dependent prompt from the hidden states of a single middle layer. VAPT generates prompts at every block."},{"target":"l2p","type":"competes-with","note":"Prompt-pool methods (cited as Wang et al., 2022) first forward the image through a frozen pretrained encoder and use its [CLS] feature as the query that selects prompts for the layers. The paper therefore treats their prompts as functions of the first-layer input \\(X^{(0)}\\). VAPT conditions each layer's prompts on that layer's input \\(X^{(l)}\\)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18936","https://arxiv.org/html/2501.18936","https://github.com/Minhchuyentoancbn/VAPT","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Minhchuyentoancbn/VAPT/HEAD/src/models/vit_prompt/vit.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Minhchuyentoancbn/VAPT/HEAD/src/configs/config.py","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Revisit%20Visual%20Prompt%20Tuning&source=forum","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://openreview.net/forum?id=EbjbESm8MD","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Minh Le","Anh Nguyen","Huy Nguyen","Chau Nguyen","Anh Tran","Nhat Ho"],"bibkey":"le2025revisit","bibtex":"@inproceedings{le2025revisit,\n  title = {{Revisit Visual Prompt Tuning: The Expressiveness of Prompt Experts}},\n  author = {Minh Le and Anh Nguyen and Huy Nguyen and Chau Nguyen and Anh Tran and Nhat Ho},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2501.18936},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.18936}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over inputs: VPT-Deep prompts regenerated at every block from that block's own patch tokens, \\(p^{(l)}_j=g\\big(\\sum_k\\alpha^{(l)}_{j,k}(F^{(l)}*X^{(l)}_{img})_k\\big)\\), with a channel-shared kernel, token pooling and one shared bottleneck MLP (residual in the code).","shape":"Function-level: input-dependent extra keys and values at every block.","base_point":"Zero-initialised generators give zero prompt tokens, which pre-LN maps to the LayerNorm bias and then to nonzero keys and values that take softmax mass; block 1 keeps random static prompts. On heads with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) no parameter value is neutral (Thm VI.5(f)): unpointed.","gauge":"\\((\\alpha^{(l)},F^{(l)})\\mapsto(c_l\\alpha^{(l)},F^{(l)}/c_l)\\) per block (pooling and convolution are bilinear): an \\(L\\)-dimensional torus; the shared MLP adds only discrete hidden-unit symmetries.","covariance":"The channel-shared convolution and the pooling commute with every map on the \\(d\\) channels and the bottleneck MLP is dense: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\), as VPT; the spatial grid is the image's own structure. Caveat: the generator's trained per-block LayerNorm (placement not stated) would cut this to the LayerNorm's coordinate symmetries if it acts on the generated prompts; recorded as GLxGL following VPT.","merge":"The prompts depend on the input and lengthen the sequence (Thm VI.5(f)).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"VAPT makes each deep prompt an \\(X\\)-dependent function of the current block's tokens, so in the attention-as-mixture reading the prompt experts become one-hidden-layer functions of the sequence instead of constants. Fibrewise over inputs it is VPT-Deep: every image runs VPT with its own prompts, which is also why no single static prompt reproduces it.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"vpt","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(q)=\\big((P^{(l)}(\\tilde X^{(l)}(x;q)))_{l\\le L},\\ \\mathrm{Head}\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over inputs, into VPT-Deep (prompt outputs discarded per block, as in VAPT), pointed at h_x(q_0) (unpointed slice); block 1's static prompts map to VPT's block-1 prompts."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"l2p","test":"O1","reason":"L2P's prompts are piecewise constant in x (hard top-N retrieval from a pool), while VAPT's vary continuously with the block's tokens, so generic VAPT realisations are not L2P's (as for CODA-Prompt)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"adapad","name":"AdaPaD","full_name":"Adaptive Parallel Deflation","paper_title":"AdaPaD: Adaptive Parallel Deflation for PEFT with Self-Correcting Rank Discovery","authors":"Su et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv preprint","arxiv":"2605.10741","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10741","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_i=s\\sum_{k=1}^{r_i}b_{i,k}a_{i,k}^{\\top},\\ \\ b_{i,k}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}},\\ a_{i,k}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{bilinear model (theory): } Y_{k,\\ell}=Y-\\sum_{k'<k}b_{k',\\ell-1}a_{k',\\ell-1}^{\\top}X,\\ \\ (a_{k,\\ell},b_{k,\\ell})=\\mathrm{Rank1}\\big(Y_{k,\\ell},X,T;\\ \\text{warm start }(a_{k,\\ell-1},b_{k,\\ell-1})\\big)\\approx\\arg\\min_{a,b}\\|Y_{k,\\ell}-ba^{\\top}X\\|_F^2\\ \\ \\text{for all active }k\\le\\ell\\text{ in parallel};\\ \\ \\text{deep net: every }\\Delta\\text{ steps snapshot }\\{\\bar b_{i,k'},\\bar a_{i,k'}\\};\\ \\text{component }k\\text{ trained on }\\mathcal{L}\\big(W_{0,i}+s\\sum_{k'<k}\\bar b_{i,k'}\\bar a_{i,k'}^{\\top}+s\\,b_{i,k}a_{i,k}^{\\top}\\big);\\ \\ \\text{growth: } r_i\\leftarrow r_i+1\\text{ for the top-}h\\text{ modules by }\\bar S_iU_i\\ (r_i<r_{\\max})\\text{ until }\\textstyle\\sum_i r_i\\ge B,\\ \\ S_i=\\|\\theta_i\\|_2^{-1/2}\\sum_{p\\in\\theta_i}|p\\,\\nabla_p\\mathcal{L}|;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: }W_0;\\ \\text{trained: rank-1 pairs (plus one privately warmed reserve per module)}","trainable_params":"r_i(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix, plus one reserve }(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ per module while growing};\\ \\ 1\\le r_i\\le r_{\\max},\\ \\textstyle\\sum_i r_i\\le B\\ \\text{(GLUE, DeBERTaV3-base: }B=144\\text{ over 72 modules, }r_{\\max}=4\\text{, }0.34\\text{M trainable parameters in Table 3, against }0.32\\text{-}0.47\\text{M for the baselines; Qwen3-0.6B SQuAD: 196 modules, }r_{\\max}=8\\text{, average-rank budget }\\bar r\\in\\{4,6\\}\\text{, }1.93\\text{-}2.30\\text{M)}","locus":"all linear layers: \\(W_q,W_k,W_v,W_o,W_{f1},W_{f2}\\) of every DeBERTaV3-base layer (72 modules); the seven attention and MLP projections \\(W_q,W_k,W_v,W_o,W_{gate},W_{up},W_{down}\\) of every Qwen3-0.6B layer (196 modules)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"The deployed object is an ordinary additive update \\(\\Delta W_i=s\\sum_{k\\le r_i}b_{i,k}a_{i,k}^{\\top}\\) whose rank \\(r_i\\) differs per module. What changes is how the rank-1 terms are trained. Component \\(k\\) is fitted to a deflation target that removes only its predecessors, \\(k'<k\\), using their latest estimates. All components are refined in parallel, and each worker's target is refreshed every round. Sequential (Hotelling-style) deflation, of which the paper counts ReLoRA as an instance, freezes earlier errors into every later residual. Here the mismatch \\(\\|Y_{k,\\ell}-Y_k^{\\star}\\|_F\\) vanishes as rounds proceed (self-correction, Proposition 2). Activation is staggered (worker \\(k\\) starts at round \\(k\\)), and before activation a component is trained privately against its current target (advance learning). In the network the target is implicit: component \\(k\\) is trained with the task loss through a forward pass containing the round-start snapshot of components \\(0,\\dots,k-1\\) and itself, with later components absent. Per-module rank grows from 1 with IncreLoRA's importance-times-uncertainty score under a shared budget, so the rank profile is an output. On CoLA it settles at 16/44/8/4 modules with rank 1/2/3/4. The theory covers a single bilinear layer \\(Y=W^{\\star}X+\\mathcal{E}\\) under a spectral-gap assumption. The total error obeys \\(G_{k,\\ell}\\le 3\\mathcal{R}_k(\\ell-s_k+2)m_k^{\\ell-s_k+1}\\), and the weight-recovery bound splits into a vanishing algorithmic term plus a statistical floor. Two practical details depart from the analysed algorithm. The Frobenius-ball projection \\(\\Pi_Q\\) that the theory needs is switched off (\\(Q=\\infty\\)) in all experiments. On GLUE an optional IncreLoRA orthogonality penalty \\(\\lambda_{\\text{orth}}(\\|A^{\\top}A-I\\|_F+\\|BB^{\\top}-I\\|_F)/(2n_{\\text{layers}})\\) is added, with \\(\\lambda_{\\text{orth}}\\) up to 0.5 per task; it is disabled on SQuAD.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training \\(\\Delta W_i=s\\sum_k b_{i,k}a_{i,k}^{\\top}\\) is a fixed matrix of rank at most \\(r_i\\) that folds into \\(W_{0,i}\\), as with LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not exact. Each module starts with one committed component whose \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are both drawn from \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,0.02)\\), so \\(\\Delta W_i=s\\,b_{i,1}a_{i,1}^{\\top}\\) is small but non-zero. Components added later arrive pre-trained by advance learning, not at zero.","key_idea":"Treat a LoRA update as a sum of rank-1 deflation steps, but train all steps at once, each against a target rebuilt from its predecessors' current estimates. Errors in early components are then corrected over rounds instead of being frozen into later residuals. In the single-layer bilinear model, individual rank-1 directions converge nearly linearly (exponential decay with a linear prefactor). On top of this, ranks grow per module under a global budget, so the rank allocation is an output.","results_note":"On GLUE with DeBERTaV3-base, AdaPaD with 0.34M trainable parameters (baselines 0.32-0.47M) has the highest average (89.34), ahead of AdaLoRA (89.03) and a reproduced IncreLoRA (88.99). On Qwen3-0.6B SQuAD/SQuAD v2 it matches fixed-rank LoRA F1 with adapters 15.9-44.3% (mean 30.7%) smaller.","relations":[{"target":"increlora","type":"builds-on","note":"Borrows IncreLoRA's importance-times-uncertainty score and reserve components (turned into advance learning) and likewise grows ranks from 1 under a shared budget. Here each new component is trained against a self-correcting deflation target."},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"GLUE baseline. AdaLoRA prunes from a high initial rank using SVD-triplet importance; AdaPaD grows from rank 1."},{"target":"sora","type":"competes-with","note":"GLUE baseline. SoRA learns sparse gates on rank-1 components; AdaPaD trains rank-1 components as parallel deflation steps."},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"The paper calls ReLoRA's sequence of merged low-rank updates sequential deflation, whose early errors persist. AdaPaD instead trains all components in parallel with refreshed targets."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"The deployed adapter is a standard LoRA update with per-module ranks. AdaPaD changes how its rank-1 terms are trained and how many each module gets."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10741","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.10741","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2605.10741?fields=title,year,venue,citationCount","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=AdaPaD%20Adaptive%20Parallel%20Deflation&source=forum","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Barbara Su","Fangshuo Liao","Anastasios Kyrillidis"],"bibkey":"su2026adapad","bibtex":"@article{su2026adapad,\n  title = {{AdaPaD: Adaptive Parallel Deflation for PEFT with Self-Correcting Rank Discovery}},\n  author = {Barbara Su and Fangshuo Liao and Anastasios Kyrillidis},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2605.10741},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.10741},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10741}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA with per-module ranks \\(r_i\\) grown from 1 under a global budget; the rank-1 terms are trained as parallel deflation steps, each against a target rebuilt from its predecessors' current snapshot.","shape":"Per module \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_i}\\), a filtration of cones whose level is an output of training (as IncreLoRA).","base_point":"Each module starts with one component whose \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are both drawn from \\(\\mathcal N(0,0.02)\\): a small nonzero rank-1 start, a defect; later components arrive pre-trained (advance learning).","gauge":"\\(GL_{r_i}\\) on \\(\\rho\\); the deflation targets order the components, so training respects only the per-component torus.","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cones (the optional orthogonality penalty is only \\(O\\)-equivariant).","merge":"\\(s\\sum_kb_{i,k}a_{i,k}^\\top\\) fuses.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\) at the deployed ranks."},"categorical_reading":"AdaPaD keeps LoRA's object and changes how its rank-one terms are fitted: all at once, each against a deflation target refreshed every round, so early errors are corrected instead of being frozen into later residuals as in sequential deflation (which the paper identifies with ReLoRA). The rank profile is an output, so the deployed object is a point of the rank filtration rather than a fixed level.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\,b_{i,1}\\ \\cdots\\ b_{i,r_i}\\,],\\ [\\,a_{i,1}^\\top;\\dots;a_{i,r_i}^\\top\\,]\\big)","note":"Per module, into LoRA_{r_i} (scale absorbed), re-pointed at h(q_0): the start is a nonzero rank-1 point, so this is an arrow of the unpointed slice (weak)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"AdaPaD starts at a nonzero rank-1 point (rho(q_0) != theta_0, S_1 = {x a_1^T + b_1 y^T} of dimension m + n - 1 per module), AdaLoRA at Lambda = 0 with dim S_1 = r: no pointed arrow."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-da","name":"LoRA-DA","full_name":"LoRA-DA: Data-Aware initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"LoRA-DA: Data-Aware Initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation via Asymptotic Analysis","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2510.24561","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24561","code_url":"https://github.com/zqy0126/LoRA-DA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=(W_0+BA)x,\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ AA^{\\top}=I_r;\\ \\ \\text{on }|\\mathcal{S}|=256\\text{ target samples: } G=\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0),\\ Z_F=\\mathbb{E}[xx^{\\top}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}},\\ Y_F=\\mathbb{E}[\\nabla_h\\ell\\,\\nabla_h\\ell^{\\top}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{out}}\\ \\text{(K-FAC)};\\ \\ \\widehat{\\Delta}=-D\\,G\\,Z_F^{-1},\\ D=\\mathrm{Diag}\\big([Y_F^{-1}]_{11},\\dots,[Y_F^{-1}]_{d_{out}d_{out}}\\big)\\ \\ (\\text{Fisher-gradient estimate of }W_{\\text{tgt}}-W_0);\\ \\ \\Omega=\\frac{\\mathrm{tr}(Y_F^{-1})}{N}Z_F^{-1}-\\widehat{\\Delta}^{\\top}\\widehat{\\Delta}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ A_0=\\arg\\min_{AA^{\\top}=I_r}\\mathrm{tr}(A\\,\\Omega\\,A^{\\top})\\ (\\text{the }r\\text{ smallest-eigenvalue eigenvectors of }\\Omega\\text{, via LOBPCG}),\\ \\ B_0=\\widehat{\\Delta}A_0^{\\top}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ B_0A_0=\\widehat{\\Delta}A_0^{\\top}A_0;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: }W_0;\\ \\text{trained: }A,B\\ (\\text{or }B\\text{ only in the LoRA-FA setting})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix }(rd_{out}\\text{ with frozen }A)","locus":"any linear layer; paper: Q, K, V, O, Gate, Up, Down of LLaMA-2-7B and Q, K, V of LLaMA-2-13B (\\(r=8\\), \\(\\alpha=16\\))","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Plain LoRA with a closed-form, data-dependent starting point. The derivation is done in the frozen-\\(A\\) (LoRA-FA) setting with \\(A\\) row-orthonormal. It minimises \\(\\mathbb{E}\\|\\hat W-W_{\\text{tgt}}\\|_F^2\\) for the constrained MLE under \\(\\|W_{\\text{tgt}}-W_0\\|_F=O(N^{-1/2})\\). The bound reduces to the quadratic \\(\\mathrm{tr}(A\\Omega A^{\\top})\\), whose Initialization Guidance Matrix \\(\\Omega\\) has two parts. The variance part is the diagonal \\(d_{in}\\times d_{in}\\) blocks of the inverse Fisher of \\(\\mathrm{vec}(W)\\), summed over outputs, divided by \\(N\\). Under K-FAC this is \\(\\tfrac{1}{N}\\mathrm{tr}(Y_F^{-1})Z_F^{-1}\\). The bias part is \\(-\\widehat{\\Delta}^{\\top}\\widehat{\\Delta}\\), with the displacement estimated by a block-diagonal natural-gradient step \\(\\widehat{\\Delta}=-DGZ_F^{-1}\\). This is not the full K-FAC step \\(-Y_F^{-1}GZ_F^{-1}\\), since only the diagonal of \\(Y_F^{-1}\\) is kept. \\(A_0\\) takes the \\(r\\) bottom eigenvectors of \\(\\Omega\\), and \\(B_0=\\widehat{\\Delta}A_0^{\\top}\\) makes \\(W_0+B_0A_0\\) the projection of the estimated target onto that row space. Two limits are informative. Dropping the variance term and using the raw gradient (\\(\\widehat{\\Delta}\\to-G\\)) gives \\(A_0=V_r^{\\top}\\) and \\(B_0A_0=-U_rS_rV_r^{\\top}\\), the LoRA-One/LoRA-GA gradient-SVD choice (Remark 4.3). Keeping only the variance term gives the top eigenvectors of \\(Z_F\\), the principal input-activation directions an EVA-type init uses (our observation, not in the paper). The released code adds three implementation details: \\(\\widehat{\\Delta}\\) is normalised by its top singular value, the variance term is down-weighted (\\(\\beta=0.03\\)), and both factors are scaled by \\(1/\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\) with \\(\\gamma=1024\\). The released config also enables rsLoRA scaling (\\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\)), which the paper's hyperparameter table does not mention.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the starting point changes. The trained \\(BA\\) is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) delta that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No. \\(B_0A_0=\\widehat{\\Delta}A_0^{\\top}A_0\\neq 0\\), so training starts at a projected Fisher-gradient step from \\(W_0\\) (in the paper's derivation, the projection of the estimated target). Like LoRA-One, the official code does not subtract \\(B_0A_0\\) from \\(W_0\\), although it does for other init modes. With the \\(1/\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\) factor scaling the initial offset is small.","key_idea":"Choose the LoRA subspace to minimise the expected distance between the fine-tuned and target weights. That distance splits into a bias term, the target's distance from the subspace estimated by a Fisher-preconditioned gradient, and a variance term from sampling noise through the inverse Fisher. Both enter one symmetric matrix \\(\\Omega\\), and \\(A_0\\) is its bottom-\\(r\\) eigenspace. Gradient-SVD inits such as LoRA-One are recovered when the variance term is dropped and the Fisher is ignored.","results_note":"With LLaMA-2-7B (\\(r=8\\)), LoRA-DA averages 84.3% on eight commonsense benchmarks against 84.0% for MiLoRA, the best baseline. After MetaMathQA-100K it reaches 55.0% GSM8K and 9.2% MATH, 1.0 point above LoRA-One on average.","relations":[{"target":"lora-one","type":"generalizes","note":"Remark 4.3: dropping the variance term and replacing the Fisher-gradient by the raw gradient makes \\(A_0\\) the top-\\(r\\) right singular vectors of \\(G\\) (left singular vectors in the paper's \\(Y=ZW\\) convention), with \\(B_0A_0=-U_rS_rV_r^{\\top}\\). That is LoRA-One's spectral initialisation up to factor balancing and scale (initialisation only; LoRA-One's preconditioned training is not part of LoRA-DA). Like LoRA-One, the released code does not offset \\(W_0\\)."},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"builds-on","note":"Gradient-based data-aware init that LoRA-DA criticises for being single-step and ignoring anisotropy. Remark 4.3 says the degenerate case 'coincides' with LoRA-GA, but only the \\(A\\) side does: LoRA-GA takes \\(B\\) from the next \\(r\\) left singular vectors (\\(r+1\\) to \\(2r\\)) and offsets \\(W_0\\)."},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"builds-on","note":"The optimality theory is derived for frozen \\(A\\) (LoRA-FA); the same \\(A_0\\) and \\(B_0\\) are then used for standard LoRA, and a frozen-\\(A\\) experiment is reported."},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"Data-agnostic baseline built from the top singular directions of \\(W_0\\)."},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"Data-agnostic baseline built from the minor singular directions of \\(W_0\\); the strongest commonsense baseline (84.0 vs 84.3)."},{"target":"eva","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. In the variance-only limit \\(\\Omega\\) is proportional to \\(Z_F^{-1}\\), whose bottom eigenvectors are the top principal directions of the (uncentred) layer inputs. These are the directions EVA uses for \\(A\\), up to centring, so LoRA-DA interpolates between an activation-variance init and a gradient-SVD init (our observation, not in the paper)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24561","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.24561","https://github.com/zqy0126/LoRA-DA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zqy0126/LoRA-DA/HEAD/lora_da.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zqy0126/LoRA-DA/HEAD/run_exp.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zqy0126/LoRA-DA/HEAD/conf/lora_da.yaml","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=LoRA-DA%20Data-Aware&source=forum","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://openreview.net/forum?id=LvKSVUbXc9","https://openreview.net/forum?id=4nNqVpC3td","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Qingyue Zhang","Chang Chu","Tianren Peng","Qi Li","Xiangyang Luo","Zhihao Jiang","Shao-Lun Huang"],"bibkey":"zhang2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-DA: Data-Aware Initialization for Low-Rank Adaptation via Asymptotic Analysis}},\n  author = {Qingyue Zhang and Chang Chu and Tianren Peng and Qi Li and Xiangyang Luo and Zhihao Jiang and Shao-Lun Huang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2510.24561},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.24561}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA with a closed-form data-aware start: \\(A_0\\) the bottom-\\(r\\) eigenvectors of \\(\\Omega=\\tfrac{\\mathrm{tr}(Y_F^{-1})}{N}Z_F^{-1}-\\widehat\\Delta^\\top\\widehat\\Delta\\) and \\(B_0=\\widehat\\Delta A_0^\\top\\), with \\(\\widehat\\Delta=-DGZ_F^{-1}\\), \\(D=\\mathrm{Diag}(Y_F^{-1})\\) (K-FAC factors).","shape":"LoRA's cone at \\(W_0\\): the released code adds \\(B_0A_0\\) without subtracting it.","base_point":"\\(\\rho(q_0)=W_0+B_0A_0\\neq W_0\\) (small after the \\(1/\\sqrt\\gamma\\) scaling): a defect, as LoRA-One.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"The image is LoRA's basis-free cone at \\(W_0\\). The start alone is covariant only under \\(\\mathrm{Mon}(d_{out})\\times CO(d_{in})\\): \\(D\\) keeps only the diagonal of \\(Y_F^{-1}\\), and the orthonormal-row eigenproblem turns the congruence \\(\\Omega\\mapsto h^{-\\top}\\Omega h^{-1}\\) into \\(CO\\).","merge":"A plain rank-\\(r\\) delta.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-DA chooses LoRA's starting subspace by minimising an expected distance to the target weight, which splits into a Fisher-preconditioned bias term and an inverse-Fisher variance term. Its two limits are known pointings: the bias-only, raw-gradient limit is LoRA-One's gradient-SVD start, and the variance-only limit takes \\(\\mathrm{row}A_0\\) from the top input-activation directions, an EVA-type frame. Without the residual subtraction it starts off \\(\\theta_0\\), as LoRA-One does.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Same image, but rho(q_0) != theta_0: h = id is only a weak morphism, and the start is a rank-r smooth point rather than the apex (as LoRA-One)."},{"target":"lora-ga","test":"O1","reason":"LoRA-GA subtracts its initial adapter (vertex W_0 - P_GA); LoRA-DA does not (vertex W_0). The two cones are incomparable, as in Thm II.2(d)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"wasi","name":"WASI","full_name":"Weight-Activation Subspace Iteration","paper_title":"Efficient Resource-Constrained Training of Transformers via Subspace Optimization","authors":"Nguyen et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 (oral)","arxiv":"2510.09160","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09160","code_url":"https://github.com/Le-TrungNguyen/ICLR2026-WASI","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{start: } W_0=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ K_i=\\min\\Big\\{K:\\tfrac{\\sum_{j\\le K}s_j^2}{\\sum_j s_j^2}\\ge\\varepsilon\\Big\\},\\ L=U_{K_i}\\Sigma_{K_i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times K_i},\\ R=V_{K_i}^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K_i\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{forward: } h=L(Rx);\\ \\ \\text{saved activation: } \\tilde{\\mathcal{A}}=\\tilde{\\mathcal{S}}\\times_1\\tilde U^{(1)}\\times_2\\tilde U^{(2)}\\times_3\\tilde U^{(3)}\\ (\\text{Tucker over batch, token, channel modes; ranks }\\mathbf{r}_i\\text{ chosen once by a perplexity-vs-memory search});\\ \\ \\text{backward: } \\widetilde{\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}}=f_{\\text{LR}}(\\tilde{\\mathcal{A}},\\nabla_h\\mathcal{L})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}},\\ \\ \\nabla_x\\mathcal{L}=R^{\\top}L^{\\top}\\nabla_h\\mathcal{L};\\ \\ \\text{update: } W\\leftarrow LR-\\eta\\,\\widetilde{\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}},\\ \\ R^{\\top}\\leftarrow W^{\\top}L_{\\text{prev}},\\ \\ L\\leftarrow\\mathrm{orth}(WR^{\\top})\\ (\\text{one warm-started subspace-iteration step, rank }K_i\\text{ fixed});\\ \\ \\text{activation factors }\\tilde U^{(m)}\\text{ also refreshed by warm-started subspace iteration};\\ \\ \\text{trained: all weights (and biases) of the selected layers; nothing added}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ (+d_{out}\\text{ bias})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix: the whole weight is updated with a dense gradient assembled from the compressed activation, but held and run as rank-}K_i\\text{ factors with }K_i(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ entries; no added parameters}","locus":"linear layers of the MLP blocks of ViT and Swin-T, both ImageNet-1K pretrained (the paper does not name the ViT size); all attention and MLP linear layers of ViT in an appendix ablation (Tab. 1); the last 1-5 decoder layers of TinyLlama (\\(\\varepsilon=0.1\\)); the last 1-4 convolutions of MCUNet for the weight part (WSI) only","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Compression-aware fine-tuning without an adapter. Each fine-tuned weight is replaced by its rank-\\(K_i\\) truncation, with \\(K_i\\) set once by an explained-variance threshold \\(\\varepsilon\\) on \\(W_0\\)'s spectrum. Thereafter the factored weight \\(LR\\) is tracked across steps by one power/subspace-iteration step warm-started from the previous \\(L\\), not by fresh SVDs. This relies on the observed stability of layer ranks during fine-tuning (Fig. 3a). Forward and input-gradient passes cost \\(O(K_i(d_{in}+d_{out}))\\) per token. The weight gradient is formed from a Tucker/HOSVD compression of the stored input activation, whose mode factors are also warm-started by subspace iteration (the ASI method, here with a dynamic-programming rank search and 3-D/4-D activation support). The update is therefore a full-matrix step in weight space followed by re-projection onto a rank-\\(K_i\\) manifold, close to training a low-rank model in factored form. It is not a frozen-\\(W_0\\) plus \\(\\Delta W\\) method. In the released code the dense weight remains the optimised parameter and \\(L,R\\) are recomputed from it at each forward pass; the paper's Eq. (11) writes the update directly on \\(LR\\). The method targets on-device transfer learning, where activation and weight memory dominate. Two further code-versus-paper details: the code starts from \\(L=U_{K_i}\\) (orthonormal) and \\(R=\\Sigma_{K_i}V_{K_i}^{\\top}\\), the reverse of Eq. (7), so \\(L\\) stays orthonormal as in later iterations. Eq. (11) is printed with \\(+\\eta\\), whereas the code applies an ordinary descent step to the dense weight.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"There is no side branch to merge: the trained model is the factored weight \\(L_iR_i\\) per layer. It can be multiplied out to a dense matrix or kept factored, which is cheaper at inference (about 1.4x faster than vanilla on a Raspberry Pi 5).","identity_at_init":"No. From the first step the forward pass uses the truncation \\(U_{K_i}\\Sigma_{K_i}V_{K_i}^{\\top}\\) of \\(W_0\\), not \\(W_0\\), so the starting function differs unless \\(\\varepsilon=1\\), which recovers vanilla training. Activation compression also makes the first gradients approximate.","key_idea":"On edge devices the memory of backpropagation, not the trainable-parameter count, is the bottleneck. WASI keeps both the weights and the saved activations in fixed low-rank subspaces and follows those subspaces with warm-started subspace iteration instead of repeated SVDs. Training memory falls by up to 62x and FLOPs by about 1.5-2x, while the low-rank weights also speed up inference.","results_note":"Fine-tuning Swin-T at \\(\\varepsilon=0.9\\), WASI matches vanilla accuracy while cutting training memory by up to 62x and FLOPs by 1.5x; on a Raspberry Pi 5 it is about 1.4x faster than vanilla training for ViT on CIFAR-10.","relations":[{"target":"tinytl","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Both target activation-dominated on-device training memory. TinyTL freezes weights and trains biases plus lite residual branches; WASI updates the weights inside low-rank weight and activation subspaces."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. GaLore keeps full weights and projects gradients onto a periodically refreshed SVD subspace for optimiser memory. WASI factorises the weights themselves to a fixed rank, tracks them by power iteration, and compresses the saved activations."},{"target":"velora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Both compress the activations saved for the weight gradient: VeLoRA projects sub-tokens to rank 1, WASI uses a warm-started Tucker/HOSVD."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"WASI argues that LoRA keeps the frozen dense weights and full activations in memory and loses the low-rank speed-up once merged. WASI trains and deploys a factored model instead (compared against SVD-LLM, which pairs truncated SVD with a LoRA adapter)."},{"target":"intrinsic-dimensionality","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited low-intrinsic-dimension evidence (Aghajanyan et al.) motivating training inside a stable low-dimensional subspace."},{"target":"intrinsic-dimension-objective-landscapes","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited (Li et al., 2018) for the claim that over-parameterised models reside in low-dimensional subspaces."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09160","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09160","https://github.com/Le-TrungNguyen/ICLR2026-WASI","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Le-TrungNguyen/ICLR2026-WASI/HEAD/main/custom_op/linear/linear_WASI.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Le-TrungNguyen/ICLR2026-WASI/HEAD/main/custom_op/compression/rank/power_iteration.py","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Weight-Activation%20Subspace%20Iteration&source=forum","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://openreview.net/forum?id=0nvQ5kHXf4","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Le-Trung Nguyen","Enzo Tartaglione","Van-Tam Nguyen"],"bibkey":"nguyen2025efficient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{nguyen2025efficient,\n  title = {{Efficient Resource-Constrained Training of Transformers via Subspace Optimization}},\n  author = {Le-Trung Nguyen and Enzo Tartaglione and Van-Tam Nguyen},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2510.09160},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09160}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each selected weight is replaced by its rank-\\(K_i\\) truncation and trained as a factored low-rank matrix: a dense step from a Tucker-compressed activation gradient, re-projected by one warm-started subspace iteration. No adapter, and \\(W_0\\) is not kept.","shape":"\\(\\mathcal M_{\\le K_i}\\), a cone with vertex at the zero matrix, started at \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_{K_i}(W_0)\\), a smooth point: a translated cone with apex \\(0\\neq\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"The forward pass uses the truncation from step 0, so \\(\\rho(q_0)-W_0\\) is the truncation error (zero only at \\(\\varepsilon=1\\)): a defect, like LoftQ's attained residual.","gauge":"Factors \\((L,R)\\) with \\(L\\) re-orthonormalised each step: residual \\(O(K_i)\\), nearest key GL. The released code keeps the dense \\(W\\) as the parameter and recomputes \\(L,R\\) from optimizer-carried state.","covariance":"\\(K_i\\) comes from an explained-variance threshold on \\(W_0\\)'s spectrum and the start is its truncated SVD: \\(CO\\times CO\\) (OxO). The set \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le K}\\) itself is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-invariant.","merge":"The trained model is the factored \\(L_iR_i\\); multiply out or keep factored.","rebasing":"\\(\\mathcal M_{\\le K}\\) does not depend on the base and re-truncation cannot raise \\(K\\): idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"WASI is a low-rank reparametrisation of the whole box rather than an adapter: the image is the rank-\\(K\\) variety itself, whose vertex is the zero matrix, so \\(\\theta_0\\) lies outside the image and the start is its nearest point. Training is a projected-gradient (retraction) scheme on \\(\\mathcal M_K\\) with the gradient assembled from compressed activations, which is where the memory saving comes from.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"PiSSA keeps the residual W_0 - [W_0]_r (vertex W_0 - [W_0]_r); WASI drops it (vertex 0). Translated cones with distinct vertices are not nested."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Im WASI = M_{<=K}; a generic X of rank K has X - W_0 of rank min(m,n) > r (checked), so X is not in W_0 + M_{<=r} = Im LoRA and Im WASI is not inside Im LoRA. WASI's start is also not theta_0 (truncation defect), so no pointed arrow either."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"tera","name":"TeRA","full_name":"Vector-based random Tensor network for high-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"TeRA: Vector-based Random Tensor Network for High-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Gu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ACL 2026 (long)","arxiv":"2509.03234","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03234","code_url":"https://github.com/guyuxuan9/TeRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta\\mathcal{W}(i_1,\\dots,i_N)=\\sum_{r_1=1}^{R_1}\\cdots\\sum_{r_N=1}^{R_N}\\mathcal{G}(r_1,\\dots,r_N)\\prod_{n=1}^{N}d^{(n)}_{r_n}A^{(n)}(r_n,i_n),\\ \\ \\Delta W=\\mathrm{Unfold}_{[N;k]}(\\Delta\\mathcal{W})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}},\\ \\prod_{n\\le k}I_n=d_{out},\\ \\prod_{n>k}I_n=d_{in};\\ \\ \\text{equivalently } \\Delta W=L\\big(\\mathcal{G}_{[N;k]}\\odot uv^{\\top}\\big)M,\\ L=\\big(\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{n\\le k}A^{(n)}\\big)^{\\top},\\ M=\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{n>k}A^{(n)},\\ u=\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{n\\le k}d^{(n)},\\ v=\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{n>k}d^{(n)};\\ \\ h=(W_0+\\Delta W)x;\\ \\ \\text{frozen and shared by all adapted layers: random core }\\mathcal{G}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{R_1\\times\\cdots\\times R_N}\\text{ and factors }A^{(n)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{R_n\\times I_n};\\ \\text{trained per layer: }d^{(n)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{R_n},\\ n=1,\\dots,N","trainable_params":"\\sum_{n=1}^{N}R_n\\ \\text{per adapted matrix; paper default }R_n=I_n\\text{ with only one side split, e.g. a }4096\\times4096\\text{ matrix folded to }4096\\times8\\times8\\times8\\times8\\text{ gives }4096+4\\cdot8=4128\\ (\\approx0.0039\\%\\text{ of Llama-2-7B with }W_q,W_v\\text{ adapted})","locus":"\\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) of every attention layer (paper: Llama-2-7B, Llama-3-8B); any linear layer in principle, with one core and factor set sliced to each layer's shape","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"The update matrix is folded into an order-\\(N\\) tensor and written in Tucker form. The core and factor matrices are large, randomly initialised (the factors with Kaiming init, Appendix F), frozen and shared by every adapted layer. The trainable part is a set of \\(N\\) per-layer vectors \\(d^{(n)}\\) acting as diagonal matrices on the core's modes. After unfolding, \\(\\Delta W=L\\,\\mathrm{diag}(u)\\,\\mathcal{G}_{[N;k]}\\,\\mathrm{diag}(v)\\,M\\), with \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) Kronecker products of the trained vectors. This is a VeRA-type double diagonal scaling of a frozen random matrix, except that the diagonals are rank-1 tensors and the outer projections are Kronecker-structured. The rank bound is \\(\\mathrm{rank}(\\Delta W)\\le\\min(\\prod_{n\\le k}R_n,\\prod_{n>k}R_n)\\), so \\(R_n=I_n\\) permits full rank with only \\(\\sum_n I_n\\le d_{in}+d_{out}\\) parameters (Theorems 1-2). Measured updates are near full rank in every layer. Theorem 3 bounds the approximation error by the energy of \\(W^{\\star}\\) outside \\(\\mathrm{span}(L)\\otimes\\mathrm{span}(M^{\\top})\\) plus a tensor-spectral-norm term that grows with the tensor order \\(N\\). By our derivation (the paper does not state this reduction), VeRA is the case \\(N=2\\) with \\(A^{(1)}=I\\), core equal to VeRA's \\(B\\), \\(R_2=r\\) and \\(A^{(2)}\\) equal to VeRA's \\(A\\), which gives \\(\\mathrm{diag}(d^{(1)})B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(d^{(2)})A\\). In ablations, replacing the random factors by identities (\\(\\text{TeRA}_{iden}\\)) or using unshared per-layer cores both hurt accuracy.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training, \\(\\Delta W=\\mathrm{Unfold}_{[N;k]}(\\Delta\\mathcal{W})\\) is materialised once and added to \\(W_0\\) (\\(W_{\\text{final}}=W_0+\\Delta W\\)), so inference has no overhead. Training is slower because \\(\\Delta W\\) is contracted from the core at every step.","identity_at_init":"Exact. All \\(d^{(n)}\\) are initialised as ones (0.1 in the released code) except one, which is zero, so \\(\\Delta W=0\\).","key_idea":"Decouple rank from parameter count by putting the trainable parameters on the diagonal of a shared random Tucker tensor network. The frozen core and factors supply the high-rank directions, and each layer learns only \\(\\sum_n R_n\\) mode-wise scalings. The result is near-full-rank updates at VeRA-level parameter counts.","results_note":"On Commonsense170k with \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) adapted, TeRA averages 85.31% on Llama-3-8B (0.0033% trainable). That ties HiRA (\\(r=32\\), 0.1695%) with about 51x fewer trainable parameters and beats the parameter-matched LoRETTA (83.58%).","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"generalizes","note":"VeRA's \\(\\mathrm{diag}(b)B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(d)A\\) is the \\(N=2\\) instance with an identity first factor and the core set to VeRA's \\(B\\) (our derivation; the paper only compares parameter counts with VeRA). TeRA tensorises the update so the shared frozen randomness can reach full rank with a similar number of trained scalars."},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"Main high-rank baseline. TeRA matches HiRA \\(r=32\\) with 51-64x fewer parameters and beats HiRA \\(r=1\\)."},{"target":"loretta","type":"competes-with","note":"Tensor-train adapter with trained cores, the strongest parameter-matched baseline. TeRA trains only diagonal scalings of a frozen shared network."},{"target":"lotr","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited tensor-based adapter. LoTR trains shared bases and a per-layer \\(r\\times r\\) core; TeRA freezes the shared core and factors and trains only per-layer mode-wise diagonals."},{"target":"randlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. RandLoRA also reaches full rank from frozen random bases with learned diagonal scalings, but sums many low-rank terms and so uses far more trainable parameters."},{"target":"quanta","type":"competes-with","note":"Cited quantum-inspired high-rank baseline. TeRA edges it out at a fraction of its parameter count (Llama-3-8B commonsense: 85.31 vs 85.27 at 0.0033% vs 0.0343%; Llama-2-7B: 78.63 vs 77.53)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03234","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.03234","https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.106/","https://aclanthology.org/volumes/2026.acl-long/","https://github.com/guyuxuan9/TeRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/guyuxuan9/TeRA/HEAD/peft_local/tuners/tera/layer.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Yuxuan Gu","Wuyang Zhou","Giorgos Iacovides","Danilo Mandic"],"bibkey":"gu2025tera","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gu2025tera,\n  title = {{TeRA: Vector-based Random Tensor Network for High-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yuxuan Gu and Wuyang Zhou and Giorgos Iacovides and Danilo Mandic},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2509.03234},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03234}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Additive \\(W_0+L\\,\\mathrm{diag}(u)\\,\\mathcal G_{[N;k]}\\,\\mathrm{diag}(v)\\,M\\), with \\(u,v\\) Kronecker products of the trained mode vectors \\(d^{(n)}\\); the random core \\(\\mathcal G\\) and factors \\(A^{(n)}\\) are frozen and shared by all layers.","shape":"Multilinear in \\((d^{(1)},\\dots,d^{(N)})\\): a toric (Segre-type) cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), of dimension \\(\\sum_nR_n-(N-1)\\) (checked: 8 of 10 at \\(N=3\\)), rank up to \\(\\min(\\prod_{n\\le k}R_n,\\prod_{n>k}R_n)\\).","base_point":"All mode vectors start at ones except one at zero: only that vector moves to first order (zero gate, Lemma II.4; checked \\(\\dim S_1=R_j\\)), an apex, as VeRA.","gauge":"\\(d^{(n)}\\mapsto c_nd^{(n)}\\) with \\(\\prod_nc_n=1\\): an \\((N-1)\\)-torus (VeRA's \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) when \\(N=2\\)).","covariance":"Frozen random factors (in law) composed with an imposed tensor reshape, Kronecker-structured on the split side: \\(\\Pi\\), as TT adapters.","merge":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is materialised once and added.","rebasing":"With the core and factors frozen and \\(\\mathcal G\\) zero-free, the span is the whole box at \\(R_n=I_n\\) (checked: 24 of 24): \\(\\to\\Theta\\), against VeRA's \\(\\to\\) LoRA-FA\\((A)\\)."},"categorical_reading":"TeRA tensorises VeRA: the two diagonal scalings become Kronecker products of mode vectors acting on a frozen, shared random Tucker network, so the number of trained scalars stays near \\(d_{in}+d_{out}\\) while the rank ceiling rises to full. The cone is still toric and the start still an apex; what changes is the frozen frame, which now carries a tensor structure the network does not have.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"vera","rule":"A3","h":"h(d^{(1)},d^{(2)})=\\big(b=d^{(1)},\\ d=d^{(2)}\\big)","note":"Instance N = 2, A^{(1)} = I, core = VeRA's B, A^{(2)} = VeRA's A: an isomorphism (our derivation; the paper only compares parameter counts). Pointed when the zeroed vector is d^{(1)} and d^{(2)} starts at VeRA's d_0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Default instance (R_n = I_n): updates reach full rank (measured near full rank in every layer), above LoRA_r's maximum rank r."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"hybrid-lora","name":"Hybrid-LoRA","full_name":"Hybrid-LoRA: score-based split of modules into full fine-tuning and LoRA","paper_title":"Hybrid-LoRA: Bridging Full Fine-Tuning and Low-Rank Adaptation for Post-Training","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv preprint","arxiv":"2605.18822","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18822","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{probe (500 warm-up steps, all }(l,m)\\in\\mathcal{U}=[L]\\times\\{W_q,W_k,W_v,W_o,W_{gate},W_{up},W_{down}\\}\\text{): } h=W_{0}x+b+\\alpha_{l,m}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(e_{l,m})A\\,x;\\ \\ s^{(t)}_{l,m}=\\tfrac{1}{r}\\big\\|e_{l,m}\\odot\\nabla_{e_{l,m}}[\\mathcal{L}_{val}(\\mathcal{B}_t;z^{(t)}_{-A})+\\mathcal{L}_{val}(\\mathcal{B}_t;z^{(t)}_{-B})]\\big\\|_1\\ (\\text{random complementary bipartition }\\mathcal{U}=\\mathcal{U}_A^{(t)}\\cup\\mathcal{U}_B^{(t)});\\ \\ H(l,m)=\\mu_{l,m}\\,\\sigma_{l,m}\\text{ over batches};\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}_{fft}=\\text{lowest-}H\\text{ modules, added greedily while }\\textstyle\\sum_{\\mathcal{S}_{fft}}P_{l,m}\\le R_{fft}\\sum_{\\mathcal{U}}P_{l,m};\\ \\ \\text{final (re-initialised from }W_0\\text{): } W_{l,m}=W_{0,l,m}+\\Delta W_{l,m}\\ (\\text{dense, trained})\\ \\text{for }(l,m)\\in\\mathcal{S}_{fft},\\ \\ W_{l,m}=W_{0,l,m}+BA\\ (r=16)\\ \\text{for }(l,m)\\in\\mathcal{U}\\setminus\\mathcal{S}_{fft};\\ \\ \\text{optimised with GRPO or GSPO}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{(l,m)\\in\\mathcal{S}_{fft}}d_{out}d_{in}+\\sum_{(l,m)\\in\\mathcal{S}_{lora}}r(d_{in}+d_{out}),\\ r=16,\\ R_{fft}=10\\%\\ (\\approx105\\text{M of }1{,}544\\text{M on Qwen2.5-1.5B},\\ 449\\text{M on Qwen2.5-7B})","locus":"the seven attention and MLP linear projections of every decoder layer of Qwen2.5-1.5B/3B/7B-Instruct; the fully fine-tuned modules concentrate in the attention projections of later layers","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"The trainable locus is a union. A small set \\(\\mathcal{S}_{fft}\\) of whole matrices receives dense updates, and every other candidate matrix receives a rank-\\(r\\) LoRA update. The partition is chosen once, before the real run. A probe attaches AdaLoRA-style SVD-form LoRA (\\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(e)A\\), scaled by a per-module architecture weight \\(\\alpha_{l,m}\\)) to all modules and trains it briefly. Each module is then scored by the first-order sensitivity \\(\\tfrac1r\\|e\\odot\\nabla_e\\mathcal{L}_{val}\\|_1\\) of its LoRA coefficients. The score is measured under random complementary on/off masks of the other modules (Shapley-NAS style; \\(K=20\\) random partitions in the experiments) and aggregated as mean times standard deviation over batches. A low score is read as LoRA being insufficient for that module, so the lowest-scoring modules are fully fine-tuned up to the budget. Eq. (4) states the budget as a parameter fraction, while the experiments call it a 10% module ratio. The model is then re-initialised and trained with RLVR using separate learning rates (\\(10^{-6}\\) for dense modules, \\(2.5\\times10^{-5}\\) for LoRA). Unlike rank-reallocation methods (AdaLoRA, AutoLoRA, SoRA), which stay inside the low-rank space, it upgrades a few whole modules to dense full-rank updates.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The dense modules are simply updated in place, and each \\(BA\\) folds into its \\(W_0\\). The result is a single dense model of the original shape with no inference overhead.","identity_at_init":"For the final stage, fully fine-tuned modules start exactly at \\(W_0\\). The LoRA modules presumably use standard \\(B=0\\) initialisation (not stated), giving \\(h=W_0x\\) at the start of RL. The probe's adapters are discarded.","key_idea":"Modules differ in how well a low-rank update can adapt them. Hybrid-LoRA measures this with a cheap LoRA probe and spends a small full-fine-tuning budget on the modules where LoRA's coefficients show the weakest sensitivity, keeping LoRA elsewhere. The aim is to close the LoRA-to-full-fine-tuning gap in RLVR post-training while tuning roughly 6-7% of the parameters (105M of 1,544M, 6.8%, at 1.5B; 449M, about 6%, at 7B).","results_note":"On Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct with GRPO, Hybrid-LoRA (105M tunable of 1,544M) reaches 80.2 Math-500 pass@1, against 80.6 for full fine-tuning and 77.4 for the best module-selection baseline (AutoLoRA criterion).","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"The probe reuses AdaLoRA's SVD-form LoRA (\\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(e)A\\)), and the mean-times-variability score echoes AdaLoRA's sensitivity-times-uncertainty importance. Here it selects whole modules for full fine-tuning rather than pruning ranks. An AdaLoRA-rank criterion is also a baseline."},{"target":"autolora","type":"competes-with","note":"AutoLoRA's learned architecture weight \\(\\alpha\\) is the main selection baseline (bottom-10% modules by \\(\\alpha\\) assigned to full fine-tuning). It is the best baseline on Math-500 at 1.5B and the only one reported at 3B and 7B; LoRA-Drop is higher on some 1.5B benchmarks."},{"target":"lora-drop","type":"competes-with","note":"Baseline criterion: modules with the smallest LoRA output contribution are assigned to full fine-tuning."},{"target":"spectrum","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. Spectrum fully trains the matrices with the highest weight-only random-matrix SNR and freezes the rest. Hybrid-LoRA selects by LoRA-probe gradient sensitivity and puts LoRA on everything else."},{"target":"plop","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited. PLoP decides where LoRA should go from module-data alignment; Hybrid-LoRA decides which modules should get full-rank updates instead of LoRA."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Combines dense full fine-tuning on about 10% of modules with standard LoRA (\\(r=16\\)) on the rest."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18822","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.18822","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2501.18936,2605.10741,2510.24561,2510.09160,2509.03234,2605.18822","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2605.18822?fields=title,year,venue,publicationVenue,authors","https://iclr.cc/static/virtual/data/iclr-2026-orals-posters.json","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-4"],"authors_full":["Chengqian Zhang","Wei Zhu","Kyumin Lee"],"bibkey":"zhang2026hybrid","bibtex":"@article{zhang2026hybrid,\n  title = {{Hybrid-LoRA: Bridging Full Fine-Tuning and Low-Rank Adaptation for Post-Training}},\n  author = {Chengqian Zhang and Wei Zhu and Kyumin Lee},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2605.18822},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.18822},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.18822}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A module partition chosen once by a LoRA probe: dense updates on the lowest-scoring modules \\(\\mathcal S_{fft}\\) (about 10%), rank-16 LoRA on the rest; the final RLVR run restarts from \\(W_0\\).","shape":"\\(\\prod_{\\mathcal S_{fft}}\\Theta_{l,m}\\times\\prod_{\\rm rest}(W_{0,l,m}+\\mathcal M_{\\le16})\\): an \\(\\oplus\\)-word of whole boxes and cones (Obs I.7), a non-linear cone.","base_point":"Dense modules start at \\(W_0\\) (regular there); LoRA modules presumably start at \\(B=0\\) (not stated), so the whole start is an apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_{16}\\) on each LoRA module; none on the dense ones.","covariance":"Both parts are basis-free and the selection frees whole boxes: \\(GL\\times GL\\) (as LISA).","merge":"Dense modules are updated in place and every \\(BA\\) fuses.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\): the LoRA parts span and the dense parts are already full."},"categorical_reading":"Hybrid-LoRA is a fixed \\(\\oplus\\)-word, full fine-tuning on selected boxes plus LoRA on the others. Its content is the selection rule: a first-order sensitivity of the probe's SVD-form LoRA coefficients, measured under random on/off masks of the other modules, with low sensitivity read as 'a low-rank update is not enough here'.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Selected modules receive dense updates of rank up to min(m,n) > 16."},{"target":"spectrum","test":"O1","reason":"Even on a common selected set, Hybrid-LoRA also moves every unselected module through its LoRA, which Spectrum freezes."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"dc-merge","name":"DC-Merge","full_name":"DC-Merge: directional-consistency model merging (energy smoothing + cover-space merging)","paper_title":"DC-Merge: Improving Model Merging with Directional Consistency","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"CVPR 2026","arxiv":"2603.06242","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.06242","code_url":"https://github.com/Tobeginwith/DC-Merge","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta_i\\approx U_i\\Sigma_iV_i^{\\top}\\ \\ (\\text{rank-}r\\text{ truncated SVD of the task vector }\\Delta_i=W_i-W_0\\text{ or }\\Delta_i=B_iA_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}},\\ i=1,\\dots,T);\\ \\ \\bar\\Delta_i=U_i\\bar\\Sigma_iV_i^{\\top},\\ \\text{where for LoRA inputs }\\bar{\\boldsymbol\\sigma}_i=\\Big(\\tfrac1r\\textstyle\\sum_{j=1}^{r}\\sigma_i^{j}\\Big)\\mathbf 1_r\\ \\text{(averaging) or }\\bar{\\boldsymbol\\sigma}_i=\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_{j=1}^{r}\\sigma_i^{j}\\Big)\\mathbf w,\\ \\mathbf w\\text{ linearly decreasing},\\ \\textstyle\\sum_j w_j=1,\\ \\tfrac{w_1}{w_r}=\\min\\!\\big(\\tfrac{\\sigma_i^{1}}{\\sigma_i^{r}},\\rho\\big)\\ \\text{(linear smoothing)},\\ \\text{and for full fine-tuning }\\bar\\Sigma_i=\\Sigma_i\\ \\text{(truncation only)};\\ \\ U=[U_1,\\dots,U_T]=PSQ^{\\top}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\widetilde U=PQ^{\\top}=U(U^{\\top}U)^{-1/2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times k},\\ \\ \\widetilde V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times k}\\text{ likewise from }[V_1,\\dots,V_T],\\ \\ k=rT;\\ \\ M_i=\\widetilde U^{\\top}\\bar\\Delta_i\\widetilde V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times k};\\ \\ \\widetilde M=\\mathrm{Merge}(M_1,\\dots,M_T)\\ (\\text{entry-wise; TIES after keeping the top }0.1\\%\\text{ of entries of each }M_i\\text{ in all main experiments, TA in ablations});\\ \\ \\Delta\\widetilde W=\\widetilde U\\big(\\widetilde M\\odot\\mathcal M\\big)\\widetilde V^{\\top},\\ \\ \\mathcal M=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(\\mathbf 1_{r\\times r},\\dots,\\mathbf 1_{r\\times r});\\ \\ W=W_0+\\alpha\\,\\Delta\\widetilde W;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: }W_0\\text{ and all task vectors; nothing is trained (}\\alpha\\text{ is picked on held-out validation data, fixed to }2\\text{ on MM-MergeBench)}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(training-free; hyper-parameters: rank }r\\ (=16=\\text{LoRA rank for LoRA inputs};\\ r=\\lfloor\\min(d_{out},d_{in})/T\\rfloor\\text{ for full fine-tuning}),\\ \\text{smoothing rule and }\\rho=5\\ \\text{(LoRA inputs only)},\\ \\text{TIES trim fraction }10^{-3},\\ \\text{scale }\\alpha)","locus":"Every 2-D weight matrix of \\(T\\) task-adapted copies of one base model: CLIP ViT-B/32, ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 visual encoders, either fully fine-tuned (8/14/20 tasks) or LoRA-tuned on the q, k, v and output projections at \\(r=16\\) (8/12/16 tasks), and LLaVA-v1.5-7B LoRA checkpoints (MM-MergeBench, 8 seen tasks plus 4 unseen). In the released full-fine-tuning code, non-matrix parameters (and the text projection) are simply averaged.","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Training-free, layer-wise merge of \\(T\\) task matrices in three steps. (1) Energy smoothing: each task vector is truncated to rank \\(r\\), and for LoRA inputs its singular values are flattened (to their mean, or to a linear profile whose max/min ratio is at most \\(\\rho\\)) while its singular directions \\(U_i,V_i\\) are kept. For full fine-tuning the paper and the released code only truncate to the top \\(r=\\lfloor\\min(d_{out},d_{in})/T\\rfloor\\) components, which the paper calls implicit smoothing; explicit smoothing hurt in that setting (Appendix E.4, Fig. 10). (2) Cover space: the column-concatenated bases \\(U=[U_1,\\dots,U_T]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times rT}\\) and \\(V\\) are replaced by their polar (whitened) factors \\(\\widetilde U=PQ^{\\top}\\), \\(\\widetilde V\\). These are the orthonormal matrices nearest to \\(U\\) and \\(V\\), the same orthogonalisation TSV-M uses. The paper motivates them as a near-optimal solution of a shared-dyadic-basis reconstruction problem (its Eqs. 7-8, 23-25). (3) Every smoothed task vector is written in the cover coordinates as a \\(k\\times k\\) matrix \\(M_i=\\widetilde U^{\\top}\\bar\\Delta_i\\widetilde V\\) (\\(k=rT\\le\\min(d_{out},d_{in})\\)). These matrices are combined entry-wise; all main experiments use TIES after keeping the top 0.1% of entries of each \\(M_i\\) (a \\(10^{-3}\\) fraction in both the LoRA and the full-fine-tuning code), and TA appears only in ablations. Only the \\(T\\) diagonal \\(r\\times r\\) blocks are kept before mapping back, which gives one dense delta of rank at most \\(rT\\) per matrix. Because \\(\\mathrm{span}(\\widetilde U)=\\mathrm{span}(U)\\supseteq\\mathrm{col}(\\bar\\Delta_i)\\) (and likewise for \\(\\widetilde V\\) and the row space), the round trip \\(\\widetilde U M_i\\widetilde V^{\\top}=\\bar\\Delta_i\\) is exact. With TA and no mask, the cover-space step therefore reduces to summing the smoothed task vectors. Its effect comes from the block-diagonal mask, which drops cross-task coordinates, and from coordinate-wise rules such as TIES applied in the cover basis. This follows from the construction and the paper does not state it. It is consistent with the ablations: for rank-16 LoRA inputs truncation is lossless, so the +6.40 points of 'TA + CSM' over vanilla TA in Table 6 can only come from the mask, and Table 5 shows the mask adding 0.3-1.4 points (LoRA) and 5.6-10.6 points (full fine-tuning) on top of TIES with smoothing. Diagnostic metric: \\(\\mathrm{DirSim}(\\Delta_s,\\Delta_t)=\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{nm}}\\mathbf 1_n^{\\top}R(s,t)\\mathbf 1_m\\) with \\(R_{ij}=(u_s^{i\\top}u_t^{j})(v_t^{j\\top}v_s^{i})\\). This is cosine similarity with every singular value set equal, and its projected version correlates with post-merge per-task normalized accuracy improvement (Pearson \\(R=0.64\\)).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is one static delta per matrix, \\(W=W_0+\\alpha\\,\\widetilde U(\\widetilde M\\odot\\mathcal M)\\widetilde V^{\\top}\\), folded into the base weights. The result is a single model with no adapters, routing or inference overhead. Merging LoRA inputs gives a dense rank-\\(\\le rT\\) delta, not a rank-\\(r\\) LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free). \\(\\alpha=0\\) recovers \\(W_0\\). For \\(T=1\\) with TA, \\(\\widetilde U=U_1\\), \\(\\widetilde V=V_1\\), \\(M_1=\\bar\\Sigma_1\\) and the mask is all ones, so the method returns \\(U_1\\bar\\Sigma_1V_1^{\\top}\\). For LoRA inputs this is the energy-smoothed version of the task vector, not the task vector itself. For full fine-tuning \\(r=\\min(d_{out},d_{in})\\) when \\(T=1\\) and there is no smoothing, so TA returns the task vector exactly (the default TIES step with top-0.1% trimming does not).","key_idea":"How much of each task survives merging depends on keeping the singular directions of its task vector, not the energy in its singular values. The paper measures this with an energy-blind directional similarity. DC-Merge therefore flattens each task's spectrum (for LoRA inputs; full fine-tuning uses truncation only) and then merges all tasks in one shared orthonormal cover basis \\((\\widetilde U,\\widetilde V)\\), keeping only the diagonal \\(r\\times r\\) blocks so that each task's directions survive aggregation.","results_note":"On LoRA-tuned (\\(r=16\\)) CLIP ViT-B/32, DC-Merge reaches 64.17/68.40/66.27% average absolute accuracy for 8/12/16 merged tasks, against 63.01/66.28/64.61% for the strongest baseline Iso-CTS (Table 1). It is best in all 9 LoRA settings and in 7 of the 9 full-fine-tuning settings (Iso-CTS wins ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 at 8 tasks, Table 2).","relations":[{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"builds-on","note":"Cited and used as a baseline. The cover basis is TSV-M's orthogonalisation of the concatenated per-task singular vectors (\\(U_\\perp=PQ^{\\top}\\)); the released code reuses TSV-M's construction. TSV-M places each \\(\\Sigma_i\\) in its own diagonal block. DC-Merge first smooths each spectrum, projects every smoothed task vector into the shared basis, merges with TIES (or TA) and keeps the diagonal blocks, so cross-task contributions inside each block are retained."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"builds-on","note":"TIES is the rule applied entry-wise to the cover-space coefficient matrices \\(M_i\\) in all main experiments (TA in ablations). TIES applied in cover coordinates does better than TIES applied to raw weights (Table 6: 69.27 vs 62.09 normalized accuracy on ViT-B/32, 8 tasks)."},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"Task vectors and the final \\(W_0+\\alpha\\,\\Delta\\widetilde W\\) follow Task Arithmetic, and TA is one of the two in-cover-space aggregators."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-merging baseline (KnOTS-TIES). KnOTS also aligns tasks in a shared SVD basis before applying TIES. DC-Merge beats it in all nine reported LoRA settings (Table 1) and on KnOTS's own released ViT-B/32 checkpoints (77.8 vs 68.0 normalized accuracy, Table 9)."},{"target":"core-space-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited (checked against the reference list). Core Space also maps each task's LoRA into a shared \\(Tr\\times Tr\\) coordinate matrix, merges there with an off-the-shelf rule and maps back. Its bases come from SVDs of the stacked LoRA factors. DC-Merge uses whitened per-task singular bases, spectral smoothing and a block-diagonal mask."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.06242","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.06242","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2026/html/Zhang_DC-Merge_Improving_Model_Merging_with_Directional_Consistency_CVPR_2026_paper.html","https://openreview.net/forum?id=LZacqcAohT","https://github.com/Tobeginwith/DC-Merge","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Tobeginwith/DC-Merge/main/vision_fft_merge/src/utils/DC_utils.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Tobeginwith/DC-Merge/main/vision_lora_merge/merging_functions.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2603.06242","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=DC-Merge%20Directional%20Consistency","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-5"],"authors_full":["Han-Chen Zhang","Zi-Hao Zhou","Mao-Lin Luo","Shimin Di","Min-Ling Zhang","Tong Wei"],"bibkey":"zhang2026dc","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026dc,\n  title = {{DC-Merge: Improving Model Merging with Directional Consistency}},\n  author = {Han-Chen Zhang and Zi-Hao Zhou and Mao-Lin Luo and Shimin Di and Min-Ling Zhang and Tong Wei},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2603.06242},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.06242}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and training-free: truncate each task vector (and, for LoRA inputs, flatten its spectrum), express all of them in the polar cover basis \\((\\widetilde U,\\widetilde V)\\) of the concatenated singular bases, merge entrywise (TIES with top-0.1% trimming), keep the diagonal \\(r\\times r\\) blocks, map back and scale by a validated \\(\\alpha\\).","shape":"The coefficient line \\(W_0+\\alpha\\,\\widetilde U(\\widetilde M\\odot\\mathcal M)\\widetilde V^\\top\\), \\(d=1\\).","base_point":"\\(\\alpha=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"None.","covariance":"Truncated SVDs and polar factors are \\(CO\\times CO\\)-covariant, spectral flattening is scale-equivariant, and the entrywise rule acts in cover coordinates that move with the frames: OxO, as TSV-M and KnOTS.","merge":"One dense delta of rank \\(\\le rT\\) per matrix.","rebasing":"Idempotent with the frames frozen."},"categorical_reading":"DC-Merge merges in TSV-M's whitened cover basis with two extra moves: flattening each LoRA task's spectrum, so that directions rather than energies are merged, and a block-diagonal mask that drops cross-task coordinates. The cover basis contains every task's row and column spaces, so the round trip into it is exact; with task arithmetic inside and no mask the method is just the sum of the smoothed task vectors, and its effect lives in the mask and in rules applied in cover coordinates.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\big(\\alpha\\,\\widetilde U(\\widetilde M\\odot\\mathcal M),\\ \\widetilde V^\\top\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_{rT} pointed at (0, V~^T), a T_A point with orthonormal rows when rT < min(m,n) (LoRA inputs); in the full fine-tuning setting rT can reach min(m,n), outside the strata (as Compress-then-Merge -> LoRA)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"Spectral flattening (LoRA inputs) and the block mask move the merged delta out of span{Delta_i}."},{"target":"tsv-merge","test":"O1","reason":"TSV-M puts each task's own Sigma_i in its diagonal block of the whitened basis; DC-Merge puts the merged cover coordinates there, including cross-task contributions inside each block, so the merged deltas differ."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"malora-mamba","name":"MaLoRA / MaRA","full_name":"MaLoRA: Mamba-modulated Low-Rank Adaptation, with the MaRA Mamba Retrieval Adapter","paper_title":"Selective State-Space Adaptation and Retrieval for Language Model Reasoning","authors":"Dokme and Heck","year":2026,"venue":"EMNLP 2026 (main)","arxiv":"2607.19326","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19326","code_url":"https://github.com/atahandokme/malora-mara","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_t=W_0x_t+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\,\\lambda_t\\,BAx_t,\\ \\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ (\\text{the paper's Eq. 3 writes }(W_0+B\\lambda_tA)x_t\\text{ with the LoRA scale absorbed; released code: }\\alpha/r=32/16=2);\\ \\ \\lambda_t=\\operatorname{softplus}\\big(w_\\phi^{\\top}o_t+b_\\phi\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}_{>0};\\ \\ (o_1,\\dots,o_t)=\\mathrm{Mamba}\\big(Px_1,\\dots,Px_t\\big),\\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{one Mamba-1 block of width }r\\text{: }[u_t';z_t]=W_{\\mathrm{up}}Px_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{4r},\\ u_t=\\mathrm{SiLU}(\\mathrm{conv}_4(u'_{1:t})_t),\\ s_t=\\bar A_t s_{t-1}+\\bar B_t u_t,\\ y_t=C_t s_t+D\\odot u_t\\ (\\bar A_t,\\bar B_t,C_t\\text{ computed from }u_t;\\ d_s=16),\\ o_t=W_{\\mathrm{dn}}\\big(y_t\\odot\\mathrm{SiLU}(z_t)\\big);\\ \\ \\text{i.e. }\\Delta W_t=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\,\\lambda(x_{1:t})\\,BA;\\ \\ \\text{frozen: }W_0;\\ \\text{trained jointly: }A,B,P,\\text{ the Mamba block},w_\\phi,b_\\phi;\\ B_0=0,\\ b_\\phi\\approx\\operatorname{softplus}^{-1}(1);\\ \\ \\text{MaRA (separate stage, LM frozen): }H_i=\\mathrm{LM}_{1:K}(Q\\Vert C_i),\\ p_i=\\textstyle\\sum_j\\operatorname{softmax}_j\\big(q_a^{\\top}\\tanh(W_kH_{i,j})\\big)H_{i,j},\\ (s_1,\\dots,s_N)=\\mathrm{Mamba}_{2\\text{ layers}}(W_{in}p_1,\\dots,W_{in}p_N),\\ r_i=\\mathbf w_\\ell^{\\top}s_i,\\ \\tilde{\\mathbf r}=W_gs_N\\ (\\text{training only}),\\ \\text{keep the top-}k\\text{ segments by }r_i","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ (\\text{LoRA})+r\\,d_{in}\\ (P)+6r^2+6r\\,d_s+4r\\lceil r/16\\rceil+16r\\ (\\text{Mamba-1 block of width }r,\\text{ expand }2,\\text{ conv }4,\\ d_s=16,\\text{ plus the }r\\to1\\text{ readout and an }r\\text{-vector bias of which one entry is used})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix}\\ (=3{,}392\\text{ beyond }P\\text{ at }r=d_s=16);\\ \\text{totals: }42.45\\text{M (Qwen-2.5-7B)},\\ 44.58\\text{M (Llama-3.1-8B; }44{,}582{,}912\\text{ exactly as in the released metrics)},\\ 56.79\\text{M (Gemma-2-9B)},\\ \\text{mean }47.9\\text{M vs }30.7\\text{M for LoRA }(\\approx+56\\%);\\ \\text{MaRA: }\\approx2.9\\text{–}3.1\\text{M per model}","locus":"q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, up_proj and down_proj of every decoder layer of Qwen-2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B and Gemma-2-9B (also Qwen-2.5-3B). Each projection in each layer has its own modulator, which reads that projection's input \\(x_t\\) and runs causally over the token sequence. MaRA reads the hidden states of the first \\(K\\) frozen layers (\\(K=12\\) to \\(20\\)) for each (query, paragraph) pair and acts on the context before generation.","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"A rank-\\(r\\) LoRA delta whose magnitude is a positive scalar gate that depends on the token history, \\(\\Delta W_t=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\lambda(x_{1:t})BA\\): the direction \\(BA\\) is static and only the strength changes per token. The gate is a causal, stateful function of the prefix: a selective state-space (Mamba/S6) recurrence of width \\(r\\) over the projected inputs \\(Px_1,\\dots,Px_t\\), read out by a linear head and a softplus. It can therefore both suppress (\\(\\lambda<1\\)) and amplify (\\(\\lambda>1\\)) the update. The TopLoRA baseline instead inserts a stateless per-token diagonal \\(\\Lambda(x_t)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\exp(\\mathrm{RMSNorm}(Px_t)))\\). MaLoRA trades the diagonal for a scalar (a stateful diagonal variant gave no consistent gain, Appendix C) and gives the gate memory across tokens. READ and SSMLoRA also use recurrence, but across layers; MaLoRA's runs across tokens. At the token level, GRU and LSTM gates perform about the same as the Mamba gate, so recurrence itself is what matters there. The paper notes that with KV caching the recurrence is restarted at each generated position, so only the prompt is modulated with its full history. MaRA is a separate adapter at the retrieval level, not a weight update. It combines an attention pool over frozen early-layer states, a two-layer Mamba that runs over paragraphs rather than tokens, and linear local and global scoring heads. It selects the top-\\(k\\) paragraphs (\\(k=12\\) on MuSiQue, \\(k=4\\) on 2WikiMultihopQA) over \\(N_p=4\\) chained passes and is trained on supporting-paragraph labels against the unadapted backbone, separately from MaLoRA.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The effective delta \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\lambda(x_{1:t})BA\\) changes from token to token, so it cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\). The modulator must run at inference: one \\(d_{in}\\to r\\) projection plus a width-\\(r\\), \\(d_s=16\\) Mamba step per token. Folding is possible only if \\(\\lambda\\) is frozen to a constant \\(c\\), giving \\(W_0+c\\,\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\), and that changes the model.","identity_at_init":"\\(B_0=0\\) gives \\(\\Delta W_t=0\\) and \\(h_t=W_0x_t\\) for every token. In the released code the gate bias starts at \\(\\operatorname{softplus}^{-1}(1)\\approx0.54\\) (plus small noise) and the readout weights are small, so \\(\\lambda_t\\approx1\\) and training starts as plain LoRA. The paper notes that its Eq. 3 reduces to LoRA when \\(\\lambda\\equiv1\\).","key_idea":"LoRA applies one static update to every token. MaLoRA rescales that update per token with a gate \\(\\lambda(x_{1:t})\\) produced by a Mamba recurrence over the prefix, so the adapter's strength depends on the whole trajectory up to token \\(t\\), not only on \\(x_t\\). The learned gate is mostly suppressive and separates token roles (markers and query versus content), but it does not separate supporting from distractor paragraphs. That limit motivates MaRA, which applies the same state-space idea one level up as a paragraph selector of about 3M parameters.","results_note":"At \\(r=16\\), MaLoRA alone raises MuSiQue F1 over LoRA from 51.1/54.4/59.8 to 56.4/62.3/63.7 on Qwen-2.5-7B/Llama-3.1-8B/Gemma-2-9B (ahead of stateless TopLoRA in every cell, Table 1). Adding MaRA gives +6.4 F1 over LoRA on average across the \\(3\\times2\\) backbone-dataset grid (+2.1 to +8.1 per cell, Table 4).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"Setting \\(\\lambda_t\\equiv1\\) recovers LoRA (the paper's Eq. 3 reduces to Eq. 1)."},{"target":"alora","type":"competes-with","note":"Not cited (no Activated LoRA reference in the paper). aLoRA also makes the LoRA contribution depend on token position, through a hard causal switch that turns on at the invocation token. MaLoRA learns a soft, content-dependent causal gate that is active on prompt tokens too, so it gives up aLoRA's reuse of the base model's KV cache."},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Static baseline in Tables 4 and 6. DoRA trains 1.90x slower than LoRA (20.3 h vs 10.8 h), MaLoRA 1.16x slower."},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"Static baseline in Tables 4 and 6 (+50% parameters over LoRA, versus +56% for MaLoRA)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19326","https://arxiv.org/html/2607.19326","https://github.com/atahandokme/malora-mara","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atahandokme/malora-mara/main/model/malora.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atahandokme/malora-mara/main/configs/canonical_pool/musique_malora_n1d16_scalar_r16_qwen.yaml","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alxndrTL/mamba.py/main/mambapy/mamba.py","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23123","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2607.19326","https://2026.emnlp.org/program/","https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1aXGTy_7Xeh-OXIs3iJSbDpUsZ6YhbfA76b17kBZH0Yk/edit?gid=943432490#gid=943432490","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atahandokme/malora-mara/main/results/metrics/MU_Llama_malora_s42.json","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-5"],"authors_full":["Atahan Dokme","Larry Heck"],"bibkey":"dokme2026selective","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dokme2026selective,\n  title = {{Selective State-Space Adaptation and Retrieval for Language Model Reasoning}},\n  author = {Atahan Dokme and Larry Heck},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2607.19326},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.19326}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over token prefixes: \\(\\Delta W_t=\\tfrac\\alpha r\\,\\lambda(x_{1:t})\\,BA\\), with \\(\\lambda=\\mathrm{softplus}(w^\\top o_t+b)>0\\) read from a width-\\(r\\) Mamba recurrence over \\(Px_1,\\dots,Px_t\\). MaRA, the paragraph selector, acts on the context before generation and is not a weight-space method.","shape":"A causal, stateful scalar gate on one LoRA direction: no single weight-space image.","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\) gives the frozen function at every token (neutral, as LiME); the gate starts near 1 (bias \\(\\mathrm{softplus}^{-1}(1)\\)), so training starts as LoRA.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\((B,A)\\), since the scalar gate commutes with the rank wire; the gate's Mamba block adds only discrete hidden-unit symmetries.","covariance":"The gate reads a dense projection of the box input and the LoRA is dense: function-level \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Token-dependent gate (Thm VI.3); a constant \\(\\lambda\\equiv c\\) would merge as \\(W_0+c\\,\\tfrac\\alpha rBA\\).","rebasing":"Undefined: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"MaLoRA pulls LoRA back along a causal gate that depends on the whole prefix: per token the weight is \\(W_0+\\tfrac\\alpha r\\lambda_tBA\\), a point on one fixed ray of LoRA's cone, and \\(\\lambda\\equiv1\\) is LoRA. It replaces aLoRA's position switch by a learned, content-dependent soft gate that is also active on the prompt.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{x_{1:t}}(A,B,P,\\mathrm{Mamba},w,b)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{rs}\\,\\lambda(x_{1:t})\\,B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over token prefixes, into LoRA_r with scale s, pointed at (0, A_0) since B_0 = 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"alora","test":"O1","reason":"aLoRA keeps every token before the invocation position at W_0; MaLoRA's gate is strictly positive on prompt tokens, so for generic prompts its realisations are not aLoRA's. With the invocation at the first token aLoRA is one static LoRA, which cannot reproduce MaLoRA's token-varying lambda_t BA."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"model-soups","name":"Model Soups","full_name":"Model soups (uniform / greedy weight averaging of fine-tuned models)","paper_title":"Model soups: averaging weights of multiple fine-tuned models improves accuracy without increasing inference time","authors":"Wortsman et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICML 2022","arxiv":"2203.05482","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05482","code_url":"https://github.com/mlfoundations/model-soups","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{\\mathrm{soup}}=\\tfrac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}}W_i=W_0+\\tfrac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\Delta W_i,\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,N\\};\\ \\text{uniform soup: }\\mathcal{S}=\\{1,\\dots,N\\};\\ \\text{greedy soup: add models in order of validation accuracy, keep }i\\text{ only if held-out accuracy does not drop};\\ \\text{all }W_i\\text{ frozen, nothing trained}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(post-hoc average of }N\\text{ fine-tuned models sharing one pre-trained initialization)}","locus":"all parameters of fully fine-tuned models that share a pre-trained initialization","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Equal-weight barycentre (convex combination) of \\(N\\) points in weight space that share the base point \\(W_0\\); the greedy soup is the barycentre of a validation-selected subset.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The soup is a single weight vector of the original architecture, so inference costs exactly one model.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free); a soup of a single model returns that model.","key_idea":"Models fine-tuned from the same pre-trained weights with different hyper-parameters tend to lie in one low-error basin, so averaging their weights gives ensemble-like accuracy and robustness at single-model cost.","results_note":"A greedy soup of fine-tuned ViT-G/14 models reached 90.94% ImageNet top-1, above the best single model of the sweep (90.78%).","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"special-case-of","note":"uniform soup = W_0 + (1/N) sum_i tau_i, i.e. task arithmetic with lambda = 1/N"},{"target":"fisher-merging","type":"special-case-of","note":"plain averaging is Fisher merging with identity precision (isotropic merging)"},{"target":"adaptersoup","type":"generalizes","note":"AdapterSoup applies the soup recipe to adapter parameters only"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05482","https://arxiv.org/html/2203.05482"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Mitchell Wortsman","Gabriel Ilharco","Samir Yitzhak Gadre","Rebecca Roelofs","Raphael Gontijo-Lopes","Ari S. Morcos","Hongseok Namkoong","Ali Farhadi","Yair Carmon","Simon Kornblith","Ludwig Schmidt"],"bibkey":"wortsman2022model","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wortsman2022model,\n  title = {{Model soups: averaging weights of multiple fine-tuned models improves accuracy without increasing inference time}},\n  author = {Mitchell Wortsman and Gabriel Ilharco and Samir Yitzhak Gadre and Rebecca Roelofs and Raphael Gontijo-Lopes and Ari S. Morcos and Hongseok Namkoong and Ali Farhadi and Yair Carmon and Simon Kornblith and Ludwig Schmidt},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2203.05482},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05482}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc operation \\(\\Theta^N\\to\\Theta\\), \\((\\theta_i)\\mapsto\\frac1{|S|}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\theta_i\\), on fine-tuned checkpoints that share \\(\\theta_0\\).","shape":"n/a: there are no continuous coefficients. The uniform soup is one point, and the greedy soup is a finite search over subset barycentres in \\(\\mathrm{aff}\\{\\theta_i\\}\\). Neither has a germ at \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"Barycentric: the weights sum to 1, so no admissible choice returns \\(\\theta_0\\) (generically \\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{aff}\\{\\theta_i\\}\\)). The operation never uses \\(\\theta_0\\).","gauge":"n/a: there are no trainable parameters, so there is no fibre to read.","covariance":"Averaging commutes with every affine map of \\(\\Theta\\), translations included: \\(\\mathrm{Aff}(\\Theta)\\supseteq GL(\\Theta)\\supseteq GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot post-hoc operation, so merge-and-restart does not apply (– in Table VIII.B)."},"categorical_reading":"A soup is the barycentre map of the affine space \\(\\Theta\\), applied to a tuple of trained points. It is natural under all of \\(\\mathrm{Aff}(\\Theta)\\), and unlike task arithmetic it never refers to the base point. As an object it is the point \\(\\lambda=\\mathbf 1_S/|S|\\) of task arithmetic's coefficient family, and so it lives in the unpointed slice.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=\\mathbf 1_S/|S|","note":"The soup is the point \\(\\lambda=\\mathbf 1_S/|S|\\) of task arithmetic's family. This is an arrow of the unpointed slice (task arithmetic pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"fisher-merging","name":"Fisher Merging","full_name":"Fisher-weighted averaging","paper_title":"Merging Models with Fisher-Weighted Averaging","authors":"Matena and Raffel","year":2021,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2111.09832","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09832","code_url":"https://github.com/mmatena/model_merging","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{\\star}_{[p]}=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lambda_i\\,F_{i,[p]}\\,W_{i,[p]}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lambda_i\\,F_{i,[p]}}\\ \\ (\\text{per coordinate }p),\\quad F_i=\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\,\\mathbb{E}_{y\\sim p_{\\theta_i}(y\\mid x)}\\big[(\\nabla_{\\theta}\\log p_{\\theta_i}(y\\mid x))^{2}\\big],\\ \\lambda_i\\ge0,\\ \\textstyle\\sum_i\\lambda_i=1;\\ \\text{models frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(one diagonal Fisher estimate per model, same size as the model)}","locus":"all shared parameters of models fine-tuned from the same initialization (task heads excluded)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Per-coordinate precision-weighted mean: the maximizer of \\(\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\log\\mathcal{N}(\\theta;\\theta_i,F_i^{-1})\\) under a diagonal Laplace approximation of each posterior; isotropic averaging is the case \\(F_i=I\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is one set of weights for the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"View merging as maximizing the joint likelihood of the models' posteriors; using each model's diagonal Fisher information as its posterior precision lets every coordinate be dominated by the model that is most certain about it.","results_note":"Fisher merging beats isotropic averaging for robust fine-tuning and model ensembling and is competitive with intermediate-task transfer and domain-adaptive pre-training at much lower cost.","relations":[{"target":"model-soups","type":"generalizes","note":"reduces to uniform averaging when every F_i = I"},{"target":"regmean","type":"competes-with","note":"both are closed-form weighted means; RegMean weights by input Gram matrices instead of Fisher diagonals"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"TIES reports gains over Fisher merging"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09832","https://arxiv.org/html/2111.09832"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Michael Matena","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"matena2021merging","bibtex":"@inproceedings{matena2021merging,\n  title = {{Merging Models with Fisher-Weighted Averaging}},\n  author = {Michael Matena and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2111.09832},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09832}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc, \\(\\Theta^N\\to\\Theta\\): a per-coordinate precision-weighted mean of fine-tuned checkpoints.","shape":"n/a: the family \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\sum_i\\lambda_iF_i\\theta_i/\\sum_i\\lambda_iF_i\\) is rational, neither affine nor multihomogeneous, and its weights are hyperparameters.","base_point":"Barycentric per coordinate: each merged coordinate lies between the fine-tuned ones, so \\(\\theta_0\\) is not reached generically.","gauge":"n/a: no trainable coefficients.","covariance":"A diagonal Fisher transforms as \\(D^{-2}F\\) under a diagonal reparametrisation. So the merge is equivariant under the monomial group \\(\\mathrm{Mon}_N\\) of \\(\\Theta\\) (hence \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) per matrix), but not under rotations. Full-Fisher merging would be \\(GL(\\Theta)\\)-natural.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot post-hoc operation."},"categorical_reading":"Fisher merging is the barycentre of the \\(\\theta_i\\) for the diagonal metrics \\(F_i\\), i.e. the maximiser of a product of diagonal Laplace posteriors. The diagonal approximation fixes a privileged basis, so this is a coordinate (Mon) operation. It sits between plain soups (\\(F_i=I\\), affine-natural) and full-Fisher merging (\\(GL\\)-natural).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"\\(\\theta^\\star-\\theta_0=\\sum_i\\mathrm{diag}\\big(\\lambda_iF_i/\\textstyle\\sum_j\\lambda_jF_j\\big)\\tau_i\\) has coordinate-dependent weights, so it lies outside \\(\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{\\tau_i\\}\\) generically."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"regmean","name":"RegMean","full_name":"Regression Mean (dataless knowledge fusion)","paper_title":"Dataless Knowledge Fusion by Merging Weights of Language Models","authors":"Jin et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2212.09849","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09849","code_url":"https://github.com/bloomberg/dataless-model-merging","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{\\star}=\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{N}W_i\\tilde G_i\\Big)\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\tilde G_i\\Big)^{-1}=\\arg\\min_{W}\\sum_i\\|X_iW^{\\top}-X_iW_i^{\\top}\\|_F^2\\ (\\text{with regularization}),\\ G_i=X_i^{\\top}X_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}},\\ \\tilde G_i=\\alpha G_i+(1-\\alpha)\\operatorname{diag}(G_i);\\ \\text{non-linear-layer weights simply averaged}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(stores one }d_{in}\\times d_{in}\\text{ Gram matrix per linear layer per model)}","locus":"every linear layer (embeddings and biases averaged)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Layer-wise least-squares merge: the merged linear map is the Gram-weighted matrix mean of the individual maps; equals simple averaging when all \\(G_i\\) are equal, and the off-diagonal shrinkage \\(\\alpha\\) (\\(\\approx0.9\\)) acts as a pull toward the individual weights.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Produces one weight matrix per layer of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free, closed form).","key_idea":"Merge each linear layer so that on every model's own training inputs it reproduces that model's outputs as closely as possible; this regression has a closed form that needs only inner-product statistics of the inputs, not the data itself.","results_note":"Across GLUE-style merging settings RegMean significantly outperforms Fisher-weighted averaging and model ensembling baselines and can preserve or improve individual-model performance without training data.","relations":[{"target":"fisher-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"activation-Gram weighting vs Fisher weighting"},{"target":"mix-of-show","type":"equivalent-to","note":"same layer-wise activation-matched least-squares merge operator (RegMean Eq. 1 with alpha = 1); Mix-of-Show solves it by gradient descent on activations sampled from each concept LoRA, RegMean in closed form with off-diagonal shrinkage alpha"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"competes-with","note":"standard baseline pair in merging benchmarks"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09849","https://arxiv.org/html/2212.09849"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Xisen Jin","Xiang Ren","Daniel Preotiuc-Pietro","Pengxiang Cheng"],"bibkey":"jin2022dataless","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jin2022dataless,\n  title = {{Dataless Knowledge Fusion by Merging Weights of Language Models}},\n  author = {Xisen Jin and Xiang Ren and Daniel Preotiuc-Pietro and Pengxiang Cheng},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2212.09849},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09849}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and closed form, per linear layer: \\(W^\\star=(\\sum_iW_i\\tilde G_i)(\\sum_i\\tilde G_i)^{-1}\\).","shape":"n/a: there is no coefficient family. The shrinkage \\(\\alpha\\) is a fixed hyperparameter and enters rationally.","base_point":"A matrix-weighted barycentre, with weights summing to \\(I\\). It does not reach \\(W_0\\), and the formula never uses \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"n/a: no trainable coefficients.","covariance":"Under \\(W\\mapsto gWh^{-1}\\) with \\(G_i\\mapsto hG_ih^\\top\\), \\(W^\\star\\mapsto gW^\\star h^{-1}\\) for all \\(g,h\\) when \\(\\alpha=1\\) (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The diagonal shrinkage \\(\\alpha<1\\) commutes only with monomial \\(h\\), so the default is \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\): the MonxGL key, with the monomial side on the input.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot post-hoc operation."},"categorical_reading":"RegMean is a barycentre of the linear maps \\(W_i\\), weighted by their input Gram matrices: the least-squares map that reproduces every model on its own inputs. It does not use \\(\\theta_0\\), and at \\(\\alpha=1\\) it is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-natural. The off-diagonal shrinkage is what brings back a privileged input basis.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"\\(W^\\star-W_0=\\sum_i\\Delta W_i\\tilde G_i(\\sum_j\\tilde G_j)^{-1}\\) right-multiplies each update by a matrix weight, so it leaves \\(W_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{\\Delta W_i\\}\\) generically."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"task-arithmetic","name":"Task Arithmetic","full_name":"Task arithmetic with task vectors","paper_title":"Editing Models with Task Arithmetic","authors":"Ilharco et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2212.04089","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089","code_url":"https://github.com/mlfoundations/task_vectors","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraModel.add_weighted_adapter(weights, combination_type='svd' | 'cat' | 'linear'): 'svd' sums the weighted dense deltas exactly and then truncates them to svd_rank by SVD, 'cat' is exact at rank sum r_i, and 'linear' mixes the A and B factors (an approximation with cross terms); low-level operator peft.utils.merge_utils.task_arithmetic","formula_latex":"\\tau_i=\\theta_i-\\theta_0\\ \\ (\\text{per matrix }\\Delta W_i=W_i-W_0);\\quad \\theta_{\\mathrm{new}}=\\theta_0+\\lambda\\,\\tau_{\\mathrm{new}},\\ \\ \\tau_{\\mathrm{new}}\\in\\Big\\{\\textstyle\\sum_i\\tau_i\\ (\\text{addition}),\\ -\\tau\\ (\\text{negation}),\\ \\tau_C+(\\tau_B-\\tau_A)\\ (\\text{analogy})\\Big\\};\\ \\lambda\\text{ tuned on held-out data; no training}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(one scalar }\\lambda\\text{ chosen on validation data)}","locus":"all parameters shared between the pre-trained and fine-tuned models","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Vector-space operations (sum, negation, analogy) on displacement vectors from the common base point \\(\\theta_0\\); the edited model is \\(\\theta_0\\) plus a linear combination of task vectors.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The edited model is a single weight vector of the original architecture; only element-wise operations on weights are used.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free); \\(\\lambda=0\\) recovers the pre-trained model and \\(\\lambda=1\\) with one task vector recovers that fine-tuned model.","key_idea":"A fine-tuning run defines a direction in weight space; adding such task vectors builds multi-task models, negating one makes the model forget a task or behaviour, and analogies transfer skills to tasks with little data.","results_note":"Adding task vectors from 8 CLIP image-classification tasks gives one model at 91.2% normalized accuracy (98.9% for pairs), and negating a toxicity task vector reduces toxic generations with little loss of fluency.","relations":[{"target":"model-soups","type":"generalizes","note":"uniform soup is task arithmetic with lambda = 1/N"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"TIES replaces the plain sum by trim/elect/disjoint-mean"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089","https://arxiv.org/html/2212.04089","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/utils/merge_utils.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/model.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Gabriel Ilharco","Marco Tulio Ribeiro","Mitchell Wortsman","Suchin Gururangan","Ludwig Schmidt","Hannaneh Hajishirzi","Ali Farhadi"],"bibkey":"ilharco2022editing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ilharco2022editing,\n  title = {{Editing Models with Task Arithmetic}},\n  author = {Gabriel Ilharco and Marco Tulio Ribeiro and Mitchell Wortsman and Suchin Gururangan and Ludwig Schmidt and Hannaneh Hajishirzi and Ali Farhadi},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2212.04089},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc operation on task vectors \\(\\tau_i=\\theta_i-\\theta_0\\).","shape":"Read on the coefficient family \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\tau_i\\), which is the Table VIII.B cell. It is affine, with \\(d=\\dim\\mathrm{span}\\{\\tau_i\\}\\).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_0\\), and the map is injective for independent \\(\\tau_i\\), so the point is regular. Table VIII.B leaves this cell blank.","gauge":"The family is injective for linearly independent task vectors.","covariance":"\\(GL(\\Theta)\\)-equivariant on updates (Prop V.4(c)), a group that contains \\(GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"A fixed linear shape with frozen task vectors, so idempotent (Thm V.3, Cor 2). Table VIII.B leaves this cell blank."},"categorical_reading":"Task arithmetic uses the vector-space structure of \\(T_{\\theta_0}\\Theta\\) post hoc: its coefficient family is the linear method spanned by the task vectors, pointed at \\(\\lambda=0\\). It is the \\(GL(\\Theta)\\)-natural merge. For LoRA inputs it is the gauge-invariant rule, landing in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Nr}\\) (Prop V.4(a)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"adamerging","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\lambda_i^{l}=\\lambda_i)_{i,l}","note":"Global coefficients are the layer-constant points of AdaMerging's family (AdaMerging pointed at \\(h(0)=0\\), not at its \\(0.3\\) init)."},{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\alpha_i^{l}=\\lambda_i)_{i,l}","note":"For LoRA task vectors \\(\\tau_i=B_iA_i\\), task arithmetic is the layer-constant sub-object of CAT."},{"target":"ziplora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_c,\\lambda_s)=(\\lambda_c\\mathbf 1,\\ \\lambda_s\\mathbf 1)","note":"With two LoRA updates, scalar weights are constant merger vectors (ZipLoRA pointed at \\(m=0\\), not at its all-ones init)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"Take generic modules, whose \\(N^2\\) products \\(B_iA_j\\) are linearly independent. Then LoraHub's \\(\\sum_{i,j}w_iw_jB_iA_j\\) equals \\(\\sum_i\\lambda_iB_iA_i\\) only if \\(w_iw_j=0\\) for \\(i\\ne j\\), so only one module can be active. O2 also applies: LoraHub's updates have rank \\(\\le r\\), against up to \\(\\min(Nr,m,n)\\) for task arithmetic."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"ties-merging","name":"TIES-Merging","full_name":"TrIm, Elect Sign & Merge","paper_title":"TIES-Merging: Resolving Interference When Merging Models","authors":"Yadav et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2306.01708","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01708","code_url":"https://github.com/prateeky2806/ties-merging","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraModel.add_weighted_adapter(combination_type='ties' | 'ties_svd', density=k, majority_sign_method='total' | 'frequency'); 'ties' trims and elects signs on the lora_A and lora_B factors separately, 'ties_svd' on the dense Delta W followed by SVD truncation; also IA3 vectors via merge_utils.ties","formula_latex":"\\hat\\tau_i=\\operatorname{TopK}_{k\\%}(\\tau_i)\\ (\\text{trim});\\quad \\gamma^{p}=\\operatorname{sgn}\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_{i}\\hat\\tau_i^{p}\\Big)\\ (\\text{elect});\\quad \\tau_m^{p}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{A}^{p}|}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{A}^{p}}\\hat\\tau_i^{p},\\ \\mathcal{A}^{p}=\\{i:\\operatorname{sgn}(\\hat\\tau_i^{p})=\\gamma^{p}\\}\\ (\\text{disjoint merge});\\quad \\theta_m=\\theta_0+\\lambda\\,\\tau_m","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(hyper-parameters }k\\approx20\\%,\\ \\lambda\\approx1)","locus":"task vectors of full fine-tuning or of PEFT parameters (e.g. (IA)\\(^3\\) vectors, LoRA factors in HF PEFT)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Coordinate-wise non-linear aggregation: magnitude sparsification, sign election by total mass, and a mean over agreeing non-zero entries only; not a linear combination of task vectors.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The merged task vector is added to the base weights once, giving a standard model.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"Merging interference comes from redundant small updates and sign conflicts; trimming small entries, electing one sign per parameter and averaging only agreeing values recovers much of the accuracy lost by plain averaging.","results_note":"With validation-tuned hyper-parameters, TIES outperforms averaging, Fisher merging, RegMean and Task Arithmetic across T5, ViT and (IA)\\(^3\\)-PEFT merges, in-domain and out-of-domain. Without a validation set, the fixed recipe (\\(k = 20\\%\\), \\(\\lambda = 1\\)) is weaker than Task Arithmetic for the T5-base (IA)\\(^3\\) merge (69.7 vs 73.2).","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"operates on the same task vectors"},{"target":"dare","type":"combines","note":"DARE-TIES: random drop-and-rescale followed by TIES sign election"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01708","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.01708","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/utils/merge_utils.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/developer_guides/model_merging","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/model_merging","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/model.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Prateek Yadav","Derek Tam","Leshem Choshen","Colin Raffel","Mohit Bansal"],"bibkey":"yadav2023ties","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yadav2023ties,\n  title = {{TIES-Merging: Resolving Interference When Merging Models}},\n  author = {Prateek Yadav and Derek Tam and Leshem Choshen and Colin Raffel and Mohit Bansal},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2306.01708},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.01708}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc operation on task vectors: trim, elect sign, disjoint mean.","shape":"Read on the scale: \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\lambda\\tau_m\\) is a line. The trim–elect–mean map that produces \\(\\tau_m\\) is non-linear in its inputs; the covariance axis records that.","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_0\\), and the line is injective.","gauge":"The line is injective (\\(\\tau_m\\ne0\\)).","covariance":"With sign election by total mass, equivariant under \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_N\\), homotheties times signed coordinate permutations (Prop V.4(c)). It fails under generic positive diagonals, so only the signed-permutation part of \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) survives.","rebasing":"Idempotent with \\(\\tau_m\\) frozen. Recomputing the task vectors at the new base changes the line."},"categorical_reading":"TIES applies a coordinatewise, magnitude- and sign-dependent operation to task vectors in \\(T_{\\theta_0}\\Theta\\), followed by the line method \\(\\theta_0+\\mathbb R\\tau_m\\). Its naturality group is \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_N\\): it presupposes the coordinate basis and is not \\(O(N)\\)-natural. Applied to LoRA factors (HF's non-SVD `ties`), it is also gauge-dependent.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"Trimming and disjoint means act coordinatewise, so \\(\\tau_m\\) is generically not in \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{\\tau_i\\}\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"dare","name":"DARE","full_name":"Drop And REscale","paper_title":"Language Models are Super Mario: Absorbing Abilities from Homologous Models as a Free Lunch","authors":"Yu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2311.03099","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03099","code_url":"https://github.com/yule-BUAA/MergeLM","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraModel.add_weighted_adapter(combination_type='dare_linear' | 'dare_ties' | 'dare_linear_svd' | 'dare_ties_svd', density=1-p)","formula_latex":"m_i\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\ \\text{elementwise},\\quad \\hat\\tau_i=\\frac{(\\mathbf{1}-m_i)\\odot\\tau_i}{1-p};\\quad \\theta_m=\\theta_0+\\lambda\\textstyle\\sum_i\\hat\\tau_i\\ (\\text{DARE+TA})\\ \\text{or TIES on }\\{\\hat\\tau_i\\};\\ \\text{no training}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(drop rate }p\\text{, up to }0.9\\text{--}0.99)","locus":"delta parameters (fine-tuned minus pre-trained) of homologous SFT models; LoRA factors in HF PEFT","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Random sparse coordinate mask with inverted-dropout rescaling \\(1/(1-p)\\), which preserves the expectation of each delta and of its action on inputs; a pre-processing operator applied before any merge rule.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Sparsified deltas are added back to the base weights, giving one ordinary model.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"SFT deltas of language models are extremely redundant: randomly dropping 90-99% of them and rescaling the rest barely changes behaviour, and sparsified deltas of several homologous models interfere less when merged.","results_note":"DARE removes 90% or even 99% of delta parameters with little performance loss, and a DARE-merged 7B model ranked first among 7B models on the Open LLM Leaderboard at the time.","relations":[{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"commonly used as DARE-TIES"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"DARE+Task Arithmetic is the basic merge"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03099","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.03099","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/utils/merge_utils.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/developer_guides/model_merging","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/model_merging"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Le Yu","Bowen Yu","Haiyang Yu","Fei Huang","Yongbin Li"],"bibkey":"yu2023language","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yu2023language,\n  title = {{Language Models are Super Mario: Absorbing Abilities from Homologous Models as a Free Lunch}},\n  author = {Le Yu and Bowen Yu and Haiyang Yu and Fei Huang and Yongbin Li},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2311.03099},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03099}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc pre-processing operator on task vectors, applied before a merge rule.","shape":"Read on the scale of the merge it feeds: \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\lambda\\sum_i\\hat\\tau_i\\) (DARE+TA), a random line.","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_0\\).","gauge":"The line is injective.","covariance":"\\(D(m\\odot\\tau)=m\\odot D\\tau\\) for every invertible diagonal \\(D\\), so the torus acts exactly, signs included. i.i.d. masks are permutation-invariant in law, so the group contains \\(\\mathrm{Mon}_N\\) (Prop V.4(c)). It is not \\(O(N)\\).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the mask frozen. Fresh masks in each cycle move the line."},"categorical_reading":"DARE is a random coordinate mask with inverted-dropout rescaling: a pre-processing operator on \\(T_{\\theta_0}\\Theta\\) that is exactly natural under the torus and natural under permutations in law. It preserves each task vector in expectation, so DARE+TA equals task arithmetic in law, not mask by mask.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"For a fixed mask, \\(\\sum_im_i\\odot\\tau_i/(1-p)\\) leaves \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{\\tau_i\\}\\) generically. It agrees with task arithmetic only in expectation."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"adamerging","name":"AdaMerging","full_name":"Adaptive Model Merging","paper_title":"AdaMerging: Adaptive Model Merging for Multi-Task Learning","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.02575","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02575","code_url":"https://github.com/EnnengYang/AdaMerging","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta_m^{l}=\\theta_0^{l}+\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lambda_i^{l}\\,\\tau_i^{l}\\ \\ (\\text{layer-wise; task-wise uses one }\\lambda_i\\text{ per model});\\quad \\{\\lambda\\}=\\arg\\min_{\\lambda}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}^{\\mathrm{unlab}}_{\\mathrm{test}}}H\\big(f_{\\theta_m(\\lambda)}(x)\\big),\\ H=\\text{prediction entropy};\\ \\text{task vectors frozen}","trainable_params":"N\\ \\text{per layer (layer-wise) or }N\\text{ in total (task-wise)}","locus":"task vectors of all layers (CLIP ViT image encoders in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Learned layer-wise linear combination of task vectors, fitted by test-time entropy minimization on unlabeled multi-task inputs.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After learning the coefficients the merged weights form one ordinary model.","identity_at_init":"Coefficients initialized to the task-arithmetic default \\(\\lambda=0.3\\) (not an identity initialization).","key_idea":"Merging coefficients matter a lot; they can be learned per task and per layer without labels by minimizing the entropy of the merged model's predictions on unlabeled test inputs.","results_note":"Merging eight ViT task models, AdaMerging improves average accuracy by about 11% over Task Arithmetic and generalizes better to unseen tasks and distribution shift.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"generalizes","note":"learned per-layer coefficients instead of one global lambda"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"also applied on top of TIES task vectors"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"both fit merging coefficients post hoc (AdaMerging by test-time entropy gradients, LoraHub by gradient-free few-shot search); conceptual pairing, neither paper benchmarks the other"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02575","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.02575"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Enneng Yang","Zhenyi Wang","Li Shen","Shiwei Liu","Guibing Guo","Xingwei Wang","Dacheng Tao"],"bibkey":"yang2023adamerging","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2023adamerging,\n  title = {{AdaMerging: Adaptive Model Merging for Multi-Task Learning}},\n  author = {Enneng Yang and Zhenyi Wang and Li Shen and Shiwei Liu and Guibing Guo and Xingwei Wang and Dacheng Tao},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.02575},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02575}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: layer-wise merge coefficients over frozen task vectors, fitted by test-time entropy minimisation.","shape":"The coefficient family \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\sum_{i,l}\\lambda_i^l\\tau_i^l\\) is linear, with \\(d=NL\\) for independent layer task vectors.","base_point":"The coefficients start at \\(\\lambda=0.3\\), the task-arithmetic merge, rather than at \\(0\\). That is a pointing defect by design, \\(e=0.3\\sum_i\\tau_i\\).","gauge":"The family is injective for independent layer task vectors.","covariance":"A linear combination of task vectors, fitted through the function \\(f_\\theta\\) (prediction entropy), hence \\(GL\\times GL\\) per layer.","rebasing":"A fixed linear shape with frozen task vectors, so idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"AdaMerging trains a \\(\\oplus\\) of \\(NL\\) frozen line methods \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda\\tau_i^l\\) by test-time entropy minimisation. For LoRA task vectors this is exactly CAT's coefficient object. The two differ only in loss and starting point.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ \\lambda_i^{l}\\mapsto\\alpha_i^{l}","note":"When \\(\\tau_i^l=B_i^lA_i^l\\), layer-wise AdaMerging and CAT are the same object (CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\)). They differ in loss and initialisation."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d=NL\\) layer-wise coefficients, against \\(N\\) global ones."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"tsv-merge","name":"TSV-Merge","full_name":"Task Singular Vectors Merge (TSV-M) and Compress (TSV-C)","paper_title":"Task Singular Vectors: Reducing Task Interference in Model Merging","authors":"Gargiulo et al.","year":2024,"venue":"CVPR 2025","arxiv":"2412.00081","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.00081","code_url":"https://github.com/AntoAndGar/task_singular_vectors","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta_i=U_i\\Sigma_iV_i^{\\top}\\ (\\text{keep top }1/N\\text{ components});\\ U=[U_1|\\cdots|U_N],\\ \\Sigma=\\operatorname{blockdiag}(\\Sigma_1,\\dots,\\Sigma_N),\\ V=[V_1|\\cdots|V_N];\\ U=P_UD_UQ_U^{\\top}\\Rightarrow U_\\perp=P_UQ_U^{\\top}\\ (\\text{same for }V);\\ W=W_0+\\alpha\\,U_\\perp\\Sigma V_\\perp^{\\top}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(one scaling }\\alpha\\text{; the paper finds }\\alpha=1\\text{ best, so no tuning is needed)}","locus":"per-layer task matrices of all weight matrices (ViT CLIP encoders in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Per-layer low-rank truncation of each task matrix, then orthogonalization (whitening, equivalently orthogonal Procrustes) of the concatenated singular-vector bases to remove cross-task overlap measured by the Singular Task Interference \\(\\|(U^{\\top}U-I)\\Sigma(V^{\\top}V-I)\\|_1\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The merged matrix is added once to the pre-trained weights.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"Layer task matrices are low-rank, so each can be kept as its top singular vectors; interference appears as overlap between different tasks' singular vectors and can be removed by orthogonalizing the stacked bases before summation.","results_note":"TSV-C compresses task matrices to 10% of their size while retaining 99% of accuracy, and TSV-M significantly outperforms prior merging methods on 8/14/20-task ViT benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"replaces flat task vectors with per-layer SVD structure"},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"both merge in an SVD basis; KnOTS shares U Sigma across LoRAs, TSV orthogonalizes per-task bases"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.00081","https://arxiv.org/html/2412.00081"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Antonio Andrea Gargiulo","Donato Crisostomi","Maria Sofia Bucarelli","Simone Scardapane","Fabrizio Silvestri","Emanuele Rodolà"],"bibkey":"gargiulo2024task","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gargiulo2024task,\n  title = {{Task Singular Vectors: Reducing Task Interference in Model Merging}},\n  author = {Antonio Andrea Gargiulo and Donato Crisostomi and Maria Sofia Bucarelli and Simone Scardapane and Fabrizio Silvestri and Emanuele Rodolà},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2412.00081},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.00081}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: per-layer SVD truncation of each task matrix, then whitening of the stacked singular bases.","shape":"Read on the scale \\(\\alpha\\mapsto W_0+\\alpha U_\\perp\\Sigma V_\\perp^\\top\\), a line (the paper uses \\(\\alpha=1\\)).","base_point":"\\(\\alpha=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"The line is injective. The per-task SVD sign gauge \\((u_k,v_k)\\mapsto(-u_k,-v_k)\\) commutes with the polar factor and cancels in \\(U_\\perp\\Sigma V_\\perp^\\top\\), so the rule is a well-defined function of the task matrices.","covariance":"Truncated SVDs and the polar factor \\(U\\mapsto P_UQ_U^\\top\\) are \\(CO\\)-equivariant on each side (Prop II.16), so \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the merged matrix frozen."},"categorical_reading":"TSV-M works in the spectral frames of the task matrices. It truncates each matrix to its top singular triples and replaces the stacked bases by their polar factors, the nearest orthonormal frames. This is a \\(CO\\times CO\\) (spectral) operation, and its output generally leaves the span of the task vectors.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"Whitening mixes the tasks' singular bases, so in general \\(U_\\perp\\Sigma V_\\perp^\\top\\notin\\mathrm{span}\\{\\Delta_i\\}\\): TSV-M is not a reweighted task arithmetic."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"knots","name":"KnOTS","full_name":"Knowledge Orientation Through SVD","paper_title":"Model merging with SVD to tie the Knots","authors":"Stoica et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.19735","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.19735","code_url":"https://github.com/gstoica27/KnOTS","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\big[\\Delta W^{(1)}\\ \\cdots\\ \\Delta W^{(N)}\\big]=U\\Sigma\\,\\big[V^{(1)};\\dots;V^{(N)}\\big]^{\\top}\\ \\ (\\Delta W^{(i)}=B_iA_i);\\quad V^{(m)}=\\operatorname{Merge}\\big(V^{(1)},\\dots,V^{(N)}\\big)\\ (\\text{TA, TIES, DARE},\\dots);\\quad W=W_0+U\\Sigma\\,V^{(m)\\top}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(data-free, gradient-free)}","locus":"LoRA-adapted attention projections (\\(q\\), \\(k\\), \\(v\\), \\(o\\)) of ViT-B/32, ViT-L/16 and Llama3-8B LoRA models in the paper (rank 16)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Joint SVD of the column-concatenated LoRA updates: a shared orthonormal output basis \\(U\\Sigma\\) (rank \\(\\le N r\\)) and task-specific input-side factors \\(V^{(i)}\\); any existing merge rule is applied in this aligned coordinate system.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The merged update \\(U\\Sigma V^{(m)\\top}\\) is a dense matrix added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"LoRA updates of different tasks are much less aligned than full fine-tuning deltas; re-expressing them in a common SVD basis aligns them so that standard merging rules stop destroying each other's information.","results_note":"KnOTS improves LoRA merging by up to 4.3% across vision and language benchmarks, including a new joint-task benchmark for general models.","relations":[{"target":"ties-merging","type":"combines","note":"TIES/DARE/TA are applied to the aligned V factors"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"designed for merging LoRA-finetuned models"},{"target":"tsv-merge","type":"competes-with","note":"SVD-based merging"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses TIES (and DARE / task arithmetic) as the merge operator inside the aligned space."},{"target":"dare","type":"builds-on","note":"Can plug DARE into the aligned space."},{"target":"core-space-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Core Space achieves lossless alignment in a much smaller Tr x Tr space."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.19735","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.19735","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/poster/30908","https://github.com/gstoica27/KnOTS"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","frontier"],"authors_full":["George Stoica","Pratik Ramesh","Boglarka Ecsedi","Leshem Choshen","Judy Hoffman"],"bibkey":"stoica2024model","bibtex":"@inproceedings{stoica2024model,\n  title = {{Model merging with SVD to tie the Knots}},\n  author = {George Stoica and Pratik Ramesh and Boglarka Ecsedi and Leshem Choshen and Judy Hoffman},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.19735},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.19735}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: a joint SVD of the concatenated LoRA updates, after which an existing merge rule acts on the aligned factors.","shape":"Read on the inner rule's scale \\(\\lambda\\): \\(W_0+\\lambda\\,U\\Sigma V^{(m)\\top}\\), a line (task coefficients \\(\\lambda_i\\) with task arithmetic inside).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"The line is injective. Joint-SVD sign flips act on the \\(V^{(i)}\\) by signed coordinate permutations common to all tasks. TIES and DARE respect these, so for distinct singular values KnOTS-TIES is a function of the updates \\(B_iA_i\\).","covariance":"The joint SVD gives exactly \\(CO(m)\\) on the output side. The inner TIES/DARE rule is coordinatewise in the \\(V\\)-coordinates, so only signed permutations survive on the input side. With task arithmetic inside, the method is \\(GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the merged update frozen."},"categorical_reading":"KnOTS is the joint gauge fixing of Prop V.4(b): a joint SVD of the concatenated updates produces a shared factor \\(U\\Sigma\\), and coordinatewise merge rules then act on the task-specific factors. With task arithmetic as the inner rule it is exactly task arithmetic, since \\(U\\Sigma\\sum_i\\lambda_iV^{(i)\\top}=\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\Delta W^{(i)}\\). Its content lies in the coordinatewise rules it makes well defined.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\lambda","note":"With TA as the inner rule, KnOTS and task arithmetic have the same coefficient family."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ties-merging","test":"O1","reason":"KnOTS-TIES trims and elects signs in the aligned \\(V\\)-coordinates, not in weight coordinates. So its \\(\\tau_m\\) differs from dense TIES, and the two lines differ."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"pem-arithmetic","name":"PEM Arithmetic","full_name":"Composing parameter-efficient modules with arithmetic operations","paper_title":"Composing Parameter-Efficient Modules with Arithmetic Operations","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2306.14870","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14870","code_url":"https://github.com/hkust-nlp/PEM_composition","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraModel.add_weighted_adapter(combination_type='linear'): PEFT scales each factor by sqrt(|w_i| * scaling_i) and puts the sign on lora_A only, so weights w_i = lambda_i^2 reproduce PEM addition and a negative weight reproduces PEM negation; IA3Model.add_weighted_adapter gives (IA)^3 addition, but the affine negation l -> 2 - l is not built in","formula_latex":"\\theta^{\\mathrm{add}}=\\lambda\\theta^{(1)}\\oplus(1-\\lambda)\\theta^{(2)}:=\\lambda\\theta^{(1)}+(1-\\lambda)\\theta^{(2)}\\ \\ (\\text{component-wise on }(A,B)\\text{ or on }l);\\quad \\ominus\\theta_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}=\\{A,-B\\}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta h=BAx\\mapsto-\\Delta h;\\quad \\ominus l_{(IA)^3}=\\mathbf{2}-l;\\ \\text{e.g. domain transfer }\\theta^{(1)}\\ominus\\theta^{(2)}\\oplus\\theta^{(3)};\\ \\text{no training}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(weight }\\lambda\\text{ tuned on validation)}","locus":"LoRA factors or (IA)\\(^3\\) scaling vectors of modules trained from the same base model","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Addition is defined on module parameters (for LoRA \\((A_1+A_2),(B_1+B_2)\\), which creates cross terms \\(B_1A_2+B_2A_1\\)), whereas negation is defined through the induced hidden-state change \\(\\Delta h\\): negate only \\(B\\) for LoRA, \\(l\\mapsto 2-l\\) for (IA)\\(^3\\), because negating both factors leaves \\(BA\\) unchanged.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The composed module is again a LoRA/(IA)\\(^3\\) module, which can be folded into the base weights.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"PEFT modules trained from one base model can be composed by weight-space arithmetic; the subtle part is negation, which must flip the module's effect on hidden states rather than its raw parameters.","results_note":"Arithmetic compositions of LoRA/(IA)\\(^3\\) modules (RoBERTa-base, T5-base) beat the individual modules for distribution generalization, multi-tasking, unlearning and domain transfer, and LoRA negation detoxifies Alpaca-LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"task arithmetic restricted to PEFT parameters with a corrected negation"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"same factor-wise linear composition; LoraHub searches the weights"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"composes LoRA modules"},{"target":"ia3","type":"builds-on","note":"composes (IA)^3 modules with l -> 2 - l negation"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14870","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.14870","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/model.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/ia3/model.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Jinghan Zhang","Shiqi Chen","Junteng Liu","Junxian He"],"bibkey":"zhang2023composing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023composing,\n  title = {{Composing Parameter-Efficient Modules with Arithmetic Operations}},\n  author = {Jinghan Zhang and Shiqi Chen and Junteng Liu and Junxian He},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2306.14870},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.14870}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Training-free composition of trained LoRA or (IA)\\(^3\\) modules in parameter space.","shape":"n/a. For LoRA, addition \\(\\lambda\\mapsto(\\lambda B_1+(1-\\lambda)B_2)(\\lambda A_1+(1-\\lambda)A_2)\\) is a quadratic curve through \\(\\theta_1\\) and \\(\\theta_2\\), with no germ at \\(\\theta_0\\). For (IA)\\(^3\\) it is the line through \\(\\theta_1\\) and \\(\\theta_2\\).","base_point":"The barycentric addition \\(\\lambda\\theta^{(1)}+(1-\\lambda)\\theta^{(2)}\\) does not pass through \\(\\theta_0\\) generically.","gauge":"n/a: no trained coefficients. Factor-wise addition is not \\(\\prod GL_r\\)-invariant (Prop V.4(a)), like HF's `linear`. The negation \\((A,B)\\mapsto(A,-B)\\) equals negating \\(BA\\), so it is gauge-invariant.","covariance":"Factors transform as \\(B\\mapsto gB\\) and \\(A\\mapsto Ah^{-1}\\), so the composite is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-natural for fixed representatives (Mon×GL for (IA)\\(^3\\)).","merge":"The composite is again a LoRA (M1) or an (IA)\\(^3\\) module (which fuses into a neighbour, M1→).","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot composition."},"categorical_reading":"PEM arithmetic composes modules in factor space: barycentric addition of factors, plus a negation defined through the induced update. For (IA)\\(^3\\), whose \\(\\rho\\) is linear and gauge-free, this is exactly task arithmetic on updates. For LoRA the addition is a factor-wise rule with cross terms \\(B_1A_2+B_2A_1\\), and it depends on the factor representatives.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\lambda,\\,1-\\lambda)","note":"(IA)\\(^3\\) modules only: \\(\\ell\\mapsto\\lambda\\ell_1+(1-\\lambda)\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell\\mapsto2-\\ell\\) are task arithmetic on the updates \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)W_0\\). This is an arrow of the unpointed slice."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-soups","test":"O1","reason":"For LoRA modules, the cross terms \\(B_1A_2+B_2A_1\\) lie outside \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{B_iA_i\\}\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"adaptersoup","name":"AdapterSoup","full_name":"AdapterSoup: weight averaging of domain adapters","paper_title":"AdapterSoup: Weight Averaging to Improve Generalization of Pretrained Language Models","authors":"Chronopoulou et al.","year":2023,"venue":"Findings of EACL 2023","arxiv":"2302.07027","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-eacl.153/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta_{\\mathrm{soup}}=\\tfrac{1}{l}\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\theta_i,\\ |\\mathcal{S}|=l\\le k;\\quad y=f\\big(x;\\theta_m,\\theta_{\\mathrm{soup}}\\big);\\ \\mathcal{S}\\text{ chosen by sentence-BERT similarity or domain clustering on a little novel-domain text};\\ \\text{PLM }\\theta_m\\text{ and all adapters frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{for the novel domain (}k\\text{ domain adapters are trained beforehand, each a bottleneck adapter of }\\approx2rd\\text{ per layer)}","locus":"Bapna and Firat (2019)-style bottleneck adapters (bottleneck size 64) in every Transformer layer of GPT-2","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Uniform average (a model soup) of a retrieved subset of adapter parameter vectors; one averaged adapter is executed at test time.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The soup collapses the selected adapters into one adapter, so inference costs a single adapter. That adapter is a nonlinear bottleneck module and cannot fold into \\(W_0\\). The same recipe applied to LoRAs (mentioned only as future work) would be foldable.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free combination of trained adapters).","key_idea":"For an unseen domain, rather than picking one related-domain adapter, average the weights of several selected domain adapters to obtain ensemble-like gains at the cost of a single adapter.","results_note":"On 10 held-out web domains with GPT-2, AdapterSoup with clustering-based selection gives the best average out-of-domain perplexity among single-adapter-cost methods.","relations":[{"target":"model-soups","type":"builds-on","note":"model soup restricted to adapter parameters"},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"averages serial bottleneck adapters of the Houlsby family (Bapna and Firat 2019 variant)"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"LoraHub learns signed weights instead of uniform averaging"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07027","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07027","https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-eacl.153/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Alexandra Chronopoulou","Matthew E. Peters","Alexander Fraser","Jesse Dodge"],"bibkey":"chronopoulou2023adaptersoup","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chronopoulou2023adaptersoup,\n  title = {{AdapterSoup: Weight Averaging to Improve Generalization of Pretrained Language Models}},\n  author = {Alexandra Chronopoulou and Matthew E. Peters and Alexander Fraser and Jesse Dodge},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EACL 2023},\n  eprint = {2302.07027},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-eacl.153/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc uniform average of trained bottleneck adapters, selected by sentence similarity or clustering.","shape":"The averaged parameters are a point of the adapter extension \\(\\Theta\\times\\Theta'\\). There is no weight-space image, only the realisation \\(\\Phi_{\\tilde f}\\).","base_point":"A soup of trained adapters is not the neutral point \\(U=0\\), and no selection returns it.","gauge":"n/a: no trained coefficients. Lemma VI.2 leaves each adapter with a gauge of hidden-unit permutations (and positive rescalings under ReLU). Averaging independently trained adapters is not invariant under it, so the soup depends on the representatives.","covariance":"n/a: a function-level extension, where the weight-space test does not apply (– in Table VIII.B).","merge":"The soup is a non-linear bottleneck adapter, which is not box-locally mergeable (Thm VI.3(c)).","rebasing":"n/a: an extension."},"categorical_reading":"AdapterSoup is the model-soup barycentre applied in the parameter space \\(\\Theta'\\) of an adapter extension, with the backbone fixed at \\(\\theta_0\\). The adapter is non-linear, so averaging parameters is not averaging functions, and the result stays M∞. It reduces inference cost to one adapter but never returns to \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"model-soups","test":"O1","reason":"Its functions are realised only on the adapter extension. The averaged adapter is non-affine, so it is not box-locally mergeable (Thm VI.3(c)) and generically not \\(\\Phi_f\\) of any weight. Whole-network non-realisability is not proved (Exposé XI). So the soup does not factor through a weight-space soup over \\(\\Theta\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"lorahub","name":"LoraHub","full_name":"LoraHub: dynamic LoRA composition","paper_title":"LoraHub: Efficient Cross-Task Generalization via Dynamic LoRA Composition","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"COLM 2024","arxiv":"2307.13269","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13269","code_url":"https://github.com/sail-sg/lorahub","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W=\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_iB_i\\Big)\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_iA_i\\Big),\\ \\ w\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N}\\ (\\text{signed});\\quad w^{\\star}=\\arg\\min_{w}\\ \\mathcal{L}_{Q}(w)+\\alpha\\textstyle\\sum_i|w_i|\\ \\text{by gradient-free CMA-ES (Nevergrad Shiwa)};\\ W=W_0+\\Delta W;\\ \\text{base model and all }(A_i,B_i)\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"N\\ \\text{scalars in total (e.g. }N=20\\text{ randomly sampled LoRAs), shared by all layers}","locus":"all LoRA-adapted matrices of the base model (Flan-T5 in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Element-wise linear combination of the factor matrices (a product of sums, which introduces cross terms \\(B_iA_j\\)), with one global coefficient per module found by black-box optimization on a few examples.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The composed LoRA has fixed weights and can be merged into \\(W_0\\) like any LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (only composition weights are searched; modules are pre-trained).","key_idea":"Given a hub of task LoRAs, an unseen task can be handled by searching, without gradients, for a weighted combination of existing LoRAs that minimizes loss on a handful of examples.","results_note":"On Big-Bench Hard with Flan-T5-large and 20 candidate LoRAs, LoraHub approaches few-shot in-context learning while using far fewer tokens per example at inference.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"composes trained LoRA modules"},{"target":"polytropon","type":"competes-with","note":"same factor-wise mixing, but frozen modules and gradient-free weights"},{"target":"pem-arithmetic","type":"competes-with","note":"same linear composition operator"},{"target":"lora-soups","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA Soups' CAT avoids the cross terms by summing B_iA_i"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13269","https://arxiv.org/html/2307.13269"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Chengsong Huang","Qian Liu","Bill Yuchen Lin","Tianyu Pang","Chao Du","Min Lin"],"bibkey":"huang2023lorahub","bibtex":"@inproceedings{huang2023lorahub,\n  title = {{LoraHub: Efficient Cross-Task Generalization via Dynamic LoRA Composition}},\n  author = {Chengsong Huang and Qian Liu and Bill Yuchen Lin and Tianyu Pang and Chao Du and Min Lin},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Conference on Language Modeling (COLM)},\n  eprint = {2307.13269},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.13269}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: gradient-free (CMA-ES) search over signed weights for frozen LoRA modules.","shape":"The coefficient family \\(w\\mapsto W_0+\\sum_{i,j}w_iw_jB_iA_j\\) is homogeneous quadratic: a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), of dimension \\(N\\) generically.","base_point":"The official code starts the search at \\(w=0\\) (nevergrad init \\([0]^N\\); recalled, not re-checked in this pass). There \\(d\\rho=0\\) and \\(S_1=0\\), so the whole dimension is first-order deficit. Gradient methods would be stationary at this point (Prop III.8); a gradient-free search is not.","gauge":"Only the discrete \\(w\\mapsto-w\\), so no continuous fibre. The Table VIII.B entry \\(\\prod GL_r\\) broken refers to the input modules: the rule is not invariant under \\((B_i,A_i)\\mapsto(B_ig_i^{-1},g_iA_i)\\) (Prop V.4(a)).","covariance":"\\(GL\\times GL\\)-natural for fixed factor representatives, but gauge-dependent across representatives.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot search. Formally \\(R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\{\\sum_{i,j}P_{ij}B_iA_j:P\\succeq0\\}\\), the image of the PSD cone (Thm V.3(a), with a cone closed only under positive scaling). That set matches none of the four values."},"categorical_reading":"LoraHub's coefficient object is a quadratic cone over the frozen modules, pointed at its vertex \\(w=0\\). Its composition rule is a factor-wise product of sums that does not descend to a function of the adapters \\(B_iA_i\\) (Prop V.4). It behaves like update arithmetic only when the modules share an \\(A\\)-seed (Prop V.4, prediction).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(w)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_iw_iB_i,\\ \\sum_iw_iA_i\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at the origin \\((0,0)\\), the degenerate pointing with \\(S_1=0\\). There is no pointed arrow into the standard pointing \\((0,A_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-soups","test":"O1","reason":"The cross terms \\(w_iw_jB_iA_j\\) with \\(i\\ne j\\) leave \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{B_iA_i\\}\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-soups","name":"LoRA Soups (CAT)","full_name":"Learnable concatenation of LoRAs (CAT)","paper_title":"LoRA Soups: Merging LoRAs for Practical Skill Composition Tasks","authors":"Prabhakar et al.","year":2024,"venue":"COLING 2025 (Industry Track)","arxiv":"2410.13025","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13025","code_url":"https://github.com/aksh555/LoRA-Soups","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W^{l}=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\alpha_i^{l}\\,B_iA_i=\\big[\\alpha_1^{l}B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\alpha_N^{l}B_N\\big]\\begin{bmatrix}A_1\\\\ \\vdots\\\\ A_N\\end{bmatrix};\\ \\ \\alpha_i^{l}\\text{ trained per layer on a small data mixture};\\ W_0\\text{ and all }(A_i,B_i)\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"N\\ \\text{per layer}","locus":"all LoRA-adapted matrices (Llama-family models in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Rank-concatenation (direct sum along the rank axis) of LoRA factors with learned layer-wise scalars: a weighted sum of the LoRA updates without the cross terms of factor averaging; merged rank \\(\\sum_i r_i\\). HF PEFT's add_weighted_adapter(combination_type='cat') builds the same concatenated adapter with fixed, user-given weights.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Coefficients are input-independent, so the concatenated LoRA is a fixed low-rank update that can be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable; only the merging coefficients are trained after the skill LoRAs exist.","key_idea":"For skill composition (e.g. hard math word problems solved by writing code), concatenating independently trained skill LoRAs and learning only per-layer mixing weights beats both data mixing and parameter-averaging merges.","results_note":"On GSM-Hard, CAT beats existing model-merging and data-mixing baselines by 43% and 12% on average and shows super-linear gains over the individual skill LoRAs.","relations":[{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"sum of products instead of product of sums; learned per-layer weights"},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"compared as linear-merging baseline"},{"target":"lora-lego","type":"competes-with","note":"both build a merged LoRA from rank units without factor averaging; LoRA-LEGO with k = sum r_i returns the static concatenation scaled by sqrt(r/k), whereas CAT learns per-layer weights, so CAT is not a special case"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13025","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.13025","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/model.py","https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-industry.55/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Akshara Prabhakar","Yuanzhi Li","Karthik Narasimhan","Sham Kakade","Eran Malach","Samy Jelassi"],"bibkey":"prabhakar2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{prabhakar2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Soups: Merging LoRAs for Practical Skill Composition Tasks}},\n  author = {Akshara Prabhakar and Yuanzhi Li and Karthik Narasimhan and Sham Kakade and Eran Malach and Samy Jelassi},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Computational Linguistics (COLING)},\n  eprint = {2410.13025},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13025}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: learned layer-wise weights over frozen skill LoRAs.","shape":"A \\(\\oplus\\) of frozen line methods \\(\\alpha_i^l\\mapsto\\alpha_i^lB_iA_i\\) (Obs I.7). Per layer the image is the span of \\(\\{B_iA_i\\}\\), \\(\\subseteq\\mathrm{LoRA}_{\\sum r_i}\\).","base_point":"Pointed at \\(\\alpha=0\\), as a \\(\\oplus\\) of lines. The catalog does not record the paper's starting \\(\\alpha\\); a nonzero start would be a defect.","gauge":"Injective when the \\(B_iA_i\\) are linearly independent. The input gauge cancels because only products enter (Prop V.4(a)).","covariance":"\\(GL\\times GL\\) (Table VIII.B).","rebasing":"A fixed subspace with frozen modules, so idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"CAT is a \\(\\oplus\\)-word in the pointed slice: \\(N\\) frozen line methods per layer, whose sum is a linear sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{\\sum r_i}\\). It combines products rather than factors, so it is gauge-invariant. That is exactly what separates it from LoraHub.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\big([\\alpha_1B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\alpha_NB_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Per layer, into LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_i}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1;\\dots;A_N])\\). The pointing is standard iff the stacked \\(A\\) has full row rank (Obs I.7)."},{"target":"adamerging","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ \\alpha_i^{l}\\mapsto\\lambda_i^{l}","note":"The same object as layer-wise AdaMerging on LoRA task vectors (AdaMerging pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\mathrm{CAT})=NL\\) per-layer weights, against \\(N\\) global LoraHub weights."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-lego","name":"LoRA-LEGO","full_name":"LoRA-LEGO: rank-wise clustering of minimal semantic units","paper_title":"Merging LoRAs like Playing LEGO: Pushing the Modularity of LoRA to Extremes Through Rank-Wise Clustering","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2409.16167","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=j6fsbpAllN","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"s_{ij}=[a_{ij},b_{ij}]\\ (a_{ij}\\text{: row }j\\text{ of }A_i,\\ b_{ij}\\text{: column }j\\text{ of }B_i);\\ \\Phi=\\textstyle\\bigcup_i\\{s_{i1},\\dots,s_{ir_i}\\};\\ \\{\\mu_c\\}_{c=1}^{k}=\\operatorname{KMeans}_k(\\Phi);\\ \\mu'_c=\\frac{\\overline{\\|s\\|}_{\\mathbb{C}_c}}{\\|\\mu_c\\|}\\mu_c;\\ A'=[a'_1;\\dots;a'_k],\\ B'=[b'_1\\cdots b'_k];\\ \\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\sqrt{r}}{\\sqrt{k}}\\,B'A'","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(merged rank }k,\\ 1\\le k\\le\\textstyle\\sum_i r_i)","locus":"all LoRA-adapted matrices","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Decomposes each LoRA into rank-1 'minimal semantic units' \\(b_ja_j\\) and uses two algebraic facts: permutation invariance \\(BA=(BP^{\\top})(PA)\\) and concatenation-summation equivalence \\([B_1\\ B_2][A_1;A_2]=B_1A_1+B_2A_2\\); units from all LoRAs are clustered and \\(k\\) centroids (norm- and variance-rescaled) form the merged LoRA.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The result is an ordinary rank-\\(k\\) LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"A LoRA is an unordered bag of rank-1 units, so merging should regroup similar units across LoRAs instead of averaging factor matrices position by position, which mixes misaligned units.","results_note":"Outperforms existing LoRA merging approaches on multi-task and mixed-task benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"lora-soups","type":"competes-with","note":"at k = sum r_i every cluster is a singleton and LoRA-LEGO returns the plain concatenation scaled by sqrt(r/k); CAT instead learns per-layer weights, so neither generalizes the other"},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"both address misalignment of LoRA bases before merging"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"exploits the GL(r)/permutation gauge of the BA factorization"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.16167","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.16167"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ziyu Zhao","Tao Shen","Didi Zhu","Zexi Li","Jing Su","Xuwu Wang","Kun Kuang","Fei Wu"],"bibkey":"zhao2024merging","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2024merging,\n  title = {{Merging LoRAs like Playing LEGO: Pushing the Modularity of LoRA to Extremes Through Rank-Wise Clustering}},\n  author = {Ziyu Zhao and Tao Shen and Didi Zhu and Zexi Li and Jing Su and Xuwu Wang and Kun Kuang and Fei Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2409.16167},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=j6fsbpAllN}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc and training-free: k-means over the rank-one units \\(s_{ij}=[a_{ij},b_{ij}]\\) of trained LoRAs.","shape":"n/a: there are no coefficients (\\(k\\) is discrete). The output is one point of \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le k}\\).","base_point":"No parameter value returns \\(\\theta_0\\).","gauge":"n/a: no trained coefficients. The clustering respects only permutations of rank-one units within each module. Sign flips \\((b_j,a_j)\\mapsto(-b_j,-a_j)\\), unit rescalings and general \\(GL_r\\) all change \\(s_{ij}\\), so the result is not a function of the adapters (Prop V.4(a)).","covariance":"Euclidean k-means and the norm rescaling in \\([a,b]\\)-space are equivariant under \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\). They are also equivariant under a common scaling \\(s\\mapsto cs\\), i.e. \\((B,A)\\mapsto(cB,cA)\\), which induces the homothety \\(\\Delta W\\mapsto c^2\\Delta W\\). Independent output and input scalings rescale the \\(b\\)- and \\(a\\)-blocks unequally and change the clusters. So for fixed representatives the group is \\((O\\times O)\\cdot\\mathbb R_{>0}\\): the OxO key, slightly smaller than \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot operation."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-LEGO regroups rank-one units across modules, using the permutation part of LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge together with the concatenation isomorphism \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_s\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}\\). It fixes only the finite permutation gauge, not \\(GL_r\\), so it is a rule on factor representatives, not on adapters.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=N^{-1/2}\\mathbf 1","note":"Only at \\(k=\\sum_ir_i=Nr\\), where clusters are singletons: the output \\(N^{-1/2}\\sum_iB_iA_i\\) is a point of CAT's family (CAT pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"For \\(k<\\sum r_i\\) the centroids mix units from different LoRAs, so \\(\\Delta W\\notin\\mathrm{span}\\{B_iA_i\\}\\) generically."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"ziplora","name":"ZipLoRA","full_name":"ZipLoRA: merging subject and style LoRAs","paper_title":"ZipLoRA: Any Subject in Any Style by Effectively Merging LoRAs","authors":"Shah et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ECCV 2024","arxiv":"2311.13600","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13600","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_m=\\Delta W_c\\operatorname{diag}(m_c)+\\Delta W_s\\operatorname{diag}(m_s)\\ (\\text{one coefficient per column});\\ \\min_{m_c,m_s}\\ \\|(D\\oplus L_m)(x_c,p_c)-(D\\oplus L_c)(x_c,p_c)\\|_2+\\|(D\\oplus L_m)(x_s,p_s)-(D\\oplus L_s)(x_s,p_s)\\|_2+\\lambda\\sum_{l}|m_c^{(l)}\\cdot m_s^{(l)}|;\\ W_0,\\Delta W_c,\\Delta W_s\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"2\\times(\\#\\text{columns of }\\Delta W)\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (two merger vectors), optimized for}\\approx100\\text{ steps}","locus":"LoRA-adapted layers of the SDXL UNet","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Column-wise diagonal re-weighting of two frozen dense LoRA updates, with a penalty on the overlap \\(|m_c\\cdot m_s|\\) so that columns from the two LoRAs are not superimposed; relies on the sparsity of LoRA updates.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The merged update is a fixed dense matrix added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Merger vectors initialized to all ones, i.e. training starts from the direct merge \\(\\Delta W_c+\\Delta W_s\\).","key_idea":"Independently trained subject and style LoRAs interfere when their columns are aligned; learning per-column merger coefficients that keep the two LoRAs' columns apart while preserving each LoRA's own outputs yields a single subject-in-style LoRA.","results_note":"On SDXL, ZipLoRA improves subject and style fidelity over direct LoRA merging and other baselines while needing only about 100 optimization steps.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"generalizes","note":"direct merge w_c dW_c + w_s dW_s is the scalar special case"},{"target":"mole","type":"competes-with","note":"both learn how to combine trained diffusion LoRAs"},{"target":"orthogonal-adaptation","type":"competes-with","note":"post-hoc decorrelation vs orthogonality by construction"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13600","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.13600","https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-73232-4_24"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Viraj Shah","Nataniel Ruiz","Forrester Cole","Erika Lu","Svetlana Lazebnik","Yuanzhen Li","Varun Jampani"],"bibkey":"shah2023ziplora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shah2023ziplora,\n  title = {{ZipLoRA: Any Subject in Any Style by Effectively Merging LoRAs}},\n  author = {Viraj Shah and Nataniel Ruiz and Forrester Cole and Erika Lu and Svetlana Lazebnik and Yuanzhen Li and Varun Jampani},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2311.13600},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13600}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: merger vectors fitted for about 100 steps over two frozen LoRA updates.","shape":"The coefficient family \\((m_c,m_s)\\mapsto W_0+\\Delta W_c\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_c)+\\Delta W_s\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_s)\\) is linear, with \\(d\\le2n\\) per matrix.","base_point":"The merger vectors start at \\(\\mathbf 1\\), the direct merge: a defect \\(e=\\Delta W_c+\\Delta W_s\\) by design. Starting at \\(m=0\\) would give the regular pointing.","gauge":"Injective for generic nonzero, non-parallel columns.","covariance":"Column (input-side) diagonal scalings: \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\), the one-sided torus geometry of (IA)\\(^3\\) on \\(W_{\\rm down}\\).","rebasing":"A fixed linear shape with frozen updates, so idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"ZipLoRA is a \\(\\oplus\\) of two input-scaling methods on frozen updates, i.e. a column-wise (IA)\\(^3\\) on each \\(\\Delta W\\), pointed at the direct merge. Its image lies in the frozen-\\(B\\) LoRA \\(\\{[B_c\\ B_s]Y\\}\\). Its overlap penalty is a loss, not part of \\(\\rho\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(m_c,m_s)=\\big([B_c\\ B_s],\\ [A_c\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_c);\\ A_s\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_s)]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_{r_c+r_s}\\) pointed at \\(h(\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 1)\\), with the same defect. Only the \\(A\\)-side moves, so the image lands in the frozen-\\(B\\) sub-object."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-soups","test":"O2","reason":"Per adapted matrix, \\(d=2n\\) merger coefficients against CAT's 2 weights."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"mix-of-show","name":"Mix-of-Show","full_name":"Mix-of-Show: ED-LoRA with gradient fusion","paper_title":"Mix-of-Show: Decentralized Low-Rank Adaptation for Multi-Concept Customization of Diffusion Models","authors":"Gu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.18292","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18292","code_url":"https://github.com/TencentARC/Mix-of-Show","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=\\arg\\min_{W}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\big\\|(W_0+\\Delta W_i)X_i-WX_i\\big\\|_F^2\\ \\ \\Big(\\text{closed form }W=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_i(W_0+\\Delta W_i)X_iX_i^{\\top}\\big)\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_iX_iX_i^{\\top}\\big)^{-1}\\Big),\\ X_i:\\ \\text{layer inputs collected by sampling concept }i\\text{ with its own ED-LoRA (data-free)}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{per fused layer during center-node fusion; each concept trains an ED-LoRA (}r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ per matrix plus token embeddings)}","locus":"LoRA-adapted layers of the Stable Diffusion UNet and text encoder","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Activation-matched least-squares fusion of several concept LoRAs into one dense weight per layer, optimized with gradients; formally a data-free RegMean on self-generated activations.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Gradient fusion outputs one updated weight matrix per layer, so the multi-concept model has the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (post-hoc fusion of trained concept LoRAs).","key_idea":"Weight averaging of concept LoRAs destroys concept identity; fuse layer by layer so the merged weights reproduce each concept LoRA's input-output behaviour on activations decoded from that concept's own prompts.","results_note":"Fusing independently trained ED-LoRAs this way preserves concept identity much better than weight fusion and enables multi-concept generation together with regionally controllable sampling.","relations":[{"target":"regmean","type":"equivalent-to","note":"identical layer-wise least-squares merge operator (RegMean with alpha = 1); Mix-of-Show obtains X_i by sampling each concept LoRA (data-free) and optimizes with gradients"},{"target":"orthogonal-adaptation","type":"competes-with","note":"orthogonal adaptation reaches similar identity preservation with near-instant summation"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"per-concept embedding-decomposed LoRA (ED-LoRA)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18292","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.18292"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Yuchao Gu","Xintao Wang","Jay Zhangjie Wu","Yujun Shi","Yunpeng Chen","Zihan Fan","Wuyou Xiao","Rui Zhao","Shuning Chang","Weijia Wu","Yixiao Ge","Ying Shan","Mike Zheng Shou"],"bibkey":"gu2023mix","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gu2023mix,\n  title = {{Mix-of-Show: Decentralized Low-Rank Adaptation for Multi-Concept Customization of Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Yuchao Gu and Xintao Wang and Jay Zhangjie Wu and Yujun Shi and Yunpeng Chen and Zihan Fan and Wuyou Xiao and Rui Zhao and Shuning Chang and Weijia Wu and Yixiao Ge and Ying Shan and Mike Zheng Shou},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.18292},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18292}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: layer-wise fusion of trained concept LoRAs by an activation-matched least-squares objective.","shape":"n/a: the fused weight is the unique least-squares minimiser, so there is no coefficient family.","base_point":"An activation-weighted barycentre, which does not reach \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"n/a: there are no coefficients; gradient fusion optimises the weight itself.","covariance":"This is RegMean at \\(\\alpha=1\\): exactly \\(GL\\times GL\\)-equivariant, with the self-generated activations transforming with the input basis.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot operation."},"categorical_reading":"Mix-of-Show's gradient fusion is the RegMean barycentre at \\(\\alpha=1\\), with Gram matrices taken from the activations each concept LoRA generates for itself. It is a basis-free merge that does not use \\(\\theta_0\\). Its update \\(\\sum_i\\Delta W_iG_i(\\sum_jG_j)^{-1}\\) is dense but still has rank \\(\\le\\sum_ir_i\\), and it leaves \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{\\Delta W_i\\}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"regmean","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=(\\alpha=1)","note":"Read RegMean as its shrinkage family \\(\\alpha\\mapsto W^\\star(\\alpha)\\) over fixed Gram matrices. Mix-of-Show's fused weight is its point \\(\\alpha=1\\), given the same Gram matrices (Mix-of-Show obtains them by sampling). This is an arrow of the unpointed slice. It is not A2, because \\(\\alpha=1\\) is not RegMean's default \\(0.9\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"orthogonal-adaptation","test":"O1","reason":"The fused update \\(W^\\star-W_0=\\sum_i\\Delta W_iG_i(\\sum_jG_j)^{-1}\\) has rank \\(\\le\\sum_ir_i\\), the same bound as a sum of orthogonally adapted concepts, so rank does not separate them. Right-multiplication by \\(G_i(\\sum_jG_j)^{-1}\\) does move the row space off any fixed frozen frame, so generically the update is not in \\(\\{W_0+XA_S\\}\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"–"},{"id":"orthogonal-adaptation","name":"Orthogonal Adaptation","full_name":"Orthogonal adaptation for modular customization","paper_title":"Orthogonal Adaptation for Modular Customization of Diffusion Models","authors":"Po et al.","year":2023,"venue":"CVPR 2024","arxiv":"2312.02432","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.02432","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_i=B_iA_i,\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen: }r\\text{ rows drawn at random from a shared orthogonal basis }O\\in O(d_{in});\\quad \\text{merge (FedAvg-style): }W=W_0+\\sum_i\\lambda_i\\Delta W_i\\ (\\lambda=0.6);\\quad A_jA_i^{\\top}\\approx0\\Rightarrow\\Delta W_j\\,A_i^{\\top}A_i\\approx0\\ \\text{(concept }j\\text{ is blind to the subspace concept }i\\text{ reads)};\\ \\text{paper's notation: }\\Delta\\theta_i=A_iB_i^{\\top},\\ B_i\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (input-side factor frozen)}","locus":"customizable linear layers of a text-to-image diffusion model","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"LoRA whose input-side factor is frozen and (approximately) mutually orthogonal across independently trained concepts, so each concept's update reads a nearly disjoint input subspace; merging is plain summation with little crosstalk.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each concept update is a fixed low-rank matrix and the merge is a sum, foldable into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"The frozen factor is a random orthonormal slice; the update is zero at init only if the trainable factor starts at zero (as in LoRA), which the paper does not stress.","key_idea":"Make LoRAs mergeable by construction: if each user's update reads its input through a random slice of a shared orthogonal basis, independently trained concept residuals barely interfere when added.","results_note":"Merged multi-concept Stable Diffusion models preserve identity better than FedAvg-style summation and match Mix-of-Show gradient fusion while merging almost instantly instead of ~15 minutes.","relations":[{"target":"mix-of-show","type":"competes-with","note":"orthogonality at training time vs least-squares fusion afterwards"},{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with one factor frozen to an orthonormal slice"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.02432","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.02432","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2024/html/Po_Orthogonal_Adaptation_for_Modular_Customization_of_Diffusion_Models_CVPR_2024_paper.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ryan Po","Guandao Yang","Kfir Aberman","Gordon Wetzstein"],"bibkey":"po2023orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{po2023orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Adaptation for Modular Customization of Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Ryan Po and Guandao Yang and Kfir Aberman and Gordon Wetzstein},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2312.02432},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.02432}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each concept trains \\(W_0+B_iA_i\\) with \\(A_i\\) frozen, which is a reparametrisation. The multi-concept merge is a plain sum.","shape":"LoRA-FA\\((A_i)\\): \\(\\{XA_i\\}\\), with \\(d=rd_{out}\\).","base_point":"Regular if the trainable factor starts at zero, as in LoRA. The paper does not stress this; a nonzero start would be a defect.","covariance":"The \\(A_i\\) are rows of a Haar-random orthogonal basis, so covariance holds in law (\\(GL\\times\\)fr, as for LoRA-FA).","rebasing":"LoRA-FA with a fixed \\(A\\), so idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"Orthogonal adaptation is LoRA-FA whose frames are drawn from one shared orthogonal basis. Independently trained concepts are therefore sub-objects \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_i)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{FA}(A_S)\\) of a single linear method with stacked frozen frame \\(A_S\\). Merging is addition inside that linear space, which is the shared-frozen-factor fix of Prop V.4(b), and the crosstalk is \\(\\Delta W_jA_i^\\top A_i\\approx0\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"It is LoRA-FA\\((A_i)\\), with \\(A_i\\) an orthonormal slice of a shared random basis."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\,A_i)","note":"The frozen-\\(A\\) sub-object of LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_i)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"Despite the name, the update is additive: \\(W_0+B_iA_i\\) changes the Gram matrix that OFT preserves."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"multi-lora-composition","name":"LoRA Switch / LoRA Composite","full_name":"Decoding-centric multi-LoRA composition","paper_title":"Multi-LoRA Composition for Image Generation","authors":"Zhong et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2024","arxiv":"2402.16843","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16843","code_url":"https://github.com/maszhongming/Multi-LoRA-Composition","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{Switch: }i(t)=\\big\\lfloor((t-1)\\bmod k\\tau)/\\tau\\big\\rfloor+1,\\ W_t=W_0+w_{i(t)}B_{i(t)}A_{i(t)};\\quad \\text{Composite: }\\tilde\\epsilon(z_t,c)=\\tfrac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k}w_i\\big[\\epsilon_{\\theta_i'}(z_t)+s\\,(\\epsilon_{\\theta_i'}(z_t,c)-\\epsilon_{\\theta_i'}(z_t))\\big];\\ \\text{training-free, LoRA weights untouched}","trainable_params":"0","locus":"denoising trajectory of a text-to-image diffusion model","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Composition in time (round-robin activation of one LoRA every \\(\\tau\\) denoising steps) or in score space (averaging classifier-free-guided noise predictions of \\(k\\) LoRA-adapted models) instead of in weight space.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"LoRA Switch runs one merged LoRA at a time (original cost, weights swapped every \\(\\tau\\) steps). LoRA Composite runs \\(2k\\) UNet evaluations per denoising step (conditional and unconditional for each of the \\(k\\) LoRA-adapted models), so it never reduces to one set of weights.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"Instead of merging LoRA weights, compose LoRAs during decoding, either alternating them across denoising steps or averaging their guided scores, which scales to many LoRAs without weight interference.","results_note":"On the ComposLoRA testbed (480 composition sets), both methods beat weight-space LoRA merging in GPT-4V and human evaluations, increasingly so as more LoRAs are composed.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"competes-with","note":"replaces weight-space merging"},{"target":"ziplora","type":"competes-with","note":"handles more than two LoRAs without training"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"composes diffusion LoRAs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16843","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.16843"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ming Zhong","Yelong Shen","Shuohang Wang","Yadong Lu","Yizhu Jiao","Siru Ouyang","Donghan Yu","Jiawei Han","Weizhu Chen"],"bibkey":"zhong2024multi","bibtex":"@article{zhong2024multi,\n  title = {{Multi-LoRA Composition for Image Generation}},\n  author = {Ming Zhong and Yelong Shen and Shuohang Wang and Yadong Lu and Yizhu Jiao and Siru Ouyang and Donghan Yu and Jiawei Han and Weizhu Chen},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2402.16843},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16843}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Training-free composition of trained LoRAs during decoding.","shape":"Composite averages the guided noise predictions of \\(k\\) LoRA-adapted models, which is function-level. Switch is a \\(T\\)-point over denoising steps, \\(t\\mapsto W_0+w_{i(t)}B_{i(t)}A_{i(t)}\\).","base_point":"Some LoRA is always active with fixed weights, so \\(\\theta_0\\) is never reproduced.","gauge":"n/a: no trainable coefficients.","covariance":"n/a: function-level, so the weight-space test does not apply.","merge":"Composite needs \\(2k\\) UNet evaluations per step and never reduces to one weight. Switch is M1 at each step but has no single merged weight.","rebasing":"n/a."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA Switch is a family over the base \\(T\\) of denoising steps, each fibre a frozen line method. LoRA Composite is an ensemble in score space: a post-hoc operation that lands in functions rather than in \\(\\Theta\\). Neither ever forms a single weight, which is how both avoid weight-space interference.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=w_{i(t)}\\,e_{i(t)}","note":"Switch only, fibrewise over steps: each step's weight is a point of CAT's family (CAT pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\)). Composite has no weight-space fibre."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"Composite's averaged guided noise prediction is an ensemble of \\(2k\\) UNet passes. Generically it is not \\(\\Phi_f\\) of any single weight (not proved at whole-network level), so it is not in task arithmetic's image."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"polytropon","name":"Polytropon (Poly)","full_name":"Polytropon: latent-skill routing over parameter-efficient modules","paper_title":"Combining Parameter-efficient Modules for Task-level Generalisation","authors":"Ponti et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EACL 2023","arxiv":"2202.13914","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.eacl-main.49/","code_url":"https://github.com/McGill-NLP/polytropon","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PolyConfig(poly_type='poly', n_skills, n_tasks, n_splits=1)","formula_latex":"\\hat z_{\\tau,j}\\sim\\text{Gumbel-sigmoid}(Z_{\\tau,j}),\\ \\alpha_j=\\frac{\\hat z_{\\tau,j}}{\\sum_{j'}\\hat z_{\\tau,j'}};\\ A^{\\tau}=\\sum_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{S}|}\\alpha_jA_j,\\ B^{\\tau}=\\sum_{j}\\alpha_jB_j;\\ h=W_0x+s\\,B^{\\tau}A^{\\tau}x;\\ \\text{trained: skill inventory }\\{A_j,B_j\\}\\text{ and task-skill logits }Z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|\\mathcal{T}|\\times|\\mathcal{S}|}\\text{ per layer}","trainable_params":"|\\mathcal{S}|\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+|\\mathcal{T}||\\mathcal{S}|\\ \\text{per adapted matrix}","locus":"LoRA skills on attention projections (q, k, v, o) of T5; sparse LT-SFT skills also studied","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Task-conditioned normalized combination of a small inventory of LoRA skills; the task-skill allocation is a relaxed binary soft partition (optional Indian Buffet Process prior); factors are mixed separately (product of sums).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For a known task the mixed \\((A^\\tau, B^\\tau)\\) is fixed and can be folded into \\(W_0\\) (one merged copy per task). Mixed-task batches need per-task routing. HF PEFT's Poly layer implements no merge/unmerge.","identity_at_init":"HF PEFT: routing logits \\(\\sim U(-10^{-3}, 10^{-3})\\) (near uniform) and LoRA up-projections zero-initialized, so the model starts unchanged.","key_idea":"Assume tasks share a small inventory of latent skills; jointly learn which skills each task uses and the skills themselves, so new tasks can be few-shot adapted by recombining skills. (arXiv title: 'Combining Modular Skills in Multitask Learning'.)","results_note":"Improves sample efficiency on 8 BabyAI levels and few-shot generalization on CrossFit (160 NLP tasks) over fully shared, task-specific and hypernetwork-generated baselines.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"skills are LoRA modules"},{"target":"mhr","type":"special-case-of","note":"MHR with a single head (n_splits = 1)"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"same mixing form; Poly trains modules and routing jointly"},{"target":"hyperformer","type":"competes-with","note":"compared against conditionally generated (hypernetwork) parameters"},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Each skill is a LoRA adapter."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.13914","https://arxiv.org/html/2202.13914","https://aclanthology.org/2023.eacl-main.49/","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/poly/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/poly/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/poly/router.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/poly"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Edoardo M. Ponti","Alessandro Sordoni","Yoshua Bengio","Siva Reddy"],"bibkey":"ponti2022combining","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ponti2022combining,\n  title = {{Combining Parameter-efficient Modules for Task-level Generalisation}},\n  author = {Edoardo M. Ponti and Alessandro Sordoni and Yoshua Bengio and Siva Reddy},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL)},\n  eprint = {2202.13914},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.eacl-main.49/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tasks: the task-indexed point \\((A^\\tau,B^\\tau)=(\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}A_j,\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}B_j)\\) of LoRA.","shape":"For each task the inventory is free, so the fibre's image is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"HF zero-initialises the \\(B_j\\), so each task fibre sits at LoRA's apex, and the routing logits have no first-order effect (Lemma II.4).","gauge":"Joint training through the product of sums keeps the diagonal \\(GL_r\\), \\((B_jg^{-1},gA_j)\\) for all \\(j\\) at once (Prop V.4(a)). The allocations are trained too, which adds two symmetries. One is skill mixing: \\((A_j,B_j)\\mapsto(\\sum_lN_{jl}A_l,\\sum_lN_{jl}B_l)\\) and \\(\\alpha_\\tau\\mapsto N^{-\\top}\\alpha_\\tau\\), for \\(N\\in GL_S\\) with \\(N\\mathbf 1=\\mathbf 1\\). The other is a per-task rescaling of \\(\\sigma(Z_\\tau)\\), which the normalisation removes. For \\(T>S\\) the generic fibre has dimension \\(r^2+S(S-1)+T\\) (Jacobian rank, checked numerically). All three act consistently on products, so the cross terms stay well defined. The key remains GL, on the rank and skill wires.","covariance":"Each fibre is LoRA-type, and the joint image is covariant under simultaneous \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"For a known task the mixed LoRA fuses. Mixed-task batches need routing, and HF implements no merge.","rebasing":"Per task as LoRA: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Poly is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over the base of tasks, with a shared skill inventory: \\(\\rho\\circ a:T\\to\\Theta\\). Its product-of-sums rule has the same form as LoraHub's, but it is trained jointly. With generic allocations fixed and fewer skills than tasks, the only symmetry on the inventory is the diagonal \\(GL_r\\) (checked numerically). Training the allocations adds a skill-mixing group and a normalisation scale. Either way, the cross terms are a design choice rather than an ill-definedness.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"mhr","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,Z)=(A,\\,B,\\,Z\\otimes\\mathbf 1_h)","note":"Copy the routing logits to every head (MHR pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); its heads are initialised independently)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(A,B,Z)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}B_j,\\ \\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}A_j\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}A_j^0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O2","reason":"The mixing form is the same, but each task fibre of Poly reaches all of \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) (\\(d=r(m+n-r)\\)), while LoraHub's frozen modules give an \\(N\\)-dimensional cone."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mhr","name":"MHR","full_name":"Multi-Head adapter Routing","paper_title":"Multi-Head Adapter Routing for Cross-Task Generalization","authors":"Caccia et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2211.03831","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03831","code_url":"https://github.com/microsoft/mttl","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PolyConfig(poly_type='poly', n_splits=h>1)","formula_latex":"A^{\\tau}=\\operatorname{concat}_{k=1}^{h}\\Big(\\sum_{j}\\frac{\\hat{\\mathsf{Z}}_{\\tau,j,k}}{\\sum_{j'}\\hat{\\mathsf{Z}}_{\\tau,j',k}}\\,A_j[k]\\Big)\\ (\\text{same for }B^{\\tau}),\\ A_j[k]:\\ k\\text{-th of }h\\text{ disjoint blocks of the factor};\\ h_{out}=W_0x+s\\,B^{\\tau}A^{\\tau}x;\\ \\mathsf{Z}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|\\mathcal{T}|\\times|\\mathcal{M}|\\times h}","trainable_params":"|\\mathcal{M}|\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+|\\mathcal{T}||\\mathcal{M}|h\\ \\text{per adapted matrix; MHR-}z\\text{ fine-tunes only }|\\mathcal{M}|h\\text{ routing logits per new task}","locus":"LoRA modules on attention projections (T5 / T0 backbones)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Block-wise task-conditioned mixing: a separate Polytropon routing for each of \\(h\\) disjoint blocks of the factor matrices (in HF PEFT, \\(A\\) is split along \\(d_{in}\\) and \\(B\\) along \\(d_{out}\\)), with the routed blocks concatenated.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For a known task the routed factors are fixed and the LoRA can be merged; routing must be resolved per task.","identity_at_init":"As Polytropon (near-uniform routing logits; zero-initialized up-projections in HF PEFT).","key_idea":"Finer-grained routing is more expressive: letting different blocks of each adapter come from different modules beats whole-module mixing at equal budget, and the benefit of routing lies mostly in multi-task pre-training.","results_note":"MHR outperforms Poly at comparable budget; routing-only MHR-z is competitive with extreme parameter efficiency, and MHR-mu (fine-tuning the averaged adapters) shows routing matters chiefly during multi-task pre-training.","relations":[{"target":"polytropon","type":"generalizes","note":"Poly is MHR with h = 1"},{"target":"arrow","type":"competes-with","note":"same research line (MTTL); Arrow routes post hoc without training"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03831","https://arxiv.org/html/2211.03831","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/poly/config.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/poly"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Lucas Caccia","Edoardo Ponti","Zhan Su","Matheus Pereira","Nicolas Le Roux","Alessandro Sordoni"],"bibkey":"caccia2022multi","bibtex":"@inproceedings{caccia2022multi,\n  title = {{Multi-Head Adapter Routing for Cross-Task Generalization}},\n  author = {Lucas Caccia and Edoardo Ponti and Zhan Su and Matheus Pereira and Nicolas Le Roux and Alessandro Sordoni},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2211.03831},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.03831}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tasks with block-wise routing: each of \\(h\\) blocks of the factors has its own Poly routing.","shape":"For each task the routed factors are free, so the fibre's image is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"As in Poly: zero \\(B_j\\) and near-uniform logits, so each task fibre is at the apex.","gauge":"Block \\((k,k')\\) of \\(B^\\tau A^\\tau\\) is \\(B^\\tau[k]A^\\tau[k']\\), so the rank-wire gauge is a single \\(g\\in GL_r\\) common to all skills and blocks. Each head also carries Poly's skill-mixing group \\(\\{N_k\\in GL_S:N_k\\mathbf 1=\\mathbf 1\\}\\) and a per-(task, head) normalisation scale. For \\(T>S\\) the generic fibre has dimension \\(r^2+hS(S-1)+Th\\) (Jacobian rank, checked numerically).","covariance":"The \\(h\\) blocks split \\(d_{in}\\) and \\(d_{out}\\) with separate routings, an imposed block partition. The family is covariant only under the block normaliser (\\(\\Pi\\)).","merge":"Merge for a known task, as in Poly.","rebasing":"Per task as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"MHR is Poly with its routing refined over a partition of the input and output coordinates into \\(h\\) blocks. It is still a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA, but the family now carries an imposed block structure \\(\\Pi\\) that Poly lacks. Poly is the diagonal sub-object \\(Z\\mapsto Z\\otimes\\mathbf 1_h\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(A,B,Z)=(B^\\tau,\\,A^\\tau)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, with \\(A^\\tau=\\mathrm{concat}_k\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau jk}A_j[k]\\) and likewise \\(B^\\tau\\). The target is LoRA pointed at \\((0,A^\\tau_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"polytropon","test":"O2","reason":"At equal inventory, \\(d(\\mathrm{MHR})-d(\\mathrm{Poly})=(h-1)(S-1)(T-S)\\) generically (Jacobian rank). The extra \\(TS(h-1)\\) logits are partly absorbed by the per-head skill-mixing and normalisation gauges. So \\(d(\\mathrm{MHR})>d(\\mathrm{Poly})\\) exactly when \\(h\\ge2\\), \\(S\\ge2\\) and \\(T>S\\), which is the multi-task regime. For \\(T\\le S\\) the two dimensions coincide in the cases checked, and O2 does not separate them."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"smear","name":"SMEAR","full_name":"Soft Merging of Experts with Adaptive Routing","paper_title":"Soft Merging of Experts with Adaptive Routing","authors":"Muqeeth et al.","year":2023,"venue":"TMLR 2024","arxiv":"2306.03745","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=7I199lc54z","code_url":"https://github.com/r-three/smear","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y=\\bar f\\Big(u;\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}R(v)_i\\,\\theta_i\\Big),\\ \\ R(v)=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_rv);\\ \\theta_i:\\text{ parameters of expert }i\\text{ (adapters)};\\ \\text{experts and router trained end-to-end, backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"N\\cdot(2rd+r+d)+dN\\ \\text{per routing block (}N\\text{ bottleneck-adapter experts plus a linear router), plus router/expert LayerNorms and the backbone LayerNorms}","locus":"expert routing blocks inserted after self-attention, feed-forward and cross-attention (T5) or after residual groups (ResNet)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Per-example soft parameter merging: the router's distribution averages expert parameters and the input passes through the single merged expert, keeping routing fully differentiable at the cost of one expert.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The merged expert changes with every input, so it cannot be folded into fixed weights.","identity_at_init":"Experts are randomly initialized with different values to break symmetry; no identity guarantee.","key_idea":"Learned discrete routing underperforms because of poor gradient estimators; averaging expert parameters with the router's probabilities makes routing differentiable without extra compute.","results_note":"On T5-GLUE and ResNet-DomainNet, SMEAR outperforms metadata-based routing and routing learned with gradient estimators at similar inference cost.","relations":[{"target":"adamix","type":"competes-with","note":"learned vs random routing"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03745","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.03745"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Mohammed Muqeeth","Haokun Liu","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"muqeeth2023soft","bibtex":"@article{muqeeth2023soft,\n  title = {{Soft Merging of Experts with Adaptive Routing}},\n  author = {Mohammed Muqeeth and Haokun Liu and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2306.03745},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=7I199lc54z}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over inputs: the router \\(R(v)=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_rv)\\) selects, per example, a point \\(\\sum_iR(v)_i\\theta_i\\) of an adapter's parameter space.","shape":"Adapter experts on an extension, so the method is function-level.","base_point":"The experts are initialised randomly and differently to break symmetry, so the merged expert is not the neutral adapter: a pointing defect.","gauge":"Averaging forces a common gauge on all experts: one hidden-unit permutation and, under ReLU, one positive rescaling for all of them. The softmax translation \\(W_r\\mapsto W_r+\\mathbf 1c^\\top\\) is a further symmetry.","covariance":"n/a: a function-level extension (– in Table VIII.B).","merge":"The merged expert depends on each input and is a non-linear adapter.","rebasing":"n/a: an extension."},"categorical_reading":"SMEAR is an \\(X\\)-point of an adapter extension. For each example it evaluates the adapter at the router-weighted barycentre of the expert parameters, i.e. a soup indexed by inputs. Parameter averaging is what makes the router differentiable, and it also ties all experts to one gauge representative.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h_v(\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_N,W_r)=\\textstyle\\sum_iR(v)_i\\theta_i","note":"Fibrewise over inputs: each example runs one bottleneck adapter (the target is pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), a defect)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adamix","test":"O1","reason":"SMEAR's merged expert varies with the input, while AdaMix averages its experts into one fixed adapter at inference. Generically no fixed adapter of the same width realises the routed functions, so they are not in that image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"mov","name":"MoV / MoLoRA","full_name":"Mixture of (IA)^3 Vectors / Mixture of LoRA experts","paper_title":"Pushing Mixture of Experts to the Limit: Extremely Parameter Efficient MoE for Instruction Tuning","authors":"Zadouri et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.05444","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05444","code_url":"https://github.com/for-ai/parameter-efficient-moe","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{MoV: }E_{\\mathrm{mix}}(x)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_i(x)\\,l_i,\\ s(x)=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_gx),\\ h\\leftarrow E_{\\mathrm{mix}}(x)\\odot h\\ \\text{at each (IA)}^3\\text{ site};\\quad \\text{MoLoRA: }\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_{i}s_i(x)\\,B_iA_i;\\ W_0\\text{ frozen, experts and router trained}","trainable_params":"\\text{MoV: }N\\,d_{site}+N\\,d_{model}\\ \\text{per (IA)}^3\\text{ site (}N\\text{ scaling vectors of the site's width plus a }d_{model}\\times N\\text{ router)};\\ \\text{MoLoRA: }N\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+N\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{per matrix}","locus":"(IA)\\(^3\\) rescaling sites (keys, values, FFN intermediate) for MoV; linear layers for MoLoRA; T5 encoder-decoder","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Token-level soft merging of lightweight experts: a router-weighted average of diagonal (IA)\\(^3\\) scaling vectors (multiplicative) or of LoRA experts, applied as one merged expert.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The merged scaling vector / LoRA depends on each token through the router.","identity_at_init":"MoV expert vectors initialized to ones (identity scaling), so the network is unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Mixture-of-experts can be pushed to extreme parameter efficiency by making each expert an (IA)\\(^3\\) vector or a LoRA and merging experts softly, which also generalizes to unseen tasks without task metadata.","results_note":"Instruction-tuning T5 on P3 with <1% of parameters updated, MoV matches full fine-tuning on unseen tasks at scale and improves over standard (IA)\\(^3\\) by up to 14.57% (3B) and 8.39% (11B).","relations":[{"target":"ia3","type":"generalizes","note":"a mixture of (IA)^3 vectors; N = 1 recovers (IA)^3"},{"target":"smear","type":"builds-on","note":"uses SMEAR-style soft merging"},{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"token-routed LoRA experts"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05444","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.05444"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ted Zadouri","Ahmet Üstün","Arash Ahmadian","Beyza Ermiş","Acyr Locatelli","Sara Hooker"],"bibkey":"zadouri2023pushing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zadouri2023pushing,\n  title = {{Pushing Mixture of Experts to the Limit: Extremely Parameter Efficient MoE for Instruction Tuning}},\n  author = {Ted Zadouri and Ahmet Üstün and Arash Ahmadian and Beyza Ermiş and Acyr Locatelli and Sara Hooker},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2309.05444},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05444}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: at each site \\(\\ell(x)=\\sum_is_i(x)\\ell_i\\) is an \\(X\\)-point of (IA)\\(^3\\).","shape":"The scaling depends on the input, so there is no single weight-space image.","base_point":"The experts start at \\(\\mathbf 1\\), so every fibre is the identity scaling. The router is then inert to first order, since \\(\\sum_i\\partial s_i\\cdot\\mathbf 1=\\partial(\\sum_is_i)=0\\) (a variant of Lemma II.4).","gauge":"The expert vectors carry no gauge. The only continuous fibre is the softmax translation \\(W_g\\mapsto W_g+\\mathbf 1c^\\top\\). It is the kernel of the linear logit map rather than a group acting on the experts, so it is recorded as none (\\(+d_{\\rm model}\\)), like FourierFT's conjugate pairs.","covariance":"n/a: function-level (– in Table VIII.B). Fibrewise it has (IA)\\(^3\\)'s Mon×GL.","merge":"The scaling depends on each token through the router.","rebasing":"n/a: function-level."},"categorical_reading":"MoV is an \\(X\\)-point of (IA)\\(^3\\): a router-weighted barycentre of scaling vectors, evaluated per token. At its identity initialisation the router gets no task-loss gradient until the experts separate. That is a first-order fact about the family (the zero-gate lemma), not about any single expert.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"ia3","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_N,W_g)=\\textstyle\\sum_is_i(x)\\ell_i","note":"Fibrewise over tokens and pointed: \\(h_x(q_0)=\\mathbf 1\\)."},{"target":"loramoe","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_i,A_i,W_g)=(\\tfrac r\\alpha B_i,\\,A_i,\\,W_g^\\top)\\ \\text{(MoLoRA variant)}","note":"MoLoRA's \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_is_i(x)B_iA_i\\) is LoRAMoE's routed family. \\(h\\) absorbs LoRAMoE's scale \\(\\alpha/r\\) and its transposed router convention, so it is the inverse of LoRAMoE's arrow to MoV."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"MoV variant (\\((IA)^3\\) experts), against \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\): per token the update \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell(x)-\\mathbf 1)W_0\\) has rank up to \\(\\min(m,n)>r\\). (The MoLoRA variant is isomorphic to LoRAMoE and maps fibrewise into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Nr}\\).)"}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"loramoe","name":"LoRAMoE","full_name":"LoRAMoE: MoE-style LoRA plugin with localized balancing","paper_title":"LoRAMoE: Alleviating World Knowledge Forgetting in Large Language Models via MoE-Style Plugin","authors":"Dou et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2312.09979","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.106/","code_url":"https://github.com/Ablustrund/LoRAMoE","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"o=W_0x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\omega_i(x)\\,B_iA_ix,\\ \\ \\omega(x)=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_g^{\\top}x);\\quad \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{task}}+\\beta\\,\\frac{\\sigma^2(I\\circ Q)}{\\mu(I\\circ Q)},\\ I_{n,m}=1\\pm\\delta\\ \\text{(localized balancing constraint)};\\ W_0\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"N\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})+N\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted FFN matrix}","locus":"linear layers of the feed-forward block","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Dense softmax mixture of \\(N\\) LoRA experts per FFN linear layer, with a soft balancing loss that dedicates one expert group to world-knowledge tasks and another to other downstream tasks.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Expert weights are computed per token by the router.","identity_at_init":"Not discussed in the paper. The official code (Ablustrund/LoRAMoE, peft/tuners/lora.py) initializes every \\(B_i\\) to zero (\\(A_i\\) and router Kaiming-uniform), so the plugin starts as the identity regardless of the router.","key_idea":"Large-scale SFT erodes world knowledge stored in the backbone; freezing it and adding router-mixed LoRA experts, part of which are pushed toward knowledge tasks, keeps that knowledge while still learning downstream tasks.","results_note":"Compared with vanilla SFT, LoRAMoE avoids the knowledge collapse and improves world-knowledge benchmarks by up to 63.9% (35.3% on average) while matching or beating SFT on other tasks.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"experts are LoRAs"},{"target":"mixlora","type":"competes-with","note":"dense vs top-2 sparse LoRA-MoE in the FFN"},{"target":"moelora","type":"competes-with","note":"token-level vs task-level gating"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.09979","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.09979","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.106/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Shihan Dou","Enyu Zhou","Yan Liu","Songyang Gao","Jun Zhao","Wei Shen","Yuhao Zhou","Zhiheng Xi","Xiao Wang","Xiaoran Fan","Shiliang Pu","Jiang Zhu","Rui Zheng","Tao Gui","Qi Zhang","Xuanjing Huang"],"bibkey":"dou2023loramoe","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dou2023loramoe,\n  title = {{LoRAMoE: Alleviating World Knowledge Forgetting in Large Language Models via MoE-Style Plugin}},\n  author = {Shihan Dou and Enyu Zhou and Yan Liu and Songyang Gao and Jun Zhao and Wei Shen and Yuhao Zhou and Zhiheng Xi and Xiao Wang and Xiaoran Fan and Shiliang Pu and Jiang Zhu and Rui Zheng and Tao Gui and Qi Zhang and Xuanjing Huang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2312.09979},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.106/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: \\(o=W_0x+\\frac\\alpha r\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_iA_ix\\), with a softmax router.","shape":"Input-dependent mixture: function-level (Table VIII.B).","base_point":"The official code zero-initialises every \\(B_i\\), so the point is neutral. At step one the router and the \\(A_i\\) get no task-loss gradient; only the balancing loss moves the router.","gauge":"The experts are trainable: \\(\\prod_iGL_r\\), one factor per expert (Table VIII.B), plus the softmax translation of \\(W_g\\).","covariance":"n/a: function-level (– in Table VIII.B).","merge":"The expert weights are computed per token.","rebasing":"n/a: function-level."},"categorical_reading":"LoRAMoE is an \\(X\\)-point of LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\): per token, the routed sum \\(\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_iA_i\\) is a point of a stacked LoRA. So each fibre is in weight space while the family is function-level (M∞). Its localized balancing constraint is a loss on the router, not part of \\(\\rho\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,W_g)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r[\\omega_1(x)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\omega_N(x)B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_N^0])\\)."},{"target":"mov","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_i,A_i,W_g)=(\\tfrac\\alpha rB_i,\\,A_i,\\,W_g^\\top)","note":"Isomorphic to MoLoRA's routed family."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"moelora","test":"O1","reason":"LoRAMoE's update varies token by token, while MOELoRA's is constant per task. Per-token routing is not realised by any task-indexed weight."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"moelora","name":"MOELoRA","full_name":"Mixture-of-experts LoRA with task-motivated gate","paper_title":"When MOE Meets LLMs: Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning for Multi-task Medical Applications","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"SIGIR 2024","arxiv":"2310.18339","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18339","code_url":"https://github.com/Applied-Machine-Learning-Lab/MOELoRA-peft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_j=W_0x_j+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\omega_{ji}\\,B_iA_ix_j,\\ \\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r/N},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r/N\\times d_{in}};\\quad \\omega_j=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_Te_j)\\ \\ (\\text{one task-motivated gate shared by all layers},\\ e_j\\text{ task embedding})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (same as one rank-}r\\text{ LoRA) plus one global gate }(|\\mathcal{T}|d_T+Nd_T)","locus":"query, key, value and FFN linear layers","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A rank-\\(r\\) LoRA split into \\(N\\) rank-\\((r/N)\\) experts mixed by task-specific weights, i.e. a task-dependent block-diagonal re-weighting of the rank axis; sparse top-\\(K\\) gate variant also studied.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Mixing weights depend only on the task id, so each task's update can be precomputed and merged into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not discussed in the paper. The official code (MOELoRA-peft, src/MLoRA/peft/tuners/mmoelora.py) zero-initializes the expert up-projections, so the model is unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Different medical tasks need different parameter combinations; splitting LoRA into experts and letting a task-id-driven gate mix them yields task-specific updates at LoRA's parameter cost.","results_note":"On the PromptCBLUE multi-task Chinese medical benchmark with ChatGLM-6B, MOELoRA outperforms LoRA and other PEFT baselines.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"partitions the rank of a LoRA into experts"},{"target":"polytropon","type":"competes-with","note":"both route by task identity"},{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"task-level vs token-level gating"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18339","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.18339"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Qidong Liu","Xian Wu","Xiangyu Zhao","Yuanshao Zhu","Derong Xu","Feng Tian","Yefeng Zheng"],"bibkey":"liu2023moe","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2023moe,\n  title = {{When MOE Meets LLMs: Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning for Multi-task Medical Applications}},\n  author = {Qidong Liu and Xian Wu and Xiangyu Zhao and Yuanshao Zhu and Derong Xu and Feng Tian and Yefeng Zheng},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {SIGIR},\n  eprint = {2310.18339},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18339}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tasks: \\(\\omega_\\tau=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_Te_\\tau)\\) reweights \\(N\\) experts of rank \\(r/N\\), giving a task-indexed point of LoRA\\(_r\\).","shape":"Per task the update is \\(\\sum_i\\omega_{\\tau i}B_iA_i=[\\omega_{\\tau1}B_1\\cdots][A_1;\\dots]\\), so the fibre's image is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"The official code zero-initialises the up-projections, so each task fibre is at the apex and the gate is inert to first order.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_iGL_{r/N}\\) on the experts, and \\(GL_{d_T}\\) on the wire between task embeddings and gate, \\((e,W_T)\\mapsto(ge,W_Tg^{-1})\\). The softmax translation is a further symmetry.","covariance":"The expert partition is of the internal rank wire, not of \\(\\Theta\\), so \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"The weights depend only on the task id, so there is one merged copy per task.","rebasing":"Per task as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"MOELoRA is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA\\(_r\\) over tasks. The task acts on the rank wire by a block-diagonal scaling \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\omega_\\tau\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{r/N})\\), a sum of products rather than Poly's product of sums. Each task fibre is an ordinary mergeable LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(B,A,e,W_T)=\\big([\\omega_{\\tau1}B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\omega_{\\tau N}B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_N^0])\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loramoe","test":"O1","reason":"The same token under two task ids receives different updates, but LoRAMoE's router sees only the token."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mole","name":"MoLE","full_name":"Mixture of LoRA Experts","paper_title":"Mixture of LoRA Experts","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2404.13628","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13628","code_url":"https://github.com/yushuiwx/Mixture-of-LoRA-Experts","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"E_{\\Omega}(x)=\\operatorname{Norm}\\big(E_{\\Delta\\theta_0}(x)\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus E_{\\Delta\\theta_{N-1}}(x)\\big),\\ \\varepsilon=\\operatorname{Flatten}(E_{\\Omega}(x))^{\\top}e,\\ G_i=\\frac{\\exp(\\varepsilon_i/\\tau)}{\\sum_j\\exp(\\varepsilon_j/\\tau)},\\ O(x)=F_{\\theta}(x)+\\sum_iG_i\\,E_{\\Delta\\theta_i}(x);\\ \\text{trained LoRAs frozen; per-layer gate }e\\text{ and temperature }\\tau\\text{ trained with }\\mathcal{L}_D+\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{balance}}","trainable_params":"N^{2}Ld+1\\ \\text{per gated block as written in Eq. 10 (gate }e\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\xi\\times N},\\ \\xi=N\\cdot L\\cdot d,\\ L=\\text{sequence length},\\ d=\\text{hidden size; plus a learnable temperature }\\tau);\\ \\text{all LoRAs and the backbone frozen}","locus":"each Transformer block (layer-wise gates over the outputs of trained LoRAs); matrix-wise gating also explored","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Hierarchical, layer-wise learned gating over the outputs of \\(N\\) frozen pre-trained LoRAs, with a gate-balancing loss \\(-\\log\\prod_i q^{(i)}\\) against collapse onto one LoRA.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Gate values are computed from the experts' outputs for each input.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable: starts from trained LoRAs. Each expert output \\(E_{\\Delta \\theta_i}(x)\\) is a full Transformer-block output computed with LoRA \\(i\\), and the gate initialization is not specified.","key_idea":"Linear arithmetic composition blurs the characteristics of individual LoRAs and reference-tuning is costly; treating each layer of each trained LoRA as an expert and learning layer-wise gates composes them while preserving their traits.","results_note":"In both multi-concept text-to-image generation and Flan-T5 NLP tasks, MoLE outperforms linear-arithmetic and other LoRA composition baselines.","relations":[{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"learned gating over frozen trained LoRAs"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"input-dependent layer-wise vs global static weights"},{"target":"adapterfusion","type":"competes-with","note":"learned mixing of frozen modules' outputs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13628","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.13628","https://openreview.net/forum?id=uWvKBCYh4S","https://github.com/yushuiwx/Mixture-of-LoRA-Experts"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Xun Wu","Shaohan Huang","Furu Wei"],"bibkey":"wu2024mixture","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2024mixture,\n  title = {{Mixture of LoRA Experts}},\n  author = {Xun Wu and Shaohan Huang and Furu Wei},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2404.13628},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13628}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"scalar","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over inputs: layer-wise gates over the outputs of \\(N\\) frozen trained LoRAs.","shape":"Input-dependent mixture of block outputs: function-level.","base_point":"The softmax gates sum to 1 over frozen, nonzero experts, so no gate value reproduces \\(F_\\theta\\). The method is unpointed: positive softmax mass always moves the output.","gauge":"Only the gate \\((e,\\tau)\\) is trained. \\((e,\\tau)\\mapsto(ce,c\\tau)\\) leaves \\(G=\\mathrm{softmax}(\\varepsilon/\\tau)\\) unchanged: a scalar slide, with charge \\(\\lVert e\\rVert^2/\\eta_e+\\tau^2/\\eta_\\tau\\) under gradient flow (the slide scales \\(e\\) and \\(\\tau\\) the same way, so the loss is degree-0 homogeneous and the weighted norm is conserved). The softmax translation is a further symmetry. Table VIII.B's per-expert \\(GL_r\\) does not apply, because the LoRAs are frozen.","covariance":"n/a: function-level (– in Table VIII.B).","merge":"The gates are computed from the experts' outputs for each input.","rebasing":"n/a: function-level."},"categorical_reading":"MoLE is an unpointed mixture over frozen LoRAs, indexed by inputs. Its gate reads the experts' own outputs, so even with a constant gate it is a convex combination of block outputs, not a weight-space sum. It differs from X-LoRA in mixing whole-block outputs, and from LoraHub in depending on the input.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"MoLE's input-dependent gates mix whole block outputs. So the realised functions are generically not \\(\\Phi_f\\) of any single weight (M∞; whole-network non-realisability is generic, not proved), and they are not in LoraHub's weight-space image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"mixlora","name":"MixLoRA","full_name":"MixLoRA: LoRA-based sparse mixture of experts","paper_title":"MixLoRA: Enhancing Large Language Models Fine-Tuning with LoRA-based Mixture of Experts","authors":"Li et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2404.15159","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.15159","code_url":"https://github.com/TUDB-Labs/MixLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\operatorname{MixLoRA}(h)=\\sum_{k\\in\\operatorname{Top2}}R(h)_k\\,E_k(h),\\ \\ E_k(h)=Wh+B_kA_kh\\ (W\\text{: frozen FFN weights shared by all experts}),\\ R(h)=\\operatorname{KeepTop2}\\big(\\operatorname{softmax}(W_rh)\\big);\\ \\text{plus independent LoRAs on }q,k,v,o;\\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{CE}+a\\,N\\sum_i\\mathcal{F}_i\\mathcal{P}_i","trainable_params":"N\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per FFN projection}+N\\,d\\ \\text{router}+r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per attention projection}","locus":"FFN block (sparse expert layer) plus attention projections (plain LoRA)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Sparse top-2 MoE upcycled from one dense FFN: all experts share the frozen FFN weights and differ only by their LoRA deltas; Switch-style load-balancing loss.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Each token is routed to its own top-2 experts.","identity_at_init":"Not discussed in the paper. The official code (TUDB-Labs/MixLoRA, mixlora/lora_linear.py) zero-initializes lora_B, so every expert initially equals the frozen FFN and the block is unchanged.","key_idea":"Build a resource-efficient sparse MoE from a dense model by adding LoRA experts inside the frozen FFN with a top-\\(k\\) router, plus independent attention LoRAs, and optimize the shared computation for throughput.","results_note":"Reports about 9% higher accuracy than state-of-the-art PEFT methods in multi-task learning, and its framework cuts GPU memory by 40% and token latency by 30%.","relations":[{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"sparse top-2 vs dense LoRA experts"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"experts are LoRA deltas on a shared FFN"},{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-MoE fine-tuning for heterogeneous data"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.15159","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.15159"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Dengchun Li","Yingzi Ma","Naizheng Wang","Zhengmao Ye","Zhiyuan Cheng","Yinghao Tang","Yan Zhang","Lei Duan","Jie Zuo","Cal Yang","Mingjie Tang"],"bibkey":"li2024mixlora","bibtex":"@article{li2024mixlora,\n  title = {{MixLoRA: Enhancing Large Language Models Fine-Tuning with LoRA-based Mixture of Experts}},\n  author = {Dengchun Li and Yingzi Ma and Naizheng Wang and Zhengmao Ye and Zhiyuan Cheng and Yinghao Tang and Yan Zhang and Lei Duan and Jie Zuo and Cal Yang and Mingjie Tang},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2404.15159},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.15159},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.15159}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: top-2 routing over FFN experts that share the frozen FFN and differ by LoRA deltas, plus plain LoRAs on attention.","shape":"Each expert is a whole (gated) FFN with LoRA on its projections, so the routed mixture combines non-linear expert outputs and is function-level even per token. (The catalog's \\(E_k(h)=Wh+B_kA_kh\\) linearises the expert.)","base_point":"lora_B is zero-initialised (official code), so each expert equals the frozen FFN. The kept top-2 weights are renormalised to sum to 1 (Mixtral-style, as the official code is recalled to do; not re-checked here), so the block is unchanged. Without renormalisation the block would be scaled by the kept softmax mass, which would be a defect.","gauge":"The experts are trainable: per-expert \\(GL_r\\) on each adapted projection, plus the router's softmax translation.","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"Each token is routed to its own top-2 experts.","rebasing":"n/a: function-level."},"categorical_reading":"MixLoRA upcycles a dense FFN into a sparse family over tokens, whose fibres are FFN experts sharing one frozen weight. Together with the attention LoRAs it is a \\(\\oplus\\) of an \\(X\\)-family with ordinary LoRA. Top-2 routing makes the gate vanish exactly on \\(N-2\\) experts, which separates it from dense LoRA mixtures.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"loramoe","test":"O1","reason":"The block output jumps when the second- and third-ranked experts swap: the weights agree at the tie, but the selected expert changes. So for generic trained experts MixLoRA's functions are discontinuous in the input. LoRAMoE mixes inside each linear layer with an everywhere-positive softmax, so its functions are continuous. Hence MixLoRA's routed functions are not in LoRAMoE's image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"x-lora","name":"X-LoRA","full_name":"X-LoRA: mixture of low-rank adapter experts","paper_title":"X-LoRA: Mixture of Low-Rank Adapter Experts, a Flexible Framework for Large Language Models with Applications in Protein Mechanics and Molecular Design","authors":"Buehler and Buehler","year":2024,"venue":"APL Machine Learning 2024","arxiv":"2402.07148","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07148","code_url":"https://github.com/EricLBuehler/xlora","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"XLoraConfig(hidden_size, adapters, layerwise_scalings, top_k_lora, enable_softmax, softmax_temperature, xlora_depth, scaling_pass_value, ...)","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\tfrac{\\alpha_i}{r_i}\\,B_iA_i\\,(\\lambda_{i}\\,x),\\ \\ \\lambda=\\operatorname{softmax}\\big(\\operatorname{head}_{\\phi}(\\text{hidden states of a first scaling pass})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s\\times L\\times n}\\ (\\text{per token, per layer; optional top-}k);\\ W_0\\text{ and all }(A_i,B_i)\\text{ frozen; only the scaling head }\\phi\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"\\text{scaling head only: for }\\texttt{xlora\\_depth}=1,\\ (d+1)\\,n\\ \\text{(scalings broadcast to all layers, PEFT default) or }(d+1)\\,nL\\ \\text{(layer-wise)},\\ d=\\text{model hidden size};\\ \\text{deeper heads add MLP layers of width }\\texttt{xlora\\_size};\\ \\text{LoRA experts frozen unless }\\texttt{use\\_trainable\\_adapters}","locus":"every LoRA-adapted layer of the base LLM (token-, layer- and adapter-wise scalings)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Token- and layer-wise dense (optionally top-\\(k\\)) gating of frozen LoRA experts, predicted by a small head from the model's own hidden states using a dual forward pass.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Scalings change per token and layer, and inference needs two forward passes.","identity_at_init":"Adapters are pre-trained; in HF PEFT the scaling pass uses scaling_pass_value (default 0), i.e. the base model, to compute the hidden states that drive the head.","key_idea":"Reuse a set of frozen domain LoRAs as experts and train only a light head that, after a first pass, decides how strongly each adapter acts at each token and layer, producing new deep combinations of the experts.","results_note":"The Zephyr-7B-based X-LoRA reaches \\(R^2 = 0.85\\) for predicting protein unfolding force and energy from sequence. A Gemma-7B-based variant reaches \\(R^2 = 0.87\\) on the same task and 0.96 on QM9 molecular properties, and the model answers domain questions better than its base model in qualitative comparisons.","relations":[{"target":"mole","type":"competes-with","note":"learned gating over frozen trained LoRAs"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"experts are LoRA adapters"},{"target":"phatgoose","type":"competes-with","note":"token-level routing among pre-trained LoRAs"},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Mixes multiple trained LoRA adapters."},{"target":"arrow","type":"competes-with","note":"Arrow routes LoRAs training-free; X-LoRA trains a gating head."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07148","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.07148","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/xlora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/xlora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/xlora/layer.py","https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0203126","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/xlora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Eric L. Buehler","Markus J. Buehler"],"bibkey":"buehler2024x","bibtex":"@inproceedings{buehler2024x,\n  title = {{X-LoRA: Mixture of Low-Rank Adapter Experts, a Flexible Framework for Large Language Models with Applications in Protein Mechanics and Molecular Design}},\n  author = {Eric L. Buehler and Markus J. Buehler},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {APL Machine Learning},\n  eprint = {2402.07148},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07148}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens and layers: a head reads hidden states from a first pass and outputs scalings \\(\\lambda\\in\\Delta^{n-1}\\) for frozen LoRAs.","shape":"Token- and layer-dependent scalings: function-level (Table VIII.B).","base_point":"With the softmax (HF default), the scalings sum to 1 over frozen nonzero adapters, so no head parameter reproduces \\(W_0\\). The method is unpointed, as prompt tuning is through positive softmax mass.","gauge":"Only the head is trained; the adapters are frozen unless use_trainable_adapters is set. The head's fibre is the softmax translation \\((W,b)\\mapsto(W+\\mathbf 1c^\\top,b+c_0\\mathbf 1)\\), whose mean logit weights are conserved. Table VIII.B's per-expert \\(GL_r\\) applies only when the adapters are trainable.","covariance":"n/a: function-level (– in Table VIII.B).","merge":"The scalings change per token and per layer, and inference needs two forward passes.","rebasing":"n/a: function-level."},"categorical_reading":"X-LoRA is an \\(X\\)-point of the CAT family: per token and layer its weight is \\(W_0+\\sum_i\\lambda_i(x)\\frac{\\alpha_i}{r_i}B_iA_i\\), with \\(\\lambda(x)\\) on the simplex. The simplex constraint makes it unpointed. Because of the dual pass, the family is indexed by the base model's hidden states, not by the layer input.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\phi)=\\big(\\alpha_i^{l}=\\lambda_i^{l}(x;\\phi)\\,\\alpha_i/r_i\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens: each token's weights are a point of CAT's family. This is an arrow of the unpointed slice (CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"Token-dependent scalings give functions that generically no single weight realises (M∞). So they are not in LoraHub's static weight-space image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"hydralora","name":"HydraLoRA","full_name":"HydraLoRA: asymmetric LoRA with shared A and multiple B heads","paper_title":"HydraLoRA: An Asymmetric LoRA Architecture for Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Tian et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2404.19245","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.19245","code_url":"https://github.com/Clin0212/HydraLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\omega_i(x)\\,B_iAx,\\ \\ \\omega(x)=\\operatorname{softmax}(W_g^{\\top}x),\\ W_g\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times N}\\ (\\text{official code; the paper prints }\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times N});\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\text{ shared},\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r};\\ N\\text{ chosen by k-means over the corpus (or by the developer)};\\ W_0\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{in}+N\\,r\\,d_{out}+N\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix}","locus":"LoRA-adapted linear layers of the LLM","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Asymmetric LoRA: one shared down-projection \\(A\\) and \\(N\\) up-projection heads \\(B_i\\) mixed by a router, i.e. a factorization through one shared \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace with an input-dependent output map.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The \\(B\\) heads are combined by an input-dependent router at inference.","identity_at_init":"Not discussed in the paper. The official code (Clin0212/HydraLoRA, peft/tuners/lora.py) zero-initializes every \\(B_i\\) (\\(A\\) and router Kaiming-uniform), so the model starts unchanged.","key_idea":"On heterogeneous corpora the \\(A\\) matrices of separately trained LoRAs look alike while \\(B\\) matrices differ, so share \\(A\\) to capture common knowledge and keep several router-mixed \\(B\\) heads for sub-domains discovered without labels.","results_note":"On LLaMA2-7B, HydraLoRA with \\(r = 8\\) (0.124% parameters) beats LoRA with \\(r = 32\\) and split-LoRA variants on MMLU, medical, law, HumanEval and GSM8K.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"shares A across experts"},{"target":"lily","type":"competes-with","note":"both share down-projections and route among up-projection experts"},{"target":"loramoe","type":"competes-with","note":"MoE of LoRA experts"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.19245","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.19245","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/oral/97953"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Chunlin Tian","Zhan Shi","Zhijiang Guo","Li Li","Chengzhong Xu"],"bibkey":"tian2024hydralora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tian2024hydralora,\n  title = {{HydraLoRA: An Asymmetric LoRA Architecture for Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Chunlin Tian and Zhan Shi and Zhijiang Guo and Li Li and Chengzhong Xu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2404.19245},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.19245}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over inputs \\(T=X\\): \\(h=W_0x+\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_iAx\\) with \\(\\omega(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_g^\\top x)\\) per token (router on \\(x\\), as in the official code).","shape":"Input-dependent mixture, so there is no single weight-space image; it is compared through the realisation, as LoRAMoE. Per token the weight \\(W_0+(\\sum_i\\omega_iB_i)A\\) is a point of LoRA\\(_r\\), not LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\), because \\(A\\) is shared.","base_point":"The official code zero-initialises every \\(B_i\\), so every input sees \\(f\\): neutral. At that point only the heads move to first order; \\(A\\) and the router get no task-loss gradient (zero-gate lemma, Lemma II.4).","gauge":"One \\(GL_r\\) on the shared rank wire, \\((A,B_i)\\mapsto(gA,B_ig^{-1})\\) for all heads at once (not \\(\\prod_iGL_r\\), since \\(A\\) is shared), plus the softmax translation \\(W_g\\mapsto W_g+c\\mathbf 1^\\top\\).","covariance":"n/a: a function-level family (– in Table VIII.B, as recorded for LoRAMoE and Lily).","merge":"The head mixture is recomputed per token; M1 only for a constant router (Thm VI.3, routed mixtures).","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable, so merge-and-restart is undefined."},"categorical_reading":"HydraLoRA is an \\(X\\)-point of LoRA\\(_r\\) with a fixed input factor: per token its weight \\(W_0+(\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_i)A\\) lies in the one-sided slice \\(\\{YA\\}\\), so the router moves only the output factor. It is the tied-\\(A\\) sub-family of LoRAMoE, and tying collapses LoRAMoE's \\(\\prod_iGL_r\\) gauge to a single \\(GL_r\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(A,B_1,\\dots,B_N,W_g)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_i,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\). The arrow is pointed, since every \\(B_i\\) starts at \\(0\\)."},{"target":"loramoe","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,(B_i)_i,W_g)=\\big((\\tfrac r\\alpha B_i)_i,\\ (A,\\dots,A),\\ W_g\\big)","note":"The tied embedding \\(A_i=A\\): HydraLoRA is the shared-\\(A\\) sub-family of LoRAMoE (LoRAMoE pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), whose \\(A_i\\) coincide)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"moelora","test":"O1","reason":"HydraLoRA's update changes token by token within one task, while MOELoRA's is constant for a task id, so per-token routing is not realised by any task-indexed weight."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"lily","name":"Lily","full_name":"Low-rank interconnected adaptation across layers","paper_title":"Low-Rank Interconnected Adaptation across Layers","authors":"Zhong et al.","year":2024,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2025","arxiv":"2407.09946","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.09946","code_url":"https://github.com/yibozhong/lily","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LilyConfig(r, stride_A, num_B, scaling, target_modules)","formula_latex":"x'=A_{g(l)}x,\\quad S=\\operatorname{softmax}\\Big(\\sum_{t}R\\,x'_t\\Big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_e}\\ (\\text{paper Eq. 2; HF PEFT instead averages per-token softmaxes over the batch and sequence}),\\quad \\Delta h=s\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{N_e}S_i\\,B^{i}\\Big)x';\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\text{ shared by stride}_A\\text{ consecutive layers},\\ \\{B^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\}\\text{ shared model-wide},\\ R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_e\\times r};\\ W_0\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{L}{\\mathrm{stride}_A}\\,r\\,d_{in}+N_e\\,r\\,d_{out}+L\\,N_e\\,r\\ \\text{per target-module type}","locus":"target linear layers across all Transformer layers (cross-layer sharing)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Cross-layer factor sharing: locally shared down-projections and a global pool of up-projection experts mixed per input by a router acting in the \\(r\\)-dimensional space, which allows a much larger rank at LoRA's budget.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The combination of \\(B\\) experts depends on the input activations (HF PEFT explicitly disables merge/unmerge).","identity_at_init":"HF PEFT zero-initializes the \\(B\\) experts (\\(A\\) and router Kaiming-uniform), so the model is unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Replace per-layer LoRA pairs by a few shared \\(A\\) adapters and a model-wide pool of \\(B\\) experts selected by data-dependent routers, so every layer can draw on all layers' adapters and use a higher rank for the same parameters.","results_note":"Reports better performance and efficiency than LoRA-family baselines across commonsense reasoning, GLUE, subject-driven image generation and visual adaptation benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"shared down-projection with routed up-projection experts"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"cross-layer shared LoRA factors"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"cross-layer parameter sharing for efficiency"},{"target":"vb-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both share parameters globally across layers to cut cost."},{"target":"sharelora","type":"competes-with","note":"both share LoRA factors across layers; Lily adds expert routing"},{"target":"rasa","type":"competes-with","note":"both raise per-layer rank through cross-layer sharing"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.09946","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.09946","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lily","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lily/layer.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lily","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lily/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/lily/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Yibo Zhong","Jinman Zhao","Yao Zhou"],"bibkey":"zhong2024low","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhong2024low,\n  title = {{Low-Rank Interconnected Adaptation across Layers}},\n  author = {Yibo Zhong and Jinman Zhao and Yao Zhou},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2025},\n  eprint = {2407.09946},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.09946}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Family over inputs (\\(T=X\\)): the mixture weights \\(S(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}(R A x)\\) depend on the activations (HF averages per-token softmaxes over the batch).","shape":"Input-dependent mixture: no single weight-space image, compared through the realisation (as MoLE, X-LoRA).","base_point":"Zero-initialised \\(B\\) experts reproduce \\(f\\) for every input; at that point only the experts move to first order (Lemma II.4, function-level). The table writes '-' for \\(F_X\\) rows.","gauge":"One global \\(GL_r\\) on the shared rank-\\(r\\) wire (\\(A_g\\mapsto gA_g\\), \\(B^i\\mapsto B^i g^{-1}\\), \\(R_l\\mapsto R_l g^{-1}\\), common to all layers because the \\(B^i\\) are model-wide), plus a softmax shift per router and expert permutations.","covariance":"n/a: function-level family with cross-layer sharing (table '-').","merge":"Obstructed: the expert mixture depends on the input (HF disables merge); M1 only if the router is constant.","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable, so merge-and-restart is undefined (table '-')."},"categorical_reading":"Lily is a family over inputs \\(F_X\\): a shared rank-\\(r\\) wire \\(x'=A_{g(l)}x\\) feeds a router-weighted mixture of model-wide up-projection experts, so there is no single weight-space image and merging is obstructed (M\\(\\infty\\)); at the zero-expert point it reproduces \\(f\\), and only the experts move to first order. Its continuous gauge is one global \\(GL_r\\) on the shared wire, acting on every \\(A_g\\), every \\(B^i\\) and every router at once.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Its realised functions x -> (W_0+DeltaW(x))x with input-dependent DeltaW(x) are not of the form Wx for one W, so no weight-space method (LoRA of any rank) contains its function image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"phatgoose","name":"PHATGOOSE","full_name":"Post-Hoc Adaptive Tokenwise Gating Over an Ocean of Specialized Experts","paper_title":"Learning to Route Among Specialized Experts for Zero-Shot Generalization","authors":"Muqeeth et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.05859","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05859","code_url":"https://github.com/r-three/phatgoose","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{per contributed expert }z:\\ Wu_t+B_zA_zu_t\\,\\sigma(v_z^{\\top}u_t)\\ (\\text{train only }v_z,\\ \\text{init }0,\\ \\approx100\\text{ steps});\\quad \\text{inference: }\\alpha_{t,z}=\\bar v_z^{\\top}\\bar u_t\\ (\\text{standardized}),\\ \\mathcal{E}_t=\\operatorname{top\\text{-}k}(\\alpha_t),\\ w_t=\\operatorname{softmax}\\big(\\{\\alpha_{t,z}/\\sqrt{d_{in}}\\}_{z\\in\\mathcal{E}_t}\\big),\\ h=Wu_t+\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal{E}_t}w_{t,z}B_zA_zu_t","trainable_params":"d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per contributed expert (the gate vector }v_z)","locus":"every linear layer carrying a PEFT (LoRA) module; per-token, per-module routing (LM-adapted T5.1.1 XL in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Decentralized MoE: each contributor learns a sigmoid gate vector for its frozen module; at inference the standardized gate vectors become the rows of a top-\\(k\\) router over the pooled experts.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Different tokens and modules are routed to different experts.","identity_at_init":"Gate vectors start at zero (\\(\\sigma(0)=1/2\\) scaling of the frozen module during gate training).","key_idea":"Experts produced independently by different contributors can be recycled for zero-shot generalization by having each contributor cheaply train a gate that recognizes its own activations, then using those gates as a token-level router.","results_note":"With T5-XL expert pools, PHATGOOSE outperforms prior post-hoc routing and expert-merging methods and in some cases explicit multitask training on held-out zero-shot benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"arrow","type":"competes-with","note":"post-hoc token-level routing over a LoRA library; Arrow needs no gate training"},{"target":"loraretriever","type":"competes-with","note":"routing among independently trained LoRAs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05859","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.05859"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Mohammed Muqeeth","Haokun Liu","Yufan Liu","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"muqeeth2024learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{muqeeth2024learning,\n  title = {{Learning to Route Among Specialized Experts for Zero-Shot Generalization}},\n  author = {Mohammed Muqeeth and Haokun Liu and Yufan Liu and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.05859},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05859}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens and modules, assembled post hoc: each contributor trains only a gate vector \\(v_z\\) for its frozen LoRA, and at inference the standardised \\(\\bar v_z\\) become the rows of a top-\\(k\\) router over the pooled library.","shape":"Token-dependent top-\\(k\\) mixture of frozen experts: function-level.","base_point":"At inference the top-\\(k\\) softmax weights sum to \\(1\\) over nonzero frozen experts, so no gate setting reproduces \\(f\\): unpointed. During gate training \\(v_z=0\\) already gives \\(W+\\tfrac12B_zA_z\\), a defect.","gauge":"Standardisation (zero mean and unit variance over the \\(d_{in}\\) coordinates) makes the router see \\(v_z\\) only through \\(\\bar v_z\\), and the logits enter top-\\(k\\) and the softmax only through differences across experts. The generic fibre therefore contains (i) per expert \\(\\{av_z+b\\mathbf 1:a>0\\}\\), a positive scale and a shift along \\(\\mathbf 1\\), and (ii) a common translation \\(\\bar v_z\\mapsto\\bar v_z+w\\) of all gate vectors, allowed whenever every \\(\\bar v_z+w\\) is still standardised (\\(w\\perp\\mathbf 1\\), \\(\\lVert\\bar v_z+w\\rVert^2=d_{in}\\)). That set of \\(w\\) is a quadric of dimension \\(d_{in}-1-N\\) (for \\(N<d_{in}-1\\), the realistic regime), not a group orbit, so the fibre, of dimension \\(2N+\\max(0,d_{in}-1-N)\\) per module, is non-linear (Jacobian rank and an exact finite translation, checked numerically). The frozen experts carry no trained gauge.","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"Different tokens and modules are routed to different experts.","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"PHATGOOSE glues \\(N\\) one-expert families \\(u\\mapsto Wu+\\sigma(v_z^\\top u)B_zA_zu\\), each trained by its own contributor, into one \\(X\\)-family over the pooled library by stacking their gate vectors into a router. Per token it is a point of LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\) with all but \\(k\\) blocks switched off, and the top-\\(k\\) selection makes its realised functions jump at ranking ties.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(v)=\\big([w_{x,1}(v)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ w_{x,N}(v)B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big),\\ \\ w_{x,z}=0\\ \\text{off the top-}k","note":"Fibrewise over tokens (per module), into LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice); \\(h_x\\) is smooth away from routing ties."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"arrow","test":"O1","reason":"Arrow's paper composition \\((\\sum_ip_iB_i)(\\sum_ip_iA_i)\\) has cross terms \\(p_ip_jB_iA_j\\). A top-2 PHATGOOSE update \\(w_1B_1A_1+w_2B_2A_2\\) with both weights positive would need \\(p_1p_2=0\\), so for a generic library it lies outside Arrow's per-token image."},{"target":"x-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Top-\\(k\\) routing makes PHATGOOSE's functions discontinuous in the input at ranking ties. X-LoRA's default dense softmax scalings vary continuously with the hidden states, so for generic experts PHATGOOSE's functions are not in that X-LoRA image (X-LoRA's optional top-\\(k\\) mode is not covered by this argument)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"arrow","name":"Arrow","full_name":"Arrow routing over a LoRA library (with model-based clustering, MBC)","paper_title":"Towards Modular LLMs by Building and Reusing a Library of LoRAs","authors":"Ostapenko et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2405.11157","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.11157","code_url":"https://github.com/microsoft/mttl","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(arrow_config=ArrowConfig(top_k, router_temperature, use_gks)) via create_arrow_model","formula_latex":"v_i=\\text{first right singular vector of }\\Delta W_i=B_iA_i;\\ \\ \\ell_i(h)=|v_i^{\\top}h|,\\ \\ p(h)=\\operatorname{softmax}\\big(\\ell(h)\\ \\text{restricted to top-}k\\big);\\ \\ A^{*}=\\sum_ip_iA_i,\\ B^{*}=\\sum_ip_iB_i\\ (\\text{per token, per layer});\\ h'=W_0h+s\\,B^{*}A^{*}h;\\ \\text{no training}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(router computed from the library by SVD)}","locus":"every LoRA-adapted linear layer (the paper patches attention layers only; Phi-2 and Mistral-7B, LoRA rank 4)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Data-free MoE router whose rows are the leading right singular vectors of each library LoRA, with sign-agnostic top-\\(k\\) scores; composition mixes factors in the paper and mixes expert outputs (sum of \\(B_iA_ix\\)) in the HF PEFT implementation. MBC builds the library by clustering tasks on LoRA-parameter similarity.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Each token at each layer uses its own expert mixture.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free routing over trained LoRAs).","key_idea":"The direction a LoRA amplifies most (its top right singular vector) is a free prototype of the inputs it was trained for, so a router can be read off the adapters themselves without any data or training.","results_note":"With LoRA libraries built from 256 Flan tasks on Phi-2 and Mistral, Arrow routing beats other zero-shot routing for private libraries and nearly matches oracle routing upstream; MBC libraries match or exceed multitask training.","relations":[{"target":"phatgoose","type":"competes-with","note":"zero-shot token-level routing among independent LoRAs"},{"target":"polytropon","type":"builds-on","note":"same composition operator, routing obtained post hoc"},{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"LoraHub is used as a supervised routing baseline"},{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Learned vs training-free LoRA mixture."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.11157","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11157","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/arrow.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Oleksiy Ostapenko","Zhan Su","Edoardo Maria Ponti","Laurent Charlin","Nicolas Le Roux","Matheus Pereira","Lucas Caccia","Alessandro Sordoni"],"bibkey":"ostapenko2024modular","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ostapenko2024modular,\n  title = {{Towards Modular LLMs by Building and Reusing a Library of LoRAs}},\n  author = {Oleksiy Ostapenko and Zhan Su and Edoardo Maria Ponti and Laurent Charlin and Nicolas Le Roux and Matheus Pereira and Lucas Caccia and Alessandro Sordoni},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2405.11157},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.11157}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens and layers with zero trainable parameters. The router rows \\(v_i\\) are read off the library (first right singular vector of \\(B_iA_i\\)), so the inputs are P-type, but the output is an \\(X\\)-family, not one point of \\(\\Theta\\).","shape":"Token-dependent mixture: function-level.","base_point":"The top-\\(k\\) softmax weights sum to \\(1\\) over trained nonzero LoRAs, and nothing returns \\(f\\): unpointed.","gauge":"n/a: there are no trainable parameters. The router is gauge-invariant: \\(v_i\\) depends only on \\(B_iA_i\\), and \\(\\lvert v_i^\\top h\\rvert\\) removes the SVD sign. The paper's factor-wise composition \\((\\sum p_iB_i)(\\sum p_iA_i)\\) is not a function of the adapters (Prop V.4(a)); HF PEFT's output mixing \\(\\sum_ip_is_iB_iA_ih\\) is.","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"Each token at each layer uses its own mixture.","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"Arrow is a data-free \\(X\\)-point of a LoRA library whose router is a gauge-invariant function of the adapters. In the paper its composition is LoraHub's product of sums evaluated per token, which is gauge-dependent and lands in LoRA\\(_r\\); HF PEFT's output mixing is the gauge-invariant CAT form and lands in LoRA\\(_{kr}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_ip_i(x)B_i,\\ \\sum_ip_i(x)A_i\\big)","note":"Paper composition, fibrewise over tokens and layers: a point of LoRA\\(_r\\) (rank \\(r\\), not \\(kr\\)), with LoRA pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_i=p_i(x)\\,s_i\\big)_i","note":"HF output mixing, per adapted module: a point of CAT's family (unpointed slice)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"phatgoose","test":"O1","reason":"For \\(k\\ge2\\) the paper's per-token weight contains cross terms \\(p_ip_jB_iA_j\\) (\\(i\\ne j\\)) outside \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{B_zA_z\\}\\), which contains every PHATGOOSE per-token update."},{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"Arrow's weights change per token and per layer, so its realised functions are generically not \\(\\Phi_f\\) of one weight. LoraHub's single global \\(w\\) gives one static weight."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"loraretriever","name":"LoraRetriever","full_name":"LoraRetriever: input-aware LoRA retrieval and composition","paper_title":"LoraRetriever: Input-Aware LoRA Retrieval and Composition for Mixed Tasks in the Wild","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.09997","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.263/","code_url":"https://github.com/StyxXuan/LoraRetriever","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"E(\\phi)=\\tfrac{1}{m}\\sum_{i=1}^{m}E(I\\oplus x_{i\\phi}),\\ \\ s(x,\\phi)=\\cos\\big(E(I\\oplus x),E(\\phi)\\big),\\ \\ \\Phi_x=\\operatorname{TopK}_{\\phi}\\,s(x,\\phi);\\ \\text{Mixture: }\\Delta h=\\tfrac{1}{k}\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\Phi_x}B_{\\phi}A_{\\phi}x;\\ \\text{Fusion: }\\Theta=\\tfrac{1}{k}\\sum_{\\phi\\in\\Phi_x}\\Theta_{\\phi};\\ \\text{retriever }E\\text{ contrastively instruction-tuned; LLM and LoRAs frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (only the sentence-embedding retriever is fine-tuned once)}","locus":"LoRA-adapted layers of the LLM, selected per input prompt (Llama-2 in the paper)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Retrieve-then-compose: an embedding retriever selects the top-\\(k\\) LoRAs per input, which are combined by output averaging (mixture) or parameter averaging (fusion); batched inference over heterogeneous requests.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The selected LoRAs change from input to input (fusion could be merged per request only).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free composition of trained LoRAs).","key_idea":"In the wild, prompts from many tasks arrive and the LoRA pool keeps changing, so route by retrieval: embed each LoRA by a few of its training samples and retrieve the most similar LoRAs for each input.","results_note":"On mixed-task evaluation with Llama-2 LoRA pools, LoraRetriever consistently outperforms MoE-style routing, SMEAR, AdapterSoup and LoraHub baselines in- and out-of-distribution.","relations":[{"target":"adaptersoup","type":"generalizes","note":"per-input retrieval instead of per-domain selection"},{"target":"arrow","type":"competes-with","note":"routing among an open LoRA library"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09997","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.09997","https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.263/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ziyu Zhao","Leilei Gan","Guoyin Wang","Wangchunshu Zhou","Hongxia Yang","Kun Kuang","Fei Wu"],"bibkey":"zhao2024loraretriever","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2024loraretriever,\n  title = {{LoraRetriever: Input-Aware LoRA Retrieval and Composition for Mixed Tasks in the Wild}},\n  author = {Ziyu Zhao and Leilei Gan and Guoyin Wang and Wangchunshu Zhou and Hongxia Yang and Kun Kuang and Fei Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024},\n  eprint = {2402.09997},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.263/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over input prompts: a contrastively trained retriever selects the top-\\(k\\) LoRAs \\(\\Phi_x\\) for each prompt. The LLM and the LoRAs are frozen.","shape":"Piecewise constant in the prompt: each request sees one of finitely many weights, so the method is compared through the realisation.","base_point":"Every request averages \\(k\\) trained nonzero LoRAs, and no choice returns \\(\\theta_0\\): unpointed.","gauge":"n/a: no trainable parameters on the LLM side. Mixture, \\(\\frac1k\\sum B_\\phi A_\\phi x\\), is a function of the adapters. Fusion averages the factors, \\((\\frac1k\\sum B_\\phi)(\\frac1k\\sum A_\\phi)\\), which is gauge-dependent (Prop V.4(a)). The retriever \\(E\\) is contrastively trained once, offline and independently of the LLM, and is treated as fixed design data rather than as \\(Q\\).","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"M∞ as a family, because the selection depends on the input. Per request the selected update is a static matrix, so it is M1 per request in both modes.","rebasing":"n/a."},"categorical_reading":"LoraRetriever is a hard-retrieval \\(X\\)-family over a LoRA library (L2P's construction, with LoRAs in place of prompts), so its weights are piecewise constant in the prompt. Per request, mixture is the point \\(\\alpha=\\mathbf 1_{\\Phi_x}/k\\) of CAT and fusion is the same point of LoraHub's product of sums, so the two modes differ exactly by LoraHub's cross terms.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_\\phi=\\mathbf 1[\\phi\\in\\Phi_x]/k\\big)_\\phi","note":"Mixture mode, per request: a point of CAT's family (unpointed slice)."},{"target":"lorahub","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Phi_x}/k","note":"Fusion mode, per request: LoraHub's \\((\\sum w_iB_i)(\\sum w_jA_j)\\) at \\(w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Phi_x}/k\\) (unpointed slice)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"x-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Retrieval is piecewise constant in the prompt, so for generic libraries the realised functions jump at retrieval boundaries. X-LoRA's default dense softmax scalings vary continuously and never vanish, so that mode cannot reproduce them (X-LoRA's optional top-\\(k\\) mode is not covered by this argument)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"lora-flow","name":"LoRA-Flow","full_name":"LoRA-Flow: dynamic token-level LoRA fusion","paper_title":"LoRA-Flow: Dynamic LoRA Fusion for Large Language Models in Generative Tasks","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.11455","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.695/","code_url":"https://github.com/thunlp/LoRAFlow","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"w^{l}=\\operatorname{softmax}\\big(W_{\\mathrm{gate}}^{l}x_t^{l}+b^{l}\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k},\\quad h'=h+[\\Delta h_1;\\dots;\\Delta h_k]\\,w^{l},\\ \\ \\Delta h_i=B_iA_ix;\\ \\text{only }W_{\\mathrm{gate}}^{l}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k\\times d},\\ b^{l}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k}\\text{ trained (about 200 examples); model and LoRAs frozen}","trainable_params":"k(d+1)\\ \\text{per layer (shared by all LoRA modules of that layer)}","locus":"all LoRA-adapted modules; one fusion gate per layer driven by the layer input","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Token- and layer-wise softmax gate over the outputs of \\(k\\) frozen LoRAs (output mixing, which also allows LoRAs of different ranks).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Fusion weights change at every generation step.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (fusion gate over trained LoRAs).","key_idea":"In generative tasks different tokens need different skills (e.g. language vs math LoRA for a Chinese math problem), so LoRA fusion weights should be predicted per token from the current hidden state rather than fixed per task.","results_note":"Across six generative tasks with Llama-2, LoRA-Flow consistently outperforms task-level fusion baselines such as LoraHub, with a gate of about 0.2% of a LoRA's parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lorahub","type":"competes-with","note":"token-level output-space fusion vs LoraHub task-level factor-space composition; LoraHub cross terms B_iA_j are not representable by LoRA-Flow, so neither generalizes the other; LoraHub is LoRA-Flow's main baseline"},{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"token-level gating of frozen LoRAs"},{"target":"mole","type":"competes-with","note":"learned gating of trained LoRAs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11455","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.11455","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.695/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Hanqing Wang","Bowen Ping","Shuo Wang","Xu Han","Yun Chen","Zhiyuan Liu","Maosong Sun"],"bibkey":"wang2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-Flow: Dynamic LoRA Fusion for Large Language Models in Generative Tasks}},\n  author = {Hanqing Wang and Bowen Ping and Shuo Wang and Xu Han and Yun Chen and Zhiyuan Liu and Maosong Sun},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.11455},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.695/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens and layers: \\(w^l=\\mathrm{softmax}(W^l_{\\rm gate}x_t^l+b^l)\\) weights the outputs of \\(k\\) frozen LoRAs.","shape":"Token-dependent mixture: function-level.","base_point":"Softmax weights sum to \\(1\\) over frozen nonzero LoRAs, so no gate reproduces \\(f\\): unpointed (as MoLE and X-LoRA).","gauge":"Only the gate is trained. Its fibre is the softmax translation \\((W,b)\\mapsto(W+\\mathbf 1c^\\top,b+c_0\\mathbf 1)\\), of dimension \\(d+1\\) per layer. That is an affine shift rather than a wire gauge, recorded as 'none' as for X-LoRA.","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"Fusion weights change at every generation step.","rebasing":"n/a."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Flow is an \\(X\\)-point of CAT: per token and layer its weight is \\(W_0+\\sum_iw_i^l(x)B_iA_i\\) with \\(w^l\\) in the open simplex, so it mixes products and is gauge-invariant, with none of LoraHub's cross terms. Its gate reads the current layer input, whereas X-LoRA's head reads a first-pass hidden state.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(W_{\\rm gate},b)=\\big(\\alpha_i^{l}=w_i^{l}(x)\\big)_{i,l}","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorahub","test":"O1","reason":"Even with a constant gate, LoRA-Flow's update \\(\\sum_iw_iB_iA_i\\) (\\(w\\) in the open simplex) would need LoraHub's cross terms to vanish, which for a generic library forces a single nonzero weight. With a token-dependent gate its functions are not \\(\\Phi_f\\) of any one weight."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"s-lora","name":"S-LoRA","full_name":"S-LoRA: scalable serving of many LoRA adapters","paper_title":"S-LoRA: Serving Thousands of Concurrent LoRA Adapters","authors":"Sheng et al.","year":2023,"venue":"MLSys 2024","arxiv":"2311.03285","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03285","code_url":"https://github.com/S-LoRA/S-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_b=W_0x_b+B_{a(b)}A_{a(b)}x_b;\\quad \\text{request }b\\text{ uses adapter }a(b);\\quad W_0X\\ \\text{batched over all requests};\\quad \\text{LoRA terms by gather kernels},\\ \\text{ranks may differ};\\quad \\text{(MBGMM for prefill, MBGMV for decode)};\\quad \\text{adapters and KV cache paged together};\\quad \\text{in one Unified Memory Pool}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(inference system)}","locus":"LLM inference server (adapters on all LoRA-adapted projections)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Separates the batchable shared-base computation \\(W_0X\\) from per-request low-rank terms; Unified Paging of variable-rank adapter weights with KV-cache blocks, custom heterogeneous-batching kernels and LoRA-aware tensor parallelism.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Adapters are deliberately left unmerged so requests for thousands of different adapters can share one base-model batch; any single adapter could be merged.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (serving system).","key_idea":"Many LoRAs derived from one base model can be served together: keep one copy of the base weights, batch its computation across all requests, and fetch and apply each request's small adapter with paged memory and custom kernels.","results_note":"Serves 2,000 adapters on a single A100 with small overhead and up to 4x the throughput of vLLM-packed (and far more than HF PEFT), increasing the number of served adapters by orders of magnitude.","relations":[{"target":"punica","type":"builds-on","note":"decode kernel extends an early Punica SGMV kernel"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"exploits the additive low-rank form xW_0 + xBA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03285","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.03285","https://proceedings.mlsys.org/paper_files/paper/2024"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Ying Sheng","Shiyi Cao","Dacheng Li","Coleman Hooper","Nicholas Lee","Shuo Yang","Christopher Chou","Banghua Zhu","Lianmin Zheng","Kurt Keutzer","Joseph E. Gonzalez","Ion Stoica"],"bibkey":"sheng2023s","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sheng2023s,\n  title = {{S-LoRA: Serving Thousands of Concurrent LoRA Adapters}},\n  author = {Ying Sheng and Shiyi Cao and Dacheng Li and Coleman Hooper and Nicholas Lee and Shuo Yang and Christopher Chou and Banghua Zhu and Lianmin Zheng and Kurt Keutzer and Joseph E. Gonzalez and Ion Stoica},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of Machine Learning and Systems (MLSys)},\n  eprint = {2311.03285},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03285}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":null,"gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Table VIII.B row F\\(_U\\): a family over tenants \\(U\\), \\(u\\mapsto(B_u,A_u)\\), served as \\(W_0X\\) batched once plus per-request low-rank terms (MBGMM/MBGMV kernels, unified paging).","shape":"Each fibre is a point of LoRA\\(_{r_u}\\) (heterogeneous ranks). Table VIII.B describes the row as 'relative computation over a shared base'; the column records the fibre's cone.","base_point":"n/a: a serving system has no trainable coordinates and no base point of its own (– in VIII.B). The base model is the fibre over a tenant with no adapter.","gauge":"Outputs depend on each tenant's factors only through \\(B_u(A_ux)\\), so \\(\\prod_uGL_{r_u}\\) acts on the stored adapters without changing anything served. The system never combines factors across tenants (– in VIII.B; filled in from the formula).","covariance":"Each fibre is LoRA's basis-free cone (– in VIII.B).","merge":"'Unmerged by design' (VIII.B): every fibre fuses (M1), but merging would forfeit the shared base batch, and no single weight serves all tenants.","rebasing":"n/a: nothing is trained."},"categorical_reading":"S-LoRA computes the family \\(\\rho\\circ a:U\\to\\Theta\\) relative to the shared base: it batches \\(W_0X\\) once and adds each fibre's \\(\\delta_u=B_uA_u\\), which works because LoRA is additive (\\(\\rho=\\theta_0+\\delta\\)). As a family it is CAT evaluated at the coordinate vertices \\(e_u\\), and it is gauge-safe because it only ever uses products.","entry_type":"software","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"punica","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\prod_uQ_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_u}}}","note":"The same family over tenants. The systems differ only in kernels and memory management, which \\(\\rho\\) does not see."},{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_u(\\ast)=e_u","note":"Fibrewise over tenants: request \\(u\\) is CAT at the vertex \\(\\alpha=e_u\\) (unpointed slice)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loramoe","test":"O1","reason":"The same prompt from two tenants is served with two different adapters, but an input-routed mixture sees only the tokens."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"punica","name":"Punica","full_name":"Punica: multi-tenant LoRA serving with SGMV","paper_title":"Punica: Multi-Tenant LoRA Serving","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2023,"venue":"MLSys 2024","arxiv":"2310.18547","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18547","code_url":"https://github.com/punica-ai/punica","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_b=W_0x_b+B_{a(b)}A_{a(b)}x_b;\\ \\text{requests sorted into segments }[s_i,s_{i+1})\\text{ sharing adapter }i:\\ \\ Y_{[s_i:s_{i+1}]}\\mathrel{+}=X_{[s_i:s_{i+1}]}A_i^{\\top}B_i^{\\top}\\ \\ (\\text{SGMV-shrink then SGMV-expand})","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(inference system)}","locus":"LLM inference on a shared GPU cluster (LoRA on q, k, v, o and MLP projections)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Segmented Gather Matrix-Vector multiplication batches the low-rank terms of different LoRA models in one kernel, so each GPU keeps a single copy of the base model; a scheduler consolidates tenants and migrates requests.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Adapters stay unmerged to batch heterogeneous tenants; each individual adapter is mergeable.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (serving system).","key_idea":"Batching requests for different LoRAs is almost as cheap as batching requests for the same LoRA if the low-rank terms are computed by a segmented gather kernel, so multi-tenant LoRA serving can be consolidated onto few GPUs.","results_note":"Achieves 12x higher throughput than state-of-the-art LLM serving systems for multi-LoRA workloads while adding only about 2 ms latency per token.","relations":[{"target":"s-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent multi-LoRA serving system"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"serves LoRA models"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18547","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.18547"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Lequn Chen","Zihao Ye","Yongji Wu","Danyang Zhuo","Luis Ceze","Arvind Krishnamurthy"],"bibkey":"chen2023punica","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2023punica,\n  title = {{Punica: Multi-Tenant LoRA Serving}},\n  author = {Lequn Chen and Zihao Ye and Yongji Wu and Danyang Zhuo and Luis Ceze and Arvind Krishnamurthy},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of Machine Learning and Systems (MLSys)},\n  eprint = {2310.18547},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.18547}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":null,"gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Table VIII.B row F\\(_U\\): a family over tenants, \\(u\\mapsto(B_u,A_u)\\). Requests are sorted into segments sharing an adapter, and SGMV shrink/expand computes \\(X_{[s_i:s_{i+1}]}A_i^\\top B_i^\\top\\).","shape":"Each fibre is a point of LoRA\\(_{r_u}\\) (heterogeneous ranks). Table VIII.B describes the row as 'relative computation over a shared base'; the column records the fibre's cone.","base_point":"n/a: a serving system has no trainable coordinates and no base point of its own (– in VIII.B). The base model is the fibre over a tenant with no adapter.","gauge":"Outputs depend on each tenant's factors only through \\(B_u(A_ux)\\), so \\(\\prod_uGL_{r_u}\\) acts on the stored adapters without changing anything served. The system never combines factors across tenants (– in VIII.B; filled in from the formula).","covariance":"Each fibre is LoRA's basis-free cone (– in VIII.B).","merge":"'Unmerged by design' (VIII.B): every fibre fuses (M1), but merging would forfeit the shared base batch, and no single weight serves all tenants.","rebasing":"n/a: nothing is trained."},"categorical_reading":"Punica is the same family \\(U\\to\\Theta\\) as S-LoRA: SGMV evaluates \\(\\delta_u=B_uA_u\\) on the fibre over each segment of a tenant-sorted batch, on top of one copy of \\(W_0\\). The construction uses only that LoRA is additive and that each fibre is a product, so it is gauge-safe.","entry_type":"software","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"s-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\prod_uQ_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_u}}}","note":"The same family over tenants; only the kernels differ."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_u(\\ast)=(B_u,\\ A_u)","note":"Fibrewise over tenants, into LoRA\\(_{r_u}\\) pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\) (unpointed slice)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"x-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Punica's family is indexed by tenants, so the same prompt from two tenants is served with two different adapters. X-LoRA's scalings are computed from the tokens alone, so in any mode it gives the same prompt the same scalings; with its default dense softmax they are also strictly positive on every adapter, so for linearly independent adapters it never serves exactly one of them."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"hyperdreambooth","name":"HyperDreamBooth","full_name":"HyperDreamBooth with Lightweight DreamBooth (LiDB)","paper_title":"HyperDreamBooth: HyperNetworks for Fast Personalization of Text-to-Image Models","authors":"Ruiz et al.","year":2023,"venue":"CVPR 2024","arxiv":"2307.06949","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06949","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat\\theta=\\mathcal{H}(x)\\ (\\text{one face image}\\to\\text{LiDB weights by iterative refinement from }\\theta^{(0)}=0);\\quad \\Delta W=P_{out}\\,B_{tr}A_{tr}\\,P_{in},\\ P_{out}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times b},\\ P_{in}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{a\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen random row-orthogonal},\\ B_{tr}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times r},\\ A_{tr}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times a},\\ r=1,\\ a=100,\\ b=50;\\ \\text{then rank-relaxed fast fine-tuning (LoRA with }r>1\\text{, 40 iterations) from }W_0+\\Delta W(\\hat\\theta);\\ \\text{paper's notation: }\\Delta W x=A_{aux}A_{train}B_{train}B_{aux}","trainable_params":"r(a+b)\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (}\\approx30\\text{K values, }\\approx120\\text{ KB for the whole model); hypernetwork trained once}","locus":"attention layers of the Stable Diffusion UNet (and text encoder)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Hypernetwork prediction of a rank-1 LoRA expressed in a further-compressed random orthogonal sub-basis (LiDB), used as initialization for a brief higher-rank LoRA fine-tune.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both the predicted LiDB update and the refined LoRA are additive low-rank updates that can be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable at generation time (predicted weights are non-zero); the hypernetwork's iterative refinement starts from zero weights.","key_idea":"Shrink the personalization weight space enough (LiDB) that a hypernetwork can predict it from a single face image, then finish with a short rank-relaxed fine-tune for fidelity.","results_note":"Personalizes faces in about 20 seconds from one image, 25x faster than DreamBooth and 125x faster than Textual Inversion, with a model 10,000x smaller than DreamBooth at similar quality.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"predicts and refines LoRA weights"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue: both train a tiny core between frozen random projections (LiDB: dense r = 1 factors inside random orthogonal bases; VeRA: scaling vectors around shared random matrices)"},{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"hypernetwork-generated LoRAs from a conditioning input"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06949","https://arxiv.org/html/2307.06949"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"authors_full":["Nataniel Ruiz","Yuanzhen Li","Varun Jampani","Wei Wei","Tingbo Hou","Yael Pritch","Neal Wadhwa","Michael Rubinstein","Kfir Aberman"],"bibkey":"ruiz2023hyperdreambooth","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ruiz2023hyperdreambooth,\n  title = {{HyperDreamBooth: HyperNetworks for Fast Personalization of Text-to-Image Models}},\n  author = {Nataniel Ruiz and Yuanzhen Li and Varun Jampani and Wei Wei and Tingbo Hou and Yael Pritch and Neal Wadhwa and Michael Rubinstein and Kfir Aberman},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2307.06949},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.06949}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over identities \\(T\\) (one face image each): a hypernetwork \\(\\mathcal H\\) predicts LiDB weights \\(\\hat\\theta=(B_{tr},A_{tr})\\). A short rank-relaxed LoRA fine-tune then starts from \\(W_0+\\Delta W(\\hat\\theta)\\).","shape":"Each fibre of the prediction stage is the cone \\(W_0+P_{out}\\mathcal M_{\\le r}(b\\times a)P_{in}\\) inside frozen random frames, of dimension \\(r(a+b-r)=149\\) at \\(r=1\\).","base_point":"The fast fine-tune starts at the predicted weights, a pointing defect \\(e=P_{out}\\hat B\\hat AP_{in}\\) by design. LiDB itself starts the hypernetwork's iterative refinement at \\((0,0)\\), the origin pointing with \\(S_1=0\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the LiDB rank wire, \\((B_{tr}g^{-1},gA_{tr})\\), which is \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) at the paper's \\(r=1\\). The refine stage carries LoRA's \\(GL_{r'}\\).","covariance":"The frozen row-orthogonal \\(P_{out},P_{in}\\) are random, so covariance holds only in law.","merge":"Both stages are additive low-rank updates that fold into \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"A two-stage scheme: a base change to the predicted point, then LoRA\\(_{r'}\\) at the new base. Its reach is the sum of the shapes visited, \\(W_0+P_{out}\\mathcal M_{\\le r}P_{in}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r'}\\)."},"categorical_reading":"HyperDreamBooth is a \\(T\\)-point of a NOLA-type sandwich (LoRA\\(_r\\) inside frozen random frames), used as a predicted base change, after which ordinary LoRA runs at the new base. The small LiDB frames are what make the map \\(T\\to Q\\) learnable from one image.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"nola","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_{tr},A_{tr})=\\big(\\alpha=\\mathrm{vec}\\,A_{tr},\\ \\beta=\\mathrm{vec}\\,B_{tr}\\big),\\ \\text{NOLA bases }\\{p_ke_j^\\top\\},\\{e_jq_l^\\top\\}","note":"With \\(p_k\\) the columns of \\(P_{out}\\) and \\(q_l^\\top\\) the rows of \\(P_{in}\\), LiDB is NOLA with orthonormal random bases (at \\(r=1\\) the coefficients are exactly \\(A_{tr}\\) and \\(B_{tr}\\)). Pointed at the origin."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_{tr},A_{tr})=\\big(P_{out}B_{tr},\\ A_{tr}P_{in}\\big)","note":"Per identity, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at the origin \\((0,0)\\), where \\(S_1=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"LiDB's row spaces sweep the \\(a\\)-dimensional \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,P_{in}\\) (any vector of it at \\(r=1\\)), while VeRA's updates \\(\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_dA\\) keep their rows in the fixed \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\). With independent frames \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,P_{in}\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"text-to-lora","name":"Text-to-LoRA (T2L)","full_name":"Text-to-LoRA: instant transformer adaptation from task descriptions","paper_title":"Text-to-LoRA: Instant Transformer Adaption","authors":"Charakorn et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2506.06105","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06105","code_url":"https://github.com/SakanaAI/text-to-lora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W^{i}_{m,l}=B^{i}_{m,l}A^{i}_{m,l}=h_{\\theta}\\big(\\operatorname{concat}[f(z^{i}),E[m],E[l]]\\big),\\ z^{i}\\text{: natural-language task description};\\quad \\theta=\\arg\\min_{\\theta}\\mathbb{E}_{i,z}\\,\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}\\big(\\mathcal{D}^{i},\\Psi,h_{\\theta}(\\phi^{i})\\big)\\ \\text{or reconstruction }\\mathbb{E}\\,|\\Delta W^{i}-h_{\\theta}(\\phi^{i})|;\\ \\text{base LLM }\\Psi\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"\\text{hypernetwork only, shared by all tasks: }55{,}252{,}992\\ (\\text{L}),\\ 34{,}282{,}240\\ (\\text{M}),\\ 4{,}923{,}392\\ (\\text{S})\\ \\text{for Mistral-7B q/v LoRAs of rank 8 (output heads }d_{out}\\cdot2rd,\\ d_{out}\\cdot rd,\\ d_{emb}\\cdot d,\\ d_{out}=512);\\ \\text{each generated adapter has }r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ entries per module (3.4M in total); zero training per new task}","locus":"LoRA target modules (e.g. query/value projections) of every layer of the base LLM","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Amortized adapter generation: one MLP hypernetwork maps (task-description embedding, module-type embedding, layer embedding) to the rank-\\(r\\) factors of every LoRA in a single batched forward pass.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The generated adapter is an ordinary LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Bias-HyperInit (App. G): the hypernetwork's output head starts with zero weights, a \\(U(-1/d, 1/d)\\) bias for \\(A\\) and a zero bias for \\(B\\), so the untrained hypernetwork emits \\(B = 0\\) (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)). Trained, it emits non-zero task-specific adapters.","key_idea":"Train a hypernetwork that turns a text description of a task into a LoRA in one forward pass, either by compressing a library of LoRAs or end-to-end with SFT, enabling zero-shot adaptation to unseen tasks from language alone.","results_note":"Reconstruction-trained on 9 benchmark LoRAs, T2L's generated adapters match the task-specific LoRAs on their test sets. Trained on hundreds of tasks (479 SNI tasks, SFT), it generates adapters zero-shot for entirely unseen tasks from their descriptions.","relations":[{"target":"hyperformer","type":"builds-on","note":"task-conditioned hypernetwork for PEFT modules"},{"target":"doc-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same group; D2L conditions on documents and uses context distillation"},{"target":"drag-and-drop-llms","type":"competes-with","note":"prompt-conditioned LoRA generation"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"generates LoRA factors"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06105","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.06105","https://pub.sakana.ai/doc-to-lora/","https://github.com/SakanaAI/text-to-lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","frontier"],"authors_full":["Rujikorn Charakorn","Edoardo Cetin","Yujin Tang","Robert Tjarko Lange"],"bibkey":"charakorn2025text","bibtex":"@inproceedings{charakorn2025text,\n  title = {{Text-to-LoRA: Instant Transformer Adaption}},\n  author = {Rujikorn Charakorn and Edoardo Cetin and Yujin Tang and Robert Tjarko Lange},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2506.06105},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06105}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Table VIII.B row 'Text-to-LoRA / HyperFormer': a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over task descriptions, \\((z,m,l)\\mapsto h_\\theta(\\mathrm{concat}[f(z),E[m],E[l]])=(B,A)\\).","shape":"Each task fibre is a point of LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) ('a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA', VIII.B).","base_point":"Bias-HyperInit sets the output-head weights and the \\(B\\)-bias to zero and the \\(A\\)-bias to a fixed \\(U(-1/d,1/d)\\) draw. So the untrained hypernetwork emits \\((0,A_0)\\) for every task, LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex, with \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\) per task. VIII.B leaves this cell blank.","gauge":"A global \\(GL_r\\) on the rank wire of the generated adapters, absorbed by the L head's separate linear \\(A\\)- and \\(B\\)-outputs. The M and S heads share weights across \\(A\\), \\(B\\) (and ranks), so only part of \\(GL_r\\) lifts to their parameters; the key records the fibre gauge, as for HyperFormer. VIII.B: factor targets are gauge-sensitive, i.e. the reconstruction objective regresses onto factor representatives of the library LoRAs and is not \\(\\prod GL_r\\)-invariant. The SFT objective is.","covariance":"The generated factors are dense, so each fibre is basis-free (– in VIII.B).","merge":"M1 per task (VIII.B): the generated adapter is an ordinary LoRA.","rebasing":"n/a: a zero-shot family whose hypernetwork is trained for \\(\\theta_0\\) only, so merge-and-restart is undefined (– in VIII.B)."},"categorical_reading":"T2L is a \\(T\\)-point \\(a:T\\to Q_{\\rm LoRA}\\) over natural-language task descriptions, and Bias-HyperInit points the whole family at LoRA's standard apex, so fibrewise it is a pointed arrow into LoRA. Its reconstruction mode regresses onto factors, the gauge-sensitive target Table VIII.B warns about; SFT mode depends only on \\(BA\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_z(\\theta)=h_\\theta\\big(\\mathrm{concat}[f(z),E[m],E[l]]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\). The arrow is pointed, because Bias-HyperInit gives \\(h_z(q_0)=(0,A_0)\\) for every \\(z\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"moelora","test":"O1","reason":"MOELoRA's task updates \\(\\sum_i\\omega_{\\tau i}B_iA_i\\) all have column space in \\(\\mathrm{col}[B_1\\cdots B_N]\\) and row space in \\(\\mathrm{row}[A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\), both \\(r\\)-dimensional. T2L's \\(B(z)\\) varies with the task, so two tasks can need more than \\(r\\) column directions."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"drag-and-drop-llms","name":"DnD","full_name":"Drag-and-Drop LLMs: zero-shot prompt-to-weights","paper_title":"Drag-and-Drop LLMs: Zero-Shot Prompt-to-Weights","authors":"Liang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2506.16406","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16406","code_url":"https://github.com/jerryliang24/Drag-and-Drop-LLMs","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"c_i=\\operatorname{Enc}(p_i)\\ (\\text{frozen Sentence-BERT over a batch of unlabeled task prompts}),\\quad \\hat\\Theta_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}=G_{\\phi}(c_i)\\ (\\text{cascaded hyper-convolutional decoder}\\to\\text{tokenized LoRA weights});\\quad \\phi=\\arg\\min_{\\phi}\\big\\|G_{\\phi}(c_i)-\\operatorname{tok}(m_j)\\big\\|_2^2\\ \\text{over random prompt-checkpoint pairs }(p_i,m_j);\\ \\text{base LLM frozen}","trainable_params":"\\text{generator only; zero training per new dataset (one forward pass)}","locus":"all LoRA matrices of the base LLM (Qwen2.5 family in the paper)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Prompt-conditioned parameter generator: embeddings of a few unlabeled prompts are decoded by convolutional blocks into the full set of tokenized LoRA weights, trained by MSE regression onto collected checkpoints.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The generated parameters form an ordinary LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (generated weights).","key_idea":"Unlabeled prompts act as a fingerprint of a dataset, so a generator trained on prompt-checkpoint pairs can map new prompts directly to task-specific LoRA weights without any gradient-based tuning.","results_note":"Generates task-specific LoRAs in seconds with up to 12,000x lower overhead than full fine-tuning and average gains up to 30% over the strongest training LoRAs on unseen commonsense, math, coding and multimodal benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"prompt samples vs task descriptions as the condition"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"generates LoRA weights"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16406","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.16406","https://mlanthology.org/neurips/2025/liang2025neurips-draganddrop/","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/loc/san-diego/poster/116841","https://github.com/jerryliang24/Drag-and-Drop-LLMs"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","frontier"],"authors_full":["Zhiyuan Liang","Dongwen Tang","Yuhao Zhou","Xuanlei Zhao","Mingjia Shi","Wangbo Zhao","Zekai Li","Peihao Wang","Konstantin Schürholt","Damian Borth","Michael M. Bronstein","Yang You","Zhangyang Wang","Kai Wang"],"bibkey":"liang2025drag","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liang2025drag,\n  title = {{Drag-and-Drop LLMs: Zero-Shot Prompt-to-Weights}},\n  author = {Zhiyuan Liang and Dongwen Tang and Yuhao Zhou and Xuanlei Zhao and Mingjia Shi and Wangbo Zhao and Zekai Li and Peihao Wang and Konstantin Schürholt and Damian Borth and Michael M. Bronstein and Yang You and Zhangyang Wang and Kai Wang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2506.16406},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16406}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over datasets, each represented by a batch of unlabeled prompts: \\(c=\\mathrm{Enc}(p)\\mapsto G_\\phi(c)\\), the tokenized weights of every LoRA.","shape":"Each fibre is a point of LoRA's cone.","base_point":"No neutral initialisation: an untrained generator emits nonzero weights, so the family starts with a defect (as HyperFormer).","gauge":"The generated adapters enter only through \\(BA\\), so each fibre carries LoRA's \\(GL_r\\). DnD regresses (MSE) onto tokenized factors of collected checkpoints, a gauge-sensitive target (VIII.B), since checkpoints from different runs sit at unrelated \\(GL_r\\) representatives.","covariance":"Dense generated factors: basis-free fibres.","merge":"M1 per dataset: the output is an ordinary LoRA.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot family."},"categorical_reading":"DnD is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over prompt batches, learned by regression onto factor tokens. That target is not a function of the adapters, so the generator must also learn the gauge choices of its training checkpoints; a product-level target, or a joint gauge fixing (Prop V.4(b)), would remove that burden.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\mathrm{detok}\\big(G_\\phi(c)\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over prompt batches, into LoRA pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"doc-to-lora","name":"Doc-to-LoRA (D2L)","full_name":"Doc-to-LoRA: meta-learned context distillation into LoRA","paper_title":"Doc-to-LoRA: Learning to Instantly Internalize Contexts","authors":"Charakorn et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2602.15902","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15902","code_url":"https://github.com/SakanaAI/doc-to-lora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_l=B_lA_l=h_{\\phi}(Z_{l-1}),\\ \\ U_l=\\operatorname{XAttn}\\big(Q_m,K(Z_{l-1}),V(Z_{l-1})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_u}\\ (\\text{each latent}\\to\\text{a row of }A_l\\text{ and a column of }B_l),\\ W'_l=W_l+\\alpha_lB_lA_l;\\quad \\min_{\\phi}\\mathbb{E}\\,\\mathrm{KL}\\big(p_{\\theta}(y\\mid x,c)\\,\\|\\,p_{\\theta+H_{\\phi}(c)}(y\\mid x)\\big);\\ \\text{chunks: }A_l=[A_l^{(1)};\\dots;A_l^{(K)}],\\ B_l=[B_l^{(1)}\\cdots B_l^{(K)}]","trainable_params":"\\text{hypernetwork only (Perceiver with 8 cross-attention blocks, }309\\text{M parameters for Gemma-2-2b-it, Sec. 5); learnable per-layer scales }\\alpha_l;\\ \\text{zero training per document; generated adapter }Kr(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ per down-projection for }K\\text{ chunks}","locus":"the down-projection of every MLP block of the target LLM (rank-8 LoRAs per chunk); per-layer context activations of the frozen LLM feed the hypernetwork","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Meta-learned context distillation: a Perceiver hypernetwork reads the frozen LLM's per-layer activations of a document and emits rank-\\(r\\) LoRA factors; long documents are composed by rank-concatenation (direct sum) of per-chunk adapters, giving rank \\(rK\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The generated adapter is an ordinary LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (generated weights); a learnable per-layer scale \\(\\alpha_l\\) modulates the update.","key_idea":"Amortize context distillation: instead of distilling each document into weights by gradient descent, train a hypernetwork once so that any new document is internalized into a LoRA in one sub-second forward pass, removing it from the context window.","results_note":"Achieves near-perfect zero-shot needle-in-a-haystack accuracy at lengths more than 4x the target LLM's native context window and outperforms standard context distillation on QA with much lower memory and update latency.","relations":[{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"extends T2L-style hypernetworks from task descriptions to documents"},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"dual-to","note":"App. D shows the same hypernetwork can instead emit a compressed KV cache (prefix-tuning parametrization): weight-space vs KV-space internalization of one context"},{"target":"generative-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Both map a context to a parameter delta in one forward pass."},{"target":"cartridges","type":"competes-with","note":"Cartridges also stores a document in trainable parameters (a KV prefix), but trains per document by self-study."},{"target":"shine","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent context-to-LoRA hypernetwork."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15902","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.15902","https://pub.sakana.ai/doc-to-lora/","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SakanaAI/doc-to-lora/main/README.md","https://sakana.ai/doc-to-lora/","https://github.com/SakanaAI/doc-to-lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","frontier"],"authors_full":["Rujikorn Charakorn","Edoardo Cetin","Shinnosuke Uesaka","Robert Tjarko Lange"],"bibkey":"charakorn2026doc","bibtex":"@inproceedings{charakorn2026doc,\n  title = {{Doc-to-LoRA: Learning to Instantly Internalize Contexts}},\n  author = {Rujikorn Charakorn and Edoardo Cetin and Shinnosuke Uesaka and Robert Tjarko Lange},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.15902},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.15902}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over documents: a Perceiver hypernetwork reads the frozen LLM's per-layer activations \\(Z_{l-1}\\) of a document and emits \\((B_l,A_l)\\) for every MLP down-projection.","shape":"Each document fibre is a point of LoRA\\(_{rK}\\): the \\(K\\) chunk adapters are concatenated by rank (Obs I.7).","base_point":"No zero initialisation is recorded. The generated adapter is pointed only if \\(\\alpha_l=0\\) or the heads emit \\(B=0\\) at initialisation, so it is recorded as a defect.","gauge":"Each generated adapter is a point of LoRA\\(_{rK}\\), so its fibre gauge is \\(GL_{rK}\\) on the concatenated rank wire (\\(\\prod_kGL_r\\) preserves the chunk structure; each latent gives a row of \\(A_l\\) and a column of \\(B_l\\)), plus the scalar slide \\((\\alpha_l,B_l)\\mapsto(c\\alpha_l,B_l/c)\\). Only the part that the hypernetwork's output heads can absorb acts on its own parameters.","covariance":"Dense generated factors: basis-free fibres.","merge":"M1 per document: the generated adapter is an ordinary LoRA.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot family."},"categorical_reading":"D2L amortises context distillation: \\(c\\mapsto H_\\phi(c)\\) is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over documents, and long documents use the monoidal structure \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus K}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{rK}\\) of Obs I.7. Its App. D variant emits a KV cache instead, landing the same family in the unpointed prefix extension rather than in the pointed slice.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\big(\\alpha_l[B^{(1)}_l\\ \\cdots\\ B^{(K)}_l],\\ [A^{(1)}_l;\\dots;A^{(K)}_l]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over documents: an explicit factorisation through the hypernetwork's output, composed with the isomorphism \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus K}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{rK}\\) of Obs I.7, pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"text-to-lora","test":"O2","reason":"D2L's per-input rank \\(rK\\) grows with document length, while T2L's generated adapters have rank at most \\(r\\), so long inputs exceed T2L's maximum rank."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"tina","name":"Tina","full_name":"Tiny Reasoning Models via LoRA","paper_title":"Tina: Tiny Reasoning Models via LoRA","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2504.15777","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15777","code_url":"https://github.com/shangshang-wang/Tina","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(r=32, lora_alpha=128, lora_dropout=0.05, target_modules=[\"q_proj\",\"k_proj\",\"v_proj\",\"o_proj\",\"gate_proj\",\"up_proj\",\"down_proj\"])  # plain LoRA (no dedicated tuner), trained with GRPO / Dr.GRPO via TRL","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r} B A,\\ \\ r=32,\\ \\alpha=128;\\quad \\max_{A,B}\\ \\mathcal{J}_{\\mathrm{GRPO}}\\big(\\pi_{W_0+\\frac{\\alpha}{r}BA}\\big)\\ \\text{with verifiable math rewards};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen},\\ A,B \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per adapted projection } (r=32)","locus":"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B; the paper's Table 5 lists \"query, key, value, dense\", while the released training recipes target q/k/v/o and gate/up/down projections","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Plain rank-\\(r\\) LoRA factorization \\(\\Delta W = BA\\); the contribution is the objective: on-policy GRPO / Dr.GRPO reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards on a 1.5B distilled reasoning model, instead of SFT.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapter is ordinary LoRA, so \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\) after training.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (standard LoRA initialization), so RL starts exactly at the distilled base policy.","key_idea":"Low-rank RL on a tiny distilled model recovers most of the reasoning gain of full RL at a few dollars per run; the authors attribute this to 'rapid format adaptation': LoRA learns the structure/format that RL rewards while leaving base knowledge intact.","results_note":"Best Tina checkpoint reaches 43.33% Pass@1 on AIME24 (>20% reasoning improvement over the base) for about $9 of post-training and evaluation compute.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses unmodified LoRA as the policy parameterization for RL."},{"target":"tinylora","type":"competes-with","note":"TinyLoRA pushes the same 'RL needs very few parameters' thesis to ~13 trainable parameters."},{"target":"qerl","type":"competes-with","note":"QeRL is another recipe for cheap LoRA-based RL, adding NVFP4 quantization and noise."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15777","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.15777","https://mlanthology.org/iclr/2026/wang2026iclr-tina/","https://github.com/shangshang-wang/Tina"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Shangshang Wang","Julian Asilis","Ömer Faruk Akgül","Enes Burak Bilgin","Ollie Liu","Willie Neiswanger"],"bibkey":"wang2025tina","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2025tina,\n  title = {{Tina: Tiny Reasoning Models via LoRA}},\n  author = {Shangshang Wang and Julian Asilis and Ömer Faruk Akgül and Enes Burak Bilgin and Ollie Liu and Willie Neiswanger},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2504.15777},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15777}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\): RL starts at the distilled base policy, LoRA's apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Fuses: \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA.","kind":"Plain LoRA \\(W_0+\\frac\\alpha rBA\\) (\\(r=32\\), \\(\\alpha=128\\)). Only the objective changes (GRPO with verifiable rewards), and the loss is not part of \\(\\rho\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Tina is LoRA's object with a policy-gradient loss: the RL objective changes which point of the cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le32}\\) is reached, not the cone. The 'rapid format adaptation' claim is about where GRPO lands inside that image, which the framework constrains only through the image and the cometric.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object \\((Q,q_0,\\rho)\\); only the loss differs."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"tinylora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\text{Tina})=r(m+n-r)\\) per adapted matrix with \\(r=32\\), far above TinyLoRA's \\(u\\)-dimensional linear image, although both papers argue that RL needs few parameters."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"qerl","name":"QeRL","full_name":"Quantization-enhanced Reinforcement Learning","paper_title":"QeRL: Beyond Efficiency -- Quantization-enhanced Reinforcement Learning for LLMs","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2510.11696","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11696","code_url":"https://github.com/NVlabs/QeRL","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = \\mathrm{Dequant}(\\tilde W_0)\\,x + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BAx,\\ \\ \\mathrm{Dequant}(\\tilde W)=S_{\\mathrm{FP32}}\\cdot(S_{\\mathrm{E4M3}}\\odot \\tilde W)\\ (\\text{NVFP4, frozen});\\ \\ \\text{AQN: } w_{\\mathrm{noise}} = w_{\\mathrm{RMSNorm}} + Z,\\ Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_k^2 I),\\ \\sigma_k=\\sigma_{\\mathrm{start}}\\big(\\tfrac{\\sigma_{\\mathrm{end}}}{\\sigma_{\\mathrm{start}}}\\big)^{\\frac{k-1}{K-1}};\\ A,B \\text{ trained by GRPO/DAPO}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per adapted matrix } (r=32 \\text{ default})","locus":"LoRA on the linear layers of an NVFP4-quantized LLM (Qwen2.5 3B-32B); exploration noise injected through RMSNorm gains","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"QLoRA-type composite: additive rank-\\(r\\) delta on a frozen 4-bit (NVFP4) base, plus a scheduled channel-wise Gaussian noise vector added to the RMSNorm scale. This is equivalent to row-wise multiplicative noise (\\(Z/w + 1\\)) on the following \\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\) (shared norm) and \\(W_{gate}\\), \\(W_{up}\\) (shared norm).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"BA can be merged into the dequantized weights (giving up the NVFP4 format) or kept as a side adapter; the noise is used only during training.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\), but the starting policy is the NVFP4-quantized base, not the exact bf16 model. In stage 0 only the inherent quantization noise acts (\\(\\sigma = 0\\)); AQN then starts at \\(\\sigma_{start}\\) (1e-2 in the main text, range 5e-2 to 5e-4 in experiments) and decays exponentially over \\(K\\) stages.","key_idea":"In RL, quantization noise helps: it raises policy entropy and so aids exploration. QeRL schedules it with Adaptive Quantization Noise and combines it with fast NVFP4 rollouts and LoRA.","results_note":"First RL training of a 32B LLM on a single H100-80GB, >1.5x rollout speedup, and Qwen2.5-7B reaching 90.8% GSM8K / 77.4% MATH500, matching full-parameter RL.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"Frozen low-bit base + LoRA, here with NVFP4 and for RL rather than SFT."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Trainable part is standard LoRA."},{"target":"tina","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative low-cost LoRA-RL recipe."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11696","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.11696","https://github.com/NVlabs/QeRL","https://mlanthology.org/iclr/2026/huang2026iclr-qerl/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Wei Huang","Yi Ge","Shuai Yang","Yicheng Xiao","Huizi Mao","Yujun Lin","Hanrong Ye","Sifei Liu","Ka Chun Cheung","Hongxu Yin","Yao Lu","Xiaojuan Qi","Song Han","Yukang Chen"],"bibkey":"huang2025qerl","bibtex":"@inproceedings{huang2025qerl,\n  title = {{QeRL: Beyond Efficiency -- Quantization-enhanced Reinforcement Learning for LLMs}},\n  author = {Wei Huang and Yi Ge and Shuai Yang and Yicheng Xiao and Huizi Mao and Yujun Lin and Hanrong Ye and Sifei Liu and Ka Chun Cheung and Hongxu Yin and Yao Lu and Xiaojuan Qi and Song Han and Yukang Chen},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2510.11696},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11696}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"QLoRA-type recipe: LoRA on a frozen NVFP4 base, trained by GRPO/DAPO, with scheduled noise on the RMSNorm gains during training.","shape":"The cone \\(\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), translated to the quantised base.","base_point":"Pointed at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), with defect \\(e=\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0\\) (Prop V.2(a)). \\(B=0\\) starts RL at the NVFP4 policy, not at the bf16 one.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"The NVFP4 grid with 16-element micro-block scales is coordinate structure. As for QLoRA it is recorded as \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\); the micro-blocks restrict the input side further, to block-preserving monomials.","merge":"An exact merge of \\(BA\\) leaves the NVFP4 grid; a type-preserving merge is lossy (Thm VI.3(d)).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\) span over the quantised base, as QLoRA."},"categorical_reading":"QeRL is QLoRA under another quantiser (NVFP4) and an RL loss. Its AQN noise on RMSNorm gains is, by norm absorption (R6), a random input-side diagonal scaling of the following projections, i.e. a random point of LN-tuning's linear image used for exploration and absent from \\(\\rho\\).","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O1","reason":"Different quantisers give different translates of the same cone: \\(\\kappa_{\\rm NVFP4}\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\subseteq\\kappa_{\\rm NF4}\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) forces \\(\\kappa_{\\rm NVFP4}\\theta_0=\\kappa_{\\rm NF4}\\theta_0\\). The two are related by a translation, a transport rather than an arrow."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"\\(\\theta_0\\notin\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) unless \\(\\mathrm{rank}\\,e\\le r\\) (Prop V.2(a)), and the translate lies inside \\(\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) only if \\(e=0\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"plop","name":"PLoP","full_name":"Precise LoRA Placement","paper_title":"PLoP: Precise LoRA Placement for Efficient Finetuning of Large Models","authors":"Hayou et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2506.20629","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20629","code_url":"https://github.com/soufiane001/plop","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{NFN}(W,x)=\\frac{\\|W z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x)\\|}{\\|W \\tilde z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x)\\|},\\ \\tilde z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x)\\ \\text{i.i.d. centered Gaussian, same dimension and norm as } z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x);\\ \\ \\mathrm{NFN}(W,\\mathcal{D})=\\tfrac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}|}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}}\\frac{\\|W z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x)\\|^2}{\\|W \\tilde z_{\\mathrm{in}}(x)\\|^2},\\ \\ \\mathrm{NFN}(T,\\mathcal{D})=\\tfrac{1}{|N_T|}\\sum_{W\\in T}\\mathrm{NFN}(W,\\mathcal{D});\\ \\ W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\text{ inserted only in the module types } T \\text{ with the lowest } \\mathrm{NFN}(T,\\mathcal{D})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per selected matrix; total depends on the selected module types}","locus":"data-dependent subset of module types (e.g. V, O, down-projection), chosen per model and dataset before training","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A placement rule that chooses the support (set of module types) of an otherwise standard LoRA, scored by how aligned each frozen \\(W_0\\) is with its real inputs relative to random inputs of the same norm.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapters placed are ordinary LoRA and merge as usual.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA; the placement is computed from the frozen model before training starts.","key_idea":"From infinite-width feature-learning analysis, modules whose weights are least aligned with the fine-tuning data (low normalized feature norm) gain most from adaptation, so LoRA should be placed there instead of a fixed attention or MLP default.","results_note":"SFT on MetaMathQA -> GSM8K with Qwen3-0.6B: PLoP (Down-Up-Value, \\(r=76\\), 21.8M params) reaches 63.8%, vs 63.3% for MLP-only (\\(r=64\\), 22.0M) and 58.6% for attention-only (\\(r=64\\), 12.8M); also evaluated with GRPO on Qwen3-1.7B.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Changes only where LoRA is inserted."},{"target":"lora-without-regret","type":"competes-with","note":"Gives a different placement prescription (data-driven NFN versus all layers / MLP-first)."},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both allocate adaptation budget across modules, AdaLoRA during training, PLoP before training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20629","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.20629","https://github.com/soufiane001/plop"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Soufiane Hayou","Nikhil Ghosh","Bin Yu"],"bibkey":"hayou2025plop","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hayou2025plop,\n  title = {{PLoP: Precise LoRA Placement for Efficient Finetuning of Large Models}},\n  author = {Soufiane Hayou and Nikhil Ghosh and Bin Yu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2506.20629},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20629}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"LoRA's cone on the selected module types; all other modules stay at \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\): LoRA's apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Fuses: \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA.","kind":"A placement rule: standard LoRA, inserted only in the module types with the lowest normalised feature norm. The score is computed from the frozen model before training, so the object is fixed."},"categorical_reading":"PLoP picks a sub-object of LoRA-on-all-modules by freezing whole module types at their base value (A2), using a statistic of the frozen model and the data. The NFN score decides only the support; everything else is LoRA.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_T)=\\big(q_T,\\ q_0|_{T^{c}}\\big)","note":"The adapters of unselected module types are frozen at \\((0,A_0)\\): a pointed monomorphism into LoRA on all modules."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"\\(S_1(\\text{PLoP})=\\{XA_0\\}\\) has dimension \\(mr\\) per adapted matrix, while AdaLoRA at \\(\\Lambda=0\\) has \\(\\dim S_1=r\\) (VIII.B). \\(S_1\\) is monotone along pointed arrows (Obs I.6), so there is no pointed arrow, although both allocate budget across modules."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lori","name":"LoRI","full_name":"LoRA with Reduced Interference","paper_title":"LoRI: Reducing Cross-Task Interference in Multi-Task Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"COLM 2025","arxiv":"2504.07448","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07448","code_url":"https://github.com/juzhengz/LoRI","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + (B_t\\odot M_t)\\,A_t,\\ \\ A_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}} \\text{ frozen random (Kaiming-uniform)},\\ B_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r} \\text{ trained},\\ M_t=\\mathbb{1}\\big(|B_t|\\ge\\tau_t\\big),\\ \\tau_t=\\mathrm{Quantile}_s\\big(\\textstyle\\bigcup_{\\text{layers}}|B_t|\\big);\\ \\ \\text{merge: } W=W_0+\\sum_t\\alpha_t (B_t\\odot M_t)A_t","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out} \\text{ per adapted matrix (LoRI-D)};\\ \\ (1-s)\\sum_{\\text{adapted matrices}} r\\,d_{out} \\text{ in total (LoRI-S, global mask, } s=0.9\\text{)}","locus":"any linear layer (attention and MLP projections of Llama-3-8B / Mistral-7B in the paper)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through a fixed random \\(r\\)-dimensional projection of the input; only the up-projection is learned, and only on a sparse coordinate mask. Independent random \\(A_t\\) per task give nearly orthogonal row spaces, so merged adapters interfere little.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each task delta is a fixed matrix \\((B_t \\odot M_t)A_t\\), and multi-task merging is a weighted sum, all foldable into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B_t = 0\\) with \\(A_t\\) random (Kaiming-uniform) and frozen. Masks \\(M_t\\) come from a calibration run that trains \\(B_t\\) unmasked (\\(A_t\\) frozen); \\(B_t\\) is then reset to zero and retrained under the fixed mask.","key_idea":"LoRA's down-projection can be a frozen random map and its up-projection can be about 90% sparse. Random task-specific projections make adapters nearly orthogonal (easy merging), and sparse masks protect earlier adapters, such as a safety adapter, in continual learning.","results_note":"Up to 95% fewer trainable parameters than LoRA while matching or beating it, e.g. LoRI-D reaches 63.2% HumanEval pass@10 vs 50.8% for LoRA on Llama-3-8B.","relations":[{"target":"lora-fa","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA-FA also freezes a random A; LoRI adds task-specific sparse masks on B."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Restricts both factors of LoRA."},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"Both rely on frozen random projections to cut parameters."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Reduces merge interference by construction (orthogonal subspaces + sparsity) rather than post hoc."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative fix for cross-task interference when merging LoRAs."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07448","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.07448","https://github.com/juzhengz/LoRI"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Juzheng Zhang","Jiacheng You","Ashwinee Panda","Tom Goldstein"],"bibkey":"zhang2025lori","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025lori,\n  title = {{LoRI: Reducing Cross-Task Interference in Multi-Task Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Juzheng Zhang and Jiacheng You and Ashwinee Panda and Tom Goldstein},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Conference on Language Modeling (COLM)},\n  eprint = {2504.07448},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07448}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Reparametrisation \\(W_0+(B\\odot M)A_0\\) with \\(A_0\\) frozen random and a fixed sparse mask \\(M\\) on \\(B\\).","shape":"Linear: \\(\\{XA_0:\\mathrm{supp}\\,X\\subseteq M\\}\\), of dimension \\(|M|\\) (on average \\((1-s)rd_{out}\\) per matrix for LoRI-S).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) on a linear method: regular. The calibration run that sets \\(M\\) is discarded and \\(B\\) is reset to zero.","gauge":"None: \\(A_0\\) has full row rank, so \\(X\\mapsto XA_0\\) is injective.","covariance":"A random frame on the input side (covariant in law) and a coordinate mask on the output side: Mon×fr, recorded as 'frame' as for VeRA.","merge":"Fuses; multi-task merging is a weighted sum of static updates.","rebasing":"Frame and mask are fixed: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"LoRI is a masked LoRA-FA, a coordinate sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\subseteq\\) LoRA. Multi-task merging is the \\(\\oplus\\) of such objects with independent random frames, and the cross-talk \\(\\Delta W_jA_i^\\top\\) is small because independent random \\(r\\)-frames are nearly orthogonal: a random-frame version of the shared-frozen-factor fix of Prop V.4(b).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=\\iota_M X","note":"Freezing the masked-out entries of \\(B\\) at \\(0\\): a sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\) pointed at \\(B=0\\)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(\\iota_M X,\\ A_0)","note":"The composite with \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_0)}\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\text{LoRI})=|M|\\), about \\(3.2\\,d_{out}\\) per matrix at \\(s=0.9\\), \\(r=32\\), exceeds VeRA's \\(d=d_{out}+r-1\\), although both rest on frozen random projections."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"singlora","name":"SingLoRA","full_name":"Single-matrix Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"SingLoRA: Low Rank Adaptation Using a Single Matrix","authors":"Bensaïd et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026 Workshop (DeLTa)","arxiv":"2507.05566","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05566","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\,u(t)\\,A A^{\\top},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r},\\ u(t)=\\min(t/T,1)\\ \\ (d_{in}=d_{out}=d);\\ \\ \\text{rectangular: } \\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}u(t)\\,A_{[1:d_{out}]}A_{[1:d_{in}]}^{\\top},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\max(d_{in},d_{out})\\times r};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen},\\ A \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r\\,\\max(d_{in},d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (attention projections in the LLM and Stable Diffusion experiments)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Gram-type update from a single factor: for square \\(W_0\\), \\(\\Delta W = AA^\\top\\) is symmetric positive semidefinite of rank at most \\(r\\); rectangular layers use a row-truncated copy of the same factor. The reparameterization symmetry shrinks from LoRA's \\(GL(r)\\) (\\(B\\mapsto BG\\), \\(A\\mapsto G^{-1}A\\)) to \\(O(r)\\) (\\(A\\mapsto AQ\\)).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(AA^T\\) (or its truncated version) is a fixed matrix after training and adds to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Ramp \\(u(0)=0\\) with Kaiming-initialized \\(A\\), so the update is zero at step 0 and phased in over \\(T\\) steps (\\(T = 1\\%\\) of training steps in the main experiments, \\(10^3\\) steps for RoBERTa/GPT-2; robust for \\(T\\) from 0.5% to 8%).","key_idea":"Replacing LoRA's two factors by one matrix and its transpose removes the scale imbalance between factors. The authors prove stable feature learning at infinite width and transformation-invariance of standard optimizers, while halving parameters. For square layers the symmetric-PSD form restricts expressivity, which the paper argues the transformer architecture compensates for.","results_note":"Llama-7B on MNLI: 91.3% vs 89.1% (LoRA) and 90.2% (LoRA+) with about 60% of their parameters; DreamBooth DINO 0.151 vs 0.148 (DoRA) and 0.143 (LoRA).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same additive low-rank role with one factor instead of two."},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA+ fixes factor imbalance with separate learning rates; SingLoRA removes the second factor."},{"target":"lora-rite","type":"competes-with","note":"Both target transformation-invariant optimization of low-rank adapters."},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative route to scale-stable LoRA training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05566","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.05566","https://openreview.net/pdf/edc1a91c35fc48c860a49795a08d3309376cb8d8.pdf"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["David Bensaïd","Noam Rotstein","Roy Velich","Daniel Bensaïd","Ron Kimmel"],"bibkey":"bensaid2025singlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bensaid2025singlora,\n  title = {{SingLoRA: Low Rank Adaptation Using a Single Matrix}},\n  author = {David Bensaïd and Noam Rotstein and Roy Velich and Daniel Bensaïd and Ron Kimmel},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2507.05566},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.05566}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Reparametrisation \\(W_0+\\frac\\alpha ru(t)AA^\\top\\) for square layers, \\(A_{[1:d_{out}]}A_{[1:d_{in}]}^\\top\\) for rectangular ones.","shape":"Square: the PSD determinantal cone \\(W_0+\\mathrm{PSD}_{\\le r}\\), of dimension \\(rd-r(r-1)/2\\), closed under positive scalings only. Rectangular: \\(\\{A_{[1:d_{out}]}A_{[1:d_{in}]}^\\top\\}\\) (for \\(d_{in}>d_{out}\\), \\(\\{[A_1A_1^\\top\\ \\ A_1A_2^\\top]\\}\\)), of dimension \\(r\\max(d_{in},d_{out})-r(r-1)/2\\).","base_point":"The ramp makes \\(\\rho_0\\) constant (\\(S_1=0\\)) and phases in the data-independent offset \\(\\frac\\alpha ru(t)A_0A_0^\\top\\). At \\(u=1\\) the object is pointed at Kaiming \\(A_0\\) with defect \\(\\frac\\alpha rA_0A_0^\\top\\). The exactly pointed choice \\(A=0\\) is the vertex, where \\(S_1=0\\) and every gradient vanishes.","gauge":"\\(O(r)\\), \\(A\\mapsto AQ\\), with fibre \\(r(r-1)/2\\) (exact also for rectangular layers): the compact form of LoRA's \\(GL_r\\), coded GL as for PoLAR. The \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_r\\) half and its balancedness charges are gone, which is the paper's 'no scale imbalance'.","covariance":"The update is symmetric under a pairing of input and output coordinates (the identity for square layers, truncation for rectangular ones). The image transforms only under congruence pairs \\((g,\\lambda g^{-\\top})\\), and independent neuron permutations on the two sides break it: imposed structure \\(\\Pi\\).","merge":"\\(AA^\\top\\) is a static matrix.","rebasing":"Thm V.3(a) for a cone closed only under positive scalings: \\(R_K=W_0+\\mathrm{PSD}_{\\le Kr}\\), converging to the convex cone \\(W_0+\\mathrm{PSD}\\), not to a span. 'span' is the nearest value."},"categorical_reading":"SingLoRA is the diagonal \\(B=A^\\top\\) of LoRA's parameter space, an A3 sub-object whose stabiliser in \\(GL_r\\) is \\(O(r)\\). Cutting the gauge to its compact half removes the charges behind factor imbalance, but for square layers it also cuts the image to PSD updates, \\(x^\\top\\Delta Wx\\ge0\\) for every \\(x\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r\\,u\\,A_{[1:d_{out}]},\\ A_{[1:d_{in}]}^\\top\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\(h(A_0)\\), which is not LoRA's \\(B_0=0\\) pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Once the ramp ends, \\(\\rho(q_0)-\\theta_0=\\frac\\alpha rA_0A_0^\\top\\ne0\\), so SingLoRA lies in none of LoRA's standard strata and there is no pointed arrow into LoRA at \\((0,A_0)\\); the arrow above lives in the unpointed slice."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"shine","name":"SHINE","full_name":"Scalable In-Context Hypernetwork","paper_title":"SHINE: A Scalable In-Context Hypernetwork for Mapping Context to LoRA in a Single Pass","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2602.06358","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06358","code_url":"https://github.com/MuLabPKU/SHINE","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathcal{M}=\\mathrm{LLM}_{\\theta_0+\\Delta_{\\mathrm{meta}}}\\big([c;\\,m_{1:M}]\\big)\\big|_{\\text{last } M \\text{ tokens of all } L \\text{ layers}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times M\\times H},\\ \\ \\{(A_l,B_l)\\}_{l}=\\mathrm{M2P}_\\psi(\\mathcal{M}),\\ \\ W_l=W_{0,l}+B_lA_l;\\ \\theta_0 \\text{ frozen},\\ \\Delta_{\\mathrm{meta}},\\,m,\\,\\psi \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"\\text{meta-LoRA (rank 128) + } M=\\lceil rD/H\\rceil \\text{ memory embeddings + 4-layer M2P Transformer (shared); generated adapter has rank } r=8","locus":"all linear layers of each backbone layer (Qwen3-8B; generated rank-8 LoRA); \\(D\\) is the summed input and output width of those linear layers","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"In-context hypernetwork: the frozen LLM itself (with a meta-LoRA and learnable memory tokens) encodes the context into an \\(L\\times M\\) memory grid, which an axial (row/column) attention Transformer maps jointly to every LoRA factor. The deployed modification is additive low-rank.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Generated per context; it can be merged for a fixed context but is normally kept as a swappable adapter.","identity_at_init":"No identity guarantee for the generated adapter; base weights are untouched.","key_idea":"Reuse the LLM as its own hypernetwork encoder so that context-to-LoRA generation scales with the backbone, producing an adapter for an arbitrary context in one pass, pretrained with reconstruction/completion objectives and then instruction-tuned on QA.","results_note":"About 0.3 s to generate an adapter in one forward pass. On SQuAD it beats test-time-training baselines such as SEAL (58.2) and PaST, and perplexity improves with backbone size, M2P depth, meta-LoRA rank and generated LoRA rank.","relations":[{"target":"generative-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are self-supervised context-to-adapter generators; SHINE generates all layers jointly."},{"target":"doc-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent context-to-LoRA hypernetwork."},{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Extends the text-to-adapter hypernetwork idea to arbitrary contexts."},{"target":"seal","type":"competes-with","note":"Amortized one-pass alternative to iterative test-time LoRA training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06358","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.06358","https://github.com/MuLabPKU/SHINE","https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZMexYcAibv"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Yewei Liu","Xiyuan Wang","Yansheng Mao","Yoav Gelbery","Haggai Maron","Muhan Zhang"],"bibkey":"liu2026shine","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2026shine,\n  title = {{SHINE: A Scalable In-Context Hypernetwork for Mapping Context to LoRA in a Single Pass}},\n  author = {Yewei Liu and Xiyuan Wang and Yansheng Mao and Yoav Gelbery and Haggai Maron and Muhan Zhang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.06358},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06358}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over contexts: the LLM, with a meta-LoRA and memory tokens, encodes the context into an \\(L\\times M\\) memory grid, and the M2P transformer maps the grid to every LoRA factor jointly.","shape":"Each context fibre is a rank-8 LoRA point on every linear layer.","base_point":"There is no identity guarantee for the generated adapter: defect.","gauge":"\\(GL_8\\) on each generated rank wire, absorbed by M2P's linear output heads. The meta-LoRA carries its own \\(GL_{128}\\), inside the encoder pass only.","covariance":"Dense generated factors: basis-free fibres.","merge":"M1 per context, though normally kept as a swappable adapter.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot family."},"categorical_reading":"SHINE is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA over contexts whose encoder is the base model itself, turned into a hypernetwork by a meta-LoRA that lives only in the encoder pass and never reaches the deployed weights. Generating all layers jointly makes \\(a:T\\to\\prod_lQ_l\\) a single map, not a product of per-layer maps.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\Delta_{\\rm meta},m,\\psi)=\\mathrm{M2P}_\\psi\\big(\\mathcal M(c)\\big)_l","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, per layer into LoRA\\(_8\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"doc-to-lora","test":"O1","reason":"SHINE writes rank-8 updates into every linear layer of each block, D2L only into the MLP down-projections. A SHINE context with a nonzero attention, gate or up update is outside D2L's image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"generative-adapter","name":"GenerativeAdapter","full_name":"Generative Adapter","paper_title":"Generative Adapter: Contextualizing Language Models in Parameters with A Single Forward Pass","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2411.05877","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.05877","code_url":"https://github.com/chentong0/generative-adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_l = A_1\\,\\mathrm{norm}_r\\big(A_2 H_{l-1}^{\\top}H_{l-1} B_1\\big)\\,B_2,\\ \\ H_{l-1}^{\\top}H_{l-1}=\\textstyle\\sum_{m=1}^{M} h_m h_m^{\\top}\\ (\\text{context hidden states from block } l-1),\\ \\ \\mathrm{norm}_r(S)=U_rV_r^{\\top}\\ (\\text{rank-}r \\text{ SVD } S\\approx U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^{\\top},\\ r=128);\\ \\ \\text{streaming: } S_t\\leftarrow S_{t-1}+A_2H_t^{\\top}H_tB_1;\\ A_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_r},A_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_r\\times d_h},B_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_h\\times d_r},B_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\text{base LM frozen, generator trained}","trainable_params":"d_r(d_{in}+d_{out}+2d_h) \\text{ generator parameters per adapted layer (about 500M total for 7B models, } d_r=1024\\text{)}; \\text{ generated adapter rank } 128 \\text{ (about 32M parameters)}","locus":"attention output projections of every layer (Mistral-7B-Instruct, Llama2-7B-Chat)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Bilinear, linear-attention-like map from context hidden states to a parameter delta: the context enters only through its second-moment matrix \\(\\sum_m h_m h_m^\\top\\), compressed into a \\(d_r\\times d_r\\) state that accumulates additively over streamed chunks, then SVD-normalized (singular values reset to 1).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"A generated delta can be added to \\(W_o\\) for a fixed context, but it is context-specific and regenerated per context.","identity_at_init":"Not identity by construction; the base LM is frozen and recovered by dropping the delta.","key_idea":"A lightweight generator, trained self-supervised (reconstruction + completion), turns any context into a parameter delta in one forward pass, giving test-time knowledge injection, in-context learning and personalization without fine-tuning or long prompts.","results_note":"StreamingQA F1 rises from 19.5 (SFT) to 31.5 for contexts up to 32K tokens; 44.9 average accuracy on 26 MetaICL tasks.","relations":[{"target":"text-to-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both generate adapters with a forward pass; GenerativeAdapter conditions on raw context rather than task descriptions."},{"target":"cartridges","type":"competes-with","note":"Both put context into parameters; Cartridges trains per document."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.05877","https://arxiv.org/html/2411.05877","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/447708674d7f30492ca5338b36b07d0c-Abstract-Conference.html","https://github.com/chentong0/generative-adapter"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Tong Chen","Hao Fang","Patrick Xia","Xiaodong Liu","Benjamin Van Durme","Luke Zettlemoyer","Jianfeng Gao","Hao Cheng"],"bibkey":"chen2024generative","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024generative,\n  title = {{Generative Adapter: Contextualizing Language Models in Parameters with A Single Forward Pass}},\n  author = {Tong Chen and Hao Fang and Patrick Xia and Xiaodong Liu and Benjamin Van Durme and Luke Zettlemoyer and Jianfeng Gao and Hao Cheng},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2411.05877},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.05877}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over contexts: \\(\\Delta W_l=A_1\\,\\mathrm{norm}_r(A_2H^\\top HB_1)\\,B_2\\). The context enters only through \\(\\sum_mh_mh_m^\\top\\), accumulated over streamed chunks.","shape":"Each fibre is a point of the rank-128 cone inside the trained frames \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,A_1\\), \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,B_2\\).","base_point":"\\(\\mathrm{norm}_r\\) resets the \\(r\\) singular values to 1, so for a generic context the delta is never 0. The family is pointed only at \\(A_1=0\\) or \\(B_2=0\\), which the method does not use: defect.","gauge":"\\(\\mathrm{norm}_r\\) is the polar factor: equivariant under orthogonal maps and invariant under positive scalings. So \\((A_1O_1^\\top,O_1A_2,B_1O_2,O_2^\\top B_2)\\), \\((aA_2,bB_1)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\), and the outer slide \\((A_1,B_2)\\mapsto(cA_1,B_2/c)\\) leave \\(\\rho\\) unchanged: \\(CO(d_r)\\times CO(d_r)\\) times a scalar slide, with generic fibre \\(d_r(d_r-1)+3\\) (Jacobian rank, checked numerically). It is the compact form of the \\(GL_{d_r}\\) wire gauges, coded GL as for PoLAR.","covariance":"Trained dense \\(A_1,B_2\\): basis-free fibres.","merge":"M1 per context.","rebasing":"n/a: a one-shot family."},"categorical_reading":"GenerativeAdapter is a \\(T\\)-point of LoRA\\(_{128}\\) that factors through a linear-attention state: contexts act through the additive monoid of Gram matrices \\(\\sum h_mh_m^\\top\\), so streaming is a monoid homomorphism, and the polar normalisation then forgets the state's singular values. With the generator fixed, the family lies in the sandwich \\(\\{W_0+A_1XB_2\\}\\) of Prop I.8(a).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2)=\\big(A_1U_r(c),\\ V_r(c)^\\top B_2\\big),\\ \\ U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(A_2H_c^\\top H_cB_1)","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, into LoRA\\(_{128}\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); smooth where \\(\\sigma_r>\\sigma_{r+1}\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"shine","test":"O2","reason":"GenerativeAdapter's per-context update on \\(W_o\\) has rank 128; SHINE's generated adapters have rank 8."},{"target":"doc-to-lora","test":"O1","reason":"GenerativeAdapter writes only into the attention output projections and D2L only into the MLP down-projections, and GenerativeAdapter's normalised update is never zero, so for generic contexts their images do not meet."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"goat","name":"GOAT","full_name":"Great LoRA Mixture-of-Experts (adaptive SVD priors + optimization alignment)","paper_title":"Make LoRA Great Again: Boosting LoRA with Adaptive Singular Values and Mixture-of-Experts Optimization Alignment","authors":"Fan et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2502.16894","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16894","code_url":"https://github.com/Facico/GOAT-PEFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=(W_0-W_{\\mathrm{res}})x+\\sum_{i=1}^{E} w^{i}(x)\\,s\\,B^{i}A^{i}x,\\ \\ w(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\ \\text{over the top-}k \\text{ router logits } W_z^{\\top}x \\ (0 \\text{ elsewhere}),\\ \\ B_0^{i}=\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{s\\rho}}\\,U_i\\Sigma_i^{1/2},\\ A_0^{i}=\\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{s\\rho}}\\,\\Sigma_i^{1/2}V_i^{\\top},\\ \\ W_{\\mathrm{res}}=\\tfrac{s}{E}\\sum_{i}B_0^{i}A_0^{i},\\ \\ s=\\sqrt{3n\\eta/r}","trainable_params":"E\\cdot\\tfrac{r}{E}(d_{in}+d_{out})+E\\,d_{in}=r(d_{in}+d_{out})+E\\,d_{in}\\ \\ (E=8,\\ \\text{top-}2)","locus":"linear layers of ViT-B/32, RoBERTa-large and LLaMA-2-7B (MoE-LoRA in place of a single LoRA)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Mixture of \\(E\\) rank-\\((r/E)\\) LoRA experts with input-dependent top-\\(k\\) gating; expert \\(i\\) is initialized on the \\(i\\)-th contiguous segment of the SVD of \\(W_0\\) (segments tile the whole spectrum: \\(U_i\\Sigma_iV_i^\\top\\) with start index \\((i-1)\\min(d_{in},d_{out})/E\\)), and a closed-form scale \\(s\\) aligns MoE-LoRA gradients with full fine-tuning of an upcycled MoE.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Expert weights depend on the input through the router, so no single static matrix reproduces the layer.","identity_at_init":"The residual \\((s/E)\\sum_i B_0^i A_0^i\\) is subtracted from \\(W_0\\); the initial layer equals \\(W_0\\) exactly only when routing weights are uniform (\\(1/E\\)), so identity holds approximately, not pointwise.","key_idea":"Give each LoRA expert a different SVD-derived starting subspace spread across the whole spectrum, and derive the scaling factor that makes MoE-LoRA optimization mimic full-parameter MoE fine-tuning.","results_note":"Across 25 datasets (image classification, GLUE, NLG, commonsense reasoning) GOAT beats LoRA and MoE-LoRA baselines and closes most of the gap to full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses SVD of W_0 to initialize adapters, distributed across experts and the whole spectrum."},{"target":"lora-pro","type":"competes-with","note":"Both align low-rank updates with full fine-tuning dynamics."},{"target":"adamix","type":"competes-with","note":"Mixture of adaptation modules."},{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Learned gating over several LoRA experts."},{"target":"hydralora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main LoRA-MoE baseline in the paper; GOAT beats it on IC and commonsense reasoning."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16894","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.16894","https://github.com/Facico/GOAT-PEFT"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Chenghao Fan","Zhenyi Lu","Sichen Liu","Chengfeng Gu","Xiaoye Qu","Wei Wei","Yu Cheng"],"bibkey":"fan2025make","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fan2025make,\n  title = {{Make LoRA Great Again: Boosting LoRA with Adaptive Singular Values and Mixture-of-Experts Optimization Alignment}},\n  author = {Chenghao Fan and Zhenyi Lu and Sichen Liu and Chengfeng Gu and Xiaoye Qu and Wei Wei and Yu Cheng},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2502.16894},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16894}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: top-\\(k\\) softmax routing over \\(E\\) rank-\\(r/E\\) LoRA experts, on the frozen residual \\(W_0-W_{\\rm res}\\).","shape":"Input-dependent mixture: function-level.","base_point":"The subtracted \\(W_{\\rm res}=\\frac sE\\sum_iB_0^iA_0^i\\) cancels the experts only for uniform weights \\(1/E\\), which top-\\(k\\) routing (\\(k<E\\)) never produces. The base point is an input-dependent defect \\(e(x)=\\sum_i(w^i(x)-\\frac1E)sB_0^iA_0^i\\). No parameter value reproduces \\(f\\) either: on an open set of inputs with a fixed top-\\(k\\) set, the routed update maps \\(x\\) into a space of dimension \\(\\le kr/E<r=\\operatorname{rank}W_{\\rm res}\\), so it cannot cancel \\(W_{\\rm res}x\\). As for QLoRA with \\(\\operatorname{rank}e>r\\), this is recorded as a defect rather than as unpointed.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_iGL_{r/E}\\) on the experts, plus the softmax translation of \\(W_z\\).","covariance":"n/a: function-level. The SVD-segment initialisation is a \\(CO\\times CO\\) frame of \\(W_0\\).","merge":"Expert weights depend on the input.","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"GOAT spreads a Type II (PiSSA-type) split of \\(W_0\\) over \\(E\\) experts, one SVD segment each, and subtracts their average; routing then breaks the split, so the base point becomes an input-dependent defect rather than the smooth point of a translated cone. Per token the weight is a point of LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) at \\(\\theta_0\\): the routed experts plus the frozen \\(-W_{\\rm res}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,W_z)=\\big([s\\,w^1(x)B^1\\ \\cdots\\ s\\,w^E(x)B^E\\ \\ -\\tfrac sEB_0^1\\ \\cdots\\ -\\tfrac sEB_0^E],\\ [A^1;\\dots;A^E;A_0^1;\\dots;A_0^E]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) at \\(\\theta_0\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice); smooth away from routing ties."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hydralora","test":"O1","reason":"GOAT's top-\\(k\\) routed functions jump at ranking ties, while HydraLoRA's dense softmax gives functions continuous in the input. So generic GOAT functions are outside HydraLoRA's image (its main baseline)."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"core-space-merging","name":"Core Space","full_name":"Core Space merging of low-rank adapters","paper_title":"Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space","authors":"Panariello et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2509.17786","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17786","code_url":"https://github.com/apanariello4/core-space-merging","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\big[A^{(1)};\\dots;A^{(T)}\\big]=U_A\\Sigma_AV_A^{\\top},\\ \\ \\big[B^{(1)},\\dots,B^{(T)}\\big]=U_B\\Sigma_BV_B^{\\top},\\ \\ M^{(t)}=\\big(U_B^{\\top}B^{(t)}\\big)\\big(A^{(t)}V_A\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{Tr\\times Tr},\\ \\ W=W_0+U_B\\,\\mathcal{M}\\big(\\{M^{(t)}\\}_{t}\\big)\\,V_A^{\\top}","trainable_params":"0 \\text{ (training-free merging)}","locus":"every LoRA-adapted layer (Llama-3-8B, ViT-B/32, ViT-L/14)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"All rank-\\(r\\) task updates lie in the span of the union of their factor spaces; shared orthonormal bases of the stacked \\(A\\)'s (row space) and concatenated \\(B\\)'s (column space) map each update losslessly to a small \\(Tr\\times Tr\\) core matrix, where any merging operator acts before mapping back.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The reconstructed merged update is a single static matrix added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free).","key_idea":"Merge LoRAs in their joint core space rather than at full size: the projection is provably lossless, aligns the tasks, and costs far less than full-matrix or KnOTS-style SVD merging.","results_note":"State-of-the-art merging accuracy on Llama-3-8B NLI and ViT-B/32 / ViT-L/14 vision benchmarks, with up to 600x faster merging than merging in full space or KnOTS space.","relations":[{"target":"knots","type":"builds-on","note":"Same alignment-before-merging idea, done in a lossless reduced space."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"builds-on","note":"Off-the-shelf merging operators are applied inside the core space."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17786","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.17786","https://github.com/apanariello4/core-space-merging"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Aniello Panariello","Daniel Marczak","Simone Magistri","Angelo Porrello","Bartłomiej Twardowski","Andrew D. Bagdanov","Simone Calderara","Joost van de Weijer"],"bibkey":"panariello2025accurate","bibtex":"@inproceedings{panariello2025accurate,\n  title = {{Accurate and Efficient Low-Rank Model Merging in Core Space}},\n  author = {Aniello Panariello and Daniel Marczak and Simone Magistri and Angelo Porrello and Bartłomiej Twardowski and Andrew D. Bagdanov and Simone Calderara and Joost van de Weijer},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2509.17786},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17786}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc: joint SVD frames of the stacked \\(A\\)'s and the concatenated \\(B\\)'s map every update to a \\(Tr\\times Tr\\) core, an existing merge rule acts there, and the result is mapped back.","shape":"Read on the inner rule's coefficients, as for KnOTS. With task arithmetic inside, \\(\\lambda\\mapsto W_0+U_B(\\sum_t\\lambda_tM^{(t)})V_A^\\top=W_0+\\sum_t\\lambda_tB^{(t)}A^{(t)}\\): a linear method.","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"The coefficient line is injective. The input gauge \\(\\prod_tGL_r\\) does not cancel, though: it fixes the subspaces \\(\\mathrm{col}[B^{(1)}\\cdots B^{(T)}]\\) and \\(\\mathrm{row}[A^{(1)};\\dots]\\) but moves the singular bases inside them, rotating every core by a common \\((O_B,O_A)\\in O(Tr)^2\\). Linear inner rules are unaffected; coordinatewise ones (TIES, DARE) are gauge-dependent.","covariance":"SVD frames on both sides: \\(CO\\times CO\\), as KnOTS; \\(GL\\times GL\\) with task arithmetic inside.","merge":"The merged update is one static matrix.","rebasing":"Idempotent with the merged update frozen."},"categorical_reading":"Core Space is a joint frame fixing done on factors rather than on products: the projection \\(\\Delta W\\mapsto U_B^\\top\\Delta WV_A\\) is lossless on the span of all updates, so linear rules are exact. Its frames come from the stacked factors, so a coordinatewise rule in core coordinates is not a function of the adapters \\(B^{(t)}A^{(t)}\\) (Prop V.4(a)), whereas KnOTS takes its frame from the concatenated products.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\lambda","note":"With task arithmetic as the inner rule, Core Space and task arithmetic have the same coefficient family (the projection is lossless)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"knots","test":"O1","reason":"With TIES inside, trimming and sign election act in different coordinates (core frames from stacked factors, KnOTS's frame from concatenated updates), so the two merged lines differ."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"k-lora","name":"K-LoRA","full_name":"Top-K training-free subject-style LoRA fusion","paper_title":"K-LoRA: Unlocking Training-Free Fusion of Any Subject and Style LoRAs","authors":"Ouyang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"CVPR 2025","arxiv":"2502.18461","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18461","code_url":"https://github.com/HVision-NKU/K-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W^{l}(t)=\\begin{cases}\\Delta W_c^{l} & \\text{if } S_c^{l}\\ \\ge\\ \\gamma\\,\\mathcal{S}(t)\\,S_s^{l}\\\\ \\Delta W_s^{l} & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases},\\ \\ S_\\bullet^{l}=\\sum_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(|\\Delta W_\\bullet^{l}|)}|\\Delta W_\\bullet^{l}|_i,\\ K=r_cr_s,\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}(t)=\\alpha\\tfrac{t_{\\mathrm{now}}}{t_{\\mathrm{all}}}+\\beta,\\ \\ \\gamma=\\frac{\\sum_l\\sum_i|\\Delta W_c^{l}|_i}{\\sum_l\\sum_j|\\Delta W_s^{l}|_j}","trainable_params":"0 \\text{ (training-free)}","locus":"attention-layer LoRA weights of SDXL and FLUX diffusion models","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Layer-wise, timestep-dependent hard selection between two pretrained LoRA deltas (content vs style), decided by comparing their Top-\\(K\\) \\(\\ell_1\\) mass with a time-ramped scale (\\(\\alpha=1.5\\), \\(\\beta=0.5\\)): content dominates early denoising steps, style later ones.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"At each timestep the selected LoRA per layer can be merged, but the selection changes over timesteps, so no single static merged weight reproduces it.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (training-free; no new parameters).","key_idea":"Only the few largest LoRA entries matter, and early denoising steps set content while later ones set style; so at each attention layer use whichever LoRA has more Top-\\(K\\) mass, rescaled over time, with no training.","results_note":"Highest subject similarity among compared methods (CLIP score 69.4% vs 64.4% ZipLoRA and 63.8% B-LoRA; DINO 46.9% vs 35.7%), with style similarity 58.7% (ZipLoRA 60.4%, B-LoRA 58.0%); preferred in user studies.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Operates on community LoRAs."},{"target":"knots","type":"competes-with","note":"Different training-free answer to combining LoRAs (selection vs aligned merging)."},{"target":"ziplora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main baseline: ZipLoRA learns merge coefficients, whereas K-LoRA selects per layer without training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18461","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.18461","https://k-lora.github.io/K-LoRA.io/","https://github.com/HVision-NKU/K-LoRA"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Ziheng Ouyang","Zhen Li","Qibin Hou"],"bibkey":"ouyang2025k","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ouyang2025k,\n  title = {{K-LoRA: Unlocking Training-Free Fusion of Any Subject and Style LoRAs}},\n  author = {Ziheng Ouyang and Zhen Li and Qibin Hou},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2502.18461},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18461}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":null,"base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Built post hoc from two trained LoRAs (P-type inputs), but its output is a family over denoising timesteps and layers, \\((t,l)\\mapsto\\Delta W^l(t)\\in\\{\\Delta W_c^l,\\Delta W_s^l\\}\\), not one point of \\(\\Theta\\).","shape":"n/a: there are no trainable or continuous coefficients. Each timestep selects one of finitely many weights, so there is no germ at \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"Every layer always carries one of the two LoRAs, so no timestep returns \\(\\theta_0\\): unpointed.","gauge":"n/a: no trainable parameters. The selection score (Top-\\(K\\) \\(\\ell_1\\) mass of \\(\\Delta W\\)) is a function of the products, so it is gauge-invariant.","covariance":"Top-\\(K\\) magnitudes and \\(\\ell_1\\) masses are coordinatewise: natural under signed permutations and common homotheties, not under rotations (as TIES).","merge":"M1 per timestep. The selection depends only on \\((l,t)\\) and the two LoRAs, never on the latent, so the sampler can run on a precomputed schedule of merged weights, but no single static weight reproduces it.","rebasing":"n/a: nothing is trained."},"categorical_reading":"K-LoRA is a family over the denoising time \\(T\\) that takes values at the vertices of a layer-wise CAT simplex: content LoRA early, style LoRA late. It is ZipLoRA's object evaluated at 0/1 coefficients, chosen per timestep by a training-free magnitude rule instead of learned once.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_c^{l},\\alpha_s^{l}\\big)=\\begin{cases}(1,0)&\\text{layer }l\\text{ selects content at }t\\\\ (0,1)&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}","note":"Fibrewise over timesteps: a vertex of CAT's layer-wise family (unpointed slice)."},{"target":"ziplora","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=\\big(m_c^{l},m_s^{l}\\big)\\in\\{(\\mathbf 1,0),(0,\\mathbf 1)\\}","note":"Fibrewise over timesteps: ZipLoRA's column coefficients at 0/1 values (ZipLoRA pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"At one timestep K-LoRA uses the content LoRA in some layers and the style LoRA in others. Task arithmetic's global coefficients cannot select per layer, so that weight is outside its image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"–"},{"id":"ominicontrol","name":"OminiControl","full_name":"Minimal and Universal Control for Diffusion Transformer","paper_title":"OminiControl: Minimal and Universal Control for Diffusion Transformer","authors":"Tan et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICCV 2025","arxiv":"2411.15098","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15098","code_url":"https://github.com/Yuanshi9815/OminiControl","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{MMA}\\big([X;C_T;C_I]\\big)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\Big(\\tfrac{QK^{\\top}}{\\sqrt d}+B(\\gamma)\\Big)V,\\ \\ C_I=\\mathrm{VAE}(\\text{condition image}),\\ \\ B(\\gamma)=\\log\\gamma \\text{ on the } X\\leftrightarrow C_I \\text{ blocks, } 0 \\text{ elsewhere};\\ \\ \\text{per token } z:\\ (W_0+\\mathbb{1}[z\\in C_I]\\,BA)\\,z \\text{ (rank-4 LoRA, scale 0 on non-condition tokens)};\\ W_0,\\ \\mathrm{VAE} \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"\\approx 0.1\\% \\text{ of FLUX.1 } (\\approx 14.5\\mathrm{M} \\text{ LoRA parameters for 12B})","locus":"all FLUX.1 DiT blocks (attention projections and norms matter most) plus condition-image tokens appended to the token sequence","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Input augmentation plus a token-restricted low-rank update: VAE-encoded condition tokens are concatenated into the multimodal attention sequence (shared RoPE positions for spatially aligned tasks, shifted positions for subject-driven tasks). A rank-4 LoRA in the shared DiT blocks acts only on condition tokens, and condition strength is a \\(\\log\\gamma\\) attention-bias block.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The LoRA is applied only to condition tokens, so merging it into the shared weights would change text and image tokens; inference must also process the extra condition tokens.","identity_at_init":"LoRA uses \\(B = 0\\) and is off on text and noise tokens, but appending condition tokens changes attention outputs from the start; \\(\\gamma = 0\\) (bias of minus infinity) removes the condition's influence.","key_idea":"A DiT can serve as its own condition encoder: pass the condition image through the same VAE and transformer as extra tokens and train only a tiny LoRA, giving one mechanism for both spatial and subject-driven control.","results_note":"With about 0.1% extra parameters on FLUX.1 it outperforms ControlNet / T2I-Adapter on spatially aligned control and IP-Adapter on subject-driven generation; releases the Subjects200K dataset.","relations":[{"target":"controlnet","type":"competes-with","note":"Replaces ControlNet's trainable encoder copy with token concatenation + LoRA."},{"target":"t2i-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative lightweight conditioning of diffusion models."},{"target":"ip-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Subject/image-prompt conditioning without a separate image encoder."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"The only trainable weights are LoRA."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15098","https://arxiv.org/html/2411.15098","https://github.com/Yuanshi9815/OminiControl"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Zhenxiong Tan","Songhua Liu","Xingyi Yang","Qiaochu Xue","Xinchao Wang"],"bibkey":"tan2024ominicontrol","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tan2024ominicontrol,\n  title = {{OminiControl: Minimal and Universal Control for Diffusion Transformer}},\n  author = {Zhenxiong Tan and Songhua Liu and Xingyi Yang and Qiaochu Xue and Xinchao Wang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2411.15098},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15098}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An extension over the enlarged input \\(X\\times C\\): VAE-encoded condition tokens \\(C_I\\) join the DiT's token sequence, and a rank-4 LoRA acts only on those tokens. With \\(B=0\\) the frozen blocks already attend to \\(C_I\\), so the trainable parameters contain no neutral point.","shape":"Function-level: the condition enters on the sequence wire.","base_point":"On a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\), positive softmax mass on condition tokens moves the output (Thm VI.5(f)). The bias \\(\\log\\gamma\\) rescales that mass, i.e. the gate \\(\\lambda_q\\) of Thm VI.5(b), and \\(\\gamma\\to0\\) would be neutral. That limit lies outside the parameter domain, and \\(\\gamma\\) is not trained.","gauge":"LoRA's \\(GL_4\\) on each token-restricted adapter.","covariance":"The LoRA part is basis-free, as recorded for other E° entries.","merge":"A token-restricted LoRA plus extra tokens: position-gated edits are M∞ (Thm VI.3(a)).","rebasing":"n/a: an extension."},"categorical_reading":"OminiControl is an unpointed extension on the sequence wire whose new tokens are computed by the frozen model itself, plus a LoRA pulled back along a token-type gate (aLoRA's construction, with a type gate instead of a position gate). Its strength knob \\(\\gamma\\) rescales the condition's softmax mass, the gate \\(\\lambda_q\\) in the gated-parallel-adapter form of Thm VI.5(b).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"easycontrol","test":"O1","reason":"Under full bidirectional attention, OminiControl's condition-token states depend on the noisy latent; EasyControl's causal mutual mask makes them functions of the condition alone. Generic OminiControl functions are therefore outside EasyControl's image (generic, not proved at network level)."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"easycontrol","name":"EasyControl","full_name":"EasyControl (condition-injection LoRA for DiT)","paper_title":"EasyControl: Adding Efficient and Flexible Control for Diffusion Transformer","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICCV 2025","arxiv":"2503.07027","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.07027","code_url":"https://github.com/Xiaojiu-z/EasyControl","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"Q_c'=Q_c+B_QA_QZ_c,\\ \\ K_c'=K_c+B_KA_KZ_c,\\ \\ V_c'=V_c+B_VA_VZ_c;\\ \\ Q,K,V \\text{ of text and noise tokens computed with frozen } W_0;\\ \\ \\mathrm{Attn}\\big([Z_t;Z_n;Z_{c_1};\\dots]\\big) \\text{ under a causal mutual-attention mask}","trainable_params":"\\approx 15\\mathrm{M} \\text{ per condition (FLUX.1-dev): } 3r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per block for the condition branch}","locus":"Q/K/V projections applied only to condition-image tokens in the FLUX.1-dev DiT","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Token-restricted LoRA: the low-rank delta acts only on condition tokens, so the map on text and noise tokens is exactly \\(W_0\\); conditions interact with generation only through masked attention (separate conditions do not attend to each other), and condition K/V are computed once and cached across denoising steps.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The LoRA applies only to condition tokens, so folding it into shared weights would alter text/noise tokens; inference also processes extra condition tokens.","identity_at_init":"Condition-branch LoRA starts at \\(B = 0\\), but the frozen base still attends to condition tokens, so outputs change once a condition is added.","key_idea":"Keep the control signal in its own LoRA-adapted token branch so that conditions are plug-and-play, compose zero-shot across condition types, stay compatible with community style LoRAs, and can be KV-cached.","results_note":"About 15M parameters per condition. Combining the position-aware training paradigm (resolution-normalized conditions) with KV caching cuts 1024x1024 / 25-step inference time by 58% for a single condition (16.3 s) and 75% for two conditions (18.3 s), relative to the variant without both; KV caching alone gives -36% / -50%.","relations":[{"target":"ominicontrol","type":"builds-on","note":"Same token-concatenation conditioning on FLUX with condition-token LoRA. EasyControl adds a causal (mutual) attention mask so condition K/V can be cached and several conditions composed; it compares directly with OminiControl."},{"target":"controlnet","type":"competes-with","note":"Lightweight alternative for spatial control."},{"target":"alora","type":"competes-with","note":"Structural analogue in LLMs: aLoRA also applies its adapter to only some tokens so the base computation stays intact and cacheable."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.07027","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.07027","https://github.com/Xiaojiu-z/EasyControl"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Yuxuan Zhang","Yirui Yuan","Yiren Song","Haofan Wang","Jiaming Liu"],"bibkey":"zhang2025easycontrol","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025easycontrol,\n  title = {{EasyControl: Adding Efficient and Flexible Control for Diffusion Transformer}},\n  author = {Yuxuan Zhang and Yirui Yuan and Yiren Song and Haofan Wang and Jiaming Liu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2503.07027},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.07027}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An extension over \\(X\\times C\\): condition tokens with a QKV LoRA on the condition branch only. Text and noise tokens use exactly \\(W_0\\), and a causal mutual-attention mask stops condition tokens from attending to text, noise or other conditions.","shape":"Function-level.","base_point":"With \\(B=0\\) the frozen model still attends to the condition tokens, so outputs change once a condition is added: unpointed.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on each of the three condition-branch LoRAs.","covariance":"The LoRA parts are basis-free.","merge":"A token-restricted LoRA plus extra tokens: M∞.","rebasing":"n/a: an extension."},"categorical_reading":"Under its causal mask each condition's states depend only on that condition, which is the hypothesis of Thm VI.5(e), so EasyControl is a condition-indexed deep prefix computed by the frozen DiT plus a branch LoRA; that is why its KV can be cached across denoising steps. Because conditions do not attend to each other, several conditions compose through the monoid homomorphism \\(\\Sigma_q(P_1\\uplus P_2\\uplus C)=\\Sigma_q(P_1)+\\Sigma_q(P_2)+\\Sigma_q(C)\\) of Thm VI.5(a).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(B,A)=\\big(K_l(c;B,A),\\ V_l(c;B,A)\\big)_{l\\le L}","note":"Fibrewise over conditions: an arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation, read at content positions, as in Thm VI.5(e). It needs the mask and the condition positions to be independent of the content."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ominicontrol","test":"O1","reason":"EasyControl blocks condition-to-content attention while keeping content-to-condition. OminiControl's bias \\(\\log\\gamma\\) acts symmetrically on both \\(X\\leftrightarrow C\\) blocks and its condition tokens see the latent, so generic EasyControl functions are outside its image (generic, not proved at network level)."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"icedit","name":"ICEdit","full_name":"In-Context Edit","paper_title":"In-Context Edit: Enabling Instructional Image Editing with In-Context Generation in Large Scale Diffusion Transformer","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2504.20690","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20690","code_url":"https://github.com/River-Zhang/ICEdit","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{Out}(x)=W_0x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}G(x)_i\\,B_iA_ix \\ \\text{(attention output projection)},\\ \\ G(x)=\\mathrm{softmax}\\big(\\mathrm{TopK}(g(x),k)\\big),\\ N=4,\\ r=\\alpha=32,\\ k=1;\\ \\ \\text{other DiT modules: standard LoRA } W_0+BA \\ (r=\\alpha=32);\\ \\ \\text{input: diptych [source | masked target] with the prompt ``A diptych with two side-by-side images of the same scene. On the right, the scene is exactly the same as on the left but \\{instruction\\}''}","trainable_params":"\\approx 0.2\\mathrm{B} \\text{ (Table 1; about 1.7\\% of the 12B FLUX.1 Fill, the abstract rounds to }\\sim 1\\%\\text{): LoRA-MoE experts, router and standard LoRAs}","locus":"FLUX.1 Fill (inpainting DiT): LoRA-MoE in the output projection of the multimodal attention blocks, standard LoRA in the other modules","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"In-context input augmentation (an edit is inpainting the right half of a diptych conditioned on the left half) combined with a top-1 routed mixture of four rank-32 LoRA experts.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Routing over experts is input-dependent and the diptych doubles the image sequence at inference.","identity_at_init":"Experts presumably start at \\(B_i = 0\\) (standard LoRA initialization; not stated). The training-free diptych prompt already produces edits, which the LoRA-MoE then sharpens.","key_idea":"Instruction-based editing can be posed as in-context inpainting of a diptych in a large DiT. A small LoRA-MoE trained on 50K pairs (about 0.1% of prior methods' data) is enough, and VLM-guided early filtering of noise seeds improves results at inference.","results_note":"Strong results on Emu Edit (CLIP-I 0.907, DINO 0.866) and MagicBrush with about 0.1% of the training data of prior methods.","relations":[{"target":"goat","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use mixtures of LoRA experts with top-k routing."},{"target":"ominicontrol","type":"competes-with","note":"Both reuse the DiT's own attention over concatenated image tokens for conditioning."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Experts are LoRA modules."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20690","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.20690","https://github.com/River-Zhang/ICEdit"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Zechuan Zhang","Ji Xie","Yu Lu","Zongxin Yang","Yi Yang"],"bibkey":"zhang2025context","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025context,\n  title = {{In-Context Edit: Enabling Instructional Image Editing with In-Context Generation in Large Scale Diffusion Transformer}},\n  author = {Zechuan Zhang and Ji Xie and Yu Lu and Zongxin Yang and Yi Yang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2504.20690},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20690}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":null,"merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over tokens: a top-1 routed mixture of four rank-32 LoRA experts on the attention output projection, \\(\\oplus\\) standard LoRA on the other DiT modules. The diptych is fed through FLUX.1 Fill's existing inpainting interface, so it is not an architectural extension.","shape":"Input-dependent routing: function-level.","base_point":"With every \\(B_i=0\\) (presumed standard initialisation; the paper does not say) each token sees \\(W_0\\), and the standard LoRAs start at \\(B=0\\): neutral.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_iGL_{32}\\) on the experts and \\(GL_{32}\\) on each standard LoRA, plus the router's softmax translation. With \\(k=1\\) only the argmax of the router logits matters, so positive rescalings of the router (indeed any change that keeps every argmax region) also lie in the fibre.","covariance":"n/a: function-level.","merge":"Routing is input-dependent; the standard LoRA parts alone are M1.","rebasing":"n/a: not mergeable."},"categorical_reading":"ICEdit is a family over tokens \\(\\oplus\\) ordinary LoRA, applied to an inpainting model that reads an edit as completing a diptych. With \\(k=1\\) the router is a hard selector: the softmax over one surviving logit is identically 1, so under the catalog's formula the router gets no task-loss gradient and moves only through the discrete selection or an auxiliary loss.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,g)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r[G_1(x)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ G_4(x)B_4],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_4]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens on \\(W_o\\), into LoRA\\(_{4r}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_4^0])\\), pointed if every \\(B_i=0\\); the standard LoRAs map by the identity."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loramoe","test":"O1","reason":"Top-1 routing switches experts at ties, so ICEdit's functions are discontinuous in the input. LoRAMoE's dense softmax mixture is continuous, so generic ICEdit functions are outside its image."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"sd-lora","name":"SD-LoRA","full_name":"Scalable Decoupled Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"SD-LoRA: Scalable Decoupled Low-Rank Adaptation for Class Incremental Learning","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2501.13198","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13198","code_url":"https://github.com/WuYichen-97/SD-Lora-CL","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{(t)}=W_0+\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\alpha_k\\,\\frac{B_kA_k}{\\|B_kA_k\\|_F};\\ \\ \\text{during task } t:\\ \\Big\\{\\tfrac{B_kA_k}{\\|B_kA_k\\|_F}\\Big\\}_{k<t} \\text{ frozen},\\ \\{\\alpha_k\\}_{k\\le t} \\text{ and } (B_t,A_t) \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"\\text{task } t:\\ r_t(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per adapted matrix} + t \\text{ magnitudes (one set shared across all adapted projections)};\\ r_1=10","locus":"query and value projections of all ViT blocks (rank 10, reduced for later tasks in SD-LoRA-RR)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Linear combination of frozen unit-Frobenius-norm rank-\\(r\\) directions with trainable scalar magnitudes (initialized to 1; one magnitude set shared across the adapted projections): each task adds one direction, while the magnitudes of all earlier directions keep being re-weighted. Variants shrink later ranks (RR) or absorb a new direction into old ones by least squares (KD).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training the sum of scaled directions is a single static matrix, and inference needs no task identity or component selection.","identity_at_init":"Not identity: magnitudes are initialized to 1 and each direction has unit norm, so a new task starts with a nonzero perturbation (initialization of \\(A_t\\), \\(B_t\\) is not specified in the paper).","key_idea":"Decouple LoRA magnitude from direction in continual learning: freeze old directions but keep re-weighting their magnitudes. Training then follows a low-loss path into a region shared by all tasks, giving rehearsal-free, selection-free inference.","results_note":"ImageNet-R (20 tasks) accuracy 75.26% vs 73.59% for HiDe-Prompt (which stores samples) and 69.89% for InfLoRA; state of the art on ImageNet-A, DomainNet, CIFAR-100 and CUB-200 class-incremental benchmarks with ViT-B/16.","relations":[{"target":"dora","type":"builds-on","note":"Applies magnitude/direction decoupling to a growing set of LoRA components."},{"target":"l2p","type":"competes-with","note":"Rehearsal-free continual learning with prompts instead of LoRA."},{"target":"dualprompt","type":"competes-with","note":"Prompt-pool continual learning baseline."},{"target":"osf","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative PEFT-style continual learning by constraining update subspaces."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13198","https://arxiv.org/html/2501.13198","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/oral/31918","https://github.com/WuYichen-97/SD-Lora-CL"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Yichen Wu","Hongming Piao","Long-Kai Huang","Renzhen Wang","Wanhua Li","Hanspeter Pfister","Deyu Meng","Kede Ma","Ying Wei"],"bibkey":"wu2025sd","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2025sd,\n  title = {{SD-LoRA: Scalable Decoupled Low-Rank Adaptation for Class Incremental Learning}},\n  author = {Yichen Wu and Hongming Piao and Long-Kai Huang and Renzhen Wang and Wanhua Li and Hanspeter Pfister and Deyu Meng and Kede Ma and Ying Wei},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2501.13198},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13198}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Stage \\(t\\): \\(W_0+\\sum_{k<t}\\alpha_kD_k+\\alpha_t\\,B_tA_t/\\lVert B_tA_t\\rVert_F\\), with the unit directions \\(D_k\\) of earlier tasks frozen and all magnitudes trainable.","shape":"\\(W_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{D_k\\}_{k<t}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): \\(\\{\\alpha N(BA)\\}\\) is the whole rank-\\(r\\) cone, plus a frozen \\(\\oplus\\) of lines. A cone with a linear factor.","base_point":"The normalisation is undefined at \\(BA=0\\) and \\(\\alpha_t\\) starts at 1, so each task starts at a unit-norm offset: a pointing defect \\(e=N(B_t^0A_t^0)\\) by design. The exact pointing \\(\\alpha_t=0\\) exists, but there the new summand contributes only the line \\(\\mathbb R\\,N(B_t^0A_t^0)\\) to \\(S_1\\), a deeper apex than LoRA's.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\((B_t,A_t)\\), plus the scale \\(B_t\\mapsto cB_t\\) (\\(c>0\\)) that the normalisation kills.","covariance":"LoRA's cone plus frozen learned directions: basis-free.","merge":"The sum of scaled directions is one static matrix.","rebasing":"A stage scheme over tasks: each task adds one direction and then freezes it into a CAT-type \\(\\oplus\\). After \\(T\\) tasks the reach is \\(W_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{D_k\\}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\subseteq W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le Tr}\\) (A5)."},"categorical_reading":"At stage \\(t\\), SD-LoRA is CAT on the frozen directions of earlier tasks \\(\\oplus\\) a normalised LoRA for the new one. The normalisation \\(\\alpha N(BA)\\) leaves LoRA's image unchanged but removes the \\(B=0\\) pointing: the stage's base point is reached only at \\(\\alpha_t=0\\), where the new summand's first-order image is the single line \\(\\mathbb R\\,N(B_tA_t)\\), and the paper starts at \\(\\alpha_t=1\\) instead, a defect. \\(\\alpha\\) carries the scale that \\(B\\) loses.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\alpha_1\\hat B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\alpha_{t-1}\\hat B_{t-1}\\ \\ \\alpha_tB_t/\\lVert B_tA_t\\rVert_F],\\ [\\hat A_1;\\dots;\\hat A_{t-1};A_t]\\big),\\ \\ D_k=\\hat B_k\\hat A_k","note":"Stage \\(t\\) into LoRA\\(_{tr}\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect); smooth where \\(B_tA_t\\ne0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"o-lora","test":"O2","reason":"An SD-LoRA stage also re-weights every earlier direction, so \\(d=r(m+n-r)+(t-1)\\), while an O-LoRA stage is a single LoRA\\(_r\\) with earlier components frozen, \\(d=r(m+n-r)\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sparse-memory-finetuning","name":"Sparse Memory Finetuning","full_name":"Continual learning via sparse memory-layer finetuning","paper_title":"Continual Learning via Sparse Memory Finetuning","authors":"Lin et al.","year":2025,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2510.15103","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15103","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{Mem}(x)=W_2^{\\top}\\Big[\\Big(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(K q(x))}s_i(x)\\,V_i\\Big)\\odot\\mathrm{silu}(W_1 x)\\Big],\\ s=\\mathrm{softmax}(K_{\\mathbb{I}}q(x)),\\ k=32;\\ \\ \\text{update only } \\{V_i: i\\in\\mathcal{T}\\},\\ \\ \\mathcal{T}=\\text{top-}t \\text{ slots by } \\frac{c(i)}{\\sum_j c(j)}\\cdot\\log\\frac{|\\mathcal{B}|+1}{\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbb{1}[c_b(i)>0]+1};\\ \\text{all other parameters frozen (gradients stopped)}","trainable_params":"t\\cdot d_v \\text{ memory values per step } (d_v=1024;\\ t=500 \\text{ for TriviaQA}, 10^4 \\text{ for SimpleQA, out of } 10^6 \\text{ slots})","locus":"value table of a product-key memory layer that replaces one FFN (layer 12 of 22) in a 1.3B model","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"Input-dependent sparse coordinate mask over rows of the memory value table, chosen by a TF-IDF score: slot access counts \\(c(i)\\) on the current batch, discounted by how often the slot is used on background pretraining batches \\(B\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updated memory values overwrite the originals in place, so the memory-layer model keeps its architecture and inference cost.","identity_at_init":"No new parameters; training starts exactly from the pretrained memory-layer model.","key_idea":"Memory layers are sparsely accessed by design; updating only the slots specifically activated by new knowledge (relative to their pretraining usage) learns new facts with much less interference than full fine-tuning or LoRA.","results_note":"After learning new TriviaQA facts, NaturalQuestions F1 drops 89% with full fine-tuning and 71% with LoRA but only 11% with sparse memory finetuning, at the same level of new-knowledge acquisition.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA is the main forgetting baseline it improves on."},{"target":"lt-sft","type":"competes-with","note":"Sparse fine-tuning, but here the sparse support is defined by memory-slot access statistics."},{"target":"spiel","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative sparse fine-tuning method."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15103","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.15103","https://openreview.net/forum?id=LGo7U1m24L"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Jessy Lin","Luke Zettlemoyer","Gargi Ghosh","Wen-Tau Yih","Aram Markosyan","Vincent-Pierre Berges","Barlas Oğuz"],"bibkey":"lin2025continual","bibtex":"@article{lin2025continual,\n  title = {{Continual Learning via Sparse Memory Finetuning}},\n  author = {Jessy Lin and Luke Zettlemoyer and Gargi Ghosh and Wen-Tau Yih and Aram Markosyan and Vincent-Pierre Berges and Barlas Oğuz},\n  year = {2025},\n  eprint = {2510.15103},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.15103},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.15103}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is the identity on the memory-layer model. Each step projects the gradient onto the rows \\(\\mathcal T_t\\) of the value table chosen by a TF-IDF score of slot access (compression = anchor = that coordinate projection): a backward lens.","shape":"Per step, the moving coordinate subspace \\(\\{V+X:\\mathrm{rows}(X)\\subseteq\\mathcal T_t\\}\\).","base_point":"Each step is a linear method at its own base: regular.","gauge":"None.","covariance":"The rows are memory slots, permuted together with their keys, and the value dimension is updated densely. That is a coordinate mask on the slot (input) side and nothing on the value side: GL×Mon, recorded under the one-sided value Mon×GL.","merge":"The method writes the value table in place.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme with period 1 whose mask moves with the batch; its reach is the set of slots ever selected."},"categorical_reading":"Sparse memory finetuning sits in the 'coordinate blocks, resampled every step' cell of the cotangent table (Exposé IV): by the degree-one collapse each step is a fixed-mask linear method re-based at \\(\\theta_t\\), with the mask chosen by access counts relative to background usage. Its control of forgetting comes from the smallness and specificity of the moving support, not from a constraint on the image.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id},\\ \\ M=\\mathrm{rows}(\\mathcal T_t)","note":"Within one step at base \\(\\theta_t\\), the method is the fixed-mask linear method with mask \\(\\mathrm{rows}(\\mathcal T_t)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"One step updates \\(t\\) rows of a \\(d_v=1024\\) table, an update of rank up to \\(\\min(t,d_v)\\) (500 on TriviaQA), above a rank-\\(r\\) LoRA on the same table."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"seal","name":"SEAL","full_name":"Self-Adapting Language Models","paper_title":"Self-Adapting Language Models","authors":"Zweiger et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2506.10943","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10943","code_url":"https://github.com/Continual-Intelligence/SEAL","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"SE\\sim \\mathrm{LM}_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid C),\\ \\ \\theta'=\\mathrm{SFT}_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}(\\theta,SE)\\ \\ (W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA;\\ r=64,\\alpha=128 \\text{ for knowledge incorporation};\\ r=128,\\alpha=16 \\text{ for ARC TTT});\\ \\ \\text{outer loop: ReST}^{EM}\\ \\text{(SFT on self-edits with } r(SE)=1),\\ r(SE)=\\mathbb{1}\\big[\\text{adapting with } SE \\text{ improves } \\mathrm{LM}_\\theta \\text{ on } \\tau\\big]","trainable_params":"\\text{inner: } r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per adapted matrix per self-edit } (r=64 \\text{ or } 128);\\ \\text{outer: the self-edit generation policy (LoRA } r=16 \\text{ in the ARC RL-SFT)}","locus":"inner LoRA on all attention and MLP projections (Qwen2.5-7B, knowledge incorporation) or on q/v/gate/up/down projections (Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, ARC)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Bi-level: the model writes its own fine-tuning data and hyperparameters ('self-edits'); a LoRA SFT step turns each self-edit into \\(\\Delta W = BA\\); RL (filtered behavior cloning) reinforces self-edits whose induced update improves downstream performance.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each realized update is an ordinary LoRA that can be merged into the weights.","identity_at_init":"Each inner LoRA update starts from \\(B = 0\\).","key_idea":"Let the LLM learn, through RL, to generate the training data and optimization directives that best update its own weights, turning LoRA fine-tuning into an action the model can take.","results_note":"On SQuAD knowledge incorporation (no-context), accuracy rises from 32.7% to 47.0%, beating synthetic data written by GPT-4.1 (46.3%); ARC few-shot success 72.5% vs 20% without RL. The paper flags catastrophic forgetting under repeated self-edits.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Self-edits are realized as LoRA SFT updates."},{"target":"generative-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Context-to-weights via iterative self-generated SFT rather than an amortized generator."},{"target":"shine","type":"competes-with","note":"SHINE amortizes the same context-to-weights step in one forward pass."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10943","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10943","https://github.com/Continual-Intelligence/SEAL"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Adam Zweiger","Jyothish Pari","Han Guo","Ekin Akyürek","Yoon Kim","Pulkit Agrawal"],"bibkey":"zweiger2025self","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zweiger2025self,\n  title = {{Self-Adapting Language Models}},\n  author = {Adam Zweiger and Jyothish Pari and Han Guo and Ekin Akyürek and Yoon Kim and Pulkit Agrawal},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2506.10943},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10943}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"Each inner update starts from \\(B=0\\): LoRA's apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Fuses: \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA.","kind":"A bi-level recipe: the model writes self-edits, each realised as a LoRA SFT update from \\(B=0\\), and an outer RL loop (ReST\\(^{EM}\\)) trains the self-edit policy. The weight-space object is plain LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"SEAL realises a context-to-weights family \\(C\\mapsto\\mathrm{SFT}_{\\rm LoRA}(\\theta,\\mathrm{SE}(C))\\) by optimisation rather than by a forward pass: each fibre is an ordinary LoRA point, and RL shapes the data that selects it. SHINE and GenerativeAdapter amortise the same map into a hypernetwork.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Each realised update is an ordinary LoRA; the self-edit loop changes the data and the loss, not \\(\\rho\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"shine","test":"O2","reason":"SEAL's inner updates have rank 64 (knowledge incorporation) or 128 (ARC) per matrix, while SHINE's generated adapters have rank 8."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sparselora","name":"SparseLoRA","full_name":"LoRA fine-tuning with contextual sparsity","paper_title":"SparseLoRA: Accelerating LLM Fine-Tuning with Contextual Sparsity","authors":"Khaki et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2506.16500","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16500","code_url":"https://github.com/z-lab/sparselora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0\\big|_{\\mathcal{S}(x)}\\,x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BAx\\ \\ (\\text{training only}),\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}(x)=\\mathrm{TopK}\\ \\text{channels of the cheap proxy } (U_k\\Sigma_k^{1/2})(\\Sigma_k^{1/2}V_k^{\\top})x\\ \\ \\big(U_k\\Sigma_kV_k^{\\top}=\\text{offline rank-}k \\text{ SVD of } W_0\\big);\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen},\\ A,B \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ (same as LoRA)}","locus":"frozen main branch of attention (QKVO) and MLP projections, with non-uniform layer sparsity (deeper layers sparser); output tokens and up to the first 10% of steps stay dense. The LoRA branch (QKVO, \\(r=32\\), \\(\\alpha=64\\) in experiments) stays dense.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Standard LoRA update; the forward/backward pass through the frozen \\(W_0\\) uses input-dependent channel sparsity, predicted by a training-free rank-\\(k\\) SVD sketch of \\(W_0\\) (activation norms for FFN/VO, query-key norm products for QK).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Sparsity is used only during training; the dense LoRA adapter merges into \\(W_0\\) and inference is unchanged.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA; the sparse frozen branch only approximates \\(W_0 x\\) during training.","key_idea":"LoRA saves memory but not compute; predicting per input which channels of the frozen weights matter, with a cheap SVD proxy, lets fine-tuning skip most frozen-branch FLOPs without hurting accuracy.","results_note":"Up to 2.2x compute reduction and 1.6x wall-clock speedup over LoRA at matched accuracy on commonsense and arithmetic reasoning, code generation and instruction following (LLaMA-2/3).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Adapter is unchanged; only frozen-branch computation is sparsified."},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Different axis of LoRA training efficiency (compute sparsity vs weight quantization)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16500","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.16500","https://z-lab.ai/projects/sparselora"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"authors_full":["Samir Khaki","Xiuyu Li","Junxian Guo","Ligeng Zhu","Chenfeng Xu","Konstantinos N. Plataniotis","Amir Yazdanbakhsh","Kurt Keutzer","Song Han","Zhijian Liu"],"bibkey":"khaki2025sparselora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{khaki2025sparselora,\n  title = {{SparseLoRA: Accelerating LLM Fine-Tuning with Contextual Sparsity}},\n  author = {Samir Khaki and Xiuyu Li and Junxian Guo and Ligeng Zhu and Chenfeng Xu and Konstantinos N. Plataniotis and Amir Yazdanbakhsh and Kurt Keutzer and Song Han and Zhijian Liu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2506.16500},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16500}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"The deployed object starts at \\(B=0\\) at \\(\\theta_0\\), the apex. During training the sparsified branch acts as an input-dependent pointing defect \\(e(x)=-W_0|_{\\mathcal S(x)^c}\\), dropped at inference.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Fuses: \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA.","kind":"Standard LoRA. During training the frozen branch is evaluated as \\(W_0|_{\\mathcal S(x)}x\\), with input-dependent channel sparsity predicted by a rank-\\(k\\) SVD sketch of \\(W_0\\). The sparsity approximates the forward pass and is invisible to \\(\\rho\\)."},"categorical_reading":"SparseLoRA keeps LoRA's object and trains it on an input-dependent approximation of the network: an input-indexed base change of the forward pass, like QLoRA's \\(\\kappa\\) but data-dependent and discarded at inference. The gradients are those of the sparsified model, so it changes the dynamics, not the image.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object; the sparsity lives in the training forward pass, not in \\(\\rho\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loreft","name":"LoReFT","full_name":"Low-rank Linear Subspace Representation Finetuning (ReFT family)","paper_title":"ReFT: Representation Finetuning for Language Models","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2404.03592","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.03592","code_url":"https://github.com/stanfordnlp/pyreft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Phi_{\\text{LoReFT}}(h)=h+R^{\\top}\\big(Wh+b-Rh\\big)=(I-R^{\\top}R)\\,h+R^{\\top}(Wh+b),\\quad R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}\\ (RR^{\\top}=I_r),\\ W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r};\\ \\text{applied to the residual-stream vector } h^{(l)}_i \\text{ at the first } p \\text{ and last } s \\text{ prompt positions of the chosen layers } l\\in\\mathcal{L};\\ \\text{LM frozen},\\ \\{R,W,b\\}\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\text{DiReFT ablation: } \\Phi(h)=h+W_2^{\\top}(W_1h+b),\\ W_1,W_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}","trainable_params":"(2rd+r)\\ \\text{per intervention } (R,W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r});\\ |\\mathcal{L}|\\,(2rd+r)\\ \\text{with prefix and suffix weights tied},\\ 2|\\mathcal{L}|\\,(2rd+r)\\ \\text{untied (one intervention for the prefix and one for the suffix positions in each layer)}","locus":"residual-stream hidden states (Transformer block outputs) at a fixed number of prefix and suffix prompt positions in selected layers; generated tokens are not intervened on","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Low-rank affine edit of hidden states confined to an \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace. With the orthogonal projector \\(P = R^T R\\), \\(\\Phi(h) = (I - P)h + R^T(Wh + b)\\): the component of \\(h\\) in \\(\\mathrm{row}(R)\\) is replaced by a learned affine function of \\(h\\), and the orthogonal complement is untouched. The edit is sparse in both positions (prompt prefix and suffix) and layers. DiReFT drops the orthonormality and the difference term, giving a LoRA-shaped map \\(h \\to h + W_2^T(W_1 h + b)\\) on activations.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The edit acts on the residual stream, including the skip path, at specific token positions, so it cannot be folded into any weight matrix; its cost is a small constant per prompt rather than per generated token.","identity_at_init":"Not identity-preserving. In pyreft \\(R\\) gets a random orthogonal initialization (torch orthogonal parametrization) and \\(W\\), \\(b\\) use the default nn.Linear initialization, so \\(\\Phi(h) \\neq h\\) at step 0; the identity is recovered only when \\(W = R\\) and \\(b = 0\\).","key_idea":"Learn task-specific interventions on hidden representations instead of changing weights. LoReFT adapts the distributed interchange intervention from causal-abstraction interpretability (DAS): it rewrites the hidden state only inside a learned low-rank orthonormal subspace and only at a few prompt positions, using 15x-65x fewer parameters than LoRA.","results_note":"With 0.031% trainable parameters on LLaMA-7B it averages 80.2 on eight commonsense-reasoning tasks, the best among the compared PEFTs. On Llama-2 7B instruction tuning it reaches an 85.60 AlpacaEval win rate with 0.0039% of parameters (LoRA: 81.48). It trails LoRA and adapters on arithmetic reasoning.","relations":[{"target":"red","type":"competes-with","note":"both edit representations; RED applies a diagonal affine map to FFN outputs at all positions, LoReFT a low-rank subspace edit at selected positions"},{"target":"road","type":"competes-with","note":"both are framed as representation interventions; RoAd's is an element-wise 2D rotation that can be merged"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"DiReFT is essentially LoRA applied to hidden states at chosen positions; LoReFT reports 15x-65x fewer parameters than LoRA"},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"main weight-based baseline on commonsense and arithmetic reasoning"},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"both act only on activations at token positions and leave the weights untouched; prefix-tuning adds key/value states, LoReFT edits existing states"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.03592","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.03592","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/94174","https://github.com/stanfordnlp/pyreft","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stanfordnlp/pyreft/main/pyreft/interventions.py","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=ReFT%20Representation%20Finetuning%20for%20Language%20Models"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Zhengxuan Wu","Aryaman Arora","Zheng Wang","Atticus Geiger","Dan Jurafsky","Christopher D. Manning","Christopher Potts"],"bibkey":"wu2024reft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2024reft,\n  title = {{ReFT: Representation Finetuning for Language Models}},\n  author = {Zhengxuan Wu and Aryaman Arora and Zheng Wang and Atticus Geiger and Dan Jurafsky and Christopher D. Manning and Christopher Potts},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2404.03592},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.03592}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"VIII.B: an extension by an affine identity box on the residual stream at selected positions, \\(\\Phi(h)=h+R^\\top(Wh+b-Rh)\\) (Prop VI.4(c)).","shape":"Per hidden vector the inserted box ranges over \\((I,0)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}(d\\times(d+1))\\), of dimension \\(r(2d+1-r)\\): LoRA\\(_r\\) on \\(\\mathrm{Aff}(I_d)\\) (VIII.B). Applied at selected positions only, the method is function-level.","base_point":"pyreft initialises \\(W,b\\) independently of \\(R\\), so the edit \\(R^\\top((W_0-R_0)h+b_0)\\) is nonzero at step 0: a defect (VIII.B). It is neutral iff \\(W=R\\) and \\(b=0\\).","gauge":"\\(O(r)\\), \\((R,W,b)\\mapsto(OR,OW,Ob)\\) (VIII.B): the compact form of LoRA's \\(GL_r\\), since \\(R\\) has orthonormal rows. Coded GL, as for PoLAR.","covariance":"VIII.B leaves '–'. Per hidden vector the image is basis-free on the residual stream, and the orthonormality of \\(R\\) is a partial gauge fixing that does not change it.","merge":"A position-selective edit on the residual stream: M∞ (Thm VI.3(a), Prop VI.4).","rebasing":"n/a: nothing can be merged."},"categorical_reading":"Per hidden vector LoReFT is weight-space LoRA on an affine identity box that the architecture did not have, with \\(B=R^\\top\\) on the Stiefel manifold, so it is simulated by, but not isomorphic to, LoRA\\(_r\\). Its neutral point \\((B_0,A_0)=(R_0^\\top,0)\\) lies in LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum, and position-selectivity is what blocks merging.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(R,W,b)=\\big(R^\\top,\\ [W-R\\mid b]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) on the inserted affine box \\([I_d\\mid0]\\) in homogeneous coordinates (Prop VI.4(c)), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), the pyreft defect."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"At its neutral point LoReFT maps to \\((R_0^\\top,0)\\), with \\(S_1=\\{R_0^\\top Y\\}\\): the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum. Standard LoRA is pointed in \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) (\\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\)), so there is no pointed arrow into it."},{"target":"red","test":"O1","reason":"RED's edit \\(h\\mapsto\\ell\\odot h+b\\) is diagonal and acts at every position. LoReFT's rank-\\(r\\) edit is non-diagonal and position-selective, so its functions lie outside RED's image."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"longlora","name":"LongLoRA","full_name":"LongLoRA (LoRA with trainable embeddings and norms, plus Shifted Sparse Attention)","paper_title":"LongLoRA: Efficient Fine-tuning of Long-Context Large Language Models","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.12307","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=6PmJoRfdaK","code_url":"https://github.com/dvlab-research/LongLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_{\\{q,k,v,o\\}}=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\ \\ (B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}});\\ \\ E=E_0+\\Delta E\\ \\text{(input embedding, dense)};\\ \\ \\gamma_{\\text{norm}}\\ \\text{(all RMSNorm gains) trained};\\ \\ \\text{training-time attention } \\mathrm{S^2\\text{-}Attn}:\\ \\text{tokens split into groups of size } G,\\ \\text{half of the heads attend within groups, the other half within groups shifted by } G/2;\\ \\ \\text{inference: standard full attention};\\ \\ \\text{RoPE positions rescaled by Position Interpolation};\\ \\ \\text{MLP weights frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted attention matrix } (4L \\text{ matrices}) \\;+\\; |\\mathcal{V}|\\,d\\ \\text{(input embedding)} \\;+\\; (2L+1)\\,d\\ \\text{(norm gains)}","locus":"\\(W_q, W_k, W_v, W_o\\) of every attention layer (LoRA, \\(r = 8\\), \\(\\alpha = 16\\) in the official code), plus the input token-embedding matrix and all normalization layers (trained densely); S\\(^2\\)-Attn changes only the training-time attention pattern","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Composite of three factors. (i) Rank-\\(r\\) additive updates \\(\\Delta W = BA\\) on the attention projections. (ii) Dense, selective updates of the input-embedding table and the norm gains, which together are under 2% of Llama2-7B's parameters. (iii) A training-only sparsification of the attention graph into block-diagonal token groups, with a \\(G/2\\) shift on half of the heads so that information flows between neighbouring groups (Swin-style). The paper calls (i)+(ii) 'LoRA+', which is unrelated to LoRA+ by Hayou et al.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The LoRA factors merge into \\(W_0\\), the trained embedding and norm tensors replace the originals, and S\\(^2\\)-Attn is used only in training, so inference runs the original dense-attention architecture (with Position-Interpolation RoPE scaling) at the original cost.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA init (\\(B = 0\\)) and the pretrained embedding and norm values, so the weights are unchanged at step 0. The training-time function still differs from the base model, because attention is restricted to shifted local groups and positions are interpolated.","key_idea":"Plain LoRA fails at context extension at any rank, but also training the cheap embedding and normalization layers closes almost all of the gap to full fine-tuning. Training can use shifted sparse local attention, which approximates full attention and takes two lines of code, while inference keeps dense attention.","results_note":"Extends Llama2 7B, 13B and 70B to 100k, 64k and 32k tokens on a single 8xA100 machine. At 32k context on PG19, LoRA with trainable norms and embeddings reaches perplexity 8.12, versus 8.08 for full fine-tuning and 11.44 for plain rank-8 LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA on W_q, W_k, W_v, W_o, extended with dense updates of the embeddings and norms"},{"target":"ln-tuning","type":"combines","note":"trains all normalization gains alongside LoRA; the norms account for the largest single perplexity gain per parameter"},{"target":"sparselora","type":"competes-with","note":"both cut fine-tuning compute with training-only sparsity and keep dense inference; LongLoRA sparsifies attention over tokens, SparseLoRA sparsifies channels of the frozen weights"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.12307","https://arxiv.org/html/2309.12307","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=LongLoRA","https://github.com/dvlab-research/LongLoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dvlab-research/LongLoRA/main/fine-tune.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Yukang Chen","Shengju Qian","Haotian Tang","Xin Lai","Zhijian Liu","Song Han","Jiaya Jia"],"bibkey":"chen2023longlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2023longlora,\n  title = {{LongLoRA: Efficient Fine-tuning of Long-Context Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yukang Chen and Shengju Qian and Haotian Tang and Xin Lai and Zhijian Liu and Song Han and Jiaya Jia},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2309.12307},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=6PmJoRfdaK}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Reparametrisation: LoRA on \\(W_{q,k,v,o}\\) \\(\\oplus\\) the dense input embedding \\(\\oplus\\) all RMSNorm gains (the paper's 'LoRA+'). S\\(^2\\)-Attn and Position Interpolation change \\(f\\), not \\(\\rho\\).","shape":"The Minkowski sum of LoRA's cone with two linear blocks: a cone with a linear factor.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) with the pretrained embedding and gains: the apex of the LoRA summand (the linear summands are regular).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on each LoRA; the linear summands have none.","covariance":"LoRA and the dense embedding are basis-free. The norm gains are diagonal on the residual stream, so that side is only monomial (LN-tuning's Mon×GL).","merge":"LoRA fuses, and the trained embedding and gains replace the originals in place.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\) span: the LoRA summand reaches its full blocks after \\(\\lceil d/r\\rceil\\) cycles, and the linear summands are idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"LongLoRA is the \\(\\oplus\\)-word LoRA\\(_{qkvo}\\oplus\\) Embedding \\(\\oplus\\) LN-tuning, trained on a different realisation from the one it is deployed on: S\\(^2\\)-Attn and Position Interpolation change \\(f\\), while \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\) depends only on \\((\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\). Its finding that LoRA alone fails at any rank is an O1 statement: the embedding and gain directions lie outside every LoRA\\(_{qkvo}\\) image, and LoRA and LN-tuning both map into it by A5.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"LoRA on \\(W_{q,k,v,o}\\) keeps the embedding and the norm gains at \\(\\theta_0\\), while LongLoRA moves them, so its image is not in any LoRA\\(_{qkvo}\\) image."},{"target":"lora-plus","test":"O1","reason":"The paper's 'LoRA+' (LoRA \\(\\oplus\\) embedding \\(\\oplus\\) norms) is unrelated to Hayou et al.'s LoRA+, which has LoRA's image (only the \\(B/A\\) learning-rate ratio differs). The embedding and gain summands lie outside that image."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"o-lora","name":"O-LoRA","full_name":"Orthogonal Low-Rank Adaptation (orthogonal subspace learning for continual learning)","paper_title":"Orthogonal Subspace Learning for Language Model Continual Learning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"Findings of EMNLP 2023","arxiv":"2310.14152","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.715/","code_url":"https://github.com/cmnfriend/O-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{(t)}=W_0+\\sum_{i<t}B_iA_i+B_tA_t,\\quad A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r};\\quad \\text{paper: }\\min_{A_t,B_t}\\ \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{task}}(\\mathcal{D}_t)+\\lambda_1\\sum_{i=1}^{t-1}\\big\\|B_i^{\\top}B_t\\big\\|_F^2\\ \\ (\\mathcal{U}_t=\\operatorname{col}(B_t)\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}};\\ \\text{the paper writes }\\Delta W=AB\\text{ with its }A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\text{ the output-side factor});\\quad \\text{official code: }\\lambda_1\\sum_{i<t}\\big\\|A_iA_t^{\\top}\\big\\|_{1}\\ (\\text{entry-wise, on PEFT lora\\_A})+\\lambda_2\\big(\\|A_t\\|_F+\\|B_t\\|_F\\big);\\quad W_0\\ \\text{and}\\ \\{A_i,B_i\\}_{i<t}\\ \\text{frozen; after task } t:\\ W_0\\leftarrow W_0+\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\le t}B_iA_i","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per task (after } t \\text{ tasks } t\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ are stored unless merged)}","locus":"\\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) of every attention layer (T5-large, LLaMA-7B)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Sum of per-task rank-\\(r\\) updates. A soft penalty on an \\(r\\times r\\) cross-Gram matrix, which vanishes exactly when two tasks' subspaces are orthogonal, pushes each new task's subspace away from all earlier ones. \\(\\Delta W\\) is therefore close to a direct sum of mutually orthogonal rank-\\(r\\) pieces; orthogonality is a regularizer, not a hard constraint. The paper defines each task's subspace as the column span of the output-side factor (catalog \\(B_t\\), in \\(\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\)) and squares the entries of \\(B_i^\\top B_t\\). The official trainer instead penalizes the input-side rows (PEFT lora_A) with an entry-wise absolute value and adds an L2 term (\\(\\lambda_2\\)).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each task's \\(BA\\) is a static low-rank matrix that can be merged into \\(W_0\\) after training, and inference needs no task ID.","identity_at_init":"Each new task's LoRA uses the standard init (\\(B_t = 0\\)), so the model function is unchanged at the start of every task.","key_idea":"Use each finished task's LoRA subspace as a cheap stand-in for its gradient subspace, instead of storing gradients as OGD does, and learn the next task's LoRA in a subspace orthogonal to all earlier ones. This gives continual learning without rehearsal or task IDs and preserves instruction-following generalization.","results_note":"On the standard 5-task continual-learning benchmark with T5-large, O-LoRA averages 75.8% over three task orders, against 72.7% for LFPT5 (the paper's previous state of the art) and 80.0% for multi-task training. On the 15-task benchmark it averages 69.6% against 69.2% for LFPT5. ProgPrompt, which needs task IDs at inference, scores 75.1% and 77.9%.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"one LoRA per task, with earlier LoRAs frozen"},{"target":"inflora","type":"competes-with","note":"O-LoRA uses a soft orthogonality penalty between learned subspaces; InfLoRA uses a hard, data-designed frozen down-projection"},{"target":"osf","type":"competes-with","note":"both enforce orthogonality to the update subspaces of earlier tasks; OSF does so inside full-rank SVD components"},{"target":"sd-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both grow per-task LoRA components for rehearsal-free continual learning without task IDs; SD-LoRA decouples magnitude and direction instead of enforcing orthogonality (SD-LoRA does not benchmark O-LoRA)"},{"target":"orthogonal-adaptation","type":"competes-with","note":"both make per-task or per-concept LoRAs orthogonal so their sum does not interfere; O-LoRA uses a penalty during sequential training, Orthogonal Adaptation fixed orthogonal bases for later merging"},{"target":"l2p","type":"competes-with","note":"rehearsal-free continual-learning baseline that uses a prompt pool"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14152","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.14152","https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.715/","https://github.com/cmnfriend/O-LoRA","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cmnfriend/O-LoRA/main/src/uie_trainer_lora.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Xiao Wang","Tianze Chen","Qiming Ge","Han Xia","Rong Bao","Rui Zheng","Qi Zhang","Tao Gui","Xuanjing Huang"],"bibkey":"wang2023orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2023orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Subspace Learning for Language Model Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Xiao Wang and Tianze Chen and Qiming Ge and Han Xia and Rong Bao and Rui Zheng and Qi Zhang and Tao Gui and Xuanjing Huang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023},\n  eprint = {2310.14152},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-emnlp.715/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per task, LoRA at the merged base \\(W_0+\\sum_{i<t}B_iA_i\\), with a soft orthogonality penalty against earlier tasks. The penalty is a loss, not part of \\(\\rho\\).","shape":"LoRA's cone at each stage.","base_point":"\\(B_t=0\\): each task starts at the apex of its stage.","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) has \\(GL_r\\), and the penalty breaks it: the paper's \\(\\lVert B_i^\\top B_t\\rVert_F^2\\) is only \\(O(r)\\)-invariant in \\(B_t\\), and the code's entrywise \\(\\sum\\lvert A_iA_t^\\top\\rvert\\) only signed-permutation-invariant. Their zero sets (exact orthogonality of column or row spaces) are \\(GL_r\\)-invariant.","covariance":"The soft penalty leaves LoRA's basis-free image. Its hard limit \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B_t\\perp\\mathrm{col}\\,B_{<t}\\) would use the output inner product (CO×GL; the code's version is GL×CO).","merge":"Each task's \\(BA\\) is static and merges.","rebasing":"Across tasks it is ReLoRA's merge-and-restart scheme with one stage per task (Def V.1(c)); after \\(T\\) tasks the reach is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le Tr}\\). Kind stays R, since the stages change the loss rather than the compression."},"categorical_reading":"O-LoRA is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus T}\\) unrolled over tasks, i.e. ReLoRA with one stage per task, plus a penalty that pushes each new stage away from the earlier ones. The paper penalises column spaces of the up-projections and the official code row spaces of the down-projections, so the two versions enforce orthogonality on opposite sides of the update.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A5","h":"h=\\big([B_1\\ \\cdots\\ B_T],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_T]\\big)","note":"\\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus T}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Tr}\\) (Obs I.7), pointed iff the stacked \\(A\\)'s have full row rank."},{"target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{on each stage's }(B_t,A_t)","note":"The same merge-and-restart scheme with one stage per task; only the loss differs per stage."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"inflora","test":"O2","reason":"Each O-LoRA stage is a full LoRA\\(_r\\), with \\(d=r(m+n-r)\\), while an InfLoRA stage is LoRA-FA\\((A_t)\\) with \\(d=rm\\), so O-LoRA stages do not factor through InfLoRA's."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"inflora","name":"InfLoRA","full_name":"Interference-Free Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"InfLoRA: Interference-Free Low-Rank Adaptation for Continual Learning","authors":"Liang & Li","year":2024,"venue":"CVPR 2024","arxiv":"2404.00228","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00228","code_url":"https://github.com/liangyanshuo/InfLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_t=W_{t-1}+B_tA_t,\\quad A_t=(U_t)_{[1:r]}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen, from the SVD } \\hat H_t^{\\top}=V_t\\Sigma_tU_t,\\ \\ \\hat H_t=(I-M_tM_t^{\\top})H_t\\ \\ \\text{or}\\ \\ M_t^{\\perp}M_t^{\\perp\\top}H_t;\\quad B_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained, init } 0;\\quad \\Rightarrow\\ \\Delta_{B_t}W_t=(\\Delta_{W}W_t)\\,A_t^{\\top}A_t,\\ \\ (B_tA_t)\\,M_t=0","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per task (}A_t\\text{ is designed, not trained; one } r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ branch is kept at a time, earlier branches are merged)}","locus":"key and value projections of every ViT-B/16 attention block (variant InfLoRA-b5: bottom 5 blocks only)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Rank-\\(r\\) additive update with a frozen, data-designed down-projection \\(A_t\\). Its orthonormal rows are the top principal directions of the current task's layer inputs \\(H_t\\) after projection onto the orthogonal complement of the old tasks' input subspace \\(\\mathrm{span}(M_t)\\), which DualGPM maintains; they therefore lie in \\(N_t\\) intersected with \\(M_t^{\\perp}\\). Training \\(B_t\\) alone equals full fine-tuning projected onto \\(\\mathrm{row}(A_t)\\), \\(\\Delta W = G A_t^T A_t\\), so \\(\\Delta W\\) annihilates old-task inputs. Notation is swapped relative to the paper, which calls the up-projection \\(A_t\\) and the down-projection \\(B_t\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After each task the branch \\(B_t A_t\\) is merged into \\(W_{t-1}\\), and inference uses a single weight matrix without task identity.","identity_at_init":"\\(B_t = 0\\) (zero-initialized up-projection), so the function is unchanged at the start of each task. \\(A_t\\) is set before training by an SVD of the projected current-task activations.","key_idea":"Fine-tuning only the up-projection of a LoRA branch is exactly full fine-tuning restricted to the row space of the frozen down-projection. Choosing that row space inside the new task's input span but orthogonal to the old tasks' input space removes interference by construction.","results_note":"With an ImageNet-21K ViT-B/16, InfLoRA reaches final accuracies of 75.65% on 10-task ImageNet-R (77.52% with 5 tasks, 71.01% with 20), 86.51% on 10-task CIFAR-100 and 74.53% on 5-task DomainNet. It leads L2P, DualPrompt, CODA-P, C-LoRA and LAE on ImageNet-R and DomainNet. On CIFAR-100, CODA-P has a slightly higher final accuracy (86.62%), while InfLoRA has the best averaged accuracy (91.70% vs 91.08%).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"one LoRA-like branch per task, merged after training"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"special-case-of","note":"per task it is structurally LoRA-FA (frozen down-projection, trained up-projection), with a designed rather than random down-projection"},{"target":"o-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"hard, data-designed subspace versus O-LoRA's soft orthogonality penalty"},{"target":"sd-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"SD-LoRA reports 75.26% vs InfLoRA's 69.89% on 20-task ImageNet-R"},{"target":"osf","type":"competes-with","note":"both restrict continual-learning updates to subspaces orthogonal to what earlier tasks use"},{"target":"dualprompt","type":"competes-with","note":"prompt-based rehearsal-free class-incremental baseline"},{"target":"l2p","type":"competes-with","note":"prompt-pool baseline"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00228","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.00228","https://github.com/liangyanshuo/InfLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Yan-Shuo Liang","Wu-Jun Li"],"bibkey":"liang2024inflora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liang2024inflora,\n  title = {{InfLoRA: Interference-Free Low-Rank Adaptation for Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Yan-Shuo Liang and Wu-Jun Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2404.00228},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.00228}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per task, \\(W_{t-1}+B_tA_t\\) with a designed frozen \\(A_t\\) and a trained \\(B_t\\); the branch is merged after each task.","shape":"Linear: \\(\\{XA_t\\}\\), of dimension \\(rd_{out}\\), with \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\) inside the new task's input span and orthogonal to the old tasks' input subspace \\(M_t\\).","base_point":"\\(B_t=0\\) on a linear method: regular.","gauge":"None: \\(A_t\\) has orthonormal rows.","covariance":"\\(A_t\\) comes from an SVD of activations projected with the standard inner product, so the input side is only \\(CO(n)\\) (a data frame), while the output side is free: \\(GL\\times CO\\).","merge":"Each branch is merged after its task.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme with one stage per task and a moving designed frame; its reach is \\(W_0+\\{X:\\mathrm{row}\\,X\\subseteq\\sum_t\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\}\\). Each stage alone is idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"InfLoRA is LoRA-FA with a data-designed frame, re-based once per task; in the cotangent table it shares GaLore's cell ('data frame, re-based periodically'), with the frame taken from projected activations rather than gradients. By the degree-one collapse a stage is full fine-tuning projected onto \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\), and \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\perp M_t\\) gives \\(\\Delta W\\,M_t=0\\): old-task inputs are annihilated by construction.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per task it is LoRA-FA\\((A_0)\\) with \\(A_0=A_t\\)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_t)","note":"Freezing \\(A\\) at \\(A_t\\): \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_t)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_t)}\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"sd-lora","test":"O3","reason":"Each InfLoRA stage is pointed at \\(\\theta_{t-1}\\) (\\(B_t=0\\)), while SD-LoRA's normalisation forces a unit-norm offset at the start of every task, so no pointed arrow maps one stage into the other."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"textual-inversion","name":"Textual Inversion","full_name":"Textual Inversion (learned pseudo-word embedding)","paper_title":"An Image is Worth One Word: Personalizing Text-to-Image Generation using Textual Inversion","authors":"Gal et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2208.01618","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01618","code_url":"https://github.com/rinongal/textual_inversion","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"v_*=\\arg\\min_{v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{z\\sim\\mathcal{E}(x),\\,y,\\,\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I),\\,t}\\Big[\\big\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_\\theta\\big(z_t,t,c_\\theta(y)\\big)\\big\\|_2^2\\Big],\\quad y=\\text{neutral template (e.g. ``A photo of } S_*\\text{'') whose token } S_* \\text{ is embedded as } v;\\quad c_\\theta,\\ \\epsilon_\\theta,\\ \\mathcal{E}\\ \\text{and all existing token embeddings frozen}","trainable_params":"d\\ \\ \\text{(one token embedding; } k\\,d \\text{ for a } k\\text{-vector pseudo-word)}","locus":"token-embedding table of the frozen text encoder (one new row for the pseudo-word \\(S_*\\)); the pseudo-word can appear anywhere in a prompt","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Extends the frozen vocabulary with one learned point \\(v_*\\) in input-embedding space. The update is a single new row of the embedding matrix, used compositionally inside arbitrary natural-language prompts rather than as a fixed task prefix.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The learned vector is just a new vocabulary entry: inference runs the unchanged text encoder and diffusion model at the same cost, with one token standing in for a word.","identity_at_init":"The model itself is untouched. \\(v\\) is initialized to the embedding of a single-word coarse descriptor (e.g. 'cat', 'sculpture'), so prompts without \\(S_*\\) behave exactly as before and \\(S_*\\) starts out as that word.","key_idea":"Personalize a frozen text-to-image model by inverting 3-5 images into a new word in its text-embedding space, trained with the ordinary denoising loss. One word embedding is enough to capture an object or style, which can then be composed into new prompts.","results_note":"On LDM a single learned embedding reproduces object and style concepts from 3-5 images more faithfully than human-written captions or DALL-E 2 image prompts. CLIP-based and user-study evaluations place it at a favourable point on the reconstruction-editability trade-off, which the learning rate can shift.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"same frozen-model soft-token principle; a single reusable pseudo-word placed anywhere in a prompt instead of a fixed task prefix"},{"target":"trainable-tokens","type":"special-case-of","note":"equivalent to training only the embedding row of one newly added token in the text encoder"},{"target":"custom-diffusion","type":"competes-with","note":"Custom Diffusion also trains a new token (V*), together with the cross-attention K/V weights"},{"target":"hyperdreambooth","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative personalization of text-to-image models"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01618","https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01618","https://textual-inversion.github.io/","https://github.com/rinongal/textual_inversion","https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/training/text_inversion","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2208.01618?fields=title,year,venue"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Rinon Gal","Yuval Alaluf","Yuval Atzmon","Or Patashnik","Amit H. Bermano","Gal Chechik","Daniel Cohen-Or"],"bibkey":"gal2022image","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gal2022image,\n  title = {{An Image is Worth One Word: Personalizing Text-to-Image Generation using Textual Inversion}},\n  author = {Rinon Gal and Yuval Alaluf and Yuval Atzmon and Or Patashnik and Amit H. Bermano and Gal Chechik and Daniel Cohen-Or},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2208.01618},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01618}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The extension adds one row \\(v\\) to the embedding table, i.e. one new input symbol \\(S_*\\). On the original input space \\(X\\) every \\(v\\) is neutral, since no prompt without \\(S_*\\) reads the row. So on \\(X\\) the method is the frozen model, and its whole effect lives on the enlarged input space \\(X^+\\supset X\\).","shape":"Affine in \\(v\\): \\(E^+=[E_0;\\,v]\\), with \\(d=d_{\\rm emb}\\) (\\(kd\\) for a \\(k\\)-vector pseudo-word). Linear in the extended weight space \\(\\Theta^+\\); function-level relative to \\(\\Theta\\), which has no row for \\(S_*\\).","base_point":"\\(v_0=E_w\\) for a coarse descriptor \\(w\\) ('cat'). Then \\(\\tilde f(\\theta_0,v_0,-)=f(\\theta_0,-)\\circ(S_*\\mapsto w)\\), so the extension is pointed relative to substituting \\(w\\) for \\(S_*\\).","gauge":"\\(v\\mapsto[E_0;v]\\) is injective.","covariance":"As for Trainable Tokens: the row is a free vector on the embedding side (\\(GL\\)); on the vocabulary side only token-preserving (monomial) maps are allowed.","merge":"The learned row is an ordinary embedding entry, so inference cost is unchanged: M1 into its own box. It cannot be merged into \\(\\Theta\\) itself only because \\(f\\) has no input \\(S_*\\). That is a change of input type, not an M∞ obstruction.","rebasing":"One fixed row: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"Textual inversion is a pointed extension of the input alphabet rather than of the network. It adjoins one symbol \\(S_*\\) whose embedding is a free point \\(v\\in\\mathbb R^d\\), neutral at \\(v_0=E_w\\) via the substitution \\(S_*\\mapsto w\\). On the old inputs it is the initial object (Frozen). On prompts containing \\(S_*\\) it is Trainable Tokens on the extended table, the Lin row method of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta^+,\\theta_0^+)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"trainable-tokens","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ v\\mapsto V_{\\{S_*\\}}","note":"Trainable Tokens on the extended vocabulary with \\(T=\\{S_*\\}\\), pointed at the descriptor row \\(E_w\\). This is HF PEFT's TrainableTokensConfig on a newly added token after resize_token_embeddings."},{"target":"custom-diffusion","rule":"A2","h":"h(v)=(\\Delta W^k=0,\\ \\Delta W^v=0,\\ V^*=v)","note":"Freezing Custom Diffusion's cross-attention \\(W^k,W^v\\) at \\(W_0\\) leaves its modifier-token row. The target is Custom Diffusion pointed at \\((0,0,E_w)\\) rather than at its rare-token init."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","test":"O3","reason":"Textual inversion is pointed: \\(v_0=E_w\\) reproduces the prompt with the word \\(w\\). A soft prompt always draws attention mass, and on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) it has no neutral value (Thm VI.5(f)). The base-point strata differ, so there is no pointed arrow either way."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"custom-diffusion","name":"Custom Diffusion","full_name":"Custom Diffusion (cross-attention K/V fine-tuning for multi-concept customization)","paper_title":"Multi-Concept Customization of Text-to-Image Diffusion","authors":"Kumari et al.","year":2022,"venue":"CVPR 2023","arxiv":"2212.04488","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04488","code_url":"https://github.com/adobe-research/custom-diffusion","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"K=W^{k}c,\\ V=W^{v}c\\ \\text{in every U-Net cross-attention layer};\\quad W^{k}=W^{k}_0+\\Delta W^{k},\\ W^{v}=W^{v}_0+\\Delta W^{v}\\ \\text{(dense)},\\ \\text{plus a new modifier-token embedding } V^{*};\\ \\text{all other weights frozen; loss: denoising loss on the target images plus 200 retrieved real regularization images};\\quad \\text{merging } N \\text{ concepts: } \\hat W=\\arg\\min_W\\|WC_{\\text{reg}}^{\\top}-W_0C_{\\text{reg}}^{\\top}\\|_F\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ \\ WC^{\\top}=V_{\\text{tgt}},\\ \\ \\hat W=W_0+\\mathbf{v}^{\\top}\\mathbf{d},\\ \\ \\mathbf{d}=C(C_{\\text{reg}}^{\\top}C_{\\text{reg}})^{-1},\\ \\ \\mathbf{v}^{\\top}=(V_{\\text{tgt}}-W_0C^{\\top})(\\mathbf{d}C^{\\top})^{-1}","trainable_params":"2\\,d_{out}\\,d_{text}\\ \\text{per cross-attention layer } (W^{k},W^{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{text}}) \\;+\\; d_{text}\\ \\text{per modifier token}","locus":"key and value projections of the text features in all cross-attention layers of the diffusion U-Net (Stable Diffusion), plus the modifier-token embedding","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"Block mask over the parameter tensors: only the two matrices that read the text condition (\\(W^k, W^v\\)) are updated, densely. Multi-concept merging is a closed-form, equality-constrained least-squares projection: it maps each concept's target-word features to the fine-tuned values while staying close to \\(W_0\\) on about 1000 regularization captions.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The fine-tuned \\(W^k\\) and \\(W^v\\) replace the originals and \\(V^*\\) is one extra vocabulary row, so inference runs the original architecture at the original cost; the closed-form merge folds several concepts into a single pair of matrices.","identity_at_init":"Training starts from the pretrained \\(W^k, W^v\\) (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)), and \\(V^*\\) is initialized to a rarely occurring token's embedding, so the model is initially unchanged on ordinary prompts.","key_idea":"Fine-tuning moves the cross-attention weights much more than any others, so update only the K/V projections that map text into latent features, together with a learned modifier token and retrieved real images as regularization. The method is fast, small and resists language drift, and concepts can be combined by joint training or a closed-form constrained merge.","results_note":"On Stable Diffusion it beats Textual Inversion and matches or beats DreamBooth on text/image alignment and human preference, while training about 5x faster (~6 minutes) and storing 75MB instead of 3GB per concept. The closed-form multi-concept merge takes about 2 seconds.","relations":[{"target":"textual-inversion","type":"combines","note":"also trains a new token embedding V* jointly with W^k and W^v"},{"target":"regmean","type":"competes-with","note":"both merge linear layers in closed form by least squares on input features; Custom Diffusion adds hard equality constraints on the concepts' target-word features"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"the paper reports that the K/V updates are effectively low-rank (5x SVD compression with similar quality), though training them as low-rank directly was worse"},{"target":"svdiff","type":"competes-with","note":"compact diffusion personalization that also supports multi-concept generation"},{"target":"mix-of-show","type":"competes-with","note":"multi-concept customization by merging per-concept adapters"},{"target":"ziplora","type":"competes-with","note":"merging independently trained subject and style adapters"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04488","https://arxiv.org/html/2212.04488","https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~custom-diffusion/","https://github.com/adobe-research/custom-diffusion","https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers/tree/main/examples/custom_diffusion"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Nupur Kumari","Bingliang Zhang","Richard Zhang","Eli Shechtman","Jun-Yan Zhu"],"bibkey":"kumari2022multi","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kumari2022multi,\n  title = {{Multi-Concept Customization of Text-to-Image Diffusion}},\n  author = {Nupur Kumari and Bingliang Zhang and Richard Zhang and Eli Shechtman and Jun-Yan Zhu},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2212.04488},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04488}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Two summands. One is a selective block: dense \\(W^k,W^v\\) in every cross-attention layer, an R part. The other is a new modifier-token row \\(V^*\\), a vocabulary extension as in Textual Inversion. The extension makes the whole object E: an extension followed by a reparametrisation of \\(\\Theta\\times\\mathbb R^{d_{\\rm text}}\\).","shape":"Affine: a coordinate block of the extended weight space, of dimension \\(2d_{\\rm out}d_{\\rm text}\\) per cross-attention layer plus \\(d_{\\rm text}\\) for \\(V^*\\).","base_point":"\\(\\Delta W=0\\), and \\(V^*_0\\) is a rare token's embedding. The model is unchanged on ordinary prompts and treats \\(V^*\\) as that rare token.","gauge":"A coordinate inclusion: injective.","covariance":"Each selected box is fully trainable (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The modifier row is Mon×GL on the vocabulary side, as for Textual Inversion.","merge":"The fine-tuned \\(W^k,W^v\\) replace the originals and \\(V^*\\) is one vocabulary row. The closed-form multi-concept merge is a separate post-hoc operation, a constrained least squares of RegMean type.","rebasing":"Fixed block: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"Custom Diffusion is Textual Inversion \\(\\oplus\\) a selective block. The block is surgical fine-tuning of exactly the boxes that read the text wire (\\(W^k,W^v\\)), plus one adjoined vocabulary symbol. Its multi-concept merge is a P operation \\(\\Theta^N\\to\\Theta\\). It solves a least-squares problem under hard equality constraints on the concepts' features, so it is a constrained RegMean rather than task arithmetic.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Dense \\(W^k,W^v\\) updates reach rank \\(\\min(d_{\\rm out},d_{\\rm text})>r\\). The paper finds low-rank training worse; only post-hoc SVD compression comes close."},{"target":"svdiff","test":"O1","reason":"SVDiff keeps \\(W_0\\)'s singular frames, an absolute invariant; dense K/V updates move them."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"esft","name":"ESFT","full_name":"Expert-Specialized Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Let the Expert Stick to His Last: Expert-Specialized Fine-Tuning for Sparse Architectural Large Language Models","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024","arxiv":"2407.01906","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.46/","code_url":"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/ESFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"g_i^{l}=\\frac{1}{N_s}\\sum_{j=1}^{N_s}\\frac{1}{L_j}\\sum_{k=1}^{L_j}g_{i,k}^{l}\\ \\ (\\text{ESFT-Gate})\\quad\\text{or}\\quad r_i^{l}=\\frac{1}{N_s}\\sum_{j=1}^{N_s}\\frac{1}{L_j}\\sum_{k=1}^{L_j}\\frac{\\mathbb{1}(g_{i,k}^{l}>0)}{K}\\ \\ (\\text{ESFT-Token});\\quad E_s^{l}=\\text{top-scored experts with }\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in E_s^{l}}R_i^{l}\\ge p;\\quad \\theta_e\\ (e\\in E_s^{l})\\ \\text{trained; all other routed experts, shared experts, attention, router and embeddings frozen}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{l=1}^{L}|E_s^{l}|\\cdot 3\\,d\\,d_{ff}^{(e)}\\ \\ (\\text{gate/up/down projections of each selected routed expert; on average 2-15 of 66 experts per layer})","locus":"the routed (non-shared) FFN experts in each MoE layer that are most relevant to the task; attention, router, shared experts and embeddings stay frozen","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"Data-dependent block mask over experts. In each layer the selected set is the smallest group of top-scored routed experts whose cumulative relevance (average gate score or token-selection ratio on sampled task data) reaches p. Selected experts are updated densely, everything else is frozen, and routing stays free during training and inference.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The updated experts simply replace the originals, so the architecture, routing and inference cost are unchanged.","identity_at_init":"Training starts from the pretrained weights (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); expert selection is computed from router statistics on a task sample.","key_idea":"In fine-grained MoE LLMs, each task activates a concentrated and distinct set of experts, so fine-tune only the experts most relevant to the task. This nearly matches full fine-tuning on the target task, forgets less, and cuts storage by up to 90%.","results_note":"On DeepSeek-V2-Lite, ESFT-Gate averages 50.2 on downstream tasks (FFT 51.0, LoRA 44.9) while keeping more general ability (ESFT-Token 61.5 vs FFT 58.8 and LoRA 59.1), with 2.57-3.20 GB of trained parameters versus 28.6 GB for FFT.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"main PEFT baseline (rank 8); ESFT beats it on both task performance and general-ability retention"},{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"block-level selective tuning placed where the task lives; ESFT chooses experts from router statistics instead of choosing layers"},{"target":"lisa","type":"competes-with","note":"selective full-parameter tuning of a subset of blocks (layers rather than experts)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01906","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.01906","https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.46/","https://github.com/deepseek-ai/ESFT"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Zihan Wang","Deli Chen","Damai Dai","Runxin Xu","Zhuoshu Li","Y. Wu"],"bibkey":"wang2024let","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024let,\n  title = {{Let the Expert Stick to His Last: Expert-Specialized Fine-Tuning for Sparse Architectural Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Zihan Wang and Deli Chen and Damai Dai and Runxin Xu and Zhuoshu Li and Y. Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2407.01906},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.46/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The selected routed experts are fine-tuned in place. Router, attention, shared experts and embeddings stay frozen.","shape":"A coordinate subspace: the full weights of the selected experts \\(E_s^l\\) in each layer, \\(d=\\sum_l|E_s^l|\\cdot3dd_{ff}^{(e)}\\).","covariance":"Each selected expert is fully trainable box by box (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). Experts are blocks the MoE already has, so no partition is imposed. The selection is a data statistic of the frozen router, which every function-preserving reparametrisation carries along.","rebasing":"Fixed expert set: idempotent. Recomputing relevance scores after a merge could change the set and gain."},"categorical_reading":"ESFT is surgical fine-tuning indexed by experts instead of layers: the trainable set is a union of whole expert boxes, chosen from router statistics on task data. Like surgical FT and Spectrum, it is a Lin, regular, idempotent coordinate block. Only the selection rule (routing mass) is new.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Each selected expert gets a dense update of rank up to \\(\\min(d,d_{ff}^{(e)})>r\\)."},{"target":"lisa","test":"O1","reason":"ESFT trains a few experts in every MoE layer at once, on a fixed support. A LISA stage trains only \\(\\gamma\\) sampled layers (all experts in them) plus embeddings and head. So, when \\(\\gamma\\) is below the number of MoE layers, no single stage contains ESFT's image. (That ESFT never touches the embeddings blocks only the converse LISA \\(\\to\\) ESFT.)"}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"intruder-dimension-reduction","name":"Intruder-dimension reduction","full_name":"Intruder Dimension Reduction (post-hoc spectral surgery on LoRA adapters)","paper_title":"LoRA vs Full Fine-tuning: An Illusion of Equivalence","authors":"Shuttleworth et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2410.21228","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/intruders.py","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"from peft.tuners.lora.intruders import reduce_intruder_dimension; reduce_intruder_dimension(peft_model, old_adapter_name='default', new_adapter_name='intruder_reduced', top_k=10, threshold_epsilon=0.5, mitigation_lambda=0.75)  # adds and activates a new LoRA adapter","formula_latex":"W_0+\\Delta W=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ \\ W_0=U^{0}\\Sigma^{0}V^{0\\top};\\quad \\mathcal{I}=\\{\\,j\\le k:\\ \\max_i|\\langle u_j,u^{0}_i\\rangle|<\\epsilon\\,\\};\\quad W'=W_0+\\Delta W+(\\lambda-1)\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{I}}\\sigma_j\\,u_jv_j^{\\top};\\quad (B',A')=\\text{rank-}r\\ \\text{truncated SVD of } (W'-W_0)/s,\\ \\ s=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\ (\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}\\text{ with rsLoRA}),\\ \\ B'=U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\ A'=\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^{\\top};\\quad \\text{no training: } W_0 \\text{ frozen, the trained } (B,A) \\text{ replaced by } (B',A')\\ \\ (\\text{paper: only the top-ranked } j\\in\\mathcal{I}\\text{ is scaled, no re-factorization})","trainable_params":"0\\ \\ (\\text{training-free; the output is again a rank-}r\\text{ adapter with } r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ parameters per matrix})","locus":"every LoRA-adapted linear (or embedding) layer of an already trained adapter","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Spectral endomorphism on the space of trained adapters. Among the top-\\(k\\) singular directions of the merged matrix, those nearly orthogonal to every pretrained left singular vector (the intruders) are shrunk by \\(\\lambda\\), and the edited \\(\\Delta W\\) is projected back to rank \\(r\\). The paper scales only the top intruder per matrix and does not re-project; HF PEFT scales every detected intruder and re-factorizes to rank \\(r\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The result is an ordinary LoRA adapter (\\(B', A'\\)) that merges into \\(W_0\\) like any other LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (post-hoc edit). \\(\\lambda = 1\\) leaves the adapter unchanged up to rank-\\(r\\) truncation, \\(\\lambda = 0\\) removes the intruder components, and \\(\\lambda > 1\\) amplifies them.","key_idea":"LoRA creates high-ranking intruder singular vectors that are nearly orthogonal to the pretrained spectrum; full fine-tuning does not. These directions drive most of the forgetting, so shrinking them after training recovers pretrained behaviour at little cost in task accuracy.","results_note":"Scaling the top intruder dimension of each matrix with \\(\\lambda = 0.7\\) (RoBERTa-base on QQP, LoRA \\(r = 8\\)) left test accuracy unchanged and cut fine-tuning-induced forgetting (pre-training loss) by 33.2%. For LLaMA2-7B on MetaMath (\\(r = 256\\)), \\(\\lambda = 0.3\\) cost 0.1% accuracy and cut forgetting by 33.3%.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"post-hoc edit of a trained LoRA adapter, returning a LoRA adapter of the same rank"},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"a preventive alternative: train in the minor singular directions so the principal spectrum is not disturbed; intruder reduction is a post-hoc repair"},{"target":"lora-null","type":"competes-with","note":"knowledge-preserving initialization versus post-hoc removal of the components that cause forgetting"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"the paper finds that PiSSA, which edits principal singular vectors, also produces many intruder dimensions"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.21228","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=LoRA%20vs%20Full%20Fine-tuning%20Illusion%20of%20Equivalence","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/intruders.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/lora","https://api.github.com/repos/huggingface/peft/releases/tags/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"authors_full":["Reece Shuttleworth","Jacob Andreas","Antonio Torralba","Pratyusha Sharma"],"bibkey":"shuttleworth2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shuttleworth2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA vs Full Fine-tuning: An Illusion of Equivalence}},\n  author = {Reece Shuttleworth and Jacob Andreas and Antonio Torralba and Pratyusha Sharma},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2410.21228},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Training-free: an endomorphism of the space of trained LoRA adapters. The catalog files it as a training procedure, but it is post hoc.","shape":"With \\(W_1=W_0+\\Delta W\\), the \\(\\lambda\\)-family \\(\\lambda\\mapsto W_1+(\\lambda-1)\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal I}\\sigma_ju_jv_j^\\top\\) rescales the intruder singular values of \\(W_1\\) along fixed singular vectors. It is affine in \\(\\lambda\\) (the closure of a one-parameter torus orbit). Since a single \\(\\lambda\\) (HF mitigation_lambda) is shared by every adapted matrix, \\(d=1\\) for the whole model, not per matrix. HF's rank-\\(r\\) re-factorisation makes it smooth but not affine near \\(\\lambda=1\\).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=1\\) returns the trained adapter, so relative to \\(\\theta_0\\) the family carries the defect \\(e=\\Delta W\\) by design. In the slice at \\(W_1\\) it is regular. No \\(\\lambda\\) returns \\(\\theta_0\\) unless \\(\\Delta W\\) lies in the intruder span.","gauge":"Injective in \\(\\lambda\\) once an intruder is detected. The rule reads only \\(W_0+sBA\\), so it is constant on LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) fibres: gauge-invariant (Prop V.4(a)), unlike factor-wise rules.","covariance":"Intruders are detected by \\(|\\langle u_j,u^0_i\\rangle|\\) between the left singular frames of \\(W_1\\) and \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\).","merge":"The output is again a LoRA adapter.","rebasing":"Applying the rule again to \\(W'\\) finds the same singular vectors with values \\(\\lambda\\sigma_j\\). The family is a multiplicative monoid on fixed directions, hence idempotent (until an intruder drops out of the top \\(k\\))."},"categorical_reading":"A post-hoc spectral operation on the image of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\). A one-parameter torus acts on those singular values of the merged weight whose left singular vectors are nearly orthogonal to \\(W_0\\)'s. In other words it is SVDiff re-based at the trained point and restricted to the intruder directions. It reads only the product \\(BA\\), so it descends to adapters (Prop V.4(a)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"svdiff","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=\\delta,\\quad \\delta_j=(\\lambda-1)\\sigma_j\\ (j\\in\\mathcal I),\\ \\ \\delta_j=0\\ \\text{otherwise}","note":"Into SVDiff re-based at \\(W_1=W_0+\\Delta W\\) (its SVD frame), with \\(\\lambda=1\\) sent to \\(\\delta=0\\). Paper variant (no rank-\\(r\\) re-truncation), \\(\\lambda\\ge0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"wise-ft","test":"O1","reason":"WiSE-FT moves along \\(W_0+\\alpha\\Delta W\\), shrinking the whole update toward \\(\\theta_0\\). Intruder reduction moves along \\(W_1+(\\lambda-1)\\sigma_ju_jv_j^\\top\\). The two lines meet only at \\(W_1\\), unless \\(\\Delta W\\) is itself the intruder component."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"coda-prompt","name":"CODA-Prompt","full_name":"COntinual Decomposed Attention-based Prompting (CODA-P)","paper_title":"CODA-Prompt: COntinual Decomposed Attention-based Prompting for Rehearsal-Free Continual Learning","authors":"Smith et al.","year":2022,"venue":"CVPR 2023","arxiv":"2211.13218","url":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2023/html/Smith_CODA-Prompt_COntinual_Decomposed_Attention-Based_Prompting_for_Rehearsal-Free_Continual_Learning_CVPR_2023_paper.html","code_url":"https://github.com/GT-RIPL/CODA-Prompt","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"p(x)=\\sum_{m=1}^{M}\\alpha_m(x)\\,P_m,\\quad \\alpha_m(x)=\\gamma\\big(q(x)\\odot A_m,\\;K_m\\big),\\quad q(x)=\\theta(x)_{[\\mathrm{CLS}]}\\ \\text{(frozen ViT pass)},\\ \\gamma=\\cos;\\quad p=[p_K;p_V],\\ p_K,p_V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{\\ell}{2}\\times d}:\\ \\mathrm{MSA}\\big(h_Q,[p_K;h_K],[p_V;h_V]\\big)\\ \\text{at each prompted layer};\\quad \\min_{P^{n},K^{n},A^{n},\\phi_n}\\ \\mathcal{L}\\big(f_\\phi(f_{\\theta,P,K,A}(x)),y\\big)+\\lambda\\big(\\|PP^{\\top}-I\\|_2+\\|KK^{\\top}-I\\|_2+\\|AA^{\\top}-I\\|_2\\big);\\ \\ \\theta\\ \\text{frozen},\\ \\text{components of earlier tasks frozen}","trainable_params":"n_p\\,M\\,(\\ell d+2d)+\\text{head}\\quad(n_p=\\text{prompted layers, 5 in the paper};\\ M/N\\ \\text{new components trained per task})","locus":"keys/values (prefix form) of MSA layers 1-5 of a frozen ViT-B/16; the query comes from a separate frozen forward pass ([CLS] output)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Input-conditioned linear combination of a growing dictionary of prefix components, \\(p(x) = \\sum_m \\alpha_m(x) P_m\\). The weights are unnormalized cosine similarities between a feature-gated query \\(q(x) \\odot A_m\\) and a key \\(K_m\\). Each task adds \\(M/N\\) components, and the components, keys and attention vectors of earlier tasks are frozen, so the trainable space is an increasing chain of component sets with soft orthogonality penalties.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prompt depends on the input through the query pass, and its prefix slots lengthen the key/value sequence, so nothing folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No identity: prefix slots change attention from the first step. \\(P\\), \\(K\\) and \\(A\\) are orthogonally initialized.","key_idea":"L2P and DualPrompt select prompts from a pool by hard key-query matching. CODA-Prompt replaces that with an attention-weighted sum of prompt components, so the selection mechanism is trained end to end with the task loss. Capacity grows by adding components rather than lengthening prompts, and freezing past components plus orthogonality limits forgetting.","results_note":"Rehearsal-free class-incremental ImageNet-R with ViT-B/16: 75.45% average final accuracy on 10 tasks vs 71.32% for DualPrompt (up to +4.5% across the 5/10/20-task splits). It is also ahead on CIFAR-100, DomainNet and a dual-shift ImageNet-R benchmark.","relations":[{"target":"l2p","type":"builds-on","note":"Replaces hard top-N retrieval from a prompt pool, whose keys are trained by a separate matching loss, with a continuous, end-to-end-trained weighted sum of components."},{"target":"dualprompt","type":"builds-on","note":"Keeps DualPrompt's prefix attachment at layers 1-5 of ViT-B/16 and changes only how the prompt is formed; outperforms it by up to 4.5% A_N on ImageNet-R."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"The prompt is split into p_K, p_V and prepended to the keys and values (prefix-tuning attachment)."},{"target":"attempt","type":"competes-with","note":"Both build instance-conditioned prompts as attention-weighted mixtures of prompt components. ATTEMPT mixes pre-trained source prompts for multi-task NLP; CODA mixes continually grown components for continual vision learning."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.13218","https://arxiv.org/html/2211.13218","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2023/html/Smith_CODA-Prompt_COntinual_Decomposed_Attention-Based_Prompting_for_Rehearsal-Free_Continual_Learning_CVPR_2023_paper.html","https://github.com/GT-RIPL/CODA-Prompt"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["James Seale Smith","Leonid Karlinsky","Vyshnavi Gutta","Paola Cascante-Bonilla","Donghyun Kim","Assaf Arbelle","Rameswar Panda","Rogerio Feris","Zsolt Kira"],"bibkey":"smith2022coda","bibtex":"@inproceedings{smith2022coda,\n  title = {{CODA-Prompt: COntinual Decomposed Attention-based Prompting for Rehearsal-Free Continual Learning}},\n  author = {James Seale Smith and Leonid Karlinsky and Vyshnavi Gutta and Paola Cascante-Bonilla and Donghyun Kim and Assaf Arbelle and Rameswar Panda and Rogerio Feris and Zsolt Kira},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2211.13218},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2023/html/Smith_CODA-Prompt_COntinual_Decomposed_Attention-Based_Prompting_for_Rehearsal-Free_Continual_Learning_CVPR_2023_paper.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\): the prefix \\(p(x)=\\sum_m\\alpha_m(x)P_m\\) is a family over inputs, with weights read from a separate frozen forward pass.","shape":"Function-level: for each \\(x\\), a prefix in the span of the components at layers 1–5.","base_point":"Prefix slots always draw softmax mass, even for \\(p=0\\): zero keys have logit 0.","gauge":"\\(\\alpha_m=\\cos(q\\odot A_m,K_m)\\) is invariant under positive rescalings of \\(A_m\\) and of \\(K_m\\), and under a joint sign flip: a torus of dimension \\(2M\\). \\(P_m\\) has no slide, since \\(\\alpha_m\\) is not a parameter.","covariance":"The Hadamard feature gate \\(q\\odot A_m\\) on the frozen query wire commutes only with monomial changes of basis there. The prefix components are free vectors on the key/value wire (\\(GL\\)).","merge":"Input-dependent prefix slots lengthen the key/value sequence (Thm VI.5(f)).","rebasing":"Nothing merges. Continual learning grows the component set instead, with earlier components frozen. That is an increasing chain of objects, not a rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"CODA-Prompt is a continuous family over inputs, \\(x\\mapsto\\sum_m\\alpha_m(x)P_m\\), of points of prefix tuning, with unnormalised cosine weights read from a frozen query pass. Each task adds components and freezes the earlier ones at their trained values. That freezing, not any property of the image, is how it limits forgetting.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"l2p","test":"O1","reason":"CODA's prompt varies continuously with \\(x\\), while L2P's is piecewise constant (hard top-\\(N\\) retrieval). So generic CODA realisations are not L2P realisations."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"A static prefix is a single point. CODA's prefix depends on \\(x\\) through \\(q(x)\\), so its realisations are generically not those of any single prefix."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"trans-lora","name":"Trans-LoRA","full_name":"Trans-LoRA (nearly data-free transfer of PEFT modules across base models by distillation on filtered synthetic data)","paper_title":"Trans-LoRA: towards data-free Transferable Parameter Efficient Finetuning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.17258","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/708fdc7911f11585ee7161518e509ae6-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\phi^\\ast=\\arg\\max_\\phi\\ \\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\log p_{\\phi}(\\text{yes}\\mid t(x))+\\mathbb{E}_{x\\sim\\mathcal{M}_s}\\log p_{\\phi}(\\text{no}\\mid t(x));\\quad \\mathcal{D}_{syn}=\\{x\\sim\\mathcal{M}_{gen}(\\cdot\\mid\\bar{\\mathcal{D}}):\\ p_{\\phi^\\ast}(\\text{yes}\\mid t(x))>p_{\\phi^\\ast}(\\text{no}\\mid t(x))\\},\\ |\\bar{\\mathcal{D}}|=5,\\ |\\mathcal{D}_{syn}|=|\\mathcal{D}|;\\quad \\theta_t=\\arg\\min_{\\theta}\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal{D}_{syn}}H\\big((\\mathcal{M}_t+\\theta)(x),\\,(\\mathcal{M}_s+\\theta_s)(x)\\big);\\ \\ \\mathcal{M}_s,\\mathcal{M}_t,\\theta_s\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\theta_t\\ \\text{trained (e.g. } W_t=W_{t,0}+B_tA_t)","trainable_params":"r\\,(d^{t}_{in}+d^{t}_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted target matrix (whatever the target PEFT needs)}\\ +\\ \\text{a one-off LoRA discriminator on }\\mathcal{M}_s","locus":"the target model's adapter locations (default LoRA on \\(W_q, W_v\\)). The transfer acts on the model's input-output behavior, not on weights.","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Function-level transport of an adapter along a change of base model. The source pair (\\(M_s, \\theta_s\\)) acts as a teacher, and the target adapter is fitted by knowledge distillation on synthetic inputs that a discriminator has filtered to imitate the distribution of the original training inputs. No weight correspondence between source and target is needed, so they may differ in family, size and even PEFT type (LoRA, DoRA, prompt tuning).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The output is an ordinary adapter of the chosen target type: a transferred LoRA or DoRA merges into the target weights, a transferred soft prompt does not.","identity_at_init":"The target adapter starts from the standard initialization of its type (for LoRA, \\(B = 0\\), so \\(M_t + \\theta_t = M_t\\) at the start). Algorithm 1 only says 'Initialize \\(\\theta_t\\)'.","key_idea":"When a base model is retired, its LoRAs can be retrained on the new base without the client's data. An LLM seeded with 5 examples synthesizes task inputs. A discriminator, trained once alongside the source LoRA, keeps only samples that look like the real training inputs. The target LoRA is then distilled from the source model plus LoRA on those samples.","results_note":"Across 86 BBH, MMLU, MBPP and GSM8K tasks with Llama-2 and Gemma (within and across families), the transferred LoRA matches or beats both the source LoRA and the unadapted target. For example, BBH Gemma-2b to Gemma-7b: 43.61 vs 31.84 (source LoRA) and 37.75 (target base). It also transfers LoRA<->DoRA and prompt tuning, and survives two consecutive transfers.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"The default transported object is a LoRA, retrained on the target as a LoRA."},{"target":"lora-x","type":"competes-with","note":"Same base-model-change problem. Trans-LoRA transports behavior by distillation, which needs synthetic data and training; LoRA-X transports weights by subspace projection, with no data and no training."},{"target":"dora","type":"combines","note":"The same pipeline transfers DoRA adapters and converts between LoRA and DoRA (Table 6)."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"combines","note":"Also transfers soft prompts, showing that the transport lives in function space and is independent of the adapter's parametrization."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17258","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.17258","https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/708fdc7911f11585ee7161518e509ae6-Abstract-Conference.html","https://github.com/raywang4/TransLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Runqian Wang","Soumya Ghosh","David Cox","Diego Antognini","Aude Oliva","Rogerio Feris","Leonid Karlinsky"],"bibkey":"wang2024trans","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024trans,\n  title = {{Trans-LoRA: towards data-free Transferable Parameter Efficient Finetuning}},\n  author = {Runqian Wang and Soumya Ghosh and David Cox and Diego Antognini and Aude Oliva and Rogerio Feris and Leonid Karlinsky},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.17258},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/708fdc7911f11585ee7161518e509ae6-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The transported object is an ordinary target-side LoRA (or DoRA, or soft prompt) trained by distillation. The source pair and the discriminator are training data, not part of \\(\\rho\\).","base_point":"Target LoRA at \\(B=0\\): the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta_t,\\theta_t)\\).","rebasing":"As LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"Trans-LoRA moves an adapter to a new base model that has no weight correspondence with the old one, so no base-change functor \\(\\phi_!\\) exists. Instead it lifts the source realisation \\(\\Phi_{f_s}(\\theta_s+\\Delta_s)\\) along the target realisation \\(\\Phi_{f_t}\\circ\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}\\), by distillation on synthetic inputs. The output is an object of the target slice, which is why the PEFT type may change in transit.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object on the target base. The distillation loss and the synthetic data are trained-method data."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-x","test":"O2","reason":"The target is the full rank-\\(r\\) cone, \\(d=r(m+n-r)\\). LoRA-X's target is confined to frozen singular subspaces, with \\(d=r\\) (or \\(r^2\\)). So a Trans-LoRA output is generically not a LoRA-X point."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-x","name":"LoRA-X","full_name":"Cross-Model Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"LoRA-X: Bridging Foundation Models with Training-Free Cross-Model Adaptation","authors":"Farhadzadeh et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2501.16559","url":"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1add3bbdbc20c403a383482a665eb5a4-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=U\\Sigma V^{\\top}\\ \\text{(SVD, cached)},\\quad W=W_0+\\tilde U_r\\,\\Delta\\Sigma\\,\\tilde V_r^{\\top},\\ \\ \\tilde U_r\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\tilde V_r\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r}\\ \\text{(top-}r\\text{ singular vectors, frozen)},\\ \\ \\Delta\\Sigma=\\operatorname{diag}(\\delta\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\delta\\sigma_r)\\ \\text{(or a full } r\\times r\\text{ core) trained},\\ \\sigma_i+\\delta\\sigma_i\\ge 0;\\quad \\text{transfer (no training): }\\Delta W_{t\\leftarrow s}=U_tU_t^{\\top}\\,\\Delta W_s\\,V_tV_t^{\\top}=U_t\\big(U_t^{\\top}\\tilde U_s\\,\\Delta\\Sigma_s\\,\\tilde V_s^{\\top}V_t\\big)V_t^{\\top}","trainable_params":"r\\ \\ (\\text{diagonal }\\Delta\\Sigma)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad r^{2}\\ \\ (\\text{full core})","locus":"attention projections (to_q, to_k, to_v, to_out) of diffusion UNets: SDXL family (UNet only) and SD-v1.5 family (UNet and text-encoder attention); TinyLlama in an appendix. Transfer skips 'filter blocks' with low source-target subspace similarity and re-matches some blocks to a more similar source block.","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"The update lives in \\(\\mathrm{span}(U_r) \\otimes \\mathrm{span}(V_r)\\) of \\(W_0\\), i.e. a spectral core between frozen top-\\(r\\) SVD bases. Cross-model transfer is a two-sided orthogonal projection onto the target weight's column and row spaces (Eq. 3). Layers are matched by subspace similarity (Eq. 4); for mismatched sizes a least-squares map replaces the source bases (Sec. 4.2.2). Across architectures, an optimal-transport cost over the layer-similarity matrix (the Adapter Transferability Cost) scores how hard a transfer is; it is a metric, not the transfer map itself. Caveat (derived): the released pseudocode uses a thin SVD, so for square full-rank layers the projection is the identity and transfer copies \\(\\Delta W_s\\) unchanged.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both the trained and the transferred adapter are plain additive matrices that fold into the source or target \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not stated in the paper. The natural choice \\(\\Delta \\Sigma = 0\\) (inferred) gives \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"An adapter becomes portable if it may not create new feature directions. LoRA-X trains only a rescaling or mixing of the base weight's top singular directions, then moves it to a new base model (an updated, distilled or fine-tuned version) by projecting onto that model's singular subspaces, with no data and no training.","results_note":"Training-free transfer of LoRA-X style adapters, from SD-v1.5 to SD Eff-v1.0/RV-v3.0 and from SDXL to SSD-1B/RVXL-v3.0, gives HPSv2, LPIPS-diversity and DINOv2 scores close to adapters trained directly on the target, whereas projected plain LoRA degrades markedly. A TinyLlama 3T to 2.5T text experiment shows the same.","relations":[{"target":"svdiff","type":"special-case-of","note":"The diagonal variant (the one used in all experiments, r = 320) is SVDiff restricted to the top-r singular values: W = U diag(s) V^T with s_i = sigma_i + delta sigma_i >= 0 for i <= r and s_i = sigma_i otherwise. The paper calls SVDiff 'a similar adapter structure' and differs by truncation, by touching only attention layers, and by targeting transferability."},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"equivalent-to","note":"With a full r x r core, Delta W = U_r Delta Sigma V_r^T has the same reachable set as LoRA-XS (U_r R Sigma_r V_r^T, where R Sigma_r ranges over all r x r matrices). Derived here, not stated in either paper."},{"target":"svft","type":"special-case-of","note":"The diagonal variant is SVFT with the fixed sparsity pattern Omega = {(i,i): i <= r} in W_0's singular basis. Derived here."},{"target":"trans-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Weight-space, training-free transport vs. function-space transport by distillation on synthetic data."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Projecting a plain LoRA onto the target subspaces transfers much worse (Table 2), which motivates the subspace constraint."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.16559","https://arxiv.org/html/2501.16559","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1add3bbdbc20c403a383482a665eb5a4-Abstract-Conference.html","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2501.16559"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Farzad Farhadzadeh","Debasmit Das","Shubhankar Borse","Fatih Porikli"],"bibkey":"farhadzadeh2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{farhadzadeh2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-X: Bridging Foundation Models with Training-Free Cross-Model Adaptation}},\n  author = {Farzad Farhadzadeh and Debasmit Das and Shubhankar Borse and Fatih Porikli},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2501.16559},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1add3bbdbc20c403a383482a665eb5a4-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Training uses \\(W_0+\\tilde U_r\\Delta\\Sigma\\tilde V_r^\\top\\) with frozen top-\\(r\\) SVD frames. Transfer is a separate, training-free map between slices.","shape":"Diagonal \\(\\Delta\\Sigma\\): \\(W_0+U_r\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^r)V_r^\\top\\), the closure of the torus orbit on the top-\\(r\\) singular values, \\(d=r\\). The constraint \\(\\sigma_i+\\delta\\sigma_i\\ge0\\) keeps \\(\\theta_0\\) an interior point. Full core: \\(d=r^2\\), the image of LoRA-XS.","base_point":"\\(\\Delta\\Sigma=0\\) is inferred; the paper does not state the init.","gauge":"Injective (diagonal or full core).","covariance":"Truncated SVD frames: \\(CO\\times CO\\) (Prop II.16).","rebasing":"With the top-\\(r\\) frames frozen: idempotent (a diagonal in \\(W_0\\)'s own frame keeps the frames). Recomputing the SVD after a merge keeps the same singular vectors, but if a shifted value \\(\\sigma_i+\\delta\\sigma_i\\) drops below \\(\\sigma_{r+1}\\) the top-\\(r\\) set changes and a new direction becomes trainable, so recomputation can gain (up to the full-frame torus \\(U\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^{\\min})V^\\top\\))."},"categorical_reading":"Diagonal LoRA-X is the torus acting on the top-\\(r\\) singular values of \\(W_0\\), i.e. SVDiff truncated to rank \\(r\\); its full-core variant is LoRA-XS. The transfer \\(\\Delta W\\mapsto U_tU_t^\\top\\Delta W\\,V_tV_t^\\top\\) is a two-sided orthogonal projection between slices at different base points \\(\\theta_s\\to\\theta_t\\), not an arrow. With a thin SVD it is the identity on full-rank square layers (catalog caveat).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"svdiff","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\delta\\sigma)=(\\delta\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\delta\\sigma_r,0,\\dots,0)","note":"Freeze SVDiff's shifts for \\(i>r\\) at 0. On the constraint set \\(\\sigma_i+\\delta\\sigma_i\\ge0\\) the ReLU is the identity."},{"target":"svft","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\delta\\sigma)=M,\\ \\ M_{ii}=\\delta\\sigma_i\\ (i\\le r),\\ \\ 0\\ \\text{otherwise}","note":"SVFT with the plain (diagonal) pattern pointed at \\(M=0\\), its tail \\(i>r\\) frozen. SVFT's official init is a small defect."},{"target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Delta\\Sigma)=\\Delta\\Sigma\\,\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"LoRA-XS in the form \\(W_0+U_rR\\,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\), pointed at \\(R=0\\) (needs \\(\\sigma_r>0\\)). An isomorphism for the full-core variant."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Delta\\Sigma)=(U_r,\\ \\Delta\\Sigma V_r^\\top)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at \\((U_r,0)\\), the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"At LoRA's default pointing \\((0,A_0)\\), \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\) has the random row space \\(\\mathrm{row}A_0\\), which does not contain LoRA-X's directions \\(u_iv_i^\\top\\). The arrow exists only into the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lofit","name":"LoFiT","full_name":"Localized Fine-Tuning on LLM Representations","paper_title":"LoFiT: Localized Fine-tuning on LLM Representations","authors":"Yin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.01563","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/122ea6470232ee5e79a2649243348005-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"https://github.com/fc2869/lo-fit","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{Step 1 (localize): } z^{(l,i)}\\leftarrow(1+A_l^{i})\\odot z^{(l,i)},\\ \\ \\min_{A}\\ \\mathcal{L}_{CE}+\\lambda\\textstyle\\sum_{l,i}\\|A_l^{i}\\|_1,\\ \\ T=\\operatorname{top\\text{-}K}_{(l,i)}\\|A_l^{i}\\|;\\quad \\text{Step 2 (tune): } z^{(l,i)}\\leftarrow z^{(l,i)}+v_l^{i}\\ \\ \\text{for }(l,i)\\in T,\\ \\ \\mathrm{MultiHead}(h)=\\operatorname{concat}\\big(z^{(l,1)},\\dots,z^{(l,H)}\\big)W^{O};\\ \\ \\text{all } W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ v_l^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{head}}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"K\\,d_{head}\\ \\ (\\text{final; }K=48,96,160\\ \\text{for Gemma-7B, Llama 2-7B, Llama 2-13B}\\Rightarrow 48\\cdot256=96\\cdot128\\approx12\\text{K},\\ 160\\cdot128\\approx20\\text{K});\\ \\text{the discarded selection step trains }L\\,H\\,d_{head}\\ \\text{scalings }(=L\\,d\\ \\text{when }H\\,d_{head}=d,\\ \\text{e.g. Llama 2})","locus":"outputs of a learned sparse set of attention heads (3-10% of all heads), before the output projection \\(W^O\\)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Input-independent translation of selected head-output coordinates, \\(z \\mapsto z + v\\), on a support \\(T\\) chosen by an L1-regularized multiplicative probe. Because \\(z\\) enters \\(W^O\\) linearly, the edit equals a bias \\(\\Delta b_O = \\sum_{i\\in T_l} W^O_{(i)} v_l^i\\) on the attention output projection (derived here). It is therefore sparse, localized bias tuning in representation space, spanning at most \\(K d_{head}\\) directions.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The offset is constant and passes through the linear \\(W^O\\), so it folds into an output-projection bias. Llama and Gemma o_proj layers have no bias, so one bias vector per edited layer must be added (derived from the paper's equations).","identity_at_init":"Approximate only: \\(v \\sim N(0, \\sigma_v)\\) and, in the selection step, \\(A \\sim N(0, \\sigma_A)\\), with \\(\\sigma_A = \\sigma_v = 0.001\\) in all experiments (App. D), so the model starts near the base model but not exactly at it.","key_idea":"LoFiT borrows localization from interpretability. It first learns per-head scaling factors with an L1 penalty to find which attention heads matter for the task, then fine-tunes only additive offset vectors on those heads. Task-specific localization beats random head sets and head sets chosen for other tasks.","results_note":"With 10% of heads (about 12K-20K parameters) on Gemma-7B and Llama 2-7B/13B, LoFiT beats ReFT on average and is comparable to LoRA (about 200x more parameters) and RED (about 20x more) on TruthfulQA, MQuAKE and CLUTRR. It is slightly below them on SIQA, ARC-c, BoolQ and SVAMP, and it outperforms ITI.","relations":[{"target":"loreft","type":"competes-with","note":"Both learn interventions on hidden representations. LoReFT edits a low-rank subspace of the residual stream at chosen layers without localization; LoFiT adds offsets at learned heads."},{"target":"red","type":"competes-with","note":"RED learns a scaling plus a bias on every layer's representations, roughly LoFiT's Step 1 and Step 2 combined but without localization; LoFiT uses about 20x fewer parameters."},{"target":"bitfit","type":"builds-on","note":"Step 2 is bias tuning, as in BitFit, restricted to the head outputs of a learned sparse set of heads."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Comparable accuracy with about 200x fewer parameters in the paper's low-data settings."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01563","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.01563","https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/122ea6470232ee5e79a2649243348005-Abstract-Conference.html","https://github.com/fc2869/lo-fit"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Fangcong Yin","Xi Ye","Greg Durrett"],"bibkey":"yin2024lofit","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yin2024lofit,\n  title = {{LoFiT: Localized Fine-tuning on LLM Representations}},\n  author = {Fangcong Yin and Xi Ye and Greg Durrett},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.01563},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/122ea6470232ee5e79a2649243348005-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Step 2 is an input-independent translation \\(z\\mapsto z+v\\) of selected head outputs, which enter \\(W^O\\) linearly. By Thm VI.3(a) it equals the bias edit \\(\\Delta b_O=\\sum_{i\\in T_l}W^O_{(i)}v_l^i\\), so it reads as a weight-space method on the affine closure of \\(o\\_proj\\) (the Table VIII.B convention for SSF and RED). Step 1, the \\(L_1\\)-penalised scaling probe, only chooses the support and is then discarded.","shape":"Linear: \\(\\{\\sum_{i\\in T}W^O_{(i)}v_i\\}\\), \\(d=Kd_{head}\\).","base_point":"The exactly pointed variant \\(v=0\\) is regular. The official init \\(v\\sim\\mathcal N(0,10^{-6})\\) leaves a negligible defect (the \\(\\approx\\) convention of Table VIII.B).","gauge":"Injective when the selected head blocks of \\(W^O\\) have independent columns (\\(|T_l|d_{head}\\le d\\), generic).","covariance":"As for BitFit, the offsets are free vectors on each selected head's wire. The support is a set of whole heads, a partition the attention already has, and the per-head \\(W_V\\)–\\(W_O\\) gauge carries the image to itself. So no structure is imposed.","merge":"M1→ into \\(o\\_proj\\)'s bias, by R1 through the linear \\(W^O\\). Llama and Gemma \\(o\\_proj\\) have no bias slot, so one bias vector per edited layer must be added.","rebasing":"Fixed heads: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"LoFiT is BitFit on the attention-output bias, restricted to a learned sparse set of heads. It is a Lin method whose image \\(\\sum_{i\\in T}W^O_{(i)}\\mathbb R^{d_{head}}\\) lies inside the bias space. Its localisation step is an (IA)\\(^3\\)-type scaling probe, used only to choose the support.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"bitfit","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\big(\\Delta b_O^{(l)}=\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in T_l}W^{O}_{(i)}v_l^i,\\ \\text{other biases }0\\big)","note":"Into BitFit on a model with \\(o\\_proj\\) biases, pointed at 0. The image is a proper subspace of the \\(o\\_proj\\) bias space."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loreft","test":"O1","reason":"LoReFT edits the residual stream only at selected positions (M∞); LoFiT shifts head outputs at every position. A position-uniform shift lies outside LoReFT's realisations, and conversely."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"moslora","name":"MoSLoRA","full_name":"Mixture-of-Subspaces LoRA","paper_title":"Mixture-of-Subspaces in Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024","arxiv":"2406.11909","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.450/","code_url":"https://github.com/wutaiqiang/MoSLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+s\\,B\\,M\\,A,\\quad s=\\alpha/r\\ \\text{(LoRA scaling, used in the official code; Eq. 7 omits it)},\\quad B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ M\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\quad \\Delta W=s\\textstyle\\sum_{i,j=1}^{r}m_{ij}\\,b_i\\,a_j^{\\top};\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ A,M,B\\ \\text{trained}\\quad\\big(M=I_r:\\ \\text{LoRA};\\ M=\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}I&I\\\\ I&I\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]:\\ \\text{two-subspace mixing}\\big)","trainable_params":"r\\,(d_{in}+d_{out})+r^{2}","locus":"any linear layer; \\(q\\), \\(k\\), \\(v\\), up and down projections in the LLaMA-3 8B commonsense experiments (Table 7); \\(q\\), \\(k\\), \\(v\\), \\(o\\), up, down and gate for LLaVA visual instruction tuning (Table 8); also used for SDXL subject-driven generation","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Three-factor low-rank update \\(B M A\\), in which a learnable \\(r \\times r\\) mixer weights all \\(r^2\\) rank-one 'subspaces' \\(b_i a_j^T\\). The reachable set is still \\(\\{\\mathrm{rank} \\le r\\}\\), the same as LoRA's, so the mixer changes the parametrization and the optimization, not the expressivity. The gauge symmetry grows from \\(GL(r)\\) to \\(GL(r) \\times GL(r)\\): \\((B, M, A) \\sim (BG, G^{-1}MH, H^{-1}A)\\) (derived here).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(BMA\\) is a single \\(d_{out} \\times d_{in}\\) matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (\\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform; mixer Kaiming-uniform or orthogonal), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\). A zero-initialized mixer gives all three factors zero gradient and training stalls (paper, Appendix C).","key_idea":"Splitting LoRA into rank-one subspaces and mixing them improves results. MoSLoRA generalizes the fixed identity between \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) (LoRA), or the fixed butterfly (two-subspace mixing), to a learnable \\(r \\times r\\) mixer, at negligible extra parameters and compute.","results_note":"On LLaMA-3 8B commonsense reasoning (8 tasks) it averages 85.6, vs 82.8 for LoRA, 85.0 for DoRA and 84.2 for FLoRA, at about the same parameter count and LoRA-level training time. It also improves LLaVA visual instruction tuning and DreamBooth-style subject-driven generation.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"M = I_r recovers LoRA."},{"target":"flora-tucker","type":"special-case-of","note":"On 2-D weights FLoRA uses the same three-factor form A G B^T, with a possibly rectangular core. The two methods differ in initialization: FLoRA zero-inits the core, MoSLoRA zero-inits B and Kaiming/orthogonal-inits M (84.2 vs 85.6 in MoSLoRA's Table 4)."},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA-XS is B M A with B and A frozen at the truncated SVD of W_0 and only the mixer trained."},{"target":"lora-sb","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA-SB is B M A with B and A frozen at the SVD of an estimated first full fine-tuning step and only the mixer trained."},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"AdaLoRA uses a diagonal, pruned middle factor (P Lambda Q); MoSLoRA's core is dense and unpruned."},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Higher commonsense average at lower training time and memory (Table 4)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11909","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.11909","https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.450/","https://github.com/wutaiqiang/MoSLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Taiqiang Wu","Jiahao Wang","Zhe Zhao","Ngai Wong"],"bibkey":"wu2024mixtureb","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2024mixtureb,\n  title = {{Mixture-of-Subspaces in Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Taiqiang Wu and Jiahao Wang and Zhe Zhao and Ngai Wong},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2406.11909},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.450/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\{BMA\\}=\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): exactly LoRA's cone. The mixer changes the parametrisation, not the image.","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\) with \\(M_0,A_0\\) random, so \\(S_1=\\{XM_0A_0\\}\\) has dimension \\(mr\\) when \\(M_0\\) is invertible. The deficit is \\(r(n-r)\\), as for LoRA.","gauge":"\\((B,M,A)\\sim(BG,G^{-1}MH,H^{-1}A)\\): \\(GL_r\\times GL_r\\). The generic fibre dimension is \\(|Q|-d=r(m+n)+r^2-r(m+n-r)=2r^2\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MoSLoRA is LoRA with a gauge-enlarging middle factor. \\(\\rho(B,M,A)=W_0+sBMA\\) factors through LoRA by \\(h=(BM,A)\\), and LoRA factors back through it by freezing \\(M\\). The two simulate each other with the same image and the same \\(S_1\\), but they are not isomorphic (\\(|Q|\\) differs by \\(r^2\\)). Any gain is therefore in the cometric \\(K=D\\rho\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\rho^\\top\\), not in expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,M,A)=(BM,\\ A)","note":"Pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\), with equal images and equal \\(S_1=\\{YA_0\\}\\). The reverse arrow \\(h(B,A)=(BM_0^{-1},M_0,A)\\) also exists."},{"target":"flora-tucker","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same three-factor form on 2-D weights, but FLoRA is pointed at \\(h(q_0)=(0,M_0,A_0)\\) rather than at its zero-core init."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"flora-tucker","test":"O3","reason":"At FLoRA's default zero-core pointing, \\(S_1=\\{U_0XV_0^\\top\\}\\) has dimension \\(r^2\\), against MoSLoRA's \\(mr\\). \\(S_1\\) is not contained, so there is no pointed arrow at the default pointings."},{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d=r(m+n-r)>r^2\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"noah","name":"NOAH","full_name":"Neural prOmpt seArcH","paper_title":"Neural Prompt Search","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2022,"venue":"IEEE TPAMI 2025","arxiv":"2206.04673","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2024.3435939","code_url":"https://github.com/ZhangYuanhan-AI/NOAH","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{block } l:\\ \\ x\\leftarrow[\\mathrm{CLS},P_l,E]\\ (P_l\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell_l\\times d},\\ l<D_V);\\ \\ Q=W_qx+s\\,B^{l}_{q}A^{l}_{q}x,\\ K=W_kx+s\\,B^{l}_{k}A^{l}_{k}x\\ \\ (\\text{rank } r^{L}_l,\\ l<D_L);\\ \\ h\\leftarrow h+W^{l}_{up}\\,\\phi\\big(W^{l}_{down}h\\big)\\ \\ (\\text{width } r^{A}_l,\\ l<D_A);\\quad a=\\big(D_V,D_L,D_A,\\{\\ell_l,r^{L}_l,r^{A}_l\\}\\big),\\ \\ \\ell_l,r_l\\in\\{5,10,50,100\\}\\ \\text{or}\\ \\{1,5,10\\},\\ D\\in\\{3,6,9,12\\};\\ \\text{one-shot supernet (AutoFormer weight entanglement), then evolutionary search under a parameter cap};\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"\\textstyle\\sum_{l<D_V}\\ell_l\\,d+\\sum_{l<D_L}4d\\,r^{L}_l+\\sum_{l<D_A}2d\\,r^{A}_l\\ (+\\text{biases, head});\\ \\text{capped at }0.64\\text{M on ViT-B/16 (found subnets average }0.43\\text{M)}","locus":"every ViT-B/16 block up to each module's searched depth: VPT-Deep tokens at the block input, LoRA on \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_k\\) as written in Eq. 3 (the official code instead attaches one rank-\\(r\\) LoRA to the fused qkv projection, \\(d \\to 3d\\), with no scaling; it has the same \\(4dr\\) parameter count), and a bottleneck adapter applied residually to the MLP output","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Per-dataset architecture search over a product design space. Each point combines three PEFT modules, each truncated in depth and in width. With weight entanglement, every candidate is a coordinate restriction (a prefix slice) of one supernet's maximal-width modules. The found subnet is a sum of an additive part (LoRA), a serial insertion (adapter) and an input augmentation (VPT).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Only the LoRA part folds into \\(W_q\\) and \\(W_k\\); the adapter and prompt parts remain at inference unless the search gives them zero depth.","identity_at_init":"No identity at init. LoRA starts at zero (LoRA_b = 0, LoRA_a Kaiming-uniform in the official code), but the adapter's down- and up-projections are both Xavier-uniform initialized (model/module/adapter_super.py), so its output is not near zero, and VPT tokens (also Xavier-uniform) change attention from the first step. The paper does not discuss this.","key_idea":"NOAH treats Adapter, LoRA and VPT as interchangeable 'prompt modules' and lets neural architecture search decide, per downstream dataset, which modules to use, at what depth and with what width, instead of hand-designing a PEFT recipe.","results_note":"On VTAB-1k with ViT-B/16 (ImageNet-22K) it averages 75.5% with 0.43M parameters, vs 74.5 for LoRA, 73.9 for Adapter, 72.0 for VPT and 68.9 for full fine-tuning. It is also stronger in few-shot learning and ImageNet domain generalization.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"LoRA (on W_q, W_k) is one of the three searched modules."},{"target":"vpt","type":"combines","note":"VPT-Deep tokens are one of the three searched modules; depth and token count are searched."},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"combines","note":"A bottleneck adapter after the MLP is one of the three searched modules."},{"target":"glora","type":"competes-with","note":"GLoRA also trains a one-shot supernet and runs an evolutionary search per layer, but over a merge-friendly unified affine space."},{"target":"autopeft","type":"competes-with","note":"Both search a combined PEFT design space. NOAH uses a weight-shared supernet with evolutionary search on vision; AutoPEFT uses multi-objective Bayesian optimization on NLP."},{"target":"unipelt","type":"competes-with","note":"UniPELT combines modules with learned input-dependent gates instead of searching over them."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.04673","https://arxiv.org/html/2206.04673","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2206.04673","https://github.com/ZhangYuanhan-AI/NOAH","https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2024.3435939","https://github.com/ZhangYuanhan-AI/NOAH/blob/main/model/module/adapter_super.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Yuanhan Zhang","Kaiyang Zhou","Ziwei Liu"],"bibkey":"zhang2022neural","bibtex":"@article{zhang2022neural,\n  title = {{Neural Prompt Search}},\n  author = {Yuanhan Zhang and Kaiyang Zhou and Ziwei Liu},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {IEEE TPAMI},\n  eprint = {2206.04673},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2024.3435939}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each searched subnet is LoRA (on \\(W_q,W_k\\)) \\(\\oplus\\) a serial bottleneck adapter \\(\\oplus\\) VPT-Deep tokens, each truncated in depth and width. The VPT summand has no neutral value, so the sum is unpointed (as for UniPELT and MAM).","shape":"Function-level: the adapter and prompt parts live on extensions, so the object is not a \\(\\oplus\\)-word of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\).","base_point":"VPT tokens always draw attention. The official adapter is Xavier-initialised on both projections, so even the adapter starts off its neutral point \\(U=0\\).","gauge":"LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) on its summand; the adapter and the prompts add only discrete symmetries.","covariance":"All parts are dense: basis-free.","merge":"Only the LoRA part fuses (M1). Adapter and prompts remain unless the search sets their depth to 0.","rebasing":"Nothing merges as a whole."},"categorical_reading":"NOAH searches the truncations of one supernet \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}\\oplus\\mathrm{Adapter}\\oplus\\mathrm{VPT}\\). For LoRA and adapter widths, a prefix slice is an A2 sub-object (freeze the trailing coordinates at 0). For VPT it is not: dropping a token is not freezing it at a base value, because a zero token still draws attention mass. The candidates therefore form a lattice of sub-objects only along the LoRA and adapter axes, and the chosen object is an unpointed extension.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"glora","test":"O1","reason":"Every GLoRA path is a weight-space reparametrisation (M1), so its realisations lie in \\(\\Phi_f(\\Theta)\\). NOAH's VPT tokens and non-linear adapter leave that set (Thm VI.3(c), VI.5(f))."},{"target":"autopeft","test":"O1","reason":"NOAH's LoRA on \\(W_q,W_k\\) changes every token's queries and keys. AutoPEFT has no module there: its adapters sit at the FFN and its prefixes only add key/value slots, so neither reproduces a generic low-rank change of the attention logits. (The VPT-Deep part alone would not obstruct, since a deep prompt acts at its layer as a constrained K/V prefix; the LoRA summand does.)"}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"autopeft","name":"AutoPEFT","full_name":"AutoPEFT (automatic, multi-objective configuration search for PEFT)","paper_title":"AutoPEFT: Automatic Configuration Search for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zhou et al.","year":2023,"venue":"TACL 2024","arxiv":"2301.12132","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.tacl-1.29/","code_url":"https://github.com/cambridgeltl/autopeft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{layer } l\\ (m_l=1):\\ \\ h\\leftarrow F(x)+W^{up}_{SA}\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(W^{down}_{SA}F(x)\\big)+W^{up}_{PA}\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(W^{down}_{PA}x\\big),\\ \\ K'=[P_k;K],\\ V'=[P_v;V],\\ P_k,P_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L_{PT}\\times d};\\quad a=\\big(m\\in\\{0,1\\}^{L},\\,D_{SA},\\,D_{PA},\\,L_{PT}\\big)\\in\\mathcal{A};\\quad \\max_{a\\in\\mathcal{A}}\\big(f(a,W^\\ast(a)),\\,-g(a)\\big)\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ W^\\ast(a)=\\arg\\min_W\\mathcal{L}_{train}(a,W)\\ \\ \\text{(SAAS-GP surrogate, NEHVI acquisition, low-fidelity inner loop)};\\ \\text{PLM frozen}","trainable_params":"\\textstyle\\sum_{l}m_l\\,\\big(2d\\,D_{SA}+2d\\,D_{PA}+2L_{PT}\\,d\\big)\\ (+\\text{biases})","locus":"in each selected Transformer layer: a serial adapter after the FFN, a parallel adapter around the FFN, and prefixes on the attention keys/values","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Bi-level, bi-objective search (performance vs parameter count) over a discrete product space: a layer mask times three module sizes on a log scale, where 0 means the module is absent. The answer is a Pareto front of configurations, not a single PEFT. Each configuration is a sum of serial and parallel bottleneck insertions plus key/value prefixes.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"ReLU bottleneck adapters and prefix slots cannot be folded into the frozen weights.","identity_at_init":"Not addressed. Modules use AdapterHub defaults (near-zero adapter outputs), while prefixes change attention from the first step.","key_idea":"Instead of hand-picking a PEFT recipe, AutoPEFT searches jointly over which layers get modules and how large the serial adapter, parallel adapter and prefix are. It uses multi-objective Bayesian optimization with cheap low-fidelity evaluations, and the discovered configurations transfer across tasks and models.","results_note":"On GLUE with BERT-base, a configuration searched on RTE (0.76% parameters) averages 83.17 vs 83.15 for full fine-tuning, beating serial and parallel adapters, prefix-tuning, LoRA, UniPELT, MAM and AdaMix; per-task search reaches 83.49. Gains are similar on SuperGLUE, RoBERTa-large and T5, where it is compared with S3PET.","relations":[{"target":"pfeiffer-adapter","type":"combines","note":"The serial adapter (SA) after the FFN is one of the three building blocks."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"combines","note":"The parallel adapter (PA) around the FFN is a building block; the paper treats LoRA as a scaled variant of PA and leaves it out."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"combines","note":"The prefix module (PT) on attention keys and values is a building block."},{"target":"mam-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"MAM is a Table 1 baseline (80.25 GLUE avg at 6.97% params vs 83.17 at 0.76%). Its module types (parallel FFN adapter plus key/value prefix) are a subset of AutoPEFT's, but MAM itself is not a point of the search space: its sizes (prefix length 30, adapter width 512) and fixed scaling s = 4 are not in AutoPEFT's per-module grid {0,1,3,6,12,24,48,96,192,384,768} (11 values, inferred from 5,451,776 = 2^12 x 11^3 on BERT-base and the reported 12/96/1 values)."},{"target":"unipelt","type":"competes-with","note":"UniPELT also combines adapter, prefix and LoRA, but through learned gates rather than a search."},{"target":"noah","type":"competes-with","note":"Both search a combined PEFT design space; NOAH uses a weight-shared supernet with evolutionary search on ViTs."},{"target":"adamix","type":"competes-with","note":"A baseline mixture of homogeneous adapters; AutoPEFT beats it at similar parameter cost."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.12132","https://arxiv.org/html/2301.12132","https://aclanthology.org/2024.tacl-1.29/","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2301.12132","https://github.com/cambridgeltl/autopeft"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Han Zhou","Xingchen Wan","Ivan Vulić","Anna Korhonen"],"bibkey":"zhou2023autopeft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhou2023autopeft,\n  title = {{AutoPEFT: Automatic Configuration Search for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Han Zhou and Xingchen Wan and Ivan Vulić and Anna Korhonen},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {TACL},\n  eprint = {2301.12132},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.tacl-1.29/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each configuration is serial adapter \\(\\oplus\\) parallel adapter \\(\\oplus\\) K/V prefix on a layer mask. Any nonzero prefix length leaves no neutral point.","shape":"Function-level (non-linear adapters and prefixes).","base_point":"AdapterHub adapters start near \\(U=0\\), but prefixes always draw attention mass. Only configurations with \\(L_{PT}=0\\) are pointed (neutral).","gauge":"ReLU bottlenecks: the positive per-unit torus (R3) times permutations. The prefix adds nothing continuous.","covariance":"Dense modules: basis-free.","merge":"ReLU adapters and prefix slots are generically non-affine in the box input (Thm VI.3(c), VI.5(f)).","rebasing":"Nothing merges."},"categorical_reading":"AutoPEFT optimises over a finite grid of objects \\(\\mathrm{SA}_{D_{SA}}\\oplus\\mathrm{PA}_{D_{PA}}\\oplus\\mathrm{Prefix}_{L_{PT}}\\) on a layer mask and returns a Pareto front in (performance, budget \\(|M|\\)). Size 0 removes a module, so the grid contains Pfeiffer-adapter, parallel-adapter and prefix-tuning objects as faces. The base-point type of a configuration jumps from neutral to unpointed as soon as \\(L_{PT}>0\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"mam-adapter","test":"O1","reason":"AutoPEFT's serial post-FFN adapter reads \\(F(x)\\), and a generic ReLU function of \\(F(x)\\) is not reproduced by MAM's parallel FFN adapter (which reads \\(x\\)) plus a key/value prefix (as for UniPELT vs MAM). Separately, the converse also fails: MAM's sizes (prefix length 30, fixed scaling) are not points of AutoPEFT's grid."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"ReLU adapters and prefixes are non-affine inserted boxes (Thm VI.3(c)); no weight of the original architecture realises them."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"laplace-lora","name":"Laplace-LoRA","full_name":"Laplace-LoRA (Bayesian low-rank adaptation via a post-hoc Laplace approximation over LoRA parameters)","paper_title":"Bayesian Low-rank Adaptation for Large Language Models","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2308.13111","url":"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/07c256a163a7559186ec1c71e95b9ec9-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"https://github.com/adamxyang/laplace-lora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+BA,\\ \\ \\theta=\\{A,B\\}\\ \\text{fit by standard LoRA}\\to\\theta_{MAP};\\quad p(\\theta\\mid\\mathcal{D})\\approx\\mathcal{N}(\\theta_{MAP},\\Sigma),\\ \\ \\Sigma^{-1}=\\textstyle\\sum_{n}\\mathbb{E}\\big[(a\\,a^{\\top})\\otimes(g\\,g^{\\top})\\big]+\\lambda I\\ \\ \\text{(K-FAC per factor, large Kronecker factor stored at rank } n_{kfac});\\quad f(x_\\ast)\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\big(f_{\\theta_{MAP}}(x_\\ast),\\,J_\\ast\\Sigma J_\\ast^{\\top}\\big),\\ \\ J_\\ast=\\nabla_\\theta f_\\theta(x_\\ast)\\big|_{\\theta_{MAP}};\\ \\ \\lambda\\ \\text{maximizes the Laplace marginal likelihood};\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r\\,(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(MAP, as LoRA)}\\ +\\ \\text{post-hoc K-FAC statistics (not trained)}\\approx n_{kfac}(d_{in}+d_{out})+2r^{2}\\ \\text{per matrix}\\ +\\ \\text{one prior precision }\\lambda","locus":"LoRA factors on \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\) and the output (LM-head) layer in the paper; the LLLA variant uses only the output-layer LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A Gaussian (Laplace) posterior over the LoRA factors, treated as two separate linear layers with Kronecker-factored precision (the \\(d\\times d\\) factor kept low-rank). Prediction integrates the linearized network over this posterior: a Bayesian model average over adapter parameters with the base weights fixed.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The posterior mean \\(BA\\) merges into \\(W_0\\), but the calibrated predictive needs the Jacobian with respect to the LoRA factors and the stored covariance at test time.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)) for the MAP fit. The Laplace step is post-hoc and leaves the mean unchanged.","key_idea":"Fine-tuned LLMs are overconfident on small datasets. After ordinary LoRA training, Laplace-LoRA fits a Laplace approximation over the LoRA parameters only, with low-rank K-FAC so memory stays LoRA-sized, and predicts with the linearized model. This calibrates the model without a validation set.","results_note":"On six commonsense reasoning tasks with LLaMA2-7B, full Laplace-LoRA keeps accuracy while greatly reducing ECE and NLL relative to MAP, MC dropout, checkpoint ensembles and LoRA ensembles. This holds under distribution shift too, at about 1-5% extra memory.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses an unchanged LoRA fit as the MAP point; the Bayesian layer is post-hoc."},{"target":"monteclora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both put a distribution on LoRA parameters. MonteCLoRA learns a Gaussian-mixture posterior during training; Laplace-LoRA fits a Gaussian after training."},{"target":"blob","type":"competes-with","note":"BLoB (NeurIPS 2024) learns a variational posterior over LoRA during training, as an alternative to post-hoc Laplace."},{"target":"lora-dropout","type":"competes-with","note":"Both put a distribution over LoRA adapters and average predictions at test time: LoRA Dropout (Lin et al., 2024, later work) samples sparsified adapters, Laplace-LoRA uses a Gaussian posterior. Laplace-LoRA's own baseline is standard MC dropout on a LoRA fine-tune (rate 0.1, 10 samples), which it beats on ECE/NLL; that baseline is not the LoRA Dropout method itself."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.13111","https://arxiv.org/html/2308.13111","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/07c256a163a7559186ec1c71e95b9ec9-Abstract-Conference.html","https://github.com/adamxyang/laplace-lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"authors_full":["Adam X. Yang","Maxime Robeyns","Xi Wang","Laurence Aitchison"],"bibkey":"yang2023bayesian","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2023bayesian,\n  title = {{Bayesian Low-rank Adaptation for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Adam X. Yang and Maxime Robeyns and Xi Wang and Laurence Aitchison},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2308.13111},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/07c256a163a7559186ec1c71e95b9ec9-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"It is built post hoc from a trained LoRA (P-like), but the deployed object is a family over a probability space \\(T=(\\mathbb R^{|Q|},\\mathcal N(0,I))\\). The \\(T\\)-point is \\(a(\\varepsilon)=q_{MAP}+\\Sigma^{1/2}\\varepsilon\\), and the predictive \\(\\int\\Phi_f(\\rho(a(\\varepsilon)))\\,d\\mu\\) is taken in linearised form. axes.json lists tasks, inputs, simplices and tenants as bases; this one is a noise base.","shape":"Every sample lies in LoRA's cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\); the mean is the MAP point.","base_point":"The underlying LoRA is trained from the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. The Laplace family is centred at \\(q_{MAP}\\), not at \\(q_0\\).","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) has LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) fibres, but the Laplace map is not constant on them. Writing \\(G\\) for the GGN in factor coordinates, a gauge change \\(\\phi\\) turns the predictive \\(J\\,D\\rho\\,(G+\\lambda I)^{-1}D\\rho^\\top J^\\top\\) into the same expression with \\(\\lambda I\\) replaced by \\(\\lambda\\,d\\phi^\\top d\\phi\\). So only the \\(O(r)\\) half of \\(GL_r\\) preserves it, and per-factor K-FAC does not restore the rest. Rescaling to \\((cB,A/c)\\) changes the uncertainty while fixing the mean.","covariance":"The image is LoRA's (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The isotropic prior on the factors makes the posterior geometry only \\(O\\times O\\).","merge":"The mean merges (M1). The calibrated predictive needs \\(J_\\ast\\) and \\(\\Sigma\\) at test time and averages outputs, so it is M∞.","rebasing":"A one-shot post-hoc step."},"categorical_reading":"Laplace-LoRA turns a trained point of LoRA into a Gaussian family over \\(Q\\), pushed forward by the linearised realisation. The Gaussian lives on \\(Q\\) rather than on \\(\\Theta\\), and its isotropic prior is not gauge-invariant. As a result the predictive keeps only the isometric \\(\\mathfrak{so}(r)\\) half of LoRA's gauge, and the \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_r\\) half changes the calibrated uncertainty while fixing the mean.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\ \\text{(the MAP object)}","note":"The family's mean (\\(\\varepsilon=0\\)) is the LoRA point it was built from; the Laplace step adds trained-method data."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"blob","test":"O1","reason":"BLoB's samples \\(W_0+BA\\) keep the trained \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\), since \\(B\\) is deterministic. Laplace samples perturb \\(B\\) as well and change the column space, so the Laplace posterior's support is not contained in BLoB's."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"blob","name":"BLoB","full_name":"Bayesian Low-Rank Adaptation by Backpropagation","paper_title":"BLoB: Bayesian Low-Rank Adaptation by Backpropagation for Large Language Models","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.11675","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11675","code_url":"https://github.com/Wang-ML-Lab/bayesian-peft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h = W_0x + B A x,\\quad A\\sim q(A\\mid M,\\Omega)=\\prod_{ij}\\mathcal{N}(A_{ij}\\mid M_{ij},\\Omega_{ij}^2),\\ \\Omega_{ij}=G_{ij}^2,\\ B\\ \\text{deterministic};\\quad \\min_{M,G,B}\\ -\\mathbb{E}_{q(A)}\\big[\\log P(\\mathcal{D}\\mid A,B)\\big]+\\mathrm{KL}\\big[q(A)\\,\\Vert\\,\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_p^2 I)\\big];\\quad \\text{equivalently } q(\\mathrm{vec}\\,W)=\\mathcal{N}\\big(\\mathrm{vec}(W_0+BM),\\,(I_{d_{in}}\\otimes B)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathrm{vec}\\,\\Omega)^2\\,(I_{d_{in}}\\otimes B^{\\top})\\big);\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}; M,G,B\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out} + 2\\,r\\,d_{in}","locus":"LoRA-targeted linear layers (paper: \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\) of every attention layer plus the output layer of Llama2-7B; RoBERTa for classification)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Stochastic low-rank update \\(\\Delta W = BA\\) in which only the down-projection \\(A\\) is a random matrix with a mean-field Gaussian posterior (mean \\(M\\), std \\(\\Omega = G^{\\circ 2}\\)); \\(B\\) is a point estimate. The induced full-weight posterior is a degenerate Gaussian centred at \\(W_0 + BM\\) whose covariance \\((I\\otimes B)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Omega)^2(I\\otimes B^\\top)\\) has rank at most \\(r\\,d_{in}\\). The prior is a matching low-rank Gaussian, so the full-weight KL reduces exactly to the KL on \\(A\\). Sampling uses Flipout restricted to \\(A\\).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The posterior mean \\(W_0 + BM\\) can be merged into \\(W_0\\) (BLoB with \\(N=0\\) samples), but the Bayesian predictive that gives the calibration gains averages \\(N\\) (default 10) sampled \\(A\\) matrices, and this cannot be folded into one weight.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\), as in LoRA. The mean \\(M\\) of \\(A\\) is uniform-Kaiming and \\(G \\sim \\mathcal{U}(\\epsilon/\\sqrt2,\\epsilon)\\). The sampled update \\(B A\\) is therefore exactly zero at initialization. The authors fix the variance of \\(B\\) to 0 for this reason: a stochastic \\(B\\) would make the early forward passes noisy.","key_idea":"BLoB does variational Bayes inside the LoRA subspace and trains the posterior mean and variance jointly during fine-tuning, instead of adding a Laplace approximation afterwards. Only \\(A\\) is made stochastic (asymmetric Bayesianization), and the prior is designed so that the full-weight ELBO equals a cheap ELBO on \\(A\\). The posterior std is parameterized as \\(G\\) squared for fast KL convergence, and LoRA-Flipout decorrelates the samples within a mini-batch.","results_note":"On Llama2-7B over six commonsense tasks, BLoB (\\(N=10\\) samples) keeps accuracy close to MLE-LoRA while cutting ECE sharply (e.g. WG-S 29.8% to 9.4%, ARC-C 29.0% to 9.6%); it also improves NLL over MC-dropout, ensembles, BBB and Laplace-LoRA on most datasets, and the gains extend to OOD shifts.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same W_0 + BA parameterization; the A factor is replaced by a Gaussian variational distribution"},{"target":"laplace-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Laplace-LoRA (Yang et al., ICLR 2024) fits a post-hoc Kronecker-factored Laplace posterior over LoRA weights; BLoB learns the mean and covariance jointly during training"},{"target":"monteclora","type":"competes-with","note":"both put distributions on low-rank adapter parameters; MonteCLoRA targets robustness to hyperparameters, BLoB targets calibrated uncertainty"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11675","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.11675","https://openreview.net/forum?id=MaDykgj4Ru","https://github.com/Wang-ML-Lab/bayesian-peft"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Yibin Wang","Haizhou Shi","Ligong Han","Dimitris Metaxas","Hao Wang"],"bibkey":"wang2024blob","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024blob,\n  title = {{BLoB: Bayesian Low-Rank Adaptation by Backpropagation for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yibin Wang and Haizhou Shi and Ligong Han and Dimitris Metaxas and Hao Wang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.11675},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11675}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A family over the noise space \\(T=(\\mathbb R^{r\\times n},\\mathcal N(0,I))\\), with \\(a(\\varepsilon)=(B,\\,M+\\Omega\\odot\\varepsilon)\\). It is trained jointly through the ELBO, whereas Laplace-LoRA is built post hoc.","shape":"Each sample lies in LoRA's cone. For the trained \\(B\\), all samples lie in the linear fibre \\(\\mathrm{FB}(B)=\\{W_0+BA\\}\\). Since \\(\\rho\\) is of degree one in \\(A\\), the Gaussian on \\(A\\) pushes forward to a Gaussian. That is why the full-weight KL reduces exactly to the KL on \\(A\\).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\), so every sample equals \\(W_0\\) at initialisation: the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex for each \\(\\varepsilon\\).","gauge":"The mean map \\((B,M)\\mapsto W_0+BM\\) has \\(GL_r\\) fibres. The mean-field family \\(\\mathcal N(M,\\Omega^2)\\), however, is preserved only by monomial \\(g\\) (permutations and nonzero diagonals, with \\(\\Omega\\mapsto|D|\\Omega\\)): a torus, times \\(S_r\\).","covariance":"The image is LoRA's (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The mean-field posterior is diagonal in the input coordinates of \\(A\\), so the distribution itself is covariant only under \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\).","merge":"The mean merges (BLoB with \\(N=0\\) samples). The Bayesian predictive averages \\(N\\) sampled \\(A\\)'s, so it is M∞.","rebasing":"Nothing merges as a whole."},"categorical_reading":"BLoB is a family over a Gaussian noise space whose samples all lie in one linear sub-object \\(\\mathrm{FB}(B)\\) of LoRA. Making only \\(A\\) stochastic keeps \\(\\rho\\) affine in the random variable, so the induced weight posterior is an honest, degenerate Gaussian. Because it is trained jointly, its symmetry is the monomial part of \\(GL_r\\). Laplace-LoRA is its post-hoc counterpart, as LoraHub is to Poly.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(M,G,B)=(B,\\ M)","note":"Projection to the mean, forgetting the variance coordinates \\(G\\) (as for MonteCLoRA). Pointed at \\((0,M_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loraprune","name":"LoRAPrune","full_name":"LoRAPrune: LoRA-guided structured pruning","paper_title":"LoRAPrune: Structured Pruning Meets Low-Rank Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2024","arxiv":"2305.18403","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18403","code_url":"https://github.com/aim-uofa/LoRAPrune","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h = \\big((W_0 + BA)\\,x\\big)\\odot m,\\quad m\\in\\{0,1\\}^{G}\\ \\text{(one bit per channel/head group, broadcast)};\\quad \\hat I_{ij}=\\Big[\\big(\\nabla_{B_{i:}}\\mathcal{L}\\,A_{:j}+B_{i:}\\nabla_{A_{:j}}\\mathcal{L}-\\nabla_{B_{i:}}\\mathcal{L}\\,\\nabla_{A_{:j}}\\mathcal{L}\\big)\\big(W_{0,ij}+(BA)_{ij}\\big)\\Big]^2,\\ \\ \\hat{\\mathcal{G}}_g=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in g}\\hat I_{ij},\\ \\ \\bar{\\mathcal{G}}_t=\\lambda\\bar{\\mathcal{G}}_{t-1}+(1-\\lambda)\\hat{\\mathcal{G}}_t,\\ \\ m_g=\\mathbb{1}[\\bar{\\mathcal{G}}_g>p];\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen (optionally 8-bit)}, A,B\\ \\text{trained}, m\\ \\text{set progressively}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all LoRA-adapted linear layers of LLaMA-family LLMs; pruning acts on dependency groups (attention heads, MLP channels) across coupled layers","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Additive rank-\\(r\\) update \\(\\Delta W = BA\\) composed with a structured 0/1 mask on output channels or heads (a coordinate projection onto a sub-network). Mask importance is a first-order Taylor score in which the full-weight gradient \\(\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial W\\) is replaced by the LoRA proxy \\(\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial(BA) \\approx \\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,A + B\\,\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L} - \\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L}\\), which is derived from one SGD step on \\(B\\) and \\(A\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Because the pruning is structured, \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\) and the masked heads and channels are removed, which leaves a smaller dense model with no extra modules. The mask is the point of the method: the result is cheaper than the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"LoRA init (\\(B = 0\\) in the official code; Alg. 1 only says 'randomly initialized \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)') and an all-ones mask make the function exactly the pretrained one at step 0. Pruning then removes groups progressively and on purpose, while LoRA recovers the lost accuracy at the same time.","key_idea":"LoRAPrune estimates the importance of the frozen weights using only LoRA's gradients. It treats pruning \\(W_{ij}\\) as setting \\((BA)_{ij} = -W_{ij}\\) and approximates the needed full gradient from the gradients of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Pruning and LoRA fine-tuning then run in one memory-light loop with no backward pass through \\(W_0\\).","results_note":"At 50% structured compression of LLaMA-7B, LoRAPrune lowers perplexity versus LLM-Pruner by 4.81 on WikiText2 and 3.46 on PTB; the LoRA-guided criterion cuts pruning memory by 52.6% versus the vanilla full-gradient criterion (64.2% with an 8-bit base), and the method scales to LLaMA-65B on one GPU.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"uses the LoRA factors both as the recovery adapter and as the gradient proxy for pruning importance"},{"target":"llm-pruner","type":"competes-with","note":"LLM-Pruner (Ma et al., NeurIPS 2023) uses the same dependency groups but needs gradients of W_0; LoRAPrune replaces them with LoRA gradients"},{"target":"qlora","type":"combines","note":"both run LoRA on a quantized frozen base: because no gradient of W_0 is needed, LoRAPrune-8bit stores W_0 in 8 bits (used for LLaMA-30B/65B); this is 8-bit LLM.int8-style quantization, not QLoRA's 4-bit NF4, which the paper only cites as a compatible LoRA variant"},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"dual-to","note":"diff-pruning sparsifies the update \\Delta W; LoRAPrune keeps a dense low-rank update and sparsifies the backbone W_0 instead"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18403","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.18403","https://openreview.net/forum?id=ox7ChEaLdP","https://github.com/aim-uofa/LoRAPrune"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Mingyang Zhang","Hao Chen","Chunhua Shen","Zhen Yang","Linlin Ou","Xinyi Yu","Bohan Zhuang"],"bibkey":"zhang2023loraprune","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023loraprune,\n  title = {{LoRAPrune: Structured Pruning Meets Low-Rank Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Mingyang Zhang and Hao Chen and Chunhua Shen and Zhen Yang and Linlin Ou and Xinyi Yu and Bohan Zhuang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024},\n  eprint = {2305.18403},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18403}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Sat","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(\\rho(B,A,m)=\\mathrm{diag}(m)(W_0+BA)\\), with a discrete mask \\(m\\in\\{0,1\\}^G\\) broadcast over dependency groups and set by a score rather than by gradient.","shape":"The 0/1 diagonal monoid acting on LoRA's cone: \\(\\mathrm{Im}=\\{0,1\\}^G\\cdot(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\). A Sat object like DoRA, with a discrete idempotent monoid in place of the torus. Because the monoid is discrete, the germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is only the \\(m=\\mathbf 1\\) component, LoRA's cone; the masked components are isolated pieces away from \\(\\theta_0\\) (for generic \\(W_0\\) with no near-zero rows).","base_point":"\\(m=\\mathbf 1\\) and \\(B=0\\): LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. Pruning then leaves the base point on purpose.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\((B,A)\\). Rows killed by \\(m\\) add a non-generic fibre.","covariance":"The mask acts on output channels and heads (dependency groups), so only group-preserving monomial changes of basis are allowed on the masked side. \\(BA\\) itself is \\(GL\\)-covariant.","merge":"\\(BA\\) fuses and the masked groups are deleted, leaving a smaller dense model.","rebasing":"The LoRA part spans. Masks compose by \\(m\\cdot m'\\), an idempotent monoid, so \\(R_\\infty=\\{0,1\\}^G\\cdot\\Theta\\), which as a set is \\(\\Theta\\) itself (\\(\\mathrm{diag}(m)\\Theta\\subseteq\\Theta\\))."},"categorical_reading":"LoRAPrune is a 0/1 diagonal monoid acting on LoRA's cone (Sat). Its importance score replaces the weight gradient \\(G\\) by the one-step LoRA update \\(\\nabla_BA+B\\nabla_A-\\nabla_B\\nabla_A=K(G)-\\nabla_B\\nabla_A\\). This is the cometric \\(K=D\\rho\\,D\\rho^\\top\\) applied to \\(G\\), plus the second-order term. So pruning needs no backward pass through \\(W_0\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A,m)=\\big(\\mu=m\\odot\\lVert W_0+sBA\\rVert_{\\rm row},\\ B,\\ A\\big)","note":"On each mask component, for boxes masked by output rows (q, k, v, gate, up); DoRA pointed at \\((\\lVert W_0\\rVert_{\\rm row},0,A_0)\\). It fails on o_proj and down_proj, where head and channel masks act on input columns."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Pruned groups contribute the update \\(-\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbf 1-m)W_0\\), whose rank, up to the number of pruned rows, exceeds \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Sat"},{"id":"modulora","name":"ModuLoRA","full_name":"Modular Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"ModuLoRA: Finetuning 2-Bit LLMs on Consumer GPUs by Integrating with Modular Quantizers","authors":"Yin et al.","year":2023,"venue":"TMLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.16119","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16119","code_url":"https://github.com/kuleshov-group/llmtools","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"(\\hat W_q, z, s)=\\mathcal{Q}(W_0)\\ \\text{(any black-box quantizer: OPTQ/GPTQ 3-4 bit, QuIP\\# 2 bit)},\\quad h=\\hat W x + B A x,\\ \\ \\hat W=\\mathcal{D}(\\hat W_q,z,s)=s\\odot\\hat W_q+z;\\quad \\nabla_x\\mathcal{L}=\\hat W^{\\top}\\nabla_h\\mathcal{L}+A^{\\top}B^{\\top}\\nabla_h\\mathcal{L}\\ \\text{with }\\hat W\\ \\text{re-dequantized in the backward pass (never cached)};\\ \\hat W_q\\ \\text{frozen}, A,B\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"every linear layer of the quantized LLM (LLaMA, OPT, BLOOM up to 65B)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Low-rank additive update \\(\\Delta W = BA\\) on top of a frozen, quantizer-agnostic low-precision weight \\(\\hat W = \\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{Q}(W_0))\\). The quantizer is a black-box module that only has to expose dequantization (or a vector-by-quantized-matrix product). Memory is saved by recomputing \\(\\hat W\\) row by row or layer by layer in both the forward and the backward pass, so only one dequantized matrix is ever resident.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) can be added exactly to a high-precision copy of \\(\\hat W\\). Keeping the 2-4-bit format would require re-running the quantizer on \\(\\hat W + BA\\), which is lossy.","identity_at_init":"\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) use the LoRA initialization of Hu et al. (\\(B = 0\\)), so at step 0 the model equals the quantized model \\(\\hat W\\). That is the original \\(W_0\\) only up to quantization error, and ModuLoRA, unlike LoftQ, does not try to compensate for that error.","key_idea":"The quantizer is decoupled from the adapter. The forward and backward passes need only dequantization, which is recomputed on the fly and never stored, so LoRA runs on top of any state-of-the-art PTQ method. This made LoRA fine-tuning of 2-bit (QuIP#) and 3-bit (OPTQ) 65B LLMs possible on a single 24-48 GB GPU.","results_note":"A 4-bit LLaMA-65B fine-tuned with ModuLoRA set a then state-of-the-art ROUGE on SAMSum. 3-bit and 4-bit ModuLoRA models outperform 8-bit bitsandbytes LoRA and 4-bit QLoRA across the 7B-65B range, and 2-bit QuIP# models match them.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"same frozen-quantized-base-plus-LoRA forward, but QLoRA hard-wires NF4 with double quantization, whereas ModuLoRA plugs in any PTQ quantizer (OPTQ, QuIP#) and reaches 2-3 bits"},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"LoftQ initializes BA to cancel the quantization residual; ModuLoRA keeps B = 0 and relies on a stronger quantizer instead"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"QA-LoRA restructures the adapter so it merges into the quantized weights; ModuLoRA leaves merging aside"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"unchanged LoRA parameterization; the contribution is the quantizer-agnostic mixed-precision backward pass"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16119","https://arxiv.org/html/2309.16119","https://openreview.net/forum?id=r9p9CV52MV","https://github.com/kuleshov-group/llmtools"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Junjie Yin","Jiahao Dong","Yingheng Wang","Christopher De Sa","Volodymyr Kuleshov"],"bibkey":"yin2023modulora","bibtex":"@article{yin2023modulora,\n  title = {{ModuLoRA: Finetuning 2-Bit LLMs on Consumer GPUs by Integrating with Modular Quantizers}},\n  author = {Junjie Yin and Jiahao Dong and Yingheng Wang and Christopher De Sa and Volodymyr Kuleshov},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2309.16119},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16119}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA pointed at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) for a black-box quantiser \\(\\kappa=\\mathcal D\\circ\\mathcal Q\\) (OPTQ, QuIP#). Prop V.2(a) holds for any \\(\\kappa\\).","shape":"LoRA's cone translated to \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(e=\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0\\), generically of full rank. Unlike LoftQ, ModuLoRA does not compensate it.","covariance":"Mon (grid), as for QLoRA. QuIP#'s incoherence processing uses fixed random orthogonal maps, so for it the grid geometry holds only in that rotated frame.","merge":"An exact merge leaves the 2–4-bit grid; requantising is lossy.","rebasing":"As LoRA, at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ModuLoRA is the family \\(\\kappa\\mapsto\\mathrm{LoRA}_r(\\kappa\\theta_0)\\) over all quantisers, not just NF4. Additive families commute with translation, so changing the quantiser only moves the base point and its defect \\(e\\). Its real contribution is on the lens side: the backward pass recomputes \\(\\hat W\\) instead of caching it (Obs IV.4), which changes memory, not the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Valid only when \\(\\kappa\\) is NF4 with double quantisation; then the objects are identical (an isomorphism). For the paper's quantisers (OPTQ, QuIP#) the vertices \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) and \\(\\kappa_{\\rm NF4}\\theta_0\\) differ by a generically full-rank matrix, so the cones are disjoint translates and O1 blocks any arrow; ModuLoRA is then the same construction at a different base point, not an object over QLoRA."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loftq","test":"O3","reason":"Over a common \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), ModuLoRA sits at the apex (\\(B=0\\)) and LoftQ at the smooth point \\(-\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\): same image, different strata."},{"target":"qa-lora","test":"O1","reason":"QA-LoRA's updates are constant on quantisation groups. ModuLoRA's generic \\(BA\\) is not, so its image is not contained."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sltrain","name":"SLTrain","full_name":"Sparse plus Low-rank Training","paper_title":"SLTrain: a sparse plus low-rank approach for parameter and memory efficient pretraining","authors":"Han et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.02214","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.02214","code_url":"https://github.com/andyjm3/SLTrain","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{pretraining: } W=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA+S;\\quad \\text{fine-tuning (SLTrain-FT): } W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA+S;\\quad S=\\mathrm{scatter}_{\\mathcal{I}}(\\mathcal{V}),\\ \\mathcal{I}\\ \\text{uniformly random and fixed},\\ |\\mathcal{I}|=\\delta\\,d_{out}d_{in};\\quad h=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\oplus_{\\mathcal{I}}\\mathcal{V}\\big)x,\\ \\nabla_{\\mathcal{V}}\\mathcal{L}=(\\nabla_h\\mathcal{L}\\,x^{\\top})_{\\mathcal{I}};\\ B,A,\\mathcal{V}\\ \\text{trained}, \\mathcal{I}\\ \\text{and (FT) } W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+\\delta\\,d_{in}d_{out}","locus":"all attention and MLP linear layers (LLaMA 60M-7B pretraining; RoBERTa-base GLUE for SLTrain-FT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Sum of a rank-\\(r\\) factorization and a sparse matrix whose support is a uniformly random index set fixed before training; only the values on that support are learned. Stored as \\((B, A, \\mathcal{I}, \\mathcal{V})\\); the dense \\(BA \\oplus_{\\mathcal{I}} \\mathcal{V}\\) is formed transiently in each forward/backward and never saved. With \\(\\delta = \\Omega(\\log n/n)\\), \\(BA+S\\) is full rank w.h.p. (Prop. 1), so the low-rank bottleneck is lifted at negligible cost.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"In the fine-tuning form, both \\(BA\\) and \\(S\\) are dense additive terms that fold into \\(W_0\\). In pretraining the factors are kept on purpose to save memory, although they can also be densified into a single \\(W\\) for standard inference.","identity_at_init":"The low-rank part uses the LoRA init (\\(A\\) Kaiming, \\(B = 0\\)), and the sparse values are drawn uniformly from \\([-1/\\sqrt{d_{in}}, 1/\\sqrt{d_{in}}]\\), which suits pretraining from scratch. For SLTrain-FT, Appendix G only says \\(B\\), \\(A\\) and \\(S\\) are learned 'as in Section 3.2', which implies the same non-zero uniform init for \\(S\\); the paper never states a zero init, so exact identity at init is not guaranteed. The public code supports pretraining only (SpLoRaLinear 'cannot be used for fine-tune').","key_idea":"Low-rank and sparse are complementary parsimonies: low rank keeps the top subspace, and a random-support sparse matrix captures the small, evenly spread residual. A fixed random support needs no stored mask, so the memory cost is only \\(O(\\mathrm{nnz})\\), and a low-rank model gains near-full-rank capacity for pretraining.","results_note":"SLTrain pretrains LLaMA 60M-7B on C4 with perplexity close to full-rank training and far better than pure low-rank and ReLoRA at the same rank, while using fewer parameters; it is slightly behind GaLore at 130M-1B (e.g. 1B: 16.14 vs 15.64 PPL, full-rank 15.56) and slightly ahead at 60M (34.15 vs 34.88). With 8-bit optimizer states and per-layer updates it uses 51/58/73% less memory than full-rank Adam for 350M/1B/7B and 29/34/17% less than GaLore.","relations":[{"target":"rosa","type":"competes-with","note":"RoSA uses the same low-rank-plus-sparse adapter W_0+BA+S for fine-tuning but picks the support from gradient/Fisher information; SLTrain fixes it uniformly at random and targets pretraining"},{"target":"dsee","type":"competes-with","note":"DSEE also combines a low-rank update with a sparse update; its sparse support is taken from the largest entries of a robust-PCA (GreBsmo) residual of W_0 rather than chosen at random"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"GaLore projects gradients to low rank while training full weights; SLTrain reparameterizes the weights, so it saves parameters as well as optimizer states"},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"ReLoRA reaches high rank by merging sequential low-rank updates into a stored full W; SLTrain reaches it with one persistent sparse factor and no full matrix"},{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"SLTrain-FT with \\delta = 0 is LoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.02214","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.02214","https://openreview.net/forum?id=MXze4H7opg","https://github.com/andyjm3/SLTrain"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Andi Han","Jiaxiang Li","Wei Huang","Mingyi Hong","Akiko Takeda","Pratik Jawanpuria","Bamdev Mishra"],"bibkey":"han2024sltrain","bibtex":"@inproceedings{han2024sltrain,\n  title = {{SLTrain: a sparse plus low-rank approach for parameter and memory efficient pretraining}},\n  author = {Andi Han and Jiaxiang Li and Wei Huang and Mingyi Hong and Akiko Takeda and Pratik Jawanpuria and Bamdev Mishra},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.02214},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.02214}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"SLTrain-FT is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\) a fixed-support sparse method (Obs I.7), with the support \\(\\mathcal I\\) drawn uniformly at random and frozen.","shape":"The Minkowski sum \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}+\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}\\): a cone, full rank w.h.p. for \\(\\delta=\\Omega(\\log n/n)\\) (the paper's Prop. 1).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\), but the sparse values start uniform in \\(\\pm1/\\sqrt{d_{in}}\\) (App. G refers back to the pretraining init). So \\(\\rho(q_0)=W_0+S_0\\ne W_0\\), a defect of weight scale on \\(\\delta d_{out}d_{in}\\) entries. At that point \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}+\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal I}\\), so the LoRA deficit \\(r(n-r)\\) persists (checked numerically). With \\(S_0=0\\) it would be RoSA-type: an apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the LoRA summand. The sparse summand is injective, and for \\(|\\mathcal I|+r(m+n-r)\\le mn\\) the two summands generically do not trade.","covariance":"Support structure in the standard basis (\\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\)). A uniformly random support is permutation-invariant in law.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), since the LoRA summand already spans."},"categorical_reading":"SLTrain is the \\(\\oplus\\)-word \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{Mask}_{\\mathcal I}\\) with a uniformly random frozen mask, i.e. RoSA with the support drawn instead of selected. The random sparse summand lifts \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\)'s rank obstruction (O2) at cost \\(O(\\mathrm{nnz})\\), but its non-zero init moves the base point.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"rosa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"RoSA with its mask taken to be \\(\\mathcal I\\), pointed at \\(S=S_0\\). With RoSA's own gradient-chosen support the images differ."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(BA+S\\) is full rank w.h.p., so \\(\\rho_{\\max}>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"meft","name":"MEFT","full_name":"Memory-Efficient Fine-Tuning (reversible PLM via adapters)","paper_title":"Make Pre-trained Model Reversible: From Parameter to Memory Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Liao et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2306.00477","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00477","code_url":"https://github.com/BaohaoLiao/mefts","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h^1_n=\\lambda h^1_{n-1}+\\mathcal{F}_n(h^2_{n-1}),\\quad h^2_n=\\beta h^2_{n-1}+\\mathcal{G}_n(h^1_n);\\qquad h^2_{n-1}=\\big(h^2_n-\\mathcal{G}_n(h^1_n)\\big)/\\beta,\\quad h^1_{n-1}=\\big(h^1_n-\\mathcal{F}_n(h^2_{n-1})\\big)/\\lambda;\\ \\ \\text{MEFT}_1:\\ \\mathcal{F}_n=f_n\\!+\\!\\text{adapter},\\ \\mathcal{G}_n=\\text{adapter},\\ \\lambda\\!\\to\\!0\\ (\\beta=1),\\ \\text{swap streams};\\ \\ \\text{MEFT}_2:\\ \\mathcal{F}_n=\\text{adapter},\\ \\mathcal{G}_n=f_n\\!+\\!\\text{adapter},\\ \\lambda=1,\\ \\beta\\!\\to\\!0,\\ \\text{swap};\\ \\ \\text{MEFT}_3:\\ \\mathcal{F}_n=\\text{Attn}_n\\!+\\!\\text{adapter},\\ \\mathcal{G}_n=\\text{MLP}_n\\!+\\!\\text{adapter},\\ \\lambda=\\beta\\!\\to\\!0,\\ \\text{no swap};\\ \\ (\\text{scaling sent to }0\\text{ set to }0.1);\\ h^1_0=h^2_0=h_0,\\ \\text{head on }(h^1_L+h^2_L)/2;\\ \\text{PLM frozen, only adapters } W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r},W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\approx 4\\,d\\,r\\ \\text{per Transformer layer (two bottleneck adapters)},\\ \\text{i.e. }\\approx 4\\,d\\,r\\,L\\ \\text{per model plus the task head}","locus":"every Transformer layer, which is recast as a two-stream reversible coupling block; adapters sit parallel to the FFN inside the pretrained sub-block and stand alone as the other coupling function","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Bottleneck adapters inserted so that each pretrained layer becomes the \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) or \\(\\mathcal{G}\\) of an additive RevNet coupling with scalings \\(\\lambda\\), \\(\\beta\\) (0.1 for the coupling scaled toward 0) and an optional stream swap. The layer map is then a bijection \\((h^1,h^2)\\mapsto({h^1}',{h^2}')\\) with an explicit inverse, so activations are recomputed backwards from the outputs and activation memory is \\(O(1)\\) in depth instead of \\(O(L)\\).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Inference keeps the two-stream reversible topology, the \\(\\lambda/\\beta\\) scalings and the nonlinear bottleneck adapters, so the result cannot be folded back into the original single-stream PLM weights.","identity_at_init":"Approximate only. Adapters are initialized with \\(W_{down}\\), \\(W_{up} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 0.02^2)\\) rather than zero, and the scaling of the coupling sent toward 0 is 0.1 rather than exactly 0, so one stream reproduces the PLM's hidden state \\(h_n\\) up to a small perturbation (the 'starting point hypothesis'). The stream swap keeps the input of each pretrained block continuous with the original network.","key_idea":"MEFT turns a pretrained, non-reversible Transformer into a reversible one without re-pretraining. Each pretrained layer becomes one coupling function and an adapter becomes the other. The coupling scalars and the stream order are chosen so the network starts out computing what the PLM computed. Backpropagation then recomputes activations instead of caching them, which reduces activation memory as well as parameter count.","results_note":"On GLUE with BERT-base, RoBERTa-large and BART-large, MEFT matches full fine-tuning while training about 0.2% of the parameters and saving up to 84% of activation memory. On OPT-1.3B question answering, MEFT1 is within 0.3 points of full fine-tuning at about half its activation memory (0.64% trainable parameters) and comparable to LoRA at 65% of LoRA's activation memory.","relations":[{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"both reduce activation memory; LST avoids backprop through the PLM with a side network, MEFT backprops through the PLM but recomputes activations by reversibility, which avoids LST's accuracy gap"},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"the adapter placement (in parallel to the FFN) follows He et al.'s unified view"},{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"uses down/up bottleneck adapters as the trainable coupling functions"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA saves optimizer memory but still caches >75% of full-FT activations; MEFT targets the activation term"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00477","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.00477","https://openreview.net/forum?id=J8McuwS3zY","https://github.com/BaohaoLiao/mefts"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Baohao Liao","Shaomu Tan","Christof Monz"],"bibkey":"liao2023make","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liao2023make,\n  title = {{Make Pre-trained Model Reversible: From Parameter to Memory Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Baohao Liao and Shaomu Tan and Christof Monz},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2306.00477},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00477}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each layer becomes an additive RevNet coupling with fixed scalars \\(\\lambda,\\beta\\) (0.1 for the one sent toward 0), and adapters serve as the second coupling function. With \\(\\lambda=0.1\\) fixed, no adapter value reproduces \\(f\\) exactly, so the extension is unpointed. It is only approximately pointed, with a defect of order \\(\\lambda\\) plus the \\(\\mathcal N(0,0.02^2)\\) adapter init.","shape":"Function-level: a two-stream topology the PLM does not have.","base_point":"Exact pointing needs \\(\\lambda=0\\) (or \\(\\beta=0\\)). But the inverse \\(h^1_{n-1}=(h^1_n-\\mathcal F_n(h^2_{n-1}))/\\lambda\\) needs \\(\\lambda\\ne0\\). Invertibility and pointedness are incompatible, and MEFT gives up the neutral point to keep reversibility. In addition the new head reads \\((h^1_L+h^2_L)/2\\), where one stream carries only \\(\\approx\\beta h_{L-1}\\) (MEFT\\(_1\\)), so even at the adapters' init the head input is not \\(h_L\\).","gauge":"Bottleneck adapters: hidden-unit permutations (discrete), plus the positive torus \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}^r\\) if the bottleneck is ReLU (the catalog does not record the activation). The coupling scalars are fixed.","covariance":"Dense adapters: basis-free.","merge":"The two-stream topology, the coupling scalars and the non-linear adapters all remain at inference.","rebasing":"Nothing merges."},"categorical_reading":"MEFT embeds the PLM into a reversible two-stream architecture. The would-be neutral point of this extension (\\(\\lambda=0\\)) is exactly where the coupling stops being invertible. MEFT therefore lives in the unpointed slice with a small fixed defect. Its payoff is on the lens side: reversibility lets the backward pass recompute activations instead of storing them (Obs IV.4).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"parallel-adapter","test":"O3","reason":"A parallel adapter is pointed at \\(U=0\\). MEFT's fixed coupling scalar \\(\\lambda=0.1\\) leaves no exactly neutral value, so the two lie in different base-point strata."},{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Non-linear adapters inside a two-stream coupling are non-affine inserted boxes (Thm VI.3(c)); no weight of the original architecture realises them."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"spectrum","name":"Spectrum","full_name":"Spectrum: SNR-targeted layer-selective training","paper_title":"Spectrum: Targeted Training on Signal to Noise Ratio","authors":"Hartford et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2406.06623","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06623","code_url":"https://github.com/QuixiAI/spectrum","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ \\ \\hat\\sigma=\\mathrm{IQR}(\\{\\sigma_k\\})/1.349,\\ \\ \\varepsilon=\\hat\\sigma\\big(1+\\sqrt{\\beta}\\big),\\ \\beta=\\tfrac{\\min(d_{out},d_{in})}{\\max(d_{out},d_{in})}\\ \\text{(Marchenko-Pastur edge)};\\quad \\mathrm{SNR}(W_0)=\\frac{1}{\\sigma_{\\max}}\\,\\frac{\\sum_{\\sigma_k>\\varepsilon}\\sigma_k}{\\sum_{\\sigma_k\\le\\varepsilon}\\sigma_k};\\quad \\mathcal{S}=\\text{top-}p\\%\\ \\text{matrices by SNR within each module type};\\ \\ W=W_0+\\Delta W\\cdot\\mathbb{1}[W\\in\\mathcal{S}]\\ \\text{(selected matrices fully trained, the rest frozen)}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{W\\in\\mathcal{S}} d_{out}d_{in}\\ +\\ |\\mathcal{V}|\\,d_{model}\\,(\\text{embed\\_tokens})\\ +\\ d_{model}|\\mathcal{V}|\\,(\\text{lm\\_head})\\ \\ (\\mathcal{S}=\\text{top-}p\\%\\ \\text{of each module type; default } p=25\\text{ in the paper, 50 in the script; the official script always unfreezes embeddings and lm\\_head})","locus":"whole weight matrices of a chosen subset of layers, ranked separately within each module type (q/k/v/o projections, MLP gate/up/down); the official script additionally always unfreezes model.embed_tokens and lm_head (not SNR-ranked)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"Coordinate projection onto a union of whole weight matrices chosen before training by a data-free spectral score: the Marchenko-Pastur bulk edge of each \\(W_0\\) splits its singular values into signal and noise, and matrices with the highest normalized signal/noise mass are unfrozen. Updates inside a selected matrix are dense and full-rank.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Selected weights are fine-tuned in place, so the result is an ordinary checkpoint with the original architecture and cost.","identity_at_init":"Exact: no parameters are added, and training starts from the pretrained weights. The selection only decides which matrices may move.","key_idea":"Spectrum chooses which layers to fine-tune from the pretrained weights alone. Random-matrix theory gives a noise threshold for each matrix's singular values, and only the matrices with the highest signal-to-noise ratio, about the top 25-50% per module type, are trained in full precision. This keeps much of full fine-tuning's quality at reduced memory and time, especially under ZeRO-3/FSDP sharding.","results_note":"On Llama-3-8B and Mistral-7B (airoboros-3.1), Spectrum-50/25 are competitive with full fine-tuning (sometimes better) and above QLoRA on most reported benchmarks. For Llama-3-8B under DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 they use 17.72%/23.05% less per-GPU memory than full fine-tuning (QLoRA: 14.73%) and train 15.48%/36.78% faster; on a single GPU QLoRA uses less memory.","relations":[{"target":"lisa","type":"competes-with","note":"both train a subset of full layers; LISA re-samples layers randomly during training, Spectrum fixes them up front by SNR"},{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"layer-subset fine-tuning; surgical FT selects by the depth at which the distribution shift lives, Spectrum by a data-free spectral SNR"},{"target":"qlora","type":"combines","note":"Spectrum-25 + QLoRA (adapters only on SNR-selected modules) gave the lowest VRAM in the paper"},{"target":"badam","type":"competes-with","note":"BAdam also trains full blocks one at a time to save memory, cycling through all blocks rather than selecting a fixed subset"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06623","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.06623","https://github.com/QuixiAI/spectrum","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QuixiAI/spectrum/main/spectrum.py","https://github.com/axolotl-ai-cloud/axolotl/tree/main/src/axolotl/integrations/spectrum"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Eric Hartford","Lucas Atkins","Fernando Fernandes Neto","David Golchinfar"],"bibkey":"hartford2024spectrum","bibtex":"@article{hartford2024spectrum,\n  title = {{Spectrum: Targeted Training on Signal to Noise Ratio}},\n  author = {Eric Hartford and Lucas Atkins and Fernando Fernandes Neto and David Golchinfar},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2406.06623},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.06623},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06623}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The selected whole matrices are fully trained, plus embeddings and lm_head in the official script; everything else is frozen.","shape":"A coordinate subspace, \\(d=\\sum_{W\\in\\mathcal S}d_{out}d_{in}\\) plus embeddings and head.","covariance":"Within a selected box the image is \\(GL\\times GL\\), but the selection reads \\(W_0\\)'s singular values. The SNR is \\(O\\times O\\)-invariant and homogeneous of degree \\(-1\\) under scaling (the \\(1/\\sigma_{\\max}\\) factor). So only orthogonal changes of basis preserve the selected set. Recorded under the spectral-frame key, restricted to \\(O\\times O\\).","rebasing":"Fixed set: idempotent. Recomputing the SNR after a merge could change the set."},"categorical_reading":"Spectrum is a Lin coordinate block of whole matrices, chosen data-free by a Marchenko–Pastur signal-to-noise score of \\(W_0\\). Its image is of the same type as surgical fine-tuning's. What is new is the selection rule: it depends on \\(W_0\\) only through its spectrum, so it is natural only under orthogonal changes of basis.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Selected matrices get dense full-rank updates, so \\(\\rho_{\\max}>r\\)."},{"target":"lisa","test":"O1","reason":"Spectrum's fixed support spans matrices in many layers, chosen per module type. A LISA stage trains \\(\\gamma\\) whole sampled layers, so no single stage contains Spectrum's image, and Spectrum never resamples."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-dash","name":"LoRA-Dash","full_name":"LoRA-Dash (task-specific direction amplification)","paper_title":"Unleashing the Power of Task-Specific Directions in Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Si et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2409.01035","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=RYrJqz44p4","code_url":"https://github.com/Chongjie-Si/Subspace-Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{pre-launch } (t=100\\text{ LoRA steps}):\\ W_0=\\textstyle\\sum_i\\sigma_i u_i v_i^{\\top},\\ \\ \\delta_i=\\Big|\\frac{u_i^{\\top}\\Delta W\\,v_i}{\\sigma_i+\\epsilon}\\Big|,\\ \\Delta W=BA,\\ \\ \\{\\bar u_k\\bar v_k^{\\top}\\}_{k=1}^{s}=\\text{top-}s\\ \\text{by }\\delta_i;\\quad \\text{dash: } W=W_0+BA+\\textstyle\\sum_{k=1}^{s}\\Delta\\sigma_k\\,\\bar u_k\\bar v_k^{\\top},\\ \\ \\Delta\\sigma_k\\leftarrow 0;\\ W_0,\\bar u_k,\\bar v_k\\ \\text{frozen}; A,B,\\Delta\\sigma\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+s\\quad(s=8\\ \\text{by default})","locus":"LoRA-targeted linear layers (\\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\), \\(W_{up}\\), \\(W_{down}\\) in the LLaMA commonsense setting; also DeBERTa and SDXL)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA update plus a diagonal update in \\(W_0\\)'s own singular basis restricted to \\(s\\) core directions \\(u_k v_k^\\top\\). These are the task-specific directions (TSDs) whose coordinates change the most relative to their singular value, estimated from LoRA's \\(\\Delta W\\) after a short warm-up. The second term is a learned \\(s\\)-dimensional diagonal perturbation of \\(W_0\\)'s spectrum along data-chosen singular pairs.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both \\(BA\\) and \\(\\sum_k \\Delta\\sigma_k \\bar u_k \\bar v_k^\\top\\) are dense additive matrices that fold into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Exact. LoRA starts at \\(B = 0\\), and the dash coefficients \\(\\Delta\\sigma_k\\) are zero-initialized when the dash phase begins. The method needs a one-off SVD of each \\(W_0\\), and the directions are chosen from the LoRA update after about 100 steps. The companion LoRA-Init/LoRA-TSD variants instead initialize \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) from the top-\\(r\\) TSDs, which is a PiSSA-style split.","key_idea":"LoRA's own paper says \\(\\Delta W\\) amplifies directions of \\(W_0\\) that matter for the task. LoRA-Dash defines these as the core singular directions of \\(W_0\\) with the largest relative coordinate change, and shows that a briefly trained LoRA already identifies them with high precision. It then gives each identified direction its own scalar so its change can grow beyond what the low-rank product allows.","results_note":"On commonsense reasoning, LoRA-Dash improves LoRA across LLaMA-7B, LLaMA2-7B and LLaMA3-8B (e.g. LLaMA3-8B \\(r=32\\): 80.8 to 85.4 average) with only \\(s\\) extra scalars per matrix; gains are also reported on GLUE (DeBERTaV3) and subject-driven SDXL generation.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"adds s singular-direction scalars on top of the standard BA update"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"PiSSA adapts the top singular directions of W_0, whereas the TSD analysis shows task directions sit among smaller, though not the smallest, singular values"},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"MiLoRA adapts the smallest singular directions; LoRA-Dash picks directions by measured change rate, not by singular-value rank"},{"target":"kasa","type":"competes-with","note":"KaSA also learns singular-value-level updates in an SVD basis; LoRA-Dash uses W_0's frozen singular vectors selected by LoRA's update"},{"target":"svft","type":"competes-with","note":"SVFT learns coefficients on the outer products of W_0's singular vectors; LoRA-Dash learns diagonal coefficients only on s data-selected pairs, added to LoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01035","https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01035v1","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.01035","https://openreview.net/forum?id=RYrJqz44p4","https://github.com/Chongjie-Si/Subspace-Tuning","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/c3d101702194a5a345d81cdee4fe966f-Abstract-Conference.html","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/poster/32083"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Chongjie Si","Zhiyi Shi","Shifan Zhang","Xiaokang Yang","Hanspeter Pfister","Wei Shen"],"bibkey":"si2024unleashing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{si2024unleashing,\n  title = {{Unleashing the Power of Task-Specific Directions in Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Chongjie Si and Zhiyi Shi and Shifan Zhang and Xiaokang Yang and Hanspeter Pfister and Wei Shen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2409.01035},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=RYrJqz44p4}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\) an \\(s\\)-dimensional diagonal method in \\(W_0\\)'s own singular frame. The pairs \\(\\bar u_k\\bar v_k^\\top\\) are chosen from LoRA's update after a 100-step warm-up.","shape":"The Minkowski sum \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}+\\mathrm{span}\\{\\bar u_k\\bar v_k^\\top\\}\\): a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), of rank up to \\(r+s\\).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\) and \\(\\Delta\\sigma_0=0\\): LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex. The dash coordinates join at step 100 at their base value 0.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the LoRA summand. The dash coordinates are injective and generically do not trade with \\(BA\\).","covariance":"The dash directions are singular pairs of \\(W_0\\), selected by \\(|u_i^\\top\\Delta Wv_i|/\\sigma_i\\): an SVD frame plus a data statistic, so \\(CO\\times CO\\) (data).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\) through the LoRA summand."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Dash is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\) a frozen-frame spectral line method: SVDiff on \\(s\\) singular pairs, selected from LoRA's own early trajectory. The dash summand moves along directions already present in \\(W_0\\)'s spectrum, so it supplies \\(s\\) dimensions of 'amplification' on which \\(BA\\) would otherwise spend rank.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A,\\Delta\\sigma)=\\big([B\\ \\ \\bar U\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma)],\\ [A;\\ \\bar V^\\top]\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_0;\\bar V^\\top])\\). This is a standard \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) pointing iff \\(\\mathrm{row}A_0\\cap\\mathrm{span}\\bar V=0\\), which is generic (Obs I.7)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O3","reason":"PiSSA is a Type II object (a smooth point of a translated cone); LoRA-Dash sits at the apex of an untranslated cone. The strata differ, and the images are incomparable."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Against \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) at equal rank: \\(\\rho_{\\max}=r+s>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lcm-lora","name":"LCM-LoRA","full_name":"Latent Consistency Model LoRA (universal acceleration module)","paper_title":"LCM-LoRA: A Universal Stable-Diffusion Acceleration Module","authors":"Luo et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2311.05556","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05556","code_url":"https://github.com/luosiallen/latent-consistency-model","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig (plain LoRA weights; loaded in diffusers through the PEFT backend with load_lora_weights(..., adapter_name='lcm') and combined via set_adapters(['lcm','style'], adapter_weights=[1.0, 0.8]))","formula_latex":"\\text{train: } W=W_0+B_{\\mathrm{LCM}}A_{\\mathrm{LCM}}\\ \\text{on the U-Net, minimizing the latent consistency distillation loss } d\\big(f_\\theta(z_{t_{n+k}},\\omega,c,t_{n+k}),\\,f_{\\theta^-}(\\hat z^{\\Psi,\\omega}_{t_n},\\omega,c,t_n)\\big),\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\quad \\text{use: } \\theta'=\\theta_{\\mathrm{pre}}+\\lambda_1\\tau'+\\lambda_2\\tau_{\\mathrm{LCM}},\\ \\text{i.e. } W=W_0+\\lambda_1 B'A'+\\lambda_2 B_{\\mathrm{LCM}}A_{\\mathrm{LCM}}\\ \\ (\\lambda_1=0.8,\\lambda_2=1.0),\\ \\text{no further training}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted linear},\\ r(c_{in}k_hk_w+c_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted conv};\\ r=64\\ \\text{(67.5M for SD-v1.5, 105M for SSD-1B, 197M for SDXL)}","locus":"all LoRA-targeted modules of the Stable Diffusion U-Net (SD-v1.5, SSD-1B, SDXL): attention q/k/v/out, proj_in/proj_out and feed-forward linears, plus ResNet conv1/conv2/conv_shortcut, down/upsampler convolutions and time_emb_proj (diffusers LCM-LoRA training script; the released checkpoints contain conv LoRA tensors); applied to fine-tuned community checkpoints of the same base","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Two additive low-rank task vectors on a shared base: an 'acceleration vector' \\(\\tau_{LCM} = B_{LCM}A_{LCM}\\), trained by consistency distillation of the probability-flow ODE, and a 'style vector' \\(\\tau' = B'A'\\), trained by ordinary fine-tuning. They are combined linearly with fixed scalars in weight space, which is task arithmetic in the commutative monoid of additive low-rank updates. The result is a few-step (2-8) sampler for any style.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both LoRAs and their weighted sum are additive low-rank terms that fuse into the U-Net weights, giving a standard model run with the LCM scheduler.","identity_at_init":"LoRA init (\\(B = 0\\)) during distillation, so training starts from the teacher diffusion model. At composition time no training occurs, and the base is recovered with \\(\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = 0\\).","key_idea":"Few-step sampling can be distilled into a LoRA instead of a full model. That acceleration LoRA works as a portable 'neural PF-ODE solver': adding it to the base or to a fine-tuned checkpoint of the same base, or to any style LoRA, gives 4-step generation in that style with no extra training. It is a widely cited example of skill arithmetic between LoRA modules.","results_note":"","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"latent consistency distillation is run as LoRA fine-tuning of the diffusion U-Net"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"special-case-of","note":"\\theta_{pre} + \\lambda_1\\tau' + \\lambda_2\\tau_{LCM} is task-vector addition with LoRA task vectors"},{"target":"pem-arithmetic","type":"builds-on","note":"cited as prior work on arithmetic over parameter-efficient modules; but LCM-LoRA sums the weight updates \\lambda_1B'A'+\\lambda_2B_{LCM}A_{LCM}, whereas PEM addition interpolates the factors A and B themselves (creating cross terms), so it is not literally a special case"},{"target":"ziplora","type":"competes-with","note":"ZipLoRA learns per-column merge coefficients to avoid interference between subject and style LoRAs; LCM-LoRA combines its acceleration LoRA with a style LoRA using fixed scalars and no learned merge"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05556","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.05556","https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/main/en/using-diffusers/inference_with_lcm","https://github.com/luosiallen/latent-consistency-model","https://huggingface.co/docs/diffusers/main/en/api/pipelines/latent_consistency_models","https://github.com/huggingface/diffusers/blob/main/examples/consistency_distillation/train_lcm_distill_lora_sdxl.py","https://huggingface.co/latent-consistency/lcm-lora-sdxl"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"authors_full":["Simian Luo","Yiqin Tan","Suraj Patil","Daniel Gu","Patrick von Platen","Apolinário Passos","Longbo Huang","Jian Li","Hang Zhao"],"bibkey":"luo2023lcm","bibtex":"@article{luo2023lcm,\n  title = {{LCM-LoRA: A Universal Stable-Diffusion Acceleration Module}},\n  author = {Simian Luo and Yiqin Tan and Suraj Patil and Daniel Gu and Patrick von Platen and Apolinário Passos and Longbo Huang and Jian Li and Hang Zhao},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2311.05556},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.05556},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05556}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The acceleration LoRA \\(\\tau_{\\rm LCM}=B_{\\rm LCM}A_{\\rm LCM}\\) is trained once, as LoRA, by latent consistency distillation. The method as used is post hoc: \\(\\theta_{\\rm pre}+\\lambda_1\\tau'+\\lambda_2\\tau_{\\rm LCM}\\), with no further training.","shape":"The coefficient family \\((\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)\\mapsto W_0+\\lambda_1B'A'+\\lambda_2B_{\\rm LCM}A_{\\rm LCM}\\) (applied to every adapted matrix at once) is affine, \\(d=2\\) for the whole model: the two strengths are global scalars, not per-matrix.","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) returns \\(\\theta_0\\), and the family is injective: regular.","gauge":"Injective when the two updates are linearly independent; only products enter, so the factor gauge cancels.","covariance":"Task arithmetic on updates is \\(GL\\)-natural.","merge":"The weighted sum fuses, conv LoRAs included.","rebasing":"Frozen task vectors: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"LCM-LoRA is task arithmetic with two LoRA task vectors, one of which encodes a sampler (a distilled PF-ODE solver) rather than a skill. It ports to fine-tuned checkpoints of the same base because LoRA is additive (Def I.2, Prop V.2(a)). Since \\(\\delta_{\\rm LCM}\\) does not depend on \\(\\theta_0\\), the same update is a method at every base \\(\\theta_{\\rm pre}+\\tau'\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)=(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2),\\quad \\tau_1=B'A',\\ \\tau_2=B_{\\rm LCM}A_{\\rm LCM}","note":"Task arithmetic with two LoRA task vectors (the catalog's special-case edge)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)=\\big([\\lambda_1B'\\ \\ \\lambda_2B_{\\rm LCM}],\\ [A';A_{\\rm LCM}]\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r'+r}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A';A_{\\rm LCM}])\\), as for HF's cat (Obs I.7)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pem-arithmetic","test":"O2","reason":"PEM addition merges factors, so its result has rank \\(\\le r\\); LCM-LoRA's sum of products reaches rank \\(r'+r\\). Conversely, PEM's cross terms \\(B'A_{\\rm LCM}\\) leave LCM-LoRA's span."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"concept-sliders","name":"Concept Sliders","full_name":"Concept Sliders (attribute-direction LoRA with a continuous inference-time strength)","paper_title":"Concept Sliders: LoRA Adaptors for Precise Control in Diffusion Models","authors":"Gandikota et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ECCV 2024","arxiv":"2311.12092","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12092","code_url":"https://github.com/rohitgandikota/sliders","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W(\\alpha)=W_0+\\alpha\\,BA,\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ \\alpha\\in\\mathbb{R}\\ \\text{(slider strength, set at inference)};\\ \\ \\text{text sliders regress the slider model onto the frozen model's guided score: }\\epsilon_{\\theta^{*}}(X,c_t,t)\\leftarrow\\epsilon_{\\theta}(X,c_t,t)+\\eta\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal{P}}\\big(\\epsilon_{\\theta}(X,(c_{+},p),t)-\\epsilon_{\\theta}(X,(c_{-},p),t)\\big);\\ \\ \\text{image-pair sliders: }\\min_{A,B}\\|\\epsilon_{\\theta_{-}}(x^{A}_t,\\varnothing,t)-\\epsilon\\|^2+\\|\\epsilon_{\\theta_{+}}(x^{B}_t,\\varnothing,t)-\\epsilon\\|^2\\ (\\theta_{\\pm}:\\ \\alpha=\\pm1);\\ \\ \\text{composition: }W=W_0+\\sum_k\\alpha_kB_kA_k;\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix per slider (default } r=4\\text{)}","locus":"LoRA on the diffusion U-Net (SD-XL, SD-1.4); the official text-slider config adapts all layers except cross-attention ('noxattn'), including \\(3\\times3\\) conv layers ('c3lier'), with rank 4 and \\(\\alpha\\) 1, so the code applies the fixed internal scale \\(\\alpha/r = 1/4\\) times the slider multiplier; at inference the slider is switched on only after the first \\(t\\) denoising steps (SDEdit-style)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Each slider is a rank-\\(r\\) ray \\(\\{W_0+\\alpha BA:\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}\\) through \\(W_0\\), i.e. a one-parameter additive family in weight space; \\(K\\) sliders span the affine slice \\(W_0+\\mathrm{span}_{\\mathbb{R}}\\{B_kA_k\\}_{k=1}^{K}\\), navigated by the coefficient vector (\\(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_K\\)). The training target is an output-space (score) contrast \\(\\epsilon(c_+)-\\epsilon(c_-)\\) distilled into this weight direction.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"For any fixed strength vector the update \\(\\sum_k \\alpha_k B_k A_k\\) is a plain matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\); the SDEdit-style schedule only switches between \\(W_0\\) and the merged weights across denoising steps.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)); in addition, \\(\\alpha = 0\\) recovers the base model exactly at any time after training, since every slider is a ray through \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"Train a LoRA whose direction encodes a single attribute, using the frozen model's own guidance-style score difference between opposing prompts (with preservation prompts to disentangle) or before/after image pairs as the target; scaling the LoRA multiplier then dials the attribute continuously up or down, and sliders add.","results_note":"On SD-XL, a 'fix hands' slider cut the rater-judged rate of distorted hands from 62% to 22%, the disentanglement objective lowered attribute interference from 0.36 to 0.10, and up to 50 sliders were composed in one model.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"architecturally each slider is a LoRA whose multiplier alpha is exposed as a continuous control"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"special-case-of","note":"composing sliders, W_0 + sum_k alpha_k B_k A_k, is task arithmetic with low-rank task vectors and user-chosen signed coefficients"},{"target":"proxy-tuning","type":"dual-to","note":"sliders distil an output-space contrast eps(c+) - eps(c-) into a weight-space direction; proxy-tuning applies an output-space (logit) contrast at decode time without touching weights"},{"target":"ziplora","type":"competes-with","note":"both combine several diffusion LoRAs; ZipLoRA learns per-column merge coefficients instead of exposing user-set scalar strengths"},{"target":"multi-lora-composition","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative way to apply several diffusion LoRAs at once (switching/compositing across denoising steps)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12092","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.12092","https://github.com/rohitgandikota/sliders","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rohitgandikota/sliders/main/trainscripts/textsliders/data/config.yaml","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Concept%20Sliders"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Rohit Gandikota","Joanna Materzynska","Tingrui Zhou","Antonio Torralba","David Bau"],"bibkey":"gandikota2023concept","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gandikota2023concept,\n  title = {{Concept Sliders: LoRA Adaptors for Precise Control in Diffusion Models}},\n  author = {Rohit Gandikota and Joanna Materzynska and Tingrui Zhou and Antonio Torralba and David Bau},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2311.12092},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12092}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Architecturally LoRA. The contribution is the training target, either a guidance-style score contrast \\(\\epsilon(c_+)-\\epsilon(c_-)\\) or image pairs at \\(\\alpha=\\pm1\\), together with the exposed multiplier \\(\\alpha\\).","shape":"LoRA's cone. A slider is the ray \\(\\{W_0+\\alpha BA\\}\\) through the trained point; the image is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), so the whole line stays inside it.","merge":"For fixed \\(\\alpha\\) the update fuses. The SDEdit-style schedule switches between two merged models across denoising steps.","rebasing":"As LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"A concept slider is a ruling line of LoRA's cone through a trained point. \\(\\alpha\\mapsto W_0+\\alpha BA\\) is the scalar action that every cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) carries, exposed as a user control. \\(K\\) sliders give the \\(\\oplus\\) of \\(K\\) frozen line methods inside \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Kr}\\), i.e. task arithmetic with low-rank task vectors.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The trained object is LoRA (rank 4, \\(\\alpha/r=1/4\\) in the official config)."},{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_K)=(\\lambda_k=\\alpha_k),\\quad \\tau_k=B_kA_k","note":"The source here is the deployed P-object (strengths \\(\\alpha_k\\) trainable, slider factors frozen after training), not the training object classified above, whose \\(Q=(B,A)\\) has no map to coefficients. On that deployed object \\(\\rho_{\\rm TA}\\circ h=W_0+\\sum_k\\alpha_kB_kA_k\\), pointed at \\(\\alpha=0\\); signed coefficients are allowed."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"wise-ft","test":"O1","reason":"For the training object O2 already applies (\\(d=r(m+n-r)>1\\)). Even on its deployed line, a slider's strength ranges over \\(\\mathbb R\\) (negative values erase a concept). WiSE-FT's \\(\\alpha\\in[0,1]\\) gives a segment whose germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is a half-line. The converse arrow exists when \\(\\theta_1-\\theta_0=BA\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"ic-lora","name":"IC-LoRA","full_name":"In-Context LoRA for diffusion transformers","paper_title":"In-Context LoRA for Diffusion Transformers","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2410.23775","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23775","code_url":"https://github.com/ali-vilab/In-Context-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\ \\text{in the FLUX.1-dev DiT},\\ r=\\alpha=16\\ \\text{(official config)};\\ \\ \\text{training input: one composite image }X=[\\,x_1\\,|\\,x_2\\,|\\cdots|\\,x_n\\,]\\ \\text{(n panels concatenated spatially) with one merged caption }c=[c_{\\text{set}};c_1;\\dots;c_n];\\ \\ \\text{loss: the base model's own flow-matching objective on }(X,c);\\ \\ \\text{image-conditioned use: mask some panels and inpaint them from the rest with SDEdit (training-free)};\\ W_0\\ \\text{and text encoders frozen},\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained on 20--100 image sets per task}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix},\\ r=16","locus":"LoRA on the layers of a text-to-image diffusion transformer (FLUX.1-dev); the task itself is carried by the input format (panel-concatenated image + merged prompt), not by new modules","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Plain low-rank \\(\\Delta W=BA\\); the cross-image conditioning is supplied by the DiT's existing full self-attention over the tokens of all panels in one canvas, so the adapter only re-weights an in-context capability the base model already has (no token-routing masks, no extra encoders).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapter is a standard LoRA on an unchanged DiT, so \\(BA\\) folds into \\(W_0\\); the multi-panel behaviour comes from the input canvas and prompt.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)) on an unmodified architecture, so the zero adapter is exactly FLUX.1-dev.","key_idea":"Text-to-image DiTs can already draw coherent multi-panel images from one merged prompt; concatenating image sets into a single canvas with a joint caption and training a small task-specific LoRA on 20-100 examples activates this in-context ability, and SDEdit-style masking turns it into reference-conditioned generation.","results_note":"Qualitative results only: storyboards, font design, portrait photography, home decoration, visual identity and visual effects, each trained for 5,000 steps (batch 4, rank 16) on one A100; no quantitative benchmark is reported.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"the trainable object is an ordinary LoRA; the contribution is the data/input recipe"},{"target":"ominicontrol","type":"competes-with","note":"both reuse the DiT's own attention over concatenated image tokens; OminiControl concatenates condition tokens in sequence and adds an attention bias, IC-LoRA concatenates panels in pixel space"},{"target":"easycontrol","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative LoRA-based conditioning of DiTs on reference images"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23775","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.23775","https://github.com/ali-vilab/In-Context-LoRA","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2410.15027"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Lianghua Huang","Wei Wang","Zhi-Fan Wu","Yupeng Shi","Huanzhang Dou","Chen Liang","Yutong Feng","Yu Liu","Jingren Zhou"],"bibkey":"huang2024context","bibtex":"@article{huang2024context,\n  title = {{In-Context LoRA for Diffusion Transformers}},\n  author = {Lianghua Huang and Wei Wang and Zhi-Fan Wu and Yupeng Shi and Huanzhang Dou and Chen Liang and Yutong Feng and Yu Liu and Jingren Zhou},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2410.23775},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.23775},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23775}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Plain LoRA on FLUX.1-dev. The task is carried by the input format (a panel canvas plus a merged caption), which is data, not part of \\(\\rho\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) on an unmodified architecture: the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex."},"categorical_reading":"IC-LoRA changes nothing in the object: it is LoRA, and the in-context behaviour comes from the base DiT's full attention over a panel canvas. Categorically it is a choice of data distribution on an unchanged input space, which the slice does not see. OminiControl, by contrast, extends the input sequence and gates its LoRA by token.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The trainable object is an ordinary LoRA (\\(r=\\alpha=16\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ominicontrol","test":"O1","reason":"OminiControl's LoRA acts only on condition tokens, a position-selective edit (M∞ by Thm VI.3(a)), so its noise and text tokens always pass through \\(W_0\\). IC-LoRA's position-uniform \\(W_0+BA\\) on every token lies outside its realisations."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"proxy-tuning","name":"Proxy-Tuning","full_name":"Proxy-tuning (decoding-time tuning of a large model through a small tuned/untuned pair)","paper_title":"Tuning Language Models by Proxy","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"COLM 2024","arxiv":"2401.08565","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.08565","code_url":"https://github.com/alisawuffles/proxy-tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"p_{\\tilde{\\mathcal M}}(X_t\\mid x_{<t})=\\mathrm{softmax}\\big[s_{\\mathcal M}(X_t\\mid x_{<t})+s_{\\mathcal M^{+}}(X_t\\mid x_{<t})-s_{\\mathcal M^{-}}(X_t\\mid x_{<t})\\big];\\ \\ \\mathcal M:\\ \\text{large base model (frozen; only its logits are used)},\\ \\mathcal M^{-}:\\ \\text{small base model (frozen)},\\ \\mathcal M^{+}:\\ \\mathcal M^{-}\\ \\text{after tuning};\\ \\text{models must share a vocabulary}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{in }\\mathcal M;\\ \\text{only the small proxy }\\mathcal M^{-}\\to\\mathcal M^{+}\\ \\text{is tuned (any method, done once)}","locus":"next-token logits at decoding time; the large model's weights are never accessed","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Output-space (logit) arithmetic: the large model's logit vector is translated by the small model's tuning vector \\(s_{\\mathcal M^{+}}-s_{\\mathcal M^{-}}\\), i.e. task arithmetic in logit space, equivalently a product of experts \\(\\propto p_{\\mathcal M}\\,p_{\\mathcal M^{+}}/p_{\\mathcal M^{-}}\\) with one expert and one anti-expert (DExperts).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Every decoding step needs forward passes of three models; nothing is folded into the large model's weights.","identity_at_init":"If the proxy is untuned (\\(\\mathcal M^+ = \\mathcal M^-\\)), the offset vanishes and the large model's distribution is recovered exactly.","key_idea":"Tune a small model, then add the difference between its tuned and untuned logits to a large (possibly black-box) model's logits at each decoding step, transferring the effect of tuning without touching the large model's weights.","results_note":"Steering Llama2-13B and 70B base with Llama2-7B chat/base proxies closed 91.1% and 88.1% of the gap to the true chat models on average over five settings (AlpacaFarm, GSM, ToxiGen, TruthfulQA multiple-choice and open-ended); it was also applied to GPT-3.5 using only its top-5 log-probabilities (+2.3% on RealTimeQA).","relations":[{"target":"eft","type":"equivalent-to","note":"the per-token EFT up-scaling rule pi_ref^N * pi^M / pi_ref^M normalizes to the same softmax of s_M + s_{M+} - s_{M-}; the two papers are concurrent"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"dual-to","note":"adds a 'tuning vector' in logit space instead of in weight space"},{"target":"black-box-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"both adapt a model whose weights are inaccessible; BBT searches a soft prompt gradient-free, proxy-tuning needs output logits but no prompt optimization"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"the paper's appendix compares proxy-tuning with LoRA for task fine-tuning"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.08565","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.08565","https://github.com/alisawuffles/proxy-tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Alisa Liu","Xiaochuang Han","Yizhong Wang","Yulia Tsvetkov","Yejin Choi","Noah A. Smith"],"bibkey":"liu2024tuning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2024tuning,\n  title = {{Tuning Language Models by Proxy}},\n  author = {Alisa Liu and Xiaochuang Han and Yizhong Wang and Yulia Tsvetkov and Yejin Choi and Noah A. Smith},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Conference on Language Modeling (COLM)},\n  eprint = {2401.08565},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.08565}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A post-hoc operation on three trained checkpoints, \\(\\mathrm{softmax}(s_{\\mathcal M}+s_{\\mathcal M^+}-s_{\\mathcal M^-})\\). It acts on realisations (next-token logits) rather than on \\(\\Theta^N\\), so it is the realisation-level analogue of axes.json's P (\\(\\Theta^N\\to\\Theta\\)).","shape":"No weight-space image: the large model's weights are never accessed.","base_point":"The logit offset \\(s_{\\mathcal M^+}-s_{\\mathcal M^-}\\) is a parallel box on the logit wire. Its neutral value \\(\\mathcal M^+=\\mathcal M^-\\) returns \\(\\mathcal M\\) exactly.","gauge":"n/a: the method has no parameters of its own. The tuned object is the small model (any method), and its fibres play no role here.","covariance":"It reads only realisations, so it is invariant under every function-preserving reparametrisation of the large model. The only structure it imposes is a shared vocabulary basis on the logit wire.","merge":"Three forward passes per token; nothing folds into the large model.","rebasing":"n/a: nothing is merged."},"categorical_reading":"Proxy-tuning is task arithmetic transported from weight space to logit space. The tuning vector \\(s_{\\mathcal M^+}-s_{\\mathcal M^-}\\) of a small model is added to a large model's logits, a product of experts \\(p_{\\mathcal M}p_{\\mathcal M^+}/p_{\\mathcal M^-}\\). Because it acts on realisations, it needs no weight correspondence between the two scales. Trans-LoRA works through \\(\\Phi_f\\) for the same reason.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"eft","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per-token EFT up-scaling normalises to the same softmax: isomorphic objects (concurrent work)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"eft","name":"EFT","full_name":"Emulated Fine-Tuning (LM up-scaling)","paper_title":"An Emulator for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models using Small Language Models","authors":"Mitchell et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.12962","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12962","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\pi^{N}_{M}(y\\mid x)\\propto\\pi^{N}_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y\\mid x)\\,\\frac{\\pi^{M}(y\\mid x)}{\\pi^{M}_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y\\mid x)}=\\pi^{N}_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y\\mid x)\\exp\\big(r^{M}_{\\pi}(x,y)\\big),\\ \\ r^{M}_{\\pi}=\\log\\tfrac{\\pi^{M}}{\\pi^{M}_{\\mathrm{ref}}};\\ \\ \\text{sampled per token: }\\tilde\\pi(y_t\\mid x,y_{<t})=\\tfrac{1}{Z(x,y_{<t})}\\pi^{N}_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y_t\\mid x,y_{<t})\\tfrac{\\pi^{M}(y_t\\mid x,y_{<t})}{\\pi^{M}_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y_t\\mid x,y_{<t})};\\ \\ \\text{up-scaling: }N>M\\ \\text{(large base, small fine-tuned and small base)};\\ \\text{all models frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{(composes existing pre-trained and fine-tuned checkpoints)}","locus":"next-token distributions at sampling time","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Reward transport across scales: fine-tuning is viewed as KL-regularized RL, so a fine-tuned model equals its base times \\(\\exp(\\text{implicit reward})\\); EFT moves the small-scale implicit reward \\(\\log(\\pi^{M}/\\pi^{M}_{\\mathrm{ref}})\\) onto a large-scale base, a log-linear (logit-additive) composition of three models.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Sampling requires the large base and both small models at every step (speculative decoding reduces but does not remove this cost).","identity_at_init":"Setting \\(N = M\\) returns the original fine-tuned policy; an untuned small model (\\(\\pi^M = \\pi^M_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\)) returns the large base model.","key_idea":"Fine-tuning's effect can be isolated as the log-ratio between a fine-tuned model and its base; adding a small model's log-ratio to a large base model emulates fine-tuning the large model, and decouples the scales of pre-training and fine-tuning.","results_note":"Averaged over Llama-1, Llama-2 and Falcon on Anthropic-HH and ELI5, up-scaling recovered nearly 70% of the factuality gain of the truly fine-tuned large model (scores normalized so that the small fine-tuned model is 0 and the large fine-tuned model is 1); speculative EFT decoding sped up sampling by nearly 2.5x when up-scaling Llama-2-7B-chat with Llama-2-70B-base.","relations":[{"target":"proxy-tuning","type":"equivalent-to","note":"concurrent work with the same per-token logit-arithmetic rule; EFT gives the KL-regularized-RL derivation"},{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"dual-to","note":"transports a fine-tuning delta in log-probability space rather than weight space"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12962","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.12962","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=An%20Emulator%20for%20Fine-Tuning%20Large%20Language%20Models%20using%20Small%20Language%20Models"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Eric Mitchell","Rafael Rafailov","Archit Sharma","Chelsea Finn","Christopher D. Manning"],"bibkey":"mitchell2023emulator","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mitchell2023emulator,\n  title = {{An Emulator for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models using Small Language Models}},\n  author = {Eric Mitchell and Rafael Rafailov and Archit Sharma and Chelsea Finn and Christopher D. Manning},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.12962},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12962}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":null,"covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Composes frozen checkpoints at sampling time, per token: \\(\\pi^N_{\\rm ref}\\,\\pi^M/\\pi^M_{\\rm ref}\\). A realisation-level P, as for Proxy-tuning.","shape":"No weight-space image.","base_point":"An untuned small model (\\(\\pi^M=\\pi^M_{\\rm ref}\\)) returns the large base exactly. The implicit reward \\(r=\\log(\\pi^M/\\pi^M_{\\rm ref})\\) is a parallel logit box with neutral value 0.","gauge":"n/a: no trainable parameters (all models frozen).","covariance":"Reads only realisations; it needs a shared vocabulary basis.","merge":"Sampling needs the large base and both small models at every step.","rebasing":"n/a."},"categorical_reading":"EFT reads fine-tuning as a KL-regularised RL update, so a fine-tuned model is its base times \\(\\exp(r)\\). It transports the implicit reward \\(r\\) from scale \\(M\\) to scale \\(N\\) in log-probability space. The per-token rule is Proxy-tuning's. EFT's derivation explains why the transport is meaningful: \\(r\\) is a base-independent, additive object in log space, as LoRA updates are in weight space.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"proxy-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same per-token logit-arithmetic rule."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"wise-ft","name":"WiSE-FT","full_name":"Weight-space ensembles for fine-tuning","paper_title":"Robust fine-tuning of zero-shot models","authors":"Wortsman et al.","year":2021,"venue":"CVPR 2022","arxiv":"2109.01903","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.01903","code_url":"https://github.com/mlfoundations/wise-ft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"peft.helpers.rescale_adapter_scale(model, multiplier=alpha) with alpha in [0,1]: LoRA-adapter form only (scales Delta W toward W_0, which the PEFT docstring calls strictly equivalent to WiSE-FT); heads trained via modules_to_save are not interpolated","formula_latex":"\\theta_{\\alpha}=(1-\\alpha)\\,\\theta_0+\\alpha\\,\\theta_1=\\theta_0+\\alpha(\\theta_1-\\theta_0),\\ \\ \\alpha\\in[0,1];\\ \\ \\theta_0=[V_{\\mathrm{enc}},W_{\\text{zero-shot}}]\\ \\text{(zero-shot CLIP: pre-trained encoder + text-derived head)},\\ \\theta_1=\\text{standard (end-to-end or head-only) fine-tuned weights};\\ \\ \\text{per matrix: }W=W_0+\\alpha\\,\\Delta W;\\ \\text{only }\\alpha\\ \\text{is chosen post hoc (default }0.5\\text{)}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{beyond the underlying fine-tuning (which updates all parameters); one scalar }\\alpha","locus":"all parameters of the image encoder and the classification head","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"The segment \\(\\{\\theta_0+\\alpha(\\theta_1-\\theta_0)\\}_{\\alpha\\in[0,1]}\\) joining the pre-trained point and the fine-tuned point in weight space (a two-point convex combination); when only the linear head is fine-tuned it coincides with the output-space ensemble \\((1-\\alpha)f(x,\\theta_0)+\\alpha f(x,\\theta_1)\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The interpolated weights form a single model of the original architecture, so inference costs exactly one model.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\alpha = 0\\) returns the zero-shot model and \\(\\alpha = 1\\) the fine-tuned one; for end-to-end fine-tuning the head is initialized with the (temperature-scaled) zero-shot classifier.","key_idea":"Zero-shot CLIP is robust under distribution shift while fine-tuning is accurate in distribution; because the two endpoints are linearly connected in weight space, simply interpolating their weights keeps most of both at no extra inference cost.","results_note":"On ImageNet and five derived distribution shifts with CLIP, WiSE-FT improved accuracy under shift by 4-6 pp over prior work while raising ImageNet accuracy by 1.6 pp.","relations":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","type":"special-case-of","note":"WiSE-FT is task arithmetic with a single task vector tau = theta_1 - theta_0 and lambda = alpha in [0,1]"},{"target":"model-soups","type":"competes-with","note":"same group's follow-up averages several fine-tuned endpoints instead of interpolating back toward the zero-shot start"},{"target":"lp-ft","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent robust fine-tuning recipe addressing the same ID/OOD trade-off by initialization rather than interpolation"},{"target":"linear-probing","type":"generalizes","note":"with head-only fine-tuning, WiSE-FT returns the linear probe at alpha = 1 (warm-started from the zero-shot head) and the output ensemble of the zero-shot head and the probe for 0 < alpha < 1"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.01903","https://arxiv.org/html/2109.01903","https://github.com/mlfoundations/wise-ft"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Mitchell Wortsman","Gabriel Ilharco","Jong Wook Kim","Mike Li","Simon Kornblith","Rebecca Roelofs","Raphael Gontijo-Lopes","Hannaneh Hajishirzi","Ali Farhadi","Hongseok Namkoong","Ludwig Schmidt"],"bibkey":"wortsman2021robust","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wortsman2021robust,\n  title = {{Robust fine-tuning of zero-shot models}},\n  author = {Mitchell Wortsman and Gabriel Ilharco and Jong Wook Kim and Mike Li and Simon Kornblith and Rebecca Roelofs and Raphael Gontijo-Lopes and Hannaneh Hajishirzi and Ali Farhadi and Hongseok Namkoong and Ludwig Schmidt},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2109.01903},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.01903}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc on two trained points: \\((\\theta_0,\\theta_1)\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\alpha(\\theta_1-\\theta_0)\\).","shape":"Affine in \\(\\alpha\\), \\(d=1\\). The paper restricts \\(\\alpha\\in[0,1]\\), so the image is a segment whose germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is a half-line. With \\(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R\\) it is task arithmetic's line.","base_point":"\\(\\alpha=0\\) returns the zero-shot model, and \\(d\\rho=\\tau\\ne0\\): regular (a boundary point when \\(\\alpha\\ge0\\)).","gauge":"Injective for \\(\\tau\\ne0\\).","covariance":"Interpolation commutes with every linear change of basis.","merge":"One model of the original architecture. HF's rescale_adapter_scale implements it for LoRA.","rebasing":"A fixed line: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"WiSE-FT is task arithmetic with one task vector and \\(\\lambda\\in[0,1]\\): the segment from the zero-shot point to the fine-tuned point. When only the head is tuned, the segment is also an output ensemble, because \\(f\\) is linear in the head. Otherwise its success rests on linear mode connectivity, a property of \\(\\Phi_f\\) along the segment rather than of the object.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=(\\lambda=\\alpha)","note":"\\(N=1\\), \\(\\tau=\\theta_1-\\theta_0\\), with \\(\\lambda\\) restricted to \\([0,1]\\)."},{"target":"concept-sliders","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=(\\alpha B_1,\\ A_1)","note":"When \\(\\theta_1-\\theta_0=B_1A_1\\) is a LoRA update (HF rescale_adapter_scale): the slider line through it, pointed at \\((0,A_1)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"model-soups","test":"O1","reason":"A soup averages fine-tuned endpoints with weights summing to 1 and never returns \\(\\theta_0\\). WiSE-FT's segment contains \\(\\theta_0\\) and every \\(\\theta_0+\\alpha\\tau\\)."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"linear-probing","name":"Linear probing","full_name":"Linear probing (head-only tuning on frozen pre-trained features)","paper_title":"DeCAF: A Deep Convolutional Activation Feature for Generic Visual Recognition","authors":"Donahue et al.","year":2013,"venue":"ICML 2014","arxiv":"1310.1531","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.1531","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"f(x)=V^{\\top}g_{B_0}(x)\\ (+\\,b),\\ \\ B_0\\ \\text{(entire pre-trained backbone) frozen},\\ \\ V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times K}\\ \\text{new linear head, trained by }\\min_{V}\\sum_i\\ell\\big(V^{\\top}g_{B_0}(x_i),y_i\\big)+\\lambda\\|V\\|^2\\ \\text{(convex: logistic regression or linear SVM)}","trainable_params":"dK\\ (+K)\\ \\text{for }d\\text{-dim features and }K\\text{ classes; nothing inside the backbone}","locus":"a new final linear classifier on top of frozen penultimate (or intermediate) features","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"The trivial selection: the trainable coordinate set is exactly the task head, so the backbone map \\(g_{B_0}\\) is held fixed (the identity on the backbone factor of parameter space); any PEFT method that also trains the head contains linear probing as its restriction to \\(\\Delta(\\text{backbone})=0\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the task head is learned and the backbone is untouched, so inference is the original network plus the head that any fine-tuning would need.","identity_at_init":"The backbone function is preserved exactly throughout; the head is new (random or zero initialized), so there is no pre-trained output to preserve.","key_idea":"Reuse a frozen pre-trained representation and fit only a linear classifier on it; the problem is convex and cheap and keeps the features intact (good out-of-distribution extrapolation), but it cannot adapt the features to the task.","results_note":"In DeCAF, a linear SVM on frozen DeCAF6 features (trained with dropout) reached 86.9% mean per-class accuracy on Caltech-101 with 30 images per class, 2.6 points above the prior multi-kernel state of the art (84.3%); Kumar et al. (2022) later found linear probing about 7 points better out of distribution than full fine-tuning on 10 shift datasets (66.2% vs 59.3%).","relations":[{"target":"bitfit","type":"special-case-of","note":"bias-only tuning plus the head reduces to linear probing when the bias update is zero"},{"target":"lp-ft","type":"competes-with","note":"LP-FT uses the linear-probing optimum as the head initialization of full fine-tuning"},{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"surgical fine-tuning's block choices include the final linear layer ('Last Layer'); selecting only that block is exactly linear probing"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.1531","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/donahue14.html","https://arxiv.org/html/2202.10054","https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01644"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Jeff Donahue","Yangqing Jia","Oriol Vinyals","Judy Hoffman","Ning Zhang","Eric Tzeng","Trevor Darrell"],"bibkey":"donahue2013decaf","bibtex":"@inproceedings{donahue2013decaf,\n  title = {{DeCAF: A Deep Convolutional Activation Feature for Generic Visual Recognition}},\n  author = {Jeff Donahue and Yangqing Jia and Oriol Vinyals and Judy Hoffman and Ning Zhang and Eric Tzeng and Trevor Darrell},\n  year = {2013},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1310.1531},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.1531}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The trainable coordinates are exactly the task head; the backbone is frozen.","shape":"\\(\\{\\theta_{B_0}\\}\\times\\mathbb R^{d\\times K}\\): linear, \\(d=dK\\,(+K)\\).","base_point":"The head is new (random or zero), so \\(\\theta_0\\) includes its initial value. Within the slice the point is regular.","covariance":"A dense head on frozen features: \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Nothing to merge beyond the head that every method needs.","rebasing":"Fixed block: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"Linear probing is the smallest non-trivial selective object: the head block alone. It is a linear sub-object of every method that also trains the head (A2, by freezing everything else). It is convex in \\(V\\), and the backbone factor stays at its base value, so the features are an absolute invariant.","entry_type":"baseline","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"bitfit","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=(\\Delta b=0,\\ V)","note":"BitFit with its usual trainable head, the biases frozen at their base value."},{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=V\\ \\ \\text{(block = last layer)}","note":"Surgical FT with the 'Last Layer' block."},{"target":"lp-ft","rule":"A5","h":"V\\mapsto(V,\\ q_0^{(2)})","note":"The one-stage scheme embeds as stage 1 of LP-FT, padded with stage 2's base value."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lp-ft","name":"LP-FT","full_name":"Linear Probing then Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Fine-Tuning can Distort Pretrained Features and Underperform Out-of-Distribution","authors":"Kumar et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2202.10054","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.10054","code_url":"https://github.com/AnanyaKumar/transfer_learning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"f_{v,B}(x)=v^{\\top}g_B(x);\\ \\ \\text{stage 1 (LP): }v_{\\mathrm{lp}}=\\arg\\min_{v}\\widehat L(v,B_0)\\ \\text{with }B=B_0\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\ \\text{stage 2 (FT): gradient descent on }\\widehat L(v,B)\\ \\text{over all of }(v,B)\\ \\text{starting from }(v_{\\mathrm{lp}},B_0)","trainable_params":"\\text{stage 1: }dK;\\ \\ \\text{stage 2: all parameters (full fine-tuning)}","locus":"first the linear head only, then every layer","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"A two-stage training schedule: the head-only (selective) optimum initializes full fine-tuning, which shrinks the head-alignment error \\(\\varphi^2=|(v_0^{\\top}v_\\star)^2-(v_\\star^{\\top}v_\\star)^2|\\) that otherwise drives large, ID-only feature updates; in the two-layer linear theory, features in directions outside the training span are then not distorted.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The output is a single fine-tuned network of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Stage 2 starts at the pre-trained backbone \\(B_0\\) with the linear-probing head \\(v_{lp}\\) instead of a random head, i.e. at the best frozen-feature predictor.","key_idea":"With a random head, full fine-tuning distorts pretrained features: gradients from the badly aligned head change the features of in-distribution directions while out-of-distribution directions stay put. Fitting the head by linear probing first removes most of this coupling, so the method gets fine-tuning's in-distribution accuracy and linear probing's robustness.","results_note":"Across 10 distribution-shift datasets, LP-FT was about 1% better in distribution and 10% better out of distribution than full fine-tuning, and changed features 10-100x less than fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"linear-probing","type":"builds-on","note":"stage 1 is linear probing"},{"target":"wise-ft","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent robust fine-tuning recipe (interpolation instead of initialization)"},{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"another selective remedy for fine-tuning under distribution shift"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.10054","https://arxiv.org/html/2202.10054","https://github.com/AnanyaKumar/transfer_learning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Ananya Kumar","Aditi Raghunathan","Robbie Jones","Tengyu Ma","Percy Liang"],"bibkey":"kumar2022fine","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kumar2022fine,\n  title = {{Fine-Tuning can Distort Pretrained Features and Underperform Out-of-Distribution}},\n  author = {Ananya Kumar and Aditi Raghunathan and Robbie Jones and Tengyu Ma and Percy Liang},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2202.10054},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.10054}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A two-stage rebasing scheme: linear probing at \\(\\theta_0\\), then Full FT re-pointed at \\((v_{\\rm lp},B_0)\\).","shape":"Stage 1 is the head subspace and stage 2 all of \\(\\Theta\\): a moving Lin shape.","base_point":"Each stage starts at its own base point; the second starts at the LP optimum rather than at a random head.","gauge":"Coordinate blocks: injective.","covariance":"Both stages are basis-free blocks.","merge":"Stage 2 writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"A schedule of two stages; the reach is \\(\\Theta\\) after stage 2."},"categorical_reading":"LP-FT does not change the object: its second stage is Full FT, the terminal object, with image \\(\\Theta\\). What changes is the base point on the head factor, \\((v_0,B_0)\\to(v_{\\rm lp},B_0)\\). The theory's head-alignment error \\(\\varphi^2\\) is a function of that base point, so the gain is a purely dynamical (Exposé III) effect that the image invariants cannot see.","entry_type":"recipe","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"wise-ft","test":"O2","reason":"LP-FT's second stage is Full FT, so its reach is all of \\(\\Theta\\) (\\(d=\\dim\\Theta\\)); WiSE-FT is a segment (\\(d=1\\)). In addition the base points differ: LP-FT's FT stage is pointed at \\((v_{\\rm lp},B_0)\\), WiSE-FT's family at the zero-shot point."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"ffa-lora","name":"FFA-LoRA","full_name":"Federated Freeze-A LoRA","paper_title":"Improving LoRA in Privacy-preserving Federated Learning","authors":"Sun et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2403.12313","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12313","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+BA_0,\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained (init }0),\\ A_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{random Gaussian, frozen and shared by all clients};\\ \\ \\text{FedAvg: }B^{+}=\\textstyle\\sum_kp_kB_k\\Rightarrow W_0+B^{+}A_0=W_0+\\sum_kp_kB_kA_0\\ \\text{(exact average of client updates, unlike }(\\sum_kp_kB_k)(\\sum_kp_kA_k));\\ \\ \\text{DP-SGD noise enters only as }\\xi_BA_0","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}","locus":"standard LoRA sites (attention and feed-forward linear layers in the RoBERTa experiments)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) ranges over the linear subspace \\(\\{BA_0:B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\}\\) with a fixed random row space, so \\(\\Delta W\\) is linear in the trainable parameters and client aggregation commutes with the parametrization (no cross terms \\(B_iA_j\\), no quadratic noise term \\(\\xi_B\\xi_A\\)).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA_0\\) is an ordinary matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\) exactly as in LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\); \\(A_0\\) is random Gaussian and never trained; the scaling factor \\(\\alpha\\) becomes unnecessary (the paper shows LoRA's trajectory tends to FFA-LoRA's as \\(\\alpha\\) goes to infinity).","key_idea":"LoRA's bilinear \\(BA\\) breaks federated averaging (the average of products is not the product of averages) and amplifies differential-privacy noise; freezing the random \\(A\\) makes the update linear in \\(B\\), so FedAvg is exact, DP noise is not squared, and communication is halved.","results_note":"In a 3-client heterogeneous federated setting with RoBERTa-Large, FFA-LoRA beat LoRA on GLUE tasks with and without DP (e.g. MNLI-m 78.8% vs 39.5% at \\(\\epsilon = 6\\)), and reached 17.12% vs 15.68% on GSM-8K with LLaMA-7B.","relations":[{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"identical parametrization to LoRA-FA v1 (frozen random A, trainable zero-initialized B); LoRA-FA is motivated by activation memory and its current version adds a projected B-gradient, FFA-LoRA by exact federated aggregation and DP"},{"target":"asylora","type":"competes-with","note":"same train-B / freeze-random-A design (AsyLoRA samples A with orthonormal rows rather than Gaussian), there justified by generalization bounds"},{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with A fixed at its random initialization"},{"target":"flora-federated","type":"competes-with","note":"both remove LoRA's FedAvg aggregation error (cross terms B_iA_j); FLoRA keeps A trainable and stacks client modules instead (the FLoRA paper does not compare against FFA-LoRA directly)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12313","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.12313"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Youbang Sun","Zitao Li","Yaliang Li","Bolin Ding"],"bibkey":"sun2024improving","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sun2024improving,\n  title = {{Improving LoRA in Privacy-preserving Federated Learning}},\n  author = {Youbang Sun and Zitao Li and Yaliang Li and Bolin Ding},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2403.12313},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.12313}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"LoRA with \\(A=A_0\\) frozen random Gaussian and shared by all clients: \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\) of Prop I.8.","shape":"\\(W_0+\\mathbb R^{m}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}A_0\\): linear, \\(d=mr\\).","covariance":"Random frozen \\(A_0\\): covariance in law (\\(GL\\times\\) frame).","rebasing":"Fixed \\(A_0\\): idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"FFA-LoRA is the one-sided method \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\), i.e. LoRA-FA v1, chosen for its degree. \\(\\rho\\) is linear in \\(B\\), so FedAvg commutes with \\(\\rho\\), \\((\\sum p_kB_k)A_0=\\sum p_kB_kA_0\\), and DP noise enters linearly. This is Prop V.4(b): exactness needs a shared factor, not just a fixed gauge.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_0)","note":"Freeze \\(A\\) at its base value: LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\)."},{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same parametrisation as LoRA-FA v1. LoRA-FA v2 changes only the cometric (a projected \\(B\\)-gradient)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mica","test":"O1","reason":"\\(\\{BA_0\\}\\) has a fixed row space and MiCA's \\(\\{U_{\\min}Y\\}\\) a fixed column space; neither contains the other."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"flora-federated","name":"FLoRA (federated)","full_name":"FLoRA: stacking-based federated fine-tuning with heterogeneous LoRA ranks","paper_title":"FLoRA: Federated Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with Heterogeneous Low-Rank Adaptations","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2409.05976","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05976","code_url":"https://github.com/ziyaow1010/FederatedLLM","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{client }k:\\ W_0+B_kA_k,\\ B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r_k},\\ A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_k\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{(heterogeneous }r_k,\\ \\text{re-initialized every round)};\\ \\ \\text{server: }A=p_1A_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus p_KA_K\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(\\sum_kr_k)\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{(row stacking)},\\ B=B_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus B_K\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times\\sum_kr_k}\\ \\text{(column stacking)};\\ \\ BA=\\textstyle\\sum_kp_kB_kA_k,\\ \\ p_k=|D_k|/\\sum_j|D_j|;\\ \\ \\text{clients then update }W_0\\leftarrow W_0+BA","trainable_params":"r_k(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{on client }k;\\ \\text{the broadcast global module has }(\\textstyle\\sum_kr_k)(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{entries}","locus":"LoRA on the self-attention layers only (following the original LoRA paper) of TinyLlama-1.1B, Llama-7B and Llama2-7B","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Direct sum of the clients' rank spaces \\(\\mathbb{R}^{r_1}\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\mathbb{R}^{r_K}\\): block concatenation of factors satisfies \\([B_1\\cdots B_K][A_1;\\dots;A_K]=\\sum_kB_kA_k\\) exactly, so the aggregate factors through \\(\\sum_kr_k\\) dimensions with no cross terms \\(B_iA_j\\) (which FedAvg of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) separately introduces) and with no constraint that ranks agree.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After each round the aggregated \\(BA\\) is added into the base weights, so the final model is the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Clients re-initialize their local LoRA each round (the official code uses PEFT's LoraConfig, i.e. \\(B = 0\\)), so each round starts exactly at the current global model.","key_idea":"Averaging LoRA's \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) separately does not average the products; stacking the client factors instead gives the exact weighted sum of updates and naturally supports clients with different ranks.","results_note":"On MMLU and MT-bench with TinyLlama, Llama and Llama2 fine-tuned federatedly on Dolly, Alpaca, Wizard and ShareGPT, FLoRA outperformed FedIT in homogeneous settings and zero-padding in heterogeneous-rank settings (zero-padded FedIT dropped to 7.97% MMLU for Llama on Alpaca).","relations":[{"target":"ffa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative cure for LoRA's FedAvg aggregation error (freeze A vs stack factors)"},{"target":"hetlora","type":"competes-with","note":"both support heterogeneous client ranks; HetLoRA zero-pads and averages factors with norm-based weights"},{"target":"lora-soups","type":"competes-with","note":"uses the same block-stacking identity sum_k B_k A_k = [B_1 ... B_K][A_1; ...; A_K], with data-size weights instead of learned per-layer coefficients"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"client modules are standard LoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05976","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.05976","https://github.com/ATP-1010/FederatedLLM","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=FLoRA%3A%20Federated%20Fine-Tuning%20Large%20Language%20Models%20with%20Heterogeneous%20Low-Rank%20Adaptations","https://github.com/ziyaow1010/FederatedLLM/blob/main/main.py","https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/28312c9491d60ed0c77f7fff4ad86dd1-Abstract-Conference.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Ziyao Wang","Zheyu Shen","Yexiao He","Guoheng Sun","Hongyi Wang","Lingjuan Lyu","Ang Li"],"bibkey":"wang2024flora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024flora,\n  title = {{FLoRA: Federated Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with Heterogeneous Low-Rank Adaptations}},\n  author = {Ziyao Wang and Zheyu Shen and Yexiao He and Guoheng Sun and Hongyi Wang and Lingjuan Lyu and Ang Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2409.05976},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05976}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Each round, clients train fresh LoRAs (ranks \\(r_k\\), \\(B=0\\)), the server stacks the factors, and every client merges \\(W_0\\leftarrow W_0+BA\\). This is a merge-and-restart scheme: ReLoRA across clients.","shape":"Per round the aggregate is \\(\\sum_kp_kB_kA_k\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le\\sum r_k}\\): a cone at the current base.","base_point":"Each round restarts at the apex (\\(B_k=0\\)).","gauge":"\\(\\prod_kGL_{r_k}\\) per round. Stacking acts on products, so the aggregation is gauge-invariant (Prop V.4(a)).","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme: \\(R_K=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(K\\sum_k r_k,m,n)}\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 1)."},"categorical_reading":"FLoRA is a federated ReLoRA. Each round is the \\(\\oplus\\) of the clients' LoRAs, \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1}\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_K}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{\\sum r_k}\\) by concatenation (Obs I.7), merged into the base before the next round. Stacking aggregates products, so it is exact and gauge-invariant, and heterogeneous ranks cost nothing. Its server step alone, with the client modules frozen, is the point \\(\\alpha=p\\) of CAT's coefficient family; that is a point of LoRA soups, not an arrow from FLoRA's object, whose factors are trainable.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_k,A_k)_k\\big)=\\big([p_1B_1\\cdots p_KB_K],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_K]\\big)\\ \\ \\text{per round}","note":"Stagewise into ReLoRA of rank \\(\\sum_kr_k\\), pointed at the stacked \\(A_0\\)'s; the pointing is standard iff their rows are independent."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hetlora","test":"O2","reason":"HetLoRA's zero-padded factor average has rank \\(\\le r_{\\max}\\); FLoRA's stacked aggregate reaches rank \\(\\sum_kr_k\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"hetlora","name":"HetLoRA","full_name":"Heterogeneous LoRA for federated fine-tuning","paper_title":"Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Fine-tuning of On-Device Foundation Models","authors":"Cho et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024","arxiv":"2401.06432","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.717/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{distribute by truncation: }B_k^{(t,0)}=\\overline B^{(t)}_{:,:r_k},\\ A_k^{(t,0)}=\\overline A^{(t)}_{:r_k,:};\\ \\ \\text{local: }\\min_{B_k,A_k}\\ \\ell(B_k,A_k\\mid W_0)+\\lambda\\,\\|B_{k,:,\\gamma r_k:r_k}\\|\\,\\|A_{k,\\gamma r_k:r_k,:}\\|\\ (\\gamma<1,\\ \\text{rank self-pruning});\\ \\ \\text{server (zero-pad to }r_{\\max}):\\ \\overline B^{(t+1)}=\\textstyle\\sum_kp_kB_k,\\ \\overline A^{(t+1)}=\\sum_kp_kA_k,\\ \\ p_k\\propto\\|B_kA_k\\|_F;\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r_k(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{on client }k\\ (r_{\\min}\\le r_k\\le r_{\\max})","locus":"LoRA on the self-attention layers only (following the original LoRA paper) of on-device PaLM 2 XXS and XS","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Nested rank truncations: client \\(k\\) sees the leading \\(r_k\\) columns of \\(B\\) and rows of \\(A\\) of a global rank-\\(r_{\\max}\\) pair; aggregation zero-pads and averages factors separately with weights proportional to \\(\\|B_kA_k\\|_F\\) (so cross terms remain), and a tail-norm penalty lets clients shrink \\(r_k\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The final global pair \\(BA\\) is a plain low-rank matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not specified in the paper; clients receive truncations of the current global LoRA pair, so with LoRA's standard \\(B = 0\\) initialization the first round starts exactly at \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"Let resource-limited clients train smaller-rank slices of one global LoRA, prune unnecessary rank locally, and weight the server average toward clients whose updates carry more signal.","results_note":"With PaLM 2 XXS/XS on multi-session chat (perplexity) and Reddit summarization (RougeL), HetLoRA beat homogeneous-rank LoRA and a reconstruct-then-SVD baseline, slightly below full federated fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"flora-federated","type":"competes-with","note":"FLoRA replaces zero-padded factor averaging with exact factor stacking"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"client modules are truncations of one LoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.06432","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.06432","https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.717/"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Yae Jee Cho","Luyang Liu","Zheng Xu","Aldi Fahrezi","Gauri Joshi"],"bibkey":"cho2024heterogeneous","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cho2024heterogeneous,\n  title = {{Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Fine-tuning of On-Device Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Yae Jee Cho and Luyang Liu and Zheng Xu and Aldi Fahrezi and Gauri Joshi},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2401.06432},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.717/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The global object is one \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\) pair \\((\\bar B,\\bar A)\\), kept across rounds; clients train its leading-\\(r_k\\) truncations. The federation is a training procedure on this object.","base_point":"Assumes LoRA's \\(B=0\\); the paper does not state the global init.","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) has \\(GL_{r_{\\max}}\\) fibres. Truncation to leading columns, however, is defined only up to the block-diagonal (Levi) subgroup of the flag \\(\\mathbb R^{r_{(1)}}\\subset\\cdots\\subset\\mathbb R^{r_{\\max}}\\) of client ranks. Averaging factors separately is bilinear (Prop V.4(b)), so the procedure is not a function of the adapters.","rebasing":"As LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"HetLoRA trains one \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\) through a filtration of truncations, as DyLoRA does, except that the truncation levels are the clients' ranks rather than sampled ones. Its aggregation \\((\\sum p_kB_k)(\\sum p_kA_k)\\) acts on factors, so it carries LoRA's bilinearity error even in a fixed gauge. FLoRA (stacking) and FFA-LoRA (frozen \\(A\\)) are the two exact repairs of Prop V.4(b).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The global pair is a \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\) point."},{"target":"dylora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same nested object, the truncation filtration of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\), with levels set by client capacity instead of sampled."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ffa-lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r})=r(m+n-r)>mr=d(\\mathrm{FFA})\\): HetLoRA's trainable \\(A\\) leaves the fixed row space."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"tinytl","name":"TinyTL","full_name":"Tiny Transfer Learning (bias tuning with lite residual modules)","paper_title":"TinyTL: Reduce Memory, Not Parameters for Efficient On-Device Learning","authors":"Cai et al.","year":2020,"venue":"NeurIPS 2020","arxiv":"2007.11622","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11622","code_url":"https://github.com/mit-han-lab/tinyml/tree/master/tinytl","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"a_{i+1}=\\mathcal F_{W}(a_i)+b+\\mathcal F_{w_r}\\big(a_i'\\big),\\ \\ a_i'=\\mathrm{reduce}(a_i)\\ \\text{(}2\\times2\\ \\text{average pooling)};\\ \\ W\\ \\text{frozen (8-bit)},\\ \\text{trained: biases }b,\\ \\text{lite residual weights }w_r\\ \\text{(group convolution, no }1\\times1\\ \\text{expansion, output bilinearly upsampled), classifier head};\\ \\ \\text{rationale: }\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial W}=a_i^{\\top}\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial a_{i+1}}\\ \\text{needs the stored activation }a_i,\\ \\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial b}=\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial a_{i+1}}\\ \\text{does not}","trainable_params":"\\textstyle\\sum_{\\text{layers}}d_{out}\\ \\text{(biases)}+\\sum_{\\text{blocks}}|w_r|\\ \\text{(lite residual group convs)}+\\text{head}","locus":"all bias vectors, one lite residual branch per mobile inverted-bottleneck block, and the classifier head (BatchNorm replaced by GroupNorm)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Selective translation of every layer's output (bias shift) plus a parallel low-resolution, narrow residual branch (downsample, group conv, upsample) per block; chosen to minimize stored activations rather than parameter count.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Bias updates fold in trivially, but the lite residual branches are extra nonlinear modules that stay at inference (in the default protocol they are already part of the pre-trained architecture).","identity_at_init":"By default the lite residual modules are present during pre-training and initialized from those weights, so transfer starts exactly at the pre-trained function; with randomly initialized lite residuals (TinyTL-RandomL+B) there is no such guarantee and accuracy drops.","key_idea":"On-device training memory is dominated by the activations needed for weight gradients, not by the parameter count; bias gradients need no stored activations, so freeze weights, train biases, and recover capacity with tiny residual branches whose activations are small.","results_note":"TinyTL saved up to 6.5x training memory relative to full fine-tuning with little accuracy loss, was up to 34.1% more accurate than last-layer tuning, and with feature-extractor adaptation saved 7.3-12.9x memory without accuracy loss.","relations":[{"target":"bitfit","type":"generalizes","note":"the TinyTL-B variant (biases + head) is bias-only tuning; TinyTL adds lite residual modules"},{"target":"linear-probing","type":"generalizes","note":"last-layer tuning (FT-Last) is the paper's baseline and the restriction of TinyTL to the head"},{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"both target activation memory rather than parameter count; LST moves the trainable path to a side network"},{"target":"residual-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"parallel convolutional residual adapters for vision transfer"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11622","https://arxiv.org/html/2007.11622","https://github.com/mit-han-lab/tinyml/tree/master/tinytl"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Han Cai","Chuang Gan","Ligeng Zhu","Song Han"],"bibkey":"cai2020tinytl","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cai2020tinytl,\n  title = {{TinyTL: Reduce Memory, Not Parameters for Efficient On-Device Learning}},\n  author = {Han Cai and Chuang Gan and Ligeng Zhu and Song Han},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2007.11622},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.11622}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"In the default protocol the lite residual branches are part of the pretrained architecture. TinyTL therefore trains a coordinate block (all biases, the lite-residual weights and the head) of that network. With randomly initialised lite residuals (TinyTL-RandomL+B) it would be an extension without an exact neutral point.","shape":"A coordinate subspace: biases \\(\\cup\\) lite-residual group convolutions \\(\\cup\\) head.","covariance":"The trainable block itself is basis-free (each selected box fully trainable within its architectural class; the group convolutions are the network's own structure). But the frozen boxes sit on the 8-bit quantisation grid, which commutes only with monomial changes of basis: Mon x Mon (grid), as for QLoRA.","merge":"Biases fold into their full-precision bias slots and nothing is added to a quantised box, so the grid is not left: M1 (not Mq). Nothing new at inference relative to the pretrained lite-residual network. Relative to a plain MobileNet, the lite branches would be non-linear extras (M∞).","rebasing":"Fixed block: idempotent.","base_point":"The paper applies 8-bit weight quantisation to the frozen weights during transfer (Sec. 4, Training Details; the catalog's 'W frozen (8-bit)'). So \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\kappa\\theta_0\\), with defect \\(e=\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0\\) (small; the paper reports a negligible accuracy drop), as for QLoRA. Within the slice at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) the trainable block (biases, pretrained lite residuals, head) is regular."},"categorical_reading":"TinyTL picks its coordinate block by the backprop cone rather than by parameter count. Biases need no stored activation (Obs IV.4), and the lite residual branches see downsampled, narrow activations. As an object it is BitFit \\(\\oplus\\) a selective block of small pretrained branches \\(\\oplus\\) the head.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"bitfit","test":"O2","reason":"The lite-residual weights add dimensions outside the bias space: \\(d(\\mathrm{TinyTL})>d(\\mathrm{BitFit})\\)."},{"target":"ladder-side-tuning","test":"O3","reason":"Both target activation memory, but LST's side network is an unpointed extension: its initial realisation is not the frozen network's. TinyTL is a coordinate block of the pretrained network pointed at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) (the 8-bit base), so the base points differ and no pointed arrow exists."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"autoprompt","name":"AutoPrompt","full_name":"AutoPrompt (gradient-guided discrete trigger-token search)","paper_title":"AutoPrompt: Eliciting Knowledge from Language Models with Automatically Generated Prompts","authors":"Shin et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EMNLP 2020","arxiv":"2010.15980","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.346/","code_url":"https://github.com/ucinlp/autoprompt","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"x_{\\text{prompt}}=\\lambda(x_{\\text{inp}},x_{\\text{trig}}),\\ \\ x_{\\text{trig}}=(x^{(1)}_{\\text{trig}},\\dots,x^{(\\ell)}_{\\text{trig}})\\in\\mathcal V^{\\ell}\\ \\text{(discrete tokens shared across inputs, initialized to [MASK])};\\ \\ p(y\\mid x_{\\text{prompt}})=\\textstyle\\sum_{w\\in\\mathcal V_y}p([\\mathrm{MASK}]=w\\mid x_{\\text{prompt}});\\ \\ \\mathcal V_{\\text{cand}}=\\operatorname{top-}k_{w\\in\\mathcal V}\\big[w_{\\text{in}}^{\\top}\\nabla\\log p(y\\mid x_{\\text{prompt}})\\big]\\ \\text{(gradient w.r.t. the embedding of }x^{(j)}_{\\text{trig}}),\\ \\text{keep the candidate with the highest }p(y\\mid\\cdot);\\ \\text{LM frozen}","trainable_params":"0\\ \\text{continuous parameters};\\ \\ell\\ \\text{discrete token choices (search space }|\\mathcal V|^{\\ell})\\ \\text{plus label-token sets }\\mathcal V_y","locus":"input token sequence (trigger tokens placed by a template around the input and the [MASK] slot)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"A discrete prompt: the prompt matrix is constrained to the vocabulary lattice \\(\\{E_w\\}^{\\ell}\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d}\\) and optimized by first-order (HotFlip-style) coordinate swaps; prompt tuning is the continuous relaxation of this set.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The trigger tokens lengthen every input, so inference is not at the original cost (though no new parameters exist).","identity_at_init":"Triggers start as [MASK] tokens; there is no identity guarantee, since the template itself changes the input.","key_idea":"Turn a task into masked-LM cloze form and search for a few shared discrete trigger tokens (and label words) with gradient-ranked candidate swaps, eliciting knowledge from a frozen MLM without any fine-tuning.","results_note":"Without fine-tuning, AutoPrompt-prompted RoBERTa-large reached 91.4% on SST-2; on LAMA fact retrieval, AutoPrompt prompts for BERT reached 43.3% precision@1 (7 trigger tokens) vs 34.1% for the best prior single prompt (LPAQA Top1).","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"AutoPrompt restricts the soft prompt to rows of the embedding matrix; prompt tuning is its continuous relaxation"},{"target":"warp","type":"competes-with","note":"WARP replaced the discrete search with continuous prompt and verbalizer embeddings"},{"target":"p-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"continuous-prompt successor evaluated on the same LAMA probing setup"},{"target":"black-box-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"both tune only the prompt of a frozen LM; AutoPrompt needs gradients, BBT is derivative-free"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15980","https://arxiv.org/html/2010.15980","https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.346/","https://github.com/ucinlp/autoprompt"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"authors_full":["Taylor Shin","Yasaman Razeghi","Robert L. Logan","Eric Wallace","Sameer Singh"],"bibkey":"shin2020autoprompt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shin2020autoprompt,\n  title = {{AutoPrompt: Eliciting Knowledge from Language Models with Automatically Generated Prompts}},\n  author = {Taylor Shin and Yasaman Razeghi and Robert L. Logan and Eric Wallace and Sameer Singh},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2010.15980},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.346/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Discrete trigger tokens placed by a template around the input. The template itself changes the input, so no choice of triggers reproduces \\(f\\).","shape":"\\(Q=\\mathcal V^\\ell\\) is finite, so \\(d(M)=0\\): a finite subset of the vocabulary lattice \\(\\{E_w\\}^\\ell\\) on the input wire. Function-level relative to \\(\\Theta\\).","base_point":"Triggers start at [MASK], and the template already moves the output.","gauge":"\\(Q\\) is discrete: no continuous fibre.","covariance":"The candidate set is the rows of the embedding matrix, which move with every linear change of the embedding basis. On the vocabulary side only token permutations act.","merge":"Trigger tokens lengthen every input.","rebasing":"No weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"AutoPrompt is prompt tuning restricted to the vocabulary lattice: a 0-dimensional object whose points map into prompt tuning's \\(Q\\) by \\(w\\mapsto E_w\\). Its HotFlip search ranks swaps by the first-order term \\(E_w^\\top\\nabla\\). It uses the cotangent of the continuous relaxation to move on a discrete set.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(w_1,\\dots,w_\\ell)=(E_{w_1},\\dots,E_{w_\\ell})","note":"A smooth map from the finite set \\(\\mathcal V^\\ell\\) into prompt tuning at the template's trigger slots, pointed at \\(h([\\mathrm{MASK}]^\\ell)\\): a monomorphism of the unpointed slice."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"black-box-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"BBT reaches the random affine subspace \\(p_0+\\mathrm{col}A\\) of dimension \\(k\\ll\\ell d\\). Apart from \\(p_0\\) itself (when \\(p_0\\) is drawn from the vocabulary), it generically contains no tuple of vocabulary rows, which are exactly AutoPrompt's points."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"lora","name":"LoRA","full_name":"Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Hu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2106.09685","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685","code_url":"https://github.com/microsoft/LoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig","formula_latex":"h = W_0 x + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r} B A x,\\quad W = W_0 + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r} B A,\\quad B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen},\\ A,B \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (PEFT also: Embedding, Conv1d/2d/3d, MultiheadAttention, raw nn.Parameter e.g. MoE experts via target_parameters); originally \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\), commonly 'all-linear'","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space: \\(\\Delta W = BA\\), \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W \\le r\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) is a dense \\(d_{out} \\times d_{in}\\) matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\) after training (merge_and_unload), giving zero inference overhead.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (\\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform in PEFT, Gaussian in the paper; init_lora_weights='gaussian' keeps \\(B = 0\\)); scaling \\(\\alpha/r\\).","key_idea":"Fine-tuning updates have low intrinsic rank, so freeze \\(W_0\\) and learn only a rank-\\(r\\) factorization of the update.","results_note":"Paper reports on-par or better quality than full fine-tuning on RoBERTa, DeBERTa, GPT-2 and GPT-3 175B, with 10,000x fewer trainable parameters and 3x less GPU memory for GPT-3.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"competes-with","note":"Mergeable alternative to serial bottleneck adapters, so no extra inference latency."},{"target":"adalora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA is AdaLoRA with a fixed per-matrix rank, no singular-value pruning and no orthogonality penalty (BA and P Lambda Q span the same rank-r set)."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"compared as a baseline in the LoRA paper"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2106.09685","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Edward J. Hu","Yelong Shen","Phillip Wallis","Zeyuan Allen-Zhu","Yuanzhi Li","Shean Wang","Lu Wang","Weizhu Chen"],"bibkey":"hu2021lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hu2021lora,\n  title = {{LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Edward J. Hu and Yelong Shen and Phillip Wallis and Zeyuan Allen-Zhu and Yuanzhi Li and Shean Wang and Lu Wang and Weizhu Chen},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2106.09685},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), of dimension \\(r(m+n-r)\\) and maximal rank \\(r\\).","base_point":"\\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum (\\(B_0=0\\), \\(\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r\\)): \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\), deficit \\(r(n-r)\\) (Thm II.2). HF's embedding default is \\(\\mathrm T_B\\); `init_lora_weights=\"orthogonal\"` lies outside the three strata.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) acting by \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\): the \\(\\mathfrak{so}_r\\) half gives isometries, the \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_r\\) half the charges \\(\\Phi\\).","rebasing":"\\(R_K=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(Kr,m,n)}\\); all of \\(\\Theta\\) at \\(K=\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA is the determinantal cone \\(\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) pointed at its apex: the reference object of the slice, with gauge \\(GL_r\\) on the internal wire and merging as the canonical map \\(\\rho\\) to the terminal object. Its \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) pointings are classified by \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\in\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B\\ 0],\\,[A;\\,a_0])","note":"LoRA\\(_r\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA\\(_{r+1}\\) (the rank filtration); \\(\\mathrm T_A\\)-pointed when \\(a_0\\notin\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\)."},{"target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big((\\lVert(W_0+sBA)_{i,:}\\rVert)_i,\\ B,\\ A\\big)","note":"DoRA with the magnitude set to the current row norms reproduces LoRA. Pointed (\\(\\mu_0\\) = row norms of \\(W_0\\)); smooth wherever no row of \\(W_0+sBA\\) vanishes."},{"target":"relora","rule":"A5","h":"q\\mapsto(q,\\,q_0^{(2)},\\dots,q_0^{(K)})","note":"One stage embeds into \\(K\\) stages of the rebasing scheme."},{"target":"peanut","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([M,\\,-M],\\ [B^\\top;\\,-B^\\top]\\big),\\quad M=(W_0^{+})^\\top A^\\top","note":"LoRA\\(_r\\to\\) PEANuT\\(_{2r}\\) via \\(\\sigma(x)-\\sigma(-x)=x\\), which holds for ReLU, GELU and SiLU (so the arrow is smooth for the latter two); needs \\(W_0\\) of full column rank (\\(M^\\top W_0=A\\)). Pointed at \\(\\Theta_1=[M_0,-M_0]\\), \\(\\Theta_2=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O3","reason":"Type I apex against Type II smooth point: the images \\(\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) and \\((\\theta_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) are incomparable (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"The images agree (\\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\)), but \\(S_1(\\mathrm{LoRA})=\\{XA_0\\}\\) has dimension \\(mr\\) and \\(S_1(\\mathrm{AdaLoRA})=\\mathrm{span}\\{p_iq_i^\\top\\}\\) has dimension \\(r\\). LoRA is a special case of AdaLoRA only as an unpointed object."},{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r)=r(m+n-r)>mr=d(\\text{LoRA-FA})\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"adalora","name":"AdaLoRA","full_name":"Adaptive Budget Allocation for LoRA","paper_title":"AdaLoRA: Adaptive Budget Allocation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2303.10512","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10512","code_url":"https://github.com/QingruZhang/AdaLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"AdaLoraConfig(init_r, target_r, tinit, tfinal, deltaT, beta1, beta2, orth_reg_weight, total_step)","formula_latex":"W=W_0+P\\Lambda Q,\\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda_i),\\ Q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\quad R(P,Q)=\\lVert P^\\top P-I\\rVert_F^2+\\lVert QQ^\\top-I\\rVert_F^2;\\quad S_{k,i}=s(\\lambda_{k,i})+\\tfrac1{d_{out}}\\textstyle\\sum_js(P_{k,ji})+\\tfrac1{d_{in}}\\sum_js(Q_{k,ij}),\\ s=\\bar I\\cdot\\bar U,\\ I(w)=|w\\,\\nabla_w\\mathcal{L}|;\\quad \\lambda_{k,i}\\leftarrow0\\ \\text{unless }S_{k,i}\\in\\text{top-}b^{(t)}","trainable_params":"r_{init}(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{per matrix during training},\\ r_{init}=b^{(0)}/n\\ (\\text{masked triplets are kept and can be reactivated});\\ \\ \\text{retained after pruning: }\\textstyle\\sum_k r_k(d_{in}+d_{out}+1),\\ \\sum_k r_k=b^{(T)}","locus":"any linear layer; budget shared across all adapted matrices","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"SVD-shaped update \\(\\Delta W=P\\Lambda Q\\) with soft-orthogonal \\(P,Q\\) and a diagonal of singular values that is masked (pruned) by a global importance ranking under a cubic budget schedule","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(P\\Lambda Q\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Lambda=0\\) (\\(P,Q\\) random Gaussian); importance scores are EMA-smoothed sensitivity times uncertainty.","key_idea":"Parameterize each update in SVD form so that rank can be reduced by zeroing singular values without expensive SVDs, and move rank budget to the matrices whose triplets are most important for the loss.","results_note":"Reported better or on-par GLUE results vs LoRA at all budgets with DeBERTaV3-base and a 1.2% F1 gain on SQuAD 2.0 with under 0.1% trainable parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"fixed uniform-rank LoRA is AdaLoRA with no pruning"},{"target":"increlora","type":"dual-to","note":"IncreLoRA grows ranks with the same style of importance score instead of pruning them"},{"target":"sora","type":"competes-with","note":"SoRA prunes ranks with a proximal L1 gate instead of sensitivity scores"},{"target":"elalora","type":"competes-with","note":"ElaLoRA adds rank expansion to AdaLoRA-style pruning and keeps the total budget fixed, whereas AdaLoRA shrinks it from b^{(0)} to b^{(T)}"},{"target":"eva","type":"competes-with","note":"Both reallocate ranks across layers (EVA from activation variance, AdaLoRA from importance scores)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10512","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.10512","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/adalora/config.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/adalora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/adalora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Qingru Zhang","Minshuo Chen","Alexander Bukharin","Nikos Karampatziakis","Pengcheng He","Yu Cheng","Weizhu Chen","Tuo Zhao"],"bibkey":"zhang2023adalora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023adalora,\n  title = {{AdaLoRA: Adaptive Budget Allocation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Qingru Zhang and Minshuo Chen and Alexander Bukharin and Nikos Karampatziakis and Pengcheng He and Yu Cheng and Weizhu Chen and Tuo Zhao},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2303.10512},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10512}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0+\\{P\\Lambda Q\\}=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) per matrix. The budget mask makes the trained object a union of cones of ranks \\(r_k\\) with \\(\\sum_kr_k=b^{(T)}\\).","base_point":"\\(\\Lambda_0=0\\) is a zero gate: \\(S_1=\\mathrm{span}\\{p_iq_i^\\top\\}\\) has dimension \\(r\\) (checked), so the deficit is \\(r(m+n-r)-r\\).","gauge":"The generic fibre has dimension \\(r(r+1)\\) (checked): two linear torus edges plus absorption of \\(\\Lambda\\), i.e. a non-linear action of \\(GL_r\\times T_r\\) (\\(P\\mapsto P\\Lambda g^{-1}\\Lambda^{-1}\\), \\(Q\\mapsto gQ\\), plus \\(\\Lambda\\)-scalings). This is the table's \"torus (+ absorption)\"; the torus alone is not locally transitive on fibres for \\(r\\ge2\\).","rebasing":"Converges to \\(\\Theta\\) like LoRA, with the mask re-applied in each cycle."},"categorical_reading":"AdaLoRA charts LoRA's cone by an SVD-shaped triple whose diagonal \\(\\Lambda\\) is a zero gate, so it starts at an apex with only \\(r\\) first-order directions, and its budget mask moves between nested sub-cones (a union over rank allocations). It is simulated by LoRA via \\(h=(P\\Lambda,Q)\\), but as pointed objects LoRA does not map into it.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,Q_0)\\)."},{"target":"polar","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=\\big(P(P^\\top P)^{-1/2},\\ (P^\\top P)^{1/2}\\Lambda(QQ^\\top)^{1/2},\\ Q^\\top(QQ^\\top)^{-1/2}\\big)","note":"Polar factors of \\(P\\) and \\(Q^\\top\\); smooth where \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) have full rank; pointed at PoLAR with \\(\\Theta_0=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Both are SVD-shaped, but AdaLoRA's cone has vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) (\\(\\Lambda_0=0\\)) while PiSSA's is translated to \\(W_0-[W_0]_r\\): the images are incomparable (Type I against Type II)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"adamss","name":"AdaMSS","full_name":"Adaptive Multi-Subspace Approach for PEFT","paper_title":"AdaMSS: Adaptive Multi-Subspace Approach for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zheng et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"","url":"https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1c85c302ece39939c1b334c78f7ee1b8-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"https://github.com/jzheng20/AdaMSS","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"AdamssConfig(r, num_subspaces, subspace_rank, use_asa=True)","formula_latex":"\\hat W_0=U_{[:,1:R]}S_{[1:R]}V_{[:,1:R]}^\\top,\\ \\text{output units clustered into }K\\text{ subspaces }\\mathcal{S}_k;\\quad \\Delta W_{\\mathcal{S}_k,:}=C_k\\,B_k\\,\\tilde A_k,\\ \\tilde A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{R_k\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen orthonormal},\\ B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_k\\times R_k}\\ \\text{(QR-orthogonal init)},\\ C_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|\\mathcal{S}_k|\\times r_k},\\ C_k=0;\\ \\text{ASA keeps the top-}K_t\\text{ blocks by AdaLoRA-style importance}","trainable_params":"\\textstyle\\sum_{k}r_k\\,(R_k+|\\mathcal{S}_k|)\\ \\text{over the active subspaces}","locus":"linear layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"block-structured low-rank update: the principal rank-\\(R\\) part of \\(W_0\\) is segmented (low-rank-representation clustering) into \\(K\\) subspaces, each receiving its own tiny low-rank update \\(A_kB_kC_k\\); whole subspaces are frozen by adaptive subspace allocation","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The scattered block updates form a dense matrix of the original shape (HF PEFT implements merge).","identity_at_init":"\\(C_k=0\\) (\\(W_0\\) untouched); frozen bases come from a data-free truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\) plus subspace clustering.","key_idea":"The principal row space of a pretrained weight decomposes into several smaller subspaces; adapting each with its own small rank and pruning irrelevant subspaces gives a tighter generalization bound and far fewer parameters than a single-subspace adapter like PiSSA.","results_note":"Reported +4.7% average accuracy over LoRA on seven ViT-Large tasks with 15.4% of its parameters, and +7% over PiSSA on RoBERTa-Large with about 94% fewer trainable parameters.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"builds-on","note":"starts from the principal rank-R SVD part of W_0, then segments it"},{"target":"adalora","type":"combines","note":"uses AdaLoRA's sensitivity-uncertainty importance and cubic schedule at the level of subspaces"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"Both start from the SVD of W_0; AdaMSS splits it into multiple subspaces."},{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"Adaptive Subspace Allocation prunes subspaces via importance scores, analogous to AdaLoRA's budget allocation."}],"sources":["https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1c85c302ece39939c1b334c78f7ee1b8-Abstract-Conference.html","https://neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/1c85c302ece39939c1b334c78f7ee1b8-Paper-Conference.pdf","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/adamss/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/adamss.md","https://openreview.net/forum?id=8ZdWmpYxT0","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/adamss","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/adamss","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"zheng2025adamss","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zheng2025adamss,\n  title = {{AdaMSS: Adaptive Multi-Subspace Approach for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zheng and others},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  url = {https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/1c85c302ece39939c1b334c78f7ee1b8-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\bigoplus_k\\{E_{\\mathcal S_k}C_kB_k\\tilde A_k\\}\\): a product of determinantal cones (rank \\(\\le r_k\\) on \\(|\\mathcal S_k|\\times R_k\\)) transported by the frozen frames \\(\\tilde A_k\\), of dimension \\(\\sum_kr_k(|\\mathcal S_k|+R_k-r_k)\\) (checked).","base_point":"\\(C_k=0\\) is a zero gate: \\(S_1=\\bigoplus_k\\{E_{\\mathcal S_k}XB_{k,0}\\tilde A_k\\}\\) has dimension \\(\\sum_k|\\mathcal S_k|r_k\\) (checked). An apex, with no residual split.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_kGL_{r_k}\\) acting by \\((C_kg_k^{-1},g_kB_k)\\).","covariance":"The input frames come from the truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\) (\\(CO(n)\\)). The clustering of output units reduces the output side to scalars times signed permutations, a subgroup of CO×CO.","rebasing":"With the frames \\(\\tilde A_k\\) and the clusters frozen, cycles converge to the linear span \\(\\bigoplus_k\\mathbb R^{\\mathcal S_k}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}\\,\\tilde A_k\\) (dimension \\(\\sum_k|\\mathcal S_k|R_k\\)), not to \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"AdaMSS is a \\(\\oplus\\)-word of \\(K\\) small LoRAs, each living on an output block \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) and transported into a frozen principal-subspace frame of \\(W_0\\), with an AdaLoRA-style mask over whole summands. Although it starts from PiSSA's SVD, it keeps \\(\\theta_0\\) as the residual and starts at an apex (Type I), so it is incomparable with PiSSA.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((C_k,B_k)_k)=\\big([E_{\\mathcal S_1}C_1\\cdots E_{\\mathcal S_K}C_K],\\ [B_1\\tilde A_1;\\dots;B_K\\tilde A_K]\\big)","note":"Factors through LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_k}\\) on \\(W_0\\), pointed at \\((0,[B_{1,0}\\tilde A_1;\\dots;B_{K,0}\\tilde A_K])\\). This is a \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point when the stacked rows are independent; with HF's single shared \\(R\\times d_{in}\\) frame that needs \\(\\sum_kr_k\\le R\\). Otherwise it is a degenerate \\(B_0=0\\) pointing outside the three strata (Thm II.2(e)); the arrow is still pointed."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O3","reason":"Same SVD frames, but AdaMSS is zero-init (apex, vertex \\(\\theta_0\\)) while PiSSA is a split (smooth point, vertex \\(W_0-[W_0]_r\\)): different strata."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"beft","name":"BEFT","full_name":"Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models in Low-Data Regimes","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ACL 2026","arxiv":"2509.15974","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1799/","code_url":"https://github.com/whubaichuan/BEFT","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"BeftConfig","formula_latex":"h = W_v x + b_v + \\Delta b_v,\\ \\ \\Delta b_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}} \\text{ trained, all weights frozen};\\ \\ a(V+\\mathbf{1}b_v^\\top)=aV+\\mathbf{1}b_v^\\top \\text{ (rows of } a \\text{ sum to 1)}","trainable_params":"d_{out} \\text{ per value projection } (\\approx 0.01\\% \\text{ of parameters})","locus":"bias of the value projection \\(W_v\\) in every attention block (bias added, zero-initialized, for bias-free LLMs)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"additive shift of the value-projection output; because attention rows sum to one, the shift passes straight to the attention output","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Delta b_v\\) adds to an existing value-projection bias after training. PEFT raises an error when merging into a bias-free layer (for example LLaMA-style projections), so merging is only possible when the base layer has a bias.","identity_at_init":"PEFT adds a separate zero-initialized \\(\\Delta b_v\\) (init_weights=True), so the function is unchanged at init.","key_idea":"Among q/k/v biases, fine-tuning only the value bias \\(b_v\\) gives the best accuracy per parameter in low-data regimes.","results_note":"Paper reports \\(b_v\\) fine-tuning consistently beating \\(b_q\\) and \\(b_k\\) fine-tuning across encoder- and decoder-only LLMs up to 6.7B, with about 9x fewer parameters than tuning all biases.","relations":[{"target":"bitfit","type":"special-case-of","note":"Restricts BitFit to a single bias term (value projection)."},{"target":"ia3","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are vector-sized interventions on attention; IA3 scales, BEFT shifts."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"The paper also applies LoRA/VeRA/DoRA-style reparameterizations to the bias terms."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.15974","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.15974","https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1799/","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/beft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/beft"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","selective"],"authors_full":["Baichuan Huang","Ananth Balashankar","Amir Aminifar"],"bibkey":"huang2025beft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{huang2025beft,\n  title = {{BEFT: Bias-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Language Models in Low-Data Regimes}},\n  author = {Baichuan Huang and Ananth Balashankar and Amir Aminifar},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2509.15974},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1799/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Edits the existing value-projection bias; in bias-free LLMs HF adds a new zero bias, which is a pointed extension of that box.","covariance":"As BitFit in the table: the bias set is a full vector space, \\(b\\mapsto gb\\).","merge":"Fuses into \\(b_v\\) when the layer has a bias slot (HF raises on bias-free layers). By row-stochasticity, \\(aV+\\mathbf 1\\Delta b_v^T\\), it also equals an o_proj bias shift \\(W_O\\Delta b_v\\)."},"categorical_reading":"A one-bias coordinate subspace of BitFit, hence an A2 sub-object of it, translating the value projection; since attention rows sum to one it equals a constant shift of the attention output. It is Lin, regular and idempotent.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"bitfit","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Delta b_v)=\\big(\\Delta b_v,\\,0_{\\text{other biases}}\\big)","note":"Freezing every other bias at its pretrained value."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"(IA)^3 scales l_v⊙(W_vx+b_v) and contains no translation independent of x (unless rows of W_v vanish), so a bias shift lies outside its image."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"boft","name":"BOFT","full_name":"Butterfly Orthogonal Finetuning","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Butterfly Factorization","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2311.06243","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06243","code_url":"https://github.com/wy1iu/butterfly-oft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"BOFTConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 R(m,b)^{\\top},\\quad R(m,b)=\\prod_{i=1}^{m}\\tilde{B}^{b}_{(d_{in},i)},\\ \\big(\\tilde{B}^{b}_{(d_{in},i)}\\big)^{\\top}\\tilde{B}^{b}_{(d_{in},i)}=I,\\ \\text{each factor a permuted block-diagonal matrix of } b\\times b \\text{ Cayley-parameterized orthogonal blocks};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,m\\,(b-1)\\,d_{in}\\quad(\\mathcal{O}(d_{in}\\log d_{in})\\ \\text{for } b=2,\\ m=\\log_2 d_{in})","locus":"any linear / conv layer (attention and MLP of ViTs, LLMs, SAM, Stable Diffusion); acts on the input side of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"right action of a product of \\(m\\) sparse butterfly factors, each a permutation conjugate of the block-diagonal subgroup \\(O(b)^{d/b}\\); the product is a dense orthogonal matrix. The paper frames this as information transmission on a butterfly grid graph (a Cooley-Tukey style factorization of an \\(O(d)\\) element).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The product \\(R(m,b)\\) is a fixed orthogonal matrix after training, so \\(W_0 R(m,b)^T\\) can be precomputed.","identity_at_init":"All skew-symmetric generators start at 0, so every butterfly factor is the identity and \\(R = I\\).","key_idea":"Build a dense orthogonal matrix as a product of sparse butterfly-structured orthogonal factors. OFT then gets dense rotations from \\(O(d \\log d)\\) parameters. Block-diagonal OFT is the \\(m=1\\) case.","results_note":"Reports higher scores than LoRA and OFT on GLUE (DeBERTaV3), the best VTAB-1K average in its table (77.9% for BOFT \\(m=2\\), \\(b=8\\) with DINOv2-large), better Llama-2-7B MMLU and math (GSM8K/MATH) results, HQ-SAM-level segmentation with 3% of its parameters, and better controllable and subject-driven Stable Diffusion generation.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"generalizes","note":"BOFT(1,b) is block-diagonal OFT; BOFT(1,d) is full OFT"},{"target":"goft","type":"competes-with","note":"both build O(d) elements from log-depth sparse factors; GOFT uses one Givens angle per pair and fewer pairs per layer"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"multiplicative sparse factorization in place of low-rank addition"},{"target":"hra","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative structured parameterization of an orthogonal multiplicative update."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06243","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.06243","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/boft/config.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/boft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/boft/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Weiyang Liu","Zeju Qiu","Yao Feng","Yuliang Xiu","Yuxuan Xue","Longhui Yu","Haiwen Feng","Zhen Liu","Juyeon Heo","Songyou Peng","Yandong Wen","Michael J. Black","Adrian Weller","Bernhard Schölkopf"],"bibkey":"liu2023parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2023parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Orthogonal Finetuning via Butterfly Factorization}},\n  author = {Weiyang Liu and Zeju Qiu and Yao Feng and Yuliang Xiu and Yuxuan Xue and Longhui Yu and Haiwen Feng and Zhen Liu and Juyeon Heo and Songyou Peng and Yandong Wen and Michael J. Black and Adrian Weller and Bernhard Schölkopf},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2311.06243},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.06243}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Sat","base_point":"regular","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"HF: \\(W=\\mathrm{diag}(s)W_0R^\\top\\) with \\(R\\) a product of butterfly block-Cayley factors: a torus saturation of an orthogonal-orbit method (VIII.B).","base_point":"Regular when the factors' plane sets are disjoint (\\(b = 2\\)) and at the HF default factor 1 (block OFT times a scale). For two factors it is regular even when blocks overlap: \\(S_1\\) contains \\(\\mathfrak g_1+\\mathfrak g_2\\) and the generic fibre is \\(G_1\\cap G_2\\) (Jacobian \\(29 = 29\\) at \\(d=8\\), \\(b=4\\), with scale). Deeper overlapping products were not checked.","gauge":"VIII.B 'inter-factor': elements of \\(G_i\\cap G_{i+1}\\) slide between consecutive factors and act non-linearly on the Cayley generators; trivial at the HF default factor 1 and for \\(b=2\\). Check: fibre \\(4 = \\dim SO(2)^4=G_1\\cap G_2\\) at \\(d=8\\), \\(b=4\\), two factors; fibre 0 for \\(b=2\\) at full depth.","covariance":"VIII.B: \\(\\mathrm{Mon} \\times \\Pi\\). \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\) on the output side from boft_s; the butterfly blocks are imposed on the input side. axes.json also lists 'BOFT as in HF PEFT' under MonxGL, which is wrong on the input side.","rebasing":"Iterated BOFT reaches \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^m)\\cdot W_0\\cdot P\\big(\\prod SO(b\\,2^{m'-1})\\big)P^\\top\\) for a fixed permutation \\(P\\), and, for injective \\(W_0\\), \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^m)\\cdot W_0\\cdot SO(n)\\) only at full depth (Thm V.3 cor. 4)."},"categorical_reading":"BOFT (HF) is the torus saturation \\(\\mathrm{Diag}_m\\cdot\\mathrm{Im}(\\text{butterfly OFT})\\): an orthogonal action on the input side assembled from sparse block factors, plus a free output scale that weakens the Gram invariant to \\(|\\cos|\\) between neurons (Cor II.6 remarks). Butterfly permutations connect the block hypergraph, and the rebasing closure is the group generated by its components.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"boft","rule":"A2","h":"(Q_1,\\dots,Q_{m'},s)\\mapsto(Q_1,\\dots,Q_{m'},0,s)","note":"BOFT with m' butterfly factors into m'+1 factors of the same block size, the extra factor frozen at its base value R = I."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"boft_s rescales neurons, while exact OFT preserves K_out = W W^T (Cor II.6)."},{"target":"goft","test":"O2","reason":"Full-depth BOFT(log2 d, 2) has (d/2) log2 d angles plus a scale, so d(BOFT) > d(GOFT) = d - 1."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Sat"},{"id":"c3a","name":"C3A","full_name":"Circular Convolution Adaptation","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Circular Convolution","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2025 (Findings)","arxiv":"2407.19342","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.102/","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"C3AConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\Delta W,\\quad \\Delta W = \\big[\\,\\mathcal{C}(w_{ij})\\,\\big]_{i\\le d_{out}/b,\\ j\\le d_{in}/b},\\ \\mathcal{C}(w)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times b}\\ \\text{circulant with first row } w\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b}\\ (\\text{trained});\\ \\mathcal{C}(w) = F\\,\\mathrm{diag}\\big(\\tfrac{1}{b}Fw\\big)F^{-1}\\ (\\text{paper's convention}),\\ \\text{so } \\Delta W x \\text{ is computed blockwise with FFTs};\\ \\text{HF PEFT additionally divides } \\Delta W \\text{ by } d_{in}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{d_{in}\\,d_{out}}{b}","locus":"any linear layer whose dimensions are divisible by the block size \\(b\\) (paper: RoBERTa, LLaMA-3-8B, ViT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"block-circulant update: each \\(b\\times b\\) block is a circulant matrix (simultaneously diagonalised by the DFT), so rank can reach \\(\\min(d_{in}, d_{out})\\) independently of the parameter count","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The block-circulant matrix can be materialised and added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"HF default initialises the kernels with xavier_uniform (non-zero, so not an exact identity); init_weights=True sets them to zero (no-op)","key_idea":"Use circular convolution (a circulant matrix) as the update: rank is decoupled from parameter count, and FFTs make forward and backward passes fast and memory-light; block-circulant structure handles non-square weights and tunes the budget.","results_note":"Consistently outperforms LoRA and its variants across fine-tuning tasks (e.g. on LLaMA-3-8B) while being efficient in compute and memory.","relations":[{"target":"fourierft","type":"competes-with","note":"both use the DFT; C3A makes Delta W DFT-diagonalisable rather than sparse in the 2-D spectrum"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"high-rank update with LoRA-like efficiency"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"positioned as more efficient than random-projection methods"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.19342","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.19342","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/c3a/config.py","https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.102/","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/c3a","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/c3a/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.16.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.19342","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/c3a.md","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2407.19342","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/c3a/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape","sharing"],"authors_full":["Aochuan Chen","Jiashun Cheng","Zijing Liu","Ziqi Gao","Fugee Tsung","Yu Li","Jia Li"],"bibkey":"chen2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Circular Convolution}},\n  author = {Aochuan Chen and Jiashun Cheng and Zijing Liu and Ziqi Gao and Fugee Tsung and Yu Li and Jia Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2025},\n  eprint = {2407.19342},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.102/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Block-circulant \\(\\Delta W\\), linear and injective in the kernels: \\(d=mn/b\\), rank up to \\(\\min(m,n)\\).","base_point":"Regular for init_weights=True (zero kernels); HF default xavier init leaves a small defect (the table classifies the exactly pointed variant).","covariance":"Imposed cyclic order \\(\\Pi_{\\rm cyc}\\) in every block: a neuron permutation does not preserve it.","rebasing":"Fixed block size and frame: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"C3A is a linear method: a coordinate subspace in the block-circulant frame (a DFT transport of a mask, Prop II.13), so its rank is decoupled from \\(|M|=mn/b\\) while its covariance is the imposed cyclic order \\(\\Pi_{\\rm cyc}\\). Its blocks include odd circulants, which FourierFT's point-symmetric image never reaches.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"Every FourierFT update satisfies DeltaW_{j,l}=DeltaW_{-j,-l}; a circulant block c(j-l) with odd part c(k)!=c(-k) violates it (checked numerically)."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Max rank min(m,n) independent of |M|, above r at matched budgets."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"cartridges","name":"Cartridges","full_name":"Cartridges (trained KV-cache prefixes via self-study)","paper_title":"Cartridges: Lightweight and general-purpose long context representations via self-study","authors":"Eyuboglu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2506.06266","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06266","code_url":"https://github.com/HazyResearch/cartridges","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"CartridgeConfig","formula_latex":"\\forall l:\\ K_l\\leftarrow[Z_k^{(l)};K_l],\\ V_l\\leftarrow[Z_v^{(l)};V_l],\\ Z_k^{(l)},Z_v^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times d};\\ \\ \\min_Z\\sum_{(x,\\tilde c)}\\sum_i D_{KL}\\big(F(\\cdot\\mid\\tilde c\\oplus x_{:i})\\,\\|\\,F_Z(\\cdot\\mid x_{:i})\\big)","trainable_params":"2L\\,(p-p_f)\\,d_{kv} \\ \\ (p=\\text{num\\_virtual\\_tokens},\\ p_f=\\text{num\\_frozen\\_tokens}=1 \\text{ by default},\\ d_{kv}=\\text{per-layer key/value width})","locus":"keys/values at every layer (a directly parameterized KV-cache prefix)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"learned key/value vectors prepended at every layer, trained directly (no MLP reparameterization) by context distillation on synthetic self-study conversations about a corpus","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The cartridge is extra KV state attended by the model, not a weight change.","identity_at_init":"Not an identity. The paper recommends initializing \\(Z\\) from the prefill KV cache of the first \\(p\\) corpus tokens (PEFT: initialize_kv_prefix_from_text / initialize_kv_prefix_from_past_key_values). PEFT get_peft_model alone gives random \\(\\mathcal N(0,1)\\) init. The first num_frozen_tokens (default 1) are frozen as an attention sink.","key_idea":"Compress a long corpus offline into a small trainable KV cache via self-study distillation, so it can replace in-context documents at inference.","results_note":"Paper reports matching ICL quality with 38.6x less memory and 26.4x higher throughput, and extending effective context to 484k tokens.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same serving mechanism (past_key_values), but the KV prefix itself is the parameter and is distilled from a corpus."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"Self-described simplified prefix-tuning (direct KV parameterization); new objective and initialization."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"The paper finds KV-cache parameterization beats a LoRA parameterization for this use."},{"target":"initial-state-tuning","type":"dual-to","note":"Transformer counterpart of learning the recurrence's initial state."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06266","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.06266","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/cartridges","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10011902","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.06266","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/cartridge","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/cartridges.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Sabri Eyuboglu","Ryan Ehrlich","Simran Arora","Neel Guha","Dylan Zinsley","Emily Liu","Will Tennien","Atri Rudra","James Zou","Azalia Mirhoseini","Christopher Re"],"bibkey":"eyuboglu2025cartridges","bibtex":"@inproceedings{eyuboglu2025cartridges,\n  title = {{Cartridges: Lightweight and general-purpose long context representations via self-study}},\n  author = {Sabri Eyuboglu and Ryan Ehrlich and Simran Arora and Neel Guha and Dylan Zinsley and Emily Liu and Will Tennien and Atri Rudra and James Zou and Azalia Mirhoseini and Christopher Re},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2506.06266},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.06266}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Learned key/value slots prepended at every layer: the same unpointed extension as prefix tuning, in the direct (HF prefix_projection=False) parameterisation.","base_point":"Unpointed relative to \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\); the recommended init is the prefill KV cache of the first \\(p\\) corpus tokens, so it is pointed only relative to in-context reading of that corpus (cf. CPT). HF get_peft_model alone gives \\(\\mathcal N(0,1)\\).","gauge":"Direct KV parameters; attention reads the prefix as a multiset (Thm VI.5(a)), so only the discrete slot permutations per head and layer survive. No continuous fibre.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prefix tuning re-pointed: a deep key/value prefix \\(Z\\) on the sequence wire of every attention layer, an unpointed extension whose base point is the corpus prefill cache and whose attention-sink slot is frozen, which makes it an A2 sub-object of the direct prefix. Self-study distillation changes the loss, not the object \\((Q,q_0,\\rho)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(Z)=\\big([z^{(l)}_{\\mathrm{sink}};Z_k^{(l)}],\\,[z^{(l)}_{v,\\mathrm{sink}};Z_v^{(l)}]\\big)_{l\\le L}","note":"Freezing the sink slot at its base value; prefix tuning pointed at the corpus KV cache. Pure A2 in the direct parameterisation; with Li–Liang's MLP compose with the section \\(W_2\\mapsto W_2^0+H_0^{+}(\\cdot)\\) of Obs III.2 (hidden ≥ p). Arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"On length-one contexts every weight setting of a standard head (LoRA included) is affine in x, a generically prefixed head with \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) is not (Thm VI.5(f)); established head-locally, whole-network containment is open."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"cpt","name":"CPT","full_name":"Context-aware Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Context-aware Prompt Tuning: Advancing In-Context Learning with Adversarial Methods","authors":"Blau et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2410.17222","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17222","code_url":"https://github.com/tsachiblau/Context-aware-Prompt-Tuning-Advancing-In-Context-Learning-with-Adversarial-Methods","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"CPTConfig","formula_latex":"X_{ctx}=[T_i(x_i),S_{intra},T_o(y_i),S_{inter}]_{i=1}^{N};\\ E\\leftarrow\\Pi_{\\|E-E_0\\|_2\\le\\epsilon}\\big(E-\\eta\\nabla_E\\mathcal{L}\\big);\\ \\mathcal{L}=\\sum_{k=1}^{N}\\gamma^{N+1-k}\\,\\mathrm{CE}(\\hat t_O^{(k)},t_O^{(k)})+\\mathrm{CE}(\\hat t_O^{(N+1)},t_O^{(N+1)})","trainable_params":"\\ell\\,d \\ \\ (\\ell=\\text{number of context input/format tokens optimized; label tokens frozen})","locus":"input embedding sequence (embeddings of the in-context demonstrations)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"soft prompt initialized from real few-shot demonstrations; per-token embedding changes projected onto an L2 ball (projected gradient descent) with a recency-decayed context loss","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Optimized context tokens are extra inputs, not weights.","identity_at_init":"Not a no-op relative to the bare query: at init it equals ordinary in-context learning with the given demonstrations.","key_idea":"Treat the in-context demonstrations themselves as trainable soft prompts, but keep them within an \\(\\varepsilon\\)-ball of the real tokens and down-weight early examples to counter recency bias.","results_note":"Paper reports gains over LoRA, prompt tuning, IPT and ICL on SST-2, AG News, DBpedia and TREC in 2/4/6-shot settings with BLOOM-1.7B, GPT-J-6B and Llama-3-8B.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Soft prompt over demonstration tokens with projected (adversarial-style) updates."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17222","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.17222","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/cpt","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.14.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.17222","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/conceptual_guides/prompting","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/cpt","https://openreview.net/forum?id=J6Xgra2bE5"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Tsachi Blau","Moshe Kimhi","Yonatan Belinkov","Alexander Bronstein","Chaim Baskin"],"bibkey":"blau2024context","bibtex":"@article{blau2024context,\n  title = {{Context-aware Prompt Tuning: Advancing In-Context Learning with Adversarial Methods}},\n  author = {Tsachi Blau and Moshe Kimhi and Yonatan Belinkov and Alexander Bronstein and Chaim Baskin},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2410.17222},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.17222},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17222}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Prompt over the demonstration positions with a projected \\(\\varepsilon\\)-ball constraint; the ball is open at \\(q_0\\), so the germ is that of prompt tuning and the ball only bounds the reach.","base_point":"As in the table: unpointed relative to the bare query, but pointed at the in-context-learning state (\\(q_0\\) = the real demonstration embeddings).","gauge":"Prompt embeddings enter at fixed positions; generic Jacobian rank \\(\\ell d\\), no continuous symmetry.","covariance":"The per-token Euclidean \\(\\varepsilon\\)-ball \\(\\|E-E_0\\|_2 \\le \\varepsilon\\) is \\(O(d)\\)-equivariant on the embedding wire (\\(CO\\) only if \\(\\varepsilon\\) is rescaled), not \\(GL\\); read at function level (table: '–').","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning restricted (A2) to the context tokens of real demonstrations, with label tokens frozen and every token confined to an \\(\\varepsilon\\)-ball around its embedding; the base point is the in-context-learning state, not the frozen zero-shot function. The constraint is global: at \\(q_0\\) the germ is open in prompt space.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(E_{\\mathrm{ctx}})=\\big(E_{\\mathrm{ctx}},\\,E^{0}_{\\mathrm{label}}\\big)\\quad(\\|E_{\\mathrm{ctx}}-E^0_{\\mathrm{ctx}}\\|\\le\\varepsilon)","note":"Label-token coordinates frozen at their embeddings and the domain restricted to the ε-ball; prompt tuning over the whole demonstration context, pointed at the demonstration embeddings."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"deft","name":"DEFT","full_name":"Decompositional Efficient Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"DEFT: Decompositional Efficient Fine-Tuning for Text-to-Image Models","authors":"Kumar et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2509.22793","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22793","code_url":"https://github.com/MAXNORM8650/DEFT","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"DeftConfig","formula_latex":"W=(I-QQ^\\top)W_0+QR,\\ \\ Q=\\mathrm{qr}(P),\\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{(PEFT default 'relu'): } W=(I-P\\,\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top)W_0+PR","trainable_params":"r(d_{out}+d_{in})","locus":"Linear / Conv1D layers (mainly text-to-image diffusion transformers/UNets)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"rank-\\(r\\) update that removes an \\(r\\)-dimensional output subspace of \\(W_0\\) and injects new content into it: \\(\\Delta W = Q(R - Q^\\top W_0)\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The update equals a low-rank additive delta \\(Q(R - Q^T W_0)\\) that can be merged into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"PEFT (init_weights=True) draws \\(P \\sim N(0, 0.02^2)\\) and sets \\(R\\) so that the injection exactly cancels the removal term, so \\(W = W_0\\) at init.","key_idea":"Split adaptation into 'forget a subspace' (projection) and 'write new content there' (low-rank injection) to personalize while preserving the base model's instruction-following/editability.","results_note":"Paper reports DreamBench Plus CLIP-T 0.361 on SDXL vs 0.341 for LoRA and 0.354 for PaRa.","relations":[{"target":"para","type":"generalizes","note":"Dropping the injection term QR gives PaRa (DeftConfig(para=True))."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same rank-r parameter budget, different (subspace-replacing) structure."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"the injection is a rank-r additive term"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22793","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.22793","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/deft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/deft/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/118053","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/docs/source/package_reference/deft.md","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/deft/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Komal Kumar","Rao Muhammad Anwer","Fahad Shahbaz Khan","Salman Khan","Ivan Laptev","Hisham Cholakkal"],"bibkey":"kumar2025deft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kumar2025deft,\n  title = {{DEFT: Decompositional Efficient Fine-Tuning for Text-to-Image Models}},\n  author = {Komal Kumar and Rao Muhammad Anwer and Fahad Shahbaz Khan and Salman Khan and Ivan Laptev and Hisham Cholakkal},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2509.22793},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22793}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W=W_0+P(R-\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top W_0)\\) (HF default; \\(Q(P)\\) for the paper's QR). For each \\(P\\) the second factor ranges over all of \\(\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}\\), so \\(\\mathrm{Im}=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), LoRA's cone.","base_point":"HF sets \\(R_0=\\mathrm{relu}(P_0)^\\top W_0\\) with random \\(P_0\\), so the effective LoRA point is \\((P_0,0)\\), the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum: \\(S_1=\\{P_0Y\\}\\) of dimension \\(nr\\), deficit \\(r(m-r)\\) (Jacobian 12 against \\(d=22\\) at \\((m,n,r)=(7,6,2)\\)).","gauge":"The shear \\((P,R)\\mapsto(P,R-\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top W_0)\\) conjugates DEFT to LoRA, so the generic gauge is LoRA's \\(GL_r\\), acting non-linearly in DEFT's coordinates (QR variant: \\(B_r^+\\) on \\(P\\) and an \\(O(r)\\) remnant on \\(R\\)).","covariance":"The image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant.","rebasing":"As LoRA: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"DEFT's 'forget a subspace, then write into it' is LoRA in disguise: the shear \\((P,R)\\mapsto(P,R-\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top W_0)\\) identifies it, as germs at the HF initialisation, with \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((P_0,0)\\) in the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum of Thm II.2. What separates it from default LoRA is the stratum of its pointing, not its image.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(P,R)\\mapsto\\big(P,\\ R-\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top W_0\\big)\\quad(\\text{QR: }(Q(P),\\ R-Q(P)^\\top W_0))","note":"LoRA_r (scale absorbed) pointed at (P_0, 0), the T_B stratum; for the relu default an isomorphism of germs (relu is smooth away from zero entries of P_0)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Against LoRA's default T_A pointing (0, A_0): S_1(DEFT) = {P_0 Y} and S_1(LoRA) = {X A_0} are incomparable, so neither simulates the other despite equal images (Thm II.2(a))."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"para","name":"PaRa","full_name":"Parameter Rank Reduction","paper_title":"PaRa: Personalizing Text-to-Image Diffusion via Parameter Rank Reduction","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2406.05641","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05641","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"DeftConfig(para=True, decomposition_method='qr')","formula_latex":"W=(I-QQ^\\top)W_0,\\ \\ Q=\\mathrm{qr}(B),\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r} \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}","locus":"attention/linear layers of text-to-image diffusion models","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"left-multiplication of \\(W_0\\) by an orthogonal projector of corank \\(r\\), shrinking the output space of the layer (rank reduction)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\((I - QQ^\\top) W_0\\) is a single dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"Not an identity: PEFT notes PaRa cannot be a no-op at initialization since only the projection is trained.","key_idea":"Personalization works by reducing the rank of generation: projecting out a few output directions narrows the model's output space toward the target concept.","results_note":"Paper reports about 2x fewer parameters than LoRA and 82-97% human preference over LoRA across tested concepts.","relations":[{"target":"deft","type":"special-case-of","note":"DEFT without the injection term."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Subtractive (rank-reducing) instead of additive low-rank adaptation."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05641","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.05641","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/deft","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://openreview.net/forum?id=KZgo2YQbhc","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/docs/source/package_reference/deft.md"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Shangyu Chen","Zizheng Pan","Jianfei Cai","Dinh Phung"],"bibkey":"chen2024para","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024para,\n  title = {{PaRa: Personalizing Text-to-Image Diffusion via Parameter Rank Reduction}},\n  author = {Shangyu Chen and Zizheng Pan and Jianfei Cai and Dinh Phung},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2406.05641},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05641}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W=(I-\\Pi_U)W_0\\) with \\(U=\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\): the conjugacy class of corank-\\(r\\) orthogonal projectors (in the monoid \\(\\mathrm{End}\\,\\mathbb R^m\\)) acting on \\(W_0\\). For \\(W_0\\) of full row rank the image is a copy of \\(\\mathrm{Gr}(r,m)\\), dimension \\(r(m-r)\\); it has no germ at \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\): \\((I-\\Pi_U)W_0=W_0\\) would need \\(\\Pi_UW_0=0\\). HF's relu projector is pointed only at \\(P=0\\), where \\(d\\rho=0\\) (\\(S_1=0\\)) and training cannot leave; its random init is a defect. This assumes \\(W_0\\) of full row rank (e.g. square attention layers). On a tall layer with \\(d_{out}-\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\ge r\\) the image does contain \\(\\theta_0\\), at any \\(B\\) with \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\perp\\mathrm{col}\\,W_0\\). There \\(\\dim S_1=rd_{in}<r(d_{out}-r)=d\\) (Jacobian 8 against 16 at \\((10,4,2)\\)), a deficient pointing the method does not use.","gauge":"The projector depends only on \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\), so \\(GL_r\\) acts linearly by \\(B\\mapsto Bg\\) (fibre \\(r^2\\)).","covariance":"Output side: \\(g\\Pi_Ug^{-1}\\) is an orthogonal projector for every \\(U\\) iff \\(g\\in CO(m)\\); input side \\(GL(n)\\). This is the mirror of GLxCO, i.e. \\(CO(m)\\times GL(n)\\).","rebasing":"Re-merging composes corank-\\(r\\) projectors; the generated monoid consists of contractions, and each cycle can only shrink norms and ranks (a monoid, not a group)."},"categorical_reading":"PaRa is an unpointed object of action type: \\(\\rho(B)=(I-\\Pi_{\\mathrm{col}B})W_0\\) is the orbit of \\(W_0\\) under the Grassmannian of corank-\\(r\\) projectors, a compact set at positive distance from \\(\\theta_0\\). It is DEFT restricted to \\(R=0\\), but only as a weak morphism, since no parameter value reproduces the frozen model.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"deft","test":"O3","reason":"PaRa has no base point over theta_0, so only the weak morphism B -> (B, 0) into DEFT exists (its image lies in W_0 + M_{<=r})."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"delora","name":"DeLoRA","full_name":"Decoupled Low-rank Adaptation","paper_title":"DeLoRA: Decoupling Angles and Strength in Low-rank Adaptation","authors":"Bini et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2503.18225","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18225","code_url":"https://github.com/ExplainableML/DeLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"DeloraConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\frac{\\lambda\\|W_0\\|}{r}\\,B\\,\\Xi\\,A,\\ \\ \\Xi=\\mathrm{diag}\\Big(\\tfrac{1}{\\|b_i\\|\\,\\|a_i\\|}\\Big),\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ \\lambda \\text{ trainable}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+1","locus":"nn.Linear layers only (PEFT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sum of \\(r\\) unit-Frobenius rank-1 terms with a learnable global strength, so \\(\\|\\Delta W\\|_F\\le\\lambda\\|W_0\\|\\) (a Frobenius-ball boundary)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The normalized low-rank update is a fixed dense matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"Paper: subtract a frozen copy of the initial update from \\(W_0\\); PEFT default: \\(B = 0\\) (\\(A\\) Kaiming) with \\(\\lambda\\) initialized to delora_lambda.","key_idea":"Normalize the low-rank directions and learn their strength separately, bounding how far the weights can drift and making training robust to learning rate and length.","results_note":"Paper reports GLUE avg 85.6 vs 84.7 for LoRA, parity with LoRA/DoRA on subject-driven generation, and better robustness to learning rate and extended training.","relations":[{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both decouple angle and strength. The paper contrasts itself with DoRA (it normalizes the update, DoRA normalizes the full weight); it does not build on DoRA."},{"target":"ether","type":"builds-on","note":"The paper derives DeLoRA as an evolution of ETHER that keeps its bounded, normalized update but adds rank r and a learnable strength. It is not a strict generalization (ETHER is a multiplicative reflection)."},{"target":"ether-plus","type":"builds-on","note":"derived from ETHER+: its fixed distance bound becomes a learnable bound and the rank becomes arbitrary"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"normalized LoRA factors with a learnable norm bound"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18225","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.18225","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/delora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/delora/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Massimo Bini","Leander Girrbach","Zeynep Akata"],"bibkey":"bini2025delora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bini2025delora,\n  title = {{DeLoRA: Decoupling Angles and Strength in Low-rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Massimo Bini and Leander Girrbach and Zeynep Akata},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2503.18225},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18225}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\lambda}{r}\\sum_i\\hat b_i\\hat a_i^\\top D_w\\) (unit rank-ones, one global \\(\\lambda\\), per-input-column norms \\(D_w\\) of \\(W_0\\) in the official and HF code): image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), with \\(\\|\\Delta W\\|\\) controlled by \\(\\lambda\\); the Jacobian rank equals \\(r(m+n-r)\\).","base_point":"HF: \\(B=0\\) with the norm clamp \\(10^{-4}\\), so near \\(q_0\\) the map is LoRA with a constant scale: \\(S_1=\\{X\\hat A_0D_w\\}\\) of dimension \\(mr\\) (checked 7, 14, 21), an apex. The paper's subtract-a-copy init is split (Cone*, regular).","gauge":"axes.json and VIII.B list per-term scalings (a \\(2r\\) torus), but the generic fibre has dimension \\(r^2+1\\) (Jacobian 22 of 27 at \\((7,6,2)\\), 30 of 40 at \\(r=3\\)). Beyond the torus there is an \\((r-1)^2\\)-dimensional non-linear part, which redistributes a rank-\\(r\\) update among \\(r\\) equal-weight unit rank-ones. For \\(r = 1\\) the fibre is just the 2-dimensional torus (Jacobian 12 of 14), so the deviation from the axes.json torus label starts at \\(r = 2\\).","covariance":"\\(D_w\\) is invertible and \\(\\lambda\\) is free, so the image is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): \\(GL\\times GL\\) (agrees with VIII.B).","rebasing":"As LoRA: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"DeLoRA normalises each rank-one term and puts the strength in one scalar \\(\\lambda\\). Its image is still LoRA's cone, and under HF's \\(B=0\\) initialisation it starts at the same apex, so LoRA simulates it through an explicit normalising \\(h\\). The normalisation changes the fibre (a non-linear gauge of dimension \\(r^2+1\\)) and hence the training geometry, not the expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\Big(\\tfrac{\\lambda}{r}\\,B\\,\\mathrm{diag}\\big(1/\\max(\\|b_i\\|,\\varepsilon)\\big),\\ \\mathrm{diag}\\big(1/\\|a_i\\|\\big)\\,A\\,D_w\\Big)","note":"LoRA_r (scale 1) pointed at (0, diag(1/||a_{i,0}||) A_0 D_w), a T_A point; D_w = diagonal of the column norms of W_0, epsilon = 1e-4. h is smooth near q_0, where ||b_i|| < epsilon, and away from ||b_i|| = epsilon. It is a germ arrow, as HF's clamped rho itself is."},{"target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\big(B',A',\\ \\mu=(\\|(W_0+B'A')_{i,:}\\|)_i\\big),\\ (B',A')\\ \\text{as in the LoRA arrow}","note":"Composite of DeLoRA -> LoRA with LoRA -> DoRA (magnitudes set to the current row norms); DoRA pointed at A_0' = diag(1/||a_{i,0}||) A_0 D_w."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ether","test":"O1","reason":"ETHER's image is a compact set of reflections W_0 H^B inside W_0 O(n): it excludes theta_0 and preserves K_out, which DeLoRA's additive updates change. DeLoRA is derived from ETHER, but neither simulates the other."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"fourierft","name":"FourierFT","full_name":"Fourier Fine-Tuning (Discrete Fourier Transform PEFT)","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Discrete Fourier Transform","authors":"Gao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2405.03003","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03003","code_url":"https://github.com/Chaos96/fourierft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"FourierFTConfig","formula_latex":"\\Delta W=\\alpha\\,\\mathrm{Re}\\big(\\mathcal{F}^{-1}_{2D}(F)\\big),\\ F\\in\\mathbb{C}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}} \\text{ zero except at } n \\text{ fixed random frequency locations (shared across layers) holding trainable } c\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}","trainable_params":"n \\ \\ (n=\\text{n\\_frequency})","locus":"any linear layer (typically \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\))","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is the real part of the inverse 2D DFT of a sparse spectral matrix with \\(n\\) learnable coefficients","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The spatial-domain \\(\\Delta W\\) can be computed once and added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"PEFT default init_weights=False draws coefficients from \\(N(0,1)\\), so it is NOT an exact identity; init_weights=True zero-initializes for a no-op.","key_idea":"Weight updates are compressible in the frequency domain, so learn only a handful of spectral coefficients.","results_note":"Paper reports outperforming LoRA on LLaMA2-7B instruction tuning with 0.064M trainable parameters versus LoRA's 33.5M.","relations":[{"target":"c3a","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are Fourier-domain parameterizations of Delta W."},{"target":"waveft","type":"competes-with","note":"Same sparse-transform-coefficient idea with a wavelet instead of Fourier basis."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"replaces the low-rank structure with a sparse spectral one"},{"target":"loca","type":"competes-with","note":"LoCA analyses FourierFT's random-location iDFT and moves to learned locations with iDCT"},{"target":"ssh","type":"competes-with","note":"Hartley transform with energy-based frequency selection"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03003","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/fourierft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/fourierft/config.py","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.03003v1","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/fourierft/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Ziqi Gao","Qichao Wang","Aochuan Chen","Zijing Liu","Bingzhe Wu","Liang Chen","Jia Li"],"bibkey":"gao2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gao2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Discrete Fourier Transform}},\n  author = {Ziqi Gao and Qichao Wang and Aochuan Chen and Zijing Liu and Bingzhe Wu and Liang Chen and Jia Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2405.03003},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.03003}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\alpha\\,\\mathrm{Re}(\\mathrm{ifft2}(\\text{sparse } c))\\) is linear; \\(d=|M|\\) minus the number of sampled conjugate pairs (Prop II.12).","base_point":"Regular for init_weights=True; HF default \\(N(0,1)\\) coefficients leave a defect (table: 'HF default ~').","gauge":"Trivial, +1 per sampled conjugate frequency pair.","covariance":"\\(\\Pi_{\\mathrm{cyc}}\\): the image lies in the point-symmetric subspace \\(\\Delta W_{j,l}=\\Delta W_{-j,-l}\\) of the cyclic index order.","rebasing":"Fixed (seeded, layer-shared) indices: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"FourierFT is a mask in the real Fourier frame: each frequency contributes a rank-two real pattern, so \\(\\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{mn}B_F\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c,-c)A_F\\) with frozen trigonometric frames and FourierFT factors through LoRA-FA\\((A_F)\\), as VeRA does through LoRA-FA\\((A)\\). Its image lies in the point-symmetric subspace \\(\\Delta W_{j,l}=\\Delta W_{-j,-l}\\), an absolute invariant that neuron permutations do not respect.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(c)=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{mn}\\,B_F\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c,-c),\\ \\ B_F=[\\cos\\theta_k\\ \\sin\\theta_k]_k,\\ A_F=[\\cos\\varphi_k;\\ \\sin\\varphi_k]_k","note":"Into LoRA-FA_{2n_c} with frozen trigonometric A_F, pointed at 0; Re(e^{i(theta+phi)})=cos cos - sin sin (checked to 1e-16)."},{"target":"fourierft","rule":"A2","h":"h(c_\\Omega)=(c_\\Omega,\\ 0_{\\Omega'\\setminus\\Omega})","note":"FourierFT on index set Omega is a sub-object of FourierFT on Omega' containing it."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"c3a","test":"O1","reason":"Random frequencies off the anti-diagonal {(u,-u)} give non-circulant patterns, so the image is not inside the block-circulant subspace."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Max rank min(2n_c,m,n), far above r at LoRA-matched budgets."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"frod","name":"FRoD","full_name":"Full-Rank Efficient Fine-Tuning with Rotational Degrees","paper_title":"FRoD: Full-Rank Efficient Fine-Tuning with Rotational Degrees for Fast Convergence","authors":"Wan et al.","year":2025,"venue":"AAAI 2026","arxiv":"2512.23485","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23485","code_url":"https://github.com/Bane-Elvin/AAAI2026-FRoD","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"FrodConfig","formula_latex":"W_i = U_i\\big(\\Sigma_i + S_i\\big)V^{\\top},\\quad V\\ \\text{global orthonormal basis shared by all layers (hierarchical joint QR + eigendecomposition of the stacked weights)},\\ U_i\\ \\text{per-layer factor (frozen)};\\ \\Sigma_i\\ \\text{diagonal strengths and } S_i\\ \\text{random-pattern sparse off-diagonal perturbation (density } s)\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"d_{in} + s\\,d_{in}^{2}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (diagonal plus sparse off-diagonal entries of an } d_{in}\\times d_{in} \\text{ core)}","locus":"selected linear layers of ViT, LLaMA-style and encoder models (grouped by layer category for the joint decomposition)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"cross-layer shared latent basis with a per-layer diagonal-plus-sparse core: on-axis updates rescale generalised singular values, sparse off-diagonal entries inject small rotations; full-rank updates","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(U_i(\\Sigma_i + S_i)V^T\\) is a dense matrix that replaces \\(W_i\\) at no extra inference cost.","identity_at_init":"\\(S_i = 0\\) and \\(U_i \\Sigma_i V^{\\top}\\) reconstructs \\(W_i\\) (maximum error below 1e-5), so the model is essentially unchanged at init; \\(\\Sigma_i\\) (trained) starts at the decomposition's strengths; the joint decomposition is a costly one-off initialisation. HF PEFT: frod_lambda_l = diag strengths, frod_lambda_s_values = 0","key_idea":"Extract a basis shared by all layers via a hierarchical joint decomposition, then fine-tune only per-layer scaling factors plus sparse off-diagonal rotations in that basis, giving full-rank updates and fast convergence.","results_note":"On 20 vision, reasoning and language benchmarks FRoD matches full fine-tuning accuracy using 1.72% of trainable parameters under identical training budgets.","relations":[{"target":"svft","type":"builds-on","note":"sparse core in a singular-vector-like basis, made cross-layer"},{"target":"fact","type":"competes-with","note":"shared basis across layers with small per-layer cores"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"compared against shared-random-basis methods"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"Both operate in a spectral basis of W_0; FRoD trains a sparse full-rank core instead of a rank-r factor."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23485","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.23485","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/frod","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/frod/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Guoan Wan","Tianyu Chen","Fangzheng Feng","Haoyi Zhou","Runhua Xu"],"bibkey":"wan2025frod","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wan2025frod,\n  title = {{FRoD: Full-Rank Efficient Fine-Tuning with Rotational Degrees for Fast Convergence}},\n  author = {Guoan Wan and Tianyu Chen and Fangzheng Feng and Haoyi Zhou and Runhua Xu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2512.23485},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23485}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho(\\Sigma_i,S_i)=U_i(\\Sigma_i+S_i)V^T\\) is affine (diagonal plus fixed sparse pattern); full-rank updates.","base_point":"\\(S_0=0\\), \\(\\Sigma_0\\) = decomposition strengths: \\(U_i \\Sigma_0 V^T = W_i\\) (the paper reports max error <1e-5; the verified catalog states the PEFT reconstruction is exact), so \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\theta_0\\) and the linear method is regular.","gauge":"Trivial when \\(U_i\\) is injective (\\(d_{out}\\ge d_{in}\\)); otherwise translations by the kernel.","covariance":"Spectral frames: at least CO on each side, but \\(V\\) is shared across a layer group, so the same input transformation must act on the whole group (Obs II.15 caveat).","rebasing":"Frames and pattern frozen: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"FRoD is the linear method \\((\\Sigma_i,S_i)\\mapsto U_i(\\Sigma_i+S_i)V^\\top\\): a diagonal-plus-sparse mask in a cross-layer spectral frame \\((U_i,V)\\), pointed (up to reconstruction error) at the decomposition's strengths. Because \\(V\\) is shared across a layer group, its covariance holds only for transformations common to the whole group.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"frod","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Sigma)=(\\Sigma,\\ S=0)","note":"The diagonal-only (spectral-rescaling) variant is the sub-object obtained by freezing the sparse off-diagonal entries at 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"svft","test":"O1","reason":"Same U(Sigma+M)V^T form, but FRoD's V is a joint cross-layer basis, not W_i's own right-singular frame: linear images in different frames."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Updates are full rank, above any fixed r."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"glora","name":"GLoRA","full_name":"Generalized LoRA (One-for-All)","paper_title":"One-for-All: Generalized LoRA for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Chavan et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2306.07967","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07967","code_url":"https://github.com/Arnav0400/ViT-Slim/tree/master/GLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"GloraConfig","formula_latex":"h=(W_0+W_0\\odot A+B)\\,x+b_0+b_0\\odot D+E+W_0C;\\ \\ A,B\\in\\{\\text{LoRA},\\text{vector},\\text{scalar},\\text{none}\\},\\ C\\in\\{\\text{LoRA},\\text{vector},\\text{none}\\},\\ D,E\\in\\{\\text{vector},\\text{scalar},\\text{none}\\}","trainable_params":"\\text{config-dependent; PEFT default (A,B=LoRA, C=LoRA, D,E=constant) on a layer with bias: } 2r(d_{in}+d_{out})+r(d_{in}+1)+2;\\ \\text{bias-free layers: } 2r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"nn.Linear layers (weights and biases)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"unified reparameterization: Hadamard rescaling of \\(W_0\\), additive (low-rank) weight shift, bias rescaling/shift, and a \\(W_0\\)-coupled bias (layer-wise prompt) path; per-layer path types chosen by evolutionary search in the paper","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"All paths are linear in the parameters and fold into \\(W_{uni} = W_0 + W_0\\odot A + B\\) and \\(b_{uni} = b_0 + b_0\\odot D + E + W_0 C\\).","identity_at_init":"Zero-initialized down-projections make every path zero (no-op) by default.","key_idea":"Subsume LoRA, scaling/shifting and prompt-like methods in one reparameterizable layer and search the best path configuration per layer.","results_note":"Paper reports 78.0% average on VTAB-1K, up to 2.9% above prior methods, and strong few-shot and domain-generalization results.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"B path in LoRA mode with all other paths disabled is LoRA."},{"target":"hira","type":"generalizes","note":"The W_0 (.) A path with a LoRA-type A is HiRA's update (PEFT's elementwise implementation)."},{"target":"ia3","type":"generalizes","note":"Vector-mode A path is a per-channel rescaling of W_0."},{"target":"ssf","type":"generalizes","note":"per-channel scaling and shifting via vector supports"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07967","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.07967","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/glora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/glora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Arnav0400/ViT-Slim/master/GLoRA/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/glora/config.py"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Arnav Chavan","Zhuang Liu","Deepak Gupta","Eric Xing","Zhiqiang Shen"],"bibkey":"chavan2023one","bibtex":"@article{chavan2023one,\n  title = {{One-for-All: Generalized LoRA for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Arnav Chavan and Zhuang Liu and Deepak Gupta and Eric Xing and Zhiqiang Shen},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2306.07967},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.07967},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07967}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Every path is linear in \\(W_0,b_0\\) and folds into \\(W_{uni}=W_0+W_0\\odot A+B\\) and \\(b_{uni}=b_0+b_0\\odot D+E+W_0C\\).","shape":"With PEFT defaults (\\(A, B, C\\) low-rank; \\(D, E\\) scalar): the Minkowski sum of HiRA\\(_r\\)'s transported cone \\(W_0\\odot\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), LoRA\\(_r\\)'s cone and a linear bias part \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{b_0,\\mathbf 1\\}+\\mathrm{col}\\,W_0\\).","base_point":"Zero-initialised down-projections put every low-rank path at its zero factor: an apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on each low-rank wire (HiRA and LoRA paths). The \\(C\\) path factors a vector, \\(C=C_uC_d\\) with \\(C_u\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times r}\\), \\(C_d\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times1}\\). Its fibre has dimension \\(nr+r-n\\): \\(GL_r\\) plus the linear unipotent slack \\(C_u\\mapsto C_u+x(JC_d)^\\top\\) (\\(J\\) skew), which adds \\((n-r)(r-1)\\). Jacobian check: fibre \\(26=2r^2+nr+r-n\\) at \\((m,n,r)=(20,16,2)\\).","covariance":"The Hadamard path restricts the image to \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) (Prop II.9(e)); the scalar bias shift \\(E\\mathbf 1\\) further cuts the output side to permutations in the PEFT default.","rebasing":"The Hadamard and LoRA summands each reach \\(\\Theta\\) (for \\(W_0\\) without zeros)."},"categorical_reading":"GLoRA is an \\(\\oplus\\)-word on the slice: \\(\\mathrm{HiRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus(\\text{bias paths})\\), where HiRA \\(=(D_{W_0})_*\\mathrm{LoRA}\\) supplies the coupling to \\(\\theta_0\\). Its 'unification' is the statement that the torus ladder \\(W_0\\odot(J+Z)\\) of Prop II.9 plus an additive term simulates (IA)\\(^3\\), SSF-type scalings, DoRA and LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The W_0 (.) A path alone reaches rank min(m, n, r rank W_0), generically full rank (the rank of a Hadamard product is at most the product of the ranks). That is beyond LoRA_r, and beyond LoRA_{2r} at the same budget."}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"gralora","name":"GraLoRA","full_name":"Granular Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"GraLoRA: Granular Low-Rank Adaptation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Jung et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2505.20355","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20355","code_url":"https://github.com/SqueezeBits/GraLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"GraloraConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\big[\\,B_{ij}A_{ij}^{\\top}\\,\\big]_{i,j=1}^{k},\\quad A_{ij}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{in}}{k}\\times\\frac{r}{k}},\\ B_{ij}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{k}\\times\\frac{r}{k}};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A_{ij},B_{ij}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer of LLMs","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(k\\times k\\) grid of independent rank-\\((r/k)\\) blocks, equivalently \\(B_G A_G^{\\top}\\) with sparse block-structured factors; rank up to \\(k r\\) at LoRA's parameter count; Hybrid GraLoRA adds a plain LoRA term.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The block matrix is an ordinary dense update.","identity_at_init":"\\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform and \\(B = 0\\) (HF PEFT, all \\(k\\) blocks and the optional hybrid LoRA), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\). HF scales by \\(\\alpha/(r + \\mathtt{hybrid\\_r})\\).","key_idea":"Partition the weight into sub-blocks with their own small adapters so that input outlier channels only perturb local gradients and effective rank grows by k.","results_note":"Up to +8.5% absolute Pass@1 on HumanEval+ over LoRA, with consistent gains on commonsense reasoning across model sizes and ranks.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"k = 1 recovers LoRA"},{"target":"melora","type":"generalizes","note":"MELoRA keeps only the diagonal blocks"},{"target":"mora","type":"competes-with","note":"both raise rank at LoRA's budget"},{"target":"bd-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both impose block structure on LoRA (GraLoRA for expressivity, BD-LoRA for TP serving)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.20355","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/gralora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/gralora/layer.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2505.20355","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=GraLoRA%20Granular%20Low-Rank%20Adaptation","https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20355","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.20355","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/gralora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape"],"authors_full":["Yeonjoon Jung","Daehyun Ahn","Hyungjun Kim","Taesu Kim","Eunhyeok Park"],"bibkey":"jung2025gralora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jung2025gralora,\n  title = {{GraLoRA: Granular Low-Rank Adaptation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Yeonjoon Jung and Daehyun Ahn and Hyungjun Kim and Taesu Kim and Eunhyeok Park},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2505.20355},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20355}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A product of \\(k^2\\) block cones \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r/k}(\\tfrac mk\\times\\tfrac nk)\\), with \\(d=r(m+n-r)\\) and \\(\\rho_{\\max}=\\min(kr,m,n)\\) (Prop II.12; table).","base_point":"HF: \\(A\\) Kaiming and \\(B = 0\\) in every block, so each block sits at its own LoRA apex.","gauge":"\\(\\prod GL_{r/k}\\), one per block (table).","covariance":"The \\(k\\times k\\) block partition is imposed (\\(\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Each block cone generates its block, so \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\) (table)."},"categorical_reading":"GraLoRA is the \\(\\oplus\\)-word of \\(k^2\\) block-embedded \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r/k}\\) (Obs I.7). It has the same budget and the same \\(d\\) as \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) and a larger cut (rank \\(kr\\)), but every block is capped at rank \\(r/k\\): it moves the cut rather than enlarging the image.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{U_{ij},V_{ij}\\})=\\big(\\mathrm{blockdiag}(L_1,\\dots,L_k),\\;S\\big),\\ L_i=[U_{i1}\\cdots U_{ik}],\\ S=\\text{stack of the }V_{ij}^\\top\\text{ on column block }j","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{kr}\\) (proof of Prop II.12), pointed at \\((0,S_0)\\); the scale \\(\\alpha/r\\) is absorbed into h."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Against \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) at the same r, \\(\\rho_{\\max}=kr>r\\). Conversely \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) does not map into GraLoRA, because a generic rank-r update has rank r on every block; the images are incomparable (Prop II.12(c))."},{"target":"melora","test":"O1","reason":"MELoRA's image keeps every off-diagonal block at zero (an absolute invariant) and GraLoRA fills them. MELoRA to GraLoRA is the A2 arrow, not the reverse."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"hira","name":"HiRA","full_name":"Hadamard High-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"HiRA: Parameter-Efficient Hadamard High-Rank Adaptation for Large Language Models","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=TwJrTz9cRS","code_url":"https://github.com/hqsiswiliam/hira","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"HiraConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0+W_0\\odot(BA)=W_0\\odot(\\mathbf{1}\\mathbf{1}^{\\top}+BA),\\quad B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ B,A\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (LLM attention and MLP projections)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"entry-wise (Hadamard) multiplicative modulation of \\(W_0\\) by \\(J + BA\\). \\(\\Delta W = W_0 \\odot BA\\) has rank \\(\\le \\mathrm{rank}(W_0)\\cdot r\\), so the update is high-rank at LoRA's parameter cost. LoRA is recovered by replacing \\(W_0\\) with the all-ones matrix.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0 + W_0 \\odot BA\\) is precomputed for deployment; the original weight can be recovered by element-wise division by \\(1 + BA\\).","identity_at_init":"One factor is zero (the paper zero-initializes its \\(d\\times r\\) factor and Kaiming-initializes the other), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Multiply the pretrained matrix element-wise by a low-rank matrix. The Hadamard product with \\(W_0\\) raises the possible rank of the update (up to \\(\\mathrm{rank}(W_0)\\cdot r\\)) without extra parameters and carries the pretrained structure into the update.","results_note":"Reports gains over LoRA and other PEFT baselines on commonsense reasoning with Llama-2-7B and Llama-3-8B, plus further generation tasks, at LoRA's parameter count (ICLR 2025 oral).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"with W_0 replaced by the all-ones matrix, HiRA's update is LoRA's BA"},{"target":"glora","type":"special-case-of","note":"GLoRA's official code already implements W_0 + W_0 ⊙ A + B; HiRA is the case of a low-rank A and B = 0"},{"target":"liera","type":"equivalent-to","note":"the LieRA variant evaluated in its paper is the first-order form W_0 + W_0 (.) BA, the same parameterization as HiRA (concurrent work); LieRA's exact exponential form W_0 (.) exp(BA) differs"},{"target":"flora-batched","type":"competes-with","note":"both modulate W_0 entry-wise by a low-rank matrix; HiRA's J + BA has rank <= r+1"},{"target":"loha","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use Hadamard products to escape the rank bound; LoHa multiplies two learned low-rank factors."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same parameter budget r(d_in+d_out), but rank no longer bounded by r"}],"sources":["https://openreview.net/forum?id=TwJrTz9cRS","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/48c368f105e8145b945227b73255635a-Abstract-Conference.html","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/48c368f105e8145b945227b73255635a-Paper-Conference.pdf","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/hira/config.py","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/oral/31839","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/hira","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/hira/config.py","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=HiRA%20Hadamard%20High-Rank%20Adaptation","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/hira/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative","shape"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"huang2025hira","bibtex":"@inproceedings{huang2025hira,\n  title = {{HiRA: Parameter-Efficient Hadamard High-Rank Adaptation for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Huang and others},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=TwJrTz9cRS}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\((D_{W_0})_*\\) of LoRA's cone (Prop II.9(a)), with \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W\\le r\\,\\mathrm{rk}W_0\\).","base_point":"HF sets \\(B_0=0\\); the apex is inherited through the transport (\\(S_1=D_{W_0}\\{XA_0\\}\\)).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\): pushforward along a linear isomorphism preserves the gauge.","covariance":"Contains \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) and is never \\(GL\\times GL\\) (Prop II.9(e)).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\) for \\(W_0\\) without zeros. The transport depends on \\(\\theta_0\\), so this is not transported ReLoRA: two cycles give relative-update rank up to \\(2r+r^2\\) (Prop II.13)."},"categorical_reading":"HiRA is LoRA transported by the \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent diagonal automorphism \\(D_{W_0}:X\\mapsto W_0\\odot X\\), i.e. LoRA in the Hadamard frame of \\(W_0\\). It has LoRA's \\(d\\), gauge and deficit, but a different cut (rank up to \\(r\\,\\mathrm{rk}W_0\\)) and only monomial covariance.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"loha","rule":"A2","h":"h(B,A)=(U_0,V_0,B,A),\\ \\ U_0V_0=W_0/\\gamma\\ \\text{a fixed rank-}\\mathrm{rk}W_0\\text{ factorisation}","note":"HiRA is \\(\\mathrm{LoHa}_{\\mathrm{rk}W_0,r}\\) with its first Hadamard factor frozen at \\(W_0\\); the target is pointed at \\((U_0,V_0,0,A_0)\\), not at LoHa's random default."},{"target":"glora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B,A)=\\big(\\mathcal A\\text{-path}=BA,\\ \\text{all other GLoRA paths frozen at }0\\big)","note":"GLoRA's \\(W_0\\odot\\mathcal A\\) path with a LoRA-type \\(\\mathcal A\\) and every other path off is HiRA. HiRA's \\(B_0=0\\) pointing differs from GLoRA's zero down-projection default."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(\\rho_{\\max}=r\\,\\mathrm{rk}W_0>r\\)."},{"target":"ia3","test":"O2","reason":"(IA)\\(^3\\) maps into \\(\\mathrm{HiRA}_1\\) (A3), but not back: \\(d(\\mathrm{HiRA}_r)=r(m+n-r)>m\\)."}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"hra","name":"HRA","full_name":"Householder Reflection Adaptation","paper_title":"Bridging The Gap between Low-rank and Orthogonal Adaptation via Householder Reflection Adaptation","authors":"Yuan et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.17484","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17484","code_url":"https://github.com/DaShenZi721/HRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"HRAConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0H,\\ \\ H=\\prod_{i=1}^{r}\\Big(I-2\\frac{u_iu_i^\\top}{\\|u_i\\|^2}\\Big),\\ u_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}};\\ \\ \\text{optional } \\lambda\\|I-U^\\top U\\|_F^2 \\text{ (or Gram-Schmidt, apply\\_GS)}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{in}","locus":"linear and Conv2d layers (rotation of the input space)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"product of \\(r\\) Householder reflections (orthogonal) acting on the input side of \\(W_0\\); equivalently \\(W_0 + W_0U\\Gamma U^\\top\\), an 'adaptive LoRA'","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0 H\\) is a single dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"Reflection vectors initialized in identical pairs (\\(u_{2i} = u_{2i-1}\\)), so consecutive reflections cancel and \\(H = I\\); requires even \\(r\\) in PEFT.","key_idea":"A chain of \\(r\\) Householder reflections is simultaneously an orthogonal transform and a rank-\\(r\\) additive update, bridging OFT and LoRA.","results_note":"Paper reports better accuracy than LoRA/OFT-style baselines with fewer parameters on GLUE, LLaMA2-7B math reasoning, and subject-driven/controllable image generation.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"competes-with","note":"Orthogonal multiplicative update with a different (reflection-based) parameterization."},{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"W_0H - W_0 = W_0 U Gamma U^T has rank <= r, so HRA's reachable updates are a subset of rank-r additive updates (the paper calls HRA an 'adaptive LoRA')."},{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"orthogonal right action on W_0, generated by reflections instead of Cayley blocks"},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"the paper shows W_0 H^(r) = W_0 + A(W_0,B)B, an adaptive rank-r LoRA whose left factor depends on W_0"},{"target":"ether","type":"generalizes","note":"a block-diagonal ETHER with n blocks equals a product of n Householder reflections whose vectors have disjoint supports, i.e. HRA with r=n mutually orthogonal u_i (HRA also makes the identity reachable by pairing)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17484","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.17484","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/hra","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/hra/layer.py","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/95554","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/hra/config.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/hra/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Shen Yuan","Haotian Liu","Hongteng Xu"],"bibkey":"yuan2024bridging","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yuan2024bridging,\n  title = {{Bridging The Gap between Low-rank and Orthogonal Adaptation via Householder Reflection Adaptation}},\n  author = {Shen Yuan and Haotian Liu and Hongteng Xu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.17484},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17484}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Meet","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Thm II.7(a), for injective \\(W_0\\), even \\(r\\le n-2\\) and no Gram–Schmidt (HF's default): \\(\\mathrm{Im}=(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap W_0\\,SO(n)\\), an image-level meet (not a product), \\(d=rn-r(r+1)/2\\).","base_point":"HF's paired init has \\(\\dim S_1=kn-k(k+1)/2<d\\) (\\(k=r/2\\)): an apex (Thm II.7(b,c)).","gauge":"Generic fibre of dimension \\(r(r+1)/2\\), non-linear; the only exhibited Lie gauge is the \\(r\\) scalings of \\(u_i\\).","covariance":"A right orthogonal action on the input side: \\(GL\\times CO(n)\\).","rebasing":"Re-HRA (injective \\(W_0\\), even \\(r\\)) reaches \\(W_0\\,SO(n)\\) in \\(\\lceil n/r\\rceil\\) cycles (Thm V.3 cor. 5)."},"categorical_reading":"HRA is the image-level meet of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) with the full orthogonal orbit \\(W_0\\cdot SO(n)\\), and HF's paired initialisation is an apex, the multiplicative twin of LoRA's dead \\(A\\)-direction. Rebasing commutes with the meet, so re-merged HRA walks \\(W_0\\cdot SO(n)\\) as ReLoRA walks \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"u\\mapsto\\big(W_0B(u),\\ A(u)\\big),\\quad B(u)=\\big[-2H_{u_1}\\cdots H_{u_{i-1}}\\hat u_i\\big]_{i=1}^r,\\ A(u)=\\big[\\hat u_1^\\top;\\dots;\\hat u_r^\\top\\big]","note":"Telescoping R - I = sum_i H_1...H_{i-1}(H_i - I); LoRA_r pointed at h(q_0), where B_0 A_0 = 0 with both factors non-zero (outside the three strata)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"HRA reaches rotations with eigenvalue -1 (e.g. H_{e1} H_{e2}), which the Cayley map never produces; for block OFT its moved planes also break the partial Grams (Thm II.7(d)). The eigenvalue argument is at image level for injective W_0 (out_features >= in_features), the setting of Thm II.7."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Meet"},{"id":"ia3","name":"(IA)³","full_name":"Infused Adapter by Inhibiting and Amplifying Inner Activations","paper_title":"Few-Shot Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning is Better and Cheaper than In-Context Learning","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2205.05638","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05638","code_url":"https://github.com/r-three/t-few","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"IA3Config","formula_latex":"k=l_k\\odot(W_kx),\\ v=l_v\\odot(W_vx),\\ h_{ff}=W_2\\big(l_{ff}\\odot\\gamma(W_1x)\\big);\\ l_k,l_v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}},\\ l_{ff}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{ff}} \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"d_{out} \\text{ per key/value projection},\\ d_{ff} \\text{ per FFN}","locus":"outputs of key/value projections and inputs of the FFN down-projection (feedforward_modules)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"diagonal scaling: \\(W=\\mathrm{diag}(l)W_0\\) (output rows) or \\(W=W_0\\mathrm{diag}(l)\\) (FFN input columns)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Diagonal scalings fold into the rows/columns of \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Scaling vectors \\(l\\) initialized to 1.","key_idea":"Learn only rescaling vectors that inhibit or amplify inner activations; with the T-Few recipe this beats in-context learning cheaply.","results_note":"Paper reports T-Few (T0 + (IA)\\(^3\\)) outperforming GPT-3 in-context learning and reaching super-human performance on RAFT.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Vector-sized (rank-free) alternative with far fewer parameters."},{"target":"glora","type":"special-case-of","note":"For the key/value scalings, diag(l)W_0 equals GLoRA's vector-mode weight-scaling path (1+A)(.)W_0. (IA)^3's FFN input-column scaling is not covered by that path."},{"target":"flora-batched","type":"special-case-of","note":"the FLoRA paper describes IA3 as a 'rank 0.5' FLoRA that rescales only columns"},{"target":"ssf","type":"competes-with","note":"same per-channel scaling idea; SSF adds a shift and targets vision backbones"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05638","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/ia3","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/ia3/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2205.05638","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/ia3/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Haokun Liu","Derek Tam","Mohammed Muqeeth","Jay Mohta","Tenghao Huang","Mohit Bansal","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"liu2022few","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2022few,\n  title = {{Few-Shot Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning is Better and Cheaper than In-Context Learning}},\n  author = {Haokun Liu and Derek Tam and Mohammed Muqeeth and Jay Mohta and Tenghao Huang and Mohit Bansal and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2205.05638},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.05638}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0\\) on \\(k,v\\) and \\(W_0\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)\\) on \\(W_{\\rm down}\\): the closure of a torus orbit, a linear space.","covariance":"\\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times GL\\) on output-scaled boxes, \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) on \\(W_{\\rm down}\\) (Prop II.9(e)).","merge":"Fuses into \\(W_K,W_V,W_{\\rm down}\\) for every \\(\\sigma\\); into \\(W_{\\rm up}\\) only under SwiGLU (any \\(\\ell\\)) or ReLU (\\(\\ell>0\\)), never GELU (Thm VI.3)."},"categorical_reading":"(IA)\\(^3\\) is the left (or right) torus acting on \\(W_0\\); its orbit closure is linear, keeps the zeros of \\(W_0\\), and fixes row lines while scales are non-zero (Thm II.5(4)). It is the lowest rung \\(W_0\\odot(J+Z)\\) of the torus ladder, a sub-object of HiRA\\(_1\\) at a non-default pointing, and it merges only into a neighbouring box by the calculus of Thm VI.3.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"\\ell\\mapsto(\\ell-\\mathbf 1,\\ \\mathbf 1^\\top)\\ \\ (k,v);\\qquad \\ell\\mapsto(\\mathbf 1,\\ (\\ell-\\mathbf 1)^\\top)\\ \\ (W_{\\rm down})","note":"HiRA_1 pointed at (0, 1^T) resp. (1, 0), not HiRA's random-A default pointing (Prop II.9(b))."},{"target":"dora","rule":"A2","h":"\\ell\\mapsto\\big(B=0,\\ A=A_0,\\ \\mu=\\ell\\odot(\\|w_{0,i}\\|)_i\\big)","note":"On the output-scaled k, v boxes only: DoRA with B, A frozen at their base values is the output-scaling method; on W_down (IA)^3 scales the input side, which DoRA's magnitude cannot."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The rank of diag(l - 1) W_0 reaches rank W_0 > r; conversely LoRA does not map to (IA)^3 (O1)."},{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"Scales change neuron norms, which exact OFT preserves."},{"target":"road","test":"O1","reason":"RoAd_1 ties the scale within each rotated output pair, so independent scalings of the two rows of a pair lie outside Im RoAd_1 (they lie in RoAd_2/4)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"ln-tuning","name":"LN Tuning","full_name":"LayerNorm Tuning","paper_title":"Tuning LayerNorm in Attention: Towards Efficient Multi-Modal LLM Finetuning","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2312.11420","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11420","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LNTuningConfig","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{LN}(x)=\\gamma\\odot\\frac{x-\\mu(x)}{\\sigma(x)}+\\beta,\\ \\ \\text{only } \\gamma,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} \\text{ of (attention-block) LayerNorms trained}","trainable_params":"2d \\text{ per LayerNorm } (\\gamma,\\beta);\\ d \\text{ per RMSNorm (no } \\beta)","locus":"LayerNorm gains and biases","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"per-channel affine rescaling/shift of normalized activations (diagonal), applied by training existing LayerNorm parameters","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing parameters change; the architecture is untouched.","identity_at_init":"Trivially identity: starts from the pretrained \\(\\gamma\\) and \\(\\beta\\).","key_idea":"Turning an LLM into a multimodal LLM is largely a domain-adaptation problem that LayerNorm parameters alone can absorb.","results_note":"Paper reports, on 13B MLLMs, about 20% better average performance than LoRA across five multimodal benchmarks with 41.9% fewer trainable parameters and 17.6% less GPU memory.","relations":[{"target":"bitfit","type":"competes-with","note":"Both tune tiny existing parameter subsets (norms vs biases)."},{"target":"ia3","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are diagonal per-channel rescalings."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"main baseline in the paper"},{"target":"ia3","type":"dual-to","note":"Changing a pre-norm gain gamma is a right multiplication of the next projection, W diag(gamma) (input-channel scaling); (IA)^3 scales outputs, diag(l) W (left multiplication). The two are transposes of each other."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11420","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.11420","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/layernorm_tuning","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2024/poster/18422","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/ln_tuning/config.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/layernorm_tuning","https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08682"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative","selective"],"authors_full":["Bingchen Zhao","Haoqin Tu","Chen Wei","Jieru Mei","Cihang Xie"],"bibkey":"zhao2023tuning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2023tuning,\n  title = {{Tuning LayerNorm in Attention: Towards Efficient Multi-Modal LLM Finetuning}},\n  author = {Bingchen Zhao and Haoqin Tu and Chen Wei and Jieru Mei and Cihang Xie},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2312.11420},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11420}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Trains a coordinate block of \\(\\theta_0\\) itself: \\(\\gamma,\\beta\\) of the targeted norm layers.","shape":"The identity method on the coordinate subspace of norm parameters.","covariance":"VIII.B: Mon. The gain is a diagonal (privileged-basis) parameter; folded into the next box it is an input-side scaling, \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) (Prop II.9(b)).","merge":"Trivially mergeable as a selective method (it replaces the norm parameters); folding \\(\\gamma\\), \\(\\beta\\) into the next linear box (M1->) needs a pre-norm block and a bias slot, and never works into a tied embedding/LM head (Thm VI.3)."},"categorical_reading":"LN-tuning is a selective sub-object of full fine-tuning: it frees the coordinates \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) of the norm layers. By norm absorption (R6) a pre-norm gain is an input-side scaling of the consuming projections, tied across all of them: the transpose of (IA)\\(^3\\)'s output scaling.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"ssf","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(\\gamma'=\\gamma\\oslash\\gamma_0,\\ \\beta'=\\beta-(\\gamma\\oslash\\gamma_0)\\odot\\beta_0\\big)\\ \\text{on SSF's post-norm wrappers; other wrappers at }(\\mathbf 1,0)","note":"Requires gamma_0 without zero entries and an SSF that wraps every norm that LN-tuning trains; SSF pointed at its exact identity (its official N(1, 0.02) init is a small defect). rho_SSF o h = rho_LN holds in Theta when SSF's post-norm scale-shift is realised by folding into that norm's (gamma, beta). If it is folded into the next linear box instead, the identity holds only at function level."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"bitfit","test":"O1","reason":"LN-gain edits act as column scalings of the consuming boxes (and move different coordinates of Theta), not as bias translations; neither image contains the other."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"loha","name":"LoHa","full_name":"LoHa (Low-rank adaptation with Hadamard product; FedPara parameterization)","paper_title":"FedPara: Low-Rank Hadamard Product for Communication-Efficient Federated Learning","authors":"Hyeon-Woo et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2108.06098","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.06098","code_url":"https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/LyCORIS","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoHaConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\gamma\\,(B_1A_1)\\odot(B_2A_2),\\quad B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ (k=1,2),\\ \\gamma=\\alpha/r;\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear or convolutional layer; popular on Stable Diffusion UNet/text-encoder (LyCORIS)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Hadamard (entrywise) product of two rank-\\(r\\) matrices; \\(\\mathrm{rank}(\\Delta W)\\le r^2\\) (FedPara Prop. 1) at the parameter cost of a rank-\\(2r\\) LoRA. Equivalently \\((B_1\\odot_r B_2)(A_1^{\\top}\\odot_r A_2^{\\top})^{\\top}\\): a rank-\\(r^2\\) factorization through row-wise Khatri-Rao products. Conv kernels can use a Tucker-style core variant (FedPara Prop. 3, use_effective_conv2d).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is an explicit dense \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\) after training.","identity_at_init":"HF PEFT: three factors Kaiming-uniform and one (hada_w2_b) zero so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\); the original LyCORIS code uses normal init with one factor zeroed.","key_idea":"Entrywise-multiplying two low-rank matrices multiplies their ranks, so a LoRA-sized budget yields an update of rank up to \\(r^2\\). Proposed for communication-efficient federated learning (FedPara) and turned into a fine-tuning adapter by LyCORIS.","results_note":"FedPara matches original-layer accuracy with 3-10x lower federated communication; as a PEFT adapter LoHa is benchmarked mainly for text-to-image customization in the LyCORIS study (ICLR 2024).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"each Hadamard factor is a LoRA product BA"},{"target":"lokr","type":"competes-with","note":"sibling LyCORIS adapter: Hadamard vs Kronecker product of factors"},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"HiRA replaces one trainable Hadamard factor by the frozen W_0"},{"target":"kradapter","type":"generalizes","note":"KRAdapter's [U ⊙_KR V]_{1:d_out} equals LoHa's (B_1A_1)⊙(B_2A_2) with A_1=U, A_2=V (ranks k_1,k_2) and B_1,B_2 frozen 0/1 row selectors (row j picks e_a, e_b with (a,b) the j-th Kronecker index), since (B_1A_1)⊙(B_2A_2)=(B_1⊙_r B_2)(A_1⊙_{KR}A_2)"},{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"Squared-rank capacity from two LoRA-style factors combined multiplicatively."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.06098","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2108.06098","https://arxiv.org/html/2309.14859","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/loha/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/loha/config.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2108.06098","https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/LyCORIS/blob/main/lycoris/modules/loha.py","https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14859","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/loha"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape"],"authors_full":["Nam Hyeon-Woo","Moon Ye-Bin","Tae-Hyun Oh"],"bibkey":"hyeonwoo2021fedpara","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hyeonwoo2021fedpara,\n  title = {{FedPara: Low-Rank Hadamard Product for Communication-Efficient Federated Learning}},\n  author = {Nam Hyeon-Woo and Moon Ye-Bin and Tae-Hyun Oh},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2108.06098},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.06098}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GLxTorus","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Hadamard variety of dimension at most \\(\\min(mn,\\,r_1(m+n-r_1)+r_2(m+n-r_2)-(m+n-1))\\), with equality checked numerically below the \\(mn\\) cap, and rank \\(\\le\\min(r_1r_2,m,n)\\) (table, Prop II.12).","base_point":"HF zero-initialises hada_w2_b, so only that factor moves to first order (Lemma II.4).","gauge":"\\(GL_{r_1}\\times GL_{r_2}\\) times the two-sided torus sliding through the copy spider.","covariance":"Hadamard structure in the standard basis: \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\) (table)."},"categorical_reading":"LoHa is the Hadamard (copy-spider) composite of two LoRA cones; the face-splitting map exhibits it as factoring through \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1r_2}\\). It reaches high rank on a thin curved variety, because a whole two-sided torus is spent on gauge: rank is a cut, dimension a volume. Its Hadamard product involves no \\(\\theta_0\\), so it is filed under R-additive (HiRA is the R-Hadamard row).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)=\\big(\\gamma\\,B_1\\bullet B_2,\\;(A_1^\\top\\bullet A_2^\\top)^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1r_2}\\) by the face-splitting (row-wise Khatri-Rao) product. HF zero-initialises \\(A_2\\) (hada_w2_b, \\(r\\times d_{in}\\)), so \\(h(q_0)=(\\gamma B_1^0\\bullet B_2^0,\\,0)\\): the target is pointed at \\(B_0\\ne0\\), \\(A_0=0\\), stratum \\(T_B\\) when \\(B_1^0\\bullet B_2^0\\) has full column rank [Num]. For \\(r_1=r_2=r\\le2\\), \\(r^2\\le2r\\), so LoHa is dominated by the equal-budget \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r}\\) (Prop II.12(c))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(\\mathrm{LoHa}_{r_1,r_2}\\) does not map into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r'}\\) for \\(r'<\\min(r_1r_2,m,n)\\) (generic rank \\(r_1r_2\\))."},{"target":"hira","test":"O1","reason":"HiRA's image keeps every zero of \\(W_0\\) (Prop II.9(e)), while LoHa's update does not depend on \\(W_0\\) and fills them. For \\(W_0\\) without zeros, \\(\\mathrm{Im\\,HiRA}_r=W_0\\odot\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), and a generic LoHa update divided entrywise by a generic \\(W_0\\) has full rank [Num: rank 10 of 10 already for \\(\\mathrm{LoHa}_{1,1}\\)], so \\(\\mathrm{Im\\,LoHa}\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im\\,HiRA}_r\\) for every \\(r<\\min(m,n)\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lokr","name":"LoKr","full_name":"LoKr (Low-rank adaptation with Kronecker product)","paper_title":"Navigating Text-To-Image Customization: From LyCORIS Fine-Tuning to Model Evaluation","authors":"Yeh et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.14859","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14859","code_url":"https://github.com/KohakuBlueleaf/LyCORIS","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoKrConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\gamma\\,C\\otimes(BA),\\quad C\\in\\mathbb{R}^{u_p\\times u_q},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{v_p\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times v_q},\\ u_pv_p=d_{out},\\ u_qv_q=d_{in};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ C,B,A\\ \\text{trained (C optionally factorized: decompose\\_both)}","trainable_params":"u_pu_q + r(v_p+v_q)\\ \\text{(right factor factorized, default when } r<\\max(v_p,v_q)/2\\text{)};\\ r(u_p+u_q)+r(v_p+v_q)\\ \\text{(decompose\\_both)};\\ u_pu_q+v_pv_q\\ \\text{(neither factorized)}","locus":"any linear or convolutional layer; widely used for diffusion-model customization","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Kronecker product of a small dense block \\(C\\) with a low-rank block \\(BA\\): \\(\\mathrm{rank}(\\Delta W)=\\mathrm{rank}(C)\\,\\mathrm{rank}(BA)\\le \\min(u_p,u_q)\\,r\\). Block sizes: paper Eq. 5 sets \\(u_p\\) = largest divisor of \\(d_{out}\\) not exceeding \\(\\min(f, \\sqrt{d_{out}})\\); HF's factorization() returns \\((f, d/f)\\) when \\(f\\) divides \\(d\\) and otherwise the divisor pair nearest \\(\\sqrt{d}\\) subject to the smaller factor \\(\\le f\\) (default \\(f = -1\\), i.e. near-square).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(C \\otimes (BA)\\) is materialised as a dense matrix of \\(W_0\\)'s shape and added to it.","identity_at_init":"HF PEFT zero-initializes the left Kronecker factor \\(C\\) (lokr_w1) and Kaiming-initializes the right factors, so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Ranks multiply under the Kronecker product, so a tiny dense block times a low-rank block gives a high-rank, block-structured update with very few parameters.","results_note":"Evaluated alongside LoRA and LoHa in the LyCORIS study of Stable Diffusion customization, which reports method-dependent trade-offs in fidelity, diversity and controllability rather than a single winner.","relations":[{"target":"krona","type":"generalizes","note":"with the right factor left unfactorized LoKr is exactly KronA's A⊗B"},{"target":"loha","type":"competes-with","note":"sibling LyCORIS adapter"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"the right Kronecker factor is a LoRA product"},{"target":"krona","type":"builds-on","note":"Kronecker-product adapters (KronA) with an extra low-rank factor."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/html/2309.14859","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lokr/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lokr/config.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2309.14859","https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14859","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lokr"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape"],"authors_full":["Shih-Ying Yeh","Yu-Guan Hsieh","Zhidong Gao","Bernard B W Yang","Giyeong Oh","Yanmin Gong"],"bibkey":"yeh2023navigating","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yeh2023navigating,\n  title = {{Navigating Text-To-Image Customization: From LyCORIS Fine-Tuning to Model Evaluation}},\n  author = {Shih-Ying Yeh and Yu-Guan Hsieh and Zhidong Gao and Bernard B W Yang and Giyeong Oh and Yanmin Gong},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2309.14859},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14859}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(C\\otimes BA\\) is \\(\\mathcal R^{-1}_*\\) of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\) with its second factor constrained to \\(\\mathrm{vec}\\,\\mathcal M_{\\le r'}\\) (Prop II.13); \\(\\rho_{\\max}=\\mathrm{rk}C\\cdot r'\\).","base_point":"HF zero-initialises \\(C\\) (lokr_w1): \\(S_1=\\{\\delta C\\otimes B_0A_0\\}\\), an apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_{r'}\\) on the inner bond times \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) between \\(C\\) and \\(BA\\) (table; axes.json files it under GL).","covariance":"Kronecker reshape: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","rebasing":"The \\(\\mathcal R^{-1}\\)-transported cone generates \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoKr is LoRA across a different cut of the same 4-leg tensor. The Van Loan-Pitsianis rearrangement \\(\\mathcal R\\) transports it to a rank-one LoRA with a low-rank second factor, so 'ranks multiply' is a statement about the ordinary cut, and LoKr with small inner rank sits inside \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{\\min(u_p,u_q)r'}\\) (Thm II.11(e)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,B,A)=\\big(C\\otimes B,\\;I_{u_q}\\otimes A\\big)","note":"By the mixed-product rule, into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{u_qr'}\\) pointed at \\((0,I_{u_q}\\otimes A_0)\\) (zero C); this is stratum \\(T_A\\) when \\(u_qr'<\\min(m,n)\\). The mirror \\(h=(I_{u_p}\\otimes B,\\,C\\otimes A)\\) lands in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{u_pr'}\\)."},{"target":"krona","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,B,A)=(C,\\;BA)","note":"Into KronA with its second factor constrained to rank \\(r'\\); KronA pointed at \\((0,B_0A_0)\\), not at its dense Kaiming default."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(\\mathrm{LoKr}_{r'}\\) does not map into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) when \\(r<\\min(u_p,u_q)\\,r'\\): the maximum rank exceeds r (Thm II.11(e))."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"miss","name":"MiSS (formerly Bone)","full_name":"Matrix Shard Sharing (successor of Block-Affine Adaptation, Bone)","paper_title":"MiSS: Revisiting the Trade-off in LoRA with an Efficient Shard-Sharing Structure","authors":"Kang & Yin","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2409.15371","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.15371","code_url":"https://github.com/Joluck/MiSS","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"MissConfig (init_weights=True | 'bat' | 'mini'); BoneConfig removed in v0.19.0","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\Delta W,\\ \\ \\Delta W=[\\,D\\ D\\ \\cdots\\ D\\,]=D\\,(\\mathbf{1}^\\top_{d_{in}/r}\\otimes I_r),\\ D\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r};\\ \\text{equivalently } \\Delta W\\,x=D\\sum_{j=1}^{d_{in}/r}x^{(j)},\\ x^{(j)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r} \\text{ the } j\\text{-th input shard}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"shard sharing: one \\(d_{out}\\times r\\) matrix replicated across the \\(d_{in}/r\\) input shards (rank \\(\\le r\\)). Optional modes: 'bat' (Bone-style block-affine \\(W_0\\)-dependent update \\(W_{blk} D + D\\)) and 'mini' (\\(D\\) shared across output groups of size mini_r)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"expand(D) is a fixed dense matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"\\(D = 0\\).","key_idea":"A single small matrix shared across input shards keeps LoRA-like expressivity with lower compute, memory and no expensive initialization.","results_note":"Paper reports Llama2-7B GSM8K 48.16 vs 40.75 for LoRA and MATH 8.58 vs 5.22.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Single-matrix alternative with O(d_out r) parameters and cheaper FLOPs."},{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with one factor frozen to the 0/1 replication matrix"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"both freeze part of the factorization to cut trainable parameters"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"both freeze the down-projection; MiSS uses a deterministic summing projection"},{"target":"prolora","type":"competes-with","note":"both tile shared chunks to build Delta W"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.15371","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.15371","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/miss","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/miss/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.17.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.15371","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/miss/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/miss.md","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2409.15371","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=MiSS%20Shard-Sharing","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.15371v5","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/miss/config.py","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/file/e365c5fd3e67afdc398519a1de690068-Paper-Conference.pdf","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape","sharing"],"authors_full":["Jiale Kang","Qingyu Yin"],"bibkey":"kang2024miss","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kang2024miss,\n  title = {{MiSS: Revisiting the Trade-off in LoRA with an Efficient Shard-Sharing Structure}},\n  author = {Jiale Kang and Qingyu Yin},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2409.15371},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.15371}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Balance mode: \\(\\Delta W=D(\\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{in}/r}\\otimes I_r)\\), linear and injective, \\(d=r\\,d_{out}\\). The 'bat' mode adds \\((W_{blk}+I)D\\) blockwise: linear in \\(D\\) but \\(W_0\\)-dependent (bilinear in \\((W_0,D)\\)), so it is Lin but not additive (verified catalog: 'bilinear, not nonlinear'); 'mini' adds output-group tying.","covariance":"\\(GL\\) on the output side, the imposed input sharding \\(\\Pi\\) on the input side (table: \\(GL\\times\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Fixed summing projection: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"MiSS (balance mode) is isomorphic to LoRA-FA with the frozen summing projection \\(\\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{\\rm in}/r}\\otimes I_r\\): a linear sub-object of LoRA pointed at \\((0,\\mathbf 1^\\top\\otimes I_r)\\) whose covariance is the imposed shard structure \\(\\Pi\\). Its BAT mode \\((W_{\\rm blk}+I)D\\) is still linear in \\(D\\) but depends on \\(W_0\\), so it is not additive.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(D)=D,\\ \\ A_0=\\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{\\rm in}/r}\\otimes I_r","note":"Isomorphism MiSS = LoRA-FA(1^T (x) I_r) (axes.json A3 example)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(D)=(D,\\ \\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{\\rm in}/r}\\otimes I_r)","note":"Freezing A at the summing projection: monomorphism into LoRA pointed at (0, 1^T (x) I_r)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O2","reason":"d(MiSS)=r d_out > d(VeRA)=d_out+r-1 for r>=2, although both freeze part of the factorisation."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"multitask-prompt-tuning","name":"MPT","full_name":"Multitask Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Multitask Prompt Tuning Enables Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2303.02861","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02861","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"MultitaskPromptTuningConfig","formula_latex":"P_k=P^*\\odot(u_kv_k^\\top),\\ \\ P^*\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d} \\text{ shared (distilled from source tasks)},\\ u_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell},v_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d};\\ \\text{input}=[P_k;E(x)]","trainable_params":"\\ell d+\\ell+d \\text{ per target task}","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"soft prompt = Hadamard product of a shared prompt matrix and a task-specific rank-one matrix","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Prompt tokens extend the input sequence; they are not a weight change.","identity_at_init":"Not an identity (adds tokens); target prompts initialized from the distilled shared source prompt.","key_idea":"Distill many source-task prompts into one shared prompt and adapt it to each target task with a cheap multiplicative rank-one update.","results_note":"Paper reports SuperGLUE average 74.1 vs 57.8 for vanilla prompt tuning while tuning 0.035% task-specific parameters.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Soft prompt with multitask-shared structure."},{"target":"spot","type":"builds-on","note":"Prompt transfer from source tasks, made multiplicative and multitask."},{"target":"attempt","type":"competes-with","note":"Distils sources into one prompt instead of mixing them per instance."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02861","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.02861","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/multitask_prompt_tuning","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.02861","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/conceptual_guides/prompting","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/multitask_prompt_tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Zhen Wang","Rameswar Panda","Leonid Karlinsky","Rogerio Feris","Huan Sun","Yoon Kim"],"bibkey":"wang2023multitask","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2023multitask,\n  title = {{Multitask Prompt Tuning Enables Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Zhen Wang and Rameswar Panda and Leonid Karlinsky and Rogerio Feris and Huan Sun and Yoon Kim},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2303.02861},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02861}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"With \\(P^*\\) also trained on the target (paper's \\(\\ell d+\\ell+d\\) budget; HF trains embedding.weight), \\(P^*\\odot uv^\\top\\) ranges over all of \\(\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}\\): the prompt image is that of prompt tuning, \\(d = \\ell d\\).","base_point":"Unpointed; pointed at the distilled shared prompt \\(P^*\\) and source-task rank-one factors.","gauge":"Disagrees with the table's \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\): with \\(P^*\\) trainable the generic fibre is the torus \\((P^*,u,v)\\mapsto(P^*\\odot ab^\\top, u\\oslash a, v\\oslash b)\\), \\(a\\in(\\mathbb R^\\times)^\\ell\\), \\(b\\in(\\mathbb R^\\times)^d\\), of dimension \\(\\ell+d\\) (it contains the scalar slide). \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) is correct only with \\(P^*\\) frozen. HF's num_ranks=\\(r>1\\) gives \\(GL_r\\times\\) this torus (LoHa-type).","covariance":"Image-level: the image is the full prompt space, so basis-free at function level; the Hadamard parameterisation privileges coordinates only in the dynamics, not in the image.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"With the shared prompt also trained, \\(\\rho(P^*,u,v)=P^*\\odot uv^\\top\\) is a Hadamard reparametrisation of prompt tuning: its image is all of \\(\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}\\) and its gauge is the torus \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^\\ell\\times(\\mathbb R^\\times)^d\\). It is expressively equal to prompt tuning and differs only in the cometric and in the pointing at the distilled \\(P^*\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P^*,u,v)=P^*\\odot uv^\\top","note":"Prompt tuning pointed at \\(P^*_0\\odot u_0v_0^\\top\\); h is the quotient by the torus gauge."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"oft","name":"OFT","full_name":"Orthogonal Finetuning","paper_title":"Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning","authors":"Qiu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2306.07280","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07280","code_url":"https://github.com/Zeju1997/oft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"OFTConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0R^\\top,\\ \\ R=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(R_1,\\dots,R_n)\\in O(d_{in}),\\ R_i=(I+Q_i)(I-Q_i)^{-1},\\ Q_i^\\top=-Q_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times b},\\ n=d_{in}/b;\\ \\text{COFT: }\\|Q\\|\\le\\epsilon","trainable_params":"n\\,\\tfrac{b(b-1)}{2}=\\tfrac{d_{in}(b-1)}{2} \\ \\ (\\text{or one shared block if block\\_share})","locus":"linear, Conv2d and Embedding layers in PEFT (rotation of the input space of \\(W_0\\))","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"block-diagonal orthogonal \\(R\\in O(d_{in})\\) (Cayley-parameterized) multiplying \\(W_0\\), preserving hyperspherical energy (pairwise angles between neurons)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0 R^T\\) is a dense matrix that replaces \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(Q = 0\\), so \\(R = I\\).","key_idea":"Fine-tune by rotating neurons rather than adding to them, which provably preserves the pairwise angular structure of pretrained weights.","results_note":"Paper reports better generation quality and faster convergence than existing methods on subject-driven and controllable text-to-image generation.","relations":[{"target":"boft","type":"special-case-of","note":"BOFT with a single butterfly factor."},{"target":"hra","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative orthogonal parameterization."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"OFT saves parameters with a sparse block-diagonal multiplicative structure where LoRA uses a low-rank additive one (128x128 example: OFT r=8 needs 960 vs LoRA r=8 2048 parameters)"},{"target":"oftv2","type":"equivalent-to","note":"with exact Cayley (use_cayley_neumann=False) OFTv2 computes the same function as OFT and only changes the evaluation order (rotate the input, then apply W_0); with the truncated Neumann series it is an approximation of OFT"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07280","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/oft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/oft/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.07280","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/docs/source/package_reference/oft.md","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/oft/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Zeju Qiu","Weiyang Liu","Haiwen Feng","Yuxuan Xue","Yao Feng","Zhen Liu","Dan Zhang","Adrian Weller","Bernhard Schölkopf"],"bibkey":"qiu2023controlling","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qiu2023controlling,\n  title = {{Controlling Text-to-Image Diffusion by Orthogonal Finetuning}},\n  author = {Zeju Qiu and Weiyang Liu and Haiwen Feng and Yuxuan Xue and Yao Feng and Zhen Liu and Dan Zhang and Adrian Weller and Bernhard Schölkopf},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2306.07280},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07280}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Exact Cayley: \\(W_0\\cdot C(\\mathfrak{so}(b)^{n/b})^\\top\\), an orbit of \\(\\prod SO(b)\\) in the \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) level set.","base_point":"Cayley is a local diffeomorphism at 0 and the orbit map is injective for injective \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"VIII.B 'stabiliser': the stabiliser of \\(W_0\\) in \\(\\prod SO(b)\\). It is trivial when \\(W_0\\) is injective (out_features \\(\\ge\\) in_features, since HF acts on in_features). For wide \\(W_0\\) it is still trivial for generic \\(W_0\\) when \\(b\\le\\) out_features, because a generic kernel meets no \\(b\\)-dimensional coordinate block.","covariance":"The block normaliser in \\(CO(n)\\) on the input side, \\(GL\\) on the output side.","rebasing":"With a fixed partition and exact Cayley: idempotent up to closure (gains only the measure-zero eigenvalue \\(-1\\) set). HF's default Cayley-Neumann drifts (see oftv2)."},"categorical_reading":"OFT is an action-type object \\(\\rho(Q)=W_0\\,C(Q)^\\top\\) whose image is an orbit of the block-orthogonal group. It can never change \\(K_{\\rm out}\\), the spectrum or the block partial Grams (Cor II.6), however often it is re-merged. Its expressivity is that of a group orbit, regular at \\(\\theta_0\\), with the block normaliser as covariance.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"boft","rule":"A2","h":"Q\\mapsto(Q,\\ s=\\mathbf 1)\\ \\text{in BOFT}(1,b)","note":"BOFT with one butterfly factor and boft_s frozen at its base value 1."},{"target":"goft","rule":"A3","h":"t\\mapsto\\big(\\theta^{(1)}_k=2\\arctan t_k,\\ \\theta^{(\\ell)}=0\\ (\\ell\\ge2)\\big)","note":"Block size b = 2 only: the 2x2 Cayley blocks are GOFT's first Givens layer on the pairs (2k, 2k+1) (up to GOFT's sign convention)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oftv2","test":"O1","reason":"HF's Cayley-Neumann R = C(Q)(I - Q^4) is orthogonal only when Q^4 has eigenvalues in {0, 2}, so near theta_0 the two images meet only at theta_0."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"rank(R - I) reaches n > r."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"oftv2","name":"OFTv2","full_name":"Orthogonal Finetuning v2 (input-centric OFT with Cayley-Neumann parameterization; QOFT for quantized models)","paper_title":"Orthogonal Finetuning Made Scalable","authors":"Qiu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"EMNLP 2025","arxiv":"2506.19847","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19847","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"OFTConfig (use_cayley_neumann=True is the default; num_cayley_neumann_terms=5)","formula_latex":"h = W_0\\,(R^{\\top}x),\\quad R=\\operatorname{diag}(R_1,\\dots,R_r),\\ R_i=(I+Q_i)\\Big(I+\\sum_{k=1}^{K}Q_i^{k}\\Big)\\approx(I+Q_i)(I-Q_i)^{-1},\\ Q_i=-Q_i^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times b};\\ \\text{QOFT: } h=\\mathrm{Dequant}(W_q)\\,(R^{\\top}x);\\ W_0 \\text{ (or } W_q) \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{d_{in}(b-1)}{2},\\ b=d_{in}/r","locus":"any linear layer (input activations are rotated before the frozen matmul); works on top of quantized base weights","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"the same block-diagonal (now only approximately) orthogonal action as OFT, evaluated as an action on the input vector \\(x\\) (two matrix-vector products, \\(O(nd + d^2)\\)) instead of on \\(W_0\\) (matrix-matrix, \\(O(nd^2)\\)). The truncated Neumann series makes the Cayley map only approximately orthogonal.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"For a full-precision base, \\(R\\) can be folded into the weight (\\(W_0 R^T\\)); for QOFT, merging requires dequantizing and re-quantizing the merged weight.","identity_at_init":"\\(Q = 0\\) gives \\(R = I\\).","key_idea":"Rotate the input instead of the weight, which cuts OFT's cost from cubic to quadratic. Replace the matrix inverse in the Cayley transform with a truncated Neumann series. Because \\(R\\) acts on activations, the method runs on any quantized backbone (QOFT).","results_note":"Reports up to 10x faster training and 3x lower GPU memory than the original OFT, and better results than QLoRA when fine-tuning quantized Qwen2.5 models (0.5B-72B) for math reasoning; also applied to Stable Diffusion 3.5.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"same group action; input-centric evaluation and Cayley-Neumann approximation"},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"QOFT targets the same quantized-base setting as QLoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19847","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.19847","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/oft/config.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/oft/layer.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/oft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.16.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Zeju Qiu","Weiyang Liu","Adrian Weller","Bernhard Schölkopf"],"bibkey":"qiu2025orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qiu2025orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Finetuning Made Scalable}},\n  author = {Zeju Qiu and Weiyang Liu and Adrian Weller and Bernhard Schölkopf},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2506.19847},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.19847}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The input-centric evaluation \\(W_0(R^\\top x)\\) is the same \\(\\rho\\) as \\(W_0R^\\top\\); evaluation order is invisible to Meth.","shape":"Action type: \\(\\gamma(Q)=C(Q)(I-Q^4)\\in GL^+(b)^{n/b}\\) acting on \\(W_0\\); the image is a \\(b(b-1)/2\\)-per-block slice through \\(GL^+(b)\\), not a subgroup orbit (axes.json: Orb, only approximately orthogonal).","base_point":"\\(d\\gamma_0=2\\,\\mathrm{id}\\) on skew matrices.","covariance":"The Neumann map is a polynomial, so conjugation by the orthogonal block normaliser commutes with it: \\(GL\\times N_{\\rm blk}\\).","merge":"M1 for a full-precision base; QOFT on a quantised base is Mq (dequantise, merge, requantise).","rebasing":"Cayley-Neumann factors generate \\(\\prod GL^+(b)\\) (\\(\\prod CO^+(2)\\) for \\(b=2\\)): \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) and the spectrum drift over merges (Thm V.3 cor. 4)."},"categorical_reading":"OFTv2 (HF's default OFTConfig) is OFT with the Cayley map replaced by its truncated Neumann series, \\(R=C(Q)(I-Q^4)\\). This is a quasi-orthogonal action whose image leaves the \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) level set at order \\(\\|Q\\|^4\\) and whose rebasing closure is \\(\\prod GL^+(b)\\) rather than \\(\\prod SO(b)\\). With exact Cayley it is the same object as OFT.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"qgoft","rule":"A3","h":"t\\mapsto\\Big(\\tilde G^{(1)}_k=(1-t_k^2)\\begin{pmatrix}1-t_k^2&-2t_k\\\\2t_k&1-t_k^2\\end{pmatrix},\\ \\tilde G^{(\\ell)}=I\\ (\\ell\\ge2)\\Big)","note":"Block size b = 2 only: a 2x2 Cayley-Neumann block is a scaled rotation in CO^+(2), i.e. a quasi-Givens block of qGOFT's first layer."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"Cayley-Neumann images are not orthogonal (R^T R = (I - Q^4)^2), so they change K_out; near theta_0 the images of OFT and OFTv2 meet only at theta_0."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"osf","name":"OSF","full_name":"Orthogonal Subspace Fine-tuning (adaptive-SVD constrained full fine-tuning)","paper_title":"Sculpting Subspaces: Constrained Full Fine-Tuning in LLMs for Continual Learning","authors":"Nayak et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2504.07097","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07097","code_url":"https://github.com/Red-Hat-AI-Innovation-Team/orthogonal-subspace-learning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"OSFConfig","formula_latex":"W_0=U_{hi}\\Sigma_{hi}V_{hi}^\\top+U_{lo}\\Sigma_{lo}V_{lo}^\\top;\\ U_{hi},\\Sigma_{hi},V_{hi} \\text{ frozen};\\ U_{lo},\\Sigma_{lo},V_{lo} \\text{ trained with projected gradients } \\nabla_{U_{lo}}\\leftarrow(I-U_{hi}U_{hi}^\\top)\\nabla_{U_{lo}},\\ \\nabla_{V_{lo}^\\top}\\leftarrow\\nabla_{V_{lo}^\\top}(I-V_{hi}V_{hi}^\\top)","trainable_params":"k(d_{out}+d_{in}+1),\\ \\ k=\\min(d_{in},d_{out})-\\text{effective\\_rank}","locus":"any linear layer ('all-linear' supported)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"SVD split of \\(W_0\\): top singular subspace (important for prior tasks) frozen; minor singular components trained with gradients projected orthogonally to the frozen subspace","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The frozen and trained SVD components recombine into one dense \\(W\\).","identity_at_init":"Trainable components initialized from \\(W_0\\)'s own SVD, so \\(W = W_0\\) at init.","key_idea":"For continual learning, update only directions orthogonal to the dominant singular subspaces of earlier tasks, giving full-rank-capacity updates with little forgetting.","results_note":"Paper reports 71.3 vs 69.6 (O-LoRA) on the 15-task T5-Large benchmark and 48.4 vs 41.3 on TRACE with LLaMA-2 7B.","relations":[{"target":"o-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use orthogonality to prior-task subspaces; OSF does it within full-rank SVD components."},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both train minor singular components of W_0."},{"target":"psoft","type":"dual-to","note":"PSOFT adapts only inside the principal subspace; OSF freezes it and adapts its complement"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07097","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.07097","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/osf","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/osf/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/osf/config.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/docs/source/package_reference/osf.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Nikhil Shivakumar Nayak","Krishnateja Killamsetty","Ligong Han","Abhishek Bhandwaldar","Prateek Chanda","Kai Xu","Hao Wang","Aldo Pareja","Oleg Silkin","Mustafa Eyceoz","Akash Srivastava"],"bibkey":"nayak2025sculpting","bibtex":"@inproceedings{nayak2025sculpting,\n  title = {{Sculpting Subspaces: Constrained Full Fine-Tuning in LLMs for Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Nikhil Shivakumar Nayak and Krishnateja Killamsetty and Ligong Han and Abhishek Bhandwaldar and Prateek Chanda and Kai Xu and Hao Wang and Aldo Pareja and Oleg Silkin and Mustafa Eyceoz and Akash Srivastava},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2504.07097},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07097}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map \\(W_{hi}+U_{lo}\\Sigma_{lo}V_{lo}^\\top\\) is a reparametrisation; the projected gradients are optimizer data (a backward compression \\(c_t\\)).","shape":"With \\(U_{lo}\\perp U_{hi}\\), \\(V_{lo}\\perp V_{hi}\\) enforced and \\(k=\\min(m,n)-e\\), the image is \\(\\theta_0+\\{U_\\perp ZV_\\perp^\\top\\}\\), a linear block of dimension \\((m-e)(n-e)\\) in the SVD frame (rank \\(\\le k\\) is automatic). This holds for SGD-type rules; Adam's elementwise preconditioner leaves the projected subspace, and the object is then the split cone \\(W_{hi}+\\mathcal M_{\\le k}\\) (Cone*).","base_point":"Initialised at \\(W_0\\)'s own SVD with \\(\\Sigma_{lo}\\) invertible: regular.","gauge":"\\(U\\Sigma V^\\top\\) with a trainable diagonal \\(\\Sigma\\): the generic fibre has dimension \\(k^2+k\\); only the torus \\((D_1,D_1^{-1}\\Sigma D_2^{-1},D_2)\\) acts linearly, the rest is absorption of \\(\\Sigma\\), as in AdaLoRA's \\(P\\Lambda Q\\).","covariance":"SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"Frames are frozen within a task, so idempotent; the continual-learning protocol recomputes the SVD per task and shrinks the trainable block (a schedule across tasks)."},"categorical_reading":"OSF is a split (MiLoRA-type) pointing of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_k\\) with \\(k\\) half the rank, plus a backward projection that confines the factors to the complements of the frozen top singular subspaces. Under that projection its reach is the linear minor block \\(\\theta_0+U_\\perp(\\cdot)V_\\perp^\\top\\) of the SVD frame, the exact dual of PSOFT, which adapts only inside the principal block.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"milora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,\\Sigma,V)\\mapsto\\big(U\\,\\Sigma\\,S_{lo}^{-1/2},\\ S_{lo}^{1/2}V^\\top\\big)","note":"MiLoRA_k with k = min(m,n) - effective_rank, pointed at (U_lo S_lo^{1/2}, S_lo^{1/2} V_lo^T); holds with or without the gradient projection."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"psoft","test":"O1","reason":"OSF moves only the minor block U_perp (x) V_perp, PSOFT only the principal block U_r (x) V_r: near theta_0 the images meet only at theta_0. This needs PSOFT's rank r <= effective_rank e, so that PSOFT's principal block lies inside OSF's frozen top block."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"p-tuning","name":"P-Tuning","full_name":"P-Tuning (continuous prompts with a prompt encoder)","paper_title":"GPT Understands, Too","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"AI Open 2024","arxiv":"2103.10385","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aiopen.2023.08.012","code_url":"https://github.com/THUDM/P-tuning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PromptEncoderConfig","formula_latex":"T=\\{[P_{0:i}],\\,x,\\,[P_{i+1:j}],\\,y,\\,[P_{j+1:k}]\\}\\ \\longmapsto\\ \\{h_0,\\dots,h_i,\\,e(x),\\,h_{i+1},\\dots,h_j,\\,e(y),\\,h_{j+1},\\dots,h_k\\},\\quad (h_0,\\dots,h_k)=f_\\psi(P_0,\\dots,P_k)\\ \\ (f_\\psi:\\ \\text{LSTM or MLP prompt encoder});\\ \\ P,\\psi\\ \\text{trained, LM frozen (or tuned)}","trainable_params":"\\ell d+|\\psi|\\ \\text{(encoder dropped after training, leaving } \\ell d\\text{)}","locus":"input embedding sequence, at arbitrary template positions interleaved with discrete anchor tokens","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Virtual-token embeddings produced by a shared reparametrization network \\(h=f_\\psi(P)\\) (bi-LSTM or MLP) that models dependencies between prompt tokens, inserted at template positions and concatenated with discrete prompt/anchor tokens.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The continuous prompt tokens lengthen the input; only the encoder can be discarded (its outputs cached as embeddings).","identity_at_init":"No identity: the continuous prompt embeddings are freshly (randomly) initialized and concatenated with discrete prompt/anchor tokens, which the paper finds improves performance. The paper ablates LSTM, MLP and identity (EMB) prompt encoders and finds EMB unstable.","key_idea":"Make prompts continuous and let a small encoder couple the prompt tokens, which stabilizes training against the large variance of discrete prompts and lets GPT-style models compete with BERT-style models on NLU.","results_note":"With a frozen LM on LAMA knowledge probing P-Tuning beats manual prompts by 20+ points and searched prompts by 9 points; with tuned LMs it beats PET on SuperGLUE in both fully supervised and few-shot settings.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"Prompt tuning = P-Tuning with identity encoder and all virtual tokens at the front."},{"target":"residual-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both reparametrize prompt embeddings through a shared network; Residual PT adds a skip connection."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Same input-level soft prompt, but reparameterized through an encoder."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Input-only vs every-layer prompts."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10385","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.10385","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/conceptual_guides/prompting","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/p_tuning","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/j.aiopen.2023.08.012","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/p_tuning","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Xiao Liu","Yanan Zheng","Zhengxiao Du","Ming Ding","Yujie Qian","Zhilin Yang","Jie Tang"],"bibkey":"liu2021gpt","bibtex":"@article{liu2021gpt,\n  title = {{GPT Understands, Too}},\n  author = {Xiao Liu and Yanan Zheng and Zhengxiao Du and Ming Ding and Yujie Qian and Zhilin Yang and Jie Tang},\n  year = {2021},\n  journal = {AI Open},\n  eprint = {2103.10385},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aiopen.2023.08.012}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Virtual tokens \\(h = f_\\psi(P)\\) at template positions (HF prepends them); once the encoder's last layer has width \\(\\ge \\ell\\) the prompt image is all of \\(\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}\\), as for prompt tuning (table: 'v1 adds an LSTM/MLP 2-cell').","gauge":"Training parameters \\((P, \\psi)\\) with \\(|\\psi| \\gg \\ell d\\): the generic fibre of \\((P,\\psi)\\mapsto f_\\psi(P)\\) has dimension \\(\\ell d+|\\psi|-\\ell d = |\\psi|\\) and is not an orbit of a linear group (ReLU/LSTM symmetries are a small part). After training only \\(\\ell d\\) embeddings are stored.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning precomposed with a 2-cell \\(\\sigma=f_\\psi\\) (LSTM or MLP encoder): the prompt image is unchanged and \\(\\sigma\\) only replaces the flat metric on prompt space by the point-dependent \\(D\\sigma\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\sigma^\\top\\) (Obs III.2). The encoder is a training device; the stored object is a soft prompt.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\psi)=f_\\psi(P)","note":"All-front template (HF PromptEncoder); prompt tuning pointed at \\(f_{\\psi_0}(P_0)\\). With interleaved anchors the same h lands in prompt tuning at the template positions."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"peanut","name":"PEANuT","full_name":"Parameter-Efficient Adaptation with Weight-aware Neural Tweakers","paper_title":"PEANuT: Parameter-Efficient Adaptation with Weight-aware Neural Tweakers","authors":"Zhong et al.","year":2024,"venue":"KDD 2026","arxiv":"2410.01870","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01870","code_url":"https://github.com/yibozhong/peanut","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PeanutConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0+f(W_0;\\theta),\\ \\ f(W_0;\\theta)^\\top=\\sigma(W_0^\\top\\Theta_1)\\,\\Theta_2,\\ \\Theta_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\Theta_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{out}} \\ (\\text{paper writes } \\sigma(W_0\\Theta_1)\\Theta_2 \\text{ with } W_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{out}};\\ \\text{optional depth: extra } r\\times r \\text{ encoder/decoder layers with residuals})","trainable_params":"2r\\,d_{out}+2\\,\\text{depth}\\,r^2","locus":"nn.Linear layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"nonlinear weight-conditioned update: a small MLP applied to the columns of \\(W_0\\) (one \\(d_{out}\\)-vector per input feature) produces \\(\\Delta W\\) (rank \\(\\le r\\))","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(f(W_0)\\) is computed once after training and added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Output projection \\(\\Theta_2\\) (\\(B\\)) zero-initialized.","key_idea":"Generate the update as a nonlinear function of the frozen weight itself instead of a weight-agnostic linear factorization.","results_note":"Paper reports consistent gains over strong PEFT baselines on four benchmarks spanning 20+ NLP and vision datasets at comparable or fewer parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"Paper theorem: with ReLU, PEANuT of rank 2r matches any rank-r LoRA (Theta_1 = [W_0^+A; -W_0^+A] uses sigma(x) - sigma(-x) = x), so its reachable set contains LoRA's."},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"Both condition the update on W_0 (nonlinear MLP vs Hadamard)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01870","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.01870","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/peanut","https://github.com/yibozhong/peanut","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Yibo Zhong","Haoxiang Jiang","Lincan Li","Ryumei Nakada","Tianci Liu","Linjun Zhang","Huaxiu Yao","Haoyu Wang"],"bibkey":"zhong2024peanut","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhong2024peanut,\n  title = {{PEANuT: Parameter-Efficient Adaptation with Weight-aware Neural Tweakers}},\n  author = {Yibo Zhong and Haoxiang Jiang and Lincan Li and Ryumei Nakada and Tianci Liu and Linjun Zhang and Huaxiu Yao and Haoyu Wang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining (KDD)},\n  eprint = {2410.01870},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01870}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Weight-space reparametrisation: \\(W=W_0+\\Theta_2^\\top\\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0)\\) (the catalog's \\(f^\\top=\\sigma(W_0^\\top\\Theta_1)\\Theta_2\\) transposed), run in the original architecture; the update is \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent, so the method is not additive in the sense of Def I.2.","shape":"The update is linear in \\(\\Theta_2\\), so the image is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) inside \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\). With GELU/SiLU and \\(W_0\\) of full column rank it is all of \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) (the range of \\(\\sigma\\) contains a neighbourhood of \\(0\\)); when \\(d_{out}+r\\le d_{in}\\) it is a proper sub-cone of dimension \\(2mr\\). With ReLU and \\(W_0\\) of full column rank its \\(A\\)-part \\(\\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0)\\) ranges over entrywise nonnegative factors.","base_point":"\\(\\Theta_2=0\\) is a zero gate (Lemma II.4): \\(S_1=\\{X\\sigma(\\Theta_{1,0}^\\top W_0)\\}\\) has dim \\(mr<d\\) (checked numerically), a \\(\\mathrm T_A\\)-type apex whose frame is read off \\(W_0\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) acts only through \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\), plus translations of \\(\\Theta_1\\) along the left kernel of \\(W_0\\). For full-rank \\(W_0\\) and smooth \\(\\sigma\\) the generic fibre has dimension \\(r\\max(0,m+r-n)\\), i.e. \\(d=\\min(2mr,\\,r(m+n-r))\\): \\(r^2+r(m-\\operatorname{rk}W_0)\\) when \\(d_{out}\\ge d_{in}\\), trivial when \\(d_{out}+r\\le d_{in}\\) (Jacobian rank, GELU and SiLU: fibres 8, 4, 0, 2 at \\((m,n,r)=(8,6,2),(6,6,2),(5,9,2),(6,7,2)\\)). With ReLU the gauge is linear \\(GL_r\\) only on the chamber \\(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0>0\\).","covariance":"Output side \\(GL_m\\), absorbed by \\(\\Theta_1\\). Input side: scalars (also absorbed by \\(\\Theta_1\\)) times the monomials that commute with the entrywise \\(\\sigma\\) (Lemma VI.2): positive monomials for ReLU, permutations for GELU/SiLU. So \\(GL\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\), the mirror of the \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times GL\\) key. Numerically (GELU, \\((m,n,r)=(3,7,1)\\)), input permutations, input homotheties and output \\(GL_3\\) map the image onto the image to \\(10^{-15}\\), while a generic input \\(GL_7\\) leaves a residual of 3.3.","merge":"\\(f(W_0;\\theta)\\) is computed once after training and added to \\(W_0\\): the non-linearity is on the parameter side.","rebasing":"The shape is recomputed from the merged base (as for HiRA). It is closed under real scaling through \\(\\Theta_2\\), so cycles converge to the span, which is all of \\(\\Theta\\) when \\(\\sigma(\\mathrm{row}\\,W_0)\\) spans \\(\\mathbb R^{n}\\)."},"categorical_reading":"PEANuT is a \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent object over LoRA: \\(\\rho=\\theta_0+\\Theta_2^\\top\\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top\\theta_0)\\) factors through LoRA by \\(h=(\\Theta_2^\\top,\\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0))\\), so it is LoRA's cone with a non-linear chart whose \\(A\\)-frame is computed from the base point (the whole cone when \\(d_{out}\\ge d_{in}\\), a sub-cone when \\(d_{out}+r\\le d_{in}\\)). Conversely, with \\(W_0\\) of full column rank, LoRA\\(_r\\) embeds in PEANuT\\(_{2r}\\) through \\(\\sigma(x)-\\sigma(-x)=x\\), which holds for ReLU, GELU and SiLU alike.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Theta_1,\\Theta_2)=(\\Theta_2^\\top,\\ \\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0))","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,\\sigma(\\Theta_{1,0}^\\top W_0))\\). Smooth for GELU/SiLU; for ReLU, smooth near any \\(q_0\\) where \\(\\Theta_{1,0}^\\top W_0\\) has no zero entry."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hira","test":"O1","reason":"Every point of Im HiRA vanishes wherever \\(W_0\\) does (Prop II.9(e)), while PEANuT's update \\(\\Theta_2^\\top\\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0)\\) is dense, so Im PEANuT is not inside Im HiRA when \\(W_0\\) has zeros. For \\(W_0\\) without zeros, Im HiRA \\(=W_0+W_0\\odot\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), and a generic rank-one update \\(uv^\\top\\) would need \\(uv^\\top\\oslash W_0\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), which generically has full rank; so the inclusion fails for \\(r<\\min(m,n)\\) as well, although both methods condition on \\(W_0\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"prefix-tuning","name":"Prefix-Tuning","full_name":"Prefix-Tuning","paper_title":"Prefix-Tuning: Optimizing Continuous Prompts for Generation","authors":"Li & Liang","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2101.00190","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.353/","code_url":"https://github.com/XiangLi1999/PrefixTuning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PrefixTuningConfig","formula_latex":"h_i=\\begin{cases}P_\\theta[i,:] & i\\in P_{\\mathrm{idx}}\\\\ \\mathrm{LM}_\\phi(z_i,h_{<i}) & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases};\\quad \\text{equivalently, in each layer } l=1,\\dots,L:\\quad \\mathrm{head}^{(l)}=\\mathrm{Attn}\\big(XW_q^{(l)},\\,[P_k^{(l)};XW_k^{(l)}],\\,[P_v^{(l)};XW_v^{(l)}]\\big),\\quad P_k^{(l)},P_v^{(l)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d};\\quad \\text{training: } P_\\theta[i,:]=\\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta(P'_\\theta[i,:]),\\quad P'_\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times k};\\quad \\phi\\ (W_0)\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"2L\\ell d\\ \\text{(stored, one key and one value prefix per attention layer of a decoder-only LM; an encoder-decoder prefixes each attention stack)};\\ \\ \\ell k+|\\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta|\\ \\text{during training (MLP discarded afterwards)}","locus":"keys/values at every attention layer (decoder; encoder and decoder for enc-dec models in the paper)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Learned key/value vectors prepended at every layer, i.e. a learned initial KV-cache state of length \\(\\ell\\). By He et al. (2022) this is equivalently a gated parallel attention-adapter: \\(h \\leftarrow (1-\\lambda(x))\\,h+\\lambda(x)\\,\\mathrm{softmax}(xW_qP_k^\\top)P_v\\), with gate \\(\\lambda(x)\\) = attention mass on the prefix.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prefix adds \\(\\ell\\) extra key/value slots to every attention layer (extra attention cost per token); it can be served as a cached prefix but not folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No exact identity: any prefix diverts attention mass \\(\\lambda(x)>0\\) away from the content. HF PEFT's init_weights='zero' zeroes keys and values, which still rescales attention outputs by \\(1-\\lambda(x)\\). Li & Liang initialize the prefix with activations of real (preferably task-relevant) words, which substantially helps in low-data settings; direct optimization of \\(P\\) is unstable, hence the MLP reparametrization.","key_idea":"Optimize a short continuous 'context' that every token can attend to at every layer, steering a frozen LM like a prompt would but with far more expressive power than input embeddings alone (discrete prompt < embedding-only < prefix-tuning).","results_note":"With 0.1% of parameters, GPT-2 prefix-tuning is comparable to fine-tuning on table-to-text (E2E, WebNLG, DART), outperforms it in low-data settings and extrapolates better to unseen categories, but trails full fine-tuning on XSUM summarization with BART.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"Prompt tuning only frees the layer-0 slice of the prefix."},{"target":"p-tuning-v2","type":"equivalent-to","note":"P-Tuning v2 is the same deep-prompt parameterization with an NLU-oriented recipe (optional reparametrization, classification head)."},{"target":"parallel-adapter","type":"dual-to","note":"He et al. (2022) rewrite prefix-tuning as a parallel adapter with W_1=W_qP_k^⊤, W_2=P_v, softmax nonlinearity and input-dependent gate λ(x)."},{"target":"cartridges","type":"generalizes","note":"Cartridges are prefix-tuning without the MLP reparametrization, trained by context distillation."},{"target":"initial-state-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"On S4 SSMs every prefix acts only through an initial-state update (Galim et al., Prop. 1); initial-state tuning is an upper bound, equal when \\ell \\ge H under a genericity condition."},{"target":"cartridges","type":"competes-with","note":"Same KV-prefix mechanism trained by self-study distillation."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.00190","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.00190","https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/prefix_tuning","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/prefix_tuning.md","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/prefix_tuning","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/prefix_tuning/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Xiang Lisa Li","Percy Liang"],"bibkey":"li2021prefix","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2021prefix,\n  title = {{Prefix-Tuning: Optimizing Continuous Prompts for Generation}},\n  author = {Xiang Lisa Li and Percy Liang},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2101.00190},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.353/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Function-level: each layer becomes a gated parallel adapter \\((1-\\lambda_q)\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\lambda_q \\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)\\) (Thm VI.5(b)), with rigid content ratios at that layer.","base_point":"As in the table. Soft tokens always take softmax mass, and on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) no prompt leaves the output unchanged, so no prompt reproduces \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\) (Thm VI.5(f)). HF init_weights='zero' still rescales attention by \\(1-\\lambda(x)\\).","gauge":"Li–Liang train \\(P = \\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta(P')\\), a non-linear fibre of dimension \\(\\ell k+|\\mathrm{MLP}|-2L\\ell d\\) (image unchanged when hidden \\(\\ge \\ell-1\\), Obs III.2). The stored prefix (and HF's default prefix_projection=False) has only the discrete slot permutations of Thm VI.5(a).","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"An unpointed extension on the key/value wire of every attention layer: by Thm VI.5(b) each layer becomes the gated parallel adapter \\((1-\\lambda_q)\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\lambda_q\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)\\), so it has no neutral point and is not box-mergeable. Li–Liang's MLP is a training 2-cell that leaves the prefix image unchanged (Obs III.2).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(P',\\theta)=\\big(\\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta(P'),\\,W^{0}_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\big)","note":"Obs III.2: the MLP is the 2-cell \\(\\sigma\\); P-tuning v2 taken with its head frozen at the LM head/verbalizer (A2) and pointed at \\(\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\theta_0}(P'_0)\\)."},{"target":"initial-state-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big(x^{(l)}_{-\\ell..-1}\\big)=\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_{i=1}^{\\ell}\\bar A_l^{\\,i-1}\\bar B_l\\,x^{(l)}_{-i}\\Big)_{l}","note":"On S4 (LTI) SSM layers only (Galim et al., Prop 1): a per-layer prefix acts solely through the state it leaves; same causality argument as Thm VI.5(e). Initial-state tuning pointed at h(q0)."},{"target":"mam-adapter","rule":"A5","h":"h(P',\\theta)=\\big(P',\\,\\theta,\\,W_{\\mathrm{up}}=0,\\,W^{0}_{\\mathrm{down}}\\big)","note":"MAM = prefix ⊕ scaled parallel adapter, with its prefix trained through the same Li–Liang MLP (He et al.), so h is the identity on (P',θ) and puts the adapter summand at its neutral point W_up=0. MAM pointed at (P'_0,θ_0,0,W^0_down)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Head-level (Thm VI.5(f)): on length-one contexts every LoRA setting is affine in x, a generically prefixed head with \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) is not; whole-network non-mergeability is open (Exposé XI)."},{"target":"llama-adapter","test":"O3","reason":"LLaMA-Adapter is pointed (g=0 reproduces f(θ0,-)), prefix tuning is unpointed, so no pointed h exists in either direction; at a layer the prefix rescales content by 1-λ_q while LLaMA-Adapter leaves content weights unchanged (Thm VI.5(c,d))."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"prompt-tuning","name":"Prompt Tuning","full_name":"Prompt Tuning (soft prompts)","paper_title":"The Power of Scale for Parameter-Efficient Prompt Tuning","authors":"Lester et al.","year":2021,"venue":"EMNLP 2021","arxiv":"2104.08691","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08691","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/prompt-tuning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PromptTuningConfig","formula_latex":"\\text{input}=[P;\\,E(x)],\\ \\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d} \\text{ trained},\\ \\text{model frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell\\,d","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"learned soft-token embeddings prepended to the input","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Soft tokens extend the input; nothing to fold into weights.","identity_at_init":"Not an identity (adds tokens); initialized randomly, from text (vocab embeddings), or by random discrete vocab sampling (PEFT >= 0.18).","key_idea":"With large enough models, a few learned input embeddings match full fine-tuning.","results_note":"Paper reports prompt tuning closing the gap to model tuning as T5 scales past billions of parameters.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"Prefix tuning restricted to the input layer."},{"target":"p-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"P-tuning reparameterizes the prompt through an encoder."},{"target":"p-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"P-Tuning with identity prompt encoder and all virtual tokens placed at the front."},{"target":"p-tuning-v2","type":"special-case-of","note":"Shallow (input-only) instance of deep prompt tuning."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Standard additive-weight baseline; prompt tuning uses orders of magnitude fewer parameters but needs scale."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08691","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/prompt_tuning","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.08691","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/conceptual_guides/prompting","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/prompt_tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","prompts"],"authors_full":["Brian Lester","Rami Al-Rfou","Noah Constant"],"bibkey":"lester2021power","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lester2021power,\n  title = {{The Power of Scale for Parameter-Efficient Prompt Tuning}},\n  author = {Brian Lester and Rami Al-Rfou and Noah Constant},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2104.08691},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08691}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"base_point":"As in the table. Soft tokens always take softmax mass, and on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) no prompt leaves the output unchanged, so no prompt reproduces \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\) (Thm VI.5(f)).","gauge":"Table '–'. The prompt sits at fixed positions; the generic Jacobian of \\(P\\mapsto f(\\theta_0,[P;\\cdot])\\) has rank \\(\\ell d\\), so the fibre is trivial.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"The basic unpointed extension: soft tokens on the input sequence wire, \\(\\tilde f(\\theta_0,P,x)=f(\\theta_0,[P;x])\\), with no \\(P\\) reproducing \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\). In causal decoders it maps into deep prompt tuning by \\(p\\mapsto(K_j(p),V_j(p))_j\\), an arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation, not of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\) (Thm VI.5(e)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(p)=\\big((K_j(p),V_j(p))_{j\\le L},\\,W^0_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\big)","note":"Thm VI.5(e): causal decoder, prompt first, matched position offsets (or relative positions); P-tuning v2's head frozen at the verbalizer. Fails in bidirectional encoders of depth ≥ 2."},{"target":"p-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\big(P'_0,\\;\\psi_0\\ \\text{with}\\ W_2\\mapsto W_2^0+H_0^{+}(P-P_0)\\big)","note":"Section of the encoder through its last linear layer (H_0 = penultimate features at q0, full row rank when hidden ≥ ℓ, cf. Obs III.2); a shift of the shared output bias would not do, since the encoder acts row-wise."},{"target":"multitask-prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\big(P\\oslash u_0v_0^\\top,\\,u_0,\\,v_0\\big)","note":"Section of the Hadamard torus bundle, valid when u_0,v_0 have no zero entries; MPT pointed at h(P_0)."},{"target":"attempt","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\Big(P_{\\mathrm{tgt}}=\\tfrac{(t+1)P-\\sum_{j\\le t}P_j}{t+2},\\ \\gamma=0,\\ \\beta=0,\\ W^0_{\\mathrm{down}},\\ W^0_{\\mathrm{up}}\\Big)","note":"Router LayerNorm gain and bias set to 0, so H_out=0 and the mixture weights are uniform, a_j=1/(t+1); then P_inst=((t+2)P_tgt+Σ_{j≤t}P_j)/(t+1)=P for every input. ATTEMPT pointed at h(P_0) (zero router gain), an arrow of the unpointed slice; with the O1 obstruction in ATTEMPT's entry the inclusion is strict."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"llama-adapter","test":"O3","reason":"LLaMA-Adapter is pointed at g=0, while on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) every soft prompt moves the output (Thm VI.5(f)), so no pointed arrow exists either way; the two are comparable only in the unpointed slice."},{"target":"cpt","test":"O1","reason":"CPT's reach is confined to an ε-ball around the demonstrations with label tokens frozen; unconstrained prompts leave that set."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"psoft","name":"PSOFT","full_name":"Principal Subspace Orthogonal Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Efficient Orthogonal Fine-Tuning with Principal Subspace Adaptation","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2505.11235","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11235","code_url":"https://github.com/fei407/PSOFT","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PsoftConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_{res}+B\\,\\operatorname{diag}(\\beta)\\,R^{\\top}\\operatorname{diag}(\\alpha)\\,A,\\quad W_0=U\\Sigma V^{\\top},\\ A=V_{:,:r}^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{(row-orthonormal)},\\ B=U_{:,:r}\\Sigma_{:r,:r}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ W_{res}=W_0-BA\\ \\text{(all frozen)};\\ R\\in SO(r)\\ \\text{(Cayley or Cayley-Neumann)},\\ \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r}\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\alpha=\\beta=\\mathbf{1}\\Rightarrow W=W_0\\big(I+A^{\\top}(R^{\\top}-I)A\\big)","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{r(r-1)}{2}+2r","locus":"any linear layer; acts on the principal input subspace of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"OFT restricted to the \\(r\\)-dimensional principal input subspace. \\(O(r)\\) acts on \\(W_0\\) from the right through the embedding \\(R \\mapsto I + A^\\top(R-I)A\\) in \\(O(d_{in})\\), which is the identity on the orthogonal complement. Two diagonal scalings (\\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\)) gradually relax exact orthogonality.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapted weight \\(W_{res} + B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\beta)\\,R\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)\\,A\\) is a fixed dense matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"\\(Q = 0\\) (so \\(R = I\\)) and \\(\\alpha = \\beta = 1\\) give \\(W = W_0\\) exactly.","key_idea":"Confine orthogonal fine-tuning to the principal subspace of \\(W_0\\) found by SVD. An \\(r\\times r\\) rotation plus two tunable scaling vectors then preserves the subspace geometry the way OFT does, with LoRA-like memory and far fewer parameters.","results_note":"Across 35 NLP/CV tasks and four backbones, avoids the out-of-memory failures of BOFT and GOFTv2/qGOFTv2, gives up to 18x higher parameter efficiency than BOFT, OFTv2 and LoRA variants on the smaller models, the best VTAB-1K average (73.4%), +2.28% over LoRA on LLaMA-3.2-3B GSM8K and +1.4% over LoRA on LLaMA-3.1-8B commonsense reasoning.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"orthogonal action restricted to a principal subspace"},{"target":"oftv2","type":"builds-on","note":"uses the Cayley-Neumann parameterization"},{"target":"spectral-adapter-r","type":"competes-with","note":"with alpha=beta=1, PSOFT is mathematically a one-sided (right) Spectral Adapter^R: a rotation of the top-r right singular subspace"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"both use the top-r SVD (principal subspace) of W_0; PiSSA is a baseline in the PSOFT paper (+1.02% on MATH)"},{"target":"pissa","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses the principal SVD subspace of W_0 like PiSSA."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Implemented as the additive low-rank update B(R-I)A, but with frozen principal-subspace bases and an r x r orthogonal core it is strictly more constrained than LoRA, not equivalent."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11235","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.11235","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/psoft/config.py","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2026/poster/10010597","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/psoft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/psoft/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Fei Wu","Jia Hu","Geyong Min","Shiqiang Wang"],"bibkey":"wu2025efficient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2025efficient,\n  title = {{Efficient Orthogonal Fine-Tuning with Principal Subspace Adaptation}},\n  author = {Fei Wu and Jia Hu and Geyong Min and Shiqiang Wang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2505.11235},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11235}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Sat","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=B(D_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha-I)A\\) in the top-\\(r\\) SVD frame: the core ranges over \\(D_\\beta\\,SO(r)\\,D_\\alpha\\), a two-sided torus saturation of the principal-subspace orthogonal orbit, of dimension \\(r(r-1)/2+2r-1\\).","base_point":"At \\(R=I\\) the two diagonal scalings coincide: \\(\\dim S_1=r(r-1)/2+r\\) against \\(d=r(r-1)/2+2r-1\\), a deficit \\(r-1>0\\) (Jacobian 3/4, 6/8, 10/13 at \\(r=2,3,4\\)): an apex. With \\(\\alpha=\\beta=\\mathbf 1\\) frozen the method is an orbit with a regular base point.","gauge":"\\((\\beta,\\alpha)\\mapsto(c\\beta,\\alpha/c)\\): one scalar slide (fibre dimension 1).","covariance":"Top-\\(r\\) SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"With frozen frames the core generates \\(GL(r)\\) (signs allowed), so re-merging fills the \\(r^2\\)-dimensional principal block \\(W_{res}+U_r\\,\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}\\,V_r^\\top\\). The additive reading gives the same limit: with \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) frozen, the increments \\(B(M-I)A\\) span the same \\(r^2\\)-dimensional block."},"categorical_reading":"PSOFT is OFT restricted to the principal singular block and saturated by two diagonal tori: \\(\\Delta W=U_r\\Sigma_r(D_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha-I)V_r^\\top\\). Its image lies in LoRA-XS's linear core (hence in PiSSA's cone), and its default pointing is an apex, because the two relaxation tori are first-order indistinguishable at \\(R=I\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"(Q,\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\Sigma_r\\big(D_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha-I\\big)\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"The LoRA-XS core R in the frames B_0 = U_r, A_0 = Sigma_r V_r^T (up to its transpose convention), pointed at R = 0."},{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(Q,\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(U_r\\Sigma_rD_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha\\Sigma_r^{-1/2},\\ \\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"PiSSA_r at its own point (U_r Sigma_r^{1/2}, Sigma_r^{1/2} V_r^T): the composite of the LoRA-XS arrow with LoRA-XS -> PiSSA."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"spectral-adapter-r","test":"O1","reason":"alpha, beta change the principal singular values, which Spectral Adapter^R preserves; only at alpha = beta = 1 is PSOFT a one-sided Spectral Adapter^R."},{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"The scalings change K_out, and even at alpha = beta = 1 the rotation of the principal input subspace is not block-aligned, so it breaks OFT's partial Grams."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Sat"},{"id":"pvera","name":"PVeRA","full_name":"Probabilistic Vector-Based Random Matrix Adaptation","paper_title":"PVeRA: Probabilistic Vector-Based Random Matrix Adaptation","authors":"Fillioux et al.","year":2025,"venue":"WACV 2026","arxiv":"2512.07703","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07703","code_url":"https://github.com/leofillioux/pvera","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PveraConfig","formula_latex":"h = W_0x + \\alpha\\,\\Lambda_b B\\, z,\\quad z = \\mu + \\epsilon\\odot\\sigma,\\ \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I),\\quad [\\mu;\\sigma] = \\Lambda_d A x;\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2r\\times d_{in}},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{frozen random shared};\\ d\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2r},\\ b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\beta\\sum \\mathrm{KL}(\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal{N}(0,I));\\ \\text{deterministic inference: } W = W_0 + \\alpha\\Lambda_b B\\,\\Lambda_{d_\\mu}A_\\mu","trainable_params":"d_{out} + 2r","locus":"linear layers (paper: \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\) of ViT); shared random matrices as in VeRA","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"VeRA with a stochastic (VAE-style reparameterised) rank-\\(r\\) bottleneck: the \\(r\\)-dim code is sampled from a Gaussian whose mean and std are VeRA-style scaled random projections of \\(x\\); KL prior on the code (HF PEFT reads the second half of \\(\\Lambda_d A x\\) as a log-variance, \\(\\sigma = \\exp(\\tfrac12\\log\\sigma^2)\\), and adds no explicit \\(\\alpha\\))","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"With mean (deterministic) inference \\(z = \\mu\\) the update is a fixed matrix that merges like VeRA; Monte-Carlo sampling mode at test time is input-dependent and cannot be merged.","identity_at_init":"\\(b = 0\\) (HF: \\(\\lambda_b\\) zeros), \\(d = d_{initial}\\) (0.1); so the adapter output is zero at init","key_idea":"Make VeRA's shared-random-basis bottleneck probabilistic so the adapter learns a distribution over adaptations, enabling uncertainty estimates while keeping VeRA's tiny footprint and mergeability.","results_note":"Outperforms VeRA and the other benchmarked adapters (seven adapters in total in the comparison) on average on VTAB-1k with ViT backbones; deterministic (mean) inference keeps it mergeable.","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"builds-on","note":"same frozen shared random A, B and trainable scaling vectors, plus a reparameterised Gaussian bottleneck"},{"target":"monteclora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both make low-rank adapters Bayesian/stochastic."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07703","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.07703","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/pvera/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://doi.org/10.1109/WACV61042.2026.00243","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/pvera","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Leo Fillioux","Enzo Ferrante","Paul-Henry Cournède","Maria Vakalopoulou","Stergios Christodoulidis"],"bibkey":"fillioux2025pvera","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fillioux2025pvera,\n  title = {{PVeRA: Probabilistic Vector-Based Random Matrix Adaptation}},\n  author = {Leo Fillioux and Enzo Ferrante and Paul-Henry Cournède and Maria Vakalopoulou and Stergios Christodoulidis},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {IEEE/CVF Winter Conference on Applications of Computer Vision (WACV)},\n  eprint = {2512.07703},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.07703}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"R under mean (deterministic) inference \\(W=W_0+\\alpha \\Lambda_b B \\Lambda_{d_\\mu} A_\\mu\\); the training forward adds input-dependent noise \\(\\varepsilon \\odot \\sigma(x)\\), a family over the noise.","shape":"VeRA's toric cone, \\(d=d_{out}+r-1\\) (checked).","base_point":"\\(b_0=0\\): apex, only \\(b\\) moves to first order.","gauge":"VeRA's scalar slide \\((b,d_\\mu)\\mapsto(cb,d_\\mu/c)\\), plus the \\(r\\)-dim \\(\\sigma\\)-half of \\(d\\) on which the merged \\(\\rho\\) does not depend at all (\\(\\rho\\) factors through the projection).","covariance":"Frozen shared random \\(A,B\\): covariance in law (Mon on the output side through \\(\\Lambda_b\\)).","merge":"M1 under mean inference; Monte-Carlo inference at test time is input-dependent and obstructed.","rebasing":"As VeRA: \\(\\to\\) LoRA-FA\\((A_\\mu)\\) when \\(B\\) has no zero entries."},"categorical_reading":"PVeRA is VeRA times a variational coordinate: its merged map factors as \\(\\rho_{\\rm VeRA}\\circ\\mathrm{pr}\\), the \\(\\sigma\\)-half of \\(d\\) never reaching \\(\\Theta\\) and only shaping the stochastic training forward and the KL term. Under mean inference it is VeRA (toric cone, apex, scalar gauge); Monte-Carlo inference makes it a family over noise that cannot be merged.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"vera","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d_\\mu,d_\\sigma)=(b,\\ d_\\mu)","note":"Projection forgetting the variance half; intertwines the merged rho, not the stochastic training objective."},{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d_\\mu,d_\\sigma)=\\alpha\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_{d_\\mu}","note":"Composite with VeRA -> LoRA-FA(A_mu), pointed at 0."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"randlora","name":"RandLoRA","full_name":"RandLoRA: full-rank PEFT via scaled random bases","paper_title":"RandLoRA: Full-rank parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large models","authors":"Albert et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2502.00987","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00987","code_url":"https://github.com/PaulAlbert31/RandLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"RandLoraConfig","formula_latex":"\\Delta W=\\sum_{j=1}^{n}B_j\\Lambda_jA\\,\\Gamma_j,\\ \\ B_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r} \\text{ and shared } A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}} \\text{ frozen random},\\ \\Lambda_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r},\\ \\Gamma_j \\text{ trainable diagonal},\\ n=\\lceil\\min(d_{in},d_{out})/r\\rceil \\ (\\text{PEFT applies }\\Gamma_j\\text{ on the smaller dimension})","trainable_params":"n\\,\\big(r+\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\big)\\ \\approx\\ \\tfrac{d^2}{r}+d \\text{ for } d_{in}=d_{out}=d","locus":"nn.Linear layers only (PEFT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sum of \\(n\\) frozen random rank-\\(r\\) bases with trainable diagonal scalings on both sides, reaching full rank; parameter count grows as the basis rank shrinks","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The scaled sum of bases is a fixed dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"PEFT sets \\(\\Lambda_j = 0\\) and \\(\\Gamma_j\\) to the constant \\(1/\\max(n, \\min(d_{in},d_{out}))\\) (in practice \\(1/\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\)), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Separate parameter count from rank: combine many frozen random low-rank bases with learned diagonal scalings to get full-rank updates cheaply.","results_note":"Paper reports RandLoRA overtaking LoRA as the parameter budget grows on vision (DinoV2, CLIP), vision-language and commonsense reasoning (e.g. LLaMA3-8B).","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"builds-on","note":"The paper says its formulation is similar to VeRA's (VeRA approximates only the first block of a full-rank update). VeRA scales the output side whereas RandLoRA scales the input side, so VeRA is not literally its n = 1 case."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Full-rank alternative at similar budgets."},{"target":"nola","type":"competes-with","note":"both use random bases; RandLoRA targets full rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00987","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00987","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/randlora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/randlora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.16.0","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/randlora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Paul Albert","Frederic Z. Zhang","Hemanth Saratchandran","Cristian Rodriguez-Opazo","Anton van den Hengel","Ehsan Abbasnejad"],"bibkey":"albert2025randlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{albert2025randlora,\n  title = {{RandLoRA: Full-rank parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large models}},\n  author = {Paul Albert and Frederic Z. Zhang and Hemanth Saratchandran and Cristian Rodriguez-Opazo and Anton van den Hengel and Ehsan Abbasnejad},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2502.00987},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00987}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Sum of \\(n=\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil\\) VeRA-type cones \\(B_j \\Lambda_j A \\Gamma_j\\); \\(d=n(r-1+\\min(m,n))\\) (checked), full rank.","base_point":"\\(\\Lambda_0=0\\), \\(\\Gamma_0\\) constant: \\(\\dim S_1=nr < d\\) (checked), apex.","gauge":"One scalar slide \\((c \\Lambda_j, \\Gamma_j/c)\\) per term: an \\(n\\)-dim torus.","covariance":"Frozen random \\(B_j\\), \\(A\\): in law.","rebasing":"In PEFT the distinct \\(B_j\\) sit on the larger side and the shared \\(A\\) and \\(\\Gamma_j\\) on the smaller side (min dim), so \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,C = \\mathrm{span}\\{b_{jk}\\} \\otimes \\mathbb{R}^{\\min}\\) when \\(A\\) has no zero entries: \\(R_\\infty = \\Theta\\) iff \\(N r \\ge \\max(m,n)\\), i.e. only for (near-)square boxes; for rectangular (MLP) boxes it is a proper subspace (checked: span dim \\(36 < 60\\) at \\(10\\times6\\), \\(r=2\\); \\(81 = mn\\) at \\(9\\times9\\), \\(r=3\\)). The table's '\\(\\to \\Theta\\)' needs this condition."},"categorical_reading":"RandLoRA is an \\(\\oplus\\)-sum of \\(n=\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil\\) two-sided-diagonal random-frame terms \\(B_j\\Lambda_jA\\Gamma_j\\), with one scalar slide per term (a torus of dimension \\(n\\)) and \\(\\dim S_1=nr\\) at \\(\\Lambda=0\\); the sum reaches full rank and its rebasing span is \\(\\Theta\\). It factors through LoRA\\(_{nr}\\) at a degenerate pointing whose stacked \\(A_0\\) repeats rows.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Lambda,\\Gamma)=\\big([B_1\\Lambda_1,\\dots,B_n\\Lambda_n],\\ [A\\Gamma_1;\\dots;A\\Gamma_n]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_{nr} pointed at (0,[A Gamma_1^0;...;A Gamma_n^0]), a rank-r stacked A_0, outside the three strata."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"VeRA scales the output side (column space varies, row space = row A fixed), RandLoRA's terms scale the rank and input side; VeRA is not its n=1 case and the images are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"road","name":"RoAd","full_name":"2D Rotary Adaptation","paper_title":"3-in-1: 2D Rotary Adaptation for Efficient Finetuning, Efficient Batching and Composability","authors":"Liao & Monz","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2409.00119","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.00119","code_url":"https://github.com/BaohaoLiao/road","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"RoadConfig(variant='road_1'|'road_2'|'road_4')","formula_latex":"W=RW_0,\\ \\ R=\\mathrm{blockdiag}_i\\Big(\\alpha_i\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta_i&-\\sin\\theta_i\\\\\\sin\\theta_i&\\cos\\theta_i\\end{bmatrix}\\Big) \\text{ over output pairs};\\ \\ h=r_1\\odot(W_0x)+r_2\\odot\\widehat{(W_0x)}","trainable_params":"d_{out}\\ (\\text{RoAd}_1),\\ 2d_{out}\\ (\\text{RoAd}_2),\\ 4d_{out}\\ (\\text{RoAd}_4)","locus":"linear layers (outputs); works with bitsandbytes-quantized bases","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"block-diagonal 2x2 scaled rotations applied to pairs of output dimensions, implemented element-wise","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(R W_0\\) is a dense matrix; unmerged it is cheap element-wise ops enabling mixed-adapter batches.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\theta = 0\\) and \\(\\alpha = 1\\), so \\(R = I\\).","key_idea":"Fine-tuning mostly rotates representations, so learn tiny 2D rotations that are element-wise (batchable across adapters) and composable.","results_note":"Paper reports matching or beating full fine-tuning on GLUE with <0.1% trainable parameters and 2x LoRA's throughput in heterogeneous multi-adapter batches.","relations":[{"target":"ia3","type":"generalizes","note":"With theta = 0, RoAd_2/RoAd_4 reduce to per-output-channel scaling, the (IA)^3 form on keys/values (RoAd_1 ties the scale within each rotated pair)."},{"target":"oft","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are orthogonal-style multiplicative updates; RoAd uses 2x2 blocks on the output side."},{"target":"loreft","type":"competes-with","note":"both are framed as representation interventions; RoAd's intervention is an element-wise rotation"},{"target":"qgoft","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue: both act with 2x2 blocks (qGOFT on the input side with butterfly-style mixing, RoAd on fixed output pairs); neither paper compares against the other"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.00119","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.00119","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/road","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/road/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/road/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Baohao Liao","Christof Monz"],"bibkey":"liao20243","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liao20243,\n  title = {{3-in-1: 2D Rotary Adaptation for Efficient Finetuning, Efficient Batching and Composability}},\n  author = {Baohao Liao and Christof Monz},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2409.00119},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.00119}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"RoAd\\(_1\\) (paper main, HF road_1): the left action of \\(CO^+(2)^{m/2}\\cong(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{m/2}\\) on output pairs. Its orbit closure is a real-linear space of dimension \\(m\\) (Prop II.9(d)), the same situation as (IA)\\(^3\\), which axes.json files under Lin; kept Orb as in axes.json and VIII.B.","base_point":"Polar coordinates \\((\\alpha,\\theta)\\) at \\((1,0)\\) span \\(\\mathbb C\\) in each pair.","covariance":"Pair-monomial on the output side \\(\\times\\ GL\\) (VIII.B).","rebasing":"The generated group's orbit closure is the orbit closure itself: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"RoAd\\(_1\\) is (IA)\\(^3\\) over \\(\\mathbb C\\): the torus \\((\\mathbb C^\\times)^{m/2}\\) of scaled 2D rotations acting on pairs of output neurons, with a linear orbit closure of dimension \\(m\\). It multiplies each pair's input Gram by \\(\\alpha_i^2\\); RoAd\\(_{2,4}\\) enlarge the torus to the non-abelian block monoid \\(M_2(\\mathbb R)^{m/2}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"Pair rotations move row lines, which (IA)^3 fixes while its scales are non-zero."},{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"The alpha_i rescale neuron pairs and change K_out; OFT acts on the input side and preserves it."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"shira","name":"SHiRA","full_name":"Sparse High Rank Adapters","paper_title":"Sparse High Rank Adapters","authors":"Bhardwaj et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.13175","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13175","code_url":"https://github.com/Qualcomm-AI-research/SHiRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"ShiraConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + M\\odot\\Delta W,\\quad \\|M\\|_0 \\approx 1\\text{--}2\\%\\ \\text{of } d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\big(\\text{HF PEFT: } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\big),\\quad M\\in\\{\\text{Struct (rows/cols + diagonal)},\\ \\text{Rand},\\ \\text{WM: top-}|W_0|,\\ \\text{Grad: top-}|g|,\\ \\text{SNIP: top-}|g\\odot W_0|\\}","trainable_params":"\\text{paper: } 1\\text{--}2\\%\\ \\text{of each adapted matrix};\\ \\text{HF PEFT: } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(budget matched to LoRA; the update itself is high-rank)}","locus":"any nn.Linear (e.g. attention projections of LLMs, UNet layers of SDXL)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"fixed sparse, generally high-rank coordinate mask; SHiRA-Struct = diagonal (high-rank) plus a few full rows/columns (rank-1 part); non-overlapping SHiRA-Struct adapters are nearly orthogonal","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The adapter overwrites only the masked entries of \\(W_0\\) (scatter), so it runs in fused mode at original cost while still allowing rapid switching.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W\\) initialized to 0 (HF PEFT init_weights=True).","key_idea":"Directly fine-tune 1-2% of the base weights through a static sparse mask; the update is high-rank, can be switched by overwriting a few entries, and multiple sparse adapters interfere less when fused.","results_note":"On LLaMA-7B and LLaMA2-7B commonsense reasoning SHiRA is up to 2.7% better than LoRA, and on SDXL style adapters it reduces concept loss in multi-adapter fusion; loading is 5x-16x faster than LoRA fusion on CPU.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same parameter budget r(d_{in}+d_{out}) but sparse/high-rank instead of dense/low-rank."},{"target":"spiel","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent sparse fine-tuning; static mask versus SpIEL's dynamic mask."},{"target":"fish-mask","type":"builds-on","note":"SHiRA revisits fixed-sparse-mask partial fine-tuning and cites FISH Mask for it; SHiRA-Grad is a FISH-like gradient mask."},{"target":"super-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both train a fixed sparse support of W_0; Super-Tuning picks it by magnitude."},{"target":"rosa","type":"special-case-of","note":"RoSA without its low-rank part"},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"builds-on","note":"sparse update of pretrained weights with a fixed mask"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13175","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.13175","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/shira","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/shira","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/shira/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.17.0","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.13175","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2406.13175","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Sparse%20High%20Rank%20Adapters"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","selective","shape"],"authors_full":["Kartikeya Bhardwaj","Nilesh Prasad Pandey","Sweta Priyadarshi","Viswanath Ganapathy","Shreya Kadambi","Rafael Esteves","Shubhankar Borse","Paul Whatmough","Risheek Garrepalli","Mart Van Baalen","Harris Teague","Markus Nagel"],"bibkey":"bhardwaj2024sparse","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bhardwaj2024sparse,\n  title = {{Sparse High Rank Adapters}},\n  author = {Kartikeya Bhardwaj and Nilesh Prasad Pandey and Sweta Priyadarshi and Viswanath Ganapathy and Shreya Kadambi and Rafael Esteves and Shubhankar Borse and Paul Whatmough and Risheek Garrepalli and Mart Van Baalen and Harris Teague and Markus Nagel},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.13175},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13175}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0+M\\odot\\Delta\\) for a fixed coordinate mask: a linear subspace (table).","base_point":"\\(\\Delta W=0\\) at init (HF init_weights=True), and the method is linear, hence regular.","covariance":"Support structure in the standard basis: \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) (the WM, Grad and SNIP masks move with \\(W_0\\) only under monomial maps).","rebasing":"Fixed mask: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"SHiRA is a coordinate sub-object of full fine-tuning, the linear method \\(\\Delta\\mapsto W_0+M\\odot\\Delta\\) (full FT with the complementary coordinates frozen at their base values). Its high rank is a cut property of the support, not of a factorisation.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"rosa","rule":"A5","h":"h(\\Delta)=\\big(B=0,\\;A=A_0,\\;S=M\\odot\\Delta\\big)","note":"\\(M\\to M\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}\\), with the LoRA summand at its base point; requires RoSA's mask to equal SHiRA's (SHiRA-Grad is close to RoSA's Fisher mask)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A 1-2% mask that includes a diagonal reaches full rank, so \\(\\rho_{\\max}>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"super-tuning","name":"Super-Tuning (Super / Supra)","full_name":"Super-Tuning: sparse fine-tuning from activation-aware pruning saliency","paper_title":"Super-Tuning: From Activation-Aware Pruning to Sparse Fine-Tuning","authors":"Ilin et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2607.09287","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09287","code_url":"https://github.com/vectozavr/SuperTuning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"SupertuningConfig (r=None: Super; r>0: Supra hybrid; select_top=False: bottom-k)","formula_latex":"W=W_0+M\\odot U\\ \\big(+\\,\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\text{ in Supra}\\big),\\ \\ M=\\mathrm{TopK}\\ \\text{or}\\ \\mathrm{BottomK}(S),\\ S_{ij}=|W_{0,ij}|\\,\\|X_{:,j}\\|_2 \\text{ (paper: Wanda score from a calibration pass) or } S_{ij}=|W_{0,ij}| \\text{ (magnitude; the only option in PEFT)}","trainable_params":"(1-\\text{sparsity})\\,d_{in}d_{out}\\ \\big(+\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ for Supra}\\big)","locus":"nn.Linear layers only (PEFT)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"sparse coordinate mask chosen by Wanda saliency (paper) or data-free weight magnitude (PEFT); Supra = sparse-plus-low-rank","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Sparse and low-rank deltas are fixed matrices that add into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Sparse values \\(U = 0\\) and LoRA \\(B = 0\\).","key_idea":"Reuse pruning saliency to choose an unrestricted-rank sparse trainable support at a fixed budget, optionally adding a low-rank part.","results_note":"Paper reports Supra beating LoRA on Math17K (Llama-3.2-1B 62.23 vs 61.07; Meta-Llama-3-8B 79.12 vs 73.17).","relations":[{"target":"shira","type":"competes-with","note":"Sparse fixed-support fine-tuning with a different mask criterion."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Supra adds a LoRA term to the sparse update."},{"target":"pafi","type":"generalizes","note":"The magnitude-only BottomK support is PaFi's rule; Wanda reweights magnitudes by input activation norms."},{"target":"rosa","type":"competes-with","note":"Supra is a sparse plus low-rank hybrid like RoSA but with a training-free support."},{"target":"sift","type":"competes-with","note":"Gradient-based (SIFT) vs. activation-aware magnitude (Super) supports."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09287","https://arxiv.org/html/2607.09287","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/supertuning","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/supertuning/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.21.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/tuners/supertuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","selective"],"authors_full":["Ivan Ilin","Philip Zmushko","Peter Richtárik"],"bibkey":"ilin2026super","bibtex":"@article{ilin2026super,\n  title = {{Super-Tuning: From Activation-Aware Pruning to Sparse Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Ivan Ilin and Philip Zmushko and Peter Richtárik},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2607.09287},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.09287},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09287}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Super (HF r=None default): \\(W_0+M\\odot U\\) with a fixed saliency mask, a coordinate subspace. Supra (\\(r>0\\)) adds a LoRA summand: a cone at the apex with \\(GL_r\\) gauge that rebases to its span.","covariance":"A coordinate mask in the standard frame; the magnitude score is invariant under signed permutations and Wanda's \\(|W_{ij}|\\cdot\\|X_j\\|\\) also under input-side diagonal scalings, still monomial.","rebasing":"Fixed mask: idempotent (a mask recomputed from the merged weight would gain)."},"categorical_reading":"A fixed coordinate mask \\(W_0+M\\odot U\\) chosen once from saliency: a Lin method in the standard frame, the same object as SHiRA-WM for TopK magnitude and PaFi's mask for BottomK. Supra adds a LoRA summand (A5), which turns the germ into a cone at the apex.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"shira","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"PEFT magnitude mode with select_top=True is SHiRA with its weight-magnitude (WM) mask, top-|W_0|; isomorphism."},{"target":"pafi","rule":"A5","h":"h(U)=\\big(U,\\,\\gamma_0,\\,\\beta_0\\big)","note":"BottomK magnitude mask = PaFi's support; PaFi also trains norm parameters, which sit at their base values."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A generic update on a fixed (1-s)mn-entry support has rank up to min(m,n), above LoRA_r's maximum rank r."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"tinylora","name":"TinyLoRA","full_name":"TinyLoRA (learning to reason in 13 parameters)","paper_title":"Learning to Reason in 13 Parameters","authors":"Morris et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2602.04118","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04118","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"TinyLoraConfig","formula_latex":"W=W_0+U_r\\Sigma_r\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{u}v_iP_i\\Big)V_r^\\top,\\ \\ U_r,\\Sigma_r,V_r \\text{ from truncated SVD of } W_0 \\text{ (frozen)},\\ P_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r} \\text{ fixed random},\\ v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{u} \\text{ trained, optionally tied across modules} \\ (\\text{PEFT splits } \\Sigma_r^{1/2} \\text{ to both sides: } U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}R\\,\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top)","trainable_params":"u \\text{ per module, or } u \\text{ for the whole model with full weight tying}","locus":"Linear, Embedding and Conv1D layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA-XS core \\(R=\\sum_i v_iP_i\\) confined to a \\(u\\)-dimensional random subspace of \\(r\\times r\\) matrices in the top-\\(r\\) singular frame of \\(W_0\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The update is a fixed rank-\\(r\\) matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"PEFT default init_weights=True sets \\(v = 0\\), an exact no-op. init_weights='uniform' draws \\(v \\sim U(-0.02, 0.02)\\).","key_idea":"With RL, reasoning can be unlocked by a handful of parameters projected through fixed random tensors into the principal singular frame.","results_note":"Paper reports Qwen2.5-8B reaching 91% on GSM8K with 13 trained parameters under RL, and RL vastly outperforming SFT at tiny budgets.","relations":[{"target":"lora-xs","type":"builds-on","note":"Replaces LoRA-XS's trainable r x r core with a random projection of a tiny vector."},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both project a tiny global vector into LoRA-style parameters."},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"generalizes","note":"u = r^2 with P_i the standard basis recovers LoRA-XS"},{"target":"nola","type":"combines","note":"structural observation (NOLA is not cited): the core sum_i v_i P_i is a NOLA-style learned combination of fixed random matrices, placed inside the LoRA-XS SVD sandwich"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"combines","note":"structural observation: the trainable vector is tied across n_tie modules and layers (the paper cites ShareLoRA rather than Tied-LoRA for sharing)"},{"target":"lora-sb","type":"competes-with","note":"tiny core in frozen subspace"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04118","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04118","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/tinylora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/tinylora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/tinylora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["John X. Morris","Niloofar Mireshghallah","Mark Ibrahim","Saeed Mahloujifar"],"bibkey":"morris2026learning","bibtex":"@article{morris2026learning,\n  title = {{Learning to Reason in 13 Parameters}},\n  author = {John X. Morris and Niloofar Mireshghallah and Mark Ibrahim and Saeed Mahloujifar},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2602.04118},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.04118},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04118}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(v \\mapsto W_0+U_r \\Sigma_r^{1/2}(\\sum v_i P_i) \\Sigma_r^{1/2} V_r^T\\): linear, \\(d=u\\) for generic \\(P_i\\).","base_point":"HF default init_weights=True sets \\(v=0\\) (exact, per the verified catalog); the table and axes.json mark TinyLoRA as near-identity, which matches only init_weights='uniform'.","covariance":"Top-\\(r\\) SVD frame of \\(W_0\\) with a fixed random core subspace: \\(CO \\times CO\\) (in law for the \\(P_i\\)). Full weight tying shares \\(v\\) across boxes (Obs II.15 caveat).","rebasing":"Frames frozen: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"TinyLoRA is a \\(u\\)-dimensional random linear subspace of LoRA-XS's \\(r\\times r\\) core in the top-\\(r\\) SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): a linear method that maps into LoRA-XS and, composing with LoRA-XS\\(\\to\\)PiSSA, into PiSSA. Full weight tying shares one \\(v\\) across modules, so the per-box covariance of Obs II.15 needs the same transformation on every tied box.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_iv_iP_i\\Big)\\Sigma_r^{-1/2}","note":"Into LoRA-XS in the form W_0+U_rR Sigma_rV_r^T (Prop I.8(a)), pointed at R=0."},{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\Big(U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\big(I+\\textstyle\\sum_iv_iP_i\\big),\\ \\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\Big)","note":"Composite with LoRA-XS -> PiSSA; h(0)=PiSSA's q_0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"uni-lora","test":"O1","reason":"TinyLoRA's image is a u-dim linear space in W_0's top-r SVD frame; Uni-LoRA's is a quadratic cone in theta_d with W_0-independent random factors: neither contains the other generically."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"trainable-tokens","name":"Trainable Tokens","full_name":"Trainable Tokens (selective embedding-row tuning)","paper_title":"","authors":"Hugging Face PEFT team","year":2025,"venue":"Hugging Face PEFT (software, no paper)","arxiv":"","url":"https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/trainable_tokens","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"TrainableTokensConfig; or LoraConfig(trainable_token_indices=[...])","formula_latex":"E_{t,:}\\leftarrow v_t\\ (t\\in\\mathcal{T}),\\ \\ v_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d} \\text{ trained, initialized to } E_{0,t,:};\\ \\text{other rows frozen (equivalently } E=E_0+\\textstyle\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}}e_t(v_t-E_{0,t,:})^\\top)","trainable_params":"|\\mathcal{T}|\\,d","locus":"rows of the input embedding (and tied LM head)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"sparse row mask on the embedding matrix","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updated rows are written back into the embedding matrix.","identity_at_init":"New row values initialized to the existing token embeddings (no-op).","key_idea":"Train only the embeddings of new or special tokens (e.g. reasoning tokens) without full-embedding fine-tuning or a LoRA on the embedding.","results_note":"PEFT docs report about 4 GiB VRAM saved on gemma-2-2b versus fully fine-tuning the embedding matrix.","relations":[{"target":"shira","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are sparse selective updates of existing parameters (rows vs entries)."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Usable together with LoRA through trainable_token_indices."}],"sources":["https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/trainable_tokens","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/trainable_tokens/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.15.0","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/trainable_tokens"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","selective"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"team2025trainable","bibtex":"@misc{team2025trainable,\n  title = {{Trainable Tokens}},\n  author = {Hugging Face PEFT team},\n  year = {2025},\n  url = {https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/trainable_tokens},\n  howpublished = {Hugging Face PEFT (software, no paper)}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(E_0+S_TX\\) with \\(S_T\\) the \\(V\\times|T|\\) row selector: a fixed row mask on the embedding (and tied head).","covariance":"Disagrees with the table's Mon×Mon (shared row 'SHiRA / FISH / Trainable Tokens'): a row mask leaves every embedding coordinate free, so the image is \\(GL\\)-covariant on the embedding side, \\(E\\mapsto EQ\\), and only token-preserving (monomial) maps are allowed on the vocabulary side. It therefore descends through SliceGPT residual rotations (Obs II.15), unlike entrywise masks."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-FB with frozen \\(B=S_T\\), the row selector of the chosen tokens: a Lin row mask on the embedding, regular and idempotent. It lies in the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum of LoRA, not in the default \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) one.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(S_T,\\,X),\\quad X=V_T-E_{0,T}","note":"Freezing B at the selector: LoRA_{|T|} on the embedding pointed at (S_T,0), the T_B stratum (cf. MiCA → LoRA pointed at (B_0,0))."},{"target":"shira","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"SHiRA with the support T × [d] (whole rows); the mask methods form one family indexed by support."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Against LoRA's default pointing (B_0=0, A_0 random; T_A stratum): S_1={BA_0} has fixed row space row A_0, which does not contain row-supported updates with arbitrary rows; the arrow above exists only into the T_B stratum."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"uni-lora","name":"Uni-LoRA","full_name":"Uni-LoRA: one vector for all LoRA parameters via an isometric projection","paper_title":"Uni-LoRA: One Vector is All You Need","authors":"Li et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2506.00799","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00799","code_url":"https://github.com/KaiyangLi1992/Uni-LoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"UniLoraConfig","formula_latex":"\\theta_D = P\\,\\theta_d,\\quad \\theta_D = \\mathrm{Concat}_{\\ell}\\big(\\mathrm{vec}(B^{\\ell}),\\mathrm{vec}(A^{\\ell})\\big)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D},\\ D=\\sum_\\ell r\\,(d^{\\ell}_{in}+d^{\\ell}_{out}),\\quad W_\\ell = W_{0,\\ell} + B^{\\ell}A^{\\ell};\\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D\\times d}\\ \\text{frozen: each row one-hot at a uniformly random column, column } j \\text{ scaled to } 1/\\sqrt{n_j};\\ \\theta_d\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ \\text{trained (a single vector for the whole model)}","trainable_params":"d\\ \\text{for the whole model (independent of } L,\\ d_{in},\\ d_{out},\\ r)","locus":"all LoRA-adapted modules of the model jointly (global, cross-layer)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"global random isometric projection from one vector to all LoRA factors: a random partition of the \\(D\\) LoRA entries into \\(d\\) groups, each group sharing one (\\(1/\\sqrt{n_j}\\)-scaled) value; the projection framework \\(\\theta_D = P\\theta_d\\) also expresses LoRA (\\(P=I\\)), Tied-LoRA, VeRA, LoRA-XS and VB-LoRA","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\theta_d\\) determines fixed \\(B\\), \\(A\\) for every layer, and each \\(BA\\) merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not exact: \\(\\theta_d \\sim U(-0.02, 0.02)\\) (HF default; zeros avoided to prevent vanishing gradients), so \\(B\\), \\(A\\) are small and \\(\\Delta W = BA\\) is near zero but non-zero","key_idea":"All parameter-efficient LoRA variants are projections of a low-dimensional vector into LoRA's parameter space and differ only in the projection matrix; a global, uniform, isometric random-grouping projection gives maximal cross-layer sharing at negligible cost (matching Fastfood).","results_note":"Matches or outperforms state-of-the-art LoRA variants on GLUE, mathematical reasoning (GSM8K/MATH) and instruction tuning while training a single vector for the whole LLM; the random-grouping projection matches Fastfood at much lower cost.","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"generalizes","note":"VeRA is a structured layer-wise projection in the Uni-LoRA framework"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"generalizes","note":"Tied-LoRA corresponds to a tying projection"},{"target":"vb-lora","type":"generalizes","note":"VB-LoRA corresponds to a trainable projection"},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA-XS corresponds to a fixed SVD-based projection"},{"target":"intrinsic-said","type":"builds-on","note":"intrinsic-dimension random projections (Fastfood) applied to LoRA's parameter space"},{"target":"mosa","type":"competes-with","note":"MoSA applies the same equal-size random grouping directly to Delta W entries instead of LoRA factor entries"},{"target":"tinylora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both train a tiny vector projected into low-rank adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00799","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.00799","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/unilora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/unilora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/unilora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Kaiyang Li","Shaobo Han","Qing Su","Wei Li","Zhipeng Cai","Shihao Ji"],"bibkey":"li2025uni","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2025uni,\n  title = {{Uni-LoRA: One Vector is All You Need}},\n  author = {Kaiyang Li and Shaobo Han and Qing Su and Wei Li and Zhipeng Cai and Shihao Ji},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2506.00799},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00799}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho=\\rho_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}\\circ P\\) with \\(P\\) linear: quadratic, homogeneous of degree 2 in \\(\\theta_d\\), a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) at \\(\\theta_d=0\\).","base_point":"Defect: HF draws \\(\\theta_d\\sim U(-0.02,0.02)\\). The exactly pointed variant \\(\\theta_d=0\\) has \\(S_1=0\\) (checked), which is why zero init is avoided.","gauge":"Discrete only (\\(\\theta_d \\mapsto -\\theta_d\\)): the random grouping mixes \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) entries, so no \\(GL_r\\) or scalar slide survives; Jacobian rank \\(= |M|\\) (checked). Table: 'discrete'.","covariance":"Frozen random one-hot projection: covariance in law, and only under coordinate permutations (a uniform random grouping is exchangeable but not rotation-invariant).","rebasing":"\\(C = \\{q(\\theta_d)\\}\\) is a quadratic image closed only under positive scaling (\\(q(-\\theta)=q(\\theta)\\)), so Thm V.3(a) gives \\(R_\\infty = \\theta_0 + \\mathrm{Mon}(C) = \\theta_0 + L(\\mathrm{PSD}_d)\\), \\(L\\) the linear map \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_d \\to \\Theta\\) with \\(q(\\theta)=L(\\theta\\theta^\\top)\\): the convex cone generated by \\(C\\), of dimension \\(\\dim\\mathrm{span}\\,C \\le d(d+1)/2\\). It equals \\(\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\,C\\) only if \\(\\ker L\\) contains a positive-definite form; \\(L\\) is injective on \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_d\\) at \\(d=4,8,12\\) (span dim \\(= d(d+1)/2\\), checked), so there the reach is a pointed cone that never contains \\(-q(\\theta)\\). \\(\\Theta\\) additionally needs \\(d(d+1)/2 \\ge \\dim\\Theta_{adapted}\\). 'span' is the nearest axis value; the table's '\\(\\to\\Theta\\)' fails at small \\(d\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Uni-LoRA is LoRA precomposed with a frozen random isometric linear map \\(P:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^D\\): \\(\\rho=\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}\\circ P\\), a quadratic cone in \\(\\theta_d\\) with \\(S_1=0\\) at \\(\\theta_d=0\\), which is why it must start off the base point. Its 'projection framework' parametrises a family of objects by \\(P\\); different fixed \\(P\\) give incomparable images, so it supplies arrows into LoRA, not arrows from the methods it 'generalises'.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\theta_d)=P\\theta_d=\\big(B^\\ell(P\\theta_d),A^\\ell(P\\theta_d)\\big)_\\ell","note":"The defining projection, pointed at (B_0,A_0)=P theta_{d,0} (an arrow over the common start), or at the origin (0,0) for the exactly pointed variant."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"VeRA is LoRA o P_VeRA for a structured P; a fixed random-grouping Uni-LoRA instance has a different image. 'Generalizes' is a relation between families of P, not an arrow."},{"target":"vb-lora","test":"O1","reason":"VB-LoRA's mixing is trained and chunk-structured, Uni-LoRA's projection frozen and random: incomparable images at fixed hyperparameters."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"vb-lora","name":"VB-LoRA","full_name":"Vector-Bank Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"VB-LoRA: Extreme Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning with Vector Banks","authors":"Li et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.15179","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15179","code_url":"https://github.com/leo-yangli/VB-LoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"VBLoRAConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + BA,\\quad \\text{every length-}b\\text{ sub-vector } u \\text{ of every column of } B \\text{ and row of } A:\\ u = \\sum_{s=1}^{h} w_s(\\sigma)\\,\\alpha_s,\\ \\ w(\\sigma) = \\mathrm{Softmax}\\big(\\mathrm{TopK}(\\sigma,k)\\big);\\ \\text{bank } \\{\\alpha_s\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b}\\}_{s=1}^{h}\\ \\text{shared by all modules and layers (trained)},\\ \\sigma\\in\\mathbb{R}^{h}\\ \\text{logits per sub-vector (trained)}","trainable_params":"\\text{training: } \\tfrac{r(d_{in}+d_{out})}{b}\\,h\\ \\text{logits per matrix} + hb\\ \\text{shared};\\ \\ \\text{stored } (k=2,\\ \\text{uint8 indices}): \\tfrac{3}{2}\\,\\tfrac{r(d_{in}+d_{out})}{b}\\ \\text{per matrix} + hb\\ \\text{shared}","locus":"any linear layer, sharing across modules and layers (paper: query/value or all linear layers of RoBERTa, GPT-2, Llama2, Mistral, Gemma)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"divide-and-share: LoRA's rank-one component vectors are split into sub-vectors, each a sparse top-\\(k\\) convex combination (simplex-constrained) of a globally shared vector bank -- a hierarchical, constrained CP/tensor factorisation spanning the whole model","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training the logits are discarded, the selected mixtures define fixed \\(B\\), \\(A\\), and \\(BA\\) merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not exact: bank \\(\\sim U(-0.02, 0.02)\\) and logits \\(\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, 0.1^2)\\) (HF defaults), so \\(\\Delta W\\) is small but non-zero; an all-zero bank is avoided because it would give zero gradients","key_idea":"Treat all LoRA parameters of a model as sub-vectors drawn from one small learned vector bank via differentiable sparse top-\\(k\\) admixture, so storage stops scaling with model width, depth and number of modules.","results_note":"On Llama2-13B instruction tuning VB-LoRA uses only 0.4% of LoRA's stored parameters yet scores higher on MT-Bench; competitive with or better than VeRA, Tied-LoRA and LoRA on GLUE, E2E (about 20% fewer stored parameters than VeRA) and GSM8K/MATH (Mistral-7B, Gemma-7B).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"global reparameterisation of LoRA factors"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"fewer stored parameters than VeRA at comparable quality"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"finer-grained, learned sharing"},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Uni-LoRA writes VB-LoRA as a trainable projection P from the bank to theta_D"},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"compared on GSM8K/MATH"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15179","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.15179","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/vblora/config.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/vblora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/vblora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/vblora/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Yang Li","Shaobo Han","Shihao Ji"],"bibkey":"li2024vb","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2024vb,\n  title = {{VB-LoRA: Extreme Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning with Vector Banks}},\n  author = {Yang Li and Shaobo Han and Shihao Ji},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.15179},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15179}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"On each top-\\(k\\) chamber \\(\\rho=\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}\\circ h\\) with \\(h\\) bilinear in (simplex weights, bank): homogeneous of degree 2 in the bank, a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) at a zero bank.","base_point":"Defect: bank \\(\\sim U(-0.02,0.02)\\), logits \\(\\sim N(0,0.01)\\); a zero bank is the exactly pointed variant with \\(S_1=0\\).","gauge":"Table: 'discrete' (mapped to none), correct in the stored coordinates (\\(k\\) simplex weights + bank). In the training coordinates the fibre has dim \\(h-k+1\\) per chunk: the softmax shift plus the unselected logits, which do not reach \\(\\rho\\).","covariance":"Table says 'fr', but the bank is trained: no frozen random frame shapes the image. What is frozen is the chunk partition \\(\\mathbb R^m=\\mathbb R^{m/b}\\otimes\\mathbb R^b\\) (and \\(\\mathbb R^n\\)), tied across all boxes through the shared bank: imposed structure \\(\\Pi\\).","rebasing":"The bank is free, so \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,C\\) contains every block-supported rank-one matrix: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"VB-LoRA assembles every length-\\(b\\) chunk of LoRA's factors as a top-\\(k\\) softmax mixture of one shared trainable bank: on each top-\\(k\\) chamber \\(\\rho=\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}\\circ h\\) with \\(h\\) of degree two, so the image is a cone with \\(S_1=0\\) at a zero bank and the method starts at a small defect. Its geometry is the imposed chunk partition \\(\\Pi\\) (a reshape \\(\\mathbb R^m=\\mathbb R^{m/b}\\otimes\\mathbb R^b\\) tied across all boxes), not a random frame, since the bank is trained.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,\\sigma)=\\big(B(\\alpha,\\sigma),\\ A(\\alpha,\\sigma)\\big),\\ \\ u=\\textstyle\\sum_s\\mathrm{softmax}(\\mathrm{TopK}(\\sigma,k))_s\\,\\alpha_s","note":"Assembly map into LoRA, pointed at (B_0,A_0)=h(q_0); smooth on each top-k chamber only (top-k switches are non-smooth)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"uni-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Uni-LoRA writes VB-LoRA as a trainable projection, but a fixed Uni-LoRA instance (frozen random P) and VB-LoRA (trained bank, chunk structure) have incomparable images."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"vera","name":"VeRA","full_name":"Vector-based Random Matrix Adaptation","paper_title":"VeRA: Vector-based Random Matrix Adaptation","authors":"Kopiczko et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.11454","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11454","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"VeraConfig","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_dAx,\\ \\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r} \\text{ frozen random, shared across layers},\\ \\Lambda_d=\\mathrm{diag}(d),\\Lambda_b=\\mathrm{diag}(b) \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r+d_{out}","locus":"linear layers (shared \\(A\\)/\\(B\\) sliced to each layer's shape)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"frozen shared random low-rank bases with trainable diagonal scalings on both sides","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Lambda_b B \\Lambda_d A\\) is a fixed low-rank matrix.","identity_at_init":"\\(b = 0\\) (\\(d = 0.1\\)), so the update is zero.","key_idea":"Random projections are good enough as LoRA bases; only learn per-layer scaling vectors.","results_note":"Paper reports matching LoRA with 10x fewer trainable parameters on GLUE, E2E and instruction tuning.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Fixes LoRA's A, B to shared random matrices and learns diagonal scalings."},{"target":"randlora","type":"competes-with","note":"Closest random-basis relative. VeRA scales outputs and rank; RandLoRA sums many bases and scales rank and input, so VeRA is not literally its single-basis case."},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"A structured projection in Uni-LoRA's theta_D = P theta_d view."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"reparameterises LoRA's B, A as frozen shared random matrices with trainable diagonal scalings"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Tied-LoRA's framework recovers VeRA when tied A, B are random and frozen and only u, v are trained"},{"target":"nola","type":"competes-with","note":"contemporaneous random-basis compression of LoRA (ICLR 2024)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11454","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/vera","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/vera/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.11454","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/vera/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/vera/model.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Dawid J. Kopiczko","Tijmen Blankevoort","Yuki M. Asano"],"bibkey":"kopiczko2023vera","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kopiczko2023vera,\n  title = {{VeRA: Vector-based Random Matrix Adaptation}},\n  author = {Dawid J. Kopiczko and Tijmen Blankevoort and Yuki M. Asano},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.11454},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11454}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Toric cone \\(\\{\\Lambda_b B \\Lambda_d A\\}\\), \\(d=m+r-1\\), max rank \\(r\\) (table).","base_point":"\\(b_0=0\\): only \\(b\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4), apex.","gauge":"\\(\\mathbb{R}^\\times\\) slide \\((cb, d/c)\\) through the frozen \\(B\\) (table).","covariance":"Table: \\(\\mathrm{Mon} \\times \\mathrm{fr}\\) (\\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\) on the output side from \\(\\Lambda_b\\), random frame in law on the input side).","rebasing":"\\(\\mathrm{VeRA}^\\infty\\) = LoRA-FA\\((A)\\) when \\(B\\) has no zero entries (Thm V.3 Cor 3)."},"categorical_reading":"VeRA is the toric cone \\(\\{\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_dA\\}\\) of dimension \\(m+r-1\\): an A3 sub-object of LoRA-FA\\((A)\\) via \\(h(b,d)=\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d\\), with apex at \\(b=0\\), a one-scalar gauge and rebasing closure exactly LoRA-FA\\((A)\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 3). It is the NOLA construction with coordinate-structured bases \\(E_{ii}B\\), \\(E_{jj}A\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d)=\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d","note":"axes.json A3 example; pointed at 0 with frozen A."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d)=(\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d,\\ A)","note":"Composite with LoRA-FA(A) -> LoRA, pointed at (0,A)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"randlora","test":"O1","reason":"VeRA's row space is fixed (row A) while its column space moves; RandLoRA's terms fix column spaces in col B_j and move row spaces through Gamma_j: VeRA is not RandLoRA's single-basis case."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"waveft","name":"WaveFT","full_name":"Wavelet Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Exploring Sparsity for Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning Using Wavelets for Vision","authors":"Bilican et al.","year":2025,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2505.12532","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12532","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"WaveFTConfig","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\lambda\\,\\mathrm{IDWT}_{2D}(C),\\quad C\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{sparse: exactly } p \\text{ entries chosen uniformly at random are trainable, the rest fixed at } 0","trainable_params":"p\\ \\ (\\text{any integer, e.g. far below LoRA's rank-1 count } d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (paper: SDXL attention layers for personalisation, ViT-Base, RoBERTa on GLUE)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sparse coefficients in a 2-D discrete wavelet basis (semi-local receptive fields); SHiRA is the special case IDWT = identity; the linear, rank-preserving IDWT keeps the high-rank property of sparse updates","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The inverse wavelet transform of the trained sparse coefficients is a dense matrix added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(C = 0\\), so \\(W = W_0\\) at the start of training (Gaussian init explored as an ablation)","key_idea":"Learn highly sparse updates in the wavelet domain: wavelets aggregate spatially coherent gradients (better coverage than raw weight sparsity) without the global interference of Fourier bases, giving fine-grained parameter control below LoRA's minimum.","results_note":"On DreamBooth with SDXL WaveFT reaches 0.495 DINO similarity versus LoRA's 0.463 at equal parameter counts with higher diversity; on ViT-Base it reaches 78.29% average accuracy with 72K parameters versus LoRA's 77.58% at 581K; slightly below FourierFT on GLUE.","relations":[{"target":"shira","type":"generalizes","note":"SHiRA is WaveFT with the IDWT replaced by the identity (stated in the paper)"},{"target":"fourierft","type":"competes-with","note":"local wavelet basis versus global Fourier basis"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"fine-grained budgets below rank 1"},{"target":"shira","type":"competes-with","note":"Sparse update, but sparse in a transform domain rather than in W itself."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12532","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12532","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/package_reference/waveft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/waveft","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/waveft/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","sharing"],"authors_full":["Ahmet Bilican","M. Akın Yılmaz","A. Murat Tekalp","R. Gökberk Cinbiş"],"bibkey":"bilican2025exploring","bibtex":"@article{bilican2025exploring,\n  title = {{Exploring Sparsity for Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning Using Wavelets for Vision}},\n  author = {Ahmet Bilican and M. Akın Yılmaz and A. Murat Tekalp and R. Gökberk Cinbiş},\n  year = {2025},\n  eprint = {2505.12532},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.12532},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12532}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\lambda\\,\\mathrm{IDWT}_{2D}(C_\\Omega)\\): linear and injective (IDWT invertible), \\(d=p\\).","base_point":"\\(C_0=0\\): exactly pointed, regular.","covariance":"The dyadic wavelet structure on the index order is imposed (\\(\\Pi\\)); neuron permutations break it.","rebasing":"Fixed indices: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"WaveFT is a mask in the 2-D wavelet frame, \\(\\Delta W=\\lambda\\,\\mathrm{IDWT}_{2D}(C_\\Omega)\\), i.e. SHiRA transported by the invertible IDWT: a transport is an automorphism of the slice and not an arrow, so WaveFT and SHiRA are the same kind of object in different frames, with different images. With a separable DWT every atom is a rank-one outer product, so WaveFT factors through LoRA-FA with frozen wavelet frames.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(c)=\\lambda\\,B_W\\,C_\\Omega(c),\\ \\ A_W=[\\psi_j^\\top]_{j\\in\\mathrm{cols}(\\Omega)}","note":"Separable 2-D DWT: atom (i,j) is w_i v_j^T, so DeltaW=B_W C_Omega A_W; into LoRA-FA with frozen wavelet rows A_W, pointed at 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"shira","test":"O1","reason":"Related by the transport IDWT (an automorphism, not an arrow): p-sparse wavelet coefficients give a semi-local dense pattern, not a p-sparse matrix, so the images differ."},{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"FourierFT's image lies in the point-symmetric subspace DeltaW_{j,l}=DeltaW_{-j,-l}; wavelet atoms are not point-symmetric."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"dora","name":"DoRA","full_name":"Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"DoRA: Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.09353","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09353","code_url":"https://github.com/NVlabs/DoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(use_dora=True)","formula_latex":"W_{i,:}=m_i\\,\\frac{(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA)_{i,:}}{\\|(W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA)_{i,:}\\|_2},\\ \\ m\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}} \\text{ trained (init } m_i=\\|(W_0)_{i,:}\\|_2),\\ B,A \\text{ as LoRA}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+d_{out}","locus":"linear, embedding and Conv1d/2d/3d layers (PEFT DoRA variants)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"magnitude-direction decomposition: learned per-output norm times the normalized (\\(W_0\\) + low-rank) direction (the paper's 'column-wise' norm in its transposed convention)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training \\(m\\) and the normalized direction combine into one dense weight (merging recommended to remove the overhead).","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) and \\(m =\\) row norms of \\(W_0\\), so \\(W = W_0\\).","key_idea":"Full fine-tuning changes magnitude and direction differently; let LoRA handle direction and a separate vector handle magnitude.","results_note":"Paper reports consistent gains over LoRA on commonsense reasoning (LLaMA family), visual instruction tuning and image/video-text understanding.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA updates the direction component."},{"target":"delora","type":"competes-with","note":"DeLoRA normalizes the update instead of the full weight."},{"target":"bidora","type":"competes-with","note":"BiDoRA keeps DoRA's parameterization and changes the optimization to bi-level; DoRA is its main baseline"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09353","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora_variant_dora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.09353","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/dora.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Shih-Yang Liu","Chien-Yi Wang","Hongxu Yin","Pavlo Molchanov","Yu-Chiang Frank Wang","Kwang-Ting Cheng","Min-Hung Chen"],"bibkey":"liu2024dora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2024dora,\n  title = {{DoRA: Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Shih-Yang Liu and Chien-Yi Wang and Hongxu Yin and Pavlo Molchanov and Yu-Chiang Frank Wang and Kwang-Ting Cheng and Min-Hung Chen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.09353},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09353}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Sat","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Prop II.8: \\(\\mathrm{Im}=\\mathrm{Diag}_m\\cdot(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\), dimension \\(\\min(mn,r(m+n-r)+m)\\) for generic \\(W_0\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\), \\(\\mu=\\) row norms: inherits LoRA's deficit through the torus factor.","covariance":"Contains \\(\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times GL_n\\) (per-output magnitude)."},"categorical_reading":"DoRA's image is \\(\\mathrm{Diag}_m\\cdot(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\), exactly LoRA followed by a trainable output scale (Prop II.8): a torus saturation of LoRA's cone that inherits LoRA's apex and \\(GL_r\\) gauge. What it adds beyond that image is training geometry (the row-normalised metric, or HF's detached, non-gradient backward).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"bidora","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same parametrisation (scale absorbed): an isomorphism; BiDoRA differs only in its bi-level training."},{"target":"glora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\mu)\\mapsto\\big(B_A=c-\\mathbf 1,\\ A_A=\\mathbf 1^\\top;\\ B_B=\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,sB,\\ A_B=A\\big),\\quad c=\\mu\\oslash\\big(\\|(W_0+sBA)_{i,:}\\|\\big)_i","note":"GLoRA (A path rank >= 1, B path rank r) re-pointed at (0, 1^T; 0, A_0), since diag(c)(W_0 + sBA) = W_0 + W_0 (.) ((c - 1) 1^T) + diag(c) sBA."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Output rescalings diag(mu) W_0 have rank up to rank W_0, outside LoRA_r's image (axes.json O1 example)."},{"target":"delora","test":"O1","reason":"DeLoRA's image is LoRA's cone, which misses DoRA's output rescalings; DeLoRA normalises the update, DoRA the weight."},{"target":"loft-orthogonal","test":"O1","reason":"LOFT keeps the neuron Gram matrix W W^T exactly (Cor II.6), and DoRA changes it."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Sat"},{"id":"rslora","name":"rsLoRA","full_name":"Rank-Stabilized LoRA","paper_title":"A Rank Stabilization Scaling Factor for Fine-Tuning with LoRA","authors":"Kalajdzievski","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2312.03732","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(use_rslora=True)","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\gamma_r BA,\\quad \\gamma_r=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}\\ \\ (\\text{instead of }\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}),\\quad B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"same \\(\\Delta W=BA\\) as LoRA; only the scalar multiplier changes so that activations and gradients stay \\(\\Theta(1)\\) as \\(r\\) grows","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Identical to LoRA up to a scalar.","identity_at_init":"\\(B=0\\) as in LoRA.","key_idea":"Shows that the standard \\(\\alpha/r\\) scaling makes higher-rank adapters learn too slowly (gradient collapse), and that \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\) is the rank-stable choice that lets larger ranks actually help.","results_note":"Fine-tuning Llama 2 on 20k OpenOrca examples with \\(r \\in \\{4,\\dots,2048\\}\\): with \\(\\alpha/r\\) larger ranks learn no better (gradient collapse), while with \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\) perplexity keeps improving as \\(r\\) grows and gradient norms stay rank-stable.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"changes only the rank-dependent scaling factor"},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"Both fix LoRA's training dynamics through scaling (of output vs learning rates)."},{"target":"lora-without-regret","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA Without Regret argues 1/r makes the optimal LR roughly rank-independent (early training), whereas rsLoRA argues 1/sqrt(r) is needed for large-rank stability"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03732","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Damjan Kalajdzievski"],"bibkey":"kalajdzievski2023rank","bibtex":"@article{kalajdzievski2023rank,\n  title = {{A Rank Stabilization Scaling Factor for Fine-Tuning with LoRA}},\n  author = {Damjan Kalajdzievski},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2312.03732},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.03732},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(\\rho_{\\rm rs}(B,A)=\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}(\\sqrt r\\,B,A)\\): the same object (Cor III.11(c)). The rank-stability prescription concerns how \\(\\alpha\\) should scale with \\(r\\), i.e. a path through the hyperparameter torus across ranks."},"categorical_reading":"rsLoRA is LoRA itself: the scale change is the isomorphism \\(h=(\\sqrt r\\,B,A)\\) of the slice, or equivalently a point of the hyperparameter torus (Thm III.10). Its content is a choice of trajectory \\(r\\mapsto(\\alpha,\\eta)\\) across ranks, not a new object.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(\\sqrt r\\,B,\\ A)","note":"Isomorphism over \\(\\Theta\\) (inverse \\(B\\mapsto B/\\sqrt r\\)); pointed because \\(B_0=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"pissa","name":"PiSSA","full_name":"Principal Singular values and Singular vectors Adaptation","paper_title":"PiSSA: Principal Singular Values and Singular Vectors Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Meng et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2404.02948","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02948","code_url":"https://github.com/GraphPKU/PiSSA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"pissa\")  (or \"pissa_niter_[n]\" for fast randomized SVD)","formula_latex":"W_0=U S V^\\top,\\quad B=U_{[:,:r]}S_{[:r,:r]}^{1/2},\\quad A=S_{[:r,:r]}^{1/2}V_{[:,:r]}^\\top,\\quad W^{res}=U_{[:,r:]}S_{[r:,r:]}V_{[:,r:]}^\\top=W_0-BA,\\qquad h=(W^{res}+BA)\\,x;\\ W^{res}\\text{ frozen},\\ A,B\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (attention and MLP projections)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space (\\(\\Delta W = BA - B_0A_0\\)); the base point is moved from \\(W_0\\) to the rank-\\((\\min(d_{in},d_{out})-r)\\) residual \\(W^{res}\\) and the adapter starts on the principal rank-\\(r\\) SVD component of \\(W_0\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W^{res}+BA\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape; to ship as a plain LoRA on the untouched \\(W_0\\) the paper converts it to \\(\\Delta W=[B'\\ B][A';-A]\\) at rank \\(2r\\).","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W^{res}+B_0A_0=W_0\\) by construction (data-free truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\); fast randomized SVD makes it seconds).","key_idea":"Instead of zero-initializing the adapter, put the top-\\(r\\) singular triplets of \\(W_0\\) into the trainable factors and freeze the residual, so training updates the directions that matter most and converges faster; the residual also quantizes better (QPiSSA).","results_note":"Reported consistent gains over LoRA on 11 models (184M-70B), e.g. GSM8K 53.22 vs 42.85 on LLaMA-2-7B and 77.78 vs 74.53 on Gemma-7B; QPiSSA on LLaMA-3-70B reaches 86.05 vs 81.73 for QLoRA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same BA parameterization, only the initialization and base point change"},{"target":"milora","type":"dual-to","note":"PiSSA trains the top-r singular components, MiLoRA the bottom-r"},{"target":"corda","type":"special-case-of","note":"CorDA instruction-previewed mode with covariance C=I reduces to PiSSA"},{"target":"olora","type":"competes-with","note":"OLoRA uses a QR instead of an SVD factorization of W_0"},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"QPiSSA and LoftQ both reduce quantization error by splitting W_0 into a quantized residual plus a low-rank adapter"},{"target":"mica","type":"dual-to","note":"MiCA uses the minor instead of principal singular directions."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02948","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.02948","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Fanxu Meng","Zhaohui Wang","Muhan Zhang"],"bibkey":"meng2024pissa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{meng2024pissa,\n  title = {{PiSSA: Principal Singular Values and Singular Vectors Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Fanxu Meng and Zhaohui Wang and Muhan Zhang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2404.02948},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02948}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\((W_0-[W_0]_r)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\): the cone translated to the residual.","base_point":"\\(\\mathrm S\\) stratum, so \\(\\theta_0\\) is smooth: \\(\\dim S_1=r(m+n-r)\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\); balanced (\\(\\Phi_0=0\\)) iff \\(\\eta_A=\\eta_B\\).","covariance":"The truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\) gives \\(CO(m)\\times CO(n)\\) (Prop II.16)."},"categorical_reading":"PiSSA is the Type II point \\(P=[W_0]_r\\) of the stratum \\(\\mathcal M_r\\): the vertex moves to \\(W_0-[W_0]_r\\), \\(\\theta_0\\) becomes a smooth point with full first-order reach, and the pointing is balanced at equal rates (Prop III.8). Measured from \\(\\theta_0\\), its updates \\(Y-P\\) reach rank \\(2r\\), which is the PiSSA \\(\\to\\) LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) conversion.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1: PiSSA\\(_r\\) into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (HF's conversion), pointed at the degenerate pointing with product \\(0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"A Type II smooth point against a Type I apex at equal rank: the images are incomparable (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"milora","test":"O1","reason":"Top-\\(r\\) against bottom-\\(r\\) split: different vertices, \\(W_0\\) minus its top or its bottom rank-\\(r\\) part, so the images are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"olora","name":"OLoRA","full_name":"Orthonormal Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"OLoRA: Orthonormal Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Büyükakyüz","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2406.01775","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01775","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"olora\")","formula_latex":"W_0=QR,\\quad B_0=Q_{[:,1:r]},\\quad A_0=R_{[1:r,:]},\\quad W^{res}=W_0-s\\,B_0A_0,\\qquad h=(W^{res}+s\\,BA)\\,x;\\ W^{res}\\text{ frozen},\\ A,B\\text{ trained}\\ \\ (s=\\alpha/r,\\text{ or }\\alpha/\\sqrt r\\text{ with rsLoRA, in HF PEFT})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space; \\(B\\) starts with orthonormal columns (first \\(r\\) columns of the QR factor \\(Q\\) of \\(W_0\\)) and \\(A\\) with the first \\(r\\) rows of the upper-triangular factor \\(R\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W^{res}+s\\,BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Exact as implemented in HF PEFT (olora_init subtracts \\(s B_0A_0\\) and the forward adds \\(s BA\\)); data-free QR of \\(W_0\\). Note the paper's Alg. 1 subtracts \\(s B_0A_0\\) but writes the forward as \\(W+B A\\), which is exact only for \\(s=1\\).","key_idea":"Use a cheap QR decomposition of \\(W_0\\) to give the adapter an orthonormal starting basis, which the author argues improves conditioning, stability and convergence speed.","results_note":"Reported higher final accuracy than LoRA in 53 of 60 model/task/rank settings on six commonsense benchmarks for Mistral-7B, LLaMA-2-7B, TinyLlama-1.1B, Gemma-2B and OPT-1.3B.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"QR-based instead of SVD-based base-point splitting of W_0"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same BA adapter, different initialization"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01775","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.01775","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/layer.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Kerim Büyükakyüz"],"bibkey":"buyukakyuz2024olora","bibtex":"@article{buyukakyuz2024olora,\n  title = {{OLoRA: Orthonormal Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Kerim Büyükakyüz},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2406.01775},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.01775},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01775}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"The vertex is \\(W_0-sQ_rR_r\\): \\(W_0\\) minus its projection onto the span of its first \\(r\\) columns.","base_point":"S stratum, smooth (exact in HF; the paper's forward is exact only for \\(s=1\\)).","covariance":"\\(CO(m)\\times\\mathrm{Borel}_n\\) (the table's \"CO×Borel\"): \\(P=Q_rQ_r^\\top W_0\\) depends on the order of input coordinates, so input permutations change it. That is the \\(\\Pi\\) test, so the key is Pi."},"categorical_reading":"OLoRA is another point of the Type II stratum, \\(P=Q_rR_r\\), chosen by a QR flag rather than a spectral frame. The flag privileges the first \\(r\\) input coordinates, so unlike PiSSA its pointing is not permutation-natural, and its charge \\(\\Phi_0=I/\\eta_B-R_rR_r^\\top/\\eta_A\\) is generally unbalanced.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (Thm II.1), at a degenerate pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Different points \\(P\\) (QR against SVD) of the S stratum: the translated cones are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"loftq","name":"LoftQ","full_name":"LoRA-Fine-Tuning-aware Quantization","paper_title":"LoftQ: LoRA-Fine-Tuning-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models","authors":"Li et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.08659","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08659","code_url":"https://github.com/yxli2123/LoftQ","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"loftq\", loftq_config=LoftQConfig(loftq_bits=4, loftq_iter=1))  (on the full-precision model; or replace_lora_weights_loftq(peft_model) on a bnb-quantized model)","formula_latex":"\\min_{Q,A,B}\\lVert W_0-Q-BA\\rVert_F:\\quad Q_t=q_N(W_0-B_{t-1}A_{t-1}),\\ \\ W_0-Q_t=\\textstyle\\sum_i\\sigma_{t,i}u_{t,i}v_{t,i}^\\top,\\ \\ B_t=[\\sqrt{\\sigma_{t,i}}u_{t,i}]_{i\\le r},\\ A_t=[\\sqrt{\\sigma_{t,i}}v_{t,i}^\\top]_{i\\le r};\\qquad h=(Q_T+BA)x;\\ Q_T\\text{ frozen (N-bit)},\\ A,B\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer of a quantized model","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space; the base point is an \\(N\\)-bit quantized matrix \\(Q\\) and the adapter is initialized to the rank-\\(r\\) SVD of the quantization residual, alternating \\(T\\) times","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) can be added to the dequantized \\(Q\\), but the merged matrix is no longer \\(N\\)-bit unless re-quantized, which reintroduces error.","identity_at_init":"Approximate: \\(Q_T+B_TA_T\\approx W_0\\) (built to be closer than QLoRA's \\(Q+0\\); HF's initialiser ignores the LoRA scale \\(s=\\alpha/r\\), so with one iteration it is closer only for \\(0<s<2\\)); data-free alternating quantization/SVD.","key_idea":"When the base will be quantized, choose the quantized base and the low-rank adapter jointly so that their sum best approximates \\(W_0\\), instead of zero-initializing the adapter on top of an inaccurate quantized weight.","results_note":"Reported large gains over QLoRA at low bit widths, e.g. about 8% on MNLI and over 10% on SQuAD v1.1 at 2-bit, and convergence where QLoRA fails.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"same quantized-base + LoRA setting with a better joint initialization"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"QPiSSA also lowers quantization error by quantizing the SVD residual"},{"target":"lq-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent; same objective \\min\\lVert W_0-Q-BA\\rVert, LQ-LoRA starts from Q^{(0)}=0 and adds ILP mixed precision and Fisher weighting"},{"target":"apiq","type":"competes-with","note":"ApiQ replaces LoftQ's weight-space objective by an activation-space objective"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08659","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.08659","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/quantization","https://openreview.net/forum?id=LzPWWPAdY4","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/developer_guides/quantization.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Yixiao Li","Yifan Yu","Chen Liang","Pengcheng He","Nikos Karampatziakis","Weizhu Chen","Tuo Zhao"],"bibkey":"li2023loftq","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2023loftq,\n  title = {{LoftQ: LoRA-Fine-Tuning-Aware Quantization for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yixiao Li and Yifan Yu and Chen Liang and Pengcheng He and Nikos Karampatziakis and Weizhu Chen and Tuo Zhao},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.08659},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.08659}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(Q_T+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), started at \\(B_TA_T=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0-Q_T)\\): an approximate split over a quantised residual.","base_point":"\\(\\rho(q_0)=Q_T+B_TA_T\\approx\\theta_0\\). With the default `loftq_iter=1` the defect is \\(e-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\), because HF's initialiser ignores the LoRA scale \\(s=\\alpha/r\\). It is the rank-\\(r\\) tail \\(e-\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\) of the quantisation error only at \\(s=1\\), and smaller than QLoRA's defect \\(e\\) only for \\(0<s<2\\) (Prop V.2(b)).","covariance":"The quantisation grid (blockwise NF4 scales) lives in the standard basis: Mon×Mon.","merge":"An exact merge of the high-precision \\(BA\\) leaves the \\(N\\)-bit grid; requantising is lossy.","rebasing":"Converges to \\(\\Theta\\) in exact arithmetic; each merge requantises."},"categorical_reading":"LoftQ is base change along the quantiser followed by an approximate section: it chooses \\((Q,q_0)\\) with \\(\\rho(q_0)\\) close to \\(\\theta_0\\), landing at a smooth point of the cone over \\(Q_T\\). With one iteration it is QLoRA's own map \\(\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), re-pointed from the apex to \\(s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(\\theta_0-\\kappa\\theta_0)=-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\), with \\(s=\\alpha/r\\) the LoRA scale that HF's initialiser ignores (Prop V.2(b)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_T],\\,[A;\\,A_T])","note":"In the slice at the attained start \\(\\theta_0'=Q_T+B_TA_T\\): LoftQ\\(_r\\) into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) over \\(\\theta_0'\\), at a degenerate pointing (as for PiSSA)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"With `loftq_iter=1` both have the map \\(q_N(W_0)+sBA\\) and only the start differs (a rank-\\(r\\) point against the apex), so \\(h=\\mathrm{id}\\) is a weak morphism, not a pointed one."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"eva","name":"EVA","full_name":"Explained Variance Adaptation","paper_title":"Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning via Explained Variance Adaptation","authors":"Paischer et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2410.07170","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07170","code_url":"https://github.com/ml-jku/EVA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"eva\", eva_config=EvaConfig(rho=2.0))  (+ initialize_lora_eva_weights)","formula_latex":"X^{\\ell}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{(input activations)}=U\\Sigma V^\\top\\ \\text{(incremental SVD)},\\quad A^{\\ell}=V_{[:,:r_\\ell]}^\\top,\\quad B^{\\ell}=0,\\quad \\xi_j=\\frac{\\sigma_j^2}{(M-1)\\lVert\\sigma\\rVert_1},\\quad \\{r_\\ell\\}=\\text{top-}Nr\\text{ of }\\{\\xi_j^\\ell\\}_{j\\le \\rho r},\\qquad h=W_0x+\\tfrac{\\alpha_\\ell}{r_\\ell}B^{\\ell}A^{\\ell}x,\\ \\alpha_\\ell=\\alpha\\,r_\\ell/r\\ (\\text{effective scale }\\alpha/r\\text{ in every layer});\\ W_0\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out}),\\ \\ \\textstyle\\sum_\\ell r_\\ell=N r,\\ r_\\ell\\le\\rho r","locus":"any linear layer; ranks redistributed across all adapted layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=BA\\) with \\(A\\) spanning the top right-singular (principal input-activation) directions of the downstream data; per-layer rank \\(r_\\ell\\) chosen by globally ranking explained-variance ratios","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Plain LoRA update on an untouched \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B=0\\) (\\(W_0\\) untouched); \\(A\\) is data-driven: top right-singular vectors of minibatch input activations, computed by incremental SVD until cosine similarity of successive estimates exceeds a threshold \\(\\tau\\).","key_idea":"Initialize the down-projection on the directions of maximal input-activation variance for the downstream task and redistribute a fixed rank budget to the layers whose activations have the most explainable variance.","results_note":"Reported the highest average score among LoRA initializations across 51 tasks spanning language generation/understanding, image classification and RL, with initialization costing about 0.7% of training time.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"B=0 initialization kept; only A and ranks change"},{"target":"corda","type":"competes-with","note":"both use input-activation statistics, but EVA keeps W_0 untouched"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"rank allocation done once before training instead of by pruning during training"},{"target":"astra","type":"dual-to","note":"EVA uses principal input-activation directions, Astra tail output-activation directions"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07170","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.07170","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.14.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Fabian Paischer","Lukas Hauzenberger","Thomas Schmied","Benedikt Alkin","Marc Peter Deisenroth","Sepp Hochreiter"],"bibkey":"paischer2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{paischer2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning via Explained Variance Adaptation}},\n  author = {Fabian Paischer and Lukas Hauzenberger and Thomas Schmied and Benedikt Alkin and Marc Peter Deisenroth and Sepp Hochreiter},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2410.07170},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07170}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Plain LoRA update on an untouched \\(W_0\\) (\\(B_0=0\\)); only the pointing \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) and the per-layer ranks \\(r_\\ell\\) change.","shape":"Image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_\\ell}\\), the LoRA cone; the rank redistribution makes the object a product of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_\\ell}\\) over layers.","base_point":"Stratum \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) at the point \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0 =\\) top-\\(r_\\ell\\) input principal subspace (Thm II.2 table): apex, deficit \\(r_\\ell(n-r_\\ell)\\). Not isomorphic to vanilla LoRA as a pointed object: \\(S_1=\\{X V_r^T\\}\\) differs from \\(\\{X A_0\\}\\) for a random \\(A_0\\).","covariance":"The image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant; only the frame \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) and the rank allocation are \\(CO(n)\\)-covariant (Prop II.16; table: '\\(GL\\times GL\\) (frame \\(CO\\))').","rebasing":"Cone closed under scaling: \\(\\to\\Theta\\) after \\(\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r_\\ell\\rceil\\) cycles (Thm V.3 Cor 1)."},"categorical_reading":"EVA is LoRA in the stratum \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) at the data-chosen point \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0=\\) top-\\(r_\\ell\\) input principal subspace of \\(\\mathrm{Gr}(r_\\ell,n)\\): same cone \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_\\ell}\\) and apex as LoRA, but a different isomorphism class of pointed object, detected by \\(S_1=\\{XV_{r_\\ell}^\\top\\}\\) (Thm II.2(a)). Its image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant; only the pointing and the rank allocation are \\(CO(n)\\)-covariant (Prop II.16).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,A)\\mapsto(B,A)","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_{r_l} pointed at (0, V_{r_l}^T); to vanilla LoRA with a random A_0 there is only a weak morphism (different point of Gr(r,n))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"corda","test":"O3","reason":"Type I apex (B_0=0, W_0 unsplit) vs Type II smooth point: different strata of Thm II.2, images incomparable at equal rank (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"astra","test":"O3","reason":"Dual activation frames, but EVA is T_A-pointed at theta_0 = vertex while Astra is S-pointed (theta_0 smooth) on layers with rank(Q_tail^T W_0)=r; on tall layers where Astra collapses to T_B the images coincide but S_1={X V_r^T} vs {Q_tail Y} are mutually non-contained (T_A vs T_B). No arrow either way at equal rank."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"corda","name":"CorDA","full_name":"Context-Oriented Decomposition Adaptation","paper_title":"CorDA: Context-Oriented Decomposition Adaptation of Large Language Models for Task-Aware Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.05223","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05223","code_url":"https://github.com/iboing/CorDA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"corda\", corda_config=CordaConfig(corda_method=\"ipm\"|\"kpm\"))  (+ preprocess_corda)","formula_latex":"C=XX^\\top\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}},\\quad \\mathrm{SVD}(W_0C)=U\\Sigma V^\\top,\\quad W_0=U\\Sigma(V^\\top C^{-1});\\ \\ \\text{IPM: }B=U_{[:,:r]}\\Sigma_{[:r]}^{1/2},\\ A=\\Sigma_{[:r]}^{1/2}(V^\\top C^{-1})_{[:r,:]};\\ \\ \\text{KPM: same with the last }r\\text{ components};\\quad W'=W_0-BA,\\ \\ h=(W'+BA)x;\\ W'\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space; the base point is split along the singular directions of \\(W_0C\\) (covariance-whitened weight), i.e. a generalized SVD of \\(W_0\\) w.r.t. the input-activation metric \\(C\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W'+BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W'+B_0A_0=W_0\\) because the \\(C^{-1}\\) factor undoes the covariance orientation; data-driven (covariance from a few hundred task or QA samples).","key_idea":"Orient the weight decomposition by the covariance of activations from either the downstream task (instruction-previewed mode: train the dominant components) or world-knowledge data (knowledge-preserved mode: train the least-used components to limit forgetting).","results_note":"Reported IPM GSM8K 53.90 / MATH 8.52 vs PiSSA 51.48 / 7.60 on LLaMA-2-7B, KPM preserving more NQ-open knowledge than LoRA, and best GLUE average (83.38) with RoBERTa-base.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"generalizes","note":"IPM with C=I is PiSSA"},{"target":"milora","type":"generalizes","note":"KPM with C=I is MiLoRA"},{"target":"lora-null","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-Null pushes the knowledge-preserving idea to the null space of activations"},{"target":"eva","type":"competes-with","note":"both data-driven initializations from activation statistics"},{"target":"astra","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are activation-aware SVD/eigen initializations."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05223","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.05223","https://github.com/iboing/CorDA","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.15.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Yibo Yang","Xiaojie Li","Zhongzhu Zhou","Shuaiwen Leon Song","Jianlong Wu","Liqiang Nie","Bernard Ghanem"],"bibkey":"yang2024corda","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2024corda,\n  title = {{CorDA: Context-Oriented Decomposition Adaptation of Large Language Models for Task-Aware Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Yibo Yang and Xiaojie Li and Zhongzhu Zhou and Shuaiwen Leon Song and Jianlong Wu and Liqiang Nie and Bernard Ghanem},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.05223},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.05223}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Translated cone \\((W_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) with \\(P=[W_0C]_rC^{-1}\\) (IPM) or the covariance-oriented tail (KPM); \\(\\operatorname{rank} P=r\\).","base_point":"Stratum \\(\\mathrm S\\): \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point, \\(S_1=T_P\\mathcal M_r\\), deficit 0 (both IPM and KPM).","covariance":"Undamped split is \\(CO(n)\\)-covariant on the input side exactly (Prop II.16(b)) and \\(CO(m)\\) on the output side (truncated SVD of \\(W_0C\\)); damping \\(C+\\lambda I\\) reduces the input side to \\(O(n)\\). In law, data transformed.","rebasing":"Table: \\(\\to \\Theta\\) (cone closed under scaling)."},"categorical_reading":"CorDA is a Type II pointing of LoRA (stratum \\(\\mathrm S\\)): the cone is translated to the vertex \\(W_0-P\\), \\(P=[W_0C]_rC^{-1}\\) (IPM) or its covariance-oriented tail (KPM), so \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point with full first-order reach \\(S_1=T_P\\mathcal M_r\\). The split is \\(CO(m)\\times CO(n)\\)-covariant undamped (Prop II.16(b)); damping \\(C+\\lambda I\\) cuts the input side to \\(O(n)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"The PiSSA->LoRA conversion of Thm II.1 applied to CorDA: lands in LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) of rank pattern (r,r,0), outside the three strata."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Vertices differ: (W_0-[W_0C]_rC^{-1})+M_{<=r} vs (W_0-[W_0]_r)+M_{<=r}; iterated Sing determines the vertex (Thm II.2), so inclusion holds only when C is proportional to I."},{"target":"eva","test":"O3","reason":"Type II smooth point vs Type I apex (both use input-activation statistics); different strata, incomparable images at equal rank."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"astra","name":"Astra","full_name":"Activation-Space Tail-Eigenvector Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"Astra: Activation-Space Tail-Eigenvector Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2026","arxiv":"2602.19111","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1593/","code_url":"https://github.com/LyoAI/Astra","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"astra\", astra_config=AstraConfig())  (+ preprocess_astra)","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{Cov}(y)=\\mathbb{E}[yy^\\top]-\\mathbb{E}[y]\\mathbb{E}[y]^\\top=Q\\Lambda Q^\\top,\\ y=W_0x,\\quad B_0=Q_{tail}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_0=Q_{tail}^\\top W_0,\\quad W^{res}=W_0-B_0A_0=(I-Q_{tail}Q_{tail}^\\top)W_0,\\qquad h=(W^{res}+BA)x;\\ W^{res}\\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space whose output side starts on the \\(r\\) tail eigenvectors (smallest eigenvalues) of the output-activation covariance; the base is \\(W_0\\) projected off that subspace","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W^{res}+BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(B_0A_0=Q_{tail}Q_{tail}^\\top W_0\\) is subtracted from the base; data-driven from a small calibration set (e.g. 64 samples).","key_idea":"Characterize each layer by the output activations it produces on the downstream task and place the trainable update in the under-used tail eigenspace of those activations, which preserves dominant behaviour while giving room for task adaptation.","results_note":"Reported outperforming LoRA/PiSSA/CorDA-style baselines on 16 NLU and NLG benchmarks at equal or lower rank, sometimes surpassing full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"corda","type":"competes-with","note":"both are data-driven, output-preserving base-point splits; Astra uses output- instead of input-activation statistics"},{"target":"eva","type":"dual-to","note":"tail output-activation directions vs principal input-activation directions"},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"both adapt in a minor/tail subspace"},{"target":"mica","type":"competes-with","note":"Both target minor/tail directions (activation space vs weight singular space)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19111","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.19111","https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1593/","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/pull/3725"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Kainan Liu","Yong Zhang","Ning Cheng","Yun Zhu","Yanmeng Wang","Shaojun Wang","Jing Xiao"],"bibkey":"liu2026astra","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2026astra,\n  title = {{Astra: Activation-Space Tail-Eigenvector Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Kainan Liu and Yong Zhang and Ning Cheng and Yun Zhu and Yanmeng Wang and Shaojun Wang and Jing Xiao},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2026},\n  eprint = {2602.19111},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1593/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Translated cone with vertex \\(W_0-\\Pi_{\\rm tail} W_0\\), \\(\\Pi_{\\rm tail}\\) = tail eigenprojector of \\(\\mathrm{Cov}(y)=W_0\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(x)\\,W_0^\\top\\).","base_point":"Regular (stratum S) when \\(\\operatorname{rank}(Q_{\\rm tail}^\\top W_0)=r\\), i.e. on layers with \\(d_{out}\\le\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\) (q,k,v,o,down). On layers with \\(d_{out}-\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\ge r\\) (up/gate projections) the tail lies in \\(\\mathrm{coker}\\,W_0\\), \\(A_0=Q_{\\rm tail}^\\top W_0=0\\) and the pointing falls into the apex stratum \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) (checked numerically: \\(\\|A_0\\|\\approx10^{-15}\\), \\(\\dim S_1=nr\\)). The verified catalog flags that HF PEFT appears to pick the leading rather than the tail eigenvectors; that variant is the whitened split \\(\\Pi_rW_0\\) of Prop II.16(a), always rank \\(r\\) when \\(\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\ge r\\), so it stays regular with the same seven coordinates.","covariance":"Exact group is \\(CO(m)\\times GL(n)\\) (data): the split depends on the data only through the output covariance \\(W_0\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(x)\\,W_0^\\top\\) (Prop II.16(a), with \\(C\\) the centred input covariance). The axis has no 'CO x GL' value; encoded as OxO as axes.json does.","rebasing":"Table: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Astra is a Type II split with vertex \\(W_0-\\Pi_{\\rm tail}W_0\\), \\(\\Pi_{\\rm tail}\\) the tail eigenprojector of the output covariance \\(W_0\\,\\mathrm{Cov}(x)\\,W_0^\\top\\); because the split sees the data only through that output covariance it is \\(CO(m)\\times GL(n)\\)-covariant (Prop II.16(a), with \\(C\\) the centred input covariance). On layers with \\(d_{\\rm out}-\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\ge r\\) the tail lies in \\(\\mathrm{coker}\\,W_0\\), \\(A_0=0\\), and the pointing collapses into the apex stratum \\(\\mathrm T_B\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing; valid in either stratum."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"corda","test":"O1","reason":"Different vertices: Astra's P=Pi_tail W_0 (output-side projector) vs CorDA's P=[W_0C]_rC^{-1}; images are translated cones with different vertices."},{"target":"eva","test":"O3","reason":"Astra is S-pointed (smooth) on layers with rank(Q_tail^T W_0)=r, EVA T_A-pointed (apex): different strata. On tall layers where Astra collapses to T_B, T_A vs T_B still differ (S_1={XV_r^T} vs {Q_tail Y}). No arrow either way."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"mica","name":"MiCA","full_name":"Minor Component Adaptation","paper_title":"MiCA Learns More Knowledge Than LoRA and Full Fine-Tuning","authors":"Rüdiger & Raschka","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2604.01694","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01694","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights='mica')","formula_latex":"W_0=U\\Sigma V^\\top;\\ B=U_{[:,-r:]} \\text{ (frozen)},\\ A=0 \\text{ (trained)};\\ W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{in}","locus":"linear and embedding layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"update confined to the column space of the \\(r\\) minor left-singular vectors of \\(W_0\\) (\\(B\\) fixed, only \\(A\\) learned)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) is a fixed low-rank update.","identity_at_init":"\\(A = 0\\) (exact no-op without modifying the base weight).","key_idea":"Write new knowledge into the least-used singular directions of the pretrained weight to absorb more and interfere less.","results_note":"Paper reports up to 5.9x better knowledge acquisition than LoRA, e.g. 61.33% vs 58.28% on BLOGS-MC with Llama-2-7B.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"dual-to","note":"Uses minor instead of principal singular directions."},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"Both freeze one LoRA factor (MiCA freezes B, LoRA-FA freezes A)."},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both exploit minor singular components."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01694","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.01694","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/variants.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Sten Rüdiger","Sebastian Raschka"],"bibkey":"rudiger2026mica","bibtex":"@article{rudiger2026mica,\n  title = {{MiCA Learns More Knowledge Than LoRA and Full Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Sten Rüdiger and Sebastian Raschka},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2604.01694},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.01694},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01694}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho(A)=W_0+(\\alpha/r)U_{-r}A\\) is affine and injective: image \\(W_0+\\{Z:\\ \\mathrm{col}\\,Z\\subseteq\\mathrm{col}\\,U_{-r}\\}\\), \\(d=rn\\).","base_point":"Linear, hence regular; as a sub-object of LoRA it sits in stratum \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) (\\(B_0=U_{-r}\\), \\(A_0=0\\)).","covariance":"The frame \\(U_{-r}\\) is a truncated-SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO(m)\\times CO(n)\\).","rebasing":"Idempotent with the frame frozen; recomputing \\(U_{-r}\\) from the merged weight gains."},"categorical_reading":"MiCA is the one-sided method \\(\\mathrm{FB}(U_{-r})\\) in the minor left-singular frame: an A2 sub-object of LoRA pointed in stratum \\(\\mathrm T_B\\), and also of MiLoRA, whose image \\(W_p+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) contains \\(W_0+\\{U_{-r}Y\\}\\). Its image is a fixed affine subspace, so merge-and-restart with the frame frozen is idempotent.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(A)=\\big(U_{-r},\\ \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}A\\big)","note":"Freezing B at U_{-r}: monomorphism into LoRA pointed at (U_{-r},0), stratum T_B (axes.json A2 example)."},{"target":"milora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A)=\\big(U_m\\Sigma_m^{1/2},\\ \\Sigma_m^{1/2}V_m^\\top+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\Sigma_m^{-1/2}A\\big)","note":"W_0+(alpha/r)U_mA = W_p+U_m(Sigma_mV_m^T+(alpha/r)A); pointed (h(0)=MiLoRA's q_0); needs the minor singular values nonzero (checked numerically)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O1","reason":"Column-space constraint {U_{-r}Y} vs row-space constraint {XA_0}: neither image contains the other."},{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"[W_0]_r+U_{-r}Y has rank up to 2r, so W_0+{U_{-r}Y} is not inside (W_0-[W_0]_r)+M_{<=r}."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-ga","name":"LoRA-GA","full_name":"Low-Rank Adaptation with Gradient Approximation","paper_title":"LoRA-GA: Low-Rank Adaptation with Gradient Approximation","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2407.05000","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05000","code_url":"https://github.com/Outsider565/LoRA-GA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(init_lora_weights=\"lora_ga\", lora_ga_config=LoraGAConfig())  (+ preprocess_loraga)","formula_latex":"G=\\nabla_{W}\\mathcal{L}(W_0)=USV^\\top,\\quad A_0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt[4]{d_{out}}}{\\sqrt{\\gamma}}V_{[:,1:r]}^\\top,\\quad B_0=\\tfrac{\\sqrt[4]{d_{out}}}{\\sqrt{\\gamma}}U_{[:,r+1:2r]},\\quad W^{res}=W_0-\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}B_0A_0,\\qquad h=(W^{res}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}BA)\\,x;\\ W^{res}\\text{ frozen},\\ A,B\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space; \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are initialized from disjoint blocks of the top-\\(2r\\) singular vectors of a sampled full gradient so that the first LoRA step \\(\\eta^2(G A_0^\\top A_0+B_0B_0^\\top G)\\) best approximates \\(\\zeta G\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W^{res}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape (but the base differs from \\(W_0\\), so the adapter alone is not a LoRA on \\(W_0\\)).","identity_at_init":"Exact: the offset \\(W_0-\\eta B_0A_0\\) cancels the nonzero adapter; initialization is gradient-driven (one sampled batch, layer-by-layer backward for \\(O(1)\\) memory) with the stable scale factor \\(\\sqrt[4]{d_{out}}/\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\) (paired with \\(\\eta=\\alpha/\\sqrt r\\)) chosen so that forward and backward magnitudes stay rank- and width-stable.","key_idea":"Choose \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) from the SVD of the full-fine-tuning gradient so that the very first LoRA update is the best low-rank approximation of the full-fine-tuning update, with a rank- and width-stable scale.","results_note":"Reported +5.69% over LoRA on a GLUE subset with T5-Base and +0.34 / +11.52 / +5.05 points on MT-Bench / GSM8K / HumanEval with LLaMA-2-7B, converging 2-4x faster than LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same adapter, gradient-aligned initialization"},{"target":"lora-one","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA-One argues LoRA-GA's use of singular vectors r+1..2r for B leaves the iterates outside the target subspace"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"gradient-driven vs weight-driven SVD splitting of W_0"},{"target":"gora","type":"competes-with","note":"GoRA reuses the gradient-driven idea but without offsetting W_0"},{"target":"rslora","type":"builds-on","note":"adopts the rank-stabilized scale \\eta=\\alpha/\\sqrt r"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Both use gradient SVD to pick low-rank subspaces."},{"target":"rslora","type":"combines","note":"uses alpha/sqrt(r) scaling for rank stability"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05000","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.05000","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/94885","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/layer.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Shaowen Wang","Linxi Yu","Jian Li"],"bibkey":"wang2024lorab","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024lorab,\n  title = {{LoRA-GA: Low-Rank Adaptation with Gradient Approximation}},\n  author = {Shaowen Wang and Linxi Yu and Jian Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2407.05000},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05000}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"The frozen residual \\(W_0-\\eta B_0A_0\\) moves the cone's vertex to \\(W_0-P\\), with \\(P=\\eta B_0A_0\\) of rank \\(r\\) (the S stratum).","base_point":"\\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point: \\(\\dim S_1=r(m+n-r)\\).","covariance":"The frames come from the SVD of a sampled gradient \\(G=\\nabla_WL(W_0)\\), so the image transforms covariantly in distribution over the data under \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\). Homotheties are not included: with the stable scale \\(\\sqrt[4]{d_{out}}/\\sqrt\\gamma\\), \\(P\\propto U_{G,[r+1:2r]}V_{G,[1:r]}^\\top\\) does not depend on the size of \\(W_0\\) or \\(G\\), so \\(W_0\\mapsto cW_0\\) moves the vertex to \\(cW_0-P\\ne c(W_0-P)\\). The closest key is the spectral-frame \\(O\\times O\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-GA is a Type II object: it picks the point \\(P\\propto U_{G,[r+1:2r]}V_{G,[1:r]}^\\top\\) of the stratum \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) and moves the cone's vertex to \\(\\theta_0-P\\), combining smoothness, gradient alignment of \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) and a stable scale (Prop II.3). It is incomparable with zero-init LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on the untouched \\(W_0\\), pointed at the degenerate pointing \\(([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0])\\) (product \\(0\\), outside the three strata)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Different points of the S stratum (gradient-SVD \\(P\\) against weight-SVD \\(P\\)) give translated cones with different vertices, hence incomparable images (Thm II.2(a),(d))."},{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"A smooth Type II point against an apex: at equal rank the images are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"kasa","name":"KaSA","full_name":"Knowledge-aware Singular-value Adaptation","paper_title":"KaSA: Knowledge-Aware Singular-Value Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2412.06071","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06071","code_url":"https://github.com/juyongjiang/KaSA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(kasa_config=KasaConfig(beta, gamma))","formula_latex":"W_0=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\sigma_iu_iv_i^\\top,\\quad W_{world}=\\sum_{i=1}^{k-r}\\sigma_iu_iv_i^\\top,\\qquad W=W_{world}+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma)\\,A,\\qquad \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\beta\\textstyle\\sum_j\\Delta\\sigma_j^2+\\gamma\\big(\\lVert B^\\top B-I_r\\rVert_F+\\lVert AA^\\top-I_r\\rVert_F\\big);\\ W_{world}\\text{ frozen},\\ B,\\Delta\\sigma,A\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+r","locus":"linear layers (attention and MLP projections)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"SVD-shaped update \\(\\Delta W=B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma)\\,A\\) with soft orthogonality on \\(B,A\\) and a learnable singular-value vector, added on top of a base whose \\(r\\) smallest singular components were removed","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma)\\,A\\) is a dense \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) matrix that folds into the (truncated) base, though the truncation itself is irreversible.","identity_at_init":"Not exact: \\(B=0\\) (official repo and HF KasaLinearVariant; \\(\\Delta\\sigma\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\), \\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform) makes the adapter zero, but the base is permanently truncated by its \\(r\\) smallest singular components (a small, data-free perturbation of \\(W_0\\)).","key_idea":"Treat the smallest singular components of \\(W_0\\) as task-irrelevant noise, discard them, and relearn that subspace with an explicitly SVD-shaped adapter whose singular values are learned and regularized.","results_note":"Reported outperforming 14 PEFT baselines on GLUE (86.3 average with RoBERTa-base at about 0.24% trainable parameters), instruction following and commonsense benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"same P\\Lambda Q-style SVD parameterization with orthogonality regularizer"},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"both operate on the minor singular subspace of W_0"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"SVD-based base-point modification"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"Same SVD-form update with orthogonality regularization; KaSA also truncates W_0."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06071","https://arxiv.org/html/2412.06071","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/variants.py","https://github.com/juyongjiang/KaSA","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora_variant_kasa","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.21.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank"],"authors_full":["Fan Wang","Juyong Jiang","Chansung Park","Sunghun Kim","Jing Tang"],"bibkey":"wang2024kasa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024kasa,\n  title = {{KaSA: Knowledge-Aware Singular-Value Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Fan Wang and Juyong Jiang and Chansung Park and Sunghun Kim and Jing Tang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2412.06071},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06071}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Base change along the non-function-preserving truncation \\(\\kappa\\): \\(W_0 \\to W_0-[W_0]_{\\mathrm{tail}\\,r}\\), then a diagonal-core LoRA at \\(\\kappa \\theta_0\\) (Def V.1(b), the QLoRA template).","shape":"Image \\(W_{world}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), a cone whose vertex is the start \\(\\rho(q_0)=W_{world}\\) (as QLoRA's 'cone at \\(\\kappa \\theta_0\\)'). \\(\\theta_0\\) itself lies in the image, at a smooth point.","base_point":"Defect \\(e=-[W_0]_{\\mathrm{tail}\\,r}\\) (truncated base); \\(B_0=0\\) makes the adapter zero but the base is permanently truncated.","gauge":"\\((B,d\\sigma,A) \\mapsto B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(d\\sigma)\\,A\\): generic fibre \\(r^2+r\\) (Jacobian check) against the \\(2r\\)-dim torus \\((BD_1^{-1}, D_1D_2\\,d\\sigma, D_2^{-1}A)\\); the rest is absorption of \\(\\mathrm{diag}(d\\sigma)\\) into \\(B\\), as for AdaLoRA (axes.json lists AdaLoRA under nonlinear).","covariance":"The cone part is \\(GL \\times GL\\), but the truncation \\([W_0]_{\\mathrm{tail}\\,r}\\) is spectral: \\(CO(m) \\times CO(n)\\).","merge":"Folds into the truncated base; the truncation itself is irreversible.","rebasing":"With the truncation applied once, \\(W_{world}+\\mathcal M_{\\le Kr} \\to \\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KaSA is base change along the truncation \\(\\kappa:W_0\\mapsto W_0-[W_0]_{\\rm tail\\,r}\\) followed by a diagonal-core LoRA at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\): like QLoRA it carries the defect \\(e=-[W_0]_{\\rm tail\\,r}\\). Its image is exactly MiLoRA's, \\(W_p+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), but it starts at the vertex \\(W_p\\) instead of the smooth point \\(\\theta_0\\), so the two are related only by a weak morphism.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\Delta\\sigma,A)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma),\\ A\\big)","note":"Arrow in the slice at kappa theta_0 = W_world: onto zero-init LoRA at the truncated base, pointed at (0,A_0)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"milora","test":"O3","reason":"Images coincide (W_p+M_{<=r}, same W_p=W_world), but KaSA starts at the vertex W_p with defect -W_tail and MiLoRA at the smooth point theta_0; only a weak morphism exists."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"alora","name":"aLoRA","full_name":"Activated LoRA","paper_title":"Activated LoRA: Fine-tuned LLMs for Intrinsics","authors":"Greenewald et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2504.12397","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12397","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(alora_invocation_tokens=[...])","formula_latex":"h_t=\\begin{cases}W_0x_t,& t<t_{inv}\\\\(W_0+BA)\\,x_t,& t\\ge t_{inv}\\end{cases}\\quad\\text{for } W\\in\\{W_q,W_k,W_v\\}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"attention projections of causal LMs, applied only to tokens at/after the invocation sequence","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"token-position-gated low-rank update: identity before the invocation token, LoRA after it, so prefix KV caches equal the base model's","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The adapter is applied selectively by token position, so it cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA.","key_idea":"Activate the adapter only after an invocation string, so the base model's KV cache can be reused and adapters can be switched in mid-conversation for free.","results_note":"Paper reports 2-7x faster adapter generation even for 250-token prompts and over 20x on long prompts, with no consistent accuracy difference from LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Same low-rank update, gated by token position."},{"target":"x-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both condition adapter use on tokens; aLoRA uses a hard positional switch."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12397","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.12397","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Kristjan Greenewald","Luis Lastras","Thomas Parnell","Vraj Shah","Lucian Popa","Giulio Zizzo","Chulaka Gunasekara","Ambrish Rawat","David Cox"],"bibkey":"greenewald2025activated","bibtex":"@inproceedings{greenewald2025activated,\n  title = {{Activated LoRA: Fine-tuned LLMs for Intrinsics}},\n  author = {Kristjan Greenewald and Luis Lastras and Thomas Parnell and Vraj Shah and Lucian Popa and Giulio Zizzo and Chulaka Gunasekara and Ambrish Rawat and David Cox},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2504.12397},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12397}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A \\(T\\)-point over token positions: \\(a(t)=q\\) for \\(t\\ge t_{inv}\\) and \\(a(t)=q_0\\) before, so \\(\\rho\\circ a\\) takes only the values \\(\\theta_0\\) and \\(W_0+BA\\). It is a hard positional router between the frozen model and one LoRA.","shape":"No single weight-space image: different positions see different weights, so the method is compared through the realisation \\(\\Phi\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) gives the frozen function at every position. At activated positions the first-order image is LoRA's \\(\\{XA_0\\}\\) (function-level zero gate, Lemma II.4).","gauge":"LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) on the single adapter.","covariance":"At each activated position the update is LoRA's basis-free cone.","merge":"Thm VI.3(a): a position-gated edit has no position-independent weight to absorb it, so it is M∞. Keeping the prefix KV cache equal to the base model's is the design goal.","rebasing":"n/a: nothing can be merged into the base, so merge-and-restart is undefined."},"categorical_reading":"Activated LoRA is LoRA pulled back along a position gate: a family \\(T\\to Q\\) that sits at \\(q_0\\) before the invocation token, so its realisation \\(\\Phi\\circ\\rho\\circ a\\) is not \\(\\Phi_f(\\theta)\\) for any single \\(\\theta\\). Being pointed before \\(t_{inv}\\) is what makes the base KV cache reusable, and it is also what blocks merging.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"At function level the realised map (\\(W_0\\) on the prefix, \\(W_0+BA\\) after \\(t_{inv}\\)) lies outside \\(\\Phi_f(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA})\\) for generic prompts, so no weight-space LoRA lifts it (M∞). At each single position the update is still a LoRA update."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"qa-lora","name":"QA-LoRA","full_name":"Quantization-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"QA-LoRA: Quantization-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Xu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.14717","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14717","code_url":"https://github.com/yuhuixu1993/qa-lora","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(use_qalora=True, qalora_group_size=g) (GPTQ backends only)","formula_latex":"\\tilde W=\\alpha\\odot(\\hat W-\\beta),\\ \\hat W=\\lfloor W_0/\\alpha\\rceil+\\beta \\text{ (group-wise INT-}k\\text{, group size } g);\\ h=\\tilde Wx+s\\,BA\\,\\mathrm{pool}_g(x),\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}/g} \\ (\\mathrm{pool}_g=\\text{sum over each group});\\ \\text{merge: } \\beta'=\\beta-s\\,\\mathrm{expand}_g(BA)\\oslash\\alpha","trainable_params":"r\\big(\\tfrac{d_{in}}{g}+d_{out}\\big)","locus":"linear layers of a group-wise quantized (GPTQ) model","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA on group-average-pooled inputs, so the update is constant within each quantization group","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The group-constant update is absorbed into the quantization zero-points, keeping the merged model in INT4.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\).","key_idea":"Balance quantization and adaptation degrees of freedom with group-wise pooling so the fine-tuned model stays quantized after merging.","results_note":"Paper reports LLaMA-7B 5-shot MMLU 39.4 vs 36.0 for QLoRA+GPTQ at INT4, with faster inference.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"QLoRA cannot merge without leaving the quantized format."},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"Both target quantized-backbone LoRA."},{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"same frozen-quantized-base + LoRA recipe, but quantization-aware so that the merged model stays integer"},{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with the down-projection constrained to A' = AP (rows tied within input groups)"},{"target":"peqa","type":"dual-to","note":"QA-LoRA effectively learns the quantizer's additive zero-points (through a low-rank factorization), PEQA learns its multiplicative scales"},{"target":"l4q","type":"competes-with","note":"L4Q also yields a fully quantized merged model but without constraining the LoRA factors"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14717","https://arxiv.org/html/2309.14717","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.16.0","https://openreview.net/forum?id=WvFoJccpo8"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Yuhui Xu","Lingxi Xie","Xiaotao Gu","Xin Chen","Heng Chang","Hengheng Zhang","Zhengsu Chen","Xiaopeng Zhang","Qi Tian"],"bibkey":"xu2023qa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xu2023qa,\n  title = {{QA-LoRA: Quantization-Aware Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yuhui Xu and Lingxi Xie and Xiaotao Gu and Xin Chen and Heng Chang and Hengheng Zhang and Zhengsu Chen and Xiaopeng Zhang and Qi Tian},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2309.14717},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.14717}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=sBAP\\) with \\(P=I_{n/g}\\otimes\\mathbf 1_g^\\top\\) the group-sum pooling: LoRA\\(_r\\) on the pooled space composed with \\(Y\\mapsto YP\\), image \\(\\kappa\\theta_0+\\{YP:\\operatorname{rk}Y\\le r\\}\\), \\(d=r(m+n/g-r)\\).","base_point":"\\(q_0=(0,A_0)\\): \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\kappa\\theta_0\\), defect \\(e=\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0\\) as for QLoRA (apex relative to \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\): \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\).","covariance":"Group-wise INT-\\(k\\) grid plus pooling over contiguous input groups of size \\(g\\): an imposed partition on the input side (a neuron permutation across groups breaks it); monomial on the output side.","merge":"The exception of Prop V.2(d): a group-constant update is absorbed into the zero-points, \\(\\beta'=\\beta-s\\,\\mathrm{expand}_g(BA)\\oslash\\alpha\\), so the codes stay INT-\\(k\\) (the zero-points become real-valued). HF PEFT supports QA-LoRA on GPTQ layers but does not implement this merge.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart moves only zero-points: \\(R_\\infty=\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathbb R^{m\\times n/g}P\\), the group-constant subspace, not \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"QA-LoRA is QLoRA precomposed with group pooling: LoRA on the quotient of the input space by the quantisation groups, so its image is the determinantal cone inside the group-constant subspace. That subspace is exactly the zero-point direction of the quantiser, which is why this is the one adapter whose exact merge stays in the quantised type.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{s}{s^\\prime}B,\\ AP)","note":"\\(s^\\prime\\) is QLoRA's scale. Into QLoRA's construction (LoRA\\(_r\\) over \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)) pointed at \\((0,A_0P)\\): a \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) pointing whose row space lies in the group-constant subspace, hence not isomorphic to QLoRA's random-\\(A_0\\) pointing (Thm II.2(a)). Needs the same quantised base (GPTQ INT-\\(k\\)); NF4-QLoRA has a different base point."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"peqa","test":"O1","reason":"PEQA's update \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta s)\\overline W_0\\) is a row rescaling with the codes' zero pattern, QA-LoRA's is group-constant: generically the images meet only at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)."},{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Pointed at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_0\\); \\(\\theta_0\\) is generically not in its image."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"monteclora","name":"MonteCLoRA","full_name":"Monte Carlo Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"Robust and Efficient Fine-tuning of LLMs with Bayesian Reparameterization of Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Sengupta et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2025","arxiv":"2411.04358","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04358","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(monteclora_config=MontecloraConfig(num_samples, sample_scaler, kl_loss_weight, ...))","formula_latex":"A=\\sum_{k=1}^{N}\\pi_k\\theta_k,\\ \\theta_k\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\Sigma),\\ \\Sigma\\sim\\mathcal{W}_r(V,n),\\ \\pi\\sim\\mathrm{Dir}(\\alpha),\\ \\mu=A_{LoRA};\\ \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{task}+\\eta\\,(\\mathrm{KL}_{\\mathcal{D}}+\\mathrm{KL}_{\\mathcal{W}}+\\mathrm{KL}_{\\mathcal{N}})+\\textstyle\\sum_k\\pi_k^2","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+O(r+N)","locus":"same as LoRA (training-time variational sampler on lora_A)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"LoRA whose \\(A\\) factor is treated as a Gaussian mixture (Wishart covariance, Dirichlet weights) estimated by Monte Carlo during training; plain LoRA at inference","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"At inference the sampler is disabled and the adapter is a standard LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA.","key_idea":"Treat low-rank parameters as a distribution and average Monte Carlo samples to make LoRA robust to hyperparameters.","results_note":"Paper reports up to 62% lower accuracy spread than LoRA across hyperparameters on LLaMA-3.2-3B (and 53% on LLaMA-1-7B commonsense).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Bayesian reparameterization of LoRA's A matrix."},{"target":"pvera","type":"competes-with","note":"Both introduce stochastic latent parameters into low-rank adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04358","https://arxiv.org/html/2411.04358","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora_variant_monteclora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Ayan Sengupta","Vaibhav Seth","Arinjay Pathak","Aastha Verma","Natraj Raman","Sriram Gopalakrishnan","Niladri Chatterjee","Tanmoy Chakraborty"],"bibkey":"sengupta2024robust","bibtex":"@article{sengupta2024robust,\n  title = {{Robust and Efficient Fine-tuning of LLMs with Bayesian Reparameterization of Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Ayan Sengupta and Vaibhav Seth and Arinjay Pathak and Aastha Verma and Natraj Raman and Sriram Gopalakrishnan and Niladri Chatterjee and Tanmoy Chakraborty},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2411.04358},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04358}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"At inference the sampler is off and \\(\\rho=W_0+sB\\mu\\). The Gaussian-mixture, Wishart and Dirichlet sampler on \\(A\\) changes only the training signal, so this is a stochastic trained method on LoRA.","gauge":"LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) on \\((B,\\mu)\\). The variational parameters do not enter \\(\\rho\\) at inference, so they lie entirely in the generic fibre.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) as in LoRA: the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) apex at \\(A_0=\\mu_0\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MonteCLoRA is LoRA times an inert factor: LoRA \\(\\to\\) MonteCLoRA \\(\\to\\) LoRA (freeze the variational coordinates, then project them out), so the two simulate each other without being isomorphic. Everything it adds is trained-method data: a stochastic cotangent signal for \\(A\\) plus KL penalties.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\mu,\\xi)=(B,\\ \\mu)","note":"Projection forgetting the variational parameters \\(\\xi\\). The reverse inclusion is A2 (freeze \\(\\xi\\) at \\(\\xi_0\\))."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"velora","name":"VeLoRA","full_name":"Memory-efficient training with rank-1 sub-token projections","paper_title":"VeLoRA: Memory Efficient Training using Rank-1 Sub-Token Projections","authors":"Miles et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.17991","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17991","code_url":"https://github.com/roymiles/VeLoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(velora_config=VeloraConfig(num_groups, scale, init_type))","formula_latex":"\\text{fwd: } z=\\mathrm{group}_M(x),\\ z_p=z\\,v \\text{ (stored)};\\ \\text{bwd: } \\hat x=\\mathrm{ungroup}(z_pv^\\top),\\ \\nabla_A\\mathcal{L}=\\delta^\\top\\hat x;\\ v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{M} \\text{ fixed (first-batch average)}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\ (\\text{unchanged; saves activation memory})","locus":"input activations of the LoRA \\(A\\) projection","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"activation compression for the backward pass: sub-tokens projected onto a fixed rank-1 direction and coarsely reconstructed when computing weight gradients","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the backward pass changes; the adapter is a standard LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA.","key_idea":"Weight gradients tolerate coarse activations, so store each sub-token as a single scalar along a fixed vector instead of the full activation.","results_note":"Paper reports beating QLoRA by 0.5pp on LLaMA-7B with 15.4% less memory, and 45% less memory than GaLore on RoBERTa.","relations":[{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"Both reduce LoRA activation memory."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are memory-efficient training via projections."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17991","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.17991","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora_variant_velora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Roy Miles","Pradyumna Reddy","Ismail Elezi","Jiankang Deng"],"bibkey":"miles2024velora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{miles2024velora,\n  title = {{VeLoRA: Memory Efficient Training using Rank-1 Sub-Token Projections}},\n  author = {Roy Miles and Pradyumna Reddy and Ismail Elezi and Jiankang Deng},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.17991},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17991}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is LoRA's. Only the cotangent side changes: \\(\\nabla_A\\) is computed from rank-1 sub-token reconstructions \\(\\hat x\\), which modifies \\(\\mathsf R[\\rho]\\) and not \\(\\rho\\). It is not kind B, whose forward map is \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). With \\(v\\) fixed, \\(\\hat x=\\Pi x\\) for the fixed projector \\(\\Pi=I_{n/M}\\otimes vv^\\top/\\lVert v\\rVert^2\\) of rank \\(n/M\\), so \\(\\nabla_A=sB^\\top G\\Pi\\). Under (momentum) SGD \\(A\\) therefore stays in \\(A_0+\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}\\Pi\\), a sub-cone of LoRA's reach; Adam's coordinatewise normalisation leaves that subspace, so the restriction is optimizer-dependent and the object stays LoRA.","gauge":"LoRA's \\(GL_r\\). But \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) now receive different cotangents (\\(G\\) and \\(\\hat G=\\delta\\hat x^\\top\\)), so the update is not of the form \\(\\Lambda D\\rho^\\top\\gamma\\), and the charge \\(\\Phi\\) of Thm III.5 is not conserved.","covariance":"The image is LoRA's (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The backward compression groups consecutive input features into sub-tokens and projects them on a fixed \\(v\\), which is only \\(\\Pi\\)-covariant (a block partition of input coordinates)."},"categorical_reading":"VeLoRA keeps LoRA's object and replaces its backward lens by an approximate one: the \\(A\\)-block is fed \\(\\hat G\\) while \\(B\\) is fed the true \\(G\\). By Thm III.5 this block-dependent cotangent breaks LoRA's Noether charge, which is the dynamical cost of the activation-memory saving.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the trained-method data (the cotangent signal for \\(A\\)) differ."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"bd-lora","name":"BD-LoRA","full_name":"Block-Diagonal LoRA","paper_title":"Block-Diagonal LoRA for Eliminating Communication Overhead in Tensor Parallel LoRA Serving","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2510.23346","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23346","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/examples/bdlora_finetuning","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(use_bdlora=BdLoraConfig(...))","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + s\\,BA,\\ \\ B=\\mathrm{diag}(B_1,\\dots,B_N),\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{N}\\times\\frac{r}{N}}\\ (\\text{column-sharded: } W_q,W_k,W_v,\\text{up},\\text{gate})\\ \\text{or}\\ A=\\mathrm{diag}(A_1,\\dots,A_N),\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{r}{N}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{N}}\\ (\\text{row-sharded: } W_o,\\text{down}),\\ N=\\text{tensor-parallel degree};\\ s=\\alpha/\\sqrt{r/N}\\ \\text{in the paper (rsLoRA-style, since it trains independent rank-}r/N\\text{ adapters per shard); HF uses the LoraConfig scaling}","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{in}+\\tfrac{r}{N}d_{out}\\ \\ (B\\ \\text{block-diagonal})\\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\tfrac{r}{N}d_{in}+r\\,d_{out}\\ \\ (A\\ \\text{block-diagonal})","locus":"attention and MLP projections, with the block-diagonal factor chosen to match the layer's tensor-parallel sharding","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA with one factor constrained to a block-diagonal matrix aligned with the tensor-parallel shards, so the adapter factors through a direct sum of \\(N\\) per-device maps and needs no extra all-gather/all-reduce.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"It is a constrained LoRA, so \\(BA\\) can be merged; the design target is unmerged multi-adapter serving.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\), or \\(B_i = 0\\) per block), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Choose the shape of the adapter to fit the hardware sharding: block-diagonal factors make multi-LoRA serving communication-free with almost no loss in quality per parameter.","results_note":"Matches LoRA's downstream quality at similar parameter counts and gives up to 1.79x end-to-end serving speed-up over S-LoRA tensor parallelism for Llama-3.1-8B on eight A100 GPUs.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with a block-diagonal constraint on one factor"},{"target":"melora","type":"generalizes","note":"MELoRA constrains both factors block-diagonally"},{"target":"gralora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both impose block structure on LoRA factors."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.23346","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2510.23346","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/package_reference/lora_variant_bdlora.md","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Block-Diagonal%20LoRA%20Tensor%20Parallel%20Serving","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23346","https://arxiv.org/html/2510.23346","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora_variant_bdlora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.19.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","shape"],"authors_full":["Xinyu Wang","Jonas M. Kübler","Kailash Budhathoki","Yida Wang","Matthäus Kleindessner"],"bibkey":"wang2025block","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2025block,\n  title = {{Block-Diagonal LoRA for Eliminating Communication Overhead in Tensor Parallel LoRA Serving}},\n  author = {Xinyu Wang and Jonas M. Kübler and Kailash Budhathoki and Yida Wang and Matthäus Kleindessner},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2510.23346},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23346}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"With \\(B=\\mathrm{diag}(B_1,\\dots,B_N)\\), \\(\\Delta W=[B_1A^{(1)};\\dots;B_NA^{(N)}]\\): a product of \\(N\\) cones \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r/N}(\\tfrac{d_{out}}N\\times d_{in})\\). The \\(A\\)-block-diagonal variant (row-sharded \\(W_o\\), down) is the transpose.","base_point":"\\(B_i=0\\): \\(S_1=\\bigoplus_i\\{X_iA_0^{(i)}\\}\\) of dimension \\(d_{out}r/N\\), with deficit \\(r\\,d_{in}-r^2/N>0\\).","gauge":"\\(\\prod_iGL_{r/N}\\) (block-diagonal).","covariance":"The sharded side has the block normaliser \\(\\prod GL_{d/N}\\rtimes S_N\\) and the other side \\(GL\\). Tensor-parallel shards impose a partition of hidden neurons and head groups that the network does not privilege, so cross-shard neuron permutations change the image (\\(\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Each block cone generates its row block: \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"BD-LoRA is the \\(\\oplus\\) of \\(N\\) row-block-embedded \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r/N}\\), i.e. GraLoRA with the grid on one side only. It is a constrained sub-object of LoRA, chosen so that the adapter factors through the direct sum of the tensor-parallel shards (a hardware-imposed \\(\\Pi\\)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,\\dots,B_N,A)=\\big(\\mathrm{diag}(B_1,\\dots,B_N),\\;A\\big)","note":"The inclusion of block-diagonal factors; pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\), stratum \\(T_A\\)."},{"target":"gralora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{B_i\\},A)=\\big(B_{ij}=B_i,\\;A_{ij}^\\top=A^{(i)}_{:,J_j}\\big)_{i,j\\le N}","note":"GraLoRA with \\(k=N\\) blocks per side and per-block rank \\(r/N\\): copy \\(B_i\\) along block row i. Pointed at \\((0,\\text{slices of }A_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"melora","test":"O1","reason":"MELoRA keeps the off-diagonal blocks at zero. BD-LoRA's row block i is \\(B_iA^{(i)}\\) over all input columns, so it fills them. The reverse map MELoRA \\(\\to\\) BD-LoRA is the inclusion of block-diagonal \\(A\\); it is pointed (an A2 sub-object) only when BD-LoRA is pointed at a block-diagonal \\(A_0\\), since its default Gaussian \\(A_0\\) has nonzero off-diagonal parts."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"genknowsub","name":"GenKnowSub","full_name":"General Knowledge Subtraction","paper_title":"GenKnowSub: Improving Modularity and Reusability of LLMs through General Knowledge Subtraction","authors":"Bagherifard et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ACL 2025 (short)","arxiv":"2505.10939","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-short.54/","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(arrow_config=ArrowConfig(use_gks=True)); create_arrow_model(..., general_adapter_paths=[...])","formula_latex":"A_i\\leftarrow A_i-\\tfrac{1}{G}\\sum_{g=1}^{G}A^{gen}_g,\\ \\ B_i\\leftarrow B_i-\\tfrac{1}{G}\\sum_{g=1}^{G}B^{gen}_g \\text{ (PEFT implementation)};\\ \\text{then Arrow routing over the residual experts}","trainable_params":"0 \\ (\\text{post-hoc arithmetic on trained adapters})","locus":"LoRA library used by Arrow","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"adapter arithmetic: subtract the mean general-domain LoRA from each task LoRA to obtain residual task modules, then route","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Inference uses token-level Arrow routing.","identity_at_init":"Not applicable (operates on trained adapters).","key_idea":"Task LoRAs carry generic language knowledge; subtracting a general-knowledge LoRA purifies them and improves zero-shot recombination.","results_note":"Paper reports consistent gains over Arrow on Phi-3 in monolingual and cross-lingual settings, also confirmed on Phi-2.","relations":[{"target":"arrow","type":"builds-on","note":"Arrow routing over subtracted experts."},{"target":"ties-merging","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are task-vector arithmetic on adapters."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.10939","https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-short.54/","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/arrow.py","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.18.0"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Mohammadtaha Bagherifard","Sahar Rajabi","Ali Edalat","Yadollah Yaghoobzadeh"],"bibkey":"bagherifard2025genknowsub","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bagherifard2025genknowsub,\n  title = {{GenKnowSub: Improving Modularity and Reusability of LLMs through General Knowledge Subtraction}},\n  author = {Mohammadtaha Bagherifard and Sahar Rajabi and Ali Edalat and Yadollah Yaghoobzadeh},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2505.10939},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-short.54/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"P","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":null,"covariance":"OxO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Post hoc on trained factor tuples: \\((B_i,A_i)\\mapsto(B_i-\\bar B^{gen},A_i-\\bar A^{gen})\\), followed by Arrow's token routing (an \\(F_X\\) family) over the residual experts.","shape":"The deployed object is Arrow's input-dependent mixture, so it lives at function level. The subtraction itself has no image germ.","base_point":"No parameter value reproduces \\(f\\): the subtracted experts are routed by Arrow at every input, so the realised function differs from \\(f\\) for generic inputs. Parameterless post-hoc operations are classified unpointed throughout (Model Soups, AdapterSoup, LoRA Switch / LoRA Composite).","gauge":"n/a for a P operation. The factor-wise subtraction is not \\(\\prod GL_r\\)-invariant (Prop V.4(a)), so a residual expert is not a function of the trained adapters \\(B_iA_i\\).","covariance":"The factor-wise subtraction alone commutes with \\(B\\mapsto gB\\), \\(A\\mapsto Ah^{-1}\\) (GL×GL-equivariant on factor tuples, while still gauge-dependent). But the deployed object routes each token by \\(|v_i^\\top h|\\), with \\(v_i\\) the top right singular vector of the residual expert (Arrow). That frame is natural only under conformal output and orthogonal input changes of basis, so the composite is \\(CO(m)\\times O(n)\\subseteq\\) CO×CO: the spectral-frame key.","merge":"Arrow routes per token and per layer (input-dependent gates): M∞.","rebasing":"n/a: no training, so nothing is restarted."},"categorical_reading":"GenKnowSub is a post-hoc map on factor tuples composed with Arrow's \\(F_X\\) router. It subtracts factors rather than products, and \\((B_i-\\bar B)(A_i-\\bar A)=B_iA_i-B_i\\bar A-\\bar BA_i+\\bar B\\bar A\\) depends on the gauge representatives. By Prop V.4 it is well defined only after a joint gauge fixing, such as a shared frozen \\(A\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"task-arithmetic","test":"O1","reason":"The residual expert \\((B_i-\\bar B)(A_i-\\bar A)\\) differs from the task-vector difference \\(B_iA_i-\\tfrac1G\\sum_gB^{gen}_gA^{gen}_g\\) by cross terms, and it changes under \\((B_i,A_i)\\mapsto(B_ig^{-1},gA_i)\\). So it does not factor through product-level arithmetic."}],"table_row":"P","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"lora-plus","name":"LoRA+","full_name":"LoRA+ (decoupled learning rates for A and B)","paper_title":"LoRA+: Efficient Low Rank Adaptation of Large Models","authors":"Hayou et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.12354","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354","code_url":"https://github.com/nikhil-ghosh-berkeley/loraplus","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"peft.optimizers.create_loraplus_optimizer(model, optimizer_cls, lr, loraplus_lr_ratio)","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA;\\qquad A \\leftarrow A - \\eta_A\\, g_A,\\quad B \\leftarrow B - \\eta_B\\, g_B,\\quad \\eta_B = \\lambda\\,\\eta_A,\\ \\ \\lambda\\gg 1\\ (\\text{e.g. } \\lambda=2^4)","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Unchanged rank-\\(r\\) factorization; the optimizer applies a block-diagonal learning-rate operator \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\eta_A I,\\ \\lambda\\eta_A I)\\) to \\((A,B)\\). Infinite-width analysis: efficient feature learning requires \\(\\eta_A=\\Theta(n^{-1}),\\ \\eta_B=\\Theta(1)\\), i.e. \\(\\eta_B/\\eta_A=\\Theta(n)\\) (\\(n\\) = model width); this explicitly breaks the \\(GL(r)\\) gauge symmetry in favour of \\(B\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the training procedure changes; the trained adapter is a standard LoRA product that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\) at init under either zero-product scheme analysed in the paper: Init[1] (\\(B = 0\\), \\(A\\) random with variance \\(\\Theta(1/n)\\); the standard LoRA/PEFT default) or Init[2] (\\(A = 0\\), \\(B\\) random with variance \\(\\Theta(1)\\)). Most of the paper's experiments (and the recommended ratio \\(\\lambda \\sim 2^4\\) for RoBERTa) use Init[2]; with Init[1] the optimal ratio is smaller (\\(\\sim 2^2\\)-\\(2^3\\)).","key_idea":"With one shared learning rate, \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) cannot both learn features efficiently as width grows; setting the \\(B\\) learning rate a fixed large multiple of the \\(A\\) learning rate fixes this while keeping a 1-D LR search.","results_note":"Reports 1-2% accuracy improvements and up to ~2x faster fine-tuning at the same compute as LoRA (RoBERTa/GPT-2 on GLUE, Llama-7b on MNLI and flan-v2/MMLU); the best ratio is model/task/init dependent: \\(\\sim 2^4\\) for RoBERTa with Init[2], \\(\\sim 2^2\\)-\\(2^3\\) with Init[1], \\(\\sim 2^1\\)-\\(2^2\\) for Llama.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same parametrization, different per-factor learning rates"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both fix the A/B learning-rate imbalance; LoRA+ with a fixed scalar ratio, Riemannian LoRA with an r x r preconditioner and a single learning rate"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.12354","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/optimizers","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Soufiane Hayou","Nikhil Ghosh","Bin Yu"],"bibkey":"hayou2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hayou2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA+: Efficient Low Rank Adaptation of Large Models}},\n  author = {Soufiane Hayou and Nikhil Ghosh and Bin Yu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.12354},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Same \\(\\rho\\) as LoRA; only the rate operator \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\eta_AI,\\lambda\\eta_AI)\\) changes. Under Adam (\\(\\varepsilon=0\\), no decay, no clipping, shared schedule) and from \\(B_0=0\\), LoRA+ with ratio \\(\\lambda\\) is exactly LoRA with \\(\\alpha\\) multiplied by \\(\\lambda\\) (Cor III.11(b)).","base_point":"An apex in either zero-product initialisation: the paper's Init[2] (\\(A=0\\), \\(B\\) random) is the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum, HF's default the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum.","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). Under SGD the rate ratio is the scalar gauge choice \\(g=\\lambda^{1/4}I\\) together with an overall rate (Thm III.3)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA+ is not a new object but a point of LoRA's hyperparameter torus: under Adam, from zero-initialised \\(B\\), its ratio \\(\\lambda\\) is absorbed into \\(\\alpha\\), so any gain must come from torus-breaking ingredients (global clipping, a shared \\(\\varepsilon\\) or decay). Under gradient flow it moves the crossover scale \\(\\sigma^*\\) by \\(\\sqrt\\lambda\\), and LoRA-FA is the endpoint \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object. The trajectory equivalence is the torus map \\(c_B=1/\\lambda\\) (Adam) or \\(\\lambda^{-1/2}\\) (SGD), which rescales \\(s\\); it is not an arrow."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O2","reason":"LoRA-FA is the \\(\\lambda\\to\\infty\\) endpoint of the LoRA+ dynamics, yet \\(d=r(m+n-r)>mr\\): the limit is dynamical, not a simulation."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-fa","name":"LoRA-FA","full_name":"LoRA with Frozen-A","paper_title":"LoRA-FA: Memory-efficient Low-rank Adaptation for Large Language Models Fine-tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2308.03303","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03303","code_url":"https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/optimizers/lorafa.py","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"peft.optimizers.create_lorafa_optimizer(model, r, lora_alpha, lr) on a LoraConfig model","formula_latex":"W=W_0+s\\,BA_0,\\ \\ s=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r},\\ A_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{random and frozen},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained with}\\ \\ \\tilde g_B=\\tfrac{1}{s^{2}}\\,\\nabla_B\\mathcal L\\,(A_0A_0^{\\top})^{-1}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ s\\,\\tilde g_BA_0=G\\,A_0^{\\top}(A_0A_0^{\\top})^{-1}A_0=\\arg\\min_{\\hat g\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}A_0}\\lVert\\hat g-G\\rVert_F,\\ \\ G=\\nabla_W\\mathcal L","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}","locus":"any linear layer targeted by LoRA","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=sBA_0\\) with \\(A_0\\) a frozen random row space: the update lives in the fixed \\(r\\)-dimensional subspace \\(\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}(A_0)\\); the corrected \\(B\\)-gradient is the orthogonal projection of the full gradient onto that subspace","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(sBA_0\\) is an ordinary rank-\\(r\\) matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\) exactly, as in LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) with \\(A_0\\) random (Gaussian or uniform; the paper ablates the choice), so the initial model equals the pretrained one.","key_idea":"Freezing \\(A\\) has two effects. Memory: LoRA no longer stores the full \\(d_{in}\\)-dimensional input activation, only the \\(r\\)-dimensional \\(A_0x\\). Optimization: with SGD, \\(\\Delta W=-\\eta s^{2}GA_0^{\\top}A_0\\) is a fixed random projection of the gradient, and the \\((A_0A_0^{\\top})^{-1}\\) correction turns it into the exact least-squares projection of the full gradient.","results_note":"Matches existing PEFT methods and full fine-tuning on GLUE (RoBERTa), GSM8K, MT-Bench and HumanEval (Llama-2/3 and MoE models) while substantially reducing activation memory and compute.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA with eta_A = 0"},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"special-case-of","note":"under SGD and without the v3 correction, LoRA-FA's update -\\eta s^2 GA_0^{\\top}A_0 is Flora's random-projection compression without resampling"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"with A frozen the correction equals the Riemannian B-preconditioner (AA^T)^{-1} up to the constant s^{-2}"},{"target":"lora-pro","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA-Pro's gradient-matching objective with g^A constrained to 0 yields exactly LoRA-FA's corrected B-gradient"},{"target":"loqt","type":"competes-with","note":"LoQT also trains one factor only, with a gradient-SVD projector instead of a random one"},{"target":"asylora","type":"competes-with","note":"same freeze-A/train-B asymmetry; AsyLoRA supplies the generalization theory, LoRA-FA the memory argument and gradient projection"},{"target":"mica","type":"competes-with","note":"MiCA freezes B (minor singular vectors) instead of A."},{"target":"velora","type":"competes-with","note":"Both cut activation memory of LoRA training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03303","https://arxiv.org/html/2308.03303","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/optimizers/lorafa.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.16.0"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core","quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Longteng Zhang","Lin Zhang","Shaohuai Shi","Xiaowen Chu","Bo Li"],"bibkey":"zhang2023lora","bibtex":"@article{zhang2023lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-FA: Memory-efficient Low-rank Adaptation for Large Language Models Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Longteng Zhang and Lin Zhang and Shaohuai Shi and Xiaowen Chu and Bo Li},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2308.03303},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.03303},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.03303}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\{W_0+sBA_0\\}=W_0+\\mathbb R^m\\otimes\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\), linear of dimension \\(mr\\).","covariance":"The frozen \\(A_0\\) is random, so covariance holds only in law (\\(GL \\times\\) frame).","rebasing":"With \\(A_0\\) fixed it is idempotent; resampling \\(A_0\\) every cycle gives Flora."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-FA is the sub-object \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_0)}\\) obtained by freezing \\(A\\) (A2): a linear method with the same first-order image as zero-init LoRA. Its corrected gradient gives the frame-only cometric \\(K(G)=\\eta\\,GP_V\\), which depends only on \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) under SGD, but on \\(A_0\\) modulo \\(B_r\\) under the published AdamW.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_0)","note":"Freezing \\(A\\) at its base value: a pointed monomorphism into LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\)."},{"target":"asylora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B)=B\\,A_0Q^\\top","note":"Isomorphism \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\cong\\mathrm{FA}(Q)\\) when \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0=\\mathrm{row}\\,Q\\) (e.g. \\(Q\\) the orthonormalised \\(A_0\\)); for independent random frames the two agree only in law."},{"target":"flora-opt","rule":"A5","h":"B\\mapsto(B,\\,0,\\dots,0)","note":"LoRA-FA is the first period of Flora when Flora's first projection is \\(A_0\\) (shared seed): one stage embeds into the rebasing scheme."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mica","test":"O1","reason":"\\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\) has image \\(\\{XA_0\\}\\) (fixed row space), MiCA's \\(\\mathrm{FB}(U_{\\min})\\) has \\(\\{U_{\\min}Y\\}\\) (fixed column space); neither contains the other."},{"target":"vera","test":"O2","reason":"VeRA \\(\\to\\) LoRA-FA\\((A)\\) exists, but not the reverse: \\(d(\\text{LoRA-FA})=mr>m+r-1=d(\\text{VeRA})\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","name":"Riemannian LoRA","full_name":"Riemannian Preconditioned LoRA","paper_title":"Riemannian Preconditioned LoRA for Fine-Tuning Foundation Models","authors":"Zhang & Pilanci","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.02347","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347","code_url":"https://github.com/pilancilab/Riemannian_Preconditioned_LoRA","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"peft.optimizers.create_riemannian_optimizer(model, optimizer_cls, lr, reg)","formula_latex":"A\\leftarrow A-\\eta\\,(B^\\top B+\\delta I_r)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L},\\ \\ B\\leftarrow B-\\eta\\,\\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,(AA^\\top+\\delta I_r)^{-1}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"LoRA layers (optimizer-level)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"\\(r\\times r\\) preconditioners turning Euclidean gradients into scaled (Riemannian) gradients on the manifold of rank-\\(r\\) matrices","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained adapter is a standard LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) as in LoRA (\\(\\delta\\) regularizes the singular preconditioner).","key_idea":"LoRA's factorization is ill-conditioned; precondition each factor by the Gram matrix of the other to follow the geometry of the low-rank manifold.","results_note":"Paper reports faster convergence and more robustness to learning rate with SGD and AdamW on GPT-2, Mistral-7B and diffusion models, with negligible overhead.","relations":[{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"Both rebalance A/B updates. LoRA+'s fixed scalar ratio is not a special case of the Gram-matrix preconditioner except when both Gram matrices are multiples of I."},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA-FA's B-gradient correction is one half of this preconditioner."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"optimizer-only change"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.02347","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/tree/main/src/peft/optimizers","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.21.0","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/optimizers/riemannian.py","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Fangzhao Zhang","Mert Pilanci"],"bibkey":"zhang2024riemannian","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024riemannian,\n  title = {{Riemannian Preconditioned LoRA for Fine-Tuning Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Fangzhao Zhang and Mert Pilanci},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.02347},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Same \\(\\rho\\) as LoRA; the optimizer replaces the cometric by \\(\\eta s^2(P_UG+GP_V)\\) (undamped scaled GD, Prop III.4(a)).","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). The undamped step is \\(GL_r\\)-equivariant and descends to \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_r\\), but the damped \\((AA^\\top+\\delta I)^{-1}\\) needed at \\(B_0=0\\) is only \\(O(r)\\)-equivariant, and the practical scaled AdamW only \\(B_r\\)-equivariant (Thm III.12)."},"categorical_reading":"Riemannian preconditioning keeps LoRA's object and chooses a cometric that is a function of \\(W\\) on the full-rank locus, i.e. a metric descended to the quotient \\(\\mathcal M_r=Q^{\\rm full}/GL_r\\). The damping and the Adam wrapper used in practice shrink the respected gauge from \\(GL_r\\) to \\(O(r)\\) and then to \\(B_r\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object, different trained-method data (the cometric)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"qlora","name":"QLoRA","full_name":"Quantized Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs","authors":"Dettmers et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.14314","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314","code_url":"https://github.com/artidoro/qlora","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(target_modules='all-linear', ...) on a model loaded with BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type='nf4', bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True, bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16), after peft.prepare_model_for_kbit_training(model)","formula_latex":"h=\\mathrm{doubleDequant}\\big(c_1^{\\mathrm{FP32}},c_2^{\\mathrm{FP8}},W_0^{\\mathrm{NF4}}\\big)\\,x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BAx,\\quad W_0^{\\mathrm{NF4}}=q_{\\mathrm{NF4}}(W_0)\\ \\text{frozen (blockwise, constants double-quantized)},\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{trained in BF16 (paged optimizer)}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"every linear layer of the transformer (attention and MLP); base weights stored as 4-bit NormalFloat","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"rank-\\(r\\) additive update \\(BA\\) on top of a frozen 4-bit NF4 (quantile-quantized, blockwise, double-quantized constants) base weight; storage dtype NF4, compute dtype BF16, so \\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space while \\(W_0\\) lives on a 16-level grid","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) merges exactly only into a dequantized 16-bit copy of \\(W_0\\); merging and re-quantizing to NF4 adds fresh rounding error, so exact inference at the original 4-bit cost is not preserved.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (LoRA default, \\(A\\) random), so the initial function is the quantized model \\(\\mathrm{dequant}(q_{NF4}(W_0))\\), not \\(W_0\\): the quantization gap is left uncompensated at init (the gap LoftQ / LQ-LoRA / ApiQ are built to shrink).","key_idea":"Backpropagate through a frozen 4-bit base model into LoRA adapters. Three memory tricks make this work: NF4 (a quantile data type that is information-theoretically optimal for normally distributed weights), double quantization of the scale constants, and paged optimizers. Together they let a 65B model be fine-tuned on one 48GB GPU.","results_note":"Fine-tunes a 65B model on a single 48GB GPU while matching 16-bit fine-tuning; Guanaco-65B reaches 99.3% of ChatGPT's score on the Vicuna benchmark after 24 hours on one GPU.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA adapters attached to a frozen NF4-quantized backbone"},{"target":"loftq","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoftQ with T=0 alternating steps (Q=q_N(W_0), BA=0) is exactly QLoRA's initialization"},{"target":"peqa","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent (May 2023) memory-efficient fine-tuning of quantized LLMs; PEQA tunes quantization scales instead of adding adapters"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"QA-LoRA removes QLoRA's need for a 16-bit merge / post-training re-quantization"},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"LoftQ initializes LoRA to compensate quantization error."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.14314","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/docs/source/developer_guides/quantization.md","https://openreview.net/forum?id=OUIFPHEgJU","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/quantization"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Tim Dettmers","Artidoro Pagnoni","Ari Holtzman","Luke Zettlemoyer"],"bibkey":"dettmers2023qlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dettmers2023qlora,\n  title = {{QLoRA: Efficient Finetuning of Quantized LLMs}},\n  author = {Tim Dettmers and Artidoro Pagnoni and Ari Holtzman and Luke Zettlemoyer},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.14314},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14314}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"As VIII.B: LoRA's cone translated to \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) (Prop V.2(a)).","base_point":"\\(e=\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0\\), generically full rank, so \\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\) unless \\(\\operatorname{rank}e\\le r\\).","covariance":"VIII.B 'Mon (grid)'. Strictly smaller: blockwise absmax NF4 (contiguous blocks of 64 along a row) is equivariant on the input side only under block-preserving permutations and block-constant positive scalings, and NF4's asymmetric code book breaks sign flips; output-row permutations and positive row scalings are exact. Kept as MonxMon, the nearest key, as for LoftQ.","merge":"Exact merge leaves the NF4 grid; dequantise-add-requantise is lossy.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\) (span of the cone)."},"categorical_reading":"QLoRA needs no new structure: it is the LoRA cone translated to the quantised base \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), an object with pointing defect \\(e\\). Since \\(-e\\) is generically full rank, \\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\)QLoRA, and its exact merge leaves the NF4 grid (Mq).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"ir-qlora","rule":"A2","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{s}{\\alpha}B,\\ A,\\ 0,\\ 0)","note":"\\(s\\) is QLoRA's scale, \\(\\alpha\\) IR-QLoRA's. Freeze the elastic scalars \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\) at their base value 0; needs a common quantised base (ICQ's calibrated shift \\(\\tau^*\\) moves IR-QLoRA's base)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"\\(\\rho(q_0)=\\kappa\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_0\\), and \\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\)QLoRA unless \\(\\operatorname{rank}e\\le r\\) (Prop V.2(a)): no pointed arrow either way."},{"target":"loftq","test":"O3","reason":"With LoftQ's default single iteration both are LoRA\\(_r\\) over the same \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), but QLoRA is pointed at the apex (\\(B=0\\)) and LoftQ at the smooth point \\(-\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\): same image, different strata."},{"target":"qa-lora","test":"O1","reason":"A generic rank-\\(r\\) update is not constant on quantisation groups."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"layer-replication","name":"LoRA layer replication (DUS)","full_name":"Memory-efficient depth up-scaling with per-replica LoRA","paper_title":"SOLAR 10.7B: Scaling Large Language Models with Simple yet Effective Depth Up-Scaling","authors":"Kim et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NAACL 2024 (Industry)","arxiv":"2312.15166","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.15166","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(layer_replication=[[start, end), ...])","formula_latex":"f=\\ell_{\\pi(L')}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\ell_{\\pi(1)},\\ \\ \\pi:[L']\\to[L] \\text{ (stacked layer ranges, base weights shared)};\\ W^{(j)}=W_{0,\\pi(j)}+B^{(j)}A^{(j)} \\text{ (distinct LoRA per replica)}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{j=1}^{L'} r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per adapted module type}","locus":"whole transformer stack (layer duplication) plus LoRA on every replica","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"depth up-scaling by repeating layer ranges (mergekit passthrough convention) with weight sharing, differentiated by per-replica low-rank updates","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Merging requires materializing separate weights for each replica, yielding a larger (deeper) model rather than the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Not an identity of the original model: replication changes depth; each replica's LoRA starts at \\(B = 0\\).","key_idea":"Grow a model's depth by reusing its own layers, then specialize the copies cheaply with LoRA instead of storing duplicated weights.","results_note":"","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"LoRA adapters attached to replicated layers."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.15166","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/blob/main/src/peft/tuners/lora/config.py"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Dahyun Kim","Chanjun Park","Sanghoon Kim","Wonsung Lee","Wonho Song","Yunsu Kim","Hyeonwoo Kim","Yungi Kim","Hyeonju Lee","Jihoo Kim","Changbae Ahn","Seonghoon Yang","Sukyung Lee","Hyunbyung Park","Gyoungjin Gim","Mikyoung Cha","Hwalsuk Lee","Sunghun Kim"],"bibkey":"kim2023solar","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kim2023solar,\n  title = {{SOLAR 10.7B: Scaling Large Language Models with Simple yet Effective Depth Up-Scaling}},\n  author = {Dahyun Kim and Chanjun Park and Sanghoon Kim and Wonsung Lee and Wonho Song and Yunsu Kim and Hyeonwoo Kim and Yungi Kim and Hyeonju Lee and Jihoo Kim and Changbae Ahn and Seonghoon Yang and Sukyung Lee and Hyunbyung Park and Gyoungjin Gim and Mikyoung Cha and Hwalsuk Lee and Sunghun Kim},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2312.15166},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.15166}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Extends \\(f\\) to a deeper network \\(\\tilde f=\\ell_{\\pi(L')}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\ell_{\\pi(1)}\\) with tied base weights and one LoRA per replica. At \\(B=0\\) this is the stacked network, not \\(f\\), and low-rank LoRAs cannot turn a replicated block into the identity, so there is no neutral point.","shape":"The extended network is compared with \\(f\\) only through realisations.","base_point":"Unpointed relative to \\((\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\): \\(\\tilde f(\\theta_0,q_0,-)\\ne f(\\theta_0,-)\\). It is pointed only relative to the replicated model.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_jGL_r\\): one LoRA gauge per replica.","covariance":"Each replica's update is a basis-free LoRA cone, and replication is basis-free.","merge":"Each replica's LoRA fuses into a materialised copy of its block. The replicas share \\(W_{0,\\pi(j)}\\), and Thm VI.3 requires an unshared target box, so the fuse is possible only after untying (one dense copy per replica). It never returns to the depth-\\(L\\) architecture.","rebasing":"Per replica, merge-and-restart reaches the full block weight (LoRA's span), at the cost of untying the replicas."},"categorical_reading":"Layer replication is an unpointed extension: the depth-upscaled network lives over a larger \\(\\Theta\\times\\Theta'\\) with tied weights, and no parameter value recovers \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\), so it is not an object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\). The per-replica LoRAs are ordinary pointed objects over the replicated base point.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"The realised functions of the depth-\\(L'\\) network are generically not \\(\\Phi_f(\\theta)\\) for any \\(\\theta\\) of the depth-\\(L\\) architecture, so no lift into LoRA, or into any object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\), exists."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"kappatune","name":"KappaTune","full_name":"Condition-number-based selective fine-tuning","paper_title":"The Condition Number as a Scale-Invariant Proxy for Information Encoding in Neural Units","authors":"Ludwig","year":2025,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2506.16289","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16289","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"peft.helpers.find_kappa_target_modules / KappaTuneSelector -> LoraConfig(target_modules=..., target_parameters=...)","formula_latex":"\\kappa(W)=\\frac{\\sigma_{\\max}(W)}{\\sigma_{\\min}(W)};\\ \\ \\mathcal{S}=\\text{lowest-}\\kappa \\text{ top-}p \\text{ fraction of tensors};\\ \\text{train (or attach LoRA to) } W\\in\\mathcal{S},\\ \\text{freeze the rest}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{W\\in\\mathcal{S}}|W| \\ (\\text{KappaTune}) \\text{ or } \\sum_{W\\in\\mathcal{S}}r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\ (\\text{KappaTune-LoRA})","locus":"module selection over all linear layers and fused MoE expert parameters","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"data-free target selection by spectral condition number (most isotropic tensors are adapted)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Selected parameters or LoRAs are ordinary weight changes.","identity_at_init":"Inherits from the chosen tuner (LoRA: \\(B = 0\\)).","key_idea":"Well-conditioned (low-\\(\\kappa\\), high-entropy) tensors encode less specialized information and can absorb new tasks with less forgetting.","results_note":"Paper reports about 6x lower forgetting than LoRA on OLMoE and 2.8x lower with KappaTune-LoRA on DeepSeek-V2-Lite.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"KappaTune-LoRA places LoRA only on low-kappa modules."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16289","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.16289","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://github.com/huggingface/peft/releases/tag/v0.20.0"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"authors_full":["Oswaldo Ludwig"],"bibkey":"ludwig2025condition","bibtex":"@article{ludwig2025condition,\n  title = {{The Condition Number as a Scale-Invariant Proxy for Information Encoding in Neural Units}},\n  author = {Oswaldo Ludwig},\n  year = {2025},\n  eprint = {2506.16289},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.16289},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.16289}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Selective fine-tuning: the trainable set is a fixed collection \\(\\mathcal S\\) of whole tensors with the lowest condition numbers. KappaTune-LoRA attaches LoRA to \\(\\mathcal S\\) instead and then has LoRA's coordinates.","shape":"\\(\\theta_0+\\bigoplus_{W\\in\\mathcal S}\\mathbb R^{m_W\\times n_W}\\), a coordinate-block subspace.","covariance":"Inside a selected tensor the image is all of \\(\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}\\) (\\(GL\\times GL\\)). The selection statistic \\(\\kappa=\\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min}\\) is invariant only under \\(CO(m)\\times CO(n)\\), so a general change of basis can move a tensor into or out of \\(\\mathcal S\\).","rebasing":"With the mask fixed, cycles gain nothing. Recomputing \\(\\kappa\\) after merging could change \\(\\mathcal S\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KappaTune is the coordinate sub-object of full fine-tuning spanned by a spectrally chosen block of tensors, i.e. full FT with the complement frozen. Its only dependence on \\(\\theta_0\\) is through the \\(CO\\times CO\\)-invariant \\(\\kappa\\): a spectral selection at the granularity of whole tensors.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A selected tensor moves in all \\(mn\\) directions, with rank up to \\(\\min(m,n)\\). Both \\(d\\) and the maximal rank exceed LoRA\\(_r\\)'s, so LoRA\\(_r\\) on the same tensor cannot simulate it."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"milora","name":"MiLoRA","full_name":"Minor singular component LoRA","paper_title":"MiLoRA: Harnessing Minor Singular Components for Parameter-Efficient LLM Finetuning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NAACL 2025","arxiv":"2406.09044","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.248/","code_url":"https://github.com/sufenlp/MiLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_0=\\sum_{i}\\sigma_i u_i v_i^\\top=W_p+W_m,\\quad W_m=\\sum_{i>k-r}\\sigma_i u_i v_i^\\top=\\underbrace{U_m\\Sigma_m^{1/2}}_{B}\\,\\underbrace{\\Sigma_m^{1/2}V_m^\\top}_{A},\\qquad h=(W_p+BA)\\,x;\\ W_p\\text{ frozen},\\ A,B\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (attention and MLP projections)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space; base point \\(W_p\\) is the rank-\\((k-r)\\) principal truncation of \\(W_0\\) (\\(k=\\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\)) and the adapter is initialized on the \\(r\\) smallest singular triplets","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_p+BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W_p+B_0A_0=W_0\\) by construction (data-free SVD of \\(W_0\\)); no extra scaling hyperparameter is needed.","key_idea":"Keep the principal subspace (assumed to store pretrained knowledge) frozen and adapt only within the minor singular subspace, which is less likely to interfere with pretrained abilities.","results_note":"Reported gains over LoRA on LLaMA-2-7B / LLaMA-3-8B commonsense reasoning (+1.6 / +1.1), math reasoning (+2.0), instruction following and LLaVA-1.5 visual instruction tuning.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"dual-to","note":"bottom-r instead of top-r singular components"},{"target":"corda","type":"special-case-of","note":"CorDA knowledge-preserved mode with C=I reduces to MiLoRA"},{"target":"kasa","type":"competes-with","note":"KaSA also acts on the minor singular subspace, but discards it and relearns it with a learnable singular-value diagonal"},{"target":"mica","type":"competes-with","note":"MiCA also targets minor components but freezes B=U_{-r} and trains only A from zero"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09044","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09044","https://github.com/sufenlp/MiLoRA","https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.248/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Hanqing Wang","Yixia Li","Shuo Wang","Guanhua Chen","Yun Chen"],"bibkey":"wang2024milora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024milora,\n  title = {{MiLoRA: Harnessing Minor Singular Components for Parameter-Efficient LLM Finetuning}},\n  author = {Hanqing Wang and Yixia Li and Shuo Wang and Guanhua Chen and Yun Chen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2406.09044},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.248/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\((W_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) with \\(P=W_m\\) the bottom-\\(r\\) SVD part of \\(W_0\\).","base_point":"Stratum S: \\(\\theta_0\\) smooth, \\(S_1=T_P\\mathcal M_r\\) (table: regular (smooth)).","covariance":"Truncated-SVD split: input-side group exactly \\(CO(n)\\), output \\(CO(m)\\) (Prop II.16(b)).","rebasing":"Table: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MiLoRA is the Type II pointing with \\(P=\\) bottom-\\(r\\) SVD part of \\(W_0\\): image \\((W_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), \\(\\theta_0\\) smooth, \\(S_1=T_P\\mathcal M_r\\). It is the \\(C=I\\) member of CorDA-KPM, but for \\(C\\not\\propto I\\) the vertices differ and no arrow connects the two.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"Dual splits with different vertices (W_0-W_m vs W_0-[W_0]_r): the translated cones are distinct, neither contains the other."},{"target":"mica","test":"O2","reason":"d(MiLoRA)=r(m+n-r) > d(MiCA)=rn; the arrow runs only MiCA -> MiLoRA."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"lora-one","name":"LoRA-One","full_name":"LoRA-One (one-step full-gradient spectral initialization)","paper_title":"LoRA-One: One-Step Full Gradient Could Suffice for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models, Provably and Efficiently","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2502.01235","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01235","code_url":"https://github.com/YuanheZ/LoRA-One","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"G=-\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0)=U_GS_GV_G^\\top,\\ \\bar S=S_G/\\sigma_1(G),\\ \\gamma=1/s,\\quad B_0=\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\,U_{G,[:,1:r]}\\bar S_{[1:r]}^{1/2},\\quad A_0=\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\,\\bar S_{[1:r]}^{1/2}V_{G,[:,1:r]}^\\top,\\qquad W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}BA;\\ W_0\\text{ frozen},\\ A,B\\text{ trained with AdamW}\\ \\ (\\text{LoRA-One-P: precondition }\\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}(AA^\\top+\\lambda I)^{-1},\\ (B^\\top B+\\lambda I)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space and is initialized to the (normalized, scaled) best rank-\\(r\\) approximation of one full-batch negative gradient, i.e. a truncated gradient step; the LoRA-One-P variant adds \\((B^\\top B+\\lambda I)^{-1},(AA^\\top+\\lambda I)^{-1}\\) preconditioners","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Plain additive low-rank update on \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No: \\(W_0\\) is not offset, so the initial model equals one best rank-\\(r\\) full-batch gradient-descent step from \\(W_0\\) (gradient-driven spectral initialization from a large sampled batch).","key_idea":"Theory shows LoRA iterates align with the top singular subspace of the one-step full fine-tuning gradient, so initializing there (and preconditioning against ill-conditioning) achieves the alignment immediately; it also corrects LoRA-GA's choice of singular vectors.","results_note":"Reported 88.73 vs 82.72 GLUE average over LoRA (T5-base) and 28.66 vs 25.85 HumanEval, with negligible time/memory overhead.","relations":[{"target":"lora-ga","type":"builds-on","note":"gradient-SVD initialization, but uses the top-r subspace for both factors and no weight offset"},{"target":"lora-sb","type":"competes-with","note":"both initialize the adapter as a low-rank approximation of the first full-FT update"},{"target":"gora","type":"competes-with","note":"GoRA also starts at an approximate gradient step without offsetting W_0"},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"competes-with","note":"both gradient-SVD initializations; LoRA-One uses the top-r subspace for both factors and no base-weight subtraction"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"combines","note":"LoRA-One-P uses the Zhang & Pilanci preconditioned AdamW"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"changes only the initialization (and optionally the optimizer)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01235","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.01235","https://github.com/YuanheZ/LoRA-One"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank","lora-core"],"authors_full":["Yuanhe Zhang","Fanghui Liu","Yudong Chen"],"bibkey":"zhang2025lorab","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025lorab,\n  title = {{LoRA-One: One-Step Full Gradient Could Suffice for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models, Provably and Efficiently}},\n  author = {Yuanhe Zhang and Fanghui Liu and Yudong Chen},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2502.01235},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01235}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"The image is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) (no residual subtraction): LoRA's cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(\\rho(q_0)=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}B_0A_0\\ne\\theta_0\\): by design the defect \\(e\\) is a rank-\\(r\\) truncated full-batch gradient step. The start is a smooth (rank-\\(r\\)) point of the cone, so in the slice at \\(\\theta_0'=\\theta_0+e\\) it is a Type II object with residual \\(\\theta_0\\).","covariance":"Def II.14 reads covariance on the image, and the image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) is basis-free (GL×GL), as for EVA. Only the starting point (the defect \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}B_0A_0=\\tfrac{\\alpha\\gamma}{\\sqrt r}U_{G,r}\\bar S_rV_{G,r}^\\top\\), built from the SVD of the full-batch gradient and normalised by \\(\\sigma_1\\)) is data-dependent, and it is covariant in law only under \\(O(m)\\times O(n)\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-One enters LoRA's own cone at a smooth point instead of the apex: a base change \\(\\theta_0\\mapsto\\theta_0+[-\\eta G]_r\\) (one truncated full-FT step), followed by a split pointing whose residual is the original \\(\\theta_0\\). In \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\) it is only an unpointed object, related to LoRA by \\(h=\\mathrm{id}\\) without preservation of base points.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Same image, but \\(\\rho(q_0)\\ne\\theta_0\\): \\(h=\\mathrm{id}\\) is only a weak morphism, and the start is a smooth point rather than the apex."},{"target":"lora-ga","test":"O1","reason":"LoRA-GA subtracts its initial adapter (vertex \\(W_0-P_{\\rm GA}\\)), LoRA-One does not (vertex \\(W_0\\)); the two cones are incomparable, as in Thm II.2(d)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"gora","name":"GoRA","full_name":"Gradient-driven Adaptive Low Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"GoRA: Gradient-driven Adaptive Low Rank Adaptation","authors":"He et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2502.12171","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12171","code_url":"https://github.com/hhnqqq/MyTransformers","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\bar G=\\tfrac1N\\textstyle\\sum_i\\partial\\mathcal{L}_i/\\partial W_0,\\quad I(W)=\\mathrm{avg}(|W_0\\odot\\bar G|),\\quad a_\\ell=\\tfrac{I(W_\\ell)}{\\sum_j I(W_j)},\\quad r_\\ell=\\mathrm{clip}\\big(\\mathrm{round}(b\\,a_\\ell/\\sqrt{d_{in}+d_{out}}),\\,r_{min},\\,r_{max}\\big);\\quad A_0\\sim\\mathcal{U}_{\\text{Kaiming}},\\ B_0=-\\xi\\,\\bar G A_0^\\top(A_0A_0^\\top)^{-1},\\ \\xi=\\gamma\\sqrt{d_{in}}/\\alpha,\\qquad W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{r_\\ell}}BA","trainable_params":"r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out}),\\ \\ \\text{budget }b=\\textstyle\\sum_\\ell\\sqrt{d_{in}+d_{out}}\\;r_{ref}","locus":"any linear layer; ranks allocated across all adapted layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=BA\\) with per-layer rank \\(r_\\ell\\) set by gradient sensitivity; \\(B_0\\) is the least-squares solution making \\(B_0A_0\\) the best approximation of \\(-\\bar G\\) in the row space of the random \\(A_0\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Plain additive low-rank update on \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No: \\(W_0\\) is not offset; the initial adapter \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt r}B_0A_0\\approx-\\gamma\\bar G\\) restricted to the row space of the random \\(A_0\\), i.e. a compressed gradient-descent step with tunable step size \\(\\gamma\\) (\\(\\xi=\\gamma\\sqrt{d_{in}}/\\alpha\\)), computed from gradients accumulated over \\(N\\) mini-batches.","key_idea":"A single accumulated-gradient pass before training is used twice: to allocate ranks across layers by gradient sensitivity and to initialize \\(B\\) so that the adapter starts as a compressed gradient step, without touching \\(W_0\\).","results_note":"Reported Llama-3.1-8B-Base GSM8K 75.74 vs full fine-tuning 73.69 and HumanEval 52.03 vs 51.63 at reference rank 128.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"uses AdaLoRA-style |w \\cdot \\nabla w| sensitivity, but computed once before training"},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"builds-on","note":"gradient-driven initialization, without the training-inference weight offset"},{"target":"eva","type":"competes-with","note":"both pre-allocate ranks before training from a calibration pass"},{"target":"rslora","type":"builds-on","note":"uses the rank-stabilized \\alpha/\\sqrt r forward scale so larger allocated ranks are not damped"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12171","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.12171"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Haonan He","Peng Ye","Yuchen Ren","Yuan Yuan","Luyang Zhou","Shucun Ju","Lei Chen"],"bibkey":"he2025gora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2025gora,\n  title = {{GoRA: Gradient-driven Adaptive Low Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Haonan He and Peng Ye and Yuchen Ren and Yuan Yuan and Luyang Zhou and Shucun Ju and Lei Chen},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2502.12171},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12171}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_\\ell}\\) (residual \\(W_0\\) untouched): the cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\); the start \\(\\theta_0+e\\) is a smooth point of it, not \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"Defect by design: \\(e=(\\alpha/\\sqrt r)B_0A_0 \\approx -\\gamma \\bar G P_{\\mathrm{row}A_0}\\), a compressed gradient step; over its own start \\(\\theta_0+e\\) the object is a Type II split with \\(P=e\\).","covariance":"The image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant; the starting defect (a gradient step) is at best \\(O\\times O\\) in law, and the rank allocation \\(|W_0\\odot\\bar G|\\) is a coordinatewise (monomial) statistic.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as LoRA."},"categorical_reading":"GoRA is LoRA with the frozen residual \\(W_0\\) untouched but started off the base point, at \\(\\theta_0+e\\) with \\(e\\approx-\\gamma\\bar G P_{\\mathrm{row}A_0}\\) a compressed gradient step: an object of the unpointed slice with a designed defect, which over its own start is a Type II split with \\(P=e\\). Its rank allocation by \\(|W_0\\odot\\bar G|\\) is a coordinatewise statistic.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(cB,A),\\ c=\\tfrac{\\alpha/\\sqrt{r_\\ell}}{s}","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_{r_l} with residual W_0 pointed at (cB_0,A_0): an arrow over the common start theta_0+e, not in the slice at theta_0 (there only a weak morphism to zero-init LoRA)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-ga","test":"O1","reason":"LoRA-GA subtracts B_0A_0 and starts at theta_0 on (theta_0-P)+M_{<=r}; GoRA keeps W_0 and lives on W_0+M_{<=r}: incomparable images (Thm II.2(d)) and different start points."},{"target":"eva","test":"O3","reason":"Same image W_0+M_{<=r_l} at equal rank, but EVA is pointed at theta_0 and GoRA at theta_0+e; a morphism must preserve rho(q_0)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-sb","name":"LoRA-SB","full_name":"LoRA Silver Bullet","paper_title":"Initialization using Update Approximation is a Silver Bullet for Extremely Efficient Low-Rank Fine-Tuning","authors":"Ponkshe et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025 Workshop (SCOPE)","arxiv":"2411.19557","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19557","code_url":"https://github.com/CERT-Lab/lora-sb","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_{avg}=-\\eta\\,\\mathrm{sign}\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_i\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}(W_0,x_i)\\Big)=USV^\\top,\\quad B=U_{[:,1:r]},\\ A=V_{[:,1:r]}^\\top,\\ R_0=\\tfrac1s S_{[1:r,1:r]},\\qquad W=W_0+s\\,BRA;\\ B,A\\text{ frozen},\\ R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"r^2","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=BRA\\) with frozen orthonormal outer factors \\(B,A\\) (from the SVD of an approximate first full-FT update) and a trainable \\(r\\times r\\) core \\(R\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(BRA\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape and folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No: \\(W_0\\) is not offset, so the model starts at \\(W_0\\) plus the optimal rank-\\(r\\) approximation of the simulated first AdamW step (sign of summed gradients over ~0.1% of the training data).","key_idea":"With the LoRA-XS architecture (frozen \\(B,A\\), trainable \\(r\\times r\\) \\(R\\)), initializing \\(B,A\\) from the SVD of an approximate full-FT update makes the learning dynamics hyperparameter-free in the scale \\(s\\) and gives the optimal low-rank approximation of full-FT gradients.","results_note":"Reported beating LoRA with 27-90x fewer trainable parameters and comprehensively outperforming LoRA-XS on GSM8K/MATH, commonsense reasoning and GLUE with RoBERTa-large, Llama-3.2-3B, Mistral-7B and Gemma-2-9B.","relations":[{"target":"lora-xs","type":"builds-on","note":"same W_0+sBRA architecture; replaces LoRA-XS's SVD-of-W_0 bases with SVD-of-update bases"},{"target":"lora-one","type":"competes-with","note":"both initialize at a rank-r approximation of the first full-FT update"},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"competes-with","note":"gradient-based initialization"},{"target":"tinylora","type":"competes-with","note":"both train only a tiny core inside frozen bases"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19557","https://arxiv.org/html/2411.19557","https://github.com/CERT-Lab/lora-sb","https://openreview.net/forum?id=WQZFXVpQow","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CERT-Lab/lora-sb/main/README.md","https://mlanthology.org/iclrw/2025/ponkshe2025iclrw-initialization/"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["init-rank","sharing"],"authors_full":["Kaustubh Ponkshe","Raghav Singhal","Eduard Gorbunov","Alexey Tumanov","Samuel Horvath","Praneeth Vepakomma"],"bibkey":"ponkshe2024initialization","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ponkshe2024initialization,\n  title = {{Initialization using Update Approximation is a Silver Bullet for Extremely Efficient Low-Rank Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Kaustubh Ponkshe and Raghav Singhal and Eduard Gorbunov and Alexey Tumanov and Samuel Horvath and Praneeth Vepakomma},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2411.19557},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19557}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(R \\mapsto W_0+sU_rRV_r^T\\): affine and injective, \\(d=r^2\\) (a generalised LoRA-XS = \\(\\mathrm{FA}\\times\\mathrm{FB}\\) product, Prop I.8(a)).","base_point":"Defect by design: \\(R_0=S_r/s\\) puts the start at \\(W_0+[\\Delta W_{avg}]_r\\).","covariance":"Table says \\(CO\\times CO\\) (data), but the frame is the SVD of \\(\\mathrm{sign}(\\sum \\mathrm{grad})\\) (simulated first Adam step); sign() commutes only with signed permutations and homotheties, so the group is \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}B_m\\times\\mathbb R_{>0}B_n\\) inside \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) (numerically: the frame moves by 1.5 under a rotation, 2e-15 under a signed permutation). \\(CO\\times CO\\) would hold for an SGD-step frame.","rebasing":"Idempotent with frozen frames; recomputing the frame each cycle gains."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-SB is the categorical product \\(\\mathrm{FA}(V_r^\\top)\\times\\mathrm{FB}(U_r)\\) of Prop I.8(a) taken in the frame of the simulated first Adam update rather than of \\(W_0\\), re-pointed at \\(R_0=S_r/s\\): a linear \\(r^2\\)-dimensional image with a designed defect. Because that frame is the SVD of \\(\\mathrm{sign}(\\sum\\nabla)\\), its covariance is only signed permutations and homotheties on each side, not \\(CO\\times CO\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A4","h":"h(R)=sU_rR","note":"Projection of the product FA(V_r^T) x FB(U_r) onto LoRA-FA with frozen A=V_r^T, re-pointed at sU_rR_0: an arrow over the common start theta_0+e."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O1","reason":"Same W_0+sBRA architecture but frames from SVD(sign grad) vs SVD(W_0): r^2-dim linear images in different frames, equal only if the frames coincide."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-null","name":"LoRA-Null","full_name":"LoRA initialized in the Null space of activations","paper_title":"Put the Space of LoRA Initialization to the Extreme to Preserve Pre-trained Knowledge","authors":"Tang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"AAAI 2026","arxiv":"2503.02659","url":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/40608","code_url":"https://github.com/HungerPWAY/LoRA-Null","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"X_{pre}=U\\Sigma V^\\top,\\ U_{null}=U_{[:,-r:]}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r},\\quad W_0U_{null}U_{null}^\\top=U'\\Sigma'V'^\\top,\\quad B=U'_{[:,:r]}\\Sigma'^{1/2}_{[:r]},\\ A=\\Sigma'^{1/2}_{[:r]}V'^\\top_{[:r,:]},\\quad W_0'=W_0-BA,\\qquad h=(W_0'+BA)x","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) factors through an \\(r\\)-dimensional space initialized as the projection of \\(W_0\\) onto the (approximate) null space of pre-training-knowledge input activations","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0'+BA\\) is an ordinary dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Exact: \\(W_0'+B_0A_0=W_0\\); data-driven from activations of knowledge-probing (pre-training-like) data.","key_idea":"Argues that the space in which LoRA is initialized, not the residual weight, governs forgetting, and initializes the adapter in the null space of pre-trained-knowledge activations so that updates minimally disturb what the model already knows.","results_note":"Reported better knowledge preservation than CorDA (e.g. 93.18% vs 89.00% on LLaMA-3.2-3B math setting) with competitive downstream accuracy.","relations":[{"target":"corda","type":"builds-on","note":"extends CorDA's knowledge-preserved mode from small covariance-oriented components to the activation null space"},{"target":"milora","type":"competes-with","note":"knowledge preservation by adapting in a minor subspace"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.02659","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.02659","https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/40608"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Pengwei Tang","Xiaolin Hu","Yong Liu","Lizhong Ding","Dongjie Zhang","Xing Wu","Debing Zhang"],"bibkey":"tang2025put","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tang2025put,\n  title = {{Put the Space of LoRA Initialization to the Extreme to Preserve Pre-trained Knowledge}},\n  author = {Pengwei Tang and Xiaolin Hu and Yong Liu and Lizhong Ding and Dongjie Zhang and Xing Wu and Debing Zhang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2503.02659},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/40608}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(P=W_0U_{\\rm null}U_{\\rm null}^\\top\\) has rank \\(r\\) and its rank-\\(r\\) SVD is exact, so the image is \\((W_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) with \\(P=W_0P_{\\rm null}\\).","base_point":"Stratum S: exact identity at init, \\(\\theta_0\\) smooth, deficit 0.","covariance":"\\(P\\) transforms as \\(gP\\) for every \\(g\\in GL_m\\) (\\(P\\) depends on the output side only through \\(W_0\\)) and needs \\(P_{\\rm null}(hCh^\\top)=hP_{\\rm null}(C)h^{-1}\\) on the input side, i.e. \\(h\\in CO(n)\\): group \\(GL(m)\\times CO(n)\\), in law with the data transformed.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\), as for the other splits."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Null is a Type II split with \\(P=W_0P_{\\rm null}\\), the projection of \\(W_0\\) onto the minor input-activation directions of pretraining data; \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point of \\((W_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\). Since \\(P\\) depends on the output side only through \\(W_0\\), the split is \\(GL(m)\\times CO(n)\\)-covariant.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"corda","test":"O1","reason":"Different vertices: W_0P_null (input null-space projection) vs CorDA-KPM's covariance-oriented tail [W_0C]_{tail}C^{-1}."},{"target":"milora","test":"O1","reason":"Different vertices: W_0P_null vs the weight-only tail W_m; translated cones with distinct vertices are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"dylora","name":"DyLoRA","full_name":"Dynamic Search-Free Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"DyLoRA: Parameter Efficient Tuning of Pre-trained Models using Dynamic Search-Free Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Valipour et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EACL 2023","arxiv":"2210.07558","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07558","code_url":"https://github.com/huawei-noah/KD-NLP/tree/main/DyLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"b\\sim p_B\\ \\text{on}\\ \\{r_{min},\\dots,r_{max}\\},\\quad h=W_0x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{b}\\,B_{[:,1:b]}A_{[1:b,:]}\\,x,\\quad \\mathcal{L}^{DY}_{\\downarrow b}=\\textstyle\\sum_i\\ell\\big(f(x_i;A_{[1:b,:]},B_{[:,1:b]}),y_i\\big)","trainable_params":"r_{max}(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (typically \\(W_q\\), \\(W_v\\))","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"nested family of truncations \\(\\Delta W_{\\downarrow b}=B_{[:,1:b]}A_{[1:b,:]}\\) trained jointly (nested-dropout-like ordering of rank-1 components), so every prefix rank is a usable adapter","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After choosing any rank \\(b\\) at deployment, \\(B_{[:,1:b]}A_{[1:b,:]}\\) folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (up-projection \\(B=0\\)).","key_idea":"Train a single adapter for a whole range of ranks by sampling a truncation rank at every step, so the rank can be chosen after training without a search.","results_note":"Reported training 4-7x faster than searching LoRA ranks, with GLUE average 86.66 vs 86.49 for LoRA on RoBERTa-base.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"a DyLoRA adapter truncated at rank b is a rank-b LoRA"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"search-free rank flexibility vs importance-based budget allocation"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07558","https://arxiv.org/html/2210.07558","https://aclanthology.org/2023.eacl-main.239/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Mojtaba Valipour","Mehdi Rezagholizadeh","Ivan Kobyzev","Ali Ghodsi"],"bibkey":"valipour2022dylora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{valipour2022dylora,\n  title = {{DyLoRA: Parameter Efficient Tuning of Pre-trained Models using Dynamic Search-Free Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Mojtaba Valipour and Mehdi Rezagholizadeh and Ivan Kobyzev and Ali Ghodsi},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL)},\n  eprint = {2210.07558},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07558}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Filtration of cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le b}\\), \\(b\\le r_{\\max}\\) (table).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\): stratum \\(\\mathrm T_A\\), deficit \\(r_{\\max}(n-r_{\\max})\\).","gauge":"Only diagonal \\(g\\) preserves every truncation \\(B_{:,1:b}A_{1:b,:}\\) simultaneously: \\(T_r\\) (table). The full-rank object alone has \\(GL_r\\); the nested-dropout loss breaks it.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"DyLoRA's object at full rank is LoRA\\(_{r_{\\max}}\\), but its nested-dropout loss is a sum over the filtration \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le b}\\), and only the torus \\(T_r\\) preserves every truncation, so the training gauge is \\(T_r\\) rather than \\(GL_r\\). The truncations are A2 sub-objects DyLoRA\\(_b\\hookrightarrow\\)DyLoRA\\(_{r_{\\max}}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Isomorphism of underlying objects at b=r_max (pointed at (0,A_0)); it does not intertwine the nested-dropout objective, whose symmetry is only T_r."},{"target":"dylora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B_b,A_b)=\\big([\\tfrac{r_{\\max}}{b}B_b,\\ 0],\\ [A_b;\\ A_{0,(b+1):}]\\big)","note":"Truncation level b as a sub-object of level r_max: freeze the trailing B columns at 0 and A rows at their base values (scale alpha/b vs alpha/r_max absorbed)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"dim S_1(DyLoRA)=m r_max (B_0=0) but dim S_1(AdaLoRA)=r (Lambda_0=0): S_1 not contained, so no pointed arrow at the default pointings."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sora","name":"SoRA","full_name":"Sparse Low-rank Adaptation","paper_title":"Sparse Low-rank Adaptation of Pre-trained Language Models","authors":"Ding et al.","year":2023,"venue":"EMNLP 2023","arxiv":"2311.11696","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11696","code_url":"https://github.com/TsinghuaC3I/SoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+B\\big(g\\odot(Ax)\\big),\\ g\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_{max}};\\quad \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_0+\\lambda\\textstyle\\sum_k\\lVert g^{(k)}\\rVert_1;\\quad g_{t+1}=\\mathcal{T}_{\\eta_t\\lambda}\\big(g_t-\\eta_t\\nabla_g\\mathcal{L}_0\\big),\\ \\mathcal{T}_\\xi(x)=\\mathrm{sign}(x)\\max(|x|-\\xi,0)","trainable_params":"r_{max}(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{during training};\\ r'(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{after pruning zero gates}","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g)\\,A\\) with a sparse gate vector driven to exact zeros by proximal (soft-thresholding) updates; zeroed rank-1 components are removed","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After pruning, \\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g)\\,A\\) is a plain low-rank matrix that folds into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B=0\\) as in LoRA (\\(A\\) Kaiming-uniform, gate \\(g \\sim N(0,1)\\) in the official code).","key_idea":"Start with a generous rank and let an L1-regularized gate, optimized by proximal gradient, decide how many rank-1 components each matrix keeps.","results_note":"Reported GLUE average 89.36 with 0.91M parameters vs LoRA 88.38 (1.33M) and AdaLoRA 88.83 (1.27M) on DeBERTaV3-base, with about 30% faster training.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"gate sparsity instead of sensitivity-based singular value masking"},{"target":"alora-allocating","type":"competes-with","note":"both gate individual rank-1 components"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11696","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.11696","https://github.com/TsinghuaC3I/SoRA","https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.252/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Ning Ding","Xingtai Lv","Qiaosen Wang","Yulin Chen","Bowen Zhou","Zhiyuan Liu","Maosong Sun"],"bibkey":"ding2023sparse","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ding2023sparse,\n  title = {{Sparse Low-rank Adaptation of Pre-trained Language Models}},\n  author = {Ning Ding and Xingtai Lv and Qiaosen Wang and Yulin Chen and Bowen Zhou and Zhiyuan Liu and Maosong Sun},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2311.11696},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11696}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_{\\max}}\\); pruning zero gates gives sub-cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r'}\\) (a budgeted union).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\) with \\(g_0\\), \\(A_0\\) random: \\(S_1=\\{X\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g_0)A_0\\}=\\{XA_0\\}\\), dim \\(m r_{\\max}\\) (checked), stratum \\(\\mathrm T_A\\).","gauge":"\\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g)\\,A\\): generic fibre \\(r^2+r\\) against a \\(2r\\)-dim torus; the extra directions are non-linear absorption (as AdaLoRA). The \\(\\ell_1\\) penalty and proximal step act on \\(Q\\) and are not invariant under the torus slide \\((BD^{-1},Dg,A)\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"SoRA is the map \\((B,g,A)\\mapsto W_0+B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g)A\\) pointed at \\(B=0\\) (stratum \\(\\mathrm T_A\\), \\(\\dim S_1=mr_{\\max}\\)); it factors through LoRA by \\(h=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g),A)\\), and pruning zero gates moves to a lower-rank sub-object. The \\(\\ell_1\\) penalty lives on \\(Q\\), not on \\(\\Theta\\): the slide \\((BD^{-1},Dg,A)\\) fixes \\(\\rho\\) but rescales \\(\\|g\\|_1\\), so the sparsity it induces depends on the gauge.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,g,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g),\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0), same random A_0; after pruning the support S, the restriction maps into LoRA_{|S|}."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"Both gate rank-one components, but SoRA starts at B=0 (dim S_1=m r_max) and AdaLoRA at Lambda=0 (dim S_1=r): S_1(SoRA) is not inside S_1(AdaLoRA)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"increlora","name":"IncreLoRA","full_name":"Incremental Parameter Allocation LoRA","paper_title":"IncreLoRA: Incremental Parameter Allocation Method for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2308.12043","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12043","code_url":"https://github.com/FeiyuZhang98/IncreLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\textstyle\\sum_{i=1}^{r_k}\\lambda_i\\,b_ia_i^\\top=W_0+B\\Lambda A;\\quad R=\\lVert AA^\\top-I\\rVert_F^2+\\lVert B^\\top B-I\\rVert_F^2\\ (\\text{drives }\\lVert b_ia_i^\\top\\rVert_F\\to1);\\quad S_k^{(t)}=\\mathrm{avg}\\big(|\\Delta W_k\\odot\\nabla_{\\Delta W_k}\\mathcal{L}|\\big)\\ \\text{(EMA-smoothed}\\times\\text{uncertainty)};\\ \\text{every }\\nu\\text{ steps add a rank-1 component to the top-}h\\text{ modules}","trainable_params":"r_k(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{plus one reserve component }(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ per module during allocation},\\ r_k\\ \\text{growing from }1\\ \\text{until}\\ \\textstyle\\sum_k r_k=r_{final}","locus":"any linear layer; budget shared across modules","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sum of scaled rank-1 components whose number per module grows during training according to an importance score; each new component is pre-trained as a reserve with tiny scale (advance learning) and gets its own restarted warmup","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The final sum of rank-1 terms is a dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\lambda_i=0\\) for active components (reserve components held at \\(\\lambda\\approx10^{-5}\\)).","key_idea":"Grow, rather than prune, the rank of each module: start every matrix at rank 1 and add components where the importance score says capacity is needed, which avoids AdaLoRA's upper bound on per-module rank.","results_note":"Reported GLUE average 89.61 (1.33M params) vs AdaLoRA 89.46 (1.27M) and LoRA \\(r=8\\) 88.50 (1.33M) on DeBERTaV3-base, with larger advantages at low budgets.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"dual-to","note":"incremental growth vs pruning, with AdaLoRA-style sensitivity scores"},{"target":"elalora","type":"competes-with","note":"ElaLoRA both grows and prunes"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12043","https://arxiv.org/html/2308.12043","https://github.com/FeiyuZhang98/IncreLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Feiyu Zhang","Liangzhi Li","Junhao Chen","Zhouqiang Jiang","Bowen Wang","Yiming Qian"],"bibkey":"zhang2023increlora","bibtex":"@article{zhang2023increlora,\n  title = {{IncreLoRA: Incremental Parameter Allocation Method for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Feiyu Zhang and Liangzhi Li and Junhao Chen and Zhouqiang Jiang and Bowen Wang and Yiming Qian},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2308.12043},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.12043},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.12043}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Budgeted union of cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_k}\\) with \\(r_k\\) growing from 1; no per-module ceiling as in AdaLoRA.","base_point":"\\(\\lambda_i=0\\) for active components: only \\(\\Lambda\\) moves to first order, \\(\\dim S_1=r_k\\) (Lemma II.4); reserve components at \\(\\lambda\\approx10^{-5}\\) leave a negligible defect.","gauge":"\\(B \\Lambda A\\): fibre \\(r^2+r\\) beyond the \\(2r\\)-dim torus (non-linear absorption, as AdaLoRA).","covariance":"Image \\(GL\\times GL\\); the importance score \\(|\\Delta W \\cdot \\mathrm{grad}|\\) is coordinatewise and affects only the allocation.","rebasing":"\\(\\to \\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"IncreLoRA is the \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) object pointed at \\(\\Lambda=0\\) (\\(\\dim S_1=r_k\\), apex), walked up the rank filtration by A5 stages \\(r_k\\to r_k+1\\) instead of down it as in AdaLoRA; its reachable set is a budgeted union of cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_k}\\) without AdaLoRA's per-module ceiling.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\Lambda,A)=(B\\Lambda,\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0); S_1={B_0 diag(delta) A_0} lies inside {XA_0}."},{"target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,\\Lambda,A)\\mapsto(P,\\Lambda,Q)","note":"Same P Lambda Q object at Lambda_0=0, pointed at the same frames; the two differ only in the rank schedule (grow vs prune)."},{"target":"increlora","rule":"A5","h":"h(q)=\\big(q,\\ (b_{\\rm new},0,a_{\\rm new})\\big)","note":"Adding a component at lambda=0 embeds stage r_k into stage r_k+1."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"alora-allocating","name":"ALoRA","full_name":"Allocating Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"ALoRA: Allocating Low-Rank Adaptation for Fine-tuning Large Language Models","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NAACL 2024","arxiv":"2403.16187","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16187","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=W_0x+B_mG_mA_mx,\\ G_m=\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha_{m,1},\\dots,\\alpha_{m,r_m}),\\ \\alpha_{m,i}\\in\\{0,1\\}\\ (\\text{init }1,\\text{ not trained});\\quad \\mathrm{IS}(i)=S(\\mathcal{M})-S(\\mathcal{M}_{\\setminus i})+S(\\mathcal{M}_{i}),\\ S=-\\mathrm{CE}\\ \\text{on a validation batch};\\ \\text{prune the }n_A\\text{ lowest-IS ranks, give }n_A\\text{ new ranks to un-pruned modules, retrain }K_2\\text{ epochs, repeat }N_A\\text{ times}","trainable_params":"r_m(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per module, with}\\ \\textstyle\\sum_m r_m\\ \\text{fixed}","locus":"any linear layer; ranks moved between modules","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W=B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)\\,A\\) with binary per-rank gates; rank-1 components with low ablation-based importance are zeroed and the same number of new ranks is appended to modules that lost none, so \\(\\sum_m r_m\\) stays at \\(R_{target}\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After allocation, each \\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)\\,A\\) is a plain low-rank matrix.","identity_at_init":"Gates are all 1 at initialization; each module starts at \\(r_m=R_{target}/N_{mod}\\). The LoRA factors follow the usual LoRA initialization (the paper gives no separate scheme), and newly added gates are initialized to 1.","key_idea":"Estimate each rank-1 component's importance by ablation (performance with it removed and with it alone) on a validation batch, prune the useless ones, and hand their rank budget to modules that need it.","results_note":"Reported outperforming LoRA, SoRA and AdaLoRA at comparable budgets on GLUE/SuperGLUE tasks, E2E and instruction tuning with LLaMA-2-7B.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"ablation-based instead of sensitivity-based importance"},{"target":"sora","type":"competes-with","note":"both gate rank-1 components"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16187","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.16187","https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.35/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Zequan Liu","Jiawen Lyn","Wei Zhu","Xing Tian","Yvette Graham"],"bibkey":"liu2024alora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2024alora,\n  title = {{ALoRA: Allocating Low-Rank Adaptation for Fine-tuning Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Zequan Liu and Jiawen Lyn and Wei Zhu and Xing Tian and Yvette Graham},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2403.16187},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16187}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Plain LoRA with frozen binary gates; the contribution is the ablation-based reallocation of rank between modules.","shape":"Budgeted union of LoRA cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_m}\\) with \\(\\sum_m r_m\\) fixed.","base_point":"Standard LoRA init (\\(B_0=0\\)), and new components enter with \\(B\\)-column 0: apex.","gauge":"Gates are not trained, so each module has \\(GL_{r_m}\\); the ablation score is invariant under the torus but not under \\(GL_r\\), since the split of \\(BA\\) into rank-one terms is gauge-dependent.","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ALoRA trains plain LoRA with frozen binary gates, \\(\\rho=W_0+B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha)A\\), and moves between objects of the rank filtration by pruning and re-granting rank-one components: its reachable set is a budgeted union of LoRA cones at fixed total rank. Its ablation score is a function of the rank-one split of \\(BA\\), which the \\(GL_r\\) gauge does not fix.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha),\\ A)","note":"At each allocation, pointed at (0,A_0); with all gates 1 it is the identity."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"ALoRA starts at B=0 (dim S_1=m r_m), AdaLoRA at Lambda=0 (dim S_1=r): no pointed arrow, although both reallocate rank."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"autolora","name":"AutoLoRA","full_name":"Automatic rank tuning for LoRA via meta-learning","paper_title":"AutoLoRA: Automatically Tuning Matrix Ranks in Low-Rank Adaptation Based on Meta Learning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NAACL 2024","arxiv":"2403.09113","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09113","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W_\\ell=\\textstyle\\sum_{j=1}^{k_\\ell}\\alpha_\\ell^j\\,b_\\ell^j a_\\ell^{j\\top},\\quad \\alpha_\\ell^j=\\frac{\\exp\\beta_\\ell^j}{\\sum_i\\exp\\beta_\\ell^i};\\quad \\min_\\beta\\mathcal{L}_{val}\\big(W-\\eta\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}_{train}(W,\\beta)\\big);\\quad r_\\ell=\\#\\{j:\\alpha_\\ell^j\\ge 1/k_\\ell\\}","trainable_params":"k_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{during search};\\ r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{after retraining}","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"weighted sum of rank-1 components with softmax selection variables learned by bi-level (train / validation) optimization; thresholding selects the rank, then the adapter is retrained","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The retrained rank-\\(r_\\ell\\) update is a plain low-rank matrix.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA-style zero-product initialization of the rank-1 components (not separately specified beyond LoRA).","key_idea":"Treat the per-layer rank as a hyperparameter to be meta-learned: selection weights on rank-1 components are fit on validation loss through a one-step-unrolled bi-level problem, then thresholded.","results_note":"Reported GLUE average 85.5 vs LoRA 84.9 and AdaLoRA 85.0 with RoBERTa-base at about 0.3M parameters.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"meta-learned instead of sensitivity-based rank selection"},{"target":"dylora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative way to avoid manual rank search"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09113","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.09113","https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.282/"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Ruiyi Zhang","Rushi Qiang","Sai Ashish Somayajula","Pengtao Xie"],"bibkey":"zhang2024autolora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024autolora,\n  title = {{AutoLoRA: Automatically Tuning Matrix Ranks in Low-Rank Adaptation Based on Meta Learning}},\n  author = {Ruiyi Zhang and Rushi Qiang and Sai Ashish Somayajula and Pengtao Xie},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2403.09113},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.09113}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Recipe: the deliverable is plain LoRA\\(_{r_l}\\) retrained at meta-learned ranks; coordinates are LoRA's.","gauge":"\\(GL_{r_l}\\) for the retrained LoRA. In the search phase the softmax weights \\(\\alpha_j>0\\) can be absorbed into \\(b_j\\), so \\(\\beta\\) lies entirely in the fibre of \\(\\rho\\).","base_point":"LoRA-style zero-product init: apex."},"categorical_reading":"AutoLoRA is a recipe whose output is LoRA\\(_{r_\\ell}\\) with meta-learned ranks; during the search \\(\\rho=W_0+\\sum_j\\mathrm{softmax}(\\beta)_jb_ja_j^\\top\\), and since each \\(\\mathrm{softmax}(\\beta)_j>0\\) can be absorbed into \\(b_j\\), the selection variables \\(\\beta\\) lie entirely in the fibre of \\(\\rho\\). They act only through the cometric (as per-component learning rates), so the thresholded rank depends on the gauge representative the bi-level dynamics picks, not on the update.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,a,\\beta)=\\big([\\mathrm{softmax}(\\beta)_jb_j]_j,\\ [a_j^\\top]_j\\big)","note":"Search-phase object into LoRA_k pointed at (0,A_0); after thresholding and retraining the recipe's output is LoRA_{r_l} itself (h=id)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"elalora","name":"ElaLoRA","full_name":"Elastic & Learnable Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"ElaLoRA: Elastic & Learnable Low-Rank Adaptation for Efficient Model Fine-Tuning","authors":"Chang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2504.00254","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00254","code_url":"https://github.com/HuandongChang/ElaLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+P\\Lambda Q;\\quad S_i=s(\\lambda_i)+\\tfrac1{d_{out}}\\textstyle\\sum_js(P_{ji})+\\tfrac1{d_{in}}\\sum_js(Q_{ij}),\\ s(w)=|w\\,\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial w|;\\ \\text{every }t_{adjust}\\text{ steps prune the }b^{(t)}\\text{ globally least important ranks and expand }b^{(t)}\\text{ ranks in matrices whose weakest ranks are most important},\\ b^{(t)}=\\mathrm{round}\\big(b(1-P^3)\\big)","trainable_params":"r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out}+1),\\ \\textstyle\\sum_\\ell r_\\ell\\ \\text{held at the budget}","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"AdaLoRA-style SVD-shaped update whose per-matrix rank can both shrink and grow during training; new ranks are Gram-Schmidt orthogonalized","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(P\\Lambda Q\\) is a dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Lambda=0\\) as in AdaLoRA (confirmed in the official loralib/elalora.py); expanded ranks initialized by Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization.","key_idea":"Make rank allocation elastic: alongside AdaLoRA-style pruning, re-expand ranks where importance indicates under-capacity, with a cubic schedule that tapers the reallocation.","results_note":"Reported GLUE average (DeBERTaV3-base) 88.01 at \\(r=2\\) vs AdaLoRA 87.39 at \\(r=2\\) and above AdaLoRA at \\(r=4\\) (87.89), plus gains on XSum (BART-base) and VTAB-1k (ViT-B/16).","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"reuses AdaLoRA's P\\Lambda Q parameterization, orthogonality regularizer and sensitivity-uncertainty importance score, and adds rank expansion"},{"target":"increlora","type":"competes-with","note":"both can grow ranks"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00254","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.00254v1","https://github.com/HuandongChang/ElaLoRA"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Huandong Chang","Zicheng Ma","Mingyuan Ma","Zhenting Qi","Andrew Sabot","Hong Jiang","H. T. Kung"],"bibkey":"chang2025elalora","bibtex":"@article{chang2025elalora,\n  title = {{ElaLoRA: Elastic \\& Learnable Low-Rank Adaptation for Efficient Model Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Huandong Chang and Zicheng Ma and Mingyuan Ma and Zhenting Qi and Andrew Sabot and Hong Jiang and H. T. Kung},\n  year = {2025},\n  eprint = {2504.00254},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.00254},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00254}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Budgeted union of cones (\\(P\\Lambda Q\\)) whose per-matrix rank can shrink and grow.","base_point":"\\(\\Lambda_0=0\\) (official loralib): \\(\\dim S_1=r\\), apex, as AdaLoRA.","gauge":"Same \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) map as AdaLoRA: generic fibre \\(r^2+r\\) beyond the two torus edges (non-linear absorption).","rebasing":"\\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ElaLoRA is AdaLoRA's \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) object at \\(\\Lambda=0\\) with an elastic schedule that both prunes (A2 sub-objects) and expands (A5 stages, Gram–Schmidt-orthogonalised new frames at \\(\\lambda=0\\)) inside the rank filtration; it shares AdaLoRA's coordinates, with the gauge recorded as non-linear because the generic fibre \\(r^2+r\\) exceeds the torus.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same P Lambda Q object pointed at Lambda=0 with the same frames; only the rank schedule differs."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at (0,Q_0)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-drop","name":"LoRA-drop","full_name":"LoRA-drop (output-based LoRA parameter pruning)","paper_title":"LoRA-drop: Efficient LoRA Parameter Pruning based on Output Evaluation","authors":"Zhou et al.","year":2024,"venue":"COLING 2025","arxiv":"2402.07721","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.371/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"g_i=\\textstyle\\sum_{x\\in D_s}\\lVert\\Delta W_ix_i\\rVert_2^2,\\quad I_i=g_i/\\sum_jg_j;\\quad \\text{keep individual }B_iA_i\\text{ for the top layers until }\\sum I_i\\ge T,\\ \\text{all other layers share one }B_sA_s:\\ \\ h_i=W_{0,i}x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B_sA_sx","trainable_params":"(n_{keep}+1)\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted weight type (vs. }L\\,r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\text{ for LoRA)}","locus":"LoRA modules across layers; unimportant layers share one adapter","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"layer-wise allocation: important layers keep their own rank-\\(r\\) \\(\\Delta W_i=B_iA_i\\), the rest are tied to a single shared rank-\\(r\\) update","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each layer's (own or shared) \\(BA\\) folds into its \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B=0\\)); importance measured after a few warm-up steps on about 10% of the data.","key_idea":"Judge each layer's LoRA by the actual magnitude of its output on data rather than by parameter statistics, and stop paying for separate adapters in layers whose output contribution is small.","results_note":"Reported GLUE average 86.2 vs LoRA 86.1 and full fine-tuning 86.4 on RoBERTa-base while keeping about 50% of the LoRA parameters.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"layer-level, output-based allocation instead of rank-level sensitivity pruning"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"same adapters, with cross-layer parameter sharing"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.07721","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.07721","https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.371/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Hongyun Zhou","Xiangyu Lu","Wang Xu","Conghui Zhu","Tiejun Zhao","Muyun Yang"],"bibkey":"zhou2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhou2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-drop: Efficient LoRA Parameter Pruning based on Output Evaluation}},\n  author = {Hongyun Zhou and Xiangyu Lu and Wang Xu and Conghui Zhu and Tiejun Zhao and Muyun Yang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Computational Linguistics (COLING)},\n  eprint = {2402.07721},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.coling-main.371/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Product of per-layer LoRA cones on the kept layers and one LoRA cone embedded diagonally (same \\(\\Delta W\\)) on the shared layers.","base_point":"Standard LoRA init (\\(B=0\\)): apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) per adapter; one \\(GL_r\\) for the shared adapter.","covariance":"Per box \\(GL\\times GL\\), but the shared adapter violates the no-sharing hypothesis of Obs II.15: covariance needs the same \\((g,h)\\) on all tied boxes.","rebasing":"\\(\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\,C\\): on the shared layers this is the diagonal subspace \\(\\{\\Delta W_j \\text{ equal for all shared } j\\}\\), not \\(\\Theta\\), if the sharing pattern is frozen."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-drop composes per-layer LoRA on the important layers with a single LoRA pulled back along the diagonal \\(\\Theta_{\\rm box}\\to\\prod_{j\\in\\rm share}\\Theta_j\\): tying is a diagonal monomorphism into the product of per-layer LoRAs. Under merge-and-restart with the sharing pattern frozen the shared layers move together, so \\(R_\\infty\\) is the diagonal subspace on them, not \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_i,A_i)_{\\rm keep},(B_s,A_s)\\big)=\\big((B_i,A_i)_{\\rm keep},(B_s,A_s)_{j\\in\\rm share}\\big)","note":"Diagonal (tying) map into per-layer LoRA on all layers, pointed when every shared layer carries the same A_0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"tied-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Kept layers carry independent rank-r updates with their own row and column spaces, which a model-wide tied A,B with per-layer scalings cannot produce."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"dora-dynamic-rank","name":"DoRA (dynamic rank)","full_name":"Dynamic Low-Rank Adaptation (Mao et al.; not the weight-decomposed DoRA)","paper_title":"DoRA: Enhancing Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Dynamic Rank Distribution","authors":"Mao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2405.17357","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.626/","code_url":"https://github.com/MIkumikumi0116/DoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+\\textstyle\\sum_{i=1}^{r'}c_i\\,b_ia_i^\\top,\\ b_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}},a_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}};\\quad s_i=\\frac{\\lVert c_ib_ia_i^\\top\\rVert_F}{\\lVert\\sum_jc_jb_ja_j^\\top\\rVert_F}\\ \\text{(EMA-smoothed)};\\ \\text{prune by }c_i\\leftarrow0\\ \\text{under a cubic budget }b^{(0)}=r'\\to b^{(T)}=r;\\quad R_{DEM}=\\tfrac1n\\textstyle\\sum_i(\\mathrm{Var}(a_i)+\\mathrm{Var}(b_i))","trainable_params":"r'(d_{in}+d_{out}+1)\\ \\text{during training},\\ r'\\approx1.5\\,r;\\ \\ r_\\ell(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{retained}","locus":"any linear layer; budget shared across matrices","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sum of scaled rank-1 components pruned by their Frobenius-norm share of the layer update; pruned components can revive","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The retained sum of rank-1 terms is a dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(c_i=0\\) (\\(a_i,b_i\\) Kaiming-initialized).","key_idea":"Decompose each LoRA layer into single-rank components and prune by how much each actually contributes to the layer's update norm, with a variance regularizer that keeps component entries balanced.","results_note":"Reported outperforming LoRA by 0.84-0.88 and AdaLoRA by 0.45-0.59 points on GLUE, plus SQuAD and XSum results with RoBERTa and BART, at under 0.3% trainable parameters.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"same cubic budget schedule, norm-based component importance using both factors"},{"target":"increlora","type":"competes-with","note":"rank-1 component allocation"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17357","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.17357","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.626/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"authors_full":["Yulong Mao","Kaiyu Huang","Changhao Guan","Ganglin Bao","Fengran Mo","Jinan Xu"],"bibkey":"mao2024dora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mao2024dora,\n  title = {{DoRA: Enhancing Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Dynamic Rank Distribution}},\n  author = {Yulong Mao and Kaiyu Huang and Changhao Guan and Ganglin Bao and Fengran Mo and Jinan Xu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2405.17357},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.626/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Budgeted union of cones \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r_l}\\) under a cubic schedule \\(r'\\to r\\); pruned components can revive.","base_point":"\\(c_0=0\\) with random \\(a_i,b_i\\): only \\(c\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4), \\(\\dim S_1=r'\\) (checked).","gauge":"\\(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,A\\): fibre \\(r'^2+r'\\) beyond the torus; the variance regulariser \\(R_{\\rm DEM}\\) fixes the torus scale (a gauge fixing).","covariance":"Image \\(GL\\times GL\\); the importance \\(\\|c_i b_i a_i^\\top\\|_F\\) is torus-invariant but not invariant under the full fibre."},"categorical_reading":"DoRA (dynamic rank) is the map \\(W_0+B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)A\\) pointed at \\(c=0\\) with random \\(B_0,A_0\\) (only \\(c\\) moves to first order, \\(\\dim S_1=r'\\)), pruned along a cubic budget; it has nothing to do with weight-decomposed DoRA. Its importance \\(\\|c_ib_ia_i^\\top\\|_F\\) is invariant under the torus slides but not under the whole fibre, since the rank-one split of the update is not unique.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,c,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c),\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0), stratum T_A."},{"target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same P Lambda Q object pointed at the zero gate with the same frames."},{"target":"sora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,c,A)=\\big(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g_0)^{-1},\\ g_0,\\ A\\big)","note":"Into SoRA pointed at (0,g_0,A_0) with the same A_0; S_1={B_0 diag(delta) A_0} lies inside {XA_0}. The reverse fails (O3)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-rite","name":"LoRA-RITE","full_name":"Robust Invariant Transformation Equilibration for LoRA","paper_title":"LoRA Done RITE: Robust Invariant Transformation Equilibration for LoRA Optimization","authors":"Yen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.20625","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625","code_url":"https://github.com/gkevinyen5418/LoRA-RITE","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"A^{\\top}=U_AR_A\\ (\\text{polar}),\\ \\ \\bar\\nabla_B = G\\,U_A = \\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,R_A^{\\dagger},\\ \\ \\bar V_B \\leftarrow P\\,\\bar V_B P^{\\top} + \\tfrac{1}{d_{out}}\\bar\\nabla_B^{\\top}\\bar\\nabla_B,\\ \\ B \\leftarrow B - \\eta\\,\\bar\\nabla_B\\,(\\bar V_B+\\rho I_r)^{-1/2}(R_A^{\\top})^{\\dagger}\\ \\ (\\text{symmetric rule for } A;\\ P=U_{A,t}^{\\top}U_{A,t-1};\\ \\rho\\ \\text{accumulated escaped mass};\\ \\text{first moment omitted})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Adaptive one-sided \\(r\\times r\\) matrix preconditioning of 'unmagnified' gradients expressed in orthonormal bases of the factors; moments are transported between successive bases and an 'escaped mass' \\(\\rho I\\) compensates lost second-moment mass. The resulting \\(W\\)-update is exactly invariant under \\((B,A)\\mapsto(BR,R^{-1}A),\\ R\\in GL(r)\\) (transformation invariance).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the optimizer changes; the adapter remains a mergeable LoRA product.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)); the update uses pseudo-inverses \\(R^\\dagger\\) so rank-deficient factors are allowed.","key_idea":"Gradient descent, Adam, Adagrad, RMSProp and Shampoo are not even scalar-scale invariant on LoRA factors (LoRA+ inherits this), and ScaledAdam loses the transformation invariance of Riemannian GD; RITE builds an Adam-like adaptive matrix-preconditioned optimizer whose \\(W\\)-update depends only on \\(BA\\), at roughly Adam's cost for small \\(r\\).","results_note":"Replacing Adam with LoRA-RITE for LoRA tuning of Gemma-2B gives +4.6% accuracy on Super-Natural Instructions and +3.5% averaged over HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K and OpenBookQA; with Gemma-7B-IT it reaches 55.50% on GSM8K vs 48.37% for Adam.","relations":[{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Riemannian GD is transformation invariant but ScaledAdam (with element-wise Adam) is not; RITE restores invariance with adaptivity"},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"both address one factor dominating optimization"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"optimizer-only change"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.20625","https://openreview.net/forum?id=VpWki1v2P8"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Jui-Nan Yen","Si Si","Zhao Meng","Felix Yu","Sai Surya Duvvuri","Inderjit S. Dhillon","Cho-Jui Hsieh","Sanjiv Kumar"],"bibkey":"yen2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yen2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Done RITE: Robust Invariant Transformation Equilibration for LoRA Optimization}},\n  author = {Jui-Nan Yen and Si Si and Zhao Meng and Felix Yu and Sai Surya Duvvuri and Inderjit S. Dhillon and Cho-Jui Hsieh and Sanjiv Kumar},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.20625},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Optimizer-only change on LoRA's \\(\\rho\\).","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). RITE states only \\(W\\)-level invariance under \\((BR,R^{-1}A)\\) (its Definition 1), but its update is \\(GL_r\\)-equivariant at the parameter level on the full-rank locus, so it descends to \\(W\\) (Thm III.12, published variants)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-RITE is a trained-method structure on LoRA whose \\(W\\)-update factors through the quotient by \\(GL_r\\): adaptive matrix-preconditioned moments are expressed in orthonormal bases of the factors and transported between bases. It thus achieves descent of the update, which Adam's \\(B_r\\) shadow of the gauge lacks.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule differs."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-pro","name":"LoRA-Pro","full_name":"LoRA-Pro (equivalent-gradient matching)","paper_title":"LoRA-Pro: Are Low-Rank Adapters Properly Optimized?","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2407.18242","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18242","code_url":"https://github.com/mrflogs/LoRA-Pro","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\tilde g := sBg^{A}+sg^{B}A\\approx G:\\quad g^{A}=\\tfrac{1}{s^{2}}(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L}+XA,\\quad g^{B}=\\tfrac{1}{s^{2}}\\big[I-B(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}B^{\\top}\\big]\\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,(AA^{\\top})^{-1}-BX,\\quad B^{\\top}BX+XAA^{\\top}=-\\tfrac{1}{s^{2}}(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L}\\,A^{\\top}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Gradient surgery on the fixed-rank manifold: the 'equivalent gradient' becomes \\(\\tilde g = P_BG+(I-P_B)GP_A\\), the orthogonal projection of the full gradient \\(G\\) onto the tangent space of rank-\\(r\\) matrices at \\(BA\\) (closed-form minimiser of \\(\\|\\tilde g-G\\|_F\\)); the residual \\(r\\times r\\) gauge freedom \\(X\\) is fixed by a Sylvester equation keeping \\((g^A,g^B)\\) closest to the vanilla LoRA gradients. The AdamW variant keeps moments of the full-size equivalent gradient.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only the optimizer changes; the result is a standard LoRA adapter.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA init (\\(B = 0\\), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\)). The closed form assumes full-rank \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the paper observes that both factors become full rank after the first update step, so the assumption fails only at step 0. LLM experiments use rsLoRA scaling \\(s = \\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\).","key_idea":"Training LoRA is equivalent to full fine-tuning with a low-rank 'equivalent gradient' \\(sBg^A + sg^BA\\); choosing \\(g^A\\), \\(g^B\\) to make this as close as possible to the true full gradient (closed form, guaranteed loss decrease) narrows the gap to full fine-tuning.","results_note":"On a GLUE subset with T5-base LoRA-Pro beats standard LoRA by 6.36 points on average (above full fine-tuning), and on Llama-2-7B it beats LoRA-GA by 2.85 (GSM8K) and 6.45 (HumanEval) at rank 32, at ~4 GB more GPU memory than LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both precondition LoRA gradients with Gram inverses: scaled GD yields P_BG+GP_A (double-counting P_BGP_A), LoRA-Pro the exact tangent-space projection P_BG+(I-P_B)GP_A; neither update contains the other as a special case"},{"target":"lora-ga","type":"competes-with","note":"both align LoRA with full fine-tuning gradients: LoRA-GA only at initialization, LoRA-Pro at every step"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"both approximate full-gradient training with low-rank projected updates"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18242","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.18242","https://openreview.net/forum?id=gTwRMU3lJ5"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Zhengbo Wang","Jian Liang","Ran He","Zilei Wang","Tieniu Tan"],"bibkey":"wang2024lorac","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024lorac,\n  title = {{LoRA-Pro: Are Low-Rank Adapters Properly Optimized?}},\n  author = {Zhengbo Wang and Jian Liang and Ran He and Zilei Wang and Tieniu Tan},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2407.18242},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.18242}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Optimizer-only: equivalent gradient \\(\\tilde g=P_UG+(I-P_U)GP_V=\\Pi_{T\\mathcal M_r}G\\) (Prop III.4(b)).","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). The SGD variant is Riemannian gradient flow on \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_r\\); the published step with the Sylvester-optimal \\(X\\) is only \\(O(r)\\)-equivariant at the parameter level."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Pro chooses the cometric \\(K=\\eta\\,\\Pi_{T_{W-W_0}\\mathcal M_r}\\), the Frobenius-orthogonal projection of the full gradient onto the tangent space of the rank-\\(r\\) stratum, i.e. Riemannian gradient flow on LoRA's image. Its residual \\(r\\times r\\) freedom \\(X\\) is a choice of lift through the gauge fibre.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"Both approximate the full gradient, but a GaLore period has image \\(\\{P_tX\\}\\) of dimension \\(rn<r(m+n-r)\\), so it cannot simulate LoRA-Pro's (LoRA's) image."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"relora","name":"ReLoRA","full_name":"Restarted LoRA (high-rank training through low-rank updates)","paper_title":"ReLoRA: High-Rank Training Through Low-Rank Updates","authors":"Lialin et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2307.05695","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05695","code_url":"https://github.com/Guitaricet/relora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{(k)} = W^{(k-1)} + s\\,B_kA_k\\ \\ (\\text{merge every } T \\text{ steps}),\\qquad \\Delta W_{\\text{total}}=\\sum_{k=1}^{N} s\\,B_kA_k,\\ \\ \\mathrm{rank}\\le Nr;\\quad \\text{after each merge: } B_k\\leftarrow 0,\\ A_k\\ \\text{re-init},\\ \\text{prune most Adam moments, LR re-warm-up}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix at any time (after a short full-rank warm start when training from scratch); in the paper's pre-training runs embeddings and normalization layers are also trained full-rank}","locus":"attention and MLP linear layers of Transformer LMs during (pre-)training","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Sum of \\(N\\) rank-\\(r\\) increments accumulated by periodic merge-and-reinit: the total update lies in \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r}+\\cdots+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}=\\mathcal M_{\\le Nr}\\) while each phase optimizes only a rank-\\(r\\) chart; uses a jagged cosine schedule and magnitude-pruned (~99%) optimizer state at each restart.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"LoRA factors are merged into the dense weights at every restart and at the end, leaving a plain dense model.","identity_at_init":"Each restart re-initializes \\(B = 0\\) (\\(A\\) Kaiming) so merge-and-reinit leaves the function unchanged; from-scratch training needs a full-rank warm start (e.g. 5k steps) before switching to ReLoRA.","key_idea":"Since \\(\\mathrm{rank}(X+Y)\\) can exceed \\(\\mathrm{rank}(X)\\), repeatedly merging low-rank updates into the weights and restarting the adapter yields a high-rank total update with low-rank memory; partial optimizer reset and LR re-warm-up stop the new adapter from relearning the old subspace.","results_note":"Pre-training Transformer LMs up to 1.3B parameters on C4, ReLoRA performs comparably to full-rank training while saving up to 5.5 GB RAM per GPU and speeding up training by 9-40% depending on model size and hardware.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA plus periodic merge-and-restart"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"both target memory-efficient high-rank (pre-)training via low-rank updates"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05695","https://arxiv.org/html/2307.05695"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Vladislav Lialin","Namrata Shivagunde","Sherin Muckatira","Anna Rumshisky"],"bibkey":"lialin2023relora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lialin2023relora,\n  title = {{ReLoRA: High-Rank Training Through Low-Rank Updates}},\n  author = {Vladislav Lialin and Namrata Shivagunde and Sherin Muckatira and Anna Rumshisky},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2307.05695},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05695}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A merge-and-restart scheme of LoRA\\(_r\\) stages: \\(B_k\\leftarrow0\\), \\(A_k\\) re-initialised, optimizer state pruned, learning rate re-warmed.","shape":"Each stage is the cone \\(W_k+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) at the merged weight.","base_point":"Each stage starts at the apex of its cone.","rebasing":"\\(R_K=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(Kr,m,n)}\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 1): full FT at \\(K^*=\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ReLoRA is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus K}\\) unrolled in time: each restart re-points the cone at the merged weight, and the reach is the \\(K\\)-fold Minkowski sum of the cone, the rank-\\(Kr\\) variety. It is the additive twin of re-HRA.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"relora","rule":"A5","h":"(q_1,\\dots,q_K)\\mapsto(q_1,\\dots,q_K,\\,q_0^{(K+1)})","note":"ReLoRA\\(^K\\hookrightarrow\\) ReLoRA\\(^{K+1}\\), padding with the next stage's base value."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((B_k,A_k)_k)=([B_1\\cdots B_K],\\,[A_1;\\dots;A_K])","note":"\\(K\\) stages factor through LoRA\\(_{Kr}\\) (Obs I.7); pointed when the stacked \\(A_0\\)'s have independent rows."},{"target":"cola","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"Stagewise identical objects at equal link ranks; COLA differs only in its full optimizer reset and schedule."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"A ReLoRA stage is a two-sided cone of dimension \\(r(m+n-r)\\); a GaLore period is the one-sided linear \\(\\{P_tX\\}\\) of dimension \\(rn\\)."},{"target":"delta-lora","test":"O2","reason":"For \\(K\\ge3\\), ReLoRA\\(^K\\) reaches rank \\(\\min(Kr,m,n)\\), while Delta-LoRA's total update has rank at most \\(2r\\) (its partial merges telescope)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"delta-lora","name":"Delta-LoRA","full_name":"Delta-LoRA (propagating the low-rank delta into the pretrained weight)","paper_title":"Delta-LoRA: Fine-Tuning High-Rank Parameters with the Delta of Low-Rank Matrices","authors":"Zi et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2309.02411","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02411","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h = W^{(t)}x+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B^{(t)}A^{(t)}x;\\quad (A,B)\\ \\text{updated by AdamW};\\quad W^{(t+1)} = W^{(t)} + \\lambda\\,\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\big(B^{(t+1)}A^{(t+1)}-B^{(t)}A^{(t)}\\big)\\ \\ \\text{for } t> K;\\ \\ \\text{no dropout on the LoRA branch}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{with optimizer state; all } d_{out}d_{in} \\text{ entries of } W \\text{ are also modified (no gradient or moments stored for } W)","locus":"same as LoRA (paper: GPT-2, BART, RoBERTa weight matrices)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Every step after \\(K\\) adds \\(\\lambda\\) times the increment of the rank-\\(r\\) product (itself of rank \\(\\le 2r\\)) to the base weight, so \\(W\\) accumulates an unbounded-rank sum of low-rank increments; the increment acts as a surrogate for \\(-\\eta\\,\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial W\\), which is exact only without dropout in the branch.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The final model is the dense weight \\(W^{(T)} + (\\alpha/r) B A\\), but the pretrained weight itself is overwritten, so the adaptation cannot be stored or swapped as a small detachable adapter.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) and \\(W\\)-updates only start after \\(K\\) steps, so training starts from the pretrained function.","key_idea":"Reuse the change of the low-rank product between consecutive steps as a free (no gradient, no optimizer state) update to the full pretrained weight, giving high-rank adaptation at LoRA's memory cost.","results_note":"Outperforms LoRA, DyLoRA and AdaLoRA in the paper's experiments on E2E and WebNLG (GPT-2 medium), XSum (BART-large) and GLUE (RoBERTa-base).","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA branch plus a delta-driven update of W"},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"both push high-rank updates into W; Delta-LoRA does it continuously every step rather than by periodic merges"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02411","https://arxiv.org/html/2309.02411","https://openreview.net/forum?id=FAO4VS9QRV"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Bojia Zi","Xianbiao Qi","Lingzhi Wang","Jianan Wang","Kam-Fai Wong","Lei Zhang"],"bibkey":"zi2023delta","bibtex":"@article{zi2023delta,\n  title = {{Delta-LoRA: Fine-Tuning High-Rank Parameters with the Delta of Low-Rank Matrices}},\n  author = {Bojia Zi and Xianbiao Qi and Lingzhi Wang and Jianan Wang and Kam-Fai Wong and Lei Zhang},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2309.02411},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2309.02411},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02411}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A schedule: after step \\(K\\) the base \\(W\\) is rewritten at every step by \\(\\lambda\\) times the increment of \\(sBA\\). These partial merges telescope, \\(W^{(t)}=W_0+\\lambda s(B_tA_t-B_KA_K)\\), so the effective map is \\(W_0-\\lambda sB_KA_K+(1+\\lambda)sBA\\): a single re-pointing at step \\(K\\), with scale \\((1+\\lambda)s\\) and a detached backward.","shape":"A cone per stage; the overall reach is \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le2r}\\).","base_point":"Zero-init LoRA for \\(t\\le K\\) (apex at \\(\\theta_0\\)). The second stage starts at a smooth point of the translated cone with vertex \\(W_0-\\lambda sB_KA_K\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on \\((B,A)\\) in each stage.","merge":"The final weight \\(W^{(T)}+sBA\\) is dense, but by telescoping the total update \\((1+\\lambda)sBA-\\lambda sB_KA_K\\) is a rank-\\(\\le2r\\) LoRA, so it can be shipped as a detachable adapter (contrary to the catalog's reason).","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme with period 1, but the base increments are tied to the adapter's own increments, so the reach is only \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le2r}\\), ReLoRA's \\(R_2\\), not \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Delta-LoRA's per-step partial merges telescope, so it is a two-stage rebasing scheme of LoRA\\(_r\\): a zero-init stage, then one re-pointing at step \\(K\\) to \\((W_0-\\lambda sB_KA_K)+(1+\\lambda)s\\,\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), with a gradient detached by the factor \\(1/(1+\\lambda)\\). By Thm II.1 its total update has rank at most \\(2r\\), so the high-rank claim fails in exact arithmetic (checked numerically under AdamW).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([(1+\\lambda)B,\\ -\\lambda B_K],\\ [A;\\ A_K]\\big)","note":"After step \\(K\\) the effective map factors through LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (scale \\(s\\)), pointed at \\(([(1+\\lambda)B_K,-\\lambda B_K],[A_K;A_K])\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"At equal rank \\(r\\): the total update \\((1+\\lambda)sBA-\\lambda sB_KA_K\\) has rank up to \\(2r>r\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"cola","name":"COLA","full_name":"Chain of LoRA","paper_title":"Chain of LoRA: Efficient Fine-tuning of Language Models via Residual Learning","authors":"Xia et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2401.04151","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04151","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{(i)} = W_0 + \\sum_{j=1}^{i} B_jA_j,\\qquad (B_i,A_i) \\approx \\arg\\min_{B_i,A_i}\\ \\mathcal{L}\\big(W^{(i-1)} + B_iA_i\\big);\\quad \\text{then merge ('tie a knot'), re-init } B_{i+1}=0,\\ A_{i+1}\\sim\\mathcal{N},\\ \\text{reset optimizer}","trainable_params":"r_i(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{for the active link (ranks may decrease along the chain)}","locus":"same as LoRA (OPT-1.3B, Llama-2-7B)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Residual learning: a chain of \\(M\\) rank-\\(r_i\\) corrections, each fitted to the residual \\(\\Delta W^\\star-\\sum_{j<i}B_jA_j\\) on top of the merged weights; analysed as a (stochastic, non-convex) Frank-Wolfe / conditional-gradient method over low-rank atoms.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each link is merged into the frozen weights as training proceeds, so the final model is a dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Each new link starts with \\(B = 0\\) and Gaussian \\(A\\), so extending the chain does not change the function.","key_idea":"Approximate the optimal high-rank update by a Frank-Wolfe-like sequence of low-rank residual fits (tune LoRA, merge, extend the chain with a fresh LoRA and reset optimizer state), at LoRA's compute and memory.","results_note":"Across OPT-1.3B and Llama-2-7B on seven tasks COLA consistently beats LoRA, e.g. a 6.47% relative test-accuracy gain on WSC with OPT-1.3B and up to 4.4% relative gain with Llama-2-7B.","relations":[{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"same merge-and-reinit idea applied to fine-tuning, with full optimizer reset and a Frank-Wolfe convergence analysis"},{"target":"periodic-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same periodic-merge mechanism"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"chain of standard LoRA modules"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04151","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.04151"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Wenhan Xia","Chengwei Qin","Elad Hazan"],"bibkey":"xia2024chain","bibtex":"@article{xia2024chain,\n  title = {{Chain of LoRA: Efficient Fine-tuning of Language Models via Residual Learning}},\n  author = {Wenhan Xia and Chengwei Qin and Elad Hazan},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2401.04151},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2401.04151},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04151}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A Frank–Wolfe-style chain: tune LoRA\\(_{r_i}\\), merge, re-initialise (\\(B=0\\), Gaussian \\(A\\)), reset the optimizer.","base_point":"Each link starts at the apex of its cone.","rebasing":"\\(R_M=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(\\sum_ir_i,m,n)}\\), the Minkowski sum of the links' cones."},"categorical_reading":"COLA is the rebasing scheme \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1}\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_M}\\) unrolled in time with full optimizer resets. As an object it coincides with ReLoRA at equal ranks; its Frank–Wolfe reading concerns the trained-method data (which atom each stage picks), not expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"At equal link ranks; with decreasing ranks, pad each link by LoRA\\(_{r_i}\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA\\(_{r_1}\\)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((B_i,A_i)_i)=([B_1\\cdots B_M],\\,[A_1;\\dots;A_M])","note":"The chain factors through LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_i}\\) (Obs I.7)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"periodic-lora","name":"PeriodicLoRA","full_name":"PeriodicLoRA (PLoRA)","paper_title":"PeriodicLoRA: Breaking the Low-Rank Bottleneck in LoRA Optimization","authors":"Meng et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2402.16141","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16141","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_t = W_{t-1} + s\\,B_tA_t\\ \\ (\\text{unload at the end of stage } t),\\qquad W_T = W_0 + \\sum_{t=1}^{T} s\\,B_tA_t,\\ \\ \\mathrm{rank}\\le Tr;\\quad \\text{momentum unloading: merge } (1-m)\\,sB_tA_t \\text{ and keep } (B_t,A_t) \\text{ scaled by } m","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all attention and MLP linear layers (LLaMA-7B experiments)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Periodic merge-and-reinit (unloading point = 4.8k training examples per stage) accumulating a sum of rank-\\(r\\) terms, with a 'momentum' that only partially unloads the adapter to stabilize training. The paper describes momentum only in words ('scaling the \\(BA\\) product by \\((1-m)\\) and \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) matrices by \\(m\\)'); read literally the retained product is \\(m^2 sBA\\), so the unload is not exactly function-preserving.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Adapters are unloaded into the backbone during training; the final model is dense with the original shape.","identity_at_init":"Each stage re-initializes \\(B = 0\\), \\(A\\) Gaussian (when \\(m = 0\\)), together with optimizer and LR-scheduler state.","key_idea":"Unload LoRA weights into the backbone after every few hundred steps and restart them, so multiple low-rank updates accumulate into a higher-rank update at constant memory; a momentum-based partial unload prevents collapse across mini-batches.","results_note":"With LLaMA-7B instruction tuning PLoRA consistently outperforms LoRA at the same rank (reporting up to ~1.8x LoRA's 'learning ability') without extra memory.","relations":[{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"same merge-and-restart idea for fine-tuning; PLoRA adds momentum unloading"},{"target":"cola","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent periodic-merge method"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"periodically restarted LoRA"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16141","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.16141"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Xiangdi Meng","Damai Dai","Weiyao Luo","Zhe Yang","Shaoxiang Wu","Xiaochen Wang","Peiyi Wang","Qingxiu Dong","Liang Chen","Zhifang Sui"],"bibkey":"meng2024periodiclora","bibtex":"@article{meng2024periodiclora,\n  title = {{PeriodicLoRA: Breaking the Low-Rank Bottleneck in LoRA Optimization}},\n  author = {Xiangdi Meng and Damai Dai and Weiyao Luo and Zhe Yang and Shaoxiang Wu and Xiaochen Wang and Peiyi Wang and Qingxiu Dong and Liang Chen and Zhifang Sui},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2402.16141},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.16141},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16141}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Periodic unload-and-restart of LoRA, with an optional momentum \\(m\\) that unloads only part of the adapter.","base_point":"The first stage is zero-init LoRA (apex at \\(\\theta_0\\)). With \\(m>0\\) each restart starts at \\((mB,mA)\\), a rank-\\(r\\) point of the cone, and the literal unload (merge \\((1-m)sBA\\), keep \\(m^2sBA\\)) has pointing defect \\(-m(1-m)sBA\\) per restart.","rebasing":"Reach \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(Tr,m,n)}\\), as for ReLoRA: the defects have rank \\(\\le r\\) and stay inside the Minkowski sum."},"categorical_reading":"PeriodicLoRA is ReLoRA's rebasing scheme with a different restart map. At \\(m=0\\) it is ReLoRA; momentum unloading replaces the apex restart by a re-pointing at a smooth point of the cone, which as written does not preserve the function. Its reach is unchanged: the rank-\\(Tr\\) variety.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"At momentum \\(m=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"relora","test":"O3","reason":"For \\(m>0\\) a restart starts at a rank-\\(r\\) point, not the apex, and carries the defect \\(-m(1-m)sBA\\); the stage objects lie in a different stratum and are not pointed at the merged weight."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-dropout","name":"LoRA Dropout","full_name":"LoRA Dropout (sparsity-regularizing dropout on LoRA factors with test-time ensembling)","paper_title":"LoRA Dropout as a Sparsity Regularizer for Overfitting Control","authors":"Lin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2404.09610","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.09610","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + s\\,\\hat B\\hat A,\\quad \\hat A = A\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_A),\\ \\hat B = \\mathrm{diag}(m_B)\\,B,\\quad m_A\\in\\{0,1\\}^{d_{in}},\\ m_B\\in\\{0,1\\}^{d_{out}}\\sim\\mathrm{Bern}(1-p);\\quad \\text{test time: } \\bar o(x)=\\tfrac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\mathcal{M}\\big(x;\\,W_0+s\\hat B^{(n)}\\hat A^{(n)}\\big)","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA; also applied to AdaLoRA's \\(P\\), \\(Q\\) factors","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Bernoulli masks on the input columns of \\(A\\) and output rows of \\(B\\) (not on the rank dimension), so each entry of the product is zeroed with probability \\(2p-p^2\\); analysed as sparsity-regularized empirical risk minimization with a stability-based generalization bound, plus a Monte-Carlo test-time ensemble.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"Without test-time ensembling the trained \\(BA\\) merges like LoRA; the proposed test-time ensemble needs several stochastic forward passes and cannot be folded into a single weight.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)).","key_idea":"Even with few trainable parameters LoRA overfits; randomly dropping input/output coordinates of the LoRA factors acts as a sparsity regularizer that trades empirical risk against adaptation complexity, and averaging over dropout masks at test time tightens the bound further.","results_note":"Improves accuracy and calibration over LoRA/AdaLoRA on GLUE and SQuAD (DeBERTaV3-base) and on Alpaca instruction tuning of LLaMA2-7B in the paper's experiments.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"regularizer on the LoRA factors"},{"target":"adalora","type":"combines","note":"the paper also applies the masks to AdaLoRA"},{"target":"flat-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both are regularizers for LoRA generalization (noise on factors vs. noise in full weight space)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.09610","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.09610","https://openreview.net/forum?id=c4498OydLP"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Yang Lin","Xinyu Ma","Xu Chu","Yujie Jin","Zhibang Yang","Yasha Wang","Hong Mei"],"bibkey":"lin2024lora","bibtex":"@article{lin2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Dropout as a Sparsity Regularizer for Overfitting Control}},\n  author = {Yang Lin and Xinyu Ma and Xu Chu and Yujie Jin and Zhibang Yang and Yasha Wang and Hong Mei},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2404.09610},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.09610},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.09610}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The object is LoRA. During training, Bernoulli masks \\(\\hat B=\\mathrm{diag}(m_B)B\\) and \\(\\hat A=A\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_A)\\) act on the outer (neuron) indices: a stochastic training signal.","gauge":"The masks act on outer indices and commute with \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\), so the expected loss keeps LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge.","covariance":"The image is \\(GL\\times GL\\); the regulariser itself is only \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\)-natural (coordinate masks).","merge":"The trained \\(BA\\) fuses (M1). The proposed test-time ensemble \\(\\tfrac1N\\sum_nf(W_0+s\\hat B^{(n)}\\hat A^{(n)},x)\\) averages outputs and is M∞."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA Dropout keeps LoRA's object and changes the trained-method data: the loss is averaged over a family of coordinate masks, which preserves the \\(GL_r\\) gauge but breaks the rotational naturality of training. Its test-time ensemble is a family over mask space realised at function level, hence not mergeable.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object at inference without ensembling."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Ensemble deployment only (the arrow above covers single-weight deployment): the test-time ensemble's realised function \\(\\tfrac1N\\sum_nf(\\theta_n,x)\\) is generically not \\(f(\\theta,x)\\) for any single \\(\\theta\\) (\\(f\\) is non-linear), so it lies outside LoRA's realised image."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"asylora","name":"AsyLoRA","full_name":"Asymmetric LoRA (random frozen A, train only B)","paper_title":"Asymmetry in Low-Rank Adapters of Foundation Models","authors":"Zhu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.16842","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16842","code_url":"https://github.com/Jiacheng-Zhu-AIML/AsymmetryLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + s\\,B\\,Q,\\quad Q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{random with orthonormal rows (Haar/Stiefel), frozen};\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained from } B=0","trainable_params":"r\\,d_{out}","locus":"same as LoRA (RoBERTa, BART, LLaMA-2, ViT experiments)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Linear (not bilinear) parametrization: \\(\\Delta W\\) ranges over \\(\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\otimes\\mathrm{row}(Q)\\) for a fixed random \\(r\\)-dim input subspace; freezing \\(A\\) gives least-squares/logistic regression on randomly projected features \\(Qx\\), whereas freezing \\(B\\) only fits outputs projected onto a random subspace.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(B Q\\) is an ordinary additive low-rank update that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) with \\(Q\\) random orthogonal and never updated.","key_idea":"\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) play asymmetric roles: \\(A\\) extracts input features (and a random \\(A\\) works about as well as a learned one) while \\(B\\) maps them to the task; training only \\(B\\) halves parameters, tightens information-theoretic generalization bounds, and lets one double \\(r\\) at fixed budget.","results_note":"Across RoBERTa, BART-Large, LLaMA-2 and ViT, \\(B\\)-only tuning with a random orthogonal \\(A\\) matches or beats standard LoRA at matched parameter count and improves out-of-domain accuracy on DomainBed.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoRA with eta_A = 0 and orthogonal random A"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"same freeze-A design, LoRA-FA motivated by activation memory"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"both rely on frozen random projections"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16842","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.16842"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Jiacheng Zhu","Kristjan Greenewald","Kimia Nadjahi","Haitz Sáez de Ocáriz Borde","Rickard Brüel Gabrielsson","Leshem Choshen","Marzyeh Ghassemi","Mikhail Yurochkin","Justin Solomon"],"bibkey":"zhu2024asymmetry","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhu2024asymmetry,\n  title = {{Asymmetry in Low-Rank Adapters of Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Jiacheng Zhu and Kristjan Greenewald and Kimia Nadjahi and Haitz Sáez de Ocáriz Borde and Rickard Brüel Gabrielsson and Leshem Choshen and Marzyeh Ghassemi and Mikhail Yurochkin and Justin Solomon},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.16842},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16842}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0+\\mathbb R^m\\otimes\\mathrm{row}\\,Q\\), of dimension \\(mr\\).","covariance":"\\(Q\\) is Haar-random on the Stiefel manifold, so \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,Q\\) is Haar on \\(\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)\\) and covariance holds in law.","rebasing":"\\(Q\\) is frozen, so cycles gain nothing."},"categorical_reading":"AsyLoRA is the one-sided object \\(\\mathrm{FA}(Q)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,Q)}\\) with a Haar-random orthonormal frame: the same object as LoRA-FA up to the law of \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\). Orthonormality makes its plain-SGD cometric \\(s^2\\eta\\,GQ^\\top Q=s^2\\eta\\,GP_V\\), which is already LoRA-FA's corrected (least-squares) cometric.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ Q)","note":"Freezing \\(A=Q\\): a pointed monomorphism into LoRA at \\((0,Q)\\)."},{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(B)=B\\,QA_0^{+}","note":"Isomorphism when \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,Q=\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) (\\(A_0^+\\) the right pseudo-inverse); for independent draws the two agree only in law."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O2","reason":"Both freeze random projections, but \\(d=mr>m+r-1=d(\\text{VeRA})\\)."},{"target":"mica","test":"O1","reason":"Freezing \\(A\\) against freezing \\(B\\): \\(\\{XQ\\}\\) and \\(\\{U_{\\min}Y\\}\\) are incomparable, matching the paper's asymmetry."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-without-regret","name":"LoRA Without Regret","full_name":"LoRA Without Regret (Thinking Machines Lab recipe and analysis)","paper_title":"LoRA Without Regret","authors":"Schulman & Thinking Machines Lab","year":2025,"venue":"Thinking Machines Lab: Connectionism (blog), 2025","arxiv":"","url":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","code_url":"","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"LoraConfig(lora_alpha=32, target_modules='all-linear')  # same-LR AdamW, LR ~10x the FullFT LR","formula_latex":"W = W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA,\\ \\ \\alpha=32,\\ \\ A_{ij}\\sim\\mathcal{U}\\big(\\pm\\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{d_{in}}}\\big),\\ B=0,\\ \\ \\eta_A=\\eta_B=\\eta_{\\text{LoRA}}\\approx 10\\,\\eta_{\\text{FullFT}};\\ \\ \\text{apply to all linear layers incl. MLP/MoE}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all weight matrices, especially MLP and MoE layers (attention-only LoRA underperforms MLP-only LoRA)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Standard LoRA parametrization; of the four hyperparameters (\\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\eta_A\\), \\(\\eta_B\\), init scale of \\(A\\)) only two affect the dynamics, and with the \\(1/r\\) multiplier and \\(B=0\\) the expected first update \\(\\tfrac{1}{r}\\sum_i b_ia_i^{\\top}\\) is rank-independent, making the optimal LR approximately independent of \\(r\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"It is plain LoRA, so the adapter merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) with \\(A\\) uniform of scale \\(1/\\sqrt{d_{in}}\\) (the PEFT default parametrization).","key_idea":"LoRA matches full fine-tuning in the regime that matters for post-training when applied to all layers (especially MLPs) with ~10x the FullFT learning rate and when the dataset does not exceed adapter capacity; RL needs very little capacity, so even rank-1 LoRA matches FullFT for policy-gradient training.","results_note":"On Llama 3 / Qwen3 models with Tulu3 and OpenThoughts3 SFT, LoRA matches FullFT until capacity is exceeded; on MATH/GSM8K/DeepMath RL even rank-1 LoRA matches FullFT; LoRA is less tolerant of large batch sizes; LoRA costs slightly more than 2/3 of FullFT FLOPs per pass.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"empirical study and recipe for vanilla LoRA"},{"target":"rslora","type":"competes-with","note":"argues the default 1/r scaling already makes the optimal LR roughly rank-invariant"},{"target":"lora-plus","type":"competes-with","note":"uses a single LR for A and B and shows two of the four hyperparameters are redundant"}],"sources":["https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","https://doi.org/10.64434/tml.20250929"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"schulman2025lora","bibtex":"@misc{schulman2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Without Regret}},\n  author = {Schulman and Thinking Machines Lab},\n  year = {2025},\n  url = {https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/},\n  howpublished = {Thinking Machines Lab: Connectionism (blog), 2025}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"An analysis and recipe for plain LoRA (all layers, \\(\\alpha=32\\), one learning rate for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), about 10× the FullFT rate); the object is LoRA.","gauge":"Its observation that two of the four hyperparameters are redundant is the pair of torus invariants \\((s\\eta_A\\eta_B,\\ \\sigma_A/\\eta_A)\\) of Thm III.10."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA Without Regret studies LoRA's trained-method data rather than a new object: its hyperparameter redundancy is the 2-torus action of Thm III.10, and its capacity claims are statements about when the cone \\(\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) contains the needed update.","entry_type":"analysis","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Plain LoRA."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"flat-lora","name":"Flat-LoRA","full_name":"Flat-LoRA (low-rank adaptation over a flat full-space loss landscape)","paper_title":"Flat-LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation over a Flat Loss Landscape","authors":"Li et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2409.14396","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14396","code_url":"https://github.com/nblt/Flat-LoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\min_{A,B}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{\\varepsilon_W}\\,\\mathcal{L}\\big(W'+\\varepsilon_W\\big),\\quad W'=W_0+sBA,\\quad (\\varepsilon_W)_{ij}\\sim\\mathcal{N}\\!\\big(0,\\tfrac{\\sigma^2}{d_{in}}\\|W'_{i,:}\\|_2^2\\big)\\ \\ (\\text{fresh noise each step, regenerated from a seed})","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA (LLMs and text-to-image diffusion models)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Random-weight-perturbation (Bayesian expected loss) regularizer applied in the full \\(d_{out}\\times d_{in}\\) weight space rather than in the \\((A,B)\\) space, with filter(row)-norm-scaled Gaussian noise; a cheap relaxation of SAM's min-max over full-space perturbations.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Noise is used only during training; the learned \\(BA\\) merges like LoRA.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization (\\(B = 0\\)); the perturbation is zero-mean training noise only.","key_idea":"A minimum that is flat in LoRA's \\((A,B)\\) coordinates can still be sharp in the full weight space; averaging the loss over full-space random weight perturbations steers LoRA toward full-space flat minima without SAM's extra gradient step or weight copy.","results_note":"Improves in-domain and out-of-domain generalization over LoRA on mathematical reasoning, coding, dialogue, instruction following and text-to-image generation in the paper's experiments.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"regularized LoRA training objective"},{"target":"lora-dropout","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative regularizer for LoRA generalization"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14396","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.14396"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Tao Li","Zhengbao He","Yujun Li","Yasheng Wang","Lifeng Shang","Xiaolin Huang"],"bibkey":"li2024flat","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2024flat,\n  title = {{Flat-LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation over a Flat Loss Landscape}},\n  author = {Tao Li and Zhengbao He and Yujun Li and Yasheng Wang and Lifeng Shang and Xiaolin Huang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2409.14396},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14396}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The object is LoRA. The objective averages \\(L\\) over full-space Gaussian perturbations of \\(W'=\\rho(B,A)\\), with variance scaled by the row norms of \\(W'\\).","gauge":"The perturbed loss depends on \\((B,A)\\) only through \\(W'\\), so LoRA's \\(GL_r\\) gauge is exact, unlike flatness measured in \\((A,B)\\) coordinates, which is gauge-dependent.","covariance":"The image is \\(GL\\times GL\\). The noise law (rows isotropic in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), scaled by row norms) is \\(O(n)\\)-invariant on the input side and monomial-covariant on the output side."},"categorical_reading":"Flat-LoRA moves the sharpness regulariser from the chart \\(Q\\) to weight space. A loss that factors through \\(\\rho\\) is constant on gauge fibres, so its flatness is a property of the point of \\(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}\\), not of the representative \\((B,A)\\). The object stays LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object, regularised loss."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"altlora","name":"AltLoRA","full_name":"Alternating-projection LoRA","paper_title":"AltLoRA: Towards Better Gradient Approximation in Low-Rank Adaptation with Alternating Projections","authors":"Yu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2505.12455","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12455","code_url":"https://github.com/LucasXinYu/AltLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{even } t:\\ A\\leftarrow A-\\eta\\,\\tfrac{1}{s^2}(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L}\\ \\ (\\delta W=-\\eta P_BG);\\qquad \\text{odd } t:\\ B\\leftarrow B-\\eta\\,\\tfrac{1}{s^2}\\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,(AA^{\\top})^{-1}\\ \\ (\\delta W=-\\eta\\, G P_A);\\quad \\tilde M^B = M^BA_tA_{t+1}^{\\top}(A_{t+1}A_{t+1}^{\\top})^{-1}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Alternating one-sided projections of the full gradient onto \\(\\mathrm{col}(B)\\) and \\(\\mathrm{row}(A)\\) (each the exact least-squares solution for the factor being updated), with momentum transported into the new low-rank subspace by an \\(r\\times r\\) change-of-basis; avoids LoRA-Pro's non-unique \\(X\\) and full-size optimizer state.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only change; the result is a standard LoRA adapter.","identity_at_init":"Standard LoRA initialization; Gram inverses are damped as \\((B^T B + \\lambda I)^{-1}\\) to stay invertible when a factor is rank-deficient.","key_idea":"Instead of jointly matching the full gradient (LoRA-Pro), alternately update \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) so each step is an exact projection of the full gradient, and keep momentum aligned with the changing subspace without extra memory; this gives stable feature learning and transformation invariance.","results_note":"Outperforms LoRA and its variants (including LoRA-Pro) across SFT and NLU tasks while keeping LoRA-level memory, narrowing the gap to full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"lora-pro","type":"competes-with","note":"same gradient-approximation goal, alternating instead of joint update, low-rank momentum"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"each half-step uses the same (B^T B)^{-1} / (AA^T)^{-1} preconditioners"},{"target":"loft","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent alternating-update optimizer"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12455","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12455","https://openreview.net/forum?id=9YNJ03jYsU","https://github.com/LucasXinYu/AltLoRA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Xin Yu","Yujia Wang","Jinghui Chen","Lingzhou Xue"],"bibkey":"yu2025altlora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yu2025altlora,\n  title = {{AltLoRA: Towards Better Gradient Approximation in Low-Rank Adaptation with Alternating Projections}},\n  author = {Xin Yu and Yujia Wang and Jinghui Chen and Lingzhou Xue},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2505.12455},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.12455}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Optimizer-only: alternating half-steps \\(\\delta W=-\\eta P_BG\\) (\\(A\\)-step) and \\(\\delta W=-\\eta GP_A\\) (\\(B\\)-step).","gauge":"Each undamped half-step is \\(GL_r\\)-equivariant (they are the one-sided halves of scaled GD); damping reduces this to \\(O(r)\\)."},"categorical_reading":"AltLoRA alternates between the linear sub-objects \\(\\mathrm{FB}(B_t)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_t)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}\\), taking an exact least-squares step in each. By the degree-one collapse each half-step is a backward lens with anchor \\(P_B\\) or \\(P_A\\), so AltLoRA is a period-1 rebasing schedule of one-sided linear methods, with momentum transported between frames.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; each half-step uses one of the two Riemannian preconditioners."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loft","name":"LoFT","full_name":"Low-rank adaptation that mimics Full fine-Tuning","paper_title":"LoFT: Low-Rank Adaptation That Behaves Like Full Fine-Tuning","authors":"Tastan et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2505.21289","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21289","code_url":"https://github.com/tnurbek/loft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{alternating; } B\\text{-step: } W^{+}=W-\\eta\\,\\widehat G\\,P_A,\\ \\ P_A=A^{\\top}(AA^{\\top})^{-1}A,\\ \\ \\tilde\\nabla_B=\\nabla_B\\mathcal{L}\\,(AA^{\\top})^{-1},\\ \\ \\tilde\\nabla_A=(B^{\\top}B)^{-1}\\nabla_A\\mathcal{L};\\ \\ \\widehat G=\\text{AdamW direction from projected gradients with recalibrated first/second moments}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"same as LoRA","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Six building blocks (alternating updates, scaled gradients, momentum recalibration across changing subspaces via \\(C_k=(A_{k-1}A_k^{\\top})(A_kA_k^{\\top})^{-1}\\) [the paper's \\((V_{k-1}^{\\top}V_k)(V_k^{\\top}V_k)^{-1}\\) with \\(V=A^{\\top}\\)], second-moment alignment with cross terms, reconstruction of the full AdamW update followed by projection, gradient clipping) that make the low-rank step the tightest approximation to the AdamW full-fine-tuning step; \\(\\alpha\\) is fixed to 1, and LoFT reduces exactly to AdamW when \\(r=\\max(d_{in},d_{out})\\) with full-rank factors.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only change; the adapter is a standard mergeable low-rank product.","identity_at_init":"Inherits LoRA's zero-product initialization; Gram inverses fall back to pseudo-inverses when a factor is rank-deficient.","key_idea":"Not only gradients but also Adam's first and second moments are misaligned between LoRA and full fine-tuning; projecting and recalibrating them in the evolving low-rank subspace makes LoRA behave like full fine-tuning and removes the need to tune \\(\\alpha\\).","results_note":"Substantially narrows the gap to full fine-tuning and consistently outperforms standard LoRA-style methods without increasing inference cost; memory overhead \\(O((d_{in}+d_{out}) r^2)\\) for second-moment calibration.","relations":[{"target":"altlora","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent alternating-update optimizers"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Building Block 2 (scaled gradients) coincides with the Riemannian preconditioner"},{"target":"lora-pro","type":"competes-with","note":"both align low-rank updates with full fine-tuning; LoFT also aligns optimizer states"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21289","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.21289","https://openreview.net/forum?id=86P3sb1dpr"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"authors_full":["Nurbek Tastan","Stefanos Laskaridis","Martin Takac","Karthik Nandakumar","Samuel Horvath"],"bibkey":"tastan2025loft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tastan2025loft,\n  title = {{LoFT: Low-Rank Adaptation That Behaves Like Full Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Nurbek Tastan and Stefanos Laskaridis and Martin Takac and Karthik Nandakumar and Samuel Horvath},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2505.21289},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21289}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Optimizer-only: alternating scaled-gradient steps plus recalibrated, projected AdamW moments; it reduces exactly to AdamW at full rank.","gauge":"The object's gauge is \\(GL_r\\). Moments are transported between successive frames by \\(C_k=(A_{k-1}A_k^\\top)(A_kA_k^\\top)^{-1}\\), a change of gauge representative."},"categorical_reading":"LoFT keeps LoRA's object and lifts the full-space AdamW update into the moving tangent frames of the cone, transporting optimizer state along the gauge (unlike GaLore, which carries state over without transport). The cone is already \\(\\Theta\\) at \\(r=\\min(m,n)\\), but the update reduces exactly to AdamW on full FT (the terminal object) only at \\(r=\\max(m,n)\\), where both projections \\(P_A\\) and \\(P_B\\) (with pseudo-inverses) are the identity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"polar","name":"PoLAR","full_name":"Polar-Decomposed Low-Rank Adapter Representation","paper_title":"PoLAR: Polar-Decomposed Low-Rank Adapter Representation","authors":"Lion et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2506.03133","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03133","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0+X\\,\\Theta\\,Y^{\\top},\\quad X\\in\\mathrm{St}(d_{out},r),\\ Y\\in\\mathrm{St}(d_{in},r),\\ \\Theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r};\\ \\text{retraction-free landing field } \\Gamma(X)=\\psi(X)X+\\lambda\\nabla\\mathcal{N}(X);\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+r^2","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"rank-\\(r\\) additive update in polar form: two direction factors on Stiefel manifolds (orthonormal \\(r\\)-frames, i.e. points of \\(O(d)/O(d-r)\\)) and an unconstrained \\(r\\times r\\) scale/core matrix, optimized by Riemannian 'landing' without retractions","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(X \\Theta Y^\\top\\) is a fixed rank-\\(r\\) matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Theta_0 = 0\\), with \\(X_0\\) and \\(Y_0\\) drawn uniformly at random from the Stiefel manifolds.","key_idea":"LoRA updates collapse to a very low stable rank. Writing the update as two orthonormal direction frames times a free core, and training with GPU-friendly Riemannian landing, keeps the directions diverse and provably speeds up convergence on a canonical low-rank problem.","results_note":"Reports gains over LoRA on GLUE (DeBERTa-v3-base 88.94 vs 88.18), commonsense reasoning (Llama-2-7B, 73.71 vs 72.18) and math (Gemma-3-27B) across models from 350M to 27B parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"the same rank-r update, polar-decomposed"},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"orthogonality over the whole factor instead of DoRA's per-neuron normalization"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"an SVD-shaped P Lambda Q update, with hard Stiefel constraints instead of an orthogonality penalty"},{"target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both use Riemannian geometry for LoRA optimization, but PoLAR optimizes Stiefel-manifold factors with the landing field, whereas Zhang & Pilanci precondition unconstrained factors with a quotient metric; PoLAR cites them only for the matrix-factorization reduction"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03133","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.03133","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/116485"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core","multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Kai Lion","Liang Zhang","Bingcong Li","Niao He"],"bibkey":"lion2025polar","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lion2025polar,\n  title = {{PoLAR: Polar-Decomposed Low-Rank Adapter Representation}},\n  author = {Kai Lion and Liang Zhang and Bingcong Li and Niao He},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2506.03133},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.03133}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\{X\\Theta Y^\\top\\}=\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), since every rank-\\(\\le r\\) matrix has an SVD-type form; the image is LoRA's cone, of dimension \\(r(m+n-r)\\).","base_point":"\\(\\Theta_0=0\\) is a zero gate: \\(S_1=\\{X_0RY_0^\\top\\}\\) has dimension \\(r^2\\) (checked), a deeper apex than LoRA's (\\(mr\\)). To first order PoLAR is a generalised LoRA-XS in random frames.","gauge":"\\(O(r)\\times O(r)\\) acting by \\((XO_1,O_1^\\top\\Theta O_2,YO_2)\\), fibre \\(r(r-1)\\) (checked): the compact form of LoRA's bond gauge. The Stiefel constraints remove the non-compact \\(\\mathrm{Sym}_r\\) half, so there are no balancedness charges.","covariance":"The image is basis-free (GL×GL); the initial frames are Haar-random (in law)."},"categorical_reading":"PoLAR reparametrises LoRA's cone through \\(\\mathrm{St}(m,r)\\times\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}\\times\\mathrm{St}(n,r)\\), a partial gauge fixing that cuts \\(GL_r\\) down to \\(O(r)\\times O(r)\\) and so removes the non-compact directions that carry the charges \\(\\Phi\\). It factors through LoRA by \\(h=(X\\Theta,Y^\\top)\\), but starts at a deeper apex whose first-order image is the LoRA-XS-type tangent \\(\\{X_0RY_0^\\top\\}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(X,\\Theta,Y)=(X\\Theta,\\ Y^\\top)","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,Y_0^\\top)\\), which has orthonormal rows."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"adalora","test":"O3","reason":"Both are SVD-shaped, but \\(S_1(\\mathrm{PoLAR})=\\{X_0RY_0^\\top\\}\\) (dimension \\(r^2\\)) is not inside \\(S_1(\\mathrm{AdaLoRA})=\\mathrm{span}\\{p_iq_i^\\top\\}\\) (dimension \\(r\\))."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"goft","name":"GOFT","full_name":"Givens Orthogonal Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Parameter Efficient Quasi-Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Givens Rotation","authors":"Ma et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2404.04316","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316","code_url":"https://github.com/ArthurLeoM/peft-givens","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 R^{\\top},\\quad R=\\prod_{\\ell=1}^{\\log_2 d_{in}}P_\\ell,\\ P_\\ell=\\prod_{k=0}^{d_{in}/2^{\\ell}-1}G\\big(2^{\\ell}k,\\ 2^{\\ell-1}(2k+1);\\ \\theta_k^{\\ell}\\big),\\ G(i,j;\\theta) \\text{ a Givens rotation in the } (i,j) \\text{ plane};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen, angles } \\theta \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"d_{in}-1","locus":"any linear layer; acts on the input side of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"right action of \\(SO(d_{in})\\), with the group element written as a product of \\(d-1\\) Givens rotations (one-parameter subgroups \\(SO(2)\\) embedded in coordinate planes) arranged in \\(\\log_2(d)\\) parallel layers of non-overlapping rotations, a butterfly-like layout","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The product of Givens rotations is a fixed orthogonal matrix that can be multiplied into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"All angles start at 0 (each rotation initialized to the identity).","key_idea":"\\(d-1\\) Givens rotations can map any vector to any other vector on the same sphere, so OFT-level expressiveness only needs \\(O(d)\\) angles instead of \\(O(d^2)\\) parameters. A parallel arrangement keeps the depth at \\(O(\\log d)\\) sequential layers.","results_note":"On GLUE with DeBERTaV3-base, GOFT with 0.08M parameters matches OFT (\\(b=8\\), 0.66M parameters) at an 88.78 average; also evaluated on LLaMA-2-7B instruction tuning and VTAB-1K.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"same orthogonal right action; far sparser parameterization"},{"target":"boft","type":"special-case-of","note":"up to factor-ordering and permutation conventions, GOFT's parallel layer P_l keeps only the first 2x2 rotation of each stride-2^{l-1} butterfly block, so it is a sparser instance of BOFT(log2 d, 2) with the other rotations fixed to the identity (a function-class inclusion, not a statement made in either paper)"},{"target":"qgoft","type":"special-case-of","note":"qGOFT relaxes each Givens block to a general 2x2 matrix"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.04316"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Xinyu Ma","Xu Chu","Zhibang Yang","Yang Lin","Xin Gao","Junfeng Zhao"],"bibkey":"ma2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ma2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter Efficient Quasi-Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Givens Rotation}},\n  author = {Xinyu Ma and Xu Chu and Zhibang Yang and Yang Lin and Xin Gao and Junfeng Zhao},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2404.04316},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A product of \\(d-1\\) Givens rotations in \\(\\log_2d\\) parallel layers: action type into \\(SO(d)\\), image of dimension \\(d-1\\) (Jacobian rank 7 = \\(|M|\\) at \\(d=8\\)).","gauge":"Distinct planes give independent generators, at \\(\\theta = 0\\) and generically: none (VIII.B '-').","covariance":"VIII.B: \\(GL\\times\\Pi\\). The fixed plane pairing is imposed on the input side (axes.json also lists 'GOFT (plane structure)' under \\(GL\\times CO\\), meaning the normaliser of that pairing).","rebasing":"The butterfly plane graph is connected, so re-merging reaches \\(W_0\\,SO(n)\\) (Thm V.3(c))."},"categorical_reading":"GOFT writes an element of \\(SO(d)\\) as a log-depth circuit of \\(d-1\\) Givens rotations: an action-type object, regular at \\(\\theta_0\\), whose image is a thin \\((d-1)\\)-dimensional slice of the orbit \\(W_0\\cdot SO(d)\\). A single run stays far from the orbit; rebasing walks all of it because the plane graph is connected.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"qgoft","rule":"A3","h":"\\theta\\mapsto\\Big(\\tilde G_t=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta_t&-\\sin\\theta_t\\\\ \\sin\\theta_t&\\cos\\theta_t\\end{pmatrix}\\Big)_t","note":"Each Givens rotation is a particular quasi-Givens block; identity at theta = 0."},{"target":"boft","rule":"A3","h":"\\theta\\mapsto\\big(t=\\tan(\\theta/2)\\ \\text{on the kept }2\\times2\\text{ blocks},\\ 0\\ \\text{on the others},\\ s=\\mathbf 1\\big)","note":"BOFT(log2 d, 2) with the non-GOFT rotations and boft_s frozen at their base values; up to factor-ordering and permutation conventions; a germ arrow (Cayley excludes the angle pi)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"GOFT's layers couple coordinates across OFT's blocks (breaking its partial Grams) and can produce eigenvalue -1, which exact Cayley never does."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"qgoft","name":"qGOFT","full_name":"quasi-Givens Orthogonal Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Parameter Efficient Quasi-Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Givens Rotation","authors":"Ma et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2404.04316","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316","code_url":"https://github.com/ArthurLeoM/peft-givens","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0\\tilde{R}^{\\top},\\quad \\tilde{R}=\\prod_{\\ell}\\tilde{P}_\\ell,\\ \\text{each Givens block replaced by a free } \\tilde{G}_i=[\\alpha_i\\ \\beta_i]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 2};\\ \\min\\ \\mathcal{L}+\\lambda\\sum_i\\langle\\alpha_i,\\beta_i\\rangle^2;\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"4(d_{in}-1)","locus":"any linear layer; acts on the input side of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"right action of a product of embedded free \\(2\\times2\\) blocks (singular ones included) laid out like GOFT. Leaving \\(SO(d)\\) lets the update change neuron norms and angles, and a soft column-orthogonality penalty keeps it near the orthogonal group.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The product of \\(2\\times2\\) blocks is a fixed matrix that can be multiplied into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Every quasi-Givens block is initialized to the \\(2\\times2\\) identity.","key_idea":"Relax each Givens rotation to an arbitrary \\(2\\times2\\) linear map, so fine-tuning can also rescale neurons and shift their angles. A soft regularizer keeps the map close to orthogonal.","results_note":"Reports an 89.35 GLUE average on DeBERTaV3-base with 0.33M parameters (above OFT \\(b=8\\) with 0.66M) and a 79.4% VTAB-1K average.","relations":[{"target":"goft","type":"generalizes","note":"Givens rotations relaxed to general 2x2 blocks with a soft orthogonality penalty"},{"target":"road","type":"competes-with","note":"structural analogue: both act with 2x2 blocks (qGOFT on the input side with butterfly-style mixing, RoAd with scaled rotations on fixed output pairs); neither paper compares against the other"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.04316"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Xinyu Ma","Xu Chu","Zhibang Yang","Yang Lin","Xin Gao","Junfeng Zhao"],"bibkey":"ma2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ma2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter Efficient Quasi-Orthogonal Fine-Tuning via Givens Rotation}},\n  author = {Xinyu Ma and Xu Chu and Zhibang Yang and Yang Lin and Xin Gao and Junfeng Zhao},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2404.04316},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.04316}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A product of \\(d - 1\\) free \\(2\\times2\\) blocks in GOFT's layout: action type into the monoid of matrices (axes.json: 'monoid of arbitrary 2x2 blocks').","base_point":"At identity blocks \\(S_1\\) is spanned by every \\(E_{ab}\\) inside each plane: \\(2(d-1)\\) off-diagonal plus \\(d\\) diagonal \\(=3d-2=d(\\mathrm{qGOFT})\\).","gauge":"VIII.B lists '-', but a diagonal scalar slides along each internal index segment between two blocks that share an index, \\((D,D^{-1})\\): a \\((d-2)\\)-dimensional torus (Jacobian rank \\(22=28-6\\) at \\(d=8\\)).","covariance":"The pairing is imposed (\\(\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Connected planes: \\(\\to W_0\\,\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}\\), the closure of \\(W_0\\cdot GL(n)\\). The free \\(2\\times2\\) blocks include singular ones and ones of negative determinant, and on GOFT's connected planes they generate the monoid of all \\(n\\times n\\) matrices (Thm V.3(b))."},"categorical_reading":"qGOFT relaxes each Givens rotation to an arbitrary \\(2\\times2\\) block, so it is the action of a monoid circuit rather than a group. It leaves the \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) level set at first order, carries a torus gauge on internal index wires, and rebasing walks \\(W_0\\,\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}\\), the closure of \\(W_0\\cdot GL(n)\\), instead of \\(W_0\\cdot SO(n)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"goft","test":"O1","reason":"Non-conformal blocks change neuron norms and angles, which GOFT's rotations preserve."},{"target":"boft","test":"O1","reason":"BOFT preserves |cos| between neurons (Cor II.6 remarks), qGOFT does not."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"ether","name":"ETHER","full_name":"Efficient Finetuning with Hyperplane Reflections","paper_title":"ETHER: Efficient Finetuning of Large-Scale Models with Hyperplane Reflections","authors":"Bini et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2405.20271","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271","code_url":"https://github.com/mwbini/ether","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 H^{B},\\quad H^{B}=\\operatorname{diag}(H_1,\\dots,H_n),\\quad H_j=I-2u_ju_j^{\\top},\\ \\|u_j\\|=1,\\ u_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}/n};\\quad \\|H_j-I\\|_F=2;\\quad W_0 \\text{ frozen, } u_j \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"d_{in}","locus":"any linear layer (diffusion attention, LLM projections); acts on the input side of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"right action of a block-diagonal product of \\(n\\) hyperplane reflections, an element of \\(O(d_{in})\\) that lies at fixed distance \\(\\|H_j - I\\|_F = 2\\) from the identity in every block","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(H^B\\) is a fixed orthogonal matrix that can be multiplied into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No. A Householder reflection is never the identity (\\(\\|H - I\\|_F = 2\\) per block), and the official code initializes \\(u\\) with Kaiming-uniform random values, so the model is moved by a bounded reflection at step 0. Training only re-orients the reflection hyperplanes.","key_idea":"Restricting the multiplicative update to hyperplane reflections bounds how far the fine-tuned weights can move from the pretrained ones, whatever the learning rate. The result needs very few parameters and is robust to hyperparameters.","results_note":"Reports performance matching or beating LoRA and OFT with about 10-100x fewer parameters on subject-driven and controllable Stable Diffusion generation, GLUE and LLaMA instruction tuning.","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"block-diagonal orthogonal action with each block restricted to a single reflection"},{"target":"hra","type":"special-case-of","note":"diag(H_1,...,H_n) is a product of n reflections with disjoint-support vectors, i.e. HRA with r=n orthogonal reflection vectors and without the identity-preserving pairing (the two methods were concurrent)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.20271"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Massimo Bini","Karsten Roth","Zeynep Akata","Anna Khoreva"],"bibkey":"bini2024ether","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bini2024ether,\n  title = {{ETHER: Efficient Finetuning of Large-Scale Models with Hyperplane Reflections}},\n  author = {Massimo Bini and Karsten Roth and Zeynep Akata and Anna Khoreva},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2405.20271},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxCO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0H^B\\) with \\(H^B=\\mathrm{diag}(I-2u_ju_j^\\top/\\|u_j\\|^2)\\): the orbit of \\(W_0\\) under the reflection class in each block (\\(\\prod\\mathbb{RP}^{b-1}\\)), action type in \\(O(n)\\).","base_point":"A reflection is never \\(I\\) (\\(\\|H_j-I\\|_F=2\\)), so \\(\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\) for injective \\(W_0\\): \\(\\|W_0H-W_0\\|=2\\|W_0\\hat u\\|>0\\). The official code starts at a random reflection.","gauge":"\\(H\\) depends on \\(u_j/\\|u_j\\|\\): per-block scalings \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^n\\).","covariance":"A block-diagonal right action: the block normaliser in \\(CO(n)\\) on the input side, as for block OFT.","rebasing":"Products of reflections generate \\(O(b)\\) in each block (Cartan-Dieudonne), so re-merging reaches \\(W_0\\cdot\\prod O(b)\\), both determinant classes included."},"categorical_reading":"ETHER is an unpointed action-type object: its image is a compact set of block reflections \\(W_0H^B\\subset W_0\\cdot O(n)\\) at fixed distance from \\(\\theta_0\\). It preserves \\(K_{\\rm out}\\) and the spectrum (Cor II.6) but has no base point over \\(\\theta_0\\); its 'bounded deviation' is literally \\(\\|H_j-I\\|_F=2\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"hra","test":"O3","reason":"ETHER is unpointed: for an even number n of blocks its image lies in Im HRA_n (n reflections with disjoint supports), but only through a weak morphism, never a pointed one."},{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"Reflections have eigenvalue -1, which the Cayley map never produces."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"ether-plus","name":"ETHER+","full_name":"ETHER+ (relaxed two-hyperplane transformation applied on both sides)","paper_title":"ETHER: Efficient Finetuning of Large-Scale Models with Hyperplane Reflections","authors":"Bini et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2405.20271","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271","code_url":"https://github.com/mwbini/ether","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=\\tilde{H}^{+}\\,W_0\\,H^{+},\\quad H^{+}=I-uu^{\\top}+vv^{\\top}\\ (u,v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}\\ \\text{unit, block-diagonal}),\\ \\tilde{H}^{+}=I-\\tilde u\\tilde u^{\\top}+\\tilde v\\tilde v^{\\top}\\ (\\tilde u,\\tilde v\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}});\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"2(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer; acts on both the input and the output side of \\(W_0\\)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"two-sided action (left on \\(\\mathbb R^{d_{out}}\\), right on \\(\\mathbb R^{d_{in}}\\)) by rank\\(\\le 2\\) perturbations of the identity. These are not orthogonal in general, so they leave the group \\(O(d)\\), but their distance to \\(I\\) is bounded by ETHER's.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both transforms are fixed matrices after training, so \\(\\tilde H^+ W_0 H^+\\) can be precomputed.","identity_at_init":"Yes. The official code initializes the second vector as the negative copy of the first (\\(v = -u\\)), so \\(vv^\\top = uu^\\top\\) and \\(H^+ = I\\) on both sides; the paper notes that \\(H^+\\) returns the identity when the two hyperplanes coincide.","key_idea":"Relax the Householder reflection to \\(I - uu^\\top + vv^\\top\\) so that two hyperplanes can interact or cancel. Applied on both sides of \\(W_0\\), this gives finer, still norm-bounded updates.","results_note":"Reports e.g. 0.666 DINO for subject-driven generation with 0.4M parameters vs OFT's 0.652 with 11.6M, and 27.3 mIoU vs 24.5 for controllable generation.","relations":[{"target":"ether","type":"generalizes","note":"a two-hyperplane relaxation of the reflection that can reach the identity"},{"target":"delora","type":"competes-with","note":"DeLoRA is derived from ETHER+ by making the bound on the deviation learnable and the rank arbitrary"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.20271","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.18225"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Massimo Bini","Karsten Roth","Zeynep Akata","Anna Khoreva"],"bibkey":"bini2024ether","bibtex":"@inproceedings{bini2024ether,\n  title = {{ETHER: Efficient Finetuning of Large-Scale Models with Hyperplane Reflections}},\n  author = {Massimo Bini and Karsten Roth and Zeynep Akata and Anna Khoreva},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2405.20271},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.20271}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A two-sided action \\(\\tilde H^+W_0H^+\\) by matrices \\(I-uu^\\top+vv^\\top\\) that leave \\(O(d)\\).","shape":"At the base point \\(v=-u\\) (coinciding hyperplanes) each factor set \\(\\{I-uu^\\top+vv^\\top\\}\\) has the germ of the non-linear cone \\(\\{xy^\\top+yx^\\top:x\\perp y\\}\\); the image germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), not a group orbit.","base_point":"\\(S_1\\) per block and side has dimension \\(b-1\\) against \\(2(b-1)\\) generically: an apex (Jacobian 10 vs 20 for one block per side at \\(m=n=6\\); 8 vs 16 for two).","gauge":"\\(H^+\\) depends only on \\(u/\\|u\\|\\), \\(v/\\|v\\|\\): per-vector scalings, a torus (fibre = number of vectors in the check).","covariance":"Two-sided block normalisers \\(N_{\\rm blk}(CO(m))\\times N_{\\rm blk}(CO(n))\\), with no SVD frame; with one block per side exactly \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"The tangent cone spans the traceless symmetric matrices, whose brackets give \\(\\mathfrak{so}(b)\\): the generated monoid has Lie algebra \\(\\supseteq\\mathfrak{sl}(b)\\) per block and side, and \\(\\det=\\cos^2\\varphi\\le1\\) adds contractions."},"categorical_reading":"ETHER+ is a two-sided action by rank-2 symmetric perturbations of the identity. Its default pointing (\\(v=-u\\)) is the vertex of the cone of such perturbations, so unlike ETHER it is pointed, but it sits at an apex, like zero-init LoRA. Its update has rank \\(\\le2\\) per block and side, so LoRA of that rank simulates it.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"q\\mapsto\\Big(\\big[\\,[\\hat{\\tilde v}_j,-\\hat{\\tilde u}_j]_j,\\ W_0[\\hat v_j,-\\hat u_j]_j\\,\\big],\\ \\big[\\,[\\hat{\\tilde v}_j,\\hat{\\tilde u}_j]_j^\\top W_0H^+;\\ [\\hat v_j,\\hat u_j]_j^\\top\\,\\big]\\Big)","note":"From Delta W = (H~+ - I) W_0 H+ + W_0 (H+ - I); LoRA of rank 2(#left blocks + #right blocks), pointed at h(q_0) with B_0 A_0 = 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"The factors I - uu^T + vv^T are not orthogonal (singular when u is orthogonal to v), so ETHER+ changes K_out (Cor II.6 remarks)."},{"target":"ether","test":"O1","reason":"ETHER's image consists of exact block reflections and excludes theta_0; ETHER+ reaches theta_0 and non-orthogonal matrices."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"spectral-adapter-r","name":"Spectral Adapter^R","full_name":"Spectral Adapter, rotational variant","paper_title":"Spectral Adapter: Fine-Tuning in Spectral Space","authors":"Zhang & Pilanci","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.13952","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952","code_url":"https://github.com/pilancilab/spectral_adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=[U_1R_U,\\ U_2]\\,S\\,[V_1R_V,\\ V_2]^{\\top}=\\tilde{U}\\,W_0\\,\\tilde{V}^{\\top},\\quad \\tilde{U}=I+U_1(R_U-I)U_1^{\\top},\\ \\tilde{V}=I+V_1(R_V-I)V_1^{\\top};\\ W_0=USV^{\\top}\\ \\text{frozen},\\ R_U,R_V\\in O(r)\\ \\text{(Cayley) trained}","trainable_params":"2r^2\\ \\ (r(r-1)\\ \\text{with skew-symmetric Cayley generators})","locus":"any linear layer (top-\\(r\\) spectral space of each pretrained weight)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"two-sided orthogonal action of \\(O(r)\\times O(r)\\), embedded in \\(O(d_{out})\\times O(d_{in})\\) as rotations of the top-\\(r\\) left and right singular subspaces of \\(W_0\\) (identity on their complements). Singular values are unchanged and only the principal singular frames are rotated.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The rotated singular factors recombine into one dense matrix of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(R_U = R_V = I\\).","key_idea":"Run the SVD of each pretrained weight and fine-tune only by rotating its top singular vectors. The adapter is very compact, and keeping the adapted subspaces orthogonal helps when several adapters are fused.","results_note":"","relations":[{"target":"oft","type":"builds-on","note":"OFT-style orthogonal action restricted to the principal spectral subspace"},{"target":"spectral-adapter-a","type":"competes-with","note":"same paper; rotational vs additive tuning of the top singular vectors"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.13952","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/94943"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Fangzhao Zhang","Mert Pilanci"],"bibkey":"zhang2024spectral","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024spectral,\n  title = {{Spectral Adapter: Fine-Tuning in Spectral Space}},\n  author = {Fangzhao Zhang and Mert Pilanci},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.13952},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\tilde UW_0\\tilde V^\\top\\) with \\(\\tilde U=I+U_1(R_U-I)U_1^\\top\\): the orbit of \\(W_0\\) under \\(SO(r)\\times SO(r)\\) embedded on the top-\\(r\\) singular subspaces; isospectral (Thm II.5(3)).","base_point":"\\((\\Omega_U,\\Omega_V)\\mapsto\\Omega_US_1-S_1\\Omega_V\\) is injective for distinct top singular values: regular.","gauge":"None on the skew generators; the stored generators \\(A\\) with \\(Q=(A-A^\\top)/2\\) carry linear null directions (their symmetric part).","covariance":"SVD frame: \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"The rotations stay inside the top-\\(r\\) subspaces and keep the spectrum, so even a recomputed SVD returns the same subspaces: idempotent up to closure."},"categorical_reading":"Spectral Adapter\\(^R\\) is a two-sided orthogonal orbit confined to the principal singular block: it rotates the top singular frames and keeps every singular value, so the spectrum and the principal subspaces are absolute invariants. Through the SVD frame it factors through LoRA-XS's linear core.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"(Q_U,Q_V)\\mapsto C(Q_U)\\,S_1\\,C(Q_V)^\\top S_1^{-1}-I","note":"LoRA-XS core in the frames (U_1, S_1 V_1^T), pointed at 0."},{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(Q_U,Q_V)\\mapsto\\big(U_1C(Q_U)S_1C(Q_V)^\\top S_1^{-1/2},\\ S_1^{1/2}V_1^\\top\\big)","note":"PiSSA_r at its own point (U_1 S_1^{1/2}, S_1^{1/2} V_1^T)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"svdiff","test":"O1","reason":"SVDiff moves singular values with frames fixed, Spectral Adapter^R moves frames with values fixed: near theta_0 the images meet only at theta_0."},{"target":"oft","test":"O1","reason":"The left rotation R_U changes K_out = W W^T, which input-side OFT preserves."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"spectral-adapter-a","name":"Spectral Adapter^A","full_name":"Spectral Adapter, additive variant","paper_title":"Spectral Adapter: Fine-Tuning in Spectral Space","authors":"Zhang & Pilanci","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.13952","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952","code_url":"https://github.com/pilancilab/spectral_adapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=[U_1+A_U,\\ U_2]\\,S\\,[V_1+A_V,\\ V_2]^{\\top},\\quad A_U\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r}\\ \\text{trained};\\ W_0=USV^{\\top}\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (top-\\(r\\) singular vectors)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"additive perturbation of the top-\\(r\\) singular-vector factors: \\(\\Delta W = A_U S_1 V_1^T + U_1 S_1 A_V^T + A_U S_1 A_V^T\\). This has rank \\(\\le 2r\\) for LoRA's parameter budget (the paper's 'twice the rank capacity' lemma).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The perturbed factors recombine into a single dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"\\(A_U = A_V = 0\\).","key_idea":"Fine-tune the top singular vectors of the pretrained weight additively. For the same number of trainable parameters this doubles LoRA's rank capacity and uses the pretrained spectrum.","results_note":"Reports 49.73 on GSM8K with Mistral-7B vs 44.81 for LoRA and 43.82 for DoRA (\\(r=8\\)), and an 88.03 GLUE average on DeBERTaV3-base, above LoRA, DoRA, OFT and AdaLoRA.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same parameter budget, twice the rank capacity"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"both adapt the principal singular subspace of W_0 (concurrent work)"},{"target":"spectral-adapter-r","type":"competes-with","note":"same paper; additive vs rotational"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.13952"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Fangzhao Zhang","Mert Pilanci"],"bibkey":"zhang2024spectral","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024spectral,\n  title = {{Spectral Adapter: Fine-Tuning in Spectral Space}},\n  author = {Fangzhao Zhang and Mert Pilanci},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.13952},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13952}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W=W_{res}+(U_1+A_U)S_1(V_1+A_V)^\\top\\): the affine change \\(B=(U_1+A_U)S_1^{1/2}\\), \\(A=S_1^{1/2}(V_1+A_V)^\\top\\) identifies it with PiSSA, a Type II translated cone.","base_point":"A smooth point (split pointing).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\), transported from PiSSA.","covariance":"SVD frame: \\(CO\\times CO\\), as PiSSA.","rebasing":"As PiSSA."},"categorical_reading":"Spectral Adapter\\(^A\\) is PiSSA in affine coordinates: \\((A_U,A_V)\\mapsto((U_1+A_U)S_1^{1/2},S_1^{1/2}(V_1+A_V)^\\top)\\) is a pointed isomorphism, so its 'twice the rank capacity' is Thm II.1(c) for split pointings. The coordinate change alters only the induced cometric (rates weighted by \\(S_1\\)), not the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(A_U,A_V)\\mapsto\\big((U_1+A_U)S_1^{1/2},\\ S_1^{1/2}(V_1+A_V)^\\top\\big)","note":"A pointed isomorphism (affine and invertible)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"At equal rank \\(r\\), against LoRA's Type I apex: a Type II smooth point, and the images are incomparable (Thm II.2(d)), so there is no arrow either way at that rank. (Via PiSSA it does map into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r}\\) at a degenerate pointing, Thm II.1.)"}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"svdiff","name":"SVDiff","full_name":"Singular-value spectral-shift fine-tuning (SVDiff)","paper_title":"SVDiff: Compact Parameter Space for Diffusion Fine-Tuning","authors":"Han et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICCV 2023","arxiv":"2303.11305","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11305","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=U\\,\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta)\\big)\\,V^{\\top},\\quad W_0=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma)V^{\\top}\\ \\text{(SVD, frozen)},\\ \\delta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\min(d_{in},d_{out})}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\min(d_{in},d_{out})","locus":"2-D linear weights and reshaped 4-D conv kernels of the diffusion UNet (1-D weights such as biases and norms are not decomposed and are tuned directly); optimizing the cross-attention layers gives the best subject preservation in the paper's layer ablation","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"the singular values are adjusted by a diagonal (torus) map while the singular vectors stay fixed. For \\(\\sigma > 0\\) this equals \\(W = U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(s/\\sigma)\\,U^\\top W_0\\) with \\(s = \\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta) \\ge 0\\), a positive-semidefinite rescaling that commutes with \\(W_0 W_0^\\top\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta))\\,V^\\top\\) is a fixed dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\delta = 0\\).","key_idea":"Fine-tune only the singular values of every weight matrix (spectral shifts). The tiny parameter space resists overfitting in personalization and supports multi-subject training (Cut-Mix-Unmix) and single-image editing (CoSINE).","results_note":"About 1.7MB of parameters for Stable Diffusion vs 3.66GB for full DreamBooth (roughly 2,200x smaller) and 1/2-1/3 of a LoRA checkpoint.","relations":[{"target":"svf","type":"competes-with","note":"SVF scales singular values multiplicatively; SVDiff shifts them additively (equivalent reparameterizations for sigma > 0)"},{"target":"svft","type":"special-case-of","note":"SVFT with a diagonal mask is a spectral shift without the ReLU"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"much smaller checkpoint for diffusion personalization"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11305","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.11305"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Ligong Han","Yinxiao Li","Han Zhang","Peyman Milanfar","Dimitris Metaxas","Feng Yang"],"bibkey":"han2023svdiff","bibtex":"@inproceedings{han2023svdiff,\n  title = {{SVDiff: Compact Parameter Space for Diffusion Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Ligong Han and Yinxiao Li and Han Zhang and Peyman Milanfar and Dimitris Metaxas and Feng Yang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2303.11305},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.11305}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(U\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta))V^\\top\\) is the closure of the positive-torus orbit on the spectrum in the frozen SVD frame (a polyhedral cone with vertex 0); \\(\\theta_0\\) is interior (\\(\\sigma>0\\)), so the germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is the linear space \\(U\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^{\\min})\\,V^\\top\\).","base_point":"ReLU is the identity near \\(\\delta=0\\).","gauge":"Injective near \\(\\theta_0\\).","covariance":"SVD frame: \\(CO\\times CO\\) (positive scalings preserve the orthant).","rebasing":"Re-merging keeps the frames (up to reordering): idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"SVDiff is the positive torus acting on the singular values of \\(W_0\\) in its frozen SVD frame; its image is the orbit closure \\(\\{U\\mathrm{diag}(s)V^\\top:s\\ge0\\}\\), linear at \\(\\theta_0\\). It is the spectral analogue of (IA)\\(^3\\): frames are absolute invariants, singular values move.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta\\mapsto\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta)-\\sigma","note":"SVFT with the plain (diagonal) pattern, pointed at M = 0 (SVFT's official init is a small defect); piecewise smooth, smooth near delta = 0."},{"target":"svf","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta\\mapsto\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta)\\oslash\\sigma","note":"SVF in the paper's form U diag(sigma (.) z) V^T, pointed at z = 1."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"spectral-adapter-r","test":"O1","reason":"SVDiff changes singular values with fixed frames, while Spectral Adapter^R preserves the spectrum."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"svf","name":"SVF (Transformer²)","full_name":"Singular Value Fine-tuning (Transformer-Squared)","paper_title":"Transformer-Squared: Self-adaptive LLMs","authors":"Sun et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2501.06252","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06252","code_url":"https://github.com/SakanaAI/self-adaptive-llms","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=U\\big(\\Sigma\\otimes\\operatorname{diag}(z)\\big)V^{\\top}=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma\\odot z)V^{\\top}\\ \\Big(\\times\\tfrac{\\sum_i\\sigma_i}{\\sum_i\\sigma_iz_i}\\ \\text{in the official code}\\Big),\\quad W_0=U\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma)V^{\\top}\\ \\text{frozen},\\ z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\min(d_{in},d_{out})}\\ \\text{trained (REINFORCE + KL)};\\ \\text{test time: } z'=\\sum_k\\alpha_kz_k","trainable_params":"\\min(d_{in},d_{out})","locus":"weight matrices of the LLM (the official code adapts the MLP matrices)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"multiplicative diagonal (torus) action on the singular values with fixed singular vectors. Trained expert vectors \\(z_k\\) can be linearly mixed at inference.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Once \\(z\\) (or the mixture \\(z'\\)) is chosen, \\(W\\) is a fixed dense matrix; self-adaptation is a two-pass procedure that sets \\(z\\) before the second pass.","identity_at_init":"\\(z\\) starts near a constant value (the paper's hyperparameter table lists an initial mean of 0.1). The official code passes \\(z\\) through a sigmoid and renormalizes by \\(\\mathrm{sum}(\\sigma)/\\mathrm{sum}(\\sigma * z)\\), so a uniform \\(z\\) leaves \\(W_0\\) unchanged up to this normalization (approximately the identity).","key_idea":"Train one scaling vector over each weight's singular values with RL to get compact, composable 'expert' vectors. At test time a dispatcher identifies the task and mixes the experts, so the LLM adapts itself.","results_note":"On Llama-3-8B-Instruct, SVF beats LoRA on GSM8K (79.15 vs 77.18) and ARC-Easy while training less than 10% of LoRA's parameters.","relations":[{"target":"svdiff","type":"competes-with","note":"multiplicative instead of additive adjustment of singular values"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"far fewer parameters; experts can be mixed"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06252","https://arxiv.org/html/2501.06252","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SakanaAI/self-adaptive-llms/main/policy/base.py","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SakanaAI/self-adaptive-llms/main/utils.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Qi Sun","Edoardo Cetin","Yujin Tang"],"bibkey":"sun2025transformer","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sun2025transformer,\n  title = {{Transformer-Squared: Self-adaptive LLMs}},\n  author = {Qi Sun and Edoardo Cetin and Yujin Tang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2501.06252},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.06252}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Trained experts are points of the method; Transformer\\(^2\\)'s test-time mixing \\(z'=\\sum\\alpha_kz_k\\) is a family over the task simplex (\\(F_T\\)) built on it.","shape":"The paper's form \\(z\\mapsto U\\mathrm{diag}(\\sigma\\odot z)V^\\top\\) is linear and injective: the full torus closure on the spectrum, dimension \\(\\min(m,n)\\).","base_point":"\\(z=\\mathbf 1\\). The official code's sigmoid and \\(\\sum\\sigma/\\sum\\sigma z\\) renormalisation fix the nuclear norm (\\(d\\) drops by 1 and a 1-dimensional non-linear fibre appears), and its near-uniform init is a small defect.","covariance":"SVD frame: \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"The image is a linear space in the frozen SVD frame. A merge recovers the frames up to sign and order, so re-merging gains nothing: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"SVF trains a torus element acting on the spectrum of \\(W_0\\) in its frozen SVD frame; for \\(\\sigma>0\\) it is isomorphic to diagonal SVFT and, near \\(\\theta_0\\), to SVDiff. Transformer\\(^2\\)'s self-adaptation is a \\(T\\)-point of this linear method over the simplex of expert mixtures, where mixing parameters is legitimate because the map is of degree one.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"z\\mapsto\\sigma\\odot(z-\\mathbf 1)","note":"A pointed isomorphism onto diagonal SVFT (pointed at M = 0)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"svdiff","test":"O1","reason":"z_i < 0 flips the sign of sigma_i u_i v_i^T, which SVDiff's ReLU(sigma + delta) >= 0 never reaches; the two agree only as germs at theta_0."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"svft","name":"SVFT","full_name":"Singular Vectors guided Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"SVFT: Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Singular Vectors","authors":"Lingam et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.19597","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.19597","code_url":"https://github.com/VijayLingam95/SVFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=U(\\Sigma+M)V^{\\top}=W_0+UMV^{\\top},\\quad U,\\Sigma,V\\ \\text{from the SVD of frozen } W_0,\\ M\\ \\text{sparse with fixed pattern } \\Omega\\ \\text{(plain/diagonal, banded, random, top-}k\\text{)};\\ \\text{only } M_{\\Omega} \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"|\\Omega|\\ (+1\\ \\text{gate scalar per layer in the official code});\\ \\ \\min(d_{in},d_{out})\\ \\text{for SVFT}^{P}","locus":"any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sparse additive update in the singular-vector basis of \\(W_0\\): \\(\\Delta W = \\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\Omega} m_{ij} u_i v_j^T\\), a fixed dictionary that depends on the weight rather than a free low-rank factorization. A diagonal \\(\\Omega\\) reduces it to a spectral shift.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(U M V^T\\) is a fixed dense matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"Not exactly in the official code: the coefficients of \\(M\\) are Kaiming-normal initialized and multiplied by a learnable gate \\(\\mathrm{sigmoid}(g)\\) with \\(g = 0\\), so \\(\\Delta W = U (0.5 M_{\\mathrm{init}}) V^T\\) is non-zero at step 0. The paper does not state an initialization.","key_idea":"Use the outer products of \\(W_0\\)'s own singular vectors as a fixed dictionary and learn sparse coefficients for it, including off-diagonal ones. This trades parameters against expressivity smoothly.","results_note":"Recovers up to 96% of full fine-tuning performance while training 0.006-0.25% of parameters (e.g. Gemma-2B and LLaMA-3-8B on GSM8K/MATH); baselines reach at most 85% with 0.03-0.8%.","relations":[{"target":"svdiff","type":"generalizes","note":"a diagonal mask gives SVDiff's spectral shift without the ReLU"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"weight-dependent fixed basis instead of learned low-rank factors"},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"generalizes","note":"our observation: a dense top-left r x r block of M gives the LoRA-XS form"},{"target":"svdiff","type":"competes-with","note":"Plain SVFT (diagonal M) shifts singular values like SVDiff, but SVDiff clamps with ReLU(Sigma + delta) and SVFT does not, so neither strictly contains the other; SVFT does not cite SVDiff"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"both train O(d) parameters per matrix; SVFT uses weight-derived rather than random bases"},{"target":"frod","type":"competes-with","note":"FRoD builds on the U(Sigma+S)V^T form with a basis shared across layers"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.19597","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.19597","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/93272","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/VijayLingam95/SVFT/main/svft/svft_layers.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative","sharing"],"authors_full":["Vijay Lingam","Atula Tejaswi","Aditya Vavre","Aneesh Shetty","Gautham Krishna Gudur","Joydeep Ghosh","Alex Dimakis","Eunsol Choi","Aleksandar Bojchevski","Sujay Sanghavi"],"bibkey":"lingam2024svft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lingam2024svft,\n  title = {{SVFT: Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning with Singular Vectors}},\n  author = {Vijay Lingam and Atula Tejaswi and Aditya Vavre and Aneesh Shetty and Gautham Krishna Gudur and Joydeep Ghosh and Alex Dimakis and Eunsol Choi and Aleksandar Bojchevski and Sujay Sanghavi},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.19597},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.19597}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=U M_\\Omega V^T\\): linear, \\(d=|\\Omega|\\).","base_point":"Exactly pointed variant (table). The official code multiplies Kaiming-init \\(M\\) by \\(\\mathrm{sigmoid}(g)\\), \\(g=0\\), so it starts at a defect and adds one scalar slide \\((g,M)\\) per layer.","covariance":"SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"Frames frozen: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"SVFT is a coordinate mask \\(\\Omega\\) in the SVD frame of \\(W_0\\): \\(\\Delta W=\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\Omega}m_{ij}u_iv_j^\\top\\), linear and regular, a \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent transport of a sparse method. LoRA-XS is its dense top-left \\(r\\times r\\) block, and the diagonal mask SVFT\\(^P\\) shifts the spectrum at full rank.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(M)=U_{:,R}\\,M_{R,C},\\ \\ A_0=V_{:,C}^\\top","note":"R, C = row and column indices met by Omega; into LoRA-FA_{|C|} with frozen A_0=V_C^T, pointed at 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"svdiff","test":"O1","reason":"Im SVFT is not inside Im SVDiff = {U diag(s) V^T : s>=0}: SVFT^P reaches negative coefficients sigma_i+m_i<0 and off-diagonal Omega reaches non-spectral directions. The converse inclusion Im SVDiff in Im SVFT^P does hold (m=ReLU(sigma+delta)-sigma, checked to 2e-15), but only set-level: that h is not smooth, so it is not an arrow of Meth."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"SVFT^P (diagonal Omega) reaches rank min(m,n), above r."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"bidora","name":"BiDoRA","full_name":"Bi-level Optimization-Based Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"BiDoRA: Bi-level Optimization-Based Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Qin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.09758","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09758","code_url":"https://github.com/t2ance/BiDoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=\\operatorname{diag}(m)\\,\\frac{W_0+BA}{\\|W_0+BA\\|_{row}};\\quad \\min_{m}\\ \\mathcal{L}_{val}\\big(\\mathcal{V}^{*}(m),m\\big)\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ \\ \\mathcal{V}^{*}(m)=\\arg\\min_{\\mathcal{V}=\\{B,A\\}}\\ \\mathcal{L}_{tr}(\\mathcal{V},m)+\\gamma\\sum_k\\big\\|(V_k+\\Delta V_k)^{\\top}(V_k+\\Delta V_k)-I\\big\\|_F^2","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+d_{out}","locus":"any linear layer (as DoRA)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"DoRA's magnitude-direction parameterization, with magnitude and direction optimized at different levels of a bi-level program (hypergradient for the magnitude) and an orthogonality regularizer on the direction","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Same final parameterization as DoRA, so it folds into a single dense matrix.","identity_at_init":"As DoRA: \\(B = 0\\) and \\(m = \\|W_0\\|\\) per neuron.","key_idea":"Train DoRA's magnitude and direction asynchronously: the direction on the training split and the magnitude on a validation split via hypergradients, then refit the direction. This decouples the two further and reduces overfitting.","results_note":"Reports an 85.2 GLUE average on RoBERTa-base vs 84.6 for DoRA, a smaller train-test gap, and consistent gains over DoRA on NLU, NLG, token classification and extremely small biomedical datasets (Wilcoxon p = 2.4e-4 on GLUE).","relations":[{"target":"dora","type":"builds-on","note":"same parameterization, bi-level training"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09758","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.09758","https://groups.google.com/g/tmlr-announce-weekly/c/ORN_YuQljI8"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Peijia Qin","Ruiyi Zhang","Pengtao Xie"],"bibkey":"qin2024bidora","bibtex":"@article{qin2024bidora,\n  title = {{BiDoRA: Bi-level Optimization-Based Weight-Decomposed Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Peijia Qin and Ruiyi Zhang and Pengtao Xie},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2410.09758},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09758}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Sat","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Same \\(\\rho\\) as DoRA; the bi-level program (magnitude on a validation split by hypergradient) and the orthogonality penalty change only the update rule.","shape":"As DoRA (Prop II.8)."},"categorical_reading":"BiDoRA is a training recipe on the DoRA object: the same \\(\\rho=\\mathrm{diag}(m)N(W_0+BA)\\), with magnitude and direction assigned to different levels of a bi-level program and an orthogonality penalty on the direction. Image germ, fibre and merge type are unchanged; only the cotangent signal (hypergradient-driven, non-gradient) differs.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same parametrisation: an isomorphism."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"As DoRA: output rescalings lie outside LoRA_r's image."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Sat"},{"id":"ssf","name":"SSF","full_name":"Scaling & Shifting Features","paper_title":"Scaling & Shifting Your Features: A New Baseline for Efficient Model Tuning","authors":"Lian et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2022","arxiv":"2210.08823","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08823","code_url":"https://github.com/dongzelian/SSF","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y=\\gamma\\odot x+\\beta\\ \\text{after each operation (MSA, MLP, LayerNorm)};\\ \\text{after a linear op: } W=\\operatorname{diag}(\\gamma)\\,W_0,\\ b=\\gamma\\odot b_0+\\beta;\\ W_0,b_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ \\gamma,\\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2d\\ \\text{per SSF-wrapped operation}","locus":"after every operation of a ViT block (attention, MLP, LayerNorm)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"a per-channel affine map (diagonal scaling plus shift), i.e. an action of the diagonal affine group \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^d\\ltimes\\mathbb R^d\\) on output channels, re-parameterizable into the preceding linear layer","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The paper re-parameterizes \\((\\gamma, \\beta)\\) into the preceding linear/conv weight and bias, so inference runs the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\gamma \\sim N(1, 0.02)\\) and \\(\\beta \\sim N(0, 0.02)\\) in the official code (init_ssf_scale_shift): close to, but not exactly, the identity.","key_idea":"Fine-tuning a pretrained vision backbone can be reduced to learning a per-channel scale and shift of its features. These fold back into the weights, so there is no inference overhead.","results_note":"With ViT-B/16, reports 90.72% on FGVC and 73.10% on VTAB-1k (about 0.3M trainable parameters), above full fine-tuning (88.54% and 65.57%).","relations":[{"target":"ia3","type":"competes-with","note":"per-channel scaling, plus a shift, for vision models"},{"target":"bitfit","type":"generalizes","note":"SSF's shift is a bias update; SSF adds a multiplicative scale"},{"target":"glora","type":"special-case-of","note":"GLoRA reports SSF as a special case of its unified formulation"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08823","https://arxiv.org/html/2210.08823"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Dongze Lian","Daquan Zhou","Jiashi Feng","Xinchao Wang"],"bibkey":"lian2022scaling","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lian2022scaling,\n  title = {{Scaling \\& Shifting Your Features: A New Baseline for Efficient Model Tuning}},\n  author = {Dongze Lian and Daquan Zhou and Jiashi Feng and Xinchao Wang},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2210.08823},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.08823}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"After a linear op, \\(W=\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma)W_0\\), \\(b=\\gamma\\odot b_0+\\beta\\): the closure of an orbit of the diagonal affine group, linear.","base_point":"The exactly pointed variant \\((\\gamma,\\beta)=(\\mathbf 1,0)\\); the official init \\(N(1,0.02)\\), \\(N(0,0.02)\\) is a small defect.","merge":"Re-parametrised into the preceding linear box when it has a bias slot; in bias-free LLaMA-type layers the shift needs a new bias (Thm VI.3)."},"categorical_reading":"SSF is the diagonal affine group \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^d\\ltimes\\mathbb R^d\\) acting on output channels after every operation. Its orbit closure is linear: the output-side torus of (IA)\\(^3\\) plus a BitFit-type shift. It fuses into the preceding box only under the side conditions of Thm VI.3.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"glora","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(B_A=\\gamma-\\mathbf 1,\\ A_A=\\mathbf 1^\\top;\\ D=\\gamma-\\mathbf 1;\\ E=\\beta\\big)\\ \\text{(other paths at zero)}","note":"Post-linear wrappers only; GLoRA with D, E in vector mode (not the PEFT default), re-pointed at A_A = 1^T."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"The shift beta and the scaling of every output channel (including the outputs of W_up) lie outside (IA)^3's image."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"red","name":"RED","full_name":"Representation EDiting","paper_title":"Advancing Parameter Efficiency in Fine-tuning via Representation Editing","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.15179","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.726/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_2=l_{scaling}\\odot h_1+l_{bias},\\ h_1=\\text{output of each FFN sub-layer};\\ \\text{equivalently } W_{down}=\\operatorname{diag}(l_{scaling})\\,W_{down}^{0},\\ b=l_{scaling}\\odot b^{0}+l_{bias};\\ \\text{model frozen},\\ l_{scaling},l_{bias}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"2dL","locus":"outputs of the FFN sub-layer in every Transformer layer (ablations also edit Q, K, V)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"per-channel affine edit (diagonal scaling plus shift) of each FFN's contribution to the residual stream; the same algebraic form as SSF, applied to LLM FFN outputs","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The scaling always folds into the FFN down-projection; the shift folds into its bias only if that layer has one (LLaMA-style down-projections have no bias, so a bias term must be added).","identity_at_init":"\\(l_{scaling} = 1\\) and \\(l_{bias} = 0\\).","key_idea":"Instead of adding modules, edit hidden representations directly with one scaling vector and one bias vector per layer. This uses about 25,700x fewer parameters than full fine-tuning and about 32x fewer than LoRA, with no rank to tune.","results_note":"Comparable to or better than full fine-tuning and other PEFT methods on GLUE (RoBERTa, T5), E2E NLG (GPT-2) and LLaMA-2 instruction tuning (AlpacaEval) with far fewer parameters.","relations":[{"target":"ssf","type":"competes-with","note":"same scale-and-shift form applied to LLM FFN outputs"},{"target":"ia3","type":"competes-with","note":"IA3 scales keys/values/FFN activations without a shift"},{"target":"loreft","type":"competes-with","note":"both edit representations; RED's edit is a diagonal affine map"}],"sources":["https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.726/","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.726.pdf","https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15179"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Muling Wu","Wenhao Liu","Xiaohua Wang","Tianlong Li","Changze Lv","Zixuan Ling","Jianhao Zhu","Cenyuan Zhang","Xiaoqing Zheng","Xuanjing Huang"],"bibkey":"wu2024advancing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2024advancing,\n  title = {{Advancing Parameter Efficiency in Fine-tuning via Representation Editing}},\n  author = {Muling Wu and Wenhao Liu and Xiaohua Wang and Tianlong Li and Changze Lv and Zixuan Ling and Jianhao Zhu and Cenyuan Zhang and Xiaoqing Zheng and Xuanjing Huang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.15179},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.726/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_{\\rm down}=\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell_s)W^0_{\\rm down}\\), \\(b=\\ell_s\\odot b^0+\\ell_b\\): a torus-orbit closure plus a shift, linear.","merge":"The scale always fuses into \\(W_{down}\\); the shift needs a bias slot, which LLaMA-type down-projections lack."},"categorical_reading":"RED is SSF's scale-and-shift restricted to the FFN outputs of an LLM: an output-side diagonal affine action on \\(W_{\\rm down}\\) and its bias, linear and regular at \\(\\theta_0\\). The scale always fuses into \\(W_{\\rm down}\\); the shift needs a bias slot.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"ssf","rule":"A2","h":"(\\ell_s,\\ell_b)\\mapsto\\big((\\gamma,\\beta)_{\\rm FFN\\ out}=(\\ell_s,\\ell_b);\\ \\text{all other SSF wrappers at }(\\mathbf 1,0)\\big)","note":"SSF pointed at its exact identity."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"RED scales the output side of W_down (and shifts it), (IA)^3 its input side: {diag(l) W} and {W diag(l')} meet only in scalar multiples."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"difffit","name":"DiffFit","full_name":"DiffFit (bias tuning plus learnable residual scaling for diffusion transformers)","paper_title":"DiffFit: Unlocking Transferability of Large Diffusion Models via Simple Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Xie et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICCV 2023","arxiv":"2304.06648","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06648","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"x\\leftarrow x+\\gamma_1\\odot\\mathrm{Attn}(x),\\quad x\\leftarrow x+\\gamma_2\\odot\\mathrm{FFN}(x),\\ \\gamma_1,\\gamma_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ (\\text{init } \\mathbf{1});\\ \\text{trained: } \\gamma,\\ \\text{biases, normalization parameters, class embedding; all other weights frozen}","trainable_params":"\\approx 0.12\\%\\ \\text{of DiT-XL/2 (0.83M of 673.8M)}","locus":"learnable scales \\(\\gamma\\) on the residual branches of the first 14 DiT-XL/2 blocks, plus QKV-linear and final layers; all biases, normalization parameters and the class embedding are also tuned","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"diagonal scaling of each residual branch's output (foldable into the branch's output projection) combined with BitFit-style selective tuning of biases and normalization parameters","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\gamma\\) folds into the output projection weight and bias of the attention/FFN branch; the tuned biases and norms replace the old ones.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\gamma = 1\\), and biases and norms start from their pretrained values.","key_idea":"For diffusion transformers, tuning the biases plus a learnable per-channel scale on each residual branch is enough to transfer quickly to new domains and resolutions.","results_note":"About 2x faster training than full fine-tuning, storing about 0.12% of the parameters; FID 3.02 on ImageNet 512x512 after 25 epochs from a 256x256 checkpoint.","relations":[{"target":"bitfit","type":"builds-on","note":"bias tuning plus added scaling factors"},{"target":"ssf","type":"competes-with","note":"per-channel scaling of features in vision transformers"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06648","https://arxiv.org/html/2304.06648","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2023/html/Xie_DiffFit_Unlocking_Transferability_of_Large_Diffusion_Models_via_Simple_Parameter-efficient_ICCV_2023_paper.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Enze Xie","Lewei Yao","Han Shi","Zhili Liu","Daquan Zhou","Zhaoqiang Liu","Jiawei Li","Zhenguo Li"],"bibkey":"xie2023difffit","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xie2023difffit,\n  title = {{DiffFit: Unlocking Transferability of Large Diffusion Models via Simple Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Enze Xie and Lewei Yao and Han Shi and Zhili Liu and Daquan Zhou and Zhaoqiang Liu and Jiawei Li and Zhenguo Li},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2304.06648},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.06648}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(\\gamma\\odot(\\text{branch output})\\) slides through DiT's adaLN gate (R4) into the branch's output projection: a reparametrisation of the rows of \\(W_o,W_2\\), plus selective tuning of biases, norm parameters and the class embedding.","shape":"Row scalings (torus-orbit closure), bias translations and freed coordinates: linear.","base_point":"\\(\\gamma=\\mathbf 1\\) and pretrained values: exactly pointed, regular.","covariance":"Output-side torus on the scaled projections (\\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times GL\\)); \\(GL\\times GL\\) for the biases.","merge":"\\(\\gamma\\) fuses into the neighbouring output projection (R4 through the gate); biases and norms replace their old values."},"categorical_reading":"DiffFit is BitFit plus an output-side torus on each residual branch: \\(\\gamma\\odot\\mathrm{Attn}(x)\\) becomes \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma)W_o\\) after a spider slide through the adaLN gate, so the whole method is linear, regular and idempotent. It sits in the selective row because most of its coordinates are existing biases and norms.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"bitfit","test":"O1","reason":"The gamma row scalings change W_o and W_2, outside BitFit's bias-translation image."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"liera","name":"LieRA","full_name":"Lie-group Rank Adaptation (Lie-group-based generalized tensor PEFT)","paper_title":"Generalized Tensor-based Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Lie Group Transformations","authors":"Si et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICCV 2025","arxiv":"2504.00851","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00851","code_url":"https://github.com/Chongjie-Si/Subspace-Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W=W_0\\odot\\exp_{\\odot}(\\Delta W)\\approx W_0+W_0\\odot\\Delta W\\ \\ (\\text{first-order form used in all experiments}),\\quad \\Delta W=BA\\ (\\text{LoRA-factorized; conv kernels are treated as tensors}),\\ \\exp_{\\odot}\\ \\text{entry-wise};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen (non-zero entries)},\\ B,A\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"linear layers and higher-order (convolutional) kernels","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"action of the abelian Lie group of entry-wise non-zero tensors under the Hadamard product (identity = all-ones tensor) on \\(W_0\\). The update lives in its Lie algebra (all real tensors) and is mapped back through the entry-wise exponential; truncating at first order gives \\(W_0 + W_0 \\odot \\Delta W\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained first-order update \\(W_0 + W_0 \\odot BA\\) (or \\(W_0 \\odot \\exp(BA)\\)) is a fixed tensor of the original shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (\\(A\\) Gaussian), so \\(\\exp(0) = 1\\) and \\(W = W_0\\).","key_idea":"Treat parameters as elements of a Lie group under element-wise multiplication. Matrix PEFT updates (LoRA-style) then lift to high-dimensional (conv) tensors without flattening, via the exponential map from the Lie algebra.","results_note":"Reports gains over LoRA, e.g. a 75.5 vs 74.1 VTAB-1K average with ConvNeXt-V2-B (\\(r=16\\)), plus improvements on COCO detection, LLaMA commonsense reasoning and GLUE.","relations":[{"target":"hira","type":"equivalent-to","note":"LieRA's evaluated first-order form W_0 + W_0 (.) BA is HiRA's parameterization (concurrent work)"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"Delta W in the Lie algebra is LoRA-factorized"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00851","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.00851","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/ICCV2025/papers/Si_Generalized_Tensor-based_Parameter-Efficient_Fine-Tuning_via_Lie_Group_Transformations_ICCV_2025_paper.pdf"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Chongjie Si","Zhiyi Shi","Xuehui Wang","Yichen Xiao","Xiaokang Yang","Wei Shen"],"bibkey":"si2025generalized","bibtex":"@inproceedings{si2025generalized,\n  title = {{Generalized Tensor-based Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Lie Group Transformations}},\n  author = {Chongjie Si and Zhiyi Shi and Xuehui Wang and Yichen Xiao and Xiaokang Yang and Wei Shen},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2504.00851},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.00851}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Weight-space: the trained layer is \\(W_0\\odot\\exp_\\odot(BA)\\) (first-order form \\(W_0+W_0\\odot BA\\)) with the original architecture.","shape":"Classified in the evaluated first-order form, which is HiRA: \\(\\mathrm{Im}=W_0+D_{W_0}(\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\), the determinantal cone transported by \\(D_{W_0}:X\\mapsto W_0\\odot X\\) (Prop II.9(a)). The defining form is \\(\\Psi_*\\) of the same germ for the pointed diffeomorphism \\(\\Psi(W)=W_0\\odot\\exp_\\odot((W-W_0)\\oslash W_0)\\), with \\(d\\Psi_{\\theta_0}=\\mathrm{id}\\); it escapes HiRA's rank bound \\(r\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\).","base_point":"\\(B_0=0\\), \\(A_0\\) Gaussian: only \\(B\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4); the deficit is LoRA's \\(r(n-r)\\), transported by \\(D_{W_0}\\) (and unchanged by \\(\\Psi\\), since \\(d\\Psi_{\\theta_0}=\\mathrm{id}\\)).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the internal wire, \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\); \\(\\exp_\\odot\\) is injective, so the exponential form has the same fibres.","covariance":"Hadamard product with \\(W_0\\): covariant under monomial pairs only, never \\(GL\\times GL\\) (Prop II.9(e), HiRA).","rebasing":"First-order form = HiRA: \\(R_\\infty=W_0\\odot\\Theta=\\{W:Z(W_0)\\subseteq Z(W)\\}\\) (\\(=\\Theta\\) if \\(W_0\\) has no zeros), i.e. \\(\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\,C\\), but not transported ReLoRA. The defining form is a genuine action of the positive Hadamard torus: \\(W_0\\odot e^{Z_1}\\odot e^{Z_2}=W_0\\odot e^{Z_1+Z_2}\\), so it converges to the group orbit \\(W_0\\odot(\\mathbb R_{>0})^{m\\times n}\\) and never changes the sign or zero pattern of \\(W_0\\) (Thm II.5(5))."},"categorical_reading":"As evaluated, LieRA is HiRA, \\((D_{W_0})_*\\mathrm{LoRA}\\) inside the slice at \\(\\theta_0\\). Its defining form \\(W_0\\odot\\exp_\\odot(BA)\\) is the further non-linear transport \\(\\Psi_*\\) of that object: an action of the positive Hadamard torus \\((\\mathbb R_{>0})^{m\\times n}\\), with Lie algebra \\(\\Theta\\), restricted to \\(\\exp_\\odot\\) of a determinantal cone. That action preserves both the sign and the zero pattern of \\(W_0\\), however often it is merged.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B,A)","note":"Isomorphism: the first-order LieRA map \\(W_0\\odot(J+BA)\\) is HiRA's \\(\\rho\\), with the same pointing \\(B_0=0\\)."},{"target":"flora-batched","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\mathbf 1_m\\;B],\\,[\\mathbf 1_n^\\top;A]\\big)","note":"\\(W_0\\odot(J+BA)=W_0\\odot([\\mathbf 1\\,B][\\mathbf 1^\\top;A])\\): lands in FLoRA\\(_{r+1}\\) pointed at \\(([\\mathbf 1,0],[\\mathbf 1^\\top;A_0])\\), a factorisation of \\(J\\). FLoRA's \\(1/(r+1)\\) mean reduction is absorbed by scaling both factors by \\(\\sqrt{r+1}\\)."},{"target":"loha","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B,A,U_0,V_0^\\top),\\quad U_0V_0^\\top=W_0","note":"HiRA/LieRA is LoHa with its second Hadamard factor frozen at a rank-\\(\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\) factorisation of \\(W_0\\): \\(W_0+(BA)\\odot W_0\\). Lands in LoHa\\(_{r,\\operatorname{rk}W_0}\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0,U_0,V_0^\\top)\\), not HF's default pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(\\operatorname{rank}(W_0\\odot BA)\\) reaches \\(r\\operatorname{rk}W_0>r\\) (Prop II.9(a)); the exponential form reaches full rank, so neither embeds in LoRA\\(_r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"flora-batched","name":"FLoRA","full_name":"Fast LoRA (batched, example-specific multiplicative low-rank adaptation)","paper_title":"Batched Low-Rank Adaptation of Foundation Models","authors":"Wen & Chaudhuri","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2312.05677","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05677","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_i=W_0\\odot(B_iA_i),\\quad h_i=\\operatorname{reduce}_{j=1..r}\\Big[b^{(i)}_j\\odot\\big(W_0\\,(a^{(i)}_j\\odot x_i)\\big)\\Big]\\ (\\text{the paper uses a reduction such as torch.mean over the } r \\text{ terms}),\\ B_i=[b^{(i)}_1,\\dots,b^{(i)}_r]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_i^{\\top}=[a^{(i)}_1,\\dots,a^{(i)}_r]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r}\\ \\text{per example/adapter } i;\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapter}","locus":"linear layers, with per-request adapters in multi-tenant serving","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"entry-wise (Hadamard) modulation of \\(W_0\\) by a rank-\\(r\\) matrix. The rank-1 case is a two-sided diagonal scaling \\(\\mathrm{diag}(b)\\, W_0\\, \\mathrm{diag}(a)\\), an action of the torus \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^{d_{out}}\\times(\\mathbb R^\\times)^{d_{in}}\\); rank \\(r\\) is a sum of \\(r\\) such scalings, so a batch with different adapters needs only element-wise operations around one shared matmul.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"For a fixed adapter, \\(W_0 \\odot BA\\) can be precomputed; the point of the method is to keep it unmerged so different examples in a batch can use different adapters.","identity_at_init":"Not specified in the method section read. The parameterization reproduces \\(W_0\\) only when \\(B_iA_i\\) is the all-ones matrix (e.g. rank-1 all-ones vectors), not with LoRA's \\(B = 0\\).","key_idea":"Make each request's adapter multiply \\(W_0\\) entry-wise. A minibatch with heterogeneous adapters can then be served with cheap element-wise operations around one shared matmul, giving better throughput and latency than batched LoRA.","results_note":"Reports about 2x throughput over LoRA on StarCoder-15B when each request needs a different low-rank adapter, while keeping LoRA-level accuracy on MultiPL-E multilingual code generation and on multilingual speech recognition (Whisper).","relations":[{"target":"ia3","type":"generalizes","note":"the paper describes IA3 as a 'rank 0.5' special case that rescales only columns"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same parameter count, multiplicative instead of additive, built for heterogeneous batching"},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"HiRA's J + BA modulation is a FLoRA modulation of rank <= r+1"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05677","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.05677","https://openreview.net/forum?id=w4abltTZ2f"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"authors_full":["Yeming Wen","Swarat Chaudhuri"],"bibkey":"wen2023batched","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wen2023batched,\n  title = {{Batched Low-Rank Adaptation of Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Yeming Wen and Swarat Chaudhuri},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2312.05677},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05677}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"group"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per adapter a weight-space reparametrisation \\(W_0\\odot BA\\); per-request heterogeneous batching makes the serving layer an \\(F_U\\) family (as S-LoRA/Punica), but each adapter is an object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}\\).","shape":"Global image \\(D_{W_0}(\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\): a cone whose vertex is the zero matrix, not \\(\\theta_0\\). \\(\\theta_0=W_0\\odot J\\) corresponds to \\(J\\), a rank-one point, so the germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is \\(\\mathbb R^{m+n-1}\\times\\mathcal M_{\\le r-1}\\): non-linear and singular at \\(\\theta_0\\) for \\(r\\ge2\\). For \\(r=1\\) it is the two-sided torus orbit \\(\\mathrm{diag}(b)W_0\\mathrm{diag}(a)\\) (Orb, regular; Prop II.9(c)).","base_point":"The paper does not state an initialisation; \\(\\theta_0\\) is reached only at \\(B_0A_0=J\\). Any such pointing lies on the rank-one stratum, and \\(\\dim S_1=m\\operatorname{rk}A_0+n\\operatorname{rk}B_0-\\operatorname{rk}A_0\\operatorname{rk}B_0\\) with \\(\\operatorname{rk}A_0+\\operatorname{rk}B_0\\le r+1\\), so the deficit is at least \\((r-1)(\\min(m,n)-r)>0\\) for \\(2\\le r<\\min(m,n)\\). This is an apex in the deficit sense, though not the vertex: a degenerate pointing, like HF's orthogonal LoRA init. A random init would be a defect.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the internal wire, \\((Bg^{-1},gA)\\); the mean reduction is a constant.","covariance":"Hadamard modulation of \\(W_0\\): \\((D_1PW_0QD_2)\\odot X=D_1P(W_0\\odot P^{-1}XQ^{-1})QD_2\\), so monomial pairs only.","rebasing":"Pure action of the Hadamard monoid \\((\\Theta,\\odot)\\) (Thm V.3(b)): \\(R_K=W_0\\odot(\\mathcal M_{\\le r})^{\\odot K}\\subseteq W_0\\odot\\mathcal M_{\\le r^K}\\), so rank grows multiplicatively, not as \\(Kr\\). For \\(r\\ge2\\), the elements \\(J+tE_{ij}\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le2}\\) generate, so \\(R_\\infty=W_0\\odot\\Theta=\\{W:Z(W_0)\\subseteq Z(W)\\}\\) and zeros of \\(W_0\\) are never filled. For \\(r=1\\), rank-one matrices are closed under \\(\\odot\\), so FLoRA\\(_1\\) is idempotent (the closure of the two-sided torus orbit)."},"categorical_reading":"FLoRA is the Hadamard action \\(\\rho(B,A)=W_0\\odot BA\\) of the multiplicative monoid \\((\\Theta,\\odot)\\) on \\(W_0\\), pointed at a factorisation of \\(J\\). Rank one is the two-sided torus orbit, rank \\(r\\) is the transported determinantal cone with vertex at \\(0\\), and it and HiRA simulate each other with a one-step rank shift (\\(\\mathrm{FLoRA}_r\\to\\mathrm{HiRA}_{r+1}\\to\\mathrm{FLoRA}_{r+2}\\)). Its batching advantage is a property of the realisation (one shared matmul), not of the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\tfrac1rB\\;\\,{-\\mathbf 1_m}],\\,[A;\\mathbf 1_n^\\top]\\big)","note":"With the paper's mean reduction \\(\\rho(B,A)=W_0\\odot\\tfrac1rBA\\): \\(W_0\\odot\\tfrac1rBA=W_0\\odot(J+[\\tfrac1rB,-\\mathbf 1][A;\\mathbf 1^\\top])\\), so it lands in HiRA\\(_{r+1}\\) pointed at \\(([\\tfrac1rB_0,-\\mathbf 1],[A_0;\\mathbf 1^\\top])\\), whose product \\(\\tfrac1rB_0A_0-J\\) vanishes (drop the \\(\\tfrac1r\\) for a plain sum). This is not HiRA's default \\(B=0\\) pointing."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(W_0\\odot BA-W_0=W_0\\odot(BA-J)\\) has rank up to \\((r+1)\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\), far above \\(r\\)."},{"target":"s-lora","test":"O2","reason":"Both serve per-request adapters over one shared matmul, but the LoRAs S-LoRA serves have \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W\\le r\\), while FLoRA's updates reach rank \\((r+1)\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\)."},{"target":"flora-opt","test":"O1","reason":"A name clash only. Per period, Flora (Hao et al.) is the linear one-sided method \\(\\{W_k+XA_t\\}\\), with the rows of \\(\\Delta W\\) confined to \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_t\\); FLoRA's \\(W_0\\odot BA\\) has unconfined rows and a curved Hadamard image."}],"table_row":"R-Hadamard","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"quantum-peft","name":"Quantum-PEFT","full_name":"Quantum-PEFT (Pauli-parameterized unitary adaptation)","paper_title":"Quantum-PEFT: Ultra parameter-efficient fine-tuning","authors":"Koike-Akino et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2503.05431","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05431","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + U\\Lambda V^{\\top},\\quad U=Q_P(\\theta_U)\\,I_{d_{out}\\times K},\\ V=Q_P(\\theta_V)\\,I_{d_{in}\\times K},\\ \\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K\\times K},\\ Q_P=\\prod_{l=1}^{L}\\Big(\\big(I\\otimes\\mathrm{CZ}^{\\otimes\\frac{q-1}{2}}\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{k=2}^{q}\\mathrm{RY}(\\theta_{k,2l+1})\\big)\\big(\\mathrm{CZ}^{\\otimes\\frac{q-1}{2}}\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{k=1}^{q-1}\\mathrm{RY}(\\theta_{k,2l})\\otimes I\\big)\\Big)\\textstyle\\bigotimes_{k=1}^{q}\\mathrm{RY}(\\theta_{k,1}),\\ q=\\log_2 d;\\ \\theta_U,\\theta_V,\\lambda\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"(2L+1)(\\log_2 d_{in}+\\log_2 d_{out})-4L+K\\ \\ \\text{(Pauli parameterization: } (2L+1)\\log_2 N-2L \\text{ per unitary, plus } K \\text{ diagonal entries)}","locus":"same targets as LoRA (attention/linear layers of language and vision transformers)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"SVD-form update whose orthogonal factors are quantum-circuit (Pauli) parameterizations: Kronecker products of \\(2\\times 2\\) RY rotations interleaved with diagonal \\(\\pm1\\) CZ entanglers, so parameters grow logarithmically in \\(d\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(U \\Lambda V^\\top\\) is a dense matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not stated explicitly in the sections consulted (AdaLoRA-style SVD form; a zero diagonal \\(\\Lambda\\) would give \\(\\Delta W = 0\\)).","key_idea":"Parameterize the singular-vector frames by a small quantum circuit, so trainable parameters scale like \\(\\log d\\) instead of \\(d\\).","results_note":"Reports orders of magnitude fewer trainable parameters than the lowest-rank LoRA with competitive accuracy on language and vision transfer benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"adalora","type":"builds-on","note":"same UΛV^T SVD-form update"},{"target":"boft","type":"competes-with","note":"both build orthogonal matrices from products of sparse/Kronecker-structured factors"},{"target":"quanta","type":"competes-with","note":"both quantum-circuit-inspired tensor networks"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"logarithmic instead of linear parameter growth"},{"target":"polar","type":"competes-with","note":"both use Stiefel-frame x core factorizations; Quantum-PEFT generates the frames from a small Lie-group parameterization"},{"target":"adalora","type":"competes-with","note":"both use an SVD-shaped P Lambda Q additive update"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.05431","https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05431","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2503.05431","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.05431v1","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Quantum-PEFT%20Ultra%20parameter-efficient","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.05431"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative","shape"],"authors_full":["Toshiaki Koike-Akino","Francesco Tonin","Yongtao Wu","Frank Zhengqing Wu","Leyla Naz Candogan","Volkan Cevher"],"bibkey":"koikeakino2025quantum","bibtex":"@inproceedings{koikeakino2025quantum,\n  title = {{Quantum-PEFT: Ultra parameter-efficient fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Toshiaki Koike-Akino and Francesco Tonin and Yongtao Wu and Frank Zhengqing Wu and Leyla Naz Candogan and Volkan Cevher},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2503.05431},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05431}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(U\\Lambda V^\\top\\), with \\(U,V\\) the first \\(K\\) columns of Pauli circuits, is linear in \\(\\lambda\\) and trigonometric in \\(\\theta\\). The image is closed under scaling (through \\(\\lambda\\)): a non-linear cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) inside \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le K}\\).","base_point":"The init is not stated. The natural pointing \\(\\Lambda_0=0\\) (AdaLoRA-style) is a zero gate: \\(S_1=\\{U_0\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta\\lambda)V_0^\\top\\}\\) of dimension \\(K\\), less than \\(d(M)\\), so an apex.","gauge":"Not injective in general. The truncated frames \\(U=Q_P(\\theta_U)I_{d\\times K}\\) only see the circuit on \\(\\mathrm{span}(e_1,\\dots,e_K)\\), where the leading qubits are still in \\(|0\\rangle\\), so for \\(K<d/2\\) some rotation angles are redundant [Num, reconstruction of Eq. 2 with \\(q=3\\)-6, \\(L=1\\)-3: generic fibre 1-2 per circuit when each RY sub-layer follows its entangler, 6-7 per circuit if RY sub-layers are adjacent; 0 at \\(K=d/2\\)]. This fibre is not the orbit of a linear group on internal wires, so the value is 'nonlinear'. Apart from it there are only discrete sign flips \\((\\lambda_k,u_k)\\mapsto(-\\lambda_k,-u_k)\\).","covariance":"RY and CZ layers act on the qubit factorisation \\(\\mathbb R^d=(\\mathbb R^2)^{\\otimes q}\\): an imposed tensor-product structure (\\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\)).","rebasing":"Scaling through \\(\\lambda\\) makes \\(C\\) a cone, and the circuit frames' columns span \\(\\mathbb R^d\\), so \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,C=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Quantum-PEFT is AdaLoRA's SVD-shaped object \\(P\\Lambda Q\\) precomposed with a low-dimensional map into Stiefel frames generated by a qubit circuit. This is an A3 arrow into AdaLoRA and LoRA; the parameter count is logarithmic because the frames are confined to the image of a short Lie-group word, which imposes \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\theta_U,\\theta_V,\\lambda)=\\big(P=Q_P(\\theta_U)I_{d_{out}\\times K},\\;\\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda),\\;Q=(Q_P(\\theta_V)I_{d_{in}\\times K})^\\top\\big)","note":"Into rank-K AdaLoRA pointed at \\((U_0,0,V_0^\\top)\\): orthonormal circuit frames instead of AdaLoRA's Gaussian P, Q."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(U(\\theta_U)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda),\\;V(\\theta_V)^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_K\\) pointed at \\((0,V_0^\\top)\\) when \\(\\Lambda_0=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"boft","test":"O1","reason":"BOFT is an action method that preserves |cos| between neurons (and the Gram structure up to its output scale); Quantum-PEFT is additive and changes spectra, although both build orthogonal matrices from Kronecker-structured factors."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"p-tuning-v2","name":"P-Tuning v2","full_name":"P-Tuning v2 (deep prompt tuning for NLU)","paper_title":"P-Tuning v2: Prompt Tuning Can Be Comparable to Fine-tuning Universally Across Scales and Tasks","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2110.07602","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.8/","code_url":"https://github.com/THUDM/P-tuning-v2","hf_peft":true,"hf_peft_config":"PrefixTuningConfig","formula_latex":"\\mathrm{head}^{(l)}=\\mathrm{Attn}\\big(XW_q^{(l)},\\,[P_k^{(l)};XW_k^{(l)}],\\,[P_v^{(l)};XW_v^{(l)}]\\big)\\ \\ \\forall l\\le L,\\quad \\hat y=\\mathrm{softmax}(W_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\,h_{\\mathrm{tok}});\\ \\ P^{(l)}\\ (\\text{optionally } \\mathrm{MLP}\\text{-reparametrized}),\\ W_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\ \\text{trained};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"2L\\ell d+d\\,|\\mathcal{Y}|\\ \\ (\\approx 0.1\\%\\text{--}3\\%\\ \\text{of the model})","locus":"keys/values at every layer (deep prompts) plus a randomly initialized linear head on token representations","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Learned key/value vectors prepended at every layer (identical parameter shape to prefix-tuning), with a linear classification head instead of a verbalizer, task-dependent prompt length (short for classification, ~100 for sequence labeling) and optional reparametrization / multitask initialization.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Same as prefix-tuning: \\(\\ell\\) extra key/value slots per layer that cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No identity; random initialization, optionally a multitask-pretrained prompt as initialization.","key_idea":"Inserting prompts at every layer (not just the input) gives more capacity and more direct influence on predictions, making prompt tuning match fine-tuning even for 300M-scale models and for hard sequence-labeling tasks.","results_note":"Matches fine-tuning across model scales from 300M to 10B parameters and on hard sequence tagging (NER, extractive QA, SRL) while tuning 0.1%-3% of parameters.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"equivalent-to","note":"Self-described implementation of deep prompt tuning (Li & Liang 2021; Qin & Eisner 2021) adapted to NLU."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"Prompt tuning is the input-layer-only special case."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative PEFT method for NLU (not compared in the P-Tuning v2 paper; prefix-tuning vs. LoRA is compared by He et al. 2022)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07602","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.07602","https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.8/","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/v0.4.0/README.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Xiao Liu","Kaixuan Ji","Yicheng Fu","Weng Lam Tam","Zhengxiao Du","Zhilin Yang","Jie Tang"],"bibkey":"liu2021p","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2021p,\n  title = {{P-Tuning v2: Prompt Tuning Can Be Comparable to Fine-tuning Universally Across Scales and Tasks}},\n  author = {Xiao Liu and Kaixuan Ji and Yicheng Fu and Weng Lam Tam and Zhengxiao Du and Zhilin Yang and Jie Tang},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2110.07602},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.8/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Deep key/value prompts (same parameter shape as prefix tuning) plus a new linear head; the head is an additive Lin factor on a new box and does not change the kind.","base_point":"As in the table (row 'Prefix / P-tuning v2'). Soft tokens always take softmax mass, and on a head with \\(W_oW_v\\ne0\\) no prompt leaves the output unchanged, so no prompt reproduces \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\) (Thm VI.5(f)).","gauge":"Direct parameterisation (reparametrisation optional, off by default): the prefix has only discrete slot permutations. The softmax head is injective up to its kernel translations \\(W_{\\mathrm{cls}} \\mapsto W_{\\mathrm{cls}} + \\mathbf 1c^T\\) (and \\(b \\mapsto b + c'\\mathbf 1\\) with a bias), which leave every class posterior unchanged: \\(d\\) (+1) dimensions, recorded under \"none\" as for linear methods (Prop II.12).","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable. The head itself is a linear box.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Deep prompt tuning: the unpointed key/value extension of prefix tuning in the direct parameterisation, summed with a new linear head. Prompt tuning maps into it in causal decoders (Thm VI.5(e)); whether that inclusion is strict is open.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O1","reason":"Head-level (Thm VI.5(f)): a generic prefixed head with \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) is non-affine on length-one contexts while every weight setting is affine; whole-network containment open."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"warp","name":"WARP","full_name":"Word-level Adversarial ReProgramming","paper_title":"WARP: Word-level Adversarial ReProgramming","authors":"Hambardzumyan et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2101.00121","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.381/","code_url":"https://github.com/YerevaNN/WARP","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"P_\\Theta(y\\mid x)=\\frac{\\exp\\big(\\Theta^{V}_{y}\\cdot f(T_{\\Theta^P}(x))\\big)}{\\sum_{i\\in C}\\exp\\big(\\Theta^{V}_{i}\\cdot f(T_{\\Theta^P}(x))\\big)},\\quad \\Theta^P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d}\\ \\text{(prompt embeddings inserted by template } T\\text{ around a [MASK])},\\ \\Theta^V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|C|\\times d}\\ \\text{(verbalizer embeddings)}\\ \\text{trained; MLM body frozen}","trainable_params":"(\\ell+|C|)\\,d","locus":"input embedding sequence (template positions around a [MASK] token) and the output verbalizer vectors of the MLM head","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Adversarial reprogramming in word-embedding space: learned input embeddings at template positions plus learned class vectors that replace output-embedding rows of the masked-LM head (an input-side and an output-side reprogramming map around a frozen body).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Prompt embeddings lengthen the input; only the verbalizer vectors act like a (new) linear head.","identity_at_init":"No identity; prompt tokens initialized with the [MASK] embedding or embeddings similar to the vocabulary; verbalizers from the MLM head.","key_idea":"Treat task adaptation as adversarial reprogramming: search continuous prompt and verbalizer embeddings by SGD so a frozen masked LM's [MASK] prediction solves the task.","results_note":"With up to 25K trainable parameters per task on RoBERTa-large it reaches 81.6 on the GLUE test leaderboard, beating submissions with up to three orders of magnitude more trainable parameters; strong few-shot SuperGLUE results with ALBERT.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Concurrent continuous-prompt method; adds learned output verbalizers."},{"target":"visual-prompting","type":"dual-to","note":"Same adversarial-reprogramming principle in text embedding space vs. pixel space."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.00121","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2101.00121"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Karen Hambardzumyan","Hrant Khachatrian","Jonathan May"],"bibkey":"hambardzumyan2021warp","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hambardzumyan2021warp,\n  title = {{WARP: Word-level Adversarial ReProgramming}},\n  author = {Karen Hambardzumyan and Hrant Khachatrian and Jonathan May},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2101.00121},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.381/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Sum of an unpointed input-prompt extension of a masked LM and a Lin edit of the \\(|C|\\) verbalizer rows of the output head.","base_point":"Prompts initialised from [MASK]/vocab embeddings still draw attention mass (bidirectional encoder), so unpointed; the verbalizer half starts at the MLM-head rows.","gauge":"Prompt coordinates enter injectively. The verbalizer is read through a softmax over the \\(|C|\\) classes, so a common shift \\(\\Theta^V \\to \\Theta^V + \\mathbf 1c^T\\) changes every logit by \\(c\\cdot f\\) and leaves the posterior unchanged: a \\(d\\)-dimensional kernel translation, recorded under \"none\" as for linear methods (Prop II.12); no other continuous fibre.","covariance":"Function-level: prompts and the new class rows are full vector spaces on the embedding wire (table has no entry).","merge":"Prompt half M∞ (it lengthens the input); the verbalizer half is a head edit that would fuse (M1).","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Adversarial reprogramming as a sum of two pieces: an unpointed input-prompt extension of a masked LM and a Lin edit of the class rows of the output head (Trainable Tokens on the head). The prompt summand makes the whole object unpointed and M\\(\\infty\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"trainable-tokens","test":"O3","reason":"Its verbalizer half is Trainable Tokens on head rows, but the prompt half makes WARP unpointed, so no pointed arrow in either direction; A5 holds only in the unpointed slice."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"ppt","name":"PPT","full_name":"Pre-trained Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"PPT: Pre-trained Prompt Tuning for Few-shot Learning","authors":"Gu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2109.04332","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.576/","code_url":"https://github.com/thu-coai/PPT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"P^\\star=\\arg\\min_{P}\\ \\sum_{(X,Y)\\in\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{self-sup}}}-\\log\\Pr_\\theta\\big(Y\\mid[P;X]\\big)\\ \\text{(unified pre-training tasks built from unlabeled text)};\\ \\ \\text{then prompt tuning on the target task initialized at } P^\\star;\\ \\theta\\ \\text{frozen throughout}","trainable_params":"\\ell d","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Vanilla soft prompt whose initialization is obtained by pre-training the prompt alone on self-supervised tasks cast in the same formats as downstream tasks (sentence-pair, multiple-choice, single-sentence classification, or a unified multiple-choice format).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"At inference it is an ordinary soft prompt that lengthens the input.","identity_at_init":"No identity; the contribution is the initialization itself (a prompt pre-trained on the PLM's own pre-training-style objective).","key_idea":"Prompt tuning fails in few-shot regimes mainly because of poor initialization, so pre-train the soft prompt on large unlabeled corpora using task formats that mirror downstream classification.","results_note":"With T5-XXL and CPM-2 (~11B), tuning pre-trained prompts reaches or exceeds full-model fine-tuning in both full-data and few-shot settings, far above vanilla prompt tuning in few-shot.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same parameterization; adds prompt pre-training as initialization."},{"target":"spot","type":"competes-with","note":"Self-supervised prompt pre-training versus supervised source-task transfer."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04332","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.04332"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Yuxian Gu","Xu Han","Zhiyuan Liu","Minlie Huang"],"bibkey":"gu2021ppt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gu2021ppt,\n  title = {{PPT: Pre-trained Prompt Tuning for Few-shot Learning}},\n  author = {Yuxian Gu and Xu Han and Zhiyuan Liu and Minlie Huang},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2109.04332},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.576/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Recipe: prompt tuning with a pre-trained initial prompt; coordinates of prompt tuning.","base_point":"Unpointed; \\(q_0 = P^\\star\\) from self-supervised prompt pre-training (the contribution is this pointing).","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning re-pointed: \\((Q,\\rho)\\) is that of prompt tuning and only the base point \\(q_0=P^\\star\\) changes, obtained by pre-training the prompt on self-supervised tasks in downstream formats. In the framework a change of pointing is a genuine change of object (cf. Type I versus Type II LoRA).","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Isomorphism onto prompt tuning pointed at \\(P^\\star\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"spot","name":"SPoT","full_name":"Soft Prompt Transfer","paper_title":"SPoT: Better Frozen Model Adaptation through Soft Prompt Transfer","authors":"Vu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2110.07904","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.346/","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/prompt-tuning/tree/main/prompt_tuning/spot","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"P_{s}=\\arg\\min_P\\mathcal{L}_{s}\\big([P;X]\\big),\\quad P_{t}^{(0)}\\leftarrow P_{s^\\star},\\quad P_t^\\star=\\arg\\min_P\\mathcal{L}_{t}\\big([P;X]\\big);\\ \\ s^\\star=\\arg\\max_s\\cos\\Big(\\tfrac1\\ell\\textstyle\\sum_i e^{s}_i,\\ \\tfrac1\\ell\\sum_j e^{t}_j\\Big)\\ \\text{(early-checkpoint prompts as task embeddings; a per-token average cosine is the alternative metric)};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell d","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Vanilla soft prompt initialized from a prompt trained on a source task (or multi-task mixture); early-checkpoint prompts double as task embeddings whose cosine similarity predicts transferability for source retrieval.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"At inference it is an ordinary soft prompt.","identity_at_init":"No identity; initialization from a source-task prompt is the method.","key_idea":"Soft prompts are transferable task representations: train them on source tasks, use them to initialize target prompts, and use prompt similarity as a cheap task-similarity metric to choose sources.","results_note":"Across all T5 sizes, SPoT matches or outperforms full model tuning on SuperGLUE while using up to 27,000x fewer task-specific parameters; a 26-task, 160-pair transfer study shows widespread positive transfer.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same parameterization; adds source-to-target prompt initialization."},{"target":"attempt","type":"competes-with","note":"ATTEMPT mixes many source prompts per instance instead of picking one for initialization."},{"target":"multitask-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"MPT distils many source prompts into one shared prompt."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07904","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.07904"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Tu Vu","Brian Lester","Noah Constant","Rami Al-Rfou","Daniel Cer"],"bibkey":"vu2021spot","bibtex":"@inproceedings{vu2021spot,\n  title = {{SPoT: Better Frozen Model Adaptation through Soft Prompt Transfer}},\n  author = {Tu Vu and Brian Lester and Noah Constant and Rami Al-Rfou and Daniel Cer},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2110.07904},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.346/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Recipe: prompt tuning initialised from a retrieved source-task prompt; coordinates of prompt tuning.","base_point":"Unpointed; \\(q_0 = P_{s^*}\\), the source prompt chosen by cosine similarity of early-checkpoint prompts.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis. The retrieval metric (cosine of mean prompt tokens) is \\(CO(d)\\)-invariant.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning re-pointed at a source-task prompt \\(P_{s^\\star}\\). The retrieval step compares candidate base points by a cosine, a \\(CO(d)\\)-invariant comparison of points of one unpointed slice.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Isomorphism onto prompt tuning pointed at \\(P_{s^\\star}\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"attempt","name":"ATTEMPT","full_name":"ATTEntional Mixtures of Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"ATTEMPT: Parameter-Efficient Multi-task Tuning via Attentional Mixtures of Soft Prompts","authors":"Asai et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EMNLP 2022","arxiv":"2205.11961","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.446/","code_url":"https://github.com/AkariAsai/ATTEMPT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"P_{\\mathrm{inst}}(X)=P_{\\mathrm{tgt}}+\\sum_{j=1}^{t+1}a_j(X)\\,P_j,\\quad a_j=\\frac{\\exp(\\hat P_j^{\\top}H_{\\mathrm{out}}/T)}{\\sum_{k=1}^{t+1}\\exp(\\hat P_k^{\\top}H_{\\mathrm{out}}/T)},\\quad H_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathrm{LN}\\big(W_{up}^{\\top}\\,\\mathrm{SiLU}(W_{down}^{\\top}\\hat X)\\big),\\ P_{t+1}=P_{\\mathrm{tgt}};\\ \\ \\text{input }[P_{\\mathrm{inst}}(X);X];\\ \\text{train } P_{\\mathrm{tgt}},W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r},W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d};\\ \\text{source prompts } P_{1..t}\\ \\text{and LM frozen}","trainable_params":"d\\big(\\ell+2(r+1)/N\\big)\\ \\text{per task (attention module shared across } N \\text{ target tasks)}","locus":"input embedding sequence (instance-specific prompt)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Instance-wise softmax mixture (a point of the simplex) over a library of frozen source prompts plus the trainable target prompt, added to the target prompt; mixture weights come from a bottleneck network on the max-pooled input (\\(\\hat X\\), \\(\\hat P_j\\) are max-pooled).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prompt depends on the input instance and lengthens the sequence; it can never be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"No identity; source prompts pre-trained by prompt tuning on large source tasks, target prompt newly initialized.","key_idea":"Treat pre-trained soft prompts as modular knowledge units and learn, per input, an attention-weighted interpolation of them with a new target prompt, sharing the router across tasks.","results_note":"On 21 datasets with T5-base, significantly outperforms prompt tuning and matches or beats full fine-tuning and other PEFT methods that use over 10x more parameters, while updating ~2,300x fewer parameters than fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"spot","type":"generalizes","note":"SPoT picks one source prompt for initialization; ATTEMPT mixes all sources per instance."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Source and target prompts are vanilla soft prompts."},{"target":"multitask-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative multitask prompt-transfer scheme."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.11961","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2205.11961"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Akari Asai","Mohammadreza Salehi","Matthew E. Peters","Hannaneh Hajishirzi"],"bibkey":"asai2022attempt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{asai2022attempt,\n  title = {{ATTEMPT: Parameter-Efficient Multi-task Tuning via Attentional Mixtures of Soft Prompts}},\n  author = {Akari Asai and Mohammadreza Salehi and Matthew E. Peters and Hannaneh Hajishirzi},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2205.11961},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.446/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\): the router maps each input to a point \\(a(X)\\) of the simplex and the prompt \\(P_{\\rm tgt}+\\sum_j a_j(X)P_j\\) is a \\(T\\)-point of prompt tuning over inputs.","base_point":"Instance prompts always draw attention mass; no parameter value reproduces \\(f(\\theta_0,-)\\).","gauge":"The \\(d\\)-dimensional router output \\(H_{out}=\\mathrm{LN}(W_{up}^T\\,\\mathrm{SiLU}(W_{down}^T\\hat X))\\) is read only through the \\(t+1\\) dot products \\(\\hat P_j^T H_{out}\\), with \\(t+1 \\ll d\\). So \\(\\beta\\) matters only through \\(\\hat P\\beta\\), and \\((W_{up}, \\gamma)\\) only through the \\(t\\times r\\) matrix \\(C\\hat P\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma)\\,\\Pi W_{up}^T\\) and the Gram \\(W_{up}\\Pi W_{up}^T\\) (\\(\\Pi\\) the centring of LN, \\(C\\) the centring of the logits). Generic router fibre: \\((d-t)(r+1)+d-r(r+1)/2+1\\). Exhibited Lie part: the LN scale \\(W_{up} \\mapsto cW_{up}\\), the translations \\(W_{up} \\mapsto W_{up}+u\\mathbf 1^T\\), and \\(\\beta\\)-translations in \\(\\{w: \\hat Pw \\in \\mathbb R\\mathbf 1\\}\\); the rest is point-dependent (\\(\\hat P_{t+1}=\\mathrm{maxpool}(P_{\\rm tgt})\\) is trainable). Not an orbit of a linear group, so nonlinear. When \\(t+1 > d\\) only the scalar slide and translations remain. SiLU allows only permutations (Lemma VI.2).","covariance":"Max-pooling over tokens is coordinatewise, so only (positive) monomial changes of the embedding basis commute with the router.","merge":"Input-dependent prompt that lengthens the sequence (routed families are M∞ unless the gate is constant).","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A family over inputs \\(F_X\\): the router gives a point \\(a(X)\\) of a simplex and the prompt \\(P_{\\rm tgt}+\\sum_ja_j(X)P_j\\) is a convex mixture of a frozen prompt library plus a trainable offset, fed to prompt tuning. Input dependence takes it outside every fixed-prompt image, and max-pooling makes the router coordinatewise.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"Its prompt varies with X through the router, so generically its realisation is not f(θ0,[P;-]) for any single P."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"dept","name":"DePT","full_name":"Decomposed Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"DePT: Decomposed Prompt Tuning for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Shi & Lipani","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2309.05173","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05173","code_url":"https://github.com/ShiZhengyan/DePT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{input } \\big[P_s;\\ E_X+AB\\big],\\quad P_s\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell'\\times d},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s\\times r},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d},\\ E_X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{s\\times d}\\ \\text{frozen word embeddings of the input padded to the maximum length } s;\\ \\ \\ell' d+(s+d)r=\\ell d;\\ \\ A\\sim\\mathcal{N},\\ B=0;\\ \\text{learning rates } \\alpha_1(P_s)\\gg\\alpha_2(A,B);\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell' d+(s+d)\\,r","locus":"input embedding sequence: a shorter soft prompt plus a position-wise offset to the input token embeddings","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Short soft prompt (concatenation) plus a rank-\\(r\\) additive, position-indexed offset \\(AB\\) to the frozen \\(s\\times d\\) input-embedding matrix, i.e. a LoRA-style low-rank update applied to the input activation matrix rather than to a weight (the paper writes the product as \\(BA\\); dimensionally it is the \\(s\\times d\\) matrix \\(A B\\)).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The short prompt still lengthens the input; the positional offset \\(AB\\) could only be folded into absolute position embeddings, which T5-style relative-position models lack.","identity_at_init":"The low-rank offset is zero at init (\\(B=0\\)); the short prompt is not an identity.","key_idea":"Trade prompt length for a low-rank update of the input embeddings: same parameter budget, shorter sequences, so lower time/memory, trained with two learning rates.","results_note":"Across 23 NLP and vision-language tasks, outperforms vanilla prompt tuning and other PEFT methods (sometimes full fine-tuning) while saving over 20% training/inference time and memory relative to prompt tuning with T5-Base.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Splits the prompt budget between a shorter prompt and a low-rank embedding offset."},{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Imports the BA low-rank, zero-initialized update, applied to the input embedding matrix."},{"target":"multitask-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are low-rank prompt decompositions evaluated on the same 23-task suite."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05173","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.05173"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Zhengxiang Shi","Aldo Lipani"],"bibkey":"shi2023dept","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shi2023dept,\n  title = {{DePT: Decomposed Prompt Tuning for Parameter-Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Zhengxiang Shi and Aldo Lipani},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2309.05173},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.05173}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Sum of an unpointed short prompt and an additive position-indexed offset \\(AB\\) on the frozen \\(s\\times d\\) input-embedding matrix; the prompt summand fixes the kind.","shape":"Function-level overall; the offset summand alone is LoRA\\(_r\\) on an \\(s\\times d\\) position-indexed slot (a cone with apex at \\(B=0\\)). It is a weight only where the model has learned absolute position embeddings; T5 has none.","base_point":"Unpointed (short prompt); the offset summand sits at the apex \\(B=0\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the offset factors, \\((A,B)\\mapsto(Ag,g^{-1}B)\\); the prompt adds nothing.","covariance":"Function-level: prompt basis-free; the offset image \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r}(s\\times d)\\) is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant.","merge":"Prompt half M∞; the offset folds (M1) only into learned absolute position embeddings, which T5-style models lack.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing. The offset summand alone would rebase to its span."},"categorical_reading":"A sum of an unpointed short prompt and a LoRA\\(_r\\) offset \\(AB\\) on an \\(s\\times d\\) position-indexed slot added to the input embeddings (a learned absolute position table where one exists), the latter pointed at the apex \\(B=0\\). The prompt summand makes the sum unpointed, so no pointed arrow relates DePT to LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Its offset half is LoRA_r on a position-indexed s×d slot (a weight only for models with learned absolute position embeddings, not T5), pointed at the apex, but its prompt half is unpointed, so 'DePT = prompt + LoRA' holds only in the unpointed slice, not as a pointed arrow."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"residual-prompt-tuning","name":"Residual Prompt Tuning","full_name":"Residual Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Residual Prompt Tuning: Improving Prompt Tuning with Residual Reparameterization","authors":"Razdaibiedina et al.","year":2023,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2023","arxiv":"2305.03937","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.421/","code_url":"https://github.com/arazd/ResidualPrompts","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"P'_i=\\Phi(P_i)=\\mathrm{LN}\\big(\\mathrm{ReLU}(P_iW_{down})\\,W_{up}\\big)+P_i,\\ \\ i=1,\\dots,\\ell\\ \\ (P_i\\ \\text{a row vector});\\ \\ \\text{input }[P';X];\\ \\ \\text{train } P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d},\\ W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times m},\\ W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times d},\\ \\mathrm{LN};\\ \\text{after training store only } P'=\\Phi(P);\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell d+2dm+2d\\ \\text{(training: prompt, } W_{down},W_{up}\\text{ and LayerNorm, as counted in the paper)};\\ \\ \\ell d\\ \\text{(stored)}","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Soft prompt reparametrized through a shared bottleneck MLP with LayerNorm and a residual skip, applied token-wise; the reparametrization is discarded after training (an over-parameterization that only changes the optimization geometry).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"At inference only the projected prompt \\(P'\\) remains, which still lengthens the input.","identity_at_init":"No model-level identity; the residual skip lets each virtual token choose between its free embedding and the shared projection, improving robustness to learning rate and initialization.","key_idea":"Prompt tuning is unstable because prompt tokens are optimized independently; a shared residual MLP couples them and speeds and stabilizes training at no inference cost.","results_note":"Significantly outperforms prompt tuning on SuperGLUE with T5-Large, T5-Base and BERT-Base (e.g., +7 points with T5-Base), allows 10x shorter prompts without loss, and helps in few-shot settings.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same inference-time object; different training parameterization."},{"target":"p-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Prompt-encoder reparametrization, with an added skip connection."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Inherits the MLP-reparametrization trick used for prefixes."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.03937","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.03937","https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.421/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Anastasia Razdaibiedina","Yuning Mao","Rui Hou","Madian Khabsa","Mike Lewis","Jimmy Ba","Amjad Almahairi"],"bibkey":"razdaibiedina2023residual","bibtex":"@inproceedings{razdaibiedina2023residual,\n  title = {{Residual Prompt Tuning: Improving Prompt Tuning with Residual Reparameterization}},\n  author = {Anastasia Razdaibiedina and Yuning Mao and Rui Hou and Madian Khabsa and Mike Lewis and Jimmy Ba and Amjad Almahairi},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2023},\n  eprint = {2305.03937},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.421/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Only \\(\\Phi(P)\\) is stored; \\(P\\mapsto P+g(P)\\) with bounded \\(g\\) (LayerNorm output) is onto, so the prompt image is all of \\(\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}\\).","gauge":"Training parameters \\((P,W_{\\rm down},W_{\\rm up},\\mathrm{LN})\\): generic fibre of dimension \\(2dm+2d\\), far larger than its exhibited Lie part (ReLU positive torus on the bottleneck, LN scale and shift on \\(W_{\\rm up}\\)).","covariance":"Image-level: full prompt space, basis-free at function level; the ReLU/LN basis affects only the dynamics.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning precomposed with a residual 2-cell \\(\\Phi(P)=\\mathrm{LN}(\\mathrm{ReLU}(PW_{\\rm down})W_{\\rm up})+P\\). It has the same inference object and prompt image, a non-linear fibre of dimension \\(2dm+2d\\), and a changed cometric \\(D\\Phi\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\Phi^\\top\\) (Obs III.2).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,W_{\\rm down},W_{\\rm up},\\mathrm{LN})=\\mathrm{LN}\\big(\\mathrm{ReLU}(PW_{\\rm down})W_{\\rm up}\\big)+P","note":"The reparametrisation itself; prompt tuning pointed at \\(\\Phi_0(P_0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"late-prompt-tuning","name":"LPT","full_name":"Late Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Late Prompt Tuning: A Late Prompt Could Be Better Than Many Prompts","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2022,"venue":"Findings of EMNLP 2022","arxiv":"2210.11292","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.findings-emnlp.95/","code_url":"https://github.com/xyltt/LPT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h^{(l^\\ast)}\\leftarrow\\big[p(x);\\,h^{(l^\\ast)}\\big],\\quad p(x)=W_2\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}\\big(\\mathrm{Pool}_{n\\to\\ell}(W_1h^{(l^\\ast)}+b_1)\\big)+b_2\\ \\ (\\text{pooling generator}),\\ \\ \\text{or}\\ \\ p(x)=\\mathrm{Reshape}\\big(W_2\\,\\mathrm{ReLU}(W_1h_{[\\mathrm{CLS}]}+b_1)+b_2\\big)\\ (\\text{naive});\\ \\ W_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times d};\\ \\text{single insertion layer } l^\\ast\\ (\\text{e.g. 13 of 24});\\ \\text{backbone frozen}","trainable_params":"\\approx 2dm+m+d\\ \\ \\text{(pooling generator)};\\ \\ \\approx dm+\\ell dm\\ \\ \\text{(naive generator)}","locus":"hidden-state sequence at a single intermediate layer","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Instance-dependent prompt generated by a bottleneck MLP (with optional pooling from input length \\(n\\) to prompt length \\(\\ell\\)) from the hidden states just below the insertion layer, inserted once at an intermediate layer.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prompt is input-dependent and adds tokens to the upper half of the network.","identity_at_init":"No identity.","key_idea":"There is a trade-off between how far the label signal must propagate back to the prompt and how much the prompt can influence outputs; a single 'late' prompt in a middle layer, generated from the instance's own hidden states, balances both and skips backprop through the lower layers.","results_note":"Competitive with full model tuning and other PEFT methods in full-data and few-shot settings (RoBERTa-large among backbones), with faster training and lower memory than prompt tuning.","relations":[{"target":"idpg","type":"builds-on","note":"Instance-dependent prompt generation, moved to an intermediate layer and fed by in-network hidden states."},{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Same budget class; inserts later to shorten the gradient path."},{"target":"p-tuning-v2","type":"competes-with","note":"One late prompt instead of prompts at every layer."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11292","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.11292","https://aclanthology.org/2022.findings-emnlp.95/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Xiangyang Liu","Tianxiang Sun","Xuanjing Huang","Xipeng Qiu"],"bibkey":"liu2022late","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2022late,\n  title = {{Late Prompt Tuning: A Late Prompt Could Be Better Than Many Prompts}},\n  author = {Xiangyang Liu and Tianxiang Sun and Xuanjing Huang and Xipeng Qiu},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2022},\n  eprint = {2210.11292},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.findings-emnlp.95/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"F\\(_X\\): a bottleneck hypernetwork maps the hidden states below layer \\(l^*\\) to a point of single-layer prompt space (cf. X-LoRA's hidden-state gating).","base_point":"Inserted tokens always draw attention mass; no parameter value removes them.","gauge":"ReLU generator: positive diagonal slide \\((W_1,b_1,W_2)\\mapsto(DW_1,Db_1,W_2D^{-1})\\) on the \\(m\\) hidden units (Lemma VI.2(b)); average pooling commutes with \\(D\\).","covariance":"Function-level: the generator reads \\(h\\) through a dense \\(W_1\\) and outputs a full prompt; pooling acts on positions, not on the embedding basis.","merge":"Input-dependent tokens added to the upper half of the network.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A family over inputs: a bottleneck hypernetwork reads the layer-\\(l^\\ast\\) hidden states and outputs a point of single-layer prompt space, inserted at \\(l^\\ast\\). It is unpointed and input-dependent, so it is neither mergeable nor in the image of a static prompt.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"idpg","name":"IDPG","full_name":"Instance-Dependent Prompt Generation","paper_title":"IDPG: An Instance-Dependent Prompt Generation Method","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NAACL 2022","arxiv":"2204.04497","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.403/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_p(T,x)=G\\big(M(x),T\\big)=W_2\\,\\sigma\\big(W_1M(x)+b_1\\big)+b_2\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d},\\quad \\hat y=\\mathrm{softmax}(W h_{[\\mathrm{CLS}]}),\\ h_{[\\mathrm{CLS}]}=M\\big(\\mathrm{concat}[x,W_p(T,x)]\\big);\\ \\ W_{1,2}\\ \\text{PHM-factorised as } \\textstyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i\\otimes B_i;\\ M\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"m(d+1)+\\ell d(m+1)\\ \\text{(dense generator)}+\\text{classification head};\\ \\ \\text{generator reduced by }\\approx n\\times\\text{ with PHM layers (e.g. 1.5M}\\to\\text{105K)}","locus":"input embedding sequence (optionally multiple layers, M-IDPG)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Two-layer bottleneck hypernetwork mapping a frozen sentence embedding \\(M(x)\\) to an \\(\\ell\\)-token prompt; its second-layer bias is exactly a task-level soft prompt, so the generated prompt = task prompt + instance-dependent part; weights parameterized as sums of Kronecker products (PHM).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The prompt is input-dependent and requires an extra forward pass to compute \\(M(x)\\).","identity_at_init":"No identity.","key_idea":"Condition the soft prompt on each input sentence through a lightweight generator, recovering vanilla prompt tuning as the bias term.","results_note":"On ten NLU tasks with RoBERTa-large, consistently outperforms prompt-tuning baselines and is on par with Compacter while tuning far fewer parameters.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"The generator's second-layer bias is a task-specific prompt; zero first layer recovers prompt tuning."},{"target":"compacter","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses PHM (Kronecker-sum) layers from Compacter to compress the generator."},{"target":"late-prompt-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both generate instance-dependent prompts."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.04497","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.04497"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Zhuofeng Wu","Sinong Wang","Jiatao Gu","Rui Hou","Yuxiao Dong","V. G. Vinod Vydiswaran","Hao Ma"],"bibkey":"wu2022idpg","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2022idpg,\n  title = {{IDPG: An Instance-Dependent Prompt Generation Method}},\n  author = {Zhuofeng Wu and Sinong Wang and Jiatao Gu and Rui Hou and Yuxiao Dong and V. G. Vinod Vydiswaran and Hao Ma},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2204.04497},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.403/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\): a \\(T\\)-point of prompt tuning over inputs, \\(x\\mapsto b_2+W_2\\sigma(W_1M(x)+b_1)\\); its \\(W_1=0\\) slice is prompt tuning.","base_point":"Generated prompt tokens always draw attention mass.","gauge":"PHM layers \\(\\sum_i A_i\\otimes B_i\\) are \\(R^{-1}_*\\) of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_n\\) (Prop II.13), so \\(GL_n\\) acts on the PHM index, as in the table's Compacter row.","covariance":"PHM imposes a Kronecker reshape \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\) of the generator weights (Compacter precedent).","merge":"Input-dependent prompt plus an extra frozen forward pass for \\(M(x)\\).","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A \\(T\\)-point of prompt tuning over inputs, \\(x\\mapsto b_2+W_2\\sigma(W_1M(x)+b_1)\\), with PHM (Kronecker-sum) weights; its \\(W_1=0\\) slice is prompt tuning. PHM imposes \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\) and a \\(GL_n\\) gauge on the PHM index, as for Compacter.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"The prompt depends on M(x), so generically the realisation is not f(θ0,[P;-]) for any fixed P."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"xprompt","name":"XPrompt","full_name":"XPrompt (extremely small prompts via hierarchical structured pruning)","paper_title":"XPrompt: Exploring the Extreme of Prompt Tuning","authors":"Ma et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EMNLP 2022","arxiv":"2210.04457","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.758/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat P=\\gamma\\cdot P,\\ \\gamma\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\ell}\\ \\text{(token mask)};\\quad \\hat q=\\zeta\\cdot q,\\ \\zeta\\in\\{0,1\\}^{k}\\ \\text{(piece mask; each } p_i \\text{ split into } k \\text{ pieces)};\\quad I_{p_i}=\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\Big|\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}(x)}{\\partial\\gamma_i}\\Big|,\\ I_{q_i}=\\mathbb{E}_{x}\\Big|\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}(x)}{\\partial\\zeta_i}\\Big|;\\ \\text{prune low-importance units, rewind survivors to post-PT values, retrain};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\rho\\,\\ell d,\\ \\ \\rho<1\\ \\text{(fraction of tokens/pieces kept)}","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Structured sparse mask on a soft prompt at two granularities (whole tokens, then equal-size pieces of each token embedding), found by gradient-sensitivity importance scores and lottery-ticket weight rewinding.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The pruned prompt is still a prompt that lengthens the input.","identity_at_init":"No identity; starts from a fully trained vanilla prompt (stage 1), then prunes and rewinds.","key_idea":"Some trained prompt tokens hurt the task; finding a 'winning ticket' sub-prompt by hierarchical structured pruning gives a smaller and better prompt.","results_note":"On SuperGLUE with T5, largely closes the gap between prompt tuning and fine-tuning at moderate model scales (below 11B) while using fewer prompt parameters.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Prunes and retrains a vanilla soft prompt."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.04457","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.04457"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Fang Ma","Chen Zhang","Lei Ren","Jingang Wang","Qifan Wang","Wei Wu","Xiaojun Quan","Dawei Song"],"bibkey":"ma2022xprompt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ma2022xprompt,\n  title = {{XPrompt: Exploring the Extreme of Prompt Tuning}},\n  author = {Fang Ma and Chen Zhang and Lei Ren and Jingang Wang and Qifan Wang and Wei Wu and Xiaojun Quan and Dawei Song},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2210.04457},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.758/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A structured coordinate mask on prompt space (tokens, then equal pieces of each token).","base_point":"Unpointed; pointed at the rewound post-prompt-tuning values of the surviving units.","covariance":"Piece masks split each embedding into contiguous coordinate blocks, a block partition the network does not have (Lemma VI.2 test), so \\(\\Pi\\).","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing. With the mask fixed."},"categorical_reading":"A structured mask on prompt space: the winning sub-prompt is an A2 sub-object of prompt tuning, with pruned tokens and pieces frozen at \\(0\\), pointed at the rewound trained prompt. Piece masks impose a block partition of the embedding coordinates.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(P_S)=\\big(P_S,\\,0_{S^c}\\big)","note":"Pruned tokens/pieces frozen at 0 (XPrompt's γ·P keeps them as zero vectors); prompt tuning pointed at the rewound masked prompt. If pruned tokens are instead dropped from the sequence, the same A2 arrow lands in prompt tuning of the kept length, with only the pruned pieces frozen at 0."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"black-box-tuning","name":"BBT","full_name":"Black-Box Tuning","paper_title":"Black-Box Tuning for Language-Model-as-a-Service","authors":"Sun et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICML 2022","arxiv":"2201.03514","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/sun22e.html","code_url":"https://github.com/txsun1997/Black-Box-Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"p=Az+p_0,\\quad z^\\star=\\arg\\min_{z\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k}}\\ \\mathcal{L}\\big(f(Az+p_0;\\tilde X),\\tilde Y\\big),\\quad A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell d\\times k}\\ \\text{fixed random},\\ p_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell d}\\ \\text{(random vocab embeddings or an MNLI-pretrained prompt)};\\ z\\ \\text{searched by CMA-ES with forward (API) queries only; model frozen, no gradients}","trainable_params":"k\\ \\ (\\text{e.g. } k=500)","locus":"input embedding sequence","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"Soft prompt restricted to a random \\(k\\)-dimensional affine subspace \\(p_0+\\mathrm{col}(A)\\) (intrinsic-dimension reparametrization), optimized by derivative-free CMA-ES.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The result is a soft prompt that lengthens the input.","identity_at_init":"No identity; \\(z=0\\) gives the initial prompt \\(p_0\\).","key_idea":"Because large PLMs have low intrinsic dimension, a soft prompt can be optimized in a small random subspace by gradient-free evolution strategies, using only inference API access (Language-Model-as-a-Service).","results_note":"With RoBERTa-large on few-shot classification, black-box tuning significantly outperforms manual prompts and GPT-3 in-context learning and surpasses gradient-based prompt tuning and full model tuning.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same object (soft prompt), optimized gradient-free in a random subspace."},{"target":"mezo","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are zeroth-order adaptation of frozen LMs; MeZO perturbs weights, BBT a low-dimensional prompt."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.03514","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.03514"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Tianxiang Sun","Yunfan Shao","Hong Qian","Xuanjing Huang","Xipeng Qiu"],"bibkey":"sun2022black","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sun2022black,\n  title = {{Black-Box Tuning for Language-Model-as-a-Service}},\n  author = {Tianxiang Sun and Yunfan Shao and Hong Qian and Xuanjing Huang and Xipeng Qiu},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2201.03514},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/sun22e.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Function-level; in prompt space the reach is the affine subspace \\(p_0+\\mathrm{col}(A)\\) of dimension \\(k\\).","base_point":"\\(z=0\\) gives the prompt \\(p_0\\) (random vocab embeddings or an MNLI prompt), which moves the output.","gauge":"\\(A \\in \\mathbb R^{\\ell d\\times k}\\) with \\(k \\ll \\ell d\\) is injective almost surely.","covariance":"Fixed random Gaussian projection \\(A\\): covariance only in law.","merge":"Soft tokens / KV slots lengthen the sequence; for \\(W_o\\ne0\\), \\(W_q\\ne0\\) a generically prefixed head is non-affine on length-one contexts (Thm VI.5(f)), so not box-mergeable.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning restricted to a random affine subspace \\(p_0+\\mathrm{col}\\,A\\), the prompt-space analogue of intrinsic-dimension and frozen-frame methods; covariance holds only in law. CMA-ES is a derivative-free update on \\(Q=\\mathbb R^k\\) and does not change the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(z)=Az+p_0","note":"Prompt tuning pointed at p_0; a monomorphism (A injective)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mezo","test":"O3","reason":"Both are zeroth-order, but MeZO moves weights from θ0 (regular base point in Meth) while BBT moves a prompt of an unpointed extension; base points and slices differ."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"vpt","name":"VPT","full_name":"Visual Prompt Tuning","paper_title":"Visual Prompt Tuning","authors":"Jia et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ECCV 2022","arxiv":"2203.12119","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.12119","code_url":"https://github.com/KMnP/vpt","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{Shallow: } [x_1,Z_1,E_1]=L_1([x_0,P,E_0]),\\ [x_i,Z_i,E_i]=L_i([x_{i-1},Z_{i-1},E_{i-1}]);\\quad \\text{Deep: } [x_i,\\_\\,,E_i]=L_i([x_{i-1},P_{i-1},E_{i-1}]),\\ i=1,\\dots,L;\\quad y=\\mathrm{Head}(x_L);\\ \\ P,P_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\ell\\times d}\\ \\text{and Head trained; ViT frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell d\\ \\text{(shallow)};\\ \\ L\\ell d\\ \\text{(deep)};\\ \\ +\\ \\text{head}","locus":"token sequence after the patch embedding (shallow) / input of every Transformer layer (deep)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Learned tokens inserted into the residual stream of a ViT. In VPT-Deep the prompt outputs are discarded after each layer, so their only effect on image tokens is as extra keys/values \\(\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_k,\\ \\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_v\\): a prefix tied through the frozen projections (derived from the equations here).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Prompts add tokens at every layer (deep) or at the input (shallow).","identity_at_init":"No identity; prompts initialized with Xavier-uniform (class-prototype initialization was not better).","key_idea":"Transfer NLP prompt tuning to vision Transformers: a handful of learnable tokens per layer plus a head adapts a frozen ViT, often better than full fine-tuning.","results_note":"With ViT-B/16 (ImageNet-21k), VPT-Deep outperforms full fine-tuning on 20 of 24 tasks (FGVC + VTAB-1k) and all other PEFT protocols, while storing 0.04%/0.53% extra parameters per task (shallow/deep).","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"VPT-Shallow is input-level prompt tuning transplanted to vision Transformers."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"VPT-Deep equals a deep prefix whose keys/values are constrained to the image of the frozen LN+W_k, W_v projections."},{"target":"p-tuning-v2","type":"builds-on","note":"VPT-Deep transfers deep prompt tuning (Li & Liang; P-Tuning v2, both cited) to vision Transformers."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.12119","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.12119"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Menglin Jia","Luming Tang","Bor-Chun Chen","Claire Cardie","Serge Belongie","Bharath Hariharan","Ser-Nam Lim"],"bibkey":"jia2022visual","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jia2022visual,\n  title = {{Visual Prompt Tuning}},\n  author = {Menglin Jia and Luming Tang and Bor-Chun Chen and Claire Cardie and Serge Belongie and Bharath Hariharan and Ser-Nam Lim},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2203.12119},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.12119}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Prompt tokens in a ViT plus a trainable linear head; VPT-Deep (the main variant) replaces the prompt tokens at every layer.","base_point":"Xavier-initialised tokens draw attention mass; no neutral value.","gauge":"VPT-Deep: a layer's prompt acts only through \\(\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_k\\), \\(\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_v\\), so each token has a positive scaling \\(c_j\\) (a torus over tokens) plus kernel-type shifts along \\(\\mathbf 1\\). VPT-Shallow: none.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Prompts add tokens at every layer (deep) or at the input (shallow).","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"VPT-Shallow is prompt tuning on a ViT; VPT-Deep is a deep prefix tied through the frozen maps \\(P\\mapsto(\\mathrm{LN}(P)W_k,\\mathrm{LN}(P)W_v)\\), plus a new linear head. The LN tie adds a per-token scaling gauge, and Thm VI.5(e) (shallow into deep) does not apply because ViTs are bidirectional.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\mathrm{Head})=\\Big(\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_k^{(i)}+\\mathbf 1b_k^{(i)\\top},\\,\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_v^{(i)}+\\mathbf 1b_v^{(i)\\top}\\big)_{i\\le L},\\,\\mathrm{Head}\\Big)","note":"VPT-Deep: prompt outputs are discarded each layer, so image tokens see them only as extra keys/values; deep prompts with a linear head, pointed at h(q0)."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"visual-prompting","name":"Visual Prompting","full_name":"Pixel-space Visual Prompting","paper_title":"Exploring Visual Prompts for Adapting Large-Scale Models","authors":"Bahng et al.","year":2022,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2203.17274","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.17274","code_url":"https://github.com/hjbahng/visual_prompting","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\max_{\\phi}\\ \\log P_{\\theta;\\phi}\\big(y\\mid x+v_\\phi\\big),\\quad v_\\phi\\ \\text{a single pixel-space perturbation shared by all images (padding frame of width } p\\text{, or a patch)};\\ \\ \\theta\\ \\text{frozen; labels via CLIP text prompts}","trainable_params":"2Cp(H+W-2p)\\ \\text{(padding)};\\ \\ Cp^{2}\\ \\text{(patch)}","locus":"input pixels","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"An additive translation of input space, \\(x\\mapsto x+v_\\phi\\), supported on a border frame (or a patch).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"For a ViT with a fixed-location prompt (padding or fixed patch) the patch embedding \\(E\\) is linear, so \\(E(x+v)=Ex+Ev\\) and the per-patch constant \\(Ev\\) folds exactly into the positional embeddings (derived here, not claimed in the paper); the random-location patch variant cannot be merged, and CNN backbones would need a per-position bias map after the first convolution, which is not a standard parameter.","identity_at_init":"No identity at initialization: the official code initializes \\(v_\\phi\\) with a standard normal (torch.randn); \\(v_\\phi=0\\) would recover the frozen model exactly.","key_idea":"Adapt a frozen vision model by learning a single input perturbation (adversarial reprogramming as prompting), with the output mapped to labels through text prompts for CLIP.","results_note":"Particularly effective for CLIP, where a padding prompt (\\(p=30\\) best) is competitive with linear probing and robust to distribution shift.","relations":[{"target":"warp","type":"builds-on","note":"Adversarial reprogramming lineage, in pixel space."},{"target":"vpt","type":"dual-to","note":"Pixel-space prompts vs. token-space prompts."},{"target":"coop","type":"competes-with","note":"Image-side vs. text-side learned prompting of a frozen CLIP for the same classification task (not compared head-to-head in Bahng et al.)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.17274","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.17274"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Hyojin Bahng","Ali Jahanian","Swami Sankaranarayanan","Phillip Isola"],"bibkey":"bahng2022exploring","bibtex":"@article{bahng2022exploring,\n  title = {{Exploring Visual Prompts for Adapting Large-Scale Models}},\n  author = {Hyojin Bahng and Ali Jahanian and Swami Sankaranarayanan and Phillip Isola},\n  year = {2022},\n  eprint = {2203.17274},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2203.17274},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.17274}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1->","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A new translation box \\(x\\mapsto x+v_\\phi\\) on the pixel wire, with neutral point \\(v=0\\): a pointed extension.","shape":"Affine in \\(v\\) on a fixed border/patch support; on a ViT the linear patch embedding sends it to a linear subspace of the positional-embedding table.","base_point":"The official code initialises \\(v \\sim N(0,1)\\) (torch.randn), so \\(\\rho(q_0) \\ne \\theta_0\\): a pointing defect; \\(v=0\\) is neutral.","gauge":"Injective in pixel space; after a ViT patch embedding \\(E\\) (e.g. \\(3\\cdot32\\cdot32 \\to 768\\)) the function sees only \\(Ev\\), adding translations by \\(\\ker E\\) as for linear methods (Prop II.12).","covariance":"The support is a hand-specified spatial region (border frame or patch), fixed by image geometry and not derived from \\(\\theta_0\\) or drawn at random. On a ViT, permuting the pixels inside every patch together with the patch-embedding columns preserves the function but moves the partially covered frame rows, and on a CNN the frame breaks the convolution's translation structure: imposed structure, \\(\\Pi\\) (Lemma VI.2 test), not a \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\) mask chosen per entry.","merge":"ViT, fixed location: \\(E(x+v)=Ex+Ev\\) slides by R1 onto the positional embeddings (an additive slot). Random-location patches and CNNs (no per-position bias slot) are M∞.","rebasing":"Translations compose additively on a fixed support: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed extension by a translation box \\(x\\mapsto x+v\\) on the pixel wire, neutral at \\(v=0\\) but initialised randomly, hence a pointing defect. On a ViT with a fixed prompt location the linear patch embedding slides it onto the positional-embedding table (R1), so there it is a Lin method.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"vpt","test":"O1","reason":"f(θ0) = ρ(v=0) lies in the image of visual prompting, while no VPT parameter reproduces f(θ0) (extra tokens always draw attention mass), so Im VP ⊄ Im VPT; and the pixel shift acts as an additive input change, not as new keys/values. 'Dual' lineages, not related by an arrow."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"coop","name":"CoOp","full_name":"Context Optimization","paper_title":"Learning to Prompt for Vision-Language Models","authors":"Zhou et al.","year":2021,"venue":"IJCV 2022","arxiv":"2109.01134","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11263-022-01653-1","code_url":"https://github.com/KaiyangZhou/CoOp","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"t_i=[V]_1[V]_2\\cdots[V]_{\\ell}[\\mathrm{CLASS}_i],\\qquad p(y=i\\mid x)=\\frac{\\exp\\big(\\cos(g(t_i),f(x))/\\tau\\big)}{\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\exp\\big(\\cos(g(t_j),f(x))/\\tau\\big)};\\ \\ [V]_m\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\ \\text{trained (unified or class-specific)};\\ \\text{CLIP encoders } f,g\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell d\\ \\text{(unified context)};\\ \\ K\\ell d\\ \\text{(class-specific context)}","locus":"input embedding sequence of the CLIP text encoder","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Soft prompt (shared or per-class context vectors) prepended to each class-name token sequence; the classifier weights are the frozen text encoder's images \\(g(t_i)\\) of the prompted class names.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"In closed-set classification the \\(K\\) prompted class embeddings \\(g(t_i)\\) are computed once after training and cached as a linear classifier, so inference costs exactly as much as zero-shot CLIP; the learned context itself cannot be folded into the text encoder's weights, and any new class name requires re-running the text encoder with the prompt.","identity_at_init":"Can be initialized from the embedding of a hand-crafted context such as 'a photo of a' (starting at the zero-shot classifier) or randomly.","key_idea":"Replace hand-engineered CLIP prompts by learnable context vectors optimized end-to-end through the frozen text encoder.","results_note":"On 11 image-recognition datasets, one or two shots suffice to beat hand-crafted prompts, and with 16 shots the average gain is around 15%.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Prompt tuning applied to the text tower of a contrastive vision-language model."},{"target":"cocoop","type":"special-case-of","note":"CoCoOp with the meta-net output fixed to zero."},{"target":"maple","type":"special-case-of","note":"MaPLe with prompt depth J=1 and no vision prompts degenerates to CoOp."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.01134","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.01134","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1007/s11263-022-01653-1"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Kaiyang Zhou","Jingkang Yang","Chen Change Loy","Ziwei Liu"],"bibkey":"zhou2021learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhou2021learning,\n  title = {{Learning to Prompt for Vision-Language Models}},\n  author = {Kaiyang Zhou and Jingkang Yang and Chen Change Loy and Ziwei Liu},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {IJCV},\n  eprint = {2109.01134},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11263-022-01653-1}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1->","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Prompt tuning on CLIP's text tower, one context shared across the \\(K\\) class sequences (class-specific: \\(K\\) independent contexts).","base_point":"Unpointed relative to the text encoder; with 'a photo of a' initialisation it starts at zero-shot CLIP, i.e. pointed relative to the template (as CPT is at the ICL context). Random init is the default.","gauge":"Unified context: injective generically; no continuous symmetry.","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Closed set of class names: the realisation factors through the \\(K\\times d\\) head \\(W(V)=[g([V;c_i])]_i\\), which is cached as a linear classifier (no inference cost); a new class name needs the text encoder again (M∞ there).","rebasing":"The closed-set merge caches the head \\(W(V)\\), on which no further context can act (a new context re-runs the text encoder). Re-prompting with \\(V\\) frozen concatenates contexts, so \\(R_K\\) is CoOp at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"Prompt tuning on CLIP's text tower with a context shared across class sequences. In closed-set use the realisation factors through the \\(K\\times d\\) head \\(W(V)=[g([V;c_i])]_i\\), so CoOp is a non-linear reparametrisation of the zero-shot head that fuses into it, pointed at zero-shot CLIP under 'a photo of a' initialisation.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Same parameterisation on the CLIP text tower (context in front of the class tokens), shared across the K class sequences."},{"target":"cocoop","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=\\big(V,\\ \\theta\\ \\text{with}\\ W_2=0,\\ b_2=0\\big)","note":"Meta-net output layer frozen at 0, so h_θ(f(x))=0; CoCoOp pointed at that zero-output meta-net."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"maple","test":"O1","reason":"CoOp leaves the image features f(x) untouched; MaPLe always inserts vision prompts, which (being unpointed tokens) generically change f(x) for every parameter value, so 'J=1 MaPLe = CoOp' is architectural, not an arrow."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"cocoop","name":"CoCoOp","full_name":"Conditional Context Optimization","paper_title":"Conditional Prompt Learning for Vision-Language Models","authors":"Zhou et al.","year":2022,"venue":"CVPR 2022","arxiv":"2203.05557","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05557","code_url":"https://github.com/KaiyangZhou/CoOp","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"v_m(x)=v_m+h_\\theta\\big(f(x)\\big),\\ \\ t_i(x)=\\{v_1(x),\\dots,v_\\ell(x),c_i\\},\\qquad p(y\\mid x)=\\frac{\\exp\\big(\\mathrm{sim}(f(x),g(t_y(x)))/\\tau\\big)}{\\sum_{i=1}^{K}\\exp\\big(\\mathrm{sim}(f(x),g(t_i(x)))/\\tau\\big)};\\ \\ h_\\theta=\\text{Linear-ReLU-Linear (16}\\times\\text{ bottleneck)};\\ \\text{train } v_{1..\\ell},\\theta;\\ \\text{CLIP frozen}","trainable_params":"\\ell d+\\tfrac{d_v}{16}\\,(d_v+d)\\ \\ (\\text{plus biases; } d_v=\\text{image-feature width})","locus":"input embedding sequence of the CLIP text encoder, conditioned on the image feature","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Context vectors shifted by one image-conditioned token \\(\\pi=h_\\theta(f(x))\\) produced by a lightweight meta-network (an input-dependent translation of the shared prompt).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Class embeddings depend on the image, so the text encoder must be re-run for all \\(K\\) classes per image (much higher inference cost than CoOp).","identity_at_init":"No identity; reduces to CoOp when \\(h_\\theta\\) outputs zero.","key_idea":"Static learned prompts overfit to base classes; making the prompt conditional on each image improves generalization to unseen classes and domains.","results_note":"Generalizes much better than CoOp to unseen classes within a dataset, and improves cross-dataset transfer and domain generalization across 11 datasets.","relations":[{"target":"coop","type":"generalizes","note":"Adds an instance-conditional shift to CoOp's context vectors."},{"target":"maple","type":"competes-with","note":"MaPLe reports gains over CoCoOp on base-to-novel generalization."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05557","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.05557"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Kaiyang Zhou","Jingkang Yang","Chen Change Loy","Ziwei Liu"],"bibkey":"zhou2022conditional","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhou2022conditional,\n  title = {{Conditional Prompt Learning for Vision-Language Models}},\n  author = {Kaiyang Zhou and Jingkang Yang and Chen Change Loy and Ziwei Liu},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2203.05557},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.05557}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\): \\(x\\mapsto V+h_\\theta(f(x))\\mathbf 1^\\top\\) is a \\(T\\)-point of CoOp over image features.","base_point":"As CoOp; it reduces to CoOp when \\(h_\\theta\\) outputs 0.","gauge":"Linear-ReLU-Linear meta-net: positive torus on the hidden units (Lemma VI.2(b)); plus a kernel-type translation (\\(v_m\\mapsto v_m+c\\) for all \\(m\\), \\(b_2\\mapsto b_2-c\\)).","covariance":"Function-level reading of Def II.14 (the table leaves '–'): the prompt set is a full vector space on its wire, carried to itself by every linear function-preserving gauge of the residual stream (Obs II.15 analogue); no imposed basis.","merge":"Class embeddings depend on the image, so the text encoder runs \\(K\\) times per image; nothing caches.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A family over images: \\(x\\mapsto V+h_\\theta(f(x))\\mathbf 1^\\top\\) is a \\(T\\)-point of CoOp with \\(T\\) the image features. The classifier rows then depend on \\(x\\), so no fixed head, hence no CoOp point, realises it.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"coop","test":"O1","reason":"Closed-set CoOp realises x↦softmax(cos(W,f(x))/τ) with W constant; CoCoOp's rows W(x) vary with x, generically outside every fixed-head classifier."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"maple","name":"MaPLe","full_name":"Multi-modal Prompt Learning","paper_title":"MaPLe: Multi-modal Prompt Learning","authors":"Khattak et al.","year":2022,"venue":"CVPR 2023","arxiv":"2210.03117","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03117","code_url":"https://github.com/muzairkhattak/multimodal-prompt-learning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"[\\_\\,,W_i]=L_i([P_{i-1},W_{i-1}]),\\quad [c_i,E_i,\\_\\,]=V_i\\big([c_{i-1},E_{i-1},F_{i-1}(P_{i-1})]\\big),\\ \\ i=1,\\dots,J;\\quad \\tilde P_k=F_k(P_k),\\ F_k:\\mathbb{R}^{d_l}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{d_v}\\ \\text{linear};\\ \\ \\text{train } \\{P_i\\},\\{F_i\\};\\ \\text{CLIP frozen}","trainable_params":"J\\ell d_l+J\\,(d_ld_v+d_v)","locus":"token sequences of both the text and image encoders up to depth \\(J\\) (deep prompts)","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Deep prompts in the language branch, with vision-branch prompts obtained as linear images \\(F_k(P_k)\\) of the language prompts (one shared parameter pushed forward into both modalities, coupling their gradients).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Vision-branch prompts add tokens to every image forward pass (the text side alone could be cached).","identity_at_init":"No identity.","key_idea":"Prompt both towers of CLIP and couple vision prompts to language prompts so the two representation spaces adapt jointly instead of independently.","results_note":"Over CoCoOp, gains of +3.45% on novel classes and +2.72% harmonic mean averaged over 11 datasets in base-to-novel generalization, also improving cross-dataset and domain-shift results.","relations":[{"target":"coop","type":"generalizes","note":"J=1 language-only prompting degenerates to CoOp."},{"target":"vpt","type":"combines","note":"Deep vision prompts (VPT-style) coupled to deep language prompts."},{"target":"cocoop","type":"competes-with","note":"Main baseline."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03117","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.03117"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Muhammad Uzair Khattak","Hanoona Rasheed","Muhammad Maaz","Salman Khan","Fahad Shahbaz Khan"],"bibkey":"khattak2022maple","bibtex":"@inproceedings{khattak2022maple,\n  title = {{MaPLe: Multi-modal Prompt Learning}},\n  author = {Muhammad Uzair Khattak and Hanoona Rasheed and Muhammad Maaz and Salman Khan and Fahad Shahbaz Khan},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2210.03117},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03117}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E0","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Deep prompts on both CLIP towers up to depth \\(J\\), the vision prompts being linear images \\(F_k(P_k)\\) of the language prompts.","gauge":"Deep replacement makes each layer's language prompt act only through \\(\\mathrm{LN}_t(P)\\) and its vision prompt only through \\(\\mathrm{LN}_v(F_k(P))\\), and the coupling \\(F_k(P)=PW_k+b_k\\) is evaluated only at the \\(\\ell\\) prompt rows. Generic fibre per coupled layer: \\((d_l+1-\\ell)d_v\\) kernel translations of \\((W_k,b_k)\\), plus per-token scale and shift on each side (\\(4\\ell\\)), the language-side ones compensated by \\(P\\)-dependent changes of \\((W_k,b_k)\\). The coupling breaks the per-token torus as a linear action; only the common scale \\((P,W)\\mapsto(cP,W/c)\\), \\(W\\mapsto W+w\\mathbf 1^\\top\\), \\(b\\mapsto b+\\beta\\mathbf 1\\) and a common shift remain linear. The fibre is not an orbit of a linear group: nonlinear.","covariance":"Function-level: language prompts are a full space and the coupling is a free linear map, so basis-free.","merge":"Vision prompts add tokens to every image forward pass (only the text side could be cached).","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A coupled pair of deep prompt extensions on both CLIP towers, the vision prompts being the push-forward \\(F_k(P_k)\\) of the language prompts along trainable linear maps, so one parameter moves both towers. Deep replacement makes each layer's prompts act only through LayerNorm, and the coupling is read only at the \\(\\ell\\) prompt rows, so the gauge fibre is large and non-linear.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"coop","test":"O1","reason":"MaPLe changes the image features through its vision prompts; CoOp only changes the class embeddings over frozen features, so MaPLe's realisations are generically outside CoOp's."}],"table_row":"E0","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"l2p","name":"L2P","full_name":"Learning to Prompt (prompt pool for continual learning)","paper_title":"Learning to Prompt for Continual Learning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2021,"venue":"CVPR 2022","arxiv":"2112.08654","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.08654","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/l2p","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"K_x=\\arg\\min_{\\{s_i\\}_{i=1}^{N}\\subseteq[1,M]}\\ \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\gamma\\big(q(x),k_{s_i}\\big),\\ \\ q(x)=f(x)[0]\\ \\text{(frozen ViT [CLS])};\\ \\ x_p=[P_{s_1};\\dots;P_{s_N};x_e];\\ \\ \\min_{P,K,\\phi}\\ \\mathcal{L}\\Big(g_\\phi\\big(\\mathrm{AvgPool}(f_r(x_p)[0{:}N\\ell])\\big),y\\Big)+\\lambda\\sum_{s_i\\in K_x}\\gamma\\big(q(x),k_{s_i}\\big)","trainable_params":"M(\\ell d+d_k)+\\text{head}","locus":"input token sequence of a frozen ViT (prompts selected from a pool)","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"Key-value prompt memory \\(\\{(k_j,P_j)\\}_{j=1}^M\\): input-conditioned hard top-\\(N\\) retrieval by cosine matching between a frozen-model query and learnable keys; the selected prompts are concatenated.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Prompts are selected per input and lengthen the token sequence; the query also costs an extra frozen forward pass.","identity_at_init":"No identity.","key_idea":"For rehearsal-free continual learning, keep the backbone frozen and store knowledge in a pool of prompts that are retrieved per instance, so tasks share or separate prompts automatically without task identity at test time.","results_note":"Consistently outperforms prior state of the art in class-incremental, domain-incremental and task-agnostic continual learning (e.g., Split CIFAR-100, 5-datasets) and is competitive with rehearsal-based methods without any buffer.","relations":[{"target":"prompt-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Each pool element is a soft prompt."},{"target":"dualprompt","type":"competes-with","note":"DualPrompt (same group) refines L2P with a shared G-prompt and task-specific prefix E-prompts at chosen layers."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.08654","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2112.08654"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Zifeng Wang","Zizhao Zhang","Chen-Yu Lee","Han Zhang","Ruoxi Sun","Xiaoqi Ren","Guolong Su","Vincent Perot","Jennifer Dy","Tomas Pfister"],"bibkey":"wang2021learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2021learning,\n  title = {{Learning to Prompt for Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Zifeng Wang and Zizhao Zhang and Chen-Yu Lee and Han Zhang and Ruoxi Sun and Xiaoqi Ren and Guolong Su and Vincent Perot and Jennifer Dy and Tomas Pfister},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2112.08654},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.08654}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"OxO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\) by hard retrieval: a frozen-model query picks \\(N\\) of \\(M\\) pooled prompts (key-value memory), which are concatenated.","base_point":"Selected prompts always draw attention mass.","gauge":"Selection and its surrogate loss use cosine matching, so each key can be rescaled, \\(k_j\\mapsto c_jk_j\\) (\\(c_j>0\\)): a torus \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}^M\\).","covariance":"Cosine key matching on the frozen [CLS] query wire is \\(CO(d)\\)-invariant, not \\(GL\\); prompts themselves are basis-free.","merge":"Per-input selection plus an extra frozen forward pass for the query.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (concatenation on the token sequence; for key/value prefixes, the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A family over inputs by hard retrieval: a key–query cosine match picks \\(N\\) of \\(M\\) prompts, so the prompt is a piecewise-constant function of \\(x\\). Its realisations jump at selection boundaries, which no continuous family (static prompts, softmax routers) can reproduce.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"attempt","test":"O1","reason":"Hard top-N selection makes L2P's realisations generically discontinuous in x at selection boundaries; every ATTEMPT realisation is continuous in x (softmax router), so Im L2P ⊄ Im ATTEMPT."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"dualprompt","name":"DualPrompt","full_name":"DualPrompt (complementary general and expert prompts)","paper_title":"DualPrompt: Complementary Prompting for Rehearsal-free Continual Learning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ECCV 2022","arxiv":"2204.04799","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.04799","code_url":"https://github.com/google-research/l2p","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_g^{(i)}=f_{\\mathrm{prompt}}\\big(g,h^{(i)}\\big),\\ \\ h_e^{(j)}=f_{\\mathrm{prompt}}\\big(e_t,h^{(j)}\\big),\\ \\ f^{\\mathrm{Pre\\text{-}T}}_{\\mathrm{prompt}}(p,h)=\\mathrm{MSA}\\big(h_Q,[p_k;h_K],[p_v;h_V]\\big);\\ \\ t^\\star=\\arg\\min_t\\gamma\\big(q(x),k_t\\big);\\ \\ \\min_{g,e_t,k_t,\\phi}\\ \\mathcal{L}\\big(f_\\phi(f_{g,e_t}(x)),y\\big)+\\lambda\\,\\gamma\\big(q(x),k_t\\big)","trainable_params":"n_g\\ell_g d+T\\,(n_e\\ell_e d+d)+\\text{head}\\ \\ (n_g,n_e=\\text{number of layers carrying G/E prompts};\\ \\ell_g,\\ell_e\\ \\text{total prompt lengths, split into } \\ell/2 \\text{ key and } \\ell/2 \\text{ value vectors in prefix form})","locus":"keys/values (prefix form) of a few shallow (G-prompt) and middle (E-prompt) ViT layers","modification_kind":"composition-or-routing","structure":"A task-invariant prefix \\(g\\) shared by all tasks at shallow layers plus a pool of task-specific prefixes \\(e_t\\) at deeper layers, the latter retrieved by key matching against a frozen-model query.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"Extra prefix key/value slots and input-dependent selection at inference.","identity_at_init":"No identity.","key_idea":"Split continual-learning knowledge into a general prompt and expert prompts attached at different depths, using prefix-style (key/value) attachment, which works better than prompt-token insertion for this purpose.","results_note":"Sets state of the art for rehearsal-free class-incremental learning and even outperforms methods with sizable rehearsal buffers; introduces the Split ImageNet-R benchmark.","relations":[{"target":"l2p","type":"builds-on","note":"Adds a complementary shared G-prompt, prefix-style attachment and decoupled attachment depths to the L2P prompt-pool idea."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Uses prefix-tuning (Pre-T) as the attachment function and compares it with prompt-token insertion (Pro-T)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.04799","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.04799"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Zifeng Wang","Zizhao Zhang","Sayna Ebrahimi","Ruoxi Sun","Han Zhang","Chen-Yu Lee","Xiaoqi Ren","Guolong Su","Vincent Perot","Jennifer Dy","Tomas Pfister"],"bibkey":"wang2022dualprompt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2022dualprompt,\n  title = {{DualPrompt: Complementary Prompting for Rehearsal-free Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Zifeng Wang and Zizhao Zhang and Sayna Ebrahimi and Ruoxi Sun and Han Zhang and Chen-Yu Lee and Xiaoqi Ren and Guolong Su and Vincent Perot and Jennifer Dy and Tomas Pfister},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {2204.04799},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.04799}\n}","coords":{"kind":"F_T","shape":"Fun","base_point":"unpointed","gauge":"torus","covariance":"OxO","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(F_X\\): a static shared prefix (G-prompt) at shallow layers plus a task prefix (E-prompt) retrieved per input by key matching.","base_point":"Prefix slots always draw attention mass.","gauge":"Cosine task-key matching: each task key rescales freely (torus \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}^T\\)); prefixes contribute only discrete slot permutations.","covariance":"Cosine matching on the frozen query wire: \\(CO(d)\\)-invariant.","merge":"Extra prefix key/value slots and input-dependent selection.","rebasing":"Merge-and-restart needs a merge (M∞). Re-prompting with the trained prompt frozen stacks prompts (the multiset monoid of Thm VI.5(a)), so \\(R_K\\) is the same method at length \\(K\\ell\\) rather than a fixed shape; no weight-space rebasing."},"categorical_reading":"A static shallow prefix (G-prompt) summed with a hard-retrieved task prefix (E-prompt) at middle layers: an \\(F_X\\) family with piecewise-constant selection on the key/value wire. Like L2P it realises discontinuous functions of the input.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"Any static prefix gives a realisation continuous in x; DualPrompt's E-prompt switches with x, so its realisations are generically discontinuous at selection boundaries."}],"table_row":"F_T","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"initial-state-tuning","name":"Initial State Tuning","full_name":"Initial State Tuning (for state space models)","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of State Space Models","authors":"Galim et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2410.09016","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016","code_url":"https://github.com/furiosa-ai/ssm-peft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h_t=\\bar A_t h_{t-1}+\\bar B_t x_t,\\ \\ y_t=C_th_t\\ \\ \\text{run from } h_0=h'\\ \\text{instead of } 0\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\ \\hat y_t=y_t+C_t\\Big(\\prod_{i=1}^{t}\\bar A_i\\Big)h',\\quad h'\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H}\\ \\text{per channel trained; all SSM weights frozen}","trainable_params":"DH\\ \\text{per SSM layer}","locus":"initial hidden state of each SSM (e.g. Mamba S6) layer","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"Learned initial state of a linear recurrence. On S4, any prefix of virtual tokens acts only through \\(h_{\\mathrm{prefix}}=\\sum_{i=0}^{\\ell-1}\\bar A^{i}\\bar Bx_{-i}\\), so prefix-tuning is exactly an initial-state update (Prop. 1); conversely every initial state is realizable by a prefix iff \\(\\ell\\ge H\\) (under a genericity condition).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"No weight changes and per-token cost is unchanged, since SSM scan kernels accept an initial state natively, but the state is injected per sequence (in S6 its effect \\(C_t(\\prod_i\\bar A_i)h'\\) is input-dependent) and cannot be folded into \\(W_0\\), exactly like a cached prefix.","identity_at_init":"\\(h'=0\\) recovers the base model exactly.","key_idea":"For SSMs, the whole effect of prompt/prefix tuning is captured by the initial hidden state, so tune that state directly: an upper bound on prefix-tuning's expressivity at the same cost.","results_note":"On Mamba with <0.5% trainable parameters, initial state tuning outperforms prefix-tuning and prompt tuning on five of seven tasks, but LoRA consistently outperforms it.","relations":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"generalizes","note":"Galim et al. Prop. 1: on S4 every prefix acts as an initial-state update; the converse holds iff the prefix length is at least H (genericity assumption), so initial-state tuning upper-bounds prefix-tuning."},{"target":"state-offset-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"State-offset tuning removes the decaying coefficient ∏ A_i."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRA outperforms state-based tuning in Galim et al.'s benchmarks."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.09016","https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03499"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Kevin Galim","Wonjun Kang","Yuchen Zeng","Hyung Il Koo","Kangwook Lee"],"bibkey":"galim2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{galim2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of State Space Models}},\n  author = {Kevin Galim and Wonjun Kang and Yuchen Zeng and Hyung Il Koo and Kangwook Lee},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2410.09016},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The SSM's initial state, fixed at \\(0\\) in \\(f\\), becomes a parameter \\(h'\\) with neutral value \\(0\\): a pointed extension.","shape":"On its slot the output moves affinely: \\(\\hat y_t = y_t + C_t(\\prod_{i\\le t}\\bar A_i)h'\\) (linear in \\(h'\\)).","base_point":"Neutral at \\(h'=0\\), which recovers the base model exactly; an init elsewhere would carry a defect.","gauge":"\\(h'\\mapsto(C_t\\prod\\bar A_i h')_t\\) is injective for an observable pair (generic).","covariance":"The slot is all of \\(\\mathbb R^H\\) per channel, carried to itself by every state-space similarity (\\(C T^{-1}\\), \\(T\\bar AT^{-1}\\), \\(T\\bar B\\)).","merge":"Zero inference cost (scan kernels accept an initial state), but the decaying, position-dependent term is absorbed by no weight of \\(f\\) (Thm VI.3(a)).","rebasing":"Additive slot: merging \\(h'\\) into the initial state and restarting stays in the same slot."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed extension: the SSM's initial state, fixed at \\(0\\) in \\(f\\), becomes a parameter \\(h'\\) with neutral point \\(0\\), and the output moves affinely, \\(\\hat y_t=y_t+C_t(\\prod_{i\\le t}\\bar A_i)h'\\). On S4 every prefix acts through this slot (Galim et al., Prop 1), and the zero prefix is neutral there: prefix tuning's unpointedness is a softmax phenomenon.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(h')=\\big(\\mathcal C_{\\ell,l}^{+}\\,h'_l\\big)_l,\\quad \\mathcal C_{\\ell,l}=[\\bar B_l,\\bar A_l\\bar B_l,\\dots,\\bar A_l^{\\ell-1}\\bar B_l]","note":"S4 layers, per-layer virtual tokens of length ℓ ≥ H, controllable pair (Galim et al., converse of Prop 1); h(0)=0, and the zero prefix is neutral on S4."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"state-offset-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"Its effect C_t(ΠĀ_i)h' decays along the sequence for stable Ā, whereas state-offset's C_th' is uniform in t; the images differ unless Ā=I."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"state-offset-tuning","name":"State-offset Tuning","full_name":"State-offset Tuning","paper_title":"State-offset Tuning: State-based Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for State Space Models","authors":"Kang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ACL 2025","arxiv":"2503.03499","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03499","code_url":"https://github.com/furiosa-ai/ssm-state-tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat y_t=y_t+C_t\\,h'\\ \\ \\text{(State-offset (h))},\\qquad \\hat y_t=y_t+y'\\ \\ \\text{(State-offset (y))},\\qquad h'\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H}\\ \\text{per channel},\\ y'\\in\\mathbb{R}^{D};\\ \\text{SSM weights frozen}","trainable_params":"DH\\ \\text{(h variant)};\\ \\ D\\ \\text{(y variant) per SSM layer}","locus":"state readout at every timestep of each SSM (Mamba S6) layer","modification_kind":"input-or-activation-augmentation","structure":"A constant learned offset added to the hidden state before readout at every step (Initial State Tuning with the time-varying factor \\(\\prod_i\\bar A_i\\) removed). In S6, \\(C_th'=(W_Cx_t)^\\top h'\\), so the \\(h\\)-variant is a rank-\\(\\le H\\) linear term in \\(x_t\\); the \\(y\\)-variant is a bias on the SSM output.","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The offset enters before Mamba's multiplicative output gate (and as a linear term in \\(x_t\\) for the \\(h\\)-variant), so it cannot be absorbed into the existing diagonal skip or biases, although its cost is negligible.","identity_at_init":"\\(h'=0\\) (or \\(y'=0\\)) recovers the frozen model exactly; the official code supports zero or Kaiming-uniform initialization of the offset parameters.","key_idea":"State-based PEFT for SSMs: instead of injecting external virtual tokens, add a learnable constant to the internal state at every timestep, giving a uniform rather than decaying effect.","results_note":"On Mamba-family models, State-offset Tuning (\\(h\\)) outperforms prompt-, prefix- and initial-state tuning and BitFit, and surpasses LoRA on most datasets; the (\\(y\\)) variant beats initial state tuning with 0.01% vs 0.23% of parameters.","relations":[{"target":"initial-state-tuning","type":"builds-on","note":"Removes the time-varying coefficient of the initial-state term."},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Prompt-based methods underperform state-based ones on SSMs."},{"target":"bitfit","type":"competes-with","note":"The y-variant is bias-like; reported to outperform BitFit."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Surpasses LoRA on most reported datasets."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03499","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.03499","https://github.com/furiosa-ai/ssm-state-tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"authors_full":["Wonjun Kang","Kevin Galim","Yuchen Zeng","Minjae Lee","Hyung Il Koo","Nam Ik Cho"],"bibkey":"kang2025state","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kang2025state,\n  title = {{State-offset Tuning: State-based Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for State Space Models}},\n  author = {Wonjun Kang and Kevin Galim and Yuchen Zeng and Minjae Lee and Hyung Il Koo and Nam Ik Cho},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2503.03499},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03499}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Lin","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"Minf","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A new additive path before Mamba's output gate, neutral at 0: a pointed extension.","shape":"\\(h\\)-variant: \\(C_th'=(W_Cx_t)^\\top h'\\), i.e. the linear path \\(x_t\\mapsto H'W_Cx_t\\) with frozen frame \\(W_C\\) (LoRA-FA-type) on a new box; \\(y\\)-variant: a new bias.","base_point":"Neutral with zero init (\\(h'=0\\) or \\(y'=0\\)); the code's Kaiming-uniform option gives a defect.","gauge":"\\(H'\\mapsto H'W_C\\) is injective when \\(W_C\\) has full row rank \\(H\\).","covariance":"\\(\\{H'W_C\\}\\) transforms with \\(W_C\\) under basis changes (a frame from \\(\\theta_0\\), not random), and the \\(y\\)-variant is a full bias space.","merge":"The offset enters before the multiplicative gate \\(y\\odot\\mathrm{SiLU}(z)\\) and is not diagonal, so neither the \\(D\\) skip nor any bias absorbs it: a stream with no linear box to absorb it.","rebasing":"Lin with a frozen frame \\(W_C\\): idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"A pointed extension adding a constant state offset at every step. The \\(h\\)-variant is a LoRA-FA-type path \\(x_t\\mapsto H'W_Cx_t\\) with frozen frame \\(W_C\\) on a new parallel box, and the \\(y\\)-variant is a new bias; both are neutral at \\(0\\), but they sit before Mamba's multiplicative gate, so no existing box absorbs them.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"initial-state-tuning","test":"O1","reason":"A uniform offset C_th' is not of the decaying form C_t(ΠĀ_i)h'' for any h'' (stable Ā), so Im state-offset ⊄ Im initial-state."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lq-lora","name":"LQ-LoRA","full_name":"Low-rank plus Quantized LoRA","paper_title":"LQ-LoRA: Low-rank Plus Quantized Matrix Decomposition for Efficient Language Model Finetuning","authors":"Guo et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2311.12023","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12023","code_url":"https://github.com/HanGuo97/lq-lora","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\min_{Q\\in\\mathbb{Q}_{c},\\,B,A}\\big\\lVert\\sqrt{F}\\odot\\big(W_0-(Q+BA)\\big)\\big\\rVert_F\\ \\ (F\\equiv\\mathbf 1\\ \\text{or diagonal empirical Fisher}):\\ \\ B^{(t)}A^{(t)}=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0-Q^{(t-1)}),\\ \\ Q^{(t)}=\\mathrm{Quantize}_c(W_0-B^{(t)}A^{(t)}),\\ Q^{(0)}=0;\\ \\ c=(\\text{bits, block sizes})\\ \\text{per matrix from an ILP under a global bit budget};\\quad h=(Q+BA)x,\\ Q\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A,B\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all linear layers of the transformer, each with its own NF quantization configuration","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"robust-PCA-style low-rank-plus-quantized decomposition \\(W_0 \\approx Q + BA\\); \\(Q\\) uses a per-matrix mixed-precision configuration chosen by integer linear programming; data-aware variant uses a row/column-scaled (Fisher-weighted) SVD","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The low-rank component is high-precision and can be folded only into a dequantized copy of \\(Q\\); folding into the quantized \\(Q\\) needs re-quantization.","identity_at_init":"Approximate: \\(Q+BA\\) is the alternating approximation of \\(W_0\\) (stopped when the error rises). Since \\(Q^{(0)}=0\\), the first low-rank component is the top-\\(r\\) SVD of \\(W_0\\) itself, so the adapter starts on \\(W_0\\)'s principal subspace rather than at zero.","key_idea":"Split each pretrained matrix into a fixed, easily quantized part and a trainable high-variance low-rank part. Integer linear programming then allocates bits across matrices to meet any target memory budget.","results_note":"Beats QLoRA and GPTQ-LoRA at matched bit budgets on LLaMA-2 (C4, OpenAssistant) and RoBERTa-large (GLUE). For example, 3.5-bit Fisher LQ-LoRA roughly equals 4.127-bit NF4 QLoRA, and a 2.75-bit LLaMA-2-70B (27GB) stays competitive with 16-bit.","relations":[{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"same Q+BA objective; differs in starting point (Q^{(0)}=0 vs BA=0), ILP bit allocation and Fisher weighting"},{"target":"pissa","type":"generalizes","note":"with the identity quantizer and one iteration, the decomposition is exactly PiSSA's principal-component adapter plus frozen residual"},{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"same frozen-quantized-base + LoRA fine-tuning stage"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12023","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.12023","https://openreview.net/forum?id=xw29VvOMmU"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Han Guo","Philip Greengard","Eric P. Xing","Yoon Kim"],"bibkey":"guo2023lq","bibtex":"@inproceedings{guo2023lq,\n  title = {{LQ-LoRA: Low-rank Plus Quantized Matrix Decomposition for Efficient Language Model Finetuning}},\n  author = {Han Guo and Philip Greengard and Eric P. Xing and Yoon Kim},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2311.12023},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12023}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(Q+BA\\) with \\((Q,B_0A_0)\\) from alternating \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r\\)/Quantize from \\(Q^{(0)}=0\\): a PiSSA split first, then the residual is quantised (the split-then-quantise order of Prop V.2(b)), iterated. \\(B_0A_0\\) has rank \\(r\\), so the apex \\(Q\\) is not \\(\\theta_0\\) and \\(\\rho(q_0)\\) is a smooth point (as LoftQ).","base_point":"Pointed up to the attained residual \\(\\|W_0-Q-B_0A_0\\|\\): an approximate section (Prop V.2(c)).","covariance":"Quantisation grid with a per-matrix ILP-chosen configuration; the Fisher-weighted variant adds a data frame."},"categorical_reading":"LQ-LoRA is LoftQ's object with the other starting order: split first (\\(Q^{(0)}=0\\) gives PiSSA's top-\\(r\\) factor) and quantise the residual, iterated. It is a Type II translated cone over a quantised apex, pointed up to an attained residual, and with the identity quantiser and one iteration it is exactly PiSSA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"Type II smooth point over \\(Q\\) versus QLoRA's apex at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), with different apexes as well."},{"target":"pissa","test":"O1","reason":"For \\(\\kappa\\ne\\mathrm{id}\\) the apex \\(Q=\\kappa(W_{\\rm res})\\) differs from PiSSA's \\(W_{\\rm res}\\) by a generically full-rank error, so the translated cones are not nested."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"ir-qlora","name":"IR-QLoRA","full_name":"Information-Retention QLoRA","paper_title":"Accurate LoRA-Finetuning Quantization of LLMs via Information Retention","authors":"Qin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.05445","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05445","code_url":"https://github.com/AI-Efficiency/IR-QLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat W_0=\\mathrm{NF}k\\!\\Big(\\tfrac{W_0-\\tau^{*}}{\\mathrm{absmax}(W_0-\\tau^{*})}\\Big)\\,\\mathrm{absmax}(W_0-\\tau^{*})+\\tau^{*},\\ \\ \\tau^{*}=\\arg\\max_{\\tau}\\ \\mathcal H\\big(\\mathrm{NF}k((W_0-\\tau)/\\mathrm{absmax}(W_0-\\tau))\\big)\\ \\text{(blockwise search on } [\\tau_0\\pm\\lambda\\sigma],\\ \\tau_0=\\mathrm{median};\\ \\text{scales and }\\tau\\text{ double-quantized)};\\quad h=\\hat W_0x+\\alpha\\,U_2(U_1(x)),\\ \\ U_1(x)=Ax+\\beta_1\\,\\mathrm{avg}_r(x),\\ \\ U_2(u)=Bu+\\beta_2\\,\\mathrm{rep}_{d_{out}/r}(u),\\ \\ \\mathrm{avg}_r(x)_j=\\tfrac{r}{d_{in}}\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal G_j}x_i\\ (r\\text{ contiguous groups}),\\ \\ \\beta_1,\\beta_2\\ \\text{layer-wise learnable scalars}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+2","locus":"all linear layers of LLaMA/LLaMA2; NF2-NF4 or integer-quantized base","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA with parameter-free 'elastic' shortcuts: \\(\\Delta W=\\alpha(B+\\beta_2E)(A+\\beta_1P)\\), where \\(P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\) averages \\(r\\) contiguous input groups and \\(E\\in\\{0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\) repeats each of the \\(r\\) entries \\(d_{out}/r\\) times; still rank \\(\\le r\\). Base is an entropy-calibrated NF-k quantizer with a per-block shift \\(\\tau\\)","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The IEC shortcuts are linear and fold into the LoRA factors (and then into a 16-bit copy of the weight), but folding into the NF-k base requires re-quantization.","identity_at_init":"Exact relative to the quantized base: \\(B = 0\\) and, in the official code, \\(\\beta_1=\\beta_2=0\\) (the paper itself does not state their initialization), so the adapter is zero at step 0. The starting model is the ICQ-quantized one, not \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"View quantize-then-LoRA through information retention. Calibration Quantization (ICQ) maximizes the entropy of the quantized weights so they keep more information. The Elastic Connection (IEC) adds parameter-free pooling and tiling paths so LoRA can directly reuse the original features.","results_note":"Improves LLaMA/LLaMA2 at 2-4 bits over QLoRA and QA-LoRA, e.g. +1.4% MMLU for 4-bit LLaMA-7B over the prior state of the art, at only 0.31% extra training time.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"builds-on","note":"same NF-k + LoRA pipeline with a calibrated quantizer and augmented adapter"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both route group-pooled inputs into the adapter; IR-QLoRA uses pooling as an extra shortcut, not as a constraint"},{"target":"loftq","type":"competes-with","note":"both reduce the quantization gap at init, via entropy calibration rather than residual SVD"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05445","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.05445","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/qin24b.html","https://github.com/AI-Efficiency/IR-QLoRA"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Haotong Qin","Xudong Ma","Xingyu Zheng","Xiaoyang Li","Yang Zhang","Shouda Liu","Jie Luo","Xianglong Liu","Michele Magno"],"bibkey":"qin2024accurate","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qin2024accurate,\n  title = {{Accurate LoRA-Finetuning Quantization of LLMs via Information Retention}},\n  author = {Haotong Qin and Xudong Ma and Xingyu Zheng and Xiaoyang Li and Yang Zhang and Shouda Liu and Jie Luo and Xianglong Liu and Michele Magno},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.05445},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05445}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\alpha(B+\\beta_2E)(A+\\beta_1P)\\), rank \\(\\le r\\): the image is \\(\\kappa_{\\tau^*}\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), LoRA's cone over the ICQ-calibrated base; the elastic shortcuts do not enlarge it.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\), \\(\\beta_1=\\beta_2=0\\) (official code): \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\kappa_{\\tau^*}\\theta_0\\), defect \\(e_{\\tau^*}\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) plus two translations: \\((B,A,\\beta_1,\\beta_2)\\mapsto(B+\\beta_2E,A+\\beta_1P)\\) is a linear change of coordinates, so \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\) are absorbed into \\(A,B\\) (generic fibre dim \\(r^2+2\\)).","covariance":"Image covariance is the quantiser's (grid); \\(E\\) and \\(P\\) impose contiguous groupings only on the cometric, not on the image."},"categorical_reading":"IR-QLoRA is QLoRA over an entropy-calibrated base: ICQ moves the base point to \\(\\kappa_{\\tau^*}\\theta_0\\), and IEC is a pure reparametrisation \\((B+\\beta_2E,\\,A+\\beta_1P)\\) of LoRA's factors that leaves the image unchanged. What IEC changes is the cometric \\(K=D\\rho\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\rho^\\top\\), i.e. the dynamics, not the expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\beta_1,\\beta_2)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{s}(B+\\beta_2E),\\ A+\\beta_1P)","note":"\\(s\\) is QLoRA's scale; pointed (\\(h(q_0)=(0,A_0)\\)); needs the same quantised base, since IR-QLoRA's base differs from NF4 by \\(\\tau^*\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"qa-lora","test":"O1","reason":"IR-QLoRA's update is a general rank-\\(r\\) matrix; QA-LoRA's is group-constant."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"l4q","name":"L4Q","full_name":"Low-rank adaptive Learning quantization for LLMs","paper_title":"L4Q: Parameter Efficient Quantization-Aware Fine-Tuning on Large Language Models","authors":"Jeon et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2025","arxiv":"2402.04902","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.99/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_{comb}=W_0+\\alpha BA,\\quad \\tilde w=\\mathrm{round}\\Big(\\mathrm{clamp}\\big(\\tfrac{W_{comb}-b}{s},Q_N,Q_P\\big)\\Big),\\ \\ W_q=\\tilde w\\,s+b,\\quad h=W_qx;\\ \\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ (A,B,s,b)\\ \\text{trained with STE};\\ \\ \\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial B}=\\alpha\\big(\\tfrac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial W_q}\\odot\\mathbb 1_{[Q_N,Q_P]}\\big)A^{\\top}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+2\\,d_{out}\\,d_{in}/g\\ \\ \\text{(LoRA factors + one scale and bias per quantization group of size } g)","locus":"all linear layers (LLaMA 7B-33B, OpenLLaMA-3B, Mistral-7B)","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"learned-step-size quantizer composed after a rank-\\(r\\) additive update: \\(W=Q_{s,b}(W_0+\\alpha BA)\\); custom backward reuses \\(\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial W_q\\) so no weight-gradient buffer is stored","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The deployed model is exactly the fully quantized \\(W_q=Q_{s,b}(W_0+\\alpha BA)\\); no adapter or 16-bit branch remains at inference.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\), so training starts from the 'L4Q init' symmetric quantization of \\(W_0\\) (scale \\(s=\\max(|\\min W/Q_N|,|\\max W/Q_P|)\\), chosen to avoid clipping outliers), not from \\(W_0\\) itself.","key_idea":"Put the LoRA update inside the quantizer rather than beside it, and train LoRA jointly with the quantization scale and bias. The result is a fully quantized model without QA-LoRA's constraints on the factors, at roughly LoRA's training memory.","results_note":"On LLaMA and Mistral models with instruction data, 4-bit and 3-bit L4Q is more accurate than decoupled quantize-then-fine-tune schemes and QA-LoRA, at inference speed comparable to other fully quantized models.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA factors inside a quantization-aware training (LSQ-style) quantizer"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both produce merged integer models; L4Q leaves A and B unconstrained by re-quantizing W_0+\\alpha BA"},{"target":"peqa","type":"competes-with","note":"both learn quantization scales; L4Q also learns a LoRA update inside the quantizer"},{"target":"efficientqat","type":"competes-with","note":"both are memory-efficient quantization-aware training procedures for LLMs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04902","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.04902","https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.99/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Hyesung Jeon","Yulhwa Kim","Jae-joon Kim"],"bibkey":"jeon2024l4q","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jeon2024l4q,\n  title = {{L4Q: Parameter Efficient Quantization-Aware Fine-Tuning on Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Hyesung Jeon and Yulhwa Kim and Jae-joon Kim},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.04902},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.99/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"nonlinear","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"For fixed integer codes \\(\\rho=s\\odot\\tilde w+b\\) is affine in \\((s,b)\\); at a generic \\(q_0\\) small changes of \\((B,A)\\) flip no code, so the germ at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) is the per-group scale-and-shift family on frozen codes (SSF-like, \\(d=2\\cdot\\#\\)groups). Globally the image is the union, over code patterns reachable from \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\), of such linear families.","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) and the L4Q-init symmetric quantiser: \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\kappa\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_0\\).","gauge":"\\(\\partial\\rho/\\partial(B,A)=0\\) almost everywhere: \\((B,A)\\) move inside open code cells without changing \\(\\rho\\), a generic fibre of dim \\(r(m+n)\\) that is not a group orbit. \\(B,A\\) are moved only by the STE surrogate, a non-gradient update for the exact \\(\\rho\\).","covariance":"Per-group scales and offsets over contiguous groups of size \\(g\\): an imposed partition.","merge":"Quantise-after-update order: the deployed \\(W_q=Q_{s,b}(W_0+\\alpha BA)\\) is on the grid by construction, exact and type-preserving (the commuting order of Prop V.2(d)).","rebasing":"Merging and restarting re-quantises \\(W_0+\\alpha BA\\); accumulated rank-\\(r\\) code changes reach every code pattern, the quantised analogue of \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"L4Q postcomposes LoRA with a trainable quantiser, \\(\\rho=\\kappa_{s,b}\\circ\\mathrm{LoRA}\\). For the exact map the LoRA factors are fibre directions, because codes are locally constant, and the smooth germ is only the quantiser's scale-and-shift action on frozen codes; LoRA acts through discrete code flips and the STE surrogate. This is the fine-tune-then-quantise order, which is why its merge stays exactly in the quantised type.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O1","reason":"L4Q's image lies in grid families \\(s\\odot\\tilde w+b\\), QLoRA's \\(\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) is off the grid: no inclusion either way."},{"target":"peqa","test":"O1","reason":"PEQA's image is the per-row scale family on its frozen codes, \\(\\{\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\overline W_0\\}\\) (dim \\(d_{out}\\)); L4Q's is a union over code patterns of per-group scale-and-shift families. L4Q's germ is larger (per-group scales and shifts), and large PEQA rescalings would flip L4Q's recomputed codes, so neither image contains the other; the base points also differ (RTN/OPTQ versus the L4Q-init symmetric quantiser)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"peqa","name":"PEQA","full_name":"Parameter-Efficient and Quantization-aware Adaptation","paper_title":"Memory-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Compressed Large Language Models via sub-4-bit Integer Quantization","authors":"Kim et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.14152","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14152","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat W=\\mathrm{diag}(s_0+\\Delta s)\\,\\overline W_0,\\quad \\overline W_0=\\mathrm{clamp}\\big(\\lfloor W_0/s_0\\rceil+z_0,\\,0,\\,2^b-1\\big)-z_0\\ \\ \\text{(frozen integer codes)},\\ \\ s_0,z_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}};\\ \\ \\text{only } s_0+\\Delta s \\text{ trained}","trainable_params":"d_{out}\\ \\text{(per-channel; } d_{out}\\,d_{in}/g \\text{ for group size } g)","locus":"every linear layer of a weight-only (3-4 bit) quantized LLM","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"diagonal rescaling of the output rows of a frozen integer matrix: \\(\\hat W=\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\,\\overline W_0\\) (group-wise: one scale per output row and input group); the integer codes never change","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The trained parameters are the quantization scales themselves, so the result is a standard quantized model with the same integer codes and kernels.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta s = 0\\): the model starts as the RTN/OPTQ-quantized model whose scales \\(s_0\\) minimize \\(\\lVert W_0-\\hat W_0\\rVert_F\\), so the starting point is close to but not exactly \\(W_0\\).","key_idea":"Treat the quantization step sizes as the PEFT parameters. Freeze the integer codes and fine-tune only per-channel scales, so each task adapter is a tiny scale vector and the deployed model stays quantized.","results_note":"Scales to LLaMA-65B. After instruction tuning, sub-4-bit PEQA models recover or exceed their full-precision language-modeling, few-shot in-context and comprehension performance.","relations":[{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent memory-efficient fine-tuning of quantized LLMs (scales vs adapters)"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"dual-to","note":"PEQA learns the quantizer's multiplicative scales, QA-LoRA its additive zero-points"},{"target":"ia3","type":"competes-with","note":"algebraically an (IA)^3-style output-channel rescaling (\\hat W=\\mathrm{diag}(1+\\Delta s/s_0)\\,\\hat W_0) applied to every quantized linear layer; not equivalent, since (IA)^3 scales only keys, values and the input of W_2"},{"target":"efficientqat","type":"special-case-of","note":"PEQA is EfficientQAT's E2E-QP stage alone (train only step sizes on frozen integer codes, here from RTN/OPTQ init) without the Block-AP stage"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14152","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.14152"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Jeonghoon Kim","Jung Hyun Lee","Sungdong Kim","Joonsuk Park","Kang Min Yoo","Se Jung Kwon","Dongsoo Lee"],"bibkey":"kim2023memory","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kim2023memory,\n  title = {{Memory-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Compressed Large Language Models via sub-4-bit Integer Quantization}},\n  author = {Jeonghoon Kim and Jung Hyun Lee and Sungdong Kim and Joonsuk Park and Kang Min Yoo and Se Jung Kwon and Dongsoo Lee},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.14152},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14152}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\hat W=\\mathrm{diag}(s_0+\\Delta s)\\overline W_0\\): linear in \\(\\Delta s\\), the closure of the output-side torus orbit of \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) ((IA)\\(^3\\)-type at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)); group-wise, one scale per row and input group.","base_point":"Starts at the RTN/OPTQ model \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\): defect \\(e\\).","covariance":"Output side: row permutations and scalings are absorbed by \\(s_0\\) (Mon); input side: per-row rounding commutes with permutations only. The group-wise variant imposes a partition (\\(\\Pi\\)).","merge":"The trained parameters are the quantiser's own scales: same integer codes with new scales, so the merge stays in the quantised box.","rebasing":"A diagonal torus closure is a monoid: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"PEQA is (IA)\\(^3\\)'s output-side torus acting on the quantised base \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) instead of \\(\\theta_0\\): a linear object, regular relative to \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\), whose parameters are the quantiser's own scales, so merging never leaves the quantised type. It is the multiplicative twin of QA-LoRA, which moves the zero-points instead.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"efficientqat","rule":"A3","h":"\\Delta s\\mapsto\\Delta s\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{n/g}^\\top","note":"In increment coordinates (\\(s=s_0+\\Delta s\\) on both sides): broadcast each channel scale to the groups of its row. Valid over common frozen codes and zero-points and row-constant initial step sizes \\(s_0\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{n/g}^\\top\\) (EfficientQAT's E2E-QP is group-wise PEQA over the Block-AP base, whose step sizes are generally not row-constant)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"(IA)\\(^3\\) scales only \\(k\\), \\(v\\) (output) and \\(W_{\\rm down}\\) (input) at \\(\\theta_0\\); PEQA rescales every linear layer's output rows at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) (different boxes, and O3 by the base point)."},{"target":"qa-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Row rescalings of the codes versus group-constant shifts: generically non-nested images."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"apiq","name":"ApiQ","full_name":"Activation-preserved initialization of Quantized LLMs","paper_title":"ApiQ: Finetuning of 2-Bit Quantized Large Language Model","authors":"Liao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024","arxiv":"2402.05147","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.1168/","code_url":"https://github.com/BaohaoLiao/ApiQ","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\min_{Q,A,B}\\ \\big\\lVert W_0X-(Q+BA)X^{q}\\big\\rVert_F\\ \\ \\text{(layer-wise; block-wise: } \\lVert\\mathcal F(W_s,X)-\\mathcal F(Q_s,A_s,B_s,X^{q})\\rVert),\\ \\ Q=q_b(W_0;\\gamma,\\beta)\\ \\text{with learnable clipping }(\\gamma,\\beta)\\ \\text{via STE},\\ X^{q}=\\text{inputs from the already-quantized preceding layers};\\ \\text{then fine-tune } h=(Q+BA)x,\\ Q\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{during fine-tuning}","locus":"all linear layers (or whole transformer blocks for ApiQ-bw); DeBERTa-v3, RoBERTa, Llama-2, Mistral","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"the same \\(Q+BA\\) shape as LoftQ, but fitted in activation space with calibration data, sequentially layer-by-layer or block-by-block so that quantization error does not accumulate with depth","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"\\(BA\\) can be merged only into a high-precision copy; merging into the \\(b\\)-bit \\(Q\\) requires re-quantization.","identity_at_init":"Approximate: \\(Q\\), \\(A\\), \\(B\\) are calibrated so that the quantized layer or block reproduces the full-precision activations given the already-quantized inputs \\(X^{q}\\); the model function is close to, not equal to, the original.","key_idea":"Initialize LoRA and quantize jointly by matching activations, not weights. Each quantized layer is fitted given its quantized inputs, so the adapter stops error propagation from shallow to deep layers, which is crucial at 2 bits.","results_note":"Attains the lowest activation error and the best fine-tuning results against QLoRA, LoftQ and LQ-LoRA across 2-4 bits on GLUE (DeBERTa-v3, RoBERTa) and on Llama-2/Mistral language modeling, arithmetic and commonsense reasoning, with the largest gains at 2 bits.","relations":[{"target":"loftq","type":"builds-on","note":"same joint (Q,A,B) initialization, objective moved from weight error \\lVert W_0-Q-BA\\rVert to activation error"},{"target":"lq-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative quantization-aware LoRA initialization"},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"replaces QLoRA's zero-init adapters on a naively quantized base"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.05147","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.05147","https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.1168/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Baohao Liao","Christian Herold","Shahram Khadivi","Christof Monz"],"bibkey":"liao2024apiq","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liao2024apiq,\n  title = {{ApiQ: Finetuning of 2-Bit Quantized Large Language Model}},\n  author = {Baohao Liao and Christian Herold and Shahram Khadivi and Christof Monz},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2402.05147},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.1168/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(Q+BA\\) as LoftQ, with \\((Q,A,B)\\) and the clipping \\((\\gamma,\\beta)\\) fitted to activations layer by layer (or block by block) given already-quantised inputs \\(X^q\\); \\(B_0A_0\\) has rank \\(r\\): a translated cone over a quantised apex.","base_point":"Pointed up to an attained activation residual \\(\\|W_0X-(Q+B_0A_0)X^q\\|\\), not a weight residual.","covariance":"Quantisation grid; the calibration data add a data frame on the input side (the objective is invariant under \\((W_0h^{-1},hX)\\) before rounding)."},"categorical_reading":"ApiQ builds the same Type II object as LoftQ, a rank-\\(r\\) translated cone over a quantised apex, but chooses the section by matching activations sequentially. Its defect is therefore measured through the realisation \\(\\Phi_f\\) on calibration data rather than in \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"loftq","test":"O1","reason":"Activation-fitted and weight-fitted quantised apexes \\(Q\\) differ by a generically full-rank matrix, so the translated cones are not nested."},{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"Smooth Type II pointing over \\(Q\\) versus QLoRA's apex at \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"efficientqat","name":"EfficientQAT","full_name":"Efficient Quantization-Aware Training","paper_title":"EfficientQAT: Efficient Quantization-Aware Training for Large Language Models","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2025","arxiv":"2407.11062","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11062","code_url":"https://github.com/OpenGVLab/EfficientQAT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{Block-AP: }\\min_{W,s,z}\\lVert\\mathcal F_{blk}(W_0,X)-\\mathcal F_{blk}(Q_{s,z}(W),X)\\rVert\\ \\text{block by block};\\quad \\text{E2E-QP: } \\hat W=s\\odot(\\overline W-z),\\ \\overline W\\ \\text{frozen integers},\\ \\text{only step sizes } s \\text{ trained end-to-end on the task loss}","trainable_params":"d_{out}\\,d_{in}/g\\ \\text{step sizes in E2E-QP (group size } g)","locus":"all linear layers of the LLM; 2-4 bit group-wise uniform quantization","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"two stages: block-wise reconstruction training of all weights plus quantization parameters, then end-to-end training of group-wise multiplicative step sizes only (diagonal-per-group scaling of a frozen integer matrix)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Block-AP trains the latent weights together with step sizes and zero points and then rounds them to integer codes; E2E-QP trains only step sizes on those frozen codes. The output is a standard group-wise uniformly quantized model, with no adapter.","identity_at_init":"E2E-QP starts from the Block-AP-calibrated quantized model (step sizes from Block-AP); there is no exact identity with \\(W_0\\), by design of quantization.","key_idea":"Make quantization-aware training feasible for 70B models in two stages. First, train all parameters block by block against a reconstruction loss. Then, end to end, train only the quantization step sizes, which is a PEQA-style parameter-efficient stage on a frozen integer model.","results_note":"Produces a 2-bit Llama-2-70B on a single A100-80GB in 41 hours with less than 3 points of accuracy loss (69.48 vs 72.41), and outperforms prior quantization methods from 7B to 70B, including instruction-tuned and multimodal LLMs.","relations":[{"target":"peqa","type":"builds-on","note":"the E2E-QP stage trains only quantization scales on frozen integer weights, like PEQA"},{"target":"l4q","type":"competes-with","note":"both are memory-efficient QAT for LLMs"},{"target":"qa-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative route to accurate low-bit fine-tuned models without a 16-bit branch"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11062","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.11062"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Mengzhao Chen","Wenqi Shao","Peng Xu","Jiahao Wang","Peng Gao","Kaipeng Zhang","Ping Luo"],"bibkey":"chen2024efficientqat","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024efficientqat,\n  title = {{EfficientQAT: Efficient Quantization-Aware Training for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Mengzhao Chen and Wenqi Shao and Peng Xu and Jiahao Wang and Peng Gao and Kaipeng Zhang and Ping Luo},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2407.11062},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11062}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Two stages: Block-AP trains all weights and quantiser parameters block-wise against reconstruction (a data-driven base change \\(\\kappa_{\\rm BAP}\\), not a PEFT object); E2E-QP trains only group-wise step sizes on frozen codes. Classified by E2E-QP.","shape":"\\(\\hat W=s\\odot(\\overline W-z)\\), linear in the step sizes \\(s\\).","base_point":"Pointed at \\(\\kappa_{\\rm BAP}\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_0\\).","covariance":"Group-wise step sizes over contiguous groups: an imposed partition.","merge":"The step sizes are the quantiser's own parameters: the output stays a standard group-wise quantised model.","rebasing":"Scale families on fixed codes are closed under composition."},"categorical_reading":"EfficientQAT is a base change followed by PEQA: Block-AP constructs a calibrated quantised base \\(\\kappa_{\\rm BAP}\\theta_0\\) by block-wise full training, and E2E-QP is the group-wise scale torus acting on its frozen codes. Only the second stage is an object of the slice; the first chooses where it is pointed.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"peqa","test":"O2","reason":"Group-wise scales have \\(d=d_{out}d_{in}/g>d_{out}\\), so E2E-QP does not factor through per-channel PEQA."},{"target":"qlora","test":"O1","reason":"On-grid scale families versus QLoRA's off-grid cone."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"loqt","name":"LoQT","full_name":"Low-Rank Adapters for Quantized Training","paper_title":"LoQT: Low-Rank Adapters for Quantized Pretraining","authors":"Loeschcke et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2405.16528","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16528","code_url":"https://github.com/sebulo/LoQT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h=\\big(W_Q+s\\,P_QB\\big)x,\\ \\ W_Q=q_{NF4}(W),\\ \\ P=U_{[:,1:r]}\\ \\text{from}\\ \\mathrm{SVD}(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L)\\ \\text{(frozen, } P_Q=q_{NF4}(P)\\text{; projector on the smaller side, shown for } d_{out}\\le d_{in}),\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{trained},\\ B_{init}=P^{+}(W-Q)\\ \\text{refined for up to 5 steps with } Q=q_{NF4}(W-PB);\\quad \\text{at } t\\in\\{T_i\\}:\\ W\\leftarrow W_Q+sP_QB,\\ \\text{recompute }P,\\ \\text{re-quantize, re-init }B;\\ \\ T_{i}=\\tau+\\psi^{i}","trainable_params":"r\\max(d_{in},d_{out})\\ \\text{(only } B\\text{; } W \\text{ and } P \\text{ frozen and NF4-quantized; } P \\text{ is placed on the smaller dimension, so } B \\text{ is } r\\times\\max(d_{in},d_{out}))","locus":"all attention and MLP linear layers; used for both pre-training and fine-tuning","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"one-sided low-rank adapter with a frozen gradient-derived projector, \\(W=W_Q+sPB\\) with \\(P\\) the top-\\(r\\) left singular vectors of \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L\\); periodically merged into the NF4 weight and re-quantized at exponentially growing intervals","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(PB\\) is periodically merged into the (quantized) full-rank weight, with a final merge at the end, so inference uses a plain dense or quantized matrix.","identity_at_init":"Approximate: \\(B\\) is initialized to compensate the quantization error (\\(B \\approx P^{+}(W-q_{NF4}(W))\\)) instead of 0; without quantization (LoQT-nq), \\(B=0\\) gives exact identity.","key_idea":"Realize GaLore's projected step as a trainable adapter. Freeze a gradient-SVD projector \\(P\\) and train only \\(B\\) in \\(W+PB\\); since \\(\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial B=P^{\\top}\\nabla_W\\mathcal L\\), Adam on \\(B\\) equals GaLore within a window. Periodically fold \\(PB\\) into an NF4 weight, so neither \\(W\\) nor \\(P\\) ever needs 16-bit storage or gradients.","results_note":"Trains models up to 7B on a single 24GB GPU (13B with per-layer gradient updates). Perplexity is close to unquantized low-rank baselines in LLaMA pre-training on C4, with competitive DeBERTaV3 GLUE and GSM8K fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"builds-on","note":"rewrites GaLore as an adapter: without quantization and within one interval (B_0=0), Adam on B in W+PB applies P\\,\\rho(P^{\\top}G) with the same P, i.e. GaLore's one-sided projected step (paper Eq. 3-7); across intervals LoQT merges and re-initializes B instead of carrying projected states"},{"target":"relora","type":"builds-on","note":"periodic merging of low-rank factors into the base weight to accumulate a high-rank update"},{"target":"qlora","type":"combines","note":"NF4 quantization of the frozen weight (and here also of the projector)"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"both train only one factor of a low-rank product with the other frozen (random A in LoRA-FA, gradient-SVD P in LoQT)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16528","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.16528","https://openreview.net/forum?id=Pnv8C0bU9t"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Sebastian Loeschcke","Mads Toftrup","Michael J. Kastoryano","Serge Belongie","Vésteinn Snæbjarnarson"],"bibkey":"loeschcke2024loqt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{loeschcke2024loqt,\n  title = {{LoQT: Low-Rank Adapters for Quantized Pretraining}},\n  author = {Sebastian Loeschcke and Mads Toftrup and Michael J. Kastoryano and Serge Belongie and Vésteinn Snæbjarnarson},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2405.16528},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16528}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"Mq","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Per interval an adapter \\(W_Q+sP_QB\\) (one-sided, frozen gradient-SVD projector on the smaller side); at \\(T_i=\\tau+\\psi^i\\) it merges into the NF4 weight, re-quantises, recomputes \\(P\\) and re-initialises \\(B\\): a merge-and-restart schedule like ReLoRA.","shape":"Per stage the linear method \\(\\{W_Q+P_QX\\}\\).","base_point":"\\(B_{\\rm init}\\approx P^+(W-Q)\\), refined, compensates the part of the quantisation error inside \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,P\\): defect = attained residual. Exact pointing only for LoQT-nq with \\(B_0=0\\).","gauge":"\\(B\\mapsto P_QB\\) is injective.","covariance":"The gradient-SVD frame (\\(CO\\times CO\\) for GaLore) is broken by NF4 quantisation of both \\(W\\) and \\(P\\): coordinate grid. LoQT-nq is \\(CO\\times CO\\) (gradient).","merge":"Dequantise-add-requantise at each \\(T_i\\): type-preserving but lossy; the re-initialised \\(B\\) absorbs part of the new error.","rebasing":"A schedule with exponentially growing intervals; reach \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoQT realises GaLore's projected step as a forward one-sided adapter (Prop IV.2) and re-bases it ReLoRA-style into a quantised weight. Unlike GaLore it resets \\(B\\) at each re-basing, so it shares GaLore's per-stage image but not its trajectory.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"galore","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto sB","note":"Per stage, LoQT-nq with \\(B_0=0\\) and the same \\(P_k\\): both are the linear method \\(W_k+P_kX\\); dynamics differ (reset versus carried-over state)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"B\\mapsto(P_k,\\ \\tfrac{s}{s^\\prime}B)","note":"Per stage (LoQT-nq): freezing LoRA's left factor at \\(P_k\\), into LoRA pointed at \\((P_k,0)\\) (FB\\((P_k)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA); \\(s^\\prime\\) is LoRA's scale."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"qlora","test":"O3","reason":"LoQT's \\(B_{\\rm init}\\) compensates quantisation error, so \\(\\rho(q_0)\\ne\\kappa\\theta_0\\) (O3). Moreover the refinement re-quantises \\(W_Q=q_{\\rm NF4}(W-PB)\\ne q_{\\rm NF4}(W)\\), generically by a high-rank difference, so the first-stage image \\(W_Q+P_QX\\) is not even inside QLoRA's \\(\\kappa\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) (O1); only without refinement steps is it contained, with the pointings still disagreeing."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"galore","name":"GaLore","full_name":"Gradient Low-Rank Projection","paper_title":"GaLore: Memory-Efficient LLM Training by Gradient Low-Rank Projection","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2403.03507","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03507","code_url":"https://github.com/jiaweizzhao/GaLore","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"G_t=\\nabla_W\\mathcal L(W_t)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}},\\ \\ P_t=U_{[:,1:r]}\\ \\text{of}\\ \\mathrm{SVD}(G_{t_0})\\ \\text{(refreshed every }T\\text{ steps)};\\ \\ R_t=P_t^{\\top}G_t\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ (M_t,V_t)=\\mathrm{Adam}(R_t),\\ N_t=M_t/(\\sqrt{V_t}+\\epsilon);\\quad W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta\\,\\alpha\\,P_tN_t\\ \\ \\text{(all of }W\\text{ trained; no adapter; shown for } d_{out}\\le d_{in}\\text{, else project on the right)}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight); optimizer state } 2r\\,d_{in}+r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{vs. } 2d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{for Adam (for } d_{out}\\le d_{in})","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices of attention and MLP blocks (embeddings / LM head usually kept on standard Adam)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"each step's update lies in an \\(r\\)-dimensional left subspace: \\(\\Delta W_t=-\\eta\\alpha P_tN_t\\) with \\(P_t\\in\\mathrm{St}(d_{out},r)\\) the gradient's top singular vectors; the subspace switches every \\(T\\) steps, so the accumulated \\(\\sum_t\\Delta W_t\\) is high-rank","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"There is no auxiliary module: updates are written straight into \\(W\\), so the result is an ordinary dense checkpoint with the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: training starts at \\(W_0\\) with no reparameterization; the projector is initialized from the SVD of the first gradient.","key_idea":"Put the low-rank structure on the gradient rather than the weights. Project each gradient onto a periodically refreshed top-\\(r\\) singular subspace, run Adam there, and project back. This gives full-parameter learning with LoRA-like optimizer memory.","results_note":"Cuts optimizer-state memory by up to 65.5% (82.5% with 8-bit GaLore) while matching full-rank LLaMA 1B/7B pre-training on C4 (up to 19.7B tokens) and RoBERTa GLUE fine-tuning. It was the first to pre-train a 7B model on a single 24GB RTX 4090 without model parallelism, checkpointing or offloading.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"dual-to","note":"LoRA confines the weight update to a fixed r-dimensional subspace (parameter side); GaLore confines each gradient step to a periodically re-chosen r-dimensional subspace (optimizer side)"},{"target":"relora","type":"competes-with","note":"both aim at high-rank updates with low-rank memory, by periodic merging (ReLoRA) vs periodic subspace switching (GaLore)"},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"competes-with","note":"SVD-chosen vs resampled random projections of the gradient"},{"target":"loqt","type":"dual-to","note":"optimizer-side vs parameter-side form of the same step: a frozen-P adapter W+PB trained with Adam (LoQT, without quantization, B_0=0) reproduces GaLore's update inside one subspace window"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03507","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.03507","https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/optimizers","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/zhao24s.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Jiawei Zhao","Zhenyu Zhang","Beidi Chen","Zhangyang Wang","Anima Anandkumar","Yuandong Tian"],"bibkey":"zhao2024galore","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2024galore,\n  title = {{GaLore: Memory-Efficient LLM Training by Gradient Low-Rank Projection}},\n  author = {Jiawei Zhao and Zhenyu Zhang and Beidi Chen and Zhangyang Wang and Anima Anandkumar and Yuandong Tian},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2403.03507},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03507}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"As VIII.B: per period the linear method \\(\\{W_k+P_kX\\}\\) with \\(P_k\\) the gradient's top-\\(r\\) singular vectors.","covariance":"VIII.B: \\(CO\\times CO\\) (gradient frame).","merge":"n/a: writes \\(W\\) directly.","rebasing":"axes.json lists GaLore under 'schedule'; its reach is \\(\\Theta\\) (VIII.B '\\(\\to\\Theta\\)')."},"categorical_reading":"GaLore is a backward lens with compression \\(P_t^\\top\\) and anchor \\(P_t\\); by the degree-one collapse it is one-sided LoRA \\(W_k+P_kC\\) re-based every period, with \\(C\\)'s optimizer state carried over untransported. As a set over its possible sequences of projectors, its reach is \\(\\Theta\\), because the linear shape moves, not because any single period is high rank.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"C\\mapsto s^{-1}C\\quad(A_0:=P_k^\\top)","note":"Per period, right-projection case (\\(d_{out}>d_{in}\\); here \\(P_k^\\top\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times d_{in}}\\) denotes the transposed top-\\(r\\) right singular vectors): GaLore's period \\(W_k+CP_k^\\top\\) is LoRA-FA\\((P_k^\\top)\\); \\(s\\) is LoRA-FA's scale."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"C\\mapsto(P_k,\\ s^{-1}C)","note":"Left-projection case (\\(d_{out}\\le d_{in}\\)): per period FB\\((P_k)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA pointed at \\((P_k,0)\\); trajectories agree under Prop IV.2 (Adam, no weight decay, state carried on both sides)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Against a single \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) over \\(\\theta_0\\): after \\(K\\ge2\\) periods the reach has rank up to \\(\\min(Kr,m,n)>r\\). (The arrow above is per period.)"},{"target":"flora-opt","test":"O1","reason":"Per period, a gradient-SVD frame \\(P_k\\) versus a random Gaussian frame \\(A_t\\): the period images are generically not nested."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"galore-2","name":"GaLore 2","full_name":"GaLore 2 (scalable Gradient Low-Rank Projection)","paper_title":"GaLore 2: Large-Scale LLM Pre-Training by Gradient Low-Rank Projection","authors":"Su et al.","year":2025,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2504.20437","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20437","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta\\,\\alpha\\,P_t\\,\\rho_t\\big(P_t^{\\top}G_t\\big),\\ \\ P_t=\\mathrm{rSVD}_r(G_{t_0})\\ \\text{(fast randomized SVD, refreshed every }T\\text{ steps)};\\ \\rho_t=\\text{(8-bit) Adam on the }r\\times d_{in}\\text{ state};\\ \\text{per-layer fused update after FSDP reduce-scatter; optional low-bit projectors (Q-GaLore) and tensor projections (Tensor-GaLore)}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight); optimizer state } \\approx 2r\\,d_{in}+r\\,d_{out}","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices; designed for large-scale pre-training under FSDP","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"same projected-gradient structure as GaLore (\\(\\Delta W_t\\in\\mathrm{span}(P_t)\\otimes\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}}\\)), with randomized-SVD projectors and sharded per-layer updates","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updates go directly into \\(W\\); the output is a standard dense checkpoint.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: starts at the initial (or pretrained) \\(W\\) with no reparameterization.","key_idea":"An engineering consolidation of GaLore. Randomized SVD removes the SVD bottleneck (up to 20 minutes per subspace update on Llama 7B), FSDP integration fuses per-layer projected updates, and Q-GaLore and Tensor-GaLore extensions are folded in. The paper then tests GaLore at real pre-training scale.","results_note":"Pre-trains Llama 7B from scratch on 500B tokens with final validation loss, perplexity and downstream scores comparable to an 8-bit Adam baseline.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"builds-on","note":"same projected-gradient optimizer, made scalable (randomized SVD, FSDP)"},{"target":"q-galore","type":"combines","note":"supports Q-GaLore's low-bit projection matrices"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20437","https://arxiv.org/html/2504.20437","https://github.com/jiaweizzhao/GaLore"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["DiJia Su","Andrew Gu","Jane Xu","Yuandong Tian","Jiawei Zhao"],"bibkey":"su2025galore","bibtex":"@article{su2025galore,\n  title = {{GaLore 2: Large-Scale LLM Pre-Training by Gradient Low-Rank Projection}},\n  author = {DiJia Su and Andrew Gu and Jane Xu and Yuandong Tian and Jiawei Zhao},\n  year = {2025},\n  eprint = {2504.20437},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.20437},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20437}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"covariance":"Randomised SVD gives an approximate top-\\(r\\) frame: \\(CO\\times CO\\) in law.","rebasing":"GaLore's schedule (reach \\(\\Theta\\)); FSDP fusion and 8-bit states change cost, not the object."},"categorical_reading":"GaLore 2 is GaLore as software (randomised-SVD projector, FSDP-fused per-layer updates, 8-bit states). The object is unchanged: each period is the linear method \\(W_k+P_kX\\) with an approximately spectral frame.","entry_type":"software","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"galore","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per period, isomorphic objects for a common \\(P_k\\); the randomised SVD only perturbs the frame."}],"obstructions":[],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"q-galore","name":"Q-GaLore","full_name":"Quantized GaLore","paper_title":"Q-GaLore: Quantized GaLore with INT4 Projection and Layer-Adaptive Low-Rank Gradients","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"CPAL 2025","arxiv":"2407.08296","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v280/zhang25a.html","code_url":"https://github.com/VITA-Group/Q-GaLore","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W^{\\mathrm{INT8}}_{t+1}=\\mathrm{SR}_{8}\\Big(W_t-\\eta\\,\\alpha\\,P_t^{\\mathrm{INT4}}\\,\\rho_t\\big(P_t^{\\mathrm{INT4}\\top}G_t\\big)\\Big),\\ \\ \\mathrm{SR}=\\text{stochastic rounding (unbiased)};\\ \\ P_t^{\\mathrm{INT4}}\\ \\text{block-wise INT4};\\ \\text{SVD interval of layer } l \\text{ doubled when } \\cos(P^{(l)}_{k\\text{ intervals}})\\ge\\text{threshold (lazy layer-adaptive refresh)};\\ \\text{fused per-layer backward update}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight, stored INT8); optimizer state } \\approx 2r\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{(+ INT4 projector } r\\,d_{out})","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices; pre-training and fine-tuning","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"GaLore projected update with quantized operands: INT8 weights updated through stochastic rounding, INT4 projector, per-layer adaptive subspace-refresh schedule","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Training directly updates the (INT8) weights; no adapter remains.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: starts from the (INT8-quantized) initial weights with no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Gradient subspaces settle early in many layers and projectors tolerate 4-bit quantization. Q-GaLore therefore refreshes subspaces lazily per layer, stores projectors in INT4 and weights in INT8, and uses stochastic rounding so small updates are not lost.","results_note":"Pre-trains LLaMA-7B from scratch on a single 16GB RTX 4060 Ti. In fine-tuning it uses up to 50% less memory than LoRA and GaLore and beats QLoRA by up to 5.19 MMLU points at the same memory cost.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"builds-on","note":"quantized, lazily refreshed GaLore"},{"target":"qlora","type":"competes-with","note":"memory-matched fine-tuning comparison on MMLU"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.08296","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.08296","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v280/zhang25a.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Zhenyu Zhang","Ajay Jaiswal","Lu Yin","Shiwei Liu","Jiawei Zhao","Yuandong Tian","Zhangyang Wang"],"bibkey":"zhang2024q","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024q,\n  title = {{Q-GaLore: Quantized GaLore with INT4 Projection and Layer-Adaptive Low-Rank Gradients}},\n  author = {Zhenyu Zhang and Ajay Jaiswal and Lu Yin and Shiwei Liu and Jiawei Zhao and Yuandong Tian and Zhangyang Wang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {CPAL},\n  eprint = {2407.08296},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v280/zhang25a.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"defect","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Per period GaLore's \\(\\{W_k+P_kX\\}\\), but weights are stochastically rounded to INT8 every step, so iterates live on the INT8 lattice and each step leaves \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,P_k\\) by a rounding error that vanishes in expectation.","base_point":"Starts from the INT8 copy \\(\\kappa_8\\theta_0\\): defect.","covariance":"The INT4 projector and the INT8 weight grid break the gradient-SVD frame's orthogonal covariance: coordinate grid.","merge":"Writes INT8 weights directly.","rebasing":"A schedule with lazy layer-adaptive refresh (interval doubled when consecutive projectors align)."},"categorical_reading":"Q-GaLore is GaLore after base change to a quantised type: the moving linear shape is unchanged in expectation, but unbiased stochastic rounding pushes the iterates onto the INT8 lattice. Its lazy refresh changes the schedule, not the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O1","reason":"Per period, stochastically rounded INT8 steps leave \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,P_k\\) coordinatewise, so they are not in GaLore's period image (they agree only in law); O3 also holds, by the INT8 base point."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"flora-opt","name":"Flora","full_name":"From LoRA to high-rank updates","paper_title":"Flora: Low-Rank Adapters Are Secretly Gradient Compressors","authors":"Hao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.03293","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03293","code_url":"https://github.com/BorealisAI/flora-opt","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{LoRA dynamics} \\approx W_0-\\eta\\textstyle\\sum_t G_tA_0^{\\top}A_0\\ \\ (\\text{random } A_0,\\ \\text{only } B \\text{ moving});\\ \\ \\text{Flora: } C_t=G_tA_t^{\\top}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_t\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tfrac1r)^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{regenerated from a seed, resampled every }\\kappa\\text{ steps};\\ \\ M_t=\\beta M_{t-1}T_t+(1-\\beta)C_t,\\ \\ T_t=A_{t-1}A_t^{\\top}\\ \\text{at resampling steps (momentum transfer), else } T_t=I_r;\\ \\ W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta\\,\\mathcal U(M_tA_t)\\ \\ (\\mathcal U=\\text{base optimizer, Adafactor in the paper})","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight); compressed state } r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per EMA / accumulator (+ a random seed for } A_t)","locus":"attention and feed-forward weight matrices (embeddings and vector parameters handled uncompressed); T5, GPT-2 (ViT in the appendix)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"random-projection (Johnson-Lindenstrauss) compression of the gradient along its input dimension, \\(C=GA^{\\top}\\), decompressed by \\(CA\\); resampling \\(A_t\\) makes the accumulated update high-rank; sublinear \\(O(r\\,d_{out})\\) state","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Flora is an optimizer that writes directly into \\(W\\); no adapter is created.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: \\(W\\) starts at \\(W_0\\); only optimizer states are compressed.","key_idea":"Under SGD with a small learning rate, LoRA's update is dominated by \\(B\\) moving under a nearly fixed random \\(A_0\\), i.e. \\(-\\eta GA_0^{\\top}A_0\\), which is random-projection compression of the gradient. Resampling the projection breaks LoRA's low-rank ceiling while keeping its sublinear memory for momentum and gradient accumulation.","results_note":"With Adafactor as base optimizer, Flora-compressed gradient accumulation matches full-matrix accumulation on T5 (XSum) and GPT-2 (IWSLT17 De-En) at reasonably large \\(r\\), using only about 30% of its memory overhead for T5-3B / GPT-2-XL at \\(r=256\\). Compressed momentum recovers or surpasses naive momentum when training from scratch, where LoRA lags badly.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"dual-to","note":"shows LoRA updates are approximately random-projection gradient compressors (parameter-side LoRA = optimizer-side compression)"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"generalizes","note":"LoRA-FA without its (A_0A_0^{\\top})^{-1} gradient correction (v1) under SGD is Flora with a fixed, never-resampled projection; with the correction (v3) the fixed projection becomes the orthogonal projector onto row(A_0)"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"random (Flora) vs SVD-based (GaLore) gradient projections"},{"target":"apollo","type":"competes-with","note":"APOLLO also relies on random projections, but only to estimate scaling factors"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03293","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.03293","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/hao24a.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Yongchang Hao","Yanshuai Cao","Lili Mou"],"bibkey":"hao2024flora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hao2024flora,\n  title = {{Flora: Low-Rank Adapters Are Secretly Gradient Compressors}},\n  author = {Yongchang Hao and Yanshuai Cao and Lili Mou},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.03293},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03293}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"As VIII.B: per period the linear method \\(\\{W_k+XA_t\\}\\) with \\(A_t\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1/r)\\) regenerated from a seed.","covariance":"VIII.B 'fr': covariance in law through the Gaussian \\(A_t\\).","rebasing":"axes.json 'schedule'; reach \\(\\Theta\\). Momentum transfer \\(MA_{t-1}A_t^\\top\\) at resampling is an approximate (Johnson-Lindenstrauss) transport of optimizer state, unlike GaLore's untransported carry-over. Parameter-scaled Adafactor breaks the degree-one collapse (Prop IV.2) unless scale_parameter=False."},"categorical_reading":"Flora is the random-projection row of the cotangent table: per period LoRA-FA\\((A_t)\\) with a fresh Gaussian \\(A_t\\), re-based, reaching \\(\\Theta\\). Its momentum transfer \\(A_{t-1}A_t^\\top\\) is an approximate transport of optimizer state between frames, the ingredient GaLore lacks.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"C\\mapsto s^{-1}C\\quad(A_0:=A_t)","note":"Per period: Flora's period is LoRA-FA\\((A_t)\\) pointed at \\(C=0\\)."},{"target":"relora","rule":"A2","h":"C\\mapsto(s^{-1}C,\\ A_t)","note":"Per stage: freezing ReLoRA's \\(A\\) at its restart value; same stratum \\((0,A)\\) as ReLoRA's restart pointing when \\(A_k=A_t\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O1","reason":"Per period, a random Gaussian frame \\(A_t\\) versus a gradient-SVD frame \\(P_k\\): the period images are generically not nested."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"fira","name":"Fira","full_name":"Fira (full-rank training under a low-rank constraint)","paper_title":"Fira: Can We Achieve Full-rank Training of LLMs Under Low-rank Constraint?","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2410.01623","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01623","code_url":"https://github.com/xichen-fy/Fira","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"R_t=P_t^{\\top}G_t,\\ \\ W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta\\,P_t\\psi_t(R_t)-\\eta\\,\\big(G_t-P_tR_t\\big)\\,\\mathrm{diag}\\big(\\phi_t(R_t)\\big),\\ \\ \\phi_t(R_t)_i=\\frac{\\lVert\\psi_t(R_{t,:,i})\\rVert}{\\lVert R_{t,:,i}\\rVert}\\ \\text{(column-wise)};\\ \\ \\text{limiter: if } \\lVert S_t\\rVert/\\lVert S_{t-1}\\rVert>\\gamma,\\ S_t\\leftarrow\\gamma\\lVert S_{t-1}\\rVert\\,S_t/\\lVert S_t\\rVert,\\ \\ S_t=(G_t-P_tR_t)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\phi_t(R_t))","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight, full-rank gradient); optimizer state } \\approx 2r\\,d_{in}+r\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{(as GaLore)}","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices; pre-training and fine-tuning","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"GaLore's Adam-in-subspace term plus the out-of-subspace gradient residual \\((I-P_tP_t^{\\top})G_t\\) rescaled column-wise by the low-rank Adam norm ratio, with a relative norm-growth limiter","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only method; updates go directly into W.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Adam's effect on gradient norms (the norm ratio before and after correction) is nearly the same in a low-rank subspace and in full rank. Fira reuses the cheap low-rank ratio to scale the discarded residual gradient, giving full-rank updates at low-rank memory, and adds a norm-growth limiter against loss spikes.","results_note":"Beats LoRA and GaLore in LLaMA pre-training and fine-tuning, matching or exceeding full-rank training. In LLaMA-7B pre-training it uses 8x less optimizer-state memory than GaLore and outperforms it by a large margin.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"generalizes","note":"setting the residual scale \\phi_t\\equiv0 recovers GaLore"},{"target":"apollo","type":"competes-with","note":"concurrent use of channel-wise l2-norm-ratio scaling; APOLLO derives it from structured learning rates with random projections"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01623","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.01623","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/119690","https://openreview.net/forum?id=7aSBAw7tJf"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Xi Chen","Kaituo Feng","Changsheng Li","Xunhao Lai","Xiangyu Yue","Ye Yuan","Guoren Wang"],"bibkey":"chen2024fira","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024fira,\n  title = {{Fira: Can We Achieve Full-rank Training of LLMs Under Low-rank Constraint?}},\n  author = {Xi Chen and Kaituo Feng and Changsheng Li and Xunhao Lai and Xiangyu Yue and Ye Yuan and Guoren Wang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2410.01623},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01623}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is the identity; the update \\(P\\psi(R)+(G-PR)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\phi)\\) is a non-linear preconditioner of the full gradient with low-rank Adam state.","shape":"The residual term is full rank, so one step can move in any direction: the per-period image is all of \\(\\Theta\\) (Lin \\(=\\Theta\\), as Full FT).","covariance":"Image-level \\(GL\\times GL\\) (the image is \\(\\Theta\\)); the update rule itself is only \\(CO(m)\\times\\)Mon\\((n)\\)-equivariant (gradient-SVD frame, column-wise norm ratios).","merge":"n/a: writes \\(W\\).","rebasing":"\\(R_1=\\Theta\\), so cycles gain nothing; refreshing \\(P\\) only moves the optimizer frame."},"categorical_reading":"Fira is not a low-rank object of the slice: its forward object is Full FT, and the low-rank structure lives only in the optimizer state, reused to rescale the out-of-subspace residual. In the framework it is Full FT with a different cometric, and \\(\\phi\\equiv0\\) collapses it to GaLore's rank-\\(r\\) periods.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"A Fira step is full rank; a GaLore period has rank \\(\\le r\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"apollo","name":"APOLLO","full_name":"Approximated Gradient Scaling for Memory-Efficient LLM Optimization","paper_title":"APOLLO: SGD-like Memory, AdamW-level Performance","authors":"Zhu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"MLSys 2025","arxiv":"2412.05270","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05270","code_url":"https://github.com/zhuhanqing/APOLLO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"R_t=P_tG_t,\\ P_t\\sim\\mathcal N_{seed}(0,\\tfrac1r)^{r\\times d_{out}}\\ \\text{(resampled every }T);\\ \\ (M^R_t,V^R_t)=\\mathrm{AdamW}(R_t),\\ \\tilde R_t=M^R_t/(\\sqrt{V^R_t}+\\epsilon);\\ \\ s_j=\\frac{\\lVert\\tilde R_t[:,j]\\rVert_2}{\\lVert R_t[:,j]\\rVert_2}\\ \\text{(APOLLO; APOLLO-Mini: one tensor-wise } s=\\lVert\\tilde R_t\\rVert/\\lVert R_t\\rVert,\\ r=1);\\quad W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta\\,\\alpha\\,G_t\\,\\mathrm{diag}(s)-\\eta\\lambda W_t","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight); optimizer state } 2r\\,d_{in}\\ \\text{(}r=1\\text{ for APOLLO-Mini, i.e. } 2d_{in}\\text{: SGD-like)}","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices of attention and MLP (optim_target_modules)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"structured (channel-wise or tensor-wise) learning-rate scaling: the raw full-rank gradient is right-multiplied by a diagonal matrix \\(\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\) whose entries are estimated from Adam states kept in a random \\(r\\)-dimensional sketch","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only method; produces an ordinary dense checkpoint.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"AdamW's element-wise adaptive learning rate is redundant and can be coarsened to one rate per channel or per tensor. Those few scaling factors can be estimated from Adam run inside a cheap random projection without SVD, even of rank 1.","results_note":"Matches or beats AdamW in LLaMA 60M-7B pre-training (up to 2.8 lower validation perplexity) at near-SGD memory, with about 3x throughput on 8xA100 from larger batches. APOLLO-Mini plus weight quantization pre-trains LLaMA-7B in 12GB.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"builds-on","note":"inherits the low-rank auxiliary space idea, but uses it only to estimate scaling factors and replaces SVD with random projection"},{"target":"fira","type":"competes-with","note":"same l2-norm-ratio channel scaling form, derived from a different principle"},{"target":"adam-mini","type":"competes-with","note":"both reduce Adam's second-moment state to block-shared learning rates"},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"competes-with","note":"both use seed-regenerated random projections of the gradient; APOLLO uses them only to estimate channel-wise scaling factors and lists Flora among baselines with performance trade-offs"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05270","https://arxiv.org/html/2412.05270","https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/optimizers"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Hanqing Zhu","Zhenyu Zhang","Wenyan Cong","Xi Liu","Sem Park","Vikas Chandra","Bo Long","David Z. Pan","Zhangyang Wang","Jinwon Lee"],"bibkey":"zhu2024apollo","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhu2024apollo,\n  title = {{APOLLO: SGD-like Memory, AdamW-level Performance}},\n  author = {Hanqing Zhu and Zhenyu Zhang and Wenyan Cong and Xi Liu and Sem Park and Vikas Chandra and Bo Long and David Z. Pan and Zhangyang Wang and Jinwon Lee},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of Machine Learning and Systems (MLSys)},\n  eprint = {2412.05270},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.05270}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is the identity; the update \\(G\\,\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\) rescales the raw gradient channel-wise, with \\(s\\) estimated from Adam run in a random \\(r\\)-dimensional sketch (tensor-wise for APOLLO-Mini).","shape":"The raw gradient is unconstrained, so the per-step image is all of \\(\\Theta\\).","covariance":"Image-level \\(GL\\times GL\\); the update rule is \\(O(m)\\) in law (Gaussian sketch) times Mon\\((n)\\) (channel-wise scaling).","merge":"n/a: writes \\(W\\).","rebasing":"\\(R_1=\\Theta\\): idempotent; resampling the sketch only changes the scale estimates."},"categorical_reading":"APOLLO is Full FT with a structured learning rate: the anchor is the identity up to a diagonal channel scaling, and the random sketch only estimates that scaling. As an object it is the terminal Full FT; unlike GaLore and Flora nothing confines its update to a moving subspace.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"flora-opt","test":"O2","reason":"APOLLO's update \\(G\\,\\mathrm{diag}(s)\\) is full rank; a Flora period has rank \\(\\le r\\)."},{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"Full-rank steps versus rank-\\(\\le r\\) periods."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"online-subspace-descent","name":"Online Subspace Descent","full_name":"Online Subspace Descent","paper_title":"Memory-Efficient LLM Training with Online Subspace Descent","authors":"Liang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2408.12857","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12857","code_url":"https://github.com/kyleliang919/Online-Subspace-Descent","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_{t+1}=W_t+P_t\\,\\phi_t(\\hat S_t),\\ \\ \\hat S_t=\\psi_t\\big(\\hat S_{t-1},P_t^{\\top}\\nabla\\mathcal L(W_t)\\big);\\ \\ P_{t+1}=\\mathrm{OptimizerP.step}\\big(P_t,\\nabla_P\\mathcal L_{G_t}(P_t)\\big),\\ \\ \\mathcal L_{G}(P)=\\lVert PP^{\\top}\\tilde G-\\tilde G\\rVert^2+\\lambda\\lVert P^{\\top}P-I_r\\rVert^2,\\ \\tilde G=G/\\lVert G\\rVert","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{(full weight); optimizer state in the } r\\text{-dim subspace plus } P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{(and its optimizer state)}","locus":"all 2-D weight matrices; LLaMA pre-training (60M-7B)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"projected optimizer whose projector \\(P_t\\) evolves continuously by one online-PCA gradient step per iteration (no SVD); any Hamiltonian-descent optimizer (SGD, momentum, Lion, Adam) can run inside the subspace","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only method; updates go directly into \\(W\\).","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Replace GaLore's periodic SVD with streaming online PCA on the projector. A Hamiltonian-descent (Lyapunov) analysis then gives the first convergence guarantee for arbitrary projector update rules.","results_note":"In LLaMA 60M-7B pre-training on C4 it reaches lower perplexity and better downstream scores than other low-rank training methods, narrowing the gap to full-rank baselines.","relations":[{"target":"galore","type":"generalizes","note":"GaLore is the special choice of projector rule P_{t+1}=\\chi_t(\\cdot)= top-r left singular vectors of the current gradient, recomputed every T steps (paper Sec. 3.2)"},{"target":"apollo","type":"competes-with","note":"SVD-free memory-efficient optimizer"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12857","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.12857","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/95328","https://openreview.net/forum?id=P8rTCT6g45"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Kaizhao Liang","Bo Liu","Lizhang Chen","Qiang Liu"],"bibkey":"liang2024memory","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liang2024memory,\n  title = {{Memory-Efficient LLM Training with Online Subspace Descent}},\n  author = {Kaizhao Liang and Bo Liu and Lizhang Chen and Qiang Liu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2408.12857},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.12857}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). The update \\(W_{t+1}=W_t+P_t\\phi_t(\\hat S_t)\\) compresses the gradient by \\(P_t^\\top\\) and anchors it by \\(P_t\\) (Def IV.1).","shape":"Per step the reachable set is the moving linear space \\(\\{W_t+P_tX\\}\\). The anchor is constant within a step, so Prop IV.2 applies with period 1.","base_point":"Each step is a linear method at its own base, hence regular.","covariance":"\\(P_t\\) tracks online PCA of \\(\\tilde G=G/\\lVert G\\rVert\\). The loss \\(\\lVert PP^\\top\\tilde G-\\tilde G\\rVert^2\\) is \\(O(m)\\)-equivariant on the left, and it sees the right side only through \\(\\tilde G\\tilde G^\\top\\). So the frame geometry is \\(CO\\times CO\\) of the gradient, as for GaLore. An Adam-type OptimizerP on \\(P\\) cuts the left side down to signed permutations.","merge":"The method writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme with period 1. The frame moves every step, so the reach is all of \\(\\Theta\\). The subspace optimizer state is carried across rebasings without transport."},"categorical_reading":"A backward lens \\((\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta;\\,c_t=P_t^\\top,\\,a_t=P_t)\\) whose frame takes one online-PCA gradient step per iteration. By the degree-one collapse (Prop IV.2), each step is the one-sided method \\(\\mathrm{FB}(P_t)\\) re-based at \\(\\theta_t\\). It sits in GaLore's row of the cotangent table, with the periodic SVD replaced by a frame that moves every step.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(P_t,\\,X)","note":"Within one step at base \\(\\theta_t\\), OSD is \\(\\mathrm{FB}(P_t):X\\mapsto\\theta_t+P_tX\\), the frozen-\\(B\\) sub-object of LoRA pointed at \\((P_t,0)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"Over \\(T\\) steps OSD reaches \\(\\theta_k+\\sum_tP_t\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}\\), of dimension up to \\(\\min(m,Tr)\\,n>rn\\), so it does not factor through one GaLore period."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"adam-mini","name":"Adam-mini","full_name":"Adam-mini","paper_title":"Adam-mini: Use Fewer Learning Rates To Gain More","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2406.16793","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16793","code_url":"https://github.com/zyushun/Adam-mini","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta=(\\theta_{b})_{b\\in\\mathcal B}\\ \\text{(blocks = smallest dense Hessian sub-blocks: q,k by head; v, attn.proj, MLP by output neuron; embed/output by token)};\\ \\ m_b\\leftarrow\\beta_1m_b+(1-\\beta_1)g_b,\\ \\ v_b\\leftarrow\\beta_2v_b+(1-\\beta_2)\\,\\mathrm{mean}(g_b\\odot g_b)\\in\\mathbb{R},\\ \\ \\theta_b\\leftarrow\\theta_b-\\eta\\,m_b/(\\sqrt{v_b}+\\epsilon)","trainable_params":"\\text{all parameters; second-moment state reduced from } d_{out}d_{in} \\text{ to at most } d_{out}\\ \\text{scalars per matrix (one per block: per head for } W_q,W_k\\text{, per output neuron otherwise)}","locus":"all parameters; Hessian-structure-aware partition of Transformer weights","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"block-wise shared adaptive learning rate: Adam's per-coordinate \\(v\\) is replaced by one scalar per Hessian block (more than 99.9% of \\(v\\) removed)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Optimizer-only method; trains the original parameters directly.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Transformer Hessians are near block-diagonal, and one good learning rate per dense block is enough. Adam-mini keeps momentum but stores one second-moment scalar per block, halving Adam's optimizer memory.","results_note":"On par with or better than AdamW for 39M-13B models in pre-training, SFT and RLHF, with 50% less optimizer memory and 49.6% higher throughput for Llama 2-7B pre-training on 2xA800.","relations":[{"target":"apollo","type":"competes-with","note":"both coarsen Adam's element-wise learning rates into structured ones"},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative way to shrink optimizer state for full-parameter training"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16793","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.16793","https://openreview.net/forum?id=iBExhaU3Lc"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Yushun Zhang","Congliang Chen","Ziniu Li","Tian Ding","Chenwei Wu","Diederik P. Kingma","Yinyu Ye","Zhi-Quan Luo","Ruoyu Sun"],"bibkey":"zhang2024adam","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024adam,\n  title = {{Adam-mini: Use Fewer Learning Rates To Gain More}},\n  author = {Yushun Zhang and Congliang Chen and Ziniu Li and Tian Ding and Chenwei Wu and Diederik P. Kingma and Yinyu Ye and Zhi-Quan Luo and Ruoyu Sun},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2406.16793},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.16793}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\) and no gradient compression (\\(c_t=a_t=\\mathrm{id}\\)). Only `opt` changes: one second-moment scalar per Hessian block. As an object it is Full FT, the terminal object.","shape":"The anchor is the identity, so the per-step image is all of \\(\\Theta\\): Lin, with \\(d=\\dim\\Theta\\).","covariance":"The image is \\(\\Theta\\), so \\(GL\\times GL\\). The optimizer's own equivariance is smaller. \\(v_b\\) is a Frobenius mean over an output row (or a head), so the update is \\(O(n)\\)-equivariant on the input side but only signed-permutation-equivariant across output blocks. That is a symmetry of the dynamics (Exposé III), not of the image.","merge":"The method writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"No schedule. The reach is already \\(\\Theta\\) at \\(K=1\\), so further cycles gain nothing."},"categorical_reading":"Adam-mini is the terminal object \\(\\mathrm{Full\\ FT}=(\\Theta,\\theta_0,\\mathrm{id})\\) with different trained-method data: a block-scalar preconditioner replaces Adam's coordinatewise one. In the lens picture it is a backward method with trivial compression. Every difference from Adam lies in the induced cometric, not in the slice.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lomo","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"Both are the terminal object with forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). They differ only in optimizer data (Def III.1), not as objects."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"Adam-mini coarsens only the preconditioner, so its step is a full-rank matrix: \\(d=\\dim\\Theta\\), against \\(rn\\) for one GaLore period."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"mezo","name":"MeZO","full_name":"Memory-efficient Zeroth-order Optimizer","paper_title":"Fine-Tuning Language Models with Just Forward Passes","authors":"Malladi et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.17333","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17333","code_url":"https://github.com/princeton-nlp/MeZO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\widehat\\nabla\\mathcal L(\\theta;\\mathcal B)=\\frac{\\mathcal L(\\theta+\\epsilon z;\\mathcal B)-\\mathcal L(\\theta-\\epsilon z;\\mathcal B)}{2\\epsilon}\\,z\\ \\approx zz^{\\top}\\nabla\\mathcal L(\\theta;\\mathcal B),\\ \\ z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_d)\\ \\text{regenerated from a stored seed};\\ \\ \\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta\\,\\widehat\\nabla\\mathcal L\\ \\ \\text{(in place; two forward passes, no backprop)}","trainable_params":"\\text{all } d \\text{ parameters (or a PEFT subset such as LoRA / prefix); optimizer memory } O(1)\\text{: one scalar and one seed}","locus":"all parameters (full-parameter) or the parameters of a PEFT module","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"SPSA zeroth-order estimate: a rank-one random projection \\(zz^{\\top}\\nabla\\mathcal L\\) of the gradient, obtained from a finite difference of two forward passes; perturbations regenerated from a random seed so no extra memory is stored","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"MeZO only changes how updates are computed; the trained parameters are the model's own (or a standard PEFT module).","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Classical ZO-SGD can run in place by regenerating Gaussian perturbations from a seed, so fine-tuning costs only inference memory. Good pre-training gives a low effective Hessian rank, so convergence does not degrade with parameter count.","results_note":"With one A100-80GB it fine-tunes a 30B model where backprop fits only 2.7B. It is comparable to backprop fine-tuning on many tasks (OPT up to 66B) with up to 12x memory reduction, works with LoRA and prefix-tuning, and can optimize non-differentiable objectives.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"MeZO can optimize LoRA parameters (MeZO-LoRA)"},{"target":"prefix-tuning","type":"combines","note":"MeZO can optimize prefix parameters (MeZO-prefix)"},{"target":"flora-opt","type":"competes-with","note":"the SPSA estimate zz^{\\top}\\nabla\\mathcal L is a rank-one random projection of the vectorized gradient, a forward-only analogue of Flora's random gradient compression"},{"target":"lomo","type":"competes-with","note":"both target full-parameter fine-tuning at near-inference memory (forward-only vs fused-backward SGD)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17333","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.17333"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Sadhika Malladi","Tianyu Gao","Eshaan Nichani","Alex Damian","Jason D. Lee","Danqi Chen","Sanjeev Arora"],"bibkey":"malladi2023fine","bibtex":"@inproceedings{malladi2023fine,\n  title = {{Fine-Tuning Language Models with Just Forward Passes}},\n  author = {Sadhika Malladi and Tianyu Gao and Eshaan Nichani and Alex Damian and Jason D. Lee and Danqi Chen and Sanjeev Arora},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.17333},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17333}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Compression \\(g\\mapsto\\langle z_t,g\\rangle\\) (a central difference, no reverse pass) with anchor \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda z_t\\) (the table after Def IV.1).","shape":"Each step is the linear line method \\(\\lambda\\mapsto\\theta_t+\\lambda z_t\\), with \\(d=1\\) (Thm V.3, Cor 6).","covariance":"With \\(z_t\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I_d)\\) the method is covariant under \\(O(\\Theta)\\) in law (Table VIII.B), but not under non-orthogonal changes of basis.","merge":"The method writes the full weight (or a PEFT module's parameters) directly.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme of lines, resampled every step. Its reach is \\(\\Theta\\), since the \\(z_t\\) span it."},"categorical_reading":"MeZO is the rank-one, globally random corner of the cotangent table: a backward lens whose compression is a single directional derivative along a fresh Gaussian direction. By Prop IV.3 each step is the linear method \\(\\theta_t+\\mathbb Rz_t\\) on a moving base. So its reach is \\(\\Theta\\), and its cometric \\(\\eta zz^\\top\\) equals \\(\\eta I\\) only in expectation.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"flora-opt","test":"O2","reason":"A MeZO step \\(\\lambda z_t\\) has per-matrix rank \\(\\min(m,n)>r\\), so it lies in no Flora period \\(\\{XA_t\\}\\)."},{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"For the same reason it lies in no GaLore period \\(\\{P_tX\\}\\), whose maximum rank is \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lomo","name":"LOMO","full_name":"LOw-Memory Optimization","paper_title":"Full Parameter Fine-tuning for Large Language Models with Limited Resources","authors":"Lv et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2306.09782","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09782","code_url":"https://github.com/OpenLMLab/LOMO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{during backprop, as soon as } g_i=\\nabla_{\\theta_i}\\mathcal L \\text{ is computed: } \\theta_i\\leftarrow\\theta_i-\\eta\\,g_i,\\ \\text{then free } g_i\\ \\ \\text{(fused gradient computation + SGD update; no optimizer state; gradient memory } O(\\max_i|\\theta_i|))","trainable_params":"\\text{all parameters; optimizer state } 0,\\ \\text{gradient buffer} = \\text{largest single tensor}","locus":"all parameters (full-parameter fine-tuning)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"plain SGD whose per-tensor update is fused into the backward pass (layer-wise gradient release), plus gradient normalization and dynamic loss scaling for mixed precision","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Trains the original parameters directly; no extra modules.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"For fine-tuning, SGD works well enough to drop Adam's states entirely. Fusing each tensor's update into backpropagation also removes the full gradient buffer, leaving parameters and activations as the only memory.","results_note":"Combined with other memory-saving techniques it cuts memory to 10.8% of the DeepSpeed baseline, enabling full-parameter fine-tuning of a 65B model on one machine with 8x RTX 3090 (24GB).","relations":[{"target":"mezo","type":"competes-with","note":"both target full-parameter fine-tuning with minimal memory"},{"target":"badam","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative memory-efficient full-parameter fine-tuning"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09782","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.09782","https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/optimizers","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.445/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Kai Lv","Yuqing Yang","Tengxiao Liu","Qinghui Gao","Qipeng Guo","Xipeng Qiu"],"bibkey":"lv2023full","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lv2023full,\n  title = {{Full Parameter Fine-tuning for Large Language Models with Limited Resources}},\n  author = {Kai Lv and Yuqing Yang and Tengxiao Liu and Qinghui Gao and Qipeng Guo and Xipeng Qiu},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2306.09782},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09782}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Plain SGD, with each tensor's update fused into the backward pass. The forward map is \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\) and the compression is the identity. What changes is the memory schedule of the backward pass (Obs IV.4), not the parametrisation.","shape":"The per-step image is \\(\\Theta\\), the full gradient.","covariance":"The image is \\(\\Theta\\). SGD is \\(O(\\Theta)\\)-natural, and LOMO's global-norm gradient normalisation is orthogonally invariant.","merge":"The method writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"No schedule; the reach is \\(\\Theta\\) at once."},"categorical_reading":"LOMO is Full FT trained by SGD, with the backward lens evaluated tensor by tensor so that each parameter cotangent is consumed as soon as it is produced. It adds nothing to the object. Its contribution is an evaluation order for \\(\\mathsf R[f]\\) that keeps only one tensor's cotangent alive.","entry_type":"recipe","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"mezo","test":"O2","reason":"A LOMO step uses the full gradient (\\(d=\\dim\\Theta\\)); a MeZO step moves along one random line (\\(d=1\\))."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"adalomo","name":"AdaLomo","full_name":"Low-memory Optimization with Adaptive Learning Rate","paper_title":"AdaLomo: Low-memory Optimization with Adaptive Learning Rate","authors":"Lv et al.","year":2023,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2024","arxiv":"2310.10195","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.742/","code_url":"https://github.com/OpenLMLab/LOMO","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{fused in backprop, per matrix } \\theta_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}:\\ r_{t}=\\beta r_{t-1}+(1-\\beta)(g_t^{2})\\mathbf 1_n,\\ \\ c_t=\\beta c_{t-1}+(1-\\beta)\\mathbf 1_m^{\\top}(g_t^{2}),\\ \\ v_t=r_tc_t/(\\mathbf 1_m^{\\top}r_t);\\ \\ u_t=g_t/\\sqrt{v_t},\\ \\ \\hat u_t=\\frac{u_t}{\\max(1,\\mathrm{RMS}(u_t))}\\max(\\epsilon,\\mathrm{RMS}(\\theta_{t-1})),\\ \\ \\theta_t=\\theta_{t-1}-\\alpha_t\\hat u_t","trainable_params":"\\text{all parameters; optimizer state } d_{out}+d_{in}\\ \\text{per matrix (factored second moment), no momentum}","locus":"all parameters (full-parameter instruction tuning and (further) pre-training)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"rank-one non-negative factorization of the second-moment matrix (Adafactor-style row/column statistics) with grouped RMS update normalization, fused into the backward pass","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Trains the original parameters directly.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Of Adam's two ingredients, the adaptive learning rate matters more than momentum for closing LOMO's gap to AdamW. AdaLomo adds a factored, sublinear-memory second moment and a grouped normalization compatible with fused, single-pass backward updates. (The paper's Algorithm 1 line 10 prints \\(u=g/v\\); the official implementation lomo_optim/adalomo.py uses \\(g/\\sqrt{v}\\) via rsqrt, as written here.)","results_note":"Matches AdamW in instruction tuning, further pre-training and from-scratch pre-training of LLaMA models with LOMO-level memory.","relations":[{"target":"lomo","type":"builds-on","note":"adds an adaptive learning rate to LOMO's fused update"},{"target":"adam-mini","type":"competes-with","note":"both shrink Adam's second-moment state (factorization vs block sharing)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.10195","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.10195","https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.742/","https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/optimizers"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"authors_full":["Kai Lv","Hang Yan","Qipeng Guo","Haijun Lv","Xipeng Qiu"],"bibkey":"lv2023adalomo","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lv2023adalomo,\n  title = {{AdaLomo: Low-memory Optimization with Adaptive Learning Rate}},\n  author = {Kai Lv and Hang Yan and Qipeng Guo and Haijun Lv and Xipeng Qiu},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024},\n  eprint = {2310.10195},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-acl.742/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A fused-backward update with an Adafactor-style factored second moment \\(v_t=r_tc_t/(\\mathbf 1^\\top r_t)\\) and grouped RMS normalisation. The forward map is \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\) and the compression is the identity.","shape":"The step \\(g/\\sqrt v\\) is a full-rank matrix, so the image is \\(\\Theta\\). The rank-one factorisation is of the preconditioner, not of the update.","covariance":"The image is \\(\\Theta\\). The factored second moment is natural only under signed row and column permutations, and the relative step \\(\\max(\\epsilon,\\mathrm{RMS}(\\theta_{t-1}))\\) reads the parameter tensor.","merge":"The method writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"No schedule."},"categorical_reading":"AdaLomo is the terminal object with new optimizer data: a rank-one non-negative factorisation of the second moment, fused into the backward pass. Its step is scaled by \\(\\mathrm{RMS}(\\theta)\\), so the degree-one collapse excludes it (Prop IV.2). Run inside a projected or LoRA chart, it would not reproduce the full-weight trajectory.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lomo","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"The same object (Full FT). AdaLomo adds a factored adaptive learning rate, which is trained-method data."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"flora-opt","test":"O2","reason":"Factoring the second moment is not compressing the gradient. AdaLomo's step has full rank, so it lies in no Flora period \\(\\{XA_t\\}\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"badam","name":"BAdam","full_name":"Block coordinate descent with Adam","paper_title":"BAdam: A Memory Efficient Full Parameter Optimization Method for Large Language Models","authors":"Luo et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2404.02827","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02827","code_url":"https://github.com/Ledzy/BAdam","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta=(\\theta_{\\pi_1},\\dots,\\theta_{\\pi_D})\\ \\text{(e.g. one block per transformer layer; ascending / descending / random order)};\\ \\ \\theta_{\\pi_i}^{t+1}\\approx\\arg\\min_{\\theta_{\\pi_i}}\\mathcal L\\big(\\theta_{\\pi_{<i}}^{t+1},\\theta_{\\pi_i},\\theta_{\\pi_{>i}}^{t}\\big)\\ \\text{via } K \\text{ Adam steps; other blocks frozen; Adam state of } \\theta_{\\pi_i} \\text{ discarded when switching}","trainable_params":"\\approx|\\theta|/D \\text{ active at a time (FP32 copy, gradient, } m,v \\text{ only for the active block); all parameters over a block-epoch. Memory } \\approx 2M+16M/D \\text{ GB for an } M\\text{B-parameter model}","locus":"all parameters, cycled block by block (typically transformer layers)","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"block coordinate descent: a time-varying coordinate mask selecting one parameter block, optimized with \\(K\\) Adam steps per block, so the cumulative update is full-parameter and high-rank","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Trains the original parameters in place; the output is a standard dense model.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"Full-parameter fine-tuning at LoRA-like memory: only one block holds FP32 weights, gradients and Adam states at any time. Backpropagation can stop early at the active block, which also saves compute; convergence is proven in the deterministic case.","results_note":"Fine-tunes Llama 3-8B on a single RTX3090-24GB and Llama 3-70B on 4xA100-80GB. It outperforms LoRA on MT-bench and math benchmarks and is comparable to or better than full Adam.","relations":[{"target":"lisa","type":"competes-with","note":"Randomized layer sampling vs. block coordinate descent."},{"target":"lomo","type":"competes-with","note":"memory-efficient full-parameter fine-tuning"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"matches LoRA memory while learning high-rank updates"},{"target":"hift","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are block-coordinate full-parameter methods; BAdam runs K Adam steps per block and discards optimizer states, HiFT updates one group per step and offloads states."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Memory-efficient full-parameter alternative via gradient low-rank projection."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02827","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.02827"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory","selective"],"authors_full":["Qijun Luo","Hengxu Yu","Xiao Li"],"bibkey":"luo2024badam","bibtex":"@inproceedings{luo2024badam,\n  title = {{BAdam: A Memory Efficient Full Parameter Optimization Method for Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Qijun Luo and Hengxu Yu and Xiao Li},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2404.02827},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.02827}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\); the update is block coordinate descent: \\(K\\) Adam steps on one block, then switch with the optimizer state discarded.","shape":"Per stage, the coordinate subspace of the active block (a moving Lin shape).","covariance":"Every stage frees whole boxes (Full FT on the sampled layers, plus embedding and head for LISA), so each stage image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant box by box, as for BitFit, surgical FT and HiFT. The layer partition groups boxes; it does not reshape or pair the coordinates of any box and breaks no neuron permutation or residual rotation (Lemma VI.2 test), so the \\(\\Pi\\) (blocks) label of VIII.B and axes.json is not forced by the decision procedure. Framework IV places LISA beside BitFit (coordinate blocks), which VIII.B files under \\(GL\\times GL\\).","merge":"Writes the original weights in place; nothing to merge.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme of moving linear shapes; the reach is the sum of the blocks visited, \\(\\Theta\\) after one block-epoch (Thm V.3 Cor 6)."},"categorical_reading":"A rebasing scheme of linear block methods: each stage is the coordinate subspace of one block, merged in place after \\(K\\) Adam steps with the optimizer state reset. Its reach is the sum of the blocks visited, which is \\(\\Theta\\) after one block-epoch.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Per stage it makes full-rank updates of every matrix in the active block; LoRA_r's maximum rank is r."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lisa","name":"LISA","full_name":"Layerwise Importance Sampled AdamW","paper_title":"LISA: Layerwise Importance Sampling for Memory-Efficient Large Language Model Fine-Tuning","authors":"Pan et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2403.17919","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.17919","code_url":"https://github.com/OptimalScale/LMFlow","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{every } K \\text{ steps sample active layers with } p=\\{1,\\tfrac{\\gamma}{N_L},\\dots,\\tfrac{\\gamma}{N_L},1\\}\\ \\text{(embedding and LM head always on, } \\gamma \\text{ of } N_L \\text{ middle layers)};\\ \\ \\theta_{\\ell}\\leftarrow\\mathrm{AdamW}(\\theta_\\ell)\\ \\text{for active } \\ell,\\ \\text{all other layers frozen}","trainable_params":"\\text{embedding + head + } \\gamma \\text{ transformer layers at a time (e.g. } \\gamma=2);\\ \\text{all layers over training}","locus":"whole transformer layers (plus embedding and LM head), resampled periodically","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"stochastic block coordinate descent with a time-varying layer mask drawn by importance sampling; optimizer states exist only for the active layers","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updates the original weights in place; no adapter.","identity_at_init":"Trivial: no reparameterization.","key_idea":"LoRA's update norms are heavily skewed toward the bottom and top layers. LISA mimics this by always training the embedding and head and randomly unfreezing a few middle layers, getting full-rank layer updates at LoRA-level memory.","results_note":"Beats LoRA by 10-35% on MT-Bench at similar or lower GPU memory, is on par or better on MMLU, AGIEval and WinoGrande, and beats LoRA on MT-Bench, GSM8K and PubMedQA with LLaMA-2-70B.","relations":[{"target":"badam","type":"competes-with","note":"BAdam is block coordinate descent with Adam and a convergence analysis; LISA is its randomized, importance-sampled counterpart."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Designed as a memory-equivalent but full-rank alternative to LoRA."},{"target":"galore","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative memory-efficient full-parameter optimizer (low-rank gradient projection instead of layer sampling)."},{"target":"hift","type":"competes-with","note":"Both update one block of layers at a time; HiFT cycles deterministically, LISA samples randomly with E+H always on."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.17919","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.17919"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory","selective"],"authors_full":["Rui Pan","Xiang Liu","Shizhe Diao","Renjie Pi","Jipeng Zhang","Chi Han","Tong Zhang"],"bibkey":"pan2024lisa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pan2024lisa,\n  title = {{LISA: Layerwise Importance Sampling for Memory-Efficient Large Language Model Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Rui Pan and Xiang Liu and Shizhe Diao and Renjie Pi and Jipeng Zhang and Chi Han and Tong Zhang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2403.17919},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.17919}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"As in the table: a backward schedule over periodically resampled layers (embedding and head always on).","shape":"Per period, the coordinate subspace of embedding + head + \\(\\gamma\\) sampled layers.","covariance":"Every stage frees whole boxes (Full FT on the sampled layers, plus embedding and head for LISA), so each stage image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant box by box, as for BitFit, surgical FT and HiFT. The layer partition groups boxes; it does not reshape or pair the coordinates of any box and breaks no neuron permutation or residual rotation (Lemma VI.2 test), so the \\(\\Pi\\) (blocks) label of VIII.B and axes.json is not forced by the decision procedure. Framework IV places LISA beside BitFit (coordinate blocks), which VIII.B files under \\(GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"The table column reads \\(\\to\\Theta\\), axes.json files LISA under 'schedule'; it is a rebasing scheme whose reach is \\(\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3 Cor 6)."},"categorical_reading":"A backward schedule: each period trains the coordinate subspace of embedding, head and \\(\\gamma\\) sampled layers and merges in place, a rebasing scheme of moving Lin shapes that reaches \\(\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3 Cor 6). Because the embedding is always on, its backprop cone is the whole network (Obs IV.4).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"Per period LISA makes full-rank updates of its sampled layers, while GaLore's per-period update of each matrix has rank ≤ r."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"bitfit","name":"BitFit","full_name":"Bias-terms Fine-tuning","paper_title":"BitFit: Simple Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning for Transformer-based Masked Language-models","authors":"Ben Zaken et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2106.10199","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.1/","code_url":"https://github.com/benzakenelad/BitFit","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h = W_0 x + (b_0 + \\Delta b),\\quad W_0,\\ g_{LN}\\ \\text{frozen};\\ \\text{trainable: every bias vector } \\Delta b\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}} \\text{ (Q/K/V/O, MLP and LayerNorm biases) plus the task head}","trainable_params":"d_{out}\\ \\text{per linear layer (its bias)};\\ \\approx 0.08\\text{--}0.09\\%\\ \\text{of BERT parameters}","locus":"bias vectors only (attention Q/K/V/O, MLP, LayerNorm biases); LayerNorm gains and all weight matrices frozen","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\); only the translation part of each affine map \\(h = Wx + b\\) moves, i.e. a fixed, hand-specified coordinate mask on \\(\\theta\\) that selects exactly the bias coordinates","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The tuned biases are the model's own bias parameters, so the fine-tuned model has exactly the original architecture and cost.","identity_at_init":"Biases start at their pretrained values (\\(\\Delta b = 0\\)), so the function is unchanged at initialization.","key_idea":"Freezing all weights and fine-tuning only the additive bias terms (plus the classifier) of a pretrained Transformer is competitive with full fine-tuning on small and medium datasets; tuning only the query and middle-MLP biases (0.04% of parameters) loses little.","results_note":"On GLUE with BERT-base/large, bias-only tuning is comparable to full fine-tuning and to Diff Pruning/Adapters while training under 0.1% of parameters; it is strongest in small-to-medium data regimes.","relations":[{"target":"beft","type":"generalizes","note":"BEFT restricts bias tuning further, to the value-projection bias only."},{"target":"ln-tuning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both tune only per-channel affine parameters already present in the network (shift vs. LayerNorm scale/shift)."},{"target":"ia3","type":"dual-to","note":"(IA)^3 learns new multiplicative per-channel scalings, BitFit tunes the existing additive per-channel shifts."},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are sparse difference vectors; BitFit fixes the support a priori, Diff Pruning learns it."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10199","https://arxiv.org/html/2106.10199","https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.1/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Elad Ben-Zaken","Shauli Ravfogel","Yoav Goldberg"],"bibkey":"benzaken2021bitfit","bibtex":"@inproceedings{benzaken2021bitfit,\n  title = {{BitFit: Simple Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning for Transformer-based Masked Language-models}},\n  author = {Elad Ben-Zaken and Shauli Ravfogel and Yoav Goldberg},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2106.10199},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-short.1/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"covariance":"As in the table: the bias set is a full vector space, \\(b\\mapsto gb\\) under \\(W\\mapsto gWh^{-1}\\).","merge":"As in the table: it edits the model's own bias parameters, so the merged model is the original architecture."},"categorical_reading":"The canonical selective method: a fixed coordinate mask on the bias block, \\(\\rho(\\Delta b)=(W_0,b_0+\\Delta b)\\). It is Lin, regular and idempotent, and transforms with every change of basis because the bias set is a full vector space.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"ssf","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Delta b)=\\big(\\gamma=\\mathbf 1,\\ \\beta=\\Delta b\\big)","note":"With SSF inserted after every biased linear op and LayerNorm: freezing the scales at 1 leaves shifts that are exactly bias edits; SSF pointed at its exact identity (1,0) (official init is ≈)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"ia3","test":"O1","reason":"(IA)^3 only rescales outputs (biases included), so an x-independent shift of a bias lies outside its image."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"diff-pruning","name":"Diff Pruning","full_name":"Diff Pruning (sparse task-specific difference vectors)","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning with Diff Pruning","authors":"Guo et al.","year":2020,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2012.07463","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.378/","code_url":"https://github.com/dguo98/DiffPruning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta_\\tau = \\theta_0 + \\delta_\\tau,\\quad \\delta_\\tau = z_\\tau\\odot w_\\tau,\\quad \\min_{\\alpha_\\tau,w_\\tau}\\ \\mathbb{E}_{z_\\tau\\sim p(z;\\alpha_\\tau)}\\big[\\mathcal{L}(\\theta_0+\\delta_\\tau)\\big] + \\lambda\\sum_i \\sigma\\!\\big(\\alpha_{\\tau,i}-\\log\\tfrac{-l}{r}\\big);\\ \\text{then keep the top } t\\% \\text{ of } |\\delta_\\tau| \\text{ and re-train with the mask fixed; } \\theta_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"2\\,d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{during training } (w,\\alpha);\\ \\ \\|\\Delta W\\|_0\\approx 0.005\\,d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{stored (0.5\\% target)}","locus":"all pretrained parameters (embeddings, weight matrices, biases); structured variant groups gates per weight matrix / bias vector","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"learned sparse coordinate mask: \\(\\Delta W = Z\\odot V\\) with \\(Z\\) a stretched Hard-Concrete relaxation of a binary gate (differentiable \\(L_0\\) penalty), projected onto an \\(L_0\\) ball by magnitude pruning of the diff; optional group gates \\(z^{g}\\) shared within each weight matrix/bias vector","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The final diff vector is added to the pretrained parameters, giving an ordinary model of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"\\(w_\\tau\\) initialized to 0 so \\(\\delta_\\tau = 0\\); gate logits \\(\\alpha_\\tau\\) initialized to 5 so that \\(z \\approx 1\\) at the start.","key_idea":"Treat fine-tuning as learning a task-specific diff vector added to frozen pretrained weights and make it sparse with a relaxed \\(L_0\\) penalty, so each new task costs only a small sparse vector.","results_note":"Structured Diff Pruning matches full fine-tuning of BERT-large on GLUE while modifying only 0.5% of parameters per task.","relations":[{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"FISH Mask precomputes the support from Fisher information instead of learning it, avoiding Diff Pruning's dense training memory."},{"target":"lt-sft","type":"competes-with","note":"LT-SFT obtains the support from the largest changes of a dense fine-tuning pass instead of a relaxed L0 gate."},{"target":"masking","type":"dual-to","note":"Masking learns a binary mask on the weights themselves (W_0 \\odot M); Diff Pruning learns a masked additive update W_0 + Z \\odot V."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.07463","https://arxiv.org/html/2012.07463","https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.378/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Demi Guo","Alexander M. Rush","Yoon Kim"],"bibkey":"guo2020parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{guo2020parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning with Diff Pruning}},\n  author = {Demi Guo and Alexander M. Rush and Yoon Kim},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2012.07463},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.378/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"The stored object is \\(\\theta_0+\\bigcup_{|S|=k}\\mathbb R^S\\), a union of coordinate \\(k\\)-planes (a cone in the axes.json sense); the Hard-Concrete map \\(\\theta_0+z(\\alpha)\\odot w\\) is its training-time relaxation.","base_point":"Apex as in VIII.B: \\(\\theta_0\\) is the one point shared by all strata, where the union is not a manifold. The relaxed map at \\((z,w)=(\\mathbf 1,0)\\) has full \\(S_1\\), so 'apex' describes the \\(L_0\\)-projected image, not the relaxation.","gauge":"Each stratum \\(w_S\\mapsto\\theta_0+\\iota_Sw_S\\) is injective; the gates are clamped at \\(z=1\\) near the init \\(\\alpha_0=5\\), so the Hadamard torus \\((cz,w/c)\\) is inactive there.","covariance":"Support structure in the standard basis; magnitude/\\(L_0\\) selection commutes with signed permutations and homotheties, not with rotations.","rebasing":"The union of \\(k\\)-planes is closed under scaling and supports can be re-learned each cycle: \\(R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}=\\Theta\\) (Thm V.3(a))."},"categorical_reading":"Diff pruning is the learned-support object: its image is the union of all fixed-mask images of size \\(k\\), so every fixed-mask method (FISH, LT-SFT, PaFi, SIFT, ...) is one stratum of it via an A2 arrow. Its vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) is the only point common to all strata, which makes it a cone with apex rather than a linear method.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A \\(k\\)-sparse update has rank up to \\(\\min(k,m,n)\\), so for \\(k>r\\) its maximum rank exceeds LoRA\\(_r\\)'s (conversely a generic rank-1 update is dense, O1)."},{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"The union of all \\(k\\)-planes is not contained in the single \\(k\\)-plane of one fixed mask."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"fish-mask","name":"FISH Mask","full_name":"Fisher-Induced Sparse uncHanging Mask","paper_title":"Training Neural Networks with Fixed Sparse Masks","authors":"Sung et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NeurIPS 2021","arxiv":"2111.09839","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09839","code_url":"https://github.com/varunnair18/FISH","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\hat F_i = \\tfrac1N\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\mathbb{E}_{y\\sim p_\\theta(y|x_n)}\\big(\\partial_{\\theta_i}\\log p_\\theta(y|x_n)\\big)^2,\\quad M = \\mathbb{1}\\big[\\hat F \\ge \\mathrm{sort}(\\hat F)_k\\big],\\quad W = W_0 + M\\odot\\Delta W,\\ \\ \\Delta W_{t+1} = \\Delta W_t - \\eta\\,(M\\odot\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L})","trainable_params":"k\\ \\text{in total (one global top-}k\\text{ over all parameters, e.g. } k = 0.005\\,|\\theta|\\text{)};\\ \\text{per-matrix counts vary because the mask concentrates in some layers}","locus":"any parameter; top-\\(k\\) diagonal-Fisher coordinates selected globally across the network (classifier always included)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"fixed sparse coordinate mask chosen once before training as the top-\\(k\\) entries of the diagonal (empirical) Fisher information, then reused for all iterations","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing parameters change, so the result is a standard dense model of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Training starts at the pretrained weights (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); the Fisher is computed through the randomly initialized classifier before any training.","key_idea":"Estimate each parameter's importance by its diagonal Fisher information on a few samples, pick the top-\\(k\\), and train only those with a mask that never changes.","results_note":"On GLUE with BERT, updating 0.5% of parameters with a FISH Mask matches standard training and outperforms Diff Pruning, Adapters and random/bias masks; also reduces communication in distributed training.","relations":[{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"competes-with","note":"Precomputed Fisher mask vs. learned L0-relaxed mask; FISH avoids Diff Pruning's extra training memory."},{"target":"child-tuning","type":"equivalent-to","note":"Child-Tuning_D (the task-driven variant) uses the same rule: a fixed mask of the top empirical-Fisher entries; the two papers are concurrent (2021)."},{"target":"pafi","type":"competes-with","note":"PaFi replaces the Fisher criterion by a data-free, task-agnostic smallest-magnitude rule."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09839","https://arxiv.org/html/2111.09839"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Yi-Lin Sung","Varun Nair","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"sung2021training","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sung2021training,\n  title = {{Training Neural Networks with Fixed Sparse Masks}},\n  author = {Yi-Lin Sung and Varun Nair and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2111.09839},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.09839}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(W_0+M\\odot\\Delta W\\) with \\(M\\) the global top-\\(k\\) diagonal-Fisher mask fixed before training: a coordinate subspace of dimension \\(k\\).","covariance":"Mask in the standard frame; Fisher top-\\(k\\) selection commutes with signed permutations, not with rotations or non-uniform rescalings.","rebasing":"Fixed mask: \\(R_K=R_1\\) (Thm V.3 cor. 2); recomputing the Fisher mask at the merged weight would gain."},"categorical_reading":"FISH is a mask in the standard frame chosen by a data statistic: linear, regular and gauge-free, the frozen coordinate-block cell of the cotangent table in Exposé IV. By Prop IV.2 its masked-gradient training produces the same trajectory as training the forward linear method \\(W_0+M\\odot\\Delta W\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"Freeze Diff pruning's gates at their clamped init value \\(z=\\mathbf 1\\) and \\(w\\) at 0 off \\(M\\): FISH\\((M)\\) is the stratum \\(M\\) of the learned-support cone (within the L0 budget when \\(|M|\\le k\\))."},{"target":"rosa","rule":"A5","h":"x\\mapsto\\big((0,A_0),\\ x\\big)","note":"With RoSA's sparse support equal to \\(M\\), FISH is the sparse summand of LoRA \\(\\oplus\\) sparse, the LoRA part held at its base value."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A \\(k\\)-entry mask update can have rank up to \\(\\min(k,m,n)>r\\)."},{"target":"pafi","test":"O1","reason":"Fisher-top supports and PaFi's data-free bottom-\\(|W_0|\\) supports are generically different planes, so neither image contains the other."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"child-tuning","name":"Child-Tuning","full_name":"Child-Tuning (task-free Child-Tuning_F / task-driven Child-Tuning_D)","paper_title":"Raise a Child in Large Language Model: Towards Effective and Generalizable Fine-tuning","authors":"Xu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"EMNLP 2021","arxiv":"2109.05687","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.emnlp-main.749/","code_url":"https://github.com/alibaba/AliceMind/tree/main/ChildTuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"w_{t+1} = w_t - \\eta\\,\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}(w_t)}{\\partial w_t}\\odot M_t;\\quad \\text{Child-Tuning}_F:\\ M_t\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_F)\\ \\text{(kept gradients scaled by } 1/p_F);\\quad \\text{Child-Tuning}_D:\\ M = \\mathbb{1}\\big[F^{(i)} \\in \\text{top-}p_D\\big],\\ F^{(i)} = \\tfrac{1}{|D|}\\sum_j\\big(\\partial_{w^{(i)}}\\log p(y_j|x_j;w)\\big)^2,\\ \\text{fixed for all } t","trainable_params":"\\text{Child-Tuning}_D:\\ p_D\\,|\\theta|\\ \\text{in total (one global top-}p_D\\text{ Fisher threshold; per-matrix counts vary)};\\ \\ \\text{Child-Tuning}_F:\\ \\text{expected } p_F\\,|\\theta|\\ \\text{per step, every entry can change over training}","locus":"all pretrained parameters (masks applied to gradients inside the optimizer, e.g. AdamW)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"Hadamard gradient mask: a fixed top-\\(p_D\\) diagonal-Fisher mask (task-driven, genuinely selective) or a fresh Bernoulli\\((p_F)\\) mask every step (task-free); the task-free variant accumulates a dense update and acts as a gradient-noise regularizer rather than a parameter-efficient method","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"It only changes which existing parameters are updated; the fine-tuned network has the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Starts from the pretrained weights; no new parameters.","key_idea":"Update only a 'child network' of the pretrained model: a random Bernoulli subset each step acts as a variance-increasing regularizer favouring flat minima, while a Fisher-selected fixed subset targets task-relevant parameters.","results_note":"On GLUE, Child-Tuning outperforms vanilla fine-tuning by 1.5-8.6 average points across four PLMs (BERT, XLNet, RoBERTa, ELECTRA) and improves out-of-domain generalization.","relations":[{"target":"fish-mask","type":"equivalent-to","note":"Child-Tuning_D's fixed top empirical-Fisher mask is the FISH Mask rule; concurrent 2021 works."},{"target":"lt-sft","type":"competes-with","note":"LT-SFT selects by largest change after dense fine-tuning rather than by Fisher information."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.05687","https://arxiv.org/html/2109.05687","https://aclanthology.org/2021.emnlp-main.749/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Runxin Xu","Fuli Luo","Zhiyuan Zhang","Chuanqi Tan","Baobao Chang","Songfang Huang","Fei Huang"],"bibkey":"xu2021raise","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xu2021raise,\n  title = {{Raise a Child in Large Language Model: Towards Effective and Generalizable Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Runxin Xu and Fuli Luo and Zhiyuan Zhang and Chuanqi Tan and Baobao Chang and Songfang Huang and Fei Huang},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2109.05687},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.emnlp-main.749/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Classified by Child-Tuning\\(_D\\): a fixed top-\\(p_D\\) Fisher gradient mask, which by Prop IV.2 (frozen coordinate compression) is the forward linear method \\(W_0+M\\odot\\Delta W\\). Child-Tuning\\(_F\\) (a fresh Bernoulli\\((p_F)\\) mask each step, rescaled by \\(1/p_F\\)) is instead a B-kind schedule: per-step Lin, reach \\(\\Theta\\), merge n/a, rebasing schedule.","shape":"Fixed mask for the D variant; if decoupled weight decay is applied to all parameters, coordinates outside \\(M\\) also shrink and the identity with the masked forward method fails (Prop IV.2 excludes AdamW with \\(\\lambda>0\\)).","covariance":"Empirical-Fisher top-\\(p_D\\) selection in the standard basis: signed-permutation equivariant; the F variant's i.i.d. mask is permutation-invariant in law.","rebasing":"Idempotent for the fixed mask; Child-Tuning\\(_F\\) is itself a rebasing scheme reaching \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Child-Tuning\\(_D\\) is FISH under another name: the same coordinate subspace selected by an empirical Fisher, isomorphic to FISH whenever the supports agree. Child-Tuning\\(_F\\) leaves the slice: resampling the mask every step makes it a rebasing scheme whose reach is all of \\(\\Theta\\), a regulariser rather than a parameter-efficient object.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^M}","note":"Isomorphism for a common support \\(M\\); Child-Tuning\\(_D\\) uses the empirical Fisher with gold labels and FISH samples \\(y\\sim p_\\theta\\), so the selected masks can differ."},{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The fixed mask is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"Concerns Child-Tuning\\(_F\\) only (the classified D variant has the conditional isomorphism above): fresh Bernoulli masks every step accumulate a dense update, so its reach is not inside any fixed \\(k\\)-plane."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lt-sft","name":"LT-SFT","full_name":"Lottery Ticket Sparse Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Composable Sparse Fine-Tuning for Cross-Lingual Transfer","authors":"Ansell et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2022","arxiv":"2110.07560","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.125/","code_url":"https://github.com/cambridgeltl/composable-sft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{Phase 1: } \\theta^{(1)} = \\mathrm{FT}(\\theta^{(0)}),\\ \\mu = \\mathbb{1}\\big[\\,|\\theta^{(1)}-\\theta^{(0)}| \\in \\text{top-}K\\big];\\ \\ \\text{Phase 2: reset to } \\theta^{(0)},\\ \\text{train with } \\mu\\odot\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal{L},\\ \\phi = \\theta^{(2)}-\\theta^{(0)};\\ \\ \\text{composition: } F(\\cdot;\\theta^{(0)}+\\phi_T+\\phi_L)","trainable_params":"K\\ \\text{in total (one global top-}K\\text{; e.g. } K \\approx 7.6\\text{M} = 4.3\\%\\ \\text{of mBERT for language SFTs, 0.25--8\\% for task SFTs);\\ per-matrix counts vary}","locus":"all Transformer parameters, including the input embeddings; for language SFTs the output embedding matrix is decoupled and frozen and LayerNorm parameters are frozen; the task classifier head is trained densely in both phases","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"sparse difference vector \\(\\phi\\) supported on a lottery-ticket mask (top-\\(K\\) largest absolute change after a dense fine-tuning pass); SFTs act on the parameter space by translation, so they compose additively: \\(S_L \\circ S_T\\) = translation by \\(\\phi_T + \\phi_L\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each SFT is a sparse vector added to the pretrained weights; any sum of SFTs is again a standard dense model.","identity_at_init":"Phase 2 restarts from the pretrained values (\\(\\phi = 0\\)); optional L1 penalty \\(\\tfrac{\\lambda}{N}\\sum_i|\\theta_i-\\theta_i^{(0)}|\\) keeps the deltas small.","key_idea":"Borrow the lottery-ticket recipe (train, select, rewind, retrain) to find a sparse set of parameters per language and per task; because the resulting deltas are sparse they can be added together with little interference, giving adapter-like modularity with full expressivity.","results_note":"In zero-shot cross-lingual transfer (POS, dependency parsing, NER, NLI) with mBERT/XLM-R, LT-SFT outperforms MAD-X adapters, e.g. +2.5 POS accuracy and +3.7 LAS.","relations":[{"target":"mad-x","type":"competes-with","note":"Same language-adapter/task-adapter composition scheme, but realised by adding sparse deltas instead of stacking adapters."},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"builds-on","note":"Same sparse-difference-vector formulation; the support is chosen by lottery-ticket selection."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07560","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.07560","https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.125/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Alan Ansell","Edoardo Maria Ponti","Anna Korhonen","Ivan Vulić"],"bibkey":"ansell2021composable","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ansell2021composable,\n  title = {{Composable Sparse Fine-Tuning for Cross-Lingual Transfer}},\n  author = {Alan Ansell and Edoardo Maria Ponti and Anna Korhonen and Ivan Vulić},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2110.07560},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.acl-long.125/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Phase 2 trains on the fixed support \\(\\mu=\\text{top-}K\\,|\\theta^{(1)}-\\theta^{(0)}|\\); the dense phase 1 only computes the mask.","covariance":"Magnitudes of a dense fine-tuning diff: signed-permutation equivariant, not rotation equivariant.","merge":"Each SFT is added into \\(\\theta_0\\); the composition \\(S_L\\circ S_T\\) is translation by \\(\\phi_T+\\phi_L\\), since additive methods commute with translations (Prop V.2(a))."},"categorical_reading":"LT-SFT is a fixed-mask linear method whose mask is read off a dense fine-tuning run (a lottery-ticket support). Because translations commute, language and task SFTs compose by \\(\\oplus\\), the sum of two coordinate subspaces, with interference confined to the overlap of their supports.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The lottery-ticket support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Sparse updates on a 0.25-8\\% support have rank far above \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"spt","name":"SPT","full_name":"Sensitivity-aware visual Parameter-efficient fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Sensitivity-Aware Visual Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"He et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICCV 2023","arxiv":"2303.08566","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08566","code_url":"https://github.com/ziplab/SPT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"s_n = \\textstyle\\sum_{c=1}^{C} g_{n,c}^2\\ \\ (g_{n,c} = \\partial E/\\partial w_n \\text{ on sample } c;\\ \\text{one-step Taylor estimate of the loss reduction}),\\ \\ \\mathcal{T} = \\text{top-}\\tau(s),\\ M_{ij}=\\mathbb{1}[W_{ij}\\in\\mathcal{T}];\\quad W = \\begin{cases} W_0 + BA, & \\|M\\|_0 \\ge \\sigma_{opt}\\ \\text{(structured tuning)}\\\\ W_0 + M\\odot\\Delta W, & \\text{otherwise (unstructured tuning)}\\end{cases}","trainable_params":"\\|M\\|_0\\ \\text{(unstructured) or } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{(LoRA on sensitive matrices)},\\ \\text{under a global budget } \\tau","locus":"ViT weight matrices; positions chosen per task by gradient-squared sensitivity","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"per-matrix choice between a sparse coordinate mask (top-\\(\\tau\\) gradient-squared sensitivity) and a low-rank (LoRA) or adapter update on matrices that contain many sensitive entries","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"SPT-LoRA and the unstructured part merge into \\(W_0\\); the SPT-Adapter variant keeps inserted adapter modules.","identity_at_init":"Unstructured part starts at \\(\\Delta W = 0\\); structured part uses the zero-initialized LoRA/adapter of the host method. Sensitivity is accumulated in one pass over \\(C = 800\\) training samples before training (400 are shown to suffice).","key_idea":"Measure, per task and before training, how much tuning each pretrained weight would reduce the loss (approximated by its squared gradient), then spend the parameter budget there, upgrading heavily sensitive matrices to low-rank or adapter tuning.","results_note":"With ViT-B/16, SPT-Adapter/SPT-LoRA reach state of the art on FGVC and VTAB-1k (e.g. +4.2% and +1.4% mean top-1 over Adapter) and transfer to MAE/MoCo v3 backbones and segmentation.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Structured branch is LoRA (or an adapter) applied only to the sensitive matrices."},{"target":"gps","type":"competes-with","note":"GPS also selects by gradient magnitude on ViT but enforces K entries per neuron and stays purely sparse."},{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"SPT's unstructured criterion (sum of squared gradients) is the empirical-Fisher diagonal that FISH Mask ranks by, derived independently via a Taylor argument; SPT does not cite FISH."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08566","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.08566"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Haoyu He","Jianfei Cai","Jing Zhang","Dacheng Tao","Bohan Zhuang"],"bibkey":"he2023sensitivity","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2023sensitivity,\n  title = {{Sensitivity-Aware Visual Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Haoyu He and Jianfei Cai and Jing Zhang and Dacheng Tao and Bohan Zhuang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2303.08566},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08566}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Classified by SPT-LoRA; SPT-Adapter puts bottleneck adapters on the sensitive matrices (E, Fun, neutral, M\\(\\infty\\)).","shape":"Per weight box either a fixed squared-gradient mask (Lin) or LoRA (determinantal cone): the product is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"Apex from the LoRA boxes (deficit \\(r(n-r)\\) each at \\(B=0\\)); the masked boxes are regular.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on each LoRA box; none on masked boxes.","covariance":"Sensitivity scores, the per-box switch and the masks are coordinatewise, so the whole method is only Mon\\(\\times\\)Mon-covariant, although each LoRA box alone is \\(GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"LoRA boxes reach full matrices, masked boxes are idempotent: \\(R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\) of the product cone."},"categorical_reading":"SPT is a direct sum over weight boxes of FISH-type masks and LoRA cones, the choice per box being made by counting squared-gradient-sensitive entries. It is an \\(\\oplus\\)-word in \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\), so its expressivity is the product of its summands' images.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"rosa","rule":"A2","h":"\\big((B_s,A_s)_{s\\in S},\\,(x_u)_{u\\in U}\\big)\\mapsto\\big((B_s,A_s)_{s\\in S},\\,(0,A_{0,u})_{u\\in U};\\ \\iota(x)\\big)","note":"RoSA on all of SPT's boxes with SPT's masks as its sparse support: freeze RoSA's LoRA at its base value on the boxes where SPT uses masks."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"On masked boxes the sparse update can have rank above \\(r\\)."},{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"The LoRA boxes give dense low-rank updates outside any fixed coordinate plane."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"pafi","name":"PaFi","full_name":"PaFi (task-agnostic sparse fine-tuning of smallest-magnitude weights)","paper_title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning without Introducing New Latency","authors":"Liao et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ACL 2023","arxiv":"2305.16742","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.233/","code_url":"https://github.com/BaohaoLiao/pafi_hiwi","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"M = \\mathbb{1}\\big[\\,|W_0| \\le \\mathrm{sort}(|W_0|)_k\\big]\\ \\text{(per matrix, data-free, shared by all tasks)},\\quad W = W_0 + M\\odot\\Delta W;\\ \\text{norm-layer parameters fully trained, embeddings frozen}","trainable_params":"k\\ \\text{per matrix};\\ \\text{default } k = 0.005\\,d_{out}d_{in}","locus":"every weight matrix and bias vector (group-wise bottom-\\(k\\)), plus all normalization parameters","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"fixed sparse coordinate mask on the \\(k\\) smallest-magnitude pretrained entries of each matrix; one universal mask for every downstream task","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing entries are changed in place, so inference uses the original dense model with no latency overhead.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\) at the start; the mask needs no data or training.","key_idea":"The least important pretrained weights (smallest magnitude) are the safest to repurpose, so one data-free mask can be reused for every task, removing the task-specific mask-generation stage of sparse fine-tuning.","results_note":"On GLUE with RoBERTa-large, PaFi reaches 89.3 average (equal to full fine-tuning) with 0.5% trainable parameters, at least 1.3 points above Diff Pruning and FISH Mask.","relations":[{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"Replaces task-specific Fisher selection with a task-agnostic magnitude rule."},{"target":"super-tuning","type":"special-case-of","note":"Super-Tuning's magnitude-only BottomK support is exactly a PaFi-style mask; its Wanda variant reweights |W| by activation norms."},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"competes-with","note":"Both store sparse deltas; PaFi needs only one mask for all tasks."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16742","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.16742","https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.233/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Baohao Liao","Yan Meng","Christof Monz"],"bibkey":"liao2023parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liao2023parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning without Introducing New Latency}},\n  author = {Baohao Liao and Yan Meng and Christof Monz},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2305.16742},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.233/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Fixed bottom-\\(k\\) \\(|W_0|\\) mask per matrix (data-free, shared by all tasks), plus every normalisation parameter (an LN-tuning summand).","covariance":"The mask is a \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent function of \\(|W_0|\\): covariant under signed permutations and homotheties only, a subgroup of Mon\\(\\times\\)Mon.","merge":"Only existing entries and norm parameters change in place; the LN summand needs no fold."},"categorical_reading":"PaFi is a fixed coordinate subspace chosen from the base point alone, plus an LN-tuning summand: a \\(\\theta_0\\)-dependent mask with the opposite magnitude rule to LIFT. One mask shared by all tasks puts every PaFi adapter in the same linear space, so task-arithmetic combinations of them stay inside PaFi.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The bottom-\\(k\\) support (and LN coordinates) is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lift","test":"O1","reason":"Bottom-\\(|W_0|\\) and top-\\(|\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0)|\\) supports are generically different (and nearly disjoint, though not exactly), and LIFT never trains PaFi's normalisation parameters, so PaFi's plane is not inside LIFT's reach."},{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"A data-free magnitude support and a Fisher support are generically different planes."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"smt","name":"SMT","full_name":"Sparse Matrix Tuning","paper_title":"SMT: Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with Sparse Matrices","authors":"He et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2405.15525","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=GbgCRJedQ7","code_url":"https://github.com/HectorHHZ/Sparse_Matrix_Tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\Delta W_M,\\quad \\mathrm{supp}(\\Delta W_M) = \\bigcup_{b\\in\\mathcal{B}} \\text{block}_b,\\ \\ \\mathcal{B} = \\text{top-}m\\ \\text{of } \\operatorname{mean}_{(i,j)\\in b}\\Big|\\sum_{t\\le T_w}\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}_t\\Big|_{ij}\\ \\text{over } l\\times l \\text{ blocks } (l=256)","trainable_params":"m_W\\, l^2\\ \\text{per selected matrix } (m_W \\text{ selected } l\\times l \\text{ blocks, } l=256;\\ \\text{blocks ranked jointly over all Q/K/V matrices});\\ 0.84\\%\\text{--}4.91\\%\\ \\text{of parameters in the experiments}","locus":"attention \\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\) (mainly \\(W_v\\)); MLP layers frozen","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"block-sparse mask: \\(\\Delta W\\) is nonzero only on a set of selected \\(l\\times l\\) sub-matrices (tiles), gathered into a dense \\(m\\times l\\times l\\) tensor so compute stays dense","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updated sub-matrices are slices of the original weights; the fine-tuned model is a standard dense model.","identity_at_init":"Starts from the pretrained weights; blocks are chosen after a short gradient warm-up (about 25-100 iterations).","key_idea":"Select whole sub-matrices with the largest accumulated gradients during a short warm-up and update only those blocks, which keeps the sparsity hardware-friendly and avoids the rank plateau of low-rank methods.","results_note":"On commonsense and arithmetic reasoning with LLaMA-7B/13B, LLaMA2-7B and LLaMA3-8B, SMT outperforms DoRA (e.g. +3.0/+2.8 on commonsense for LLaMA-7B/13B) and does not plateau as the trainable budget grows.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Block-sparse instead of low-rank update at matched budget; avoids LoRA's performance plateau at large rank."},{"target":"dora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main baseline; SMT reports consistent gains over DoRA."},{"target":"sift","type":"competes-with","note":"Both select by gradient magnitude; SMT selects contiguous blocks, SIFT individual entries."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.15525","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.15525","https://openreview.net/forum?id=GbgCRJedQ7","https://github.com/HectorHHZ/Sparse_Matrix_Tuning"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Haoze He","Juncheng Billy Li","Xuan Jiang","Heather Miller"],"bibkey":"he2024smt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2024smt,\n  title = {{SMT: Fine-Tuning Large Language Models with Sparse Matrices}},\n  author = {Haoze He and Juncheng Billy Li and Xuan Jiang and Heather Miller},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2405.15525},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=GbgCRJedQ7}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Union of \\(l\\times l\\) tiles (\\(l=256\\)) selected after a short gradient warm-up and then fixed: a coordinate subspace of dimension \\(m_Wl^2\\).","covariance":"Contiguous \\(256\\times256\\) tiles impose a block partition the network does not have; a neuron permutation scatters a tile (\\(\\Pi_{\\rm blocks}\\), as for MoRA and GraLoRA).","rebasing":"Fixed tiles: cycles gain nothing."},"categorical_reading":"SMT is a block mask: the coordinate subspace of a few full tiles, GraLoRA's block partition without the low-rank factor. Each selected tile is full rank, which is why it does not plateau in rank as LoRA does.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The tile support is one fixed coordinate set, a stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A selected tile has rank up to \\(l=256>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"spiel","name":"SpIEL","full_name":"Sparse Iterative Efficient Learning (sparse fine-tuning with drop-and-grow)","paper_title":"Scaling Sparse Fine-Tuning to Large Language Models","authors":"Ansell et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2401.16405","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16405","code_url":"https://github.com/AlanAnsell/peft","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta' = \\theta + \\mathrm{scatter}(\\eta,\\phi,d_\\theta),\\ \\ \\Delta W = \\mathrm{reshape}(\\mathrm{scatter}(\\eta,\\phi));\\ \\text{every } S \\text{ steps: drop } \\arg\\mathrm{top}k(-|\\phi|),\\ \\text{grow } \\arg\\mathrm{top}k\\,\\big|\\hat{\\mathbb{E}}_{x}[\\nabla_\\theta\\mathcal{L}(x;\\theta')]\\big|\\ \\text{(SpIEL-AG) or by SM3 momenta (SpIEL-MA)}","trainable_params":"d_\\phi = \\rho\\,d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{per matrix (same density for every tensor) plus } d_\\phi \\text{ indices}","locus":"all linear-layer weight tensors of an LLM (embeddings excluded)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"dynamic sparse coordinate set: an index vector \\(\\eta\\) and value vector \\(\\phi\\), updated by RigL-style drop (smallest \\(|\\phi|\\)) and grow (largest accumulated gradient or approximate momentum); memory \\(O(d_\\phi)\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The final delta is scatter-added into the pretrained weights, producing a standard dense model.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\eta\\) a random index subset (all of it is replaced at the first update).","key_idea":"Make sparse fine-tuning memory-efficient for LLMs by storing only indices and deltas and letting the support evolve during training through drop-and-grow, instead of computing a dense mask up front.","results_note":"Instruction-tuning LLaMA 2 7B/13B on Flan v2, GPT4-Alpaca and Tulu v2, SpIEL is often better than LoRA and (IA)\\(^3\\) at comparable run time and is compatible with 4-bit quantization and SM3.","relations":[{"target":"lt-sft","type":"builds-on","note":"Same group and sparse-delta formulation; removes the need for a dense first fine-tuning phase."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Main LLM-scale baseline."},{"target":"shira","type":"competes-with","note":"SHiRA is concurrent; it uses a static mask whereas SpIEL's mask is dynamic."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16405","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.16405","https://github.com/ducdauge/sft-llm"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Alan Ansell","Ivan Vulić","Hannah Sterz","Anna Korhonen","Edoardo M. Ponti"],"bibkey":"ansell2024scaling","bibtex":"@article{ansell2024scaling,\n  title = {{Scaling Sparse Fine-Tuning to Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Alan Ansell and Ivan Vulić and Hannah Sterz and Anna Korhonen and Edoardo M. Ponti},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2401.16405},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2401.16405},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.16405}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"\\(\\theta'=\\theta+\\mathrm{scatter}(\\eta,\\phi)\\) with a discrete index set \\(\\eta\\) and real deltas \\(\\phi\\): a reparametrisation whose \\(Q\\) is a disjoint union of coordinate planes.","shape":"Dropped deltas leave the support (the weight reverts to \\(\\theta_0\\) there), so the stored diff is \\(d_\\phi\\)-sparse per tensor: a union of coordinate planes as for Diff pruning, with a per-tensor instead of a global budget.","base_point":"\\(\\phi=0\\) at \\(\\theta_0\\), the vertex shared by all strata (apex in the sense used for Diff pruning).","rebasing":"Drop-and-grow moves between strata without merging, so it is not a schedule; merge-and-restart with fresh supports reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"SpIEL is a learned-support object like Diff pruning, but it searches the strata of the union of \\(k\\)-planes by drop-and-grow instead of a relaxed L0 gate. It is not a rebasing scheme, because dropped deltas are discarded rather than merged.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"(\\eta,\\phi)\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ \\mathrm{scatter}(\\eta,\\phi)\\big)","note":"Componentwise on each support \\(\\eta\\): every per-tensor stratum is a stratum of the global \\(k\\)-sparse union with \\(k=\\sum d_\\phi\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lt-sft","test":"O1","reason":"SpIEL's union of strata is not contained in LT-SFT's single fixed plane."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A \\(d_\\phi\\)-sparse update can have rank above \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"gps","name":"GPS","full_name":"Gradient-based Parameter Selection","paper_title":"Gradient-based Parameter Selection for Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"CVPR 2024","arxiv":"2312.10136","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10136","code_url":"https://github.com/FightingFighting/GPS","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"M_{ij} = \\mathbb{1}\\Big[j\\in\\text{top-}K_{j'}\\ \\big|\\partial\\mathcal{L}_{SCL}/\\partial W_{0,ij'}\\big|\\Big]\\ \\text{for every output neuron (row) } i,\\qquad W = W_0 + M\\odot\\Delta W,\\ \\ \\Delta W_{t+1} = \\Delta W_t - \\epsilon\\,\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}\\odot M","trainable_params":"K\\,d_{out}\\ \\text{per matrix (K input connections per neuron; } \\approx 0.36\\%\\ \\text{on average)}","locus":"every weight matrix of the backbone (ViT, Swin, ConvNeXt), all layers; gradients computed with a supervised contrastive loss","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"row-balanced sparse mask: exactly \\(K\\) nonzeros in every row of \\(\\Delta W\\), so each neuron's activation can be adjusted through its \\(K\\) most gradient-relevant input connections","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only selected entries of existing weights change; inference uses the unchanged architecture.","identity_at_init":"Starts from the pretrained weights (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); selection uses a supervised contrastive loss so that no randomly initialized head biases the gradients.","key_idea":"Pick, for every neuron, the few incoming weights with the largest task gradient, which spreads the budget over all layers instead of concentrating it at the top, and train only those.","results_note":"On FGVC and VTAB-1k with ViT-B/16, GPS improves over full fine-tuning by 3.33% and 9.61% mean accuracy while tuning 0.36% of parameters on average.","relations":[{"target":"spt","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are gradient-based selections for vision transformers; GPS stays purely sparse with per-neuron balancing."},{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"FISH selects globally by squared gradients; GPS selects per neuron by gradient magnitude."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10136","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.10136"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Zhi Zhang","Qizhe Zhang","Zijun Gao","Renrui Zhang","Ekaterina Shutova","Shiji Zhou","Shanghang Zhang"],"bibkey":"zhang2023gradient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023gradient,\n  title = {{Gradient-based Parameter Selection for Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zhi Zhang and Qizhe Zhang and Zijun Gao and Renrui Zhang and Ekaterina Shutova and Shiji Zhou and Shanghang Zhang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2312.10136},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10136}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Exactly \\(K\\) entries per row (output neuron), chosen by \\(|\\nabla\\mathcal L_{SCL}|\\) before training and then fixed.","covariance":"A row-regular support is preserved by row and column permutations; selection by gradient magnitude is signed-permutation equivariant, not rotation equivariant."},"categorical_reading":"GPS is a fixed mask with a row-regular support of \\(K\\) incoming connections per neuron. Row regularity constrains which stratum of the \\(k\\)-sparse union is chosen, not the shape of the object.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The row-balanced support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"For a dataset-aggregated (generically high-rank) gradient the row-wise top-\\(K\\) columns vary across rows and the masked update has rank up to \\(\\min(m,n)>r\\). (For a rank-one gradient every row would select the same \\(K\\) columns and the rank would be at most \\(K\\).)"}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"s2ft","name":"S²FT","full_name":"Structured Sparse Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"S²FT: Efficient, Scalable and Generalizable LLM Fine-tuning by Structured Sparsity","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2412.06289","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06289","code_url":"https://github.com/Infini-AI-Lab/S2FT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta W = U_S V^{\\top},\\ \\ U_S = [e_{a_1},\\dots,e_{a_s}]\\ \\text{a frozen one-hot channel selector},\\ V \\text{ trainable};\\ \\text{in practice: the slices of } W_O \\text{ belonging to selected heads } S_{MHA} \\text{ and of } W_{down} \\text{ belonging to selected FFN channels } S_{FFN}, \\text{ co-permuted to be contiguous}","trainable_params":"s\\cdot d\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (s selected channels, i.e. selected heads}\\times d_h\\text{ or FFN channels)}","locus":"output projection \\(W_O\\) (rows of the selected attention heads) and FFN down projection (selected channels) in every block; for LLaMA3-8B and Mistral-7B only the down projection is tuned because of grouped-query attention","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"structured (row/column-block) sparsity: \\(\\Delta W = U_S V^\\top\\) with \\(U_S\\) a fixed coordinate projection, i.e. a LoRA whose one factor is a frozen selection matrix; permutation invariance of coupled head/channel structures makes the trainable slice a dense block","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Updates are in-place on existing weight slices; alternatively the sparse delta can be kept as an unmerged adapter for fast switching and fusion.","identity_at_init":"Selected slices start at their pretrained values (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); selection is random by default (S²FT-R) or by weight, activation, weight×activation or gradient magnitude.","key_idea":"Select sparsely but compute densely: choose a few attention heads and FFN channels, permute coupled weight matrices so they are contiguous, and back-propagate only through that dense slice.","results_note":"On LLaMA and Mistral models S²FT improves over other PEFT methods by 1.2-4.1% on commonsense and 0.6-1.9% on arithmetic reasoning, while saving up to 3x training memory and running 1.5-2.7x faster than full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Formally S²FT is rank-s adaptation \\Delta W = U V^\\top with U fixed to a coordinate selector instead of being learned."},{"target":"smt","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are hardware-friendly structured sparse updates; SMT uses gradient-selected l\\times l tiles, S²FT whole head/channel slices."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Reports better OOD generalization and serving scalability than LoRA."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06289","https://arxiv.org/html/2412.06289","https://github.com/Infini-AI-Lab/S2FT"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Xinyu Yang","Jixuan Leng","Geyang Guo","Jiawei Zhao","Ryumei Nakada","Linjun Zhang","Huaxiu Yao","Beidi Chen"],"bibkey":"yang2024s2ft","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2024s2ft,\n  title = {{S²FT: Efficient, Scalable and Generalizable LLM Fine-tuning by Structured Sparsity}},\n  author = {Xinyu Yang and Jixuan Leng and Geyang Guo and Jiawei Zhao and Ryumei Nakada and Linjun Zhang and Huaxiu Yao and Beidi Chen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2412.06289},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.06289}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=VU_S^\\top\\) with \\(U_S\\) a frozen one-hot selector of \\(s\\) heads/channels: the matrices supported on \\(s\\) whole columns (input side of \\(W_O\\), \\(W_{\\rm down}\\)).","gauge":"\\(V\\mapsto VU_S^\\top\\) is injective.","covariance":"\\(GL\\) on the dense (output) side, monomial on the selected side (hidden-unit permutations and scalings carry the selector): \\(GL\\times\\)Mon in (output)\\(\\times\\)(input) order, the one-sided key MonxGL.","rebasing":"Fixed selector: idempotent. Co-permuting coupled matrices to make the channels contiguous is a function-preserving base change (a hidden-unit permutation, Def V.1(a)): it changes the memory layout, not the object."},"categorical_reading":"S\\(^2\\)FT is LoRA-FA whose frozen factor is a coordinate selector, \\(\\Delta W=VU_S^\\top\\): the structured-sparse and the low-rank readings are one linear object. Its dense-compute trick is a base change by a hidden-unit permutation, a symmetry of \\(f\\), so it costs nothing in expressivity.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"V\\mapsto s^{-1}V\\quad(A_0:=U_S^\\top)","note":"Isomorphism S\\(^2\\)FT \\(\\cong\\) LoRA-FA\\((A_0=U_S^\\top)\\) of rank \\(|S|\\); \\(s\\) is LoRA-FA's scale."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"V\\mapsto(s^{-1}V,\\ U_S^\\top)","note":"LoRA pointed at \\((0,U_S^\\top)\\), the same stratum as the default pointing \\((0,A_0)\\) (Thm II.2)."},{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The \\(s\\) selected columns form one fixed coordinate set."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"Entrywise Fisher supports generically do not contain whole columns, and whole columns are not entrywise-selected."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"sift","name":"SIFT","full_name":"Sparse Increment Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Sparse is Enough in Fine-tuning Pre-trained Large Language Models","authors":"Song et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2312.11875","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11875","code_url":"https://github.com/song-wx/SIFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\mathcal{S} = \\text{top-}x\\%\\ \\text{coordinates of } |g_1|\\ (\\text{gradient of the first batch(es)}),\\quad W = W_0 + \\Delta W,\\ \\ \\mathrm{supp}(\\Delta W)\\subseteq\\mathcal{S},\\ \\ \\Delta W_{t+1} = \\Delta W_t - \\eta\\,P_{\\mathcal{S}}(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}_t)","trainable_params":"x\\%\\cdot d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (stored as values + indices)}","locus":"attention \\(W_q\\), \\(W_k\\), \\(W_v\\), \\(W_o\\) by default","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"coordinate-subspace update: the gradient is projected onto the span of the top-\\(|g|\\) standard basis directions, and the increment is stored as a sparse (value, index) tensor filled via backward hooks","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The sparse increment is added into the existing weights.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\); support fixed after the first batch.","key_idea":"Pre-training shifts the PAC-Bayes prior so that fine-tuning gradients become quasi-sparse (a few components carry most of the norm); hence updating only the top-gradient components is enough, implemented memory-efficiently with gradient hooks.","results_note":"With RoBERTa-large on GLUE (0.8% sparsity on Q/K/V/O) SIFT is competitive with LoRA and adapters, and when instruction-tuning LLaMA on Alpaca it is competitive with full fine-tuning and LoRA on MMLU and HumanEval.","relations":[{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"Both fix a gradient-statistics mask early; SIFT uses raw gradient magnitude from the first batch."},{"target":"smt","type":"competes-with","note":"Entry-level vs. block-level gradient selection."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11875","https://arxiv.org/html/2312.11875"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Weixi Song","Zuchao Li","Lefei Zhang","Hai Zhao","Bo Du"],"bibkey":"song2023sparse","bibtex":"@inproceedings{song2023sparse,\n  title = {{Sparse is Enough in Fine-tuning Pre-trained Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Weixi Song and Zuchao Li and Lefei Zhang and Hai Zhao and Bo Du},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2312.11875},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.11875}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Gradient hooks apply a frozen coordinate projection \\(P_S\\); with \\(S\\) fixed this is the forward linear method by Prop IV.2.","shape":"Support = top-\\(x\\%\\) of \\(|g_1|\\) from the first batch(es), then fixed."},"categorical_reading":"SIFT is a fixed coordinate mask chosen by the magnitudes of the first gradient, implemented as a backward projection with a frozen coordinate compression. By the degree-one collapse it is the same object as training \\(W_0+P_S(\\Delta W)\\) directly.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The first-gradient support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"sam-sparse","test":"O1","reason":"Top \\(|g_1|\\) on one batch and top \\((\\sum_{\\rm burn\\text{-}in}g)^2\\) rank coordinates differently, so the two fixed planes differ."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Sparse updates can have rank above \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"hift","name":"HiFT","full_name":"Hierarchical Full-parameter Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"HiFT: A Hierarchical Full Parameter Fine-Tuning Strategy","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024","arxiv":"2401.15207","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.1015/","code_url":"https://github.com/misonsky/HiFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta = (\\theta_{G_1},\\dots,\\theta_{G_k})\\ \\text{(k groups of m layers)};\\ \\text{at step } t \\text{ update only } \\theta_{G_{\\pi(t \\bmod k)}} \\text{ with any optimizer (others frozen, their optimizer states offloaded)};\\ \\text{learning rate advanced once per full cycle}","trainable_params":"\\approx|\\theta|/k\\ \\text{per step};\\ \\text{all parameters over one cycle}","locus":"groups of consecutive layers (embedding = bottom, head = top), iterated bottom2up, top2down or in a fixed random order","modification_kind":"optimizer-or-training-procedure","structure":"cyclic block-coordinate update over layer groups with delayed learning-rate schedule; accumulated \\(\\Delta W\\) is dense","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"It is full-parameter fine-tuning of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"Starts from the pretrained weights; no new parameters.","key_idea":"Achieve full-parameter fine-tuning with PEFT-like memory by updating one group of layers per step in a fixed cyclic order, keeping only that group's gradients and optimizer states on the GPU.","results_note":"HiFT matches PEFT and standard full fine-tuning across six models, reduces per-step trainable parameters by about 89% on average and fits full fine-tuning of 7B models in 24GB.","relations":[{"target":"lisa","type":"competes-with","note":"Deterministic cyclic vs. randomized layer selection."},{"target":"badam","type":"competes-with","note":"Both are block-coordinate full-parameter methods, but HiFT switches group every step and keeps the inactive groups' optimizer states (offloaded to CPU), whereas BAdam runs K Adam steps per block and discards the states when it switches."},{"target":"mezo","type":"competes-with","note":"Alternative memory-saving route (zeroth-order) that HiFT argues converges worse."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15207","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.15207"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Yongkang Liu","Yiqun Zhang","Qian Li","Tong Liu","Shi Feng","Daling Wang","Yifei Zhang","Hinrich Schütze"],"bibkey":"liu2024hift","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2024hift,\n  title = {{HiFT: A Hierarchical Full Parameter Fine-Tuning Strategy}},\n  author = {Yongkang Liu and Yiqun Zhang and Qian Li and Tong Liu and Shi Feng and Daling Wang and Yifei Zhang and Hinrich Schütze},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2401.15207},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.1015/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is the identity; at step \\(t\\) compression and anchor are the coordinate projection onto layer group \\(G_{\\pi(t\\bmod k)}\\), changed every step in a fixed cycle.","shape":"Per step the linear space of the active group's weights; one cycle visits every group.","covariance":"Each active box is fully trainable, so every stage image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant box by box (as for surgical FT and BitFit); the layer grouping partitions the set of boxes, not the coordinates of any box, and no neuron permutation or residual rotation breaks it. LISA and BAdam carry the same value after consolidation; VIII.B still lists LISA under \\(\\Pi\\) (blocks).","merge":"Writes the full weight directly.","rebasing":"A rebasing scheme with period 1 and a cyclic shape; \\(R=\\Theta\\) after one cycle. Each group's optimizer state is offloaded and resumed, so no state is reused across different frames (unlike GaLore)."},"categorical_reading":"HiFT is block-coordinate descent over layer groups: a backward lens whose anchor is a coordinate projection rotated every step, the resampled-every-step coordinate-block cell of Exposé IV. Since each group keeps its own optimizer state, the schedule is full fine-tuning in a cyclic order and reaches \\(\\Theta\\) after one cycle.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"surgical-fine-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}_{\\Theta_{G_j}}","note":"Per stage: stage \\(j\\) of HiFT is the forward linear method of surgical FT on the block \\(G_j\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"galore","test":"O2","reason":"A HiFT stage makes full-rank updates of every matrix in the active group; a GaLore period has rank \\(\\le r\\)."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"surgical-fine-tuning","name":"Surgical FT","full_name":"Surgical Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"Surgical Fine-Tuning Improves Adaptation to Distribution Shifts","authors":"Lee et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2210.11466","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11466","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta = (\\theta_{B_1},\\dots,\\theta_{B_n});\\ \\text{fine-tune only } \\theta_{B_j} \\text{ (one contiguous block), others frozen};\\ \\text{Auto-RGN: per-tensor learning rate } \\propto \\mathrm{RGN} = \\|g\\|_2/\\|\\theta\\|_2 \\text{ normalized to } [0,1]","trainable_params":"|\\theta_{B_j}|\\ \\text{(one block of layers)}","locus":"one contiguous block of layers: first block for input-level shift, middle blocks for feature-level shift, last layer for output-level shift","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"layer-block coordinate mask: \\(\\Delta W\\) nonzero only on the weights of one contiguous block (soft version: per-tensor learning rates from relative gradient norms)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing layers are fine-tuned; architecture unchanged.","identity_at_init":"Starts from the pretrained (source) model; no new parameters.","key_idea":"Which layers to fine-tune should depend on where the distribution shift lives: tuning only early layers fixes input-level shifts, middle layers feature-level shifts, and the head output-level shifts, often beating full fine-tuning.","results_note":"Across seven real distribution shifts (e.g. CIFAR-C, ImageNet-C, Living-17, Entity-30, Waterbirds, CelebA) tuning a single block outperforms full fine-tuning, and Auto-RGN beats full fine-tuning without per-block search.","relations":[{"target":"lisa","type":"competes-with","note":"Both choose layers to train; surgical FT fixes one block by shift type, LISA resamples layers during training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11466","https://arxiv.org/html/2210.11466"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Yoonho Lee","Annie S. Chen","Fahim Tajwar","Ananya Kumar","Huaxiu Yao","Percy Liang","Chelsea Finn"],"bibkey":"lee2022surgical","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lee2022surgical,\n  title = {{Surgical Fine-Tuning Improves Adaptation to Distribution Shifts}},\n  author = {Yoonho Lee and Annie S. Chen and Fahim Tajwar and Ananya Kumar and Huaxiu Yao and Percy Liang and Chelsea Finn},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2210.11466},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11466}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"One contiguous block of layers fully trainable: the coordinate subspace \\(\\Theta_{B_j}\\).","covariance":"Every selected box is fully trainable (Full FT on that box), so the image is \\(GL\\times GL\\)-covariant box by box.","rebasing":"Fixed block. Auto-RGN instead trains all tensors with per-tensor learning rates \\(\\propto\\) RGN: Full FT's image with a block-scalar metric \\(\\Lambda\\), not a different object."},"categorical_reading":"Surgical FT is a fixed coordinate block of whole layers: Full FT on the chosen block and frozen elsewhere, the shift type deciding which block. Auto-RGN keeps Full FT's image and changes only the metric \\(\\Lambda\\) through per-tensor learning rates (Exposé III).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The block's coordinates form one fixed support; inside the \\(k\\)-sparse union only when \\(|\\Theta_{B_j}|\\le k\\)."},{"target":"hift","rule":"A5","h":"q\\mapsto(\\text{stage } j: q;\\ \\text{other stages}: 0)","note":"When \\(B_j\\) is a HiFT group, the one-stage scheme embeds as stage \\(j\\) of HiFT's cycle, the other stages padded with their base value."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Full-rank updates on every matrix of the block exceed rank \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"masking","name":"Masking","full_name":"Learning binary weight masks instead of fine-tuning","paper_title":"Masking as an Efficient Alternative to Finetuning for Pretrained Language Models","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EMNLP 2020","arxiv":"2004.12406","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.174/","code_url":"https://github.com/ptlmasking/maskbert","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0\\odot M_{bin},\\quad (M_{bin})_{ij} = \\mathbb{1}[m_{ij}\\ge\\tau],\\quad m\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}} \\text{ trained with the straight-through estimator};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{real-valued scores during training};\\ d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{bits stored}","locus":"linear layers of selected Transformer blocks (top-down masking works best); biases and the classifier handled separately","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"Hadamard product with a learned binary mask, i.e. selection of a sub-network of pretrained connections; equivalently the additive update \\(\\Delta W = -(\\mathbf{1}-M_{bin})\\odot W_0\\) is constrained to zeroing coordinates","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The masked weight \\(W_0 \\odot M_{bin}\\) is an ordinary dense (or sparse) matrix with the original shape.","identity_at_init":"No: scores are drawn from a uniform distribution tuned so that a target fraction of weights starts masked (5% for BERT/RoBERTa/DistilBERT; 3-10% works well), so the initial function differs from the pretrained one.","key_idea":"Instead of changing pretrained weights, learn which ones to switch off for each task; a 1-bit mask per weight suffices to reach fine-tuning-level accuracy.","results_note":"Masking BERT, RoBERTa and DistilBERT on eleven NLP tasks performs comparably to fine-tuning, and masked and fine-tuned solutions are linearly connected with nearly constant accuracy.","relations":[{"target":"piggyback","type":"builds-on","note":"Transfers Piggyback's thresholded real-valued mask training from CNNs to pretrained Transformers."},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"dual-to","note":"Masks the weights themselves rather than an additive update."},{"target":"lt-sft","type":"dual-to","note":"Lottery-ticket selection of a sparse update vs. selection of a sparse sub-network of weights."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.12406","https://arxiv.org/html/2004.12406","https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.174/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Mengjie Zhao","Tao Lin","Fei Mi","Martin Jaggi","Hinrich Schütze"],"bibkey":"zhao2020masking","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2020masking,\n  title = {{Masking as an Efficient Alternative to Finetuning for Pretrained Language Models}},\n  author = {Mengjie Zhao and Tao Lin and Fei Mi and Martin Jaggi and Hinrich Schütze},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2004.12406},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.174/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"defect","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho(m)=W_0\\odot\\mathbb 1[m\\ge\\tau]\\): the orbit of \\(\\theta_0\\) under the Boolean monoid \\(\\{0,1\\}^{m\\times n}\\) acting by Hadamard product, a finite set (\\(d=0\\)) that keeps the zeros of \\(W_0\\). The straight-through surrogate treats it as the Hadamard torus closure \\(W_0\\odot S\\) (Lin).","base_point":"Scores are initialised so that about 5% of weights start masked: \\(e=-(\\mathbf 1-M_0)\\odot W_0\\ne0\\) by design.","gauge":"\\(\\rho\\) depends only on the sign pattern of \\(m-\\tau\\); the generic fibre is an open orthant cell, exactly an orbit of the positive torus \\(\\mathbb R_{>0}^{mn}\\) acting by \\(m\\mapsto\\tau+c\\odot(m-\\tau)\\) (fibre dim \\(mn\\)). The scores move only through the STE surrogate.","covariance":"Hadamard masking commutes with monomial changes of basis (diagonals commute, permutations permute the mask), not with rotations.","merge":"\\(W_0\\odot M\\) is an ordinary matrix of the original shape.","rebasing":"\\((W_0\\odot M_1)\\odot M_2=W_0\\odot(M_1M_2)\\): the Boolean monoid is idempotent, \\(S^K=S\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Masking is a monoid-orbit method: the Boolean monoid acts on \\(\\theta_0\\) by Hadamard product, so the image is the finite vertex set of the box between \\(0\\) and \\(W_0\\), which keeps \\(W_0\\)'s zeros and can only create new ones. Its real-valued scores are pure torus gauge of the exact \\(\\rho\\), and the 5% masked start makes it an object with a pointing defect.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"piggyback","test":"O3","reason":"Same unpointed object \\(M\\mapsto W_0\\odot M\\), but Masking is pointed at \\(W_0\\odot M_0\\ne\\theta_0\\) and Piggyback at \\(\\theta_0\\): no pointed arrow either way."},{"target":"diff-pruning","test":"O3","reason":"Pointing defect: \\(\\rho(q_0)=W_0\\odot M_0\\ne\\theta_0\\), although every image point with at most \\(k\\) zeroed entries lies in Diff pruning's union."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"piggyback","name":"Piggyback","full_name":"Piggyback (learning to mask weights of a fixed backbone)","paper_title":"Piggyback: Adapting a Single Network to Multiple Tasks by Learning to Mask Weights","authors":"Mallya et al.","year":2018,"venue":"ECCV 2018","arxiv":"1801.06519","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06519","code_url":"https://github.com/arunmallya/piggyback","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = (W_0\\odot m)\\,x,\\quad m_{ji} = \\mathbb{1}[m^{r}_{ji}\\ge\\tau],\\quad \\partial E/\\partial m = (\\delta y\\,x^{\\top})\\odot W_0 \\text{ used as straight-through gradient for } m^{r};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen}","trainable_params":"d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\text{real-valued mask weights during training};\\ 1\\ \\text{bit per weight stored}","locus":"all convolutional and fully-connected layers of a fixed ImageNet backbone (VGG-16, ResNet, DenseNet)","modification_kind":"multiplicative-weight","structure":"Hadamard product with a learned binary mask (task-specific sub-network of a fixed backbone)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The masked weights form an ordinary network of the original architecture.","identity_at_init":"\\(m^r\\) initialized to \\(10^{-2}\\) with threshold \\(\\tau = 5\\times 10^{-3}\\), so the mask starts as all ones and the network equals the backbone.","key_idea":"Add new tasks to one frozen network by learning only a binary mask over its weights, at 1 bit per parameter per task.","results_note":"With ImageNet-pretrained VGG-16/ResNet-50 backbones, Piggyback approaches individually fine-tuned networks on fine-grained and large-shift classification tasks (e.g. within 0.36% top-1 error on Places365).","relations":[],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06519","https://arxiv.org/html/1801.06519"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Arun Mallya","Dillon Davis","Svetlana Lazebnik"],"bibkey":"mallya2018piggyback","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mallya2018piggyback,\n  title = {{Piggyback: Adapting a Single Network to Multiple Tasks by Learning to Mask Weights}},\n  author = {Arun Mallya and Dillon Davis and Svetlana Lazebnik},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV)},\n  eprint = {1801.06519},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.06519}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Orb","base_point":"regular","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Orbit of \\(\\theta_0\\) under the Boolean Hadamard monoid \\(\\{0,1\\}^{m\\times n}\\); a finite image (\\(d=0\\)).","base_point":"\\(m^r_0=10^{-2}>\\tau=5\\cdot10^{-3}\\): the mask starts all ones, \\(\\rho(q_0)=\\theta_0\\); with \\(d=0\\), \\(\\dim S_1=0=d\\), regular trivially.","gauge":"Real-valued mask weights matter only through \\(\\mathbb 1[m^r\\ge\\tau]\\): positive torus acting about the threshold, fibre dim \\(mn\\); trained by the STE surrogate.","covariance":"Hadamard structure in the standard basis on both sides.","rebasing":"The Boolean monoid is idempotent: cycles only zero more entries."},"categorical_reading":"Piggyback is the orbit of \\(\\theta_0\\) under the Boolean Hadamard monoid, pointed at its identity \\(\\mathbf 1\\): the earliest selective method is an action method, not an additive one. Its update \\(-(\\mathbf 1-M)\\odot W_0\\) is a sparse diff forced to equal \\(-W_0\\) on its support, which is the precise sense in which it is dual to Diff pruning.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A3","h":"M\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ -(\\mathbf 1-M)\\odot W_0\\big)","note":"\\(Q\\) read as the discrete set of binary masks; defined on masks zeroing at most \\(k\\) entries (all masks for the unbudgeted relaxation), pointed since \\(h(\\mathbf 1)=(\\alpha_0,0)\\)."},{"target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"M\\mapsto(M-\\mathbf 1,\\ I_n)","note":"HiRA \\(W_0\\odot(\\mathbf 1+BA)\\) with inner rank \\(r=n\\), pointed at \\((0,I_n)\\) (for \\(r=n\\) every \\(B_0=0\\), full-rank-\\(A_0\\) pointing is isomorphic to this one); both keep the zeros of \\(W_0\\). For \\(r<\\min(m,n)\\) and \\(W_0\\) without zeros there is no arrow (O1): \\(W_0\\odot M\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HiRA}_r\\) needs \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M-\\mathbf 1)\\le r\\), which a generic binary mask violates."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"masking","test":"O3","reason":"Same unpointed object, different pointings (\\(\\mathbf 1\\) versus a 5\\%-masked \\(M_0\\))."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(-(\\mathbf 1-M)\\odot W_0\\) can have rank up to \\(\\min(m,n)>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-action","table_col":"Orb"},{"id":"sam-sparse","name":"SAM (sparse)","full_name":"Second-order Approximation Method for parameter selection","paper_title":"On the Effectiveness of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Fu et al.","year":2022,"venue":"AAAI 2023","arxiv":"2211.15583","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583","code_url":"https://github.com/fuzihaofzh/AnalyzeParameterEfficientFinetune","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta = \\theta^0 + M\\Delta\\theta,\\ \\ \\min_{\\Delta\\theta}\\ \\nabla\\mathcal{L}(\\theta^0)^{\\top}M\\Delta\\theta + \\tfrac12(M\\Delta\\theta)^{\\top}H(M\\Delta\\theta)\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ \\|M\\|_0 = \\lfloor mp\\rfloor;\\ \\ H\\preceq|\\lambda_{max}|I \\Rightarrow \\hat M_{ii} = \\mathbb{1}\\big[\\nabla\\mathcal{L}(\\theta^0)_i^2 \\in \\text{top-}\\lfloor mp\\rfloor\\big],\\ \\nabla\\mathcal{L}(\\theta^0) \\text{ summed over burn-in steps};\\ \\text{mask fixed during training}","trainable_params":"\\lfloor mp \\rfloor\\ \\text{in total over all } m \\text{ parameters (global selection; } p = 0.005 \\text{ in experiments);\\ per-matrix counts vary}","locus":"all parameters; gradients accumulated over a burn-in period","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"fixed diagonal 0/1 mask matrix \\(M\\) on parameter space, chosen as the analytic optimum of a second-order (upper-bounded) surrogate, which reduces to top squared-gradient selection","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing parameters change.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta\\theta = 0\\); mask computed from the gradient accumulated over 500-2000 burn-in steps (tuned per task) before training.","key_idea":"Casting every PEFT method as a sparse fine-tuned model, choose the sparse support that optimally decreases a second-order approximation of the loss, which under an isotropic Hessian bound means keeping the coordinates with the largest squared gradient.","results_note":"With RoBERTa on GLUE/SuperGLUE tasks (CoLA, STS-B, MRPC, RTE, CB, COPA, WSC) at 0.5% sparsity, SAM outperforms random, rule-based and projection-based PEFT baselines.","relations":[{"target":"fish-mask","type":"competes-with","note":"Both fix a mask from gradient statistics before training, but SAM squares the accumulated gradient while FISH sums squared per-sample gradients (diagonal Fisher), so the rankings differ."},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"competes-with","note":"SAM avoids the 'projection discontinuity' of methods that re-select the support during training."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583","https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15583"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Zihao Fu","Haoran Yang","Anthony Man-Cho So","Wai Lam","Lidong Bing","Nigel Collier"],"bibkey":"fu2022effectiveness","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fu2022effectiveness,\n  title = {{On the Effectiveness of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zihao Fu and Haoran Yang and Anthony Man-Cho So and Wai Lam and Lidong Bing and Nigel Collier},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2211.15583},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\theta_0+M\\Delta\\theta\\) with \\(M\\) a fixed diagonal 0/1 matrix: the minimiser of the second-order surrogate under \\(H\\preceq|\\lambda_{\\max}|I\\), i.e. the top-\\(\\lfloor mp\\rfloor\\) squared accumulated burn-in gradient.","covariance":"Coordinatewise score in the standard basis: signed-permutation equivariant."},"categorical_reading":"SAM picks one stratum of the \\(k\\)-sparse union by a second-order criterion and trains that fixed coordinate subspace. Its claim that every PEFT method is \\(\\theta_0+M\\Delta\\theta\\) holds for linear methods up to a transport \\(T\\) (Prop II.13), but not for cones such as LoRA, whose image is a union of planes rather than one plane.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The SAM support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fish-mask","test":"O1","reason":"SAM ranks by \\((\\sum_t g_t)^2\\) and FISH by \\(\\sum_n g_n^2\\): different supports, so different planes."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Sparse updates can have rank above \\(r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-selective","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lift","name":"LIFT","full_name":"Low-rank Informed Sparse Fine-Tuning","paper_title":"LIFT the Veil for the Truth: Principal Weights Emerge after Rank Reduction for Reasoning-Focused Supervised Fine-Tuning","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2506.00772","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00772","code_url":"https://github.com/zihanghliu/LIFT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_t' = \\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_t)\\ \\text{(best rank-}r\\text{ approximation of the current weight; } W_0 \\text{ at the start)},\\quad M_t = \\mathbb{1}\\big[\\,|W'_t| \\in \\text{top-}k\\big],\\quad W_{t+1} = W_t - \\eta\\,(M_t\\odot \\hat g_t);\\ M_t \\text{ recomputed every } \\texttt{update\\_mask\\_interval} \\text{ steps}","trainable_params":"k\\ \\text{per matrix active at any time (top } 5\\%\\text{ Principal Weights)};\\ \\text{the accumulated } \\Delta W \\text{ is supported on the union of successive masks}","locus":"LLM linear layers (attention and MLP)","modification_kind":"selective","structure":"sparse coordinate mask given by the largest-magnitude entries of the rank-\\(r\\) truncation of \\(W_0\\) ('Principal Weights'), refreshed at fixed intervals; the resulting update is high-rank","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Only existing entries are updated in place.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\); the mask is data-free (SVD of the pretrained weight).","key_idea":"Plain magnitude selection works poorly for LLM sparse fine-tuning, but after discarding small singular directions the largest remaining entries identify the weights that carry reasoning-relevant knowledge, so tuning only those beats full fine-tuning.","results_note":"Updating the top 5% Principal Weights, LIFT outperforms full fine-tuning on reasoning tasks (arithmetic and commonsense) with LoRA-level memory, and retains up to 20% more source-domain knowledge than full fine-tuning and LoRA.","relations":[{"target":"pafi","type":"competes-with","note":"Opposite magnitude rules for a static-structure sparse mask: PaFi tunes the smallest |W_0|, LIFT the largest entries of a rank-r truncation."},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Memory comparable to LoRA but high-rank sparse updates."},{"target":"spiel","type":"competes-with","note":"Both refresh the mask during training; LIFT uses a spectral criterion."},{"target":"sift","type":"competes-with","note":"LIFT compares against SIFT (and SpIEL) as sparse fine-tuning baselines (App. F)."}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00772","https://arxiv.org/html/2506.00772"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"authors_full":["Zihang Liu","Tianyu Pang","Oleg Balabanov","Chaoqun Yang","Tianjin Huang","Lu Yin","Yaoqing Yang","Shiwei Liu"],"bibkey":"liu2025lift","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2025lift,\n  title = {{LIFT the Veil for the Truth: Principal Weights Emerge after Rank Reduction for Reasoning-Focused Supervised Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zihang Liu and Tianyu Pang and Oleg Balabanov and Chaoqun Yang and Tianjin Huang and Lu Yin and Yaoqing Yang and Shiwei Liu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2506.00772},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.00772}\n}","coords":{"kind":"B","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"na","rebasing":"schedule"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The forward map is the identity; the masked update \\(W_{t+1}=W_t-\\eta M_t\\odot\\hat g_t\\) writes into \\(W\\), and \\(M_t\\) (top-\\(k\\) entries of \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_t)\\)) is recomputed from the current weight every interval: a coordinate-mask lens re-pointed from the merged weight.","shape":"Per interval the coordinate subspace of the current mask; the accumulated \\(\\Delta W\\) lies on the union of the masks visited.","covariance":"\\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r\\) is \\(CO\\times CO\\)-equivariant and top-\\(k\\) magnitude only signed-permutation equivariant; the composite rule keeps their intersection (signed permutations times homotheties), inside Mon\\(\\times\\)Mon.","merge":"No adapter; writes \\(W\\).","rebasing":"Masks recomputed from the merged weight gain (Thm V.3 cor. 2): a schedule whose reach grows with the union of masks."},"categorical_reading":"LIFT is a LISA-type rebasing scheme in the coordinate frame: each interval trains a fixed mask, and the next mask is read from the principal part of the merged weight, so its anchor depends on \\(\\theta\\) across intervals. The spectral filter changes which coordinates are chosen, not the coordinate geometry of the image.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_{M_0}x)","note":"First interval only (mask \\(M_0\\) from \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0)\\)); later intervals are the same kind of object re-based at \\(W_t\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"pafi","test":"O1","reason":"Top entries of \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0)\\) and bottom entries of \\(|W_0|\\) are generically different supports (nearly, not exactly, disjoint), so LIFT's first-interval plane is not inside PaFi's plane."},{"target":"spiel","test":"O2","reason":"Conditional: once LIFT's masks change across intervals, its accumulated \\(\\Delta W\\) is supported on the union of the masks visited; when that union exceeds SpIEL's per-tensor budget \\(d_\\phi\\), \\(d(\\mathrm{LIFT})>d(\\mathrm{SpIEL})\\). A single LIFT interval with \\(k\\le d_\\phi\\) is a stratum of SpIEL's union, so the obstruction is to the schedule, not to one stage."}],"table_row":"B","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"rosa","name":"RoSA","full_name":"Robust Adaptation (sparse plus low-rank)","paper_title":"RoSA: Accurate Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Robust Adaptation","authors":"Nikdan et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2401.04679","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04679","code_url":"https://github.com/IST-DASLab/RoSA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + BA + S,\\quad \\mathrm{supp}(S)\\subseteq M,\\ \\ M = \\mathrm{TopK}\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal{D}_M}(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}_s)^{\\alpha}\\big|_{W_0+\\tilde B\\tilde A},\\ d\\cdot d_{out}d_{in}\\Big),\\ \\alpha\\in\\{1,2\\},\\ \\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}};\\ W_0 \\text{ frozen};\\ \\tilde B\\tilde A = \\text{LoRA after a 64-batch warm-up}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out}) + d\\cdot d_{out}d_{in}\\ \\ (d = \\text{mask density})","locus":"all fully connected layers of the LLM","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"robust-PCA-style decomposition of the update: low-rank \\(BA\\) plus a sparse \\(S\\) on a fixed gradient-statistics mask (accumulated squared gradients, i.e. a diagonal-Fisher mask for \\(\\alpha=2\\))","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Both the low-rank and the sparse components are additive and can be folded into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"LoRA is first trained alone for 64 batches to compute the mask, then training restarts with both adapters at \\(BA = 0\\) (\\(B = 0\\)) and \\(S = 0\\); the mask is fixed from then on.","key_idea":"Full fine-tuning updates are not low-rank but are well approximated by low-rank plus sparse, so train both components jointly with system support for sparse adapters.","results_note":"Fine-tuning LLaMA2-7B on GSM8K, ViGGO and SQL generation, RoSA outperforms LoRA and pure sparse adaptation at equal budget and can match full fine-tuning.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"Adds a sparse component to LoRA."},{"target":"fish-mask","type":"combines","note":"Sparse component uses a FISH-like accumulated-gradient mask."},{"target":"spt","type":"competes-with","note":"SPT chooses sparse or low-rank per matrix; RoSA uses both on every matrix."},{"target":"shira","type":"generalizes","note":"with no low-rank part RoSA is gradient-masked sparse adaptation as in SHiRA-Grad"},{"target":"dsee","type":"competes-with","note":"DSEE picks its sparse support by robust PCA of W_0 rather than gradients"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04679","https://arxiv.org/html/2401.04679","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.04679","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2401.04679","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/","https://github.com/IST-DASLab/RoSA"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective","shape"],"authors_full":["Mahdi Nikdan","Soroush Tabesh","Elvir Crnčević","Dan Alistarh"],"bibkey":"nikdan2024rosa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{nikdan2024rosa,\n  title = {{RoSA: Accurate Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning via Robust Adaptation}},\n  author = {Mahdi Nikdan and Soroush Tabesh and Elvir Crnčević and Dan Alistarh},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2401.04679},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.04679}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"R: the \\(\\oplus\\)-word \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\) (fixed-mask sparse) of Obs I.7. It is filed under R-additive although its sparse summand is R-selective.","shape":"The Minkowski sum \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}+L_M\\) of LoRA's cone and the mask subspace \\(L_M\\): a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\), singular along the mask directions.","base_point":"After the 64-batch warm-up both adapters restart at \\(B=0\\), \\(S=0\\). LoRA's apex survives, with deficit up to \\(r(n-r)\\) less any overlap with \\(L_M\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the LoRA summand; the sparse summand is injective.","covariance":"The LoRA part is \\(GL\\times GL\\), but the Fisher mask is read in the standard basis; the intersection is \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\), as for FISH.","rebasing":"The LoRA summand alone reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"RoSA is the monoidal sum \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{Mask}_M\\) of Obs I.7: robust PCA as a Minkowski sum of a determinantal cone and a coordinate subspace. Both summands map into it by A5, and its image is strictly larger than either.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"shira","test":"O1","reason":"SHiRA's image leaves \\(W_0\\) unchanged off its mask, while RoSA's low-rank summand moves every entry."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The mask summand reaches full rank, so \\(\\rho_{\\max}>r\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"abba","name":"ABBA","full_name":"ABBA-Adapters (learnable Hadamard product of two low-rank matrices)","paper_title":"ABBA-Adapters: Efficient and Expressive Fine-Tuning of Foundation Models","authors":"Singhal et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2505.14238","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14238","code_url":"https://github.com/CERT-Lab/abba","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}\\,(B_1A_1)\\odot(B_2A_2) = W_0 + \\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}\\,(B_1\\odot_r B_2)(A_1^{\\top}\\odot_r A_2^{\\top})^{\\top},\\quad B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r_k},\\ A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_k\\times d_{in}};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"(r_1+r_2)(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer of an LLM","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Hadamard product of two independently learnable rank-\\(r_1\\) and rank-\\(r_2\\) matrices (rank \\(\\le r_1r_2\\)), executed without materialising \\(\\Delta W\\) via the row-wise Khatri-Rao identity \\((B_1A_1)\\odot(B_2A_2)=(B_1\\odot_r B_2)(A_1^{\\top}\\odot_r A_2^{\\top})^{\\top}\\); rank-stabilised scale \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r_1r_2}\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The product is an ordinary dense matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\((B_1,A_1)\\) initialised from the top-\\(r_1\\) truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\); \\(B_2 = 0\\) and \\(A_2\\) Kaiming-uniform, so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\) at init.","key_idea":"Make both Hadamard factors learnable (HiRA fixes one to \\(W_0\\)), anchor one to the principal subspace of \\(W_0\\), and use a rank-stabilising scale; the Khatri-Rao trick keeps memory at LoRA level.","results_note":"Reports state-of-the-art results on arithmetic and commonsense reasoning benchmarks across multiple LLMs, outperforming LoRA, HiRA and other PEFT baselines.","relations":[{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"ABBA makes both Hadamard factors learnable low-rank products; HiRA fixes one factor to the full-rank frozen W_0. ABBA's SVD init of B_1A_1 approximates W_0, so it starts near a HiRA-like update, but HiRA is not an instance of ABBA's rank-r_1 class"},{"target":"loha","type":"builds-on","note":"same function class (B_1A_1)⊙(B_2A_2) as LoHa; differs in SVD-anchored init and √(r1 r2) scaling"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"matched parameter budget, rank up to r_1 r_2"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.14238","https://arxiv.org/html/2505.14238v1","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2505.14238","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=ABBA-Adapters%20Efficient%20Expressive","https://github.com/CERT-Lab/abba"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Raghav Singhal","Kaustubh Ponkshe","Rohit Vartak","Praneeth Vepakomma"],"bibkey":"singhal2025abba","bibtex":"@inproceedings{singhal2025abba,\n  title = {{ABBA-Adapters: Efficient and Expressive Fine-Tuning of Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Raghav Singhal and Kaustubh Ponkshe and Rohit Vartak and Praneeth Vepakomma},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2505.14238},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.14238}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GLxTorus","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"The same Hadamard variety as LoHa (row-wise Khatri-Rao factorisation), with rank \\(\\le r_1r_2\\).","base_point":"\\(B_2=0\\), so only \\(B_2\\) moves at first order: \\(S_1=\\{[W_0]_{r_1}\\odot XA_2^0\\}\\), an apex. The SVD anchor makes \\(S_1\\) depend on \\(W_0\\), a different point of LoHa's apex stratum from HF's random pointing.","gauge":"LoHa's \\(GL_{r_1}\\times GL_{r_2}\\) times the two-sided torus.","covariance":"The image (all four factors trained) is LoHa's, \\(\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\); only the init frame \\([W_0]_{r_1}\\) is \\(CO\\times CO\\) (compare EVA).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\), as LoHa does."},"categorical_reading":"ABBA is a re-pointed LoHa. As unpointed objects \\(\\mathrm{ABBA}_{r_1,r_2}\\cong\\mathrm{LoHa}_{r_1,r_2}\\) (up to rescaling one factor); what is new is the base point, where the first Hadamard factor starts at the principal part \\([W_0]_{r_1}\\), an SVD-anchored point of LoHa's apex stratum.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"loha","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)=(B_1,A_1,cB_2,A_2),\\ \\ c=\\frac{\\alpha/\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}{\\gamma_{\\rm LoHa}}","note":"Isomorphism onto LoHa pointed at \\((\\text{factors of }[W_0]_{r_1},0,A_2^0)\\), not at HF's random default."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}\\,B_1\\bullet B_2,\\;(A_1^\\top\\bullet A_2^\\top)^\\top\\big)","note":"Face-splitting into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1r_2}\\), as for LoHa."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"hira","test":"O2","reason":"\\(d(\\mathrm{ABBA})=\\min(mn,r_1(m+n-r_1)+r_2(m+n-r_2)-(m+n-1))\\) (LoHa's count, an equality checked numerically, Prop II.12) exceeds \\(d(\\mathrm{HiRA}_{r_2})=r_2(m+n-r_2)\\) once \\(r_1\\ge2\\). HiRA fixes the first factor at \\(W_0\\); ABBA only starts near it. More strongly, for generic \\(W_0\\) a LoHa-type update divided entrywise by \\(W_0\\) has full rank [Num], so \\(\\mathrm{Im\\,ABBA}\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im\\,HiRA}_r\\) for every \\(r<\\min(m,n)\\)."},{"target":"loha","test":"O3","reason":"Against LoHa at HF's default pointing (Kaiming \\(B_1,A_1,B_2\\), zero \\(A_2\\)=hada_w2_b): \\(S_1(\\mathrm{ABBA})=\\{[W_0]_{r_1}\\odot XA_2^0\\}\\) is not contained in \\(S_1(\\mathrm{LoHa})=\\{B_1^0A_1^0\\odot B_2^0Y\\}\\) for generic random factors, so there is no pointed arrow despite equal images."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"krona","name":"KronA","full_name":"Kronecker Adapter","paper_title":"KronA: Parameter Efficient Tuning with Kronecker Adapter","authors":"Edalati et al.","year":2022,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023 Workshop (ENLSP-III)","arxiv":"2212.10650","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10650","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + s\\,(A_k\\otimes B_k),\\quad A_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{a_1\\times a_2},\\ B_k\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b_1\\times b_2},\\ a_1b_1=d_{out},\\ a_2b_2=d_{in};\\quad \\Delta W x = s\\,\\mathrm{vec}(B_k X A_k^{\\top}),\\ X=\\mathrm{mat}(x)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b_2\\times a_2};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A_k,B_k\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"a_1a_2+b_1b_2","locus":"parallel to attention/FFN weight matrices (KronA); KronA\\(^B\\) and KronA\\(^B_{res}\\) sit parallel to the whole FFN block","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Single Kronecker product of two dense small factors; \\(\\mathrm{rank}(A_k\\otimes B_k)=\\mathrm{rank}(A_k)\\,\\mathrm{rank}(B_k)\\), reaching full rank with \\(O(\\sqrt{d_{in}d_{out}})\\) parameters; forward pass by the vec-trick without materialising \\(\\Delta W\\).","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"The weight-parallel KronA merges into \\(W_0\\) like LoRA (zero added latency in the paper's Table 2); KronA\\(^B\\) / KronA\\(^B_{res}\\) are linear branches placed in parallel to the whole (nonlinear) FFN block, \\(Y = \\mathrm{FFN}(X) + sX[A_k \\otimes B_k]\\) (\\(+ s_{res} X\\)), so there is no single existing weight to fold them into (127%/136% latency).","identity_at_init":"One factor zero-initialized and the other Kaiming-uniform (\\(a = \\sqrt{5}\\)); the paper reports zero-init of one factor markedly improves results.","key_idea":"Replace LoRA's low-rank product by a Kronecker product, which is full-rank-capable for the same tiny budget and cheap to apply via reshaping.","results_note":"On GLUE with T5-base, KronA variants outperform LoRA, adapters and Compacter at comparable budgets, with KronA\\(^B_{res}\\) the strongest (at extra inference latency).","relations":[{"target":"lokr","type":"special-case-of","note":"LoKr with an unfactorized right factor"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"rank-unbounded alternative at similar parameter count"},{"target":"compacter","type":"competes-with","note":"Compacter uses a sum of n Kronecker products inside adapters"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/html/2212.10650","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.10650","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2212.10650","https://nips.cc/virtual/2023/81174"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Ali Edalati","Marzieh Tahaei","Ivan Kobyzev","Vahid Partovi Nia","James J. Clark","Mehdi Rezagholizadeh"],"bibkey":"edalati2022krona","bibtex":"@inproceedings{edalati2022krona,\n  title = {{KronA: Parameter Efficient Tuning with Kronecker Adapter}},\n  author = {Ali Edalati and Marzieh Tahaei and Ivan Kobyzev and Vahid Partovi Nia and James J. Clark and Mehdi Rezagholizadeh},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2212.10650},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10650}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(A_k\\otimes B_k\\) is a Segre cone, \\(\\mathcal R^{-1}_*\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\) (Prop II.13), with \\(d=a_1a_2+b_1b_2-1\\).","base_point":"One factor is zero and the other Kaiming: \\(S_1=\\{\\delta A\\otimes B_0\\}\\) has dimension \\(a_1a_2<d\\).","gauge":"\\((A,B)\\mapsto(cA,B/c)\\) (Prop II.12).","covariance":"Kronecker reshape: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","merge":"Weight-parallel KronA fuses (M1). The KronA\\(^B\\) and KronA\\(^B_{res}\\) variants sit parallel to the whole FFN block and are M∞.","rebasing":"Transported \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\): \\(R_K=W_0+\\mathcal R^{-1}(\\mathcal M_{\\le K})\\), reaching \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KronA generates the Kronecker family: it is \\(\\mathcal R^{-1}_*\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\), rank-one LoRA across the \\(\\{U_1V_1\\}|\\{U_2V_2\\}\\) cut. For equal square splits it is incomparable with every \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\), \\(r<d\\) (Thm II.11(d)), and it is LoKr with an unfactorised right factor.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lokr","rule":"A3","h":"h(A_k,B_k)=\\big(C=A_k,\\;\\text{right factor}=B_k\\big)\\ \\ \\text{(LoKr with neither factor decomposed)}","note":"Isomorphism onto the unfactorised LoKr configuration; pointed when KronA's zero factor is the left one (HF zero-initialises lokr_w1)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"\\(\\rho_{\\max}=\\mathrm{rk}A\\cdot\\mathrm{rk}B\\) (\\(I_d\\) has Kronecker rank 1 and rank d) exceeds r. Conversely a generic rank-one update has Kronecker rank \\(\\kappa_1\\) (Thm II.11(c)), so \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\) does not map into KronA either."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"phm-adapter","name":"PHM-Adapter","full_name":"Parameterized Hypercomplex Multiplication Adapter","paper_title":"Compacter: Efficient Low-Rank Hypercomplex Adapter Layers","authors":"Karimi Mahabadi et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NeurIPS 2021","arxiv":"2106.04647","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647","code_url":"https://github.com/rabeehk/compacter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h \\leftarrow h + U\\,\\mathrm{GeLU}(Dh),\\quad D=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i^{D}\\otimes B_i^{D},\\ U=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i^{U}\\otimes B_i^{U},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n},\\ B_i^{D}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{r}{n}\\times\\frac{d}{n}},\\ B_i^{U}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d}{n}\\times\\frac{r}{n}};\\ \\text{backbone frozen, adapters trained}","trainable_params":"2\\left(\\tfrac{dr}{n}+n^{3}\\right)\\ \\text{per adapter}","locus":"bottleneck adapters after the attention and feed-forward sublayers (Houlsby placement)","modification_kind":"architectural-insertion","structure":"Bottleneck MLP inserted serially with residual connection whose down/up projections are PHM layers: sums of \\(n\\) Kronecker products (Kronecker rank \\(\\le n\\), i.e. rank \\(\\le n\\) after Van Loan-Pitsianis rearrangement), reducing parameters by about \\(1/n\\).","mergeable":"no","mergeable_reason":"The adapter contains a GeLU nonlinearity in a new serial branch, so it cannot be folded into existing weights.","identity_at_init":"No exact zero-init guarantee is stated in the method section; like Houlsby adapters it relies on the residual connection with small-initialized adapter weights.","key_idea":"Learn the 'multiplication rule' of an \\(n\\)-dimensional hypercomplex algebra: a dense projection becomes a sum of \\(n\\) Kronecker products, cutting adapter parameters by ~\\(n\\).","results_note":"On GLUE with T5-base, PHM-Adapter (\\(n = 12\\)) trains 0.179% of parameters and is competitive with full fine-tuning and standard adapters.","relations":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","type":"builds-on","note":"replaces the adapter's dense projections by PHM layers"},{"target":"compacter","type":"generalizes","note":"Compacter shares A_i across layers and makes B_i low-rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.04647","https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08597","https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2021"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi","James Henderson","Sebastian Ruder"],"bibkey":"mahabadi2021compacter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mahabadi2021compacter,\n  title = {{Compacter: Efficient Low-Rank Hypercomplex Adapter Layers}},\n  author = {Rabeeh Karimi Mahabadi and James Henderson and Sebastian Ruder},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2106.04647},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04647}\n}","coords":{"kind":"E","shape":"Fun","base_point":"neutral","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"Minf","rebasing":null},"coord_notes":{"kind":"A bottleneck \\(h+U\\,\\mathrm{GeLU}(Dh)\\) in the Houlsby placement, with \\(U=0\\) neutral.","shape":"Function-level: projections are sums of \\(n\\) Kronecker products.","base_point":"Neutral point at \\(U=0\\); no exact zero-init is stated (near-identity as for Houlsby).","gauge":"With full fast weights \\(B_i\\), \\(\\sum_iA_i\\otimes B_i\\) is invariant under \\(GL_n\\) mixing of the summation index (\\(A'_j=\\sum_iA_ig_{ij}\\), \\(B'_j=\\sum_i(g^{-1})_{ji}B_i\\)), separately for \\(D\\) and \\(U\\). Here the table's 'GL on PHM index' is correct.","covariance":"Kronecker reshape: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","merge":"Non-affine (GeLU) serial box: M∞.","rebasing":"n/a for an extension."},"categorical_reading":"The PHM adapter is the Houlsby adapter precomposed with the Kronecker-sum map \\((A_i,B_i)\\mapsto\\sum_iA_i\\otimes B_i\\) on each projection, an A3 arrow into Houlsby. Compacter maps into it by tying the slow weights and restricting the fast weights to rank \\(\\rho\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{A_i^D,B_i^D,A_i^U,B_i^U\\})=\\big(W_{down}=\\textstyle\\sum_iA_i^D\\otimes B_i^D,\\;W_{up}=\\sum_iA_i^U\\otimes B_i^U\\big)","note":"Same extension and placement; neutral point maps to neutral point."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"compacter","test":"O2","reason":"Per-adapter slow weights and full-rank fast weights give \\(d(\\mathrm{PHM})\\gg d(\\mathrm{Compacter})\\) (shared \\(A_i\\), rank-\\(\\rho\\) fast weights); Compacter to PHM is the A3 arrow."},{"target":"kadaptation","test":"O1","reason":"Its box function is non-affine (GeLU), so its functional image is not inside that of any weight-space method such as KAdaptation."}],"table_row":"E","table_col":"Fun"},{"id":"kadaptation","name":"KAdaptation","full_name":"Kronecker Adaptation","paper_title":"Parameter-efficient Model Adaptation for Vision Transformers","authors":"He et al.","year":2022,"venue":"AAAI 2023","arxiv":"2203.16329","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16329","code_url":"https://github.com/UCSB-AI/PEViT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{n}A_i\\otimes(u_iv_i^{\\top}),\\quad A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}\\ \\text{(shared across layers)},\\ u_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{n}\\times r},\\ v_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{in}}{n}\\times r};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A_i,u_i,v_i\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per matrix} + n^{3}\\ \\text{shared}","locus":"ViT attention weight matrices (submodules selected by lowest local intrinsic dimension)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Low-rank PHM (sum of \\(n\\) Kronecker products of shared slow weights with low-rank fast weights) applied directly to the weight update \\(\\Delta W\\) rather than to an inserted adapter.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is a dense matrix added to frozen attention weights; the paper notes no added inference latency.","identity_at_init":"Not stated explicitly in the method section consulted; presumably one fast factor starts at zero so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\) (unverified).","key_idea":"Choose where to adapt via the local intrinsic dimension of each ViT submodule, then express the update as a Compacter-style Kronecker sum so it merges like LoRA.","results_note":"Best accuracy/parameter trade-off among compared methods on 20 image-classification datasets few-shot and 7 datasets full-shot with CLIP ViTs.","relations":[{"target":"compacter","type":"builds-on","note":"same LPHM parameterization, moved from inserted adapters to ΔW"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"mergeable additive update with Kronecker structure instead of plain low rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16329","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.16329","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2203.16329","https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v37i1.25160"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Xuehai He","Chunyuan Li","Pengchuan Zhang","Jianwei Yang","Xin Eric Wang"],"bibkey":"he2022parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2022parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-efficient Model Adaptation for Vision Transformers}},\n  author = {Xuehai He and Chunyuan Li and Pengchuan Zhang and Jianwei Yang and Xin Eric Wang},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2203.16329},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.16329}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Weight-space: the LPHM Kronecker sum is applied to \\(\\Delta W\\) of frozen attention matrices, so the architecture is unchanged (unlike Compacter, which is an extension).","shape":"Multilinear in \\((A_i,u_i,v_i)\\): a sum of \\(n\\) LoKr-type cones whose slow factors are shared across layers, with Kronecker rank \\(\\le n\\) and \\(\\rho_{\\max}\\le n^2r\\).","base_point":"The init is not stated. A zero fast factor (needed for \\(\\Delta W=0\\) without compensation) is the natural pointing; then only that factor moves to first order, so an apex.","gauge":"Per-term \\(GL_r\\) on \\(u_iv_i^\\top\\), plus a torus of slides \\(A_i\\mapsto a_iA_i\\) compensated in every layer. \\(GL_n\\) mixing of the index is broken by the rank-\\(r\\) fast weights, as for Compacter. For \\(r = 1\\) the gauge is a pure torus.","covariance":"Kronecker reshape (\\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\)), with the slow weights tied across layers.","rebasing":"The span of all \\(A\\otimes uv^\\top\\) is every matrix, so \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KAdaptation is the weight-space shadow of Compacter: the same LPHM parametrisation moved from an inserted non-linear box to an additive update of \\(W_0\\), which makes it an object of \\(\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)\\) (M1). It is a \\(\\oplus\\) of \\(n\\) LoKr terms with tied slow weights and maps into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{n^2r}\\) by A3.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([A_1\\otimes u_1,\\dots,A_n\\otimes u_n],\\;[I_n\\otimes v_1^\\top;\\dots;I_n\\otimes v_n^\\top]\\big)","note":"Uses \\((A_i\\otimes u_i)(I_n\\otimes v_i^\\top)=A_i\\otimes u_iv_i^\\top\\). Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{n^2r}\\), pointed at \\((0,[I_n\\otimes v_i^{0\\top}])\\) when \\(u_i^0=0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lokr","test":"O2","reason":"Cut-rank across the Kronecker cut: for the same \\(n\\times n\\) split, LoKr's update \\(C\\otimes BA\\) has Kronecker rank 1 (Thm II.11(a)), while KAdaptation's sum of n terms has Kronecker rank up to n. The framework files Kronecker-rank obstructions under O2 (axes.json O2 examples)."},{"target":"compacter","test":"O1","reason":"Compacter sits after the sublayer and cannot change attention scores at its own layer, whereas KAdaptation edits \\(W_q,W_k\\); the functional images are incomparable."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"kradapter","name":"KRAdapter","full_name":"Khatri-Rao Adapter","paper_title":"Towards Higher Effective Rank in Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning using Khatri--Rao Product","authors":"Albert et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICCV 2025","arxiv":"2508.00230","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00230","code_url":"https://github.com/PaulAlbert31/KRAdapter","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\alpha\\,\\big[U\\odot_{KR}V\\big]_{1:d_{out},\\,:},\\quad (U\\odot_{KR}V)_{:,j}=U_{:,j}\\otimes V_{:,j},\\ U\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k_1\\times d_{in}},\\ V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k_2\\times d_{in}},\\ k_1k_2\\ge d_{out};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ U,V\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"d_{in}(k_1+k_2)\\approx 2\\,d_{in}\\sqrt{d_{out}}","locus":"linear layers of vision-language models and LLMs","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Column-wise Kronecker (Khatri-Rao) product truncated to \\(d_{out}\\) rows; with random factors it is almost surely of full rank, and its singular-value tail is heavier (higher effective rank) than Kronecker or low-rank updates. \\(k_1=\\lfloor\\sqrt{d_{out}}\\rfloor\\), \\(k_2=\\lceil d_{out}/k_1\\rceil\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The Khatri-Rao product is a dense matrix of \\(W_0\\)'s shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(U\\) zero-initialized and \\(V\\) randomly initialized (Kaiming-style, negative slope \\(1/\\sqrt{k_1}\\)), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\); the scale \\(\\alpha\\) is typically 0.1.","key_idea":"Target effective rank, not just algebraic rank: the Khatri-Rao product spreads energy over many singular directions at about the cost of a rank-16-32 LoRA.","results_note":"Reports gains on vision-language models up to 1B and LLMs up to 8B parameters, especially on unseen commonsense-reasoning tasks, with LoRA-level memory and compute.","relations":[{"target":"krona","type":"competes-with","note":"column-wise vs full Kronecker product; paper contrasts their spectra"},{"target":"randlora","type":"competes-with","note":"RandLoRA (ICLR 2025) shares four of the five authors (Albert, Zhang, Saratchandran, van den Hengel, Abbasnejad) and is a baseline in the paper"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"matched budget, higher effective rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.00230","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2508.00230"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Paul Albert","Frederic Z. Zhang","Hemanth Saratchandran","Anton van den Hengel","Ehsan Abbasnejad"],"bibkey":"albert2025higher","bibtex":"@inproceedings{albert2025higher,\n  title = {{Towards Higher Effective Rank in Parameter-efficient Fine-tuning using Khatri--Rao Product}},\n  author = {Paul Albert and Frederic Z. Zhang and Hemanth Saratchandran and Anton van den Hengel and Ehsan Abbasnejad},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2508.00230},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00230}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Bilinear in \\((U,V)\\): column \\(j\\) of \\(\\Delta W\\) is \\(U_{:,j}\\otimes V_{:,j}\\), truncated to \\(d_{out}\\) rows.","base_point":"\\(U=0\\), \\(V\\) Kaiming: \\(S_1=\\{[\\delta U\\odot_{KR}V_0]\\}\\) has dimension \\(k_1d_{in}\\), less than \\(d(M)=(k_1+k_2-1)d_{in}\\).","gauge":"Column-wise slides \\((U_{:,j},V_{:,j})\\mapsto(c_jU_{:,j},V_{:,j}/c_j)\\): a torus \\(T_{d_{in}}\\).","covariance":"The output index is reshaped into a \\(k_1\\times k_2\\) grid (\\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\)); on the input side only monomial changes preserve the column-wise Kronecker structure.","rebasing":"The columns \\(u\\otimes v\\) span \\(\\mathbb R^{k_1k_2}\\), so \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"KRAdapter is the column-wise (Khatri-Rao) cousin of KronA and a sub-object of LoHa: freezing LoHa's left factors at 0/1 row selectors gives exactly \\([U\\odot_{KR}V]_{1:d_{out}}\\). Its high effective rank is LoHa's Hadamard rank multiplication, with a torus of per-column gauges.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"loha","rule":"A2","h":"h(U,V)=(B_1=S_1,\\;A_1=U,\\;B_2=S_2,\\;A_2=V),\\ \\ S_1,S_2\\ \\text{the }\\{0,1\\}\\text{ row selectors of the Kronecker index}","note":"LoHa with \\(r_1=k_1\\), \\(r_2=k_2\\) and its B-factors frozen at the selectors (one of them scaled by \\(\\alpha/\\gamma_{\\rm LoHa}\\)); pointed at \\((S_1,0,S_2,V_0)\\). Checked: \\((S_1U)\\odot(S_2V)\\) has entry \\(U_{a,j}V_{b,j}\\) at row \\((a,b)\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"krona","test":"O2","reason":"With the matched output split, \\(d(\\mathrm{KRAdapter})=(k_1+k_2-1)d_{in}\\) exceeds \\(d(\\mathrm{KronA})=a_1a_2+b_1b_2-1\\) (\\(a_2b_2=d_{in}\\)), and KronA's update has Kronecker rank 1."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"loretta","name":"LoRETTA","full_name":"Low-Rank Economic Tensor-Train Adaptation","paper_title":"LoRETTA: Low-Rank Economic Tensor-Train Adaptation for Ultra-Low-Parameter Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NAACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.11417","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11417","code_url":"https://github.com/yifanycc/loretta","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\mathrm{TT}(\\Delta W_{up})\\,\\mathrm{TT}(\\Delta W_{down}),\\quad \\Delta W_{up}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ \\Delta W_{down}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ \\mathrm{TT}(M)[a_1,\\dots,a_D]=G^{1}_{a_1}G^{2}_{a_2}\\cdots G^{D}_{a_D},\\ G^{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_{i-1}\\times k_i\\times r_i}\\ (\\text{LoRETTA}_{rep});\\ \\text{TT cores trained}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{i=1}^{D}r_{i-1}k_ir_i\\ \\text{for each of the two tensorized factors}","locus":"LoRETTA_rep: attention/MLP weight matrices (like LoRA); LoRETTA_adp: tensorized bottleneck adapters after attention and FFN sublayers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Each LoRA factor is reshaped into a \\(D\\)-way tensor and stored in tensor-train (MPS) format; the adapter variant tensorizes the up/down projections of a bottleneck adapter instead.","mergeable":"conditional","mergeable_reason":"LoRETTA_rep yields a dense \\(\\Delta W\\) that can be merged; LoRETTA_adp is a serial adapter with a nonlinearity and cannot.","identity_at_init":"All TT cores are Gaussian-initialized (a zero core would stall gradients); the noise injected by this random initialization is then compensated using the initialized weights so training starts from the pretrained function.","key_idea":"Push LoRA's parameter count down by another one to two orders of magnitude by storing its factors as tensor trains.","results_note":"Comparable or better than widely used PEFT methods with up to 100x fewer trainable parameters than LoRA/adapters on LLaMA-2-7B.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"tensorizes LoRA's two factors"},{"target":"tt-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"TT-LoRA tensorizes the whole ΔW as a single tensor train"},{"target":"quanta","type":"competes-with","note":"QuanTA uses a circuit-shaped tensor network instead of a TT chain"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11417","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.11417","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2402.11417","https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.174/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Yifan Yang","Jiajun Zhou","Ngai Wong","Zheng Zhang"],"bibkey":"yang2024loretta","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2024loretta,\n  title = {{LoRETTA: Low-Rank Economic Tensor-Train Adaptation for Ultra-Low-Parameter Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yifan Yang and Jiajun Zhou and Ngai Wong and Zheng Zhang},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.11417},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11417}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Each LoRA factor is a tensor train (MPS), so \\(\\Delta W=\\mathrm{TT}(B)\\,\\mathrm{TT}(A)\\) is multilinear in the cores. Gaussian cores with the initial product subtracted make the image the translated cone \\((W_0-B_0A_0)+\\{\\mathrm{TT}(B)\\mathrm{TT}(A)\\}\\): Cone*, not the table's untranslated 'meet of transported cones'.","base_point":"Disagrees with the table's TT/Tucker 'apex'. LoRETTA avoids zero cores and compensates the random initial product, a split (Type II) pointing at a generic point, so \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point with \\(S_1\\) of full dimension (regular). Apex is right for zero-core TT/Tucker inits (FacT, LoTR, FLoRA, MetaTT).","gauge":"TT bond gauges \\(\\prod GL_{r_e}\\) on each factor, plus \\(GL_r\\) between the two factors.","covariance":"Tensorising the factor matrices imposes a tensor-product structure on \\(d_{out}\\) and \\(d_{in}\\) (\\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\)).","merge":"LoRETTA_rep fuses (M1); LoRETTA_adp is a non-linear bottleneck adapter (E, M∞).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRETTA_rep is LoRA precomposed with two TT-format maps onto \\(\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}\\) and \\(\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}\\) and pointed at a random split. It is a Type II object (stratum S of Thm II.2, with a random rather than spectral \\(P\\)) and maps into LoRA by A3.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{G^i_{up}\\},\\{G^i_{down}\\})=\\big(\\mathrm{TT}(\\{G^i_{up}\\}),\\;\\mathrm{TT}(\\{G^i_{down}\\})\\big)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at the split \\((B_0,A_0)\\) with residual \\(W_0-B_0A_0\\) (stratum S, like PiSSA or OLoRA but with a random P)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O3","reason":"Against LoRA at its standard \\(T_A\\) pointing: LoRETTA's base point is a smooth point of a translated cone and LoRA's is an apex, and the images are incomparable (Thm II.2(d))."},{"target":"tt-lora","test":"O3","reason":"TT-LoRA starts from an uncompensated Gaussian \\(\\Delta W_0\\) (a defect), LoRETTA from a compensated split. The base points differ, so there is no pointed arrow even where the images nest."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"tt-lora","name":"TT-LoRA","full_name":"Tensor-Train Low-Rank Approximation","paper_title":"Tensor Train Low-rank Approximation (TT-LoRA): Democratizing AI with Accelerated LLMs","authors":"Anjum et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICMLA 2024","arxiv":"2408.01008","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.01008","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\alpha\\,\\mathrm{mat}\\big(\\mathcal{C}_1\\times^{1}\\mathcal{C}_2\\times^{1}\\cdots\\times^{1}\\mathcal{C}_D\\big),\\quad \\mathcal{C}_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_{i-1}\\times k_i\\times r_i},\\ r_0=r_D=1,\\ \\textstyle\\prod_i k_i=d_{out}d_{in};\\quad y=W_0x+\\alpha\\,(\\textstyle\\prod_i\\mathcal{C}_i)\\,x;\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ \\mathcal{C}_i\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{i=1}^{D}r_{i-1}k_ir_i","locus":"\\(W_q\\) and \\(W_v\\) attention projections","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"\\(\\Delta W\\) reshaped into a \\(D\\)-way tensor held in tensor-train (MPS) format; the input is contracted core-by-core without reconstructing \\(\\Delta W\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The TT cores can be contracted into a dense \\(\\Delta W\\) and added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Does not preserve the function: \\(\\Delta W\\) is drawn from a Gaussian and then TT-decomposed, so the adapted model differs from the base model at step 0.","key_idea":"Drop the LoRA matrix factorization altogether and represent the full update as one tensor train, contracted directly with the input for compression and lower latency.","results_note":"On BERT-family models (GLUE) and LLaMA-2/3, reports accuracy comparable to LoRA and other PEFT methods with substantially higher compression.","relations":[{"target":"loretta","type":"builds-on","note":"extends LoRETTA's tensor-train idea to a single TT for ΔW"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative factorization of ΔW"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/html/2408.01008","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.01008","https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10903446/","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2408.01008","https://doi.org/10.1109/ICMLA61862.2024.00085"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Afia Anjum","Maksim E. Eren","Ismael Boureima","Boian Alexandrov","Manish Bhattarai"],"bibkey":"anjum2024tensor","bibtex":"@inproceedings{anjum2024tensor,\n  title = {{Tensor Train Low-rank Approximation (TT-LoRA): Democratizing AI with Accelerated LLMs}},\n  author = {Afia Anjum and Maksim E. Eren and Ismael Boureima and Boian Alexandrov and Manish Bhattarai},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2408.01008},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.01008}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W\\) is reshaped into a \\(D\\)-way tensor in TT format. The TT variety is the meet of the bond-cut determinantal cones, itself a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(\\Delta W_0\\) is drawn from a Gaussian and TT-decomposed without compensation, so \\(\\rho(q_0)-\\theta_0=\\alpha\\,\\mathrm{mat}(\\prod\\mathcal C_i^0)\\ne0\\) by design: a defect.","gauge":"\\(\\prod_eGL_{r_e}\\) on the TT bonds (Prop II.12 table).","covariance":"Reshape: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"TT-LoRA is the tensor-train (MPS) variety of the reshaped update, a meet of transported determinantal cones, with a built-in pointing defect. It is an object of the unpointed slice; its exactly pointed variant would be a TT apex.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\mathcal C_1,\\dots,\\mathcal C_D)=\\big(\\mathcal C_1\\times^1\\cdots\\times^1\\mathcal C_c,\\;\\mathcal C_{c+1}\\times^1\\cdots\\times^1\\mathcal C_D\\big)\\ \\text{reshaped to }d_{out}\\times r_c,\\ r_c\\times d_{in}","note":"When the reshape orders all output modes before the input modes (cut after core c, bond \\(r_c\\)), TT-LoRA factors through \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_c}\\) (Thm II.10). With the Gaussian init this is a morphism between objects with a defect."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"loretta","test":"O3","reason":"TT-LoRA's base point carries an uncompensated defect; LoRETTA compensates its random product (a split). Different base points, so no pointed arrow."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lotr","name":"LoTR","full_name":"Low Tensor Rank weight adaptation","paper_title":"LoTR: Low Tensor Rank Weight Adaptation","authors":"Bershatsky et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2402.01376","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.01376","code_url":"https://github.com/daskol/lotr","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_s = W_{0,s} + \\alpha\\,B\\,G_s^{\\top}A^{\\top}\\ \\ (\\text{paper, row-vector form: } Y = XW_s + \\alpha\\,XAG_sB^{\\top}),\\quad A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{shared by all layers } s,\\ G_s\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\ \\text{layer-specific};\\ \\delta\\mathcal{W}=\\mathcal{G}\\times_1A\\times_2B","trainable_params":"|\\mathcal{W}|\\,r^{2}+r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{for a stack of } |\\mathcal{W}| \\text{ equally shaped matrices}","locus":"a stack of same-type matrices across depth (e.g. all \\(W_q\\), all \\(W_v\\))","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Tucker-2 decomposition of the 3-tensor of updates stacked over layers: shared left/right factor matrices with a per-layer \\(r\\times r\\) core.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each \\(A G_s B^\\top\\) is a dense update for its layer and can be added to \\(W_{0,s}\\).","identity_at_init":"Core \\(G = 0\\) with \\(A, B \\sim N(0,1)\\) (found best among tested schemes), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Share the column/row spaces of the update across all layers and only learn a tiny \\(r\\times r\\) mixing core per layer, so cost grows with depth as \\(r^2\\) rather than \\(r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\).","results_note":"Reports better parameter efficiency than LoRA, with savings that grow with model depth.","relations":[{"target":"fact","type":"equivalent-to","note":"Same Tucker-2 parametrization of a stack of updates (shared outer factors, per-matrix r x r core) as FacT-TT's ΔW_i = sUΣ_iV^T; they differ only in which matrices are stacked (LoTR: one family, e.g. all W_q; FacT: all 12L d x d blocks of a ViT). LoTR does not cite FacT."},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"LoRA with an r x r core and cross-layer sharing of the outer factors"},{"target":"flora-tucker","type":"competes-with","note":"FLoRA uses an unshared per-layer Tucker core"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.01376","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2402.01376"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Daniel Bershatsky","Daria Cherniuk","Talgat Daulbaev","Aleksandr Mikhalev","Ivan Oseledets"],"bibkey":"bershatsky2024lotr","bibtex":"@article{bershatsky2024lotr,\n  title = {{LoTR: Low Tensor Rank Weight Adaptation}},\n  author = {Daniel Bershatsky and Daria Cherniuk and Talgat Daulbaev and Aleksandr Mikhalev and Ivan Oseledets},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2402.01376},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.01376},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.01376}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W_s=\\alpha BG_s^\\top A^\\top\\) (Tucker-2 of the stacked updates) is multilinear; jointly the image is a cone whose layer slices share \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,B\\) and \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A^\\top\\).","base_point":"\\(G=0\\) with \\(A,B\\) Gaussian: only the cores move at first order. \\(\\dim S_1=|\\mathcal W|r^2\\) (a LoRA-XS in fixed random frames per layer) against \\(d=|\\mathcal W|r^2+r(d_{in}+d_{out})-2r^2\\).","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\times GL_r\\) acting on the shared frames and all cores at once: \\((A,B,G_s^\\top)\\mapsto(Ah,\\,Bg,\\,g^{-1}G_s^\\top h^{-\\top})\\).","covariance":"Per box \\(GL\\times GL\\). Both sides are tied across the stack, so changes of basis must agree on every stacked layer (diagonal \\(GL\\times GL\\)).","rebasing":"Each cycle can choose new shared frames: \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoTR is a multi-box generalised LoRA-XS with trained shared frames. With the frames frozen, its cores are the product \\(\\mathrm{FA}\\times\\mathrm{FB}\\) per layer; unfreezing the frames gives a Tucker-2 cone that maps by diagonal copies into FLoRA, ShareLoRA and per-layer LoRA, and is isomorphic to FacT-TT on the same stack.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,\\{G_s\\})=\\big(V=A,\\;U=B,\\;\\Sigma_s=G_s^\\top\\big)","note":"Isomorphism of unpointed objects when FacT-TT stacks the same matrices (FacT stacks all 12L ViT blocks, LoTR one family per stack); scales absorbed. As pointed objects it lands in FacT pointed at \\((U,V,\\Sigma)=(B_0,A_0,0)\\), not at FacT's default \\(V_0=0\\), whose first-order space differs."},{"target":"flora-tucker","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,\\{G_s\\})=\\big(A_F=B,\\;G_F=G_s^\\top,\\;B_F=A\\big)_s","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared frames into per-layer FLoRA, landing at FLoRA's own G = 0 pointing."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(BG_s^\\top,\\;A^\\top\\big)_s","note":"Per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0^\\top)\\) (stratum \\(T_A\\))."},{"target":"sharelora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\{B_s=BG_s^\\top\\}_s,\\;A^\\top\\big)","note":"ShareA with the shared down-projection \\(A^\\top\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"metatt","test":"O2","reason":"LoTR's per-(layer, family) cores are free (\\(|\\mathcal W|r^2\\) dimensions) while MetaTT's factorise as \\(G_2[l]G_3[m]\\) (\\((L+M)r^2\\)), so \\(d(\\mathrm{LoTR})>d(\\mathrm{MetaTT})\\)."},{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O1","reason":"LoTR trains its shared, randomly initialised frames, so column and row spaces leave each layer's top-r SVD frames, which LoRA-XS keeps frozen."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"fact","name":"FacT","full_name":"Factor-Tuning (tensorised ViT with shared factors)","paper_title":"FacT: Factor-Tuning for Lightweight Adaptation on Vision Transformer","authors":"Jie and Deng","year":2022,"venue":"AAAI 2023","arxiv":"2212.03145","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03145","code_url":"https://github.com/JieShibo/PETL-ViT","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta\\mathcal{W}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{12L\\times d\\times d}\\ (\\text{all ViT weights stacked});\\ \\text{FacT-TT: } \\Delta W_i = s\\,U\\,\\Sigma_i\\,V^{\\top},\\ U,V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ \\text{shared by all matrices},\\ \\Sigma_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\ \\text{per matrix};\\ \\text{FacT-TK: } \\Delta W_i = s\\,U\\Big(\\sum_{t=1}^{r} P_{i,t}\\,C_{t,:,:}\\Big)V^{\\top},\\ P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{12L\\times r},\\ C\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r\\times r};\\ \\text{all factors trained}","trainable_params":"\\text{TT: } 2dr + 12Lr^{2}\\ \\text{for the whole ViT};\\ \\text{TK: } 2dr + 12Lr + r^{3}","locus":"all attention and MLP weight matrices of a ViT (MLP matrices split into \\(d\\times d\\) blocks), tensorised into one 3-way tensor","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"tensor-train / Tucker factorisation of the stacked update tensor: shared row and column bases \\(U\\), \\(V\\) across all layers with a tiny per-matrix core (TT) or a shared core with per-matrix mixing weights (Tucker); LoRA is the Matrix-Batch format","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each slice \\(\\Delta W_i\\) is a dense \\(d\\times d\\) matrix that can be added to the corresponding frozen weight.","identity_at_init":"\\(V\\) zero-initialised, other factors random, so the update tensor is zero","key_idea":"Exploit inter-layer redundancy, not just intra-weight redundancy: tensorise every weight of the ViT into one tensor and fine-tune the factors of a TT/Tucker decomposition whose bases are shared by all layers.","results_note":"On VTAB-1K FacT performs on par with NOAH (the previous state of the art) while being about 5x more parameter-efficient; a tiny version with 8K trainable parameters (0.01% of ViT-B) outperforms full fine-tuning, VPT and BitFit; best among PETL baselines in few-shot settings.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"the FacT tensorisation framework contains LoRA as its Matrix-Batch format (exactly LoRA when only W_q, W_v are tensorised); the FacT-TT/TK instances themselves do not contain LoRA"},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"same U Sigma_i V^T core form, but FacT learns bases shared across layers while LoRA-XS freezes per-matrix SVD bases"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"cross-layer shared bases with tiny per-layer parameters"},{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"the paper reads LoRA as the matrix-batch format of the same tensorized update"},{"target":"lotr","type":"equivalent-to","note":"FacT-TT and LoTR share the Tucker-2 form with cross-layer U, V"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03145","https://arxiv.org/html/2212.03145","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.03145","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2212.03145","https://github.com/JieShibo/PETL-ViT"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape","sharing"],"authors_full":["Shibo Jie","Zhi-Hong Deng"],"bibkey":"jie2022fact","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jie2022fact,\n  title = {{FacT: Factor-Tuning for Lightweight Adaptation on Vision Transformer}},\n  author = {Shibo Jie and Zhi-Hong Deng},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2212.03145},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03145}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Tucker-2 tensor-network variety \\(\\{(U \\Sigma_i V^\\top)_i\\}\\): the intersection of the mode-2 and mode-3 flattening rank cones (table: 'meet of transported cones'); multihomogeneous, so Cone, as axes.json lists TT adapters.","base_point":"\\(V_0=0\\): \\(\\dim S_1=dr\\) (checked) against \\(d=2dr+12Lr^2-2r^2\\): apex.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\times GL_r\\) on the two bonds \\((U g^{-1}, g \\Sigma_i h^\\top, V h^{-1})\\): \\(\\prod GL_{r_e}\\); Jacobian deficit \\(2r^2\\) (checked).","covariance":"Shared \\(U\\), \\(V\\) across all \\(12L\\) matrices and the split of MLP matrices into \\(d\\times d\\) blocks impose a tensorisation: \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","rebasing":"\\(\\mathrm{span}\\,C\\) contains every single-box rank-\\(r\\) update: \\(\\to\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"FacT-TT is the Tucker-2 tensor network \\(\\Delta W_i=sU\\Sigma_iV^\\top\\) on the stacked update tensor: an image-level meet of the two flattening determinantal cones, with gauge \\(GL_r\\times GL_r\\) on the bonds and covariance the imposed tensorisation \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\). Box by box it maps into LoRA\\(_r\\), landing in the stratum \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) because \\(V_0=0\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,\\Sigma,V)=\\big(sU\\Sigma_i,\\ V^\\top\\big)_i","note":"Into per-box LoRA_r pointed at (sU_0Sigma_{i,0}, 0): stratum T_B in every box."},{"target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,P,C,V)=\\big(U,\\ (\\textstyle\\sum_tP_{i,t}C_t)_i,\\ V\\big)","note":"FacT-TK -> FacT-TT: the Tucker core constrains the per-matrix cores; both pointed at V=0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O2","reason":"Same U Sigma_i V^T core form, but FacT trains shared U,V: d(FacT)=2dr+12Lr^2-2r^2 exceeds d(LoRA-XS on 12L boxes)=12Lr^2, and LoRA-XS's frames are W_i's frozen SVD frames."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"flora-tucker","name":"FLoRA (Tucker core)","full_name":"Low-rank core-space adaptation for N-dimensional parameter spaces","paper_title":"Maintaining Structural Integrity in Parameter Spaces for Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning","authors":"Si et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2405.14739","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14739","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + s\\,\\mathcal{G}\\times_1A^{(1)}\\times_2\\cdots\\times_NA^{(N)};\\quad \\text{linear: } W=W_0+s\\,AGB^{\\top},\\ G\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_1\\times r_2},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r_1},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r_2};\\ \\text{conv: }\\Delta W=\\mathcal{G}\\times_1A\\times_2B\\times_3C\\times_4D","trainable_params":"\\text{linear: }r_1r_2+r_1d_{out}+r_2d_{in};\\ \\text{N-D: }\\textstyle\\prod_k r_k+\\sum_k r_kn_k","locus":"linear and convolutional layers (ConvNeXt, DeBERTa, LLaMA-3, InternViT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Tucker decomposition of \\(\\Delta W\\) in its native \\(N\\)-D shape (a low-rank 'core space' with the same topology as the weight); for matrices it is the three-factor \\(A G B^{\\top}\\).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The Tucker product is a dense tensor of the weight's shape.","identity_at_init":"Core \\(G\\) zero-initialized, factor matrices Gaussian, so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"Do not flatten conv kernels into matrices: model the update with a Tucker core that keeps the kernel's spatial structure.","results_note":"Outperforms LoRA and DoRA on ConvNeXt-V2 detection/segmentation, DeBERTaV3 GLUE, LLaMA-3-8B commonsense reasoning and InternViT multimodal tasks.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"G = I recovers LoRA; for linear layers the function class equals rank-min(r1,r2) LoRA, so the gain is in N-D layers and optimization"},{"target":"lotr","type":"competes-with","note":"LoTR shares the outer factors across layers"},{"target":"fact","type":"competes-with","note":"FacT-TK applies Tucker across layers instead of within a kernel"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.14739","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2405.14739","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Maintaining%20Structural%20Integrity%20Parameter%20Spaces"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Chongjie Si","Xuehui Wang","Xue Yang","Zhengqin Xu","Qingyun Li","Jifeng Dai","Yu Qiao","Xiaokang Yang","Wei Shen"],"bibkey":"si2024maintaining","bibtex":"@inproceedings{si2024maintaining,\n  title = {{Maintaining Structural Integrity in Parameter Spaces for Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Chongjie Si and Xuehui Wang and Xue Yang and Zhengqin Xu and Qingyun Li and Jifeng Dai and Yu Qiao and Xiaokang Yang and Wei Shen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2405.14739},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14739}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"For linear layers \\(\\Delta W=sAGB^\\top\\) has image \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(r_1,r_2)}\\), LoRA's cone; for conv layers it is a Tucker variety in the kernel's own modes.","base_point":"\\(G=0\\) is a zero gate: \\(S_1=\\{A_0\\,\\delta G\\,B_0^\\top\\}\\) of dimension \\(r_1r_2\\ll d=r(m+n-r)\\). This is a deep apex, with smaller \\(S_1\\) than LoRA's \\(T_A\\) point.","gauge":"\\(GL_{r_1}\\times GL_{r_2}\\): \\((A,G,B)\\mapsto(Ag_1^{-1},\\,g_1Gg_2^\\top,\\,Bg_2^{-1})\\), the whole fibre when \\(r_1=r_2\\) [Num: fibre \\(2r^2\\)]. For \\(r_1\\ne r_2\\) the fibre is larger by \\(|r_1-r_2|\\,(n-\\max r_i)\\) (or the \\(m\\)-analogue): the factor on the longer side can move inside \\(\\ker G\\), a non-group part [Num: 16 vs 13 at \\((m,n,r_1,r_2)=(7,6,2,3)\\)].","covariance":"The image is LoRA's (linear layers) or a Tucker variety in the weight's own modes (conv), with no imposed reshape: \\(GL\\times GL\\).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"For linear layers FLoRA is a re-pointed LoRA. It has the same image \\(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\) and an A3 arrow into LoRA, but its core-zero base point has \\(S_1=A_0\\,\\mathbb R^{r_1\\times r_2}B_0^\\top\\), the generalised LoRA-XS of Prop II.3. Its first-order reach is strictly smaller than at LoRA's \\(T_A\\) point, so LoRA does not map into FLoRA (O3).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,G,B)=\\big(sAG,\\;B^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_2}\\) (\\(=\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) for \\(r_1=r_2=r\\)) pointed at \\((0,B_0^\\top)\\), stratum \\(T_A\\); the mirror \\((A,\\,sGB^\\top)\\) lands in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1}\\) at \\((A_0,0)\\), stratum \\(T_B\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O1","reason":"The trainable A, B leave the frozen SVD frames. Only the sub-object with A, B frozen is a (random-frame) generalised LoRA-XS."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lorta","name":"LoRTA","full_name":"Low Rank Tensor Adaptation","paper_title":"LoRTA: Low Rank Tensor Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Hounie et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2410.04060","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04060","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\Delta\\mathcal{W}[:,:,k,l,i]=A\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[k,:])\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:])\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_M[i,:])\\,B^{\\top},\\ \\text{i.e. } \\Delta\\mathcal{W}=[\\![A,B,C_H,C_L,C_M]\\!]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times\\frac{d}{H}\\times H\\times L\\times M},\\quad A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\ (\\text{input side}),\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d}{H}\\times r}\\ (\\text{head-output side}),\\ C_H\\in\\mathbb{R}^{H\\times r},\\ C_L\\in\\mathbb{R}^{L\\times r},\\ C_M\\in\\mathbb{R}^{4\\times r};\\ \\text{each slice is the transpose of a } \\frac{d}{H}\\times d \\text{ per-head block of } \\Delta W","trainable_params":"\\left(d+\\tfrac{d}{H}+H+L+M\\right)r\\ \\text{(whole model; } M=4 \\text{ matrix types)}","locus":"Q, K, V and output projections of every attention head in every layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Rank-\\(r\\) CP (Candecomp/Parafac) model of the 5th-order tensor of all attention updates indexed by (input dim, head dim, head, layer, matrix type).","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each slice of the CP tensor is a dense per-head update that can be added to its weight.","identity_at_init":"Not detailed in the sections consulted.","key_idea":"Share one CP model over heads, layers and Q/K/V/O so that parameters grow additively, not multiplicatively, in these dimensions.","results_note":"Reports performance comparable to LoRA with far fewer parameters on NLU, instruction tuning, preference optimization and protein-folding benchmarks.","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"LoRTA Sec. 3 reads VeRA's frozen shared bases with per-layer diagonal scalings as a sum of CP models with frozen factors"},{"target":"lotr","type":"competes-with","note":"CP across layers/heads vs Tucker-2 across layers"},{"target":"metatt","type":"competes-with","note":"MetaTT uses a tensor train over the same index set"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.04060","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2410.04060"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Ignacio Hounie","Charilaos Kanatsoulis","Arnuv Tandon","Alejandro Ribeiro"],"bibkey":"hounie2024lorta","bibtex":"@article{hounie2024lorta,\n  title = {{LoRTA: Low Rank Tensor Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Ignacio Hounie and Charilaos Kanatsoulis and Arnuv Tandon and Alejandro Ribeiro},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2410.04060},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.04060},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04060}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A rank-\\(r\\) CP model of the 5-way tensor (input, head-dim, head, layer, type), multilinear in its five factors. Each box update is \\(\\mathcal B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:])\\,A^\\top\\) with \\(\\mathcal B=[B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[k,:])]_k\\).","base_point":"The init is not stated; any zero-factor pointing is an apex by Lemma II.4.","gauge":"The CP scaling torus: per component, rescalings of the five factor columns with product 1 (plus permutations of components). This is why parameters grow additively in the modes.","covariance":"\\(B\\) is shared across heads and \\(A\\) across all boxes, so the per-box image carries a head-block structure (\\(\\Pi\\)) and couples layers and types.","rebasing":"CP rank-one tensors span everything: \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRTA is the CP (diagonal-core) corner of the tensor-network family over (input, head, layer, type, output). Diagonal cores commute, so it maps by A3 into every Tucker-2 or TT method over the same modes (LoTR, FacT-TT, MetaTT-5D) and into per-box \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\mathcal B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:]),\\;A^\\top\\big)_{(l,i)},\\ \\ \\mathcal B=\\big[B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[1,:]);\\dots;B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[H,:])\\big]","note":"Per-box \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); with a zero output factor \\(B_0=0\\) this is stratum \\(T_A\\)."},{"target":"lotr","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(A_{\\rm LoTR}=A,\\;B_{\\rm LoTR}=\\mathcal B,\\;G_{(l,i)}^\\top=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:])\\big)","note":"LoTR with one stack per matrix type and the same frames for every type; diagonal cores."},{"target":"metatt","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(G_1=A,\\;G_2[l]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]),\\;G_3[i]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_M[i,:]),\\;G_H[k]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[k,:]),\\;G_4=B^\\top\\big)","note":"MetaTT-5D (head mode) with all TT ranks r. Diagonal cores commute, so the mode order does not matter. The catalog records this pair only as 'competes-with', a literature relation; expressively LoRTA is a sub-object of MetaTT-5D."},{"target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(U=\\mathcal B,\\;V=A,\\;\\Sigma_{(l,i)}=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:])\\big)","note":"FacT-TT restricted to the attention blocks (the other blocks frozen at \\(\\Sigma=0\\), an A2 sub-object of FacT)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"VeRA is read as a CP model with frozen factors (LoRTA Sec. 3), so arrows go from frozen-frame CP into LoRTA. LoRTA trains its frames and leaves VeRA's frozen random row and column spaces."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"metatt","name":"MetaTT","full_name":"Global Tensor-Train Adapter","paper_title":"MetaTT: A Global Tensor-Train Adapter for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Lopez-Piqueres et al.","year":2025,"venue":"TMLR (2026)","arxiv":"2506.09105","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09105","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = xW_{0,l,m} + \\alpha\\,x\\,G_1\\,G_2[l]\\,G_3[m]\\,G_4\\ \\ (\\text{row-vector convention}),\\quad G_1\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times r},\\ G_2[l],G_3[m]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r},\\ G_4\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{out}}\\ \\ (\\text{MetaTT-4D})","trainable_params":"\\text{4D: }(d_{in}+d_{out})r+(L+M)r^{2}\\ (\\le 2Dr+(L+M)r^2,\\ D=\\max(d_{in},d_{out}));\\quad \\text{5D: }\\left(D+\\tfrac{D}{H}\\right)r+(L+M+H)r^{2}","locus":"attention (and optionally MLP) projections of all layers; layer index \\(l\\) and matrix type \\(m\\) (and head \\(h\\), task \\(t\\)) are TT modes","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A single tensor train over (input, layer, matrix type, [head], [task], output) whose slices are the per-matrix updates; a DMRG-style sweep can adapt TT ranks during training.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Middle cores can be contracted into \\(G_1\\) or \\(G_4\\) to give a dense per-matrix update.","identity_at_init":"\\(G_1\\) (input core) is zero-initialized, so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\). RoBERTa runs set the remaining cores to identity slices; Llama runs draw the two middle cores from \\(N(0, 0.2^2)\\) and set the output core to identity (Appendix G, Fig. 13).","key_idea":"One global adapter for the whole network: factorize the update tensor across depth and matrix type with a tensor train, borrowing DMRG rank adaptation from many-body physics.","results_note":"Reports substantial parameter reductions at competitive accuracy versus LoRA-style baselines, and competitive multi-task results with a task mode.","relations":[{"target":"lotr","type":"competes-with","note":"MetaTT-4D is a LoTR-type Tucker-2 update whose per-matrix core is factorized as G_2[l]G_3[m] and whose outer factors are shared across matrix types; neither class contains the other in general. LoTR is a baseline in MetaTT's tables"},{"target":"lorta","type":"competes-with","note":"TT vs CP over the same layer/head/matrix modes"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.09105","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2506.09105","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=MetaTT%20Global%20Tensor-Train%20Adapter"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Javier Lopez-Piqueres","Pranav Deshpande","Archan Ray","Mattia J. Villani","Marco Pistoia","Niraj Kumar"],"bibkey":"lopezpiqueres2025metatt","bibtex":"@article{lopezpiqueres2025metatt,\n  title = {{MetaTT: A Global Tensor-Train Adapter for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Javier Lopez-Piqueres and Pranav Deshpande and Archan Ray and Mattia J. Villani and Marco Pistoia and Niraj Kumar},\n  year = {2025},\n  journal = {TMLR (2026)},\n  eprint = {2506.09105},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09105}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"A TT over (input, layer, type, output): \\(\\Delta W_{l,m}=(G_1G_2[l]G_3[m]G_4)^\\top\\) is multilinear in the cores, with per-box rank \\(\\le r\\).","base_point":"\\(G_1=0\\) (zero input core): only \\(G_1\\) moves to first order, \\(\\dim S_1=d_{in}r\\), against a much larger \\(d(M)\\).","gauge":"TT bond gauges \\(GL_r^3\\) on the three bonds.","covariance":"MetaTT-4D: per box \\(GL\\times GL\\), with \\(G_1\\) shared by all boxes and \\(G_4\\) by all types, so changes of basis must agree across every tied box. The 5D head variant adds a head-block structure (\\(\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MetaTT-4D is the TT corner of the cross-layer family. Factorising LoTR's per-box core as \\(G_2[l]G_3[m]\\) makes it a sub-object, up to an A3 factorisation, of LoTR applied per type and of per-box LoRA, while LoRTA's CP model sits inside the 5D variant.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lotr","rule":"A3","h":"h(G_1,\\{G_2[l]\\},\\{G_3[m]\\},G_4)=\\big(A=G_1,\\;B=G_4^\\top,\\;G_{(l,m)}=G_2[l]G_3[m]\\big)\\ \\text{per type }m","note":"LoTR applied to every adapted type, one stack per type, all with the same frames. MetaTT's \\(G_1=0\\) pointing lands on LoTR pointed at \\(A=0\\) (not LoTR's G = 0 default). This contradicts the catalog's 'neither contains the other' when both adapt the same matrices."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(G_4^\\top G_3[m]^\\top G_2[l]^\\top,\\;G_1^\\top\\big)_{(l,m)}","note":"Per-box \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((B_0\\ne0,A_0=0)\\), stratum \\(T_B\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lorta","test":"O2","reason":"MetaTT's \\(r\\times r\\) cores are general and LoRTA's diagonal, so \\(d(\\mathrm{MetaTT})-d(\\mathrm{LoRTA})\\approx(L+M)(r^2-r)>0\\). Only LoRTA to MetaTT-5D exists."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"quanta","name":"QuanTA","full_name":"Quantum-informed Tensor Adaptation","paper_title":"QuanTA: Efficient High-Rank Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Quantum-Informed Tensor Adaptation","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.00132","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.00132","code_url":"https://github.com/quanta-fine-tuning/quanta","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"y = W_0x + \\mathcal{T}_{\\theta}x - \\mathcal{S}x,\\quad \\mathcal{T}_{\\theta}=\\prod_{\\alpha}T^{(\\alpha)},\\ T^{(\\alpha)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{m}d_{n}\\times d_{m}d_{n}}\\ \\text{acting on axes } (m(\\alpha),n(\\alpha)) \\text{ of } x \\text{ reshaped to } d_1\\times\\cdots\\times d_N,\\ \\textstyle\\prod_n d_n=d;\\ \\mathcal{S}=\\mathcal{T}_{\\theta_0}\\ \\text{frozen}","trainable_params":"\\sum_{\\alpha}\\big(d_{m(\\alpha)}d_{n(\\alpha)}\\big)^{2}","locus":"linear layers of LLMs (attention projections)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Circuit-shaped tensor network: the hidden vector is viewed as an \\(N\\)-qudit state and a sequence of two-axis 'gates' is applied (an einsum of small tensors); by the universality and rank-representation theorems it can express full-rank updates.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The gate sequence is linear, so the full matrix \\(T\\) can be contracted and added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"A frozen copy \\(S\\) of the initial gates is subtracted (\\(T_\\theta x - S x = 0\\) at step 0, Eq. 8); because \\(S\\) is fixed it can be folded into the base weight, \\(W_0' = W_0 - S\\) (the paper's Eq. 9 prints \\(W_0 + S\\), a sign slip relative to Eq. 8).","key_idea":"Borrow quantum-circuit structure (local two-qudit gates composed in sequence) to get high-rank updates with few parameters and without zero-initialized factors.","results_note":"Improves over LoRA and DoRA on commonsense and arithmetic reasoning with LLaMA-family models while using fewer trainable parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"high-rank alternative at lower parameter count"},{"target":"loretta","type":"competes-with","note":"different tensor-network topology (circuit vs train)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.00132","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2406.00132"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Zhuo Chen","Rumen Dangovski","Charlotte Loh","Owen Dugan","Di Luo","Marin Soljačić"],"bibkey":"chen2024quanta","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2024quanta,\n  title = {{QuanTA: Efficient High-Rank Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Quantum-Informed Tensor Adaptation}},\n  author = {Zhuo Chen and Rumen Dangovski and Charlotte Loh and Owen Dugan and Di Luo and Marin Soljačić},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.00132},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.00132}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone*","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho=(W_0-S)+T_\\theta\\) with \\(T_\\theta=\\prod_\\alpha T^{(\\alpha)}\\) multilinear in the two-axis gates and \\(S=T_{\\theta_0}\\) a generic point of the circuit cone: a cone translated to the apex \\(W_0-S\\neq\\theta_0\\) (Type II, like PiSSA).","base_point":"Random gates are a generic point, so rank \\(d\\rho_{q_0}\\) equals the generic rank (Jacobian check: 37 = 37 for three gates on \\(2\\times2\\times2\\); also 37 at identity gates).","gauge":"\\(GL(d_n)\\) slides on every internal axis segment between consecutive gates that share an axis (fibre \\(11=3\\cdot 4-1\\) in the check): a product of GL on bonds.","covariance":"The reshape \\(\\mathbb R^d\\cong\\bigotimes_n\\mathbb R^{d_n}\\) and the gate layout are imposed structure \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\).","merge":"The contracted \\(T_\\theta-S\\) is a dense matrix added to \\(W_0\\) (fold \\(W_0-S\\), not the paper's printed \\(W_0+S\\)).","rebasing":"The span of a connected two-axis circuit contains every \\(a\\otimes b\\otimes c\\), hence \\(\\Theta\\); re-pointing each cycle adds \\(C-C\\)."},"categorical_reading":"QuanTA is a split (Type II) pointing of a tensor-network cone: \\(\\rho=(W_0-T_{\\theta_0})+T_\\theta\\), so \\(\\theta_0\\) is a smooth point of a translated circuit variety whose apex \\(W_0-T_{\\theta_0}\\) lies elsewhere. Its high rank is a cut statement about the circuit (Thm II.10), bought with an imposed reshape \\(\\Pi_\\otimes\\) of the hidden wire.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A connected gate circuit reaches full rank (its min-cut avoids an r-bottleneck), while the maximum rank of LoRA_r is r."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone*"},{"id":"mora","name":"MoRA","full_name":"High-rank updating with a square matrix","paper_title":"MoRA: High-Rank Updating for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Jiang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2405.12130","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12130","code_url":"https://github.com/kongds/MoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"h = W_0x + f_{decomp}\\big(M\\,f_{comp}(x)\\big),\\quad M\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\hat r\\times\\hat r},\\ \\hat r=\\big\\lfloor\\sqrt{(d_{in}+d_{out})\\,r}\\big\\rfloor;\\ \\text{reshape variant: }\\Delta W=I_{d/\\hat r}\\otimes M=\\mathrm{diag}(M,\\dots,M);\\ \\text{RoPE variant: }\\Delta W=\\mathrm{diag}(P^{1},\\dots,P^{n}),\\ P^{i}=M\\ \\text{rotated by block rotation } R_{\\theta,i}","trainable_params":"\\hat r^{2}\\approx r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"all linear layers of LLMs","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"A single trainable square matrix sandwiched between fixed, non-parameterized compression/decompression operators (truncation, row/column sharing, reshaping, RoPE-style rotations); the reshape variant is a Kronecker product with the identity, i.e. a block-diagonal matrix with tied blocks of rank up to \\(\\hat r\\) each.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The fixed operators are linear, so \\(\\Delta W\\) can be formed explicitly and merged like LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(M = 0\\) (a single matrix can be zero-initialized without stalling gradients).","key_idea":"Spend the LoRA parameter budget on one square matrix to maximize the rank of \\(\\Delta W\\), which matters for knowledge-memorizing tasks.","results_note":"Outperforms LoRA on memory-intensive tasks (UUID memorization, continual pretraining) and is comparable on instruction tuning and math reasoning.","relations":[{"target":"krona","type":"special-case-of","note":"reshape variant only: freezing KronA's left factor to I gives ΔW = I ⊗ M; the truncation/sharing/rotation variants are not Kronecker products"},{"target":"melora","type":"competes-with","note":"block-diagonal with tied full-rank blocks vs untied low-rank blocks"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"same parameter budget, much higher rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.12130","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2405.12130"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Ting Jiang","Shaohan Huang","Shengyue Luo","Zihan Zhang","Haizhen Huang","Furu Wei","Weiwei Deng","Feng Sun","Qi Zhang","Deqing Wang","Fuzhen Zhuang"],"bibkey":"jiang2024mora","bibtex":"@article{jiang2024mora,\n  title = {{MoRA: High-Rank Updating for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Ting Jiang and Shaohan Huang and Shengyue Luo and Zihan Zhang and Haizhen Huang and Furu Wei and Weiwei Deng and Feng Sun and Qi Zhang and Deqing Wang and Fuzhen Zhuang},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2405.12130},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.12130},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.12130}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=I_{d/\\hat r}\\otimes M\\) (reshape) or a block diagonal of rotated copies of \\(M\\) (RoPE variant): linear and injective in \\(M\\), dimension \\(\\hat r^2\\); agrees with VIII.B.","covariance":"The fixed compression/decompression operators (block tying, truncation, sharing, RoPE rotations) are imposed structure (VIII.B: \\(\\Pi\\), blocks).","rebasing":"With fixed operators the shape is a subspace, so cycles gain nothing (Thm V.3 cor. 2); ReMoRA's alternating operators would gain."},"categorical_reading":"MoRA is a linear method: the trainable square \\(M\\) is pushed into weight space by fixed linear operators, e.g. \\(\\Delta W=I_{d/\\hat r}\\otimes M\\), a subspace of dimension \\(\\hat r^2\\) in a transported frame. Its 'high rank' is a cut property of that frame (rank up to \\(d\\)), not an increase of \\(d(M)\\) beyond \\(|M|\\).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"krona","rule":"A2","h":"M\\mapsto(A_k,B_k)=(I_{d/\\hat r},\\ M/s)","note":"Reshape variant only; KronA re-pointed at (I, 0) in its zero-factor stratum (its default pointing has a Kaiming-random non-zero factor)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The rank of I (x) M reaches (d/r_hat) r_hat = d > r; conversely LoRA does not map to MoRA (O1), since a generic rank-one update is not block-tied."},{"target":"melora","test":"O2","reason":"MoRA's maximum rank (up to d) exceeds MELoRA_r's maximum rank r."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"melora","name":"MELoRA","full_name":"Mini-Ensemble Low-Rank Adapters","paper_title":"MELoRA: Mini-Ensemble Low-Rank Adapters for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Ren et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.17263","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17263","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\mathrm{diag}(B_1A_1,\\dots,B_nA_n)=W_0+\\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}B_iA_i,\\quad A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{r}{n}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{n}},\\ B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{n}\\times\\frac{r}{n}};\\ W_0\\ \\text{frozen},\\ A_i,B_i\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"\\tfrac{r}{n}(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"attention/MLP linear layers (as LoRA)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Block-diagonal direct sum of \\(n\\) independent mini-LoRAs acting on disjoint feature slices; rank is the sum of block ranks, so rank \\(r\\) is reached with \\(n\\) times fewer parameters than LoRA.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The block-diagonal \\(\\Delta W\\) is a dense matrix of \\(W_0\\)'s shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(A_i\\) Gaussian and \\(B_i = 0\\), as in LoRA.","key_idea":"Concatenating mini-LoRAs along the diagonal adds their ranks instead of sharing a bottleneck, trading cross-block interactions for rank per parameter.","results_note":"Compared with LoRA, better results with 8x fewer trainable parameters on natural language understanding and 36x fewer on instruction following.","relations":[{"target":"gralora","type":"special-case-of","note":"GraLoRA with all off-diagonal blocks removed"},{"target":"bd-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"BD-LoRA with both factors block-diagonal and aligned"},{"target":"more","type":"special-case-of","note":"MoRe's Monarch product with identity permutations"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"higher rank per parameter"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.17263","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2402.17263","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.168/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Pengjie Ren","Chengshun Shi","Shiguang Wu","Mengqi Zhang","Zhaochun Ren","Maarten de Rijke","Zhumin Chen","Jiahuan Pei"],"bibkey":"ren2024melora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ren2024melora,\n  title = {{MELoRA: Mini-Ensemble Low-Rank Adapters for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Pengjie Ren and Chengshun Shi and Shiguang Wu and Mengqi Zhang and Zhaochun Ren and Maarten de Rijke and Zhumin Chen and Jiahuan Pei},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.17263},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17263}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Product of \\(n\\) diagonal-block determinantal cones \\(\\bigoplus_i\\mathcal M_{\\le r/n}(\\tfrac{d_{out}}n\\times\\tfrac{d_{in}}n)\\); total rank \\(\\le r\\).","base_point":"\\(B_i=0\\) with Gaussian \\(A_i\\): every block is in the \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum, deficit \\(\\sum_i\\tfrac rn(\\tfrac{d_{in}}n-\\tfrac rn)\\).","gauge":"\\(\\prod_{i=1}^nGL_{r/n}\\subset GL_r\\).","covariance":"A fixed block partition of both sides (\\(\\Pi\\), blocks).","rebasing":"The span of the shape is the block-diagonal subspace, so \\(R_\\infty=W_0+\\{\\text{block-diagonal}\\}\\), reached after \\(\\lceil d/r\\rceil\\) cycles; not \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MELoRA is the sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) obtained by freezing the off-block entries of \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) at \\(0\\): \\(\\Delta W=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(B_i)\\,\\mathrm{blockdiag}(A_i)\\). It keeps rank \\(r\\) at \\(1/n\\) of the budget by giving up every update that is not block-diagonal, and its rebasing closure is the block-diagonal subspace, not \\(\\Theta\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"(B_i,A_i)_i\\mapsto\\big(\\mathrm{blockdiag}(B_1,\\dots,B_n),\\ \\mathrm{blockdiag}(A_1,\\dots,A_n)\\big)","note":"LoRA_r pointed at (0, blockdiag(A_{i,0})), a T_A point; off-block entries frozen at their base value 0."},{"target":"gralora","rule":"A2","h":"(B_i,A_i)_i\\mapsto\\big(B_{ii}=B_i,\\ A_{ii}=A_i^\\top;\\ (B_{ij},A_{ij})=(0,A_{ij,0})\\ (i\\neq j)\\big)","note":"GraLoRA with k = n blocks per side of rank r/k; off-diagonal blocks frozen at their base values, scales matched."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"mora","test":"O1","reason":"MoRA ties all diagonal blocks to one M, so a block-diagonal update with distinct blocks is not in Im MoRA."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"more","name":"MoRe","full_name":"Monarch Rectangular Fine-tuning","paper_title":"MoRe Fine-Tuning with 10x Fewer Parameters","authors":"Tan et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024 Workshops (ES-FoMo II, FM-Wild)","arxiv":"2408.17383","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.17383","code_url":"https://github.com/SprocketLab/sparse_matrix_fine_tuning","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + P_1\\,L\\,P_2\\,R,\\quad L=\\mathrm{diag}(L_1,\\dots,L_N),\\ R=\\mathrm{diag}(R_1,\\dots,R_N),\\ L_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\frac{d_{out}}{N}\\times r_{blk}},\\ R_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r_{blk}\\times\\frac{d_{in}}{N}},\\ P_1,P_2\\ \\text{fixed permutations};\\ L,R\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r_{blk}(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"attention/MLP linear layers","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Rectangular low-rank Monarch matrix: block-diagonal, permutation, block-diagonal, permutation; rank up to \\(N r_{blk}\\); \\(N = 1\\) recovers LoRA. MoRe's blocks are low-rank sub-blocks of the Monarch class of Dao et al. (2022), whose products (not MoRe's single low-rank factor) subsume butterfly matrices.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The Monarch product is a dense matrix that \\(W\\) absorbs before inference.","identity_at_init":"Not specified in the paper; the official code offers LoRA-style zero init and a zero-initialized output scaler, either of which gives \\(\\Delta W = 0\\) (which option the reported runs used was not confirmed).","key_idea":"Search over a hardware-efficient structured matrix family (Monarch) instead of fixing low rank, gaining rank per parameter with almost no hyperparameter tuning (\\(N = 4\\)).","results_note":"Reported to be more parameter-efficient and accurate than state-of-the-art PEFT methods on a range of tasks and models, using as little as 5% of LoRA's parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"N = 1 gives LoRA"},{"target":"melora","type":"generalizes","note":"identity permutations give MELoRA's block-diagonal update"},{"target":"boft","type":"competes-with","note":"BOFT uses orthogonal butterfly factors multiplicatively; MoRe uses non-orthogonal Monarch factors additively"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.17383","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2408.17383","https://api2.openreview.net/notes/search?term=MoRe%20Fine-Tuning%20with%2010x%20Fewer%20Parameters","https://github.com/SprocketLab/sparse_matrix_fine_tuning/blob/main/src/layers/monarch_linear.py"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Wenxuan Tan","Nicholas Roberts","Tzu-Heng Huang","Jitian Zhao","John Cooper","Samuel Guo","Chengyu Duan","Frederic Sala"],"bibkey":"tan2024more","bibtex":"@inproceedings{tan2024more,\n  title = {{MoRe Fine-Tuning with 10x Fewer Parameters}},\n  author = {Wenxuan Tan and Nicholas Roberts and Tzu-Heng Huang and Jitian Zhao and John Cooper and Samuel Guo and Chengyu Duan and Frederic Sala},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2408.17383},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.17383}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=P_1LP_2R\\), bilinear in the block-diagonal \\(L,R\\): a cone of rank \\(\\le Nr_{blk}\\) (\\(N=1\\) is LoRA).","base_point":"The official code's LoRA-style zero factor (or zero output scaler) puts it at an apex, as LoRA; the paper is silent.","gauge":"A slide \\(g\\) on the middle wire must lie in \\(\\bigoplus GL_{r_{blk}}\\cap P_2(\\bigoplus GL_{r_{blk}})P_2^{-1}\\), which the Monarch shuffle cuts to the diagonal torus (Jacobian rank \\(28=|M|-Nr_{blk}\\) at \\(d=8\\), \\(N=r_{blk}=2\\)); \\(N=1\\) restores \\(GL_{r_{blk}}\\).","covariance":"Block partitions and the fixed permutations \\(P_1,P_2\\) are imposed (\\(\\Pi\\)).","rebasing":"Span: all of \\(\\Theta\\) when the shuffle connects every block pair (\\(r_{blk}\\ge N\\)), otherwise a block-sparse subspace."},"categorical_reading":"MoRe is a sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Nr_{blk}}\\) in which \\(B=P_1LP_2\\) and \\(A=R\\) are forced into Monarch block patterns. The permutation \\(P_2\\) breaks LoRA's \\(GL\\) gauge down to a torus and caps the rank of each block of the update, as in GraLoRA (Prop II.12(c)).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"(L,R)\\mapsto(P_1LP_2,\\ R)","note":"LoRA_{N r_blk} pointed at (0, R_0) with R_0 block-diagonal (taking L as the zero factor); entries outside the Monarch patterns frozen at 0."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"melora","test":"O1","reason":"The shuffle P_2 couples off-diagonal blocks, which MELoRA's block-diagonal image never reaches (MELoRA is MoRe with P_1 = P_2 = I, a different object)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"dsee","name":"DSEE","full_name":"Dually Sparsity-Embedded Efficient Tuning","paper_title":"DSEE: Dually Sparsity-embedded Efficient Tuning of Pre-trained Language Models","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2023","arxiv":"2111.00160","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.00160","code_url":"https://github.com/VITA-Group/DSEE","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0\\odot\\mathcal{M} + UV + \\mathcal{P}_{\\Omega}(S),\\quad U\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ \\Omega=\\text{top-}c\\text{ entries of the sparse residual } S'\\text{ of a robust-PCA (GreBsmo) split } W_0\\approx AB+S',\\ \\mathcal{M}\\ \\text{optional pruning mask on the pretrained weights, chosen from } |\\Delta W|\\ (\\text{unstructured) or head scores (structured)};\\ U,V,S|_{\\Omega}\\ \\text{trained, then re-tuned after pruning}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})+c\\ \\ (c=16\\ \\text{in the paper})","locus":"attention weight matrices of BERT, RoBERTa and GPT-2","modification_kind":"hybrid","structure":"Low-rank plus sparse-residual update whose sparse support comes from a robust-PCA (GreBsmo) decomposition of \\(W_0\\), combined with unstructured or structured pruning of the resulting weights for inference efficiency.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(W_0 \\odot M + UV + P_\\Omega(S)\\) is a single matrix of \\(W_0\\)'s shape.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\Delta W = 0\\) at init: the text sets \\(V = 0\\) and \\(S = 0\\), while Algorithm 2 sets \\(U = 0\\), \\(V \\sim \\mathcal N(0, 0.02)\\), \\(S = 0\\) (the paper is internally inconsistent about which low-rank factor is zero).","key_idea":"Exploit sparsity twice: sparse-plus-low-rank structure in the update for parameter efficiency, and sparse final weights for inference efficiency.","results_note":"On BERT, saves about 25% inference FLOPs with 0.5% trainable parameters at comparable downstream performance.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"combines","note":"adds a sparse residual and weight pruning to a LoRA-style update"},{"target":"rosa","type":"competes-with","note":"both sparse-plus-low-rank updates"},{"target":"diff-pruning","type":"builds-on","note":"sparse update component"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.00160","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2111.00160","https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.acl-long.456"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Xuxi Chen","Tianlong Chen","Weizhu Chen","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Zhangyang Wang","Yu Cheng"],"bibkey":"chen2021dsee","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2021dsee,\n  title = {{DSEE: Dually Sparsity-embedded Efficient Tuning of Pre-trained Language Models}},\n  author = {Xuxi Chen and Tianlong Chen and Weizhu Chen and Ahmed Hassan Awadallah and Zhangyang Wang and Yu Cheng},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2111.00160},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.00160}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"The training phase is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{Sparse}_\\Omega\\) on the original box. The later pruning \\(W_0\\mapsto W_0\\odot\\mathcal M\\) is a non-function-preserving base change with defect \\(W_0\\odot\\mathcal M-W_0\\), after which the same method is re-tuned.","shape":"Minkowski sum of the determinantal cone and the coordinate subspace \\(L_\\Omega\\) (\\(c=16\\) entries): a non-linear cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"One LoRA factor is zero (the text and Algorithm 2 disagree which), so the LoRA summand sits at an apex; the sparse summand is linear and regular.","gauge":"\\(GL_r\\) on the LoRA wire; for generic \\(\\Omega\\), \\(T\\mathcal M_r\\cap L_\\Omega=0\\), so there is no extra slack.","covariance":"The support \\(\\Omega\\) (top-\\(c\\) entries of the robust-PCA residual of \\(W_0\\)) and the pruning mask are coordinatewise: exact under signed permutations; non-uniform scalings can change the top-\\(c\\) selection.","rebasing":"The LoRA summand alone reaches \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"DSEE is the \\(\\oplus\\)-word \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{Sparse}_\\Omega\\) (Obs I.7), like RoSA but with \\(\\Omega\\) read off a robust-PCA split of \\(W_0\\) instead of gradients. Its pruning stage is a base change \\(\\kappa(\\theta)=\\theta\\odot\\mathcal M\\) that moves the base point (Def V.1(b)).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V,S)\\mapsto\\big([\\,U\\mid E_I\\,\\mathrm{diag}(S_\\Omega)\\,],\\ [\\,V;\\ E_J^\\top\\,]\\big),\\quad E_I=[e_{i_1}\\cdots e_{i_c}],\\ E_J=[e_{j_1}\\cdots e_{j_c}]","note":"Into LoRA_{r+c} pointed at (0, [V_0; E_J^T]) (Algorithm-2 convention U_0 = 0): the sparse part is a sum of c coordinate rank-ones. LoRA's scale s is absorbed into the factors. [V_0; E_J^T] has full row rank only when the column indices of Omega are distinct; otherwise the pointing lies outside the three strata."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Against \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) at equal rank: the maximum rank \\(r+c\\) exceeds \\(r\\). (The arrow above lands in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+c}\\).)"},{"target":"rosa","test":"O1","reason":"Generically, at comparable LoRA rank, Im DSEE is not contained in Im RoSA unless Omega lies inside RoSA's gradient mask (DSEE's support comes from robust PCA of W_0). The obstruction needs that rank condition: if RoSA's rank exceeds r by at least |Omega minus mask|, each off-mask entry is absorbed as a rank-one term and the inclusion holds."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sine-lora","name":"Sine-LoRA","full_name":"Sine-activated low-rank adaptation","paper_title":"Efficient Learning With Sine-Activated Low-rank Matrices","authors":"Ji et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2403.19243","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19243","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\tfrac{1}{g}\\sin(\\omega\\,BA),\\quad \\sin\\ \\text{entrywise},\\ \\omega\\ \\text{(frequency)},\\ g\\ \\text{(gain) fixed},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"r(d_{in}+d_{out})","locus":"LoRA/DoRA target layers in LLMs and ViTs (also NeRF and 3D-shape networks)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"Entrywise sinusoid of a rank-\\(r\\) matrix; the rank of \\(\\sin(\\omega\\,BA)\\) grows with \\(\\omega\\) (the paper gives a lower bound in terms of \\(\\omega\\)), so the update is no longer low-rank.","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(\\sin(\\omega BA)/g\\) is a dense matrix computable after training and addable to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"With LoRA's \\(B = 0\\) initialization, \\(\\sin(0) = 0\\) so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\).","key_idea":"A fixed high-frequency entrywise nonlinearity raises the rank of a low-rank parameterization for free.","results_note":"Improves LoRA and DoRA at equal parameter count across ViT, LLM (e.g. LLaMA-3-8B commonsense reasoning, GLUE), NeRF and 3D-shape tasks.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"drop-in modification of LoRA's product"},{"target":"loha","type":"competes-with","note":"both raise rank with entrywise operations on low-rank matrices"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.19243","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2403.19243"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"authors_full":["Yiping Ji","Hemanth Saratchandran","Cameron Gordon","Zeyu Zhang","Simon Lucey"],"bibkey":"ji2024efficient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ji2024efficient,\n  title = {{Efficient Learning With Sine-Activated Low-rank Matrices}},\n  author = {Yiping Ji and Hemanth Saratchandran and Cameron Gordon and Zeyu Zhang and Simon Lucey},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2403.19243},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.19243}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"MonxMon","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\rho=W_0+\\varphi(BA)\\) with \\(\\varphi(X)=\\sin(\\omega X)/g\\) entrywise, a local diffeomorphism of \\(\\Theta\\) at \\(0\\); the germ at \\(\\theta_0\\) is \\(\\varphi\\) of the determinantal cone (vertex \\(\\theta_0\\)). Globally the image is not a cone and exceeds rank \\(r\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) and \\(d\\varphi_0=(\\omega/g)\\,\\mathrm{id}\\), so \\(S_1=\\{XA_0\\}\\) with deficit \\(r(n-r)\\), exactly as LoRA.","gauge":"\\(\\varphi\\) sees only \\(BA\\), so the gauge is LoRA's \\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"Entrywise sine commutes with signed permutations on both sides (\\(\\sin\\) is odd) but not with scalings: only \\(B_m\\times B_n\\subset\\mathrm{Mon}\\times\\mathrm{Mon}\\).","rebasing":"Each stage is confined to the box \\(\\|\\Delta W\\|_{\\max}\\le1/g\\); the generated monoid is still \\(\\Theta\\) (it contains all small multiples of each \\(E_{ij}\\))."},"categorical_reading":"Sine-LoRA post-composes LoRA's update with the entrywise diffeomorphism germ \\(\\varphi=\\sin(\\omega\\,\\cdot)/g\\), an automorphism of the slice like transport (Prop II.13) but non-linear. It keeps LoRA's apex, gauge and first-order image at \\(\\theta_0\\), while globally it trades the rank bound for the box \\(|\\Delta W_{ij}|\\le1/g\\) and loses every covariance except signed permutations.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"The rank of sin(omega BA)/g grows with omega beyond r; conversely LoRA does not map to Sine-LoRA (O1), since Sine-LoRA never leaves |Delta W_ij| <= 1/g."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"nola","name":"NOLA","full_name":"Compressing LoRA using Linear Combination of Random Basis","paper_title":"NOLA: Compressing LoRA using Linear Combination of Random Basis","authors":"Koohpayegani et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.02556","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=TjfXcDgvzk","code_url":"https://github.com/UCDvision/NOLA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\Big(\\sum_{j=1}^{l}\\beta_j B_j\\Big)\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\alpha_i A_i\\Big),\\quad B_j\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r},\\ A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen pseudo-random (generated from a seed, optionally shared across layers)};\\ \\alpha\\in\\mathbb{R}^{k},\\ \\beta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{l}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"k + l\\ \\ (\\text{independent of } r,\\ d_{in},\\ d_{out})","locus":"any linear layer (paper: attention projections of GPT-2, LLaMA-2, ViT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA factors constrained to the linear span of \\(k\\) (resp. \\(l\\)) frozen random basis matrices; \\(\\Delta W\\) is bilinear in the two coefficient vectors and has rank \\(\\le r\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training the two linear combinations are materialised, and their product \\(BA\\) is added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Not specified in the paper. Official code (UCDvision/NOLA, gpt/examples/NLG/src/model_nola.py) zero-initialises both coefficient vectors, and each NOLA factor also carries a frozen offset matrix (LoRA-style reset: Kaiming for \\(A\\), zero for \\(B\\)), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\) at init with non-zero gradients for the \\(B\\)-side coefficients; with the paper's pure-span formula, zero-initialising both \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) would give zero gradients. Bases are \\(U(-1,1)\\) from a stored seed and are regenerated on the fly in forward/backward to save memory","key_idea":"Decouple the parameter count from rank and width (LoRA's rank-1 floor) by expressing each LoRA factor as a learned mixture of seeded random basis matrices, PRANC-style; only the seed and the mixture coefficients (optionally 4-bit quantised) are stored.","results_note":"On LLaMA-2 70B NOLA is about 20x more compact than the most compressed (rank-1) LoRA without accuracy loss; also matches LoRA on GPT-2 E2E and ViT few-shot classification with fewer parameters.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"reparameterises LoRA's A and B"},{"target":"intrinsic-said","type":"builds-on","note":"motivated by the low-intrinsic-dimension literature it cites (Li et al. 2018; Aghajanyan et al. 2021); a random linear subspace of the LoRA factor space rather than of the full parameter space; its direct ancestor is PRANC (see papers)"},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"our observation: (alpha, beta) -> (A, B) is linear, i.e. theta_D = P theta_d with a block-diagonal dense random P"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"contemporaneous random-basis compression of LoRA"},{"target":"randlora","type":"competes-with","note":"RandLoRA compares against NOLA as a random-basis baseline"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02556","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.02556","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UCDvision/NOLA/main/README.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UCDvision/NOLA/main/gpt/examples/NLG/src/model_nola.py"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Soroush Abbasi Koohpayegani","KL Navaneet","Parsa Nooralinejad","Soheil Kolouri","Hamed Pirsiavash"],"bibkey":"koohpayegani2023nola","bibtex":"@inproceedings{koohpayegani2023nola,\n  title = {{NOLA: Compressing LoRA using Linear Combination of Random Basis}},\n  author = {Soroush Abbasi Koohpayegani and KL Navaneet and Parsa Nooralinejad and Soheil Kolouri and Hamed Pirsiavash},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.02556},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=TjfXcDgvzk}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Bilinear cone \\(\\{(\\sum \\beta_j B_j)(\\sum \\alpha_i A_i)\\}\\), \\(d=k+l-1\\) (paper form, checked); rank \\(\\le r\\).","base_point":"Paper's pure span at \\(\\alpha=\\beta=0\\): \\(S_1=0\\), apex with full deficit. Official code adds a frozen offset \\(A_{off}\\) (\\(B_{off}=0\\)): \\(\\dim S_1=l\\), still apex.","gauge":"Paper form: one scalar slide \\((c \\beta, \\alpha/c)\\) (table \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\)). The code's offset kills it: \\(d=k+l\\) (checked).","covariance":"Frozen seeded random bases: in law.","rebasing":"\\(\\to \\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\{B_jA_i\\}\\) (table); with the code offset also \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{B_j A_{off}\\}\\)."},"categorical_reading":"NOLA is LoRA precomposed with the linear map \\((\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto(\\sum\\beta_jB_j,\\sum\\alpha_iA_i)\\): a bilinear cone of dimension \\(k+l-1\\) with one scalar slide, an A3 arrow into LoRA\\(_r\\), and rebasing closure \\(\\mathrm{span}\\{B_jA_i\\}\\). The paper's pure-span zero init has \\(S_1=0\\); the official code's frozen offset \\(A_{\\rm off}\\) restores \\(\\dim S_1=l\\) and removes the scalar gauge (\\(d=k+l\\)).","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\Big(\\sum_j\\beta_jB_j,\\ \\sum_i\\alpha_iA_i\\Big)","note":"axes.json A3 example; pointed at the origin (0,0) (paper) or, with h(alpha,beta)=(sum beta_j B_j, A_off+sum alpha_i A_i), at (0,A_off) in stratum T_A (code)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"NOLA's row space moves inside span{A_i} while VeRA's is fixed at row A; with dense random bases neither image contains the other."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"intrinsic-said","name":"Intrinsic SAID","full_name":"Structure-Aware Intrinsic Dimension subspace fine-tuning","paper_title":"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning","authors":"Aghajanyan et al.","year":2020,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2012.13255","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\theta^{D}_{i} = \\theta^{D}_{0,i} + \\lambda_i\\,\\big(\\theta^{d-m}M\\big)_i,\\quad M = H\\,G\\,\\Pi\\,H\\,B\\ \\ (\\text{Fastfood transform: Hadamard } H,\\ \\text{Gaussian diagonal } G,\\ \\text{permutation } \\Pi,\\ \\pm1 \\text{ diagonal } B;\\ \\text{frozen});\\ \\theta^{d-m}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d-m},\\ \\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}\\ (\\text{one scale per layer})\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"d\\ \\text{for the whole model, including the classification head in the paper's count (e.g. } d_{90}\\approx 207 \\text{ for RoBERTa-Large on MRPC)}","locus":"the entire flattened parameter vector of the model (all layers), with layer-wise scaling","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"random linear subspace of the full parameter space: \\(\\Delta\\theta = \\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda)\\,P\\,\\theta_d\\) with a structured random (Fastfood) projection \\(P\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The projected update is a fixed parameter offset that can be added to \\(\\theta_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(\\theta^{d} = 0\\) recovers the pretrained parameters exactly","key_idea":"Fine-tuning works in a tiny random subspace: optimise a \\(d\\)-dimensional vector projected into the full parameter space; the small \\(d\\) needed (intrinsic dimension) explains why huge models fine-tune well on little data. SAID adds per-layer scales.","results_note":"Optimising about 200 parameters (\\(d_{90} = 207\\) with SAID) projected into RoBERTa-Large's full space reaches 90% of full fine-tuning performance on MRPC (about 800 for QQP); larger and longer-pretrained models have lower intrinsic dimension.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"alternative low-dimensional fine-tuning: a random linear subspace of the full parameter space versus LoRA's structured low-rank factor space; LoRA cites this intrinsic-dimension finding as motivation"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255","https://arxiv.org/html/2012.13255","https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.568/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Armen Aghajanyan","Luke Zettlemoyer","Sonal Gupta"],"bibkey":"aghajanyan2020intrinsic","bibtex":"@inproceedings{aghajanyan2020intrinsic,\n  title = {{Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Armen Aghajanyan and Luke Zettlemoyer and Sonal Gupta},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2012.13255},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"Additive whole-model reparametrisation \\(\\theta=\\theta_0+\\Lambda(\\lambda)P\\theta'\\) with a frozen Fastfood projection \\(P=HG\\Pi HB\\); the paper is an intrinsic-dimension analysis and SAID is its measuring instrument, so entry_type is analysis with SAID's own coordinates.","shape":"Bilinear in (layer scales \\(\\lambda\\), projected vector \\(\\theta'\\)), so \\(\\rho-\\theta_0\\) is multihomogeneous and the image is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\); plain DID (\\(\\lambda\\) frozen) would be Lin.","base_point":"Pointed at \\(\\theta'=0\\) with nonzero layer scales (the catalog only records \\(\\theta^{d-m}=0\\); \\(\\lambda_0=0\\) as well would make every gradient vanish by Lemma II.4). Then \\(S_1=\\mathrm{Im}\\,P\\) has dimension \\(d-m\\) against \\(d(M)=d-1\\): deficit \\(m-1>0\\), an apex as for VeRA.","gauge":"One slide \\((\\lambda,\\theta')\\mapsto(c\\lambda,\\theta'/c)\\); generic fibre of dimension 1.","covariance":"Covariance holds only in law, but the structured part is deterministic: in \\(P=HG\\Pi HB\\) the random \\(B,\\Pi,G\\) randomise the inner side, while the outer Walsh-Hadamard \\(H\\) acts on \\(\\Theta\\). The law of \\(\\mathrm{Im}\\,P\\) is invariant exactly under \\(H\\,B_D\\,H^{-1}\\) (\\(B_D\\) the signed permutations), which contains a coordinate or neuron permutation of \\(\\Theta\\) only when it is a Hadamard automorphism (a dyadic shift). So the flattening order and the dyadic Hadamard structure are imposed (\\(\\Pi\\)), unlike the dense Gaussian projection Fastfood emulates (which would be \\(O(D)\\)-covariant in law, 'frame'). The frame is also shared by all boxes, so Obs II.15's per-box hypothesis fails.","rebasing":"With \\(P\\) frozen, \\(R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\,C=\\theta_0+\\bigoplus_j\\mathrm{Im}\\,P_j\\) (per-layer row blocks of \\(P\\)), strictly larger than DID's single subspace: the SAID analogue of \\(\\mathrm{VeRA}^\\infty=\\) LoRA-FA."},"categorical_reading":"Intrinsic SAID is the whole-model additive object \\(\\rho(\\lambda,\\theta')=\\theta_0+\\Lambda(\\lambda)P\\theta'\\): freezing \\(\\lambda\\) gives the linear DID sub-object (A2), and the layer scales thicken that random subspace into a cone with an apex at \\(\\theta_0\\), exactly as VeRA's \\(\\Lambda_b\\) thickens LoRA-FA.","entry_type":"analysis","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A generic SAID update restricted to one weight matrix is a dense random vector \\(P_j\\theta'\\) reshaped, of full rank, so its maximum rank exceeds r."},{"target":"uni-lora","test":"O2","reason":"Both are a single global random projection, but Uni-LoRA projects into LoRA factor space (every matrix update has rank at most r) while SAID's per-matrix updates are generically full rank."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"tied-lora","name":"Tied-LoRA","full_name":"Tied Low-Rank Adaptation (weight tying + selective training)","paper_title":"Tied-LoRA: Enhancing parameter efficiency of LoRA with Weight Tying","authors":"Renduchintala et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NAACL 2024","arxiv":"2311.09578","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.481/","code_url":"https://github.com/NVIDIA/NeMo","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_\\ell = W_{0,\\ell} + \\Lambda_{v_\\ell}\\, B\\, \\Lambda_{u_\\ell}\\, A,\\quad A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}},\\ B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{tied (one copy shared by all layers)};\\ \\Lambda_{u_\\ell}=\\mathrm{diag}(u_\\ell),\\ u_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r},\\ \\Lambda_{v_\\ell}=\\mathrm{diag}(v_\\ell),\\ v_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}}\\ \\text{per layer};\\ \\text{each of } A,B,u,v \\text{ is selectively trained or frozen (configs TL1--TL6; TL6 trains all)}","trainable_params":"\\text{TL6: } r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{once for all layers} + L\\,(r + d_{out})\\ \\text{in total; per adapted matrix } \\approx r + d_{out} + \\tfrac{r(d_{in}+d_{out})}{L}","locus":"paper: the fused QKV projection (\\(d\\times 3d\\)) of every transformer layer; applicable to any linear layer","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"a single low-rank pair \\(BA\\) shared across depth, modulated per layer by diagonal scalings of its rows and rank channels; selective freezing yields a lattice of sub-configurations","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The low-rank path is linear, so each layer's \\(\\Lambda_v B \\Lambda_u A\\) can be merged into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Per Table 1: TL2/TL6 (VeRA-like): \\(A, B \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\), \\(u = 1\\), \\(v = 0\\) (exact identity); TL3–TL5 (LoRA-like): \\(A \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\), \\(B = 0\\), \\(u = v = 1\\) (exact identity); TL1 (only tied \\(A\\) trained): \\(A, B \\sim \\mathcal{N}\\), \\(u = v = 1\\), so \\(\\Delta W \\neq 0\\) at init; \\(\\alpha = r\\), removing the \\(\\alpha/r\\) scaling","key_idea":"Tie one LoRA pair across all layers and train only what is needed (shared factors and/or per-layer scaling vectors); a framework containing LoRA and VeRA as corners of the same design space.","results_note":"TL6 (trainable tied \\(A\\), \\(B\\) plus per-layer \\(u\\), \\(v\\)) stays within roughly 1-2% of LoRA across five tasks (on average 1.36% below LoRA with LLaMA-2 7B) and beats LoRA on IWSLT-2017 De-En translation with 12.5% of its parameters; on GSM8K it needs 4.3% of LoRA's parameters for a comparable score.","relations":[{"target":"vera","type":"generalizes","note":"VeRA = tied, frozen random A, B with trainable u, v"},{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"untied A, B with Lambda = I recovers LoRA"},{"target":"sharelora","type":"competes-with","note":"ShareLoRA shares only one of the two factors"},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Uni-LoRA writes Tied-LoRA as a structured projection P"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09578","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.09578","https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.481/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Adithya Renduchintala","Tugrul Konuk","Oleksii Kuchaiev"],"bibkey":"renduchintala2023tied","bibtex":"@inproceedings{renduchintala2023tied,\n  title = {{Tied-LoRA: Enhancing parameter efficiency of LoRA with Weight Tying}},\n  author = {Adithya Renduchintala and Tugrul Konuk and Oleksii Kuchaiev},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2311.09578},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.naacl-long.481/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"torus","covariance":"MonxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W_\\ell=\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell}A\\) is multilinear in \\((v_\\ell,B,u_\\ell,A)\\); jointly over layers the image is a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\) whose layer slices all have row space in \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\). Classified at TL6 (all four trained).","base_point":"TL6 init \\(v=0\\), \\(u=\\mathbf 1\\), \\(A,B\\) Gaussian: only \\(v\\) moves to first order (Lemma II.4), \\(\\dim S_1=L\\,d_{out}\\) [Num] against \\(d(M)=r(d_{in}+d_{out})+L(d_{out}+r)-(d_{out}+2r+L-1)\\). TL3-TL5 (\\(B=0\\)) are also apices; TL1 starts with \\(\\Delta W\\ne0\\) (a defect).","gauge":"Only diagonal slides survive (a \\(GL_r\\) slide must normalise every \\(\\Lambda_{u_\\ell}\\), so it is monomial). The torus has four parts: \\((v_\\ell,B)\\mapsto(v_\\ell\\odot a,\\mathrm{diag}(a)^{-1}B)\\) with one \\(a\\in(\\mathbb R^\\times)^{d_{out}}\\) for all layers; \\((B,u_\\ell)\\mapsto(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c),u_\\ell\\oslash c)\\); \\((u_\\ell,A)\\mapsto(u_\\ell\\odot e,\\mathrm{diag}(e)^{-1}A)\\); and a per-layer slide \\((v_\\ell,u_\\ell)\\mapsto(b_\\ell v_\\ell,u_\\ell/b_\\ell)\\). With a one-dimensional stabiliser the generic fibre has dimension \\(d_{out}+2r+L-1\\) [Num: 12, 16, 14 at \\((L,d_{out},d_{in},r)=(3,6,5,2),(4,7,6,3),(3,8,8,2)\\)].","covariance":"Multi-box group: the shared \\(B\\) carries per-layer output diagonals, so a change of output basis common to the tied boxes must normalise the diagonal monoid (monomial); the input side keeps one common \\(GL\\). For a single box the image is just \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","rebasing":"The cone is closed under scaling and its span is all of \\(\\prod_\\ell\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}\\) (take \\(v_\\ell=e_i\\delta_{\\ell\\ell_0}\\)), so \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"Tied-LoRA is a lattice of sub-objects of one multi-box cone: freezing \\((A,B)\\) at random values is VeRA (an A2 sub-object) and untying with \\(\\Lambda=I\\) is LoRA. The full TL6 object factors through ShareLoRA by absorbing the diagonals into a per-layer up-projection \\(\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell}\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"sharelora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{v_\\ell,u_\\ell\\}_\\ell,B,A)=\\big(\\{\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell}\\}_\\ell,\\;A\\big)","note":"Lands in ShareA at its standard pointing \\((B_\\ell=0,A_0)\\); strict, since ShareA has free per-layer \\(B_\\ell\\)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell},\\;A\\big)_\\ell","note":"Per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\) with the same \\(A_0\\) in every layer (stratum \\(T_A\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O2","reason":"VeRA embeds in Tied-LoRA by A2 (freeze \\(A,B\\); re-point \\(u_0\\) from 1 to VeRA's 0.1), but not back: \\(d(\\mathrm{TL6})=r(d_{in}+d_{out})+L(d_{out}+r)-(d_{out}+2r+L-1)\\) exceeds VeRA's \\(L(d_{out}+r-1)\\) by \\(r(d_{in}+d_{out})-d_{out}-2r+1>0\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"sharelora","name":"ShareLoRA","full_name":"Shared Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"ShareLoRA: Parameter Efficient and Robust Large Language Model Fine-tuning via Shared Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Song et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2406.10785","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10785","code_url":"https://github.com/Rain9876/ShareLoRA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"\\text{ShareA: } W_\\ell = W_{0,\\ell} + \\alpha\\, B_\\ell\\, A,\\quad A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{shared by all layers},\\ B_\\ell\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times r}\\ \\text{per layer};\\quad \\text{ShareB: } \\alpha\\, B\\, A_\\ell;\\quad \\text{ShareAB: } \\alpha\\, B A\\ \\text{for every layer (all factors trained)}","trainable_params":"\\text{ShareA: } r\\,d_{out} + \\tfrac{r\\,d_{in}}{L}\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (i.e. } L r d_{out} + r d_{in} \\text{ per module type)}","locus":"attention projections (ShareA_qkv: \\(A_Q\\), \\(A_K\\) and \\(A_V\\) are each shared across all layers, one shared \\(A\\) per projection type) or all linear layers ('sharing all'), on RoBERTa, GPT-2 and LLaMA 1/2/3","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"LoRA in which one factor (the down-projection \\(A\\), the up-projection \\(B\\), or both) is a single matrix shared across depth; \\(\\Delta W_\\ell = B_\\ell A\\) spans a common row space for all layers","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Each layer's \\(B_\\ell A\\) is an ordinary low-rank matrix that can be added to \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Inherits LoRA initialisation (up-projection zero, so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); not discussed separately in the paper","key_idea":"Share LoRA's down- (or up-) projection across all layers: the shared subspace removes redundancy, roughly halves parameters or more, and acts as a regulariser that improves robustness and continual-learning transfer.","results_note":"44%-96% fewer trainable parameters than LoRA with maintained performance on RoBERTa, GPT-2 and LLaMA 1/2/3, and up to 1.2% higher average accuracy in zero/few-shot and continual fine-tuning settings.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"shares one LoRA factor across layers"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"Tied-LoRA ties both factors and adds scaling vectors; ShareAB with no scalings is a Tied-LoRA corner"},{"target":"lily","type":"competes-with","note":"Lily shares A locally and B globally with a router"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10785","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.10785"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Yurun Song","Junchen Zhao","Ian G. Harris","Sangeetha Abdu Jyothi"],"bibkey":"song2024sharelora","bibtex":"@article{song2024sharelora,\n  title = {{ShareLoRA: Parameter Efficient and Robust Large Language Model Fine-tuning via Shared Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Yurun Song and Junchen Zhao and Ian G. Harris and Sangeetha Abdu Jyothi},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2406.10785},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.10785},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.10785}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"ShareA (default): \\(\\Delta W_\\ell=\\alpha B_\\ell A\\) is bilinear; the joint image consists of per-layer rank-\\(\\le r\\) updates whose row spaces lie in one common subspace \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\).","base_point":"\\(B_\\ell=0\\), \\(A_0\\) Kaiming: \\(S_1=\\{(X_\\ell A_0)_\\ell\\}\\) of dimension \\(L\\,d_{out}r\\) against \\(d(M)=L\\,d_{out}r+r\\,d_{in}-r^2\\). The deficit \\(r(d_{in}-r)\\) is LoRA's, paid once for all layers.","gauge":"\\((B_\\ell,A)\\mapsto(B_\\ell g^{-1},gA)\\) with a single \\(g\\in GL_r\\) for all layers.","covariance":"Per box \\(GL\\times GL\\). Across the tied boxes the input-side change of basis must be one common \\(h\\) (the shared row space), so Obs II.15 applies only to gauges that act identically on all tied inputs, such as a global residual-stream rotation for q, k, v.","rebasing":"Each cycle may choose a new common row space, so per-layer rank grows by \\(r\\) per cycle and \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"ShareLoRA (ShareA) is multi-box LoRA with its down-projection glued along the diagonal \\(Q_A\\to\\prod_\\ell Q_A\\). It lies between LoRA-FA (freezing \\(A\\), an A2 sub-object) and per-layer LoRA (the diagonal copy, an A3 arrow). ShareB is the mirror image and ShareAB is the fully tied corner of Tied-LoRA.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_\\ell)_\\ell,A\\big)=(B_\\ell,A)_\\ell","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared \\(A\\); per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\) with a common \\(A_0\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-fa","test":"O1","reason":"\\(A\\) is trained, so the shared row space leaves \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\); LoRA-FA\\((A_0)\\) is only the A-frozen sub-object, and that arrow goes the other way (A2)."},{"target":"tied-lora","test":"O2","reason":"ShareA's free per-layer \\(B_\\ell\\) give \\(d=L\\,d_{out}r+r\\,d_{in}-r^2\\), while Tied-LoRA's per-layer freedom is diagonal: \\(d(\\mathrm{ShareA})-d(\\text{Tied-LoRA})=(r-1)\\big[(d_{out}-1)(L-1)-r\\big]>0\\) for \\(r\\ge2\\) once \\((d_{out}-1)(L-1)>r\\) [Num: 42 vs 34, 30 vs 28]."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"prolora","name":"PRoLoRA","full_name":"Partially Rotation-enhanced Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"PRoLoRA: Partial Rotation Empowers More Parameter-Efficient LoRA","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACL 2024","arxiv":"2402.16902","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.156/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + BA,\\quad B = \\big[\\,B_u\\ \\big|\\ \\big(B_0;\\,\\mathrm{Roll}(B_0,s_B);\\dots;\\mathrm{Roll}(B_0,(n{-}1)s_B)\\big)\\big],\\quad A = \\Big[\\,A_u;\\ \\big(A_0\\,|\\,\\mathrm{Roll}(A_0,s_A)\\,|\\cdots|\\,\\mathrm{Roll}(A_0,(m{-}1)s_A)\\big)\\Big];\\ A_u\\in\\mathbb{R}^{u\\times d_{in}},\\ B_u\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times u}\\ \\text{(unshared ranks)},\\ A_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(r-u)\\times d_{in}/m},\\ B_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}/n\\times(r-u)}\\ \\text{trained};\\ \\mathrm{Roll} = \\text{cyclic shift along the rank dimension}","trainable_params":"u\\,(d_{in}+d_{out}) + (r-u)\\Big(\\tfrac{d_{in}}{m} + \\tfrac{d_{out}}{n}\\Big)","locus":"any linear layer (paper: all linear layers of LLaMA-2 / Tulu instruction tuning)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"intra-layer sharing: each LoRA factor is a broadcast of one small chunk along the hidden dimension, the copies differentiated by rank-dimension cyclic permutations, plus \\(u\\) unshared ranks; \\(\\Delta W\\) is block-structured with blocks related by rank permutations","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The expanded \\(B\\) and \\(A\\) are ordinary matrices, so \\(BA\\) merges into \\(W_0\\) exactly as in LoRA.","identity_at_init":"\\(B = 0\\) (whole matrix); \\(A_u\\) Kaiming-uniform and the shared chunk \\(A_0\\) with a rectified Kaiming bound computed from the full hidden size \\(d_{in}\\) so both parts have equal scale","key_idea":"Reuse parameters inside a layer: broadcast one chunk of each LoRA factor across the hidden dimension (more rank per parameter) and break the resulting repetition with cheap rotations and a few unshared ranks; a strict superset of LoRA.","results_note":"With half the trainable parameters of LoRA, PRoLoRA still outperforms LoRA on multiple instruction-tuning benchmarks and scales to larger LLMs.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"u = r (or m = n = 1) recovers LoRA"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"intra-layer instead of inter-layer sharing; not restricted by equal weight shapes"},{"target":"mos","type":"competes-with","note":"MoS builds on PRoLoRA-like intra-layer sharing and adds VeRA-like inter-layer sharing"},{"target":"miss","type":"competes-with","note":"MiSS also builds Delta W by tiling one shard"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16902","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.16902","https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.156/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Sheng Wang","Boyang Xue","Jiacheng Ye","Jiyue Jiang","Liheng Chen","Lingpeng Kong","Chuan Wu"],"bibkey":"wang2024prolora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024prolora,\n  title = {{PRoLoRA: Partial Rotation Empowers More Parameter-Efficient LoRA}},\n  author = {Sheng Wang and Boyang Xue and Jiacheng Ye and Jiyue Jiang and Liheng Chen and Lingpeng Kong and Chuan Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2402.16902},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.156/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(B=\\mathcal B(B_u,B_0)\\) and \\(A=\\mathcal A(A_u,A_0)\\) are linear broadcast-and-roll maps, so \\(\\Delta W=\\mathcal B\\mathcal A\\) is bilinear: a constrained LoRA cone inside \\(\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\).","base_point":"\\(B=0\\) for the whole matrix, \\(A\\) Kaiming (rectified bound): only the \\(B\\)-parameters move to first order, with \\(\\dim S_1=u\\,d_{out}+(r-u)d_{out}/n<d(M)\\) [Num: 24 vs 44 at \\((d_{out},d_{in},r,u,m,n)=(12,12,4,1,3,3)\\)].","gauge":"A gauge \\(g\\in GL_r\\) must keep both shared blocks in broadcast-and-roll form, which forces \\(g\\) block-diagonal: \\(GL_u\\) on the unshared ranks times the commutant of the roll on the shared ranks, i.e. the invertible circulants (an abelian torus, \\((\\mathbb R^\\times)^a\\times(\\mathbb C^\\times)^b\\)). No off-diagonal block survives: mixing shared columns into unshared ones would break the broadcast form of the other factor. Generic fibre \\(u^2+(r-u)\\) [Num: 4, 6, 5, 4, 8 at five sizes, including \\(u=0\\)]. For \\(u=0\\) the gauge is just the circulant torus.","covariance":"Broadcasting one chunk along the hidden dimension imposes a partition of \\(d_{in}\\) (resp. \\(d_{out}\\)) into \\(m\\) (resp. \\(n\\)) identified chunks, plus a cyclic order on ranks. A neuron permutation across chunks changes the image.","rebasing":"For \\(u\\ge1\\) the unshared ranks alone generate everything under rebasing, so \\(R_\\infty=\\Theta\\). For \\(u=0\\) the span is a proper subspace, because blocks with the same roll offset are tied [Num: span 64 of 144 at \\((12,12,4,0,3,3)\\); 144 of 144 with \\(u=1\\)]."},"categorical_reading":"PRoLoRA is LoRA precomposed with a linear tying map \\(Q_{\\rm PRoLoRA}\\to Q_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_r}\\). Its 'more rank per parameter' is a larger \\(\\rho_{\\max}\\) than the LoRA of equal budget, paid for with an imposed chunk structure (\\(\\Pi\\)).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_u,B_0,A_u,A_0)=\\big(\\mathcal B(B_u,B_0),\\;\\mathcal A(A_u,A_0)\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\mathcal A(A_u^0,A_0^0))\\), stratum \\(T_A\\) when the expanded \\(A_0\\) has full row rank. Conversely \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_u\\to\\) PRoLoRA by A2 (freeze \\(B_0=0\\) and \\(A_0\\) at init)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"No arrow into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r'}\\) for \\(r'<r\\): PRoLoRA's generic rank is r [Num]. In particular the LoRA of equal budget has rank \\(r_{\\rm eq}=\\big[u(d_{in}+d_{out})+(r-u)(d_{in}/m+d_{out}/n)\\big]/(d_{in}+d_{out})<r\\) for \\(m,n\\ge2\\), \\(u<r\\)."},{"target":"miss","test":"O1","reason":"MiSS's update \\(D(\\mathbf 1^\\top\\otimes I_r)\\) keeps its row space inside the fixed space \\(\\mathrm{row}(\\mathbf 1^\\top\\otimes I_r)\\); PRoLoRA's unshared ranks \\(B_uA_u\\) have arbitrary row spaces, although both tile shards."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mos","name":"MoS","full_name":"Mixture of Shards","paper_title":"MoS: Unleashing Parameter Efficiency of Low-Rank Adaptation with Mixture of Shards","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.00938","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00938","code_url":"https://github.com/Forence1999/MoS","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_\\ell = W_{0,\\ell} + B_\\ell A_\\ell,\\quad B_\\ell = \\mathrm{Route}^{c}\\big([B^{pub}, B^{pri}],\\, I^{b}_\\ell\\big),\\quad A_\\ell = \\mathrm{Route}^{r}\\big([A^{pub}, A^{pri}],\\, I^{a}_\\ell\\big);\\ \\text{each rank vector of } A_\\ell, B_\\ell \\text{ is the concatenation of } l \\text{ shards retrieved from global pools by fixed random index matrices } I_\\ell;\\ \\text{pools trained, indices frozen; private shards are used by one layer only}","trainable_params":"\\approx e\\,(d_{in}+d_{out})\\ \\text{per adapted matrix on average (global pools of size } eL(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ per linear-layer type), while each layer uses rank } r > e","locus":"all linear-layer types of LLaMA-2/3 (one global pool per layer type)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"global inter- and intra-layer sharing: LoRA factor vectors are assembled from shards of shared public/private pools via subset selection, pair dissociation (separate pools for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)), vector sharding and shard privatisation; routing is index-based and input-independent","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Indices are fixed, so after training each layer's \\(B_l A_l\\) is a fixed low-rank matrix that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"public and private \\(B\\) pools zero-initialised (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); \\(A\\) pools with PRoLoRA-style rectified Kaiming bounds; index matrices sampled once at random","key_idea":"Pure sharing across layers hurts because all layers get the same update; cheap differentiation strategies (random subsets, dissociated \\(A\\)/\\(B\\) pools, shards, private shards) restore diversity while keeping the high sharing ratio.","results_note":"About 8x parameter savings relative to standard LoRA at matched performance on instruction-tuning benchmarks (MMLU, BBH, GSM8K, etc.), with ablations confirming each differentiation component.","relations":[{"target":"prolora","type":"builds-on","note":"adopts intra-layer chunk sharing and rectified initialisation"},{"target":"vera","type":"builds-on","note":"adopts inter-layer sharing; replaces random scaling by subset selection"},{"target":"tied-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"pure sharing (all pool vectors used in every block) ties B and A across layers like Tied-LoRA, but without Tied-LoRA's per-layer scaling vectors, so neither method contains the other"},{"target":"vb-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both compose LoRA vectors from a global bank; MoS uses fixed hard indices, VB-LoRA learned top-k mixtures"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00938","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.00938"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Sheng Wang","Liheng Chen","Pengan Chen","Jingwei Dong","Boyang Xue","Jiyue Jiang","Lingpeng Kong","Chuan Wu"],"bibkey":"wang2024mos","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024mos,\n  title = {{MoS: Unleashing Parameter Efficiency of Low-Rank Adaptation with Mixture of Shards}},\n  author = {Sheng Wang and Liheng Chen and Pengan Chen and Jingwei Dong and Boyang Xue and Jiyue Jiang and Lingpeng Kong and Chuan Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.00938},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00938}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"scalar","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(B_\\ell,A_\\ell\\) are fixed linear selections (index matrices \\(I_\\ell\\)) from trained pools, so \\(\\Delta W_\\ell=B_\\ell A_\\ell\\) is bilinear in the two pools: a cone with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"\\(B\\)-pools are zero and \\(A\\)-pools random, so only the \\(B\\)-pools move to first order (Lemma II.4).","gauge":"Shards are shared across rank vectors and layers through random indices, so a partial rescaling cannot be compensated; the only continuous slide is the global \\((\\mathrm{pool}_B,\\mathrm{pool}_A)\\mapsto(c\\,\\mathrm{pool}_B,\\mathrm{pool}_A/c)\\) [Num, simplified MoS with \\(l=2\\) shards: fibre 1 on the used shards at three sizes]. Without sharding (\\(l=1\\)) and with small pools, extra slides can appear (fibre 5 in one simulation).","covariance":"Vector sharding builds each rank vector from \\(l\\) segments drawn from a common pool, which identifies coordinate blocks. This imposed structure breaks neuron permutations even in law. With \\(l=1\\), the image would be \\(GL\\)-covariant up to the shared-pool tying.","rebasing":"Private shards let one layer move alone and the shard vectors are free, so \\(\\mathrm{span}\\,C=\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"MoS is multi-box LoRA precomposed with a fixed random linear routing from two global pools, \\(\\rho=\\rho_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}\\circ(\\mathrm{Route}_B\\times\\mathrm{Route}_A)\\), which is an A3 arrow into per-layer LoRA. Its differentiation tricks (subsets, pair dissociation, sharding, privatisation) select which linear tying map is used.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\mathrm{pool}_B,\\mathrm{pool}_A)=\\big(\\mathrm{Route}^c(\\mathrm{pool}_B,I^b_\\ell),\\;\\mathrm{Route}^r(\\mathrm{pool}_A,I^a_\\ell)\\big)_\\ell","note":"Per-layer LoRA of rank r pointed at \\((0,A_\\ell^0)\\) (B-pools zero)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"tied-lora","test":"O1","reason":"Tied-LoRA keeps every layer's row space inside the common \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A\\) (an invariant of its image); MoS with private shards and random subsets gives layer-specific row spaces."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"rasa","name":"RaSA","full_name":"Rank-Sharing Low-Rank Adaptation","paper_title":"RaSA: Rank-Sharing Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"He et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2503.12576","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12576","code_url":"https://github.com/zwhe99/RaSA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W_i = W_{0,i} + \\big[\\tilde B_i\\ \\ B_S\\big]\\, D_i \\begin{bmatrix}\\tilde A_i\\\\ A_S\\end{bmatrix},\\quad \\tilde B_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times(r-k)},\\ \\tilde A_i\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(r-k)\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{layer-specific};\\ B_S\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times Lk},\\ A_S\\in\\mathbb{R}^{Lk\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{shared rank pool (k ranks contributed by each layer)};\\ D_i\\ \\text{diagonal } (r-k+Lk)\\ \\text{per layer; all trained}","trainable_params":"r\\,(d_{in}+d_{out}) + (r - k + Lk)\\ \\text{per adapted matrix (same as LoRA plus a diagonal)}","locus":"all linear layers of the LLM (assumes equal shapes across layers; e.g. Llama, Mistral)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"partial rank sharing: \\(\\Delta W_i = \\tilde B_i D_i^{(1)} \\tilde A_i + B_S D_i^{(2)} A_S\\), a private rank-\\((r-k)\\) term plus a shared rank-\\(Lk\\) pool reweighted per layer by a trainable diagonal; effective rank \\(r - k + Lk\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"For each layer the update is a fixed low-rank matrix that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"\\(\\tilde B_i = 0\\), \\(B_S = 0\\); \\(\\tilde A_i\\), \\(A_S\\) Kaiming; \\(D_i = \\tfrac{\\alpha}{2(r-k)}\\) on private and \\(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{2Lk}\\) on shared ranks","key_idea":"Pool \\(k\\) ranks from every layer into a shared rank pool that all layers reuse with layer-specific diagonal weights, raising effective rank by \\((L-1)k\\) at LoRA's parameter cost; its minimum reconstruction error is provably no worse than LoRA's.","results_note":"Significantly improves over LoRA and other variants on challenging code-generation and mathematical-reasoning fine-tuning at the same parameter budget.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"generalizes","note":"k = 0 recovers LoRA"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"RaSA's shared pool with per-layer diagonal weighting resembles VeRA/Tied-LoRA sharing, but the pool is trained and combined with private LoRA ranks; the paper compares against VeRA and argues full sharing is sub-optimal"},{"target":"mos","type":"competes-with","note":"cross-layer sharing of LoRA rank vectors"},{"target":"lily","type":"competes-with","note":"cross-layer sharing to raise effective rank"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12576","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.12576"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Zhiwei He","Zhaopeng Tu","Xing Wang","Xingyu Chen","Zhijie Wang","Jiahao Xu","Tian Liang","Wenxiang Jiao","Zhuosheng Zhang","Rui Wang"],"bibkey":"he2025rasa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2025rasa,\n  title = {{RaSA: Rank-Sharing Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Zhiwei He and Zhaopeng Tu and Xing Wang and Xingyu Chen and Zhijie Wang and Jiahao Xu and Tian Liang and Wenxiang Jiao and Zhuosheng Zhang and Rui Wang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2503.12576},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12576}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"apex","gauge":"GL","covariance":"GLxGL","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W_i=\\tilde B_iD_i^{(1)}\\tilde A_i+B_SD_i^{(2)}A_S\\) is multilinear: a cone of per-layer rank \\(\\le r-k+Lk\\), coupled across layers through the shared pool.","base_point":"\\(\\tilde B_i=0\\) and \\(B_S=0\\): only the \\(B\\)-sides move at first order, and the shared direction contributes \\(Y D_i^{(2)}A_S^0\\) with one \\(Y\\) for all layers.","gauge":"\\(GL_{r-k}\\) times a diagonal rescaling on each private term (\\(D_i^{(1)}\\) is absorbed). On the shared pool a slide must normalise every \\(D_i^{(2)}\\), so only the torus \\((B_S,D_i,A_S)\\mapsto(B_S\\Delta_1^{-1},\\Delta_1D_i\\Delta_2^{-1},\\Delta_2A_S)\\) of dimension \\(2Lk\\) survives. Generic fibre \\(L[(r-k)^2+(r-k)]+2Lk\\) [Num: 24, 20, 12 at three sizes].","covariance":"Per box \\(GL\\times GL\\); the shared pool forces one change of basis on both sides for all layers (a diagonal \\(GL\\times GL\\)), as for ShareLoRA.","rebasing":"The private ranks alone reach \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"RaSA is \\(\\prod_i\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r-k}\\) plus a shared rank pool with trained frames and per-layer diagonal weights. It maps by A3 into per-layer \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r-k+Lk}\\), so its effective rank is the rank of that target, bought by tying rather than by parameters.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\tilde B_i\\ \\ B_S]\\,D_i,\\;[\\tilde A_i;\\,A_S]\\big)_i","note":"Into per-layer LoRA of rank \\(r-k+Lk\\) pointed at \\((0,[\\tilde A_i^0;A_S^0])\\); for \\(k=0\\) it is the identity onto \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Against \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) at the same r, RaSA's per-layer maximum rank \\(r-k+Lk>r\\) (for \\(L\\ge2,k\\ge1\\)) is out of reach."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"lora-xs","name":"LoRA-XS","full_name":"Low-Rank Adaptation with eXtremely Small number of parameters","paper_title":"LoRA-XS: Low-Rank Adaptation with Extremely Small Number of Parameters","authors":"Bałazy et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ECAI 2025","arxiv":"2405.17604","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17604","code_url":"https://github.com/MohammadrezaBanaei/LoRA-XS","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + U_r\\,R\\,\\Sigma_r V_r^{\\top},\\quad W_0 = U\\Sigma V^{\\top}\\ (U_r, \\Sigma_r, V_r:\\ \\text{top-}r \\text{ truncated SVD, frozen}),\\ R\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\ \\text{trained}\\ \\ (\\text{paper, row-vector convention: } h = xW + xARB,\\ A = U_r\\Sigma_r,\\ B = V_r^{\\top};\\ \\text{equivalently } U_r\\Sigma_r R' V_r^{\\top} \\text{ since } \\Sigma_r \\text{ is invertible})","trainable_params":"r^{2}","locus":"any linear layer (paper: attention and MLP projections of RoBERTa, Mistral, Gemma, LLaMA)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"a trainable \\(r\\times r\\) core sandwiched between frozen top-\\(r\\) singular subspaces of \\(W_0\\) (Tucker-2 core); \\(\\Delta W\\) lives in \\(\\mathrm{span}(U_r) \\otimes \\mathrm{span}(V_r)\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"\\(U_r R \\Sigma_r V_r^T\\) is a fixed matrix that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Near-identity, not exact: \\(R \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma^2)\\) with \\(\\sigma = 10^{-5}\\)","key_idea":"Freeze LoRA's factors at the SVD of the pretrained weight and train only a small \\(r\\times r\\) mixing matrix between them, removing LoRA's width-proportional parameter floor (as few as one parameter per module).","results_note":"Outperforms or matches LoRA and VeRA on GLUE, GSM8K, MATH and commonsense reasoning while reducing storage by over 100x for 7B models.","relations":[{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"both use the top-r SVD of W_0 (LoRA-XS states the choice is inspired by such subspaces); PiSSA trains the factors and moves them out of the residual, LoRA-XS freezes them and trains only an r x r core; developed concurrently"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"replaces random frozen factors by SVD factors and per-layer vectors by an r x r core"},{"target":"svft","type":"special-case-of","note":"our observation: SVFT with M supported on a dense top-left r x r block (up to the invertible Sigma_r reparameterisation)"},{"target":"uni-lora","type":"special-case-of","note":"Uni-LoRA expresses LoRA-XS as a fixed projection"},{"target":"fact","type":"competes-with","note":"FacT-TT has the same U Sigma_i V^T form with shared, trainable bases"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17604","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.17604","https://ebooks.iospress.nl/doi/10.3233/FAIA251185","https://doi.org/10.3233/FAIA251185"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Klaudia Bałazy","Mohammadreza Banaei","Karl Aberer","Jacek Tabor"],"bibkey":"baazy2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{baazy2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-XS: Low-Rank Adaptation with Extremely Small Number of Parameters}},\n  author = {Klaudia Bałazy and Mohammadreza Banaei and Karl Aberer and Jacek Tabor},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {ECAI},\n  eprint = {2405.17604},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17604}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"Affine in \\(R\\): \\(W_0+U_rR\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\) is an \\(r^2\\)-dimensional subspace when \\(\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\ge r\\) (Lin, as in the table). Categorically it is the product \\(\\mathrm{FA}(\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\times\\mathrm{FB}(U_r)\\) of Prop I.8(a), which is why axes.json also lists it under Meet.","base_point":"The exactly pointed variant \\(R_0=0\\) is regular. The paper's \\(R_0\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2)\\) with \\(\\sigma=10^{-5}\\) leaves a negligible defect (the table's '≈').","covariance":"Frames come from the truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\) (Prop II.16).","rebasing":"With frozen frames the shape is a fixed subspace, so rebasing is idempotent; recomputing the SVD after each merge would gain."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-XS is the categorical product of the two one-sided methods in the SVD frames, \\(\\mathrm{FA}(\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\times\\mathrm{FB}(U_r)\\), i.e. the largest method simulated by both. It maps into PiSSA by A3, so it is the linear core shared by the spectral-frame family.","entry_type":"method","core":true,"arrows":[{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\big(U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}+U_rR\\,\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\;\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"The framework's LoRA-XS to PiSSA arrow written in the catalog's normalisation \\(U_rR\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\); \\(h(0)\\) is PiSSA's standard split."},{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A4","h":"h(R)=U_rR\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(A_0),\\ A_0=\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top","note":"Product projection onto the one-sided factor, here LoRA-FA with the spectral (not random) frame \\(A_0\\)."},{"target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\begin{bmatrix}R\\Sigma_r&0\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}\\ \\ (\\Omega=\\text{top-left }r\\times r\\text{ block})","note":"Isomorphism onto SVFT with a dense top-left support, pointed at \\(M=0\\) (SVFT's exactly pointed variant)."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\big(U_rR,\\;\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\): the \\(T_A\\) point whose row space is the top-r right singular space."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"vera","test":"O1","reason":"VeRA's updates have row and column spaces in frozen random frames, LoRA-XS's in the top-r SVD frames of \\(W_0\\); the two images meet only at \\(\\theta_0\\) generically."},{"target":"mica","test":"O1","reason":"MiCA uses the minor left singular vectors, LoRA-XS the top ones, so column spaces are orthogonal and the images meet only at \\(\\theta_0\\)."},{"target":"loft-orthogonal","test":"O1","reason":"LOFT keeps the neuron Gram matrix W W^T exactly (Cor II.6), and LoRA-XS changes it."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lamda","name":"LaMDA","full_name":"Large Model Fine-Tuning via Spectrally Decomposed Low-Dimensional Adaptation","paper_title":"LaMDA: Large Model Fine-Tuning via Spectrally Decomposed Low-Dimensional Adaptation","authors":"Azizi et al.","year":2024,"venue":"EMNLP 2024 (Findings)","arxiv":"2406.12832","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.563/","code_url":"https://github.com/ArminAzizi98/LaMDA","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = \\big(W_0 - U_r\\Sigma_r V_r^{\\top}\\big) + \\alpha\\,U_r\\,S\\,\\Sigma_r V_r^{\\top},\\quad \\Sigma_r V_r^{\\top}\\ (\\text{PMA, down-projection) frozen},\\ S\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times r}\\ (\\text{LDA}) \\text{ trained},\\ U_r\\ (\\text{PMB, up-projection) trainable early and frozen one rank component at a time, lowest-energy first, on a linear schedule over the first } t_i \\approx 30\\%\\ \\text{of iterations}\\ \\ (\\text{paper, row-vector convention: } Y = XW + \\alpha XASB,\\ A = U[:,{:}r]\\Sigma_r,\\ B = V[:,{:}r]^{\\top}, \\text{ rows of } B \\text{ frozen last-to-first})","trainable_params":"r^{2}\\ (+\\ \\text{a decreasing number of rows of the up-projection during the early phase; LaMDA++ adapts } r \\text{ per layer})","locus":"any linear layer (paper: DeBERTa-V3, BART, GPT-2, LLaMA-2 attention/MLP projections)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"PiSSA-style residual split with a trainable \\(r \\times r\\) core between frozen spectral factors; the up-projection is progressively frozen, so late training is a pure \\(r^2\\)-parameter basis change","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"After training the residual and the adapter path are fixed matrices that sum to a single weight.","identity_at_init":"\\(S = I_r\\) and the main path holds the residual \\(W_0 - U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^{\\top}\\), so \\(W = W_0\\) at init (for \\(\\alpha = 1\\))","key_idea":"Freeze the first projection (cutting activation memory) and learn a small square matrix in the principal spectral subspace, gradually freezing the second projection, so trainable parameters stop scaling with width; LaMDA++ allocates ranks from the normalised spectrum.","results_note":"Matches or surpasses existing methods on GLUE, summarisation, NLG and reasoning with up to 17.7x fewer parameter updates and up to 1.32x lower peak GPU memory.","relations":[{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"contemporaneous r x r core between frozen SVD factors"},{"target":"pissa","type":"competes-with","note":"same principal/residual split of W_0, but the paper calls PiSSA contemporary work with a different objective (LaMDA freezes the spectral factors and trains an r x r core)"},{"target":"vera","type":"competes-with","note":"replaces random frozen factors by spectral ones"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12832","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.12832","https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.563/"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Seyedarmin Azizi","Souvik Kundu","Massoud Pedram"],"bibkey":"azizi2024lamda","bibtex":"@inproceedings{azizi2024lamda,\n  title = {{LaMDA: Large Model Fine-Tuning via Spectrally Decomposed Low-Dimensional Adaptation}},\n  author = {Seyedarmin Azizi and Souvik Kundu and Massoud Pedram},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2024},\n  eprint = {2406.12832},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.563/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"GL","covariance":"OxO","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"R with a freezing schedule: the up-projection is frozen rank by rank over the first ~30% of steps. The base is never re-pointed, so this is not a B schedule.","shape":"\\(\\rho(U,S)=W_0+(\\alpha US-U_r)\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\). Although bilinear in \\((U,S)\\), the product \\(US\\) ranges over all of \\(\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}\\), so the image is the linear space \\(W_0+\\{X\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\}\\), which is LoRA-FA\\((\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\). The late phase (\\(U\\) frozen) is a LoRA-XS-type \\(r^2\\)-dimensional subspace of it.","base_point":"At \\((U_r,I)\\), \\(S_1=\\{\\delta U\\,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top+U_r\\,\\delta S\\,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\}=\\{X\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\}\\), the whole tangent space of the image: regular (exactly pointed for \\(\\alpha=1\\)).","gauge":"Early phase: \\((U,S)\\mapsto(Ug^{-1},gS)\\), a \\(GL_r\\) gauge with fibre dimension \\(r^2\\). It becomes trivial once \\(U\\) is frozen.","covariance":"Frames come from the truncated SVD of \\(W_0\\): \\(CO\\times CO\\).","rebasing":"The whole schedule stays inside the fixed subspace \\(W_0+\\{X\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\}\\), so rebasing with frozen frames is idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"LaMDA is PiSSA with its \\(A\\)-factor frozen (an A2 sub-object), reparametrised redundantly as \\(B=\\alpha US\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\) and then progressively restricted to the LoRA-XS sub-object. Its training is a descending chain of sub-objects inside the linear method LoRA-FA\\((\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\alpha US-U_r\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)","note":"Pointed (\\(h(U_r,I)=0\\) for \\(\\alpha=1\\)); in the early phase this is an epimorphism onto the image."},{"target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\big(\\alpha US\\,\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\;\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into PiSSA at its standard split \\((U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top)\\); LaMDA is PiSSA's A-frozen sub-object."},{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\big(\\alpha US-U_r,\\;\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\) (stratum \\(T_A\\))."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O2","reason":"The early phase moves in \\(m\\,r\\) dimensions, far more than LoRA-XS's \\(r^2\\). Only the U-frozen sub-object is LoRA-XS-like, and if U moved early its frame is \\(U'\\ne U_r\\), not the SVD frame."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"lora-mini","name":"LoRA-Mini","full_name":"LoRA-Mini: Adaptation Matrices Decomposition and Selective Training","paper_title":"LoRA-Mini : Adaptation Matrices Decomposition and Selective Training","authors":"Singh et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2411.15804","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15804","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + P\\,C\\,D\\,Q,\\quad P\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times b},\\ Q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{a\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{frozen outer (auxiliary) matrices};\\ C\\in\\mathbb{R}^{b\\times r},\\ D\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times a}\\ \\text{trained inner matrices}\\ \\ (\\text{paper: } h = (W + A_{aux}A_{train}B_{train}B_{aux})x)","trainable_params":"r\\,(a + b)","locus":"attention and dense layers (paper: BERT, RoBERTa on GLUE; T5 on WMT16 En-Ro)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"each LoRA factor split into a frozen outer projection and a trainable inner block: \\(\\Delta W\\) factors through fixed \\(a\\)- and \\(b\\)-dimensional subspaces with a trainable rank-\\(r\\) map between them","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The four-matrix product is a fixed low-rank matrix after training.","identity_at_init":"The paper reports Kaiming initialisation for all four matrices and does not state a zero-initialised factor, so an exact identity at init is not guaranteed as described","key_idea":"Freeze random outer projections and train only small inner matrices of LoRA's factors, cutting trainable parameters up to 20x.","results_note":"Up to 20x fewer trainable parameters than LoRA with comparable GLUE and WMT16 En-Ro translation performance.","relations":[{"target":"lora","type":"builds-on","note":"decomposes each LoRA factor into frozen and trainable parts"},{"target":"lora-xs","type":"competes-with","note":"both train a small inner block between frozen projections (random here, SVD there)"},{"target":"lora-fa","type":"competes-with","note":"both freeze some projections of LoRA; LoRA-Mini freezes outer blocks of both factors"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15804","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.15804"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Ayush Singh","Rajdeep Aher","Shivank Garg"],"bibkey":"singh2024lora","bibtex":"@article{singh2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA-Mini : Adaptation Matrices Decomposition and Selective Training}},\n  author = {Ayush Singh and Rajdeep Aher and Shivank Garg},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2411.15804},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.15804},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.15804}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Cone","base_point":"defect","gauge":"GL","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"span"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=P\\,CD\\,Q\\) with frozen outer frames: the transported cone \\(P\\,\\mathcal M_{\\le r}(b\\times a)\\,Q\\), with vertex \\(\\theta_0\\).","base_point":"The paper reports Kaiming init for all four matrices and no zero factor, so \\(\\rho(q_0)-\\theta_0=PC_0D_0Q\\ne0\\): a pointing defect by design (as for TT-LoRA; axes.json files the analogous LoRA init_weights=False under 'unpointed', but \\(C=0\\) does reproduce \\(\\theta_0\\), so 'defect' is the precise value). The exactly pointed variant (\\(C_0=0\\) or \\(D_0=0\\)) would be an apex like LoRA.","gauge":"\\((C,D)\\mapsto(Cg^{-1},gD)\\): \\(GL_r\\).","covariance":"Random frozen outer frames \\(P,Q\\) on both sides: covariance only in law.","rebasing":"With frames frozen, \\(R_\\infty=W_0+P\\,\\mathbb R^{b\\times a}\\,Q\\), the two-sided random-frame linear method (the analogue of \\(\\mathrm{VeRA}^\\infty=\\) LoRA-FA), not \\(\\Theta\\)."},"categorical_reading":"LoRA-Mini is \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) on a small \\(b\\times a\\) space transported into \\(\\Theta\\) by fixed random frames, \\(\\rho=W_0+P\\,\\rho_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}(C,D)\\,Q\\). It maps into LoRA by A3 via \\(h(C,D)=(PC,DQ)\\), and for \\(a=b=r\\) it is a random-frame version of LoRA-XS (Prop I.8(a) with random frames).","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,D)=(PC,\\;DQ)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at \\(h(q_0)=(PC_0,D_0Q)\\). With the paper's all-Kaiming init this is a morphism between objects with a defect (unpointed slice); with \\(C_0=0\\) it lands in stratum \\(T_A\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora-xs","test":"O1","reason":"Random outer frames versus the SVD frames of \\(W_0\\): \\(\\mathrm{col}\\,P\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{span}\\,U_r\\) generically, so the images meet only at \\(\\theta_0\\)."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Cone"},{"id":"mosa","name":"MoSA","full_name":"Mosaic Shared Adaptation","paper_title":"MoSA: Mosaic Shared Adaptation of Large Language Models","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"","url":"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/dd37fdb24a4e1cfa3ed5c247217a7394-Abstract-Conference.html","code_url":"https://github.com/XiequnWang/MoSA-ICLR26","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\lambda_k M_k,\\quad M_k\\in\\{0,1\\}^{d_{out}\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{disjoint masks of a fixed balanced random tessellation (frozen)},\\ \\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}\\ \\text{trained}","trainable_params":"K\\ \\ (\\text{set to } r(d_{in}+d_{out}) \\text{ to match a rank-}r \\text{ LoRA budget})","locus":"any linear layer (paper: LLaMA-2-7B, LLaMA-3-8B)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"random weight sharing (hashing trick) on the update: every entry of \\(\\Delta W\\) is tied to one of \\(K\\) scalars via a fixed balanced random partition (the same partition is reused for all weights of the same shape); generally full rank; gradient of \\(\\lambda_k\\) is the group sum of \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}\\)","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"Broadcasting the scalars gives a dense \\(\\Delta W\\) that is added to \\(W_0\\) for zero-overhead inference.","identity_at_init":"\\(\\lambda = 0\\), so \\(\\Delta W = 0\\) exactly (official code, src/llamafactory/model/model_utils/mosa.py: deltas = zeros(num_groups)); unlike two-factor schemes this still gives non-zero gradients (group sums of \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal{L}\\)). The tessellation is sampled once and shared by all weights of the same shape","key_idea":"Replace low-rank factorisation by randomised, non-local sharing of individual update entries; equal-size random groups provably minimise the expected deviation from the unconstrained gradient and act as a regulariser.","results_note":"Matches or surpasses strong PEFT baselines (LoRA, DoRA, MoRA, HiRA) under strictly matched budgets on commonsense reasoning, ConvAI2 dialogue and MetaMathQA to GSM8K.","relations":[{"target":"uni-lora","type":"competes-with","note":"both use balanced random grouping (Uni-LoRA on LoRA factor entries with 1/sqrt(n_j) scaling, MoSA directly on Delta W entries); MoSA does not cite Uni-LoRA"},{"target":"hira","type":"competes-with","note":"high-rank update at LoRA's parameter count"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"strictly matched parameter budgets"}],"sources":["https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/dd37fdb24a4e1cfa3ed5c247217a7394-Abstract-Conference.html","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/file/dd37fdb24a4e1cfa3ed5c247217a7394-Paper-Conference.pdf"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"wang2026mosa","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2026mosa,\n  title = {{MoSA: Mosaic Shared Adaptation of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Wang and others},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  url = {https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/hash/dd37fdb24a4e1cfa3ed5c247217a7394-Abstract-Conference.html}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"frame","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\sum_k\\lambda_kM_k\\) is linear in \\(\\lambda\\); the masks are disjoint and non-empty, so the map is injective and \\(d=K\\).","base_point":"\\(\\lambda=0\\) exactly (official code), and the method is linear, hence regular. Unlike two-factor schemes the first gradient is nonzero (group sums of \\(\\nabla_W\\mathcal L\\)).","covariance":"The image is the space of matrices constant on the cells of a fixed random tessellation. It is covariant in law under coordinate permutations only (a torus \\(D_1XD_2\\) destroys piecewise constancy), and the same tessellation is reused for all boxes of the same shape.","rebasing":"Fixed tessellation and linear shape: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"MoSA is the linear method whose shape is the image of 'broadcast along a random partition', \\(\\mathbb R^K\\to\\Theta\\). It is a randomly tied coordinate method, dual to masking: SHiRA keeps \\(K\\) coordinates free and the rest at zero, while MoSA ties all coordinates into \\(K\\) free classes.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"A balanced random tessellation gives generically full-rank updates, so the maximum rank exceeds r."},{"target":"shira","test":"O1","reason":"SHiRA's image leaves \\(W_0\\) unchanged off its mask; MoSA moves every entry."},{"target":"c3a","test":"O1","reason":"C3A ties entries along block-circulant diagonals, MoSA along a random partition. These are two different tying subspaces, which meet only at \\(\\theta_0\\) generically."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"ssh","name":"SSH","full_name":"Sparse Spectrum Adaptation via Discrete Hartley Transformation","paper_title":"SSH: Sparse Spectrum Adaptation via Discrete Hartley Transformation","authors":"Shen et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NAACL 2025","arxiv":"2502.05539","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.522/","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\alpha\\,\\mathcal{H}^{-1}(\\Delta H),\\quad \\Delta H \\text{ sparse with } n \\text{ trainable entries: } \\lfloor\\delta n\\rfloor \\text{ at the highest-energy frequencies of } \\mathcal{H}(W_0),\\ \\text{the rest at random frequencies};\\ \\mathcal{H} = \\text{2-D discrete Hartley transform (real, self-inverse up to scale)}","trainable_params":"n","locus":"any linear layer (paper: RoBERTa, LLaMA-3.1-8B, ViT and vision-language models)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sparse coefficients in the real 2-D Hartley basis with support chosen partly by the spectral energy of \\(W_0\\) (weight-guided) and partly at random; inverse DHT maps them to a dense update","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The inverse DHT of the trained spectrum is a dense matrix that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"The paper initialises trainable spectral coefficients with Kaiming initialisation, so \\(\\Delta W\\) is non-zero at init (no exact identity stated)","key_idea":"Use the real-valued, symmetric Hartley transform (no complex arithmetic) and pick the trainable frequencies by the energy of the pretrained weight's spectrum, cutting parameters and FLOPs relative to FourierFT.","results_note":"Per the paper's Fig. 1, SSH scores 7.93 (GPT-4 judge) on LLaMA3.1-8B versus 7.95 for full fine-tuning with under 0.1% of the parameters, and needs up to 55% fewer GFLOPs than FourierFT; in its Table 4 it leads LoRA, DoRA, VeRA and FourierFT on MT-Bench/Vicuna for LLaMA2-7B/13B and LLaMA3.1-8B.","relations":[{"target":"fourierft","type":"builds-on","note":"replaces DFT by DHT and random by energy-guided frequency selection"},{"target":"loca","type":"competes-with","note":"both select informative frequencies (DCT vs DHT)"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05539","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.05539","https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.522/"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Yixian Shen","Qi Bi","Jia-Hong Huang","Hongyi Zhu","Andy D. Pimentel","Anuj Pathania"],"bibkey":"shen2025ssh","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shen2025ssh,\n  title = {{SSH: Sparse Spectrum Adaptation via Discrete Hartley Transformation}},\n  author = {Yixian Shen and Qi Bi and Jia-Hong Huang and Hongyi Zhu and Andy D. Pimentel and Anuj Pathania},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2502.05539},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.naacl-long.522/}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"shape":"\\(\\Delta W=\\alpha\\,\\mathcal H^{-1}(\\Delta H)\\) is linear in the \\(n\\) trained coefficients: a coordinate subspace in the real 2-D Hartley frame.","base_point":"The exactly pointed variant (zero coefficients) is regular. The paper's Kaiming init of the coefficients leaves a defect \\(\\alpha\\mathcal H^{-1}(\\Delta H_0)\\), treated like FourierFT's '≈' in the table.","gauge":"The Hartley basis \\(\\mathrm{cas}=\\cos+\\sin\\) is real and orthogonal with no conjugate-pair identification, so \\(\\rho\\) is injective (unlike FourierFT, which gains +1 per sampled conjugate pair).","covariance":"The DHT imposes cyclic index structure on rows and columns (\\(\\Pi\\)). Even that is only partial: a cyclic shift sends \\(\\mathrm{cas}(\\theta)\\) to \\(\\cos\\varphi\\,\\mathrm{cas}(\\theta)+\\sin\\varphi\\,\\mathrm{cas}(-\\theta)\\), so the span of selected Hartley atoms is shift-covariant only when the support is closed under \\((u,v)\\mapsto(-u,-v)\\). The energy-guided part of the support is read off \\(|\\mathcal H(W_0)|\\), which is not shift-invariant either.","rebasing":"Fixed indices: idempotent."},"categorical_reading":"SSH is \\(\\mathcal H_*\\) of a coordinate mask, a transported linear method: the same object as a mask in another frame, since transport is an automorphism and not an arrow. FourierFT's image is confined to point-symmetric matrices, whereas the Hartley frame also reaches the antisymmetric (sine) half.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[],"obstructions":[{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"FourierFT's image lies in the point-symmetric subspace \\(\\Delta W_{j,l}=\\Delta W_{-j,-l}\\) (Prop II.13). A Hartley pattern has a nonzero point-antisymmetric (sine) part unless its frequency is self-conjugate (\\((0,0)\\), \\((m/2,0)\\), \\((0,n/2)\\), \\((m/2,n/2)\\)), so Im SSH is not contained in Im FourierFT for any support with a non-self-conjugate frequency."},{"target":"loca","test":"O1","reason":"Hartley versus DCT-II frames: sparse supports in two different orthogonal frames give subspaces that meet only at \\(\\theta_0\\) generically."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"},{"id":"loca","name":"LoCA","full_name":"Location-aware Cosine Adaptation","paper_title":"LoCA: Location-Aware Cosine Adaptation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Du et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2502.06820","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06820","code_url":"","hf_peft":false,"hf_peft_config":"","formula_latex":"W = W_0 + \\alpha\\,C^{\\top}\\mathcal{S}(a, l)\\,D = W_0 + \\alpha\\sum_{i=1}^{\\mathcal{B}} a_i\\,C_{l^1_i,:}^{\\top}\\,D_{l^2_i,:},\\quad C\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{out}\\times d_{out}},\\ D\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d_{in}\\times d_{in}}\\ \\text{DCT matrices (fixed)};\\ a\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{B}} \\text{ trained};\\ \\text{locations } l \\text{ optimised by finite-difference gradients in alternation with } a \\text{ for the first } \\mathcal{B}_s \\text{ steps, then frozen}","trainable_params":"\\mathcal{B}\\ \\text{coefficients (the } \\mathcal{B} \\text{ 2-D locations are optimised only during the first } \\mathcal{B}_s \\text{ steps and are not counted in the paper)}","locus":"any linear layer (paper: language models such as RoBERTa/LLaMA and ViT)","modification_kind":"additive-weight","structure":"sparse coefficients in the 2-D DCT basis with learnable discrete support: \\(\\Delta W\\) is a sum of \\(\\mathcal{B}\\) rank-1 outer products of cosine vectors","mergeable":"yes","mergeable_reason":"The iDCT of the learned sparse spectrum is a dense matrix that merges into \\(W_0\\).","identity_at_init":"Coefficients \\(a\\) initialised to zero (\\(\\Delta W = 0\\)); locations initialised uniformly at random","key_idea":"Weight updates are asymptotically Gaussian, under which frequency-domain updates with well-chosen components can beat low-rank ones in expressivity, while random locations (as in FourierFT) lose to LoRA; so learn the locations too, using the real DCT equivalent of the optimal iDFT variant.","results_note":"Offers higher parameter efficiency than low-rank and random-frequency baselines on language and vision fine-tuning tasks at comparable computational cost.","relations":[{"target":"fourierft","type":"builds-on","note":"learned locations and DCT instead of fixed random DFT locations; analyses FourierFT's expressivity"},{"target":"ssh","type":"competes-with","note":"frequency-component selection"},{"target":"lora","type":"competes-with","note":"theoretical expressivity comparison with low-rank decomposition"}],"sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06820","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.06820"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"authors_full":["Zhekai Du","Yinjie Min","Jingjing Li","Ke Lu","Changliang Zou","Liuhua Peng","Tingjin Chu","Mingming Gong"],"bibkey":"du2025loca","bibtex":"@inproceedings{du2025loca,\n  title = {{LoCA: Location-Aware Cosine Adaptation for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zhekai Du and Yinjie Min and Jingjing Li and Ke Lu and Changliang Zou and Liuhua Peng and Tingjin Chu and Mingming Gong},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2502.06820},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06820}\n}","coords":{"kind":"R","shape":"Lin","base_point":"regular","gauge":"none","covariance":"Pi","merge":"M1","rebasing":"idempotent"},"coord_notes":{"kind":"R. During the first \\(\\mathcal B_s\\) steps the discrete support also moves (locations updated by finite-difference estimates), a warm-up support search like RoSA's or FISH's mask selection; afterwards the object is fixed.","shape":"After the search, \\(\\Delta W=\\alpha C^\\top\\mathcal S(a,l)D\\) is linear in \\(a\\): a coordinate mask in the 2-D DCT frame. Over all supports the reachable set is a union of \\(\\mathcal B\\)-dimensional subspaces (a Diff-pruning-type cone); the trained object is the Lin stratum.","base_point":"\\(a=0\\) exactly, and the method is linear, hence regular.","gauge":"The DCT basis is orthonormal and the locations are distinct, so the map is injective.","covariance":"The DCT-II frame imposes an order and reflection structure on coordinates (\\(\\Pi\\)); a neuron permutation changes the image.","rebasing":"With frozen locations rebasing is idempotent; re-running the location search after a merge would gain."},"categorical_reading":"LoCA is a transported mask, \\(\\mathrm{DCT}_*(\\text{mask})\\), whose support is chosen by a short search over the discrete set of supports. That search picks one object from a family of linear methods indexed by supports. The paper's expressivity analysis compares this union of subspaces with LoRA's cone.","entry_type":"method","core":false,"arrows":[{"target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(a)=\\big[a_1C_{l^1_1,:}^\\top\\ \\cdots\\ a_{\\mathcal B}C_{l^1_{\\mathcal B},:}^\\top\\big]\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(A_0),\\ A_0=\\big[D_{l^2_1,:};\\dots;D_{l^2_{\\mathcal B},:}\\big]","note":"Each coefficient is a rank-one cosine outer product, so LoCA factors through a one-sided method with the selected input cosines frozen as \\(A_0\\). The arrow is informative only when the selected input frequencies span a proper subspace of \\(\\mathbb R^{d_{in}}\\)."}],"obstructions":[{"target":"fourierft","test":"O1","reason":"DCT-II basis matrices are not point-symmetric modulo \\((m,n)\\), so they leave FourierFT's point-symmetric image."},{"target":"lora","test":"O2","reason":"Since \\(C,D\\) are orthogonal, \\(\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W=\\operatorname{rank}\\mathcal S(a,l)\\), which for generic coefficients is the term rank (maximum matching) of the support pattern. At the matched budget \\(\\mathcal B\\approx r(d_{in}+d_{out})\\) random locations give term rank close to \\(\\min(m,n)>r\\); \\(d=\\mathcal B>r(m+n-r)\\) also exceeds LoRA's dimension."}],"table_row":"R-additive","table_col":"Lin"}],"papers":[{"id":"unified-view-peft","title":"Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning","authors":"He et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2110.04366","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","claim":"Adapters, prefix tuning and LoRA are all modifications \\(\\Delta h\\) added to specific hidden states and differ only along four axes: functional form, insertion form (sequential vs parallel), modified representation (attention vs FFN) and composition function (simple, gated, scaled); prefix tuning is exactly a softmax-gated parallel adapter on attention heads.","relevance":"Provides the canonical coordinate system for the adapter family (serial vs parallel composition, gating and scaling as composition laws), directly suggesting a categorical reading where adapters are endomorphisms post-composed with sublayers and parallel adapters are sums of morphisms with a common domain.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366","https://arxiv.org/html/2110.04366","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.04366","https://github.com/jxhe/unify-parameter-efficient-tuning","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2110.04366"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","hf-peft","prompts","theory"],"method_ids":["parallel-adapter","mam-adapter"],"authors_full":["Junxian He","Chunting Zhou","Xuezhe Ma","Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick","Graham Neubig"],"bibkey":"he2021unified","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2021unified,\n  title = {{Towards a Unified View of Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Junxian He and Chunting Zhou and Xuezhe Ma and Taylor Berg-Kirkpatrick and Graham Neubig},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2110.04366},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.04366}\n}"},{"id":"adapter-effectiveness","title":"On the Effectiveness of Adapter-based Tuning for Pretrained Language Model Adaptation","authors":"He et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2106.03164","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.03164","claim":"Adapter-based tuning yields representations that deviate less from the pretrained model than fine-tuning, mitigating forgetting; it outperforms fine-tuning on low-resource and cross-lingual tasks and is more robust to overfitting and learning-rate changes.","relevance":"Empirical evidence that the near-identity, frozen-backbone structure of adapters acts as an implicit regularizer toward the pretrained function, a key property for any theory of PEFT as a small deformation of a base model.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.03164"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Ruidan He","Linlin Liu","Hai Ye","Qingyu Tan","Bosheng Ding","Liying Cheng","Jia-Wei Low","Lidong Bing","Luo Si"],"bibkey":"he2021effectiveness","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2021effectiveness,\n  title = {{On the Effectiveness of Adapter-based Tuning for Pretrained Language Model Adaptation}},\n  author = {Ruidan He and Linlin Liu and Hai Ye and Qingyu Tan and Bosheng Ding and Liying Cheng and Jia-Wei Low and Lidong Bing and Luo Si},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2106.03164},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.03164}\n}"},{"id":"adapterhub","title":"AdapterHub: A Framework for Adapting Transformers","authors":"Pfeiffer et al.","year":2020,"venue":"EMNLP 2020 (System Demonstrations)","arxiv":"2007.07779","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07779","claim":"A framework on top of HuggingFace Transformers for training, sharing and dynamically 'stitching-in' pre-trained adapters for different tasks and languages.","relevance":"Made adapters first-class, swappable, composable objects (the infrastructure behind MAD-X, AdapterFusion and AdapterDrop), the practical counterpart of treating adapters as modular morphisms.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07779"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jonas Pfeiffer","Andreas Rücklé","Clifton Poth","Aishwarya Kamath","Ivan Vulić","Sebastian Ruder","Kyunghyun Cho","Iryna Gurevych"],"bibkey":"pfeiffer2020adapterhub","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pfeiffer2020adapterhub,\n  title = {{AdapterHub: A Framework for Adapting Transformers}},\n  author = {Jonas Pfeiffer and Andreas Rücklé and Clifton Poth and Aishwarya Kamath and Ivan Vulić and Sebastian Ruder and Kyunghyun Cho and Iryna Gurevych},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2007.07779},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.07779}\n}"},{"id":"adapters-library","title":"Adapters: A Unified Library for Parameter-Efficient and Modular Transfer Learning","authors":"Poth et al.","year":2023,"venue":"EMNLP 2023 (System Demonstrations)","arxiv":"2311.11077","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11077","claim":"Unifies about ten adapter methods (sequential/parallel bottleneck, Compacter, prefix tuning, LoRA, (IA)\\(^3\\), MAM, UniPELT, and more) behind one interface with composition blocks (Stack, Fuse, Split, BatchSplit, Parallel, Average) for building complex adapter setups.","relevance":"Its composition blocks are an explicit operational algebra of adapters (sequential composition, fusion, splitting, averaging), a natural target for a categorical semantics of adapter composition.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11077","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/methods.html","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/method_combinations.html","https://docs.adapterhub.ml/adapter_composition.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Clifton Poth","Hannah Sterz","Indraneil Paul","Sukannya Purkayastha","Leon Engländer","Timo Imhof","Ivan Vulić","Sebastian Ruder","Iryna Gurevych","Jonas Pfeiffer"],"bibkey":"poth2023adapters","bibtex":"@inproceedings{poth2023adapters,\n  title = {{Adapters: A Unified Library for Parameter-Efficient and Modular Transfer Learning}},\n  author = {Clifton Poth and Hannah Sterz and Indraneil Paul and Sukannya Purkayastha and Leon Engländer and Timo Imhof and Ivan Vulić and Sebastian Ruder and Iryna Gurevych and Jonas Pfeiffer},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2311.11077},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11077}\n}"},{"id":"llm-adapters","title":"LLM-Adapters: An Adapter Family for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models","authors":"Hu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"EMNLP 2023","arxiv":"2304.01933","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01933","claim":"A framework integrating series adapters, parallel adapters, prompt-based and reparameterization methods into LLaMA, BLOOM and GPT-J; the best placements are after the MLP for series adapters, parallel to the MLP for parallel adapters, and on both attention and MLP for LoRA, and 7B models with adapters can rival much larger zero-shot models on math and commonsense reasoning.","relevance":"Transfers the serial vs parallel adapter taxonomy to decoder-only LLMs and gives placement evidence that any structural theory of insertion sites must explain.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01933","https://arxiv.org/html/2304.01933"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zhiqiang Hu","Lei Wang","Yihuai Lan","Wanyu Xu","Ee-Peng Lim","Lidong Bing","Xing Xu","Soujanya Poria","Roy Ka-Wei Lee"],"bibkey":"hu2023llm","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hu2023llm,\n  title = {{LLM-Adapters: An Adapter Family for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Zhiqiang Hu and Lei Wang and Yihuai Lan and Wanyu Xu and Ee-Peng Lim and Lidong Bing and Xing Xu and Soujanya Poria and Roy Ka-Wei Lee},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2304.01933},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.01933}\n}"},{"id":"modular-deep-learning","title":"Modular Deep Learning","authors":"Pfeiffer et al.","year":2023,"venue":"TMLR 2023","arxiv":"2302.11529","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.11529","claim":"Surveys modular architectures in which parameter-efficient modules are conditionally routed and aggregated, giving a unified view of computation functions, routing functions and aggregation functions across many research threads.","relevance":"Its decomposition into modules, routing and aggregation is close to a categorical description (objects = representations, morphisms = modules, routing/aggregation = coproduct/colimit-like combinators) and covers adapters, fusion and mixtures.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.11529","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.11529","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.11529","https://www.modulardeeplearning.com/","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2302.11529"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","composition","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jonas Pfeiffer","Sebastian Ruder","Ivan Vulić","Edoardo Maria Ponti"],"bibkey":"pfeiffer2023modular","bibtex":"@article{pfeiffer2023modular,\n  title = {{Modular Deep Learning}},\n  author = {Jonas Pfeiffer and Sebastian Ruder and Ivan Vulić and Edoardo Maria Ponti},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2302.11529},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.11529}\n}"},{"id":"vl-adapter","title":"VL-Adapter: Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning for Vision-and-Language Tasks","authors":"Sung et al.","year":2021,"venue":"CVPR 2022","arxiv":"2112.06825","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.06825","claim":"Benchmarks Adapter, HyperFormer, Compacter and prompt tuning on image-text and video-text tasks with CLIP-BART/T5 in a unified multi-task setup; a single adapter shared across tasks (4.18% of parameters for image-text, 3.39% for video-text) matches full fine-tuning.","relevance":"Shows that weight sharing of adapters across tasks (one module serving many tasks) is often best, informing how adapter objects should be indexed by tasks in a unified theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.06825","https://arxiv.org/html/2112.06825"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yi-Lin Sung","Jaemin Cho","Mohit Bansal"],"bibkey":"sung2021vl","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sung2021vl,\n  title = {{VL-Adapter: Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning for Vision-and-Language Tasks}},\n  author = {Yi-Lin Sung and Jaemin Cho and Mohit Bansal},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2112.06825},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.06825}\n}"},{"id":"delta-tuning","title":"Parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large-scale pre-trained language models","authors":"Ding et al.","year":2022,"venue":"Nature Machine Intelligence 2023","arxiv":"2203.06904","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-023-00626-4","claim":"Formalizes delta tuning and divides methods into addition-based (adapters, prompts), specification-based (selective) and reparameterization-based (low-rank) families, with theoretical perspectives (optimization and optimal control) and experiments on over 100 NLP tasks.","relevance":"The addition-based class is the adapter family of this catalog; the three-way taxonomy is a baseline that a categorical classification should refine.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06904","https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-023-00626-4","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1038/s42256-023-00626-4","https://arxiv.org/html/2203.06904","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.06904","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2203.06904"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","prompts","selective","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Ning Ding","Yujia Qin","Guang Yang","Fuchao Wei","Zonghan Yang","Yusheng Su","Shengding Hu","Yulin Chen","Chi-Min Chan","Weize Chen","Jing Yi","Weilin Zhao","Xiaozhi Wang","Zhiyuan Liu","Hai-Tao Zheng","Jianfei Chen","Yang Liu","Jie Tang","Juanzi Li","Maosong Sun"],"bibkey":"ding2022parameter","bibtex":"@article{ding2022parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large-scale pre-trained language models}},\n  author = {Ning Ding and Yujia Qin and Guang Yang and Fuchao Wei and Zonghan Yang and Yusheng Su and Shengding Hu and Yulin Chen and Chi-Min Chan and Weize Chen and Jing Yi and Weilin Zhao and Xiaozhi Wang and Zhiyuan Liu and Hai-Tao Zheng and Jianfei Chen and Yang Liu and Jie Tang and Juanzi Li and Maosong Sun},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {Nature Machine Intelligence},\n  eprint = {2203.06904},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-023-00626-4}\n}"},{"id":"peft-design-spaces","title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning Design Spaces","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2301.01821","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01821","claim":"Parameterizes PEFT designs by layer grouping, trainable-parameter allocation, tunable groups and strategy assignment, and finds patterns (spindle grouping, uniform allocation, tune all groups, proper strategy per group) that yield methods outperforming individual adapter, prefix, BitFit and LoRA strategies.","relevance":"Treats the choice of adapter type and insertion site as points in a structured design space, a precursor to treating PEFT methods as structured objects rather than isolated recipes.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01821","https://github.com/amazon-science/peft-design-spaces","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2301.01821"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jiaao Chen","Aston Zhang","Xingjian Shi","Mu Li","Alex Smola","Diyi Yang"],"bibkey":"chen2023parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2023parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning Design Spaces}},\n  author = {Jiaao Chen and Aston Zhang and Xingjian Shi and Mu Li and Alex Smola and Diyi Yang},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2301.01821},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01821}\n}"},{"id":"peft-survey-han","title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Large Models: A Comprehensive Survey","authors":"Han et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2024","arxiv":"2403.14608","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14608","claim":"Comprehensive survey of PEFT algorithms (additive incl. adapters, selective, reparameterized, hybrid), their computational overhead, applications and system design.","relevance":"Standard reference taxonomy for placing the adapter family (serial, parallel, hybrid adapters) alongside other PEFT families.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14608","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.14608","https://openreview.net/pdf?id=lIsCS8b6zj","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2403.14608"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["adapters","hf-peft","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zeyu Han","Chao Gao","Jinyang Liu","Jeff Zhang","Sai Qian Zhang"],"bibkey":"han2024parameter","bibtex":"@article{han2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Large Models: A Comprehensive Survey}},\n  author = {Zeyu Han and Chao Gao and Jinyang Liu and Jeff Zhang and Sai Qian Zhang},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2403.14608},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14608}\n}"},{"id":"lora-lr-scaling-across-ranks","title":"Learning Rate Scaling across LoRA Ranks and Transfer to Full Finetuning","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2602.06204","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06204","claim":"Maximal-Update Adaptation (\\( \\mu \\)A): in the joint limit of width \\( n \\) and rank \\( r\\to\\infty \\), with SignSGD as a proxy for Adam and a fixed number of steps, requiring the LoRA feature increment to be \\( \\Theta(1) \\) fixes the learning-rate scaling. For Init[A] (random \\( A\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1/n) \\), \\( B=0 \\)) and multiplier \\( \\alpha=r^{-\\gamma} \\) on \\( \\alpha BAx \\), the increment is \\( \\max\\{\\Theta(\\eta\\alpha r),\\Theta(\\eta^2\\alpha rn)\\} \\) and \\( \\eta=\\Theta(n^{-1/2}r^{-(1-\\gamma)/2}) \\). So the optimal LR shrinks as \\( r^{-1/2} \\) for \\( \\alpha=1 \\) and as \\( r^{-1/4} \\) for rsLoRA's \\( \\alpha=r^{-1/2} \\), and is rank-invariant for \\( \\alpha=r^{-1} \\) (standard \\( \\alpha/r \\)), though with a narrow stable range. For Init[B] (random \\( B\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1/r) \\), \\( A=0 \\)) with \\( \\alpha=1 \\), \\( \\eta=\\Theta(n^{-1}) \\): rank-invariant and equal to full fine-tuning's scaling, so LRs tuned on LoRA transfer to full FT. Under Init[A] learning runs mainly through \\( B \\), and under Init[B] mainly through \\( A \\). Experiments span LLM SFT, VLMs, diffusion and RLVR.","relevance":"Settles the rsLoRA versus LoRA-Without-Regret tension in the lora-core notes: which LR-rank law holds depends jointly on the init side and on how the multiplier scales with \\( r \\). Under this abstraction rsLoRA's \\( \\alpha\\propto r^{-1/2} \\) still needs \\( \\eta\\propto r^{-1/4} \\). The near rank-invariant optimum with \\( \\alpha/r \\) and Init[A] reported by LoRA Without Regret, and its LoRA LR well above full FT's, are consistent in direction with the \\( \\gamma=1 \\) case, whose \\( \\Theta(n^{-1/2}) \\) exceeds full FT's \\( \\Theta(n^{-1}) \\). It is the rank-direction companion to the width-direction analyses already catalogued (lora-plus-width-scaling, hayou-lora-init-dynamics, rslora-rank-stabilization). Its first- and second-order terms (\\( \\eta\\alpha \\) versus \\( \\eta^2\\alpha \\)) are the same signal/drift split as the scaling-factor paper below. PreFT cites it to choose its ReFT prefactor.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06204","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.06204","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/60938"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Nan Chen","Soledad Villar","Soufiane Hayou"],"bibkey":"chen2026learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2026learning,\n  title = {{Learning Rate Scaling across LoRA Ranks and Transfer to Full Finetuning}},\n  author = {Nan Chen and Soledad Villar and Soufiane Hayou},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.06204},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06204}\n}"},{"id":"lora-alpha-signal-drift","title":"The Hidden Power of Scaling Factor in LoRA Optimization","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2606.12883","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12883","claim":"For \\( W=W_0+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA \\), a step decomposes exactly into a signal \\( \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}(B\\Delta A+\\Delta B\\,A) \\) and a bilinear drift \\( \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\Delta B\\,\\Delta A \\). The parameter Hessian splits likewise into \\( J^{\\top}H_\\ell J\\succeq0 \\) and an indefinite structural term. At LoRA init (\\( B=0 \\), \\( A_{ij}\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma_A^2) \\)) the signal curvature is suppressed, \\( \\mathbb E\\,\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{Signal}}=\\alpha^2(\\sigma_A^2/r)\\,\\mathrm{Tr}\\,H_\\ell \\), so full-FT hyperparameters are overly conservative. Under Adam the signal scales as \\( \\alpha\\eta \\) and the drift as \\( \\alpha\\eta^2 \\): raising \\( \\alpha \\) accelerates without degrading the signal-to-drift ratio \\( \\Theta(1/\\eta) \\), while raising \\( \\eta \\) degrades it, so \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\eta \\) are not interchangeable. Matching curvature to full FT gives \\( \\alpha=\\Theta(\\sqrt r/\\sigma_A) \\). Empirically \\( \\alpha^*\\approx C\\sqrt r \\) with \\( C\\approx256 \\) (Llama-3-1B, Tulu-3), 512 (8B) and 1024 (OpenThoughts). LoRA-\\( \\alpha \\) sets \\( \\Delta W=\\tfrac{\\alpha\\,\\alpha_{base}}{r}BA \\) with \\( \\alpha_{base}=256\\sqrt r \\) (or \\( \\sqrt r/\\sigma_A \\)) and uses the full-FT learning rate.","relevance":"Pairs with the \\( \\mu \\)A paper on the same scaling question from the multiplier side. The effective multiplier \\( \\alpha/r=C/\\sqrt r \\) is rsLoRA's \\( 1/\\sqrt r \\) law (rslora, rslora-rank-stabilization) with prefactor \\( C\\approx256 \\), which is \\( 256/\\sqrt r \\) times the \\( \\alpha=r \\) heuristic, i.e. 16x to 256x larger for \\( r \\) from 256 down to 1 (App. F). The signal/drift split (\\( \\alpha\\eta \\) versus \\( \\alpha\\eta^2 \\)) agrees with \\( \\mu \\)A's first- and second-order increment terms, and it is the same tangent-versus-bilinear split of the update of \\( BA \\) that PRISM exploits to keep DP noise in the tangent space.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12883","https://arxiv.org/html/2606.12883","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/153734"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zicheng Zhang","Haoran Li","Jiaxing Wang","Guoqiang Gong","Anqi Li","Yudong Hu","Ting Xiong","Yurong Gao","Junxing Hu","Zhida Jiang","Yifeng Zhang","Pengzhang Liu","Qixia Jiang"],"bibkey":"zhang2026hidden","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2026hidden,\n  title = {{The Hidden Power of Scaling Factor in LoRA Optimization}},\n  author = {Zicheng Zhang and Haoran Li and Jiaxing Wang and Guoqiang Gong and Anqi Li and Yudong Hu and Ting Xiong and Yurong Gao and Junxing Hu and Zhida Jiang and Yifeng Zhang and Pengzhang Liu and Qixia Jiang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2606.12883},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.12883}\n}"},{"id":"muon-lora-uniform-spectral-growth","title":"Uniform Spectral Growth and Convergence of Muon in LoRA-Style Matrix Factorization","authors":"Kang et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026 (poster; accepted as 'Uniform Spectral Growth under Factor-wise Muon Orthogonalization in Matrix Factorization and LoRA')","arxiv":"2602.06385","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06385","claim":"For \\( \\min_{B,A}\\tfrac12\\|BA-Y\\|_F^2 \\) from LoRA-style init (output-side factor \\( B(0)=0 \\), input-side factor \\( A(0)=\\gamma N \\) small Gaussian), spectral gradient flow orthogonalises each factor's gradient separately with \\( \\mathcal T_\\beta(M)=(MM^{\\top}+\\beta I)^{-1/2}M \\). Under this flow the core product stays near-diagonal (\\( O(\\gamma) \\) off-diagonal), and the square roots of all active singular values of \\( BA \\) grow at unit rate, \\( \\tfrac{d}{dt}\\sqrt{d_i}=1+O(\\varepsilon^{1/4}) \\). So the smallest target singular value is reached first, the opposite of plain gradient flow's largest-first, saddle-to-saddle learning. For almost all inits the flow converges to a global minimum (the best rank-\\( r \\) approximation of \\( Y \\)) or the factor norms diverge; with \\( \\ell_2 \\) regularisation convergence is global. The balancedness quantity \\( B^{\\top}B-AA^{\\top} \\) conserved by plain gradient flow need not be conserved here. Near-uniform growth is also observed with Newton-Schulz Muon in LoRA fine-tuning of LLMs.","relevance":"Extends the catalogued gradient-flow papers (lora-gradient-flow-matrix-factorization, implicit-regularization-deep-matrix-factorization, conservation-laws-gradient-flows) to Muon-type optimizers. Factor-wise orthogonalisation can break the conservation law tied to LoRA's \\( GL(r) \\) symmetry and replaces the incremental (largest-first, saddle-to-saddle) learning of plain gradient flow with equal-rate growth in \\( \\sqrt{\\sigma_i} \\), so arguments that rely on largest-first ordering do not carry over to factor-wise Muon. It also cautions that uniform growth amplifies all \\( r \\) directions, which can hurt when the useful update has rank below \\( r \\).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06385","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.06385","https://neurips.cc/static/virtual/data/neurips-2026-orals-posters.json","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2026/poster/151601"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Changmin Kang","Jihun Yun","Baekrok Shin","Yeseul Cho","Chulhee Yun"],"bibkey":"kang2026uniform","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kang2026uniform,\n  title = {{Uniform Spectral Growth and Convergence of Muon in LoRA-Style Matrix Factorization}},\n  author = {Changmin Kang and Jihun Yun and Baekrok Shin and Yeseul Cho and Chulhee Yun},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2602.06385},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06385}\n}"},{"id":"weight-updates-as-activation-shifts","title":"Weight Updates as Activation Shifts: A Principled Framework for Steering","authors":"Adila et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2603.00425","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00425","claim":"For a GLU MLP \\( W_d(\\phi(W_gh)\\odot W_uh) \\), a first-order input perturbation \\( \\Delta h \\) reproduces the first-order effect of perturbing \\( W_g,W_u \\) but misses the down-projection term \\( (\\Delta W_d)m \\), which a post-MLP additive edit can absorb. With the fine-tuned hidden state as an oracle target, the MLP output accounts for only 40-70% of the block-output magnitude across layers (Llama-3.2, Winogrande), so a post-MLP edit misses the attention and skip-connection share. A theorem gives the best linear post-block approximation of post-MLP steering in terms of principal angles between sample-space singular subspaces. A post-block bottleneck adapter \\( h\\mapsto h+W_2\\phi(W_1h) \\) (after the skip connection) at every layer comes within 0.2-0.9% of full SFT accuracy with 0.04% of parameters and beats ReFT and LoRA. Weight and activation updates express different function classes, and joint training with \\( W_2\\leftarrow(I-VV^{\\top})W_2 \\), \\( V \\) an orthonormal basis of \\( \\mathrm{col}(B_{\\mathrm{LoRA}}) \\), beats either alone by up to 3.8%.","relevance":"A formal bridge between the activation-editing locus (loreft, red, road; representation-engineering, inference-time-intervention) and the weight locus (lora): to first order a weight update on the MLP is an activation shift, except for the output-projection term, and the post-block residual stream is where the activation locus can absorb it. It explains why ReFT-style residual-stream edits work, it is the same locus PreFT's \\( \\text{DiReFT}_P \\) uses, and its orthogonality-constrained joint adapter is a hybrid entry point between the two loci.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00425","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.00425","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/62685"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Dyah Adila","John Cooper","Alexander Yun","Avi Trost","Frederic Sala"],"bibkey":"adila2026weight","bibtex":"@inproceedings{adila2026weight,\n  title = {{Weight Updates as Activation Shifts: A Principled Framework for Steering}},\n  author = {Dyah Adila and John Cooper and Alexander Yun and Avi Trost and Frederic Sala},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2603.00425},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.00425}\n}"},{"id":"diffract-spectral-domain-adaptation","title":"Diffract: Spectral View of LLM Domain Adaptation","authors":"Borodin et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight, with oral presentation)","arxiv":"2608.10850","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10850","claim":"On OLMo 2 (1B, 7B, 13B, plus released 32B checkpoints), continual pre-training on math, instruction, code and text leaves the singular-value spectra of the weights almost unchanged. Adaptation happens mainly through singular-vector changes concentrated near spectral peaks of attention matrices. Attention heads become heterogeneous as the pre-training budget grows. A head-importance criterion (CPT-delta Frobenius norms relative to a text-CPT reference) lets up to 60% of head updates be rewound to pre-trained values without measurable loss, and rewinding about 15% of heads raises math accuracy by up to 4% (7B). Dropping the smallest singular values of the CPT delta costs no measurable GSM8K accuracy up to 50% of them for 7B and 70% for 13B (1B deltas are less compressible), and linear interpolation between CPT checkpoints of different domains is smooth ('domain connectivity').","relevance":"Full-parameter CPT evidence on the shape of useful updates; it is not a PEFT method. Fixed spectra with rotating singular vectors are the signature of multiplicative orthogonal updates (oft, boft, goft preserve the singular values of \\( W_0 \\) exactly). A one-sided rotation changes the singular vectors on one side only, and the paper measures agreement of left singular vectors only (App. A.3), so it does not settle whether the observed rotations are one- or two-sided. The same finding cuts against value-only spectral tuning (svf), which cannot rotate singular vectors, so the seed's reading of it as support for SVF/SVFT/PiSSA/MiLoRA needs this qualification. The delta-truncation result supports low-rank compressibility of adaptation deltas (lora). Head rewind is selective sparsity at head granularity, and domain connectivity supports linear merging of domain deltas (task-arithmetic).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10850","https://arxiv.org/html/2608.10850","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/oral/71079","https://github.com/Risk-AI-Research/diffract"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Nikita Borodin","Maria Krylova","Artem Zabolotnyi","Dmitry Aspisov","Egor Shikov","Nikita Tyuplyaev","Oleg Travkin","Roman Alferov","Dmitry Vinichenko"],"bibkey":"borodin2026diffract","bibtex":"@inproceedings{borodin2026diffract,\n  title = {{Diffract: Spectral View of LLM Domain Adaptation}},\n  author = {Nikita Borodin and Maria Krylova and Artem Zabolotnyi and Dmitry Aspisov and Egor Shikov and Nikita Tyuplyaev and Oleg Travkin and Roman Alferov and Dmitry Vinichenko},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2608.10850},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.10850}\n}"},{"id":"recycling-loras-adaptive-merging","title":"The Appeal and Reality of Recycling LoRAs with Adaptive Merging","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2602.12323","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12323","claim":"The study uses a pool of 958 user-contributed Hugging Face LoRAs for Llama 3.1 8B-Instruct and 62 target tasks with 100 labelled examples each. Adaptive merging (LoraHub, \\( \\pi \\)-Tuning, AdaMerging, and a new method found by a design-space search) beats the base model but gives limited benefit over simply training a target-task LoRA on the same 100 examples (80 train, 20 validation); non-adaptive merges with fixed uniform coefficients (averaging, TIES, TSV) underperform the adaptive ones. Arrow, a zero-shot per-token router, is grouped with the non-adaptive methods in Fig. 2, although Sec. 4 lists it with the adaptive ones. When the target-task LoRA is in the pool, which recycled LoRAs are chosen hardly matters, and LoRAs with random parameter values work about as well. This points to a regularisation effect rather than positive transfer. Positive transfer does appear with a bespoke pool of highly relevant, consistently trained LoRAs.","relevance":"An empirical counterweight for the composition family (lorahub, arrow, adamerging, ties-merging, tsv-merge): reported gains from merging adapters largely reflect curated, task-relevant pools. In atlas terms, a composition operator on adapter space adds task information only when the pool contains relevant modules; otherwise it acts as a regulariser on the target-task adapter. PHATGOOSE-style routing is not evaluated, so that extension of the conclusion is untested.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12323","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.12323","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/62647"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Haokun Liu","Gyung Hyun Je","Marco Ciccone","Zhenlin Xu","Prasanth YSS","Colin Raffel"],"bibkey":"liu2026appeal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2026appeal,\n  title = {{The Appeal and Reality of Recycling LoRAs with Adaptive Merging}},\n  author = {Haokun Liu and Gyung Hyun Je and Marco Ciccone and Zhenlin Xu and Prasanth YSS and Colin Raffel},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2602.12323},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12323}\n}"},{"id":"zo-learning-dynamics-kernel","title":"Learning Dynamics of Zeroth-Order Optimization: A Kernel Perspective","authors":"Li et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2605.03373","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03373","claim":"One step of multi-perturbation ZO-SGD (two-point estimator, \\( P \\) perturbations) changes model log-probabilities through a projected eNTK \\( \\mathcal K(x_o,x_u;U)=\\nabla_\\theta z(x_o)^{\\top}UU^{\\top}\\nabla_\\theta z(x_u) \\), with \\( U\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times P} \\) the stacked normalised perturbations. By an inner-product Johnson-Lindenstrauss lemma the kernel error is \\( \\epsilon=O(\\sqrt{\\ln V/P}) \\), so the gap between ZO and first-order one-step dynamics is \\( \\lesssim\\sqrt{V\\log V/P} \\) times model-dependent factors. This depends on the output size \\( V \\), not the parameter count \\( d \\), which explains ZO fine-tuning's success without the low-effective-rank Hessian assumption. Gaussian and Rademacher perturbations behave alike, and fidelity improves as \\( O(1/\\sqrt P) \\) (validated on LeNet/MNIST and OPT-125M to 1.3B on SST-2).","relevance":"Extends kernel-view-lm-finetuning, which used a JL argument to show LoRA preserves the SGD fine-tuning kernel, to zeroth-order training. In both, a random low-dimensional projection approximately preserves the eNTK: ZO (mezo, zo-bench) redraws the projection every step in parameter space, while LoRA-style subspace training fixes one. This gives a common kernel-level account of the ZO entries alongside MeZO.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03373","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.03373","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63704"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zhe Li","Bicheng Ying","Zidong Liu","Haibo Yang"],"bibkey":"li2026learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2026learning,\n  title = {{Learning Dynamics of Zeroth-Order Optimization: A Kernel Perspective}},\n  author = {Zhe Li and Bicheng Ying and Zidong Liu and Haibo Yang},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2605.03373},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03373}\n}"},{"id":"lora-knowledge-memory","title":"Understanding LoRA as Knowledge Memory: An Empirical Analysis","authors":"Back et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2603.01097","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.01097","claim":"Rank sweeps from 2 to 1024 with knowledge loads of 1K-20K tokens on Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen3-8B (PhoneBook key-value pairs, CounterFact edits) show that LoRA's memorisation capacity grows with rank but saturates at a rank-dependent load. Knowledge stored per trainable parameter peaks at low rank (around r=4). Among synthetic formats (QA, summary, rewrite), QA pairs give the largest gains and the best token-efficiency, mixing formats helps further, and a stronger generator (GPT-4.1 versus Llama-3.1-8B) helps. In multi-LoRA systems, top-1 embedding routing can underperform one LoRA holding everything, and merging the top-k retrieved modules trades routing errors against interference: TIES is best, and vanilla concatenation collapses mainly through scale mismatch: rescaling each factor by \\( 1/\\sqrt N \\) recovers most of the gap to linear averaging. LoRA memory works best alongside RAG or ICL rather than replacing them.","relevance":"Updates the 2025 lora-knowledge-capacity entry with capacity curves, rank-efficiency measurements and multi-module composition. It is empirical analysis, not theory. It treats rank as a capacity knob (cf. lora-learns-less-forgets-less) and gives composition evidence relevant to document- and knowledge-module methods (doc-to-lora, text-to-lora, loraretriever, lorahub), including the calibration fact that concatenated adapters need \\( 1/\\sqrt N \\) factor scaling.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.01097","https://arxiv.org/html/2603.01097","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/66786"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Seungju Back","Dongwoo Lee","Naun Kang","Taehee Lee","S. K. Hong","Youngjune Gwon","Sungjin Ahn"],"bibkey":"back2026understanding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{back2026understanding,\n  title = {{Understanding LoRA as Knowledge Memory: An Empirical Analysis}},\n  author = {Seungju Back and Dongwoo Lee and Naun Kang and Taehee Lee and S. K. Hong and Youngjune Gwon and Sungjin Ahn},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2603.01097},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.01097}\n}"},{"id":"lora-gd-convergence-rate","title":"On the Convergence Rate of LoRA Gradient Descent","authors":"Mu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026 (spotlight, oral presentation)","arxiv":"2512.18248","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18248","claim":"For vanilla LoRA gradient descent (\\(A\\) and \\(B\\) updated simultaneously on \\(\\mathcal{L}(BA)\\)), assuming only that the loss is \\(L\\)-smooth and bounded below in the full weight \\(W\\), \\(\\min_{t<T}\\|\\nabla\\mathcal{J}(V_t)\\|^2=O(1/\\log T)\\), improving to \\(O(1/T)\\) if the iterates stay bounded. The factor-space loss is not smooth and the iterates are not assumed bounded. The proof stacks \\(V=[B;A^{\\top}]\\), writes \\(BA=E_1VV^{\\top}E_2\\) (a symmetric Burer--Monteiro form), proves a descent lemma with extra terms up to fourth order in the step, and uses the adaptive step \\(\\eta_t=\\min\\{1/(4\\sqrt2L(\\|V_t\\|^2+\\|\\nabla\\mathcal{L}(B_tA_t)\\|)),1\\}\\) together with the worst-case growth \\(\\|V_t\\|^2=O(t)\\). The rate has no explicit dependence on the rank \\(r\\) (only through \\(\\|V_0\\|\\)), extends to many adapted matrices by block extraction, and \\(V=0\\) is always a stationary point of the reparametrised loss.","relevance":"The first non-asymptotic guarantee for plain LoRA gradient descent without boundedness assumptions, complementing the landscape result in lora-converges-or-fails-loudly. It locates the difficulty in the reparametrisation: the factor-space loss is only 'Lipschitz-like', with a constant growing like \\(\\|V\\|^2\\), so the usable step shrinks as the factors grow. This is the conditioning issue that the gauge-aware optimisers in the catalogue target (riemannian-preconditioned-lora, lora-rite, riemannion, balanced-lora). The stationary point at \\(V=0\\) exists whatever the loss of \\(W_0\\), one concrete way LoRA and full fine-tuning can end in different places.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18248","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.18248","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/65870","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2512.18248","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/oral/71053"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Siqiao Mu","Diego Klabjan"],"bibkey":"mu2025convergence","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mu2025convergence,\n  title = {{On the Convergence Rate of LoRA Gradient Descent}},\n  author = {Siqiao Mu and Diego Klabjan},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2512.18248},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.18248}\n}"},{"id":"lora-forgetting-mean-field-attention","title":"Understanding Catastrophic Forgetting In LoRA via Mean-Field Attention Dynamics","authors":"Koubbi et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2402.15415","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15415","claim":"In a tied-weight, continuous-depth self-attention model where tokens evolve as interacting particles (on the sphere under Post-LayerNorm) and LoRA is a low-rank perturbation of \\((Q,K,V)\\), forgetting is measured as drift of the emergent cluster geometry, which the authors correlate with base-task perplexity. (i) A finite-time Wasserstein bound: \\(W_2(\\mu_t,\\nu_t)\\le c\\,\\varepsilon\\,e^{c\\,e^{ct}}\\) for perturbations of operator norm \\(\\varepsilon\\) (Prop. 3.1). (ii) For \\(A=K^{\\top}Q=V\\succeq0\\) with a spectral gap and a symmetric update \\(\\Delta V=\\Delta A\\), if \\(\\mathrm{gap}+a>2\\|b\\|+\\|E\\|_{op}\\), with \\(a=u_1^{\\top}\\Delta Vu_1\\), \\(b=P_{\\perp}\\Delta Vu_1\\) and \\(E=P_{\\perp}\\Delta VP_{\\perp}\\), the limiting cluster moves from \\(u_1\\) to \\(\\tilde u_1\\) with \\(\\|u_1-\\tilde u_1\\|\\lesssim(2\\|b\\|+\\|E\\|_{op})/(\\mathrm{gap}+a)\\) (Prop. 3.3). (iii) For i.i.d. random rank-\\(r\\) value adapters scaled by \\(\\eta_L\\) at depth \\(L\\) there is a norm transition: \\(\\eta_L\\ll\\sqrt L\\) keeps the mean-square distance to the pretrained cluster at \\(O(1/L)\\), while \\(\\eta_L=\\sqrt L\\) gives a diffusive regime with \\(\\sum_i\\mathbb{E}\\|x_i^{\\ell}-u_1\\|^2\\simeq n\\sum_{i\\ge2}\\kappa/(2(\\lambda_1-\\lambda_i)+d\\kappa)\\) (Thm. 4.2). (iv) A depth transition: perturbed tokens track the base dynamics up to a critical depth \\(T^*\\) and then move to a new clustering (Thm. 4.6). Experiments on Qwen3-0.6B show a loss transition in the perturbation norm; rank matters during LoRA training but not for isotropic random adapters, learned updates align with the top direction \\(u_1\\), and an empirical bifurcation depth moves earlier during fine-tuning. Llama-2-7B is used only to check clustering and the spectral assumptions, which few of its layers satisfy. The May 2026 v2 replaces the 2024 results.","relevance":"A dynamical-systems account of why LoRA forgets less but not nothing (cf. lora-learns-less-forgets-less): forgetting is governed by the update norm relative to a \\(\\sqrt L\\) depth scale and by alignment with small-gap eigendirections of the pretrained attention. This gives a toy-model rationale for orthogonal-subspace and norm-controlled variants (o-lora, which the paper cites, and inflora) and opens a forgetting axis in the theory index, which so far covers optimisation and expressivity.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15415","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.15415","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/64536","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2402.15415"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Hugo Koubbi","Louis Hernandez","Matthieu Boussard"],"bibkey":"koubbi2024understanding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{koubbi2024understanding,\n  title = {{Understanding Catastrophic Forgetting In LoRA via Mean-Field Attention Dynamics}},\n  author = {Hugo Koubbi and Louis Hernandez and Matthieu Boussard},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.15415},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15415}\n}"},{"id":"model-merging-scaling-laws","title":"Model Merging Scaling Laws in Large Language Models","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2509.24244","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.24244","claim":"The expected cross-entropy of merging \\(k\\) experts into a base of size \\(N\\) follows a floor-plus-tail law \\(\\mathbb{E}[L\\mid N,k]=L_*+BN^{-\\beta}+A_0N^{-\\gamma}/(k+b)\\), fitted in-domain and cross-domain over 10,866 merged models (Qwen2.5 0.5B to 72B, nine math, science and code domains, \\(k=1,\\dots,9\\)) for Average, Task Arithmetic, TIES and DARE (\\(R^2>0.98\\)). Larger bases lower the floor and shrink the tail; most gains arrive early (\\(k=5\\) and \\(k=6\\) cross 85% and 90% of the nine-expert improvement); the variance across expert subsets shrinks like \\(1/k\\); and differences between methods compress at scale. A second-order Taylor argument for equal-weight merging (\\(\\alpha_{i,k}=c/k\\)) gives \\(\\mathbb{E}[L]=L_\\infty(N)+A(N)/k+O(k^{-3/2})\\), with \\(L_\\infty=L(\\theta_0)+c\\,g^{\\top}\\mu+\\tfrac{c^2}{2}\\mu^{\\top}H\\mu\\) and \\(A=\\tfrac{c^2}{2}\\mathrm{Tr}(H\\Sigma)\\), where \\(\\mu\\) and \\(\\Sigma\\) are the mean and covariance of the task vectors. Three early points (\\(k\\in\\{1,2,4\\}\\)) are enough to forecast the whole \\(k\\)-curve.","relevance":"Gives the post-hoc (P) kind a quantitative law: the return from merging is a curvature-weighted variance term \\(\\mathrm{Tr}(H\\Sigma)/k\\) on top of a mean-direction floor, the mean/covariance split that ties-merging, dare and merging-at-scale use informally. The controlled experts are full-parameter fine-tunes (App. D: lr \\(10^{-5}\\), ZeRO-3; the released OpenRLHF training script passes no LoRA option), not LoRAs; the authors note that LoRA rank and expert capacity would shift the fitted floor and tail, so transfer to adapter merging is plausible but untested.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.24244","https://arxiv.org/html/2509.24244","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63590","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2509.24244","https://github.com/InfiXAI/Merging-Scaling-Law"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yuanyi Wang","Yanggan Gu","Yiming Zhang","Qi Zhou","Zhaoyi Yan","Congkai Xie","Xinyao Wang","Jianbo Yuan","Hongxia Yang"],"bibkey":"wang2025model","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2025model,\n  title = {{Model Merging Scaling Laws in Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yuanyi Wang and Yanggan Gu and Yiming Zhang and Qi Zhou and Zhaoyi Yan and Congkai Xie and Xinyao Wang and Jianbo Yuan and Hongxia Yang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2509.24244},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.24244}\n}"},{"id":"zo-edge-of-stability","title":"Zeroth-Order Optimization at the Edge of Stability","authors":"Song et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026 (poster)","arxiv":"2604.14669","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14669","claim":"With the standard Gaussian two-point estimator, the mean dynamics of ZO-GD, ZO-GDM and frozen ZO-Adam near a minimum match their first-order counterparts (critical step \\(2/\\lambda_{\\max}\\) for GD), but mean-square linear stability depends on the whole Hessian spectrum. For ZO-GD the critical step \\(\\eta^*\\) solves \\(\\sum_i\\eta\\lambda_i/(2(1-\\eta\\lambda_i))=1\\) (with \\(\\eta\\lambda_{\\max}<1\\)) and satisfies \\(2/(\\mathrm{Tr}H+2\\lambda_{\\max})\\le\\eta^*\\le2/\\mathrm{Tr}H\\), with analogous bounds for momentum (factor \\(1-\\beta\\)) and for frozen ZO-Adam with the preconditioned Hessian \\(P^{-1}H\\) (assuming \\(PH=HP\\)). Full-batch ZO-GD, ZO-GDM and ZO-Adam on CIFAR-10 CNN, ResNet and ViT models (and LSTM and Mamba sorting tasks) settle at this mean-square edge of stability, with catapults after step-size increases. Large ZO steps therefore implicitly regularise the Hessian trace, whereas first-order steps regularise the top eigenvalue; large smoothing \\(\\mu\\) and mini-batching push training into flatter regimes.","relevance":"The implicit bias of the backward-lens (B) zeroth-order kind behind mezo, agzo, zo-muon and fzoo: ZO fine-tuning is limited by \\(\\mathrm{Tr}(H)\\) rather than \\(\\lambda_{\\max}\\), consistent with MeZO's low-effective-rank explanation and with zo-learning-dynamics-kernel. The experiments use small vision and sequence models, not LLM fine-tuning, and the main theory uses MeZO's symmetric Gaussian estimator. Appendix B extends the covariance-operator framework one ingredient at a time: forward differences (with Gaussian directions), non-Gaussian directions (for the symmetric estimator) and multiple Gaussian queries. FZOO's combination of one-sided Rademacher probes with a shared baseline and a loss-std-normalised step is therefore not covered as such, and none of these variants was tested.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14669","https://arxiv.org/html/2604.14669","https://icml.cc/static/virtual/data/icml-2026-orals-posters.json","https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/61252","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2604.14669"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Minhak Song","Liang Zhang","Bingcong Li","Niao He","Michael Muehlebach","Sewoong Oh"],"bibkey":"song2026zeroth","bibtex":"@inproceedings{song2026zeroth,\n  title = {{Zeroth-Order Optimization at the Edge of Stability}},\n  author = {Minhak Song and Liang Zhang and Bingcong Li and Niao He and Michael Muehlebach and Sewoong Oh},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2604.14669},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.14669}\n}"},{"id":"gs-orthogonal-riemannian-structure","title":"Riemannian Structure and Optimization for a Class of Low-Parametric Orthogonal Matrices","authors":"Aliev et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2609.27982","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27982","claim":"For Group-and-Shuffle (GS) matrices \\(A=B_mP_{m-1}B_{m-1}\\cdots P_1B_1\\) (block-diagonal factors interleaved with fixed permutations; Monarch and block-butterfly matrices are special cases), explicit counterexamples show that with rectangular or general square blocks the two-factor class is a product of bounded-rank sets and not a smooth manifold. With orthogonal blocks the two-factor class is an orbit of the compact Lie-group action \\((B_2,B_1)\\cdot x=B_2xB_1^{\\top}\\) and hence a closed embedded submanifold (recalled from OrthoFuse). For this case the paper derives the tangent-space projection, retractions (exponential, Cayley and polar; the experiments use polar with five Newton--Schulz steps) and vector transport. It computes Riemannian gradients by automatic differentiation without forming dense matrices, covering overlapping block supports and parameter sharing, and gives geodesics under extra block assumptions. It also proves \\(\\mathcal{A}_3^{\\mathrm{orth}}=\\mathcal{A}_3\\cap O(N)\\) and links the three-factor perfect-shuffle case to the CS decomposition. Riemannian SGD on GS-orthogonal adapters (\\(b=8\\), 1.38M parameters) for RoBERTa-base on GLUE beats Euclidean SGD (84.85 vs 83.12 average) but stays below AdamW (86.04), at 5 to 7% overhead.","relevance":"Says when the structured orthogonal groups of the OFT family (oft, boft, GSOFT) are smooth. The two-factor block-diagonal-with-permutation class is a manifold when its blocks are orthogonal (or unitary), which licenses Riemannian optimisation and base-point arguments for GSOFT-style two-factor adapters. The manifold property is proved only for \\(m=2\\) (for \\(m=3\\) the paper shows \\(\\mathcal{A}_3^{\\mathrm{orth}}=\\mathcal{A}_3\\cap O(N)\\)), so BOFT's longer butterfly products are not covered, and unconstrained block factorisations such as Monarch (monarch) are not manifolds in general. From the Rakhuba group (GSOFT, OrthoFuse, riemannion). Neither GSOFT (arXiv 2406.10019, NeurIPS 2024) nor OrthoFuse (arXiv 2604.05183, CVPR 2026) is in the catalogue yet.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27982","https://arxiv.org/html/2609.27982","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=ti:%22Group%20and%20Shuffle%22","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=ti:OrthoFuse","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2609.27982","https://openaccess.thecvf.com/CVPR2026?day=all","https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Ali Aliev","Maxim Rakhuba"],"bibkey":"aliev2026riemannian","bibtex":"@article{aliev2026riemannian,\n  title = {{Riemannian Structure and Optimization for a Class of Low-Parametric Orthogonal Matrices}},\n  author = {Ali Aliev and Maxim Rakhuba},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2609.27982},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2609.27982},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.27982}\n}"},{"id":"prefix-compile-lora-tests","title":"When Can Prefixes Compile LoRA? Exact Resource-Capped Tests for Frozen Attention","authors":"Mondal et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2609.36766","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36766","claim":"At one frozen causal softmax head with fixed content states, and a prefix of independent keys and values shared across inputs, the prefix sees the content only through \\(\\Sigma(X)=(q,Z_X,N_X)\\) (query, partition function, value numerator), and \\(\\Sigma\\) is a complete intervention statistic. Any adapter target that differs on two inputs with equal \\(\\Sigma\\) therefore has an error floor at every prefix length, and with norm caps \\(\\|\\kappa_t\\|\\le K\\), \\(\\|\\nu_t\\|\\le V\\) the floor extends, independently of length, to nearly equal summaries. At a common query \\(q_0\\) every prefix reduces to two aggregates \\((a,b)=(Z_S(q_0),N_S(q_0))\\). With \\(m\\) capped slots the attainable set is \\(\\{me^{-L}\\le a\\le me^{L},\\ \\|b\\|\\le Va\\}\\) with \\(L=K\\|q_0\\|/\\sqrt{d_k}\\), the optimal (projected) error is an attained second-order-cone program, and \\(m\\) identical slots realise the optimum; with a constant partition, the closure of prefix outputs is exactly \\(t\\,h_X+c\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le1\\). Two equal-norm rank-one value updates can fall on opposite sides of compilability, extra capped slots can raise the optimal error, and both query-side and value-side updates can fail. Under affine query exposure, \\(2r\\) signed slots approximate a rank-\\(r\\) value update, with values growing as \\(O(\\epsilon^{-3/2})\\); all 400 tolerance checks pass in float64 but only 38 in bfloat16. On three first-layer GPT-2 heads with a fixed readout token, the capped optimum (four slots) leaves 18.4% to 74.2% of a trained adapter's projected effect uncompiled. All claims are local to one head.","relevance":"A target-by-target comparison of the prefix (E0) and LoRA (R) kinds that sharpens the indexed prefix theory. It builds on the prefix decomposition of prefix-tuning-capabilities-limitations (Petrov et al. 2024b) and cites petrov-2024-prefix-universal; le-2025-revisiting-prefix and chen-2022-inducer-tuning are catalogue neighbours that it does not cite. A prefix can only mix the frozen head output with a query-dependent vector, so adapters that amplify the frozen output, or that separate inputs with equal \\((q,Z,N)\\), cannot be compiled. The same authors' 'Certification Frontiers for Gaussian LoRA' (arXiv 2609.32271, PAC-Bayes with KL minimised over the \\(GL(r)\\) gauge) was posted three days earlier (26 vs 29 September 2026) and was not added.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36766","https://arxiv.org/html/2609.36766","https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.32271","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/batch","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2609.36766,2609.32271"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["addendum2026b-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Joyanta Jyoti Mondal","Ibne Farabi Shihab"],"bibkey":"mondal2026can","bibtex":"@article{mondal2026can,\n  title = {{When Can Prefixes Compile LoRA? Exact Resource-Capped Tests for Frozen Attention}},\n  author = {Joyanta Jyoti Mondal and Ibne Farabi Shihab},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2609.36766},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2609.36766},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36766}\n}"},{"id":"tangent-task-arithmetic","title":"Task Arithmetic in the Tangent Space: Improved Editing of Pre-Trained Models","authors":"Ortiz-Jimenez et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.12827","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.12827","claim":"Weight disentanglement (distinct weight directions controlling separate, localized regions of function space) is the property that makes task arithmetic work; fine-tuning in the linearized (tangent) regime amplifies it and improves task addition and negation.","relevance":"Identifies when the map from task vectors to functions behaves like a linear map (a homomorphism of the additive structure), which is the precondition for treating merging as vector-space arithmetic.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.12827","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.12827","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2305.12827"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Guillermo Ortiz-Jimenez","Alessandro Favero","Pascal Frossard"],"bibkey":"ortizjimenez2023task","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ortizjimenez2023task,\n  title = {{Task Arithmetic in the Tangent Space: Improved Editing of Pre-Trained Models}},\n  author = {Guillermo Ortiz-Jimenez and Alessandro Favero and Pascal Frossard},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.12827},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.12827}\n}"},{"id":"git-re-basin","title":"Git Re-Basin: Merging Models modulo Permutation Symmetries","authors":"Ainsworth et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2209.04836","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.04836","claim":"After accounting for permutation symmetries of hidden units, loss landscapes often contain nearly a single basin; aligning one model's units to another's permutation lets independently trained models be merged by linear interpolation.","relevance":"Shows that merging is well-defined only up to a symmetry group acting on weights; LoRA factorizations carry an analogous \\(GL(r)\\)/permutation gauge (cf. LoRA-LEGO, KnOTS), so adapters are naturally objects up to isomorphism.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.04836","https://arxiv.org/html/2209.04836"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Samuel K. Ainsworth","Jonathan Hayase","Siddhartha Srinivasa"],"bibkey":"ainsworth2022git","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ainsworth2022git,\n  title = {{Git Re-Basin: Merging Models modulo Permutation Symmetries}},\n  author = {Samuel K. Ainsworth and Jonathan Hayase and Siddhartha Srinivasa},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2209.04836},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.04836}\n}"},{"id":"linear-mode-connectivity","title":"Linear Mode Connectivity and the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis","authors":"Frankle et al.","year":2019,"venue":"ICML 2020","arxiv":"1912.05671","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05671","claim":"Networks become stable to SGD noise early in training: from then on, runs that share a starting point end in minima connected by a linear path of low loss.","relevance":"Grounds the shared-basin assumption behind all merging of models or adapters fine-tuned from one pre-trained point, i.e. why deltas from a common base can be treated as vectors in one affine chart.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05671"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition","gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jonathan Frankle","Gintare Karolina Dziugaite","Daniel M. Roy","Michael Carbin"],"bibkey":"frankle2019linear","bibtex":"@inproceedings{frankle2019linear,\n  title = {{Linear Mode Connectivity and the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis}},\n  author = {Jonathan Frankle and Gintare Karolina Dziugaite and Daniel M. Roy and Michael Carbin},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1912.05671},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.05671}\n}"},{"id":"model-merging-survey","title":"Model Merging in LLMs, MLLMs, and Beyond: Methods, Theories, Applications and Opportunities","authors":"Yang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ACM Computing Surveys 2026","arxiv":"2408.07666","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.07666","claim":"Proposes a taxonomy of model merging methods (pre-merging alignment, during-merging weighting/subspace/routing methods), reviews the theory, and surveys applications in LLMs, multimodal models and more than ten machine-learning subfields.","relevance":"The most complete map of the merging half of this family; its taxonomy is a reference point to check against a categorical classification of merge operators.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.07666","https://arxiv.org/html/2408.07666","https://doi.org/10.1145/3787849"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Enneng Yang","Li Shen","Guibing Guo","Xingwei Wang","Xiaochun Cao","Jie Zhang","Dacheng Tao"],"bibkey":"yang2024model","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yang2024model,\n  title = {{Model Merging in LLMs, MLLMs, and Beyond: Methods, Theories, Applications and Opportunities}},\n  author = {Enneng Yang and Li Shen and Guibing Guo and Xingwei Wang and Xiaochun Cao and Jie Zhang and Dacheng Tao},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {ACM Computing Surveys},\n  eprint = {2408.07666},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.07666}\n}"},{"id":"merging-at-scale","title":"What Matters for Model Merging at Scale?","authors":"Yadav et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.03617","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03617","claim":"Merging experts fine-tuned from 1B-64B models shows that merging works better from strong (instruction-tuned) base models, larger models merge more easily and more experts, merged models generalize better, and different merging methods behave very similarly at large scale.","relevance":"Empirical evidence that at scale the choice of merge operator matters less than the base point, suggesting that the algebra of merging becomes closer to linear near strong pre-trained models.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03617","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.03617","https://openreview.net/forum?id=qjNdGpgpV8"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Prateek Yadav","Tu Vu","Jonathan Lai","Alexandra Chronopoulou","Manaal Faruqui","Mohit Bansal","Tsendsuren Munkhdalai"],"bibkey":"yadav2024matters","bibtex":"@article{yadav2024matters,\n  title = {{What Matters for Model Merging at Scale?}},\n  author = {Prateek Yadav and Tu Vu and Jonathan Lai and Alexandra Chronopoulou and Manaal Faruqui and Mohit Bansal and Tsendsuren Munkhdalai},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2410.03617},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03617}\n}"},{"id":"hypernetworks","title":"HyperNetworks","authors":"Ha et al.","year":2016,"venue":"ICLR 2017","arxiv":"1609.09106","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09106","claim":"A small network can generate the weights of a larger main network and be trained end-to-end, acting as a relaxed form of weight sharing; generated non-shared LSTM weights reach near state-of-the-art results on sequence modelling.","relevance":"Adapter generators (HyperFormer, HyperDreamBooth, Text-to-LoRA, Doc-to-LoRA, DnD) are hypernetworks: maps from a conditioning space (task, image, text, document) into the space of adapters.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09106"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["David Ha","Andrew Dai","Quoc V. Le"],"bibkey":"ha2016hypernetworks","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ha2016hypernetworks,\n  title = {{HyperNetworks}},\n  author = {David Ha and Andrew Dai and Quoc V. Le},\n  year = {2016},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {1609.09106},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09106}\n}"},{"id":"neural-network-diffusion","title":"Neural Network Diffusion","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2402.13144","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13144","claim":"An autoencoder plus a latent diffusion model trained on checkpoints can generate new, high-performing parameters for a subset of network weights that are not mere memorized copies of the training checkpoints.","relevance":"Establishes generative modelling directly over parameter (adapter) space, the line that prompt-conditioned LoRA generators such as Drag-and-Drop LLMs extend.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13144","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13144"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["composition"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Kai Wang","Dongwen Tang","Boya Zeng","Yida Yin","Zhaopan Xu","Yukun Zhou","Zelin Zang","Trevor Darrell","Zhuang Liu","Yang You"],"bibkey":"wang2024neural","bibtex":"@article{wang2024neural,\n  title = {{Neural Network Diffusion}},\n  author = {Kai Wang and Dongwen Tang and Boya Zeng and Yida Yin and Zhaopan Xu and Yukun Zhou and Zelin Zang and Trevor Darrell and Zhuang Liu and Yang You},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2402.13144},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.13144},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13144}\n}"},{"id":"rl-finetunes-small-subnetworks","title":"Reinforcement Learning Finetunes Small Subnetworks in Large Language Models","authors":"Mukherjee et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2505.11711","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11711","claim":"Across 7 RL-style algorithms (PPO, GRPO, KTO, DPO, SimPO, PRIME and rejection-sampling SFT) and 10 LLMs, RL updates only 5-30% of parameters. The updated subnetworks overlap far more than chance across seeds, datasets and algorithms, the sparse updates are nearly full-rank, and fine-tuning only that subnetwork recovers a model nearly identical to full RL fine-tuning.","relevance":"Shows the RL update is constrained in its support but not in its rank, a different algebraic type of constraint from low rank. This argues for selective/sparse rather than factorized PEFT when classifying what RL post-training actually changes.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11711"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sagnik Mukherjee","Lifan Yuan","Dilek Hakkani-Tur","Hao Peng"],"bibkey":"mukherjee2025reinforcement","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mukherjee2025reinforcement,\n  title = {{Reinforcement Learning Finetunes Small Subnetworks in Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Sagnik Mukherjee and Lifan Yuan and Dilek Hakkani-Tur and Hao Peng},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2505.11711},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11711}\n}"},{"id":"path-not-taken-rlvr","title":"The Path Not Taken: RLVR Provably Learns Off the Principals","authors":"Zhu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Efficient Reasoning (spotlight)","arxiv":"2511.08567","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08567","claim":"RLVR's apparent sparsity comes from a model-conditioned bias: updates avoid the principal singular directions of the pretrained weights, preserve the spectrum and rotate the principal subspace very little, whereas SFT targets principal weights. A 'three-gate' theory (KL anchor, model geometry, bf16 precision) explains this.","relevance":"Predicts which PEFT parameterizations suit RL: principal-subspace initializations (PiSSA-style) conflict with RLVR geometry. Under RL, the spectral position of an adapter's subspace matters as much as its rank.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08567","https://hanqing.notion.site/The-Path-Not-Taken-RLVR-Provably-Learns-Off-the-Principals-2a7f89d0c846816faffec5f1c2b24a49"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Hanqing Zhu","Zhenyu Zhang","Hanxian Huang","DiJia Su","Zechun Liu","Jiawei Zhao","Igor Fedorov","Hamed Pirsiavash","Zhizhou Sha","Jinwon Lee","David Z. Pan","Zhangyang Wang","Yuandong Tian","Kai Sheng Tai"],"bibkey":"zhu2025path","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhu2025path,\n  title = {{The Path Not Taken: RLVR Provably Learns Off the Principals}},\n  author = {Hanqing Zhu and Zhenyu Zhang and Hanxian Huang and DiJia Su and Zechun Liu and Jiawei Zhao and Igor Fedorov and Hamed Pirsiavash and Zhizhou Sha and Jinwon Lee and David Z. Pan and Zhangyang Wang and Yuandong Tian and Kai Sheng Tai},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2511.08567},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08567}\n}"},{"id":"rls-razor","title":"RL's Razor: Why Online Reinforcement Learning Forgets Less","authors":"Shenfeld et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICLR 2026","arxiv":"2509.04259","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04259","claim":"Forgetting is predicted by the forward KL between the fine-tuned and base policy on the new task; on-policy RL is implicitly biased toward KL-minimal solutions among those that solve the task, while SFT can converge arbitrarily far, which is why RL forgets less at equal new-task performance.","relevance":"Gives a distributional (KL) rather than parametric measure of how far an adaptation moves, a candidate invariant for comparing PEFT methods. It also helps explain why tiny-parameter adapters suffice for RL.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04259","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/file/618c95f4557c15b253fb0e6f548ea0c0-Paper-Conference.pdf"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Idan Shenfeld","Jyothish Pari","Pulkit Agrawal"],"bibkey":"shenfeld2025rl","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shenfeld2025rl,\n  title = {{RL's Razor: Why Online Reinforcement Learning Forgets Less}},\n  author = {Idan Shenfeld and Jyothish Pari and Pulkit Agrawal},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2509.04259},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04259}\n}"},{"id":"evaluating-peft-rlvr","title":"Evaluating Parameter Efficient Methods for RLVR","authors":"Yin et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2512.23165","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23165","claim":"Benchmarking 12+ PEFT methods under RLVR on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen 1.5B/7B math reasoning: structural variants (DoRA, AdaLoRA, MiSS) beat standard LoRA, with DoRA even exceeding full fine-tuning on several benchmarks; SVD-informed initializations (PiSSA, MiLoRA) suffer 'spectral collapse'; and extreme compression (VeRA, rank-1) bottlenecks reasoning.","relevance":"The first systematic map of the PEFT design space under RL. Its rank-1 result is in tension with the LoRA Without Regret and TinyLoRA findings that a tiny rank or parameter count suffices for RL.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23165","https://arxiv.org/html/2512.23165","https://openreview.net/forum?id=76CIL1O0bz"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Qingyu Yin","Yulun Wu","Zhennan Shen","Sunbowen Li","Zhilin Wang","Yanshu Li","Chak Tou Leong","Jiale Kang","Jinjin Gu"],"bibkey":"yin2025evaluating","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yin2025evaluating,\n  title = {{Evaluating Parameter Efficient Methods for RLVR}},\n  author = {Qingyu Yin and Yulun Wu and Zhennan Shen and Sunbowen Li and Zhilin Wang and Yanshu Li and Chak Tou Leong and Jiale Kang and Jinjin Gu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2512.23165},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.23165}\n}"},{"id":"lora-knowledge-capacity","title":"How Much Knowledge Can You Pack into a LoRA Adapter without Harming LLM?","authors":"Pletenev et al.","year":2025,"venue":"Findings of NAACL 2025","arxiv":"2502.14502","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-naacl.243/","claim":"Fine-tuning Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct with LoRA on growing amounts of new facts shows that a mixture of known and new facts works best, but packing in more new knowledge degrades general QA benchmarks, and biased data makes the model regress to a few over-represented answers and become overconfident.","relevance":"Quantifies the capacity/interference trade-off of a single low-rank adapter used as a knowledge store, relevant to adapter-as-memory views (Doc-to-LoRA, Cartridges, sparse memory finetuning).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14502","https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-naacl.243/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["frontier"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sergey Pletenev","Maria Marina","Daniil Moskovskiy","Vasily Konovalov","Pavel Braslavski","Alexander Panchenko","Mikhail Salnikov"],"bibkey":"pletenev2025much","bibtex":"@inproceedings{pletenev2025much,\n  title = {{How Much Knowledge Can You Pack into a LoRA Adapter without Harming LLM?}},\n  author = {Sergey Pletenev and Maria Marina and Daniil Moskovskiy and Vasily Konovalov and Pavel Braslavski and Alexander Panchenko and Mikhail Salnikov},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: NAACL 2025},\n  eprint = {2502.14502},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-naacl.243/}\n}"},{"id":"gradient-projection-memory","title":"Gradient Projection Memory for Continual Learning","authors":"Saha et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2021","arxiv":"2103.09762","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=3AOj0RCNC2","claim":"A layer's gradient updates lie in the span of its inputs. GPM stores per-layer orthonormal bases of the old tasks' representation subspaces, found by SVD of activations, and steps orthogonally to them, which causes little or no interference with past tasks and needs no replay.","relevance":"This input-space orthogonality is the mechanism InfLoRA builds into a LoRA down-projection (through DualGPM) and that O-LoRA approximates with LoRA subspaces. Categorically, each task's update is a morphism required to vanish on a sub-object (the old tasks' input subspace), which makes continual-learning PEFT a kernel condition.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.09762"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Gobinda Saha","Isha Garg","Kaushik Roy"],"bibkey":"saha2021gradient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{saha2021gradient,\n  title = {{Gradient Projection Memory for Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Gobinda Saha and Isha Garg and Kaushik Roy},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2103.09762},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://openreview.net/forum?id=3AOj0RCNC2}\n}"},{"id":"orthogonal-gradient-descent","title":"Orthogonal Gradient Descent for Continual Learning","authors":"Farajtabar et al.","year":2019,"venue":"AISTATS 2020","arxiv":"1910.07104","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v108/farajtabar20a.html","claim":"Projecting new-task gradients onto the subspace in which the network's outputs on previous tasks stay unchanged mitigates catastrophic forgetting without storing data, but it does require storing gradient directions.","relevance":"Direct ancestor of O-LoRA, which replaces OGD's stored gradients with the LoRA subspaces of earlier tasks; in this sense O-LoRA is OGD transported into the low-rank adapter parameterization.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07104","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v108/farajtabar20a.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Mehrdad Farajtabar","Navid Azizan","Alex Mott","Ang Li"],"bibkey":"farajtabar2019orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{farajtabar2019orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Gradient Descent for Continual Learning}},\n  author = {Mehrdad Farajtabar and Navid Azizan and Alex Mott and Ang Li},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS)},\n  eprint = {1910.07104},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v108/farajtabar20a.html}\n}"},{"id":"distributed-alignment-search","title":"Finding Alignments Between Interpretable Causal Variables and Distributed Neural Representations","authors":"Geiger et al.","year":2023,"venue":"CLeaR 2024","arxiv":"2303.02536","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v236/geiger24a.html","claim":"Distributed alignment search (DAS) learns, by gradient descent, an orthonormal (rotated) subspace of a hidden representation in which interchange interventions realize a high-level causal variable. This replaces brute-force alignment search and allows distributed, non-neuron-aligned representations.","relevance":"LoReFT is DAS's interchange intervention with the counterfactual source replaced by a learned affine map (\\(R s \\to W h + b\\)). This links PEFT to causal abstraction: a ReFT is an endomorphism of the hidden-state space that acts only on one sub-object.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02536","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v236/geiger24a.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Atticus Geiger","Zhengxuan Wu","Christopher Potts","Thomas Icard","Noah D. Goodman"],"bibkey":"geiger2023finding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{geiger2023finding,\n  title = {{Finding Alignments Between Interpretable Causal Variables and Distributed Neural Representations}},\n  author = {Atticus Geiger and Zhengxuan Wu and Christopher Potts and Thomas Icard and Noah D. Goodman},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {CLeaR},\n  eprint = {2303.02536},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v236/geiger24a.html}\n}"},{"id":"axbench","title":"AxBench: Steering LLMs? Even Simple Baselines Outperform Sparse Autoencoders","authors":"Wu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2501.17148","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17148","claim":"On a large benchmark for concept steering and detection (Gemma-2-2B/9B), prompting outperforms every representation-based steering method, with finetuning second; difference-in-means is best for detection, and SAEs are not competitive on either task. A rank-1 representation finetuning (ReFT-r1) is competitive on both.","relevance":"Gives a sober empirical calibration of intervention-based (activation-editing) adaptation against prompts and weight finetuning, which is useful for placing the ReFT family next to input-augmentation and weight-update PEFT.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17148","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stanfordnlp/axbench/main/README.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zhengxuan Wu","Aryaman Arora","Atticus Geiger","Zheng Wang","Jing Huang","Dan Jurafsky","Christopher D. Manning","Christopher Potts"],"bibkey":"wu2025axbench","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wu2025axbench,\n  title = {{AxBench: Steering LLMs? Even Simple Baselines Outperform Sparse Autoencoders}},\n  author = {Zhengxuan Wu and Aryaman Arora and Atticus Geiger and Zheng Wang and Jing Huang and Dan Jurafsky and Christopher D. Manning and Christopher Potts},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2501.17148},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17148}\n}"},{"id":"representation-engineering","title":"Representation Engineering: A Top-Down Approach to AI Transparency","authors":"Zou et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2310.01405","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01405","claim":"Population-level representations, rather than individual neurons or circuits, can be read out and manipulated to monitor and control high-level behaviours of LLMs such as honesty and harmlessness, using simple training-free reading vectors and representation-control operators.","relevance":"Training-free activation steering is the zero-training limit of representation interventions, and ReFT is its learned, task-supervised counterpart; the ReFT paper explicitly recasts RepE's operators as parametrizations of its intervention function.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01405","https://arxiv.org/html/2404.03592"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["gap-0"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Andy Zou","Long Phan","Sarah Chen","James Campbell","Phillip Guo","Richard Ren","Alexander Pan","Xuwang Yin","Mantas Mazeika","Ann-Kathrin Dombrowski","Shashwat Goel","Nathaniel Li","Michael J. Byun","Zifan Wang","Alex Mallen","Steven Basart","Sanmi Koyejo","Dawn Song","Matt Fredrikson","J. Zico Kolter","Dan Hendrycks"],"bibkey":"zou2023representation","bibtex":"@article{zou2023representation,\n  title = {{Representation Engineering: A Top-Down Approach to AI Transparency}},\n  author = {Andy Zou and Long Phan and Sarah Chen and James Campbell and Phillip Guo and Richard Ren and Alexander Pan and Xuwang Yin and Mantas Mazeika and Ann-Kathrin Dombrowski and Shashwat Goel and Nathaniel Li and Michael J. Byun and Zifan Wang and Alex Mallen and Steven Basart and Sanmi Koyejo and Dawn Song and Matt Fredrikson and J. Zico Kolter and Dan Hendrycks},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2310.01405},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.01405},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01405}\n}"},{"id":"inference-time-intervention","title":"Inference-Time Intervention: Eliciting Truthful Answers from a Language Model","authors":"Li et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2306.03341","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03341","claim":"Shifting activations along probe-derived 'truthful' directions in a small set of top-\\(K\\) attention heads, with no training, raises Alpaca's TruthfulQA truthfulness from 32.5% to 65.1%. There is a tunable trade-off between truthfulness and helpfulness.","relevance":"ITI is the training-free precursor of learned representation interventions: LoFiT casts ITI in its localize-then-offset framework and replaces both steps with learning, and ReFT's appendix shows how inference-time interventions fit its framework. Its edit, a constant offset on selected head outputs, is the zero-training corner of representation-level PEFT and is algebraically a bias on \\(W^O\\). This makes 'steering' and 'fine-tuning' two ways of choosing the same kind of morphism.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03341","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.01563","https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.03592"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Kenneth Li","Oam Patel","Fernanda Viégas","Hanspeter Pfister","Martin Wattenberg"],"bibkey":"li2023inference","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2023inference,\n  title = {{Inference-Time Intervention: Eliciting Truthful Answers from a Language Model}},\n  author = {Kenneth Li and Oam Patel and Fernanda Viégas and Hanspeter Pfister and Martin Wattenberg},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2306.03341},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03341}\n}"},{"id":"laplace-redux","title":"Laplace Redux -- Effortless Bayesian Deep Learning","authors":"Daxberger et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NeurIPS 2021","arxiv":"2106.14806","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14806","claim":"The Laplace approximation, in cheap variants such as last-layer, K-FAC and linearized predictive, is competitive in performance with more popular Bayesian alternatives while costing much less. The paper releases the 'laplace' PyTorch library.","relevance":"It supplies the post-hoc machinery (K-FAC, linearized predictive, marginal-likelihood prior tuning) that Laplace-LoRA restricts to the adapter subspace. This turns a point adapter into a distribution over adapters: for the site, a lift of PEFT from points of a parameter space to a probability monad over it.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14806","https://arxiv.org/html/2308.13111"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-1"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Erik Daxberger","Agustinus Kristiadi","Alexander Immer","Runa Eschenhagen","Matthias Bauer","Philipp Hennig"],"bibkey":"daxberger2021laplace","bibtex":"@inproceedings{daxberger2021laplace,\n  title = {{Laplace Redux -- Effortless Bayesian Deep Learning}},\n  author = {Erik Daxberger and Agustinus Kristiadi and Alexander Immer and Runa Eschenhagen and Matthias Bauer and Philipp Hennig},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2106.14806},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14806}\n}"},{"id":"bayes-by-backprop","title":"Weight Uncertainty in Neural Networks","authors":"Blundell et al.","year":2015,"venue":"ICML 2015","arxiv":"1505.05424","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05424","claim":"Bayes by Backprop learns a factorized Gaussian posterior over the weights by minimizing the variational free energy with reparameterized Monte-Carlo gradients. This gives principled weight uncertainty using ordinary backpropagation.","relevance":"BLoB applies this variational objective to the LoRA factor \\(A\\). This makes the adapter a probability distribution over updates (a Kleisli-style lift of the additive-update functor) rather than a single point, which is the template for uncertainty-aware PEFT.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05424","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.11675"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Charles Blundell","Julien Cornebise","Koray Kavukcuoglu","Daan Wierstra"],"bibkey":"blundell2015weight","bibtex":"@inproceedings{blundell2015weight,\n  title = {{Weight Uncertainty in Neural Networks}},\n  author = {Charles Blundell and Julien Cornebise and Koray Kavukcuoglu and Daan Wierstra},\n  year = {2015},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1505.05424},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.05424}\n}"},{"id":"revnet","title":"The Reversible Residual Network: Backpropagation Without Storing Activations","authors":"Gomez et al.","year":2017,"venue":"NeurIPS 2017","arxiv":"1707.04585","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04585","claim":"Additive coupling blocks (\\(y_1 = x_1 + F(x_2)\\), \\(y_2 = x_2 + G(y_1)\\)) are exactly invertible. Activations can therefore be recomputed from the outputs during backpropagation, and activation memory becomes independent of depth.","relevance":"MEFT builds directly on this result. Reversibility makes each layer an isomorphism in the category of hidden-state spaces, which lets memory-efficient PEFT trade cached activations for recomputation.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04585","https://arxiv.org/html/2306.00477"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Aidan N. Gomez","Mengye Ren","Raquel Urtasun","Roger B. Grosse"],"bibkey":"gomez2017reversible","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gomez2017reversible,\n  title = {{The Reversible Residual Network: Backpropagation Without Storing Activations}},\n  author = {Aidan N. Gomez and Mengye Ren and Raquel Urtasun and Roger B. Grosse},\n  year = {2017},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1707.04585},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.04585}\n}"},{"id":"laser-layer-selective-rank-reduction","title":"The Truth is in There: Improving Reasoning in Language Models with Layer-Selective Rank Reduction","authors":"Sharma et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2312.13558","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.13558","claim":"LASER removes the higher-order (small singular value) components of specific weight matrices in specific layers after training. This can improve LLM reasoning and factuality without any gradient updates, so small-singular-value directions often hold noise.","relevance":"LASER is the spectral precursor of Spectrum's SNR-based layer selection. With PiSSA, MiLoRA and LoRA-Dash, it shows that the singular spectrum of \\(W_0\\) is a natural coordinate system in which to place, prune or select PEFT updates.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.13558","https://openreview.net/forum?id=ozX92bu8VA","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.06623"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Pratyusha Sharma","Jordan T. Ash","Dipendra Misra"],"bibkey":"sharma2023truth","bibtex":"@inproceedings{sharma2023truth,\n  title = {{The Truth is in There: Improving Reasoning in Language Models with Layer-Selective Rank Reduction}},\n  author = {Pratyusha Sharma and Jordan T. Ash and Dipendra Misra},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2312.13558},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.13558}\n}"},{"id":"taylor-importance-pruning","title":"Importance Estimation for Neural Network Pruning","authors":"Molchanov et al.","year":2019,"venue":"CVPR 2019","arxiv":"1906.10771","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.10771","claim":"The squared first-order Taylor term \\((\\partial\\mathcal{L}/\\partial W \\cdot W)^2\\), accumulated over groups, is an accurate and cheap estimate of the loss change from removing a parameter or filter.","relevance":"LoRAPrune's criterion reuses this Taylor score but takes the gradient through the LoRA factors. That makes the same low-rank adapter both the update and a probe of the backbone's local geometry.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.10771","https://arxiv.org/html/2305.18403"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-2"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Pavlo Molchanov","Arun Mallya","Stephen Tyree","Iuri Frosio","Jan Kautz"],"bibkey":"molchanov2019importance","bibtex":"@inproceedings{molchanov2019importance,\n  title = {{Importance Estimation for Neural Network Pruning}},\n  author = {Pavlo Molchanov and Arun Mallya and Stephen Tyree and Iuri Frosio and Jan Kautz},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {1906.10771},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.10771}\n}"},{"id":"linear-classifier-probes","title":"Understanding intermediate layers using linear classifier probes","authors":"Alain & Bengio","year":2016,"venue":"ICLR 2017 (Workshop)","arxiv":"1610.01644","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01644","claim":"Linear classifiers ('probes') trained independently on each layer's frozen features measure how linearly separable the representation is; on Inception v3 and ResNet-50 separability increases monotonically with depth.","relevance":"Names and formalizes the probe, the terminal object of selective PEFT (train only a linear map on frozen features) and the standard yardstick that every other adaptation method is compared against.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01644","https://api.openreview.net/notes/search?term=Understanding%20intermediate%20layers%20using%20linear%20classifier%20probes"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Guillaume Alain","Yoshua Bengio"],"bibkey":"alain2016understanding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{alain2016understanding,\n  title = {{Understanding intermediate layers using linear classifier probes}},\n  author = {Guillaume Alain and Yoshua Bengio},\n  year = {2016},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {1610.01644},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.01644}\n}"},{"id":"dexperts","title":"DExperts: Decoding-Time Controlled Text Generation with Experts and Anti-Experts","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2105.03023","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.522/","claim":"Combining a pretrained LM with expert and anti-expert LMs in a product of experts (adding and subtracting logits) controls attributes such as toxicity and sentiment, and works with small (anti-)experts, even on GPT-3.","relevance":"The output-space composition rule that proxy-tuning and EFT reuse for adaptation: tuning as a translation of logits.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03023","https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.522/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Alisa Liu","Maarten Sap","Ximing Lu","Swabha Swayamdipta","Chandra Bhagavatula","Noah A. Smith","Yejin Choi"],"bibkey":"liu2021dexperts","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2021dexperts,\n  title = {{DExperts: Decoding-Time Controlled Text Generation with Experts and Anti-Experts}},\n  author = {Alisa Liu and Maarten Sap and Ximing Lu and Swabha Swayamdipta and Chandra Bhagavatula and Noah A. Smith and Yejin Choi},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2105.03023},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.522/}\n}"},{"id":"universal-adversarial-triggers","title":"Universal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP","authors":"Wallace et al.","year":2019,"venue":"EMNLP 2019","arxiv":"1908.07125","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D19-1221/","claim":"A gradient-guided search over tokens finds short input-agnostic trigger sequences that force a target prediction for any input, and these triggers transfer across models.","relevance":"The discrete-token search that AutoPrompt adapts from attacks to task prompting; the 'hard prompt' end of the prompt family.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.07125","https://aclanthology.org/D19-1221/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Eric Wallace","Shi Feng","Nikhil Kandpal","Matt Gardner","Sameer Singh"],"bibkey":"wallace2019universal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wallace2019universal,\n  title = {{Universal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP}},\n  author = {Eric Wallace and Shi Feng and Nikhil Kandpal and Matt Gardner and Sameer Singh},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {1908.07125},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/D19-1221/}\n}"},{"id":"fedpetuning","title":"FedPETuning: When Federated Learning Meets the Parameter-Efficient Tuning Methods of Pre-trained Language Models","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2022,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2023","arxiv":"2212.10025","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.632/","claim":"A holistic empirical study of PEFT methods (adapters, prefix tuning, LoRA, BitFit) under federated learning: tuning and aggregating only lightweight parameters cuts communication greatly while keeping acceptable performance across heterogeneity levels, data scales and FL scenarios.","relevance":"Establishes federated PEFT as a setting where the adapter is the unit of communication and aggregation, which is where the algebraic issue of averaging factored updates (FFA-LoRA, FLoRA) arises.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10025","https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.632/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zhuo Zhang","Yuanhang Yang","Yong Dai","Lizhen Qu","Zenglin Xu"],"bibkey":"zhang2022fedpetuning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2022fedpetuning,\n  title = {{FedPETuning: When Federated Learning Meets the Parameter-Efficient Tuning Methods of Pre-trained Language Models}},\n  author = {Zhuo Zhang and Yuanhang Yang and Yong Dai and Lizhen Qu and Zenglin Xu},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2023},\n  eprint = {2212.10025},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.632/}\n}"},{"id":"fedit","title":"Towards Building the Federated GPT: Federated Instruction Tuning","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICASSP 2024","arxiv":"2305.05644","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05644","claim":"Introduces Federated Instruction Tuning (FedIT) for LLMs, combining LoRA with FedAvg so that clients tune and upload only LoRA modules, which the server averages.","relevance":"The baseline whose separate averaging of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) produces the cross-term aggregation error that FFA-LoRA and FLoRA correct.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05644","https://arxiv.org/html/2409.05976"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jianyi Zhang","Saeed Vahidian","Martin Kuo","Chunyuan Li","Ruiyi Zhang","Tong Yu","Yufan Zhou","Guoyin Wang","Yiran Chen"],"bibkey":"zhang2023building","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2023building,\n  title = {{Towards Building the Federated GPT: Federated Instruction Tuning}},\n  author = {Jianyi Zhang and Saeed Vahidian and Martin Kuo and Chunyuan Li and Ruiyi Zhang and Tong Yu and Yufan Zhou and Guoyin Wang and Yiran Chen},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP)},\n  eprint = {2305.05644},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05644}\n}"},{"id":"group-diffusion-transformers","title":"Group Diffusion Transformers are Unsupervised Multitask Learners","authors":"Huang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2410.15027","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.15027","claim":"Recasting visual generation tasks as generating a group of related images, by concatenating self-attention tokens across images in a DiT, enables task-agnostic zero-shot generation over 200 instructions in 30 tasks.","relevance":"The precursor that IC-LoRA simplifies: it shows that cross-image attention inside one DiT can carry task conditioning, which IC-LoRA then activates with a small LoRA instead of large-scale training.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.15027","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.23775"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["gap-3"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Lianghua Huang","Wei Wang","Zhi-Fan Wu","Huanzhang Dou","Yupeng Shi","Yutong Feng","Chen Liang","Yu Liu","Jingren Zhou"],"bibkey":"huang2024group","bibtex":"@article{huang2024group,\n  title = {{Group Diffusion Transformers are Unsupervised Multitask Learners}},\n  author = {Lianghua Huang and Wei Wang and Zhi-Fan Wu and Huanzhang Dou and Yupeng Shi and Yutong Feng and Chen Liang and Yu Liu and Jingren Zhou},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2410.15027},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.15027},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.15027}\n}"},{"id":"intrinsic-dimensionality","title":"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning","authors":"Aghajanyan et al.","year":2020,"venue":"ACL 2021","arxiv":"2012.13255","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255","claim":"Fine-tuning has very low intrinsic dimension: optimizing only about 200 parameters randomly projected into the full space recovers 90% of full RoBERTa performance on MRPC, larger and longer-pretrained models have lower intrinsic dimension, and this yields compression-based generalization bounds.","relevance":"The empirical foundation for low-rank adaptation and for asking how much rank each layer actually needs, the question rank-allocation methods answer adaptively.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255","https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.568/","https://arxiv.org/html/2012.13255","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2012.13255"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":["intrinsic-said"],"authors_full":["Armen Aghajanyan","Luke Zettlemoyer","Sonal Gupta"],"bibkey":"aghajanyan2020intrinsic","bibtex":"@inproceedings{aghajanyan2020intrinsic,\n  title = {{Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Armen Aghajanyan and Luke Zettlemoyer and Sonal Gupta},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL)},\n  eprint = {2012.13255},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13255}\n}"},{"id":"expressive-power-lora","title":"The Expressive Power of Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Zeng & Lee","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.17513","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17513","claim":"For fully connected networks LoRA can adapt any model \\(f\\) to accurately represent any smaller target model once \\(\\text{LoRA-rank} \\ge (\\text{width of } f)\\times(\\text{depth of target})/(\\text{depth of } f)\\), under a mild assumption, with quantified approximation error below that threshold; for Transformer networks any model can be adapted to a same-size target with rank-(embedding size/2) LoRA adapters.","relevance":"Characterizes the image of the LoRA parametrization map, i.e. which fine-tuned functions are reachable at a given rank.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17513","https://arxiv.org/html/2310.17513","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2310.17513"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yuchen Zeng","Kangwook Lee"],"bibkey":"zeng2023expressive","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zeng2023expressive,\n  title = {{The Expressive Power of Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Yuchen Zeng and Kangwook Lee},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.17513},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17513}\n}"},{"id":"lora-illusion-of-equivalence","title":"LoRA vs Full Fine-tuning: An Illusion of Equivalence","authors":"Shuttleworth et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2410.21228","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228","claim":"Even at matched task performance, LoRA and full fine-tuning produce structurally different weights. LoRA introduces new high-ranking singular vectors ('intruder dimensions') that full fine-tuning does not. These dimensions causally drive forgetting, scaling them down significantly improves modelling of the pre-training distribution with a minimal drop in task performance, and they accumulate in continual learning.","relevance":"Shows that the right notion of 'equivalence' between PEFT and full FT is not equal task loss. Invariants of the resulting weights, such as spectral structure relative to \\(W_0\\), separate methods.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2025/poster/115207","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2410.21228","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.21228","https://openreview.net/forum?id=xp7B8rkh7L","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/package_reference/lora"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","lora-core","multiplicative","theory"],"method_ids":["intruder-dimension-reduction"],"authors_full":["Reece Shuttleworth","Jacob Andreas","Antonio Torralba","Pratyusha Sharma"],"bibkey":"shuttleworth2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shuttleworth2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA vs Full Fine-tuning: An Illusion of Equivalence}},\n  author = {Reece Shuttleworth and Jacob Andreas and Antonio Torralba and Pratyusha Sharma},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2410.21228},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21228}\n}"},{"id":"task-arithmetic","title":"Editing Models with Task Arithmetic","authors":"Ilharco et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2212.04089","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089","claim":"Task vectors (fine-tuned minus pretrained weights) can be added, negated and combined arithmetically to steer model behaviour.","relevance":"Defines the vector-space (module) structure on adapters that PEFT's add_weighted_adapter (linear/svd/cat), TIES, DARE and GenKnowSub operate in.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/developer_guides/model_merging"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft"],"method_ids":["task-arithmetic"],"authors_full":["Gabriel Ilharco","Marco Tulio Ribeiro","Mitchell Wortsman","Suchin Gururangan","Ludwig Schmidt","Hannaneh Hajishirzi","Ali Farhadi"],"bibkey":"ilharco2022editing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{ilharco2022editing,\n  title = {{Editing Models with Task Arithmetic}},\n  author = {Gabriel Ilharco and Marco Tulio Ribeiro and Mitchell Wortsman and Suchin Gururangan and Ludwig Schmidt and Hannaneh Hajishirzi and Ali Farhadi},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2212.04089},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04089}\n}"},{"id":"lora-learns-less-forgets-less","title":"LoRA Learns Less and Forgets Less","authors":"Biderman et al.","year":2024,"venue":"TMLR 2024","arxiv":"2405.09673","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09673","claim":"On code and math (instruction tuning and continued pretraining) LoRA substantially underperforms full fine-tuning at standard ranks but better preserves out-of-domain abilities; full fine-tuning learns perturbations of 10-100x higher rank than typical LoRA configs.","relevance":"Quantifies the capacity/forgetting trade-off that motivates high-rank-through-low-rank methods (ReLoRA, COLA, Delta-LoRA) and regularization views of LoRA.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09673","https://arxiv.org/html/2405.09673","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2405.09673","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2405.09673"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","lora-core","shape","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Dan Biderman","Jacob Portes","Jose Javier Gonzalez Ortiz","Mansheej Paul","Philip Greengard","Connor Jennings","Daniel King","Sam Havens","Vitaliy Chiley","Jonathan Frankle","Cody Blakeney","John P. Cunningham"],"bibkey":"biderman2024lora","bibtex":"@article{biderman2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Learns Less and Forgets Less}},\n  author = {Dan Biderman and Jacob Portes and Jose Javier Gonzalez Ortiz and Mansheej Paul and Philip Greengard and Connor Jennings and Daniel King and Sam Havens and Vitaliy Chiley and Jonathan Frankle and Cody Blakeney and John P. Cunningham},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {TMLR},\n  eprint = {2405.09673},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09673}\n}"},{"id":"hayou-lora-init-dynamics","title":"The Impact of Initialization on LoRA Finetuning Dynamics","authors":"Hayou et al.","year":2024,"venue":"NeurIPS 2024","arxiv":"2406.08447","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08447","claim":"Of the two zero-product initializations, (\\(A\\) random, \\(B=0\\)) and (\\(A=0\\), \\(B\\) random), the first permits larger stable learning rates and yields more efficient feature learning, so it performs better on average; the two are not equivalent despite both giving \\(BA=0\\).","relevance":"Shows that the choice of base point within the zero-update fibre \\(\\{(B,A): BA=0\\}\\) matters dynamically, which is exactly the degree of freedom the init family exploits; a unified theory must treat initialization as a choice of point in the factor space, not just of \\(\\Delta W\\).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08447","https://neurips.cc/virtual/2024/poster/93375","https://arxiv.org/html/2406.08447","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2406.08447"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","init-rank","lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Soufiane Hayou","Nikhil Ghosh","Bin Yu"],"bibkey":"hayou2024impact","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hayou2024impact,\n  title = {{The Impact of Initialization on LoRA Finetuning Dynamics}},\n  author = {Soufiane Hayou and Nikhil Ghosh and Bin Yu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2406.08447},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.08447}\n}"},{"id":"lora-without-regret","title":"LoRA Without Regret","authors":"Schulman & Thinking Machines Lab","year":2025,"venue":"Thinking Machines Lab blog (Connectionism), 2025","arxiv":"","url":"https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","claim":"LoRA matches full fine-tuning in a 'low-regret' regime: when it is applied to all weight matrices, especially MLP/MoE layers, and is not capacity-limited. Attention-only LoRA underperforms. The optimal LoRA learning rate is about 10x the full-FT optimum, and rank 1 suffices for policy-gradient RL because RL absorbs only about 1 bit per episode.","relevance":"A widely shared capacity argument (bits learned versus parameters exposed) that states when the restriction to a rank-\\(r\\) sub-object costs nothing. It is useful for calibrating claims of 'equivalence' between PEFT and full fine-tuning.","sources":["https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/","https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/en/lora_without_regret","https://doi.org/10.64434/tml.20250929","https://huggingface.co/docs/peft/main/en/package_reference/lora"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"schulman2025lora","bibtex":"@misc{schulman2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Without Regret}},\n  author = {Schulman and Thinking Machines Lab},\n  year = {2025},\n  url = {https://thinkingmachines.ai/blog/lora/},\n  howpublished = {Thinking Machines Lab blog (Connectionism), 2025}\n}"},{"id":"scaling-down-to-scale-up","title":"Scaling Down to Scale Up: A Guide to Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Lialin et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2303.15647","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15647","claim":"Taxonomy covering over 50 papers (early 2019 to mid-2024) that sorts methods into addition-based (adapter-like, soft prompts, other), selection-based, reparametrization-based and hybrid. A head-to-head comparison of 15 methods (14 in the body text) on models up to 11B finds that Houlsby adapters and LoRA are consistently best, that methods reported to beat LoRA struggle under limited hyperparameter search, and that LayerNorm tuning is unexpectedly competitive.","relevance":"One of the standard taxonomies (additive / selective / reparametrisation / hybrid) that the categorical classification should refine. Its efficiency comparison is a useful empirical check on the theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15647","https://arxiv.org/html/2303.15647","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2303.15647"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["hf-peft","selective","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Vladislav Lialin","Vijeta Deshpande","Xiaowei Yao","Anna Rumshisky"],"bibkey":"lialin2023scaling","bibtex":"@article{lialin2023scaling,\n  title = {{Scaling Down to Scale Up: A Guide to Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Vladislav Lialin and Vijeta Deshpande and Xiaowei Yao and Anna Rumshisky},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2303.15647},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.15647},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15647}\n}"},{"id":"nystrom-init-matrix-factorization","title":"On the Crucial Role of Initialization for Matrix Factorization","authors":"Li et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.18965","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18965","claim":"Nyström initialization (\\(X_0=A\\Omega\\) with Gaussian \\(\\Omega\\), \\(Y_0=0\\)) lets ScaledGD converge quadratically for matrix factorization where only linear rates were known; applied to LoRA as NoRA (\\(B_0=W_0\\Omega\\), \\(A_0=0\\)) and NoRA+ (with ScaledGD) it improves fine-tuning of 1B-7B LLMs and diffusion models.","relevance":"Provides an optimization-theoretic justification for weight-aware (sketch-of-\\(W_0\\)) initialization and its pairing with preconditioned optimizers, the same pairing LoRA-One uses.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18965","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.18965v3","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/poster/29262"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Bingcong Li","Liang Zhang","Aryan Mokhtari","Niao He"],"bibkey":"li2024crucial","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2024crucial,\n  title = {{On the Crucial Role of Initialization for Matrix Factorization}},\n  author = {Bingcong Li and Liang Zhang and Aryan Mokhtari and Niao He},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.18965},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.18965}\n}"},{"id":"asymmetry-lora","title":"Asymmetry in Low-Rank Adapters of Foundation Models","authors":"Zhu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.16842","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/zhu24c.html","claim":"In \\(\\Delta W=BA\\), \\(A\\) acts as an input feature extractor and \\(B\\) maps features to outputs; a random untrained \\(A\\) performs nearly as well as a tuned one, and training only \\(B\\) gives better information-theoretic generalization bounds.","relevance":"Explains why fixing the input-side factor costs little (LoRA-FA-style, LoRA-SB/LoRA-XS fixed bases) and why the two factors play non-symmetric roles in any categorical description of the adapter; MiCA, which freezes the output factor \\(B\\) and trains \\(A\\), is a counterpoint to its recommendation to train \\(B\\).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16842","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/zhu24c.html","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.16842","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.16842"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank","sharing","theory"],"method_ids":["asylora"],"authors_full":["Jiacheng Zhu","Kristjan Greenewald","Kimia Nadjahi","Haitz Sáez de Ocáriz Borde","Rickard Brüel Gabrielsson","Leshem Choshen","Marzyeh Ghassemi","Mikhail Yurochkin","Justin Solomon"],"bibkey":"zhu2024asymmetry","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhu2024asymmetry,\n  title = {{Asymmetry in Low-Rank Adapters of Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Jiacheng Zhu and Kristjan Greenewald and Kimia Nadjahi and Haitz Sáez de Ocáriz Borde and Rickard Brüel Gabrielsson and Leshem Choshen and Marzyeh Ghassemi and Mikhail Yurochkin and Justin Solomon},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.16842},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/zhu24c.html}\n}"},{"id":"unified-lora-variants-study","title":"A Unified Study of LoRA Variants: Taxonomy, Review, Codebase, and Empirical Evaluation","authors":"He et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2601.22708","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22708","claim":"Organizes LoRA variants along rank, optimization dynamics, initialization and MoE axes, releases a modular codebase (LoRAFactory), and finds that LoRA and its variants are far more sensitive to learning rate than to other hyperparameters and that a well-tuned vanilla LoRA matches or beats most variants.","relevance":"A taxonomy whose initialization (weight-, gradient-, activation-driven) and rank-allocation axes coincide with this family, and an important empirical caution that apparent gains of init/rank methods may partly reflect learning-rate tuning.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22708","https://arxiv.org/html/2601.22708"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["init-rank"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Haonan He","Jingqi Ye","Minglei Li","Zhengbo Wang","Tao Chen","Lei Bai","Peng Ye"],"bibkey":"he2026unified","bibtex":"@article{he2026unified,\n  title = {{A Unified Study of LoRA Variants: Taxonomy, Review, Codebase, and Empirical Evaluation}},\n  author = {Haonan He and Jingqi Ye and Minglei Li and Zhengbo Wang and Tao Chen and Lei Bai and Peng Ye},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2601.22708},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.22708},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.22708}\n}"},{"id":"intrinsic-dimension-objective-landscapes","title":"Measuring the Intrinsic Dimension of Objective Landscapes","authors":"Li et al.","year":2018,"venue":"ICLR 2018","arxiv":"1804.08838","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.08838","claim":"Training in a random low-dimensional affine subspace of parameter space reveals an 'intrinsic dimension' at which solutions appear, often far smaller than the parameter count; for a given task and architecture family (e.g. FC nets on MNIST, \\(d_{\\mathrm{int}90} \\approx 750\\)) it stays roughly constant across a wide range of widths and depths.","relevance":"Original evidence that useful updates live in tiny subspaces; LoRA's rank-\\(r\\) factorization is a structured, learnable version of the random-subspace construction.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.08838","https://arxiv.org/html/1804.08838","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1804.08838"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core","sharing","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Chunyuan Li","Heerad Farkhoor","Rosanne Liu","Jason Yosinski"],"bibkey":"li2018measuring","bibtex":"@inproceedings{li2018measuring,\n  title = {{Measuring the Intrinsic Dimension of Objective Landscapes}},\n  author = {Chunyuan Li and Heerad Farkhoor and Rosanne Liu and Jason Yosinski},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {1804.08838},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.08838}\n}"},{"id":"kernel-view-lm-finetuning","title":"A Kernel-Based View of Language Model Fine-Tuning","authors":"Malladi et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICML 2023","arxiv":"2210.05643","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.05643","claim":"Prompt-based fine-tuning of pretrained LMs often exhibits kernel (eNTK) dynamics; in that regime, a Johnson-Lindenstrauss argument shows SGD-LoRA preserves the fine-tuning kernel, explaining why low-rank subspace methods work.","relevance":"Gives the linearized (functorial-in-the-tangent-space) picture in which LoRA is a random projection of the full fine-tuning kernel; baseline for NTK-regime LoRA theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.05643","https://arxiv.org/html/2210.05643","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.05643","https://github.com/princeton-nlp/LM-Kernel-FT","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2210.05643"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sadhika Malladi","Alexander Wettig","Dingli Yu","Danqi Chen","Sanjeev Arora"],"bibkey":"malladi2022kernel","bibtex":"@inproceedings{malladi2022kernel,\n  title = {{A Kernel-Based View of Language Model Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Sadhika Malladi and Alexander Wettig and Dingli Yu and Danqi Chen and Sanjeev Arora},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2210.05643},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.05643}\n}"},{"id":"lora-ntk-no-spurious-minima","title":"LoRA Training in the NTK Regime has No Spurious Local Minima","authors":"Jang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.11867","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11867","claim":"In the NTK regime with \\(N\\) data points, full fine-tuning admits a low-rank solution of rank about \\(\\sqrt{N}\\); LoRA with rank of order \\(\\sqrt{N}\\) has no spurious local minima, so (S)GD finds such low-rank solutions, which generalize well.","relevance":"Landscape result for the bilinear LoRA parametrization; justifies optimizer-agnostic success of LoRA in the linearized regime.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11867","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11867","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.11867"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Uijeong Jang","Jason D. Lee","Ernest K. Ryu"],"bibkey":"jang2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{jang2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Training in the NTK Regime has No Spurious Local Minima}},\n  author = {Uijeong Jang and Jason D. Lee and Ernest K. Ryu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.11867},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11867}\n}"},{"id":"lora-converges-or-fails-loudly","title":"LoRA Training Provably Converges to a Low-Rank Global Minimum or It Fails Loudly (But it Probably Won't Fail)","authors":"Kim et al.","year":2025,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2502.09376","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09376","claim":"Without linearization, the paper separates a 'special' regime (where linearization holds) from a 'generic' one. In the generic regime, LoRA training converges either to a low-rank, small-magnitude global minimizer or to a qualitatively different high-rank, large-magnitude solution. Zero initialization and weight decay bias training toward the low-rank, small-magnitude region where the global minima lie.","relevance":"A landscape result for LoRA that does not rely on linearization. It links the identity-at-init convention (\\(B = 0\\)) and weight decay to an implicit bias, which a structural theory should model as part of the reparametrisation data.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09376","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2502.09376","https://arxiv.org/html/2502.09376","https://openreview.net/forum?id=o9zDYV4Ism"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Junsu Kim","Jaeyeon Kim","Ernest K. Ryu"],"bibkey":"kim2025lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kim2025lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Training Provably Converges to a Low-Rank Global Minimum or It Fails Loudly (But it Probably Won't Fail)}},\n  author = {Junsu Kim and Jaeyeon Kim and Ernest K. Ryu},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2502.09376},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09376}\n}"},{"id":"lora-gradient-flow-matrix-factorization","title":"Understanding the Learning Dynamics of LoRA: A Gradient Flow Perspective on Low-Rank Adaptation in Matrix Factorization","authors":"Xu et al.","year":2025,"venue":"AISTATS 2025","arxiv":"2503.06982","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.06982","claim":"For LoRA on matrix factorization under gradient flow, small initialization converges to a neighbourhood of the optimum whose error is governed by misalignment between pretrained and target singular spaces; a small spectral initialization converges to arbitrary precision.","relevance":"Clean dynamical model connecting initialization scale and singular-subspace alignment, the mechanism exploited by LoRA-GA and LoRA-One.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.06982","https://arxiv.org/html/2503.06982"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Ziqing Xu","Hancheng Min","Lachlan Ewen MacDonald","Jinqi Luo","Salma Tarmoun","Enrique Mallada","Rene Vidal"],"bibkey":"xu2025understanding","bibtex":"@inproceedings{xu2025understanding,\n  title = {{Understanding the Learning Dynamics of LoRA: A Gradient Flow Perspective on Low-Rank Adaptation in Matrix Factorization}},\n  author = {Ziqing Xu and Hancheng Min and Lachlan Ewen MacDonald and Jinqi Luo and Salma Tarmoun and Enrique Mallada and Rene Vidal},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics (AISTATS)},\n  eprint = {2503.06982},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.06982}\n}"},{"id":"lr-matters-vanilla-lora","title":"Learning Rate Matters: Vanilla LoRA May Suffice for LLM Fine-tuning","authors":"Lee et al.","year":2026,"venue":"NeurIPS 2026","arxiv":"2602.04998","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04998","claim":"Re-evaluating nine LoRA variants (initialization, architectural and optimizer changes, incl. PiSSA, MiLoRA, LoRA-GA, DoRA, LoFT) with thorough hyperparameter search shows they prefer different learning-rate ranges but reach similar peak performance (within 1-2%) once LR is tuned; the LR differences track the largest Hessian eigenvalue.","relevance":"Important caution for any taxonomy: many training-dynamics variants may be reparametrizations of the learning rate rather than genuinely different adaptation morphisms.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04998","https://arxiv.org/html/2602.04998"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yu-Ang Lee","Ching-Yun Ko","Pin-Yu Chen","Mi-Yen Yeh"],"bibkey":"lee2026learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lee2026learning,\n  title = {{Learning Rate Matters: Vanilla LoRA May Suffice for LLM Fine-tuning}},\n  author = {Yu-Ang Lee and Ching-Yun Ko and Pin-Yu Chen and Mi-Yen Yeh},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2602.04998},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.04998}\n}"},{"id":"lora-meets-dropout","title":"LoRA Meets Dropout under a Unified Framework","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"Findings of ACL 2024","arxiv":"2403.00812","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.00812","claim":"LoRA fine-tuning is still overfitting-prone; transformer dropout variants (DropKey, DropAttention, HiddenCut) can be unified by dropping position, structural pattern and compensation, and the best combination (HiddenKey) improves LoRA on NLU and NLG.","relevance":"Complements LoRA Dropout: places regularization of the low-rank branch inside a general design space.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.00812","https://arxiv.org/html/2403.00812","https://openreview.net/forum?id=EQ9zm9ZjeR"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["lora-core"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sheng Wang","Liheng Chen","Jiyue Jiang","Boyang Xue","Lingpeng Kong","Chuan Wu"],"bibkey":"wang2024lorad","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2024lorad,\n  title = {{LoRA Meets Dropout under a Unified Framework}},\n  author = {Sheng Wang and Liheng Chen and Jiyue Jiang and Boyang Xue and Lingpeng Kong and Chuan Wu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024},\n  eprint = {2403.00812},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.00812}\n}"},{"id":"minimum-hyperspherical-energy","title":"Learning towards Minimum Hyperspherical Energy","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2018,"venue":"NeurIPS 2018","arxiv":"1805.09298","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09298","claim":"Defines hyperspherical energy, a Thomson-problem-style potential of neurons on the unit sphere, as a measure of neuron diversity, and regularizes networks to minimize it.","relevance":"Hyperspherical energy is the invariant that OFT, BOFT, GOFT and HRA preserve. Orthogonal group actions are exactly the isometries that fix pairwise neuron angles, so orthogonal PEFT is fine-tuning inside an orbit that keeps this invariant.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09298","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/peft/main/docs/source/package_reference/oft.md"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Weiyang Liu","Rongmei Lin","Zhen Liu","Lixin Liu","Zhiding Yu","Bo Dai","Le Song"],"bibkey":"liu2018learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2018learning,\n  title = {{Learning towards Minimum Hyperspherical Energy}},\n  author = {Weiyang Liu and Rongmei Lin and Zhen Liu and Lixin Liu and Zhiding Yu and Bo Dai and Le Song},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1805.09298},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.09298}\n}"},{"id":"orthogonal-over-parameterized-training","title":"Orthogonal Over-Parameterized Training","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2020,"venue":"CVPR 2021","arxiv":"2004.04690","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04690","claim":"Trains a network by learning an orthogonal transformation applied to fixed, randomly initialized neurons. This provably keeps the hyperspherical energy constant and improves generalization.","relevance":"Direct precursor of OFT: the trainable object is only a group element of \\(O(d)\\) acting on a frozen weight, which is the template for every multiplicative PEFT method.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04690"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Weiyang Liu","Rongmei Lin","Zhen Liu","James M. Rehg","Liam Paull","Li Xiong","Le Song","Adrian Weller"],"bibkey":"liu2020orthogonal","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2020orthogonal,\n  title = {{Orthogonal Over-Parameterized Training}},\n  author = {Weiyang Liu and Rongmei Lin and Zhen Liu and James M. Rehg and Liam Paull and Li Xiong and Le Song and Adrian Weller},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR)},\n  eprint = {2004.04690},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.04690}\n}"},{"id":"weight-normalization","title":"Weight Normalization: A Simple Reparameterization to Accelerate Training of Deep Neural Networks","authors":"Salimans & Kingma","year":2016,"venue":"NeurIPS 2016","arxiv":"1602.07868","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07868","claim":"Reparameterizes each weight vector as \\(w = g v/\\|v\\|\\), separating magnitude from direction, which improves conditioning and speeds up SGD.","relevance":"The template for the magnitude-direction decompositions in DoRA, BiDoRA and DeLoRA: a polar-type factorization of each neuron into a radial scalar and a point on the sphere.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07868","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.09353"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Tim Salimans","Diederik P. Kingma"],"bibkey":"salimans2016weight","bibtex":"@inproceedings{salimans2016weight,\n  title = {{Weight Normalization: A Simple Reparameterization to Accelerate Training of Deep Neural Networks}},\n  author = {Tim Salimans and Diederik P. Kingma},\n  year = {2016},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1602.07868},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.07868}\n}"},{"id":"film","title":"FiLM: Visual Reasoning with a General Conditioning Layer","authors":"Perez et al.","year":2017,"venue":"AAAI 2018","arxiv":"1709.07871","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07871","claim":"A feature-wise affine modulation (\\(\\gamma \\odot x + \\beta\\)), predicted from a conditioning input, is a simple and general conditioning layer for neural networks.","relevance":"Feature-wise scale and shift is the algebraic building block behind (IA)\\(^3\\), SSF, RED, DiffFit and LN tuning: an action of the diagonal affine group on channels, learned as a static vector instead of predicted per input.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07871"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Ethan Perez","Florian Strub","Harm de Vries","Vincent Dumoulin","Aaron Courville"],"bibkey":"perez2017film","bibtex":"@inproceedings{perez2017film,\n  title = {{FiLM: Visual Reasoning with a General Conditioning Layer}},\n  author = {Ethan Perez and Florian Strub and Harm de Vries and Vincent Dumoulin and Aaron Courville},\n  year = {2017},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {1709.07871},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07871}\n}"},{"id":"butterfly-factorization","title":"Learning Fast Algorithms for Linear Transforms Using Butterfly Factorizations","authors":"Dao et al.","year":2019,"venue":"ICML 2019","arxiv":"1903.05895","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05895","claim":"Products of \\(O(\\log n)\\) sparse butterfly factors can represent and learn fast transforms (FFT, DCT, Hadamard, ...) with \\(O(n \\log n)\\) parameters.","relevance":"The structural basis of BOFT and of GOFT's parallel Givens layers: dense group elements written as short words in sparse generators, which is how multiplicative PEFT reaches dense rotations cheaply.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05895","https://arxiv.org/html/2311.06243"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Tri Dao","Albert Gu","Matthew Eichhorn","Atri Rudra","Christopher Ré"],"bibkey":"dao2019learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dao2019learning,\n  title = {{Learning Fast Algorithms for Linear Transforms Using Butterfly Factorizations}},\n  author = {Tri Dao and Albert Gu and Matthew Eichhorn and Atri Rudra and Christopher Ré},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1903.05895},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.05895}\n}"},{"id":"cheap-orthogonal-constraints","title":"Cheap Orthogonal Constraints in Neural Networks: A Simple Parametrization of the Orthogonal and Unitary Group","authors":"Lezcano-Casado & Martínez-Rubio","year":2019,"venue":"ICML 2019","arxiv":"1901.08428","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08428","claim":"Parameterizes the special orthogonal and unitary groups through the exponential map from skew-symmetric (Lie-algebra) matrices, so optimization is unconstrained while orthogonality holds exactly.","relevance":"Supplies the Lie algebra to Lie group charts (exponential, Cayley) that OFT, BOFT, OFTv2, PSOFT and Spectral Adapter use. In these terms, multiplicative PEFT is gradient descent in a Lie algebra pushed forward to a group acting on \\(W_0\\).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08428"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Mario Lezcano-Casado","David Martínez-Rubio"],"bibkey":"lezcanocasado2019cheap","bibtex":"@inproceedings{lezcanocasado2019cheap,\n  title = {{Cheap Orthogonal Constraints in Neural Networks: A Simple Parametrization of the Orthogonal and Unitary Group}},\n  author = {Mario Lezcano-Casado and David Martínez-Rubio},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1901.08428},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.08428}\n}"},{"id":"householder-orthogonal-rnn","title":"Efficient Orthogonal Parametrisation of Recurrent Neural Networks Using Householder Reflections","authors":"Mhammedi et al.","year":2016,"venue":"ICML 2017","arxiv":"1612.00188","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00188","claim":"Parameterizes orthogonal transition matrices as products of Householder reflections, which gives exact orthogonality and a tunable trade-off between the number of reflections and expressivity at low cost.","relevance":"The ancestor of HRA and ETHER: Householder words generate \\(O(d)\\), and word length controls capacity. HRA's '\\(r\\) reflections = identity plus rank \\(r\\)' is the PEFT version of this fact.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00188"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zakaria Mhammedi","Andrew Hellicar","Ashfaqur Rahman","James Bailey"],"bibkey":"mhammedi2016efficient","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mhammedi2016efficient,\n  title = {{Efficient Orthogonal Parametrisation of Recurrent Neural Networks Using Householder Reflections}},\n  author = {Zakaria Mhammedi and Andrew Hellicar and Ashfaqur Rahman and James Bailey},\n  year = {2016},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1612.00188},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00188}\n}"},{"id":"primacy-of-magnitude","title":"The Primacy of Magnitude in Low-Rank Adaptation","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2025,"venue":"NeurIPS 2025","arxiv":"2507.06558","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06558","claim":"Argues that the magnitude of the weight update is the main driver of LoRA performance and that low-rank structure bounds it. Proposes LoRAM, a magnitude-driven initialization that matches spectral initializations without their SVD cost.","relevance":"Isolates the magnitude half of magnitude-direction decompositions (DoRA, DeLoRA, PoLAR) and suggests that many spectral or initialization gains come down to controlling update magnitude.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06558"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["multiplicative"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Zicheng Zhang","Haoran Li","Yifeng Zhang","Guoqiang Gong","Jiaxing Wang","Junxing Hu","Pengzhang Liu","Qixia Jiang"],"bibkey":"zhang2025primacy","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2025primacy,\n  title = {{The Primacy of Magnitude in Low-Rank Adaptation}},\n  author = {Zicheng Zhang and Haoran Li and Yifeng Zhang and Guoqiang Gong and Jiaxing Wang and Junxing Hu and Pengzhang Liu and Qixia Jiang},\n  year = {2025},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2507.06558},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.06558}\n}"},{"id":"prefix-tuning-capabilities-limitations","title":"When Do Prompting and Prefix-Tuning Work? A Theory of Capabilities and Limitations","authors":"Petrov et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2310.19698","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19698","claim":"Although continuous embeddings are more expressive than discrete tokens, soft prompting and prefix-tuning are potentially less expressive than full fine-tuning, even with the same number of learnable parameters. A prefix cannot change the relative attention pattern over the content and can only bias an attention layer's output in a fixed direction, so these methods elicit existing skills but may fail on tasks that need new attention patterns.","relevance":"Precisely characterises the image of the prefix reparametrisation: a prefix cannot change the relative attention pattern over the content and can only bias an attention layer's output in a fixed direction. This justifies placing prompt/prefix methods in a separate class (input composition) from weight-space updates.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19698","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2310.19698"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Aleksandar Petrov","Philip H. S. Torr","Adel Bibi"],"bibkey":"petrov2023prompting","bibtex":"@inproceedings{petrov2023prompting,\n  title = {{When Do Prompting and Prefix-Tuning Work? A Theory of Capabilities and Limitations}},\n  author = {Aleksandar Petrov and Philip H. S. Torr and Adel Bibi},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2310.19698},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19698}\n}"},{"id":"wang-2023-prompt-universality","title":"Universality and Limitations of Prompt Tuning","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2305.18787","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18787","claim":"There exists a transformer for which prompts approximate any Lipschitz sequence-to-sequence function; conversely, for a given single encoder layer there are datasets that no prompt of any length can memorize, with a lower bound on prompt parameters compared against a LoRA update in the single-layer setting, and sufficient conditions in the multi-layer case under which only invertible functions can be learned.","relevance":"Gives both a universality (density) statement and an obstruction for prompt tuning, and a direct parameter-count comparison with low-rank weight updates.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18787","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2305.18787"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yihan Wang","Jatin Chauhan","Wei Wang","Cho-Jui Hsieh"],"bibkey":"wang2023universality","bibtex":"@inproceedings{wang2023universality,\n  title = {{Universality and Limitations of Prompt Tuning}},\n  author = {Yihan Wang and Jatin Chauhan and Wei Wang and Cho-Jui Hsieh},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2305.18787},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18787}\n}"},{"id":"petrov-2024-prefix-universal","title":"Prompting a Pretrained Transformer Can Be a Universal Approximator","authors":"Petrov et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.14753","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/petrov24a.html","claim":"Prefix-tuning a single attention head suffices to approximate any continuous function, and any sequence-to-sequence function can be approximated by prefixing a transformer of depth linear in the sequence length, with Jackson-type bounds on the required prefix length.","relevance":"Complements the limitation results: the prompt family is universal for suitably chosen (even small) pretrained models, so its expressivity depends on the frozen backbone, a functorial dependence worth formalizing.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14753","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.14753"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts","theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Aleksandar Petrov","Philip H. S. Torr","Adel Bibi"],"bibkey":"petrov2024prompting","bibtex":"@inproceedings{petrov2024prompting,\n  title = {{Prompting a Pretrained Transformer Can Be a Universal Approximator}},\n  author = {Aleksandar Petrov and Philip H. S. Torr and Adel Bibi},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.14753},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/petrov24a.html}\n}"},{"id":"oymak-2023-attention-prompt-tuning","title":"On the Role of Attention in Prompt-tuning","authors":"Oymak et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICML 2023","arxiv":"2306.03435","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/oymak23a.html","claim":"In one-layer attention models on contextual mixture data, softmax prompt-attention is provably more expressive than softmax self-attention and linear prompt-attention; gradient descent learns the prompt with near-optimal sample complexity and the prompt attends to sparse context-relevant tokens.","relevance":"Isolates the attention mechanism as the channel through which prompts act, with learning-theoretic guarantees.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03435"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Samet Oymak","Ankit Singh Rawat","Mahdi Soltanolkotabi","Christos Thrampoulidis"],"bibkey":"oymak2023role","bibtex":"@inproceedings{oymak2023role,\n  title = {{On the Role of Attention in Prompt-tuning}},\n  author = {Samet Oymak and Ankit Singh Rawat and Mahdi Soltanolkotabi and Christos Thrampoulidis},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2306.03435},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/oymak23a.html}\n}"},{"id":"galim-2025-ssm-prefix-equivalence","title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of State Space Models","authors":"Galim et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2410.09016","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016","claim":"For S4, any prefix acts on the outputs only through an initial hidden state, so prefix-tuning is upper-bounded by initial-state tuning, with equality iff the prefix length is at least the state size \\(H\\) (under a genericity assumption); prompt-based methods are therefore weak on SSMs, LoRA works best on linear projections, and Sparse Dimension Tuning (SDT) is proposed for SSM modules.","relevance":"A clean theorem embedding the prompt family into the 'learned initial state' family: on SSMs, prompts are points of the state space the recurrence starts from (all of it once \\(\\ell \\ge H\\)).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.09016"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":["initial-state-tuning"],"authors_full":["Kevin Galim","Wonjun Kang","Yuchen Zeng","Hyung Il Koo","Kangwook Lee"],"bibkey":"galim2024parameter","bibtex":"@inproceedings{galim2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of State Space Models}},\n  author = {Kevin Galim and Wonjun Kang and Yuchen Zeng and Hyung Il Koo and Kangwook Lee},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2410.09016},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09016}\n}"},{"id":"le-2025-revisiting-prefix","title":"Revisiting Prefix-tuning: Statistical Benefits of Reparameterization among Prompts","authors":"Le et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.02200","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02200","claim":"The MLP reparametrization in prefix-tuning implicitly shares structure between prefix keys and values; via the prefix-tuning/mixture-of-experts connection this shared structure provably improves sample efficiency of parameter estimation, with similar benefits for prompt tuning.","relevance":"Explains why training-time reparametrizations (prefix MLP, P-Tuning encoder, Residual PT) matter even though they vanish at inference: they change the statistical geometry, not the hypothesis class.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02200"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Minh Le","Chau Nguyen","Huy Nguyen","Quyen Tran","Trung Le","Nhat Ho"],"bibkey":"le2024revisiting","bibtex":"@inproceedings{le2024revisiting,\n  title = {{Revisiting Prefix-tuning: Statistical Benefits of Reparameterization among Prompts}},\n  author = {Minh Le and Chau Nguyen and Huy Nguyen and Quyen Tran and Trung Le and Nhat Ho},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.02200},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02200}\n}"},{"id":"qin-2021-intrinsic-task-subspace","title":"Exploring Universal Intrinsic Task Subspace via Prompt Tuning","authors":"Qin et al.","year":2021,"venue":"IEEE/ACM TASLP 2024","arxiv":"2110.07867","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2024.3430545","claim":"Soft prompts of 100 NLP tasks can be decomposed into a shared 250-dimensional nonlinear subspace; tuning only 250 free parameters recovers 97% (seen tasks) and 83% (20 unseen tasks) of full prompt-tuning performance.","relevance":"Empirical evidence that the 'space of task adaptations' reachable by prompts is low-dimensional and shared, motivating a moduli-space view of PEFT.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.07867"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yujia Qin","Xiaozhi Wang","Yusheng Su","Yankai Lin","Ning Ding","Jing Yi","Weize Chen","Zhiyuan Liu","Juanzi Li","Lei Hou","Peng Li","Maosong Sun","Jie Zhou"],"bibkey":"qin2021exploring","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qin2021exploring,\n  title = {{Exploring Universal Intrinsic Task Subspace via Prompt Tuning}},\n  author = {Yujia Qin and Xiaozhi Wang and Yusheng Su and Yankai Lin and Ning Ding and Jing Yi and Weize Chen and Zhiyuan Liu and Juanzi Li and Lei Hou and Peng Li and Maosong Sun and Jie Zhou},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {IEEE/ACM TASLP},\n  eprint = {2110.07867},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1109/TASLP.2024.3430545}\n}"},{"id":"su-2022-prompt-transferability","title":"On Transferability of Prompt Tuning for Natural Language Processing","authors":"Su et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NAACL 2022","arxiv":"2111.06719","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.290/","claim":"Soft prompts transfer zero-shot to similar tasks on the same PLM and, through a trained cross-model projector, to other PLMs; as initialization they accelerate and improve prompt tuning; the overlap rate of activated neurons predicts transferability better than embedding similarity.","relevance":"Treats prompts as transportable objects between tasks and between models (a projector is literally a map between prompt spaces of different backbones).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06719"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yusheng Su","Xiaozhi Wang","Yujia Qin","Chi-Min Chan","Yankai Lin","Huadong Wang","Kaiyue Wen","Zhiyuan Liu","Peng Li","Juanzi Li","Lei Hou","Maosong Sun","Jie Zhou"],"bibkey":"su2021transferability","bibtex":"@inproceedings{su2021transferability,\n  title = {{On Transferability of Prompt Tuning for Natural Language Processing}},\n  author = {Yusheng Su and Xiaozhi Wang and Yujia Qin and Chi-Min Chan and Yankai Lin and Huadong Wang and Kaiyue Wen and Zhiyuan Liu and Peng Li and Juanzi Li and Lei Hou and Maosong Sun and Jie Zhou},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2111.06719},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.290/}\n}"},{"id":"khashabi-2022-prompt-waywardness","title":"Prompt Waywardness: The Curious Case of Discretized Interpretation of Continuous Prompts","authors":"Khashabi et al.","year":2021,"venue":"NAACL 2022","arxiv":"2112.08348","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.266/","claim":"Continuous prompts can solve a task while their nearest-neighbour discrete projection is arbitrary text (even a contradictory task definition), within ~2% of the best prompt's accuracy; the effect grows with model size.","relevance":"Shows that the map from continuous prompts back to discrete text is far from faithful: soft prompts live outside the image of the vocabulary embedding, so 'discrete prompting' is a thin subobject of the prompt space.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.08348"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Daniel Khashabi","Shane Lyu","Sewon Min","Lianhui Qin","Kyle Richardson","Sean Welleck","Hannaneh Hajishirzi","Tushar Khot","Ashish Sabharwal","Sameer Singh","Yejin Choi"],"bibkey":"khashabi2021prompt","bibtex":"@inproceedings{khashabi2021prompt,\n  title = {{Prompt Waywardness: The Curious Case of Discretized Interpretation of Continuous Prompts}},\n  author = {Daniel Khashabi and Shane Lyu and Sewon Min and Lianhui Qin and Kyle Richardson and Sean Welleck and Hannaneh Hajishirzi and Tushar Khot and Ashish Sabharwal and Sameer Singh and Yejin Choi},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (NAACL)},\n  eprint = {2112.08348},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.naacl-main.266/}\n}"},{"id":"liu-2023-pretrain-prompt-predict","title":"Pre-train, Prompt, and Predict: A Systematic Survey of Prompting Methods in Natural Language Processing","authors":"Liu et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACM Computing Surveys 2023","arxiv":"2107.13586","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3560815","claim":"Systematizes prompt-based learning (templates, answer spaces, discrete and continuous prompts, tuning strategies such as fixed-LM prompt tuning and prompt+LM tuning).","relevance":"Standard reference for the discrete/continuous prompt landscape and its notation.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13586"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Pengfei Liu","Weizhe Yuan","Jinlan Fu","Zhengbao Jiang","Hiroaki Hayashi","Graham Neubig"],"bibkey":"liu2021pre","bibtex":"@inproceedings{liu2021pre,\n  title = {{Pre-train, Prompt, and Predict: A Systematic Survey of Prompting Methods in Natural Language Processing}},\n  author = {Pengfei Liu and Weizhe Yuan and Jinlan Fu and Zhengbao Jiang and Hiroaki Hayashi and Graham Neubig},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {ACM Computing Surveys},\n  eprint = {2107.13586},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1145/3560815}\n}"},{"id":"chen-2022-inducer-tuning","title":"Inducer-tuning: Connecting Prefix-tuning and Adapter-tuning","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2022,"venue":"EMNLP 2022","arxiv":"2210.14469","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.50/","claim":"Interprets prefixes as inducing variables of a kernel estimator in attention and proposes inducer-tuning, which keeps prefix-tuning's mechanism but uses an adapter-style residual form to fix prefix initialization.","relevance":"A second, kernel-theoretic bridge between the prompt and adapter families (low citation count, kept for its structural content).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.14469"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["prompts"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yifan Chen","Devamanyu Hazarika","Mahdi Namazifar","Yang Liu","Di Jin","Dilek Hakkani-Tur"],"bibkey":"chen2022inducer","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2022inducer,\n  title = {{Inducer-tuning: Connecting Prefix-tuning and Adapter-tuning}},\n  author = {Yifan Chen and Devamanyu Hazarika and Mahdi Namazifar and Yang Liu and Di Jin and Dilek Hakkani-Tur},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP)},\n  eprint = {2210.14469},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://aclanthology.org/2022.emnlp-main.50/}\n}"},{"id":"gradient-descent-tiny-subspace","title":"Gradient Descent Happens in a Tiny Subspace","authors":"Gur-Ari et al.","year":2018,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"1812.04754","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04754","claim":"After a short period of training, the gradient converges to a very small subspace spanned by the top few Hessian eigenvectors (about as many as there are classes) and stays there.","relevance":"Empirical basis for the 'dual' (gradient-side) PEFT view: if the gradient flow is effectively low-dimensional, projecting gradients (GaLore, Flora, APOLLO) or confining updates to low-rank parameter subspaces (LoRA) loses little. It also explains why projector refresh schedules matter.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04754"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Guy Gur-Ari","Daniel A. Roberts","Ethan Dyer"],"bibkey":"gurari2018gradient","bibtex":"@article{gurari2018gradient,\n  title = {{Gradient Descent Happens in a Tiny Subspace}},\n  author = {Guy Gur-Ari and Daniel A. Roberts and Ethan Dyer},\n  year = {2018},\n  eprint = {1812.04754},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1812.04754},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1812.04754}\n}"},{"id":"adafactor","title":"Adafactor: Adaptive Learning Rates with Sublinear Memory Cost","authors":"Shazeer & Stern","year":2018,"venue":"ICML 2018","arxiv":"1804.04235","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.04235","claim":"Adam's per-parameter second moment for a weight matrix can be replaced by its row and column sums (a rank-one factorization), cutting optimizer memory from \\(O(nm)\\) to \\(O(n+m)\\) with similar results. Update clipping and an increasing decay rate fix the resulting instability.","relevance":"The prototype of optimizer-state compression: factoring the second moment is the optimizer-side counterpart of factoring the weight update. It is reused directly by Flora and AdaLomo and shares a lineage with Adam-mini, APOLLO and GaLore-Adafactor.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.04235","https://arxiv.org/html/1804.04235","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v80/shazeer18a.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Noam Shazeer","Mitchell Stern"],"bibkey":"shazeer2018adafactor","bibtex":"@inproceedings{shazeer2018adafactor,\n  title = {{Adafactor: Adaptive Learning Rates with Sublinear Memory Cost}},\n  author = {Noam Shazeer and Mitchell Stern},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1804.04235},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.04235}\n}"},{"id":"8bit-optimizers","title":"8-bit Optimizers via Block-wise Quantization","authors":"Dettmers et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2022","arxiv":"2110.02861","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.02861","claim":"Block-wise dynamic quantization, combined with a stable embedding layer, lets Adam and momentum states be stored in 8 bits with no loss in performance, removing about 75% of optimizer-state memory.","relevance":"Shows that optimizer states, not just weights, can be quantized. Its block-wise quantization (released in bitsandbytes) is the basis of QLoRA's block-wise NF4/double quantization and of 8-bit GaLore, and its 8-bit Adam is the baseline of GaLore 2. Paged optimizers were added later by QLoRA, not by this paper.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.02861","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.02861"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Tim Dettmers","Mike Lewis","Sam Shleifer","Luke Zettlemoyer"],"bibkey":"dettmers20218","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dettmers20218,\n  title = {{8-bit Optimizers via Block-wise Quantization}},\n  author = {Tim Dettmers and Mike Lewis and Sam Shleifer and Luke Zettlemoyer},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2110.02861},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.02861}\n}"},{"id":"golore-subspace-convergence","title":"Subspace Optimization for Large Language Models with Convergence Guarantees","authors":"He et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2025","arxiv":"2410.11289","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11289","claim":"GaLore does not always converge to a stationary point in the stochastic setting: SVD projectors become noise-dominated when the true gradient is small, as an explicit counterexample shows. It does converge with large batches or isotropic noise. GoLore, which samples random projections uniformly from the Stiefel manifold, provably converges at standard batch sizes.","relevance":"Gives the projector-selection morphism of gradient-side PEFT a precise convergence semantics. Data-dependent (SVD) projectors and data-independent (random) projectors behave differently, which explains the shift toward random or online projectors (Flora, APOLLO, Online Subspace Descent).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11289","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.11289","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v267/he25i.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yutong He","Pengrui Li","Yipeng Hu","Chuyan Chen","Kun Yuan"],"bibkey":"he2024subspace","bibtex":"@inproceedings{he2024subspace,\n  title = {{Subspace Optimization for Large Language Models with Convergence Guarantees}},\n  author = {Yutong He and Pengrui Li and Yipeng Hu and Chuyan Chen and Kun Yuan},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2410.11289},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11289}\n}"},{"id":"zo-bench","title":"Revisiting Zeroth-Order Optimization for Memory-Efficient LLM Fine-Tuning: A Benchmark","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.11592","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11592","claim":"A benchmark of BP-free zeroth-order fine-tuning across five LLM families, three task difficulties and five fine-tuning schemes. It finds that task alignment and the forward-gradient method matter, and proposes block-wise descent, hybrid ZO/FO training and gradient sparsity to improve ZO fine-tuning.","relevance":"Maps the design space around MeZO and shows that ZO estimators combine naturally with parameter-side PEFT (LoRA, prefix). That makes the zeroth-order axis composable with the parametrization axis in a unified theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11592","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.11592","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/zhang24ad.html"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["quant-memory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yihua Zhang","Pingzhi Li","Junyuan Hong","Jiaxiang Li","Yimeng Zhang","Wenqing Zheng","Pin-Yu Chen","Jason D. Lee","Wotao Yin","Mingyi Hong","Zhangyang Wang","Sijia Liu","Tianlong Chen"],"bibkey":"zhang2024revisiting","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024revisiting,\n  title = {{Revisiting Zeroth-Order Optimization for Memory-Efficient LLM Fine-Tuning: A Benchmark}},\n  author = {Yihua Zhang and Pingzhi Li and Junyuan Hong and Jiaxiang Li and Yimeng Zhang and Wenqing Zheng and Pin-Yu Chen and Jason D. Lee and Wotao Yin and Mingyi Hong and Zhangyang Wang and Sijia Liu and Tianlong Chen},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.11592},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.11592}\n}"},{"id":"lottery-ticket-hypothesis","title":"The Lottery Ticket Hypothesis: Finding Sparse, Trainable Neural Networks","authors":"Frankle & Carbin","year":2018,"venue":"ICLR 2019","arxiv":"1803.03635","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03635","claim":"Dense randomly initialized networks contain sparse subnetworks ('winning tickets') that, trained in isolation from their original initialization, match the full network's accuracy; iterative magnitude pruning finds them.","relevance":"Supplies the train-select-rewind-retrain recipe behind LT-SFT and the general idea that a sparse sub-object of parameter space suffices, i.e. selection as a subobject/mask functor on parameter spaces.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03635"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jonathan Frankle","Michael Carbin"],"bibkey":"frankle2018lottery","bibtex":"@inproceedings{frankle2018lottery,\n  title = {{The Lottery Ticket Hypothesis: Finding Sparse, Trainable Neural Networks}},\n  author = {Jonathan Frankle and Michael Carbin},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {1803.03635},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.03635}\n}"},{"id":"batchnorm-only","title":"Training BatchNorm and Only BatchNorm: On the Expressive Power of Random Features in CNNs","authors":"Frankle et al.","year":2020,"venue":"ICLR 2021","arxiv":"2003.00152","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00152","claim":"Training only BatchNorm's affine parameters on top of frozen random weights reaches surprisingly high accuracy (e.g. 82% on CIFAR-10 for deep ResNets), far above training an equal number of random other parameters.","relevance":"Evidence that per-channel affine (diagonal scale/shift) parameters are disproportionately expressive, the foundation for BitFit, LN Tuning and (IA)\\(^3\\)-style diagonal methods.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00152","https://arxiv.org/html/2003.00152"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jonathan Frankle","David J. Schwab","Ari S. Morcos"],"bibkey":"frankle2020training","bibtex":"@inproceedings{frankle2020training,\n  title = {{Training BatchNorm and Only BatchNorm: On the Expressive Power of Random Features in CNNs}},\n  author = {Jonathan Frankle and David J. Schwab and Ari S. Morcos},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2003.00152},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.00152}\n}"},{"id":"effectiveness-of-peft-sparse-view","title":"On the Effectiveness of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning","authors":"Fu et al.","year":2022,"venue":"AAAI 2023","arxiv":"2211.15583","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583","claim":"Shows that random, rule-based and projection-based PEFT methods are all \\(p\\)-sparse fine-tuned models (with respect to an equivalent model for structure-changing methods) and proves that sparsity regularizes by bounding stability, which yields generalization bounds.","relevance":"A theorem-level unification of PEFT under the selective view (\\(\\theta = \\theta_0 + M \\Delta \\theta\\)), directly usable as the 'mask' object in a categorical account; also motivates the SAM selection method.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583","https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15583","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2211.15583"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective","theory"],"method_ids":["sam-sparse"],"authors_full":["Zihao Fu","Haoran Yang","Anthony Man-Cho So","Wai Lam","Lidong Bing","Nigel Collier"],"bibkey":"fu2022effectiveness","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fu2022effectiveness,\n  title = {{On the Effectiveness of Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Zihao Fu and Haoran Yang and Anthony Man-Cho So and Wai Lam and Lidong Bing and Nigel Collier},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI)},\n  eprint = {2211.15583},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15583}\n}"},{"id":"skill-localization-grafting","title":"Task-Specific Skill Localization in Fine-tuned Language Models","authors":"Panigrahi et al.","year":2023,"venue":"ICML 2023","arxiv":"2302.06600","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06600","claim":"Grafting the fine-tuned values of a tiny subset (~0.01%) of parameters onto the pretrained model recovers over 95% of the fine-tuned model's performance; such grafted regions localize task skills and help continual learning.","relevance":"Empirical evidence that fine-tuning deltas are effectively sparse, supporting selective PEFT and composition of sparse deltas (as in LT-SFT).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06600","https://arxiv.org/html/2302.06600"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Abhishek Panigrahi","Nikunj Saunshi","Haoyu Zhao","Sanjeev Arora"],"bibkey":"panigrahi2023task","bibtex":"@inproceedings{panigrahi2023task,\n  title = {{Task-Specific Skill Localization in Fine-tuned Language Models}},\n  author = {Abhishek Panigrahi and Nikunj Saunshi and Haoyu Zhao and Sanjeev Arora},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2302.06600},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06600}\n}"},{"id":"elsa-freezing-layers","title":"What Would Elsa Do? Freezing Layers During Transformer Fine-Tuning","authors":"Lee et al.","year":2019,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"1911.03090","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.03090","claim":"Fine-tuning only the final quarter of BERT/RoBERTa layers (3-5 of 12 in base models) retains about 90% of full fine-tuning quality.","relevance":"Early quantification of layer-depth selection, the coarse end of the selective family (top-\\(k\\) layer tuning, surgical fine-tuning, LISA).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.03090","https://arxiv.org/html/1911.03090"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["selective"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Jaejun Lee","Raphael Tang","Jimmy Lin"],"bibkey":"lee2019would","bibtex":"@article{lee2019would,\n  title = {{What Would Elsa Do? Freezing Layers During Transformer Fine-Tuning}},\n  author = {Jaejun Lee and Raphael Tang and Jimmy Lin},\n  year = {2019},\n  eprint = {1911.03090},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1911.03090},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.03090}\n}"},{"id":"phm-layer","title":"Beyond Fully-Connected Layers with Quaternions: Parameterization of Hypercomplex Multiplications with 1/n Parameters","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ICLR 2021","arxiv":"2102.08597","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08597","claim":"A dense layer can be parameterized as a sum of \\(n\\) Kronecker products, \\(W = \\sum_i A_i \\otimes B_i\\), which subsumes the quaternion Hamilton product and learns arbitrary \\(n\\)-D hypercomplex multiplication rules with about \\(1/n\\) of the parameters.","relevance":"The algebraic origin of PHM-Adapter, Compacter and KAdaptation; makes 'Kronecker rank' an explicit design axis, the Kronecker analogue of LoRA's matrix rank.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08597","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2102.08597"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Aston Zhang","Yi Tay","Shuai Zhang","Alvin Chan","Anh Tuan Luu","Siu Cheung Hui","Jie Fu"],"bibkey":"zhang2021beyond","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2021beyond,\n  title = {{Beyond Fully-Connected Layers with Quaternions: Parameterization of Hypercomplex Multiplications with 1/n Parameters}},\n  author = {Aston Zhang and Yi Tay and Shuai Zhang and Alvin Chan and Anh Tuan Luu and Siu Cheung Hui and Jie Fu},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2102.08597},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.08597}\n}"},{"id":"kronecker-approximation","title":"Approximation with Kronecker Products","authors":"Van Loan and Pitsianis","year":1993,"venue":"Linear Algebra for Large Scale and Real-Time Applications (NATO ASI Series E, vol. 232), Springer, pp. 293-314","arxiv":"","url":"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-015-8196-7_17","claim":"The nearest Kronecker product (and sum of Kronecker products) to a matrix is obtained from an SVD of a rearrangement of that matrix, so Kronecker rank equals ordinary rank after rearrangement.","relevance":"Shows that Kronecker-sum adapters (PHM, KAdaptation, KronA) are low-rank adapters in a different matricization, a single invariant that organizes the whole 'shape' family.","sources":["https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-015-8196-7_17","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1007/978-94-015-8196-7_17"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"loan1993approximation","bibtex":"@inproceedings{loan1993approximation,\n  title = {{Approximation with Kronecker Products}},\n  author = {Van Loan and Pitsianis},\n  year = {1993},\n  booktitle = {Linear Algebra for Large Scale and Real-Time Applications (NATO ASI Series E, vol. 232), Springer, pp. 293-314},\n  url = {https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-94-015-8196-7_17}\n}"},{"id":"tensor-train-decomposition","title":"Tensor-Train Decomposition","authors":"Oseledets","year":2011,"venue":"SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 33(5):2295-2317","arxiv":"","url":"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/090752286","claim":"Introduces the tensor-train (TT) format with SVD-based TT-SVD and rounding algorithms, storing a \\(d\\)-way tensor with \\(O(d n r^2)\\) parameters.","relevance":"Mathematical basis of LoRETTA, TT-LoRA, FacT-TT and MetaTT; TT is the simplest non-trivial tensor network (a linear chain).","sources":["https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/090752286","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1137/090752286"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"oseledets2011tensor","bibtex":"@inproceedings{oseledets2011tensor,\n  title = {{Tensor-Train Decomposition}},\n  author = {Oseledets},\n  year = {2011},\n  booktitle = {SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 33(5):2295-},\n  url = {https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/090752286}\n}"},{"id":"tensor-decompositions-applications","title":"Tensor Decompositions and Applications","authors":"Kolda and Bader","year":2009,"venue":"SIAM Review 51(3):455-500","arxiv":"","url":"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/07070111X","claim":"Standard survey of CP (CANDECOMP/PARAFAC), Tucker and related decompositions, mode-\\(n\\) products and their algorithms.","relevance":"Reference notation for the Tucker (LoTR, FLoRA, FacT-TK) and CP (LoRTA) adapters and for VeRA-style shared bases read as CP models.","sources":["https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/07070111X","https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1137/07070111X"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":[],"bibkey":"kolda2009tensor","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kolda2009tensor,\n  title = {{Tensor Decompositions and Applications}},\n  author = {Kolda and Bader},\n  year = {2009},\n  booktitle = {SIAM Review 51(3):455-500},\n  url = {https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/07070111X}\n}"},{"id":"tensor-networks-dimensionality-reduction","title":"Low-Rank Tensor Networks for Dimensionality Reduction and Large-Scale Optimization Problems: Perspectives and Challenges PART 1","authors":"Cichocki et al.","year":2016,"venue":"Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning 9(4-5):249-429","arxiv":"1609.00893","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.00893","claim":"Monograph on tensor networks (TT, hierarchical Tucker, etc.) with graphical (string-diagram) notation for contractions and their use in large-scale optimization.","relevance":"Supplies the diagrammatic calculus in which every 'shape' adapter is a string diagram of trainable and frozen boxes, the natural bridge to a categorical (monoidal) treatment.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.00893","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=1609.00893"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["A. Cichocki","N. Lee","I. V. Oseledets","A. -H. Phan","Q. Zhao","D. Mandic"],"bibkey":"cichocki2016low","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cichocki2016low,\n  title = {{Low-Rank Tensor Networks for Dimensionality Reduction and Large-Scale Optimization Problems: Perspectives and Challenges PART 1}},\n  author = {A. Cichocki and N. Lee and I. V. Oseledets and A. -H. Phan and Q. Zhao and D. Mandic},\n  year = {2016},\n  booktitle = {Foundations and Trends in Machine Learning 9(4-5):249-429},\n  eprint = {1609.00893},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.00893}\n}"},{"id":"monarch","title":"Monarch: Expressive Structured Matrices for Efficient and Accurate Training","authors":"Dao et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICML 2022","arxiv":"2204.00595","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.00595","claim":"Monarch matrices, products of two block-diagonal matrices interleaved with permutations, are hardware-efficient, express many standard transforms, and admit an analytically optimal projection of a dense matrix.","relevance":"Structured-matrix foundation of MoRe; also explains MELoRA/GraLoRA/BD-LoRA as block-diagonal (direct-sum) shapes and BOFT's butterflies as the orthogonal counterpart.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.00595","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2204.00595","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Tri Dao","Beidi Chen","Nimit Sohoni","Arjun Desai","Michael Poli","Jessica Grogan","Alexander Liu","Aniruddh Rao","Atri Rudra","Christopher Ré"],"bibkey":"dao2022monarch","bibtex":"@inproceedings{dao2022monarch,\n  title = {{Monarch: Expressive Structured Matrices for Efficient and Accurate Training}},\n  author = {Tri Dao and Beidi Chen and Nimit Sohoni and Arjun Desai and Michael Poli and Jessica Grogan and Alexander Liu and Aniruddh Rao and Atri Rudra and Christopher Ré},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2204.00595},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.00595}\n}"},{"id":"compute-better-spent","title":"Compute Better Spent: Replacing Dense Layers with Structured Matrices","authors":"Qiu et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2406.06248","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06248","claim":"Different structured matrix families need different initialization scales and learning rates (derived via muP); the Block Tensor-Train (BTT) family, which contains Monarch, beats dense layers per unit of compute.","relevance":"Explains why shape-based adapters need method-specific scalings (e.g. ABBA's rank-stabilized scale, C3A's large learning rate) and gives a compute-optimal lens for comparing shapes.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06248","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2406.06248","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Shikai Qiu","Andres Potapczynski","Marc Finzi","Micah Goldblum","Andrew Gordon Wilson"],"bibkey":"qiu2024compute","bibtex":"@inproceedings{qiu2024compute,\n  title = {{Compute Better Spent: Replacing Dense Layers with Structured Matrices}},\n  author = {Shikai Qiu and Andres Potapczynski and Marc Finzi and Micah Goldblum and Andrew Gordon Wilson},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2406.06248},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06248}\n}"},{"id":"tensor-networks-meet-neural-networks","title":"Tensor Networks Meet Neural Networks: A Survey and Future Perspectives","authors":"Wang et al.","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2302.09019","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.09019","claim":"Surveys 'tensorial neural networks' that combine tensor networks with CNNs, RNNs, Transformers, LLMs and quantum neural networks, covering compression, fusion and efficient training.","relevance":"Places TT/Tucker/CP adapters within the wider practice of tensor-network parameterization of neural layers.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.09019","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2302.09019"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["shape"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Maolin Wang","Yu Pan","Zenglin Xu","Guangxi Li","Xiangli Yang","Danilo Mandic","Andrzej Cichocki"],"bibkey":"wang2023tensor","bibtex":"@article{wang2023tensor,\n  title = {{Tensor Networks Meet Neural Networks: A Survey and Future Perspectives}},\n  author = {Maolin Wang and Yu Pan and Zenglin Xu and Guangxi Li and Xiangli Yang and Danilo Mandic and Andrzej Cichocki},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2302.09019},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.09019},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.09019}\n}"},{"id":"pranc","title":"PRANC: Pseudo RAndom Networks for Compacting deep models","authors":"Nooralinejad et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICCV 2023","arxiv":"2206.08464","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08464","claim":"A deep model can be reparameterised as a linear combination of several frozen, randomly initialised basis networks, so it can be stored as one seed plus mixture coefficients, compressing classifiers about 100x.","relevance":"Direct ancestor of NOLA (and of seed-regenerated bases in VeRA/RandLoRA): storage reduces to coefficients plus an RNG seed, a free-module view of the update space.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08464","https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.02556","https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51070.2023.01561"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Parsa Nooralinejad","Ali Abbasi","Soroush Abbasi Koohpayegani","Kossar Pourahmadi Meibodi","Rana Muhammad Shahroz Khan","Soheil Kolouri","Hamed Pirsiavash"],"bibkey":"nooralinejad2022pranc","bibtex":"@inproceedings{nooralinejad2022pranc,\n  title = {{PRANC: Pseudo RAndom Networks for Compacting deep models}},\n  author = {Parsa Nooralinejad and Ali Abbasi and Soroush Abbasi Koohpayegani and Kossar Pourahmadi Meibodi and Rana Muhammad Shahroz Khan and Soheil Kolouri and Hamed Pirsiavash},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV)},\n  eprint = {2206.08464},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08464}\n}"},{"id":"hashednets","title":"Compressing Neural Networks with the Hashing Trick","authors":"Chen et al.","year":2015,"venue":"ICML 2015","arxiv":"1504.04788","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04788","claim":"Randomly grouping connection weights with a hash function so that each group shares one parameter (HashedNets) gives large compression with little accuracy loss.","relevance":"Random weight sharing is the mechanism behind Uni-LoRA's one-hot projection and MoSA's random tessellation; it frames sharing as a quotient of parameter space by a random partition.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04788","https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2026/file/dd37fdb24a4e1cfa3ed5c247217a7394-Paper-Conference.pdf"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["sharing"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Wenlin Chen","James T. Wilson","Stephen Tyree","Kilian Q. Weinberger","Yixin Chen"],"bibkey":"chen2015compressing","bibtex":"@inproceedings{chen2015compressing,\n  title = {{Compressing Neural Networks with the Hashing Trick}},\n  author = {Wenlin Chen and James T. Wilson and Stephen Tyree and Kilian Q. Weinberger and Yixin Chen},\n  year = {2015},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1504.04788},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.04788}\n}"},{"id":"subspace-tuning-decomposition-view","title":"See Further for Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning by Standing on the Shoulders of Decomposition","authors":"Si et al.","year":2024,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2407.05417","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05417","claim":"Unifies PEFT as 'subspace tuning' relative to the SVD \\(W = U\\Sigma V^\\top\\). Reconstruction-based methods transform existing subspaces (e.g. scaling singular vectors, BitFit, soft prompts). Extension-based methods add new subspaces (LoRA and its derivatives, adapters). Combination-based methods do both (e.g. DoRA, Spectral Adapter, SVDiff). The analysis motivates two new methods (SSL, SSB) and a Matrix Pattern Constraints framework.","relevance":"A recent explicit unification attempt whose reconstruction/extension split lines up with acting on \\(W_0\\) by a transformation (multiplicative, group-action-like) versus adding a new low-rank summand (additive). A categorical treatment can make this dichotomy precise.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05417","https://arxiv.org/html/2407.05417","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2407.05417"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Chongjie Si","Xiaokang Yang","Wei Shen"],"bibkey":"si2024see","bibtex":"@article{si2024see,\n  title = {{See Further for Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning by Standing on the Shoulders of Decomposition}},\n  author = {Chongjie Si and Xiaokang Yang and Wei Shen},\n  year = {2024},\n  eprint = {2407.05417},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.05417},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.05417}\n}"},{"id":"lora-plus-width-scaling","title":"LoRA+: Efficient Low Rank Adaptation of Large Models","authors":"Hayou et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.12354","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354","claim":"In the infinite-width limit with Adam, using the same learning rate for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) prevents efficient feature learning. Efficiency is achieved with \\(\\eta_A = \\Theta(n^{-1})\\) and \\(\\eta_B = \\Theta(1)\\), so the ratio \\(\\lambda = \\eta_B/\\eta_A\\) must be \\(\\Theta(n)\\) (Theorem 1). In practice a fixed ratio is used: about \\(2^4\\) for RoBERTa under the paper's Init[2] (\\(A = 0\\), \\(B\\) random), \\(2^2\\)-\\(2^3\\) under the standard Init[1] (\\(B = 0\\), \\(A\\) random), and \\(2^1\\)-\\(2^2\\) for Llama. This gives 1-2% gains and up to about 2x faster fine-tuning.","relevance":"This shows that the two factors of the \\(GL(r)\\)-redundant parametrisation are not interchangeable under standard optimizers. The scaling part of the gauge (\\(B \\mapsto cB\\), \\(A \\mapsto A/c\\)) interacts with learning rates, which motivates gauge-aware optimizers.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.12354","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.12354"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":["lora-plus"],"authors_full":["Soufiane Hayou","Nikhil Ghosh","Bin Yu"],"bibkey":"hayou2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{hayou2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA+: Efficient Low Rank Adaptation of Large Models}},\n  author = {Soufiane Hayou and Nikhil Ghosh and Bin Yu},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.12354},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.12354}\n}"},{"id":"rslora-rank-stabilization","title":"A Rank Stabilization Scaling Factor for Fine-Tuning with LoRA","authors":"Kalajdzievski","year":2023,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2312.03732","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732","claim":"The conventional \\(\\alpha/r\\) scaling slows learning and stunts performance at higher ranks. The paper proves that LoRA adapters should instead be scaled by \\(\\alpha/\\sqrt{r}\\) for rank-stable learning (rsLoRA), which makes higher ranks a useful compute/performance trade-off at no extra inference cost.","relevance":"A scaling-limit result for the factorised parametrisation, available in HF PEFT as LoraConfig(use_rslora=True). It shows that the normalisation constant is part of the reparametrisation and not a cosmetic hyperparameter.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2312.03732","https://github.com/huggingface/peft"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":["rslora"],"authors_full":["Damjan Kalajdzievski"],"bibkey":"kalajdzievski2023rank","bibtex":"@article{kalajdzievski2023rank,\n  title = {{A Rank Stabilization Scaling Factor for Fine-Tuning with LoRA}},\n  author = {Damjan Kalajdzievski},\n  year = {2023},\n  eprint = {2312.03732},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.03732},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03732}\n}"},{"id":"scaling-llm-finetuning","title":"When Scaling Meets LLM Finetuning: The Effect of Data, Model and Finetuning Method","authors":"Zhang et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2024","arxiv":"2402.17193","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17193","claim":"On bilingual LLMs from 1B to 16B parameters, fine-tuning follows a power-based multiplicative joint scaling law between fine-tuning data size and each other scaling factor. Fine-tuning benefits more from model scaling than from pretraining-data scaling, scaling PET parameters (prompt length, LoRA rank) is generally ineffective, and the best method depends on task and data.","relevance":"Scaling-law evidence that the size of the PEFT parameter object matters much less than the frozen base. This fits the picture of PEFT as a small reparametrisation of a large fixed morphism.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17193","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.17193"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Biao Zhang","Zhongtao Liu","Colin Cherry","Orhan Firat"],"bibkey":"zhang2024scaling","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024scaling,\n  title = {{When Scaling Meets LLM Finetuning: The Effect of Data, Model and Finetuning Method}},\n  author = {Biao Zhang and Zhongtao Liu and Colin Cherry and Orhan Firat},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2402.17193},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17193}\n}"},{"id":"peft-critical-review","title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning Methods for Pretrained Language Models: A Critical Review and Assessment","authors":"Xu et al.","year":2023,"venue":"IEEE TPAMI 2026","arxiv":"2312.12148","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12148","claim":"Systematic review that sorts PEFT methods for PLMs into additive, partial, reparameterized, hybrid and unified fine-tuning, with experiments on representative methods measuring parameter and memory efficiency.","relevance":"A five-class taxonomy whose 'unified' class (AdaMix, SparseAdapter, ProPETL) is kept apart from 'hybrid' combinations (MAM adapter, UniPELT, AutoPEFT, S3Delta-M). Comparing it with the Delta-Tuning and Han et al. taxonomies shows how unstable informal classifications are, which motivates a principled one.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12148","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2312.12148","https://doi.org/10.1109/tpami.2026.3657354","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2312.12148"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Lingling Xu","Haoran Xie","Si-Zhao Joe Qin","Xiaohui Tao","Fu Lee Wang"],"bibkey":"xu2023parameter","bibtex":"@article{xu2023parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning Methods for Pretrained Language Models: A Critical Review and Assessment}},\n  author = {Lingling Xu and Haoran Xie and Si-Zhao Joe Qin and Xiaohui Tao and Fu Lee Wang},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {IEEE TPAMI},\n  eprint = {2312.12148},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.12148}\n}"},{"id":"lora-survey-mao","title":"A Survey on LoRA of Large Language Models","authors":"Mao et al.","year":2024,"venue":"Frontiers of Computer Science 2025","arxiv":"2407.11046","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11046","claim":"Reviews LoRA progress along five axes: downstream-adaptation variants, cross-task generalization by mixing LoRA plugins, efficiency improvements, privacy-preserving and federated use, and applications. Maintains an Awesome-LoRAs list.","relevance":"The standard map of the LoRA sub-family, including mixture and composition methods that a categorical treatment models as coproducts or monoidal combinations of reparametrisations.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11046","https://github.com/ZJU-LLMs/Awesome-LoRAs","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2407.11046"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yuren Mao","Yuhang Ge","Yijiang Fan","Wenyi Xu","Yu Mi","Zhonghao Hu","Yunjun Gao"],"bibkey":"mao2024survey","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mao2024survey,\n  title = {{A Survey on LoRA of Large Language Models}},\n  author = {Yuren Mao and Yuhang Ge and Yijiang Fan and Wenyi Xu and Yu Mi and Zhonghao Hu and Yunjun Gao},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Frontiers of Computer Science},\n  eprint = {2407.11046},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.11046}\n}"},{"id":"visual-peft-survey","title":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Pre-Trained Vision Models: A Survey and Benchmark","authors":"Xin et al.","year":2024,"venue":"International Journal of Computer Vision 2026","arxiv":"2402.02242","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02242","claim":"Surveys visual PEFT and sorts it into addition-based, partial-based, unified-based and multi-task tuning. It introduces V-PEFT Bench for standardized evaluation across vision tasks.","relevance":"The main vision-side taxonomy. It shows that the same structural classes recur across modalities, which supports a modality-independent categorical classification.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02242","https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11263-026-02864-6","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.02242"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Yi Xin","Jianjiang Yang","Siqi Luo","Yuntao Du","Qi Qin","Kangrui Cen","Yangfan He","Zhiwei Zhang","Bin Fu","Xiaokang Yang","Guangtao Zhai","Ming-Hsuan Yang","Xiaohong Liu"],"bibkey":"xin2024parameter","bibtex":"@article{xin2024parameter,\n  title = {{Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Pre-Trained Vision Models: A Survey and Benchmark}},\n  author = {Yi Xin and Jianjiang Yang and Siqi Luo and Yuntao Du and Qi Qin and Kangrui Cen and Yangfan He and Zhiwei Zhang and Bin Fu and Xiaokang Yang and Guangtao Zhai and Ming-Hsuan Yang and Xiaohong Liu},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {International Journal of Computer Vision},\n  eprint = {2402.02242},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02242}\n}"},{"id":"lora-redux","title":"Low-Rank Adaptation Redux for Large Models","authors":"Li et al.","year":2026,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2604.21905","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21905","claim":"A signal-processing overview of LoRA along three axes. Architectural design covers SVD-based factorisation, rank augmentation and cross-layer tensorisation. Efficient optimization covers initialization, alternating solvers, gauge-invariant optimization and parameterization-aware methods. Applications span pre-training, post-training and serving.","relevance":"A recent survey that names 'gauge-invariant optimization' as a category of its own. This indicates that the \\(GL(r)\\) symmetry viewpoint has become an organising principle for LoRA research.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21905","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2604.21905"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Bingcong Li","Yilang Zhang","Georgios B. Giannakis"],"bibkey":"li2026low","bibtex":"@article{li2026low,\n  title = {{Low-Rank Adaptation Redux for Large Models}},\n  author = {Bingcong Li and Yilang Zhang and Georgios B. Giannakis},\n  year = {2026},\n  eprint = {2604.21905},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.21905},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.21905}\n}"},{"id":"implicit-acceleration-overparameterization","title":"On the Optimization of Deep Networks: Implicit Acceleration by Overparameterization","authors":"Arora et al.","year":2018,"venue":"ICML 2018","arxiv":"1802.06509","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.06509","claim":"For deep linear networks with balanced initialization, \\(W_{j+1}^\\top W_{j+1} = W_j W_j^\\top\\), gradient flow preserves balancedness. The end-to-end matrix then follows a preconditioned update whose preconditioner depends on the singular values of the product (powers of \\(W W^\\top\\) and \\(W^\\top W\\)), so depth acts as an accelerating preconditioner. This effect cannot be obtained from the gradient of any regularizer.","relevance":"For depth 2 (\\(\\Delta W = BA\\)), balancedness \\(B^\\top B = A A^\\top\\) is a \\(GL(r)\\)-gauge condition, and the induced end-to-end dynamics explain why the factorised LoRA parametrisation changes optimization geometry relative to training \\(\\Delta W\\) directly. This is the root of balanced and Riemannian LoRA variants.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.06509","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.06509","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1802.06509"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sanjeev Arora","Nadav Cohen","Elad Hazan"],"bibkey":"arora2018optimization","bibtex":"@inproceedings{arora2018optimization,\n  title = {{On the Optimization of Deep Networks: Implicit Acceleration by Overparameterization}},\n  author = {Sanjeev Arora and Nadav Cohen and Elad Hazan},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {1802.06509},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.06509}\n}"},{"id":"layers-automatically-balanced","title":"Algorithmic Regularization in Learning Deep Homogeneous Models: Layers are Automatically Balanced","authors":"Du et al.","year":2018,"venue":"NeurIPS 2018","arxiv":"1806.00900","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.00900","claim":"For homogeneous networks (linear, ReLU, Leaky ReLU), gradient flow keeps the differences between squared layer norms invariant, and for linear layers the matrix \\(W_{j+1}^\\top W_{j+1} - W_j W_j^\\top\\) is conserved. Small initialization therefore yields automatically balanced layers. For asymmetric low-rank matrix factorization, gradient descent with decaying step sizes balances the factors and converges to a global optimum.","relevance":"Applied to LoRA, gradient flow conserves \\(A A^\\top - B^\\top B\\), which is the Noether charge of the \\(GL(r)\\) symmetry. With the default init (\\(B = 0\\)), this charge equals \\(A_0 A_0^\\top\\), so standard LoRA starts and stays off the balanced set.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.00900","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1806.00900"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Simon S. Du","Wei Hu","Jason D. Lee"],"bibkey":"du2018algorithmic","bibtex":"@inproceedings{du2018algorithmic,\n  title = {{Algorithmic Regularization in Learning Deep Homogeneous Models: Layers are Automatically Balanced}},\n  author = {Simon S. Du and Wei Hu and Jason D. Lee},\n  year = {2018},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1806.00900},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.00900}\n}"},{"id":"implicit-regularization-deep-matrix-factorization","title":"Implicit Regularization in Deep Matrix Factorization","authors":"Arora et al.","year":2019,"venue":"NeurIPS 2019","arxiv":"1905.13655","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.13655","claim":"Gradient descent on deep (multi-factor) matrix factorizations has an implicit bias toward low-rank solutions that grows with depth. The paper argues that this bias is not captured by simple norms such as the nuclear norm.","relevance":"Explains why factorised parametrisations of \\(\\Delta W\\) (\\(BA\\), or deeper products and tensorised variants) carry an implicit low-rank bias beyond their explicit rank constraint. A theory of PEFT should treat the parametrisation, and not only its image, as meaningful.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.13655","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1905.13655"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sanjeev Arora","Nadav Cohen","Wei Hu","Yuping Luo"],"bibkey":"arora2019implicit","bibtex":"@inproceedings{arora2019implicit,\n  title = {{Implicit Regularization in Deep Matrix Factorization}},\n  author = {Sanjeev Arora and Nadav Cohen and Wei Hu and Yuping Luo},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {1905.13655},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.13655}\n}"},{"id":"neural-mechanics-symmetry","title":"Neural Mechanics: Symmetry and Broken Conservation Laws in Deep Learning Dynamics","authors":"Kunin et al.","year":2020,"venue":"ICLR 2021","arxiv":"2012.04728","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.04728","claim":"Continuous symmetries of the architecture (translation from softmax, scale from normalization, rescale from homogeneous activations) constrain gradients and Hessians and give Noether-like conservation laws under gradient flow. Finite learning rates break these laws, and modified gradient flow yields exact integral expressions for the dynamics of symmetric parameter combinations, validated on VGG-16.","relevance":"Supplies the Noether framework (symmetry -> conserved quantity, broken at finite step size) needed to treat LoRA's \\(GL(r)\\) gauge and the scaling symmetries of DoRA- and (IA)\\(^3\\)-style methods uniformly.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.04728","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04728","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2012.04728"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Daniel Kunin","Javier Sagastuy-Brena","Surya Ganguli","Daniel L. K. Yamins","Hidenori Tanaka"],"bibkey":"kunin2020neural","bibtex":"@inproceedings{kunin2020neural,\n  title = {{Neural Mechanics: Symmetry and Broken Conservation Laws in Deep Learning Dynamics}},\n  author = {Daniel Kunin and Javier Sagastuy-Brena and Surya Ganguli and Daniel L. K. Yamins and Hidenori Tanaka},\n  year = {2020},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2012.04728},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.04728}\n}"},{"id":"symmetries-conserved-quantities-gradient-flow","title":"Symmetries, Flat Minima, and the Conserved Quantities of Gradient Flow","authors":"Zhao et al.","year":2022,"venue":"ICLR 2023","arxiv":"2210.17216","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17216","claim":"A general framework for continuous parameter-space symmetries, including nonlinear data-dependent ones, that carve out flat low-loss valleys. For a two-layer linear network \\(UV\\) the loss is \\(GL_h(\\mathbb R)\\)-invariant and every entry of \\(V V^\\top - U^\\top U\\) is conserved. Conserved quantities parametrise flat minima and are tied to convergence rate and sharpness, so the initialization fixes which part of the minimum set gradient flow explores.","relevance":"The cleanest statement that LoRA's factor pair \\((U, V) = (B, A)\\) carries a \\(GL(r)\\) symmetry with conserved charge \\(A A^\\top - B^\\top B\\), and that this charge (fixed at init) affects convergence and sharpness. The gauge-theoretic layer of a categorical PEFT theory rests on this.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17216","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2210.17216","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2210.17216"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Bo Zhao","Iordan Ganev","Robin Walters","Rose Yu","Nima Dehmamy"],"bibkey":"zhao2022symmetries","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhao2022symmetries,\n  title = {{Symmetries, Flat Minima, and the Conserved Quantities of Gradient Flow}},\n  author = {Bo Zhao and Iordan Ganev and Robin Walters and Rose Yu and Nima Dehmamy},\n  year = {2022},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2210.17216},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17216}\n}"},{"id":"conservation-laws-gradient-flows","title":"Abide by the Law and Follow the Flow: Conservation Laws for Gradient Flows","authors":"Marcotte et al.","year":2023,"venue":"NeurIPS 2023","arxiv":"2307.00144","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00144","claim":"Defines conservation laws as quantities preserved by gradient flow for any data and loss, and shows that the maximal number of independent laws can be computed by finite-dimensional algebra on the Lie algebra generated by the model's Jacobian vector fields. Algorithms recover all known laws for several ReLU architectures and show there are no others.","relevance":"A complete, algorithmic answer to 'what is conserved' for a given parametrisation. In principle it lets one compute the full invariant set of any PEFT parametrisation (LoRA, DoRA, Kronecker or tensor factorisations), not only the obvious \\(GL(r)\\) charge.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00144","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2307.00144"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Sibylle Marcotte","Rémi Gribonval","Gabriel Peyré"],"bibkey":"marcotte2023abide","bibtex":"@inproceedings{marcotte2023abide,\n  title = {{Abide by the Law and Follow the Flow: Conservation Laws for Gradient Flows}},\n  author = {Sibylle Marcotte and Rémi Gribonval and Gabriel Peyré},\n  year = {2023},\n  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n  eprint = {2307.00144},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00144}\n}"},{"id":"fixed-rank-riemannian-quotient","title":"Fixed-rank matrix factorizations and Riemannian low-rank optimization","authors":"Mishra et al.","year":2012,"venue":"Computational Statistics 2014","arxiv":"1209.0430","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.0430","claim":"Optimization over fixed-rank matrices is cast on Riemannian quotient manifolds. The full-rank factorization \\(W = GH^\\top\\) has the symmetry \\((G, H) \\mapsto (GM^{-1}, HM^\\top)\\), \\(M \\in GL(r)\\), giving the quotient \\(\\mathbb R_*^{d_1\\times r} \\times \\mathbb R_*^{d_2\\times r}/GL(r)\\). Equivalent polar (\\(\\mathrm{St} \\times S_{++} \\times \\mathrm{St} / O(r)\\)) and subspace-projection quotients are given, and gradient and trust-region algorithms are derived for each geometry.","relevance":"Classical geometry behind LoRA's gauge: the space of factor pairs \\((B, A)\\) is a principal \\(GL(r)\\)-bundle over the manifold of rank-\\(r\\) updates. Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA, LoRA-RITE and BaLoRA all choose a metric or gauge on this bundle.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.0430","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1209.0430","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1209.0430"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["B. Mishra","G. Meyer","S. Bonnabel","R. Sepulchre"],"bibkey":"mishra2012fixed","bibtex":"@inproceedings{mishra2012fixed,\n  title = {{Fixed-rank matrix factorizations and Riemannian low-rank optimization}},\n  author = {B. Mishra and G. Meyer and S. Bonnabel and R. Sepulchre},\n  year = {2012},\n  booktitle = {Computational Statistics},\n  eprint = {1209.0430},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.0430}\n}"},{"id":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","title":"Riemannian Preconditioned LoRA for Fine-Tuning Foundation Models","authors":"Zhang & Pilanci","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.02347","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347","claim":"Adds an \\(r \\times r\\) preconditioner to each LoRA step (\\(W = W_0 + BA\\)): \\(A \\leftarrow A - \\eta (B^\\top B)^{-1}\\nabla_A L\\) and \\(B \\leftarrow B - \\eta \\nabla_B L\\,(A A^\\top)^{-1}\\), i.e. left-multiplication for \\(A\\) and right-multiplication for \\(B\\). It is derived from a Riemannian metric on the quotient manifold of fixed-rank matrices. In the infinite-width limit, with Adam, this gives stable feature learning with a single \\(\\Theta(1)\\) learning rate for both factors (Theorem 4.1), and it makes SGD and AdamW more robust on LLMs and diffusion models.","relevance":"Shows directly that choosing a \\(GL(r)\\)-compatible (quotient) metric removes the \\(A\\)/\\(B\\) learning-rate asymmetry of LoRA+. Optimization geometry on the gauge bundle is a first-class part of a PEFT method.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347","https://arxiv.org/html/2402.02347","https://github.com/pilancilab/Riemannian_Preconditioned_LoRA","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.02347"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":["riemannian-preconditioned-lora"],"authors_full":["Fangzhao Zhang","Mert Pilanci"],"bibkey":"zhang2024riemannian","bibtex":"@inproceedings{zhang2024riemannian,\n  title = {{Riemannian Preconditioned LoRA for Fine-Tuning Foundation Models}},\n  author = {Fangzhao Zhang and Mert Pilanci},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.02347},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02347}\n}"},{"id":"lora-rite-transformation-invariance","title":"LoRA Done RITE: Robust Invariant Transformation Equilibration for LoRA Optimization","authors":"Yen et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025","arxiv":"2410.20625","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625","claim":"Standard LoRA optimizers (Adam, Shampoo, LoRA+, ScaledAdam) lack transformation invariance: equivalent factorizations \\((A R, B R^{-\\top})\\) (paper's notation \\(Z = AB^\\top\\); \\((BR, R^{-1}A)\\) in this site's notation) receive different weight updates. LoRA-RITE computes 'unmagnified' gradients in polar-decomposition bases of the factors and preconditions only in the \\(r\\)-dimensional space, which makes updates invariant. Replacing Adam with it on Gemma-2B gives a 4.6% accuracy gain on Super-Natural Instructions.","relevance":"Turns \\(GL(r)\\) gauge invariance into a design requirement for optimizers and shows that respecting it improves results.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.20625","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2410.20625"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":["lora-rite"],"authors_full":["Jui-Nan Yen","Si Si","Zhao Meng","Felix Yu","Sai Surya Duvvuri","Inderjit S. Dhillon","Cho-Jui Hsieh","Sanjiv Kumar"],"bibkey":"yen2024lora","bibtex":"@inproceedings{yen2024lora,\n  title = {{LoRA Done RITE: Robust Invariant Transformation Equilibration for LoRA Optimization}},\n  author = {Jui-Nan Yen and Si Si and Zhao Meng and Felix Yu and Sai Surya Duvvuri and Inderjit S. Dhillon and Cho-Jui Hsieh and Sanjiv Kumar},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.20625},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20625}\n}"},{"id":"learning-on-loras-gl-equivariance","title":"Learning on LoRAs: GL-Equivariant Processing of Low-Rank Weight Spaces for Large Finetuned Models","authors":"Putterman et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICLR 2025 Workshop (Neural Network Weights as a New Data Modality)","arxiv":"2410.04207","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04207","claim":"LoRA factor pairs \\((U, V)\\) and \\((UR, VR^{-\\top})\\) for \\(R\\) in \\(GL(r)\\) give the same update \\(\\Delta W = UV^\\top\\) (paper's notation), an inner symmetry that differs from the outer permutation symmetries of ordinary networks. The paper builds GL-invariant or equivariant models on LoRA weights (canonicalization via O-Align, singular values as invariant features, multiplied-out dense features, and the equivariant GL-net) that predict CLIP score, data attributes, membership and downstream accuracy over thousands of fine-tuned models.","relevance":"Treats the \\(GL(r)\\) symmetry of LoRA as a structural fact about the space of PEFT modules and builds equivariant learners on it. The space of adapters becomes an object with a group action, which is the natural home for a functorial classification.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04207","https://arxiv.org/html/2410.04207","https://iclr.cc/virtual/2025/33086","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2410.04207"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Theo Putterman","Derek Lim","Yoav Gelberg","Stefanie Jegelka","Haggai Maron"],"bibkey":"putterman2024learning","bibtex":"@inproceedings{putterman2024learning,\n  title = {{Learning on LoRAs: GL-Equivariant Processing of Low-Rank Weight Spaces for Large Finetuned Models}},\n  author = {Theo Putterman and Derek Lim and Yoav Gelberg and Stefanie Jegelka and Haggai Maron},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n  eprint = {2410.04207},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.04207}\n}"},{"id":"balanced-lora","title":"Balanced LoRA: Removing Parameter Invariance to Accelerate Convergence","authors":"Castin et al.","year":2026,"venue":"ICML 2026","arxiv":"2605.31484","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.31484","claim":"Factor pairs giving the same adapted matrix can have very different condition numbers, so the minimizer LoRA converges to affects its convergence rate. After each optimizer step, BaLoRA applies a product-preserving balancing map onto the 'hyperbalanced' manifold, on which (in this site's notation) \\(B^\\top B = A A^\\top = S\\) with \\(S\\) diagonal, non-negative and non-increasing; it is computed from the SVD of the product and so leaves the adapted matrix unchanged while improving conditioning. It converges faster and performs better than standard LoRA.","relevance":"Turns gauge fixing into an algorithm: it fixes the (hyper)balanced section of the \\(GL(r)\\)-bundle, i.e. the zero level set of the conserved quantity of Du et al. and Arora et al., with only a residual sign symmetry left when the singular values are distinct. It closes the loop between deep-linear-network theory and practical PEFT.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.31484","https://arxiv.org/html/2605.31484v1","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2605.31484"],"confidence":"medium","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Valérie Castin","Kimia Nadjahi","Pierre Ablin","Gabriel Peyré"],"bibkey":"castin2026balanced","bibtex":"@inproceedings{castin2026balanced,\n  title = {{Balanced LoRA: Removing Parameter Invariance to Accelerate Convergence}},\n  author = {Valérie Castin and Kimia Nadjahi and Pierre Ablin and Gabriel Peyré},\n  year = {2026},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2605.31484},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.31484}\n}"},{"id":"backprop-as-functor","title":"Backprop as Functor: A compositional perspective on supervised learning","authors":"Fong et al.","year":2017,"venue":"LICS 2019","arxiv":"1711.10455","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.10455","claim":"Defines a symmetric monoidal category Learn whose morphisms are learners \\((P, I, U, r)\\): a parameter space, implementation \\(I: P \\times A \\to B\\), update \\(U: P \\times A \\times B \\to P\\) and request function \\(r: P \\times A \\times B \\to A\\). For a fixed step size \\(\\epsilon > 0\\) and an error function \\(e\\) with \\(\\partial e/\\partial x(x_0,-)\\) invertible, gradient descent defines a faithful, injective-on-objects, strong symmetric monoidal functor \\(L_{\\epsilon,e}: \\mathbf{Para} \\to \\mathbf{Learn}\\), so backpropagation is functorial.","relevance":"The original categorical semantics of learning. A PEFT method changes the parameter object \\(P\\) of each learner (restricting, extending or reparametrising it) while keeping \\(I\\)'s frozen part fixed. Whether and how such changes commute with \\(L_{\\epsilon,e}\\) is a natural first theorem for a categorical PEFT theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.10455","https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.10455","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1711.10455"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Brendan Fong","David I. Spivak","Rémy Tuyéras"],"bibkey":"fong2017backprop","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fong2017backprop,\n  title = {{Backprop as Functor: A compositional perspective on supervised learning}},\n  author = {Brendan Fong and David I. Spivak and Rémy Tuyéras},\n  year = {2017},\n  booktitle = {Symposium on Logic in Computer Science (LICS)},\n  eprint = {1711.10455},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.10455}\n}"},{"id":"lenses-and-learners","title":"Lenses and Learners","authors":"Fong & Johnson","year":2019,"venue":"Bx 2019 (International Workshop on Bidirectional Transformations)","arxiv":"1903.03671","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.03671","claim":"There is a faithful, identity-on-objects symmetric monoidal functor embedding a category of asymmetric lenses into the category of learners, and another embedding learners into a category of symmetric lenses. The learners of Backprop as Functor are therefore tightly linked to lenses.","relevance":"Bridges Learn to the lens/optic formalism, which later work uses as the standard home for gradient-based learning and therefore for PEFT.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.03671","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1903.03671"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Brendan Fong","Michael Johnson"],"bibkey":"fong2019lenses","bibtex":"@inproceedings{fong2019lenses,\n  title = {{Lenses and Learners}},\n  author = {Brendan Fong and Michael Johnson},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {Bx 2019 (International Workshop on Bidirectional Transformations)},\n  eprint = {1903.03671},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.03671}\n}"},{"id":"reverse-derivative-categories","title":"Reverse Derivative Categories","authors":"Cockett et al.","year":2019,"venue":"CSL 2020","arxiv":"1910.07065","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07065","claim":"Gives a direct axiomatization of categories with a reverse derivative, analogous to Cartesian differential categories for forward derivatives. A reverse derivative implies a forward derivative but not conversely: it is equivalent to a forward derivative together with a dagger structure on the subcategory of linear maps, which forms an additively enriched category with dagger biproducts.","relevance":"The axiomatic basis for backpropagating through any reparametrisation. The chain rule for a PEFT map \\(\\alpha: Q \\to P\\) (e.g. \\((A, B) \\mapsto W_0 + BA\\)) is functoriality of the reverse derivative, and the dagger is the transpose that appears in LoRA's gradients.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07065","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:1910.07065"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Robin Cockett","Geoffrey Cruttwell","Jonathan Gallagher","Jean-Simon Pacaud Lemay","Benjamin MacAdam","Gordon Plotkin","Dorette Pronk"],"bibkey":"cockett2019reverse","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cockett2019reverse,\n  title = {{Reverse Derivative Categories}},\n  author = {Robin Cockett and Geoffrey Cruttwell and Jonathan Gallagher and Jean-Simon Pacaud Lemay and Benjamin MacAdam and Gordon Plotkin and Dorette Pronk},\n  year = {2019},\n  booktitle = {CSL},\n  eprint = {1910.07065},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.07065}\n}"},{"id":"categorical-foundations-gradient-learning","title":"Categorical Foundations of Gradient-Based Learning","authors":"Cruttwell et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ESOP 2022","arxiv":"2103.01931","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01931","claim":"Gives a categorical semantics of gradient-based learning in which models are morphisms of \\(\\mathbf{Para}(\\mathbf{Lens}(C))\\) for a Cartesian reverse differential category \\(C\\). Para accounts for parameters, Lens for bidirectional information flow, and the CRDC for differentiation. Optimisers (SGD, Adam, AdaGrad, Nesterov), losses (MSE, softmax cross-entropy) and learning rates are uniformly lenses or reparametrisations, Boolean-circuit learning is covered, and there is a Python implementation.","relevance":"The formal home for PEFT. It defines a reparametrisation of \\((P, f): A \\to B\\) by \\(\\alpha: Q \\to P\\) as \\((Q, (\\alpha \\otimes 1_A); f)\\), and every weight-space PEFT method (LoRA's \\((A, B) \\mapsto W_0 + BA\\), BitFit's coordinate inclusion, OFT's \\(R \\mapsto W_0 R\\)) is exactly such a 2-cell. Optimisers-as-lenses also cover training-procedure methods such as LoRA+ and GaLore.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01931","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.01931","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2103.01931"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["G. S. H. Cruttwell","Bruno Gavranović","Neil Ghani","Paul Wilson","Fabio Zanasi"],"bibkey":"cruttwell2021categorical","bibtex":"@inproceedings{cruttwell2021categorical,\n  title = {{Categorical Foundations of Gradient-Based Learning}},\n  author = {G. S. H. Cruttwell and Bruno Gavranović and Neil Ghani and Paul Wilson and Fabio Zanasi},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {European Symposium on Programming (ESOP)},\n  eprint = {2103.01931},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.01931}\n}"},{"id":"categorical-cybernetics","title":"Towards Foundations of Categorical Cybernetics","authors":"Capucci et al.","year":2021,"venue":"ACT 2021 (EPTCS 372, 2022)","arxiv":"2105.06332","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06332","claim":"Combines the Para construction over actegories with optics to obtain parametrised optics, a framework for processes that interact bidirectionally with an environment and a controller. Examples include open learners (controller = optimiser such as gradient descent) and compositional game theory via selection functions.","relevance":"Provides the actegory-based Para construction and the 'controller' perspective. A PEFT method restricts which part of a parametrised optic the controller (optimiser) can act on, which echoes the optimal-control reading of delta tuning.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06332","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.06332","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2105.06332"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Matteo Capucci","Bruno Gavranović","Jules Hedges","Eigil Fjeldgren Rischel"],"bibkey":"capucci2021foundations","bibtex":"@inproceedings{capucci2021foundations,\n  title = {{Towards Foundations of Categorical Cybernetics}},\n  author = {Matteo Capucci and Bruno Gavranović and Jules Hedges and Eigil Fjeldgren Rischel},\n  year = {2021},\n  booktitle = {Applied Category Theory (ACT)},\n  eprint = {2105.06332},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.06332}\n}"},{"id":"category-theory-in-ml-survey","title":"Category Theory in Machine Learning","authors":"Shiebler et al.","year":2021,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2106.07032","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.07032","claim":"Survey of applications of category theory to machine learning, documenting motivations, goals and common themes across gradient-based learning, probability and Bayesian learning, and invariant or equivariant learning.","relevance":"The standard entry point to categorical ML. Its three threads (gradient-based learning, probability, equivariance) map onto the optimizer, Bayesian/regularization and symmetry aspects of a categorical PEFT theory.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.07032","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2106.07032"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Dan Shiebler","Bruno Gavranović","Paul Wilson"],"bibkey":"shiebler2021category","bibtex":"@article{shiebler2021category,\n  title = {{Category Theory in Machine Learning}},\n  author = {Dan Shiebler and Bruno Gavranović and Paul Wilson},\n  year = {2021},\n  eprint = {2106.07032},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.07032},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.07032}\n}"},{"id":"categorical-deep-learning","title":"Position: Categorical Deep Learning is an Algebraic Theory of All Architectures","authors":"Gavranovic et al.","year":2024,"venue":"ICML 2024","arxiv":"2402.15332","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15332","claim":"Proposes the universal algebra of monads valued in a 2-category of parametric maps as a single theory that subsumes both constraints on models and their implementations. Equivariant layers are monad-algebra homomorphisms for group-action monads, which recovers geometric deep learning, and the theory also yields RNNs, weight tying and constructs from automata theory.","relevance":"Gives the 2-categorical (Para-valued) algebraic language in which architectures, and therefore modifications of architectures, can be specified. PEFT structure constraints (weight sharing in VeRA, orthogonality in OFT, block structure in BOFT) can be phrased as algebraic constraints on parametric morphisms.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15332","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.15332","https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/gavranovic24a.html","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2402.15332"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Bruno Gavranović","Paul Lessard","Andrew Dudzik","Tamara von Glehn","João G. M. Araújo","Petar Veličković"],"bibkey":"gavranovic2024position","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gavranovic2024position,\n  title = {{Position: Categorical Deep Learning is an Algebraic Theory of All Architectures}},\n  author = {Bruno Gavranović and Paul Lessard and Andrew Dudzik and Tamara von Glehn and João G. M. Araújo and Petar Veličković},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML)},\n  eprint = {2402.15332},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.15332}\n}"},{"id":"fundamental-components-deep-learning-thesis","title":"Fundamental Components of Deep Learning: A category-theoretic approach","authors":"Gavranovic","year":2024,"venue":"PhD thesis, University of Strathclyde","arxiv":"2403.13001","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13001","claim":"Develops an end-to-end, uniform and prescriptive categorical foundation for deep learning. Parametricity is modelled with actegories and Para, and bidirectionality with weighted optics; combining them gives parametric weighted optics as a model of neural networks. The thesis gives a lens-theoretic axiomatisation of differentiation (smooth and Boolean), categorical models of architectures, formalised optimisers and a compositional account of supervised learning.","relevance":"The most complete reference for the Para/optics machinery a categorical PEFT theory would build on, including formal optimisers needed to place optimizer-side PEFT methods (LoRA+, GaLore, Riemannian preconditioning).","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13001","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2403.13001"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Bruno Gavranović"],"bibkey":"gavranovic2024fundamental","bibtex":"@inproceedings{gavranovic2024fundamental,\n  title = {{Fundamental Components of Deep Learning: A category-theoretic approach}},\n  author = {Bruno Gavranović},\n  year = {2024},\n  booktitle = {PhD thesis, University of Strathclyde},\n  eprint = {2403.13001},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.13001}\n}"},{"id":"geometric-deep-learning","title":"Geometric Deep Learning: Grids, Groups, Graphs, Geodesics, and Gauges","authors":"Bronstein et al.","year":2021,"venue":"arXiv","arxiv":"2104.13478","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13478","claim":"In the spirit of Klein's Erlangen Programme, derives CNNs, RNNs, GNNs and Transformers from symmetry and scale-separation priors, with group equivariance, gauge structure and geodesics as organising principles. It also gives a constructive recipe for building architectures from prior physical knowledge.","relevance":"The Erlangen-style template for organising a zoo of methods by their symmetry groups. Applied to PEFT, it suggests classifying updates by the group acting on \\(W_0\\) or on the adapter (\\(GL(r)\\) for LoRA, \\(O(d)\\) for OFT/BOFT, diagonal tori for (IA)\\(^3\\) and scaling methods), with LoRA's \\(GL(r)\\) redundancy acting as a gauge group.","sources":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13478","https://export.arxiv.org/api/query","https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/arXiv:2104.13478"],"confidence":"high","verified":true,"families":["theory"],"method_ids":[],"authors_full":["Michael M. Bronstein","Joan Bruna","Taco Cohen","Petar Veličković"],"bibkey":"bronstein2021geometric","bibtex":"@article{bronstein2021geometric,\n  title = {{Geometric Deep Learning: Grids, Groups, Graphs, Geodesics, and Gauges}},\n  author = {Michael M. Bronstein and Joan Bruna and Taco Cohen and Petar Veličković},\n  year = {2021},\n  eprint = {2104.13478},\n  archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2104.13478},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13478}\n}"}],"axes":[{"key":"kind","name":"Kind (where the method acts)","question":"Does the method reparametrise the existing weights, extend the architecture (with or without a neutral point), act only on the backward pass / schedule, operate post hoc on several trained points, or form a family over an auxiliary base T?","values":[{"key":"R","name":"Reparametrisation","definition":"A pointed smooth map rho:(Q,q0)->(Theta,theta0) into the existing weight space: an object of Meth(Theta,theta0)=Diff_*/(Theta,theta0). Decide: the trained model is f(rho(q),-) with the original architecture f.","examples":["LoRA","rsLoRA","LoRA+","DoRA","PiSSA","MiLoRA","OLoRA","LoRA-GA","CorDA","EVA","QLoRA","LoftQ","AdaLoRA","DyLoRA","VeRA","NOLA","RandLoRA","VB-LoRA","LoRA-FA","MiSS","MiCA","LoRA-XS","SVFT","TinyLoRA","LoRA-SB","DeLoRA","GraLoRA","LoHa","LoKr","KronA","TT adapters","MoRA","HiRA","FourierFT","WaveFT","C3A","SHiRA","FISH mask","Trainable Tokens","Diff pruning","BitFit","LN tuning","OFT","BOFT","GOFT","qGOFT","HRA","(IA)^3","SSF","RED","RoAd","LoRA-Pro","Riemannian preconditioned LoRA","LoRA-RITE"]},{"key":"E","name":"Pointed extension","definition":"A function-preserving embedding iota(theta)=(theta,theta0') into a larger architecture f~ with a neutral point theta0' (f~(theta,theta0',-)=f(theta,-)), followed by a reparametrisation of Theta x Theta'. Decide: new boxes are inserted and some parameter value makes them the identity.","examples":["Houlsby adapter","Pfeiffer adapter","Parallel adapter","Compacter","LLaMA-Adapter","LoReFT"]},{"key":"E0","name":"Unpointed extension","definition":"An extension with no neutral point: no parameter value reproduces the frozen function (generically, positive softmax mass on soft tokens always moves the output). Decide: try to solve f~(theta0,p,-)=f(theta0,-) for p.","examples":["Prompt tuning","P-tuning v1","Prefix tuning","P-tuning v2","MPT","CPT","LST (the side network's up-projection has rank <= d/r, so no parameter value reproduces f)","UniPELT, MAM (the prefix part has no neutral value)"]},{"key":"B","name":"Backward lens / schedule","definition":"The forward map is id_Theta; the update is theta <- theta - eta a_t(opt(c_t(grad L))) with a compression c_t and an anchor a_t, or a sequence of merge-and-restart stages (rebasing scheme), or an optimizer that compresses only its own state (Flora). Decide: the method changes how gradients or optimizer state are compressed, or when the base is re-pointed, not the parametrisation.","examples":["GaLore","GaLore with a random projector","Flora (compresses only the momentum; full-space steps)","LISA","MeZO","ReLoRA"]},{"key":"P","name":"Post-hoc operation","definition":"An operation Theta^N -> Theta (or on factor tuples) applied to several trained points. Decide: inputs are already-trained adapters or checkpoints.","examples":["Task arithmetic","TIES","DARE","LoRA soups / CAT","LoraHub","KnOTS"]},{"key":"F_T","name":"Family over a base T","definition":"A T-point a:T->Q of a method, giving rho o a: T -> Theta, with T = tasks (hypernetworks), inputs X (routing), a simplex (mixing) or tenants U (serving). Decide: the adapter depends on an auxiliary variable.","examples":["MoLE","LoRAMoE","X-LoRA","AdapterFusion","Poly","MHR","S-LoRA","Punica","Text-to-LoRA","HyperFormer"]}]},{"key":"shape","name":"Shape of the image germ (expressivity)","question":"What is the germ at theta0 of the reachable set Im(rho)? Read it from the formula: is rho affine, multihomogeneous, a group action, a group acting on a cone, an intersection, or only defined at function level?","values":[{"key":"Lin","name":"Linear (flat)","definition":"rho is affine in q, so Im(rho) is an affine subspace; d(M) is the rank of the linear map (= |M| when it is injective; FourierFT loses one dimension per sampled conjugate frequency pair). Every point is regular, and rebasing is idempotent when frames, masks and operators are frozen across cycles.","examples":["LoRA-FA","MiSS","MiCA","LoRA-XS","SVFT","TinyLoRA","LoRA-SB","MoRA","FourierFT","WaveFT","C3A","SHiRA","FISH mask","Trainable Tokens","BitFit","LN tuning","(IA)^3","SSF","RED","GaLore (per period)","GaLore with a random projector (per period)","LISA (per stage)"]},{"key":"Cone","name":"Cone with vertex at the start","definition":"rho - rho(q0) is multihomogeneous (or a union of linear spaces) and the image is a non-linear cone with vertex at the start rho(q0); the start is then a singular point (apex). The start is theta0 for pointed methods, and the quantised or truncated base theta0 + e for QLoRA-type defects (Prop V.2). Includes determinantal, Segre, Hadamard and tensor-network varieties.","examples":["LoRA","rsLoRA","LoRA+","EVA","QLoRA (at kappa theta0)","AdaLoRA","DyLoRA","GraLoRA","DeLoRA","VeRA","NOLA","RandLoRA","VB-LoRA","LoHa","LoKr","KronA","TT adapters","HiRA","Diff pruning","ReLoRA (per stage)"]},{"key":"Cone*","name":"Translated cone (split)","definition":"rho = theta_res + delta(q) with delta multihomogeneous and q0 chosen so that delta(q0)=theta0-theta_res has full rank: the image is a cone whose apex theta_res is not theta0, and theta0 is a smooth point (Type II objects).","examples":["PiSSA","MiLoRA","OLoRA","LoRA-GA","CorDA","Astra","LoftQ (approximate split over a quantised residual)","DeLoRA (paper init)","LoRETTA (compensated split init of the TT factors)"]},{"key":"Orb","name":"Group orbit","definition":"rho(q)=gamma(q).theta0 with gamma(q) in a group (or monoid) acting linearly on Theta. Exactly orthogonal actions preserve the Gram matrix and the spectrum; torus and Hadamard actions only keep the zeros of W0 (and row lines while scales are nonzero), since unconstrained scales can create new zeros.","examples":["OFT (exact Cayley; HF default Cayley-Neumann is only approximately orthogonal)","GOFT (strict Givens rotations; the official code adds an input scaler by default)","qGOFT (monoid of arbitrary 2x2 blocks)","RoAd_1 (orbit closure of (C^x)^{m/2}; RoAd_2/4 are linear, dim 2m)","ETHER"]},{"key":"Sat","name":"Torus saturation","definition":"A group or monoid acting on a cone or orbit: Im = T.Im(N) for a torus or diagonal monoid T and a method N.","examples":["DoRA (Diag_m . LoRA = image of LoRA followed by a trainable output scale)","BOFT as in HF PEFT (diag(R^m) x butterfly product)"]},{"key":"Meet","name":"Meet (pullback)","definition":"The image is an intersection of a cone and an orbit (an image-level meet), or the object is a fibre product of two methods (a categorical product, which exists only when the set-level fibre product is a manifold).","examples":["HRA (image) = Im LoRA_r cap W0.SO(n): image-level meet, not a product of methods","LoRA-XS = FA x FB (categorical product)","Im LoRA_r cap Im (IA)^3 (k,v): set-level only, a union of coordinate r-planes; no product in Diff"]},{"key":"Fun","name":"Function-level","definition":"No weight-space image: the method lives on an extension and is compared through the realisation Phi_f.","examples":["Houlsby adapter","Parallel adapter","Compacter","LST","LLaMA-Adapter","Prompt tuning","Prefix tuning","P-tuning v2","MoLE","X-LoRA","LoReFT","UniPELT / MAM (adapter and prefix parts; input-dependent gates)"]}]},{"key":"base_point","name":"Base point (position of theta0)","question":"Is theta0 = rho(q0), and if so is the first-order image S1 = d rho_{q0}(T Q) as large as the expressive dimension d(M)?","values":[{"key":"regular","name":"Regular","definition":"dim S1 = d(M) (first-order deficit 0). Linear methods, orbit methods with injective orbit map, and split (Type II) initialisations.","examples":["LoRA-FA","LoRA-XS","FourierFT","BitFit","(IA)^3","RoAd","OFT","GOFT","PiSSA","MiLoRA","OLoRA","LoRA-GA","CorDA","GaLore","LoRETTA"]},{"key":"apex","name":"Apex (first-order deficient)","definition":"dim S1 < d(M). For cone-shaped germs theta0 is the vertex: LoRA's deficit is r(n-r); HRA's paired init has dim S1 = kn - k(k+1)/2 against d = rn - r(r+1)/2 (k=r/2). DoRA inherits LoRA's deficit through its torus factor.","examples":["LoRA","rsLoRA","LoRA+","EVA","DoRA","AdaLoRA","DyLoRA","GraLoRA","VeRA","NOLA","RandLoRA","LoHa","LoKr","KronA","TT/Tucker with zero cores (FacT, LoTR)","HiRA","Diff pruning","HRA (paired init)","ReLoRA (each stage)","LoRA with HF init_lora_weights=\"orthogonal\" (stratum (r/2, r/2, 0), outside the three strata of Thm II.2)"]},{"key":"neutral","name":"Neutral (pointed extension)","definition":"An extension evaluated at its neutral point: zero up-projection, zero gate, identity box.","examples":["Houlsby adapter (zero-up init; the paper's near-identity init leaves a small defect)","Pfeiffer adapter","Parallel adapter","LLaMA-Adapter (g=0)","LoReFT (W=R, b=0)"]},{"key":"defect","name":"Pointing defect","definition":"rho(q0) differs from theta0, by design or by base change; the defect e = rho(q0) - theta0 is part of the data.","examples":["QLoRA (e = kappa theta0 - theta0)","LoftQ (attained residual when the LoRA scale s = alpha/r is 1; HF ignores s, so with alpha = 2r the one-step defect is as large as e)","LoRA-SB (by design)","KaSA (truncated base)","HF default FourierFT (N(0,1) spectrum with scaling 150) / C3A (Xavier init)","VB-LoRA","Uni-LoRA","TinyLoRA (near-identity)","LoRA-XS (R0 ~ N(0, 1e-10))","LoReFT (pyreft initialises W, b independently of R)"]},{"key":"unpointed","name":"Unpointed","definition":"No parameter value reproduces the frozen function.","examples":["Prompt tuning","P-tuning v1","Prefix tuning","P-tuning v2","MPT","CPT (pointed only relative to the in-context-learning state)","LoRA with HF init_lora_weights=False (random A and B, no subtraction)","LST","UniPELT, MAM"]}]},{"key":"gauge","name":"Gauge (generic fibre of rho)","question":"Which transformations of the trainable parameters leave rho unchanged, and what is the dimension of the generic fibre (|M| - d(M))? Read off by inserting g g^{-1} on internal wires between trainable boxes and sliding diagonals through spiders; confirm by the generic Jacobian rank.","values":[{"key":"none","name":"Trivial","definition":"rho is injective near generic points; no charges; factor-wise operations are well defined.","examples":["LoRA-FA","MiSS","LoRA-XS","FourierFT (+1 per sampled conjugate frequency pair)","C3A","MoRA","BitFit","(IA)^3","SSF","RoAd","GaLore","FISH mask","SHiRA","Houlsby adapter (GELU in the reference code, swish in AdapterHub: only the discrete S_r)"]},{"key":"scalar","name":"One scalar slide","definition":"A one-parameter group (b,d)->(cb,d/c) through frozen boxes; one conserved charge under gradient flow.","examples":["VeRA","NOLA","KronA"]},{"key":"torus","name":"Torus","definition":"A diagonal group (per rank channel or per hidden unit); charges are diagonal entries.","examples":["DyLoRA (T_r)","RandLoRA","ReLU bottleneck adapters: Pfeiffer, parallel adapter (positive per-unit slides)","MPT (P* trainable: fibre (P*,u,v) -> (P* . ab^T, u/a, v/b); R^x with P* frozen)","Compacter (per-term slides of the PHM fast weights; GL on the PHM index would leave the rank-rho class)"]},{"key":"GL","name":"General linear on internal wires","definition":"GL_r (or a product of GL on bonds) acting by (B g^{-1}, gA); compact half so(r) gives isometries, non-compact half Sym_r gives r(r+1)/2 charges.","examples":["LoRA","rsLoRA","LoRA+","DoRA","PiSSA","EVA","QLoRA","LoftQ","HiRA","GraLoRA (prod GL_{r/k})","TT adapters (prod GL_{r_e})","LoKr (GL_r x R^*)","Riemannian preconditioned LoRA (gauge respected only undamped; damped and LoRA-Pro steps are O(r)-equivariant)"]},{"key":"GLxTorus","name":"GL times a Hadamard torus","definition":"GL on each factor plus the torus (D1 X D2, D1^{-1} Y D2^{-1}) sliding through the copy spider: dim r1^2 + r2^2 + m + n - 1.","examples":["LoHa"]},{"key":"nonlinear","name":"Non-linear gauge","definition":"The generic fibre is not an orbit of a linear group action.","examples":["HRA (generic fibre dim r(r+1)/2; the only exhibited Lie gauge is the r scalings of u_i)","AdaLoRA (fibre r(r+1): two torus edges plus absorption of Lambda)","DeLoRA (fibre r^2+1; its per-term scalings are a 2r-torus inside it; r = 1: the torus)"]}]},{"key":"covariance","name":"Covariance group (Erlangen geometry of the image)","question":"For which (g,h) in GL_m x GL_n does Im M(g W0 h^{-1}) = g Im M(W0) h^{-1} hold for every W0 (in distribution if random frames or data are involved)?","values":[{"key":"GLxGL","name":"GL x GL (basis-free)","definition":"The image transforms with every change of basis on both sides; it satisfies the sufficient condition of Obs II.15 for every box-wise linear function-preserving gauge.","examples":["LoRA","rsLoRA","LoRA+","AdaLoRA","DyLoRA","DeLoRA","ReLoRA","EVA at a fixed rank (its init frame is only CO(n); HF's default rho = 2 redistributes ranks by explained variance, which makes the image GL x CO(n))","LoRA-Pro","Full FT","BitFit","LISA (each stage frees whole boxes)","function-level methods with dense inserted families: Houlsby, Pfeiffer and parallel adapters, prompts, prefixes, LST, LoReFT, routed LoRA mixtures (function-level analogue of Def II.14)"]},{"key":"OxO","name":"CO x CO (spectral frame)","definition":"The method uses an SVD or another orthogonally equivariant frame of W0, gradients or data. Truncated SVDs are also equivariant under positive scalings, so the group is CO(m) x CO(n) = (R_{>0} O(m)) x (R_{>0} O(n)) (Prop II.16). Methods that use bare singular frames without singular values (LoRA-GA) are scale-invariant rather than covariant and get only O(m) x O(n); they are coded with this nearest key.","examples":["PiSSA","MiLoRA","LoRA-GA (O(m) x O(n) only; sign-convention dependent)","CorDA (formula level, with or without its trace-relative damping; absolute damping gives O(n); the shipped code max-normalises samples)","Astra (CO(m) x GL(n): its split is a left multiple Phi(W0 C W0^T) W0, Prop II.16; coded with this key)","LoRA-XS","SVFT","TinyLoRA","MiCA","GaLore","whitened CorDA / PiSSA (CO(m) x GL(n); Prop II.16)"]},{"key":"GLxCO","name":"GL x CO(n) (input-side orthogonal)","definition":"Right action of (a subgroup of) O(n); covariance on the input side is the normaliser of the structure group (CO(n) for full OFT, block normaliser for block OFT).","examples":["OFT (block normaliser)","HRA","GOFT (strict; the normaliser of its plane pairing)"]},{"key":"MonxGL","name":"Mon x GL (one-sided torus)","definition":"A diagonal torus acts on one side, so only monomial changes of basis are allowed there.","examples":["(IA)^3 on k, v (GL x Mon on down_proj, where it scales the input)","SSF","RED","DoRA","RoAd (pair-monomial on the output side)","BOFT as in HF PEFT","Trainable Tokens (GL on the embedding dimensions, Mon on the vocabulary)"]},{"key":"MonxMon","name":"Mon x Mon (coordinate geometry)","definition":"Hadamard or support structure in the standard basis on both sides; it satisfies the sufficient condition of Obs II.15 for neuron permutations but not for rotations.","examples":["HiRA","LoHa","SHiRA","FISH mask","Diff pruning","QLoRA / LoftQ (quantisation grid)","LoRA-SB (R^x B_m x R^x B_n: the frame is the SVD of a sign-gradient)"]},{"key":"Pi","name":"Imposed structure Pi_X","definition":"The method imposes a reshape (tensor factorisation), a block partition, a cyclic order or a pairing that the network does not have; it breaks even neuron permutations (Lemma VI.2 test).","examples":["LoKr","KronA","TT adapters","Compacter","MoRA","GraLoRA","MiSS","FourierFT","WaveFT","C3A","BOFT butterfly","GOFT / qGOFT pairing","VB-LoRA (chunk partition tied across boxes through the shared bank)","OLoRA (CO(m) x Borel_n: the QR split depends on the order of input coordinates)"]},{"key":"frame","name":"Random or data frame (in distribution)","definition":"Covariance holds only in law, through the distribution of frozen random matrices or data statistics.","examples":["LoRA-FA","VeRA","NOLA","RandLoRA","GaLore with a random projector; Flora (random momentum projection)","MeZO (O(Theta) in law)"]}]},{"key":"merge","name":"Merge type (lifting along the realisation)","question":"After training, does the modified box function stay in the box's function class, and if not, can R1-R6 (fusion, permutation slide, positive-diagonal slide, spider slide, cup slide, norm absorption) move it into a neighbouring linear box?","values":[{"key":"M1","name":"Fuses into its own box","definition":"All non-linearity is on the parameter side; merge = canonical map rho to the terminal object.","examples":["LoRA","DoRA","PiSSA","AdaLoRA","VeRA","LoHa","LoKr","HiRA","FourierFT","C3A","MoRA","OFT","BOFT","HRA","GOFT","RoAd","GraLoRA","MiSS","SHiRA","S-LoRA / Punica (each tenant fibre fuses; left unmerged by design to share the base batch)"]},{"key":"M1->","name":"Fuses into a neighbour","definition":"A diagonal method merges by R1-R7 into a neighbouring linear box, under side conditions: a position-independent path (under RoPE, scales constant on rotary pairs), an unshared target box (no tying, no GQA sharing unless the scale is constant across the group), and a bias slot for shifts. (IA)^3 always fuses into W_K, W_V and W_down; W_up is an alternative only under SwiGLU/GeGLU (every scale) or ReLU (nonnegative scales), and under GELU only for scales in {0,1}. The rules give sufficient conditions, not a decision procedure.","examples":["(IA)^3","SSF","RED","LN tuning"]},{"key":"Mq","name":"Leaves the quantised type","definition":"An exact merge of a generic high-precision update leaves the quantised type (NF4 and recomputed-scale absmax grids are not even closed under addition); type-preserving merges (dequantise-add-requantise, as HF PEFT does for bitsandbytes layers) stay on the grid but are lossy. QA-LoRA's group-constant adapters are the exception.","examples":["QLoRA","LoftQ"]},{"key":"Minf","name":"Obstructed","definition":"The activation side is non-affine in the input for some parameter value (non-linear adapters, softmax-gated prefixes, input-dependent routing), or the edit acts only at selected positions (ReFT, position-gated LoRA variants). This is box-local non-mergeability under the rewrite calculus of Thm VI.3; whole-network non-mergeability is open, and some residual-stream edits do merge (into an untied embedding table, a writer's bias, a post-LN affine, or globally for orthogonal edits in RMSNorm models).","examples":["Houlsby adapter","Pfeiffer adapter","Parallel adapter (non-linear)","Compacter","LST","LLaMA-Adapter","Prompt tuning","Prefix tuning","P-tuning v2","MoLE","LoRAMoE","X-LoRA","AdapterFusion","LoReFT","UniPELT / MAM (all parts, including UniPELT's input-gated LoRA)","HyperFormer (generated non-linear adapters)","AdapterFusion (even with a constant gate)"]},{"key":"na","name":"Not applicable (legacy key; read as null)","definition":"Legacy key, kept because classified methods of kind B use it: backward lenses and schedules write the full weight directly, so there is nothing to merge. It means the same as a null coordinate (axis not applicable) and should be read as such; no other axis has a 'not applicable' value.","examples":["GaLore","Flora","LISA","MeZO"]}]},{"key":"rebasing","name":"Rebasing closure (merge-and-restart reach)","question":"What does the reachable set R_K = theta0 + C^{+K} (additive) or S^K theta0 (action) converge to as the number of merge-and-restart cycles K grows?","values":[{"key":"idempotent","name":"Idempotent","definition":"The shape is exactly a subspace, subgroup or submonoid independent of the base, with frames, masks and operators frozen across cycles: cycles gain nothing (up to closure). Recomputing frames or masks from the merged weight does gain.","examples":["LoRA-FA (fixed A)","LoRA-XS (frames frozen)","MoRA (fixed operators)","FourierFT (fixed indices)","WaveFT","C3A","MiSS","SHiRA","FISH mask (fixed)","BitFit","LN tuning","(IA)^3","SSF","RoAd","OFT (fixed block partition, exact Cayley; gains only the measure-zero set with eigenvalue -1)"]},{"key":"span","name":"Converges to the span","definition":"For a cone closed under real scaling, R_infinity = theta0 + span(C); for LoRA, R_K = W0 + M_{<= min(Kr,m,n)} and full FT is reached exactly at K = ceil(min(m,n)/r).","examples":["LoRA (-> Theta)","ReLoRA","LoHa (-> Theta)","LoKr","HiRA (W0 without zeros; theta0-dependent transport, so not transported ReLoRA)","DoRA","AdaLoRA","GraLoRA","Diff pruning","VeRA (-> LoRA-FA(A))","NOLA (-> span{B_i A_j})","RandLoRA"]},{"key":"group","name":"Converges to a generated group","definition":"For action methods, R_infinity is the orbit of the monoid generated by one cycle's image; it is the connected Lie subgroup generated when that image contains a neighbourhood of e in each generating subgroup (Thm V.3(b)). For block-orthogonal factors it is prod_C SO(C) over connected components of the block hypergraph. HF's Cayley-Neumann OFT does not satisfy the hypothesis: for small generators it generates only a contraction semigroup.","examples":["BOFT iterated (-> diag . W0 . prod SO(b 2^{m'-1}); SO(n) only at full butterfly depth; HF default factor 1 is block OFT)","GOFT, strict (-> SO(n) if the plane graph is connected)","qGOFT (-> GL+(n))","HRA (-> W0 SO(n) in ceil(n/r) cycles; R_K = (W0 + M_{<=Kr}) cap W0 SO(n))","OFT with HF default Cayley-Neumann map (||Q||_op <= 2^{1/4}: a contraction semigroup, K_out shrinks monotonically; unconstrained Q: prod(GL+(b) u M_{<=b-2}))"]},{"key":"schedule","name":"Is itself a rebasing scheme","definition":"The method is defined as a sequence of base changes with a moving shape; its reach is the sum of the shapes visited.","examples":["ReLoRA","GaLore with a random projector","GaLore","LISA","MeZO"]}]}],"arrow_rules":[{"key":"A1","name":"Interval","rule":"For every weight-space method M: Frozen -> M -> Full FT.","h":"Frozen -> M: h = const_{q0}. M -> Full FT: h = rho_M (merging).","examples":["Frozen -> LoRA -> Full FT"]},{"key":"A2","name":"Freezing gives sub-objects","rule":"If M is obtained from N by freezing a block of coordinates q'' at its base value q0'', then M -> N is a pointed monomorphism.","h":"h(q') = (q', q0'').","examples":["LoRA-FA(A0) -> LoRA pointed at (0,A0)","FB(B0) -> LoRA","MiCA -> LoRA pointed at (B0,0)","DyLoRA_k -> DyLoRA_{k+1} (zero padding)"]},{"key":"A3","name":"Factorisation through an explicit polynomial map","rule":"If rho_M = rho_N o h for an explicit smooth pointed h, then M -> N.","h":"Exhibit h; check h(q0^M) = q0^N.","examples":["VeRA -> LoRA-FA(A): h(b,d) = Lambda_b B Lambda_d","NOLA -> LoRA_r: h(alpha,beta) = (sum alpha_i B_i, sum beta_j A_j)","LoHa_{r1,r2} -> LoRA_{r1 r2}: h = (B1 . B2, (A1^T . A2^T)^T) with the face-splitting product","LoRA_r -> LoRA_{r+1}: h(B,A) = ([B 0],[A; a0])","LoRA-XS -> PiSSA: h(R) = (U_r (S_r + R) S_r^{-1/2}, S_r^{1/2} V_r^T)","HRA_r -> LoRA_r pointed at h(q0): h(u) = (W0 B(u), A(u)) from R - I = sum_i H_1...H_{i-1}(H_i - I)","(IA)^3 -> HiRA_1 pointed at (0, 1^T): h(l) = (l - 1, 1^T)","Isomorphisms: rsLoRA = LoRA via h(B,A) = (sqrt(r) B, A); MiSS = LoRA-FA(1^T (x) I_r); a linear adapter parallel to one linear box = LoRA"]},{"key":"A4","name":"Products (and image-level meets)","rule":"If K is the fibre product of M and N over Theta and it exists in Diff, the projections K -> M and K -> N are arrows and K is universal among methods simulated by both. An intersection of images gives only inclusions of images, not arrows of Meth.","h":"The two projections of the fibre product; for LoRA-XS, R -> (B0 R) and R -> (R A0).","examples":["LoRA-XS -> FA(A0), LoRA-XS -> FB(B0)","Im HRA in W0.SO(n) and Im HRA in Im LoRA_r (image-level inclusions only)","Im LoRA_r cap Im (IA)^3: set-level only, no product in Diff"]},{"key":"A5","name":"Sums and stages","rule":"For additive N pointed at theta0, M -> M (+) N; K stages of a rebasing scheme embed in K+1 stages.","h":"q -> (q, q0^N); for stages, pad with the base value of the new stage.","examples":["LoRA_r -> LoRA_r (+) sparse (RoSA)","LoRA_r (+) LoRA_s = LoRA_{r+s} by concatenation, with the A-blocks rescaled under alpha/r scales (unpointed; pointed at the concatenated init, which is a standard zero-B pointing iff the stacked A0 has full row rank and r+s < min(m,n))","ReLoRA^K -> ReLoRA^{K+1}"]},{"key":"T","name":"Transport is an automorphism, not an arrow","rule":"For T in GL(Theta), T_* is an automorphism of the slice Meth(Theta,theta0); T_* M and M are the same object in another frame, not related by an arrow inside the slice. For additive families and a base-independent T it also commutes with merge-and-restart; for action-type or theta0-dependent transports it need not.","h":"None inside the slice; record the frame T instead.","examples":["Sum of k Kronecker terms = R^{-1}_* LoRA_k (Van Loan-Pitsianis rearrangement)","HiRA = (D_{W0})_* LoRA inside the slice at theta0 (theta0-dependent transport)","FourierFT = mask in the real cosine Fourier frame (a transport only without sampled conjugate pairs, and only for init_weights=True)"]},{"key":"O1","name":"Obstruction: image","rule":"No arrow M -> N if Im M is not contained in Im N; in particular if N preserves an absolute invariant (Gram matrix, spectrum, row lines, zero pattern) that M can change.","h":"n/a","examples":["LoRA -/-> OFT","LoRA -/-> (IA)^3","DoRA -/-> LoRA_r"]},{"key":"O2","name":"Obstruction: dimension and cut-rank","rule":"No arrow M -> N if d(M) > d(N) or max rank(M) > max rank(N).","h":"n/a","examples":["LoHa_{r,r} -/-> LoRA_{r'} for r' < r^2 (generic rank r^2)","LoRA_r -/-> k-term Kronecker sums for k < kappa_1 = min(m1,m2) min(n1,n2), the Kronecker rank of a generic rank-one update (maximal for aligned splits; = N for equal square N x N splits)"]},{"key":"O3","name":"Obstruction: first order and base point","rule":"No smooth arrow M -> N if S1(M) is not contained in S1(N), or if M and N lie in different strata of the moduli of initialisations (Type I apex vs Type II smooth point), even when images agree.","h":"n/a","examples":["T_A-stratum LoRA -/-> T_B-stratum LoRA","PiSSA -/-> LoRA and LoRA -/-> PiSSA (images incomparable)"]}],"edges":[{"source":"peanut","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Theta_1,\\Theta_2)=(\\Theta_2^\\top,\\ \\sigma(\\Theta_1^\\top W_0))","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,\\sigma(\\Theta_{1,0}^\\top W_0))\\). Smooth for GELU/SiLU; for ReLU, smooth near any \\(q_0\\) where \\(\\Theta_{1,0}^\\top W_0\\) has no zero entry."},{"source":"monteclora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\mu,\\xi)=(B,\\ \\mu)","note":"Projection forgetting the variational parameters \\(\\xi\\). The reverse inclusion is A2 (freeze \\(\\xi\\) at \\(\\xi_0\\))."},{"source":"velora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the trained-method data (the cotangent signal for \\(A\\)) differ."},{"source":"lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B\\ 0],\\,[A;\\,a_0])","note":"LoRA\\(_r\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA\\(_{r+1}\\) (the rank filtration); \\(\\mathrm T_A\\)-pointed when \\(a_0\\notin\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\)."},{"source":"lora","target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big((\\lVert(W_0+sBA)_{i,:}\\rVert)_i,\\ B,\\ A\\big)","note":"DoRA with the magnitude set to the current row norms reproduces LoRA. Pointed (\\(\\mu_0\\) = row norms of \\(W_0\\)); smooth wherever no row of \\(W_0+sBA\\) vanishes."},{"source":"lora","target":"relora","rule":"A5","h":"q\\mapsto(q,\\,q_0^{(2)},\\dots,q_0^{(K)})","note":"One stage embeds into \\(K\\) stages of the rebasing scheme."},{"source":"lora","target":"peanut","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([M,\\,-M],\\ [B^\\top;\\,-B^\\top]\\big),\\quad M=(W_0^{+})^\\top A^\\top","note":"LoRA\\(_r\\to\\) PEANuT\\(_{2r}\\) via \\(\\sigma(x)-\\sigma(-x)=x\\), which holds for ReLU, GELU and SiLU (so the arrow is smooth for the latter two); needs \\(W_0\\) of full column rank (\\(M^\\top W_0=A\\)). Pointed at \\(\\Theta_1=[M_0,-M_0]\\), \\(\\Theta_2=0\\)."},{"source":"rslora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(\\sqrt r\\,B,\\ A)","note":"Isomorphism over \\(\\Theta\\) (inverse \\(B\\mapsto B/\\sqrt r\\)); pointed because \\(B_0=0\\)."},{"source":"lora-ga","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on the untouched \\(W_0\\), pointed at the degenerate pointing \\(([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0])\\) (product \\(0\\), outside the three strata)."},{"source":"lora-plus","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object. The trajectory equivalence is the torus map \\(c_B=1/\\lambda\\) (Adam) or \\(\\lambda^{-1/2}\\) (SGD), which rescales \\(s\\); it is not an arrow."},{"source":"lora-fa","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_0)","note":"Freezing \\(A\\) at its base value: a pointed monomorphism into LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\)."},{"source":"lora-fa","target":"asylora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B)=B\\,A_0Q^\\top","note":"Isomorphism \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\cong\\mathrm{FA}(Q)\\) when \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0=\\mathrm{row}\\,Q\\) (e.g. \\(Q\\) the orthonormalised \\(A_0\\)); for independent random frames the two agree only in law."},{"source":"lora-fa","target":"flora-opt","rule":"A5","h":"B\\mapsto(B,\\,0,\\dots,0)","note":"LoRA-FA is the first period of Flora when Flora's first projection is \\(A_0\\) (shared seed): one stage embeds into the rebasing scheme."},{"source":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object, different trained-method data (the cometric)."},{"source":"lora-rite","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule differs."},{"source":"lora-pro","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."},{"source":"relora","target":"relora","rule":"A5","h":"(q_1,\\dots,q_K)\\mapsto(q_1,\\dots,q_K,\\,q_0^{(K+1)})","note":"ReLoRA\\(^K\\hookrightarrow\\) ReLoRA\\(^{K+1}\\), padding with the next stage's base value."},{"source":"relora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((B_k,A_k)_k)=([B_1\\cdots B_K],\\,[A_1;\\dots;A_K])","note":"\\(K\\) stages factor through LoRA\\(_{Kr}\\) (Obs I.7); pointed when the stacked \\(A_0\\)'s have independent rows."},{"source":"relora","target":"cola","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"Stagewise identical objects at equal link ranks; COLA differs only in its full optimizer reset and schedule."},{"source":"delta-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([(1+\\lambda)B,\\ -\\lambda B_K],\\ [A;\\ A_K]\\big)","note":"After step \\(K\\) the effective map factors through LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (scale \\(s\\)), pointed at \\(([(1+\\lambda)B_K,-\\lambda B_K],[A_K;A_K])\\)."},{"source":"cola","target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"At equal link ranks; with decreasing ranks, pad each link by LoRA\\(_{r_i}\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA\\(_{r_1}\\)."},{"source":"cola","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((B_i,A_i)_i)=([B_1\\cdots B_M],\\,[A_1;\\dots;A_M])","note":"The chain factors through LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_i}\\) (Obs I.7)."},{"source":"periodic-lora","target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{(stagewise)}","note":"At momentum \\(m=0\\)."},{"source":"lora-dropout","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object at inference without ensembling."},{"source":"asylora","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ Q)","note":"Freezing \\(A=Q\\): a pointed monomorphism into LoRA at \\((0,Q)\\)."},{"source":"asylora","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(B)=B\\,QA_0^{+}","note":"Isomorphism when \\(\\mathrm{row}\\,Q=\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\) (\\(A_0^+\\) the right pseudo-inverse); for independent draws the two agree only in law."},{"source":"lora-without-regret","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Plain LoRA."},{"source":"flat-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object, regularised loss."},{"source":"altlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."},{"source":"altlora","target":"riemannian-preconditioned-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; each half-step uses one of the two Riemannian preconditioners."},{"source":"loft","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object."},{"source":"polar","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(X,\\Theta,Y)=(X\\Theta,\\ Y^\\top)","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,Y_0^\\top)\\), which has orthonormal rows."},{"source":"adalora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at LoRA's \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point \\((0,Q_0)\\)."},{"source":"adalora","target":"polar","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=\\big(P(P^\\top P)^{-1/2},\\ (P^\\top P)^{1/2}\\Lambda(QQ^\\top)^{1/2},\\ Q^\\top(QQ^\\top)^{-1/2}\\big)","note":"Polar factors of \\(P\\) and \\(Q^\\top\\); smooth where \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) have full rank; pointed at PoLAR with \\(\\Theta_0=0\\)."},{"source":"adamss","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h((C_k,B_k)_k)=\\big([E_{\\mathcal S_1}C_1\\cdots E_{\\mathcal S_K}C_K],\\ [B_1\\tilde A_1;\\dots;B_K\\tilde A_K]\\big)","note":"Factors through LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_k}\\) on \\(W_0\\), pointed at \\((0,[B_{1,0}\\tilde A_1;\\dots;B_{K,0}\\tilde A_K])\\). This is a \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) point when the stacked rows are independent; with HF's single shared \\(R\\times d_{in}\\) frame that needs \\(\\sum_kr_k\\le R\\). Otherwise it is a degenerate \\(B_0=0\\) pointing outside the three strata (Thm II.2(e)); the arrow is still pointed."},{"source":"pissa","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1: PiSSA\\(_r\\) into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (HF's conversion), pointed at the degenerate pointing with product \\(0\\)."},{"source":"olora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) on \\(W_0\\) (Thm II.1), at a degenerate pointing."},{"source":"loftq","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_T],\\,[A;\\,A_T])","note":"In the slice at the attained start \\(\\theta_0'=Q_T+B_TA_T\\): LoftQ\\(_r\\) into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) over \\(\\theta_0'\\), at a degenerate pointing (as for PiSSA)."},{"source":"eva","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,A)\\mapsto(B,A)","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_{r_l} pointed at (0, V_{r_l}^T); to vanilla LoRA with a random A_0 there is only a weak morphism (different point of Gr(r,n))."},{"source":"corda","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"The PiSSA->LoRA conversion of Thm II.1 applied to CorDA: lands in LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) of rank pattern (r,r,0), outside the three strata."},{"source":"astra","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing; valid in either stratum."},{"source":"mica","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(A)=\\big(U_{-r},\\ \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}A\\big)","note":"Freezing B at U_{-r}: monomorphism into LoRA pointed at (U_{-r},0), stratum T_B (axes.json A2 example)."},{"source":"mica","target":"milora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A)=\\big(U_m\\Sigma_m^{1/2},\\ \\Sigma_m^{1/2}V_m^\\top+\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}\\Sigma_m^{-1/2}A\\big)","note":"W_0+(alpha/r)U_mA = W_p+U_m(Sigma_mV_m^T+(alpha/r)A); pointed (h(0)=MiLoRA's q_0); needs the minor singular values nonzero (checked numerically)."},{"source":"kasa","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\Delta\\sigma,A)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma),\\ A\\big)","note":"Arrow in the slice at kappa theta_0 = W_world: onto zero-init LoRA at the truncated base, pointed at (0,A_0)."},{"source":"milora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing."},{"source":"gora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(cB,A),\\ c=\\tfrac{\\alpha/\\sqrt{r_\\ell}}{s}","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_{r_l} with residual W_0 pointed at (cB_0,A_0): an arrow over the common start theta_0+e, not in the slice at theta_0 (there only a weak morphism to zero-init LoRA)."},{"source":"lora-sb","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A4","h":"h(R)=sU_rR","note":"Projection of the product FA(V_r^T) x FB(U_r) onto LoRA-FA with frozen A=V_r^T, re-pointed at sU_rR_0: an arrow over the common start theta_0+e."},{"source":"lora-null","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=([B,\\,-B_0],\\,[A;\\,A_0])","note":"Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} with residual theta_0, pointed at the degenerate (r,r,0) pointing."},{"source":"dylora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Isomorphism of underlying objects at b=r_max (pointed at (0,A_0)); it does not intertwine the nested-dropout objective, whose symmetry is only T_r."},{"source":"dylora","target":"dylora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B_b,A_b)=\\big([\\tfrac{r_{\\max}}{b}B_b,\\ 0],\\ [A_b;\\ A_{0,(b+1):}]\\big)","note":"Truncation level b as a sub-object of level r_max: freeze the trailing B columns at 0 and A rows at their base values (scale alpha/b vs alpha/r_max absorbed)."},{"source":"sora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,g,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g),\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0), same random A_0; after pruning the support S, the restriction maps into LoRA_{|S|}."},{"source":"increlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,\\Lambda,A)=(B\\Lambda,\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0); S_1={B_0 diag(delta) A_0} lies inside {XA_0}."},{"source":"increlora","target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,\\Lambda,A)\\mapsto(P,\\Lambda,Q)","note":"Same P Lambda Q object at Lambda_0=0, pointed at the same frames; the two differ only in the rank schedule (grow vs prune)."},{"source":"increlora","target":"increlora","rule":"A5","h":"h(q)=\\big(q,\\ (b_{\\rm new},0,a_{\\rm new})\\big)","note":"Adding a component at lambda=0 embeds stage r_k into stage r_k+1."},{"source":"alora-allocating","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\alpha),\\ A)","note":"At each allocation, pointed at (0,A_0); with all gates 1 it is the identity."},{"source":"autolora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,a,\\beta)=\\big([\\mathrm{softmax}(\\beta)_jb_j]_j,\\ [a_j^\\top]_j\\big)","note":"Search-phase object into LoRA_k pointed at (0,A_0); after thresholding and retraining the recipe's output is LoRA_{r_l} itself (h=id)."},{"source":"elalora","target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same P Lambda Q object pointed at Lambda=0 with the same frames; only the rank schedule differs."},{"source":"elalora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at (0,Q_0)."},{"source":"lora-drop","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_i,A_i)_{\\rm keep},(B_s,A_s)\\big)=\\big((B_i,A_i)_{\\rm keep},(B_s,A_s)_{j\\in\\rm share}\\big)","note":"Diagonal (tying) map into per-layer LoRA on all layers, pointed when every shared layer carries the same A_0."},{"source":"dora-dynamic-rank","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,c,A)=(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c),\\ A)","note":"Pointed at (0,A_0), stratum T_A."},{"source":"dora-dynamic-rank","target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same P Lambda Q object pointed at the zero gate with the same frames."},{"source":"dora-dynamic-rank","target":"sora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,c,A)=\\big(B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(g_0)^{-1},\\ g_0,\\ A\\big)","note":"Into SoRA pointed at (0,g_0,A_0) with the same A_0; S_1={B_0 diag(delta) A_0} lies inside {XA_0}. The reverse fails (O3)."},{"source":"fourierft","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(c)=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{mn}\\,B_F\\,\\mathrm{diag}(c,-c),\\ \\ B_F=[\\cos\\theta_k\\ \\sin\\theta_k]_k,\\ A_F=[\\cos\\varphi_k;\\ \\sin\\varphi_k]_k","note":"Into LoRA-FA_{2n_c} with frozen trigonometric A_F, pointed at 0; Re(e^{i(theta+phi)})=cos cos - sin sin (checked to 1e-16)."},{"source":"fourierft","target":"fourierft","rule":"A2","h":"h(c_\\Omega)=(c_\\Omega,\\ 0_{\\Omega'\\setminus\\Omega})","note":"FourierFT on index set Omega is a sub-object of FourierFT on Omega' containing it."},{"source":"frod","target":"frod","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Sigma)=(\\Sigma,\\ S=0)","note":"The diagonal-only (spectral-rescaling) variant is the sub-object obtained by freezing the sparse off-diagonal entries at 0."},{"source":"miss","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(D)=D,\\ \\ A_0=\\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{\\rm in}/r}\\otimes I_r","note":"Isomorphism MiSS = LoRA-FA(1^T (x) I_r) (axes.json A3 example)."},{"source":"miss","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(D)=(D,\\ \\mathbf 1^\\top_{d_{\\rm in}/r}\\otimes I_r)","note":"Freezing A at the summing projection: monomorphism into LoRA pointed at (0, 1^T (x) I_r)."},{"source":"pvera","target":"vera","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d_\\mu,d_\\sigma)=(b,\\ d_\\mu)","note":"Projection forgetting the variance half; intertwines the merged rho, not the stochastic training objective."},{"source":"pvera","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d_\\mu,d_\\sigma)=\\alpha\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_{d_\\mu}","note":"Composite with VeRA -> LoRA-FA(A_mu), pointed at 0."},{"source":"randlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Lambda,\\Gamma)=\\big([B_1\\Lambda_1,\\dots,B_n\\Lambda_n],\\ [A\\Gamma_1;\\dots;A\\Gamma_n]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_{nr} pointed at (0,[A Gamma_1^0;...;A Gamma_n^0]), a rank-r stacked A_0, outside the three strata."},{"source":"tinylora","target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\Big(\\sum_iv_iP_i\\Big)\\Sigma_r^{-1/2}","note":"Into LoRA-XS in the form W_0+U_rR Sigma_rV_r^T (Prop I.8(a)), pointed at R=0."},{"source":"tinylora","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\Big(U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\big(I+\\textstyle\\sum_iv_iP_i\\big),\\ \\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\Big)","note":"Composite with LoRA-XS -> PiSSA; h(0)=PiSSA's q_0."},{"source":"uni-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\theta_d)=P\\theta_d=\\big(B^\\ell(P\\theta_d),A^\\ell(P\\theta_d)\\big)_\\ell","note":"The defining projection, pointed at (B_0,A_0)=P theta_{d,0} (an arrow over the common start), or at the origin (0,0) for the exactly pointed variant."},{"source":"vb-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,\\sigma)=\\big(B(\\alpha,\\sigma),\\ A(\\alpha,\\sigma)\\big),\\ \\ u=\\textstyle\\sum_s\\mathrm{softmax}(\\mathrm{TopK}(\\sigma,k))_s\\,\\alpha_s","note":"Assembly map into LoRA, pointed at (B_0,A_0)=h(q_0); smooth on each top-k chamber only (top-k switches are non-smooth)."},{"source":"vera","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d)=\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d","note":"axes.json A3 example; pointed at 0 with frozen A."},{"source":"vera","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(b,d)=(\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d,\\ A)","note":"Composite with LoRA-FA(A) -> LoRA, pointed at (0,A)."},{"source":"waveft","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(c)=\\lambda\\,B_W\\,C_\\Omega(c),\\ \\ A_W=[\\psi_j^\\top]_{j\\in\\mathrm{cols}(\\Omega)}","note":"Separable 2-D DWT: atom (i,j) is w_i v_j^T, so DeltaW=B_W C_Omega A_W; into LoRA-FA with frozen wavelet rows A_W, pointed at 0."},{"source":"svft","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(M)=U_{:,R}\\,M_{R,C},\\ \\ A_0=V_{:,C}^\\top","note":"R, C = row and column indices met by Omega; into LoRA-FA_{|C|} with frozen A_0=V_C^T, pointed at 0."},{"source":"fact","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,\\Sigma,V)=\\big(sU\\Sigma_i,\\ V^\\top\\big)_i","note":"Into per-box LoRA_r pointed at (sU_0Sigma_{i,0}, 0): stratum T_B in every box."},{"source":"fact","target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,P,C,V)=\\big(U,\\ (\\textstyle\\sum_tP_{i,t}C_t)_i,\\ V\\big)","note":"FacT-TK -> FacT-TT: the Tucker core constrains the per-matrix cores; both pointed at V=0."},{"source":"nola","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\Big(\\sum_j\\beta_jB_j,\\ \\sum_i\\alpha_iA_i\\Big)","note":"axes.json A3 example; pointed at the origin (0,0) (paper) or, with h(alpha,beta)=(sum beta_j B_j, A_off+sum alpha_i A_i), at (0,A_off) in stratum T_A (code)."},{"source":"tied-lora","target":"sharelora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{v_\\ell,u_\\ell\\}_\\ell,B,A)=\\big(\\{\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell}\\}_\\ell,\\;A\\big)","note":"Lands in ShareA at its standard pointing \\((B_\\ell=0,A_0)\\); strict, since ShareA has free per-layer \\(B_\\ell\\)."},{"source":"tied-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\Lambda_{v_\\ell}B\\Lambda_{u_\\ell},\\;A\\big)_\\ell","note":"Per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\) with the same \\(A_0\\) in every layer (stratum \\(T_A\\))."},{"source":"sharelora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_\\ell)_\\ell,A\\big)=(B_\\ell,A)_\\ell","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared \\(A\\); per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\) with a common \\(A_0\\)."},{"source":"prolora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_u,B_0,A_u,A_0)=\\big(\\mathcal B(B_u,B_0),\\;\\mathcal A(A_u,A_0)\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\mathcal A(A_u^0,A_0^0))\\), stratum \\(T_A\\) when the expanded \\(A_0\\) has full row rank. Conversely \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_u\\to\\) PRoLoRA by A2 (freeze \\(B_0=0\\) and \\(A_0\\) at init)."},{"source":"mos","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\mathrm{pool}_B,\\mathrm{pool}_A)=\\big(\\mathrm{Route}^c(\\mathrm{pool}_B,I^b_\\ell),\\;\\mathrm{Route}^r(\\mathrm{pool}_A,I^a_\\ell)\\big)_\\ell","note":"Per-layer LoRA of rank r pointed at \\((0,A_\\ell^0)\\) (B-pools zero)."},{"source":"rasa","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\tilde B_i\\ \\ B_S]\\,D_i,\\;[\\tilde A_i;\\,A_S]\\big)_i","note":"Into per-layer LoRA of rank \\(r-k+Lk\\) pointed at \\((0,[\\tilde A_i^0;A_S^0])\\); for \\(k=0\\) it is the identity onto \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\)."},{"source":"lora-xs","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\big(U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2}+U_rR\\,\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\;\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"The framework's LoRA-XS to PiSSA arrow written in the catalog's normalisation \\(U_rR\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\); \\(h(0)\\) is PiSSA's standard split."},{"source":"lora-xs","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A4","h":"h(R)=U_rR\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(A_0),\\ A_0=\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top","note":"Product projection onto the one-sided factor, here LoRA-FA with the spectral (not random) frame \\(A_0\\)."},{"source":"lora-xs","target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\begin{bmatrix}R\\Sigma_r&0\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}\\ \\ (\\Omega=\\text{top-left }r\\times r\\text{ block})","note":"Isomorphism onto SVFT with a dense top-left support, pointed at \\(M=0\\) (SVFT's exactly pointed variant)."},{"source":"lora-xs","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(R)=\\big(U_rR,\\;\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\): the \\(T_A\\) point whose row space is the top-r right singular space."},{"source":"lamda","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\alpha US-U_r\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)","note":"Pointed (\\(h(U_r,I)=0\\) for \\(\\alpha=1\\)); in the early phase this is an epimorphism onto the image."},{"source":"lamda","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\big(\\alpha US\\,\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\;\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into PiSSA at its standard split \\((U_r\\Sigma_r^{1/2},\\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top)\\); LaMDA is PiSSA's A-frozen sub-object."},{"source":"lamda","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(U,S)=\\big(\\alpha US-U_r,\\;\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top)\\) (stratum \\(T_A\\))."},{"source":"lora-mini","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,D)=(PC,\\;DQ)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at \\(h(q_0)=(PC_0,D_0Q)\\). With the paper's all-Kaiming init this is a morphism between objects with a defect (unpointed slice); with \\(C_0=0\\) it lands in stratum \\(T_A\\)."},{"source":"loca","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(a)=\\big[a_1C_{l^1_1,:}^\\top\\ \\cdots\\ a_{\\mathcal B}C_{l^1_{\\mathcal B},:}^\\top\\big]\\ \\text{ into }\\ \\mathrm{FA}(A_0),\\ A_0=\\big[D_{l^2_1,:};\\dots;D_{l^2_{\\mathcal B},:}\\big]","note":"Each coefficient is a rank-one cosine outer product, so LoCA factors through a one-sided method with the selected input cosines frozen as \\(A_0\\). The arrow is informative only when the selected input frequencies span a proper subspace of \\(\\mathbb R^{d_{in}}\\)."},{"source":"compacter","target":"phm-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big(\\{A_i\\},\\{s_i^{\\bullet,\\ell},t_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}\\}\\big)=\\big(A_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}=A_i,\\;B_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}=s_i^{\\bullet,\\ell}t_i^{\\bullet,\\ell\\top}\\big)_{\\ell,\\ \\bullet\\in\\{D,U\\}}","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared slow weights and the rank-\\(\\rho\\) product; neutral point maps to neutral point (Houlsby placement; Compacter++ maps to the Pfeiffer placement instead)."},{"source":"compacter","target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(W_{down}=\\textstyle\\sum_iA_i\\otimes s_i^Dt_i^{D\\top},\\;W_{up}=\\sum_iA_i\\otimes s_i^Ut_i^{U\\top}\\big)","note":"Same extension and placement, with a GeLU bottleneck and matching bias convention; Houlsby's dense projections contain Compacter's PHM ones."},{"source":"gralora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{U_{ij},V_{ij}\\})=\\big(\\mathrm{blockdiag}(L_1,\\dots,L_k),\\;S\\big),\\ L_i=[U_{i1}\\cdots U_{ik}],\\ S=\\text{stack of the }V_{ij}^\\top\\text{ on column block }j","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{kr}\\) (proof of Prop II.12), pointed at \\((0,S_0)\\); the scale \\(\\alpha/r\\) is absorbed into h."},{"source":"hira","target":"loha","rule":"A2","h":"h(B,A)=(U_0,V_0,B,A),\\ \\ U_0V_0=W_0/\\gamma\\ \\text{a fixed rank-}\\mathrm{rk}W_0\\text{ factorisation}","note":"HiRA is \\(\\mathrm{LoHa}_{\\mathrm{rk}W_0,r}\\) with its first Hadamard factor frozen at \\(W_0\\); the target is pointed at \\((U_0,V_0,0,A_0)\\), not at LoHa's random default."},{"source":"hira","target":"glora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B,A)=\\big(\\mathcal A\\text{-path}=BA,\\ \\text{all other GLoRA paths frozen at }0\\big)","note":"GLoRA's \\(W_0\\odot\\mathcal A\\) path with a LoRA-type \\(\\mathcal A\\) and every other path off is HiRA. HiRA's \\(B_0=0\\) pointing differs from GLoRA's zero down-projection default."},{"source":"loha","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)=\\big(\\gamma\\,B_1\\bullet B_2,\\;(A_1^\\top\\bullet A_2^\\top)^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1r_2}\\) by the face-splitting (row-wise Khatri-Rao) product. HF zero-initialises \\(A_2\\) (hada_w2_b, \\(r\\times d_{in}\\)), so \\(h(q_0)=(\\gamma B_1^0\\bullet B_2^0,\\,0)\\): the target is pointed at \\(B_0\\ne0\\), \\(A_0=0\\), stratum \\(T_B\\) when \\(B_1^0\\bullet B_2^0\\) has full column rank [Num]. For \\(r_1=r_2=r\\le2\\), \\(r^2\\le2r\\), so LoHa is dominated by the equal-budget \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r}\\) (Prop II.12(c))."},{"source":"lokr","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,B,A)=\\big(C\\otimes B,\\;I_{u_q}\\otimes A\\big)","note":"By the mixed-product rule, into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{u_qr'}\\) pointed at \\((0,I_{u_q}\\otimes A_0)\\) (zero C); this is stratum \\(T_A\\) when \\(u_qr'<\\min(m,n)\\). The mirror \\(h=(I_{u_p}\\otimes B,\\,C\\otimes A)\\) lands in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{u_pr'}\\)."},{"source":"lokr","target":"krona","rule":"A3","h":"h(C,B,A)=(C,\\;BA)","note":"Into KronA with its second factor constrained to rank \\(r'\\); KronA pointed at \\((0,B_0A_0)\\), not at its dense Kaiming default."},{"source":"shira","target":"rosa","rule":"A5","h":"h(\\Delta)=\\big(B=0,\\;A=A_0,\\;S=M\\odot\\Delta\\big)","note":"\\(M\\to M\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}\\), with the LoRA summand at its base point; requires RoSA's mask to equal SHiRA's (SHiRA-Grad is close to RoSA's Fisher mask)."},{"source":"bd-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,\\dots,B_N,A)=\\big(\\mathrm{diag}(B_1,\\dots,B_N),\\;A\\big)","note":"The inclusion of block-diagonal factors; pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\), stratum \\(T_A\\)."},{"source":"bd-lora","target":"gralora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{B_i\\},A)=\\big(B_{ij}=B_i,\\;A_{ij}^\\top=A^{(i)}_{:,J_j}\\big)_{i,j\\le N}","note":"GraLoRA with \\(k=N\\) blocks per side and per-block rank \\(r/N\\): copy \\(B_i\\) along block row i. Pointed at \\((0,\\text{slices of }A_0)\\)."},{"source":"quantum-peft","target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\theta_U,\\theta_V,\\lambda)=\\big(P=Q_P(\\theta_U)I_{d_{out}\\times K},\\;\\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda),\\;Q=(Q_P(\\theta_V)I_{d_{in}\\times K})^\\top\\big)","note":"Into rank-K AdaLoRA pointed at \\((U_0,0,V_0^\\top)\\): orthonormal circuit frames instead of AdaLoRA's Gaussian P, Q."},{"source":"quantum-peft","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(U(\\theta_U)\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\lambda),\\;V(\\theta_V)^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_K\\) pointed at \\((0,V_0^\\top)\\) when \\(\\Lambda_0=0\\)."},{"source":"abba","target":"loha","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)=(B_1,A_1,cB_2,A_2),\\ \\ c=\\frac{\\alpha/\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}{\\gamma_{\\rm LoHa}}","note":"Isomorphism onto LoHa pointed at \\((\\text{factors of }[W_0]_{r_1},0,A_2^0)\\), not at HF's random default."},{"source":"abba","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{r_1r_2}}\\,B_1\\bullet B_2,\\;(A_1^\\top\\bullet A_2^\\top)^\\top\\big)","note":"Face-splitting into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1r_2}\\), as for LoHa."},{"source":"krona","target":"lokr","rule":"A3","h":"h(A_k,B_k)=\\big(C=A_k,\\;\\text{right factor}=B_k\\big)\\ \\ \\text{(LoKr with neither factor decomposed)}","note":"Isomorphism onto the unfactorised LoKr configuration; pointed when KronA's zero factor is the left one (HF zero-initialises lokr_w1)."},{"source":"phm-adapter","target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{A_i^D,B_i^D,A_i^U,B_i^U\\})=\\big(W_{down}=\\textstyle\\sum_iA_i^D\\otimes B_i^D,\\;W_{up}=\\sum_iA_i^U\\otimes B_i^U\\big)","note":"Same extension and placement; neutral point maps to neutral point."},{"source":"kadaptation","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([A_1\\otimes u_1,\\dots,A_n\\otimes u_n],\\;[I_n\\otimes v_1^\\top;\\dots;I_n\\otimes v_n^\\top]\\big)","note":"Uses \\((A_i\\otimes u_i)(I_n\\otimes v_i^\\top)=A_i\\otimes u_iv_i^\\top\\). Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{n^2r}\\), pointed at \\((0,[I_n\\otimes v_i^{0\\top}])\\) when \\(u_i^0=0\\)."},{"source":"kradapter","target":"loha","rule":"A2","h":"h(U,V)=(B_1=S_1,\\;A_1=U,\\;B_2=S_2,\\;A_2=V),\\ \\ S_1,S_2\\ \\text{the }\\{0,1\\}\\text{ row selectors of the Kronecker index}","note":"LoHa with \\(r_1=k_1\\), \\(r_2=k_2\\) and its B-factors frozen at the selectors (one of them scaled by \\(\\alpha/\\gamma_{\\rm LoHa}\\)); pointed at \\((S_1,0,S_2,V_0)\\). Checked: \\((S_1U)\\odot(S_2V)\\) has entry \\(U_{a,j}V_{b,j}\\) at row \\((a,b)\\)."},{"source":"loretta","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\{G^i_{up}\\},\\{G^i_{down}\\})=\\big(\\mathrm{TT}(\\{G^i_{up}\\}),\\;\\mathrm{TT}(\\{G^i_{down}\\})\\big)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at the split \\((B_0,A_0)\\) with residual \\(W_0-B_0A_0\\) (stratum S, like PiSSA or OLoRA but with a random P)."},{"source":"tt-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\mathcal C_1,\\dots,\\mathcal C_D)=\\big(\\mathcal C_1\\times^1\\cdots\\times^1\\mathcal C_c,\\;\\mathcal C_{c+1}\\times^1\\cdots\\times^1\\mathcal C_D\\big)\\ \\text{reshaped to }d_{out}\\times r_c,\\ r_c\\times d_{in}","note":"When the reshape orders all output modes before the input modes (cut after core c, bond \\(r_c\\)), TT-LoRA factors through \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_c}\\) (Thm II.10). With the Gaussian init this is a morphism between objects with a defect."},{"source":"lotr","target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,\\{G_s\\})=\\big(V=A,\\;U=B,\\;\\Sigma_s=G_s^\\top\\big)","note":"Isomorphism of unpointed objects when FacT-TT stacks the same matrices (FacT stacks all 12L ViT blocks, LoTR one family per stack); scales absorbed. As pointed objects it lands in FacT pointed at \\((U,V,\\Sigma)=(B_0,A_0,0)\\), not at FacT's default \\(V_0=0\\), whose first-order space differs."},{"source":"lotr","target":"flora-tucker","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,\\{G_s\\})=\\big(A_F=B,\\;G_F=G_s^\\top,\\;B_F=A\\big)_s","note":"Diagonal copy of the shared frames into per-layer FLoRA, landing at FLoRA's own G = 0 pointing."},{"source":"lotr","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(BG_s^\\top,\\;A^\\top\\big)_s","note":"Per-layer LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0^\\top)\\) (stratum \\(T_A\\))."},{"source":"lotr","target":"sharelora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\{B_s=BG_s^\\top\\}_s,\\;A^\\top\\big)","note":"ShareA with the shared down-projection \\(A^\\top\\)."},{"source":"flora-tucker","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,G,B)=\\big(sAG,\\;B^\\top\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_2}\\) (\\(=\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) for \\(r_1=r_2=r\\)) pointed at \\((0,B_0^\\top)\\), stratum \\(T_A\\); the mirror \\((A,\\,sGB^\\top)\\) lands in \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_1}\\) at \\((A_0,0)\\), stratum \\(T_B\\)."},{"source":"lorta","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\mathcal B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:]),\\;A^\\top\\big)_{(l,i)},\\ \\ \\mathcal B=\\big[B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[1,:]);\\dots;B\\,\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[H,:])\\big]","note":"Per-box \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); with a zero output factor \\(B_0=0\\) this is stratum \\(T_A\\)."},{"source":"lorta","target":"lotr","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(A_{\\rm LoTR}=A,\\;B_{\\rm LoTR}=\\mathcal B,\\;G_{(l,i)}^\\top=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:])\\big)","note":"LoTR with one stack per matrix type and the same frames for every type; diagonal cores."},{"source":"lorta","target":"metatt","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(G_1=A,\\;G_2[l]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]),\\;G_3[i]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_M[i,:]),\\;G_H[k]=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_H[k,:]),\\;G_4=B^\\top\\big)","note":"MetaTT-5D (head mode) with all TT ranks r. Diagonal cores commute, so the mode order does not matter. The catalog records this pair only as 'competes-with', a literature relation; expressively LoRTA is a sub-object of MetaTT-5D."},{"source":"lorta","target":"fact","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(U=\\mathcal B,\\;V=A,\\;\\Sigma_{(l,i)}=\\mathrm{Diag}(C_L[l,:]\\odot C_M[i,:])\\big)","note":"FacT-TT restricted to the attention blocks (the other blocks frozen at \\(\\Sigma=0\\), an A2 sub-object of FacT)."},{"source":"metatt","target":"lotr","rule":"A3","h":"h(G_1,\\{G_2[l]\\},\\{G_3[m]\\},G_4)=\\big(A=G_1,\\;B=G_4^\\top,\\;G_{(l,m)}=G_2[l]G_3[m]\\big)\\ \\text{per type }m","note":"LoTR applied to every adapted type, one stack per type, all with the same frames. MetaTT's \\(G_1=0\\) pointing lands on LoTR pointed at \\(A=0\\) (not LoTR's G = 0 default). This contradicts the catalog's 'neither contains the other' when both adapt the same matrices."},{"source":"metatt","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(G_4^\\top G_3[m]^\\top G_2[l]^\\top,\\;G_1^\\top\\big)_{(l,m)}","note":"Per-box \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\) pointed at \\((B_0\\ne0,A_0=0)\\), stratum \\(T_B\\)."},{"source":"mora","target":"krona","rule":"A2","h":"M\\mapsto(A_k,B_k)=(I_{d/\\hat r},\\ M/s)","note":"Reshape variant only; KronA re-pointed at (I, 0) in its zero-factor stratum (its default pointing has a Kaiming-random non-zero factor)."},{"source":"melora","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"(B_i,A_i)_i\\mapsto\\big(\\mathrm{blockdiag}(B_1,\\dots,B_n),\\ \\mathrm{blockdiag}(A_1,\\dots,A_n)\\big)","note":"LoRA_r pointed at (0, blockdiag(A_{i,0})), a T_A point; off-block entries frozen at their base value 0."},{"source":"melora","target":"gralora","rule":"A2","h":"(B_i,A_i)_i\\mapsto\\big(B_{ii}=B_i,\\ A_{ii}=A_i^\\top;\\ (B_{ij},A_{ij})=(0,A_{ij,0})\\ (i\\neq j)\\big)","note":"GraLoRA with k = n blocks per side of rank r/k; off-diagonal blocks frozen at their base values, scales matched."},{"source":"more","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"(L,R)\\mapsto(P_1LP_2,\\ R)","note":"LoRA_{N r_blk} pointed at (0, R_0) with R_0 block-diagonal (taking L as the zero factor); entries outside the Monarch patterns frozen at 0."},{"source":"dsee","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V,S)\\mapsto\\big([\\,U\\mid E_I\\,\\mathrm{diag}(S_\\Omega)\\,],\\ [\\,V;\\ E_J^\\top\\,]\\big),\\quad E_I=[e_{i_1}\\cdots e_{i_c}],\\ E_J=[e_{j_1}\\cdots e_{j_c}]","note":"Into LoRA_{r+c} pointed at (0, [V_0; E_J^T]) (Algorithm-2 convention U_0 = 0): the sparse part is a sum of c coordinate rank-ones. LoRA's scale s is absorbed into the factors. [V_0; E_J^T] has full row rank only when the column indices of Omega are distinct; otherwise the pointing lies outside the three strata."},{"source":"boft","target":"boft","rule":"A2","h":"(Q_1,\\dots,Q_{m'},s)\\mapsto(Q_1,\\dots,Q_{m'},0,s)","note":"BOFT with m' butterfly factors into m'+1 factors of the same block size, the extra factor frozen at its base value R = I."},{"source":"deft","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(P,R)\\mapsto\\big(P,\\ R-\\mathrm{relu}(P)^\\top W_0\\big)\\quad(\\text{QR: }(Q(P),\\ R-Q(P)^\\top W_0))","note":"LoRA_r (scale absorbed) pointed at (P_0, 0), the T_B stratum; for the relu default an isomorphism of germs (relu is smooth away from zero entries of P_0)."},{"source":"delora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\Big(\\tfrac{\\lambda}{r}\\,B\\,\\mathrm{diag}\\big(1/\\max(\\|b_i\\|,\\varepsilon)\\big),\\ \\mathrm{diag}\\big(1/\\|a_i\\|\\big)\\,A\\,D_w\\Big)","note":"LoRA_r (scale 1) pointed at (0, diag(1/||a_{i,0}||) A_0 D_w), a T_A point; D_w = diagonal of the column norms of W_0, epsilon = 1e-4. h is smooth near q_0, where ||b_i|| < epsilon, and away from ||b_i|| = epsilon. It is a germ arrow, as HF's clamped rho itself is."},{"source":"delora","target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\big(B',A',\\ \\mu=(\\|(W_0+B'A')_{i,:}\\|)_i\\big),\\ (B',A')\\ \\text{as in the LoRA arrow}","note":"Composite of DeLoRA -> LoRA with LoRA -> DoRA (magnitudes set to the current row norms); DoRA pointed at A_0' = diag(1/||a_{i,0}||) A_0 D_w."},{"source":"hra","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"u\\mapsto\\big(W_0B(u),\\ A(u)\\big),\\quad B(u)=\\big[-2H_{u_1}\\cdots H_{u_{i-1}}\\hat u_i\\big]_{i=1}^r,\\ A(u)=\\big[\\hat u_1^\\top;\\dots;\\hat u_r^\\top\\big]","note":"Telescoping R - I = sum_i H_1...H_{i-1}(H_i - I); LoRA_r pointed at h(q_0), where B_0 A_0 = 0 with both factors non-zero (outside the three strata)."},{"source":"ia3","target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"\\ell\\mapsto(\\ell-\\mathbf 1,\\ \\mathbf 1^\\top)\\ \\ (k,v);\\qquad \\ell\\mapsto(\\mathbf 1,\\ (\\ell-\\mathbf 1)^\\top)\\ \\ (W_{\\rm down})","note":"HiRA_1 pointed at (0, 1^T) resp. (1, 0), not HiRA's random-A default pointing (Prop II.9(b))."},{"source":"ia3","target":"dora","rule":"A2","h":"\\ell\\mapsto\\big(B=0,\\ A=A_0,\\ \\mu=\\ell\\odot(\\|w_{0,i}\\|)_i\\big)","note":"On the output-scaled k, v boxes only: DoRA with B, A frozen at their base values is the output-scaling method; on W_down (IA)^3 scales the input side, which DoRA's magnitude cannot."},{"source":"ln-tuning","target":"ssf","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(\\gamma'=\\gamma\\oslash\\gamma_0,\\ \\beta'=\\beta-(\\gamma\\oslash\\gamma_0)\\odot\\beta_0\\big)\\ \\text{on SSF's post-norm wrappers; other wrappers at }(\\mathbf 1,0)","note":"Requires gamma_0 without zero entries and an SSF that wraps every norm that LN-tuning trains; SSF pointed at its exact identity (its official N(1, 0.02) init is a small defect). rho_SSF o h = rho_LN holds in Theta when SSF's post-norm scale-shift is realised by folding into that norm's (gamma, beta). If it is folded into the next linear box instead, the identity holds only at function level."},{"source":"oft","target":"boft","rule":"A2","h":"Q\\mapsto(Q,\\ s=\\mathbf 1)\\ \\text{in BOFT}(1,b)","note":"BOFT with one butterfly factor and boft_s frozen at its base value 1."},{"source":"oft","target":"goft","rule":"A3","h":"t\\mapsto\\big(\\theta^{(1)}_k=2\\arctan t_k,\\ \\theta^{(\\ell)}=0\\ (\\ell\\ge2)\\big)","note":"Block size b = 2 only: the 2x2 Cayley blocks are GOFT's first Givens layer on the pairs (2k, 2k+1) (up to GOFT's sign convention)."},{"source":"oftv2","target":"qgoft","rule":"A3","h":"t\\mapsto\\Big(\\tilde G^{(1)}_k=(1-t_k^2)\\begin{pmatrix}1-t_k^2&-2t_k\\\\2t_k&1-t_k^2\\end{pmatrix},\\ \\tilde G^{(\\ell)}=I\\ (\\ell\\ge2)\\Big)","note":"Block size b = 2 only: a 2x2 Cayley-Neumann block is a scaled rotation in CO^+(2), i.e. a quasi-Givens block of qGOFT's first layer."},{"source":"osf","target":"milora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,\\Sigma,V)\\mapsto\\big(U\\,\\Sigma\\,S_{lo}^{-1/2},\\ S_{lo}^{1/2}V^\\top\\big)","note":"MiLoRA_k with k = min(m,n) - effective_rank, pointed at (U_lo S_lo^{1/2}, S_lo^{1/2} V_lo^T); holds with or without the gradient projection."},{"source":"psoft","target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"(Q,\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\Sigma_r\\big(D_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha-I\\big)\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"The LoRA-XS core R in the frames B_0 = U_r, A_0 = Sigma_r V_r^T (up to its transpose convention), pointed at R = 0."},{"source":"psoft","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(Q,\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(U_r\\Sigma_rD_\\beta C(Q)^\\top D_\\alpha\\Sigma_r^{-1/2},\\ \\Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^\\top\\big)","note":"PiSSA_r at its own point (U_r Sigma_r^{1/2}, Sigma_r^{1/2} V_r^T): the composite of the LoRA-XS arrow with LoRA-XS -> PiSSA."},{"source":"dora","target":"bidora","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same parametrisation (scale absorbed): an isomorphism; BiDoRA differs only in its bi-level training."},{"source":"dora","target":"glora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\mu)\\mapsto\\big(B_A=c-\\mathbf 1,\\ A_A=\\mathbf 1^\\top;\\ B_B=\\mathrm{diag}(c)\\,sB,\\ A_B=A\\big),\\quad c=\\mu\\oslash\\big(\\|(W_0+sBA)_{i,:}\\|\\big)_i","note":"GLoRA (A path rank >= 1, B path rank r) re-pointed at (0, 1^T; 0, A_0), since diag(c)(W_0 + sBA) = W_0 + W_0 (.) ((c - 1) 1^T) + diag(c) sBA."},{"source":"goft","target":"qgoft","rule":"A3","h":"\\theta\\mapsto\\Big(\\tilde G_t=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta_t&-\\sin\\theta_t\\\\ \\sin\\theta_t&\\cos\\theta_t\\end{pmatrix}\\Big)_t","note":"Each Givens rotation is a particular quasi-Givens block; identity at theta = 0."},{"source":"goft","target":"boft","rule":"A3","h":"\\theta\\mapsto\\big(t=\\tan(\\theta/2)\\ \\text{on the kept }2\\times2\\text{ blocks},\\ 0\\ \\text{on the others},\\ s=\\mathbf 1\\big)","note":"BOFT(log2 d, 2) with the non-GOFT rotations and boft_s frozen at their base values; up to factor-ordering and permutation conventions; a germ arrow (Cayley excludes the angle pi)."},{"source":"ether-plus","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"q\\mapsto\\Big(\\big[\\,[\\hat{\\tilde v}_j,-\\hat{\\tilde u}_j]_j,\\ W_0[\\hat v_j,-\\hat u_j]_j\\,\\big],\\ \\big[\\,[\\hat{\\tilde v}_j,\\hat{\\tilde u}_j]_j^\\top W_0H^+;\\ [\\hat v_j,\\hat u_j]_j^\\top\\,\\big]\\Big)","note":"From Delta W = (H~+ - I) W_0 H+ + W_0 (H+ - I); LoRA of rank 2(#left blocks + #right blocks), pointed at h(q_0) with B_0 A_0 = 0."},{"source":"spectral-adapter-r","target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"(Q_U,Q_V)\\mapsto C(Q_U)\\,S_1\\,C(Q_V)^\\top S_1^{-1}-I","note":"LoRA-XS core in the frames (U_1, S_1 V_1^T), pointed at 0."},{"source":"spectral-adapter-r","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(Q_U,Q_V)\\mapsto\\big(U_1C(Q_U)S_1C(Q_V)^\\top S_1^{-1/2},\\ S_1^{1/2}V_1^\\top\\big)","note":"PiSSA_r at its own point (U_1 S_1^{1/2}, S_1^{1/2} V_1^T)."},{"source":"spectral-adapter-a","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(A_U,A_V)\\mapsto\\big((U_1+A_U)S_1^{1/2},\\ S_1^{1/2}(V_1+A_V)^\\top\\big)","note":"A pointed isomorphism (affine and invertible)."},{"source":"svdiff","target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta\\mapsto\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta)-\\sigma","note":"SVFT with the plain (diagonal) pattern, pointed at M = 0 (SVFT's official init is a small defect); piecewise smooth, smooth near delta = 0."},{"source":"svdiff","target":"svf","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta\\mapsto\\mathrm{ReLU}(\\sigma+\\delta)\\oslash\\sigma","note":"SVF in the paper's form U diag(sigma (.) z) V^T, pointed at z = 1."},{"source":"svf","target":"svft","rule":"A3","h":"z\\mapsto\\sigma\\odot(z-\\mathbf 1)","note":"A pointed isomorphism onto diagonal SVFT (pointed at M = 0)."},{"source":"bidora","target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Same parametrisation: an isomorphism."},{"source":"ssf","target":"glora","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big(B_A=\\gamma-\\mathbf 1,\\ A_A=\\mathbf 1^\\top;\\ D=\\gamma-\\mathbf 1;\\ E=\\beta\\big)\\ \\text{(other paths at zero)}","note":"Post-linear wrappers only; GLoRA with D, E in vector mode (not the PEFT default), re-pointed at A_A = 1^T."},{"source":"red","target":"ssf","rule":"A2","h":"(\\ell_s,\\ell_b)\\mapsto\\big((\\gamma,\\beta)_{\\rm FFN\\ out}=(\\ell_s,\\ell_b);\\ \\text{all other SSF wrappers at }(\\mathbf 1,0)\\big)","note":"SSF pointed at its exact identity."},{"source":"liera","target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B,A)","note":"Isomorphism: the first-order LieRA map \\(W_0\\odot(J+BA)\\) is HiRA's \\(\\rho\\), with the same pointing \\(B_0=0\\)."},{"source":"liera","target":"flora-batched","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\mathbf 1_m\\;B],\\,[\\mathbf 1_n^\\top;A]\\big)","note":"\\(W_0\\odot(J+BA)=W_0\\odot([\\mathbf 1\\,B][\\mathbf 1^\\top;A])\\): lands in FLoRA\\(_{r+1}\\) pointed at \\(([\\mathbf 1,0],[\\mathbf 1^\\top;A_0])\\), a factorisation of \\(J\\). FLoRA's \\(1/(r+1)\\) mean reduction is absorbed by scaling both factors by \\(\\sqrt{r+1}\\)."},{"source":"liera","target":"loha","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=(B,A,U_0,V_0^\\top),\\quad U_0V_0^\\top=W_0","note":"HiRA/LieRA is LoHa with its second Hadamard factor frozen at a rank-\\(\\operatorname{rk}W_0\\) factorisation of \\(W_0\\): \\(W_0+(BA)\\odot W_0\\). Lands in LoHa\\(_{r,\\operatorname{rk}W_0}\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0,U_0,V_0^\\top)\\), not HF's default pointing."},{"source":"flora-batched","target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\tfrac1rB\\;\\,{-\\mathbf 1_m}],\\,[A;\\mathbf 1_n^\\top]\\big)","note":"With the paper's mean reduction \\(\\rho(B,A)=W_0\\odot\\tfrac1rBA\\): \\(W_0\\odot\\tfrac1rBA=W_0\\odot(J+[\\tfrac1rB,-\\mathbf 1][A;\\mathbf 1^\\top])\\), so it lands in HiRA\\(_{r+1}\\) pointed at \\(([\\tfrac1rB_0,-\\mathbf 1],[A_0;\\mathbf 1^\\top])\\), whose product \\(\\tfrac1rB_0A_0-J\\) vanishes (drop the \\(\\tfrac1r\\) for a plain sum). This is not HiRA's default \\(B=0\\) pointing."},{"source":"residual-adapter","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha,D_1,D_2)=D_2(I+\\alpha D_1)-I\\ \\ \\text{as the trainable }B,\\ \\text{with }A_0=W_0,\\ r=C","note":"\\(GW_0=W_0+(G-I)W_0\\), so it is LoRA-FA with frozen \\(A_0=W_0\\) (unfolded, \\(C\\times9C\\)), pointed at \\(B=0\\). Here \\(W_0\\) is the base conv with the base BN affine maps folded in (so \\(D_1^0=D_2^0=I\\) and \\(h(q_0)=0\\)); the merged BN shift is a bias change, so the exact target is LoRA-FA\\((W_0)\\oplus\\) BitFit (A5). Composing with LoRA-FA\\((A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA (A2) gives residual adapter \\(\\to\\) LoRA\\(_C\\)."},{"source":"adapterdrop","target":"pfeiffer-adapter","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_{>n})=\\big((U_l{=}0,\\,D_l{=}D_l^0)_{l\\le n},\\ q_{>n}\\big)","note":"Specialised AdapterDrop is the Pfeiffer adapter with the bottom-\\(n\\) adapters frozen at the neutral point; pointed, since all-neutral maps to all-neutral."},{"source":"adapterdrop","target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_{>n})=\\big((U_l{=}0,\\,D_l{=}D_l^0)_{l\\le n},\\ q_{>n}\\big)","note":"The same freezing when AdapterDrop is applied to Houlsby adapters (two per layer)."},{"source":"parallel-adapter","target":"adaptformer","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_{down},b_{down},W_{up},b_{up})=\\big(W_{down},b_{down},\\tfrac{s}{0.1}W_{up},\\tfrac{s}{0.1}b_{up}\\big)","note":"An isomorphism: the same ReLU bottleneck on the FFN input, differing only in the fixed scale (\\(s\\) against AdaptFormer's \\(0.1\\))."},{"source":"adamix","target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((W^{down}_k)_{k\\le M},\\,W^{up}\\big)=\\big(\\tfrac1M\\textstyle\\sum_kW^{down}_k,\\ W^{up}\\big)","note":"The inference collapse is a linear pointed map into one Houlsby adapter; experts at the neutral point (\\(W^{up}=0\\)) go to the neutral point. Average the \\(W^{up}_j\\) too when they are not shared."},{"source":"llama-adapter","target":"llama-adapter-v2","rule":"A2","h":"h(P,g)=\\big(P,\\,g,\\ s=\\mathbf 1,\\ b=0,\\ \\gamma=\\gamma_0,\\ \\text{visual branch at its zero gate}\\big)","note":"V1 is V2 with the bias/scale, norm and early visual-fusion parameters frozen at their neutral base values. This assumes V2 keeps V1's zero-gated adaption prompts in the last \\(L'\\) layers."},{"source":"adaptformer","target":"parallel-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_{down},b_{down},W_{up},b_{up})=\\big(W_{down},b_{down},\\tfrac{0.1}{s}W_{up},\\tfrac{0.1}{s}b_{up}\\big)","note":"An isomorphism: the same ReLU bottleneck reading the MLP input \\(\\mathrm{LN}(x')\\); only the fixed scale differs."},{"source":"repadapter","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(W_u,W_d)=\\big((sW_0^{(x)}W_u,\\ W_d)\\big)_{x\\in\\{q,k,v\\}}","note":"The merged update \\(\\Delta W^{(x)}=sW_0^{(x)}W_uW_d\\) is a point of LoRA\\(_r^{\\times3}\\) pointed at \\((0,W_d^0)\\) (\\(\\mathrm T_A\\) stratum); the bias shift needs \\(\\oplus\\) BitFit (A5). With a shared \\(A\\) and tied \\(B\\) it is a constrained LoRA, not an equivalent one."},{"source":"cartridges","target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(Z)=\\big([z^{(l)}_{\\mathrm{sink}};Z_k^{(l)}],\\,[z^{(l)}_{v,\\mathrm{sink}};Z_v^{(l)}]\\big)_{l\\le L}","note":"Freezing the sink slot at its base value; prefix tuning pointed at the corpus KV cache. Pure A2 in the direct parameterisation; with Li–Liang's MLP compose with the section \\(W_2\\mapsto W_2^0+H_0^{+}(\\cdot)\\) of Obs III.2 (hidden ≥ p). Arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation."},{"source":"cpt","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(E_{\\mathrm{ctx}})=\\big(E_{\\mathrm{ctx}},\\,E^{0}_{\\mathrm{label}}\\big)\\quad(\\|E_{\\mathrm{ctx}}-E^0_{\\mathrm{ctx}}\\|\\le\\varepsilon)","note":"Label-token coordinates frozen at their embeddings and the domain restricted to the ε-ball; prompt tuning over the whole demonstration context, pointed at the demonstration embeddings."},{"source":"multitask-prompt-tuning","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P^*,u,v)=P^*\\odot uv^\\top","note":"Prompt tuning pointed at \\(P^*_0\\odot u_0v_0^\\top\\); h is the quotient by the torus gauge."},{"source":"p-tuning","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\psi)=f_\\psi(P)","note":"All-front template (HF PromptEncoder); prompt tuning pointed at \\(f_{\\psi_0}(P_0)\\). With interleaved anchors the same h lands in prompt tuning at the template positions."},{"source":"prefix-tuning","target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(P',\\theta)=\\big(\\mathrm{MLP}_\\theta(P'),\\,W^{0}_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\big)","note":"Obs III.2: the MLP is the 2-cell \\(\\sigma\\); P-tuning v2 taken with its head frozen at the LM head/verbalizer (A2) and pointed at \\(\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\theta_0}(P'_0)\\)."},{"source":"prefix-tuning","target":"initial-state-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big(x^{(l)}_{-\\ell..-1}\\big)=\\Big(\\textstyle\\sum_{i=1}^{\\ell}\\bar A_l^{\\,i-1}\\bar B_l\\,x^{(l)}_{-i}\\Big)_{l}","note":"On S4 (LTI) SSM layers only (Galim et al., Prop 1): a per-layer prefix acts solely through the state it leaves; same causality argument as Thm VI.5(e). Initial-state tuning pointed at h(q0)."},{"source":"prefix-tuning","target":"mam-adapter","rule":"A5","h":"h(P',\\theta)=\\big(P',\\,\\theta,\\,W_{\\mathrm{up}}=0,\\,W^{0}_{\\mathrm{down}}\\big)","note":"MAM = prefix ⊕ scaled parallel adapter, with its prefix trained through the same Li–Liang MLP (He et al.), so h is the identity on (P',θ) and puts the adapter summand at its neutral point W_up=0. MAM pointed at (P'_0,θ_0,0,W^0_down)."},{"source":"prompt-tuning","target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(p)=\\big((K_j(p),V_j(p))_{j\\le L},\\,W^0_{\\mathrm{cls}}\\big)","note":"Thm VI.5(e): causal decoder, prompt first, matched position offsets (or relative positions); P-tuning v2's head frozen at the verbalizer. Fails in bidirectional encoders of depth ≥ 2."},{"source":"prompt-tuning","target":"p-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\big(P'_0,\\;\\psi_0\\ \\text{with}\\ W_2\\mapsto W_2^0+H_0^{+}(P-P_0)\\big)","note":"Section of the encoder through its last linear layer (H_0 = penultimate features at q0, full row rank when hidden ≥ ℓ, cf. Obs III.2); a shift of the shared output bias would not do, since the encoder acts row-wise."},{"source":"prompt-tuning","target":"multitask-prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\big(P\\oslash u_0v_0^\\top,\\,u_0,\\,v_0\\big)","note":"Section of the Hadamard torus bundle, valid when u_0,v_0 have no zero entries; MPT pointed at h(P_0)."},{"source":"prompt-tuning","target":"attempt","rule":"A3","h":"h(P)=\\Big(P_{\\mathrm{tgt}}=\\tfrac{(t+1)P-\\sum_{j\\le t}P_j}{t+2},\\ \\gamma=0,\\ \\beta=0,\\ W^0_{\\mathrm{down}},\\ W^0_{\\mathrm{up}}\\Big)","note":"Router LayerNorm gain and bias set to 0, so H_out=0 and the mixture weights are uniform, a_j=1/(t+1); then P_inst=((t+2)P_tgt+Σ_{j≤t}P_j)/(t+1)=P for every input. ATTEMPT pointed at h(P_0) (zero router gain), an arrow of the unpointed slice; with the O1 obstruction in ATTEMPT's entry the inclusion is strict."},{"source":"ppt","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Isomorphism onto prompt tuning pointed at \\(P^\\star\\)."},{"source":"spot","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Isomorphism onto prompt tuning pointed at \\(P_{s^\\star}\\)."},{"source":"residual-prompt-tuning","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,W_{\\rm down},W_{\\rm up},\\mathrm{LN})=\\mathrm{LN}\\big(\\mathrm{ReLU}(PW_{\\rm down})W_{\\rm up}\\big)+P","note":"The reparametrisation itself; prompt tuning pointed at \\(\\Phi_0(P_0)\\)."},{"source":"xprompt","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(P_S)=\\big(P_S,\\,0_{S^c}\\big)","note":"Pruned tokens/pieces frozen at 0 (XPrompt's γ·P keeps them as zero vectors); prompt tuning pointed at the rewound masked prompt. If pruned tokens are instead dropped from the sequence, the same A2 arrow lands in prompt tuning of the kept length, with only the pruned pieces frozen at 0."},{"source":"black-box-tuning","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(z)=Az+p_0","note":"Prompt tuning pointed at p_0; a monomorphism (A injective)."},{"source":"vpt","target":"p-tuning-v2","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\mathrm{Head})=\\Big(\\big(\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_k^{(i)}+\\mathbf 1b_k^{(i)\\top},\\,\\mathrm{LN}(P_{i-1})W_v^{(i)}+\\mathbf 1b_v^{(i)\\top}\\big)_{i\\le L},\\,\\mathrm{Head}\\Big)","note":"VPT-Deep: prompt outputs are discarded each layer, so image tokens see them only as extra keys/values; deep prompts with a linear head, pointed at h(q0)."},{"source":"coop","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^{\\ell\\times d}}","note":"Same parameterisation on the CLIP text tower (context in front of the class tokens), shared across the K class sequences."},{"source":"coop","target":"cocoop","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=\\big(V,\\ \\theta\\ \\text{with}\\ W_2=0,\\ b_2=0\\big)","note":"Meta-net output layer frozen at 0, so h_θ(f(x))=0; CoCoOp pointed at that zero-output meta-net."},{"source":"initial-state-tuning","target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(h')=\\big(\\mathcal C_{\\ell,l}^{+}\\,h'_l\\big)_l,\\quad \\mathcal C_{\\ell,l}=[\\bar B_l,\\bar A_l\\bar B_l,\\dots,\\bar A_l^{\\ell-1}\\bar B_l]","note":"S4 layers, per-layer virtual tokens of length ℓ ≥ H, controllable pair (Galim et al., converse of Prop 1); h(0)=0, and the zero prefix is neutral on S4."},{"source":"beft","target":"bitfit","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Delta b_v)=\\big(\\Delta b_v,\\,0_{\\text{other biases}}\\big)","note":"Freezing every other bias at its pretrained value."},{"source":"super-tuning","target":"shira","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"PEFT magnitude mode with select_top=True is SHiRA with its weight-magnitude (WM) mask, top-|W_0|; isomorphism."},{"source":"super-tuning","target":"pafi","rule":"A5","h":"h(U)=\\big(U,\\,\\gamma_0,\\,\\beta_0\\big)","note":"BottomK magnitude mask = PaFi's support; PaFi also trains norm parameters, which sit at their base values."},{"source":"trainable-tokens","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(S_T,\\,X),\\quad X=V_T-E_{0,T}","note":"Freezing B at the selector: LoRA_{|T|} on the embedding pointed at (S_T,0), the T_B stratum (cf. MiCA → LoRA pointed at (B_0,0))."},{"source":"trainable-tokens","target":"shira","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"SHiRA with the support T × [d] (whole rows); the mask methods form one family indexed by support."},{"source":"bitfit","target":"ssf","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\Delta b)=\\big(\\gamma=\\mathbf 1,\\ \\beta=\\Delta b\\big)","note":"With SSF inserted after every biased linear op and LayerNorm: freezing the scales at 1 leaves shifts that are exactly bias edits; SSF pointed at its exact identity (1,0) (official init is ≈)."},{"source":"fish-mask","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"Freeze Diff pruning's gates at their clamped init value \\(z=\\mathbf 1\\) and \\(w\\) at 0 off \\(M\\): FISH\\((M)\\) is the stratum \\(M\\) of the learned-support cone (within the L0 budget when \\(|M|\\le k\\))."},{"source":"fish-mask","target":"rosa","rule":"A5","h":"x\\mapsto\\big((0,A_0),\\ x\\big)","note":"With RoSA's sparse support equal to \\(M\\), FISH is the sparse summand of LoRA \\(\\oplus\\) sparse, the LoRA part held at its base value."},{"source":"child-tuning","target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}_{\\mathbb R^M}","note":"Isomorphism for a common support \\(M\\); Child-Tuning\\(_D\\) uses the empirical Fisher with gold labels and FISH samples \\(y\\sim p_\\theta\\), so the selected masks can differ."},{"source":"child-tuning","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The fixed mask is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"lt-sft","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The lottery-ticket support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"spt","target":"rosa","rule":"A2","h":"\\big((B_s,A_s)_{s\\in S},\\,(x_u)_{u\\in U}\\big)\\mapsto\\big((B_s,A_s)_{s\\in S},\\,(0,A_{0,u})_{u\\in U};\\ \\iota(x)\\big)","note":"RoSA on all of SPT's boxes with SPT's masks as its sparse support: freeze RoSA's LoRA at its base value on the boxes where SPT uses masks."},{"source":"pafi","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The bottom-\\(k\\) support (and LN coordinates) is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"smt","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The tile support is one fixed coordinate set, a stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"spiel","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"(\\eta,\\phi)\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ \\mathrm{scatter}(\\eta,\\phi)\\big)","note":"Componentwise on each support \\(\\eta\\): every per-tensor stratum is a stratum of the global \\(k\\)-sparse union with \\(k=\\sum d_\\phi\\)."},{"source":"gps","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The row-balanced support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"s2ft","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"V\\mapsto s^{-1}V\\quad(A_0:=U_S^\\top)","note":"Isomorphism S\\(^2\\)FT \\(\\cong\\) LoRA-FA\\((A_0=U_S^\\top)\\) of rank \\(|S|\\); \\(s\\) is LoRA-FA's scale."},{"source":"s2ft","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"V\\mapsto(s^{-1}V,\\ U_S^\\top)","note":"LoRA pointed at \\((0,U_S^\\top)\\), the same stratum as the default pointing \\((0,A_0)\\) (Thm II.2)."},{"source":"s2ft","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The \\(s\\) selected columns form one fixed coordinate set."},{"source":"sift","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The first-gradient support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"hift","target":"surgical-fine-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}_{\\Theta_{G_j}}","note":"Per stage: stage \\(j\\) of HiFT is the forward linear method of surgical FT on the block \\(G_j\\)."},{"source":"surgical-fine-tuning","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The block's coordinates form one fixed support; inside the \\(k\\)-sparse union only when \\(|\\Theta_{B_j}|\\le k\\)."},{"source":"surgical-fine-tuning","target":"hift","rule":"A5","h":"q\\mapsto(\\text{stage } j: q;\\ \\text{other stages}: 0)","note":"When \\(B_j\\) is a HiFT group, the one-stage scheme embeds as stage \\(j\\) of HiFT's cycle, the other stages padded with their base value."},{"source":"piggyback","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A3","h":"M\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ -(\\mathbf 1-M)\\odot W_0\\big)","note":"\\(Q\\) read as the discrete set of binary masks; defined on masks zeroing at most \\(k\\) entries (all masks for the unbudgeted relaxation), pointed since \\(h(\\mathbf 1)=(\\alpha_0,0)\\)."},{"source":"piggyback","target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"M\\mapsto(M-\\mathbf 1,\\ I_n)","note":"HiRA \\(W_0\\odot(\\mathbf 1+BA)\\) with inner rank \\(r=n\\), pointed at \\((0,I_n)\\) (for \\(r=n\\) every \\(B_0=0\\), full-rank-\\(A_0\\) pointing is isomorphic to this one); both keep the zeros of \\(W_0\\). For \\(r<\\min(m,n)\\) and \\(W_0\\) without zeros there is no arrow (O1): \\(W_0\\odot M\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HiRA}_r\\) needs \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M-\\mathbf 1)\\le r\\), which a generic binary mask violates."},{"source":"sam-sparse","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_M x)","note":"The SAM support is one stratum of the learned-support cone."},{"source":"lift","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"x\\mapsto(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_{M_0}x)","note":"First interval only (mask \\(M_0\\) from \\(\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W_0)\\)); later intervals are the same kind of object re-based at \\(W_t\\)."},{"source":"qa-lora","target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{s}{s^\\prime}B,\\ AP)","note":"\\(s^\\prime\\) is QLoRA's scale. Into QLoRA's construction (LoRA\\(_r\\) over \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\)) pointed at \\((0,A_0P)\\): a \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) pointing whose row space lies in the group-constant subspace, hence not isomorphic to QLoRA's random-\\(A_0\\) pointing (Thm II.2(a)). Needs the same quantised base (GPTQ INT-\\(k\\)); NF4-QLoRA has a different base point."},{"source":"qlora","target":"ir-qlora","rule":"A2","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{s}{\\alpha}B,\\ A,\\ 0,\\ 0)","note":"\\(s\\) is QLoRA's scale, \\(\\alpha\\) IR-QLoRA's. Freeze the elastic scalars \\(\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\) at their base value 0; needs a common quantised base (ICQ's calibrated shift \\(\\tau^*\\) moves IR-QLoRA's base)."},{"source":"ir-qlora","target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A,\\beta_1,\\beta_2)\\mapsto(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{s}(B+\\beta_2E),\\ A+\\beta_1P)","note":"\\(s\\) is QLoRA's scale; pointed (\\(h(q_0)=(0,A_0)\\)); needs the same quantised base, since IR-QLoRA's base differs from NF4 by \\(\\tau^*\\)."},{"source":"peqa","target":"efficientqat","rule":"A3","h":"\\Delta s\\mapsto\\Delta s\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{n/g}^\\top","note":"In increment coordinates (\\(s=s_0+\\Delta s\\) on both sides): broadcast each channel scale to the groups of its row. Valid over common frozen codes and zero-points and row-constant initial step sizes \\(s_0\\otimes\\mathbf 1_{n/g}^\\top\\) (EfficientQAT's E2E-QP is group-wise PEQA over the Block-AP base, whose step sizes are generally not row-constant)."},{"source":"loqt","target":"galore","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto sB","note":"Per stage, LoQT-nq with \\(B_0=0\\) and the same \\(P_k\\): both are the linear method \\(W_k+P_kX\\); dynamics differ (reset versus carried-over state)."},{"source":"loqt","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"B\\mapsto(P_k,\\ \\tfrac{s}{s^\\prime}B)","note":"Per stage (LoQT-nq): freezing LoRA's left factor at \\(P_k\\), into LoRA pointed at \\((P_k,0)\\) (FB\\((P_k)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA); \\(s^\\prime\\) is LoRA's scale."},{"source":"galore","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"C\\mapsto s^{-1}C\\quad(A_0:=P_k^\\top)","note":"Per period, right-projection case (\\(d_{out}>d_{in}\\); here \\(P_k^\\top\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times d_{in}}\\) denotes the transposed top-\\(r\\) right singular vectors): GaLore's period \\(W_k+CP_k^\\top\\) is LoRA-FA\\((P_k^\\top)\\); \\(s\\) is LoRA-FA's scale."},{"source":"galore","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"C\\mapsto(P_k,\\ s^{-1}C)","note":"Left-projection case (\\(d_{out}\\le d_{in}\\)): per period FB\\((P_k)\\hookrightarrow\\) LoRA pointed at \\((P_k,0)\\); trajectories agree under Prop IV.2 (Adam, no weight decay, state carried on both sides)."},{"source":"galore-2","target":"galore","rule":"A3","h":"\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per period, isomorphic objects for a common \\(P_k\\); the randomised SVD only perturbs the frame."},{"source":"flora-opt","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"C\\mapsto s^{-1}C\\quad(A_0:=A_t)","note":"Per period: Flora's period is LoRA-FA\\((A_t)\\) pointed at \\(C=0\\)."},{"source":"flora-opt","target":"relora","rule":"A2","h":"C\\mapsto(s^{-1}C,\\ A_t)","note":"Per stage: freezing ReLoRA's \\(A\\) at its restart value; same stratum \\((0,A)\\) as ReLoRA's restart pointing when \\(A_k=A_t\\)."},{"source":"online-subspace-descent","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(P_t,\\,X)","note":"Within one step at base \\(\\theta_t\\), OSD is \\(\\mathrm{FB}(P_t):X\\mapsto\\theta_t+P_tX\\), the frozen-\\(B\\) sub-object of LoRA pointed at \\((P_t,0)\\)."},{"source":"adam-mini","target":"lomo","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"Both are the terminal object with forward map \\(\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta\\). They differ only in optimizer data (Def III.1), not as objects."},{"source":"adalomo","target":"lomo","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"The same object (Full FT). AdaLomo adds a factored adaptive learning rate, which is trained-method data."},{"source":"model-soups","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=\\mathbf 1_S/|S|","note":"The soup is the point \\(\\lambda=\\mathbf 1_S/|S|\\) of task arithmetic's family. This is an arrow of the unpointed slice (task arithmetic pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."},{"source":"task-arithmetic","target":"adamerging","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\lambda_i^{l}=\\lambda_i)_{i,l}","note":"Global coefficients are the layer-constant points of AdaMerging's family (AdaMerging pointed at \\(h(0)=0\\), not at its \\(0.3\\) init)."},{"source":"task-arithmetic","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\alpha_i^{l}=\\lambda_i)_{i,l}","note":"For LoRA task vectors \\(\\tau_i=B_iA_i\\), task arithmetic is the layer-constant sub-object of CAT."},{"source":"task-arithmetic","target":"ziplora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_c,\\lambda_s)=(\\lambda_c\\mathbf 1,\\ \\lambda_s\\mathbf 1)","note":"With two LoRA updates, scalar weights are constant merger vectors (ZipLoRA pointed at \\(m=0\\), not at its all-ones init)."},{"source":"adamerging","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ \\lambda_i^{l}\\mapsto\\alpha_i^{l}","note":"When \\(\\tau_i^l=B_i^lA_i^l\\), layer-wise AdaMerging and CAT are the same object (CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\)). They differ in loss and initialisation."},{"source":"knots","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\lambda","note":"With TA as the inner rule, KnOTS and task arithmetic have the same coefficient family."},{"source":"pem-arithmetic","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=(\\lambda,\\,1-\\lambda)","note":"(IA)\\(^3\\) modules only: \\(\\ell\\mapsto\\lambda\\ell_1+(1-\\lambda)\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell\\mapsto2-\\ell\\) are task arithmetic on the updates \\(\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)W_0\\). This is an arrow of the unpointed slice."},{"source":"lorahub","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(w)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_iw_iB_i,\\ \\sum_iw_iA_i\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at the origin \\((0,0)\\), the degenerate pointing with \\(S_1=0\\). There is no pointed arrow into the standard pointing \\((0,A_0)\\)."},{"source":"lora-soups","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\big([\\alpha_1B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\alpha_NB_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Per layer, into LoRA\\(_{\\sum r_i}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1;\\dots;A_N])\\). The pointing is standard iff the stacked \\(A\\) has full row rank (Obs I.7)."},{"source":"lora-soups","target":"adamerging","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ \\alpha_i^{l}\\mapsto\\lambda_i^{l}","note":"The same object as layer-wise AdaMerging on LoRA task vectors (AdaMerging pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\))."},{"source":"lora-lego","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=N^{-1/2}\\mathbf 1","note":"Only at \\(k=\\sum_ir_i=Nr\\), where clusters are singletons: the output \\(N^{-1/2}\\sum_iB_iA_i\\) is a point of CAT's family (CAT pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."},{"source":"ziplora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(m_c,m_s)=\\big([B_c\\ B_s],\\ [A_c\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_c);\\ A_s\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m_s)]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_{r_c+r_s}\\) pointed at \\(h(\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 1)\\), with the same defect. Only the \\(A\\)-side moves, so the image lands in the frozen-\\(B\\) sub-object."},{"source":"mix-of-show","target":"regmean","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=(\\alpha=1)","note":"Read RegMean as its shrinkage family \\(\\alpha\\mapsto W^\\star(\\alpha)\\) over fixed Gram matrices. Mix-of-Show's fused weight is its point \\(\\alpha=1\\), given the same Gram matrices (Mix-of-Show obtains them by sampling). This is an arrow of the unpointed slice. It is not A2, because \\(\\alpha=1\\) is not RegMean's default \\(0.9\\)."},{"source":"orthogonal-adaptation","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"It is LoRA-FA\\((A_i)\\), with \\(A_i\\) an orthonormal slice of a shared random basis."},{"source":"orthogonal-adaptation","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\,A_i)","note":"The frozen-\\(A\\) sub-object of LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_i)\\)."},{"source":"multi-lora-composition","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=w_{i(t)}\\,e_{i(t)}","note":"Switch only, fibrewise over steps: each step's weight is a point of CAT's family (CAT pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\)). Composite has no weight-space fibre."},{"source":"polytropon","target":"mhr","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,B,Z)=(A,\\,B,\\,Z\\otimes\\mathbf 1_h)","note":"Copy the routing logits to every head (MHR pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); its heads are initialised independently)."},{"source":"polytropon","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(A,B,Z)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}B_j,\\ \\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}A_j\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau j}A_j^0)\\)."},{"source":"mhr","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(A,B,Z)=(B^\\tau,\\,A^\\tau)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, with \\(A^\\tau=\\mathrm{concat}_k\\sum_j\\alpha_{\\tau jk}A_j[k]\\) and likewise \\(B^\\tau\\). The target is LoRA pointed at \\((0,A^\\tau_0)\\)."},{"source":"smear","target":"houlsby-adapter","rule":"A3","h":"h_v(\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_N,W_r)=\\textstyle\\sum_iR(v)_i\\theta_i","note":"Fibrewise over inputs: each example runs one bottleneck adapter (the target is pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), a defect)."},{"source":"mov","target":"ia3","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_N,W_g)=\\textstyle\\sum_is_i(x)\\ell_i","note":"Fibrewise over tokens and pointed: \\(h_x(q_0)=\\mathbf 1\\)."},{"source":"mov","target":"loramoe","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_i,A_i,W_g)=(\\tfrac r\\alpha B_i,\\,A_i,\\,W_g^\\top)\\ \\text{(MoLoRA variant)}","note":"MoLoRA's \\(\\Delta W(x)=\\sum_is_i(x)B_iA_i\\) is LoRAMoE's routed family. \\(h\\) absorbs LoRAMoE's scale \\(\\alpha/r\\) and its transposed router convention, so it is the inverse of LoRAMoE's arrow to MoV."},{"source":"loramoe","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,W_g)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r[\\omega_1(x)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\omega_N(x)B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_N^0])\\)."},{"source":"loramoe","target":"mov","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_i,A_i,W_g)=(\\tfrac\\alpha rB_i,\\,A_i,\\,W_g^\\top)","note":"Isomorphic to MoLoRA's routed family."},{"source":"moelora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\tau(B,A,e,W_T)=\\big([\\omega_{\\tau1}B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\omega_{\\tau N}B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_N^0])\\)."},{"source":"x-lora","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\phi)=\\big(\\alpha_i^{l}=\\lambda_i^{l}(x;\\phi)\\,\\alpha_i/r_i\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens: each token's weights are a point of CAT's family. This is an arrow of the unpointed slice (CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\))."},{"source":"hydralora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(A,B_1,\\dots,B_N,W_g)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_i\\omega_i(x)B_i,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\). The arrow is pointed, since every \\(B_i\\) starts at \\(0\\)."},{"source":"hydralora","target":"loramoe","rule":"A3","h":"h(A,(B_i)_i,W_g)=\\big((\\tfrac r\\alpha B_i)_i,\\ (A,\\dots,A),\\ W_g\\big)","note":"The tied embedding \\(A_i=A\\): HydraLoRA is the shared-\\(A\\) sub-family of LoRAMoE (LoRAMoE pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), whose \\(A_i\\) coincide)."},{"source":"phatgoose","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(v)=\\big([w_{x,1}(v)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ w_{x,N}(v)B_N],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_N]\\big),\\ \\ w_{x,z}=0\\ \\text{off the top-}k","note":"Fibrewise over tokens (per module), into LoRA\\(_{Nr}\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice); \\(h_x\\) is smooth away from routing ties."},{"source":"arrow","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\textstyle\\sum_ip_i(x)B_i,\\ \\sum_ip_i(x)A_i\\big)","note":"Paper composition, fibrewise over tokens and layers: a point of LoRA\\(_r\\) (rank \\(r\\), not \\(kr\\)), with LoRA pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"arrow","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_i=p_i(x)\\,s_i\\big)_i","note":"HF output mixing, per adapted module: a point of CAT's family (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"loraretriever","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_\\phi=\\mathbf 1[\\phi\\in\\Phi_x]/k\\big)_\\phi","note":"Mixture mode, per request: a point of CAT's family (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"loraretriever","target":"lorahub","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(\\ast)=w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Phi_x}/k","note":"Fusion mode, per request: LoraHub's \\((\\sum w_iB_i)(\\sum w_jA_j)\\) at \\(w=\\mathbf 1_{\\Phi_x}/k\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"lora-flow","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(W_{\\rm gate},b)=\\big(\\alpha_i^{l}=w_i^{l}(x)\\big)_{i,l}","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into CAT pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"s-lora","target":"punica","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\prod_uQ_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_u}}}","note":"The same family over tenants. The systems differ only in kernels and memory management, which \\(\\rho\\) does not see."},{"source":"s-lora","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_u(\\ast)=e_u","note":"Fibrewise over tenants: request \\(u\\) is CAT at the vertex \\(\\alpha=e_u\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"punica","target":"s-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_{\\prod_uQ_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_u}}}","note":"The same family over tenants; only the kernels differ."},{"source":"punica","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_u(\\ast)=(B_u,\\ A_u)","note":"Fibrewise over tenants, into LoRA\\(_{r_u}\\) pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\) (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"hyperdreambooth","target":"nola","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_{tr},A_{tr})=\\big(\\alpha=\\mathrm{vec}\\,A_{tr},\\ \\beta=\\mathrm{vec}\\,B_{tr}\\big),\\ \\text{NOLA bases }\\{p_ke_j^\\top\\},\\{e_jq_l^\\top\\}","note":"With \\(p_k\\) the columns of \\(P_{out}\\) and \\(q_l^\\top\\) the rows of \\(P_{in}\\), LiDB is NOLA with orthonormal random bases (at \\(r=1\\) the coefficients are exactly \\(A_{tr}\\) and \\(B_{tr}\\)). Pointed at the origin."},{"source":"hyperdreambooth","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B_{tr},A_{tr})=\\big(P_{out}B_{tr},\\ A_{tr}P_{in}\\big)","note":"Per identity, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at the origin \\((0,0)\\), where \\(S_1=0\\)."},{"source":"text-to-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_z(\\theta)=h_\\theta\\big(\\mathrm{concat}[f(z),E[m],E[l]]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tasks, into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\). The arrow is pointed, because Bias-HyperInit gives \\(h_z(q_0)=(0,A_0)\\) for every \\(z\\)."},{"source":"drag-and-drop-llms","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\mathrm{detok}\\big(G_\\phi(c)\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over prompt batches, into LoRA pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect)."},{"source":"doc-to-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\big(\\alpha_l[B^{(1)}_l\\ \\cdots\\ B^{(K)}_l],\\ [A^{(1)}_l;\\dots;A^{(K)}_l]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over documents: an explicit factorisation through the hypernetwork's output, composed with the isomorphism \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus K}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{rK}\\) of Obs I.7, pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\)."},{"source":"tina","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object \\((Q,q_0,\\rho)\\); only the loss differs."},{"source":"plop","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(q_T)=\\big(q_T,\\ q_0|_{T^{c}}\\big)","note":"The adapters of unselected module types are frozen at \\((0,A_0)\\): a pointed monomorphism into LoRA on all modules."},{"source":"lori","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=\\iota_M X","note":"Freezing the masked-out entries of \\(B\\) at \\(0\\): a sub-object of \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\) pointed at \\(B=0\\)."},{"source":"lori","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(X)=(\\iota_M X,\\ A_0)","note":"The composite with \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_0)}\\)."},{"source":"singlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(A)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r\\,u\\,A_{[1:d_{out}]},\\ A_{[1:d_{in}]}^\\top\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) pointed at \\(h(A_0)\\), which is not LoRA's \\(B_0=0\\) pointing."},{"source":"shine","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\Delta_{\\rm meta},m,\\psi)=\\mathrm{M2P}_\\psi\\big(\\mathcal M(c)\\big)_l","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, per layer into LoRA\\(_8\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect)."},{"source":"generative-adapter","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2)=\\big(A_1U_r(c),\\ V_r(c)^\\top B_2\\big),\\ \\ U_r\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top=\\mathrm{SVD}_r(A_2H_c^\\top H_cB_1)","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, into LoRA\\(_{128}\\) pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\); smooth where \\(\\sigma_r>\\sigma_{r+1}\\)."},{"source":"goat","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,W_z)=\\big([s\\,w^1(x)B^1\\ \\cdots\\ s\\,w^E(x)B^E\\ \\ -\\tfrac sEB_0^1\\ \\cdots\\ -\\tfrac sEB_0^E],\\ [A^1;\\dots;A^E;A_0^1;\\dots;A_0^E]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA\\(_{2r}\\) at \\(\\theta_0\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (unpointed slice); smooth away from routing ties."},{"source":"core-space-merging","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\lambda","note":"With task arithmetic as the inner rule, Core Space and task arithmetic have the same coefficient family (the projection is lossless)."},{"source":"k-lora","target":"lora-soups","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=\\big(\\alpha_c^{l},\\alpha_s^{l}\\big)=\\begin{cases}(1,0)&\\text{layer }l\\text{ selects content at }t\\\\ (0,1)&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}","note":"Fibrewise over timesteps: a vertex of CAT's layer-wise family (unpointed slice)."},{"source":"k-lora","target":"ziplora","rule":"A3","h":"h_t(\\ast)=\\big(m_c^{l},m_s^{l}\\big)\\in\\{(\\mathbf 1,0),(0,\\mathbf 1)\\}","note":"Fibrewise over timesteps: ZipLoRA's column coefficients at 0/1 values (ZipLoRA pointed at \\(h(\\ast)\\))."},{"source":"easycontrol","target":"prefix-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(B,A)=\\big(K_l(c;B,A),\\ V_l(c;B,A)\\big)_{l\\le L}","note":"Fibrewise over conditions: an arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation, read at content positions, as in Thm VI.5(e). It needs the mask and the condition positions to be independent of the content."},{"source":"icedit","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,g)=\\big(\\tfrac\\alpha r[G_1(x)B_1\\ \\cdots\\ G_4(x)B_4],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_4]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens on \\(W_o\\), into LoRA\\(_{4r}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_1^0;\\dots;A_4^0])\\), pointed if every \\(B_i=0\\); the standard LoRAs map by the identity."},{"source":"sd-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\alpha_1\\hat B_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\alpha_{t-1}\\hat B_{t-1}\\ \\ \\alpha_tB_t/\\lVert B_tA_t\\rVert_F],\\ [\\hat A_1;\\dots;\\hat A_{t-1};A_t]\\big),\\ \\ D_k=\\hat B_k\\hat A_k","note":"Stage \\(t\\) into LoRA\\(_{tr}\\), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\) (defect); smooth where \\(B_tA_t\\ne0\\)."},{"source":"sparse-memory-finetuning","target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id},\\ \\ M=\\mathrm{rows}(\\mathcal T_t)","note":"Within one step at base \\(\\theta_t\\), the method is the fixed-mask linear method with mask \\(\\mathrm{rows}(\\mathcal T_t)\\)."},{"source":"seal","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Each realised update is an ordinary LoRA; the self-edit loop changes the data and the loss, not \\(\\rho\\)."},{"source":"sparselora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object; the sparsity lives in the training forward pass, not in \\(\\rho\\)."},{"source":"loreft","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(R,W,b)=\\big(R^\\top,\\ [W-R\\mid b]\\big)","note":"Into LoRA\\(_r\\) on the inserted affine box \\([I_d\\mid0]\\) in homogeneous coordinates (Prop VI.4(c)), pointed at \\(h(q_0)\\), the pyreft defect."},{"source":"o-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A5","h":"h=\\big([B_1\\ \\cdots\\ B_T],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_T]\\big)","note":"\\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_r^{\\oplus T}\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{Tr}\\) (Obs I.7), pointed iff the stacked \\(A\\)'s have full row rank."},{"source":"o-lora","target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\text{on each stage's }(B_t,A_t)","note":"The same merge-and-restart scheme with one stage per task; only the loss differs per stage."},{"source":"inflora","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per task it is LoRA-FA\\((A_0)\\) with \\(A_0=A_t\\)."},{"source":"inflora","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_t)","note":"Freezing \\(A\\) at \\(A_t\\): \\(\\mathrm{FA}(A_t)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_t)}\\)."},{"source":"textual-inversion","target":"trainable-tokens","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ v\\mapsto V_{\\{S_*\\}}","note":"Trainable Tokens on the extended vocabulary with \\(T=\\{S_*\\}\\), pointed at the descriptor row \\(E_w\\). This is HF PEFT's TrainableTokensConfig on a newly added token after resize_token_embeddings."},{"source":"textual-inversion","target":"custom-diffusion","rule":"A2","h":"h(v)=(\\Delta W^k=0,\\ \\Delta W^v=0,\\ V^*=v)","note":"Freezing Custom Diffusion's cross-attention \\(W^k,W^v\\) at \\(W_0\\) leaves its modifier-token row. The target is Custom Diffusion pointed at \\((0,0,E_w)\\) rather than at its rare-token init."},{"source":"intruder-dimension-reduction","target":"svdiff","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda)=\\delta,\\quad \\delta_j=(\\lambda-1)\\sigma_j\\ (j\\in\\mathcal I),\\ \\ \\delta_j=0\\ \\text{otherwise}","note":"Into SVDiff re-based at \\(W_1=W_0+\\Delta W\\) (its SVD frame), with \\(\\lambda=1\\) sent to \\(\\delta=0\\). Paper variant (no rank-\\(r\\) re-truncation), \\(\\lambda\\ge0\\)."},{"source":"trans-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object on the target base. The distillation loss and the synthetic data are trained-method data."},{"source":"lora-x","target":"svdiff","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\delta\\sigma)=(\\delta\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\delta\\sigma_r,0,\\dots,0)","note":"Freeze SVDiff's shifts for \\(i>r\\) at 0. On the constraint set \\(\\sigma_i+\\delta\\sigma_i\\ge0\\) the ReLU is the identity."},{"source":"lora-x","target":"svft","rule":"A2","h":"h(\\delta\\sigma)=M,\\ \\ M_{ii}=\\delta\\sigma_i\\ (i\\le r),\\ \\ 0\\ \\text{otherwise}","note":"SVFT with the plain (diagonal) pattern pointed at \\(M=0\\), its tail \\(i>r\\) frozen. SVFT's official init is a small defect."},{"source":"lora-x","target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Delta\\Sigma)=\\Delta\\Sigma\\,\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"LoRA-XS in the form \\(W_0+U_rR\\,\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top\\), pointed at \\(R=0\\) (needs \\(\\sigma_r>0\\)). An isomorphism for the full-core variant."},{"source":"lora-x","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\Delta\\Sigma)=(U_r,\\ \\Delta\\Sigma V_r^\\top)","note":"Into LoRA pointed at \\((U_r,0)\\), the \\(\\mathrm T_B\\) stratum."},{"source":"lofit","target":"bitfit","rule":"A3","h":"h(v)=\\big(\\Delta b_O^{(l)}=\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in T_l}W^{O}_{(i)}v_l^i,\\ \\text{other biases }0\\big)","note":"Into BitFit on a model with \\(o\\_proj\\) biases, pointed at 0. The image is a proper subspace of the \\(o\\_proj\\) bias space."},{"source":"moslora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,M,A)=(BM,\\ A)","note":"Pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\), with equal images and equal \\(S_1=\\{YA_0\\}\\). The reverse arrow \\(h(B,A)=(BM_0^{-1},M_0,A)\\) also exists."},{"source":"moslora","target":"flora-tucker","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same three-factor form on 2-D weights, but FLoRA is pointed at \\(h(q_0)=(0,M_0,A_0)\\) rather than at its zero-core init."},{"source":"laplace-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}\\ \\ \\text{(the MAP object)}","note":"The family's mean (\\(\\varepsilon=0\\)) is the LoRA point it was built from; the Laplace step adds trained-method data."},{"source":"blob","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(M,G,B)=(B,\\ M)","note":"Projection to the mean, forgetting the variance coordinates \\(G\\) (as for MonteCLoRA). Pointed at \\((0,M_0)\\)."},{"source":"loraprune","target":"dora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A,m)=\\big(\\mu=m\\odot\\lVert W_0+sBA\\rVert_{\\rm row},\\ B,\\ A\\big)","note":"On each mask component, for boxes masked by output rows (q, k, v, gate, up); DoRA pointed at \\((\\lVert W_0\\rVert_{\\rm row},0,A_0)\\). It fails on o_proj and down_proj, where head and channel masks act on input columns."},{"source":"modulora","target":"qlora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Valid only when \\(\\kappa\\) is NF4 with double quantisation; then the objects are identical (an isomorphism). For the paper's quantisers (OPTQ, QuIP#) the vertices \\(\\kappa\\theta_0\\) and \\(\\kappa_{\\rm NF4}\\theta_0\\) differ by a generically full-rank matrix, so the cones are disjoint translates and O1 blocks any arrow; ModuLoRA is then the same construction at a different base point, not an object over QLoRA."},{"source":"sltrain","target":"rosa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"RoSA with its mask taken to be \\(\\mathcal I\\), pointed at \\(S=S_0\\). With RoSA's own gradient-chosen support the images differ."},{"source":"lora-dash","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A,\\Delta\\sigma)=\\big([B\\ \\ \\bar U\\mathrm{diag}(\\Delta\\sigma)],\\ [A;\\ \\bar V^\\top]\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A_0;\\bar V^\\top])\\). This is a standard \\(\\mathrm T_A\\) pointing iff \\(\\mathrm{row}A_0\\cap\\mathrm{span}\\bar V=0\\), which is generic (Obs I.7)."},{"source":"lcm-lora","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)=(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2),\\quad \\tau_1=B'A',\\ \\tau_2=B_{\\rm LCM}A_{\\rm LCM}","note":"Task arithmetic with two LoRA task vectors (the catalog's special-case edge)."},{"source":"lcm-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)=\\big([\\lambda_1B'\\ \\ \\lambda_2B_{\\rm LCM}],\\ [A';A_{\\rm LCM}]\\big)","note":"Into \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r'+r}\\) pointed at \\((0,[A';A_{\\rm LCM}])\\), as for HF's cat (Obs I.7)."},{"source":"concept-sliders","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The trained object is LoRA (rank 4, \\(\\alpha/r=1/4\\) in the official config)."},{"source":"concept-sliders","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha_1,\\dots,\\alpha_K)=(\\lambda_k=\\alpha_k),\\quad \\tau_k=B_kA_k","note":"The source here is the deployed P-object (strengths \\(\\alpha_k\\) trainable, slider factors frozen after training), not the training object classified above, whose \\(Q=(B,A)\\) has no map to coefficients. On that deployed object \\(\\rho_{\\rm TA}\\circ h=W_0+\\sum_k\\alpha_kB_kA_k\\), pointed at \\(\\alpha=0\\); signed coefficients are allowed."},{"source":"ic-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The trainable object is an ordinary LoRA (\\(r=\\alpha=16\\))."},{"source":"proxy-tuning","target":"eft","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Per-token EFT up-scaling normalises to the same softmax: isomorphic objects (concurrent work)."},{"source":"eft","target":"proxy-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same per-token logit-arithmetic rule."},{"source":"wise-ft","target":"task-arithmetic","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=(\\lambda=\\alpha)","note":"\\(N=1\\), \\(\\tau=\\theta_1-\\theta_0\\), with \\(\\lambda\\) restricted to \\([0,1]\\)."},{"source":"wise-ft","target":"concept-sliders","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=(\\alpha B_1,\\ A_1)","note":"When \\(\\theta_1-\\theta_0=B_1A_1\\) is a LoRA update (HF rescale_adapter_scale): the slider line through it, pointed at \\((0,A_1)\\)."},{"source":"linear-probing","target":"bitfit","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=(\\Delta b=0,\\ V)","note":"BitFit with its usual trainable head, the biases frozen at their base value."},{"source":"linear-probing","target":"surgical-fine-tuning","rule":"A2","h":"h(V)=V\\ \\ \\text{(block = last layer)}","note":"Surgical FT with the 'Last Layer' block."},{"source":"linear-probing","target":"lp-ft","rule":"A5","h":"V\\mapsto(V,\\ q_0^{(2)})","note":"The one-stage scheme embeds as stage 1 of LP-FT, padded with stage 2's base value."},{"source":"ffa-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"h(B)=(B,\\ A_0)","note":"Freeze \\(A\\) at its base value: LoRA pointed at \\((0,A_0)\\)."},{"source":"ffa-lora","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same parametrisation as LoRA-FA v1. LoRA-FA v2 changes only the cometric (a projected \\(B\\)-gradient)."},{"source":"flora-federated","target":"relora","rule":"A3","h":"h\\big((B_k,A_k)_k\\big)=\\big([p_1B_1\\cdots p_KB_K],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_K]\\big)\\ \\ \\text{per round}","note":"Stagewise into ReLoRA of rank \\(\\sum_kr_k\\), pointed at the stacked \\(A_0\\)'s; the pointing is standard iff their rows are independent."},{"source":"hetlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The global pair is a \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\) point."},{"source":"hetlora","target":"dylora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same nested object, the truncation filtration of \\(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r_{\\max}}\\), with levels set by client capacity instead of sampled."},{"source":"autoprompt","target":"prompt-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h(w_1,\\dots,w_\\ell)=(E_{w_1},\\dots,E_{w_\\ell})","note":"A smooth map from the finite set \\(\\mathcal V^\\ell\\) into prompt tuning at the template's trigger slots, pointed at \\(h([\\mathrm{MASK}]^\\ell)\\): a monomorphism of the unpointed slice."},{"source":"loft-orthogonal","target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto Q=-P_r^{\\top}EP_r\\in\\mathfrak{so}(n)","note":"Into exact full-block Cayley OFT (block size n), with the same Cayley map on both sides. With the catalogue's convention W = W_0 C(Q)^T, h(E) = -P_r^T E P_r; with HF's exact code R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, W = W_0 R^T, it is h(E) = +P_r^T E P_r. Uses C(P_r^T E P_r) = I + P_r^T (C(E) - I) P_r."},{"source":"loft-orthogonal","target":"lora-xs","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto R=\\Sigma_r\\big(C(E)-I\\big)\\Sigma_r^{-1}","note":"Principal support, needs sigma_r(W_0) > 0; LoRA-XS pointed at R = 0. Holds for any transform Q, exact or not."},{"source":"loft-orthogonal","target":"psoft","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto\\big(R=C(E)^{\\top},\\ \\alpha=\\beta=\\mathbf 1\\big)","note":"Principal support, same exactly orthogonal transform on both sides; freeze PSOFT's two scaling vectors at 1 (A2 up to E -> -E). With alpha = beta = 1, PSOFT's W_res + B R^T A equals W_0 (I + V_r (R^T - I) V_r^T)."},{"source":"loft-orthogonal","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"E\\mapsto\\big(B=W_0P_r^{\\top}(C(E)-I_r),\\ A=P_r\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_r pointed at (B, A) = (0, P_r), a T_A-type pointing with a fixed row-orthonormal A_0."},{"source":"preft","target":"loreft","rule":"A3","h":"(W_1,W_2,b)\\mapsto\\big(R=(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{-1/2}W_2,\\ W=R+s_r(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{1/2}W_1,\\ b'=s_r(W_2W_2^{\\top})^{1/2}b\\big)","note":"DiReFT_P instance, into LoReFT intervening at every prompt position (prefix length >= ell) after every block: s_r W_2^T(W_1 h + b) = R^T((W - R)h + b'). Smooth where W_2 has full row rank. Pointed: the released init (W_1 = 0, b = 0, W_2 with orthonormal rows) goes to LoReFT's neutral point W = R, b = 0. Checked numerically (error 2e-16). The target is LoReFT pointed at its neutral point (W = R, b = 0), not at pyreft's default init, which is a defect."},{"source":"orthomerge","target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"\\ast\\mapsto Q_m=\\tfrac{c}{N}\\textstyle\\sum_iQ_i","note":"OFT experts sharing one block partition, exact Cayley on both sides: the merged point is a point of OFT's image (unpointed slice, as soups into task arithmetic). With OFT written as W_0 C(Q)^T, C(Q) = (I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1} (OrthoMerge's own convention), h(*) = Q_m; under HF's R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, h(*) = -Q_m. Experts trained with HF's default Cayley-Neumann map are read through exact Cayley only approximately."},{"source":"prism-dp-lora","target":"pissa","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(\\sqrt s\\,B,\\ \\sqrt s\\,A)","note":"Isomorphism onto PiSSA_r (scale 1) on the same residual W_0 - [W_0]_r. It sends PRISM's q_0 = (U_r(Sigma_r/s)^{1/2}, (Sigma_r/s)^{1/2}V_r^T) to PiSSA's (U_r Sigma_r^{1/2}, Sigma_r^{1/2}V_r^T). Only the optimiser differs. Checked numerically."},{"source":"prism-dp-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ B_0],\\ [A;\\ -A_0]\\big)","note":"Target instance: LoRA_{2r} at the degenerate pointing. The released spectral_rebase_adapter_inplace: PRISM_r into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0 (same scale s), pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,B_0],[A_0;-A_0]) with product 0 (Thm II.1, as for PiSSA). Checked numerically."},{"source":"keeplora","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto B-B_t^{(0)}","note":"Per task: LoRA-FA(A_t) over the base W_{t-1} (same scale), pointed at 0. Checked numerically."},{"source":"keeplora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"B\\mapsto\\big(B-B_t^{(0)},\\ A_t\\big)","note":"Per task, into LoRA pointed at (0, A_t), a T_A point with orthonormal A_t (composite with FA(A_t) -> LoRA)."},{"source":"lora-pre","target":"adam-mini","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_\\Theta","note":"Both are the terminal object with forward map id_Theta; they differ only in optimiser data (Def III.1)."},{"source":"structlora","target":"sora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,B,m)\\mapsto\\big(B,\\ \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}m,\\ A\\big)","note":"Source instance: gated (coordinator off, App. E.7). Gated instance only (coordinator off): W_0 + (alpha/r) B diag(m) A is SoRA's map with gate g = (alpha/r) m, SoRA re-pointed at (0, (alpha/r) m_0, A_0) with m_0 = sigmoid(2) from the code's gate logits; same T_A stratum. The released coordinated instance has no such arrow (see the obstruction)."},{"source":"structlora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,B,m)\\mapsto\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(m),\\ A\\big)","note":"Source instance: gated (coordinator off, App. E.7). Gated instance only, pointed at (0, A_0)."},{"source":"fedrot-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The global pair is a LoRA_r point, pointed at (0, A_0); only the aggregation differs."},{"source":"rlpo-rlmo","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:(B,A)\\mapsto(B,A)","note":"Pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_r pointed at (0, V_{[:,1:r]}^T) (resp. (0, V_{[:,-r:]}^T)). Into LoRA with a random A_0 there is only a weak morphism (a different point of Gr(r,n))."},{"source":"nara","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\lambda(B,A,\\phi)=\\big(B\\,C_\\phi(\\lambda),\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over noise levels, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0); pointed because B starts at 0."},{"source":"nara","target":"moslora","rule":"A3","h":"h_\\lambda(B,A,\\phi)=\\big(B,\\ C_\\phi(\\lambda)/s,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise, into MoSLoRA re-pointed at (0, I_r/s, A_0), with s MoSLoRA's scale. Its default random-mixer pointing differs, but S_1 = {XA_0} on both sides."},{"source":"lime","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(B,A,(p_i)_i,p_s,\\gamma)=\\big(\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathcal P(x)+\\gamma p_s)\\,B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0). Piecewise smooth only: h_x is smooth where the selected set S_theta(x) is locally constant and z-hat_{1:E}(x) != 0. At q_0 (B = 0) the router is 0/0; h_x is continuous there with h_x(q_0) = (0, A_0), because P(x) is a convex combination and hence bounded, but it is not differentiable (the infinity-norm normalisation flips with the sign of B). At q_0 the arrow certifies the inclusion of each realised fibre in Im LoRA_r rather than a smooth pointed morphism."},{"source":"agzo","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"\\lambda\\mapsto\\lambda R_t","note":"Per step at base theta_t: the line theta_t + lambda R_t U_t^T is a line of LoRA-FA(A_0 := U_t^T), pointed at 0. Linear layers only: AGZO's dense Gaussian perturbation of the other parameters (norms, biases, embeddings) lies outside LoRA-FA's boxes."},{"source":"zo-muon","target":"lora","rule":"A2","h":"X\\mapsto(P_k,\\ X)","note":"Per period at base W_k: FB(P_k) is LoRA with B frozen at P_k, pointed at (P_k, 0) (stratum T_B), as for GaLore's left-projection case and Online Subspace Descent. Weight matrices only; 1-D parameters (biases, norms) get dense Gaussian RGE and lie outside the frozen-B slice."},{"source":"flexlora-entropy","target":"adalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same P Lambda Q object pointed at Lambda = 0 with the same frames, at any fixed allocation; only the rank schedule differs."},{"source":"flexlora-entropy","target":"elalora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same object; both prune and expand inside the rank filtration, with different scores."},{"source":"flexlora-entropy","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(P,\\Lambda,Q)=(P\\Lambda,\\ Q)","note":"Pointed at (0, Q_0)."},{"source":"fouriermoe","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((c_i)_i,\\Phi\\big)=\\eta\\textstyle\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal S_\\Phi(x)}g_{\\Phi,i}(x)\\,B(c_i),\\ \\ B(c)=\\tfrac{2}{d_{out}d_{in}}\\big[\\,a_k\\cos\\theta_k-b_k\\sin\\theta_k,\\ -(a_k\\sin\\theta_k+b_k\\cos\\theta_k)\\,\\big]_k","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA-FA(A_F) with the frozen input frame A_F = [cos phi_k; sin phi_k] over the union of the experts' supports. Here c_k = a_k + i b_k, and theta_k, phi_k are the output and input phase vectors of frequency (u_k, v_k); the factor is 1 instead of 2 for self-conjugate frequencies. Pointed at 0 for the zero-coefficient pointing. Checked numerically (2e-17). As for routed mixtures in general, the top-k selection makes h_x only piecewise smooth in the router parameters; it is smooth in the coefficients c_i, and h_x(q_0) = 0 for every router value."},{"source":"smoa","target":"hira","rule":"A3","h":"(B_k,A_k)_k\\mapsto\\big(P_{out}^\\top\\,\\mathrm{blkdiag}(B_1,\\dots,B_K),\\ \\mathrm{blkdiag}(A_1,\\dots,A_K)\\,P_{in}\\big)","note":"Into HiRA_r pointed at (0, blkdiag(A_{k,0}) P_in), a T_A point (zero-factor init assumed). Checked numerically (exact)."},{"source":"bayesian-lora-sgp","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big(\\bar B,\\ \\bar A\\big),\\ \\ \\bar B=T_r^B\\,\\mathbb E_q[U^B]\\,T_c^B,\\ \\ \\bar A=T_r^A\\,\\mathbb E_q[U^A]\\,T_c^A","note":"Projection to the posterior mean (B and A are independent, so E[BA] = B-bar A-bar), scales absorbed. Pointed at h(q_0), which is not LoRA's B = 0 pointing because the released init is a defect, so the arrow lives in the unpointed slice."},{"source":"florg","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"A\\mapsto\\big(s\\,LA^\\top,\\ AR\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_r, pointed at h(A^1) (unpointed slice: both factors nonzero)."},{"source":"florg","target":"singlora","rule":"A3","h":"A\\mapsto\\sqrt{sr/\\alpha}\\,A^\\top","note":"Square weights with L = R^T = I only; otherwise FLoRG is a frame transport of SingLoRA, an automorphism rather than an arrow. Into SingLoRA after its ramp (u = 1), in the unpointed slice."},{"source":"loda-cl","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"(B_G,B_I)\\mapsto[\\,w_GB_G,\\ B_I\\,]","note":"Per task, into LoRA-FA([A_G; A_I]) over W^{t-1}, pointed at 0. The pre-merge shrink replaces w_G B_G by w_G B_G Lambda_G, another point of the same stage."},{"source":"loda-cl","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B_G,B_I)\\mapsto\\big([\\,w_GB_G,\\ B_I\\,],\\ [A_G;A_I]\\big)","note":"Per task, into LoRA_{2r} pointed at (0, [A_G; A_I])."},{"source":"lragent","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_i(A,B_1,\\dots,B_N)=(B_i,\\ A)","note":"Fibrewise over agents, into LoRA_r pointed at (0, A_0)."},{"source":"lragent","target":"s-lora","rule":"A3","h":"(A,(B_i)_i)\\mapsto\\big((B_i,A)\\big)_i","note":"Into S-LoRA's family over tenants with tied A_u = A. Exact only without cross-agent cache reuse, which changes the realised function."},{"source":"geora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ -B_0],\\ [A;\\ A_0]\\big)","note":"Target instance: LoRA_{2r} at the degenerate pointing. Thm II.1 conversion into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0 (same scale), pointed at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) with product 0, as for PiSSA."},{"source":"matryoshka-lora","target":"sora","rule":"A2","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto(B,\\ p,\\ A)","note":"Freezing SoRA's gate at p (and dropping its l1 penalty), SoRA re-pointed at (0, p, A_0): the training-time map."},{"source":"matryoshka-lora","target":"dylora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"Instance s_r = c/r only (DyLoRA's alpha = c): the same underlying LoRA_R pointed at (0, A_0), with each deployed prefix s_kB_{:,1:k}A_{1:k,:} equal to DyLoRA's level k. For the released s_r = 1/sqrt(r) (and for s_r = 1) no single h exists: matching level k needs B_{:,j} -> (k s_k/alpha) B_{:,j} for every j <= k, and k s_k is not constant over S (1, 1.414, 2 for S = {1,2,4}), so the comparison holds only level by level, through the LoRA_k arrow. The objectives differ in any case: a deterministic p against a sampled b."},{"source":"matryoshka-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big(s_kB_{:,1:k},\\ A_{1:k,:}\\big)","note":"Deployed at rank k, into LoRA_k pointed at (0, A_{0,1:k})."},{"source":"loco-orthogonal","target":"oft","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V)\\mapsto Q=-(UV^\\top-VU^\\top)","note":"n = 1, into exact full-block Cayley OFT (block size d_in), with OFT written as W_0 C(Q)^T and C(Q) = (I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1}, so that C(Q)^T = C(-Q). Under HF's exact R = (I-Q)(I+Q)^{-1}, h = +(UV^T - VU^T). Pointed: V = 0 goes to Q = 0. Checked numerically."},{"source":"loco-orthogonal","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(U,V)\\mapsto\\big(2W_0X(I_{2r}-Y^\\top X)^{-1},\\ Y^\\top\\big),\\ \\ X=[U\\,|\\,-V],\\ Y=[V\\,|\\,U]","note":"Woodbury form of W_0(C(A) - I): into LoRA_{2r} (LoRA_{2nr} for n components, by concatenation). Pointed at h(U_0, 0) = (2W_0[U_0, 0], [0; U_0^T]), whose product is 0 with both factors nonzero, outside the three strata (as HRA's arrow). Checked numerically."},{"source":"compress-then-merge","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"\\lambda\\mapsto\\big(U\\textstyle\\sum_t\\lambda_tO^{(t)},\\ V^\\top\\big)","note":"Task arithmetic inside: into LoRA_r pointed at (0, V^T), a T_A point with orthonormal rows. Checked numerically."},{"source":"srr","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(B,A)\\mapsto\\big([B,\\ -B_0],\\ [A;\\ A_0]\\big)","note":"In the slice at the attained start theta_0' = Q + B_0A_0: SRR_r into LoRA_{2r} over theta_0', at a degenerate pointing (as LoftQ)."},{"source":"prompt2effect","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\phi)=\\big(\\hat B_\\phi(c,W_0),\\ \\hat A_\\phi(c,W_0)\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over effect prompts, into LoRA_r pointed at h_c(q_0) (unpointed slice: the family is a defect by design)."},{"source":"giva","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\lambda)\\mapsto\\Gamma B\\Lambda","note":"Into LoRA-FA(V_r^T), pointed at 0 (gamma_0 = 0), as VeRA -> LoRA-FA(A)."},{"source":"giva","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"(\\gamma,\\lambda)\\mapsto(\\Gamma B\\Lambda,\\ V_r^\\top)","note":"Composite with FA -> LoRA, pointed at (0, V_r^T)."},{"source":"activation-prompt","target":"bitfit","rule":"A3","h":"\\delta=\\mathbf 1\\otimes v\\ \\mapsto\\ \\Delta b_{\\rm down}^{(l-1)}=v","note":"Position-uniform instance only (the hypothesis of Prop. 1), on a ViT whose residual stream at layer l is last written by the biased MLP output projection of block l-1: the shift is that bias edit, BitFit pointed at 0. The released position-specific delta has no such arrow (M-infinity)."},{"source":"stable-lora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule differs (the shrink is not a gauge move)."},{"source":"mdpd","target":"ln-tuning","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}","note":"The same deployed object (LayerNorm (gamma, beta) of every block, with the same new task head on both sides); only the training signal differs."},{"source":"riemannion","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"X\\mapsto\\big([\\,X(B_0^{+}X)^{+},\\ -B_0\\,],\\ [\\,B_0^{+}X;\\ A_0\\,]\\big),\\qquad X_0=B_0A_0","note":"LOI instance. Thm II.1 conversion: Riemannion_r into LoRA_{2r} on the untouched W_0, at the degenerate pointing ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]) (product 0, as for LoRA-GA and PiSSA). The first block is a smooth local section of the quotient map near X_0 (rank r), with h(X_0) = ([B_0,-B_0],[A_0;A_0]); checked numerically (error 2e-15). With the stored thin factors (B,A) it reads h(B,A) = ([B,-B_0],[A;A_0])."},{"source":"lora-mixer","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_e,A_e)_e,G\\big)=\\big(\\,[\\,\\mathbb 1_{e\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(x)}\\,p_e(x)\\,B_e\\,]_{e=1}^{E},\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_E]\\,\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens (per adapted projection), into LoRA_{Er} pointed at h(q_0) (unpointed slice, as PHATGOOSE -> LoRA); smooth away from top-K ties. A renormalisation over the selected set, if used, is absorbed into p_e."},{"source":"in-place-ttt","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{c,i}(W_{target},k)=\\big(\\eta\\,[\\hat V_{[1]}^\\top\\ \\cdots\\ \\hat V_{[i-1]}^\\top],\\ [Z_{[1]};\\dots;Z_{[i-1]}]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over (document c, chunk index i), into LoRA_{(i-1)C} on W_down (scale 1), pointed at (0,[Z_{[1]};...;Z_{[i-1]}]) because the zero kernel gives V-hat = 0: a T_A-type point whose A_0 is the context's own activations. Uses sum_j V_j^T Z_j = [V_1^T ... V_{i-1}^T][Z_1;...;Z_{i-1}] (checked, 2e-15). Backbone fixed (inference); for long contexts the rank is capped at min(d, d_ff)."},{"source":"ld-mole","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_i,A_i)_i,W_{gate},f\\big)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{rs}\\,[\\,p_{x,1}B_1\\ \\cdots\\ p_{x,E}B_E\\,],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_E]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_{Er} with scale s, pointed at (0,[A_1^0;...;A_E^0]) since every B_i starts at 0 (as HydraLoRA -> LoRA). h_x is continuous and piecewise smooth (Sparsegen's support changes across hyperplanes)."},{"source":"ar1-zo","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{s}B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Isomorphism onto LoRA_r with scale s (as rsLoRA = LoRA), pointed: B_0 = 0 maps to 0."},{"source":"pico","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\ast)=\\big([\\,\\gamma SB_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\gamma SB_T\\,],\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_T]\\big)","note":"Pico-TA instance: the merged point is a point of LoRA_{Tr}'s image (unpointed slice, LoRA pointed at h(*)), as model soups into task arithmetic."},{"source":"frameft","target":"lora-fa","rule":"A3","h":"h(C_\\Omega)=\\tfrac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt{d_{out}d_{in}}}\\,P_{out}\\,C_{:,J},\\qquad A_0=(P_{in})_{:,J}^\\top","note":"J = columns of C met by Omega; into LoRA-FA_{|J|} with the frozen frame rows A_0 (LoRA-FA scale 1; otherwise divide h by it), pointed at 0 (as SVFT -> LoRA-FA). Checked numerically (6e-17)."},{"source":"gradfix","target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\alpha\\,(m\\odot\\tau_A)","note":"Per transport, over the base theta_B: the line lies in FISH with mask M = supp(m), pointed at 0 (as Sparse Memory Finetuning -> FISH)."},{"source":"coa-lora","target":"lq-lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{\\mathbf C}(\\theta,L_1,L_2)=\\big(L_1\\,(I_r+U_\\theta(Q_{\\mathbf C})),\\ L_2\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over configurations, per layer, into LQ-LoRA over the quantised base W~_C, pointed at h_C(q_0) (approximately (L_1, L_2), since the mapper's last layer starts at N(0,1e-6)). This is LQ-LoRA's own start when W~_C and the factors come from the same low-rank-plus-quantised split at that configuration."},{"source":"slao","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"First stage: a pointed isomorphism onto LoRA_r at (0, A_0). Later stages start at W_0 + B_{ft,i-1}Q_i^T and map only by a weak morphism (LoRA re-pointed at that start)."},{"source":"smartfed-more","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(W_{router})=\\big(\\widehat B\\,\\mathrm{diag}(\\tilde g_x(W_{router})),\\ \\widehat A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_M (M = sum_n r_n) with A frozen at A-hat, pointed at h(q_0) (unpointed slice); smooth away from top-K ties."},{"source":"rotmole","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_x\\big((B_i,A_i,q_i)_i,W_g,W_\\theta\\big)=\\big(\\,[\\,\\mathbb 1_{i\\in\\mathrm{TopK}(x)}\\,g_i(x)\\,B_iR_i(x)\\,]_{i=1}^{n},\\ [A_1;\\dots;A_n]\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over tokens, into LoRA_{nr} pointed at (0,[A_1^0;...;A_n^0]), since every B_i starts at 0. Smooth away from routing ties and from A_ix = 0 (r > 2)."},{"source":"scalora","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(B,A)=\\big([\\,B,\\ -\\widetilde B_t\\,],\\ [\\,A;\\ \\widetilde A_t\\,]\\big)","note":"Per stage: the stage over W~_0^{(t)} = W_t - B~_tA~_t factors through LoRA_{2r} on the pre-split weight W_t (Thm II.1 conversion), at the degenerate pointing ([B~_t,-B~_t],[A~_t;A~_t])."},{"source":"lora-s","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}_Q","note":"Same object; only the update rule (trained-method data) differs, as for LoRA-Pro and Riemannian-preconditioned LoRA."},{"source":"diablo","target":"fish-mask","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id},\\qquad M=\\mathrm{supp}\\,\\mathrm{blkdiag}(\\mathbf 1_{d_{out}/N\\times d_{in}/N},\\dots)","note":"FISH taken with the fixed block-diagonal mask instead of its Fisher-selected one, pointed at 0."},{"source":"diablo","target":"diff-pruning","rule":"A2","h":"D\\mapsto\\big(\\alpha_0,\\ \\iota_{\\rm blk}D\\big)","note":"The block support is one fixed coordinate set, a stratum of Diff pruning's learned-support cone (within the L0 budget when k >= d_in d_out / N), as SMT -> Diff pruning."},{"source":"qzo","target":"efficientqat","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\mathrm{id}:\\ S\\mapsto S","note":"The same group-wise scale family over frozen integer codes (EfficientQAT's E2E-QP stage), valid over common codes, zero-points and group size; both are pointed at their own quantised start."},{"source":"perk","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_c(\\theta_{adapter},\\alpha,\\psi)=\\phi^{(N)}(\\theta_{adapter},\\alpha,\\psi;c)","note":"Fibrewise over contexts, into LoRA_r (rsLoRA scale absorbed) pointed at h_c(q_0) (defect): the unrolled inner loop is an explicit map, smooth for Adam with eps > 0."},{"source":"vapt","target":"vpt","rule":"A3","h":"h_x(q)=\\big((P^{(l)}(\\tilde X^{(l)}(x;q)))_{l\\le L},\\ \\mathrm{Head}\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over inputs, into VPT-Deep (prompt outputs discarded per block, as in VAPT), pointed at h_x(q_0) (unpointed slice); block 1's static prompts map to VPT's block-1 prompts."},{"source":"adapad","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h=\\big([\\,b_{i,1}\\ \\cdots\\ b_{i,r_i}\\,],\\ [\\,a_{i,1}^\\top;\\dots;a_{i,r_i}^\\top\\,]\\big)","note":"Per module, into LoRA_{r_i} (scale absorbed), re-pointed at h(q_0): the start is a nonzero rank-1 point, so this is an arrow of the unpointed slice (weak)."},{"source":"tera","target":"vera","rule":"A3","h":"h(d^{(1)},d^{(2)})=\\big(b=d^{(1)},\\ d=d^{(2)}\\big)","note":"Instance N = 2, A^{(1)} = I, core = VeRA's B, A^{(2)} = VeRA's A: an isomorphism (our derivation; the paper only compares parameter counts). Pointed when the zeroed vector is d^{(1)} and d^{(2)} starts at VeRA's d_0."},{"source":"dc-merge","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h(\\alpha)=\\big(\\alpha\\,\\widetilde U(\\widetilde M\\odot\\mathcal M),\\ \\widetilde V^\\top\\big)","note":"Into LoRA_{rT} pointed at (0, V~^T), a T_A point with orthonormal rows when rT < min(m,n) (LoRA inputs); in the full fine-tuning setting rT can reach min(m,n), outside the strata (as Compress-then-Merge -> LoRA)."},{"source":"malora-mamba","target":"lora","rule":"A3","h":"h_{x_{1:t}}(A,B,P,\\mathrm{Mamba},w,b)=\\big(\\tfrac{\\alpha}{rs}\\,\\lambda(x_{1:t})\\,B,\\ A\\big)","note":"Fibrewise over token prefixes, into LoRA_r with scale s, pointed at (0, A_0) since B_0 = 0."}],"rosetta":[{"peft":"pretrained checkpoint","category":"base point theta0: 1 -> Theta; a pointed 1-cell (Theta, theta0, f) of Para(Diff)","explanation":"Fine-tuning moves a point; everything is measured relative to it (Def I.1)."},{"peft":"PEFT method","category":"object of the pointed slice Meth(Theta,theta0) = Diff_*/(Theta,theta0), equivalently the opposite of the pointed coslice of reparametrisation 2-cells under F","explanation":"A method is a pointed map rho:(Q,q0)->(Theta,theta0). The slice depends only on (Theta,theta0); Para is bookkeeping for weight-space methods (Remark I.3)."},{"peft":"\"method N can do whatever M can\"","category":"morphism (simulation) h: M -> N with rho_N o h = rho_M","explanation":"Smooth simulations also carry parameter paths; at the level of sets they reduce to image inclusion (Obs I.4)."},{"peft":"frozen model / full fine-tuning","category":"initial / terminal object","explanation":"Every weight-space method lies in the interval [frozen, full FT]; adapters, prefixes and ReFT are extensions, not objects of Meth (Obs I.4)."},{"peft":"merging an adapter into the weights","category":"the canonical morphism to the terminal object (it is rho itself); for extensions, a lift along the realisation Phi_f","explanation":"For weight-space methods merging is definitional; for extensions zero inference overhead is a lifting problem, certified box-locally by rewriting (sufficient conditions and box-local obstructions) under side conditions such as position-uniformity, no weight sharing and bias slots (Thm VI.3)."},{"peft":"expressivity / effective number of parameters","category":"image germ of rho at theta0; its dimension d(M) = generic rank of d rho","explanation":"Rank is a cut invariant; dimension is a volume invariant (Thm II.10, Prop II.12)."},{"peft":"lazy / NTK-regime capability","category":"first-order image S1 = d rho_{q0}(T Q), a functorial isomorphism invariant","explanation":"The lazy kernel has range J_f(S1); S1 separates zero-init pointings (T_A vs T_B, the row space of A0) that the image cannot, while the image already separates split inits (Obs I.6)."},{"peft":"zero-init vs PiSSA-type init","category":"apex (singular vertex) vs smooth point of the image; Type I (new q0 in the apex fibre) vs Type II (new rho by splitting)","explanation":"Zero-init loses r(n-r) first-order directions; split inits are different objects, incomparable with LoRA (Thm II.2, Prop III.8)."},{"peft":"the zoo of LoRA initialisations","category":"points of three distinguished strata of full-rank pointings, classified as a set by Gr(r,n) disjoint-union Gr(r,m) disjoint-union M_r","explanation":"Vanilla LoRA on linear layers, EVA, Init[B], HF's default LoRA on embedding layers, PiSSA, MiLoRA, OLoRA, CorDA, LoRA-GA and Astra are points; LoRA-FA and MiCA are frozen-factor sub-objects of the zero-B and zero-A strata; HF's 'orthogonal' init lies outside the three strata, and LoftQ/QLoRA are defect pointings. The optimizer sees a finer quotient (Thm II.2, Cor III.13)."},{"peft":"no init buys rank above 2r","category":"union over pointings of theta0 + (C - C) = theta0 + M_{<=2r}","explanation":"An exact initialisation picks a translate of the shape; from any starting point the update has rank at most 2r. This is the known rank-2r PiSSA-to-LoRA conversion (Thm II.1)."},{"peft":"redundant parameters","category":"gauge group = automorphisms of Q over Theta; generic fibre","explanation":"LoRA wastes r^2 parameters; LoHa wastes r1^2 + r2^2 + m + n - 1 below the mn cap, and more where the cap binds (Prop II.12)."},{"peft":"high-rank PEFT (LoHa, LoKr, HiRA, MoRA, C3A)","category":"tensor networks whose min-cut avoids the bond; transport of structure along GL(Theta)","explanation":"High rank is not GL(Theta)-invariant (though rank is GL_m x GL_n-invariant) and need not mean large volume; a large min-cut is necessary, not sufficient (Thms II.10-II.11, Prop II.13)."},{"peft":"Kronecker adapters (unconstrained Kronecker sums, KronA, LoKr)","category":"LoRA across a different cut of the same 4-leg tensor (Van Loan-Pitsianis rearrangement, a coordinate permutation)","explanation":"For aligned splits (equal square splits, and every split HF PEFT's factorisation produces), a generic rank-one update has maximal Kronecker rank, so unconstrained Kronecker sums with fewer terms and LoRA_r below the rank of a full-rank Kronecker product are incomparable. LoKr with inner rank r' sits inside LoRA_r only when r >= min(m1,n1) r'; Compacter's PHM weights live in a nonlinear adapter (Thm II.11)."},{"peft":"\"new method = old method\"","category":"transport T_* along T in GL(Theta): an automorphism of the category, not an arrow inside it","explanation":"FourierFT (up to sampled conjugate pairs), WaveFT, C3A, LoRA-XS, SVFT and SHiRA are coordinate masks in transported frames; for LoRA-XS, SVFT and HiRA the frame depends on theta0 (Prop II.13)."},{"peft":"orthogonal fine-tuning preserves knowledge","category":"the image lies in a fibre of the complete invariant W -> W W^T (first fundamental theorem for O(n))","explanation":"In exact arithmetic, exactly orthogonal methods (OFT with an exact Cayley map, strict GOFT without its default scaler, HRA) can never change the spectrum or neuron angles, however often they are merged; HF's default Cayley-Neumann OFT is only approximately orthogonal (Cor II.6)."},{"peft":"HRA bridges LoRA and OFT","category":"image-level meet with the rotation orbit: (W0 + M_{<=r}) cap W0 SO(n) for injective W0 and even r, not a product of methods","explanation":"Exactly orthogonal (exact-Cayley) OFT is strictly smaller, and HF's default Cayley-Neumann OFT is incomparable; HRA's paired identity init is an apex: the germ is the skew-determinantal cone (Thm II.7)."},{"peft":"LoRA-XS","category":"categorical product FA(A0) x FB(B0) of the two one-sided LoRAs","explanation":"The overlap that a split init double-counts (Prop I.8, Prop II.3)."},{"peft":"DoRA's magnitude/direction decoupling","category":"diagonal saturation Diag_m . (W0 + M_{<=r}), the image of LoRA followed by a trainable output scale","explanation":"Equal images. Under gradient flow the exact gradient changes the metric and the detached backward is a non-gradient update; under AdamW the magnitude coordinates matter too (Prop II.8)."},{"peft":"(IA)^3, SSF, LN tuning, RoAd, HiRA","category":"rungs of the Hadamard-torus ladder W0 (.) (J + Z); RoAd_1 is the complex torus (C^x)^{m/2}, RoAd_2/4 are block-GL_2","explanation":"Zeros of W0 stay zero; covariance Mon x GL on output-scaled boxes, GL x Mon on down_proj, never GL x GL (Prop II.9)."},{"peft":"\"the geometry a method lives in\"","category":"covariance group G_M (Klein): the largest (g,h) with Im M(g W0 h^{-1}) = g Im M(W0) h^{-1}","explanation":"Descent depends on whether each induced pair (g,h) lies in G_M, a question of side for one-sided methods and of group for coordinate-structured ones. LoRA and (IA)^3 (HF defaults) provably keep their reachable function sets under a global residual rotation of a gain-fused pre-norm model; block OFT, LoHa (r >= 2), HiRA and masks fail the sufficient condition (Obs II.15, Conj A)."},{"peft":"how a method trains","category":"induced cometric K = D rho Lambda D rho^T (pulled-back gradient of the lens)","explanation":"Two isomorphic objects can train differently; gradient descent is natural exactly where cometrics agree, which for LoRA's linear gauge means O(r) (Thm III.3)."},{"peft":"balancedness B^T B = A A^T","category":"Noether charge of the non-compact half Sym_r of gl_r; the Kempf-Ness slice of the gauge action","explanation":"Conserved under gradient flow for any cotangent signal of the form Lambda D rho^T gamma (DoRA's detached norm, clipping), after Zhao et al. 2023 (Thm III.5, Prop III.7)."},{"peft":"LoRA+ ratio, alpha, init scale, rsLoRA","category":"coordinates on the orbit space of a hyperparameter torus","explanation":"Under Adam with eps=0, no weight decay, no clipping and a shared schedule, LoRA+ with ratio lambda is exactly LoRA with alpha times lambda (the LoRA case is Schulman et al. 2025); rsLoRA is isomorphic to LoRA (Thm III.10, Cor III.11)."},{"peft":"transformation-invariant optimizer (Riemannian preconditioning, ScaledGD, LoRA-Pro, LoRA-RITE)","category":"update rule equivariant under the whole gauge GL_r, so it descends to the quotient M_r","explanation":"Undamped scaled GD and LoRA-RITE (on the full-rank locus) are GL_r-equivariant at the parameter level; damped variants and LoRA-Pro's step are O(r)-equivariant, and Adam sees only B_r (Prop III.4, Thm III.12)."},{"peft":"GaLore vs LoRA","category":"cotangent-side vs tangent-side lens 2-cells; they coincide for linear charts (degree-one collapse)","explanation":"GaLore = one-sided LoRA with a moving frame, with optimizer state carried across re-basings (not transported) (Prop IV.2, credited to Torroba-Hennigen et al. 2025)."},{"peft":"when is a gradient-projection method really a reparametrisation","category":"Frobenius integrability of the anchor distribution","explanation":"Every practical cotangent method has a theta-independent anchor within its period, hence affine leaves, and is a rebasing scheme of a linear forward method (Prop IV.2); Frobenius integrability matters only for theta-dependent anchors such as full-batch GaLore with T=1 (Prop IV.3)."},{"peft":"activation memory","category":"backward reach of the composite lens (the backprop cone)","explanation":"An upper bound: LST avoids the backbone's backward pass; LoRA-FA stores A0 x; LISA's cone is the whole network because its embedding is always trained, though its frozen layers still drop their linear-input residuals; MeZO has no reverse pass (Obs IV.4)."},{"peft":"QLoRA / LoftQ","category":"base change along a map that does not preserve the function; pointing defect; approximate section","explanation":"Split-then-quantise, quantise-then-fit (LoftQ's default, whose HF init ignores the LoRA scale) and quantise-split-requantise leave three different defects (Prop V.2)."},{"peft":"ReLoRA, re-OFT, iterated BOFT/HRA","category":"the monoid (or Lie subgroup) generated by the reachable displacements","explanation":"ReLoRA reaches exactly M_{<=Kr}; exact-Cayley block OFT is stuck; iterated BOFT reaches SO(n) only at full butterfly depth; re-HRA reaches (W0 + M_{<=Kr}) cap W0 SO(n) (Thm V.3)."},{"peft":"task arithmetic vs TIES/DARE","category":"GL(Theta)-natural vs coordinatewise operations (TIES: homotheties times signed permutations; DARE: the monomial group)","explanation":"Coordinate merges depend on the basis; factor-space merges depend on the gauge (Prop V.4)."},{"peft":"LoraHub / Poly factor mixing","category":"a rule on factors that is not invariant under prod GL_r, hence not a function of the adapters","explanation":"Post-hoc LoraHub (and HF's 'linear' combination) needs a joint gauge fixing that produces a shared factor (KnOTS) or a shared frozen factor (FFA-LoRA); Poly/MHR are trained jointly and have only the diagonal GL_r symmetry (Prop V.4)."},{"peft":"adapters (Houlsby, parallel), LLaMA-Adapter, LoReFT","category":"pointed extensions: function-preserving embeddings with a neutral point","explanation":"LoReFT is LoRA on an inserted affine identity box (bias confined to col B), applied at selected positions; pyreft's default init is not neutral (Prop VI.4)."},{"peft":"prompt / prefix tuning","category":"unpointed extensions on the sequence wire","explanation":"Prefix = gated parallel adapter (attention = monoid homomorphism followed by a perspective map); content-attention ratios are rigid only within the layer where the prefix acts; prompt tuning maps into deep prefix tuning in causal decoders (Thm VI.5)."},{"peft":"privileged neuron basis","category":"automorphism group of the elementwise activation (the sigma-spider)","explanation":"For ungated FFNs: permutations for GELU/SiLU, signed permutations for tanh, positive monomials for ReLU; gated FFNs (SwiGLU, GeGLU) have the full monomial group (Lemma VI.2)."},{"peft":"RoSA, GraLoRA, HF 'cat' merges, ReLoRA stages","category":"words in the convolution monoidal product (+) of additive methods (Minkowski sum of images)","explanation":"(+) is not the categorical product, whose image is the intersection when it exists. LoRA-soup CAT is a (+) of frozen line methods (a span), and UniPELT/MAM are not (+)-words (Obs I.7)."},{"peft":"target_modules / applying a method to every layer","category":"assigning a 2-cell to each generator box and composing horizontally; T-points for routing, tasks and tenants","explanation":"Non-local methods (VB-LoRA, Compacter, FacT, Uni-LoRA) share parameters across boxes and are not generator-wise."}],"propositions":[{"id":"I.4","name":"universal properties","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation I.4 (universal properties).** [Obs] In $\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)$:\n\n(a) the frozen model $(\\mathrm{pt},*,\\theta_0)$ is initial, and full fine-tuning $(\\Theta,\\theta_0,\\mathrm{id})$ is terminal. The unique morphism $M\\to$ Full FT is $\\rho_M$ itself.\n\n(b) If the fibre product $Q_M\\times_\\Theta Q_N$ exists in $\\mathbf{Diff}$, it is the product $M\\times N$. It exists, for instance, when the set-level fibre product is an embedded submanifold of $Q_M\\times Q_N$: a clean intersection, or linear/affine $\\rho$'s as in Prop I.8(a). Transversality $\\rho_M\\pitchfork\\rho_N$ is never the reason when $\\lvert M\\rvert+\\lvert N\\rvert<\\dim\\Theta$, since at $(q_0,q_0')$ it would force $\\lvert M\\rvert+\\lvert N\\rvert\\ge\\dim\\Theta$. In $\\mathbf{Set}$ the fibre product always exists and $\\mathrm{Im}(M\\times N)=\\mathrm{Im}\\,M\\cap\\mathrm{Im}\\,N$. Whenever the product exists in $\\mathsf{Meth}$, its underlying set is this set-level fibre product, so the image identity holds in every case. The product can fail to exist: on $\\Theta=\\mathbb R$ ($1\\times1$ matrices), $\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\times\\text{Frozen}$ would have the cross $\\{BA=0\\}\\subset\\mathbb R^2$ as underlying set, and no manifold structure on it represents smooth maps into the cross. Both axes would have to lift smoothly through the point over the origin, which forces rank $2$ there.\n\n(c) $\\mathrm{Im}$ is a functor to the poset of subsets of $\\Theta$ containing $\\theta_0$. Conversely, $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im}\\,N$ yields a morphism of the set-level slice $\\mathbf{Set}_*/(\\Theta,\\theta_0)$, but not necessarily a smooth one. Take $\\rho_M(t)=t$ and $\\rho_N(u)=u^3$ on $\\mathbb R$, pointed at $0$: the images agree, yet no smooth $h:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfies $\\rho_N\\circ h=\\mathrm{id}$, i.e. $h(t)^3=t$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) The only pointed map $\\mathrm{pt}\\to Q$ is $*\\mapsto q_0$, and it lies over $\\Theta$ because $\\rho(q_0)=\\theta_0$. A morphism into the terminal object must satisfy $\\mathrm{id}\\circ h=\\rho_M$.\n\n(b) Products in a pointed slice are pullbacks in $\\mathbf{Diff}_*$. The forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Diff}_*\\to\\mathbf{Diff}$ creates limits, since $\\mathbf{Diff}_*$ is the coslice $\\mathrm{pt}/\\mathbf{Diff}$, and the induced map out of a pointed test object is pointed because its components are. Smooth maps landing in an embedded submanifold are smooth into it, which gives the sufficient condition. Transversality at $(q_0,q_0')$ means $d\\rho_M(T_{q_0})+d\\rho_N(T_{q_0'})=T_{\\theta_0}\\Theta$, and the left side has dimension at most $\\lvert M\\rvert+\\lvert N\\rvert$. A point $\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}M\\cap\\mathrm{Im}N$ has preimages $q,q'$, and $(q,q')$ lies in the fibre product. Testing a product in $\\mathsf{Meth}$ against pointed two-point $0$-manifolds shows that its points are exactly the pairs $(q,q')$ over a common $\\theta$. For $\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\times\\text{Frozen}$, suppose a product $P$ exists, with $\\iota:P\\to Q_{\\mathrm{LoRA}_1}=\\mathbb R^2$. The forgetful functor $\\mathbf{Diff}_*\\to\\mathbf{Diff}$ is a right adjoint (its left adjoint is $X\\mapsto X\\sqcup\\mathrm{pt}$), so $P$ is also the pullback in $\\mathbf{Diff}$ and may be tested with unpointed curves. Let $p^*$ be the unique point of $P$ over $(0,0)$. The two axis curves $t\\mapsto(t,0)$ and $t\\mapsto(0,t)$ lie over $\\theta_0$, so each lifts to a smooth curve in $P$ through $p^*$, and their velocities map under $d\\iota_{p^*}$ to $(1,0)$ and $(0,1)$. Hence $\\operatorname{rank}d\\iota_{p^*}=2$. Rank is lower semicontinuous and at most $2$, so it equals $2$ near $p^*$, and $\\iota$ maps a neighbourhood of $p^*$ onto a set containing an open subset of $\\mathbb R^2$. The cross contains none. The argument works for every pointing of $\\mathrm{LoRA}_1$. Under the standard pointing $(0,a_0)$ with $a_0\\neq0$, the base point is a smooth point of the cross and the obstruction sits at the origin.\n\n(c) Functoriality is $\\rho_N\\circ h=\\rho_M$. For the converse, choose (axiom of choice) for each $\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}M$ a preimage $s(\\theta)$ under $\\rho_N$ with $s(\\theta_0)=q_0'$, and put $h=s\\circ\\rho_M$. In the example the only candidate is the cube root, which is not differentiable at $0$. ∎","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Both referees confirmed (a)-(c). One referee (major) found the PEFT reading overreaching: transversality can never hold for two PEFT methods (|M|+|N| < dim Theta), Prop I.8(b) and Thm II.7 are image-level meets rather than products, 'every method' holds only for weight-space methods, merge-as-canonical-map is definitional, and 'no smooth h with h^3=id' was misworded. Repaired: the sufficient condition is now an embedded-submanifold (clean or linear) fibre product; added that products can fail to exist (LoRA_1 x Frozen on 1x1) and that an existing product has the set-level fibre product as underlying set; the proof addresses pointedness; S_1 is presented as only a first-order obstruction (t^3 vs u^5); commentary restricted.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor, faithful minor): statement and proof hold. Fixed: the non-existence of LoRA_1 x Frozen is now argued by the rank argument at p*, the point over the origin, with unpointed test curves (the forgetful functor is a right adjoint), so it covers every pointing including (0,a_0); 'PEFT regime' replaced by |M|+|N| < dim Theta. Commentary: the interval claim is restricted to methods pointed at theta_0 (HF FourierFT, VB-LoRA, init=False, LoRA-XS and quantised bases carry defects); mergeability as definitional is restricted to exact arithmetic on a full-precision base; Im M ∩ Im N separated from the product; meets are taken in the poset of subsets. Prop I.8's commentary now places the singularity at the origin, not at the base point.","public_statement":"Treat a weight-space PEFT method as a pointed smooth map $\\rho:(Q,q_0)\\to(\\Theta,\\theta_0)$ with $\\rho(q_0)=\\theta_0$. In this pointed slice category the frozen model is initial and full fine-tuning is terminal, and the unique map to full fine-tuning is $\\rho$ itself, i.e. merging into full-precision weights. The product of two methods is their fibre product over weight space whenever that fibre product exists as a manifold, for example for affine methods such as the two one-sided LoRAs whose product is LoRA-XS. When the product exists its image is the intersection of the two images. Products can fail to exist ($\\mathrm{LoRA}_1\\times$ Frozen on $1\\times1$ weights), and an inclusion of images gives only a set-level simulation, which need not be smooth ($t$ against $u^3$ on $\\mathbb R$)."},{"id":"I.6","name":"$S_1$ is functorial","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation I.6 ($S_1$ is functorial).** [Obs] If $M\\to N$ is a (pointed) morphism, then $S_1(M)\\subseteq S_1(N)$. In particular, isomorphic methods have equal $S_1$ and equal first-order deficit. Monotonicity holds only along pointed morphisms: a weak morphism, such as a gauge map moving $q_0$ inside its fibre, can change $S_1$.","proof":"*Proof.* Apply the chain rule $d\\rho_N\\circ dh=d\\rho_M$ at $q_0$, using $h(q_0)=q_0'$. If $h$ is an isomorphism, $dh_q$ is invertible for every $q$, so $\\operatorname{rank}d\\rho_M(q)=\\operatorname{rank}d\\rho_N(h(q))$. Hence $d(M)=d(N)$ (equivalently $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M=\\mathrm{Im}\\,N$), and since $dh_{q_0}$ is surjective, $S_1(M)=S_1(N)$. So the deficits agree. ∎","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: true as written. Wording fixes: the proof now shows d(M)=d(N) for isomorphisms (rank is invariant under a diffeomorphism); monotonicity is stated only along pointed morphisms. 'What this explains' rewritten: the lazy kernel depends on J_f and Lambda as well as S_1, Malladi et al. found random-A0 LoRA's kernel close to full fine-tuning's, and the image does distinguish split inits (it determines P).","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"If one weight-space method simulates another by a pointed map, the first-order image of the first lies inside that of the second, so isomorphic methods have the same first-order image and the same first-order deficit. Monotonicity holds only along pointed maps; a gauge move of the starting point inside its fibre can change the first-order image."},{"id":"I.7","name":"additive monoidal structure","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation I.7 (additive monoidal structure).** [Obs] $\\Theta$ is a vector space, so every object of $\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)$ can be written $\\rho=\\theta_0+\\delta$ with $\\delta:=\\rho-\\theta_0$ and $\\delta(q_0)=0$. Put\n$$M\\oplus N:=(Q_M\\times Q_N,(q_0,q_0'),\\theta_0+\\delta_M+\\delta_N).$$\nThis is a symmetric monoidal structure with the frozen model as unit. Its image is the Minkowski sum $\\theta_0+(\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\delta_M+\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\delta_N)$. The operation $\\oplus$ is not the categorical product, whose image, when the product exists, is $\\cap$ (Obs I.4(b)). \"Additive\" in Def I.2 ($\\delta$ independent of $\\theta_0$) is a condition on *families* $\\theta_0\\mapsto M(\\theta_0)$; it matters for base change (Exposé V), not here.\n\nWrite $\\mathrm{LoRA}_k$ for LoRA with frozen residual $\\theta_0$; for split pointings the sum below has residual $\\theta_0-P-P'$. Concatenation $h(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)=([B_1\\,B_2],[A_1;A_2])$ is an isomorphism $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_s\\cong\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}$ of unpointed objects. It is a pointed isomorphism onto $\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}$ pointed at $([B_0\\,B_0'],[A_0;A_0'])$. For $\\mathrm T_A$ pointings ($B_0=0$, $\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r$), the target pointing has $\\operatorname{rank}[A_0;A_0']=r+s$ iff $\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\cap\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0'=0$; it lies in the stratum $\\mathrm T_A$ of Thm II.2 when moreover $r+s<\\min(m,n)$.\n\nStandard pointings ($\\mathrm T_A$, or symmetrically $\\mathrm T_B$) exist only for $r<\\min(m,n)$ (Thm II.2), so the monoidal question is pointwise. If $F(r)$ and $F(2r)$ are standard ($2r<\\min(m,n)$), then $F(r)\\oplus F(r)\\not\\cong F(2r)$ as pointed objects; for $2r\\ge\\min(m,n)$ no standard $F(2r)$ exists. Hence no assignment $r\\mapsto\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ with $F(1)$ and $F(2)$ standard is **strong monoidal** into pointed objects. For $r\\neq s$ and standard pointings, $F(r)\\oplus F(s)\\cong F(r+s)$ as pointed objects exactly when the row space of the target's $A_0$ is the direct sum of the two source row spaces. The assignment is strong monoidal on unpointed objects, up to weak morphisms, and on pointed objects with degenerate pointings: the origin $(B_0,A_0)=(0,0)$, where $S_1=0$, or the stacked pointings $F(r)=F(1)^{\\oplus r}$. Concatenation is strictly associative and unital, so discrete $(\\mathbb N,+)$ suffices for a plain strong monoidal functor. *Symmetric* strong monoidality needs the permutation groupoid, since swapping and then concatenating permutes the blocks.\n\nWith per-adapter scales $s_r$ ($\\alpha/r$ for LoRA, $\\alpha/\\sqrt r$ for rsLoRA) and any target scale $s_*$, the map $h=([B_1\\,B_2],[c_1A_1;c_2A_2])$ with $c_i=s_{r_i}/s_*$ is an isomorphism over $\\Theta$. It is a linear bijection and preserves row spaces, so the pointing criterion is unchanged. HF PEFT's `add_weighted_adapter(..., combination_type=\"cat\")` evaluates such a map at trained points: it sets `lora_alpha` to the new rank, so $s_*=1$, and multiplies each $A_i$ by $w_is_{r_i}$, so $c_i=w_is_{r_i}$. It is the isomorphism $h$ when all weights $w_i=1$ and the merged adapter has `use_rslora=False`. For rsLoRA sources, released PEFT versions through v0.21.1 copy `use_rslora=True` into the merged config and so overscale the merge by $\\sqrt{r+s}$; PR #3449 on main fixes this.","proof":"*Proof.* The coherence isomorphisms of $\\oplus$ are those of the cartesian structure on $\\mathbf{Diff}_*$. They lie over $\\Theta$ because $+$ is associative and commutative, and the frozen model is a unit because its $\\delta$ is $0$. Next, $[B_1\\,B_2][A_1;A_2]=B_1A_1+B_2A_2$, and $h$ is a linear bijection. The pointing claim is $\\dim(U+V)=r+s-\\dim(U\\cap V)$ for $U=\\mathrm{row}A_0$, $V=\\mathrm{row}A_0'$. For the negative claim, any pointed isomorphism preserves $S_1$ (Obs I.6). If $F(r)$ is standard, $S_1(F(r)\\oplus F(r))=\\{XA_0+X'A_0\\}$ has dimension $mr$, while a standard $F(2r)$ has $\\dim S_1=2mr$. For $r\\neq s$, $S_1(F(r)\\oplus F(s))=\\{Y[A_0;A_0']\\}$ equals $S_1(F(r+s))=\\{XA_0''\\}$ iff $\\mathrm{row}A_0''=\\mathrm{row}A_0\\oplus\\mathrm{row}A_0'$; then concatenation followed by the gauge $g\\in GL_{r+s}$ with $g[A_0;A_0']=A_0''$ is a pointed isomorphism. The degenerate cases are direct. ∎ [Num: at $m=7$, $\\dim S_1=28$ for a standard $\\mathrm{LoRA}_4$, against $14$ for $\\mathrm{LoRA}_2\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_2$ with the same $A_0$ and $28$ with independent $A_0$'s; $F(1)\\oplus F(2)$ and a standard $F(3)$ both give $21$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted as written: 'strong monoidal only on unpointed objects' is false (the origin pointing and stacked pointings give pointed strong monoidal functors), and the claim was unproved for standard pointings. Repaired: no choice of standard pointings makes r -> LoRA_r strong monoidal, proved via S_1 dimensions (mr vs 2mr); (+) defined on all of Meth via the vector-space structure ('additive' is a condition on families); residuals and HF's alpha/r scaling handled; symmetric only over the permutation groupoid; the product's image is the intersection only when it exists. Commentary corrected: UniPELT and MAM are not (+)-words, and LoRA-soup CAT is a span of frozen line methods.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted on a PEFT sentence, minor; faithful refuted, major): the HF claim was false. HF's add_weighted_adapter(cat) sets the merged scale to 1 (lora_alpha = new rank) with c_i = w_i s_{r_i}, and released PEFT through v0.21.1 copies use_rslora=True, overscaling rsLoRA merges by sqrt(r+s) (fixed on main by PR #3449). Repaired: the isomorphism is stated for any target scale s_*, and HF's cat is described exactly. The negative monoidal claim is now pointwise (standard pointings exist only for r < min(m,n); threshold 2r >= min(m,n)), the r != s case is characterised by a direct-sum row-space condition, and plain strong monoidality from discrete (N,+) is separated from symmetric monoidality. Proof gains the coherence line. Commentary: the sum of k LoRAs is isomorphic (not equal) to LoRA_{kr}, with different training dynamics under alpha/r scaling; HF cat is h at trained points; CAT soups give a span of dimension at most k; ReLoRA ignores its warm start. Prop V.4's cat bullet made consistent.","public_statement":"Because weight space is a vector space, adding the updates of two weight-space methods on the product of their parameter spaces makes pointed methods a symmetric monoidal category, with the frozen model as unit and the Minkowski sum of updates as image. This sum is not the categorical product, whose image, when it exists, is the intersection. Block concatenation, with the $A$-blocks rescaled to match $\\alpha/r$-type scales, identifies $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_s$ with $\\mathrm{LoRA}_{r+s}$ over weight space, and as pointed methods exactly at the concatenated initialisation. For zero-$B$ initialisations the result is again a standard one iff $\\mathrm{row}A_0\\cap\\mathrm{row}A_0'=0$ and $r+s<\\min(m,n)$. No choice of standard initialisations makes $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ isomorphic to $\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r}$, since the first-order image has dimension $mr$ against $2mr$. HF PEFT's `cat` merge applies this map at trained points with merged scale $1$."},{"id":"I.8","name":"a product that produces a named method; an image-level meet","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition I.8 (a product that produces a named method; an image-level meet).** [Prop]\n\n(a) Let $A_0\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times n}$ have full row rank and $B_0\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}$ full column rank. Define the one-sided methods\n$$\\mathrm{FA}(A_0):B\\mapsto W_0+BA_0,\\qquad\\mathrm{FB}(B_0):A\\mapsto W_0+B_0A,$$\nboth pointed at $0$. Then\n$$\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\times\\mathrm{FB}(B_0)\\;\\cong\\;(\\mathbb R^{r\\times r},0,\\ R\\mapsto W_0+B_0RA_0).$$\nThe product supplies this sandwich for *any* frames. Assume $\\operatorname{rank}W_0\\ge r$ ($\\sigma_r>0$) and take the SVD frames $B_0=U_r$, $A_0=\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top$: the result is **LoRA-XS pointed at $R=0$**. The LoRA-XS paper writes $xW+xARB$ in row-vector convention with $A=U_r\\Sigma_r$ and $B=V_r^\\top$; transposed, the update is $B_0R^\\top A_0$, so the isomorphism is $R\\mapsto\\alpha R^\\top$. LoRA-XS initialises $R\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma=10^{-5}$, a negligible pointing defect.\n\n(b) Let $W_0$ have full row rank (so $m\\le n$), and let $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ carry its standard pointing ($B_0=0$, image $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$). The image-level meet of $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ with the output-scaling method $\\ell\\mapsto\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0$ is $\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0:\\#\\{i:\\ell_i\\neq1\\}\\le r\\}$, that is, \"rescale at most $r$ output neurons\". The output-scaling method is the key/value part of (IA)$^3$. On the FFN, (IA)$^3$ scales the *input* of $W_{\\rm down}$, $W\\mapsto W\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)$; the mirror statement then needs full column rank, or holds generically for $r<d$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) A point of the fibre product is a pair $(B,A)$ with $BA_0=B_0A=:D$. Then $\\mathrm{row}\\,D\\subseteq\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0$ and $\\mathrm{col}\\,D\\subseteq\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0$, so $D=B_0RA_0$ for a unique $R$. Since $A_0$ has full row rank, $BA_0=B_0RA_0$ forces $B=B_0R$, and likewise $A=RA_0$. So the fibre product is the $r^2$-dimensional linear subspace $\\{(B_0R,RA_0)\\}$, and $R\\mapsto(B_0R,RA_0)$ is a smooth bijection onto it with smooth inverse $(B,A)\\mapsto B_0^+B$. Any pair of maps $(h_1,h_2):T\\to Q_{\\rm FA}\\times Q_{\\rm FB}$ that agree over $\\Theta$ factors through it via $t\\mapsto B_0^+h_1(t)$, so this is the product in $\\mathbf{Diff}$. If $\\operatorname{rank}W_0<r$, then $\\Sigma_rV_r^\\top$ is rank-deficient and the LoRA-XS instance breaks.\n\n(b) $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0-W_0=\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)W_0$, and its rank is $\\#\\{i:\\ell_i\\ne1\\}$ because the rows of $W_0$ are independent. *No product in (b) for $n\\ge2$.* The set-level fibre product is $\\mathcal F=\\{(B,A,\\ell):W_0+sBA=\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0\\}$, whatever the pointing of LoRA$_r$. If a product $P$ existed, then, as in the proof of Obs I.4(b), its canonical map $\\iota$ would be a bijection onto $\\mathcal F$, $P$ would also be the pullback in $\\mathbf{Diff}$, and smooth curves in $\\mathcal F$ would lift to $P$. For every $B'$ and $A'$ the curves $t\\mapsto(tB',0,\\mathbf 1)$ and $t\\mapsto(0,tA',\\mathbf 1)$ lie in $\\mathcal F$ and lift through the point $p^*$ over $(0,0,\\mathbf 1)$, so the projection of $\\iota$ to the LoRA parameters has surjective differential at $p^*$; it is then a submersion near $p^*$, and its image contains a nonempty open set of pairs $(B,A)$. But every pair in that image has $BA=\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)W_0/s$, so the first row of $BA$ is parallel to the first row of $W_0$, which is nonzero. For $n\\ge2$ one of the $2\\times2$ minors expressing this is a polynomial in $(B,A)$ that is not identically zero, and its zero set has empty interior, a contradiction. For $m=n=1$ the fibre product is the graph of $\\ell=1+sBA/W_0$, and the product exists. ∎ [Num: the fibre product of (a) has dimension $4=r^2$ at $(m,n,r)=(6,8,2)$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct. Wording fixes: rank W0 >= r is needed for the LoRA-XS instance; LoRA-XS initialises R ~ N(0, 1e-10), a negligible defect now in the approximate-pointing footnote, and its row-vector convention gives the isomorphism R -> alpha R^T; LoRA carries its standard pointing in (b); (IA)^3 is an output scaling only on k and v (input-side on W_down). The gloss that LoRA_r x (IA)^3 is a new sparse-rescaling method was withdrawn: the meet is set-level only (the fibre product is singular), and with a fixed support it is masked (IA)^3 (cf. Stoehr et al. 2024).","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept. Round-2 knock-on (from I.4's faithful review): the commentary now places the singularity of the (b) fibre product at the origin, not at the base point, and describes its components.","public_statement":"Freezing the frame on either side of LoRA gives two one-sided methods, $B\\mapsto W_0+BA_0$ and $A\\mapsto W_0+B_0A$. Their categorical product is the sandwich $R\\mapsto W_0+B_0RA_0$, and with the SVD frames of $W_0$ it is exactly LoRA-XS. By contrast, what LoRA$_r$ and the output-scaling part of (IA)$^3$ can both reach is the set of weights that rescale at most $r$ output neurons; this is an intersection of images only, and on any weight with at least two inputs the two methods have no product.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the proof now contains the argument that LoRA_r and the output-scaling method of (b) have no product for n >= 2 (submersion at the point over the origin against a fibre product with empty interior; on a 1x1 weight the product exists), as in the paper's Appendix C; the public statement and the commentary in framework.md carry the n >= 2 condition."},{"id":"II.1","name":"pointings of an additive method","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.1 (pointings of an additive method).** [Thm; elementary. The rank-$2r$ consequence is known in practice as the PiSSA$\\to$LoRA conversion (Meng et al. 2024), implemented in HF PEFT] Let $M=(Q,q_0,\\theta_0+\\delta)$ be additive with shape $C=\\delta(Q)$. Write $-v+C$ for a translate, and $C-C:=\\{c-c':c,c'\\in C\\}$ for the Minkowski difference.\n\n(a) For each $q\\in Q$, the re-pointed method $M_q:=(Q,q,\\theta_0-\\delta(q)+\\delta(\\cdot))$, with frozen residual $\\theta_0-\\delta(q)$, is pointed at $\\theta_0$, and $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M_q=\\theta_0+(-\\delta(q)+C)$.\n\n(b) $\\bigcup_q\\mathrm{Im}\\,M_q=\\theta_0+(C-C)$.\n\n(c) For LoRA$_r$ on a single linear box with $s\\ne0$, $C-C=\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(2r,m,n)}$; for several boxes, $C-C$ is the product of the per-box varieties $\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(2r,m_i,n_i)}$. Hence **no exact pointing of LoRA$_r$ (frozen residual $\\theta_0-\\delta(q)$), whether zero-init or split (PiSSA, OLoRA, CorDA, LoRA-GA), makes an update $\\theta-\\theta_0$ of rank $>2r$ reachable in any adapted matrix.** More robustly, from *any* starting point $q_0$ of LoRA$_r$, pointed or not, $\\rho(q)-\\rho(q_0)=\\delta(q)-\\delta(q_0)$ has rank $\\le\\min(2r,m,n)$. Quantised-residual schemes (QLoRA, LoftQ, QPiSSA) are pointed only up to an order-dependent defect ($e$ for QLoRA; see Prop V.2(b) for the others); measured from $\\theta_0$ rather than from their starting weight, their updates contain this defect, whose rank is generically at least $\\min(m,n)-r$. The bound $2r$ is attained by split pointings when $2r\\le\\min(m,n)$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) and (b) are immediate from the definitions. (c) $\\operatorname{rank}(X-Y)\\le 2r$. Conversely, $C$ is a cone and so closed under negation; split the SVD of a rank-$\\le 2r$ matrix as $Z=Z_1+Z_2$ with $\\operatorname{rank}Z_i\\le r$, and write $Z=Z_1-(-Z_2)$. ∎","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct. Wording fixes: the method, not delta, is additive; translate and Minkowski difference distinguished; single box with s != 0 in (c), per-box products for several boxes. The bold conclusion now concerns exact pointings and adds the robust form (rank <= 2r measured from any starting point); quantised-residual schemes fall outside it when measured from theta0. Attributed to the PiSSA-to-LoRA conversion of Meng et al. 2024; split inits lose generic updates of every rank >= 1.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept. Round-2 knock-on (from V.2): the sentence on quantised-residual schemes now cites their order-dependent defects instead of a single defect e.","public_statement":"For an additive method, moving the starting point while subtracting a frozen residual keeps the method pointed at the pretrained weights, and the union of all such re-pointed images is $\\theta_0$ plus the difference set $C-C$ of its update shape. For LoRA$_r$ that difference set is the matrices of rank at most $\\min(2r,m,n)$, so no exact initialisation of LoRA$_r$, zero-init or split (PiSSA, OLoRA, CorDA, LoRA-GA), makes an update of rank above $2r$ reachable in any adapted matrix. Split initialisations attain $2r$ when $2r\\le\\min(m,n)$.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the quantised-residual sentence narrowed as in the paper: measured from theta_0, the updates contain the pointing defect, whose rank is generically at least min(m,n) - r (for one-step LoftQ at s = 1 it is exactly min(m,n) - r), instead of 'a generically full-rank part of the quantisation error'."},{"id":"II.2","name":"three strata of LoRA initialisations","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.2 (three strata of LoRA initialisations).** [Thm] Let $\\delta(B,A)=BA$ with $1\\le r<\\min(m,n)$. Consider pointed LoRA objects in three distinguished strata of full-rank pointings (pointings whose nonzero factors have full rank). Only $\\mathrm S$ is open and dense in the space of pointings; $\\mathrm T_A$ and $\\mathrm T_B$ are nowhere dense.\n- $(\\mathrm T_A)$: $B_0=0$ and $\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r$;\n- $(\\mathrm T_B)$: $A_0=0$ and $\\operatorname{rank}B_0=r$;\n- $(\\mathrm S)$: $\\operatorname{rank}B_0A_0=r$, with frozen residual $\\theta_0-B_0A_0$.\n\n(a) **Classification.** Within a stratum, $M_{q_0}\\cong M_{q_0'}$ iff, respectively, $\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0=\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0'$; $\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0=\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0'$; or $B_0A_0=B_0'A_0'$. Objects in different strata are never isomorphic. As a *set*, the isomorphism classes of the three strata therefore form\n$$\\mathrm{Gr}(r,\\mathbb R^n)\\ \\sqcup\\ \\mathrm{Gr}(r,\\mathbb R^m)\\ \\sqcup\\ \\mathcal M_r(m\\times n).$$\n(b) **First order.** $\\dim S_1$ is $mr$, $nr$ and $r(m+n-r)$ respectively, while $d=r(m+n-r)$ in every case. The deficits are $r(n-r)$, $r(m-r)$ and $0$.\n\n(c) **Geometry.** In $\\mathrm T_A$ and $\\mathrm T_B$, $\\mathrm{Im}=\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$, whose vertex $\\theta_0$ is an **apex**. In $\\mathrm S$, $\\mathrm{Im}=(\\theta_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ with $P=B_0A_0$, and $\\theta_0$ is a **smooth point**.\n\n(d) **Incomparability.** The images of $\\mathrm S$-type and $\\mathrm T$-type objects are incomparable.\n\n(e) **Scope.** The three strata do not exhaust the pointings of LoRA$_r$. Outside them lie: $B_0=0$ with $\\operatorname{rank}A_0<r$; $A_0=0$ with $\\operatorname{rank}B_0<r$; $B_0=A_0=0$; $0<\\operatorname{rank}B_0A_0<r$; and pointings with $B_0,A_0$ both nonzero and $B_0A_0=0$. HF PEFT ships one of the last kind: `init_lora_weights=\"orthogonal\"` (which requires $r$ even) lies in the stratum $(\\operatorname{rk}B_0,\\operatorname{rk}A_0,\\operatorname{rk}B_0A_0)=(r/2,r/2,0)$, an apex with $\\dim S_1=\\tfrac r2(m+n-\\tfrac r2)$. It is isomorphic to no object of the three strata. Its image $\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ rules out $\\mathrm S$, and its $S_1$ determines $(\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0,\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0)$, of dimensions $(r/2,r/2)$, against $(0,r)$ in $\\mathrm T_A$ and $(r,0)$ in $\\mathrm T_B$. The dimension of $S_1$ alone does not separate it, since at $(m,n,r)=(4,5,2)$ it equals $mr$. This placement holds in exact arithmetic; HF casts the two factors to the base dtype separately, and in bf16 the result is a generic pointing with a tiny defect. For every pointing, the image determines $P=B_0A_0$ (its vertex is $\\theta_0-P$), and $S_1=\\{XA_0+B_0Y\\}$ determines $(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0,\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0)$ whenever these are proper subspaces. The classification is set-level: in the space of pointings $\\mathrm T_A$ lies in the closure of $\\mathrm S$, since $(\\varepsilon B',A_0)\\to(0,A_0)$, so the quotient is not a topological disjoint union.","proof":"*Proof.*\n- *\"If\" in (a).* Two full-row-rank matrices with the same row space satisfy $A_0'=gA_0$ for a unique $g\\in GL_r$, and $(B,A)\\mapsto(Bg^{-1},gA)$ is a pointed isomorphism over $\\Theta$. $\\mathrm T_B$ is symmetric. In $\\mathrm S$, the full-rank factorisations of $P$ form one $GL_r$-orbit, and the residuals agree.\n- *\"Only if\" in (a).* In $\\mathrm T_A$, $d\\rho_{(0,A_0)}(\\dot B,\\dot A)=\\dot BA_0$. So $S_1=\\{XA_0\\}$ is the set of matrices whose row space lies in $\\mathrm{row}A_0$; it determines $\\mathrm{row}A_0$, and it is an isomorphism invariant by Obs I.6. $\\mathrm T_B$ is symmetric. In $\\mathrm S$, the image $(\\theta_0-P)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ is an isomorphism invariant and determines its vertex: $\\mathrm{Sing}\\,\\mathcal M_{\\le s}=\\mathcal M_{\\le s-1}$ for $1\\le s\\le r<\\min(m,n)$, algebraically and in the $C^1$ sense (a non-linear cone is not $C^1$ at its vertex), so iterating the singular locus $r$ times leaves the single point $\\theta_0-P$. Hence the image determines $P$. A shorter route avoids singular loci: if $c+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}=c'+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ with $D=c-c'\\neq0$, a generic $X$ of rank $r$ gives $\\operatorname{rank}(D+X)=\\min(\\operatorname{rk}D+r,m,n)\\ge r+1$, so $c+X\\notin c'+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$.\n- *Separating the strata.* $S_1(\\mathrm T_A)=\\{Z:\\mathrm{row}Z\\subseteq V\\}$ contains $e_iv^\\top$ for every $i$ and every $v\\in V$, so it equals $S_1(\\mathrm T_B)=\\{Z:\\mathrm{col}Z\\subseteq U\\}$ only if $U=\\mathbb R^m$, which is impossible since $r<m$. (When $m\\ne n$ the dimensions $mr\\ne nr$ already differ.) $\\mathrm S$ differs from both because $\\dim S_1=r(m+n-r)$ differs from $mr$ (as $n>r$) and from $nr$ (as $m>r$).\n- *(b).* At a full-rank pair, $S_1=\\{XA_0+B_0Y\\}=T_P\\mathcal M_r$. In general $\\dim\\{XA_0+B_0Y\\}=m\\operatorname{rk}A_0+n\\operatorname{rk}B_0-\\operatorname{rk}A_0\\operatorname{rk}B_0$ (Prop II.3).\n- *(c).* $\\mathcal M_r$ is open in $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ and is a manifold of dimension $r(m+n-r)$, so $P$, of rank $r$, is a smooth point, and $\\theta_0$ is a smooth point of the $\\mathrm S$-image. In the $\\mathrm T$ strata, $\\theta_0$ is the vertex of the non-linear cone $\\theta_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$, a singular point (Def I.5), and by (b) its deficit is positive, so it is an apex.\n- *(d).* A generic rank-one $X$ satisfies $\\operatorname{rank}(P+X)=\\operatorname{rank}(X-P)=r+1$. So $\\theta_0+X$ lies in the $\\mathrm T$-image but not in the $\\mathrm S$-image, and $\\theta_0-P+X$ lies in the $\\mathrm S$-image but not in the $\\mathrm T$-image.\n- *(e).* The dimension is Prop II.3. For every pointing, $S_1=\\{XA_0+B_0Y\\}=\\{Z:Z(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0)^\\perp\\subseteq\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0\\}$, whose Frobenius annihilator is $(\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0)^\\perp\\otimes(\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0)^\\perp$. When both subspaces are proper, the column and row spans of this annihilator recover them, so $S_1$ determines the pair and its dimensions, and by Obs I.6 so does every pointed isomorphism class. The image determines its vertex $\\theta_0-P$ by the translation argument above. HF's `orthogonal` init draws one orthogonal $Q\\in O(r)$ and sets $A_0=(G_AQ_{[0::2]})^\\top/10$, $B_0=G_BQ_{[1::2]}/10$, from the even- and odd-indexed rows of $Q$, so $B_0A_0=0$ with both factors of rank $r/2$. ∎ [Num: $\\dim S_1=(14,10,20)$ for $(\\mathrm T_A,\\mathrm T_B,\\mathrm S)$ at $(m,n,r)=(7,5,2)$, and $11$ for HF's `orthogonal` init.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Mathematics confirmed on the three strata, but the presentation overreached: the strata do not exhaust LoRA pointings (HF init_lora_weights='orthogonal' gives B0A0=0 with rank-r/2 factors, a new isomorphism class), LoftQ and QLoRA are defect pointings, init=False is unpointed, and 'moduli space' is only a set-level bijection. Repaired: title and claims restricted to three distinguished strata; scope clause (e) lists the excluded pointings and the invariants valid for every pointing; m=n separation argument added; the S-stratum 'only if' moved into the right bullet with the iterated-singular-locus argument; (c) argued from M_r open in M_{<=r} rather than 'follows from (b)'; table and Exposé 0 headline updated.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor, faithful minor): (a)-(d) hold. Fixed: 'generic (full-rank)' is now 'full-rank', with S open dense and T_A, T_B nowhere dense (also in Exposé 0); HF orthogonal init is separated from the strata by the S_1 subspace (annihilator (col B0)^perp ⊗ (row A0)^perp), since dim S_1 alone coincides with mr at (4,5,2); r even and exact-arithmetic caveats added; the S 'only if' gains the translation argument. Table: vanilla LoRA restricted to Linear/Conv; HF embedding LoRA added as the main T_B member; MiCA moved to a frozen-factor sub-object of T_B (also in rosetta.json); Astra and OLoRA rows match HF code; the PiSSA/LoRA-GA speed mechanism is now labelled [Conj].","public_statement":"For LoRA of rank $r<\\min(m,n)$ on one $m\\times n$ weight, take three strata of exact full-rank initialisations: $B_0=0$ with full-rank $A_0$ (vanilla LoRA on linear layers, EVA), $A_0=0$ with full-rank $B_0$ (Init[B], HF's default on embedding layers), and a rank-$r$ $B_0A_0$ subtracted from the frozen weight (PiSSA, MiLoRA, OLoRA, CorDA, LoRA-GA, Astra). Two pointed LoRA methods in these strata are isomorphic over weight space exactly when they share $\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0$, $\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0$ or $B_0A_0$ respectively, so as a set the classes are $\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)\\sqcup\\mathrm{Gr}(r,m)\\sqcup\\mathcal M_r$. Zero-factor initialisations sit at the apex of the cone $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ with $r(n-r)$ or $r(m-r)$ fewer first-order directions; split initialisations sit at a smooth point, and neither image contains the other. The result is set-level and in exact arithmetic, concerns expressivity and first-order reach, and excludes rank-deficient pointings such as HF's `orthogonal` init, frozen-factor sub-objects (LoRA-FA, MiCA) and quantised-base defects (QLoRA, LoftQ)."},{"id":"II.3","name":"first-order rank formula: smoothness, alignment and scale","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.3 (first-order rank formula: smoothness, alignment and scale).** [Prop] For $\\kappa(B,A)=W_{\\rm base}+sBA$ with $s\\ne0$,\n$$\\operatorname{rank}d\\kappa_{(B,A)}=m\\operatorname{rk}A+n\\operatorname{rk}B-\\operatorname{rk}A\\operatorname{rk}B\\qquad\\text{for all }(B,A),$$\nand $\\{XA\\}\\cap\\{BY\\}=\\{BRA:R\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times r}\\}$. For full-rank frames this overlap is the tangent space of the product $\\mathrm{FA}(A)\\times\\mathrm{FB}(B)$ of Prop I.8(a), re-based at $\\kappa(B,A)$, whose image is the meet of the two one-sided images: a generalised LoRA-XS. It is LoRA-XS proper when $(B,A)$ span the top-$r$ singular subspaces of $W_0$ ($\\sigma_r>0$), as at a PiSSA init; bottom frames (MiLoRA) give a generalised LoRA-XS.\n\nUnder gradient flow $\\dot B=-\\eta_B\\nabla_BL$, $\\dot A=-\\eta_A\\nabla_AL$, with $G=\\nabla_WL$,\n$$\\tfrac{d}{dt}L=-s^2\\big(\\eta_B\\|GA^\\top\\|_F^2+\\eta_A\\|B^\\top G\\|_F^2\\big)$$\nat every time, in particular at $(B_0,A_0)$. To first order in the step sizes the same holds for full-batch gradient descent, and in expectation for unbiased minibatch SGD. Under Adam ($\\varepsilon\\to0$, with bias correction) the first step changes the loss by $-\\lvert s\\rvert\\big(\\eta_B\\|GA_0^\\top\\|_1+\\eta_A\\|B_0^\\top G\\|_1\\big)+O(\\eta^2)$, with entrywise $\\ell_1$ norms. This first-order term is linear in $\\lvert s\\rvert$, against quadratic under gradient flow.","proof":"*Proof.* The image of $d\\kappa$ is $\\{XA\\}+\\{BY\\}$, whose summands are the matrices with row space in $\\mathrm{row}A$ (dimension $m\\operatorname{rk}A$) and with column space in $\\mathrm{col}B$ (dimension $n\\operatorname{rk}B$). A matrix $D$ lies in both iff $D\\in\\mathrm{col}B\\otimes\\mathrm{row}A$, of dimension $\\operatorname{rk}A\\operatorname{rk}B$, and then $D=B(B^+DA^+)A$. The gradient-flow rate is $\\langle G,\\dot W\\rangle$ with $\\dot W=-s^2(\\eta_BGA^\\top A+\\eta_ABB^\\top G)$. Adam's first step is $\\delta B=-\\eta_B\\,\\mathrm{sign}(sGA^\\top)$, so $\\langle G,s\\,\\delta B\\,A\\rangle=-\\lvert s\\rvert\\eta_B\\|GA^\\top\\|_1$, and likewise for $A$. ∎ [Num: the rank formula holds in 400 random cases including rank-deficient factors; the rate matches finite differences.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Rank formula and overlap identity confirmed for all (B,A). Defects: kappa lacked the scale s although the rate carries s^2; the rate needs a gradient-flow hypothesis (Adam's first step gives an entrywise l1 rate, linear in s); 'LoRA-XS in the frames' is a generalised LoRA-XS. Commentary errors: EVA does not maximise ||G A0^T|| (it is a data-only proxy valid under its own assumptions; LoRA-GA's gradient SVD gives the maximising A_0 (its B_0 is not the maximiser; see round 2)), and factor scale is a third, confounding mechanism. All repaired in the statement and the commentary.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor, faithful minor): statement and proof hold. Fixed: the overlap is the tangent space of the product FA x FB (whose image is the meet); LoRA-XS proper needs top-r frames; SGD holds to first order for full-batch GD and in expectation for minibatches; Adam rate written with |s| and as a first-order term. Commentary: LoRA-GA aligns only A_0 (its B_0 = U_{r+1:2r} gives up rate to approximate the rank-2r truncation of G); HF's scale='stable' scales only B; EVA's Thm 3.2 assumptions and HF's centred PCA stated correctly; alignment is a gradient-flow/SGD notion; 'leave the apex' corrected to 'start off the apex'. The round-1 note that LoRA-GA's gradient SVD is the maximiser holds for A_0 only.","public_statement":"For LoRA's map $W_{\\rm base}+sBA$ with $s\\ne0$, the differential has rank $m\\,\\mathrm{rk}A+n\\,\\mathrm{rk}B-\\mathrm{rk}A\\,\\mathrm{rk}B$ at every point. The $A$-moves and $B$-moves overlap exactly in $\\{BRA\\}$, so zero-factor initialisations (LoRA, QLoRA, EVA, LoRA-FA) start with $mr$ first-order directions and full-rank split initialisations (PiSSA, OLoRA, CorDA, LoRA-GA) with $r(m+n-r)$; at a PiSSA init the overlap is LoRA-XS's update space. Under gradient flow the loss falls at rate $s^2(\\eta_B\\|GA^\\top\\|_F^2+\\eta_A\\|B^\\top G\\|_F^2)$. Among orthonormal $A_0$ at zero init, the top right singular space of $G$ maximises this, and that is LoRA-GA's $A_0$ (not its $B_0$). Adam's first step ($\\varepsilon\\to0$) lowers the loss, to first order in the step size, by $\\lvert s\\rvert(\\eta_B\\|GA_0^\\top\\|_1+\\eta_A\\|B_0^\\top G\\|_1)$, which is linear in $\\lvert s\\rvert$ and depends on the basis of $A_0$."},{"id":"II.4","name":"zero gate","kind":"lemma","statement":"**Lemma II.4 (zero gate).** [Lemma] Let $Q=U\\times V$ with $U$ a vector space, let $\\rho=\\theta_0+\\beta(u,v)$ with $\\beta$ smooth, and suppose $\\beta(u_0,v)=0$ for all $v$; for example $\\beta(\\cdot,v)$ linear and $u_0=0$. Then $D_v\\rho_{q_0}=0$. Hence $\\nabla_v(L\\circ\\rho)(q_0)=0$, $S_1=D_u\\beta(u_0,v_0)(U)$ ($=\\beta(U,v_0)$ in the linear case), and the cometric at $q_0$ is $K_{q_0}=D_u\\beta\\,\\Lambda_{uu}\\,D_u\\beta^\\top$, where $\\Lambda_{uu}$ is the $uu$-block of $\\Lambda$; no block-diagonality is needed, since the $v$-columns of $D\\rho$ vanish. The weight trajectory $\\theta(t)$ agrees with that of the method with $v$ frozen at $v_0$ to first order in $t$ (gradient flow), or in the step size (one or more Adam steps). The parameter $v$ itself may still move: under terms not of the form $L\\circ\\rho$ (regularisers, decoupled weight decay), and under Adam, whose step-2 move of $v$ is $O(\\eta)$ although its effect on $\\theta$ is $O(\\eta^2)$.\n\n*Function-level variant.* For an extension with realised function $F(u,v,x)$, if $F(u_0,v,x)=F_0(x)$ for all $v$ and $x$, then $D_vF=0$ at $q_0$, with the same conclusions at the level of functions. No linearity is needed.","proof":"*Proof.* $\\beta(u_0,v)=0$ for all $v$, so $D_v\\beta(u_0,v_0)=0$. The rest follows from the chain rule. The function-level variant is identical. ∎ [Num: under Adam with $\\eta\\in\\{10^{-2},10^{-3},10^{-4}\\}$, the step-2 move of $A$ in zero-init LoRA scales as $\\eta$, while the deviation of $\\Delta W$ from the frozen-$A$ method scales as $\\eta^2$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: lemma correct. Wording repaired: hypotheses stated explicitly and weakened to beta(u0,v)=0; K uses the uu-block of Lambda; 'frozen' stated for the weight trajectory to first order (v itself may move under regularisers, weight decay, or Adam at step 2); a function-level variant added for LLaMA-Adapter and zero-up-projection adapters. Instance corrections: AdaLoRA's orthogonality penalty moves P and Q at step one; dim S_1 = r only for generic P, Q; LoRA and LoRA-FA agree at first order only for LoRA-FA's original plain-gradient form.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"If a method's update vanishes identically while one block of parameters sits at its initial value, as LoRA's does at $B=0$, then the other block receives zero gradient at initialisation, the first-order image and the initial cometric come from the first block alone, and the weight trajectory agrees to first order with the method that freezes the other block (under gradient flow, or Adam to first order in the step size). Examples are LoRA at $B=0$, VeRA, AdaLoRA at $\\Lambda=0$, LLaMA-Adapter's zero gate, and adapters with a zero up-projection."},{"id":"II.5","name":"complete invariants of the basic actions","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.5 (complete invariants of the basic actions).** [Thm; classical] Let $W,W'\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$.\n1. *Right orthogonal (input side).* $W'=WR$ for some $R\\in O(n)$ iff $WW^\\top=W'W'^\\top$. For $SO(n)$, when $\\operatorname{rank}W=n$, also require that the then unique $R$ have $\\det R=1$.\n2. *Left orthogonal.* $W'=PW$ for some $P\\in O(m)$ iff $W^\\top W=W'^\\top W'$.\n3. *Two-sided.* $W'=PWR$ for some $P\\in O(m)$ and $R\\in O(n)$ iff $W$ and $W'$ have the same singular values, counted with multiplicity.\n4. *Left torus.* If $W$ has no zero row, $W'=DW$ for some $D\\in T_m^+$ iff $w_i'\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}w_i$ for every $i$. For the full torus $T_m$, whose signs are unconstrained, the complete invariant is the set of zero rows together with the row *lines*.\n5. *Full Hadamard torus.* $W'=E\\odot W$ for some $E\\in(\\mathbb R^\\times)^{m\\times n}$ iff $W$ and $W'$ have the same zero pattern.","proof":"*Proof.* (1) If all inner products of rows agree, the map $\\sum c_iw_i\\mapsto\\sum c_iw_i'$ is well defined and isometric on the row spans, because $\\|\\sum c_iw_i\\|^2=c^\\top WW^\\top c$. The two row spans have the same dimension, $\\operatorname{rank}WW^\\top$. Extend the map by an isometry of the orthogonal complements to $R^\\top\\in O(n)$; then $W'=WR$. The converse is immediate. (2) is (1) applied to transposes. (3) follows from the SVD. (4) and (5) hold entry by entry; for (5) put $E_{ij}=W'_{ij}/W_{ij}$ on the support and $1$ off it. ∎","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: all five items true and classical. Wording fixes: quantifiers P in O(m), R in O(n) and multiplicity in (3); T_m^+ defined in the Conventions; (4) stated for rays, and for the full torus T_m (row lines plus zero rows); the full Hadamard torus distinguished from LoHa's two-sided diagonal torus; orientation condition for SO(n) when rank W = n; the first-fundamental-theorem remark made precise. Added the caveat that real methods ((IA)^3, DoRA, HiRA) can create zeros, so only 'zeros of W0 stay zero' transfers.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"Classical complete invariants decide when two weights differ by a basic action. They are related by an input-side orthogonal map iff they have the same neuron Gram matrix $WW^\\top$, by an output-side one iff they have the same $W^\\top W$, by orthogonal maps on both sides iff they have the same singular values, by a positive output scaling (for weights without zero rows) iff corresponding rows are positive multiples of each other, and by an entrywise nonzero Hadamard factor iff they have the same zero pattern."},{"id":"II.6","name":"what exactly orthogonal methods can never do","kind":"corollary","statement":"**Corollary II.6 (what exactly orthogonal methods can never do).** [Cor] Let a method have image in $W_0\\cdot O(n)$ (input side), and work in exact arithmetic. This covers:\n- OFT and block OFT with an exactly orthogonal parametrisation: the original paper's exact Cayley map, and HF PEFT from 0.16 with `use_cayley_neumann=False` on Linear and Conv targets;\n- GOFT with strict Givens rotations and no scaler, as in the paper (`no_scaling=True` in the official peft-givens code);\n- HRA, and ETHER as defined in its paper (not ETHER+);\n- any number of merge-and-restart cycles of these.\n\nThen:\n- it preserves $K_{\\rm out}$, hence every neuron norm and the angle between any two nonzero neurons, and so the hyperspherical energy, which depends only on these angles;\n- it preserves the whole singular spectrum of $W_0$;\n- block-diagonal OFT with input blocks $J$, with the same partition in every cycle, preserves each partial Gram matrix $W_{:,J}W_{:,J}^\\top$.\n\n*Remarks; these do not follow from the hypothesis.*\n- *BOFT* as implemented in HF PEFT, $W=\\mathrm{diag}(s)W_0R^\\top$ with $R$ exactly orthogonal, has $K_{\\rm out}(W)=\\mathrm{diag}(s)K_{\\rm out}(W_0)\\mathrm{diag}(s)$. So $\\cos'_{ij}=\\mathrm{sign}(s_is_j)\\cos_{ij}$ whenever $s_is_j\\neq0$. It preserves $\\lvert\\cos\\rvert$ when every $s_i$ is nonzero, and preserves $\\cos_{ij}$ whenever $s_is_j>0$. Hence it preserves every cosine when all $s_i$ are nonzero and share a sign (for instance $s\\in T_m^+$, the constraint in the re-scaled OFT of Qiu et al. 2023, §3.5); when no cosine of $W_0$ is zero, that condition is also necessary. HF's `boft_s` is an unconstrained real initialised at $1$. At the default `boft_n_butterfly_factor=1` the butterfly permutation is the identity, BOFT is block OFT times an output scale, and each input block satisfies $W_{:,J}W_{:,J}^\\top=\\mathrm{diag}(s)(W_0)_{:,J}(W_0)_{:,J}^\\top\\mathrm{diag}(s)$. HF PEFT OFT 0.14 to 0.15 (exact Cayley, input side) carried a trainable output scale `oft_s` and has the same form.\n- *GOFT's official code* by default (`no_scaling=False`) adds a trainable input-side diagonal $g$, initialised at ones. Then $W=W_0\\mathrm{diag}(g)G$ and $K_{\\rm out}(W)=W_0\\mathrm{diag}(g)^2W_0^\\top$, whatever the Givens part $G$ is. This is the paper's GOFT* ablation, and it is not covered.\n- *Output-side implementations* (LyCORIS diag-OFT at its defaults; HF PEFT OFT up to 0.13, which merged to $R^\\top W_0$; HF OFT on embedding layers; ETHER with `flip_side=True`) have image in $O(m)\\cdot W_0$. They preserve $K_{\\rm in}$, the partial input Grams $W_{I,:}^\\top W_{I,:}$ and the spectrum, not $K_{\\rm out}$. ETHER's official code with `ether_nb>1` applies a different reflection to each output-row group, which keeps neuron norms and within-group angles but neither the cross-group cosines nor the spectrum.\n- *HF PEFT's default OFT* since 0.18 (`use_cayley_neumann=True`, 5 terms) builds $R=I+2Q+2Q^2+2Q^3+Q^4=C(Q)(I-Q^4)$, where $C(Q)=(I+Q)(I-Q)^{-1}$ is the exact Cayley map (HF's exact branch evaluated at $-Q$). Hence $R^\\top R=(I-Q^4)^2\\ne I$, and the singular values of $R$ are $\\lvert1-\\theta_k^4\\rvert$, where $\\pm i\\theta_k$ are the eigenvalues of $Q$: a systematic contraction for small $Q$, and $R$ is singular when some $\\theta_k=1$. Already the adapted weight has $K_{\\rm out}=W_0(I-Q^4)^2W_0^\\top$, a drift of order $\\lVert Q\\rVert^4$, and merge cycles compound it. For $\\lVert Q\\rVert_{\\rm op}\\le2^{1/4}$ every factor is a contraction, so the drift is a monotone shrinkage of neuron norms and singular values (Thm V.3, corollary 4). In 0.16 and 0.17 the last coefficient was $2$, so $R^\\top R=I+4Q^6+4Q^8$, still not orthogonal, with drift of order $\\lVert Q\\rVert^6$. (HF's docstring says the default is `False`; the dataclass default is `True`.)\n- *Quasi-orthogonal variants* preserve neither $K_{\\rm out}$ nor its normalised form exactly: qGOFT, whose $2\\times2$ blocks are arbitrary matrices with only a soft orthogonality penalty; ETHER+, which is two-sided and whose factor $I-uu^\\top+vv^\\top$ is singular when $u\\perp v$; and the default HF OFT above. Once $n>2$, a single non-orthogonal $2\\times2$ block, conformal or not, generically changes some cosine.\n- *Finite precision.* The invariance is exact only in exact arithmetic; low-precision merges add a rounding drift that grows like a random walk over merge cycles.","proof":"*Proof.* Use Thm II.5(1) and (3), and the fact that a product of orthogonal factors is orthogonal. $(WR)_{:,J}=W_{:,J}R_J$ for block-diagonal $R$. For BOFT, $K_{\\rm out}(W)_{ij}=s_is_jK_{\\rm out}(W_0)_{ij}$, so $\\cos'_{ij}=\\mathrm{sign}(s_is_j)\\cos_{ij}$ when $s_is_j\\ne0$. For GOFT with its scaler, $W_0\\mathrm{diag}(g)GG^\\top\\mathrm{diag}(g)W_0^\\top=W_0\\mathrm{diag}(g)^2W_0^\\top$. For the Cayley–Neumann map, $(I-Q)(I+Q+Q^2+Q^3)=I-Q^4$, $C(Q)$ is orthogonal and commutes with $Q^4$, and $Q^4$ is symmetric with eigenvalues $\\theta_k^4$ for skew $Q$. ∎ [Num: with $s=(-0.5,1,\\dots,1)$, BOFT changes a cosine by $1.5$ while $\\lvert\\cos\\rvert$ is fixed to $4\\times10^{-16}$, and an all-negative $s$ fixes every cosine to $10^{-15}$; GOFT with a scaler $g=1\\pm0.05$ moves cosines and singular values; a conformal block $\\sqrt2\\,\\mathrm{Rot}(0.3)$ on two of $8$ input coordinates changes a cosine; HF's default OFT satisfies $R^\\top R=(I-Q^4)^2$ to $5\\times10^{-16}$, with $\\lVert R^\\top R-I\\rVert=1.5\\times10^{-2}$ already for block size $8$ and skew entries of size about $0.07$, and is singular at $\\theta=1$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted in two places. (1) The BOFT bullet was false: HF's boft_s is an unconstrained real, so cosines are preserved only up to sign(s_i s_j) (exactly when all s_i are nonzero and share a sign, a condition that is necessary when no cosine of W_0 is zero), and at the default butterfly factor 1 BOFT preserves more than the cosines. (2) 'Covers OFT' was false for HF PEFT's default Cayley-Neumann OFT, where R = C(Q)(I - Q^4) and R^T R = (I - Q^4)^2 != I, so K_out and the spectrum drift across merges; HF <= 0.13 and LyCORIS OFT act on the output side. Repaired: hypothesis restricted to exactly orthogonal input-side parametrisations with a fixed partition; BOFT, output-side OFT, Cayley-Neumann OFT, qGOFT (arbitrary 2x2 blocks) and ETHER+ moved to separate remarks; the knowledge-preservation gloss softened (ETHER control study).","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, minor): the corollary and proof hold, but coverage and remarks overreached. GOFT's official code adds a trainable input scaler by default (no_scaling=False), so only strict GOFT is covered. The BOFT 'iff s in T_m^+' was false (all-negative s also preserves cosines); the round-1 phrase 'exactly iff s > 0' is corrected to 'when all s_i are nonzero and share a sign, necessary if no cosine of W_0 is zero'. Repaired: coverage restricted (HF OFT from 0.16 with the flag off on Linear/Conv; strict GOFT; ETHER as in its paper); remarks added for GOFT's scaler, HF OFT 0.14-0.15's oft_s, OFT on embeddings and ETHER flip_side/ether_nb>1 (output side or group-wise), the PEFT-version history of the Cayley-Neumann map (0.16-0.17: R^T R = I+4Q^6+4Q^8; since 0.18: (I-Q^4)^2, singular values |1-theta^4|, singular at theta=1, drift already in the adapted weight), conformal 2x2 blocks, and finite precision. Headline and commentary now say 'in exact arithmetic'; DoRA dropped as an example of a non-orthogonal factor.","public_statement":"If every weight a method produces, including after merge-and-restart cycles, has the form $W_0R$ with $R$ exactly orthogonal on the input side, then in exact arithmetic the neuron Gram matrix $WW^\\top$ never changes. So every neuron norm, every angle between neurons (and with them the hyperspherical energy) and every singular value of $W_0$ stay fixed, and block OFT with a fixed partition also fixes each block Gram. This covers exact-Cayley OFT and block OFT, strict GOFT without its optional scaler, HRA, and ETHER as defined in its paper. It does not cover variants with a per-neuron or per-input scale (HF BOFT, HF OFT 0.14 to 0.15, GOFT's default code), output-side variants, or approximately orthogonal ones such as HF PEFT's default Cayley-Neumann OFT, qGOFT and ETHER+."},{"id":"II.7","name":"HRA is the meet of LoRA with the rotation orbit, and it starts at an apex","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.7 (HRA is the meet of LoRA with the rotation orbit, and it starts at an apex).** [Thm] Let $W_0\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ be injective ($\\operatorname{rank}W_0=n$), and let $r$ be even with $2\\le r\\le n-2$. Define $\\mathrm{HRA}_r:(u_1,\\dots,u_r)\\mapsto W_0H_{u_1}\\cdots H_{u_r}$ with $u_i\\ne0$, where $H_u=I-2uu^\\top/\\|u\\|^2$. Write $\\mathrm{OFT}_{SO}:=W_0\\cdot SO(n)$ for the rotation orbit. (The $O(n)$-orbit would add the odd component: $W_0H_{e_1}$ lies in $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ but not in $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r$.)\n\n(a) **Meet.** $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r=(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap W_0\\cdot SO(n)$. Further, $d(\\mathrm{HRA}_r)=rn-\\tfrac{r(r+1)}2$, so the generic fibre has dimension $\\tfrac{r(r+1)}2=rn-d$. This gauge is non-linear. The evident continuous global symmetries are the $r$ scalings $u_i\\mapsto c_iu_i$; the braid group also acts globally by Hurwitz moves $(u_i,u_{i+1})\\mapsto(H_{u_i}u_{i+1},u_i)$, since $H_aH_b=H_{H_ab}H_a$.\n\n(b) **First-order collapse.** At the paired initialisation $u_{2i-1}=u_{2i}=w_i$ ($i\\le k=r/2$, with the $w_i$ independent and $U=\\mathrm{span}\\{w_i\\}$),\n$$S_1=W_0\\cdot\\{\\Omega\\in\\mathfrak{so}(n):P_{U^\\perp}\\Omega P_{U^\\perp}=0\\},\\qquad\\dim S_1=kn-\\tfrac{k(k+1)}2<d(\\mathrm{HRA}_r).$$\n(c) **Vertex.** The germ of $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r$ at $W_0$ is diffeomorphic to the germ at $0$ of the skew-determinantal cone $\\Sigma_r=\\{\\Omega\\in\\mathfrak{so}(n):\\operatorname{rank}\\Omega\\le r\\}$, which is not linear. So $W_0$ is a singular point (the vertex) of the image. Together with (b), the paired initialisation is an **apex**.\n\n(d) **Scope.**\n- *Not a product.* (a) is a meet in the poset of subsets of $\\Theta$ containing $W_0$ (Obs I.4(c)). HRA is not the categorical product of LoRA$_r$ and OFT$_{SO}$: the set-level fibre product $\\{(B,A,R):W_0+BA=W_0R\\}$ has local dimension $d+r^2=rn+\\tfrac{r(r-1)}2\\ne rn$ over a generic point of the meet. (It is not pure-dimensional: over $W_0$ alone it contains $\\{(B,0,I)\\}$, of dimension $mr$.)\n- *Exactly orthogonal OFT is smaller.* The Cayley map never produces a rotation with eigenvalue $-1$. For exact full-block Cayley OFT (the original paper; HF with `use_cayley_neumann=False`), $(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{OFT}$ is dense in $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r$ but not equal to it: $W_0H_{e_1}H_{e_2}$, with $H_{e_1}H_{e_2}=\\mathrm{diag}(-1,-1,1,\\dots,1)$, is missing. For exact block Cayley OFT with $b<n$ the intersection is a proper subset of $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r$.\n- *HF's default OFT is incomparable.* HF PEFT's default OFT (`use_cayley_neumann=True`) is not orthogonal (Cor II.6). Since $R^\\top R=(I-Q^4)^2=I$ forces $Q=0$ near $Q=0$, its image meets $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HRA}_r$ near $W_0$ only at $W_0$, while its intersection with $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ contains non-orthogonal points such as $W_0(I+2Q+2Q^2+2Q^3+Q^4)$ with $\\operatorname{rank}Q=2$.\n- *Implementation.* The theorem concerns HRA with $\\lambda<\\infty$ (HF `apply_GS=False`). The strictly orthogonal Gram–Schmidt variant has the smaller image $W_0(I-2P_U)$, of dimension $r(n-r)$. The paired initialisation is HF's default (`repeat_interleave`) and is not stated in the HRA paper. HF HRA acts on `in_features`, so injectivity requires `out_features` $\\ge$ `in_features` (and full column rank); the theorem does not cover `down_proj` or GQA key/value projections. The Gram–Schmidt variant never contains $W_0$, since $I-2P_U\\ne I$, and at HF's default paired initialisation it runs Gram–Schmidt on duplicated columns, which is numerically degenerate.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) \"$\\subseteq$\": each $H_u-I$ has rank one, so $R=\\prod H_{u_i}$ fixes $\\bigcap u_i^\\perp$ and $\\operatorname{rank}(R-I)\\le r$. Also $\\det R=(-1)^r=1$.\n\n\"$\\supseteq$\": suppose $W_0R=W_0+X$ with $\\operatorname{rank}X\\le r$ and $R\\in SO(n)$. Injectivity gives $k':=\\operatorname{rank}(R-I)\\le r$. By the Cartan–Dieudonné theorem in Scherk's sharp form, $R$ is a product of exactly $k'$ reflections, and $\\det R=(-1)^{k'}$ forces $k'$ to be even. Pad with $(r-k')/2$ pairs $H_uH_u=I$.\n\nDimension: a generic element is determined by its moved space $\\mathrm{im}(R-I)\\in\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)$, of dimension $r(n-r)$, together with a fixed-point-free element of $SO(r)$ on it, an open set of dimension $r(r-1)/2$. The generic fibre then has dimension $rn-d$.\n\n(b) Normalise $\\hat w=w/\\|w\\|$. A direct computation gives\n$$\\tfrac{d}{d\\varepsilon}\\big(H_{w+\\varepsilon a}H_{w+\\varepsilon b}\\big)\\big|_0=\\tfrac{2}{\\|w\\|}\\big(v\\hat w^\\top-\\hat wv^\\top\\big),\\qquad v=(I-\\hat w\\hat w^\\top)(a-b).$$\nSince every pair equals $I$ at $q_0$, the differential of the product is the sum of the pair differentials. So $S_1=W_0\\cdot\\{\\sum_iv_i\\wedge\\hat w_i\\}$, where $v\\wedge w:=vw^\\top-wv^\\top$.\n\nThis set is contained in $\\{\\Omega:P_{U^\\perp}\\Omega P_{U^\\perp}=0\\}$, because $y^\\top(v\\hat w^\\top-\\hat wv^\\top)x=0$ for $x,y\\perp\\hat w$. Conversely, the right-hand side is spanned by $e_a\\wedge e_b$ with $e_a\\in U$, and each of these is a combination of the elements $\\hat w_i\\wedge e_b$. Its dimension is $\\dim\\mathfrak{so}(n)-\\dim\\mathfrak{so}(n-k)$, and $W_0$ is injective. Finally,\n$$d-\\dim S_1=k(2n-3k-1)/2>0\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 2k+2.$$\n\n(c) Use the Cayley map $C(\\Omega)=(I+\\Omega)(I-\\Omega)^{-1}$. It is a diffeomorphism from a neighbourhood of $0\\in\\mathfrak{so}(n)$ onto a neighbourhood of $I\\in SO(n)$, and $C(\\Omega)-I=2\\Omega(I-\\Omega)^{-1}$, so $\\operatorname{rank}(C(\\Omega)-I)=\\operatorname{rank}\\Omega$. Composing with the linear embedding $R\\mapsto W_0R$ identifies the germs. $\\Sigma_r$ is a closed cone. It is not linear, because a rank-$r$ and a rank-$2$ skew matrix can sum to rank $r+2\\le n$. A closed cone that is $C^1$ at its vertex is linear, so $0$ is singular. The apex statement is (b): a singular image alone would not give a positive deficit.\n\n(d) Over a generic point of the meet, the LoRA fibre is a $GL_r$-orbit and the $SO(n)$ fibre is a point, since $W_0$ is injective. If $C(\\Omega)x=-x$, then $y=(I-\\Omega)^{-1}x$ satisfies $(I+\\Omega)y=-(I-\\Omega)y$, so $y=0$. Density: perturb the angles $\\pi$ inside the same moved planes. The Gram–Schmidt variant uses orthonormal $\\hat u_i$, and $\\prod(I-2\\hat u_i\\hat u_i^\\top)=I-2P_U$. ∎ [Num: at $n=9$, the paired-init Jacobian has rank $8$ against generic rank $15$ for $r=2$, and $15$ against $26$ for $r=4$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Mathematics of (a)-(c) confirmed. Refuted identification: 'HRA = LoRA cap OFT' and 'pullback' hold only at the level of images and only for the full orbit W0.SO(n): Cayley OFT misses rotations with eigenvalue -1, block OFT is smaller, and the fibre product has dimension rn + r(r-1)/2 != rn, so HRA is not the categorical product. Repaired: retitled to the full orthogonal orbit; scope clause (d) added (not a product; Cayley and block OFT smaller; lambda < infinity / apply_GS=False only; the paired init is HF's code convention; injectivity needs out_features >= in_features); 'generic gauge' renamed generic fibre dimension; the apex is derived from (b), not from singularity.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, minor; faithful refuted, major): (a)-(c) re-verified numerically. The OFT comparison failed for HF's default Cayley-Neumann OFT, which is not orthogonal: its meet with W_0 + M_{<=r} contains non-orthogonal points and near W_0 meets Im HRA only at W_0. Repaired: 'Real OFT is smaller' restricted to exactly orthogonal (exact-Cayley) OFT, with a new bullet that HF's default OFT is incomparable; 'full orthogonal orbit' renamed the rotation orbit W_0.SO(n) (the O(n)-orbit adds W_0 H_{e1}); the fibre product's dimension is local over a generic point (it is not pure-dimensional); Hurwitz braid moves added to the global symmetries; the HF even-r note and the injectivity condition reworded; the Gram-Schmidt variant never contains W_0 and is degenerate at the paired init. Exposé 0 headline restores 'W_0 injective, r even'; rosetta.json updated.","public_statement":"Let $W_0$ have full column rank (out_features $\\ge$ in_features) and let $r\\le n-2$ be even. Then the image of $r$-reflection HRA without Gram–Schmidt, HF PEFT's default (`apply_GS=False`), is exactly $\\{W_0R:R\\in SO(n),\\ \\operatorname{rank}(W_0R-W_0)\\le r\\}$, the image-level intersection of $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ with the rotation orbit $W_0\\cdot SO(n)$, of dimension $rn-r(r+1)/2$. This is a statement about images; HRA is not the categorical product of the two. At HF's default paired initialisation ($u_{2i-1}=u_{2i}$), $W_0$ is a singular vertex of the image and the first-order image has dimension $kn-k(k+1)/2$ with $k=r/2$, so that initialisation is an apex like zero-init LoRA. HRA's image strictly contains LoRA $\\cap$ OFT for exactly orthogonal (Cayley) OFT; HF's default Cayley–Neumann OFT is not orthogonal, and the two are incomparable.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (d) 'a meet in the image poset' narrowed to 'a meet in the poset of subsets of Theta containing W_0' (Obs I.4(c)), as in the paper."},{"id":"II.8","name":"DoRA is LoRA followed by an output scale, at the level of images","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.8 (DoRA is LoRA followed by an output scale, at the level of images).** [Prop] Assume $1\\le r\\le\\min(m,n)$ and $s\\ne0$. Let $N$ normalise rows; DoRA's magnitude $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R^m$ is per output neuron, as in HF PEFT and the official code (the paper's notation says \"column-wise\", in the transposed convention). Images refer to the merged (eval-mode) weight; HF's train-mode forward with `lora_dropout`$>0$ is not of the form $Wx$. Let $W_0$ have no zero row, let $\\mathcal U$ be the open set of matrices with no zero row, and let $\\mathrm{Diag}_m$ be the monoid of all diagonal matrices. Then\n$$\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{DoRA}_r=\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\mu)\\,N(V):V\\in(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap\\mathcal U,\\ \\mu\\in\\mathbb R^m\\}=\\mathrm{Diag}_m\\cdot(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}),$$\nwhich is exactly the image of \"LoRA$_r$ followed by a trainable output scale $\\ell\\in\\mathbb R^m$\". With $\\mu$ restricted to $(\\mathbb R^\\times)^m$, or equivalently after intersecting with $\\mathcal U$: $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{DoRA}_r\\cap\\mathcal U=T_m\\cdot(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})\\cap\\mathcal U$. Moreover:\n- $d(\\mathrm{DoRA}_r)\\le\\min\\big(mn,\\ r(m+n-r)+m\\big)$. At a parameter point with $\\mu\\in(\\mathbb R^\\times)^m$ and $X=sBA\\in\\mathcal M_r$, with $U=\\mathrm{col}X$ and $R=\\mathrm{row}X$, the Jacobian rank is $r(m+n-r)+m-\\dim\\ker\\big(\\delta\\mapsto P_{U^\\perp}\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)W_0P_{R^\\perp}\\big)$; where some $\\mu_i=0$ it is smaller when $r\\ge2$, and for $r=1$ when some row $w_{0,i}$ with $\\mu_i=0$ lies outside $R$. For generic $W_0$, $d=\\min\\big(mn,\\ r(m+n-r)+m\\big)$; in particular $d=r(m+n-r)+m$ iff $(m-r)(n-r)\\ge m$, which holds for all practical shapes. For special $W_0$ it is smaller: for $W_0=ac^\\top$ with all $a_i\\ne0$ and $r<n$, $d=r(m+n-r)+m-r$, strictly below the generic value iff $r<m$ and $r\\le n-2$.\n- The image covariance group contains $\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times GL_n$.\n- At $B=0$, in HF's magnitude coordinates, $\\Delta W=(\\mathrm{diag}(\\mu_i/\\|w_{0,i}\\|)-I)W_0$, which can have rank up to $\\operatorname{rank}W_0$, so DoRA escapes LoRA's rank bound.","proof":"*Proof.* $\\mathrm{diag}(\\mu)N(V)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\mu_i/\\|v_i\\|)V\\in\\mathrm{Diag}_m\\cdot V$. Conversely, take $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)V$ with $V=W_0+X$, $\\operatorname{rank}X\\le r$, and let $Z$ be the set of zero rows of $V$. Put $V'=W_0+(I-\\varepsilon\\sum_{i\\in Z}E_{ii})X$. Its rows outside $Z$ are those of $V$, its rows in $Z$ are $\\varepsilon w_{0,i}\\ne0$, and its update still has rank $\\le r$. With $\\ell'_i=0$ on $Z$ and $\\ell'_i=\\ell_i$ elsewhere, $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell')V'=\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)V$ and $V'\\in\\mathcal U$. For the $T_m$ statement, $DV\\in\\mathcal U$ with $D$ invertible forces $V\\in\\mathcal U$. Dimension: $\\varphi(\\mu,B,A)=(\\mu_i/\\lVert v_i\\rVert,B,A)$ is a diffeomorphism of DoRA's domain onto that of LoRA plus an output scale, and $\\rho_{\\rm DoRA}=\\rho_{\\text{LoRA+scale}}\\circ\\varphi$. The tangent space at $(D,X)$ of $(D,X)\\mapsto D(W_0+X)$ is $D\\big[\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)V\\}+T_X\\mathcal M_r\\big]$ after $\\delta\\mapsto D^{-1}\\delta$, so for invertible $D$ it suffices to work at $(I,X)$. There $\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)V\\}$ has dimension $m$ because $V\\in\\mathcal U$, and $\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)V\\in T_X\\mathcal M_r$ iff $P_{U^\\perp}\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)VP_{R^\\perp}=0$, where $VP_{R^\\perp}=W_0P_{R^\\perp}$. Zero magnitudes: $\\mu_i=0$ iff $\\ell_i=0$ under $\\varphi$; let $Z_0=\\{i:\\ell_i=0\\}\\ne\\emptyset$, write $\\mathcal S=\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)V\\}$ and $T=T_X\\mathcal M_r$, and let $F=\\dim(\\mathcal S+T)$ be the value of the formula. In the image of the differential $(\\delta,\\dot B,\\dot A)\\mapsto\\mathrm{diag}(\\delta)V+\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)\\,s(\\dot BA+B\\dot A)$ the rows in $Z_0$ are $\\delta_iv_i$, so its dimension is $\\lvert Z_0\\rvert$ plus that of the projection of $\\mathcal S+T$ to the other rows. The part of $\\mathcal S+T$ supported on the rows in $Z_0$ contains every $\\sum_{i\\in Z_0}e_i(c_iv_i+y_i)^\\top$ with $y_i\\in R$, and $v_i-w_{0,i}\\in R$, so the rank is at most $F-\\sum_{i\\in Z_0}\\big(\\dim\\mathrm{span}(w_{0,i},R)-1\\big)\\le F-\\lvert Z_0\\rvert(r-1)$. This is below $F$ when $r\\ge2$, or when some $w_{0,i}$ with $i\\in Z_0$ lies outside $R$; for $r=1$ with every such row in $R$ it can equal $F$. Generic value: with bases $E$ of $U^\\perp$ and $F$ of $R^\\perp$ the map is $\\delta\\mapsto\\sum_i\\delta_i\\,(E^\\top e_i)\\otimes(F^\\top w_{0,i})$. For generic $(W_0,U,R)$ these are $m$ generic points of the Segre cone in $\\mathbb R^{m-r}\\otimes\\mathbb R^{n-r}$, which is irreducible and spans the whole space, so they span $\\min(m,(m-r)(n-r))$ dimensions (a standard-basis witness shows the open condition is non-empty). Since $r(m+n-r)+(m-r)(n-r)=mn$, $d=\\min(mn,r(m+n-r)+m)$ off a proper algebraic subset. For $W_0=ac^\\top$, the kernel is $\\mathrm{diag}(a)^{-1}U$, of dimension $r$, whenever $c\\notin R$. Covariance: a monomial $g$ normalises $\\mathrm{Diag}_m$ and $g\\mathcal M_{\\le r}h^{-1}=\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$, so $g\\,\\mathrm{Diag}_m(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})h^{-1}=\\mathrm{Diag}_m(gW_0h^{-1}+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})$. ∎ [Num: Jacobian rank $9=mn$ at $(m,n,r)=(3,3,2)$, where the formula gives $11$; $36$ at $(8,8,2)$, matching, and $34$ there when one $\\mu_i=0$; $24$ for a rank-one $W_0$ at $(6,6,2)$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: (1) the set equality with T_m fails because mu ranges over R^m (zero rows allowed); (2) d = r(m+n-r)+m is false in general: it can exceed mn, and it drops for special W0 (a rank-one W0 loses r); (3) the B=0 increment used mu instead of mu/||w0||; and the commentary conflated the exact (metric) gradient with HF's detached backward. Repaired: Im DoRA_r = Diag_m.(W0 + M_{<=r}) = image of LoRA plus an output scale (proved, so the first half of Conj D is now a theorem), with the T_m form after intersecting with matrices without zero rows; d <= min(mn, r(m+n-r)+m) with an exact kernel criterion and generic equality [Num]; the B=0 formula fixed; Conj D restated.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful minor): the image identity and bound hold. False as stated: the shortfall clause needs all mu_i != 0 (a zero magnitude lowers the rank: 34, not 36, at (8,8,2)); the rank-one value needs r < n and is strictly below generic only for r < m, r <= n-2; r <= min(m,n) and s != 0 were implicit. Repaired: hypotheses added; the clause restated at parameter points with invertible magnitude; the proof gains the diffeomorphism phi and the reduction to D = I; generic equality is now proved by a Segre-cone argument instead of [Num]; rank-one kernel diag(a)^{-1}U derived. Conj D was a tautology and is replaced by Experiment D (four AdamW arms separating parametrisation, backward rule and optimizer coordinates), also in Exposé X; 'change of metric' scoped to gradient flow/SGD; eval-weight and row-wise-norm footnotes added. Adjacent: DoRA's gauge GL_r qualified to generic W_0 with (m-r)(n-r) >= m; 'output torus x LoRA' now the Diag_m-saturation; BOFT worked example corrected.","public_statement":"For a linear layer whose frozen weight $W_0$ has no zero rows, and $r\\le\\min(m,n)$, DoRA as implemented in HF PEFT (one unconstrained magnitude per output neuron) reaches exactly the same merged weights as rank-$r$ LoRA followed by a free per-output-neuron gain, $\\{\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)(W_0+X):\\operatorname{rank}X\\le r\\}$. This set has dimension $\\min(mn,\\,r(m+n-r)+m)$ for generic $W_0$ and less for special $W_0$, for example $r(m+n-r)+m-r$ when $W_0$ has rank one. So DoRA buys no expressivity beyond LoRA plus $m$ output gains. Any difference from that baseline, starting from the same weights, comes from training dynamics, namely the row-normalised parametrisation, DoRA's detached-norm backward, and the way the optimizer treats the magnitude coordinates.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the zero-magnitude clause is narrowed to what the proof shows: the Jacobian rank drops where some mu_i = 0 when r >= 2, and for r = 1 only when some row w_{0,i} with mu_i = 0 lies outside R (the unconditional clause is false for r = 1 with that row in R; numpy check by the referee); the proof gains the bound F - sum_i (dim span(w_{0,i}, R) - 1)."},{"id":"II.9","name":"the torus ladder","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.9 (the torus ladder).** [Prop]\n\n(a) **HiRA.** $\\rho(B,A)=W_0\\odot(J+BA)$, where $J$ is the all-ones matrix; HF PEFT has $B_0=0$ and no scale factor on Linear and Conv layers (on embeddings it uses $A_0=0$). For every $W_0$, $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HiRA}_r=W_0+D_{W_0}(\\mathcal M_{\\le r})$ with $D_{W_0}:X\\mapsto W_0\\odot X$. If $W_0$ has no zero entries, $D_{W_0}\\in GL(\\Theta)$, so inside the slice at $\\theta_0$ HiRA is LoRA transported by a diagonal automorphism: it has the same $d=r(m+n-r)$ (for $r\\le\\min(m,n)$) and the same gauge. In all cases $\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W\\le r\\cdot\\operatorname{rank}W_0$. The transport depends on $\\theta_0$ (Prop II.13).\n\n(b) **Scalings.** Output-side scalings, $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)W_0=W_0\\odot(J+(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)\\mathbf 1^\\top)$, and input-side scalings, $W_0\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)=W_0\\odot(J+\\mathbf 1(\\ell-\\mathbf 1)^\\top)$, have images in $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HiRA}_1$. Output-side examples: (IA)$^3$ on keys and values, the post-linear scale of SSF, DoRA's magnitude. Input-side examples: (IA)$^3$'s feed-forward vector, which scales the input of $W_{\\rm down}$, and a LayerNorm gain $\\gamma$ folded into the next linear map. The fold is exact when the norm's output is consumed only by linear maps: the input norms of pre-norm blocks, and a final norm before an untied head (in post-norm BERT, the last block's norm feeds only the head). It does not apply to Gemma-style post-sublayer norms, which HF's default LN-tuning targets for Gemma 2 and 3, to QK-norms, or to post-norm residual paths. Where it applies, $\\gamma$ is tied across all consumers of the norm (Q, K and V, or gate and up) rather than being a free point per box, and with tied embeddings a fold into the head breaks the tie. LayerNorm biases and SSF's shift fold into the next bias, not into $W$. Two-sided scalings $\\mathrm{diag}(a)W_0\\mathrm{diag}(b)=W_0\\odot(ab^\\top)$ lie in $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{HiRA}_2$. As a morphism, $h(\\ell)=(\\ell-\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 1^\\top)$ lands in $\\mathrm{HiRA}_1$ pointed at $(0,\\mathbf 1^\\top)$, which is not HiRA's random-$A$ default pointing.\n\n(c) **Orbit dimension.** The two-sided torus orbit $\\{\\mathrm{diag}(a)W_0\\mathrm{diag}(b)\\}$ has dimension $m+n-c(W_0)$, where $c(W_0)$ is the number of connected components of the bipartite support graph of $W_0$, an isolated vertex counting as its own component.\n\n(d) **RoAd.** RoAd$_1$ (the paper's main variant, HF `road_1`) is $W=RW_0$ with $R$ block-diagonal in scaled rotations $\\alpha_i\\mathrm{Rot}(\\theta_i)$ of output pairs. It is the left action of $CO^+(2)^{m/2}\\cong(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{m/2}$: it is **(IA)$^3$ over $\\mathbb C$**. It multiplies the input Gram matrix of each output pair by $\\alpha_i^2$. Since $\\alpha_i$ may be $0$, its image is the orbit closure, a real-linear space of dimension exactly $m$ whenever no output-pair block of $W_0$ is zero. RoAd$_2$ and RoAd$_4$ (HF `road_2`, `road_4`) let each block range over all of $M_2(\\mathbb R)$. They realise the block-diagonal action of the monoid $M_2(\\mathbb R)^{m/2}$ (its group part $GL_2^{m/2}$ is non-abelian), with linear image $\\{\\mathrm{blockdiag}(R_i)W_0\\}$ of dimension $2m$ when every pair block has rank $2$. For them the Gram statement and \"over $\\mathbb C$\" fail, and RoAd$_4$ carries a positive-dimensional gauge (two dimensions per row). HF pairs output $j$ with $j+g/2$ inside each group of size $g$ (default $64$) rather than adjacent outputs as in the paper, and also rotates the bias; the statements above do not depend on the pairing.\n\n(e) **Zeros and covariance.** Every $W$ reachable by HiRA, (IA)$^3$ or SSF vanishes wherever $W_0$ does, $Z(W_0)\\subseteq Z(W)$, and this persists under merging. New zeros can appear ($\\ell_i=0$, or $(J+BA)_{ij}=0$), so the zero pattern itself is not invariant. The image covariance group of output-scaled boxes ((IA)$^3$ on $k,v$; post-linear SSF) is $\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times GL_n$, and that of input-scaled boxes ((IA)$^3$ on $W_{\\rm down}$) is $GL_m\\times\\mathrm{Mon}_n$. HiRA's is exactly $\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times\\mathrm{Mon}_n$ ($=GL_1\\times GL_1$ when $m=n=1$).","proof":"*Proof.* (a) $W_0\\odot(J+BA)=W_0+W_0\\odot BA$. $D_{W_0}$ is diagonal in the standard basis of $\\Theta$, and invertible iff no entry vanishes. Pushforward along a linear isomorphism preserves dimension and gauge. For the rank, $W_0\\odot(BA)=\\sum_k\\mathrm{diag}(b_k)W_0\\mathrm{diag}(a_k)$, which has rank $\\le r\\operatorname{rank}W_0$.\n\n(b) Direct computation, using $ab^\\top-J=[a,-\\mathbf 1][b,\\mathbf 1]^\\top\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le2}$.\n\n(c) The tangent map at $(\\mathbf 1,\\mathbf 1)$ is $(\\alpha,\\beta)\\mapsto\\big((\\alpha_i+\\beta_j)(W_0)_{ij}\\big)$. Its kernel requires $\\alpha_i+\\beta_j=0$ along every support edge, so it is one-dimensional on each connected component. Torus orbits are homogeneous, so the dimension is the same at every point of the orbit.\n\n(d) A $2\\times n$ real block $X$ is a complex row in $\\mathbb C^n$, and $\\alpha\\mathrm{Rot}(\\theta)$ acts on it as multiplication by $\\alpha e^{i\\theta}$. So the image for one pair is $\\mathbb C\\cdot X$, of real dimension $2$ iff $X\\ne0$, and $(\\alpha\\mathrm{Rot}X)^\\top(\\alpha\\mathrm{Rot}X)=\\alpha^2X^\\top X$. In RoAd$_2$ each row of a block is an arbitrary vector of $\\mathbb R^2$ written in polar form.\n\n(e) Hadamard multiplication and row or column scaling preserve the zeros of $W_0$.\n\n*(IA)$^3$ and SSF.* A monomial $g$ normalises the diagonal monoid, so $\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times GL_n$ is contained in the covariance group of output scalings. Conversely, take $W_0=e_jc^\\top$. Then $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(gW_0h^{-1})=\\mathrm{span}\\{e_i:i\\in\\mathrm{supp}(ge_j)\\}\\,c^\\top h^{-1}$, while $g\\,\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(W_0)\\,h^{-1}=\\mathbb R\\,(ge_j)c^\\top h^{-1}$. Equality forces $\\lvert\\mathrm{supp}(ge_j)\\rvert=1$ for every $j$, so $g$ is monomial. This works for all $m,n$. Input-side scalings are the transpose.\n\n*HiRA.* Monomial pairs commute with Hadamard products up to relabelling. Conversely, take $W_0=E_{ij}$, so that $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(E_{ij})=\\mathbb RE_{ij}$, and put $u=ge_i$, $v=h^{-\\top}e_j$. Covariance would force $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(uv^\\top)=uv^\\top\\odot(J+\\mathcal M_{\\le r})$ to be the line $\\mathbb R\\,uv^\\top$. It contains $uv^\\top+t\\,u_kv_lE_{kl}$ for every $(k,l)$ in the support of $uv^\\top$, so it has dimension at least $2$ unless $\\lvert\\mathrm{supp}\\,u\\rvert=\\lvert\\mathrm{supp}\\,v\\rvert=1$. This holds for every $i,j$ only when $g$ and $h$ are monomial. ∎ [Num: RoAd$_1$ orbit dimension $8$ and RoAd$_2$ dimension $16$ at $m=8$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Mathematics of (a)-(c) confirmed. Defects: RoAd as stated covers only RoAd_1 (RoAd_2/4 have arbitrary 2x2 blocks, a non-abelian GL_2 action with image of dimension 2m); CO(2) should be CO^+(2); folding a LayerNorm gain needs a pre-norm architecture and is tied across consumers; the (IA)^3 FFN vector scales the input of W_down (covariance GL x Mon); 'the zero pattern is an absolute invariant' is false (only Z(W0) is contained in Z(W)); 'never GL x GL' fails at m=n=1; the (IA)^3 covariance proof assumed full row rank. All repaired: the (IA)^3 proof now uses W0 = e_j c^T for all m, n, and the ladder gloss was toned down.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor, faithful minor): no false mathematical claim. Fixed: d = r(m+n-r) needs r <= min(m,n); HF HiRA embeddings use A_0 = 0; HiRA's covariance group is now proved to be exactly Mon_m x Mon_n (test at W_0 = E_ij); RoAd_2/4 is a monoid action (group part GL_2^{m/2}), with a note on HF's (j, j+g/2) pairing and bias rotation. The LayerNorm fold is restricted to norms whose output feeds only linear maps (not Gemma-style post-sublayer norms, QK-norms or post-norm residual paths), and biases and SSF's shift fold into the next bias. The gloss now says only the scaling methods are rungs; DoRA and RoAd are rungs only for zero-free W_0, and RoAd lies on no low-rank rung only for generic W_0.","public_statement":"In HF PEFT's HiRA, $W=W_0\\odot(J+BA)$ with $B_0=0$ and no scale factor, so the reachable set is $W_0+W_0\\odot\\{\\operatorname{rank}\\le r\\}$. For $W_0$ with no zero entries this is LoRA's image moved by the invertible diagonal map $X\\mapsto W_0\\odot X$, with the same dimension $r(m+n-r)$ (for $r\\le\\min(m,n)$) and the same gauge. In general $\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W\\le r\\operatorname{rank}W_0$, an entry that is zero in $W_0$ stays zero, and the image covariance group is exactly $\\mathrm{Mon}_m\\times\\mathrm{Mon}_n$. One-sided rescalings ((IA)$^3$ vectors, SSF's scales, DoRA's magnitude with $B=0$, and the gain of a LayerNorm that feeds only linear maps) lie in the image of rank-1 HiRA, and two-sided rescalings in that of rank-2 HiRA. RoAd$_1$ is a complex scaling of output pairs, with an $m$-dimensional linear image, and RoAd$_2$/RoAd$_4$ apply arbitrary $2\\times2$ blocks, with a linear image of dimension up to $2m$."},{"id":"II.10","name":"cut-rank bound","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.10 (cut-rank bound).** [Thm; standard] Let $\\Gamma$ be a tensor network, evaluated in the symmetric monoidal category $(\\mathbf{FinVect},\\otimes)$, with input legs $V$ and output legs $U$. Every wire and open leg is assumed incident to a vertex: insert an identity box on any bare strand, or equivalently count each bare input–output wire as crossing every cut. A crossing hyperedge (copy spider) contributes its dimension once. Partition the vertices into $\\mathcal I\\sqcup\\mathcal O$. The weight $w$ of the cut is the product of the dimensions of the internal wires between the two sides and of the open legs attached on the wrong side (input legs at $\\mathcal O$, output legs at $\\mathcal I$). Then\n$$\\operatorname{rank}[\\![\\Gamma]\\!]\\le\\min_{\\rm cuts}w,$$\nand rank is subadditive over formal sums. The bound refers to a chosen presentation of the network.","proof":"*Proof.* Write $V_{\\mathcal I},V_{\\mathcal O}$ for the input legs attached to $\\mathcal I$ and $\\mathcal O$, $U_{\\mathcal I},U_{\\mathcal O}$ likewise for the outputs, and $C$ for the tensor product of the cut wires. Contracting each side gives $T_{\\mathcal I}:V_{\\mathcal I}\\to U_{\\mathcal I}\\otimes C$ and $T_{\\mathcal O}:C\\otimes V_{\\mathcal O}\\to U_{\\mathcal O}$. Then $[\\![\\Gamma]\\!]=(\\mathrm{id}_{U_{\\mathcal I}}\\otimes T_{\\mathcal O})\\circ(T_{\\mathcal I}\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_{V_{\\mathcal O}})$ factors through $U_{\\mathcal I}\\otimes C\\otimes V_{\\mathcal O}$, of dimension $w$. Symmetric monoidal structure suffices; no cups or caps are needed. Subadditivity is $\\operatorname{rank}(X+Y)\\le\\operatorname{rank}X+\\operatorname{rank}Y$. ∎ [Num: no violation in 283 random networks.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: the cut bound is correct under the standard convention. Wording repaired: bare input-to-output strands must be incident to a vertex (otherwise I_d (x) BA is a counterexample); hyperedges count once; the proof is rewritten as (id (x) T_O) o (T_I (x) id) through U_I (x) C (x) V_O; symmetric monoidal structure suffices (no compact closure). Consequences now say that the bound refers to a presentation (HRA and Givens via the telescoping sum, HiRA via factoring W0), that splitting inits give 2r, that FourierFT <= min(2n_c, m, n), and that a large min-cut is necessary but not sufficient.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"For a tensor network evaluated in finite-dimensional vector spaces, the rank of the map it computes is at most the smallest product of wire dimensions across any cut that separates inputs from outputs, and rank is subadditive over sums. This reads the maximum rank of a structured adapter off its diagram, for a chosen presentation of the network."},{"id":"II.11","name":"Kronecker incomparability","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem II.11 (Kronecker incomparability).** [Thm; linear algebra standard (Van Loan–Pitsianis, operator-Schmidt decomposition); PEFT reading new] Write $\\mathbb R^m=\\mathbb R^{m_1}\\otimes\\mathbb R^{m_2}$ and $\\mathbb R^n=\\mathbb R^{n_1}\\otimes\\mathbb R^{n_2}$, and view $\\Delta\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ as a 4-leg tensor. Let $\\operatorname{rk}$ be the rank across the cut $\\{U_1U_2\\}\\mid\\{V_1V_2\\}$ and $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes$ the rank across $\\{U_1V_1\\}\\mid\\{U_2V_2\\}$.\n\n(a) $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes\\Delta\\le k$ iff $\\Delta=\\sum_{i\\le k}C_i\\otimes D_i$. Every $\\Delta$ has $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes\\Delta\\le\\min(m_1n_1,m_2n_2)$.\n\n(b) $\\operatorname{rk}(C\\otimes D)=\\operatorname{rk}C\\cdot\\operatorname{rk}D$, while $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes(C\\otimes D)=1$ for $C,D\\ne0$.\n\n(c) If $u\\in\\mathbb R^m$ and $v\\in\\mathbb R^n$ have Schmidt ranks $\\rho_u,\\rho_v$, then $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes(uv^\\top)=\\rho_u\\rho_v$. A generic rank-one update therefore has Kronecker rank $\\kappa_1:=\\min(m_1,m_2)\\cdot\\min(n_1,n_2)$. For aligned splits, $(m_1-m_2)(n_1-n_2)\\ge0$, which include equal square splits and every split HF PEFT's factorisation produces (it puts the smaller factor first on both sides), $\\kappa_1=\\min(m_1n_1,m_2n_2)$ is the maximal Kronecker rank.\n\n(d) **Incomparability.** Put $\\nu:=\\min(m_1,n_1)\\min(m_2,n_2)$, the largest rank of a single Kronecker product. For $1\\le r<\\nu$ and $1\\le k<\\kappa_1$, the sets $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ and $\\mathcal K_{\\le k}:=\\{\\Delta:\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes\\Delta\\le k\\}$ of unconstrained $k$-term Kronecker sums are incomparable, for every split: a generic rank-one update lies in the first and not the second, and $C\\otimes D$ with full-rank factors, of Kronecker rank $1$ and rank $\\nu$, lies in the second and not the first. For equal square splits ($m=n=N$, all factors $s$), $\\kappa_1=\\nu=s^2=N$, and $I_N$ is such a product.\n\n(e) **Constrained Kronecker methods.** Let $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M\\subseteq W_0+\\mathcal K_{\\le k}$. Then $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im}\\,M$ whenever $k<\\kappa_1$, for every pointing of $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$. Give $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ its zero-init pointing, with image $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$. Then $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ iff some $\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}\\,M$ has $\\operatorname{rank}(\\theta-W_0)>r$. For LoKr, $C\\otimes BA$ with $C\\in\\mathbb R^{m_1\\times n_1}$, $B\\in\\mathbb R^{m_2\\times r'}$ and $A\\in\\mathbb R^{r'\\times n_2}$, put $r'_{\\rm eff}=\\min(r',m_2,n_2)$. The maximum rank is $\\min(m_1,n_1)\\cdot r'_{\\rm eff}$. So $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoKr}_{r'}\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ iff $r\\ge\\min(m_1,n_1)r'_{\\rm eff}$, and otherwise the two images are incomparable, provided $\\kappa_1>1$. In HF PEFT the second LoKr factor is low-rank only when $r<\\max(m_2,n_2)/2$; otherwise it is full and LoKr is unconstrained KronA, with maximum rank $\\min(m_1,n_1)\\min(m_2,n_2)$. On a $768$-wide layer ($24\\times32$) the maximum rank therefore jumps from $360$ at $r=15$ to $768$ at $r=16$. With `decompose_both`, $C$ is also capped at rank $r$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) and (b) follow from the Van Loan–Pitsianis rearrangement $\\mathcal R(C\\otimes D)=\\mathrm{vec}C\\,\\mathrm{vec}D^\\top$, which is wire-bending; $\\mathcal R\\Delta$ is an $(m_1n_1)\\times(m_2n_2)$ matrix. For (c), write the Schmidt decompositions $u=\\sum\\sigma_ix_i\\otimes y_i$ and $v=\\sum\\tau_jz_j\\otimes w_j$. Then $uv^\\top=\\sum_{ij}\\sigma_i\\tau_j(x_iz_j^\\top)\\otimes(y_iw_j^\\top)$ is an operator-Schmidt decomposition with Frobenius-orthonormal families and $\\rho_u\\rho_v$ nonzero coefficients; generic $u,v$ have full Schmidt ranks. Under alignment $\\min(m_1,m_2)\\min(n_1,n_2)=\\min(m_1n_1,m_2n_2)$, which is the bound of (a). (d) follows from (b) and (c) and needs no alignment. (e) The first clause: if some pointing gave $c+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_{\\le k}$, then for $X\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ and $t>0$, $(c+tX)/t\\in\\mathcal K_{\\le k}$, and letting $t\\to\\infty$ in this closed cone gives $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_{\\le k}$, contradicting (c). The second clause is the definition of $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$. For LoKr, $\\operatorname{rank}(C\\otimes BA)=\\operatorname{rk}C\\cdot\\operatorname{rk}(BA)\\le\\min(m_1,n_1)\\,r'_{\\rm eff}$ by (b), with equality for full-rank $C$ and $BA$ of rank $r'_{\\rm eff}$; $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r\\not\\subseteq\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoKr}$ because $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoKr}\\subseteq W_0+\\mathcal K_{\\le1}$ and $1<\\kappa_1$. ∎ [Num: Schmidt ranks $(1,3)\\to3$, $(2,3)\\to6$, $(3,3)\\to9$, each with ordinary rank $1$; for the $2\\times4$ split of $\\mathbb R^8$, $\\operatorname{rk}_\\otimes(uv^\\top)=4$, the maximal Kronecker rank; LoKr with $s=4$, $r'=1$ never exceeds rank $4$; at $(m_1,m_2,n_1,n_2)=(2,8,8,2)$ with inner dimension $4$, LoKr never exceeds rank $4=\\min(m_1,n_1)\\,r'_{\\rm eff}$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: (d)'s 'any k-term Kronecker-sum method' is false for constrained methods: LoKr with inner rank r' has rank <= min(m1,n1) r', so Im LoKr lies in LoRA_r whenever r >= min(m1,n1) r'. The [Pred] 'no Kronecker sum with k < d terms' fails for unequal splits, where a generic rank-one update has Kronecker rank min(m1,m2) min(n1,n2) (576 for HF's 768 -> 24 x 32). Repaired: general shapes; (d) restated as incomparability of the sets M_{<=r} and K_{<=k} for equal square splits; new (e) for constrained methods with the LoKr criterion; (b) needs C, D != 0; Compacter and MPO removed from the 'LoRA across a cut' list; [Pred] threshold corrected.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful minor): (a)-(d) hold; the LoKr clause of (e) used the wrong cap. The maximum rank is min(m1,n1) min(r',m2,n2), not min(min(m1,n1) r', min(m,n)), so the incomparability claim failed when r' > min(m2,n2) (counterexamples (2,8,8,2) and (4,2,2,8)). Repaired: r'_eff = min(r',m2,n2) with an 'iff' threshold and attainment; LoRA_r fixed to its zero-init pointing in (e), with the first clause proved for every pointing by a closed-cone argument; (d) generalised to aligned splits (where kappa_1 is the maximal Kronecker rank) with a full-rank C (x) D witness, and d renamed N. HF facts added: LoKr's second factor is full when r >= max(m2,n2)/2 (rank jumps from 360 to 768 at r = 16 on 768-wide layers), decompose_both caps C, and HF splits 1024 and 4096 square. [Pred] gains a unique-minimiser loss; 'typical move' dropped; the LoKr-inside-LoRA sentence quantified (192 or 512 at HF defaults). Knock-on statements in II.13, the worked examples and Exposé X now carry kappa_1 > 1 and the rank conditions. After round 2, (d) was stated for every split: incomparability needs only 1 <= r < nu = min(m1,n1) min(m2,n2) and 1 <= k < kappa_1, with a full-rank C (x) D witness, and the alignment remark moved to (c).","public_statement":"Split the dimensions as $m=m_1m_2$ and $n=n_1n_2$. A $k$-term Kronecker sum $\\sum C_i\\otimes D_i$ is exactly a rank-$\\le k$ matrix after the Van Loan–Pitsianis rearrangement, so unconstrained Kronecker methods are LoRA across a different cut of the same 4-leg tensor. A generic rank-one update has Kronecker rank $\\min(m_1,m_2)\\min(n_1,n_2)$, which is the largest possible for aligned splits, including equal square splits and every split HF PEFT's factorisation produces. So zero-init $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ and Kronecker sums with fewer terms than that have incomparable images whenever $r$ is below the rank $\\min(m_1,n_1)\\min(m_2,n_2)$ of a full-rank single Kronecker product. LoKr updates $C\\otimes BA$ with inner rank $r'\\le\\min(m_2,n_2)$ have rank at most $\\min(m_1,n_1)r'$, attained, so LoKr's image lies inside zero-init $\\mathrm{LoRA}_r$ iff $r\\ge\\min(m_1,n_1)r'$ and is incomparable with it otherwise (when $\\min(m_1,m_2)\\min(n_1,n_2)>1$). In HF PEFT the second LoKr factor is low-rank only when $r<\\max(m_2,n_2)/2$; above that LoKr is an unconstrained Kronecker product."},{"id":"II.12","name":"gauge census: rank is a cut, dimension is a volume","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.12 (gauge census: rank is a cut, dimension is a volume).** [Prop]\n\n(a) $d(M)\\le\\lvert M\\rvert-\\dim(\\text{generic gauge orbit})$, with equality when the gauge acts locally transitively on generic fibres.\n\n(b) The following values hold for one $m\\times n$ box, with $r\\le\\min(m,n)$ throughout except for VeRA (whose published RoBERTa-base setting has $r=1024>768$). The gauge column lists the exhibited Lie group; it is locally transitive on generic fibres away from the $mn$ cap, but not where the cap binds (for LoHa at $(4,4,2)$ the orbit has dimension $15$ and the fibre $16$).\n\n| method | budget $\\lvert M\\rvert$ | exhibited Lie gauge (orbit dim) | $d(M)$ | max rank |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LoRA$_r$ | $r(m+n)$ | $GL_r$ ($r^2$) | $r(m+n-r)$ | $r$ |\n| GraLoRA ($k\\times k$ blocks, rank $r/k$ each; HF default `hybrid_r=0`) | $r(m+n)$ | $\\prod GL_{r/k}$ ($r^2$) | $r(m+n-r)$ | $\\min(kr,m,n)$ |\n| LoHa$_{r_1,r_2}$ | $(r_1+r_2)(m+n)$ | $GL_{r_1}\\times GL_{r_2}\\times$ diagonal torus ($r_1^2+r_2^2+m+n-1$) | $\\min\\big(mn,\\ r_1(m+n-r_1)+r_2(m+n-r_2)-(m+n-1)\\big)$ [Num; the second argument is an upper bound] | $\\min(r_1r_2,m,n)$ |\n| HRA$_r$ ($\\operatorname{rank}W_0=n$, `apply_GS=False`; even $r$ is needed only for HF's identity init) | $rn$ | non-linear; fibre dimension $r(r+1)/2$; exhibited Lie gauge $(\\mathbb R^\\times)^r$ | $rn-r(r+1)/2$ (for $r\\in\\{n-1,n\\}$ this is $\\dim SO(n)$ [Num]) | $r$ |\n| DoRA$_r$ | $r(m+n)+m$ | $GL_r$ for generic $W_0$ with $(m-r)(n-r)\\ge m$ (larger otherwise: dimension $6>r^2$ at $(3,3,2)$, and $r^2+r$ for rank-one $W_0$) | $\\min\\big(mn,\\ r(m+n-r)+m\\big)$ for generic $W_0$ (Prop II.8) | up to full |\n| VeRA | $m+r$ | $\\mathbb R^\\times$ | $m+r-1$ [Num] | $\\min(r,m,n)$ |\n| KronA $A\\otimes B$ | $m_1n_1+m_2n_2$ | $\\mathbb R^\\times$ | $m_1n_1+m_2n_2-1$ | $\\min(m_1,n_1)\\min(m_2,n_2)$ |\n| TT / Tucker (admissible, minimal ranks) | $\\sum$ cores | $\\prod_eGL_{r_e}$ | $\\lvert M\\rvert-\\sum_er_e^2$ (Tucker: Koch–Lubich, Uschmajew–Vandereycken; TT: Holtz–Rohwedder–Schneider) | up to full |\n| linear methods (LoRA-FA, LoRA-XS, MiSS in every HF mode, FourierFT, C3A, masks) | $\\lvert M\\rvert$ | translations by the kernel | rank of the linear map; $=\\lvert M\\rvert$ when injective | varies |\n\nBitFit does not touch the $m\\times n$ box; it is linear in the biases. Hybrid GraLoRA (`hybrid_r>0`) is not covered by the table.\n\nFourierFT is injective only when its sampled frequencies contain no conjugate pair $(u,v),(-u,-v)$; otherwise $d=\\lvert M\\rvert-\\#\\{\\text{pairs}\\}$. At LLM sizes this is close to $\\lvert M\\rvert$, but at $768\\times768$ with $1000$ frequencies a pair occurs in about half of the draws (HF's default seed $777$ gives exactly two pairs there).\n\n(c) **Equal-budget trades.** Assume $2r\\le\\min(m,n)$, $r^2\\le\\min(m,n)$ and $2r^2<m+n-1$. LoHa$_{r,r}$ and LoRA$_{2r}$ both cost $2r(m+n)$, but LoHa reaches at least $(m+n-1)-2r^2$ *fewer* dimensions (exactly that many when the LoHa upper bound of (b) is attained [Num]). For $r\\ge3$ it gains rank up to $r^2>2r$ in exchange. For $r\\le2$, $r^2\\le2r$, so $\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoHa}_{r,r}\\subsetneq\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r}$: LoHa is strictly dominated. When $2r^2\\ge m+n-1$ (still with $2r\\le\\min(m,n)$) the bound no longer separates them, and wherever the LoHa upper bound of (b) is attained LoHa$_{r,r}$ matches or exceeds LoRA$_{2r}$ in dimension as well as in rank, for instance at $(m,n,r)=(9,9,3)$, at $r=64$ on $4096\\times4096$, and at LyCORIS-style $r=32$ on $320$- to $640$-wide layers. GraLoRA$_k$ costs the same as LoRA$_r$, reaches the same number of dimensions, and has maximum rank $\\min(kr,m,n)$, but caps every block at rank $r/k$. A generic rank-$r$ update has rank $r$ on every block, so for $k\\ge2$ and $r\\le\\min(m,n)/k$ the images of LoRA$_r$ and GraLoRA$_k$ are incomparable.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) The generic fibre contains the generic gauge orbit, and $d=\\lvert M\\rvert-\\dim(\\text{generic fibre})$.\n\nThe LoHa torus acts by $(B_1,A_1,B_2,A_2)\\mapsto(D_1B_1,A_1D_2,D_1^{-1}B_2,A_2D_2^{-1})$. It preserves the product because $(D_1XD_2)\\odot(D_1^{-1}YD_2^{-1})=X\\odot Y$. At generic parameters, the joint action of $GL_{r_1}\\times GL_{r_2}\\times T_m\\times T_n$ has a one-dimensional stabiliser, $\\{(cI,c^{-1}I,cI,c^{-1}I)\\}$: if $D_1B_1=B_1g_1$ for generic $B_1$ with $m>r_1$, every row of $B_1$ is a left eigenvector of $g_1$, so $g_1=cI$ and $D_1=cI$, and the other three equations force $D_2=c^{-1}I$ and $g_2=c^{-1}I$. So the orbit has dimension $r_1^2+r_2^2+m+n-1$, which gives the upper bound by (a). Trivially $d\\le mn$.\n\nGraLoRA's image is a product of $k^2$ cones $\\mathcal M_{\\le r/k}(\\tfrac mk\\times\\tfrac nk)$, of total dimension $k^2\\cdot\\tfrac rk\\cdot\\tfrac{m+n-r}{k}=r(m+n-r)$. For the rank, write block $(i,j)$ as $U_{ij}V_{ij}^\\top$ with $U_{ij}\\in\\mathbb R^{m/k\\times r/k}$ and $V_{ij}\\in\\mathbb R^{n/k\\times r/k}$. Then $\\Delta W=\\mathrm{blockdiag}(L_1,\\dots,L_k)\\,S$, with $L_i=[U_{i1}\\cdots U_{ik}]\\in\\mathbb R^{m/k\\times r}$ and $S\\in\\mathbb R^{kr\\times n}$ stacking the $V_{ij}^\\top$, each supported on column block $j$. For generic blocks and $r\\le\\min(m,n)/k$, each $L_i$ has full column rank $r$ and $S$ has full row rank $kr$, so $\\operatorname{rank}\\Delta W=kr$. In general the rank is at most $\\min(kr,m,n)$. (Placing rank-$r/k$ blocks only on the diagonal gives rank $r$, not $kr$.)\n\nLoHa$_{r,r}$ maps into LoRA$_{r^2}$ (the face-splitting arrow of Exposé VIII.C), which gives domination for $r\\le2$. Under $2r^2<m+n-1$ the LoHa upper bound is $2r(m+n-2r)-\\big((m+n-1)-2r^2\\big)<d(\\mathrm{LoRA}_{2r})\\le mn$, so the cap does not bind, the gap is at least $(m+n-1)-2r^2>0$, and strictness follows. Conversely, when $2r^2\\ge m+n-1$ the bound no longer separates them. The KronA image is a Segre cone. HRA is Thm II.7. The remaining rows are standard; for FourierFT, $\\mathrm{Re}\\circ\\mathrm{ifft2}$ sends the frequencies $p$ and $-p$ to the same real pattern. ∎ [Num: LoHa $38,59,93,101$ at $(m,n,r)=(8,7,2),(12,10,2),(12,10,3),(20,16,2)$, and $16=mn$ at $(4,4,2)$, where the formula gives $17$ and $d(\\mathrm{LoRA}_4)=16$ too; LoHa$_{3,3}$ against LoRA$_6$: $73$ against $72$ at $(9,9,3)$; GraLoRA dimension $48,64,108$ at $(m,n,r,k)=(8,8,4,2),(12,8,4,2),(12,12,6,3)$; GraLoRA generic rank $8=kr$ at $(16,16,4,2)$ and $12=\\min(m,n)$ at $(12,12,6,3)$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted at explicit parameter values: the GraLoRA attainment proof was wrong (diagonal blocks give rank r, not kr) and the maximum rank is min(kr, m, n); LoHa's equality and DoRA's d need the cap min(., mn); the HRA row omitted W0 injectivity (and apply_GS=False); FourierFT is not injective when conjugate frequency pairs are sampled; (c)'s 'trade' fails for r <= 2, where LoHa_r lies inside LoRA_2r; 'GraLoRA trades nothing' overreached (block ranks are capped at r/k, so the images are incomparable). Repaired: caps and hypotheses added, a correct generic-rank proof for GraLoRA, the linear-method row stated via the kernel, (c) restricted, commentary corrected, Tucker and TT citations separated.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): (a) and (b) hold; (c) failed inside its own hypotheses. At (4,4,2) LoHa_{2,2} and LoRA_4 both have d = 16 (no strict domination, and LoHa_{2,2} numerically fills R^{4x4}); at (9,9,3) and (16,16,4) LoHa beats LoRA_{2r} by one dimension and in rank, so there is no trade. Repaired: (c) now assumes 2r^2 < m+n-1, which forces a positive gap and keeps the mn cap from binding; the gap is stated as 'at least' (exact by [Num]); the no-trade regime 2r^2 >= m+n-1 is stated with examples (r=64 on 4096^2; LyCORIS r=32 on Stable Diffusion layers). The LoHa stabiliser is now proved. Table: gauge column renamed 'exhibited Lie gauge' (not locally transitive where the cap binds); VeRA exempt from r <= min(m,n) with max rank min(r,m,n); KronA max rank min(m1,n1)min(m2,n2); TT/Tucker at admissible ranks; BitFit moved out of the per-box table; HRA's even r is an init requirement and r in {n-1,n} noted; hybrid GraLoRA excluded; HF FourierFT seed note. Exposé 0 headline and the commentary now give the gap as (m+n-1)-2r^2.","public_statement":"For one $m\\times n$ weight, the expressive dimension is at most the budget minus the dimension of the generic gauge orbit, with equality when the gauge is locally transitive on generic fibres. LoRA$_r$ and GraLoRA ($k\\times k$ blocks of rank $r/k$, HF default `hybrid_r=0`) have the same budget and the same dimension $r(m+n-r)$. GraLoRA's generic rank is $\\min(kr,m,n)$, but every block is capped at rank $r/k$, so for $k\\ge2$ and $kr\\le\\min(m,n)$ neither image contains the other. At equal budget $2r(m+n)$, LoHa$_{r,r}$ has dimension at most $2r(m+n-r)-(m+n-1)$ (with equality checked numerically below the $mn$ cap). When $2r^2<m+n-1$ that is at least $(m+n-1)-2r^2$ below LoRA$_{2r}$, which makes LoHa a dimension-for-rank trade (rank up to $r^2$) for $r\\ge3$ and strictly dominated for $r\\le2$. When $2r^2\\ge m+n-1$, for example $r=64$ on $4096\\times4096$ or LyCORIS-style $r=32$ on 320- to 640-wide layers, the dimension bound no longer separates the two, and wherever it is attained, as the numerical checks find, LoHa matches or exceeds LoRA$_{2r}$ in dimension as well as in rank.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (c) and the public statement no longer assert the 2r^2 >= m+n-1 'no trade' clause outright: it holds wherever the LoHa upper bound is attained, which is checked numerically, not proved; that clause keeps 2r <= min(m,n) and drops r^2 <= min(m,n); 'exactly that many' fewer dimensions likewise needs the bound attained."},{"id":"II.13","name":"transport of structure","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.13 (transport of structure).** [Prop] For $T\\in GL(\\Theta)$ put $\\tau_T(\\theta)=\\theta_0+T(\\theta-\\theta_0)$ and $T_*M:=(Q,q_0,\\tau_T\\circ\\rho)$.\n- $T_*$ is a strict automorphism of $\\mathsf{Meth}(\\Theta,\\theta_0)$, with inverse $(T^{-1})_*$. It maps $S_1$ to $T\\,S_1$ and preserves $d$, $g$, the gauge group, the first-order deficit, polynomial degree, the regular or apex type and the germ type of the image. It does not preserve rank, and, for the family extension below, it does not preserve the covariance group.\n- $K^{T_*M}=T\\,K^M\\,T^\\top$ for every $T$. For any update rule that queries the loss only through $q\\mapsto L(\\rho(q))$, with its own state on $Q$, $T_*M$ trained on $L$ and $M$ trained on $L\\circ\\tau_T$ have identical $q$-trajectories, so their $\\theta$-trajectories are conjugate by $\\tau_T$. Rules that read $\\nabla_\\theta L$ and solve a least-squares problem on $\\Theta$ (LoRA-Pro), and $\\theta$-level weight decay or clipping, are covered only for orthogonal $T$. Orthogonality of $T$ is exactly the condition for $\\tau_T$ to be a Frobenius isometry; it is needed only to keep the Frobenius spectrum of $K$, or an isometry-invariant loss class such as $\\|\\theta-\\theta^\\star\\|^2$, unchanged.\n- Coordinatewise optimizers (SGD, Adam) are also equivariant under coordinate permutations of $Q$. The Kronecker identification below needs this, because it composes $\\mathcal R^{-1}_*$ with the vec reshape of $Q$.\n- *Families and rebasing.* For $T$ independent of the base point, define the family extension $(T_*M)(\\theta):=(Q,q_0,\\theta+T(\\rho_\\theta-\\theta))$. For an additive family with fixed shape $C$ (Def I.2), $T_*M$ is additive with shape $TC$, so transport commutes with rebasing: $R_K(T_*M)=\\tau_T(R_K(M))$ (Thm V.3(a)). For action-type families this can fail even for $T=2I$, because the transported family acts by $I+T(g-I)$: for OFT on $\\Theta=\\mathbb R^{1\\times2}$, two cycles of $\\theta\\mapsto\\theta(2R-I)$ with $R=\\mathrm{rot}(\\pi)$ reach $9\\theta_0$, outside the transported circle. Transports that depend on $\\theta_0$ may fail (HiRA below) or may not (LoRA transported by the SVD frame of $\\theta_0$, since $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ is $O(m)\\times O(n)$-invariant). The covariance group (Def II.14) is a property of a family and is compared through this extension.\n\nInstances:\n- **Kronecker family.** An isomorphism of slices over $\\Theta'=\\mathbb R^{m_1n_1\\times m_2n_2}$ and over $\\Theta$: $\\sum_{i\\le k}C_i\\otimes D_i=\\mathcal R^{-1}_*\\mathrm{LoRA}_k$, with $Q$ reshaped by vec, dynamics included for coordinatewise optimizers. KronA with unconstrained factors is $\\mathcal R^{-1}_*\\mathrm{LoRA}_1$. LoKr is $\\mathcal R^{-1}_*$ of LoRA$_1$ with the second factor constrained to $\\mathrm{vec}\\,\\mathcal M_{\\le r'}$ when $r<\\max(m_2,n_2)/2$ (HF's switch); otherwise HF's second factor is full and LoKr is KronA. LoKr's `decompose_both` option also constrains the first factor.\n- **Linear methods as coordinate subspaces in transported frames.**\n  - FourierFT: sparse in the real cosine half of the real Fourier frame. HF's $\\Delta W=c\\,\\mathrm{Re}(\\mathrm{ifft2}(S))$, with scaling $c$ (default $150$), is the transport of a mask, with $g=0$, only when the sampled index set has no conjugate pair $(u,v),(-u,-v)$; otherwise $g$ is the number of such pairs. Its image lies in the point-symmetric subspace $\\Delta W_{j,l}=\\Delta W_{-j,-l}$, and the frame is orthogonal only up to scale (self-conjugate frequencies have twice the squared norm).\n  - WaveFT: sparse in a wavelet frame, when HF's pad–IDWT–crop map is invertible (plausible for Haar on even shapes; unverified for other wavelet families).\n  - C3A: block-circulant. Over $\\mathbb R$ the frame is given by the powers $P^k$ of the cyclic shift (Gram matrix $bI$). Over $\\mathbb C$, $\\mathrm{circ}(w)=F^{-1}\\mathrm{diag}(Fw)F$ for first column $w$, and the real form has $2\\times2$ rotation–scaling blocks. HF adds a factor $1/\\mathrm{in\\_features}$.\n  - LoRA-XS, SVFT, TinyLoRA: masks or random subspaces in the SVD frame of $W_0$, a $\\theta_0$-dependent transport.\n  - SHiRA, FISH, Trainable Tokens: masks in the standard frame.\n  - MiSS/Bone in its default (balance) mode: $\\Delta W=D(\\mathbf 1_g^\\top\\otimes I_r)$, which is LoRA-FA with a frozen *summing* projection. Its BAT mode, $\\Delta W=\\mathrm{blocks}(W_0)\\,M+M$ blockwise, is linear in the trained block $M$ but depends on $\\theta_0$, so it is not additive.\n  - These frame readings of FourierFT and C3A are for `init_weights=True`. HF's defaults start off $\\theta_0$: FourierFT draws its spectrum from $\\mathcal N(0,1)$ with scaling $150$ (at $768^2$ with $1000$ frequencies, $\\lVert\\Delta W_0\\rVert_F\\approx4.3$, about $14\\%$ of a std-$0.04$ $W_0$), and C3A is Xavier-uniform. These are objects with a pointing defect.\n- **HiRA** $=(D_{W_0})_*\\mathrm{LoRA}$ inside the slice at $\\theta_0$, when $W_0$ has no zero entries. Because $D_{W_0}$ depends on $\\theta_0$, re-based HiRA is not transported ReLoRA: two cycles reach $W_0\\odot(J+Z_1)\\odot(J+Z_2)$, whose relative update $Z_1+Z_2+Z_1\\odot Z_2$ has rank up to $\\min(2r+r^2,m,n)$, attained, not $\\le2r$.","proof":"*Proof.* $D(\\tau_T\\circ\\rho)=T\\,D\\rho$ gives the statements about $S_1$, the ranks of the differential, $d$, $g$ and the deficit, and $K^{T_*M}=TD\\rho\\Lambda D\\rho^\\top T^\\top$. Since $T_*$ does not touch $Q$, $L\\circ\\rho_{T_*M}=(L\\circ\\tau_T)\\circ\\rho_M$ exactly. Coordinate permutations of $Q$ commute with every coordinatewise update rule. A rule reading $\\nabla_\\theta L$ through a Frobenius least-squares step moves by $-(D\\rho^\\top T^\\top TD\\rho)^+D\\rho^\\top T^\\top\\nabla L$ under $T_*M$, against $-(D\\rho^\\top D\\rho)^+D\\rho^\\top T^\\top\\nabla L$ for $M$ on $L\\circ\\tau_T$; they agree when $T^\\top T=I$. For additive families, $R_K$ is the $K$-fold Minkowski sum of the shape, and $T$ is linear, so $T(C^{+K})=(TC)^{+K}$ by induction. For OFT with $T=2I$, $\\theta_0(2\\,\\mathrm{rot}(\\pi)-I)^2=9\\theta_0$. The circulant identity is the DFT diagonalisation. $\\mathrm{Re}\\circ\\mathrm{ifft2}$ sends conjugate frequencies to the same real pattern. The MiSS formula is its definition in efficient form. For HiRA, $(J+Z_1)\\odot(J+Z_2)=J+Z_1+Z_2+Z_1\\odot Z_2$. ∎ [Num: $\\|K^{T_*M}-TK^MT^\\top\\|=2\\times10^{-14}$ for a Gaussian $T$; Adam on $T_*M$ with $L$ and on $M$ with $L\\circ\\tau_T$ agree to $8\\times10^{-11}$; two HiRA cycles give relative-update rank $3$, $8$, $15$ at $r=1,2,3$ ($m=n=30$).]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: the orthogonality and permutation hypotheses were misplaced (K^{T*M} = T K T^T and conjugate dynamics hold for every T, since T*M trained on L is M trained on L o tau_T); rebasing closure transfers only for theta0-independent T (re-based HiRA reaches relative rank up to 2r + r^2, not 2r); the C3A circulant identity was off by a factor b and uses a complex frame; FourierFT is not a transported mask when conjugate pairs are sampled (g >= 1); the Kronecker identification changes the shape of Theta; the covariance group is not transport-invariant. Repaired throughout, and the [Pred] made testable.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): the automorphism, cometric and conjugacy claims hold. The rebasing bullet ('transported only by T that do not depend on theta_0') was ill-typed and false both ways: a fixed T = 2I breaks closure for OFT (two cycles reach 9 theta_0), and an SVD-frame transport of LoRA keeps it. Repaired: a family extension is defined; for additive families and fixed T, R_K(T_*M) = tau_T(R_K(M)) with a Minkowski-sum proof; action-type and theta_0-dependent cases are stated as possible failures with both examples. The update-rule claim now covers rules that see the loss only through q -> L(rho(q)); LoRA-Pro-type and theta-level decay or clipping need T orthogonal. MiSS BAT is linear in its block (theta_0-dependent, not additive), not non-linear. LoKr's identification holds below HF's switch r < max(m2,n2)/2; HiRA needs W_0 without zeros and reaches rank min(2r+r^2,m,n), attained. FourierFT's scaling and the HF defaults that start off theta_0 (FourierFT N(0,1) x 150, C3A Xavier) are stated; WaveFT's mask reading needs an invertible pad-IDWT-crop map. Commentary now lists every invariant transport preserves and uses the real cosine frame instead of 'the 2D DFT'.","public_statement":"For any invertible linear map $T$ of weight space, rewriting a method as $\\theta\\mapsto\\theta_0+T(\\rho(q)-\\theta_0)$ is an automorphism of the slice of methods at $\\theta_0$. It preserves expressive dimension, gauge waste, the gauge group, the first-order deficit and the regular or apex type, sends $S_1$ to $T\\,S_1$ and the cometric $K$ to $TKT^\\top$, and leaves parameter-space training unchanged when the loss is pulled back by the same map, for any optimizer that sees the loss only through the trainable parameters. It does not preserve rank. Unconstrained Kronecker sums are LoRA after the Van Loan–Pitsianis rearrangement; FourierFT (with no sampled conjugate pair and zero init), WaveFT and C3A are coordinate masks in fixed real cosine, wavelet or circulant frames; HiRA is LoRA transported by $X\\mapsto W_0\\odot X$ when $W_0$ has no zero entries. For additive methods and a fixed $T$, transport commutes with merge-and-restart; because HiRA's transport depends on $W_0$, re-merged HiRA reaches relative rank $\\min(2r+r^2,m,n)$ rather than $2r$."},{"id":"II.15","name":"descent through function-preserving reparametrisations","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation II.15 (descent through function-preserving reparametrisations).** [Obs] Let $M=\\prod_bM_b$ be a box-wise method: each $M_b$ is a member of a natural family with covariance group $\\mathcal G_{M_b}$, and no trainable parameters or random frames are shared across boxes. Let $G_{\\rm arch}$ act on $\\Theta=\\prod_b\\Theta_b\\times\\Theta_{\\rm frozen}$, preserving this split and acting on each adapted box by $W_b\\mapsto g_bW_bh_b^{-1}$ with $(g_b,h_b)\\in\\mathcal G_{M_b}$. Assume $f(g\\theta,-)=f(\\theta,-)$ on a $G_{\\rm arch}$-stable set containing $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(\\theta_0)$. Then $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(g\\theta_0)=g\\cdot\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(\\theta_0)$, and the set of *functions* reachable by $M$ from $g\\theta_0$ equals the set reachable from $\\theta_0$. For methods with random frames, the equality holds in distribution, provided the frames are drawn independently per box. Boxes that share a frame need a pathwise condition: a law-preserving map $T_{(g,h)}$ on frame space with $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M_b(g_bWh_b^{-1};TF)=g_b\\,\\mathrm{Im}\\,M_b(W;F)\\,h_b^{-1}$ for all $W$ and $F$, with the same $T$ on every box that shares $F$ (marginal covariance in law, as in Def II.14, is not enough). For data-dependent methods, require covariance pathwise in the data, and that the box-$b$ data at $g\\theta_0$ is the Def II.14 transform under $(g_b,h_b)$ of the data at $\\theta_0$. This holds for intertwiner gauges such as hidden-unit permutations, query–key and value–output head gauges and residual rotations. The statement concerns reachable sets only; training trajectories and outcomes can still differ, for instance under Adam or non-isotropic initialisations.\n\nExamples of $G_{\\rm arch}$:\n- hidden-neuron permutations;\n- the query–key gauge $(W_Q,W_K)\\mapsto(gW_Q,g^{-\\top}W_K)$ per head. With no positional encoding or an absolute one, $g\\in GL_{d_k}$. Under RoPE, $g$ must commute with every RoPE rotation, which leaves the per-frequency $2\\times2$ rotation–scalings $(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{d_k/2}$, shared across a GQA group. Under partial RoPE on $r$ of the $d_k$ dimensions the group is $(\\mathbb C^\\times)^{r/2}\\times GL_{d_k-r}$. Under QK-norm, $g$ must in addition be orthogonal when the norm's $\\varepsilon>0$ (conformal, $g\\in CO(d_k)$, when $\\varepsilon=0$) and satisfy $g^\\top\\Gamma_qR_t\\Gamma_kg=\\Gamma_qR_t\\Gamma_k$ for every RoPE rotation $R_t$, where $\\Gamma_q,\\Gamma_k$ are the gains. OLMo 2 normalises $q$ and $k$ across all heads jointly;\n- a single global orthogonal rotation $Q$ of the residual stream of a pre-norm transformer whose norm gains can all be fused into the linear layers they feed, after LayerNorm is converted to RMSNorm (SliceGPT's computational invariance; QuaRot's offline rotation). SliceGPT's per-block rotations $Q_\\ell$ need extra residual matrices and change the architecture. Sandwich and post-sublayer norms (Gemma 2 and 3, OLMo 2) are excluded, because their gains cannot be fused and $\\mathrm{diag}(\\gamma)$ does not commute with $Q$. The comparison is between the gain-fused model and the fused-and-rotated model: gain fusion is a diagonal input rescaling outside $G_{\\rm arch}$ that already changes PiSSA's split and block OFT's image (Prop II.16), and converting LayerNorm to RMSNorm changes LoRA's reachable set on the residual writers.","proof":"*Proof.* Apply per-box covariance factor by factor; the frozen coordinates are single points, so $\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(g\\theta_0)=g\\,\\mathrm{Im}\\,M(\\theta_0)$. Then $\\{\\Phi_f(\\theta):\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}M(g\\theta_0)\\}=\\{\\Phi_f(g\\theta):\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}M(\\theta_0)\\}=\\{\\Phi_f(\\theta):\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Im}M(\\theta_0)\\}$. With independent per-box frames, the joint law is the product of the per-box laws, so per-box covariance in law gives the joint statement. With a shared frame, the pathwise condition applies the same $T$ to it in every box, and $T$ preserves its law. ∎ [Num: a tied rank-one update shared by two boxes violates the conclusion (relative distance up to $0.30$), while independent per-box LoRA satisfies it; a Haar frame shared by two boxes, each covariant in law, gives a joint statistic of $0.44$ from $\\theta_0$ against $0.81$ from $g\\theta_0$; under RoPE a generic $g\\in GL_8$ changes attention scores by up to $313$, a RoPE-commuting $g$ by $5\\times10^{-14}$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: the first equality Im M(g theta0) = g Im M(theta0) needs box-wise methods with no shared parameters or frames (a tied update is a counterexample) and box-preserving gauges, and holds only in law for random or data frames; the query-key gauge with general g fails under RoPE (only the commutant (C^x)^{d_k/2} survives); and the commentary got methods wrong: (IA)^3 with HF defaults does descend through residual rotations, while block OFT does not satisfy the hypothesis. Repaired: explicit hypotheses and a per-box lemma; RoPE, QK-norm and GQA caveats; a side-sensitive descent list; Conj A restated; 'fails the sufficient condition' instead of 'does not descend'.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): the deterministic core and proof hold. False: the shared-frame parenthetical ('or the g_b agree') fails because Def II.14 covariance is marginal in law (counterexample with a shared Haar frame); the data-dependent case needed a pathwise condition. Repaired: shared frames now need a law-preserving pathwise map T applied to every box that shares the frame; data-dependent methods need pathwise covariance and transformed data; the product-of-laws step is in the proof. Examples: QK-norm condition stated exactly (orthogonal for eps > 0, conformal for eps = 0, plus the gain/RoPE relation), partial RoPE group added; residual rotations restricted to a single global Q on a gain-fused pre-norm model (not Gemma 2/3 or OLMo 2 sandwich norms, not SliceGPT's per-block Q_l), with the fused-but-unrotated model as the control. Commentary: the criterion is membership in G_M (side for one-sided methods, group type for coordinate-structured ones); LoHa fails only for r >= 2; OFT on o/down added to the satisfying list and BOFT on o/down (boft_s) to the failing list; (IA)^3 uses q for Qwen/Gemma/Phi; QuaRot's per-head Hadamard is a value-output gauge inside G_arch. Exposé X item 4 now says reachable sets, with training outcomes unchanged only for (IA)^3 and, in law, for LoRA/DoRA under SGD with isotropic init.","public_statement":"Suppose a PEFT method acts matrix by matrix, with no trainable parameters shared between matrices, and a function-preserving reparametrisation of the base model acts on every adapted matrix as $W\\mapsto gWh^{-1}$ with $(g,h)$ in that method's covariance group. Then the method reaches exactly the same set of network functions from the reparametrised checkpoint as from the original; for random frames this holds in distribution when the frames are drawn independently per matrix, and shared frames need a pathwise condition. Examples are LoRA under hidden-unit permutations, query–key gauges that commute with RoPE, and a single global orthogonal rotation of the residual stream of a gain-fused pre-norm model, compared with the fused but unrotated model. This concerns reachable sets only, not training trajectories or outcomes under Adam or non-isotropic initialisations.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the statement now says, as the paper's and the public statement do, that it concerns reachable sets only; training trajectories and outcomes can still differ (Adam, non-isotropic initialisations)."},{"id":"II.16","name":"covariant data-aware splits","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition II.16 (covariant data-aware splits).** [Prop] Let $C=\\mathbb E[xx^\\top]\\succ0$ be the input second moment, so that under $x\\mapsto hx$ we have $W\\mapsto Wh^{-1}$ and $C\\mapsto hCh^\\top$. Let $LL^\\top=C$, write $[M]_r$ for the rank-$r$ truncated SVD, and $CO(n)=\\mathbb R_{>0}\\cdot O(n)$. Assume $1\\le r<k:=\\min(m,n)$ and the spectral gap each split needs: $\\sigma_r>\\sigma_{r+1}$ of $W_0L$ in (a); in (b), $\\sigma_r>\\sigma_{r+1}$ of $W_0C$ or $W_0$ for top-$r$ splits, and $\\sigma_{k-r}>\\sigma_{k-r+1}$ for bottom-$r$ splits. Covariance groups are taken over the generic set where these gaps hold.\n\n(a) The whitened split $X^*=\\arg\\min_{\\operatorname{rank}X\\le r}\\|(W_0-X)L\\|_F=[W_0L]_rL^{-1}=\\Pi_rW_0$, where $\\Pi_r$ is the top-$r$ eigenprojector of the output second moment $W_0CW_0^\\top$, is $GL_n$-covariant on the input side and $CO(m)$-covariant on the output side; its joint group is $CO(m)\\times GL_n$. This is the classical reduced-rank-regression (SVD-LLM) solution.\n\n(b) The formula-level CorDA splits, IPM ($X=[W_0C]_rC^{-1}$, the minimiser of $\\|(W_0-X)C\\|_F$) and KPM (the bottom-$r$ components of $W_0C$ mapped back by $C^{-1}$, $X=(W_0C-[W_0C]_{k-r})C^{-1}$), with or without CorDA's trace-relative damping $C+\\kappa(\\mathrm{tr}\\,C/n)I$, and the PiSSA and MiLoRA splits ($[W_0]_r$ and the bottom-$r$ part $W_0-[W_0]_{k-r}$), have input-side covariance group exactly $CO(n)$; jointly, $CO(m)\\times CO(n)$.\n\n(c) **Scope.** An absolute damping $C+\\lambda I$ reduces the input-side group of (a) and of (b) to $O(n)$. The official and HF CorDA code divide each calibration sample by the absolute value of its largest entry before accumulating $C$, which keeps only positive scalings and coordinate permutations, so (b) describes the CorDA formulas and does not cover shipped CorDA under rotations.","proof":"*Proof.* For $r<\\min(m,n)$ the translation stabiliser of $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ is $\\{0\\}$, so covariance of the images (Def II.14) is equivalent to covariance of the splits.\n\n(a) With $L'=hL$, $[W_0h^{-1}hL]_r(hL)^{-1}=[W_0L]_rL^{-1}h^{-1}$, and the result does not depend on the square root chosen, since $[W_0LO]_rO^\\top=[W_0L]_r$ for $O\\in O(n)$. Also $[W_0L]_r=\\Pi_rW_0L$, because the top left singular vectors of $W_0L$ are the top eigenvectors of $W_0LL^\\top W_0^\\top$. On the output side, the eigenprojector of $gW_0CW_0^\\top g^\\top$ is $g\\Pi_rg^{-1}$ for all $W_0$ exactly when $g$ is conformal (transpose the lemma below).\n\n(b) Lemma: for $1\\le s<\\min(m,n)$, $[Mh]_s=[M]_sh$ for all $M$ with a gap at $s$ iff $h\\in CO(n)$. \"If\": $M\\,cQ$ has the singular vectors of $M$ rotated by $Q$ and the singular values scaled by $c$. \"Only if\": take $M=\\sum_{i\\le s+1}\\sigma_iu_iv_i^\\top$ with distinct $\\sigma$'s. The top-$s$ left singular space of $Mh$ is $\\mathrm{span}\\{u_1,\\dots,u_s\\}$ only if the coupling $\\sigma_s\\sigma_{s+1}v_s^\\top hh^\\top v_{s+1}$ vanishes; requiring this for every orthonormal pair forces $hh^\\top\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}I$. The bottom-$r$ part of $M$ is $M-[M]_{k-r}$, so bottom-$r$ splits follow from the lemma at $s=k-r$. CorDA's covariance requires $[W_0Ch^\\top]_sh^{-\\top}C^{-1}h^{-1}=[W_0C]_sC^{-1}h^{-1}$, which is the lemma for $h^\\top$ applied to $M=W_0C$, an arbitrary matrix. Trace-relative damping commutes with $CO(n)$, since $\\mathrm{tr}(c^2QCQ^\\top)=c^2\\,\\mathrm{tr}\\,C$. PiSSA and MiLoRA are the lemma for $h^{-1}$. (c) An absolute $\\lambda I$ is fixed by $O(n)$ but not rescaled by $c$; the per-sample max-normalisation commutes only with positive scalings and permutations. ∎ [Num: under $h=3Q$ with $Q$ orthogonal, CorDA (IPM and bottom-$r$ KPM), PiSSA and bottom-$r$ MiLoRA move covariantly to $4\\times10^{-15}$; under a generic $h$ only the whitened split does ($2\\times10^{-15}$, against $0.63$ and $1.1$); at $r=n$ PiSSA is $GL_n$-covariant; damping $0.1$ breaks it under a generic $h$ ($0.14$); under $h=3Q$ an absolute damping $0.1$ breaks the whitened split (about $2\\times10^{-2}$), while trace-relative damping keeps it ($3\\times10^{-15}$).]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: 'only O(n)-covariant' names the wrong group, because truncated SVD is homogeneous, so CorDA, PiSSA and MiLoRA are exactly CO(n)-covariant; the proof conflated invariance of the criterion's value with covariance of the argmin; EVA is not a split (Type I, GL x GL image); 'iff it uses a square root of C' is false (Pi_r W0 is covariant without one); damping breaks covariance. Repaired: hypotheses (C positive definite, spectral gap); the whitened split equals Pi_r W0 (GL_n on the input side, CO(m) on the output side); CorDA IPM/KPM and PiSSA are exactly CO(n)-covariant via a new lemma; EVA, damping and one-sidedness addressed; table entries changed to CO x CO.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): (a) and the CO(n) lemma hold. False or unfaithful: KPM and MiLoRA train the bottom-r components, not the complement or tail of rank min(m,n)-r; the gap hypotheses for (b) and r < min(m,n) were missing ('canonical minimiser' was undefined); CorDA's damping is trace-relative and keeps CO(n), while the shipped code's per-sample max-normalisation breaks rotations; the design rule 'depends on the data only through W_0 C W_0^T' was not sufficient. Repaired: (b) restated with the correct bottom-r formulas and derived from the lemma at index k-r; hypotheses and gaps added; translation-stabiliser step added to the proof; new scope clause (c) on absolute damping and shipped CorDA; the output-side claim quantified over all W_0. Commentary: the sufficient condition is X = Phi(W_0 C W_0^T) W_0 (whitened split, Astra); two-sided whitening with the output moment is degenerate and no (W_0,C)-only split is GL x GL-covariant; EVA's image is GL_m x CO(n)-covariant under HF's default rho = 2. Table: LoRA-GA is O x O, Astra and EVA rows updated.","public_statement":"Assume the input second moment $C=\\mathbb E[xx^\\top]$ is positive definite, $1\\le r<\\min(m,n)$, and the relevant spectral gaps hold. The whitened rank-$r$ split $[W_0L]_rL^{-1}=\\Pi_rW_0$ ($LL^\\top=C$, $\\Pi_r$ the top-$r$ eigenprojector of $W_0CW_0^\\top$), the classical reduced-rank (SVD-LLM) solution, commutes with every invertible change of a layer's input coordinates and every conformal change of its output coordinates. The formula-level PiSSA and MiLoRA splits and the paper-form CorDA splits (top-$r$ or bottom-$r$ components of $W_0C$, mapped back by $C^{-1}$, with or without CorDA's trace-proportional damping) commute with exactly the positively scaled orthogonal maps $CO(n)$, so diagonal rescalings such as norm-gain folding or SmoothQuant change them. An absolute damping $C+\\lambda I$ cuts this to $O(n)$, and the released CorDA code divides each calibration sample by the absolute value of its largest entry, which breaks rotation covariance; the result covers the formulas, not shipped CorDA.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (c) corrected: the shipped CorDA code divides each calibration sample by |max(x)|, the absolute value of its largest entry, not by its largest absolute entry; public statement made precise; the framework.md commentary adds that Astra's paper form (tail eigenprojector of the output covariance) has the covariant form with the centred input covariance."},{"id":"III.2","name":"chain rule for cometrics","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation III.2 (chain rule for cometrics).** [Obs] Let $\\sigma:Q'\\to Q$ and $\\rho:Q\\to X$ be smooth, with $X=\\Theta$ or a function or output space. Train $Q'$ by gradient flow with a constant rate operator $\\Lambda'\\succ0$, and put $K^\\sigma_{q'}:=D\\sigma_{q'}\\Lambda'D\\sigma_{q'}^\\top$. Then\n$$K^{\\rho\\sigma}_{q'}=D\\rho_{\\sigma(q')}\\,K^\\sigma_{q'}\\,D\\rho_{\\sigma(q')}^\\top,$$\nand no metric on $Q$ is used. $K$ is a degenerate cometric (PSD, of rank $\\le\\dim Q'$); when $\\sigma$ is not injective, $K^\\sigma$ is a field along $\\sigma$, not a cometric on $Q$. For LoRA, $\\rho(B,A)=W_0+sBA$ with $s=\\alpha/r$ ($\\alpha/\\sqrt r$ for rsLoRA), block rates $\\Lambda=\\mathrm{diag}(\\eta_BI,\\eta_AI)$ and no LoRA dropout, under gradient flow (equivalently, to first order in the step size for GD):\n$$K_{(B,A)}(G)=s^2\\big(\\eta_B\\,GA^\\top A+\\eta_A\\,BB^\\top G\\big).$$\nUnder Adam there is no state-independent linear cometric; Thms III.10 and III.12 treat Adam.","proof":"*Proof.* $D(\\rho\\sigma)=D\\rho\\,D\\sigma$, so $K^{\\rho\\sigma}=D\\rho\\,D\\sigma\\Lambda'D\\sigma^\\top D\\rho^\\top$. For LoRA, use $\\nabla_B=sGA^\\top$, $\\nabla_A=sB^\\top G$ and $\\dot W=s(\\dot BA+B\\dot A)$. Discrete GD adds the term $s\\,\\delta B\\,\\delta A$, of order $\\eta^2$. ∎ [Num: finite-difference Jacobians agree to $6\\times10^{-9}$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct. Wording fixes: Lambda' is any constant rate operator (not 'Euclidean'); base points and the degeneracy of K made explicit; the target may be a function or prefix space; the LoRA formula is for gradient flow or first-order GD, without LoRA dropout, with s = alpha/r; Adam has no fixed cometric. The prefix remark was corrected: equal images need encoder_hidden_size >= num_virtual_tokens - 1, the metric is replaced rather than 'differing by', and this alone does not explain Li-Liang's instability under AdamW.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"Under gradient flow, a parametrised method moves the weights with velocity $-K\\nabla_\\theta L$, where $K=D\\rho\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\rho^\\top$ is the induced cometric, and cometrics compose by the chain rule without any metric on intermediate spaces. For LoRA with block learning rates and no dropout, $K(G)=s^2(\\eta_B\\,GA^\\top A+\\eta_A\\,BB^\\top G)$. Under Adam there is no state-independent linear cometric."},{"id":"III.3","name":"gradient descent is natural exactly where cometrics agree; for LoRA's linear gauge, exactly along $O(r)$","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem III.3 (gradient descent is natural exactly where cometrics agree; for LoRA's linear gauge, exactly along $O(r)$).** [Thm]\n\n(a) Let $\\varphi:Q\\to Q'$ be a diffeomorphism with $\\rho'\\circ\\varphi=\\rho$, and let $\\Lambda,\\Lambda'$ be the rate operators on $Q,Q'$. Under gradient flow, the two methods induce the same $\\theta$-velocity at $q$ and at $\\varphi(q)$ for every loss iff $K^\\rho_q=K^{\\rho'}_{\\varphi(q)}$, that is,\n$$D\\rho'_{\\varphi(q)}\\big(D\\varphi_q\\Lambda D\\varphi_q^\\top-\\Lambda'\\big)D\\rho'^\\top_{\\varphi(q)}=0.$$\nThis holds whenever $D\\varphi\\Lambda D\\varphi^\\top=\\Lambda'$, which is an isometry when $\\Lambda=\\Lambda'=I$. That condition is necessary when $D\\rho'_{\\varphi(q)}$ is injective, but not in general. Any linear gauge of a linear parametrisation is natural: for $\\rho(x,y)=\\theta_0+x+y$, the shear $(x,y)\\mapsto(2x,y-x)$ preserves $\\rho$ and $K$ without being orthogonal. So are pointwise-orthogonal non-linear LoRA gauges $\\varphi(q)=\\varphi_{g(q)}(q)$ with $g(q)\\in O(r)$.\n\n(b) For LoRA's linear gauge $\\varphi_g(B,A)=(Bg^{-1},gA)$ with block-scalar rates, at points where $A$ has full row rank ($B$ arbitrary, including $B=0$) and $r<n$, the criterion holds iff $g\\in O(r)$. The same holds symmetrically where $B$ has full column rank and $r<m$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) Put $\\ell=L\\circ\\rho'$, so that $L\\circ\\rho=\\ell\\circ\\varphi$. The $\\rho$-flow is $\\dot q=-\\Lambda D\\varphi^\\top\\nabla\\ell$, so $\\frac{d}{dt}\\varphi(q)=-D\\varphi\\Lambda D\\varphi^\\top\\nabla\\ell$. Apply $D\\rho'$ and use $\\nabla\\ell=D\\rho'^\\top\\nabla_\\theta L$. The $\\rho'$-flow gives $-D\\rho'\\Lambda'D\\rho'^\\top\\nabla_\\theta L$. Since $\\nabla_\\theta L$ is arbitrary, the criterion follows. For the examples: a linear $\\rho$ has constant $D\\rho$ and $D\\rho\\,P=D\\rho$ for a linear gauge $P$. For a pointwise gauge, $D\\varphi_q=D\\varphi_{g(q)}+V$ with $V$ valued in fibre directions, which $D\\rho'$ kills, so the criterion reduces to that of $\\varphi_{g(q)}$, covered by (b).\n\n(b) The two cometrics agree for all $G$ iff\n$$\\eta_B\\,GA^\\top(g^\\top g-I)A=\\eta_A\\,B(I-g^{-1}g^{-\\top})B^\\top G\\quad\\text{for every }G.$$\nAn identity $GX=YG$ for all $G$ forces $X=cI_n$ and $Y=cI_m$ (test it on the matrix units $E_{ij}$). Here $\\operatorname{rank}X\\le r<n$, so $c=0$, and full row rank of $A$ gives $g^\\top g=I$. Only the full row rank of $A$ is used. ∎ [Num: at a random point a non-orthogonal $g$ gives a cometric mismatch of about $18$, an orthogonal one about $10^{-15}$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: (a) and (b) true as written, but the title 'natural only along isometries' was false in general (linear gauges of linear parametrisations and pointwise-orthogonal LoRA gauges are natural). Wording repaired: retitled; the criterion stated as equality of cometrics with rate operators (the form Thm III.5 uses); counterexamples to necessity added; (b) needs only A of full row rank (covers B=0); index set written O(r)\\GL_r = Sym^+_r; rsLoRA reread as a torus move rather than a gauge section, LoRA+ only under GD, and AdamW's group is B_r.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"Two parametrisations related by a diffeomorphism induce the same weight velocity under gradient flow, for every loss, exactly when their cometrics agree. For LoRA's linear gauge $(B,A)\\mapsto(Bg^{-1},gA)$ with block-scalar learning rates, at points where $A$ has full row rank and $r<n$, this happens exactly for orthogonal $g$, so plain gradient descent depends on the choice of factorisation beyond $O(r)$."},{"id":"III.4","name":"cometrics that descend to the rank-$r$ manifold","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition III.4 (cometrics that descend to the rank-$r$ manifold).** [Prop; the algorithms are Known: Tong–Ma–Chi 2021 and Zhang–Pilanci 2024 (scaled GD); Wang et al. 2024 (LoRA-Pro); Zhang et al. 2023 (LoRA-FA, frozen-$A$ form), with the corrected gradient of (c) from arXiv 2308.03303 v2/v3 (2026) and HF PEFT's LoRA-FA optimizer (2025). The $GL_r$-invariance of the scaled-GD metric and its descent to the quotient $\\mathcal M_r$ are classical: Mishra et al. 2012; Mishra–Sepulchre 2016] Write $U=\\mathrm{col}\\,B$ and $V=\\mathrm{row}\\,A$. Statements (a), (b) and the first sentence of (d) are on the full-rank locus; (c) needs only $\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r$, with $B$ arbitrary, including $B_0=0$. All statements concern gradient flow, or (S)GD to first order, on the stated gradients, undamped and without Adam. Cometrics include the learning rate.\n\n(a) Scaled GD, $\\delta B=-\\eta\\nabla_B(AA^\\top)^{-1}$ and $\\delta A=-\\eta(B^\\top B)^{-1}\\nabla_A$, is $GL_r$-equivariant with first-order cometric $K(G)=\\eta s^2(P_UG+GP_V)$.\n\n(b) LoRA-Pro's equivalent gradient $\\tilde g=P_UG+(I-P_U)GP_V$ is the Frobenius-orthogonal projection $\\Pi_{T_{W-W_0}\\mathcal M_r}(G)$. The flow of its SGD variant (LoRA-Pro Alg. 1) is the Riemannian gradient flow on $W_0+\\mathcal M_r$.\n\n(c) LoRA-FA's corrected gradient $s^{-2}\\nabla_B(AA^\\top)^{-1}$, fed to SGD, gives $K(G)=\\eta\\,GP_V$, which depends only on $\\mathrm{row}A_0\\in\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)$. Its original form, plain SGD on $B$, has $K(G)=s^2\\eta_BGA_0^\\top A_0$.\n\n(d) The cometrics of (a) and (b) are functions of $W$ on the full-rank locus and define vector fields tangent to $W_0+\\mathcal M_r$. The field of (c) is $G\\mapsto\\eta GP_V$ with $V=\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0$. Its flow is the Frobenius gradient flow on the slice $W_0+\\{XA_0\\}=W_0+\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}P_V$, of dimension $mr$, which contains $W_0$ and lies in $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$. The rank-$r$ part of the slice, $N_V=\\{\\mathrm{row}(W-W_0)=V\\}$, is open and dense in it and is a submanifold of $W_0+\\mathcal M_r$. On $N_V$ the field is tangent to $\\mathcal M_r$ and equals $\\eta GP_{\\mathrm{row}(W-W_0)}$, a function of $W$. So (c) descends too, as a degenerate cometric of rank $mr$ (less than $r(m+n-r)$ when $r<n$) whose flow keeps $\\mathrm{row}(W-W_0)=\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0$ fixed. The cometric of plain GD depends on the representative (it is invariant only under $O(r)$).\n\n*Practice.* Each published method departs from these idealisations, and the departures change the symmetry (Thm III.12):\n- the standard start $B_0=0$ lies off the full-rank locus, where (a) and (b) are undefined without damping. Zhang–Pilanci use $(B^\\top B+\\delta I)^{-1}$ with an unspecified $\\delta>0$, which is only $O(r)$-equivariant. Their LLM experiments report both damped scaled GD and scaled AdamW, in which the preconditioner is applied before the Adam moments ($B_r$);\n- LoRA-Pro's experiments use its AdamW variant (Alg. 2, with $m\\times n$ moments), $\\dot W=-\\Pi_T(\\mathrm{Adam}(\\Pi_TG))$. It descends to $W_0+\\mathcal M_r$ but is not a gradient flow, and its discrete update with the Sylvester-optimal free matrix is $GL_r$-invariant only to first order. The released code also damps both inverses ($\\mathrm{pinv}(\\cdot+10^{-8}I)$, so $O(r)$), replaces the step at $B_0=0$ by a LoRA-FA-type step, and runs with HF's default global-norm clipping;\n- LoRA-FA as published (v3, Alg. 1) and HF's `create_lorafa_optimizer` apply AdamW after the correction (HF also damps with $10^{-8}I$). The trajectory is then invariant only under signed row permutations of $A_0$ (Thm III.12(b)), so it depends on the basis and scale of $A_0$, not only on $\\mathrm{row}A_0$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) Substitute $\\nabla_B=sGA^\\top$ and $\\nabla_A=sB^\\top G$ into $\\delta W=s(\\delta BA+B\\delta A)+O(\\eta^2)$, using $A^\\top(AA^\\top)^{-1}A=P_V$ and $B(B^\\top B)^{-1}B^\\top=P_U$. For equivariance: under $(Bg^{-1},gA)$ we have $\\nabla_{B'}=\\nabla_Bg^\\top$ and $(A'A'^\\top)^{-1}=g^{-\\top}(AA^\\top)^{-1}g^{-1}$, so $\\delta B'=\\delta Bg^{-1}$; the same holds for $A$.\n\n(b) $T_{W-W_0}\\mathcal M_r=\\{XA+BY\\}=\\{D:P_{U^\\perp}DP_{V^\\perp}=0\\}$. The orthogonal projection onto it is $G-P_{U^\\perp}GP_{V^\\perp}=P_UG+GP_V-P_UGP_V=\\tilde g$. For any free matrix $X$ in Alg. 1, $s(h_BA+Bh_A)=P_UG+P_{U^\\perp}GP_V$, so $\\delta W=-\\eta\\,\\Pi_TG+O(\\eta^2)$.\n\n(c) $\\Delta W=s\\,\\Delta B\\,A=-\\eta\\,GA^\\top(AA^\\top)^{-1}A=-\\eta\\,GP_V$.\n\n(d) Read off the formulas. On $N_V$, $\\mathrm{row}(W-W_0)=V$, so $GP_V=GP_{\\mathrm{row}(W-W_0)}$ lies in $\\{XA_0\\}=T N_V\\subseteq T\\mathcal M_r$. Rank $r$ is an open condition on the slice, so $N_V$ is open in it. It is dense: $XA_0$ has the rank of $X$, and every $X\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}$ ($m\\ge r$) is a limit of rank-$r$ matrices $X+tE$ as $t\\to0$, because with $E$ having $I_r$ in its first $r$ rows the top $r\\times r$ minor of $X+tE$ is a monic polynomial of degree $r$ in $t$, which vanishes for only finitely many $t$. The operator $G\\mapsto\\eta GP_V$ has rank $mr$, which is below $r(m+n-r)$ exactly when $r<n$. ∎ [Num: gauge defects $\\sim10^{-15}$ for (a), against $52$ for plain GD; LoRA-FA with AdamW from $A_0$ and from $gA_0$ differs by $O(1)$ after a few steps, against $10^{-15}$ with SGD.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Algebra confirmed for plain (S)GD, but refuted as a description of the named methods: LoRA-FA's current form and LoRA-Pro's experiments use AdamW (no cometric, and LoRA-FA's trajectory then depends on A0 beyond its row space); Zhang-Pilanci use damping delta > 0 (only O(r)) and a scaled AdamW; and (d) is false for (c) (the LoRA-FA field is not tangent to W0 + M_r; it lives on W0 + {X A0}). Repaired: explicit (S)GD scope; learning rates included consistently; (d) corrected; a 'Practice' paragraph on damping and AdamW; classical attribution (Mishra et al., Mishra-Sepulchre); table labelled (SGD); LoRA-RITE described as W-level invariance.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor, faithful minor): every formula holds. Fixed: (d) no longer suggests the LoRA-FA field fails to descend; its flow lives on the slice W_0 + {XA_0}, whose rank-r part N_V is open dense and on which the field is tangent to M_r and a function of W, so (c) descends as a degenerate cometric of rank mr; the off-leaf sentence in the proof is replaced. The full-rank hypothesis now applies to (a), (b) only; (c) allows B_0 = 0. Proof of (b) shows the first-order step is independent of the free matrix X. Attribution: the corrected LoRA-FA gradient is arXiv 2308.03303 v2/v3 and HF's LoRA-FA optimizer, not the 2023 v1. Practice: Zhang-Pilanci report damped scaled GD as well as scaled AdamW; LoRA-Pro's released code damps both inverses, special-cases B_0 = 0 and clips. Commentary: LoRA-FA is a sub-object, GaLore/Flora/MeZO are full-parameter methods; GaLore row carries alpha and the right projection.","public_statement":"Under gradient flow, or undamped SGD to first order without Adam, on the full-rank locus, Riemannian-preconditioned (scaled) GD and the SGD variant of LoRA-Pro both induce weight-space updates that depend only on $W$. Their cometrics are $\\eta s^2(P_UG+GP_V)$ and the Frobenius projection onto the tangent space of the rank-$r$ manifold, so LoRA-Pro's flow is the Riemannian gradient flow on $W_0+\\mathcal M_r$. Gradient-corrected LoRA-FA gives the cometric $\\eta GP_V$, the gradient flow on the $mr$-dimensional slice $W_0+\\{XA_0\\}$, which depends on $A_0$ only through its row space. The plain-GD cometric depends on the factorisation and is invariant only under $O(r)$. As published and implemented, these methods use damped inverses, AdamW or a Sylvester-chosen free matrix, and keep only $O(r)$ symmetry, signed-permutation symmetry, or $W$-level invariance to first order.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (d) the hidden hypothesis is explicit: the LoRA-FA cometric has rank mr, which is below r(m+n-r) only when r < n; the proof writes out the density of N_V in the slice (monic minor polynomial)."},{"id":"III.5","name":"Noether charges and isometries for cotangent signals","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem III.5 (Noether charges and isometries for cotangent signals).** [Known: Zhao et al. 2023, Prop. 5.1, including the symmetric/antisymmetric table and symmetric learning rates; Marcotte–Gribonval–Peyré 2023 for laws independent of loss and data; the principle: Du–Hu–Lee 2018, Arora–Cohen–Hazan 2018, Kunin et al. 2021. New here: only the remark that $\\gamma$ need not be a gradient, which is formally immediate (along the realised trajectory $\\gamma(t)$ is the gradient of the time-dependent linear loss $\\langle\\gamma(t),\\theta\\rangle$), and the PEFT instances.] Let a Lie group act linearly on $Q=\\mathbb R^N$ with $\\rho\\circ k=\\rho$, let $\\Lambda\\succ0$ be constant, and let $\\gamma(t)\\in T^*_{\\rho(q(t))}\\Theta$ be **any** cotangent signal, not necessarily a gradient. Consider continuous-time updates\n$$\\dot q=-\\Lambda\\,D\\rho_q^\\top\\gamma(t).$$\n(i) For every generator $\\xi$ with $\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi$ symmetric, the charge $J_\\xi(q)=\\tfrac12\\langle q,\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi q\\rangle$ is conserved.\n\n(ii) For every generator with $\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi$ antisymmetric, $k=e^{s\\xi}$ preserves $\\langle\\cdot,\\Lambda^{-1}\\cdot\\rangle$, so $K_{kq}=K_q$ along the orbit: for the same cotangent $\\gamma$, the $\\theta$-velocities at $q$ and at $kq$ agree. If moreover $\\gamma$ depends on $q$ only through $(t,\\rho(q))$, e.g. $\\gamma=\\nabla_\\theta L$ or an open-loop signal, and solutions are unique ($\\gamma$ measurable in $t$ and locally Lipschitz in $\\theta$, $\\rho$ of class $C^{1,1}_{\\rm loc}$), the $\\theta$-trajectories from $q_0$ and from $kq_0$ coincide. If $\\gamma$ uses Euclidean norms on $Q$, as global-norm clipping does, the trajectory statement holds if, in addition, $k$ is a Euclidean isometry, and can fail otherwise. So (i) and the velocity statement of (ii) hold for arbitrary $\\gamma$, while the trajectory statement needs a gradient-like signal.\n\n(iii) When $\\mathfrak g$ is closed under $\\xi\\mapsto\\Lambda\\xi^\\top\\Lambda^{-1}$, every generator splits into a part of type (i) and a part of type (ii), a Cartan decomposition $\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak k\\oplus\\mathfrak p$. This holds for LoRA's $\\mathfrak{gl}_r$ with per-block scalar rates. Without it, there are generators of neither type. With per-rank-channel rates $\\Lambda(B,A)=(BD_B,D_AA)$, $\\xi_X$ is of type (i) iff $XD_B^{-1}$ and $D_A^{-1}X$ are both symmetric, and of type (ii) iff both are antisymmetric. An off-diagonal pair $(i,j)$ contributes one charge and one isometry iff $\\eta_{B,i}\\eta_{A,i}=\\eta_{B,j}\\eta_{A,j}$, and $\\mathfrak g$ is closed iff all the products $\\eta_{B,j}\\eta_{A,j}$ are equal. In that case one gets a $D$-conjugate of $\\mathfrak{so}_r\\oplus\\mathrm{Sym}_r$ whose isometries are not all Euclidean unless the rates are block-scalar, so the clipping caveat of (ii) applies. For pairwise distinct products, only the diagonal $X$ give charges and no nonzero $X$ gives an isometry.","proof":"*Proof.* Invariance of $\\rho$ gives $D\\rho_q(\\xi q)=0$. Hence\n$$\\tfrac{d}{dt}J_\\xi=\\langle\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi q,\\dot q\\rangle=-\\langle\\xi q,D\\rho_q^\\top\\gamma\\rangle=-\\langle D\\rho_q\\xi q,\\gamma\\rangle=0.$$\nFor (ii), $\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi$ antisymmetric gives $\\tfrac{d}{ds}(e^{s\\xi^\\top}\\Lambda^{-1}e^{s\\xi})=0$, equivalently $k\\Lambda k^\\top=\\Lambda$. From $\\rho\\circ k=\\rho$ we get $D\\rho_q=D\\rho_{kq}k$, so $K_q=D\\rho_{kq}k\\Lambda k^\\top D\\rho_{kq}^\\top=K_{kq}$, and $\\tfrac{d}{dt}(kq)=-k\\Lambda k^\\top D\\rho_{kq}^\\top\\gamma=-\\Lambda D\\rho_{kq}^\\top\\gamma$. When $\\gamma$ factors through $(t,\\rho)$, $kq(t)$ solves the same ODE as the trajectory from $kq_0$, and uniqueness concludes. (iii) Write $\\xi=\\tfrac12(\\xi+\\Lambda\\xi^\\top\\Lambda^{-1})+\\tfrac12(\\xi-\\Lambda\\xi^\\top\\Lambda^{-1})$. For per-channel rates, $\\langle(B',A'),\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi_X(B,A)\\rangle=\\mathrm{tr}(B'^\\top BXD_B^{-1})-\\mathrm{tr}(A'^\\top D_A^{-1}XA)$, which is symmetric (antisymmetric) in the two arguments iff $XD_B^{-1}$ and $D_A^{-1}X$ are. For $X=xE_{ij}+yE_{ji}$ the two conditions read $y=x\\,\\eta_{B,i}/\\eta_{B,j}$ and $y=x\\,\\eta_{A,j}/\\eta_{A,i}$ (with signs flipped in the antisymmetric case), which agree iff $\\eta_{B,i}\\eta_{A,i}=\\eta_{B,j}\\eta_{A,j}$. ∎ [Num: with a non-gradient, $q$-dependent $\\gamma$ on LoRA, $\\Phi$ drifts by $1.6\\times10^{-12}$; with Euclidean clipping and a non-Euclidean isometry $k$, the $\\theta$-trajectories from $q_0$ and $kq_0$ differ by $0.06$; with per-channel rates $\\eta_B=(1,1,1)$, $\\eta_A=(1,1,2)$ at $r=3$, closure fails but $E_{12}+E_{21}$ still gives a conserved charge.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Part (i) confirmed. Refuted: (ii) 'for any cotangent signal' fails for q-dependent signals such as Euclidean clipping (it needs gamma to factor through (t, rho(q)), plus a Euclidean isometry when clipping), and citing III.3(a) did not cover it; AdaLoRA's orthogonality regulariser breaks its charges; the result is essentially Zhao et al. 2023, Prop 5.1; the 'Cartan split' needs g to be closed under xi -> Lambda xi^T Lambda^{-1}. Repaired: (ii) restated with a direct proof via uniqueness; (iii) added with the closure hypothesis; relabelled Known; AdaLoRA, momentum and AdamW scoped out.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; no faithful review): (i), (ii) and the splitting in (iii) hold. False: the closing example of (iii) ('with per-rank-channel rates only diagonal X give charges and no X gives an isometry') holds only for pairwise distinct products eta_B,j eta_A,j; with equal products (the jointly rescaled rates of Thm III.12(b)) closure holds with r(r+1)/2 charges and r(r-1)/2 non-Euclidean isometries, and partially equal products give some off-diagonal ones. Repaired: (iii) now states the exact entrywise criterion with a proof line. Also: group parameter renamed s; the Euclidean-clipping condition is sufficient, not necessary; uniqueness regularity stated; title and statement say the trajectory claim needs a gradient-like signal; the non-gradient extension is noted as formally immediate. The GD drift of Phi is O(eta) per step (exact formula given); only eta Phi, or the relative drift, is O(eta^2), corrected in Instances, Exposé X item 8 and Exposé XI.","public_statement":"Take a linear symmetry group of the parametrisation $\\rho$ and any update $\\dot q=-\\Lambda D\\rho_q^\\top\\gamma(t)$ with a constant $\\Lambda\\succ0$; this covers gradient flow on any loss, minibatching, global-norm clipping and detached backward passes. Every generator $\\xi$ with $\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi$ symmetric gives a conserved quadratic charge $\\tfrac12\\langle q,\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi q\\rangle$; for LoRA with per-block rates this is $\\Phi=B^\\top B/\\eta_B-AA^\\top/\\eta_A$. Every generator with $\\Lambda^{-1}\\xi$ antisymmetric gives a $\\Lambda$-isometry that leaves the induced cometric unchanged, so gauge-related initialisations give identical weight trajectories whenever the signal depends on $q$ only through $(t,\\rho(q))$. With per-rank-channel rates, off-diagonal charges and isometries exist exactly between channels with equal products $\\eta_{B,j}\\eta_{A,j}$. This is a mild extension of known results (Zhao et al. 2023; Marcotte–Gribonval–Peyré 2023) and does not cover Adam, momentum, or discrete steps beyond first order.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (iii) 'whose isometries are not Euclidean' narrowed to 'not all Euclidean unless the rates are block-scalar', which is what the proof shows."},{"id":"III.6","name":"charge at initialisation; weight decay","kind":"corollary","statement":"**Corollary III.6 (charge at initialisation; weight decay).** [Cor]\n\n(a) $\\Phi_0=B_0^\\top B_0/\\eta_B-A_0A_0^\\top/\\eta_A$ is the value of the conserved charge, fixed by the initialisation and the rates. Since $O(r)$ acts on $\\Phi$ by conjugation and preserves $\\theta$-trajectories (Thm III.5(ii)), only its spectrum labels a $\\theta$-trajectory.\n\n| initialisation | $\\Phi_0$ |\n|---|---|\n| HF default LoRA on Linear/Conv layers ($B_0=0$; Kaiming-uniform $A_0$, entries $U(-n^{-1/2},n^{-1/2})$, with $n$ the fan-in, $\\mathrm{in\\_channels}\\times$ kernel size for Conv) | $-A_0A_0^\\top/\\eta_A$, with $\\mathbb E[A_0A_0^\\top]=\\tfrac13I_r$ and fluctuations $O(n^{-1/2})$ on and off the diagonal |\n| HF default LoRA on embedding layers ($A_0=0$, normal $B_0$) | $+B_0^\\top B_0/\\eta_B$ (Init[B]-type) |\n| HF `mica` ($A_0=0$, frozen minor-singular $B_0$) | $+B_0^\\top B_0/\\eta_B$ (Init[B]-type, with $B$ frozen) |\n| EVA (orthonormal rows; default `whiten=False`) | $-I/\\eta_A$ exactly |\n| PiSSA in HF PEFT, and MiLoRA if implemented like `pissa_init` with the bottom-$r$ singular values (MiLoRA is not in HF PEFT) ($B_0^\\top B_0=A_0A_0^\\top=S_r/s$, with $S_r$ the top-$r$, resp. bottom-$r$, singular values and $s$ the LoRA scale) | $(S_r/s)(1/\\eta_B-1/\\eta_A)$, zero **iff** $\\eta_A=\\eta_B$ (when $S_r\\ne0$) |\n| OLoRA ($B_0=Q_r$, $A_0=R_r$) | $I/\\eta_B-R_rR_r^\\top/\\eta_A$ |\n| \"Init[B]\" ($A_0=0$) | $+B_0^\\top B_0/\\eta_B$ |\n\n(b) Let $G$ be any cotangent signal, with the scale $s$ absorbed into it. Under gradient flow, if decay acts per unit time and identically on both factors ($\\dot B=-\\eta_BGA^\\top-\\lambda B$, $\\dot A=-\\eta_AB^\\top G-\\lambda A$), then $\\dot\\Phi=-2\\lambda\\Phi$. With learning-rate-coupled decay (PyTorch SGD `weight_decay`; the decay term of PyTorch AdamW), $\\dot B=-\\eta_B(GA^\\top+\\lambda B)$ and likewise for $A$. Then $\\Phi(t)=e^{-2\\eta\\lambda t}\\Phi(0)$ holds when $\\eta_A=\\eta_B=\\eta$. For $\\eta_A\\ne\\eta_B$ it fails in general: $\\dot\\Phi=-2\\lambda(B^\\top B-AA^\\top)$, which is not a function of $\\Phi$. With an actual Adam(W) gradient term, $\\Phi$ drifts even without decay, at first order in the step size; Adam lies outside Thm III.5 [Num]. So neither law describes AdamW training.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) Substitute the initialisations. Each Kaiming entry has variance $1/(3n)$ and there are $n$ entries per row, so the diagonal has mean $1/3$ with fluctuations $O(n^{-1/2})$, and the off-diagonal entries have standard deviation $1/(3\\sqrt n)$. HF's `pissa_init` divides $S_r$ by the scale before splitting.\n\n(b) Differentiate:\n$$\\tfrac{d}{dt}\\tfrac{B^\\top B}{\\eta_B}=-(AG^\\top B+B^\\top GA^\\top)-2c_B\\tfrac{B^\\top B}{\\eta_B},$$\nwith $c_B=\\lambda$ in the per-time convention and $c_B=\\eta_B\\lambda$ in the coupled one. Do the same for $A$ and subtract. ∎ [Num: testing $\\Phi(t)=e^{-2\\eta_A\\lambda t}\\Phi(0)$ at $\\eta_B/\\eta_A=4$ with coupled decay gives relative error $10.7$; $2\\times10^{-4}$ (Euler error) in the valid cases; one Adam sign step moves $\\Phi$ by an amount proportional to the learning rate.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Formulas confirmed. Refuted commentary: the bullet 'weight decay fails to gauge-fix at LoRA+ rates' was backwards: learning-rate-coupled decay converges to the unweighted balanced slice mu = 0 (the Kempf-Ness slice), per-time decay to Phi = 0. Also: 'AdamW style' mislabelled SGD-coupled L2; HF PiSSA's charge carries S_r/s; HF embedding LoRA is Init[B]-type; EVA's -I/eta_A needs whiten=False; MiLoRA uses the bottom singular values; Phi_0 is a label, not a coordinate. All repaired, with [Num] for both slices.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor; no faithful review): statement and proof hold. Fixed: Phi_0 is the conserved value fixed by init and rates, and only its O(r)-conjugacy class (spectrum) labels a theta-trajectory; MiLoRA is not in HF PEFT (row relabelled, HF mica added as Init[B]-type with frozen B); the Adam drift is first order and lies outside Thm III.5 rather than following from it; G defined as any cotangent signal; 'fails' made 'fails in general'; the [Num] states which law was tested; Conv fan-in noted. Commentary: HF PiSSA is unweighted-balanced (mu_0 = 0) at every rate, with Phi_0 = 0 only at equal rates. Adjacent fix in Prop III.7's commentary: coupled decay with coefficient lambda matches a (lambda/s) nuclear-norm penalty; (2 lambda/s) is for the explicit penalty.","public_statement":"Under gradient flow with block-scalar learning rates (no momentum, no Adam), the LoRA charge $\\Phi=B^\\top B/\\eta_B-AA^\\top/\\eta_A$ keeps its initial value, and only its spectrum labels the weight trajectory. Its value is $-A_0A_0^\\top/\\eta_A\\approx-I/(3\\eta_A)$ for HF's default Kaiming init, exactly $-I/\\eta_A$ for EVA, $+B_0^\\top B_0/\\eta_B$ for zero-$A$ inits, $(S_r/s)(1/\\eta_B-1/\\eta_A)$ for HF PiSSA and $I/\\eta_B-R_rR_r^\\top/\\eta_A$ for OLoRA. Weight decay applied per unit time gives $\\dot\\Phi=-2\\lambda\\Phi$. Learning-rate-coupled decay (PyTorch SGD) gives $\\dot\\Phi=-2\\lambda(B^\\top B-AA^\\top)$, which is exponential decay of $\\Phi$ only at equal rates, and its equilibria lie on the unweighted balanced slice $B^\\top B=AA^\\top$."},{"id":"III.7","name":"Kempf–Ness slice; Loewner floor","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition III.7 (Kempf–Ness slice; Loewner floor).** [Prop; Known: Kempf–Ness 1979, in the real form of Richardson–Slodowy 1990; Srebro–Rennie–Jaakkola, NIPS 17 (2004)]\n\n(a) On a full-rank $GL_r$-orbit, the critical points of $\\|B\\|^2+\\|A\\|^2$ are exactly the balanced representatives, those with $\\mu=B^\\top B-AA^\\top=0$. They form one $O(r)$-orbit, they are the minima, and the minimum value is $2\\|BA\\|_*$.\n\n(b) Assume gradient flow with block-scalar rates $\\eta_A,\\eta_B$, no weight decay, a loss that depends on $(B,A)$ only through $BA$, and any cotangent signal of the form $\\Lambda D\\rho^\\top\\gamma$ (dropout, minibatches and clipping included). From $B_0=0$,\n$$B^\\top B=\\tfrac{\\eta_B}{\\eta_A}\\big(AA^\\top-A_0A_0^\\top\\big)\\succeq0,$$\nso $AA^\\top\\succeq A_0A_0^\\top$ for all time: the $A$-factor never shrinks below its initialisation in the Loewner order. $AA^\\top(t)$ need not be monotone in $t$; only this floor is guaranteed. Discrete steps break the bound by $O(\\eta^2)$ per step. Under Adam it is not guaranteed and fails empirically. With per-time decay $\\lambda$, only $AA^\\top\\succeq e^{-2\\lambda t}A_0A_0^\\top$ holds.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) For $\\xi\\in\\mathfrak{gl}_r$ with symmetric part $\\xi_s$,\n$$\\tfrac{d}{dt}\\big|_0\\big(\\|Be^{-t\\xi}\\|^2+\\|e^{t\\xi}A\\|^2\\big)=-2\\operatorname{tr}(\\xi_s\\mu).$$\nThis vanishes for all $\\xi$ iff $\\mu=0$. The function is $O(r)$-invariant, so by the polar decomposition $GL_r=O(r)\\exp(\\mathrm{Sym}_r)$ it suffices to study $h(t)=f(e^{t\\xi}\\cdot x)$ for symmetric $\\xi=P\\,\\mathrm{diag}(d)P^\\top$. Then\n$$h''(t)=4\\sum_id_i^2\\big(\\|(BP)_{:,i}\\|^2e^{-2td_i}+\\|(P^\\top A)_{i,:}\\|^2e^{2td_i}\\big)\\ge0,$$\nso $h$ is convex and critical points are minima. If $x$ and $e^{\\xi}\\cdot x$ are both balanced, then $h'(0)=h'(1)=0$, so $h''\\equiv0$ on $[0,1]$. Since $B$ has full column rank, every $(BP)_{:,i}\\ne0$, which forces all $d_i=0$. Hence the balanced points form one $O(r)$-orbit. Existence: for $BA=U\\Sigma V^\\top$, the representative $B=U\\Sigma^{1/2}O$, $A=O^\\top\\Sigma^{1/2}V^\\top$ is balanced, with value $2\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma$.\n\n(b) Conservation of $\\Phi$ (Thm III.5) with $B_0=0$; for per-time decay, Cor III.6(b). ∎ [Num: on 5 random orbits at $(m,n,r)=(7,5,3)$ the minimum equals $2\\|BA\\|_*$ to $10^{-14}$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct. Repaired: uniqueness up to O(r) now proved by strict convexity (explicit h'' and full column rank), existence from the explicit SVD representative; (b) renamed 'Loewner floor' (AA^T(t) need not be monotone in t) with its hypotheses stated (gradient flow, block-scalar rates, loss through BA, no weight decay; Adam not covered; per-time decay gives only an e^{-2 lambda t} floor); the real-form Kempf-Ness credited to Richardson-Slodowy; citation year fixed; the nuclear-norm gloss limited to explicit or coupled L2 at minimisers, with AdamW decay excluded. Prop V.4 no longer relies on III.7(b).","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept. Round-2 knock-on (from III.6): the commentary now says SGD-coupled decay with coefficient lambda matches a (lambda/s) nuclear-norm penalty, while the explicit penalty lambda(|B|^2+|A|^2) matches (2 lambda/s).","public_statement":"On a full-rank orbit of LoRA's gauge, the minimisers of $\\|B\\|^2+\\|A\\|^2$ are the balanced factorisations $B^\\top B=AA^\\top$; they form one $O(r)$-orbit, and the minimum is twice the nuclear norm of $BA$ (Kempf–Ness; Srebro et al.). Under gradient flow from $B_0=0$ with block-scalar rates and no weight decay, $B^\\top B=\\tfrac{\\eta_B}{\\eta_A}(AA^\\top-A_0A_0^\\top)$, so $AA^\\top$ never falls below its initial value in the Loewner order. Discrete steps break this by $O(\\eta^2)$ per step, and Adam does not guarantee it."},{"id":"III.8","name":"the balance–escape trilemma","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition III.8 (the balance–escape trilemma).** [Prop] No LoRA initialisation is simultaneously\n- (a) identity-at-init without a residual split ($B_0A_0=0$),\n- (b) balanced ($B_0^\\top B_0=cA_0A_0^\\top$ for some $c>0$: unweighted, $c=1$, or rate-weighted, $\\Phi_0=0$ with $c=\\eta_B/\\eta_A$), and\n- (c) non-stationary: $D\\rho$ at $(B_0,A_0)$ is not the zero map; equivalently, $\\dot W(0)\\ne0$ for some parameter velocity, under any first-order optimizer.\n\nMoreover, if $B_0A_0=0$ then $\\langle B_0^\\top B_0,A_0A_0^\\top\\rangle_F=0$, so $\\|B_0^\\top B_0-cA_0A_0^\\top\\|_F^2=\\|B_0^\\top B_0\\|_F^2+c^2\\|A_0A_0^\\top\\|_F^2$: zero-product initialisations are maximally unbalanced.","proof":"*Proof.* Assume (a) and (b). Then $0=B_0^\\top B_0A_0=cA_0A_0^\\top A_0$, so $(A_0^\\top A_0)^2=0$. Since $A_0^\\top A_0$ is PSD, it vanishes, so $A_0=0$ and then $B_0=0$. At $(0,0)$, $D\\rho(\\dot B,\\dot A)=s(\\dot BA+B\\dot A)=0$, and also $\\nabla_B=\\nabla_A=0$; so every gradient-based update rule, including Adam, momentum and weight decay, is stationary there. For the identity, $\\operatorname{tr}(B_0^\\top B_0A_0A_0^\\top)=\\operatorname{tr}(B_0^\\top(B_0A_0)A_0^\\top)=0$. ∎","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct. Wording fixes: balance generalised to B0^T B0 = c A0 A0^T (unweighted or rate-weighted, proof unchanged); non-stationarity made intrinsic (D rho != 0), so the conclusion holds for every first-order optimizer; a quantitative identity added (zero-product inits are maximally unbalanced). Commentary corrected: the converse fails (OLoRA and CorDA are Type II and unbalanced), a residual is the price of any nonzero B0 A0, and LoftQ/QPiSSA are approximate splits that are not identity-at-init.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"No LoRA initialisation is at once identity-at-init without a residual split ($B_0A_0=0$), balanced ($B_0^\\top B_0=cA_0A_0^\\top$ for some $c>0$) and non-stationary. Zero-product initialisations are maximally unbalanced, which is why balanced starts such as PiSSA subtract a frozen residual."},{"id":"III.9","name":"closed-form isotropic-charge LoRA dynamics","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem III.9 (closed-form isotropic-charge LoRA dynamics).** [Known: Tarmoun–França–Haeffele–Vidal (ICML 2021), who call this charge \"$\\lambda$-balanced\"; Min–Tarmoun–Vidal–Mallada (ICML 2021); see also Dominé et al. 2024 and Kunin et al. 2024. The PEFT reading is ours.] Let $\\rho(B,A)=W_{\\rm res}+sBA$ be trained by gradient flow with rates $\\eta_A,\\eta_B$, and suppose the charge is isotropic, $\\Phi=-\\kappa I_r$. Put $\\mu=s\\sqrt{\\eta_A\\eta_B}$, and on the locus where $Z=W-W_{\\rm res}$ has rank $r$ write its compact SVD $Z=U\\Sigma V^\\top$. Then\n$$\\dot W=-\\mu^2\\Big[G\\,V g_\\kappa(\\Sigma^2/\\mu^2)V^\\top+U f_\\kappa(\\Sigma^2/\\mu^2)U^\\top G\\Big],\\qquad f_\\kappa(x)=\\sqrt{x+\\tfrac{\\kappa^2}4}-\\tfrac\\kappa2,\\quad g_\\kappa=f_\\kappa+\\kappa .$$\nThe identity holds pointwise on $\\{\\Phi=-\\kappa I,\\ \\operatorname{rank}Z=r\\}$; gradient flow is used only to keep $\\Phi$ fixed. The two terms under the root are equal at the **crossover scale** $\\sigma^*=\\mu\\lvert\\kappa\\rvert/2$.\n- For $\\sigma\\ll\\sigma^*$ with $\\kappa>0$, $f\\to0$ and $g\\to\\kappa$, so $\\dot W\\approx-\\mu^2\\kappa\\,GP_V$. These are LoRA-FA dynamics: $A$'s contribution to $\\dot W$ is $O(\\sigma^2/(\\mu^2\\kappa))$, although $A$ itself still drifts slowly. For $\\kappa<0$ the roles swap, $\\dot W\\approx-\\mu^2\\lvert\\kappa\\rvert P_UG$, and only $A$ effectively moves (Init[B]).\n- For $\\sigma\\gg\\sigma^*$, $f\\approx g\\approx\\sigma/\\mu$, so $\\dot W\\approx-\\mu\\big[G(Z^\\top Z)^{1/2}+(ZZ^\\top)^{1/2}G\\big]$. These are the balanced dynamics of Arora–Cohen–Hazan.\n- *Off the rank-$r$ locus.* For $\\kappa\\ge0$, $T=f_\\kappa(ZZ^\\top/\\mu^2)$ still holds as a full matrix function, but $S$ gains the term $\\kappa(P_{\\mathrm{row}A}-P_V)$; symmetrically, for $\\kappa<0$, $T$ gains $\\lvert\\kappa\\rvert(P_{\\mathrm{col}B}-P_U)$. At the default-LoRA start $Z=0$ ($B_0=0$) this gives $\\dot W=-\\mu^2\\kappa\\,GP_{\\mathrm{row}A_0}$, so the $\\sigma\\ll\\sigma^*$ regime extends continuously to $t=0$.","proof":"*Proof.* Put $\\beta=B/\\sqrt{\\eta_B}$ and $\\alpha=A/\\sqrt{\\eta_A}$. Then $\\beta^\\top\\beta-\\alpha\\alpha^\\top=-\\kappa I$ and $\\beta\\alpha=Z/\\mu$. Let $T=\\beta\\beta^\\top$ and $S=\\alpha^\\top\\alpha$.\n- $ZZ^\\top/\\mu^2=\\beta(\\beta^\\top\\beta+\\kappa I)\\beta^\\top=T^2+\\kappa T$.\n- $Z^\\top Z/\\mu^2=S^2-\\kappa S$.\n- On the rank-$r$ locus, $\\alpha\\alpha^\\top\\succ0$ and $\\beta^\\top\\beta\\succ0$. The nonzero eigenvalues of $T$ equal those of $\\beta^\\top\\beta=\\alpha\\alpha^\\top-\\kappa I$, so they exceed $\\max(0,-\\kappa)$; those of $S$ exceed $\\max(0,\\kappa)$.\n- On these ranges, $t\\mapsto t^2+\\kappa t$ and $s\\mapsto s^2-\\kappa s$ are strictly increasing and send $0$ to $0$, with positive roots $f_\\kappa$ and $g_\\kappa$. The compact SVD matters here: $f_\\kappa(0)=\\lvert\\kappa\\rvert$ for $\\kappa<0$ and $g_\\kappa(0)=\\kappa$ for $\\kappa>0$, so the full matrix function would be wrong on $\\ker Z$. Hence $T=Uf_\\kappa(\\Sigma^2/\\mu^2)U^\\top$ and $S=Vg_\\kappa(\\Sigma^2/\\mu^2)V^\\top$.\n- Finally, $\\dot W=-s^2(\\eta_BGA^\\top A+\\eta_ABB^\\top G)=-\\mu^2(GS+TG)$.\n\nOff the locus, for $\\kappa>0$, $S=\\alpha^\\top\\alpha$ also has the eigenvalue $\\kappa$ on $\\mathrm{row}A\\ominus\\mathrm{row}Z$, and symmetrically for $\\kappa<0$. ∎ [Num: the formula matches direct integration to $10^{-12}$, and the pointwise identity holds to $10^{-15}$ for $\\kappa\\in\\{-2,-0.3,0,0.3,2,10\\}$ and all shape cases.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: identity and proof correct. Wording fixes: the identity holds pointwise on the rank-r locus (gradient flow only keeps Phi fixed); off-locus correction terms added, so the sigma << sigma* regime extends to B0 = 0; the kappa < 0 limit stated; 'lambda-balanced' renamed to avoid a clash with the LoRA+ lambda; the Kaiming anisotropy is O(sqrt(r/n)), a band of crossover scales, not O(n^{-1/2}); PiSSA's balance holds only under gradient flow; 'the PEFT reading is new' softened.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"Under gradient flow with an isotropic charge $\\Phi=-\\kappa I$, LoRA's weight dynamics have a closed form in the SVD of the update (Tarmoun et al. 2021; Min et al. 2021). Below the crossover scale $\\sigma^*=\\mu\\lvert\\kappa\\rvert/2$, with $\\mu=s\\sqrt{\\eta_A\\eta_B}$, zero-$B$ LoRA behaves like LoRA-FA and effectively only $B$ moves; above it, the dynamics become the balanced dynamics of Arora, Cohen and Hazan."},{"id":"III.10","name":"the hyperparameter torus","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem III.10 (the hyperparameter torus).** [Thm; the LoRA/Adam case, including the $\\varepsilon>0$ extension and the two effective hyperparameters, is Known: Schulman et al. 2025, \"LoRA Without Regret\". New here: arbitrary multihomogeneous $\\delta$ and the SGD/heavy-ball $c^2$ law] Let $\\rho(q)=\\theta_b+s\\,\\delta(q_1,\\dots,q_k)$ with $\\delta$ multihomogeneous of multidegree $(d_1,\\dots,d_k)$. Train block $i$ from $q_i(0)=\\sigma_iu_i$ with Adam ($\\beta_1\\in[0,1)$, $\\beta_2\\in(0,1)$, bias correction allowed) at learning rate $\\eta_i(t)$, on the loss $L\\circ\\rho$ with no $q$-dependent regulariser. Assume no gradient clipping (or per-block clipping with thresholds rescaled as below), common random numbers (the same data order and dropout masks in both runs), and exact arithmetic (the result is bit-exact in floating point when every $c_i$ is a power of $2$). Then the trajectory $t\\mapsto\\rho(q(t))$ is invariant under the torus action of $c\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}^k$:\n$$\\big(s,\\ \\eta_i(t),\\ \\sigma_i,\\ \\varepsilon_i,\\ \\lambda_i,\\ C_i\\big)\\mapsto\\Big(s\\prod_ic_i^{-d_i},\\ c_i\\eta_i(t),\\ c_i\\sigma_i,\\ \\varepsilon_i/c_i,\\ \\lambda_i/c_i,\\ C_i/c_i\\Big).$$\nHere $\\varepsilon_i$ is a per-block Adam $\\varepsilon$ (with the convention $0/0=0$ at $\\varepsilon=0$), $\\lambda_i$ a per-block decoupled (AdamW) weight decay, and $C_i$ a per-block clipping threshold. Under SGD, with or without heavy-ball or Nesterov momentum, the same holds with $\\eta_i(t)\\mapsto c_i^2\\eta_i(t)$ and coupled $L^2$ decay $\\lambda_i\\mapsto\\lambda_i/c_i^2$. In particular, any learning-rate schedule shared by all blocks, warm-up included, is preserved.","proof":"*Proof.* The rescaling $q_i'=c_iq_i$, together with the compensating $s$, is an isomorphism in $\\mathsf{Meth}$. At corresponding points, $\\nabla_{q_i'}=c_i^{-1}\\nabla_{q_i}$. With $\\varepsilon_i'=\\varepsilon_i/c_i$, Adam's normalised step is invariant to the positive rescaling $c_i^{-1}$ of a block's whole gradient history, so $\\Delta q_i'=c_i\\Delta q_i$ requires $\\eta_i'(t)=c_i\\eta_i(t)$, and the decoupled decay term $\\eta_i'\\lambda_i'q_i'=c_i\\eta_i\\lambda_iq_i$ requires $\\lambda_i'=\\lambda_i/c_i$. For SGD and momentum the buffers are linear in the gradients, so the gradient factor $c_i^{-1}$ combines with the required $c_i$ to give $c_i^2$, and coupled decay enters the gradient as $\\lambda_i'q_i'$, giving $\\lambda_i/c_i^2$. Clipping decisions agree when the thresholds scale like the gradients. The time-dependence of $\\eta_i$ plays no role. Conclude by induction over steps. Floating point: if every $c_i$ is a power of $2$, multiplication by $c_i^{\\pm1}$ and by $\\prod_ic_i^{\\mp d_i}$ is exact and commutes with every correctly rounded addition, multiplication, division and square root, barring overflow and underflow (exact power-of-two scaling in IEEE arithmetic; Higham 2002, Ch. 2). Then the forward pass gives bit-identical weights, the backward pass gives gradients exactly $c_i^{-1}$ times the original ones, and every step of the induction holds exactly, so the two runs agree bit for bit. ∎ [Num: trajectory differences are exactly $0.0$ for power-of-two $c$ and about $10^{-15}$ otherwise, including a warm-up schedule and rescaled $\\varepsilon$ and decay; with $\\varepsilon$ or decay held fixed, or with global-norm clipping, they are $10^{-3}$ to $O(1)$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct (numerically exact). Repaired: beta_2 in (0,1); explicit hypotheses (loss through rho with no q-dependent regulariser, no global clipping, common random numbers, exact arithmetic); generalised to any schedules eta_i(t), per-block eps_i -> eps_i/c_i, decoupled decay lambda_i -> lambda_i/c_i, coupled L2 lambda_i/c_i^2, and clipping thresholds C_i/c_i; the LoRA/Adam case relabelled Known (Schulman et al. 2025, 'LoRA Without Regret').","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"For a method whose update is $s$ times a multihomogeneous function of its parameter blocks, rescaling each block's initial scale and learning rate together with the overall scale $s$ (and, under Adam, the per-block $\\varepsilon$, decoupled decay and clipping thresholds) leaves the weight trajectory exactly unchanged under Adam, given the same data order and dropout masks. Under SGD the same holds with learning rates scaled by the square of the block factor. The LoRA/Adam case is due to Schulman et al. 2025.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the proof now justifies the statement's bit-exactness clause for power-of-two factors (exact power-of-two scaling commutes with correctly rounded operations; Higham 2002, the paper's Appendix B.39) instead of leaving it to the numerical check."},{"id":"III.11","name":"LoRA+ is $\\alpha$ in disguise; rsLoRA is LoRA","kind":"corollary","statement":"**Corollary III.11 (LoRA+ is $\\alpha$ in disguise; rsLoRA is LoRA).** [Cor; (a) and the LoRA+ reading are Known: Schulman et al. 2025] For linear LoRA modules ($k=2$, $d=(1,1)$, $\\sigma_B=0$), under the hypotheses of Thm III.10:\n\n(a) **Effective hyperparameters.** Under Adam the torus invariants of $(s,\\eta_A,\\eta_B,\\sigma_A)$ are $(s\\eta_A\\eta_B,\\ \\sigma_A/\\eta_A)$; Schulman et al.'s $\\alpha\\cdot\\mathrm{init}_A\\cdot\\mathrm{LR}_B$ and $\\mathrm{init}_A/\\mathrm{LR}_A$ are equivalent generators. Under SGD the invariants are $(s^2\\eta_A\\eta_B,\\ \\sigma_A^2/\\eta_A)$.\n\n(b) **LoRA+.** Under Adam with $\\varepsilon=0$, no weight decay, no gradient clipping and a learning-rate schedule shared by both groups, LoRA+ with ratio $\\lambda$ at scale $s$ produces *exactly* the trajectory of plain LoRA at scale $\\lambda s$ (same $\\eta_A$, same $A_0$). For $\\varepsilon>0$ the equivalence needs a per-block $\\varepsilon_B'=\\lambda\\varepsilon$, and with AdamW a per-group decay $\\lambda\\cdot\\mathrm{wd}$ on $B$. Under SGD it holds at scale $\\sqrt\\lambda\\,s$, or equivalently with plain LoRA at the single rate $\\sqrt{\\eta_A\\eta_B}$ and $A_0\\mapsto\\lambda^{1/4}A_0$.\n\n(c) **rsLoRA.** $\\rho_{\\rm rs}(B,A)=\\rho_{\\rm LoRA}(\\sqrt r\\,B,A)$ is an isomorphism of objects. Up to the hyperparameter torus, rsLoRA can be represented as LoRA at the same $\\alpha$ with $\\eta_B$ multiplied by $\\sqrt r$ under Adam (by $r$ under SGD), or equally as LoRA with $\\alpha$ multiplied by $\\sqrt r$ at the same rates. The reading \"same object, different metric\" is a choice of representative within the orbit. (c) concerns a fixed rank; rsLoRA's rank-stability prescription, how $\\alpha$ should scale with $r$, is not addressed.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) These are the invariants of the 2-torus action. (b) Take $c_B=1/\\lambda$, $c_A=1$ (Adam) or $c_B=\\lambda^{-1/2}$ (SGD); the single-rate form is $c_A=\\lambda^{1/4}$, $c_B=\\lambda^{-1/4}$. Thm III.10 gives $\\varepsilon_B'=\\varepsilon/c_B$ and $\\mathrm{wd}_B'=\\mathrm{wd}/c_B$. (c) Take $c_B=\\sqrt r$. ∎ [Num: the maximum trajectory difference is exactly $0.0$ at $\\varepsilon=0$ for power-of-two $\\lambda$ and about $10^{-14}$ otherwise, against $0.93$ for the unrescaled control; $5\\times10^{-9}$ at a shared $\\varepsilon=10^{-8}$; $0.6$ with global-norm clipping at $0.5$.]","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: correct under the hypotheses of III.10. Wording fixes: hypotheses restated in (b) (eps = 0, no decay, no clipping, shared schedule; per-block eps and decay for the general case); an exact 0.0 only for power-of-two lambda; (c) phrased as a choice of orbit representative and limited to a fixed rank; (a) credited to Schulman et al.; the slogan's chart dependence noted; [Pred] corrected: a shared warm-up does not break the torus, while the HF Trainer's default clipping and shared eps or decay do; scope limited to linear modules.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"From the standard zero initialisation ($B_0=0$), under Adam with $\\varepsilon=0$, no weight decay, no gradient clipping, a learning-rate schedule shared by both groups and the same data order and dropout masks, LoRA+ with learning-rate ratio $\\lambda$ follows, in exact arithmetic, the same trajectory as plain LoRA with $\\alpha$ multiplied by $\\lambda$ (Schulman et al. 2025); with $\\varepsilon>0$ or AdamW, the per-block $\\varepsilon$ and decay must be rescaled too. Under SGD the factor is $\\sqrt\\lambda$. At a fixed rank, rsLoRA is LoRA with a rescaled $\\alpha$ or $B$ learning rate; how $\\alpha$ should scale with rank is a separate question.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: the public statement now names the hypotheses the statement already uses (zero initialisation sigma_B = 0; the same data order and dropout masks; exact arithmetic), as the paper's abstract and contributions do."},{"id":"III.12","name":"the optimizer gauge hierarchy","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem III.12 (the optimizer gauge hierarchy).** [Thm] On LoRA's full-rank locus, with hyperparameters shared by all rank channels, the largest subgroup of the gauge $GL_r$ under which an update rule is equivariant, at the level of parameters, is as follows.\n\n(a) SGD and GD with any block rates, with or without heavy-ball momentum: $O(r)$.\n\n(b) Adam and AdamW at fixed learning rates ($\\beta_1,\\beta_2\\in[0,1)$, any $\\varepsilon\\ge0$, any weight decay): the signed permutations $B_r$, of order $2^rr!$. A positive diagonal $g=\\mathrm{diag}(d)$ is a symmetry only *jointly* with per-rank-channel hyperparameters, and only without global-norm clipping. At $\\varepsilon=0$ and without weight decay it suffices to rescale the rates, $\\eta_{B,j}\\mapsto\\eta_{B,j}/d_j$ and $\\eta_{A,j}\\mapsto d_j\\eta_{A,j}$. With $\\varepsilon>0$ one must also give each channel its own $\\varepsilon_{B,j}=d_j\\varepsilon$ and $\\varepsilon_{A,j}=\\varepsilon/d_j$. With decoupled decay multiplied by the learning rate (PyTorch AdamW) one must also set $\\lambda_{B,j}=d_j\\lambda$ and $\\lambda_{A,j}=\\lambda/d_j$; under Loshchilov–Hutter's convention, where decay is scaled only by the schedule multiplier, $\\lambda$ needs no change.\n\n(c) Undamped scaled GD ($\\delta=0$; the Riemannian preconditioner of Tong–Ma–Chi and Zhang–Pilanci, with or without heavy-ball momentum): all of $GL_r$. The $W$-trajectory from gauge-related initial states (with zero or correspondingly transformed momentum) is independent of the representative; a step may drop rank, so the iteration maps into $W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$.\n\n*Published variants.* Damped scaled GD ($\\delta>0$) is only $O(r)$-equivariant, and Zhang–Pilanci's scaled AdamW, their practical variant, is $B_r$-equivariant by (b). LoRA-Pro's $W$-level first-order dynamics descend (Prop III.4(b)), as does its variant with free matrix $X=0$; its published parameter update with the Sylvester-optimal $X$ is only $O(r)$-equivariant, and its discrete $W$-step depends on the representative at $O(\\eta^2)$. LoRA-RITE states only $W$-level invariance (its Definition 1). Its update (unmagnified gradients, transported matrix moments, then multiplication by $(R_B^\\top)^+$) is $GL_r$-equivariant at the parameter level on the full-rank locus: under a gauge change its internal quantities change only by orthogonal matrices, and the final multiplication supplies the gauge element (the proof follows its Algorithm 1 line by line; the resulting step is Eq. 15 of the LoRA-RITE paper). It is therefore an Adam-type optimizer in class (c).","proof":"*Proof.* Under $(Bg^{-1},gA)$ we have $\\nabla_{B'}=\\nabla_Bg^\\top$ and $\\nabla_{A'}=g^{-\\top}\\nabla_A$. Equivariance means $\\delta B'=\\delta Bg^{-1}$ and $\\delta A'=g\\,\\delta A$, for arbitrary gradients.\n\n(a) SGD needs $g^\\top=g^{-1}$, and orthogonal $g$ suffice. A heavy-ball or Nesterov buffer is a linear combination of past gradients at points related by the same $g$, so for orthogonal $g$ it transforms like the gradients and the step is transported. The first step from a zero buffer is a positive multiple of an SGD step ($1$ for heavy ball, $1+\\mu_m$ for Nesterov with momentum coefficient $\\mu_m$), so the inclusion $\\subseteq O(r)$ also holds with momentum.\n\n(b) \"$\\supseteq$\": coordinatewise rules commute with signed permutations, since the moment $m$ flips sign and $v$ does not change.\n\n\"$\\subseteq$\": the first Adam step from zero state is $\\delta B=-\\eta\\,\\phi(\\nabla_B)$ with $\\phi(x)=x/(\\lvert x\\rvert+\\varepsilon)$ coordinatewise. Write $h=g^{-1}$.\n- *Case $\\varepsilon=0$.* Equivariance reads $\\mathrm{sign}(xg^\\top)=\\mathrm{sign}(x)h$ for every row vector $x$. Every sign pattern $s\\in\\{\\pm1\\}^r$ is realised by some $x$ with $xg^\\top$ zero-free, since the open orthant of $s$ is not covered by the proper hyperplanes $(xg^\\top)_j=0$ (a finite union of hyperplanes has empty interior), so $sh\\in\\{\\pm1\\}^r$ for all $s$. Flipping $s_i$ changes $(sh)_j$ by $2s_ih_{ij}\\in\\{0,\\pm2\\}$, so $h_{ij}\\in\\{0,\\pm1\\}$. A column with $t\\ne1$ nonzero entries yields a sum of absolute value $0$ or $\\ge2$ for suitable $s$, so each column of $h$ has exactly one entry $\\pm1$. Hence $h$, and therefore $g$, lies in $B_r$.\n- *Case $\\varepsilon>0$.* For small $x$, $\\phi(x)\\approx x/\\varepsilon$, which forces $g\\in O(r)$. For large $x$, $\\phi\\to\\mathrm{sign}$, which forces $g\\in B_r$.\n\nFor the joint torus: with $g=\\mathrm{diag}(d)$, gradient column $j$ of $B$ is scaled by $d_j$, while equivariance requires the step to be scaled by $1/d_j$. At $\\varepsilon=0$, Adam's step is invariant to positive per-coordinate scaling of the gradient, so the rate of column $j$ must be multiplied by $1/d_j$, and the rate of row $j$ of $A$ by $d_j$. With $\\varepsilon>0$, $d_jm/(d_j\\sqrt v+\\varepsilon')=m/(\\sqrt v+\\varepsilon'/d_j)$, which requires $\\varepsilon'=d_j\\varepsilon$; the decoupled decay term $\\eta'\\lambda'B'_{:,j}=(\\eta/d_j)\\lambda'B_{:,j}/d_j$ requires $\\lambda'=d_j\\lambda$. Rows of $A$ are symmetric.\n\n(c) Prop III.4(a); heavy-ball momentum transforms like the step. Damping fails for non-orthogonal $g$ because $g^{-1}(gAA^\\top g^\\top+\\delta I)g^{-\\top}=AA^\\top+\\delta g^{-1}g^{-\\top}$. For LoRA-RITE we follow Algorithm 1 of Yen et al. line by line on the full-rank locus, where its pseudo-inverses are inverses. In their notation the update is $AB^\\top$ with $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}$ and $B\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times r}$, and the gauge is $A_2=A_1R$, $B_2=B_1R^{-\\top}$ with $R\\in GL_r$. The rule is symmetric in $A$ and $B$. For the step of $A$ it writes $B=U_BR_B$ by polar decomposition ($U_B$ with orthonormal columns) and forms, at step $t$, the unmagnified gradient $\\bar\\nabla_{A,t}=\\nabla_{A,t}R_{B_t}^{-1}$, the basis change $P_{A,t}=U_{B_t}^\\top U_{B_{t-1}}$, a second moment $\\bar V_{A,t}$ that combines $P_{A,t}\\bar V_{A,t-1}P_{A,t}^\\top$ and $\\bar\\nabla_{A,t}^\\top\\bar\\nabla_{A,t}$ with fixed weights, the escaped mass $\\rho_{A,t}=\\rho_{A,t-1}+\\max_i\\lvert\\lambda_i(\\bar V_{A,t-1})-\\lambda_i(P_{A,t}\\bar V_{A,t-1}P_{A,t}^\\top)\\rvert$, which depends only on spectra, the preconditioned step $\\bar S_{A,t}=\\bar\\nabla_{A,t}(\\bar V_{A,t}+\\rho_{A,t}I)^{-1/2}$, the first moment $\\bar M_{A,t}=\\beta_1\\bar M_{A,t-1}P_{A,t}^\\top+(1-\\beta_1)\\bar S_{A,t}$ and the step $\\delta A_t=-\\eta\\,\\bar M_{A,t}R_{B_t}^{-\\top}$. Run it from $(A_1,B_1)$ and from $(A_1R,B_1R^{-\\top})$ with zero initial moments and escaped mass, and suppose that at step $t$ the parameters are related by $R$ and the states by $\\bar V^{(2)}_{t-1}=O_{t-1}^\\top\\bar V^{(1)}_{t-1}O_{t-1}$, $\\bar M^{(2)}_{t-1}=\\bar M^{(1)}_{t-1}O_{t-1}$ and $\\rho^{(2)}_{t-1}=\\rho^{(1)}_{t-1}$ (true at $t=1$). Equal column spaces give $U_{B_{2,t}}=U_{B_{1,t}}O_t$ with $O_t\\in O(r)$ and $R_{B_{2,t}}=O_t^\\top R_{B_{1,t}}R^{-\\top}$, and $\\nabla_{A_2}=\\nabla_{A_1}R^{-\\top}$, so $\\bar\\nabla_{A_2,t}=\\bar\\nabla_{A_1,t}O_t$ and $P^{(2)}_{A,t}=O_t^\\top P^{(1)}_{A,t}O_{t-1}$. Hence $\\bar V^{(2)}_t=O_t^\\top\\bar V^{(1)}_tO_t$, the escaped masses agree because conjugation keeps eigenvalues, $\\bar S^{(2)}_t=\\bar S^{(1)}_tO_t$, $\\bar M^{(2)}_t=\\bar M^{(1)}_tO_t$, and $\\delta A_{2,t}=-\\eta\\,\\bar M^{(1)}_tO_tO_t^\\top R_{B_{1,t}}^{-\\top}R=\\delta A_{1,t}R$, the transported step; the step of $B$ is the same computation with $R^{-\\top}$ in place of $R$. This closes the induction. The resulting step agrees with Eq. 15 of Yen et al., $\\delta A_2=\\delta A_1B_1^\\top(B_2^\\top)^+$, since $(B_2^\\top)^+=B_1(B_1^\\top B_1)^{-1}R$ when $B_1$ has full column rank. ∎ [Num: defects $10^{-15}$ for signed permutations, $11.3$ for a positive diagonal, $6.6$ for a rotation, and $5\\times10^{-16}$ for the diagonal with jointly rescaled per-channel rates at $\\varepsilon=0$; LoRA-RITE (Alg. 1) from $(B,A)$ and $(Bg^{-1},gA)$ with a generic $g$ agrees to $3\\times10^{-14}$ over 30 steps (referee replication); with $\\varepsilon=10^{-8}$ left unrescaled the joint torus fails by about $2\\%$; LoRA-Pro with the Sylvester $X$ has a parameter-level defect of $1.2$ under a non-orthogonal $g$, against $2\\times10^{-15}$ with $X=0$ or with an orthogonal $g$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"(a) and the group B_r in (b) confirmed. Refuted: the joint-torus clause holds only at eps = 0 without decay (otherwise eps and lambda must be rescaled per channel); (c) is false for LoRA-Pro (its Sylvester-optimal X makes the parameter update only O(r)-equivariant) and for Riemannian preconditioning as published (damping delta > 0 gives O(r), scaled AdamW gives B_r); LoRA-RITE's invariance is at the level of W. Repaired: per-channel rescalings of eps and lambda derived; (c) restricted to undamped scaled GD; published variants listed separately; strictness needs r >= 2; the commentary on LoRA+ and rsLoRA corrected.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; no faithful review): (a)-(c) hold and were re-checked. False: 'LoRA-RITE achieves W-level invariance, not parameter-level equivariance'; its algorithm is parameter-level GL_r-equivariant on the full-rank locus (paper Eq. 15; referee replication to 3e-14). Also false in the commentary: 'only GL_r-equivariant rules define an optimizer on the model' (any orbit-preserving rule does). Repaired: LoRA-RITE moved into class (c) with a proof sketch, also in Prop III.4's commentary, the periodic table and Cor III.13 (LoRA-RITE is defined at B = 0 via pseudo-inverses, equivariance established only on the full-rank locus); commentary corrected. Minor: (b) assumes channel-uniform hyperparameters and no global-norm clipping for the joint torus; the decay rescaling depends on the decay convention (PyTorch lr-coupled vs Loshchilov-Hutter); (c)'s descent is stated for W-trajectories from gauge-related states, mapping into W_0 + M_{<=r}.","public_statement":"On LoRA's full-rank locus with channel-uniform hyperparameters, the largest subgroup of the $GL_r$ gauge under which the parameter update is equivariant is $O(r)$ for SGD (with or without momentum) and the signed permutations $B_r$, of order $2^rr!$, for Adam and AdamW with any $\\beta$'s, any $\\varepsilon\\ge0$ and decoupled decay. Positive diagonal rescalings become symmetries of Adam only jointly with per-rank-channel rescaling of the learning rates (and of $\\varepsilon$ and lr-coupled decay when these are nonzero), and only without global-norm clipping. Undamped scaled GD (Riemannian preconditioning) and LoRA-RITE are equivariant under all of $GL_r$; damping reduces scaled GD to $O(r)$ and wrapping it in Adam reduces it to $B_r$.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: hypotheses placed as in the paper (hyperparameters shared by all rank channels for the whole theorem; (a) with or without heavy-ball momentum, whose first step from a zero buffer is a positive multiple of an SGD step); the LoRA-RITE claim is now proved by following Yen et al.'s Algorithm 1 line by line (induction with orthogonal O_t on moments, basis change and escaped mass), with Eq. 15 and the numerical replication as consistency checks; the orthant step of (b) is justified."},{"id":"III.13","name":"what a zero-$B$ initialisation really chooses","kind":"corollary","statement":"**Corollary III.13 (what a zero-$B$ initialisation really chooses).** [Cor] At $q_0=(0,A_0)$ with $\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r$, with hyperparameters shared by the rank channels and the same data order and dropout masks, two choices of $A_0$ give the same $\\theta$-trajectory whenever they are related by the optimizer's gauge group. The resulting invariant of $A_0$ is:\n- $\\mathrm{row}A_0\\in\\mathrm{Gr}(r,n)$ for an update rule that is $GL_r$-equivariant at every point of its trajectory, including $B=0$ and rank-deficient $B$, run without global-norm clipping. An example is $\\delta B=-\\eta\\nabla_B(AA^\\top)^{-1}$, $\\delta A=-\\eta(AA^\\top)\\nabla_A$, which needs only $\\operatorname{rank}A=r$;\n- the PSD matrix $A_0^\\top A_0$ for SGD, which is exactly the initial preconditioner $\\dot W(0)=-s^2\\eta_BGA_0^\\top A_0$;\n- $A_0$ up to signed permutation of its rows for Adam.\n\nThe undamped scaled-GD optimizers of Thm III.12(c) are not defined at $B=0$, since they need $(B^\\top B)^{-1}$. LoRA-RITE uses pseudo-inverses and so is defined there, but its $GL_r$-equivariance is established only on the full-rank locus. Their damped forms are only $O(r)$-equivariant, so in practice their invariant is a $\\delta$-dependent function of $A_0^\\top A_0$, and their Adam-wrapped forms (Riemannian-preconditioned AdamW, HF's LoRA-FA optimizer) have the invariant $A_0$ modulo $B_r$. Conversely, different invariants give different dynamics, for SGD and for Adam at $\\varepsilon=0$.\n\nRandom-$A$ LoRA and EVA are different points of the $B=0$ stratum $\\{(0,A):\\operatorname{rank}A=r\\}$ of the apex fibre $\\rho^{-1}(\\theta_0)=\\{(B,A):BA=0\\}$; Init[B] lies in another stratum of the same fibre. LoRA-FA is a different *object*: the sub-object $\\mathrm{FA}(A_0)\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{LoRA}_{(0,A_0)}$. More precisely, EVA is a point of the apex fibre of LoRA$_{r_l}$ at its per-layer redistributed rank and adjusted $\\alpha$, with orthonormal rows only when `whiten=False`.","proof":"*Proof.* Only the pointwise \"$\\supseteq$\" half of Thm III.12 is used. For SGD and Adam it holds off the full-rank locus, and the initial Adam state $(m,v)=0$ is fixed by $B_r$. With $B_0=0$ the term $B_0g^{-1}$ stays $0$, so equivariant updates give identical $W$-trajectories. Left $O(r)$-orbits of full-rank $A_0$ are classified by $A_0^\\top A_0$ (Thm II.5(2)), and $GL_r$-orbits by the row space (proof of Thm II.2). For the example rule, $\\nabla_{A'}=g^{-\\top}\\nabla_A$ and $A'A'^\\top=gAA^\\top g^\\top$ give $\\delta A'=g\\,\\delta A$, and $\\delta B'=\\delta Bg^{-1}$ as in Prop III.4(a). Orthogonal $g$ preserve the gradient norms, so global-norm clipping factors agree in the two runs for SGD and Adam. *Converse, by comparing first steps.* For SGD the first step from $(0,A_0)$ is $\\delta W_1=-s^2\\eta_BGA_0^\\top A_0$, so equal trajectories for every linear loss force $A_0^\\top A_0=A_0'^\\top A_0'$. For Adam at $\\varepsilon=0$ it is $\\delta W_1=-s\\eta_B\\,\\mathrm{sign}(GA_0^\\top)A_0$ (a positive multiple of this without bias correction), so row by row $F(x)=\\sum_j\\mathrm{sign}(x\\cdot a_j)\\,a_j$ must equal $F'(x)=\\sum_j\\mathrm{sign}(x\\cdot a_j')\\,a_j'$ for every row vector $x$, where $a_j$ and $a_j'$ are the rows of $A_0$ and $A_0'$. The rows are independent, so the hyperplanes $a_j^\\perp$ are distinct and each contains points that lie on no other; $F$ is constant off $\\bigcup_ja_j^\\perp$, and crossing $a_j^\\perp$ at such a point changes it by $2a_j\\ne0$. So $\\bigcup_ja_j^\\perp=\\bigcup_ka_k'^\\perp$, there is a permutation $\\pi$ with $a'_{\\pi(j)}=c_ja_j$, comparing the jumps gives $\\lvert c_j\\rvert=1$, and $A_0'$ is a signed row permutation of $A_0$. For the example $GL_r$-rule the first step $-\\eta s^2GP_{\\mathrm{row}A_0}$ determines $\\mathrm{row}A_0$. ∎ [Num: SGD from $A_0$ and $QA_0$ agree to $10^{-15}$, from $A_0$ and $DA_0$ differ by $0.81$; Adam from $A_0$ and $PA_0$ agree to $10^{-16}$, from $A_0$ and $QA_0$ differ by $0.98$; the example rule from $A_0$ and $gA_0$ agrees to $3\\times10^{-15}$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"SGD and Adam bullets confirmed. Refuted: rho^{-1}(theta0) = {(0,A)} is false (the apex fibre is {BA = 0}, which contains Init[B]); the GL_r bullet cited III.12(c), whose optimizers are undefined at B = 0 and in practice only O(r)- or B_r-equivariant (damping, Adam); the proof cited II.5 for GL_r orbits. Repaired: fibre corrected; the GL_r bullet requires equivariance along the whole trajectory, with an explicit example rule valid at B = 0; practical invariants stated; the converse sketched; EVA qualified (per-layer rank, whiten=False).","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept. Round-2 knock-on (from III.12): the remark on GL_r-equivariant optimizers at B = 0 now distinguishes undamped scaled GD (undefined there) from LoRA-RITE (defined via pseudo-inverses, equivariance shown only on the full-rank locus).","public_statement":"From a zero-$B$ LoRA initialisation, with the same hyperparameters for every rank channel and the same data order and dropout masks, what training sees of $A_0$ depends on the optimizer. A rule equivariant under the whole $GL_r$ gauge at every step, run without global-norm clipping, sees only its row space, SGD sees the matrix $A_0^\\top A_0$, and Adam sees $A_0$ up to signed permutations of its rows. Different invariants give different dynamics for SGD and for Adam at $\\varepsilon=0$, so the expressive classification of initialisations by row space is coarser than what training sees.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: hypotheses added as in the paper (hyperparameters shared by the rank channels, same data order and dropout masks; the GL_r-equivariant rule run without global-norm clipping); the converse is no longer a sketch: the proof compares first steps for SGD and for Adam at eps = 0, as in the paper's Appendix C; the public statement carries the hypotheses."},{"id":"IV.2","name":"the degree-one collapse","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition IV.2 (the degree-one collapse).** [Prop; Known: Torroba-Hennigen–Lang–Guo–Kim 2025 for GaLore (Thm 1, Cor. 3; App. B for weight decay); Hao–Cao–Mou 2024 for random projections] Let $\\rho_k(C)=W_k+\\mathcal LC$ with $\\mathcal L$ linear and $C_0=0$. Let `opt` be a first-order optimizer, with no coupled or decoupled weight decay, whose state and output depend only on the sequence of gradients it is fed, and which does not read the parameter tensor: SGD, heavy-ball or Nesterov momentum, Adam/AMSGrad, or Adafactor with `scale_parameter=False` (fixed or relative step, with its factorisation applied to the compressed gradient in the same tensor shape as $C$). Run both procedures below with the same implementation of `opt`, on the same minibatches, and from the same optimizer state, fresh or carried over. Then they produce the same $W$-trajectory over a period:\n- training $C$ with `opt` and merging at the end of the period;\n- the backward method with $c=\\mathcal L^*$ and $a=\\mathcal L$, started at $W_k$.\n\nAdamW with $\\lambda>0$, LAMB/LARS trust ratios, parameter-scaled Adafactor (HF's default `scale_parameter=True`) and global-norm clipping of the full gradient (such as HF Trainer's default `max_grad_norm=1.0`, whenever it triggers) all break the identity. Injectivity of $\\mathcal L$ is not needed.\n\nIn particular, on each matrix that GaLore projects (its other parameters train in full in both procedures), GaLore with a projector $P_k$ fixed on each period equals one-sided LoRA $W_k+P_kC$ (frozen $B=P_k$, or frozen $A$ when projecting on the right), merged and re-based every period, with $C$'s optimizer state (moments and step counter) carried over unchanged across re-basings rather than reset. $P_k$ must be exactly GaLore's matrix, signs and column basis included, not just its span, because the state is reused without transport. ReLoRA-style resets give a different trajectory. The identity is exact with weight decay $0$, no full-gradient clipping, and an optimizer from the admissible class; galore_torch's `GaLoreAdafactor` defaults to `scale_parameter=True` and is excluded, and `GaLoreAdamW` is the transformers-style AdamW (it adds $\\varepsilon$ to $\\sqrt v$ before bias correction), so the LoRA side must use the same implementation. GaLore's scale $\\alpha$ multiplies the optimizer output, so it is a learning-rate factor, not a LoRA-style scale inside $\\rho$; the two differ under Adam with $\\varepsilon>0$.","proof":"*Proof.* By induction, $W_t=W_k+\\mathcal LC_t$. Both procedures feed `opt` the same sequence $\\mathcal L^*\\nabla L(W_t)$ from the same state, so they receive the same outputs $U_t$. One sets $W\\leftarrow W-\\eta\\mathcal LU_t$, the other $C\\leftarrow C-\\eta U_t$. The excluded ingredients see different tensors in the two procedures: decoupled decay acts on $C$ (pulling $W$ toward $W_k$) in one and on $W$ (toward $0$) in the other; parameter-scaled steps read $\\mathrm{RMS}(C)$ in one and $\\mathrm{RMS}(W)$ in the other; full-gradient clipping uses $\\|G\\|$ in one and $\\|\\mathcal L^*G\\|$ in the other. ∎ [Num: $7\\times10^{-16}$ under Adam over one period; $5\\times10^{-15}$ over four periods with state carried on both sides, against $0.37$ when the LoRA side resets; $0.38$ for HF-default Adafactor; with one column of $P_k$ sign-flipped and state carried, the trajectories separate.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Core identity confirmed (Torroba-Hennigen et al.). Refuted: Adafactor in its default parameter-scaled form violates the hypothesis; real GaLore carries Adam state across projector changes, so 'fresh state' made the GaLore corollary false beyond one period; decoupled decay and full-gradient clipping also break the identity; 'state transport' overstated it (moments are reused without a change of basis). Repaired: optimizer class restricted (no reading of the parameter tensor); shared initial state; the GaLore corollary with state carried over; excluded ingredients listed; injectivity dropped; commentary corrected.","round2":"verified","round2_notes":"Round 2 (faithful minor; no math review): statement and proof hold and were re-run against galore_torch. Fixed: Flora was misdescribed as 'GaLore with a random projector'; Flora compresses only the momentum or accumulation buffer, takes full-space Adafactor steps and transports momentum at resampling, so IV.2 does not apply to it. The random-projector cell and Def IV.1 now name 'GaLore with a random projector', with Flora listed separately (also in Prop III.4's table, Thm IV.3's instances, Thm V.3 corollary 6 and the periodic table). Statement: no weight decay of either kind; same optimizer implementation (GaLoreAdamW is transformers-style); P_k must be GaLore's exact matrix, not just its span; alpha is a learning-rate factor that differs from a scale inside rho under Adam with eps > 0; GaLoreAdafactor's default scale_parameter=True is excluded. Commentary: the equivalence classifies only admissible-optimizer methods, LoRA/ReLoRA rows are contrast rows, LoRA-FA's K holds under SGD (HF's optimizer gives G P_V), MeZO up to finite-difference error; Exposé 0 claims restricted accordingly.","public_statement":"If an adapter is linear in its trainable tensor ($W=W_k+\\mathcal LC$ with $C_0=0$) and the optimizer depends only on the gradients it receives (SGD or momentum, Adam without weight decay, Adafactor without parameter scaling, no global-norm clipping, the same implementation on both sides), then training $C$ and merging gives exactly the same weights as running that optimizer on the compressed gradient $\\mathcal L^*\\nabla_W L$ and mapping back with $\\mathcal L$. In particular, GaLore with weight decay $0$ is exactly one-sided LoRA whose frozen factor is GaLore's projector matrix, merged and re-initialised every period with the trainable factor's optimizer state carried over rather than reset (Torroba-Hennigen et al. 2025). Decoupled weight decay, parameter-scaled Adafactor, trust-ratio optimizers and full-gradient clipping break the identity. Flora, which compresses only the momentum and takes full-space Adafactor steps, is not covered.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: hypotheses made explicit as in the paper (same implementation of opt, same minibatches, same optimizer state); HF Trainer's default max_grad_norm=1.0 named as a full-gradient clip that breaks the identity whenever it triggers; the GaLore identity is stated per projected matrix, merged and re-based every period."},{"id":"IV.3","name":"integrability","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition IV.3 (integrability).** [Prop; Frobenius] Let $D$ be a smooth distribution of constant rank $k$ on an open $U\\subseteq\\Theta$, for instance the anchor distribution $D_\\theta=\\mathrm{im}\\,a(\\theta)$ of a time-independent backward method. Then $D$ is involutive on $U$ iff through every $\\theta\\in U$ passes an injective immersion $\\rho_\\theta:(\\mathbb R^k,0)\\to(U,\\theta)$ with $\\mathrm{im}\\,D\\rho_\\theta=D$ along its image. A single such $\\rho$ through $\\theta_0$ gives involutivity only along $\\mathrm{im}\\,\\rho$. For example, $D=\\mathrm{span}\\{\\partial_x,\\ \\partial_y+xz\\,\\partial_z\\}$ on $\\mathbb R^3$ has $[\\partial_x,\\partial_y+xz\\partial_z]=z\\partial_z\\notin D$ off $z=0$, yet the plane $z=0$ is an integral manifold.\n\nThe dynamic content needs separate hypotheses.\n- In continuous time, if $D$ is involutive, a backward flow with $\\dot\\theta\\in D_\\theta$ stays on the leaf through $\\theta_0$.\n- In the discrete update of Def IV.1, the iterates stay on a leaf for all step sizes and all optimizer outputs $s$ (all losses) iff that leaf contains $(\\theta+D_\\theta)\\cap U$ at every iterate, i.e. the leaves are affine; a constant anchor over the period gives this (Prop IV.2).\n- Under gradient flow, dynamic equivalence with a forward lens $(\\rho,\\mathsf R[\\rho])$ forces matching frames, $a_tc_t=D\\rho\\,\\Lambda\\,D\\rho^\\top$ along the trajectory. Hence $K=ac$ is symmetric PSD and, for injective $a$, $c=Ma^\\top$ with $M$ symmetric PSD on $S$, possibly depending on $\\theta$. If in addition $\\rho$ is an immersion with $\\dim Q=\\operatorname{rank}D$, then $\\rho$ is a local isometry from $(Q,\\Lambda^{-1})$ onto the leaf with metric $(aMa^\\top)^{-1}|_D$, so that metric must be flat. Forward methods with gauge fibres escape this requirement: $\\rho(B)=BB^\\top$ on symmetric matrices induces the curved Bures–Wasserstein metric, and balanced full-rank LoRA also induces a curved leaf metric. For discrete steps, exact agreement for every step size further forces $\\rho$ to be affine along each horizontal line $q+t\\,\\Lambda D\\rho_q^\\top g$.\n\n*Instances.* Every projector-type backward method listed in Def IV.1 (GaLore with any $T$ and any projector, LISA, MeZO) has an anchor that does not depend on $\\theta$ within its period. Its distribution is constant, hence trivially involutive with affine leaves (vacuously at $T=1$, where a period is a single step). Under the optimizer hypotheses of Prop IV.2 (no weight decay of either kind, no full-gradient clipping; GaLore and MeZO need weight decay $0$, and LISA's AdamW with positive decay violates them), these methods are rebasing schemes of linear forward methods with period $T$, including $T=1$: each MeZO step is a step of the linear method $\\theta_t+\\lambda z_t$, up to its SPSA estimator, and GaLore with $T=1$ is one-sided LoRA re-based every step. What separates them from LoRA-type methods is the resampling schedule plus the carry-over of optimizer state, not integrability. Flora as published (Adafactor base, with a full-rank current-gradient term in the code), APOLLO (a low-rank-estimated scaling applied to the full gradient) and Fira (a scaled full-rank residual) are not of the form of Def IV.1, because their updates leave $\\mathrm{im}\\,a$. Non-involutivity proper appears only for $\\theta$-dependent anchors. Full-batch GaLore with $T=1$, read as the distribution $D_W=\\mathrm{Hom}(\\mathbb R^n,U_r(\\nabla L(W)))$ (or $\\mathrm{Hom}(V_r(\\nabla L(W)),\\mathbb R^m)$ when $m\\ge n$, as GaLore projects) wherever a singular-value gap exists, is involutive iff $U_r(\\nabla L(W))$ (respectively $V_r(\\nabla L(W))$) is invariant along $D$. That fails generically for every $r\\ge1$ once both matrix dimensions are at least $2$. For every $r$ with $1\\le r<\\min(m,n)$ it fails for $L=\\tfrac12\\lVert W\\rVert_F^2$, and numerically it fails for least squares and cross-entropy with several samples. It holds, for instance, for single-sample least squares, and automatically only when the distribution is a line field ($nr=1$). Minibatch GaLore with $T=1$ and MeZO violate time-independence, because their anchors depend on step-wise randomness.","proof":"*Proof.* Frobenius: if $D$ is involutive, a flat chart $\\varphi$ centred at $\\theta$, in which $D$ is spanned by the first $k$ coordinate fields, gives $\\rho_\\theta(y)=\\varphi^{-1}(\\psi(y),0)$ with $\\psi:\\mathbb R^k\\to(-\\delta,\\delta)^k$ a diffeomorphism; conversely an injective immersion is locally an embedding, so its local pieces are integral manifolds, along which brackets of sections of $D$ stay in $D$. The same argument along $\\mathrm{im}\\,\\rho$ alone gives involutivity only there, as the example shows. Continuous time: in a flat chart a curve with velocity in $D$ keeps its transverse coordinates constant, so it stays in one slice, and the set of times at which it lies on the leaf through $\\theta_0$ is open and closed. Discrete statement: as $\\eta$ and $s$ vary, $\\theta-\\eta a(\\theta)s$ ranges over $(\\theta+D_\\theta)\\cap U$, so the iterates stay on the leaf for all $\\eta,s$ iff the leaf contains $(\\theta+D_\\theta)\\cap U$. If this holds at every point, a small disc of $\\theta+D_\\theta$ lies in the leaf; in a flat chart the leaf meets the chart in countably many slices, so the transverse coordinates are constant on the connected disc, and by invariance of domain the disc is open in the slice through $\\theta$. Hence $D$ is locally constant along each leaf, so constant on it, and the leaves are affine. A constant anchor gives the planes $\\theta+V$. Frames: switching at a time $t_1$ from $L$ to $L+\\langle\\gamma,\\cdot\\rangle$, for every covector $\\gamma$, gives $a_{t_1}c_{t_1}=D\\rho\\Lambda D\\rho^\\top$, which is symmetric PSD with range in $D$; for injective $a$, $M=a^+(ac)(a^+)^\\top$ gives $ac=aMa^\\top$ and $c=Ma^\\top$. If $D\\rho_q$ is an isomorphism onto $D$, $D\\rho\\Lambda D\\rho^\\top$ is the cometric of the pushforward of the flat metric $\\Lambda^{-1}$, so $\\rho$ is a local isometry onto the leaf, whose metric must then be flat. For $\\rho(B)=BB^\\top$, $D\\rho D\\rho^\\top(G)=2(G\\theta+\\theta G)$ does not depend on the fibre point, and its metric is a constant multiple of the Bures–Wasserstein metric, which is curved for $n\\ge2$. Balanced full-rank LoRA ($m=n=r\\ge2$, $s=1$, $\\eta_A=\\eta_B=1$, $B^\\top B=AA^\\top$, which gradient flow preserves by Thm III.5): $A^\\top A=\\lvert W\\rvert$ and $BB^\\top=\\lvert W^\\top\\rvert$, so $K_W(G)=G\\lvert W\\rvert+\\lvert W^\\top\\rvert G$ depends on $W$ alone. The transpose is an isometry of the leaf metric $\\langle K_W^{-1}X,X\\rangle$; its fixed positive definite matrices form a totally geodesic submanifold, since an isometry maps geodesics to geodesics, and its induced metric is a constant multiple of the Bures–Wasserstein metric, which has a plane of positive sectional curvature. By the Gauss equation the leaf metric has the same sectional curvature on that plane, so it is not flat (Lee 2018, Chs. 4, 5 and 8; Kobayashi 1958). Discrete steps: agreement of $\\rho(q-\\eta v)$, $v=\\Lambda D\\rho_q^\\top g$, with $\\rho(q)-\\eta D\\rho_qv$ for every $\\eta>0$ and for $\\pm g$ makes $\\rho$ affine along $q+tv$. GaLore field: where $\\sigma_r(\\nabla L)>\\sigma_{r+1}(\\nabla L)$, the projector $\\Pi(W)$ onto $U_r(\\nabla L(W))$ is a Riesz projector of $\\nabla L\\nabla L^\\top$, smooth because the spectral gap persists (Kato 1995). With $N(Z)=(I-\\Pi)\\Pi'(Z)\\Pi$ one has $\\Pi'(Z)=N(Z)+N(Z)^\\top$, and the normal part of $[X_E,X_F]$ for $X_E=\\Pi E$ is $N(\\Pi E)\\Pi F-N(\\Pi F)\\Pi E$. For $n\\ge2$ this vanishes for all $E,F$ iff $N=0$ on $D$, that is, iff $U_r$ is invariant along $D$; a line field ($nr=1$) is always involutive. For $L=\\tfrac12\\lVert W\\rVert_F^2$ and $W=\\sum_{i\\le p}\\sigma_ie_ie_i^\\top$ with $\\sigma_1>\\dots>\\sigma_p>0$, $p=\\min(m,n)$, one finds $N(e_1x^\\top)=\\sum_{r<j\\le p}\\frac{\\sigma_jx_j}{\\sigma_1^2-\\sigma_j^2}e_je_1^\\top$, so $E=e_1e_{r+1}^\\top$ and $F=e_1e_1^\\top$ give the normal component $\\frac{\\sigma_{r+1}}{\\sigma_1^2-\\sigma_{r+1}^2}e_{r+1}e_1^\\top\\ne0$ for every $1\\le r<p$. For least squares the normal component is real-analytic in the data and not identically zero, so it is nonzero generically; for a single sample, $U_1=\\mathrm{span}\\{Wx-y\\}$ is constant along $D$. For $r=1$ with frame $X_v=u(W)v^\\top$, $[X_{v_1},X_{v_2}]=(Mv_1)v_2^\\top-(Mv_2)v_1^\\top$ with $Mv=Du(W)[uv^\\top]\\perp u$, which lies in $D$ iff $M=0$. ∎ [Num: for full-batch $T=1$ GaLore the relative normal part of the bracket is $0.08$ to $0.94$ at $r=1$, $(m,n)\\in\\{(4,3),(5,4)\\}$, for least-squares, tanh and cross-entropy losses (referee replication), and $0.21$ to $0.95$ at $(m,n,r)=(4,3,2)$; at $W=\\mathrm{diag}(2,1)$ with $L=\\tfrac12\\|W\\|_F^2$ the bracket is $-\\tfrac13e_2e_1^\\top\\notin D$; the cometric of $BB^\\top$ takes the same value at $B$ and at $BR$, $R\\in O(2)$, to $10^{-12}$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted: the literal 'only if' is false (one integral manifold implies involutivity only along it; counterexample span{d_x, d_y + xz d_z}); 'is a forward method' ignored frames, metrics and discreteness; full-batch gradient anchors are functions of theta (generically non-involutive); and every practical backward method has a theta-independent anchor within its period, so Frobenius did no work and the claim 'they reparametrise nothing' contradicted Prop IV.2. Repaired: Frobenius stated pointwise on an open set; dynamic hypotheses separated (continuous time, affine leaves for discrete steps, frame and metric conditions); instances and 'What this explains' rewritten; the Exposé 0 claim toned down.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): the Frobenius core holds. False: (1) 'full-batch GaLore with T=1 is integrable when its rank is 1' (for r = 1 the bracket's normal part is generically nonzero; closed form at W = diag(2,1)); the 1e-9 figure only reproduces for n = 1 or single-sample least squares; (2) 'a curved metric is reproduced by no rho with constant Lambda' holds only for immersions with dim Q = rank D (counterexample: BB^T induces the curved Bures-Wasserstein metric; balanced LoRA too), and Thm III.3(a) did not prove it; (3) Flora as published is not a constant-anchor backward method. Repaired: GaLore claim restated (involutive iff U_r is invariant along D; generic failure for every r >= 1; right projection noted); flatness restated with c = M a^T and the immersion hypothesis, with a direct proof; discrete SGD needs rho affine along horizontals; continuous-time bullet assumes involutivity; the discrete iff quantifies over all optimizer outputs and intersects with U. Instances restate Prop IV.2's optimizer hypotheses (LISA's AdamW decay violates them), MeZO up to SPSA, T=1 vacuous; Flora, APOLLO and Fira are named as outside Def IV.1. Num corrected and backed by framework_checks.py.","public_statement":"By Frobenius' theorem, a smooth constant-rank anchor distribution on an open set of weight space is foliated by images of injective immersions exactly when it is involutive; a single integral manifold implies nothing off itself. GaLore (any projector), LISA and MeZO keep their anchor fixed within each period, so the condition holds trivially and does not tell them apart; with weight decay $0$ and the optimizer hypotheses of Prop IV.2 they are rebasing schemes of linear forward methods. The condition has content only for anchors that depend on the weights, and the full-batch top-$r$ gradient-subspace field is generically non-involutive for every $r\\ge1$ once both matrix dimensions are at least $2$. Flora as published (Adafactor base), APOLLO and Fira make updates outside their projected subspace and are not covered.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: statement sharpened as in the paper (matching frames a_t c_t; c = M a^T needs injective a; for discrete steps rho is affine along each horizontal line; LISA's positive decay; the V_r case; failure for L = |W|_F^2/2 at every 1 <= r < min(m,n), proved in the paper's Appendix C); the proof is no longer labelled a sketch: flat-chart, totally-geodesic/Gauss-equation and Riesz-projector steps are written out with their standard sources (Kato 1995; Lee 2018; Kobayashi 1958; the paper's Appendix B.31, B.37, B.38)."},{"id":"IV.4","name":"backward reach and the backprop cone","kind":"observation","statement":"**Observation IV.4 (backward reach and the backprop cone).** [Obs; an upper bound] Assume reverse mode with per-box residuals only (no inter-box checkpointing), with boxes taken at the granularity where adapters are separate boxes (for LoRA-FA, $A_0$ and $B$ separately). Then it suffices to keep a VJP residual, meaning an input, an output, a sufficient statistic such as a ReLU mask, or a recipe to recompute one of these inside the box, for\n- each trainable box whose parameter-VJP depends on its input: linear and multiplicative boxes such as $W$, $A$, $B$ and (IA)$^3$ scales; additive boxes such as biases and prompts need nothing;\n- each *nonlinear* box, bilinear boxes such as the attention products included, on a directed path from a trainable box to the loss.\n\nDeterministic frozen linear boxes, and all boxes off this cone, need nothing; stochastic linear boxes on the cone (dropout) need their mask or RNG state. The $k$ coordinates of $P^\\top\\nabla L$ can be computed exactly by $k$ forward-mode JVPs, with no reverse pass and $O(1)$-layer activation memory, each JVP costing a small constant multiple (about $1.5$ to $3$) of a forward pass. This always uses less activation memory than un-checkpointed reverse mode, but it is time-competitive with one VJP (about $3$ forward passes) only for $k$ of about $1$ or $2$. It also needs $P$ before the passes, so it cannot produce GaLore's SVD refresh, where $P$ depends on $\\nabla L$, more cheaply than a backward pass ($\\nabla L$ itself would take $\\lvert\\theta\\rvert$ JVPs).","proof":"*Proof.* Cotangents are needed only on the downstream cone of the trainable boxes, so everything else can be pruned. A frozen linear box has $\\mathsf R[f](x,y)=W^\\top y$, which does not depend on $x$, whereas a nonlinear box's reverse derivative depends on its input through some residual. The parameter-VJP of a trainable linear box is $yx^\\top$ (of a multiplicative scale, $\\sum y\\odot x$), so it needs the input $x$; a bias's parameter cotangent is its incoming cotangent; a dropout box's VJP is $\\mathrm{mask}\\odot y/(1-p)$. The $i$-th coordinate of $P^\\top\\nabla L$ is the directional derivative $DL(\\theta)[p_i]$, and forward-mode AD computes it alongside one forward pass with a constant-factor overhead and $O(1)$-layer memory. MeZO's central difference has bias $O(\\varepsilon^2)$ when $L$ has a Lipschitz Hessian. ∎","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Refuted as a necessity claim: additive trainable boxes (BitFit, prompts) need no stored input; residuals can be outputs, masks or recomputation; dropout needs its mask; 'k JVPs and no VJP' omitted that this is competitive only for k = O(1) (GaLore's k is huge and its SVD refresh needs a VJP); MeZO's central difference has O(eps^2) bias for grad L; LISA always trains the embedding and LM head, so its cone is the whole network. Repaired: stated as an upper bound with explicit hypotheses and a residual notion; JVP trade-off stated; MeZO, LISA, LST and prompt-tuning readings corrected.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math minor; faithful refuted on a commentary sentence, minor): the upper bound holds and was checked against autograd. False: 'LISA's savings are in gradients and optimizer state, not activations' contradicts the Observation (frozen linears on the cone drop their input residuals; about 20% per unsampled block at LLaMA proportions) and the LISA paper. Repaired: LISA reading corrected. Also fixed: the no-rematerialisation hypothesis no longer contradicts recomputation recipes; dropout needs its mask or RNG state, on the cone only; JVP cost stated as a constant multiple of a forward pass, time-competitive only for k of about 1-2, always better on activation memory; 'unavailable' for GaLore's refresh softened to infeasible; proof gains the parameter-VJP and dropout lines and the Lipschitz-Hessian condition for MeZO. Commentary: LoRA-FA's saving needs lora_dropout = 0; 'largest cone' is the maximal cone shared with layer-1 methods; HF reentrant checkpointing note; 'memory column' renamed.","public_statement":"In reverse-mode autodiff with per-box residuals only, a PEFT method needs stored activations only for trainable ops whose parameter gradient reads their input (linear or multiplicative adapters, not biases or prompts) and for nonlinear, including bilinear, ops downstream of some trainable op. Frozen deterministic linear layers and everything below the lowest trainable op need none; dropout on the cone needs its mask. This is why ladder side-tuning skips the backbone's backward pass, why LoRA-FA keeps $A_0x$ instead of $x$, why adapting only the top layers truncates backprop while training the embedding (LISA, prompt tuning) does not, and why LISA still saves the input residuals of its frozen layers. Alternatively, $k$ fixed directions of the gradient can be computed exactly by $k$ forward-mode JVPs with $O(1)$-layer memory, at a constant multiple of $k$ forward passes; that is time-competitive with one backward pass only for very small $k$, and the directions must be fixed in advance.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: bilinear boxes (the attention products) named among the nonlinear boxes on the cone, as in the paper and the public statement; the JVP remark scoped to SVD refreshes where P depends on the gradient."},{"id":"V.2","name":"quantisation and splitting","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition V.2 (quantisation and splitting).** [Prop] Write $W$ for the full-precision weight, $\\kappa$ for the quantiser, $e=\\kappa W-W$, and $\\delta_0=\\delta(q_0)$.\n\n(a) Additive families commute with translation: $M(\\theta+v)=\\tau_v\\circ M(\\theta)$. Hence QLoRA is LoRA pointed at $\\kappa\\theta_0$, with defect $e$; it needs no new weight-space structure. Since $-e$ is generically of full rank, $\\theta_0\\notin\\mathrm{Im}\\,\\mathrm{LoRA}_r(\\kappa\\theta_0)$ unless $\\operatorname{rank}e\\le r$.\n\n(b) For splitting families derived from an SVD or from data, the order of operations matters, and three orders occur in practice:\n- *split, then quantise the residual* (QPiSSA; HF's recommended PiSSA/CorDA workflow; the quantisation half-step of LoftQ for $t\\ge2$, with $W_{\\rm res}=W-\\delta(q_{t-1})$): defect $\\kappa(W_{\\rm res})-W_{\\rm res}$;\n- *quantise, then fit the error* (LoftQ with one iteration, HF's default `loftq_iter=1`; HF `replace_lora_weights_loftq`): HF sets the factors from $\\mathrm{SVD}_r(W-\\kappa W)$ without dividing by the LoRA scale $s$ ($\\alpha/r$, or $\\alpha/\\sqrt r$ under rsLoRA), unlike its PiSSA and OLoRA inits, so $\\delta_0=-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)$ and the defect is $e-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)$, with $\\|e-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\|^2=\\|e-\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\|^2+(1-s)^2\\|\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)\\|^2$. It is $e$ minus its best rank-$r$ approximation (rank $\\operatorname{rank}e-r$) iff $s=1$, HF's bare default $r=\\alpha=8$; for $s=2$ its norm equals $\\|e\\|$, no better than QLoRA. With a callback, `replace_lora_weights_loftq` can roll a module back to the zero init, whose defect is $e$;\n- *quantise, split, re-quantise* (HF `olora_init` on bitsandbytes weights): defect $e+\\kappa(r')-r'$ with $r'=\\kappa W-\\delta_0$.\n\nQuantise-then-split has defect exactly $e$ iff the frozen part equals $\\kappa W-\\delta_0$, for instance $\\kappa W$ kept quantised minus a frozen copy of the rank-$r$ $\\delta_0$, at a cost of about $r(m+n)$ extra numbers. Re-quantising the residual, as HF `olora_init` does, generically adds $\\kappa(r')-r'$.\n\nAction families whose pointing does not depend on the base (OFT at $R=I$, BOFT at $(R,s)=(I,\\mathbf 1)$, HiRA at $B=0$), evaluated at $\\kappa\\theta_0$, simply carry the defect $e$. Methods whose pointing is computed from the base have an order ambiguity of their own: DoRA's magnitude initialised from $\\kappa\\theta_0$ gives defect $e$, while initialised from $\\theta_0$ it gives $\\mathrm{diag}(\\|w_{0,i}\\|/\\|(\\kappa\\theta_0)_i\\|)\\,\\kappa\\theta_0-\\theta_0$. For action families a base change also moves the absolute invariants, not only the base point: NF4's exact zero code enlarges the zero set that HiRA must preserve, and exactly orthogonal (exact-Cayley) OFT preserves the Gram matrix of $\\kappa\\theta_0$ rather than that of $\\theta_0$; HF's default Cayley–Neumann OFT preserves it only approximately.\n\n(c) LoftQ alternates two updates on $\\|\\theta_0-Q-LR\\|$: an exact rank-$r$ SVD step, and a round-to-nearest quantisation step with blockwise scales recomputed from its input. The second is not an exact projection, so the objective need not decrease monotonically. With $s=1$ the output is pointed up to the attained residual: an approximate section, meaning a choice $(Q,q_0)$ with reduced $\\|\\rho(q_0)-\\theta_0\\|$. In general HF's deployed defect after $T$ iterations is $(Q_T+L_TR_T-W)+(s-1)L_TR_T$, because HF's internal residual also omits $s$. The official LoftQ checkpoints use $r=64$ and $\\alpha=16$, so $s=1/4$, and then the deployed defect can grow with $T$ while the attained residual falls.\n\n(d) For a generic high-precision adapter, \"fine-tune then quantise\" and \"quantise then fine-tune\" do not commute. The first lands on the quantisation grid. In the second, the exactly merged weight $\\kappa\\theta_0+\\Delta$ leaves the quantised type. For a uniform fixed-scale INT-$k$ grid, $\\kappa\\theta_0+\\Delta$ stays on the grid iff $\\Delta$ lies on the scale lattice (within range), so there the obstruction is the off-grid update. NF4 is non-uniform and not closed under addition, and absmax grids whose scales are recomputed are not closed either, so for QLoRA's NF4 the grid itself is part of the obstruction. Type-preserving merges, such as HF PEFT's dequantise–add–requantise for bitsandbytes 4/8-bit layers, re-quantise with recomputed block scales, so they stay in the quantised type but are lossy. Under affine group quantisation QA-LoRA is the structured exception: its group-pooled adapter is constant on each quantisation group and merges into the zero-points, so the merged model stays INT-$k$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) is the definition of additivity. (b) Compute $\\rho(q_0)-W$ in each pipeline: $\\kappa(W_{\\rm res})+\\delta_0-W$ with $W=W_{\\rm res}+\\delta_0$; $\\kappa W-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)-W$, whose norm follows from the Frobenius orthogonality of $\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)$ and $e-\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)$; and $\\kappa(r')+\\delta_0-W$. For DoRA, $\\rho(q_0)=\\mathrm{diag}(\\mu_0/\\|(\\kappa\\theta_0)_i\\|)\\,\\kappa\\theta_0$. (c) By construction; the failure of monotonicity is observed numerically. (d) In general $\\kappa(\\theta_0+\\Delta)\\ne\\kappa\\theta_0+\\Delta$; a group-constant $\\Delta$ is absorbed into the affine zero-points. ∎ [Num: on a $256\\times256$ NF4 example whose weight has a dominant low-rank part, with $r=8$, $\\|e\\|=5.65$, QPiSSA's defect is $1.45$, LoftQ's ($T=1$, $s=1$) is $5.22$, and the LoftQ objective rises in $27$ of $60$ half-steps; on a Gaussian weight all three defects are close to $\\|e\\|$, and with $s=2$ the one-step LoftQ defect equals $\\|e\\|$ exactly; adding an NF4-representable update to an NF4 weight leaves the NF4 grid in most entries.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"(a) confirmed. Refuted: LoftQ's initial step was misclassified (LoftQ quantises first and fits the error, with defect (I - P_r)e); 'quantise-then-split has defect e' holds only with a high-precision residual (HF olora_init re-quantises); DoRA has an order ambiguity of its own (base-derived magnitude); LoftQ's alternation is not a monotone minimisation; (d)'s reason was wrong (uniform grids are closed under + within range; the obstruction is the off-grid update), and QA-LoRA is an exception. Repaired: a three-order classification with defects; base-derived pointings and moved invariants; (c) and (d) rewritten; added that QLoRA's image misses theta0 unless rank e <= r.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): the core formulas hold. False: HF's LoftQ (loftq_init, replace_lora_weights_loftq) never divides by the LoRA scale s, so delta_0 = -s SVD_r(e) and the defect is e - s SVD_r(e), equal to the rank-r tail only for s = 1; with alpha = 2r it is as large as e, and for the official LoftQ checkpoints (s = 1/4) the deployed defect grows with T while the attained residual falls. Repaired: (b) and (c) restated with s, the Pythagorean norm formula and the general deployed defect; 'only if kept in high precision' replaced by the exact iff (a frozen copy of delta_0 keeps the memory saving); OFT's Gram statement restricted to exact Cayley; BOFT pointed at (I, 1); (d) distinguishes uniform fixed-scale grids (closed, obstruction is the off-grid update) from NF4 and recomputed-scale absmax grids (not closed); callback rollback noted; 'heuristic' and 'small' corrected; 'rank-r tail' rephrased. Knock-on: Thm II.1's defect sentence, the LoftQ worked example and the periodic-table row; Exposé 0 item 4 softened. Num states the test matrix structure, and an NF4 check is added to framework_checks.py.","public_statement":"Quantising the frozen base moves the base point. QLoRA is ordinary LoRA anchored at the quantised weight $\\kappa W$, so at initialisation it misses the pretrained $W$ by the generically full-rank quantisation error $e=\\kappa W-W$, and it can reach $W$ only if $\\operatorname{rank}e\\le r$. Splitting or error-fitting initialisations change this defect in an order-dependent way: split-then-quantise (QPiSSA, HF's PiSSA/CorDA workflow) leaves $\\kappa(W_{\\rm res})-W_{\\rm res}$; one-step LoftQ leaves $e-s\\,\\mathrm{SVD}_r(e)$, which is $e$ minus its best rank-$r$ approximation only when the LoRA scale $s=\\alpha/r$ is $1$, because HF's LoftQ init ignores the scale; quantise-split-requantise (HF OLoRA on bitsandbytes weights) leaves $e+\\kappa(r')-r'$. Exactly merging a high-precision adapter into a quantised base leaves the quantised type, and type-preserving merges are lossy, apart from group-constant adapters such as QA-LoRA under affine group quantisation.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (d) QA-LoRA's exception scoped to affine group quantisation, as in the paper and the public statement."},{"id":"V.3","name":"rebasing closure","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem V.3 (rebasing closure).** [Thm]\n\n(a) *Additive methods with a fixed shape $C\\ni0$*, with frames, masks and compression operators frozen at $\\theta_0$ across cycles. $R_K=\\theta_0+C^{+K}$, the $K$-fold Minkowski sum, and $R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\mathrm{Mon}(C)$, the additive monoid generated by $C$. If $C$ is closed under real scalings, $R_\\infty=\\theta_0+\\mathrm{span}\\,C$.\n\n(b) *Action methods with $e\\in S\\subseteq G$*, or $\\mathrm{id}\\in S\\subseteq\\mathrm{End}(\\Theta)$ for scaling methods. $R_K=S^K\\theta_0$, and $R_\\infty$ is the orbit of the generated monoid. Suppose $S$ lies in the subgroup generated by connected Lie subgroups $H_1,\\dots,H_p$ and contains a neighbourhood of $e$ in each. Then the monoid generated by $S$ is the connected Lie subgroup $H$ with $\\mathrm{Lie}(H)=\\mathrm{Lie}\\langle\\mathfrak h_1,\\dots,\\mathfrak h_p\\rangle$.\n\n(c) *Block-orthogonal factors.* Let the factors have coordinate blocks $I_j\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ (block OFT, BOFT factors, Givens planes). Then $\\mathrm{Lie}\\langle\\mathfrak{so}(I_j)\\rangle=\\bigoplus_C\\mathfrak{so}(C)$, summed over the connected components $C$ of the hypergraph $(\\{1..n\\},\\{I_j\\})$.\n\n*Corollaries.*\n1. **ReLoRA.** $R_K=W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le\\min(Kr,m,n)}$. It reaches full fine-tuning iff $K\\ge\\lceil\\min(m,n)/r\\rceil$.\n2. **Idempotent methods** gain nothing from rebasing: those whose shape is exactly a subspace, subgroup or submonoid independent of the base, with frames, masks and compression operators frozen across cycles. Examples: LoRA-FA with a fixed $A$, LoRA-XS with frozen frames, MoRA with fixed operators, FourierFT with fixed indices, WaveFT, C3A, MiSS (default balance mode; bat mode is base-dependent but still idempotent, since its blockwise maps $W\\mapsto(W+I)(I+M)-I$ form a monoid), SHiRA, BitFit, FISH with a fixed mask, LN-tuning, (IA)$^3$, SSF, RoAd. Block OFT with a fixed partition and an exact Cayley map gains only the measure-zero set of block rotations with an eigenvalue $-1$: $R_\\infty=W_0\\cdot\\prod SO(b)=\\overline{R_1}$. Constrained OFT (COFT, an $\\varepsilon$-ball) with an exact Cayley map gains up to $\\prod SO(b)$; HF's `coft=True` keeps the Cayley–Neumann map by default and so falls under corollary 4. Methods that recompute frames or masks from the merged weight (an SVD-recomputing LoRA-XS, ReMoRA's alternating operators, resampled indices) do gain.\n3. **VeRA.** $\\mathrm{VeRA}^\\infty=\\text{LoRA-FA}(A)$ whenever the frozen $B$ has no zero entries.\n4. **OFT family.** Block OFT with an exact Cayley map is stuck in $W_0\\cdot\\prod SO(b)$. HF's default Cayley–Neumann factor $R=C(Q)(I-Q^4)$ is not orthogonal: $R^\\top R=(I-Q^4)^2$, with singular values $\\lvert1-\\mu_k^4\\rvert$, where $\\pm i\\mu_k$ are the eigenvalues of $Q$. In the operating regime $\\lVert Q\\rVert_{\\rm op}\\le2^{1/4}$ every factor is a contraction, so re-merging decreases $K_{\\rm out}$ in the Loewner order and no singular value grows: the drift is monotone shrinkage, and the reachable set lies in $W_0$ times the block contraction semigroup. With unconstrained $Q$ the factors include singular ones ($\\mu_k=1$), and re-merging generates $\\prod\\big(GL^+(b)\\cup\\mathcal M_{\\le b-2}\\big)$, or $\\prod\\big(CO^+(2)\\cup\\{0\\}\\big)$ when $b=2$ [Sketch]. Iterated BOFT with $m'$ butterfly factors of block size $b$ reaches $\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^m)\\cdot W_0\\cdot P\\big(\\prod SO(b\\,2^{m'-1})\\big)P^\\top$, because the factor hypergraph has components of size $b\\,2^{m'-1}$. For injective $W_0$ this is $\\mathrm{diag}(\\mathbb R^m)\\cdot W_0\\cdot SO(n)$ iff $b\\,2^{m'-1}=n$, the full-depth butterfly $m'=1+\\log_2(n/b)$. HF's default `boft_n_butterfly_factor=1` is block OFT times $\\mathrm{diag}(s)$. A *single* full-depth BOFT has dimension at most $m'\\tfrac nb\\binom b2<\\dim SO(n)$ when $m'(b-1)<n-1$: it is a dense *matrix*, not a dense *set*.\n5. **Re-HRA**, with $W_0$ injective, $r$ even and `apply_GS=False`. $R_K=(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le Kr})\\cap W_0\\cdot SO(n)$: *rebasing commutes with the meet*. This is the multiplicative twin of ReLoRA, and it reaches $W_0\\cdot SO(n)$ in at most $\\lceil n/r\\rceil$ cycles.\n6. **GaLore (with a gradient-SVD or a random projector), LISA and MeZO**, under the optimizer hypotheses of Prop IV.2, are rebasing schemes of *linear* methods whose shape moves. That is why, as a set over the sequences of shapes they can choose, they reach all of $\\Theta$, while a fixed linear shape $L$ reaches only $\\theta_0+L$ (corollary 2). Low rank is the per-period obstruction only for GaLore; LISA and MeZO already make full-rank per-matrix updates and gain coverage. Flora proper takes full-space steps with a compressed momentum, so it has no per-period low-rank shape.\n7. **Merging never rescues an exactly orthogonal method from its absolute invariants.** In exact arithmetic, every exactly orthogonal input-side rebasing preserves $K_{\\rm out}$ and the spectrum, and every output-side one preserves $K_{\\rm in}$ and the spectrum (Cor II.6).","proof":"*Proof.* (a) follows by induction. If $C=\\mathbb RC$, the monoid generated by $C$ contains $-C$ and is closed under scaling, so it is the span.\n\n(b) Any neighbourhood of $e$ contains a symmetric one, and a symmetric neighbourhood of $e$ in a connected group generates that group as a monoid. The subgroup generated by connected Lie subgroups is the connected Lie subgroup with the generated Lie algebra (Yamabe).\n\n(c) Write $L_{ab}=E_{ab}-E_{ba}$. Then $[L_{ac},L_{cb}]=L_{ab}$ for distinct $a,b,c$. Overlapping hyperedges therefore generate $\\mathfrak{so}$ of their union, and $\\bigoplus_C\\mathfrak{so}(C)$ is a subalgebra that contains every generator.\n\nThe corollaries:\n- (1) Rank is subadditive, and an SVD splits into blocks.\n- (2) The exact Cayley image is dense and open in $SO(b)$, and every element of $SO(b)$ is a product of two elements of it.\n- (3) The entries of $\\Lambda_bB\\Lambda_d$ are $b_iB_{ij}d_j$. Their span over $(b,d)$ is all of $\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}$ when every $B_{ij}\\ne0$ (take $b=e_i$, $d=e_j$).\n- (4) Apply (c) to the butterfly blocks; the \"only if\" of the full-depth equality uses that $W_0$ is injective, as the statement assumes (for $W_0=0$ both sets are $\\{0\\}$). For the Cayley–Neumann factors, $C(Q)$ is orthogonal and commutes with $Q^4$, so the singular values of $R$ are $\\lvert1-\\mu_k^4\\rvert\\le1$ when $\\lVert Q\\rVert_{\\rm op}\\le2^{1/4}$, and $\\det R=\\prod_k(1-\\mu_k^4)^2\\ge0$. A product of contractions is a contraction, and $WR^\\top RW^\\top\\preceq WW^\\top$. The generated Lie algebra is $\\mathfrak{gl}(b)$, but that identifies only the generated group: (b) does not apply, since the factors form a $\\binom b2$-dimensional set containing no neighbourhood of $e$ in any connected subgroup. For the monoid, $R(Q)R(-Q)=(I-Q^4)^2$ gives positive pair-scalings, $R(Q)\\lvert I-Q^4\\rvert^{-1}$ gives $SO(b)$, the $KAK$ decomposition then gives $GL^+(b)$, and singular factors give $\\mathcal M_{\\le b-2}$.\n- (5) \"$\\subseteq$\" holds because $R_1R_2-I=R_1(R_2-I)+(R_1-I)$, so ranks add. \"$\\supseteq$\": an $R\\in SO(n)$ with $k=\\operatorname{rank}(R-I)\\le Kr$ is a product of exactly $k$ reflections, with $k$ even (Cartan–Dieudonné–Scherk). Split them into $K$ consecutive groups of even size at most $r$, pad each group with pairs $H_uH_u$, and observe that each group is an HRA$_r$ element.\n- The remaining corollaries are immediate. ∎ [Num: for the butterfly hypergraph at $n=64$, $b=4$, the components have sizes $4,8,16,32,64$ for $m'=1,\\dots,5$, so it is connected only at $m'=5$ (the referees found the same with HF's own permutation code); the Cayley–Neumann Lie algebra has dimension $16=\\dim\\mathfrak{gl}(4)$; $K_{\\rm out}$ moves by $0.2\\%$, $1.3\\%$, $6\\%$ and $22\\%$ (relative Frobenius change, averaged over draws) after $1$, $10$, $50$ and $200$ default-OFT merges with block size $8$ and fresh skew generators with entries of size about $0.05$ in every cycle, decreasing in the Loewner order at every merge; random skew $Q$ with $\\lVert Q\\rVert_{\\rm op}\\le2^{1/4}$ never give $\\lVert R\\rVert_{\\rm op}>1$; at $b=2$, $Q=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ gives $R=0$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"(a)-(c) and the ReLoRA, VeRA and re-HRA corollaries confirmed. Refuted corollaries: iterated BOFT reaches SO(n) only at full butterfly depth (components of size b 2^{m'-1}; HF's default factor 1 is block OFT); HF's default Cayley-Neumann OFT is not orthogonal and generates prod GL^+(b), so 'stuck' and 'preserves K_out' need an exact Cayley map; Cor 2 needs frozen frames and masks and holds only up to closure for Cayley OFT; Cor 6's 'fixed linear shapes cannot reach full rank' contradicted II.10 (it should read 'all of Theta'). All corollaries repaired; the (b) proof and the End(Theta) monoid case added.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): (a)-(c) and corollaries 1, 2, 3, 5 hold. False: corollary 4's claim that HF's Cayley-Neumann factors generate prod GL^+(b) under re-merging. det R = prod(1-mu^4)^2 >= 0, not > 0 (R = 0 at b = 2, Q = J); a generated Lie algebra identifies the generated group, not the monoid, and (b)'s hypothesis fails; in the operating regime ||Q||_op <= 2^{1/4} every factor is a contraction, so the reported K_out drift is monotone Loewner shrinkage. Repaired: corollary 4 restated (contraction regime and shrinkage; unconstrained Q generates prod(GL^+(b) u M_{<=b-2}) [Sketch]), with a new proof; COFT restricted to exact Cayley; the [Pred] replaced (it contradicted corollary 2) by a reach statement; (b) now says id in S; corollary 7 split into input-side (K_out) and output-side (K_in); MiSS bat mode, apply_GS=False for re-HRA, and BOFT factor 1 = block OFT times diag(s) noted. Knock-on: Cor II.6's Cayley-Neumann remark, the Exposé 0 headline and the OFT table row now say shrinkage rather than drift.","public_statement":"For merge-and-restart with a fixed adapter shape, the reachable set after $K$ cycles is $\\theta_0+C^{+K}$ for an additive shape $C$, or $S^K\\theta_0$ for a set $S$ of group elements containing the identity, so the limit is the orbit of the generated monoid. Hence ReLoRA reaches exactly $W_0+\\{\\operatorname{rank}\\le\\min(Kr,m,n)\\}$; methods whose shape is a fixed subspace, subgroup or submonoid gain nothing; exact-Cayley block OFT stays in $W_0\\cdot\\prod SO(b)$ up to closure unless the block hypergraph is connected, which BOFT's butterflies achieve only at full depth; and re-HRA reaches $(W_0+\\mathcal M_{\\le Kr})\\cap W_0\\cdot SO(n)$. HF PEFT's default Cayley–Neumann OFT is not orthogonal. With small generators each of its merges is a contraction, so repeated merging can only shrink neuron norms and singular values, while exactly orthogonal input-side methods preserve them through any number of merges.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: corollary 4 says that no singular value grows (singular values |1 - mu_k^4| equal 1 where mu_k = 0, so not every one is lowered) and needs injective W_0 for the full-depth BOFT equality (it fails for W_0 = 0); corollary 6 reaches Theta as a set over the possible shape sequences; [Num] corrected to 1.3% (not 1%) after 10 Cayley-Neumann merges, with block size 8 and relative Frobenius change, as replicated by the paper's referee."},{"id":"V.4","name":"combination rules: gauge and naturality","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition V.4 (combination rules: gauge and naturality).** [Prop]\n\n(a) A rule $\\Phi((B_i,A_i)_i)$ for combining LoRA factors descends to a function of the adapters $(B_iA_i)_i$ iff it is constant on the fibres of $(B_i,A_i)\\mapsto(B_iA_i)$. On the full-rank locus this is equivalent to $\\prod_iGL_r$-invariance. It is also equivalent for $\\Phi$ continuous on the whole factor space; this clause rests on the unique closed orbit in each fibre, which lies in the closure of every orbit of the fibre.\n- Task arithmetic $\\sum w_is_iB_iA_i$ ($s_i$ is HF's `scaling`, $\\alpha_i/r_i$, or $\\alpha_i/\\sqrt{r_i}$ under rsLoRA; HF's `cat` with merged scale $1$, Obs I.7) is invariant and lands in $\\mathrm{LoRA}_{\\sum_ir_i}$.\n- LoraHub's factor-wise rule $(\\sum w_iB_i)(\\sum w_jA_j)$ is not invariant. Replacing $(B_2,A_2)$ by $(cB_2,A_2/c)$ changes it by $\\sum_{l\\ne2}w_lw_2\\big[(c-1)B_2A_l+(c^{-1}-1)B_lA_2\\big]$. HF's `add_weighted_adapter(..., combination_type=\"linear\")` is a factor-wise rule of the same type: it weights $A_i$ by $\\mathrm{sign}(w_i)\\sqrt{\\lvert w_is_i\\rvert}$ and $B_i$ by $\\sqrt{\\lvert w_is_i\\rvert}$ and sums the factors separately, so it has cross terms. On adapters that share a frozen $A$ it is still not exact up to a global factor, since the relative weights become $\\sqrt{\\lvert w_is_i\\rvert}$. HF's non-SVD `ties`, `dare_*` and `magnitude_prune` variants also act on factors; the `*_svd` variants act on full updates.\n- Poly and MHR are different. Their skill inventories are trained jointly through the product-of-sums map, which is invariant under the diagonal $GL_r$. For fixed generic routing with fewer skills than tasks and full-rank factors, this is the whole continuous fibre [Num]. With at least as many skills as tasks, or once the routing is counted as a parameter, there are further symmetries (skill permutations, and re-mixings $\\alpha\\mapsto\\alpha M$ with $M\\mathbf 1=\\mathbf 1$). Their cross terms are a design choice, not an ill-definedness.\n\n(b) *Well-definedness versus exactness.* A joint gauge fixing makes a factor-wise rule well defined. For rank-preserving product-of-sums rules, exactness for all weights $w$ needs degree one in $w$, which for injective factors forces a shared factor; `cat`, which concatenates and raises the rank, is exact without one. A frozen shared factor (FFA-LoRA; LoRA-FA) gives $(\\sum w_iB_i)A=\\sum w_iB_iA$ exactly. LoraHub with a shared $A$ still returns $(\\sum_jw_j)\\sum_iw_iB_iA$, exact only up to the global factor $\\sum_jw_j$, since its weights are unnormalised. A per-module SVD canonical form leaves a $(\\mathbb Z/2)^r$ of sign flips per module, plus rotations within repeated singular values, and these change the cross terms. What is needed is a *joint* frame that produces a shared factor, as KnOTS's joint re-factorisation of the concatenated updates through a shared $U\\Sigma$ does. Federated LoRA shows the difference: FedAvg-LoRA clients start each round from the same broadcast $(B,A)$, so their failure, $\\tfrac12(B_1+B_2)\\cdot\\tfrac12(A_1+A_2)\\ne\\tfrac12(B_1A_1+B_2A_2)$, is bilinearity, and it persists in a fully fixed gauge.\n\n(c) *Naturality.* Task arithmetic is $GL(\\Theta)$-equivariant on updates. DARE is coordinatewise and magnitude-free: $D(m\\odot\\tau)=m\\odot(D\\tau)$ for every invertible diagonal $D$ and the same mask $m$. So DARE is exactly equivariant under the torus $T_N$, sign flips included, for each mask, and under permutations in distribution; its distributional group is exactly $\\mathrm{Mon}_N$. TIES with global trimming (top-$k$ magnitude, sign election by total mass as in the TIES paper and HF's default `\"total\"`, disjoint mean) is equivariant under $\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_N$, homotheties times signed permutations, generically (off magnitude ties at the threshold). With HF's `majority_sign_method=\"frequency\"`, count ties are broken to $+1$ and the group drops to $\\mathbb R_{>0}\\cdot S_N$. Per-tensor trimming changes the group to $\\prod_b(\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_{N_b})$, extended by permutations of equal-size blocks. DARE followed by full TIES (with trimming) is $\\mathbb R^\\times\\cdot B_N$-equivariant in distribution; HF's and MergeKit's `dare_ties` has no magnitude trim, so with `\"total\"` election it is exactly $T_N$-equivariant per mask, and its group contains $\\mathrm{Mon}_N$. None of the sparsifying rules (DARE, TIES, DARE-TIES) is $O(N)$-equivariant. Applied to factors and summed across adapters (HF's non-SVD variants), all of them are gauge-dependent: under independent per-adapter gauges even DARE fails, through the cross terms of (a). DARE's masking commutes with $T_r$ exactly for each mask and with $\\mathrm{Mon}_r$ in distribution, so a single masked adapter, or a factor-wise sum under one gauge shared by all adapters, is $T_r$-invariant per mask and $\\mathrm{Mon}_r$-invariant in distribution.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) On the full-rank locus, the fibres of $(B_i,A_i)\\mapsto(B_iA_i)$ are $\\prod GL_r$-orbits (Thm II.2). For $\\Phi$ continuous on the whole factor space: fix $W$ of rank $k\\le r$. The pairs with $BA=W$ and $\\operatorname{rank}B=\\operatorname{rank}A=k$ form one $GL_r$-orbit $O_W$, which is closed, and $O_W$ lies in the closure of every orbit of the fibre $\\{BA=W\\}$. Indeed, split $\\mathbb R^r=C_1\\oplus C_2\\oplus C_3\\oplus C_4$ with $\\mathrm{im}\\,A=C_1\\oplus C_2$, $\\ker B=C_2\\oplus C_3$ and $\\dim C_1=k$, and let $g_t$ act by $1$, $t$, $1$, $t^{-1}$ on the four summands; as $t\\to0$, $(Bg_t^{-1},g_tA)$ converges to a pair in the fibre whose factors have rank at most $k$, that is, to a point of $O_W$ (the real form of the closed-orbit theorem for reductive quotients; Goodman–Wallach 2009, Ch. 5; Mumford–Fogarty–Kirwan 1994, Ch. 1). Applying this adapter by adapter, two points $x,x'$ of one fibre have gauge paths with $g(t)\\cdot x\\to o$ and $g'(t)\\cdot x'\\to o'=h\\cdot o$, so invariance and continuity give $\\Phi(x)=\\Phi(o)=\\Phi(o')=\\Phi(x')$. Off the full-rank locus, invariance alone is not enough: the projector onto $\\mathrm{row}\\,A$ is $GL_r$-invariant on $\\{\\operatorname{rank}A=r\\}$, yet it differs at $(0,A)$ and $(0,A')$, which have the same product. Expand the LoraHub product. For Poly/MHR, if $\\mathrm{col}\\,B_i\\cap\\mathrm{col}\\,B_j=0$ and the routing mixes skills $i$ and $j$, invariance of the cross term under per-skill gauges $g_i,g_j$ forces $B_i(g_i^{-1}g_j-I)A_j=0$, hence $g_i=g_j$.\n\n(b) Flipping column $j$ of $B_i$ together with row $j$ of $A_i$ preserves the SVD form, and changes $B_iA_l$ for $l\\ne i$.\n\n(c) Masks and the rescaling by $1/(1-p)$ commute with diagonal maps, and an i.i.d. mask is permutation-invariant in law. Trimming by magnitude, electing signs by total mass and taking disjoint means commute with signed coordinate permutations and with $c\\cdot I$ for $c\\ne0$; with count-based election, ties have positive probability and are broken to $+1$, which is not sign-symmetric. Per-tensor trimming commutes with an independent homothety on each block. A rotation changes the magnitudes of individual coordinates. On factors, the all-ones mask has positive probability, and on it factor-wise DARE is the factor-wise rule of (a) times $(1-p)^{-2}$, so a per-adapter torus gauge changes it. ∎ [Num: DARE with a random non-unit diagonal $D$ is equivariant to $2\\times10^{-15}$ per mask; TIES is homogeneous to $9\\times10^{-16}$ and fails by $5.6$ under a generic positive diagonal; the general LoraHub change formula matches at $N=3$ to $2\\times10^{-15}$; factor-wise DARE changes by $O(1)$ under a diagonal gauge on one adapter and by $10^{-15}$ under one shared by all adapters; for Poly with $3$ skills, $r=2$ and $6\\times6$ weights, the fibre has dimension $32$ with $2$ tasks and $4=\\dim GL_2$ with $4$ tasks.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Core message confirmed. Refuted clauses: the 'iff' in (a) needs the full-rank locus (or continuity); the LoraHub change formula held only for N = 2; (c)'s groups were wrong: DARE is exactly torus-equivariant per mask (group containing Mon_N) and TIES is equivariant under homotheties times signed permutations, and 'only' was unproved. Also: HF's 'linear' combination is a LoraHub-type factor-wise rule; Poly and MHR are trained jointly (diagonal GL_r symmetry, not ill-defined); FedAvg's failure is bilinearity, not gauge; the [Pred] wrongly cited III.7(b). All repaired.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): (a), (b) and the DARE/TIES full-update groups hold. False: 'applied to factors, DARE remains invariant under the monomial sub-gauge Mon_r' (under per-adapter gauges the cross terms change it; only a gauge shared by all adapters, or a single adapter, is invariant). Also wrong or loose: DARE-TIES as HF/MergeKit implement it has no magnitude trim and is T_N-equivariant per mask; per-tensor trimming enlarges rather than restricts the group (block scalars, equal-size block swaps); TIES's group needs total-mass sign election (HF 'frequency' drops to R_{>0} S_N); Poly/MHR's 'only symmetry is diagonal GL_r' needs fixed generic routing with fewer skills than tasks. Repaired: (c) rewritten with these groups and a proof of the factor-level failure; Poly/MHR statement qualified with a proof line; LoRA_{Nr} -> LoRA_{sum r_i}; s_i is HF's scaling (rsLoRA too); HF linear with a shared A is not exact up to a global factor; exactness restricted to rank-preserving product-of-sums rules (cat is exact); KnOTS called a joint re-factorisation; 'none is O(N)-equivariant' restricted to the sparsifying rules. Num extended and backed by framework_checks.py.","public_statement":"A rule for combining LoRA factors is a well-defined function of the adapters $B_iA_i$ exactly when it is constant on the factorisation fibres; on the full-rank locus, or for continuous rules, that means invariance under each adapter's own $GL_r$ gauge. Task arithmetic on the products (HF's `cat` and `svd` merges) qualifies. Post-hoc product-of-sums rules (LoraHub, and HF's `linear` and non-SVD `ties`/`dare_*` merges) do not, because their cross terms change when a single adapter is rescaled; for rank-preserving rules exactness needs a shared factor, such as a common frozen $A$ or KnOTS's joint $U\\Sigma$. On full updates, task arithmetic is $GL$-equivariant, DARE is equivariant under every invertible coordinate rescaling for each mask and under permutations in distribution, and TIES with sign election by total mass is equivariant under homotheties times signed permutations; none of the sparsifying rules is rotation-equivariant.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: hedge lifted on (a), continuous case: the [Sketch] becomes a complete argument (a closed orbit O_W of minimal-rank factorisations lies in the closure of every orbit of the fibre, by an explicit one-parameter degeneration), as in the paper's main text and Appendix C, which the 05-base referee checked."},{"id":"VI.2","name":"the activation fixes the privileged basis","kind":"lemma","statement":"**Lemma VI.2 (the activation fixes the privileged basis).** [Lemma; classical: Sussmann 1992; Chen–Lu–Hecht-Nielsen 1993; Godfrey et al. 2022] Let $\\sigma:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be an elementwise activation that is not affine, and let $L\\in GL_d$ commute with it as maps on $\\mathbb R^d$: $L\\,\\sigma^{\\times d}=\\sigma^{\\times d}L$.\n\n(a) If $\\sigma\\in C^2$, then $L=PD$ is monomial.\n\n(b) If $\\sigma$ is ReLU or leaky ReLU with slope $\\alpha\\ne1$, then $L=PD$ with $D$ a positive diagonal.\n\n(c) If moreover $\\sigma$ is real-analytic at $0$ with $\\sigma(t)=\\sum a_kt^k$, each diagonal entry $c$ of $D$ satisfies $ca_k=c^ka_k$ for all $k$. So $c=1$ for GELU and SiLU, since $\\sigma''(0)\\neq0$, and $c=\\pm1$ for tanh.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) Fix row $i$; its row vector $\\ell_i$ is nonzero because $L$ is invertible. The left side $\\sum_jL_{ij}\\sigma(x_j)$ has vanishing mixed partials $\\partial_j\\partial_k$ for $j\\ne k$. The right side has mixed partial $L_{ij}L_{ik}\\sigma''(\\ell_i\\cdot x)$. Choose $t_0$ with $\\sigma''(t_0)\\ne0$, which exists because $\\sigma$ is not affine, and $x$ with $\\ell_i\\cdot x=t_0$. So $L_{ij}L_{ik}=0$: each row has a single nonzero entry.\n\n(b) ReLU is not $C^2$, so argue by kinks instead. The left side of row $i$ is non-differentiable exactly on $\\bigcup_{j:L_{ij}\\ne0}\\{x_j=0\\}$, and the right side exactly on the hyperplane $\\{\\ell_i\\cdot x=0\\}$. The two sets are equal, and a hyperplane equal to a finite union of coordinate hyperplanes is one of them. So row $i$ has a single entry $L_{ij}$, and $L_{ij}\\sigma(t)=\\sigma(L_{ij}t)$ at $t=1$ forces $L_{ij}>0$. Leaky ReLU is identical.\n\n(c) $P$ commutes with $\\sigma^{\\times d}$, so $D\\sigma=\\sigma D$, i.e. $c\\,\\sigma(t)=\\sigma(ct)$; compare Taylor coefficients at $0$. ∎","status":"verified","referee_notes":"Both referees: lemma true and classical. Wording fixes: standing hypotheses (sigma not affine, L in GL_d commuting as maps); leaky slope alpha != 1; Taylor coefficients at 0; proof steps (the choice of t0, both inclusions of the kink sets) made explicit; credited to Sussmann, Chen-Lu-Hecht-Nielsen and Godfrey et al. Commentary corrected: SwiGLU and GeGLU FFNs have the full monomial group, the LayerNorm-gain basis comes from the parametrisation, BOFT is equivariant on up and gate projections; the permutation [Pred] restated as a variance claim.","round2":"not-reviewed","round2_notes":"Not covered by round 2; round-1 status kept.","public_statement":"An invertible linear map that commutes with a non-affine elementwise activation is a permutation times a diagonal for $C^2$ activations, a permutation times a positive diagonal for ReLU and leaky ReLU, a signed permutation for tanh, and a plain permutation for GELU and SiLU (Sussmann 1992; Chen–Lu–Hecht-Nielsen 1993). So the activation fixes a privileged neuron basis up to these symmetries."},{"id":"VI.3","name":"the merge calculus","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem VI.3 (the merge calculus).** [Thm; the rules are elementary, and the merge facts for (IA)$^3$ (Liu et al. 2022, Sec. 3.3), SSF (re-parameterisation) and LoRA (linearity) are known. Scale migration through SwiGLU, ReLU and norms, and the GELU obstruction, are used by AWQ (Lin et al. 2024) and SmoothQuant (Xiao et al. 2023). New: only the packaging as a merge calculus for PEFT, with explicit side conditions] Work with activation-side diagrams in a symmetric monoidal category of continuous, piecewise-smooth maps with copy and discard nodes. (We call copy and the Hadamard product \"spiders\" by analogy only; in this cartesian setting they do not satisfy the Frobenius law.) A *trainable box* is a node whose weight ranges over a stated class: all linear maps, or all affine maps when the box has a bias slot. R2 and R4–R7 are identities of maps; R1 is a closure property and R3 a characterisation.\n\n| rule | statement | when it holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 (fusion) | composites and parallel sums of linear (affine) boxes are linear (affine) | the operators involved are position-independent |\n| R2 (permutation slide) | $P\\sigma^{\\times d}=\\sigma^{\\times d}P$ | every elementwise $\\sigma$ |\n| R3 (positive-diagonal slide) | $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)\\sigma^{\\times d}=\\sigma^{\\times d}\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)$ for all $\\ell\\ge0$ | iff $\\sigma$ is positively homogeneous (ReLU, leaky ReLU; not GELU, SiLU, tanh); such $\\sigma$ are piecewise linear |\n| R4 (spider slide) | $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)(a\\odot b)=(\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)a)\\odot b$ | any $\\ell$ |\n| R5 (cup slide) | $\\langle Mq,k\\rangle=\\langle q,M^\\top k\\rangle$ | always. Under RoPE, $\\langle R_mMq,R_nk\\rangle=\\langle q,M^\\top R_{n-m}k\\rangle$ holds for every $M$, but the slide onto the key side, $\\langle R_mMq,R_nk\\rangle=\\langle R_mq,R_nM^\\top k\\rangle$ for all $m,n$, holds iff $M^\\top$ commutes with every $R_{n-m}$ that occurs; for diagonal $M$ and distinct frequencies, iff $\\ell$ is constant on each rotary pair (in either pairing convention) |\n| R6 (norm absorption) | $W(\\gamma\\odot n(x)+\\beta)=(W\\mathrm{diag}\\gamma)\\,n(x)+W\\beta$ | always; the shift $W\\beta$ needs a bias slot, and an RMSNorm gain has no $\\beta$ |\n| R7 (copy slide) | applying $D$ and then copying equals copying and then applying $D$ to every copy | always |\n\nConsequences:\n\n(a) **Affine edits.** Let a method insert a position-uniform affine edit $E_q$ on a wire $w$ of the base network $f$, for every $q\\in Q$. Suppose every reader of $w$ is a trainable, unshared box (no weight tying) whose class is closed under precomposition with $E_q$; a shift needs a bias slot. Then the method merges, by $W_c\\mapsto W_cE_q$ for all readers $c$ at once. Dually, it merges into an untied trainable box that writes $w$ whenever $E_q$ composed with that box stays in its class. Absorption into a box that the extension itself inserts does not count. Edits that violate these hypotheses fall outside (a): on the reader side, R1–R7 do not merge position-selective edits or edits on a wire that is also read by a skip connection, a norm or the network output. Whole-network non-mergeability is open (Exposé XI). Position-selective edits (ReFT/LoReFT, and position-gated LoRA variants such as Activated LoRA) are not box-locally mergeable. Residual-stream edits need case analysis: an edit merges into the box that produces the wire when that box can absorb it (an untied embedding table; for a fixed-position edit at layer $0$, a learned absolute positional table), a shift folds into a writer's bias when one exists, a diagonal edit after a post-LN folds into that LN's affine parameters, and an orthogonal edit in an RMSNorm model merges globally, SliceGPT/QuaRot-style. No merge is known for LoReFT's non-orthogonal low-rank edit.\n\n(b) **Diagonal methods.** A diagonal method merges into a box reached by R1–R7 if all of the following hold: the rules move *every* copy of $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)$ into trainable boxes for all $\\ell$ in the method's parameter domain (with $\\ell\\ge0$ when R3 is used); no position-dependent operator lies on the path (for RoPE, $\\ell$ is constant on each rotary pair); the target box is unshared, or $\\ell$ is constant across the sharing group; and every shift has a bias slot. Conversely, the RoPE condition is necessary for sliding a query scaling onto $W_K$ when $W_Q$ and $W_K$ have full rank and at least two occurring relative offsets have rotation angles that are not multiples of $\\pi$. Without such genericity hypotheses the conditions are not necessary: a dead rotary pair, or zero entries of $\\ell$ under ReLU, can still merge.\n\n(c) **Non-affine edits.** For a linear or affine box, an edit whose modified box function is non-affine in the box input, for some parameter value, is not box-locally mergeable. For linear and affine boxes this is the definition; for non-linear boxes such as attention, see Thm VI.5(f).\n\n(d) **Type relativity.** A generic high-precision update is off the quantisation grid, so exact merging of a QLoRA or LoftQ update leaves the quantised type, a type-preserving merge is lossy, and QA-LoRA's group-constant adapters are the exception (Prop V.2(d)).","proof":"*Proof.* R2, R4, R5, R6 and R7 are identities; under RoPE, $R_m^\\top R_n=R_{n-m}$ gives the first form of R5 for every $M$. For R3, $\\ell\\sigma(t)=\\sigma(\\ell t)$ for all $\\ell\\ge0$ and all $t$ is positive homogeneity of degree one, and a $C^1$ positively homogeneous $\\sigma$ on $\\mathbb R$ is linear. (a) The closure and reader conditions put $W_cE_q$ in each reader's class, R7 distributes $E_q$ to every reader, and position-uniformity lets one weight absorb the edit at every position. Dually, if $w$ is the output of an untied box $p(x)=W_px+b_p$ and $E_q\\circ p$ lies in its class, then for $E_q(x)=A_qx+c_q$ replacing $p$ by $E_q\\circ p$ ($W_p\\mapsto A_qW_p$, $b_p\\mapsto A_qb_p+c_q$) is a merge (R1). Skip connections, norms and the network output carry no trainable weight that could absorb an edit they read, and R1 does not fuse a position-selective edit into a weight that acts at every position. (b) The \"if\" direction is (a) applied at the box reached. For the RoPE converse, equality of all logits with full-rank $W_Q,W_K$ forces $R_\\Delta^{-1}\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)R_\\Delta$ to be one matrix for every occurring offset $\\Delta$, including $\\Delta=0$, so $\\mathrm{diag}(\\ell)$ commutes with $R_\\Delta$; on a rotary pair whose angle is not a multiple of $\\pi$ this forces equal entries. (c) is the definition of box-local mergeability. (d) is Prop V.2(d). ∎ [Num: under RoPE, $\\langle R_mMq,R_nk\\rangle=\\langle q,M^\\top R_{n-m}k\\rangle$ holds for a generic $M$ to $10^{-15}$ while the slide onto the key side fails; a query scaling slid onto $W_K$ leaves a $7.6\\%$ least-squares residual unless $\\ell$ is pair-constant; folding post-LN gains into $W_1$ changes the output; legal rewrites agree to $10^{-15}$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Rules confirmed as identities. Refuted consequences: (a) contradicted the framework's own LoReFT analysis and fails for position-gated edits; (b) fails under GQA sharing, under RoPE (a query scaling does not slide onto W_K), under post-LN fan-out and for tied embeddings; affine shifts need bias slots; R3 is not a 'smooth' identity; (d)'s reason was wrong; 'the consequences are new' overclaimed. Repaired: a setting with copy spiders and trainable boxes (R7 copy slide added); (a)-(d) restated with side conditions (position-uniformity, unshared targets, bias slots, RoPE); corollaries corrected; prior work credited.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): R1-R4, R6, R7 and (a) as a sufficient condition hold. False: R5 under RoPE as written (<R_m M q, R_n k> = <q, M^T R_{n-m} k> holds for every M; the condition belongs to the slide onto the key side); (b)'s 'only if' was unproved and false without genericity (dead rotary pair; zero entries under ReLU); (a)'s blanket 'they are M-infinity' for residual-stream and extension edits was unproved and false (embedding fold, post-LN affine fold, bias fold, SliceGPT-style rotation; SSF and (IA)^3 themselves); 'new GLU/W_up observation' was already in AWQ's code. Repaired: R5 restated; (b) stated as sufficiency with a proved RoPE converse under genericity; (a)'s exclusion replaced by 'falls outside (a)' with a case analysis of residual-stream edits; GQA dropped from (a); R3 allows l >= 0; (d) says generic; AWQ and SmoothQuant credited; GELU non-fusion proved; RMSNorm gains fold without a bias slot; AdapterFusion stays M-infinity with a constant gate; 'not mergeable' is box-local; tied-weight caveat added; 'decided/decidable' changed to 'certified' here, in Exposé 0, rosetta.json, visuals.md and narrative.md.","public_statement":"A few exact rewrite rules give sufficient conditions for folding a scaling or affine PEFT edit into existing weights with no change in output. They are linear fusion, permutation slides through any elementwise activation, positive-diagonal slides through positively homogeneous ones such as ReLU, diagonal slides through the linear branch of a GLU, transposition across the query–key product (under RoPE only for scalings constant on each rotary pair), norm-gain absorption and copying. The conditions are that the edit is the same at every position, the absorbing weights are unshared, and any shift has a bias slot. For example, (IA)$^3$'s FFN scale always folds into $W_{\\rm down}$, and also into $W_{\\rm up}$ under SwiGLU or GeGLU for every scale (a migration AWQ already uses) but not under GELU. Position-selective edits (ReFT, Activated LoRA) and nonlinear adapters are not box-locally mergeable, and exactly merging a generic QLoRA or LoftQ update leaves the quantised format, with QA-LoRA as the exception. This is a set of sufficient conditions plus box-local obstructions, not a decision procedure for whole-network mergeability.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: (a) adds the dual merge into an untied box that writes the wire, and narrows 'R1-R7 do not merge them' to the reader-side cases (position-selective edits; wires also read by a skip connection, a norm or the network output), as stated in the paper and proved in its Appendix C."},{"id":"VI.4","name":"linear insertions, and ReFT","kind":"proposition","statement":"**Proposition VI.4 (linear insertions, and ReFT).** [Prop]\n\n(a) A linear adapter in parallel with a single linear box, $W_0x+sUDx$, *is* LoRA: it has the same $\\rho$ (He et al. 2022, Table 1). A linear adapter parallel to a whole sublayer (He et al.'s parallel adapter around attention or the FFN) edits the weightless skip connection rather than a base box, and is not box-locally mergeable when the sublayer is non-affine.\n\n(b) Let $W_0\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ be bias-free (as LLaMA-type $W_o$ and $W_{\\rm down}$ are), and $r\\ge1$. A serial linear adapter after $W_0$, $(I+UD)W_0$ with $U\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times r}$ and $D\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times m}$ (scale absorbed), has image $W_0+\\{Y\\in\\mathcal M_{\\le r}:\\mathrm{row}\\,Y\\subseteq\\mathrm{row}\\,W_0\\}$. It is expressively equivalent to LoRA iff $\\operatorname{rank}W_0=n$, and weakly (unpointed) isomorphic to LoRA iff $W_0$ is invertible. The isomorphism $(U,D)\\mapsto(U,DW_0)$ sends a base point $(U_0,D_0)$ to $(U_0,D_0W_0)$. For a fixed $\\mathrm T_A$ pointing $(0,A_0)$ with $\\operatorname{rank}A_0=r$ and $1\\le r<m=n$, a pointed isomorphism exists iff $W_0$ is invertible and $\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0=\\mathrm{row}(D_0W_0)$, and then $U_0=0$ automatically. With a bias $b_0$, the serial adapter also moves the bias by $UDb_0$, so on the affine box its image differs from weight-only LoRA's. Houlsby's near-identity initialisation (weights from a zero-mean Gaussian with standard deviation $10^{-2}$, truncated at two standard deviations) makes practical serial adapters slightly unpointed; the pointed statement applies to zero-up initialisations.\n\n(c) LoReFT (Wu et al. 2024) is $\\Phi(h)=h+R^\\top(Wh+b-Rh)$, with $R\\in\\mathbb R^{r\\times d}$ having orthonormal rows, applied at selected positions. On one hidden vector it is the extension by an **identity box** followed by a rank-$\\le r$ affine edit,\n$$\\Phi(h)=(I+R^\\top(W-R))h+R^\\top b,$$\nwhich is LoRA$_r$ on the *affine* identity box $[I_d\\mid0]$ in homogeneous coordinates, with $B=R^\\top$ constrained to the Stiefel manifold and $A=[W-R\\mid b]$. Its image is $(I,0)+\\mathcal M_{\\le r}(d\\times(d+1))$, of dimension $r(2d+1-r)$. The bias is confined to $\\mathrm{col}\\,B$, so for $r<d$ this is not LoRA$_r$ $\\oplus$ BitFit, whose dimension is larger by $d-r$. It is simulated, through $h(R,W,b)=(R^\\top,[W-R\\mid b])$, by LoRA$_r$ on that affine box pointed at $(R_0^\\top,0)$, a $\\mathrm T_B$ pointing with $\\mathrm{col}\\,B_0=\\mathrm{row}\\,R_0$; this homogeneous-coordinate LoRA has a bias column in $A$, unlike HF LoRA. It is not simulated by $\\mathrm T_A$-pointed LoRA$_r$, since its $\\dim S_1=r(d+1)$ exceeds $rd$. It is not isomorphic to LoRA$_r$: its parameter count is smaller by $r(r+1)/2$, and its residual symmetry is $O(r)$, acting by $(R,W,b)\\mapsto(OR,OW,Ob)$. It is pointed iff $W=R$ and $b=0$. The reference implementation (pyreft) initialises $W$ and $b$ by the default `nn.Linear` init, independently of $R$, so practical LoReFT has a pointing defect $R^\\top((W_0-R_0)h+b_0)$: at initialisation it replaces the $R$-component of $h$ by a random affine function, and the simulation above is then only a weak morphism. pyreft also applies dropout to the whole output, $h$ included, so the training-time map is not $\\Phi$.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) holds by definition; a sublayer-parallel adapter adds $sUDx$ to $F(x)$ for a non-affine sublayer $F$, and no base weight sees $x$ linearly. (b) $UDW_0$ ranges over the rank-$\\le r$ matrices whose rows lie in $\\mathrm{row}W_0$, which is all of $\\mathcal M_{\\le r}$ iff $\\mathrm{row}W_0=\\mathbb R^n$. If $W_0$ is invertible, $(U,D)\\mapsto(U,DW_0)$ is a linear bijection over $\\Theta$. Conversely, an isomorphism preserves $\\mathrm{Im}$ (Obs I.4(c)), forcing $\\operatorname{rank}W_0=n$, and is a diffeomorphism, forcing $2mr=r(m+n)$, i.e. $m=n$. Pointed case, \"if\": pointedness gives $U_0D_0W_0=0$, so $U_0D_0=0$; $\\operatorname{rank}D_0=r$ then forces $U_0=0$, and composing $(U,D)\\mapsto(U,DW_0)$ with the gauge $g\\in GL_r$ with $A_0=gD_0W_0$ gives the pointed isomorphism. \"Only if\": the unpointed part gives $W_0$ invertible, and Obs I.6 gives $\\{XD_0W_0+U_0Y\\}=\\{Z:\\mathrm{row}\\,Z\\subseteq\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0\\}$; since $r<n$, a nonzero $U_0$ would produce some $U_0Y$ with a row outside $\\mathrm{row}\\,A_0$, so $U_0=0$ and the row spaces agree. With a bias, $(I+UD)(W_0x+b_0)=(W_0+UDW_0)x+b_0+UDb_0$. (c) Expand. Every $d\\times(d+1)$ matrix of rank $\\le r$ factors as $R^\\top V$ with $R^\\top$ having orthonormal columns (QR), so the image is as stated. $R^\\top$ is injective, so the edit vanishes iff $W=R$ and $b=0$. Finally, $R^\\top O^\\top(OW-OR)=R^\\top(W-R)$. The parameter count is $rd-\\tfrac{r(r+1)}2+rd+r$ against $r(2d+1)$, and at the neutral point $S_1=\\{R_0^\\top V\\}$ has dimension $r(d+1)$. ∎ [Num: Jacobian rank $22=r(2d+1-r)$ at $d=6$, $r=2$, against $26$ for LoRA$_r$ $\\oplus$ bias; serial-adapter image dimension $7$ at $(m,n,r)=(5,3,1)$ and at $(4,8,1)$, against $11$ for LoRA at $(4,8,1)$.]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"(a), the image formula in (b) and the LoReFT algebra confirmed. Refuted presentation of (c): the bias is confined to col B (image dimension r(2d+1-r), not 'LoRA_r plus a bias'); 'is LoRA_r' is only an image-level simulation (Stiefel B, O(r) gauge); pyreft initialises W and b independently of R, so practical LoReFT has a pointing defect; position-selectivity is the main merge obstruction. (b)'s 'only if' was unproved and its pointing unspecified. Repaired: affine identity box, dimension, defect, an isomorphism proof via dimension and image, and the pointing condition.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful minor): (a), the serial-adapter image, the LoReFT algebra and dimension hold. False: the pointed-isomorphism criterion in (b) needs rank A_0 = r and r < m = n (counterexamples with rank-deficient A_0 and with r = m = n), and its 'only if' was not proved. Repaired: (b) restated under these hypotheses with a proof via S_1 (U_0 = 0 follows); bias-free W_0 made a hypothesis, with the bias shift UD b_0 noted; Houlsby's near-identity init noted as a small defect. (c): the simulating LoRA is pointed at (R_0^T, 0), a T_B pointing (not T_A, since dim S_1 = r(d+1) > rd); non-isomorphism argued by parameter count (difference r(r+1)/2), not by naming the gauge; 'not LoRA + BitFit' needs r < d; pyreft's whole-output dropout noted. (a) credited to He et al. and restricted to adapters parallel to a single box (sublayer-parallel adapters edit the skip connection), also in the periodic table, the arrow list and the worked example. Commentary: LoReFT/DiReFT rather than ReFT, image-level reading, Wu et al. credited, interior residual-stream sites only.","public_statement":"For a bias-free linear layer $W_0\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$, a linear adapter in parallel with $W_0$ is exactly LoRA, and a linear serial adapter $(I+UD)W_0$ reaches exactly the rank-$\\le r$ updates whose rows lie in $\\mathrm{row}\\,W_0$. That set equals LoRA's reachable set iff $\\operatorname{rank}W_0=n$, and is a reparametrisation of LoRA iff $W_0$ is square and invertible. On each hidden vector, LoReFT (Wu et al. 2024) is a rank-$\\le r$ affine edit of an inserted identity map, that is, LoRA on an affine identity box with its up-factor restricted to orthonormal columns. Its reachable set has dimension $r(2d+1-r)$ with the bias confined to the column space of $R^\\top$, its residual symmetry is $O(r)$, and it is simulated by, but not isomorphic to, LoRA$_r$ pointed with $A_0=0$. With pyreft's default initialisation it is not the identity at step 0, and because it acts only at selected positions of the weightless residual stream it does not merge into the base weights.","paper_sync":"2026-10-03: Houlsby's near-identity initialisation stated precisely (zero-mean Gaussian with standard deviation 1e-2, truncated at two standard deviations); the old N(0,10^{-2}) was ambiguous."},{"id":"VI.5","name":"attention is a homomorphism followed by a perspective map","kind":"theorem","statement":"**Theorem VI.5 (attention is a homomorphism followed by a perspective map).** [Thm; the identity is standard; (b) is due to He et al. 2022; (c) and the soft-prompt-to-prefix map in (e) are due to Petrov–Torr–Bibi 2024, §2.2 (informally Li–Liang 2021, §7.2); the monoid organisation, the explicit position hypotheses and the slice-category typing of (e) are ours] Fix a query $q$. Multisets of key–value pairs form a commutative monoid under $\\uplus$. Define\n$$\\Sigma_q(K)=\\Big(\\sum e^{\\langle q,k\\rangle/\\sqrt d},\\ \\sum e^{\\langle q,k\\rangle/\\sqrt d}\\,v\\Big).$$\n(a) $\\Sigma_q$ is a monoid homomorphism into $(\\mathbb R_{\\ge0}\\times\\mathbb R^{d_v},+)$, landing in $\\{Z>0\\}\\cup\\{(0,0)\\}$. On nonempty multisets, $\\mathrm{Attn}_q=\\pi\\circ\\Sigma_q$ with the partial map $\\pi(Z,S)=S/Z$, defined for $Z>0$.\n\n(b) **Prefix is a gated parallel adapter.** For nonempty $P$ and $C$, $\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P\\uplus C)=(1-\\lambda_q)\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\lambda_q\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)$, with $\\lambda_q=Z_q(P)/(Z_q(P)+Z_q(C))$.\n\n(c) **Per-layer pattern rigidity.** Within a single layer, for fixed $q$ and a fixed content multiset $C$: the content attention weights are multiplied by $1-\\lambda_q$, their ratios are unchanged, and the output lies on the segment from $\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)$ to $\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)\\in\\mathrm{conv}\\,V_P$. This rigidity is relative to the same layer's computation without the prefix; it is relative to the pretrained pattern only at the lowest prefixed layer. Lower-layer prefixes change the queries and the content keys and values of higher layers, so attention patterns at the level of the network are *not* rigid (Petrov–Torr–Bibi 2024, §6; the same authors show that prefix-tuning a pretrained transformer can be a universal approximator, arXiv:2402.14753).\n\n(d) **LLaMA-Adapter** normalises $P$ and $C$ separately and adds a zero-initialised gate: $\\mathrm{Attn}_q(C)+\\tanh(g_l)\\,\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)$, with one gate per head in the paper; HF PEFT uses one raw zero-initialised scalar per layer, without $\\tanh$. It uses two perspective maps, leaves the content weights exactly unchanged, and is pointed at $g=0$. Its prompt keys and values are the frozen $W_k,W_v$ applied to one learned prompt per adapted layer, and it acts in the top layers only.\n\n(e) **Prompt $\\to$ deep prefix in causal decoders.** Assume that the soft prompt comes first, that attention is causal, and that content positions are offset by the prompt length $m$ in both methods (as HF PEFT does via `cache_position`). Then at every layer the states at prompt positions depend only on $p$, and $h:p\\mapsto(K_j(p),V_j(p))_j$ satisfies $\\Phi\\circ\\rho_{\\rm prefix}\\circ h=\\Phi\\circ\\rho_{\\rm prompt}$ as functions of the content tokens, read at content positions. The two methods live on different extensions, so this is an arrow of the unpointed slice over the realisation $C^\\infty(X_{\\rm content},Y_{\\rm content})$, not of $\\mathsf{Meth}$. Under RoPE without the offset, a different $h$ works: rotate each prefix key by $-m$ (positions $-m,\\dots,-1$). For additive relative biases (ALiBi, T5) the answer depends on how the implementation places the prefix keys. The construction does not apply in bidirectional encoders of depth $\\ge2$.\n\n(f) **Obstructions.** Call a head *standard* if it is vanilla softmax attention without torch-style `add_bias_kv` (which would absorb a one-token prefix), `add_zero_attn`, learned attention sinks or softmax-plus-one. On length-one contexts, every weight setting of a standard head is affine in $x$. Some prefix makes the prefixed head non-affine on length-one contexts iff $W_o\\ne0$ and ($W_q\\ne0$ or $W_k^\\top b_q\\ne0$); for bias-free heads, iff $W_o\\ne0$ and $W_q\\ne0$. For such frozen weights and generic prefixes the prefixed head is not affine, because the gate $\\lambda(x)$ is not constant. So prefix tuning and P-tuning v2 are not box-locally mergeable into a standard head. When $W_oW_v\\ne0$, no prefix is neutral, so prompt and prefix tuning are unpointed.","proof":"*Proof.* (a) Sums over a disjoint union split, and $Z=0$ only for the empty multiset.\n\n(b) $\\pi(Z_P+Z_C,S_P+S_C)=\\lambda S_P/Z_P+(1-\\lambda)S_C/Z_C$.\n\n(c) Each content weight is $e^{\\langle q,k\\rangle/\\sqrt d}/(Z_P+Z_C)=(1-\\lambda)e^{\\langle q,k\\rangle/\\sqrt d}/Z_C$, and $\\mathrm{Attn}_q(P)$ is a convex combination of $V_P$.\n\n(d) This is the definition.\n\n(e) Induction over layers: under causal masking, prompt positions attend only to prompt positions, and with the stated offset their positional encodings agree in both methods. Without the offset under RoPE, prefix keys sit in the cache already rotated, so the content-to-prompt displacement changes unless each key is rotated back by $-m$.\n\n(f) A softmax over one token equals $1$, so the original head computes $W_o(W_vx+b_v)+b_o$. With a prefix, the output is $(1-\\lambda(x))W_o(W_vx+b_v)+\\lambda(x)W_o\\mathrm{Attn}_{q(x)}(P)+b_o$. If $W_o=0$ it is constant. If $W_q=0$ and $W_k^\\top b_q=0$, every score is constant in $x$, so $\\lambda$ is constant and the output is affine. Otherwise, take a ray $x=tu$ along which some score difference varies (with $W_qu\\ne0$, or with the content score $\\langle b_q,W_ktu\\rangle$ varying), one prefix token whose key keeps the gate $\\lambda(t)$ non-constant, and a value $v$ with $W_ov$ large. The gate is a bounded, non-constant analytic function of $t$, so $\\lambda''\\ne0$ somewhere, and for large $W_ov$ the term $\\lambda(t)W_ov$ dominates the second difference. The prefixed output is real-analytic in $(x,\\text{prefix})$, so this witness makes the prefixes with affine output a nowhere-dense analytic subset. Unpointedness: $\\lambda(x)>0$ always, and a neutral prefix would need $W_o\\mathrm{Attn}_{q(x)}(P)=W_o(W_vx+b_v)$ for all $x$; the left side lies in the compact set $W_o\\,\\mathrm{conv}\\,V_P$, while the right side is unbounded when $W_oW_v\\ne0$. ∎ [Num: on a 3-layer, 2-head causal decoder, prompt-to-prefix agreement is $9\\times10^{-16}$ with RoPE and the offset, against $1.3$ without it and $2\\times10^{-15}$ with keys rotated by $-m$; a head with $W_o=0$ stays affine although $W_q^\\top W_k\\ne0$, and one with $W_q\\ne0$, $W_q^\\top W_k=0$ does not (referee replication).]","status":"repaired","referee_notes":"Identities (a)-(d) confirmed and correctly attributed. Major: (c)'s rigidity holds per layer only (deeper prefixes change higher layers' attention, and Petrov-Torr-Bibi themselves prove universality), so 'cannot impose new attention patterns' overreached; (e)'s morphism was ill-typed (different extensions) and needs a position-offset convention; 'strict' and 'not in encoders' were unproved; (f) needs generic frozen head weights; pi is partial at Z = 0; the proof of (c) dropped 1/sqrt(d). Repaired: per-layer statement; a morphism over the realisation with the offset hypothesis; the encoder claim weakened; genericity and analyticity argument; partial pi.","round2":"repaired","round2_notes":"Round 2 (math refuted, major; faithful refuted, major): (a)-(d), (e) under the offset hypothesis and the analytic genericity argument hold. False or unproved: (e)'s 'or the positional encoding is relative' fails for the stated h under RoPE (prefix keys are cached already rotated); (f)'s example 'W_q^T W_k != 0' is neither sufficient nor necessary; 'positive softmax mass always moves the output' was unproved. Overclaimed: (e) is already in Petrov-Torr-Bibi 2024 Sec. 2.2 (informally Li-Liang 2021 Sec. 7.2), and the strictness of prompt inside P-tuning v2 is asserted there, not open. Repaired: header credits Petrov et al. and Li-Liang, keeping only the monoid organisation, explicit position hypotheses and slice typing; (e) drops the relative-encoding disjunct and gives the RoPE fix (rotate keys by -m); (f) defines 'standard head' (no add_bias_kv, add_zero_attn, sinks, softmax+1), states the exact criterion W_o != 0 and (W_q != 0 or W_k^T b_q != 0) with a witness, and proves unpointedness by a compactness argument when W_o W_v != 0; (c) rigidity is relative to the same layer's computation; (d) uses tanh(g_l) per head (paper) vs HF's per-layer raw scalar, one prompt per adapted layer. Commentary and Exposé XI open item 7 updated on strictness.","public_statement":"For a fixed query, softmax attention is a monoid homomorphism into (normaliser, weighted value sum) followed by division. As a consequence, a prefix acts as an input-gated parallel adapter (He et al. 2022) that rescales, but never reorders, the content attention weights within its layer (Petrov–Torr–Bibi 2024), while LLaMA-Adapter's separate softmax with a zero-initialised gate leaves those weights unchanged and is the identity at initialisation. In causal decoders whose content positions are offset by the prompt length, every soft prompt is realised exactly by the deep prefix made of its own per-layer keys and values (Petrov–Torr–Bibi 2024; informally Li–Liang 2021). For a vanilla softmax head with $W_o\\ne0$ and $W_q\\ne0$, a generic prefix makes the head non-affine even on single-token inputs, so prefix tuning cannot be folded into the head's own weights, and when $W_oW_v\\ne0$ no prefix is neutral."}]};
